\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Iran Stalls And Threatens; Foreign Threats Hiding In Plain Sight; Iranian Arms Dealer Living In U.S. For 10+ Years; Tech Giants Cut U.S. Jobs, Seeking H-1B Visas; 85K New H-1B Visas Annually; Laying Off U.S. Workers, Hiring Foreigners; Never-Trumpers Desperate To Be Adored By Left; Mike Luttig, The Left's New Favorite Defector; Luttig, Now A Fixture On Anti-Trump Tv; Democrats Descend On Michigan; Democrats Go Full Doomsday In Michigan; Music With Mamdani, Obama Sings With A Socialist; Former Presidents Reflects On Where America Stands; Obama Drags On Abut Nothing; America Reads The Bible Kicks Off In D.C.; Speaker Johnson Opens Up About Faith; Israeli Soldier Sledgehammered A Statue Of Jesus.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,402 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: You can follow me on \uc2 TikTok\uc2 , X, and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @BretBaier for exclusive behind-the-scenes content.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle is now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. My Angle in moments.\par \par \uc2 But first, 27 ships. That's how many the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  has turned away since the start of that blockade of Iranian ports, including one ship that didn't listen to.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Motor vessel Touska, Motor vessel Touska. Vacate your engine room. Vacate your engine room. We're prepared to subject you to disabling fire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We tried to warn them, but an Iranian cargo ship that tried to bust through our blockade was disabled by a navy strike over the weekend, and now we're just days away from the expiration of that ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran. And tonight, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is describing the situation as a tense calm.\par \par \uc2 As of this hour, new talks are expected, we guess, in Pakistan, and J.D. Vance is reportedly traveling there soon. But Iran is doing what it always does, it's stalling, and it's threatening. Its Parliament speaker posting on X, before the show, that the U.S. violated the ceasefire when it struck and seized that cargo ship, and Iran's Speaker warning, "We do not accept negotiations under the shadow of threats, and in the past two weeks, we have prepared to reveal new cards on the battlefield."\par \par \uc2 Well, factions within what's left of the Iranian leadership, from the military to the Ayatollah's crew, to the mullahs, they're all over the place, like multiple personalities. Iran's foreign minister wants to continue negotiations, and he says, ensure peace, but the hard liners in the IRGC have other ideas.\par \par \uc2 Ahmad Vahidi\uc2  took power as Commander-in-Chief of the IRGC just days into the war after Khamenei and his top generals were killed. Now, he is a tough one, and he is linked to some of the deadliest attacks by Iran-backed networks abroad, and reportedly kept up a relationship with Al Qaeda after September 11. Fabulous. He is so bad that the reporters in the Middle East described his predecessors as school teachers compared to him. So, apparently that's who is calling the shots now. But what does that mean for our peace talks, or is there any point to them?\par \par \uc2 For the latest on this, we're joined by Chuck DeVore, retired Army Intel Officer and Chief National Initiatives Officer at the \uc2 Texas Public Policy Foundation\uc2 . Chuck, that is the age-old question, is it not? It's good to see you. Does anyone really know who is calling the shots here in Iran?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK DEVORE, RETIRED ARMY INTELLIGENCE OFFICER: I don't think so, Laura. I think what's happening right now is that the leadership was so fractured, so devastated during the initial phases of the U.S.-Israeli strikes on Iran that they really don't have a unified command structure anymore, and because the IRGC has the guns, they're the ones calling the shots, and that's why you have this huge dichotomy between the foreign ministry and the negotiations in Islamabad, Pakistan, and then the results or the truth on the ground, where one side says, Oh, yeah, the strait is open, and then the IRGC says, well, not so fast.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, what happens with the administration's efforts at this point? I mean -- so, you have J.D. Vance, it looks like he is leaving at any moment to go to Pakistan for more talks, but if he is going to agree to something with the foreign minister that's going to be undermined by the IRGC, I mean, I'm sure he loves all that travel. But again, what's the point?\par \par \uc2 DEVORE: Well, Laura, I think the point is very much about international relations and diplomacy. We have to show the world and we have to show the American public that we are trying, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying very hard to have a true and lasting peace with an Iran that is not a threat to American national interests and is not a threat to the region or even to Europe. So, it's very important to try. Now, the question is, what happens when the talks obviously fail? At least I think they'll fail.\par \par \uc2 Laura, the last two weeks, you've seen enormous amounts of armament moved into the region. You've seen more aircraft. Very important maintenance has occurred on both the aircraft and the ships as well as the personnel. So, what you're likely to see is an amazing, an enormous amount of ordnance being dropped on Iran within just a few hours, record amounts of ordnance, and I think it will be targeted, not at bridges and not at power plants, but at the IRGC leadership. They have to be shown that they can't win, and we have to break their will.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Biden's former Senior Energy Advisor, whatever that means, believes that Trump opened the Pandora's box here with this war. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN, FORMER SENIOR ENERGY ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: It shouldn't take just three days to do a nuclear deal. It's really, really critical.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN, HOST, FACE THE NATION WITH MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOCHSTEIN: But if the straits are not opened soon, the leverage that they have, and my concern is, no matter how the war ends, the Iranians now have a card they never had before in practice. In theory, we knew they can close the straits, but they never did. And now, for the foreseeable future, they have this card against us and against their neighbors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. This is the talking point. You hear it on MS NOW by all their experts as well. Iran is really calling all the shots here. In fact, I heard this this morning, I believe it was this morning, that Iran has won this war. Is there any truth to that?\par \par \uc2 DEVORE: This is so laughably false. It's amazing to me to think. If you were to go back two months and to say that we were able to accomplish what we've accomplished so far against Iran, and to have something like two dozen casualties, to have no pilots lost behind enemy lines, to have no aircraft directly shot down by Iranian surface-to-air missiles, people would have said that you were insane to expect such an outcome, and now to see Iran so seriously degraded, the steel factory that made the missile bodies that Iran was firing at Israel, the Gulf Arab states, and even Europe, that's destroyed, several of the drone manufacturing facilities destroyed, and now, with this embargo, this blockade, right, we got a ship with things from China that Iran is using to create armaments, to create missiles that they're fired at everyone else.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. We have the power, Chuck. There is no doubt about that. We have the military. We have the power. But President Trump is better at politics than anyone out there, and he knows the public support for this is not where he wants it to be, and the midterms are coming like a freight train --\par \par \uc2 DEVORE: Oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- and he wants this thing settled.\par \par \uc2 But Chuck, this is fascinating. We'll see how this plays out. Thanks, as always, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Now, the strait will eventually be open, we know that, by force or negotiations, and when it is, vessels will have to still kind of move by what we think are these mines that were dropped by Iranians before their navy was destroyed. But now we could demand the regime tell us where they are, but apparently can't find them all. So, the U.S. Navy is actually going in to find them if they exist. There are different kinds of mines, but the most troublesome, of course, the most dangerous, are the sea mines lurking right at the bottom of the strait.\par \par \uc2 Tom Sauer knows all about this topic. He is a former Explosive Ordnance Disposal Officer in the U.S. Navy. Tom, educate us, please. What equipment is the navy using and why are these mines so difficult to find?\par \par \uc2 TOM SAUER (RET.), U.S. NAVY LIEUTENANT COMMANDER, & FORMER NAVY EOD OFFICER: Thanks for having me again. To give you an idea, we believe that the IRGC Navy may have laid perhaps a dozen, maybe a few more mines. But keep in mind, as of an hour ago, there have been no mines have been found. No ship has struck a mine, and we have not found any. And the thing is, the danger area, the hazard area that the Iranians laid out saying this is a hazardous area, i.e., in other words, we might have laid some mines here, it's about 540 square miles. It's still -- the Strait of Hormuz is not big, but that's still an awful lot of water.\par \par \uc2 So, what we're using right now, through my former line of work, my Navy EOD brothers, are using expeditionary mine countermeasures, and this involves primarily unmanned underwater vehicles, which like including the Mark 18 family of systems that are programmed. It looks like a torpedo and we put in the water. It can be deployed from any ship, or even small boats, to platform agnostic, and it will search the area using side scan sonar, synthetic aperture array sonar that searches the whole area. We recover the vehicle. We do our mission analysis, and then we search. But as of right now, we have been actively searching for a while now, and as of right now, we are yet to find any of these Iranian mines.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I really want to see them as great as our silhouettes are of what they look like, Tom.\par \par \uc2 SAUER: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, I 