ders. But that is super sad.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Don't worry. Don't worry, Laura. Pepe Bill Clinton rushed in to offer this vibrant message about America's future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: A lot of people didn't think we'd make it this long. I think it'd a good thing if we made it longer. Because if I do, then I have to favor cooperation over constant conflict. And I have to be willing to lose as well as win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So, America can survive, Laura. If America could go on so long as it's willing to lose, what an inspiring message for 250 years. I mean, Laura, you can go longer but you're willing to lose. OK.\par \par \uc2 Now, how a president articulates what the country means to them, Laura, it truly also demonstrates how they govern. We join this answer from Barack Obama, already in progress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: If we pay attention to our responsibilities and our duties, and if we extend respect and thoughtfulness to our fellow citizens, even if we disagree with them, if we understand that part of this democratic project.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: If I let it go on any longer, Laura, he'll be cutting into Gutfeld's hour, so I thought we should end it there. I started listening to this this morning. By the time it was over, I'd grown a beard. I had to shave again. I mean, the guy goes on and on. He never shuts up.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, I was on the Gregorian Chant, you know, I was listening to last night when I couldn't fall asleep. I went back to that during --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You could take a nap and grab a tea --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Back to Bill Clinton, Ray, he's kind of disappearing. Like, everything is getting -- right?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He and Biden are like the opposite of Benjamin Button. They're Button Benjamin. They're aging at a rapid pace. It's frightening.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. House Speaker Mike Johnson, along with other Christian leaders, kicked off a weekend long America Reads the Bible event in D.C. That's an awesome thing for America's 250th. Got actors, politicians, thinkers. Even the President is going to be reading verses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And he said, O Lord God of my Master Abraham, I pray you send me good speed this day and show kindness to my Master Abraham.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's awesome.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's beautiful that they're reading the Bible. They're doing this all week, Laura. And it's nice that faith is getting, you know, enmeshed in this celebration.\par \par \uc2 And look, speaking of faith, we've got to mention this story. The Israeli military has confirmed a disturbing photo that emerged of an IDF soldier in southern Lebanon desecrating a statue of the crucified Jesus with a sledge hammer. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 This thing, I mean, people went crazy when they saw this. Justifiably, the Israeli Prime Minister condemned it as defacing a religious symbol, writing on X that he was stunned and surprised by what he saw and saddened.\par \par \uc2 And Ambassador Huckabee said, swift, severe, and public consequences are needed. This looks awful, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, yeah, with all the -- also, all the brutal attacks on Christians who are in Lebanon. As you said, one of the oldest Christian civilizations left in Lebanon. In Bethlehem, we have Christians who have been persecuted. Palestinian Christians have suffered. You know, all people have suffered in this ongoing conflict. But this was outraged.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know the Israelis are battling Hezbollah in Lebanon. They destroyed 39 villages, OK, driving Christians out. These are ancient Maronite villages and ancient Christian villages. The Latin patriarch said how deeply disturbed he was by this. We've got to be careful.\par \par \uc2 And Israel has to be careful about displacing these ancient communities. Their lives matter too. And they're caught in the crossfire because Hezbollah hates them. And then you've got, you know, the Israelis moving into their villages.\par \par \uc2 The world has to take notice of this as well. Hezbollah should be stamped out, but watch these Christian communities.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is horrific and it made my blood boil. And for all the Christians and all those who are suffering. And Hezbollah, they've got to be wiped out for sure, but can't do that to those people. Raymond, thank you. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020260421em4k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And Iran Announce That Strait Of Hormuz Traffic Is Resuming; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Touts Popular "No Tax On Tips" In Vegas; Jimmy Kimmel To Critics: Don't Tell Me What My Job Is; Purple Democrats Freaks Out During RFK Jr. Hearing; Women Looking For Men Without No Online Presence; Are Gen Z Employees High On The Job?\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Kayleigh McEnany, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters,  Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,214 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 STEVE HILTON (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF CALIFORNIA: And because, as you say, we've got all these advantages, if we just get rid of all of that, I think California is going to thrive and flourish again, be part of the Golden Age, just as President Trump, who endorsed me, believes we can -- we can do it.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, we'll leave it there. Steve, thanks for being with us here today. Enjoy that beautiful scenery. We hope you enjoy your weekend. It's now time --\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi everyone. I'm Shannon Bream along with \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld who's having a hard time holding his liquor at this time on a Friday afternoon. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is set to talk at a Turning Point USA event in Phoenix minutes from now, it's the first time he'll be on camera after a major breakthrough in the conflict with Iran. If he stops to talk, we will listen in here. Let's see. OK, listen in.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It should be good. We've had some very good discussions, and hopefully that subject that you like to talk about would be very good, and we've done a good job, but we'll see. The talks are going on. It will go on over the weekend, and a lot of good things are happening, and that includes Lebanon too. Lebanon too is --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's a reporting today that there's still -- Iran says there's significant differences.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, there could be. Let's see what happens. If there are, we'll have to straighten it out. But I don't think there's too many significant differences.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What will it take to end the blockade?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : When the agreement is signed, the blockade ends. Soon as the agreement gets signed, that's when the blockade ends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  did have a meeting at the White House?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Who?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, just a quick couple of words there on Iran. Which by the way, we've got the Strait of Hormuz. He was addressing that there.\par \par \uc2 So, the president and Iran's foreign minister both declaring the Strait is, "Completely open to all ships after the Israel and Lebanon cease fire went into effect." You heard the president mention Lebanon there, the president posting multiple Truth Socials, where he announced among other things that the blockade will remain in place until there's a 100 percent completed deal. Just send that again. Iran agreed they will never close the Strait again. Iran has agreed to stop enriching uranium, and the U.S. will get all the nuclear dust, he called it with no money exchanging hands in any way.\par \par \uc2 So, here's the president last night on how the war is going.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I will say the war in Iran is going along swimmingly. We can do whatever we want, and it should be -- It should be ending pretty soon. It was perfect. It's perfect. It was the power we have, our military. We have the most powerful military anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Democrats and the media are not fans of Trump's Strait Talk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): There is no success in this war. This was a war that was a huge mistake to begin with, and so we should just get out now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's been a real demonstration of strategic power here by the Iranian regime.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MS NOW HOST: Do you think the president will get credit for solving a problem he created?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's like, you wake up in the morning, you take a hammer, you smash your face, you look horrible. And then -- and then you get great treatment, and in three weeks. Oh, you look good again. What was that all about?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's talk about what was all about. And Kayleigh, listen, presidents, Democrat, Republican for decades have been talking about the problem that Iran is. So, the suggestion that he created the problem. He says, I'm trying to solve the problem nobody else was brave enough to take on.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, no doubt. 47 years and Republican presidents, Democrat presidents, have articulated the goal, which is a non-nuclear Iran. He took it on and he took it on with force.\par \par \uc2 To those Democrats, I just want to postulate a counterfactual to them. Few rewound 12 months. OK, so let's go back to April 2025, and if you said to those Democrats, what would you say Democrats, if we told you 12 months from now, that Maduro would be in handcuffs, that Fordow would be bombed, that Gaza there would be a peace deal, a cease fire, that seven other conflicts would be solved, that Israel and Lebanon would be engaging in talks for the first time in oh, wait, 34 years. Perhaps joining the Abraham Accords.\par \par \uc2 If you told them the Ayatollah is dead, Fordow bombed, as I mentioned, and the Strait of Hormuz still opened, they would say impossible, impossible to do that in 12 months.\par \par \uc2 But President Trump has proven that he makes the impossible possible, and he does it because he puts force behind words, and you saw this with the naval blockade. He said, we will board your ships Iran. And what happened? No Iranian ships went out of the port, went through the Strait, because they knew he meant action. He has taken action.\par \par \uc2 And this is the hardest deal, I think, that that could ever be made, because you are sitting across from someone who is not a rational actor, someone who does not care about self-preservation, someone who does not care if they die because they have religious fanatical beliefs as to what happens when they die. This is the hardest deal ever made, and if he pulls this off, he is the greatest deal maker of all time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Harold, does the conversation on your side of the aisle change if indeed these things hold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, good to be with you and happy Friday. I think the conversation will change with both parties. I would agree, if everything that Kayleigh says comes true, the president -- the president deserves a tremendous amount of credit. I think the blockade was the smart thing to do, but even the president just now said, we don't have a deal yet.\par \par \uc2 I'm hopeful that if the four things -- the five things that we listed at the start of the show, at the top of the show, if all of those things come true, this may be the greatest deal ever achieved by a president in conflict.\par \par \uc2 But we don't know that to be the case, and I'm rooting for that to be the case. When one thing I -- it's unclear to me, when he says no cash will exchange hands and we've seen reports today from other outlets, including our own, that there's a deal on the table for $20 billion for the Iranians, which is not American dollars, even the -- even the Obama, when he was president, what they did was they unfroze Iranian assets, so there were no American tax dollars going.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Democrats like that then. So, if this does turn out to be the deal, part of the deal now, would that be OK?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I'm not -- so Shannon, I don't -- I don't care if it's 50 billion for one reason, if we can -- the only thing that matters to me, and that list of things we put up, is that they can't enrich uranium, and if they do not have a nuclear weapon, if that cost $75 billion, you think about what the life of that contract, $75 billion in the next 30, 40, 50, 60 years for my kids, your -- for grandkids, everybody around the table for kids not to have to deal with this regime. It'd be -- it'd be well worth it.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm praying the president gets this done. But as he just said there, this deal is not done yet. So, we've got to be hopeful and prayerful that we can figure out a way to get this.\par \par \uc2 Make no doubt, this blockade there, this is what's created the economic hardship and pressure enforced them to the table.\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Well, and Jesse, the president has said a lot about how the U.S. did this without NATO, without some of our other allies who he's called them out for not showing up when he said, we needed you, too late now.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, the president said we win the war in six weeks, and it took seven, so I feel lied to.\par \par \uc2 We actually did win in six weeks when Iran called for the cease fire and the blockade was just to squeeze the deal, and we've squeezed one heck of a deal. If the reporting is accurate, this basically is unconditional surrender, no enrichment. We take the uranium, no terror funding, and Iran can't rebuild its military because we hit all their defense factories.\par \par \uc2 This is it. This is what you would call a complete and total victory. And if you go back and look at the tape where he put out that Saturday, on the day of the decapitation strike, listen to the objectives. Every objective has been achieved, and that would make this war the most swift, decisive, surgical effective, probably the greatest war in American history, if this thing holds.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: World War II was pretty good, just for the record.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, it didn't take 38 days, Harold. OK, I'm just saying.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What about the war on Christmas?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're still fighting that. We call it forever war.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was not over.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You're right. There's still a lot of work to do diplomatically, absolutely, and anything could happen, but we have to win the peace.\par \par \uc2 But he has remade, if this thing goes well, the Middle East, he has brought Jews, Arabs and Persians all together in one economic zone under an American security umbrella, and boxed the Chinese and the Russians out.\par \par \uc2 And the stock markets hit an all-time high in oil futures for June, they're at like $81.00, you could not have executed this better. I'm just so proud of the War Department, of the president and all of those service members that fought so bravely in this effort.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Greg, are you going to rebook your Strait of Hormuz getaway that you had?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm so excited.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The beaches.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, it's -- I mean, he didn't -- it's what Kayleigh -- he didn't kick the can down the road. He just decided to crush it. You know, no one else did this.\par \par \uc2 So, if you -- you know, if you watch -- a lot of people watch this show who are Democrats, because this is the best show on television, but they hate watch it. They have to ask themselves, why do those guys on THE FIVE know what's going to happen and how it's going to happen, and why do we get everything wrong?\par \par \uc2 And like, I don't have any special insider information, it's the filter that we use when we're looking at what Trump does. Our filter in this era notes the patterns. This is what Trump does, not a fascist, not bloodthirsty. He goes for free money on the table, decisively, low risk, high reward. Get in, get out.\par \par \uc2 And his actions align perfectly with a movie style outcome. Think -- this would make your life so better. Think about when Trump does something. How will it look like a movie? There's always a first act, there's always a second act, there's always a third act. 2016, 2020, 2024 the second act is always the when things think like it's going to go bad, but then at the end, boom, I would say tariffs were in the second act. You know, we don't know what's going to happen, but I'm betting it's going to turn out pretty well.\par \par \uc2 But you always notice it's just like a movie. Now, you look at the other side, these people at home that are watching this and going, Greg looks great. It's got to be exhausting to be wrong every day, like you recently, believed that a female illegal was being held captive by ICE, and it turned out that was a hoax. And you try to forget about it, you can go back a couple of years the border agents with being a Haitian immigrant that turned out to be wrong.\par \par \uc2 I'm jumping all over because there's so many. Trump was going to send troops into Iran. People -- Hegseth was going to be fired any day. I could go on and on, but I won't.\par \par \uc2 It amazes me with a constant misreading of reality, you might want to question the filter that you're using to look at the world, nothing personal. It might not even be your fault, but this filter is destroying your perceptive abilities to know what is going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 I am not a military or political genius, but I saw this. Right now, the media, when they're reporting it, are a coping industry. They're designed to produce an anti-reality, so that all this great news is really bad news to help each other in the industry cope. The media is now like your worst enabling friend who keeps telling you that you don't have a drug problem and that your fly isn't really down. In this relationship, truth is now being replaced by therapy in the long term, though, every day you're wrong. Except for Harold.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: By the way, your skin does look great.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It does. I think it's the no sugar thing, but it's working for you. OK. Coming up next, President Trump hits up Las Vegas and deals out a no tax on tips jackpots.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: President Trump taking over Sin City last night, cashing in on his no tax on tips and entertaining the crowd like a Vegas headliner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The young waitress came up to me during dinner and she said, sir, we should have no tax on tips. I said, what? Say it again, we should have no tax on tips. I said that's the greatest thing I've ever heard. Sharon delivered McDonald's to the Oval Office. It was a little bit of a, you know, I mean, to be honest, a little tacky.\par \par \uc2 Restaurants, strike leaders, corner stores. What is a corner store? I've never heard that term. I know what a corner store is, but I've never heard it described a corner store. Who the hell wrote that, please?\par \par \uc2 Seniors are loving me, and I love them, of course, of course, I'm not a senior, so I -- you know, I cater to the seniors, but I don't happen to be a senior myself. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But the Democrats could care less about Americans saving money. James Carville putting all his chips on getting revenge on MAGA. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: If the Democrats were in the presidency in both houses of Congress, I think on day one, they should make Puerto Rico D.C. a state, and it should expand the Supreme Court to 13.\par \par \uc2 (EXPLETIVES DELETED) it. Eat our dust. They've done everything they could. They held up the 2,000 election. They stole it. They've stolen Supreme Court seats. They've gerrymandered everything that you can. And the only way to fight this is, don't run on it. Don't talk about it. Just do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, I mean, he is talking about it, Kayleigh, so if I were running, I would say this is what the Democrats are going to do if they win.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, James Carville has told me to my face, we will nuke the filibuster. D.C. and Puerto Rico will become states. We will pack the Supreme Court. Republicans, Republicans, Republicans. Listen to the opponent when they tell you the plan.\par \par \uc2 If I'm President Trump, I've got to be secretly so frustrated with these congressional Republicans, outside of Mike Lee, Anna Paulina Luna, Tim Burchett and a select few others that are willing to fight. Rick Scott, I would include in that list. They are making him single handedly fund DHS, find work arounds, try to secure voting via the SAVE America Act. Now, the states are trying to pick up the gap where Congress left off. Your opponents will do this.\par \par \uc2 Maybe if they take over the Senate this time, I don't think they will take the Senate back, but four years from now, six years from now, whenever that happens, it will happen. And I wish Republicans had half the backbone that Democrats have when they hold the gavel, because, as one senator said to me, "We're going to look like schmucks if we don't do this now." And that was Ron Johnson, another great senator.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Harold, so tax day, everybody got the refund. The big, beautiful bill kicking in. Is this something that Republicans can really jam down your throat in November?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I don't like things being jammed down my throat, but I do think I know where you're going. The president, where he's out and in Las Vegas, with the no taxes on tips. I think the question for a lot of people, and it's going to be curious to see. I don't -- I don't know the answer. I read some of the data and suggest that the tax break a lot of Americans are getting are a lot of that is now being consumed by over the last few months, and hopefully we'll see, if the deal gets done, you'll see lower gas prices, but certainly gas prices and higher food prices and really, probably the biggest dampers, the housing costs for so many Americans. So, we'll see, we'll see where it goes here.\par \par \uc2 But if I'd be talking about the things that you guys are talking about, I don't know if James Carville has a strong theory of the case, but he won't be in the Senate or the Congress. He'll have his theory, and we'll see if people follow it.\par \par \uc2 But the candidates that are winning around the country for the last year and a half, they've only been talking about economic issues and affordability issues. And if Democrats can stay there and not talk about the things that my friend James was talking about just now, Democrats have a much better chance of winning in the fall.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, if the president wraps up this excursion and signs a deal, do you think he'll come back and pivot back to the economy in time to get it going for the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I think that definitely Republicans want him to. We know all of our polling and all the pulling out there that people say that they personally don't feel the benefits yet. It's just like it was happening during the Biden administration, when there would be data that would say, oh, the future is looking better. People would be like, well, I filled out my gas today and it was not good, and I didn't like it.\par \par \uc2 So, the more he talks about the message, and he can do this, because I think he loves being in campaign mode. And this year, though he's not on the ballot, the GOP wants him out there as if he is on the ballot, because his base is what needs to turn out.\par \par \uc2 In these elections, he likes to be out there campaigning, and he needs to campaign as if he is on the ballot, because if he loses the House and Senate, he's going to be impeached. I think we all know that's absolutely goin