 INGRAHAM: And Darren, I tweeted over the weekend, where are the parents? Where are the parents?\par \par \uc2 PORCHER: The parents hold a significant role in this. Ultimately, the social contract needs to be upheld. And you, as a parent, should be held liable if your kid is out committing to rambunctious behavior and creating a lawless environment, such as what we see on this television screen.\par \par \uc2 And it's a problematic narrative because the elected officials can do a lot more. But in many cases, you'll have elected officials that don't want to present the militaristic approach of police standing on guard in protecting that society. They look to go the other route from the perspective of police and community relationships.\par \par \uc2 And that police and community relationship works well at one point, but at a certain point you need to be somewhat more aggressive and more forward in your approach to police.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, well, fathers have to be present, moms have to be present, whoever's a role model to these kids have to be present, it's mostly young males. Darren, this has to stop. Thank you for your perspective tonight.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the Pope and the President, or is it the Pope versus the President? Or is it the President versus the Pope? What's really at stake here? Seen and Unseen with Raymond Arroyo is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, the media running wild with this story that President Trump and the Pope are engaged in a war of words.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, there's a reason for all of this, Laura, which I'm going to get to in a moment. President Trump took to Truth Social last night, writing, Pope Leo is weak on crime and terrible on foreign policy. Later that night, he elaborated on this long social media post this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Why did you attack Pope Leo on Truth Social?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I don't think he's doing a very good job. He likes crime, I guess. We don't like a pope that's going to say that it's OK to have a nuclear weapon. We don't want a pope that says crime is OK in our cities. I don't like it. I'm not a big fan of Pope Leo.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And today, Pope Leo clapped back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 POPE LEO: I do not look at my role as being political, politician. I don't want to get into a debate with him. I don't think that the message of the gospel is meant to be abused in the way that some people are doing. And I will continue to speak out loud against war. And I think someone has to stand up and say there's a better way to do this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, I mean, look, let's start with the President. This was a sloppy and frankly disrespectful attack on the Pope. I don't think he should have issued this kind of long ad hominem. He didn't get any points for doing this. There's nothing good that comes of it. So, you know, he shouldn't have gone down this path.\par \par \uc2 On the Pope's side, the Pope says he's not a politician. That's true. He shouldn't be. But I understand in the President's reading of this, when you have a Pope saying, call your congressman, call your senator, to get them to advocate for peace and oppose this war.\par \par \uc2 When the pope says, as he did on Easter Sunday, let those who have weapons lay them down, Laura, that sounds political particularly when you've got Swiss guardsmen with arms protecting you. And I don't remember the pope saying that about China.\par \par \uc2 So, these are personal opinions. I think they're talking past each other. But this thing is way out of control.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He withdrew that post, right? He deleted it. One of the posts that he --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, no, no, no, no. He withdrew an image. You'll see in a moment. But not that post. He hasn't.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. So, you think this has been kind of intentionally? Kind of created by the media?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, yeah, last night on 60 Minutes, Norah O'Donnell featured three left-of-center cardinals. You'll remember Cardinal Cupich, who tried to give Dick Durbin, a Catholic lifelong supporter of abortion, a lifetime achievement award recently. Well, these three cardinals condemned ICE agents, the Iran War, and the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 So, look, clearly the president was watching this. Norah O'Donnell has now even confirmed. She talked to the president. He was watching. You've heard of rope-a-doping? This is Pope-a-doping. Norah O'Donnell seems to have known what she was doing, Laura.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This all kind of started after our 60 Minutes report last night that included criticism, not only from the Pope, but also these three influential cardinals. It appears that President Trump may have been watching that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mm-mm-mm.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Well, those were red cardinals there, Ray, for sure.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, here's the big problem with this. This comes on the eve of the midterms. This is clearly, it feels like a political whiff of something moving here. The Pope met with David Axelrod last week. And now you've got these three cardinals, who I talked to a number of bishops today, these three cardinals represent absolutely nobody but themselves, OK?\par \par \uc2 So, it feels like they were trying to provoke the president into a reaction, and he fell into the trap. And look, for a sense of how this is shaking out, the president of Iran, Laura, has weighed in, expressing support for Pope Leo and condemning Trump over this A.I. image generated of the Pope, or the president rather, as a Christ-like figure.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian president said it's a desecration of Jesus, Laura. The image on its face is sacrilegious. I'm glad the president took this down.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, this was much into about nothing and it all could have been avoided. Don't react to 60 Minutes and whatever they're portraying there. And the Pope should stick to doctrine and not personal opinion in politics.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. It's not the first time I've said this, Raymond, but you don't have to swing at every pitch. There's a lot going on right now and probably reacting to what Pope Leo is saying.\par \par \uc2 The church has always been, you know, not MAGA on immigration. They want more bodies in the seats. I guess their wall is good in Vatican City, but our wall isn't. You know they have a big wall.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah. And look, the Pope is always going to come down on the side of peace, Laura. That's what a Pope should do. But when they get into policy prescriptions that specific, it's always been trouble. I think broad -- the moral frameworks is what the Pope should be advocating, and he is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, thank you for that perspective. Very important tonight. Jesse's next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020260414em4d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 One Crew Member Found After F-15E Downing; U.S. Fighter Jet Show Down Over Iran; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Receives Briefing On F-15E Crew Rescue Operation; Program To Allow Coverage For Some Cannabis Products; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Task Force To Eliminate Government Benefits Fraud; Dr. Mehmet OZ Interviewed On Fraud Crackdown At Centers For Medicare And Medicaid Services; Reporting Indicates Possible Increase In Religiosity Among Young Americans; U.S. F-15 Fighter Jet Shot Down Over Iran; Pilot From Downed Fighter Jet Rescued While Second Crew Member Still Missing; March Jobs Report Shows More Hiring Than Economists Expected.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jennifer Griffin, Alexandria Hoff, Mike Tobin, Peter  Doocy, Lauren Green \par \uc2 8,026 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Tickets are almost sold out everywhere, so you better get on it while you still can.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Paul?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, if you're wondering where Greg and the other guy, oh Jesse, are today, since they're not here.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Doesn't ring a bell.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: We have some footage. And there they are. I think that's Greg right there in the red hat.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: He's the one whose feet can't touch the ground.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: All right, we got to go. All right, have a good night.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: His can't touch the ground. It's not fair for Greg.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Let's just keep Tyrus going. Hold on. OK, all right. Thanks, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, an intense search is underway right now for one of two American crew members who ejected from a fighter jet shot down over Iran. The other crew member has already been recovered.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight. Peter Doocy at the White House tells us what the Trump administration is doing and saying at this hour. \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Tel Aviv with how Israel is aiding the U.S. But we begin with Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Fox News has learned that in addition to the U.S. fighter jet, an A-10 Warthog, also was struck by enemy fire, but managed to return to safety.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. F-15E was shot down late Thursday night while it was still dark before the sun rose on Friday morning in Iran. U.S. officials tell me there was enemy fire during the rescue of one of the two F-15E crew members, and there is a very risky search and rescue operation still ongoing right now.\par \par \uc2 Video from Iran appears to show one of the American rescue helicopters being hit by enemy fire, black smoke coming off of its tail. An F-15E strike eagle has a pilot and a weapon systems operator on board. It is the workhorse of the Air Force, a dual role fighter designed to perform air to air and air to ground missions. It can fight at low altitude, day or night and in all weather.\par \par \uc2 This photo shows the ejection seat and parachute of one of the strike Eagle crew members who ejected after being shot down. Both crew members would have been given extensive SERE survival and evasion training. The rescued crew member is being treated for his wounds tonight, the extent of those injuries is not clear.\par \par \uc2 Markings on the wreckage from the tail shown here would indicate this was an aircraft assigned to the 494th Fighter Squadron at Royal Air Force Lakenheath in England, based on its red band, this is one of the units that has been deployed to the U.S. Central Command area of operation since the start of the war 35 days ago.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. F-15E was shot down over Khuzestan province in southwest Iran. The Zagros Mountain Range cuts through the remote province. Images of the Pave Hawk rescue helicopters being refueled during daylight shows the urgency of the rescue efforts, but also the vulnerability of U.S. aircraft to anti- aircraft fire, despite claims from the Pentagon and Joint Chiefs of Staff General Dan Caine that the U.S. has air superiority over Iran, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll talk about that with an expert in just a bit. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Israel is taking action to try to try to help the recovery efforts. Senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  has that part of the story live from Tel Aviv. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret, a couple of things we have from the Israeli. One is that they have halted all strikes, particularly in the area where U.S. personnel are searching for that missing flight crew member. Also that Israel is sharing Intel with their American counterparts, as they have been throughout the entirety of this war and before the war.\par \par \uc2 An Iranian television anchor with a state controlled media has put out the call for the locals to take custody of any U.S. personnel found on the ground for an unspecified reward, and this quickly went into the Iranian propaganda machine, with the spokesman for the Revolutionary Guard saying it disproves the claim that air defenses have been destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. EBRAHIM ZOLFAGHARI, IRANIAN MILITARY SPOKESMAN (through translator): Following false claims by the U.S. president about the complete destruction of Iran's air defense system, an American hostile fighter jet was shot down in Central Iranian airspace by a newly developed, advanced aerospace defense system operated by the IRGC.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: And the area of the search is scarcely populated. It's mountainous and dry, and you're talking about 6,000 square miles. It has been dark in this part of the world for better than six hours now, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin live in Tel Aviv. Mike, thank you. Let's find out what the White House is saying and doing at this hour. Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn with that. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Most recently, the latest thing we have is that President Trump just said he is not ready to announce what the United States is going to do if it turns out that Iran harms this missing U.S. crew member. That is, according to an interview he just gave with the independent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): A U.S. Marine posted outside the West Wing for most of the day Friday, because inside the president's national security team has been regularly briefing him about the F-15E crew rescue mission. After the F-15E shoot down became public, the president's first comment on Truth Social was, keep the oil, anyone? He also posted earlier today, our military, the greatest and most powerful by far anywhere in the world hasn't even started destroying what's left in Iran. Bridges next, then electric power plants. New regime leadership knows what has to be done and has to be done fast.\par \par \uc2 The president confirmed this crash won't change his plans for negotiations with Iran, telling NBC, no, not at all. No, it's war. We're in war. That suggests the timeline for ending Epic Fury laid out Wednesday night holds.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are going to finish the job, and we're going to finish it very fast. We're getting very close.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): On Capitol Hill, Democrats are promising to tank President Trump's just released budget proposal, clocking in at $2.2 trillion, up 15 percent from last year, Chuck Schumer, the Senate Minority Leader, says a budget is like an x-ray. It shows what's really going on inside. Donald Trump's budget is rotten to the core, and Democrats will make sure it never passes.\par \par \uc2 A bulk of that budget for the Department of War, $1.5 trillion. The White House says that's to cover new national defense needs that have popped up since the president's term started.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: We also have an increasing eye on the importance of national defense and the potential budgetary cost of that, and so it's fiscally responsible and increasing spending on defense because of all the things that we're seeing in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And we just got a notification a few minutes ago, President Trump signed an executive order looking to stop what they call a financial arms race threatening college sports. So, they are trying here at the White House to stick to the original schedule, while also waiting for word about the fate of this American Crew member missing in Iran, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As are we. Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZOLFAGHARI (through translator): The jet was completely destroyed, and further search operations are still ongoing.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE HARIDOPOLOS (R-FL): I'm very concerned about what could happen next. So, it's very important we won't take care of our course down member there in Iran. But we're optimistic that we can continue to push this against Iran.\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): Well, the problem is because the president has no idea how to get out of this mess that he started, he might very well escalate further. And in a sense, the war is already escalating, because now they have successfully shot down a U.S. s plane. They may have a U.S. hostage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Joining us now, former F-16 pilot Pete Gersten, served for more than 30 years of active duty, also flying the F-15 and the fifth generation F-35 Lightning, former commander of the Air Force Warfare Center. Thank you sir for being here.\par \par \uc2 You know, we're hearing that there is a very intense CSAR, combat search and rescue going on at this hour. Your thoughts at this early stage as we're searching as a country for that second crew member.\par \par \uc2 PETE GERSTEN (RET.), U.S. AIR FORCE: Good evening, Bret. Appreciate you having me. I'll tell you that we're in a very difficult situation right now. Obviously we have an American down behind enemy lines. Doesn't matter which side the line you're on, which side the aisle you're on. He's an American. He's missing right now, and every American should be praying for his safe return.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, going through this as a pilot, you take this SERE training, survival, evasion, resistance, escape, talk to us about what it is to eject after a plane has been hit, and that process.\par \par \uc2 GERSTEN: So, Bret, every air crew that we have goes through a very elaborate survival, invasion, resistance and escape training program. It's extremely comprehensive. It prepares air crew to survive in a situation where it's -- he's been down, or he or she had been down, the training is handled at the highest level. It both not only the ejection process itself, which teaches them how to successfully get out of the aircraft when it's been struck, but also the descent into the hostile territory. Once you're in hostile territory, the tactic, techniques and procedures, which are highly classified, are ingrained in the air crew. They're very well trained and versed in those tactics. They're tested on those tactics. And every time we get into a situation where we're training and fast combat scenarios, we run simulations for survival evasion.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm confident the air crew is exquisitely trained. They're doing the best they can. The air crews that are trying to find him and locate them with survival and rescue teams, the CSAR crews are absolutely the world's best at what they do. They're going to find what's going on. You've seen already that A-10s have taken hits. The combat search and rescue helicopters are taking risks. There is no limit to the amount of war fighting spirit that will go and turn it downed airman.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and I don't want to talk about the tactics, but the fact that you had the Iranians out on state T.V. saying, for locals to get these crew members, we've rescued one. And the fact that you haven't seen an image or a picture of another, does that give you hope tonight?\par \par \uc2 GERSTEN: I always have hope. I'm highly confident in that air crew. I'm highly confident of training. And I would tell you that I'm highly confident in the combat, search and rescue, the war fighters we have. I'm confident in the President, our Secretary of War, our chairman of our Joint Chief of Staff, raising cane combat with him. There has never been a better command echelon in sync and support of our American war fighters, and there is no limit to what they will do to return this downed airman.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, sir, thank you for the time, and thank you for your service.\par \par \uc2 GERSTEN: Thank you, Bret, thanks for having me. Bless you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's some other headlines tonight. New York Attorney General Letitia James and a coalition of 22 other attorneys general are filing a lawsuit to block President Trump's executive order to restrict mail in voting. The group calls the move illegal. It says the action would baselessly restrict mail in voting and threaten election officials with prosecution for carrying out their duties.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has learned the U.S. Supreme Court Justice Samuel Alito was treated for dehydration at a hospital in Philadelphia in a previously undisclosed incident, March 20th. The 76-year-old Alito was not admitted, turned home the same night without complications, then continued to hear the case before him, sources say he has not had any issues since.\par \par \uc2 A judge upholds a prior decision to block two Justice Department subpoenas targeting Federal Reserve chairman Jerome Powell, the government can appeal the decision last month that judge threw out those subpoenas relating to a criminal investigation into cost overruns during the Fed's ongoing building renovations.\par \par \uc2 And American employers added a surprisingly strong 178,000 new jobs last month, the Labor Department says the unemployment Rate dipped to 4.3 percent, markets were closed today and observed observation of Good Friday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new program from the Trump administration allowing the use of hemp products for medical purposes. And then we talk about the crackdown on fraud with the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Trump administration is making a major shift in the use of hemp for medical purposes. A pilot program is allowing some products to be incorporated into patient care plans. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It's a crop that once served as the backbone of America's economy and military might, but in recent decades, the federal government's relationship with hemp has been nothing short of a tug of war.\par \par \uc2 This week, though, Washington made clear it's turning over a new leaf. Starting April 1st, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid opened up a new pilot program allowing $500 worth of eligible hemp derived products to be incorporated into patient care plans. It fulfills a 2025 executive order by the president.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: This reclassification order will make it far easier to conduct marijuana related medical research.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Late last month, a federal judge ruled that CMS can go ahead with the plan despite a lawsuit filed by a coalition of anti- marijuana advocacy groups claiming the administrative process was ignored. The groups also highlight that cannabis treatment is not approved by the FDA.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, FDA commissioner Marty Makary wrote a letter confirming the agency will not interfere with CMS plan.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS THARP, CEO, KEEF BRANDS: I think the unique thing that's happened over the last couple months as you've seen some really strong, unlikely alliances form to have a shared front, especially at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Travis Tharp is the former CEO of Keef Brands. He says, while industry optimism is there, the biggest hurdle for cannabis advocates is yet to come. A law that changes the way hemp is defined goes into effect in November. Its aim is to close a loophole related to intoxicating unregulated products, but critics say it will effectively make all hemp illegal.\par \par \uc2 THARP: Unfortunately, it's a very, very big issue in the industry. There are many brands that would face an extinction level event if nothing changes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): So, what would CMS do in this case? Well, the center notes that if the legal limits of hemp were to ever tighten, so would its list of eligible products, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk to the head of the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, Dr. Mehmet Oz, about the government's new fraud crackdown.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 13 in Seattle, as Amazon is slapping a 3.5 percent fuel and logistics surcharge on third party sellers using its platform. The levy begins later this month amid a spike in fuel prices since the start of the Iran conflict.\par \par \uc2 Fox 29 in Philadelphia's Camden County, New Jersey police leaders say the city has reached a 90 percent reduction in homicides compared to the same period in 2012, that was the last full year before the department's overhaul. Authorities also report a 57 percent drop in violent crime and an 89 percent decrease in shootings across Camden's 19 neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at San Francisco from our affiliate Fox 2, one of the big stories there tonight, new research from Stanford University shows something previously thought impossible, a medication that can reverse age related cartilage loss in joints. The findings could drastically change the way arthritis is treated and potentially put an end to the highly common procedure of joint replacement. Researchers caution the treatment still has years of testing before public use.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Our vice president is in charge of a new division. It's called the Fraud Division. You know, if we straighten out half the fraud that took place in this country over the years, we'd have not only a balanced budget, would have a big surplus.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're going to make fraudsters know that they're going to suffer some consequences.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, the gravy train is over, the party is over, and we're coming for fraudsters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. We're pleased to be joined by the CMS administrator, Dr. Mehmet Oz, thanks for doing this.\par \par \uc2 Fraud is the topic of the day, and obviously you're at the forefront of some of this. Broadly, the American people don't have a full sense yet of how big a deal this is, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, CENTERS FOR MEDICARE AND MEDICAID SERVICES: Well, if you just look at the money that we pay the federal government for insurance, which is for Medicare and Medicaid and related programs, we think there's about $100 billion of fraud.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: 100 billion?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Now, let me just put that in perspective that folks can anchor on. A lot of Americans who are working are concerned that Medicare won't be there for them. They worry it won't be solvent. If we're able to take out the fraud in Medicare alone, we would double the life expectancy of the Medicare trust fund. It would add years more to the solvency of this program, which would give comfort to everyone working today that it's going to be there for them.\par \par \uc2 The question really is how to get so bad, and part of it is COVID, because when you start throwing money at problems, you don't watch where it's going. And fraudsters learn they can corrupt the system, because they never realized it was so easy to steal from the government, then they'll start to weaponize it and use it in other areas, like Medicare and Medicaid. We now have it all a government approach.\par \par \uc2 So, yesterday, for example, I spent the pre Dawn hours in Los Angeles, busting into a swank neighborhood home owned by a doctor and a nurse who had been defrauding the hospice system there for millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And this is a big SWAT team move. I mean, you hadn't been on one of those.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I've never been on this, but dozens of young just, you know, hyper prepared individuals. There were drones and planes and these trucks they could drive through gates that they were able to blast through, and no one got hurt. And they stunned these folks in pre Dawn hours, and they were one of seven hospice owners that were shut down yesterday.\par \par \uc2 To put that in perspective, in the prior four years, the governor of California had shut down four hospices. We shut down seven in one night, and I'll get news for you today, Bret, let's announce, make it clear to everybody, we've shut down already just in the last 10 weeks, 221 hospices in California.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wait, in the state of California, 220?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Yes. In Los Angeles that have been the state in Los Angeles. So, we're able to dramatically accelerate a process that have been meandering along in California, and we believe that there are 1,800 hospices in California. That's, by the way, about a third of the entire nation's hospice load is just in Los Angeles. We're going to be able to shut down, we hope, appropriately, half of them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We saw what happened in Minnesota. Now we're seeing the tip of the iceberg in California. Every time we see a fraud bus like this, it's framed as kind of bad actors and that it's happening on such a large scale because the system allows for it. What incentives inside Medicare and Medicaid are actually enabling this kind of systemic issue? It's obviously been going on for years, right?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Well, part of it is that government had been fragmented it in its approach. So, yesterday, the U.S. Attorney Bill Essayli led the charge. He got the office inspector general, the Department of Justice. You can have them working together. We're bringing in Scott Bessent, Treasury. Because guess what? Bank fraud is a big part of this.\par \par \uc2 And we've also got a problem, this is even larger, I think, in that we had told people in the federal government for the last four years under the Biden administration, don't do your job. Literally. We told people who were in charge of fraud that they should just sort of calm down. We don't want a big problem with this. This is not a legitimate issue. Just enroll people.\par \par \uc2 We kept score in Medicare and Medicaid by looking at how many people were in the program, as opposed to we're the right people in the program. Let's extend that now to autism.\par \par \uc2 So, what happened in Minnesota is they would pay moms to dishonestly say their children had autism. Bret, if you have a child in a neighborhood that's autistic and needs support, it's expensive and it's worth the investment. We should do it. It's the right thing.\par \par \uc2 But if every child in the neighborhood is autistic, then no one really is getting those services. So, you take scarce resources and funding and you now stretch it out. But here is the biggest message I can deliver tonight. This is critical.\par \par \uc2 If these corrupt fraudsters are willing to steal your money, they will steal your health, they will steal your life. I've seen it firsthand. Hospice owners who are willing to throw away the benefits that these older folks should have, and they die because they don't realize they've been tricked into signing up for hospice they never wanted. We have same thing happened with durable medical equipment. Bret, we're right now in the epicenter of this. Durable medical equipment suppliers in south Florida outnumber the number of McDonald's by twofold. There's twice as many people selling wheelchairs and canes and braces in south Florida than there are McDonald's. How is that possible?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, so do you have enough people, does the task force of enough stuff, are you able to get after this to the point where the American people can go, OK, this is moving the needle, this is making a difference?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Yes, because the president and the vice president and the task force says yes. So I spent yesterday, when we weren't doing the bust, talking to collaborators and talking about how we're going to be able to recruit the right people back into government, because we need people. And by the way, please, I came on this show in part to recruit folks. There's generational opportunity to serve your nation. If you're interested in this space of fraud, come on. Give us a call. We want to hear your tips.\par \par \uc2 I found out that of the referrals we were getting in my agency from the Office of Inspector General, these are like detectives finding fraud, we were only acting on about a third. Well, if you only act on a third of what detectives are telling you is fraud, you're throwing away a lot of good intel. But also the detectives stop referring cases because they don't think you're serious.\par \par \uc2 I told my team I want 100 percent of their referrals acted on, and I want to hear about it if they don't do this. And that's what everyone across government is doing because the vice president has made it really clear. He doesn't want the gaming anymore. He don't want one arm of government holding back another arm. We are all laser focused on dealing with the crisis the American public is furious about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can A.I. and data processing help with you the fraud detection real time to be able to deal with it on the front end?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Bret, you're so mart. Come work with us.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OZ: What you just said is profoundly important. One of the beauties of A.I. is that we can use it to catch fraud earlier and more accurately, but we can also use it to defend ourselves, because guess who's using A.I.? The fraudsters. They're corrupting the system with sophisticated tools to hide their ill-gotten gains from us. So by us using technology, A.I in particular, we're going to copy what's already happening in banking. You don't think if bank managers are good at fraud. If they were, they wouldn't be bank managers. A.I. is being used to help them think like a fraudster.\par \par \uc2 We're doing the same thing for the first time. We stopped billions of dollars from leaving the door of CMNS. The goal for us is the money never leaves the building and A.I. has helped us do that. Again billions of dollars already because we can identify fraud as soon as the fraudster starts corrupting the system.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And in the big picture, in the background, is insolvency of Medicare, which is now, what, 2032?\par \par \uc2 OZ: 2032 is when we predict the Medicare trust fund to go bankrupt. We can double that life expectancy. Medicaid, a program that I love and adore, the president says he loves and cherishes these programs, let me be crystal clear. If we don't protect these programs from fraud, they will be destroyed by the corrupt system. And we don't want our most vulnerable citizens, folks who have had trouble, as Hubert Humphrey said that adorns the entryway of our building, we want people at the dawn of their life, at the twilight of the life, the young and old, and those living in the shadows, to be protected. If we don't take care of these beautiful programs, the crown jewel of social program, they will implode under the weight of the corruption that's now being pushed on them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, you mentioned the president. His approval overall 43 percent disapprove. On 57 healthcare it's 38 percent, 61 percent disapprove. A lot of Republicans looking at these numbers going, the midterms right around the corner. Do you look at approval? Are you looking at how the public is perceiving some of these actions?\par \par \uc2 OZ: The president saved Medicaid. The president came into office with a proposed additional expenditure of $5.4 trillion in Medicaid alone. We were becoming a big healthcare system with a small country attached. So the president said, don't cut Medicaid. In fact, put more money into Medicaid, and we did, $200 billion more over the next decade. But we're not going to pay for legalized money laundering. And what we've been able to do is create a solvent program which will keep Medicaid alive for the rest of my life, allow Medicare's trust fund to last longer as we deal with the trust fund crisis and the fraud. And by doing those brave decisions in the working families legislation, we added a lot of other health benefits. For example, for the first time ever the largest investment in rural healthcare ever in our country, doubling the amount of money we're putting for Medicaid into these programs that help 60 million Americans who don't live in urban areas.\par \par \uc2 We have done the right things. So when people hear the stories, they nod their head up and down. They just want to know that we're doing the right thing, we're tightening our belt for the right reasons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But when you are in an environment with low approval ratings, you're getting ready for an election, you've had Secretary Noem let go of her position, the attorney general let go of her position, are people inside the administration looking over their shoulder?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I'm not. Secretary Kennedy is not. And the president calls me all the time with very precise, well-targeted questions. He's an excellent executive, a superb leader. And all I need to know is that when I make a tough decision that I know we'll get some blowback on, but it's the right decision, the president says go ahead. And you know what he usually says? I don't care what they say, do what's right for the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Mehmet Oz, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I appreciate you man.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're looking live, a live image of the Artemis II crew inside Orion spacecraft. Tonight the crew is headed toward the moon. NASA says last night's translunar injection burn, taking the capsule out of earth orbit, was flawless. Last night Trace Gallagher spoke with the astronauts on FOX NEWS AT NIGHT.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REID WISEMAN, ARTEMIS II MISSION COMMANDER: We are definitely 100 percent on our way to the moon. The lunar gravity will take over in a couple of days here and start pulling us around the far side. I know the United States has done this 1968 through 1972, but it's just, this is unbelievable that we can put our minds to something and pull it off. This is an unbelievable technical accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aboard Orion, just to be clear. You can see part two of Trace's interview with the astronauts tonight, 11:00 p.m. eastern time.\par \par \uc2 It seems appropriate on this Good Friday. We're learning that for the first time in decades, faith in this country appears to be growing. That is true, particularly among young people. Here's religion correspondent Lauren Greene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In the name of the father, the son, and the holy spirit.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS RELIGION CORRESPONDENT: Mass baptisms of hundreds of young people.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Thousands come to raise hearts of joy in song.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 GREEN: There is a palpable spirit moving on college campuses, arenas, and mega churches, younger generations gathering to worship, coming to faith, as the new FOX Nation special reveals.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's spiritual hunger in Gen Z. Bible sales are booming, things that you never thought would happen before.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: It's a kind of worship that's different from their parents and grandparents.\par \par \uc2 JESSE BRADLEY, PASTOR: This is a relationship with grace. And I think that's what young people resonate with today. This isn't achieving. It's receiving. It's not trying to be good enough to earn God's love. God already loves us.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Churches are reporting record number of young people filling pews, especially in the Catholic Church, while reports show social media is exploding with young faith influencer spreading a new approach to religion. Still, while Pew Research Center data shows religion is gaining influence in America and that Gen Z is showing higher rates of religion, researcher Ryan Burge isn't convinced there's an actual great awakening.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BURGE, WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY IN ST. LOUIS: We're not seeing anything in the data that even points close to the idea of a mass revival in America. For us to go from a 25 percent weekly attendance rate to a 35 percent weekly attendance rate means 35 million new people are going to go to be going to church this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: But for others, the numbers only tell part of the story.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: This is a radical generation that believes that God is going to do great things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREEN (on camera): But the data does show that the number of Americans who say they are non-religious is declining, and many leaders say it's because young generations are leading a new movement of faith. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lauren, thank you. Happy Easter.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the search for that pilot of the downed American fighter jet over Iran, President Trump's budget proposal, and the surprising jobs report, plus Winners and Losers. And then, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE HARIDOPOLOS, (R-FL): We're all concerned about what's happened to this downed pilot. We need to do everything we can to recover that pilot. I think we're still trying to find exactly what happened, but there is clearly a threat in Iran.\par \par \uc2 REP. SUHAS SUBRAMANYAM, (D-VA) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: The reality is, even if we have, quote-unquote, air superiority over Iranian airspace, you're never fully safe. They know that, and you're shooting down one of our planes is better than us shooting down a hundred of their planes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Combat search and rescue underway for the second crew member of that F-15 fighter jet shot down over Iran, as the second crew member has not been recovered. The first has.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Susan Page, Washington Bureau Chief at "USA Today," FOX News contributor Marc Thiessen, and Democratic strategist Meghan Hays. Marc, does this change the equation? And what do you think it means big picture?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, we're praying for that missing pilot. We're so happy that one of them has been recovered. Our military is just absolutely amazing in their ability to go in and do that, and these guys are very well trained in training to evade capture. And so we're hoping for them.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the big picture, I don't think it changes anything. I'm actually surprised that 34 days into this conflict this is the first plane they have been able to take down in this way. When you go back to the 1991 Persian Gulf war, we were losing a plane almost every day. And so, you know, our -- this has been a one-sided fight and the bad guys got a punch in, but that doesn't change the trajectory of the campaign. It doesn't change the objective. It doesn't change the outcome. We're going to finish this, and we're going to finish it, and we're going to win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan, this is war, and as Marc mentioned, the stats in other conflicts have indicated many more things happening. Obviously, the president has talked about a decimated missile defense system in Iran and decimating the Iranian military. Here's Senator Tom Cotton on maybe the exception to not putting troops on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): The president has been clear that what we should expect to see is an extended air and naval campaign. Obviously, one risk of that kind of campaign is that an aircraft could be shot down, and the president would never leave pilot behind. So, no doubt, we have combat search and rescue assets in the region that are prepared to go in and extract any downed pilot. But barring that kind of unusual circumstance, Margaret, the president has no plan for any kind of large-scale ground force inside of Iran.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "USA TODAY": Yes, obviously we're thinking about the downed crew member and hoping for the best there. But I do think this was a sobering day. The president in his Wednesday night address to the nation left the impression that they were past the point where this was likely to happen because so much damage had been done to Iranian air defenses. I think this probably increases the possibility of some kind of retaliatory action by the United States, increases the possibility of the use of ground forces, increases the possibility that this war gets escalated as opposed to moving toward an exit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, allies, Meghan, are starting to talk and maybe plan about what to do with the Strait of Hormuz. Here is the U.K. prime minister.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YVETTE COOPER, BRITISH FOREIGN SECRETARY: We thought that there were real concerns about escalation risks, impact, including on the economy, and also the need for a proper plan. But now that we are facing these reckless Iranian attacks on international shipping, we're clear that we need the diplomatic pressure, the economic pressure, and also the work that's being done separately by military planners on how to keep shipping safe for the long term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: British foreign minister there, Yvette Cooper. Meghan, there are allies saying they need to get in the game here.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yes, I mean, absolutely. I think oil is a global market, and I think that everyone is suffering here. I just don't think the United States should leave it solely up to our allies when they did not choose to go into Iran to fight this mission without being notified or even consulted that this is what we were doing. So I don't agree with the president that we can just let them, let our allies go forth and try to get this open on our own. They're definitely going to need the United States's help in doing this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Strait of Hormuz traffic, the latest, Marc, is increasing but still being threatened in that transit. And the question is, will that change dramatically in coming days? And how does it change the economic picture for the U.S. and the world?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: A hundred percent. I think first of all, the president tweeted out today that he's going to open the Strait of Hormuz, so that's a good thing. Admiral Cooper has a plan that he could execute tomorrow if he's given the order to do it that would open up the Strait of Hormuz. There's no way that the Iranians can keep the strait closed if we decide to open it.\par \par \uc2 I think, quite frankly, what President Trump ought to do is he ought to have and announce that he's going to have a Hormuz transit tariff, and that instead of Iran charging $2 million a vessel going through the Strait of Hormuz, the United States is going to charge a $2 million escort fee for every vessel going through the Strait of Hormuz, which is about $9 billion a month, $100 billion a year in revenue coming in, and we will wave that fee for any country that participates in the coalition to open up the Strait of Hormuz. That will open it up and reward the good guys who are helping us and punish those who decide to stay on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, Susan, jobs picture was rosy today. "Wall Street Journal" "Jobs report, hiring defied expectations in March, 178,000 new jobs. U.S. job growth blew past expectations last month, a resilient rebound that defied concerns about pending downturn, 178,000 jobs in March, the best month for job growth in more than a year, far better than February when the U.S. lost jobs. The March numbers also beat the gain of 59,000 jobs that economists polled by "The Wall Street Journal" had expected to see." If you look at the line graph, unemployment going down. That's a big deal in this current environment.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes, this is very good news. The administration has had some bad news. This is very good news for the administration. It shows the resilience of the American economy. It continues. We've had this remarkable job situation now for a couple years with unemployment that is really -- that's below five percent. And so this is encouraging. The predictions of, really of very serious turmoil in the U.S. economy, even a recession, these numbers do not back that idea up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. There's a lot of resilience in this economy if you talk to a lot of different people, no matter what side of the aisle they are on.\par \par \uc2 OK, Winners and Losers. Meghan, first winner, then loser.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: The winners are all the people who get the finish watching basketball March Madness with the final four this weekend and then the game Monday. And then the losers are all the Democrats who think they need to buddy up with Hasan Piker who is a misogynist antisemitic person. The Democrats do not need a tent that big for him to be in it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Marc, winner, then loser.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: My winner is NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte who has strongarmed almost all of our NATO allies into opening up their bases and their airspace for Operation Epic Fury and is organizing a coalition to help open the Strait of Hormuz. He's literally saving the NATO alliance from itself.\par \par \uc2 And my loser is "The New York Times." A lot of people today are wondering today what NATO stands for. "The New York Times" literally does not know what NATO stands for. They called it the "North American Treaty Organization."\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, dear.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, I know. Pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, dear. All right, Susan, winner and loser.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: OK, you know, you can cheer for whoever you want in the final four. You have got to salute UConn. Final four for both the men's and the women's, the men's team making it after that incredible comeback over Duke. So go Huskies.\par \par \uc2 And my loser is the Department of Homeland Security. At that rate these talks are going to end the shutdown, it will never end. They will never have full funding ever again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good tease. I have the new Homeland Security secretary on Monday for his first interview. So we'll have that. Have a great weekend, happy Easter to all of you.\par \par \uc2 You know what that sound means. It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We continue to assert dominance over the Iranian Navy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: We're going to bring them back to the stone ages where they belong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you looking for a ceasefire?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think in any negotiation process, that would be a good thing to start with.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There are many people who see the downfall of the regimes a good thing, but the question of whether or not it was pursued legally, that's a different question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There used to be a time if you sympathized with the terrorists you would be a pariah in politics.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're in a new world now where 8 billion people are one plane ride away from having a child who's a U.S. citizen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a new world. It's the same Constitution.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This attack on what it means to be America, it will be destroyed and left in the wayside.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She turned the Department of Justice into Donald Trump's vendetta factory.\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, WHITE HOUSE BORDER CZAR: I wish Pam Bondi the best and I think Todd Blanche will step up and won't skip a beat.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: You'll have a president that will be, like me, he'll knock the hell out of them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She's going to show mow her signature move. It's called a suplex.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Let me begin by congratulating the team at NASA on the successful launch of Artemis II.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Integrity, we are ready to go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are definitely 100 percent on our way to the moon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're finding out how to make this space capsule a home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was an exciting launch. Tough suplex, but exciting launch.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, an exclusive interview with Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin, his first in his new job. We'll go to the Homeland Security Department.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guest, Pennsylvania Senator Dave McCormick, Massachusetts Congressman Jake Auchincloss.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on TikTok, X, and Instagram @BretBaier for exclusive behind the scenes content.\par \par \uc2 As I said, make it a great weekend. For Christians all around the world, this is a very special weekend. Happy Easter to all of you, and have a great weekend. We'll see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020260404em4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump: Iran Has One More Opportunity To End Threat; Trump: Major Points Of Agreement With Iran; Iran :There Have Been No Negotiations With U.S.; Sen. Ron Johnson (R-WI), Is Being Interviewed About Democrats Blocking DHS Funding And Causing TSA Workers To Go Unpaid, While Republicans Have Consistently Voted To Fund It; Johnson: Need Dems To Help Pass SAVE America Act; Update On Deadly NYC Airport Collision; Illegal Migrant Charged In Loyola Student's Murder.\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Kevin Corke, Eric Shawn, Paul Mauro \par \uc2 9,286 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2026\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: "THE STORY" with Martha picks it all up from here. Hi Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hi there guys. Thank you both very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And THE STORY right now, we got a live update for you that's moments away on this horrific collision that happened at LaGuardia Airport here in New York City. Two pilots are dead and there are dozens who are injured.\par \par \uc2 And the microphone that you're looking at is where we expect to get this underway in a couple of moments. But let's take a look at the actual crash of what happened here as you see this plane, literally the nose of it, ripped off and the tail on the tarmac at LaGuardia in a very horrific crash.\par \par \uc2 The control tower before this hit the fire truck, before this \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  plane hit the fire truck, the control tower was yelling stop, stop, stop to prevent the truck from crossing the runway, according to the early reporting on this. And of course, there's going to be a huge investigation.\par \par \uc2 Well, we're going to take you to LaGuardia Live. This is the first time today that we're going to be hearing a little bit more about this investigation, and obviously, hopefully, an update on the injuries. About 70 people were injured in this terrible crash and two pilots lives lost, 70 on board.\par \par \uc2 So, first, President Trump saying that the five-day clock is indeed ticking for Iran right now. This is the other big story that we're topping the show with right now. He has given them a bit wider window on their major energy facilities which could have been taken out as early as today or early tomorrow in the earlier time frame. And also, he, of course, along with everyone else is watching for the strait to see it open up for free passage for every ship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Iran has one more opportunity to end its threats to America and our allies and we hope they take it. Either way, America and the entire world will soon be much safer and will be a much more secure planet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that was just about an hour ago in Memphis, Tennessee today from the president. He says that there are major points of agreement and that this deal is being discussed with a top person who is not the supreme leader, \uc2 Mojtaba Khamenei\uc2 , who has not been seen at all by the way. Nobody really knows what his condition is or even if he's alive or dead.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House says that they are not backing down, that that is not what is characterized here. Iran says that they are not even engaged in these discussions. So, let's unravel this a bit for you. We're going to hear in a moment from retired Vice Admiral Robert Harward, former deputy commander of CENTCOM, knows this region extraordinarily well. First to Nathan Sales, former ambassador-at-large and coordinator for counterterrorism for President Trump's first administration, distinguished fellow with \uc2 the Atlantic Council\uc2 's Middle East programs.\par \par \uc2 Nathan, great to have you here. I think my first question for you is very simple. What is going on here? What do you see?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN SALES, FORMER AMBASSADOR-AT-LARGE: It's a good question, Martha. I think there's a couple things going on here. One, the Iranian regime knows that it is getting pummeled militarily and it's looking for any way out. Two, the president a couple days ago threatened massive retaliation against Iranian energy infrastructure, and today we saw the president take a step back from that threat. I think that's prudent. I think that's wise.\par \par \uc2 Let's be clear, it's the Iranians who started this. They have been attacking Bahrain, and Saudi, Qatar, UAE, the list goes on. Not military assets there, but civilian infrastructure, including apartments, including hotels, including energy infrastructure. So, we don't want to sink to their level. We don't want to make massive changes to the way we fight. We fight against military targets, not against civilian infrastructure. So, I was glad to see the president take a step back there.\par \par \uc2 Are the Iranians serious about a deal? That is the million-dollar question. What we know about the Iranian regime is they lie and they cheat. They told us they didn't have a nuclear weapons program. They did. They told us their ballistic missiles could only go 2,000 kilometers. It turns out they can go 4,000 as we found out over the weekend when they took a shot at our base at Diego Garcia.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we need to proceed cautiously here. If diplomacy is going to be given a chance to work, great. But we need to be incredibly skeptical that the Iranians are actually taking diplomacy seriously rather than them using it like they often do as a shield to cover their malign activity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, a couple quick things here. This is the new person, Ghalibaf, who is the head of the parliament. He says no negotiations have been held with the U.S. Fake news is to manipulate the financial and oil markets and escape the quagmire in which the United States and Israel are trapped. What do you take away from that statement?\par \par \uc2 SALES: Well, it reminds me of Baghdad Bob 20-some years ago saying the American troops were nowhere near Baghdad. I don't think you can take anything the Iranian regime says seriously because the fact is that the United States and Israel have made great progress in accomplishing military objectives. They have a war plan. They have a list of targets, and they have been methodically working their way through those targets over the past three weeks. And there's nothing that Iran can do to stop us. all they can do is obfuscate and maybe play a diplomatic card in a false flag attempt to distract the United States from pursuing its military objectives.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's also the case that sometimes when you're talking quietly behind the scenes, you say something different in public. So, the fact that they are denying that talks are taking place doesn't necessarily mean that they're not happening. It just means the Iranians might be embarrassed to admit it in public.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And also, you know, I mean, the rest of the world was not really aware of this person. We've watched all of their leadership be wiped out. And the person who's supposed to be in charge we haven't heard boo from. So, nobody knows if he's even alive. So, this person may be speaking this way for his own political reasons if he's trying to hold on to some leadership in in Iran. We're going to see where all of that goes. Nathan Sales, thank you. Thank you. Very good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 SALES: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right. let's bring in the retired Vice Admiral Robert Harward. He is former deputy commander of U.S. Central Command, and he led operations in Iraq and Afghanistan, served as a Navy SEAL. He also grew up in Iran and is a member of the Iran Policy Project at the Jewish Institute for the National Security of America.\par \par \uc2 Adam, always good to have you here. Thank you very much for joining us. I'd like to start with this list of ideas reported key demands in this Israel - - Iran I should say -- U.S.-Israel negotiation as I'd love to get your thoughts on this. The U.S. and Israel says no missile program of for five years, zero uranium enrichment, decommission the three major nuclear facilities, strict observation around any machinery at these facilities, arms control, no financing for proxies.\par \par \uc2 So, that obviously has all been on the list for some time. Iran wants a ceasefire and they want money, compensation. Your thoughts on the outline of this potential plan, Admiral?\par \par \uc2 VICE ADM. ROBERT HARWARD (RET), FORMER DEPUTY COMMANDER, CENTCOM: Well, I think those goals by the president been very clear since he first backed out the JCPOA. It was more about the nuclear program than it was about anything else. But he wanted to make sure we also addressed the missile. All those points capture that.\par \par \uc2 The two that I don't see on there that I'm curious, where -- what about the existing material? Will that be handed over? Will the IAEA be able to do inspections? And then ultimately where you're seeing some divergence between Israel and U.S. strategy, what about the regime? What is the governance that's going to remain in place after these negotiations? So, I'm curious to see how that plays out as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, because we did hear, you know, the few things that come out of this back and forth. What about this 900-pounds of enriched uranium, highly enriched uranium at 60 percent which is believed to be under the rubble somewhere. And there was a rejection, at least the reporting was there was a rejection of moving that material out of the country and putting it in a safe place. As you rightly point out, that doesn't seem to be part of this agreement.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the military objectives and where we are in the stage of all of this, what's your reaction to this five-day pause?\par \par \uc2 HARWARD: Well, I think he's giving them room to consider. And just as you saw, no one wants to be a leader in Iran right now because it's kind of a death sentence. I'm the guy in charge. OK, tomorrow you're going to be gone. So, they have that dilemma on their hands. But I think the president is giving them room to work and come up with a solution and how they make that while staying in power.\par \par \uc2 And that may get us the short-term objectives, but it presents a long-term problem. So, it's a dilemma on both sides of the table. And I'm just curious to see how that's -- you have a regime that's going to agree to this. Are those the guys who are going to stay in place or are the Israelis focused on ensuring that change of the regime and people who will be good governance for long-term peace and stability in the region?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And it doesn't mention making -- you know, the strait cannot be under the guidance and control of Iran. It has to be a free open passage for the countries that use it.\par \par \uc2 Just very quickly here, this is the Iranian missile range because you brought up the missile range. We saw what happened with the missile that was fired to Diego Garcia over the weekend. And this puts their missile capability in range of all of the major cities of Europe. What was your reaction to that before I let you go, Admiral? What do you want us to know?\par \par \uc2 HARWARD: I think everyone suspected it, but this validated it. So, that raises the bar for Europe and why this was so important to do it now. If this had been unchecked for another three, five years, the plethora of missiles that would have been in the Iranian inventory for Europe presents a whole different front they have to contend with, especially with the regime that's made it, we're going to get rid of Israel, we're going to get rid of the West. That's their objective.\par \par \uc2 So, it it's driving why this coalition will ultimately come to the table because this long-term risk of both shutting down the streets and the missiles are at the front of their doorsteps now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's a great point. And you've got 22 countries that are trying to participate in some way, I guess, and the U.K. finally has ships in the region. You know, they may have fired that missile at Diego Garcia because they want that kind of opposition from Europe as well. And we'll see how this chess match continues.\par \par \uc2 Admiral Harward, always good to see you, sir. Thank you so much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, we're waiting for the transportation secretary, Sean Duffy, who is at LaGuardia right now to discuss what happened in this collision. So, as soon as he gets in front of that microphone, we're going to take you right there. They were just testing it out. So, we expect that that will happen soon.\par \par \uc2 President Trump saying that the homeland is not secure without photo ID at the polls in terms of election security. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: So, I'm tying homeland security into voter identification with picture and proof of citizen citizenship in order to vote. And those two items are the most important thing having to do with homeland security. So, it's part it should be part of the Homeland Security bill. And I'm requesting that the Republican senators do that immediately. You don't have to take a fast vote. Don't worry about Easter going home. In fact, make this one for Jesus. OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll ask Republican Senator Ron Johnson what's going to happen on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I'm suggesting strongly to the Republican Party, don't make any deal on anything. The most important thing we can have is what is called the SAVE America Act. Don't make any deal on anything unless you include voter I.D.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, this has been very important to President Trump. He wants that voter I.D. bill passed, throwing a bit of a curve ball in the talks, to reopen the DHS with funding, and insisting that Republicans refuse any deal until they have it, you heard what he said about the SAVE America Act.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson is standing by. But first, international correspondent Kevin Corke at Reagan National Airport in Washington, where ICE agents are expected shortly to help bolster TSA staffing issues. Hi, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Great to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Usually, when you come here to the upper deck at Reagan, you don't see a ton of traffic, at least not on a day like today. Let me step out of the way so Mario can show you what's happening.\par \par \uc2 You know, you see people come here. They may check a bag, they may decide to ultimately, just more or less get here, get out of the way, and get downstairs to those security lines.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you go downstairs, as you can well imagine, that's where the real bottleneck tends to be, although, I must say it was not that bad here this morning. But boy, when you look around the system, places like Atlanta, Houston, Detroit, Minneapolis, really long lines, sometimes two, three, four, five hours, even in a worst-case scenario.\par \par \uc2 If I can, let me show you a graphic. This is where ICE has been deployed. Again, we are waiting for them to make their way here to Reagan National. But there are some hot spots around the system. Among them, I should point out, Miami, Phoenix, San Juan, keep in mind spring break, IT'S that time of year.\par \par \uc2 And there are some Democrats, as you know, who are really concerned about the idea of ICE being involved in security here at America's airports. Among them, New York's governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL (D-NY): I'm very concerned about it. I think, this could have be resolved quickly if the Republicans who run Washington would listen to common sense reforms that are being recommended on ICE. I don't think there is a -- anyone in this country who thinks that they have not been unleashed in a way that is been very hostile to even American citizens. That has to stop.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: Meantime, President Trump is very excited about the idea of letting ICE do the job, especially, when you're talking about being short-staffed.\par \par \uc2 Let's face it, TSA is really doing the best they can, so ICE is here to help, although the president says he wants to see them helping without masks.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Now, you know, I'm a big believer that they should be able to wear masks when they go and hunt down, you know, murderous criminals and others. But for purposes of the airport, I've requested that they take off the mask. I don't like it for the airport --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: Probably, a good idea, also, in the sense that Martha, this may allow ICE to be seen in a different light, as again, they try to help Americans get from point A to point B quickly. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a good point. Kevin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Corke in Washington.\par \par \uc2 So, with that, let's bring in Republican Senator Ron Johnson of Wisconsin, serves on the Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator Johnson, welcome. Thank you. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Thanks for having on, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know in terms of what's happening at the airport, I want to start there, and then, I want to get right to what the president is asking for here on this DHS funding in conjunction with SAVE America Act. But politically, this is being used by Democrats against Republicans to say that it is your fault that this funding is still being held up. Watch what they are talking about on The View.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG, HOST, ABC: The Republicans have blocked seven attempts by Democrats to get TSA employees and other federal workers paid, while negotiating on ICE.\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, HOST, ABC NEWS: Let's be clear, this is a Republican choice.\par \par \uc2 This is really a Republican problem, and my --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think, the American problem.\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: Just in my view, it's a Republican problem, because they are the ones in Congress running it, and I don't feel comfortable at all having ICE agents there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. What's your response to that, Senator? Welcome.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, the votes we have taken, we have taken them repeatedly have been votes to fund DHS, and Republicans have voted consistently to fund DHS. Democrats have voted consistently not to do so.\par \par \uc2 What's missing here is an unbiased media. The fact to the matter is, it is Democrats that have caused this shutdown. Is Democrats that are preventing TSA agents from being paid, and the media does not hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 If the tables were turned, if it were Republicans that were voting no on DHS funding, we would be excoriated in the mainstream media. The ladies on The View, there would be just excoriating Republicans as they should have, but there should be doing it to Democrats right now.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact we do not have an unbiased media allows Democrats to just, you know, just be so brazen and continue to deny paychecks to people who are living paycheck to paycheck. It's simply unconscionable what Democrats are doing, and it's galling to listen to the media and Democrats simply lie about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it could be damaging politically as this drags on and both sides, you know, you make very good points about why it's being misinterpreted, but it could be politically painful anyway. And the president is now saying, don't reopen that last bit of the government to fund DHS.\par \par \uc2 And I just always like to point out ICE is already funded through the Big, Beautiful Bill, so, that should be off the table. But he doesn't want that to happen. He doesn't want you all to make any agreement unless you can pass the SAVE AMERICA Act. Can you do that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, we would need Democrats to help us pass that. So again, this issue has become so contentious. This is no longer between negotiators in the Senate. This is the White House, and President Trump's going to decide what he wants.\par \par \uc2 But the fact to the matter is, in the Senate, without ending the filibuster, and I've written a column on that. But without ending the filibuster, we need Democrat support, and right now, we do not have it, because any kind of election integrity issues is existential to their game plan of turning America into one party nation.\par \par \uc2 That's why they opened the borders, allowed millions of people to flood into sanctuary states and cities, plus, up their census numbers, plus up the numbers of members of Congress, and they have relaxed all standards on elections to make it easy to cheat.\par \par \uc2 This is their game plan. They want to continue this game plan. They are not going to do anything to cooperate with us, providing some basic election security measures.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But all we hear from other senators is that there are not the votes to end the filibuster. And you point out you wrote a piece on this in the Wall Street Journal today, move to end the filibuster now, before Democrats do it. How -- are you able to be persuasive with your colleagues who say there's no way they are going to end that filibuster, because they don't want, you know, Puerto Rico and D.C. to be states, and they don't want 12, you know, 20 Supreme Court justices?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, again, the split the Republican Party in the Senate is those of us who believe Democrats will absolutely do that the moment they have power. There is nothing we're going to do to restrain them from doing that. And those who are hoping against all hope, the Democrats will be responsible and not in the filibuster, which is why my suggestion is, when this debate is over, on SAVE act, we should move to a debate and a vote on a rule change that would take a 67-vote threshold.\par \par \uc2 Put Democrats on record. Are you willing to end the filibuster with Republicans in power? If not, don't plan on doing it when you are in power.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Senator Johnson, thank you very much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 The president has laid down where he wants us to go. You are saying there are not the votes to do that right now. So, we are going to see how this gets slugged out. Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, an illegal migrant released under the Biden administration is now accused in the murder of 18-year-old college student -- freshman college student at Loyola in Chicago. This is Sheridan Gorman on Thursday. She -- her life was cut short by a bullet that was allegedly fired by the man that you just saw.\par \par \uc2 Her family is calling her murder violent and preventable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Another young woman was killed by an illegal immigrant let in by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Yes, let in -- let in by Biden.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you have a message to her family.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: It's devastating. These people were let in by Biden. We are getting them out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We are just moments away from an update on this deadly collision at LaGuardia Airport here in New York City. Two pilots were killed, after an Air Canada plane carrying more than 70 people hit a Port Authority fire truck that was on the runway and it was trying to get to another plane to do a fix on that plane.\par \par \uc2 Listen to the air traffic control audio in the moments surrounding the crash.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Frontier 4195, just stop there please.\par \par \uc2 Stop, stop, stop, stop. You are going to stop, stop, stop.\par \par \uc2 Stop truck one. Stop! Stop truck one. Stop!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Awful. Oh my gosh. What a terrible crash.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Eric Shawn, live at LaGuardia, now, getting reopened at this hour after being closed for several hours. Hi, Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hello, Martha. Airport closed from that crash last night, opened just about an hour ago, although, there are significant delays after this tragic accident that claimed the lives of those two airline pilots.\par \par \uc2 The flight was Air Canada 8646, operated by Jazz Airlines. It was coming in from Montreal and touched down on Runway Four, just at about 11:30 last night. That was the same time a ground controller gave that port authority fire truck permission to cross that active runway.\par \par \uc2 A security camera caught the very instant the regional CRJ jet slammed into the truck, killing those two pilots, sending at least 41 of the 72 passengers on board to the hospital. As of this hour, many have been released. Two of those officers in the fire truck remain in the hospital. They are in stable condition. The ATC recordings show that the controller gave the truck permission to cross the runway and then ordered it to stop, but it was too late.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Truck one and company, LaGuardia Tower.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Truck one and company.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Truck one and company, LaGuardia Tower requesting to cross 4 Delta.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Truck one and company, cross 4 Delta.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Truck one and company, 4 Delta.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Frontier 4195, just stop there please. Stop, stop, stop, stop. I messed up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No man, you did the best you could.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Well, that fire truck was heading to a United jet that had reported an odor in the cabin (inaudible) gate. NTSB and Canadian authorities cooperating to investigate this. We expect Sean Duffy, the Secretary of Transportation, who is here to have --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Eric, thank you very much. They have stepped in front of the microphone. Treasury Secretary of Transportation Sean Duffy, he's there with Mayor Mamdani. You see them gathered and they are about ready to go. So let's hear what they have to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Sean Duffy here, along with FAA Administrator Bryan Bedford, Port Authority Executive Director Kathryn Garcia, Governor Kathy Hochul and Mayor Mamdani, all here with us. Each is going to speak in regard to this press conference.\par \par \uc2 When this is over, we'll take your questions, but just want to give you a caveat. There may be a lot of questions about a lot of issues, we are here to talk about what happened last night at LaGuardia. So if you have other questions, please don't ask them. That's a different press conference for a different time.\par \par \uc2 So moments ago, I had a chance to speak to President Trump after he finished his press conference in Memphis. Also spoke with the Transport Minister of Canada, Stephen MacKinnon. And again, our two teams work very closely together. I want to take a moment and just walk through the facts again of what happened.\par \par \uc2 There was a CJR 900 from Montreal here to LaGuardia, operated by Air Canada Express and by Jazz. It collided with a Port Authority fire truck at approximately 11:45pm last night. There were 72 passengers on the plane and four crew.\par \par \uc2 Approximately 40 people were sent to the hospital after the crash, several have also been released. As has been reported, the two pilots of the aircraft passed away in the crash last night. I do want to note that we send our deepest condolences to their families as they're going through, no doubt, a grieving process. There were two individuals in the fire truck, they too were sent to the hospital after the crash.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's going to be a lot of questions I know about. What do we know at this time, and what we are going to do is give you the information that we can. We all have respect for the NTSB. The NTSB goes through a thorough investigation, and we are conscious about the information and the facts that we give you because we want that information to be accurate.\par \par \uc2 There's some information that we hold back that the NTSB has that we allow them to do the investigation. And it takes months, sometimes up to a year before we get the findings of that investigation. But NTSB is on the ground at the airport. The FAA is on the ground, over 10 people from the FAA are here.\par \par \uc2 As well as Transport Canada is sending a team to LaGuardia as well. Our two countries work very well together. There's a well worn path where we collaborate together when there's aviation crashes in either of our countries. The administrator and I were at the crash site earlier today, which is easily visible from the airport. And it is incredibly sad, it's troubling.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to let America know that we are working our hearts off to make sure that when people travel, whether by rail or car, or by air, that they travel safely. And we put in precautions and policies to make sure that you are safe when you travel. When I was at the crash site, it was just a reminder that when you're traveling by air, you should wear your seatbelt. If you see the videos of the air crash, wear your seatbelt when you're on the airplane.\par \par \uc2 Also in a car, wear a seatbelt. As you see from last night, they do save lives. I'd also note that as of now, LaGuardia is open. There is a reduced capacity because the aircraft and the fire truck are still in their positions from last night. So we are going to have reduced capacity for some time, but the airport is operational.\par \par \uc2 Again, we'll come back and take your questions, but at this time, I want to turn it over to the FAA administrator, Bryan Bedford.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN BEDFORD, FAA ADMINISTRATOR: Thank you, Secretary Duffy. Again, I want to extend my deepest sympathies on behalf of the 44,000 men and women of the Federal Aviation Administration. We take our responsibilities quite seriously, managing the safety of the air traffic control system in the national airspace. So we're deeply, deeply moved by the tragedy of last night.\par \par \uc2 Been in contact with the leadership teams of both Air Canada and Air Canada Jazz to express our sympathies, as well as my peer at Transport Canada. And, of course, we are a supporting agency in the investigation. Now, the National Transportation Safety Board is running the investigation, so there are few facts we're going to be able to share with you today.\par \par \uc2 What I can share with you, of course, the weather conditions last night were moderate winds out of 050 at 7 knots. We had broken ceilings at 9,000ft, visibility roughly 4 miles in raining conditions. So there was mist and fog here at the LaGuardia Airport last night.\par \par \uc2 We had a situation with United Airlines aircraft that had experienced an aborted takeoff. This is the reason why the fire and rescue crew had been deployed, of course. The Air Canada flight was arriving on runway four when the incident took place at the delta intersection.\par \par \uc2 Again, I want to extend my sympathies to the families of the two pilots. These were two young men at the start of their careers, so it's an absolute tragedy, you know, that we're sitting here, you know, with their loss. So my heart really goes out to the families for sure.\par \par \uc2 But we are committed to a full, transparent investigation. We will support the NTSB in any way possible. And again, we look forward to being able to take concrete actions to ensure something like this will never happen again. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN GARCIA, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PORT AUTHORITY: Hi. I'm Kathryn Garcia, Executive Director of the Port Authority. And I want to add our voice to those that you've heard before and share our deepest sympathies with the families of the captain and the first officer who passed away last night. The Port Authority takes great pride in being able to work with all of our partners to deliver safety for the flying public. And it has been over 34 years since we had an incident with death at the airport.\par \par \uc2 As the secretary said, at approximately 11:40 last night, Air Canada Flight 8646, operated by Jazz Aviation, collided with a Port Authority aircraft rescue and firefighter vehicle on runway four. The ARFF vehicle carried Sergeant Orsillo and Officer Baez. Both were taken to New York Presbyterian Hospital. We expect one to be released later this afternoon and the other to be kept overnight for observation.\par \par \uc2 And in accordance with our emergency plan, our mutual aid partners, the Fire Department of New York and the New York Police Department, immediately responded. They transferred over 43 people to the hospital during the evening, though many have been released at this time. At approximately 5:30 this morning, the airport reopened for passengers and our first flight, a Delta flight, left about a minute after 2:00 this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Again, I want to share my deepest sympathies with the families. This could have been an even broader tragedy if everyone had not responded as quickly and effectively as they did. Thank you. And I'm going to turn it over to the governor, New York Governor Kathy Hochul.\par \par \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL, NEW YORK GOVERNOR: Thank you, Kathryn. While the facts of what happened last night, 11:45pm, were well-known, yes, it was an aviation disaster the likes of which we have not seen here in over three decades. But it's a deeply human story where two young pilots left their homes expecting a return to their families, and they will not.\par \par \uc2 And this is what pains everyone here in the state of New York. I've conveyed our condolences to the Canadian government. I have walked the halls and gone up to Port Authority personnel and police telling them how grateful we are that they put their lives out there every single day to make sure our traveling public is safe. So to the entire Port Authority and LaGuardia family, I want you to know, we are very cognizant of the traumatizing impact this has on you and indeed your own families.\par \par \uc2 So I want to first convey my gratitude to all of you and sympathies to those who have had their lives shattered as a result of this and those who have sustained injuries. It's not what you expect when you get on an airplane, but also I want to offer the full cooperation of New York State with the investigation. I'm grateful also to Secretary Duffy and Administrator Bedford for coming right here on this day.\par \par \uc2 It's important to have your presence to know that this will be addressed at the highest levels. And also support from the mayor who represents many of the people work here, as I do. And Kathryn Garcia, up until a short time ago, she was my top state operations officer in charge of every disaster, every crisis that has befallen the state of New York in the last four years, so she is a top consummate professional and I have great confidence in her ability well.\par \par \uc2 So let me close with this. Let's acknowledge the tremendous stress, particularly in recent days, that our airline workers are under. And as we come through here, know that this is an ecosystem which is not self sustaining. There are countless individuals who step up every day willing to do the hard work to make sure we're safe.\par \par \uc2 And in my judgment, they all deserve gratitude, not just on this day, but as I often do, walking through airports, just saying thank you with a heart full of gratitude for what they do to make our lives safer and better. Thank you. Mayor Zohran Mamdani.\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI, NEW YORK MAYOR: Good afternoon. I want to begin by echoing the words that have been shared before me and also by thanking our partners in city, state and federal government who are here today. As has been shared late last night, a Jazz Aviation flight operating on behalf of Air Canada struck a Port Authority aircraft rescue and firefighting vehicle on runway four at LaGuardia Airport.\par \par \uc2 Both pilots of the aircraft were killed in the crash. And I echo the words of the governor and those who spoke before in sending our deepest condolences to the families and to the many who are mourning their loss. I am grateful for the work of dedicated first responders, including the men and women from the NYPD, the FDNY and NYSOM who arrived on the scene within minutes, treated injuries and handled a chaotic scene with incredible professionalism and poise.\par \par \uc2 I also want to commend those who were thrust into a frightening accident and reacted not only with composure but by extending a hand to the person next to them. Passengers who opened the emergency door and helped one another off the plane, people who kept one another calm. And I know that this crash has shaken New Yorkers across the five boroughs, whether they are traveling today or simply watching from home, especially since it's the first fatal crash at LaGuardia in more than 30 years.\par \par \uc2 I want New Yorkers to know that the National Transportation Safety Board is investigating the cause of this accident, and that we will not rest until the conclusion of that investigation. We have been coordinating closely with our partners across government since late last night and we will continue to do so. Air Canada has set up a hotline for families and friends of the flight's passengers, and I encourage those who are worried about their loved ones to call. That number is 1-800-961-7099.\par \par \uc2 While LaGuardia was closed since the accident until 2:00pm today, it has since reopened. I want to thank the many New Yorkers and visitors who had their travel plans impacted for their patients and their understanding, as well as those who work here at LaGuardia and the Port Authority for how they have not only responded to this accident, but the work that they do each and every day. As we continue to learn information, we will share it with you.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, let us keep those who died in our thoughts and continue to hope for a quick recovery for those who are injured. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, just a couple of additional comments before we take your questions. I just want to make a note that when these incidents happen, you see politics fade away and people work together. And that's why you have people with very different political views coming together, standing together to respond together to an incident like this, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I know that Jennifer Homendy and the NTSB is going to do a press conference later today with some initial findings that they'll have with this crash. I want to make another point. You know, I've been asking the Congress for additional money. Many of you have reported that we need more money for air traffic control. We are modernizing our system, but we can't fully modernize it until the Congress gives us additional money.\par \par \uc2 It's not a partisan issue, both Democrats and Republicans agree, but they have to have the will to finish the funding. I'm not saying that this crash would have been prevented if we had all the equipment deployed. But it's important if we care about air travel safety, we care about having a brand new air traffic control system, the best in the world with the best equipment, virtually all of it developed here in America.\par \par \uc2 Before you ask me a question about our staffing for air traffic controllers, I'll just give you an update on how LaGuardia operates. I'm not going to talk about specifics, but this airport has a target of 37 controllers at LaGuardia. We have 33 controllers employed certified at LaGuardia and we have six that are, seven actually that are in training.\par \par \uc2 So as our airports go, LaGuardia is a very well-staffed airport. We're a couple controllers short in total, but it is a well-staffed airport. Again, 33 certified controllers that operate out of the tower here at LaGuardia. With that, we are happy to take any of your questions that are specific to what happened last night. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes. So the question is in regard to what was the staff here in the tower last night. And we have a very good relationship with the NTSB. We have information, we don't put that information out. We allow the NTSB to gather the information, double, triple check it and it goes into their report. So I'm not going to give the data on that.\par \par \uc2 Again, that's arrangement we have with the NTSB letting them actually do their investigation. And so that's not for us to tell you that'll be for the NTSB at the appropriate time.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Well, it's troubling that we had an aircraft hit a fire truck. I mean that's troubling. Full stop. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, should the truck have stopped? And again, this goes to information that we have, but we give it to the NTSB. They analyze it and they make the judgment call on what should have happened in regard to air traffic control, trucks and airplanes. And through that investigation, they then put out a report.\par \par \uc2 So I can't give you specifics on what went wrong. That's for Jennifer Homendy and the NTSB.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So I told you I'm not going to talk about specifics. But for that question, I'll just tell you that is inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I understand that wanting more air traffic controllers. I understand you're saying there's not enough funding for that. In the meantime, while you work that out, how do you even convince people to become controllers after seeing what happened last night, after hearing that devastating audience?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So with air traffic controllers, we have surged in our training facility which is in Oklahoma City. We are putting more controllers through our academy. Now, it does take time to train up as a controller.\par \par \uc2 If you come to a very busy space like N90 in New York or LaGuardia, it's going to take you over a year to train up in a facility like this. So there's some lag time as we get controllers through the academy and we need them trained up in a facility. As I mentioned, there's seven controllers. Many of them have a lot of experience in different facilities, but they have to be trained up specifically in this airspace.\par \par \uc2 What I've also done is I have offered bonuses to air traffic controllers who are of the age to retire. I've asked them to not retire, to stay on the job. And we're paying them a 20% bonus just so we can bridge this shortfall. But as airports go, LaGuardia is a well-staffed airport, even though we do have shortages.\par \par \uc2 I was speaking specifically in regard to the dollars that we need for the technological upgrade. We have -- there's, again, this is -- this was not tonight, but there's copper wires is analog, not digital. There's a whole suite of technological upgrades that we want to make, that will make the job of air traffic controlling much easier.\par \par \uc2 And again, it's a separate issue, but I think it underscores the point that the Congress does need to do their job. And it's a bipartisan agreement so they should, in a bipartisan way, come together and figure out a way to give us the rest of the dollars to complete the job. I'll take one more question right here.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So it's a great question. And again, you want me to front run the NTSB and I can't. I'm not going to front run the NTSB. We do -- we have a great working relationship with them, and we've set this up and it's the right way. We take a step back and I don't give you all of the information.\par \par \uc2 I let the NTSB take that information and do their analysis. And I'll just say, oftentimes what we learn is information that we have at the start of an incident like this oftentimes is wrong. And so, what we think we know right now, that's why it goes to the NTSB. They pressure test it, they investigate it, and then they come out with a report for all of you.\par \par \uc2 But I did want to clarify, I've heard the rumor that there was only one controller in the tower and that is not accurate. Listen, I want to thank you all for coming out. Again, we are committed to safety we are committed to working together to make sure when you fly, you get to your destination. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We're just watching this. Paul Mauro is watching this along with me. And there's a lot of moving parts very obviously in this tragedy, and a lot of people that were involved at different stages of it. Paul and I are just discussing this. I'm going to talk a little bit more about it in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But I also want to get you to this other very big and very tragic story today. Venezuelan migrant Jose Medina, the Department of Homeland Security says in May of 2023, he was apprehended by the Border Patrol and released into the United States. Shortly after that, he was arrested for shoplifting in Chicago, but he still got to stay in the country and he was released on bond.\par \par \uc2 And then, surprise, surprise, he never showed up for his court appearance. This is an old and very tragic story when it comes to this young woman and her grieving family. This is Sheridan Gorman, 18 years old. She was a freshman at Loyola in Chicago, starting her whole life, her college career, out for a walk with friends by the lake.\par \par \uc2 And it is the same lakefront that Illinois Governor JB Pritzker touted as very safe and said everyone was making too big of a deal out of crime in Chicago back in September.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JB PRITZKER, ILLINOIS GOVERNOR: Here on the south side, early morning, lots of runners coming by on the lakefront path, lakefront trail. Absolutely gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So her family says, "we are gravely disappointed by the policies and failures that allowed this individual to remain in a position to commit this crime. When systems fail, whether through release decisions, lack of coordination or unwillingness to act, the consequences are not abstract, they are real." And in our case, they write they are permanent, meaning the loss of their daughter.\par \par \uc2 Medina's detention hearing today was just rescheduled to Friday because he is being treated for tuberculosis, according to reports on that.\par \par \uc2 First to Republican Congressman Mike Lawler represents the 17th district, which includes Westchester County, which is where Sheridan Gorman is from. Congressman Lawler, thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts on how this happened and your reaction in terms of this family's tremendous loss.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): Well, thank you for having me, Martha, and thanks for covering this. It is a tragedy beyond measure and words. An 18-year-old girl tragically lost her life, a life that was fully in front of her because of policies that are disastrous to our country.\par \par \uc2 As the family said, this is a failure of the system. An illegal immigrant was caught at the border and released into the United States of America. He was later arrested for committing a crime, shoplifting, only to be released back out onto the street. This is precisely why I and many of my colleagues supported the Laken Riley Act, so that these types of criminal offenses are taken seriously and that illegal immigrants are turned over to ICE.\par \par \uc2 This is why immigration enforcement matters. This is why local jurisdictions refusing to cooperate, refusing to accept the detainer requests, are wrong. This poor family has been devastated by tragedy. Tom (ph), Jess (ph), Maddie (ph) are heartbreaks for them and their family, and we will be there to support them every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I, you know, it's too bad because they've got an opportunity during this shoplifting charge. It could have removed him from the country and they failed to take advantage of the second round where they could have gotten this guy out of the country. This never would have happened.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Lawler, thank you very much. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in retired NYPD inspector and attorney Paul Mauro, FOX News contributor, host of the "Weekly Rap Sheet" on FOX NATION. I want to get your quick thoughts on both of these stories, Paul, if I may.\par \par \uc2 But I want to actually start by playing this for the Chicago story for the Loyola young woman who was killed. This is Chicago alderwoman Maria Hadden giving her take in her car on her cell phone about what happened. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA HADDEN, CHICAGO ALDERWOMAN: The kids were out doing normal things people do in the neighborhood. And it sounds like this might have been a wrong place, wrong time, running into a person who had a gun. They might have startled this person at the end of the pier unintentionally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He was startled, Paul.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. The bad news here is that's reflective of an attitude in our blue cities these days. That is what obtains on the ground in our legislatures. Alderman is like, well, we have city council members here. These are important people who make laws, who contribute to the public narrative.\par \par \uc2 And she's already setting up some kind of a defense for him that would not be murder, one, which is what he's charged with, but some sort of lesser charge because, oh, he was startled he didn't mean it. It wasn't an intentional murder. It's preposterous.\par \par \uc2 As I just actually wrote on X, I thought I was beyond the point of being amazed by the people that somehow rather have become the leaders in our blue cities and even some of the governors. You know, you'll see JB Pritzker putting flowers out for, let's say, Renee Goode or somebody like that because it works politically.\par \par \uc2 You are not going to see that kind of reaction relative to this. You'll see much more likely that kind of a reaction. As Mayor Mamdani here in New York goes and observes religious observances with people in Rikers Island, New York City's prison, and yet nothing about crime victims, never with the police. The priorities are just skewed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, what you do and who you remember and what your actions are, say everything about your intentions and your heart, and where your heart is when you govern. I just want to get a quick thought on this because you and I are watching this NTSB hearing and the treasury, transportation secretary, excuse me, could there be criminal charges in that case?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: LaGuardia.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: And look, it feels unlikely because the narrative that I'm hearing, and I don't have all the facts, NTSB is going to do that. Now, understand, NTSB is not a prosecutorial agency, it's a federal agency, just does an investigation and garners the facts. If there's going to be a criminal investigation, it's going to be a completely separate track, likely DOJ, because it's in the air. It's, you know, with airplanes, it's going to be federal.\par \par \uc2 Look, I would doubt it because as I said, the narrative is that these, the air traffic controllers who are being paid, they're not on the DHS, that's FAA, but they're overworked. They're trying to hold this whole thing together. But they're going to look at everybody involved here and see if there was any negligence that could potentially rise to that level.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you that. Port Authority Police, I don't know what they're put the Port Authority agency, I don't know what their protocols are. NYPD get into a car accident, similar circumstances, you're going to get a breathalyzer.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: So I don't know what Port Authority does. I'm not casting any blame as victims here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but in terms of an investigation, it's the kind of thing you'd want to know. And you hear on the audio tape the, in the control tower they say, I messed up.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: I messed up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the other guy says, you did the best you could. So we'll see where that investigation goes. Paul, thank you very much. Always great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY for this Monday as we begin the week and THE STORY goes on. Follow me on social media and stick around. Our friend Will Cain is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020260324em3n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 RPT: Russia Sharing Drone Technology With Iran; Missile Hits Qatar LNG Hub; Dems Push New Voter Panic; University of Alabama student disappears on spring break; Spring break takeovers are out of control; Panic in Daytona Beach; Spring break chaos, multiple shootings rock Daytona Beach; Airport chaos, missed paychecks, the cost of Democrats' DHS standoff; DHS standoff leaves our homeland vulnerable; Report says Joe Kent under \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation; Democrats back controversial candidates; Bobby Pulido's lewd music video resurfaces; Kevin Hart's wax figure is unrecognizable; New film to feature AI version of Val Kilmer\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Mike Tobin, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: -- we'll take a look at that, is it real. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 06:00 p.m. in the East, 03:00 p.m. on the West Coast. You can follow me on \uc2 TikTok\uc2 , X and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @bretbaier for exclusive behind-the-scenes content. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced and unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. My Angle in a few moments. But first, tonight, a dangerous new twist in the war with Iran. A Wall Street Journal report saying that Russia is stepping in, sharing satellite imagery and advanced drone technology with Iran, helping Tehran better target U.S. forces in the region. So, no longer a regional conflict. This is a power alignment, one U.S. source is saying.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  hit 200 targets in one day, including an Iranian fuel hub. This happened just in the last 24 hours, showing the world has still got major fire power. However, Iran retaliated by hitting a large Qatari LNG plant. And a ballistic missile did extensive damage to Qatar's Ras Laffan industrial city, which houses a major liquefied natural gas hub. Four missiles were intercepted, but number five hit. Qatar is condemning both Israel and Iran for the escalation against the energy sector. No wonder, energy prices climbed again today.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to AAA gas prices are at their highest level since 2023. That's not a headline, I thought we'd see after the State of the Union. The administration says, however, this is only temporary, but nevertheless, pressure here is building to wrap up this war and get back to focusing on the economy at home. Americans want less chaos and more calm.\par \par \uc2 And now from Tel Aviv, after what was another tense day on the war front, is Fox Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 . Mike, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Well, the regional tensions, Laura, certainly are growing this as in response to firing on that LNG facility, the nation of Qatar has declared the Iranian military and security attaches at the embassy persona non grata, and they're kicking their staff out of the country. This as Iran is growing more desperate with the U.S. and Israel closing in on them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EBRAHIM ZOLFAGHARI, IRAN'S KHATM AL-ANBIYA HEADQUARTERS SPOKESPERSON (VIA TRANSLATOR): As a result of this action, the fuel, energy and gas infrastructures that are the source of the invasion will be burned and reduced to ashes at the earliest opportunity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Now, that happened after Israel struck the Pars oil field, the largest oil field in the world. It's operated jointly by Iran and Qatar. The Qataris called the strike a dangerous and irresponsible step amid the current military escalation in the region. Then when the Iranians fired back, they struck and damaged the Ras Laffan petrochemical facility in Qatar. They fired on their own partner.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the desperation of Iran, Israel has assassinated three leadership figures in Iran in the last two days. So, Iran fires missiles into Israel. About an hour and 10 minutes ago, another missile arrived in the sky over central Israel. It was carrying a cluster bomb. Those warheads break up into dozens of bomblets that come raining down and get past the air defenses. That's the primary weapon we see fired from Iran into Israel, and we've got fresh video of the bomblets that came down. A lot of property destruction, but Israelis largely listen to the warnings and get into the bomb shelter.\par \par \uc2 Now, last night, there were two Israelis who didn't make it into the bomb shelter in time, they were killed. This evening, we've got word now of two people who were killed by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket fire in the north, one of them Palestinian. Laura, back to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Mike, thank you very much. All right, it's a good time to ask where things stand, and that's the focus of tonight's Angle. All right, as Mike said, the damage inflicted on Iran by both U.S. and Israeli strikes, it's massive. The U.S. Central Command on Wednesday said it struck more than 7800 targets in total inside Iran, including more than 120 naval vessels. We've wiped out their air defenses, neutralizing or destroying anywhere between 260 and 300 ballistic missile launchers. It's about 60 percent to 70 percent of the total that they have.\par \par \uc2 And bad news for Iranian radar, that's likewise been neutralized. We dropped multiple 5000 pound bunker busters to penetrate deep into missile sites. And all of this made it easier to wipe out much of the Ayatollah's top command structures. Now, check this out this list, and no, I'm not going to try to pronounce all the names, but one note, as you see this scrolling, Esmaeil Khatib, he's the intel minister who was killed last night. He was a central player in plots targeting American officials, and he's got blood on his hands.\par \par \uc2 So, you have to ask yourself at this point with so many of these people wiped out, who's even left to make decisions? Well, we have a list, and the Director of National Intel Tulsi Gabbard was on the Hill today with her own assessment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: The I.C. assesses that if a hostile regime survives, it will likely seek to begin a year's long effort to rebuild its military missiles and UAV forces.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And as for the notion that it didn't occur to the administration that Iran would try to block the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: This has long been an assessment of the I.C. that Iran would likely hold the Strait of Hormuz as leverage.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ANGUS KING (D-ME): My question is, was that communicated to the president in the lead up to this action.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: And it's because of that longstanding assessment that the I.C. has continued to report that the Department of War took the preemptive planning measures that it did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's a point that's made, but the immediate problem is also real. Every day this goes on, will mean a longer period for prices in the energy sector to stay up and we want them to settle down. Obviously, Iran knows that causing prolonged economic damage at this point is its most lethal weapon. Americans love our military. Obviously, we do. But the longer this goes on, the less they're going to support this mission. That's how this stuff always goes. So the questions that linger are key to what happens next. Our annual military budget is over a trillion dollars, $1.1 trillion, $1.2 trillion. But we still haven't been able to open the Strait, why is that?\par \par \uc2 Did military planners underestimate the Iranian regime's military capabilities as far as that goes? And when will we know we've truly eliminated the nuclear threat and can declare victory? Will we put boots on the ground to find the enriched uranium that we know Iran possessed? How long would it take for Iran to rebuild its nuclear program and its missile stockpiles? And with Israel's continued strikes on Hezbollah targets, do we risk a refugee crisis that further destabilizes the Middle East? We remember what happened with Iraq and Syria.\par \par \uc2 And what happens when and if our objectives and Israel's objectives diverge, and with the economic drag this war is having on our economy, at least in the short-term, will midterm political realities override military priorities ever.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are a lot more questions of regime change and all of that, but those few questions really do matter, our men and our women in uniform, and the taxpayers who pay for these wars always need clarity and reassurance. But look, it's not even been 20 days. Look at the damage we've inflicted, but because of our experience in Iraq and Afghanistan as always, patience right now is running short, and that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction to this and a lot more, Stephen Miller, White House Deputy Chief of Staff for Policy. Stephen, as we started out, sources are telling The Wall Street Journal that while President Trump supported an Israeli airstrike on these key gas fields in Iran, he doesn't want any more strikes on these Iranian energy strikes, if that's true, why does he want to pull back on these strikes?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER, DEPUTY WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, Laura, I'm not going to get into very complicated issues relating to military planning and military targeting. As we know the enemy will oftentimes try to place or conceal military targets in all manner of locations. So, of course, from a military strategy, we're never going to talk about exactly what kind of targets we are going to hit or we're not going to hit.\par \par \uc2 But you can see from the military campaign what the United States focus has been on. Under President Trump, we have eliminated the Iranian navy. We've eliminated the Iranian military. We have eliminated the Iranian missile launchers increasingly, more and more. We've gone after their defense production capability, their ability to produce drones, their ability to produce missiles, their ability to threaten the world. So President Trump has outlined an extremely clear military plan and military objectives, just like we took out the military targets and the military infrastructure on Kharg Island, and the president was clear in the attack on Kharg Island what the objectives were and what the objectives were not.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, this mission that President Trump has established is very clear and very focused on eliminating the Iranian threat to the United States and to the world, preventing that regime from being able to strike and kill Americans anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stephen, to some of the other questions that I raised tonight, this one of a refugee crisis which obviously that's not a military priority, but nevertheless, it's something that ultimately affects the region and can have spillover effects to the United States. We saw what happened. We brought in all these Afghan refugees. It was a -- it's a disaster for the United States. What is the United States able to do to try to minimize any kind of refugee problem?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, remember, the -- there's not -- there's not going to be a refugee problem for the United States. That is a promise and a guarantee. Let's remember, the Afghan refugees came to the United States because Joe Biden or the Biden administration rather, put them onto an airplane or many airplanes, and flew them here, right? One after the other, after the other, after the other. They didn't just magically end up in the United States. We flew them here.\par \par \uc2 Refugees come to the United States because they are flown to the United States. You also, of course, have illegal immigrants too, and those come to United States across the southern border, which has been shut down. So, no one is getting across the border illegally and no refugees are going to be flown to the United States. So therefore, no one can get in. No problem, no concerns, no issues, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why are we having -- it seems like to be a continuing nagging problem with the Strait of Hormuz, we've all but destroyed Iran's navy, their leadership, I mean five or six people remaining who seem relevant. So I know it's been 20 days, or we're in our 20th day now, but why has this taken so long?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, I would look at it the exact opposite, which is how has this happened so fast? So, you're talking about what would normally be considered a months or even years to achieve these military objectives, instead have been achieved in a matter of days, because under the leadership of President Trump and then of course, with Secretary Hegseth of the Department of War, we are not fighting a politically correct battle. The hands of our warriors are not tied in getting up and going after the objective and going after the objective ruthlessly, because this is war, and in a war, you have to fight totally.\par \par \uc2 And so we have seen the leadership of this regime has been completely decapitated and obliterated. We have seen the IRGC dealt one humiliating blow after another. Their navy is in the bottom of the ocean. Their air force is in rubble. Their ballistic missile program has been annihilated and is getting further annihilated every single day. This is a phased operation, phase-by-phase, step-by-step, objective-by-objective, the president is achieving his goals, and right now, as you have seen, it has been reported, has been discussed, the United States military is laying waste to targets all across the Strait of Hormuz that Iran uses to menace commerce in that region.\par \par \uc2 But very importantly, the United States is better positioned than any nation in the world to take on this battle. Why? Because President Trump has unleashed America energy, and now what else is he doing? He's unleashing Venezuela energy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: NATO Secretary General Merz (sic) [Mark Rutte] spoke about opening the Strait of Hormuz. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK RUTTE, NATO SECRETARY GENERAL: When it comes to the Strait of Hormuz, I've been in contact with many allies. We all agree, of course, that Strait has to open up again. And what I know is that allies are working together, discussing how to do that. What is the best way to do it. They are working on that collectively to find a way forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Obviously, that's Rutte. Have any NATO members told you that they're going to help, because so far, France, Germany, U.K. have been adamant that this is not their problem.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, look, President Trump has been very clear in his views about the NATO alliance and his views about European countries willingness to step up, especially after everything that we have done in Ukraine, a nation that, yes, was invaded, but it's far closer to Europe than it is to the United States of America. We have a giant ocean between us, as President Trump says. The rest of the world depends on traffic through the Strait of Hormuz, not the United States.\par \par \uc2 But again, I just want to underscore, this is a conflict that has only days long. The United States has achieved its objectives even faster than anybody thought possible. President Trump was aware of and has calculated through every permutation and every degree of strategy. This has been by far the most overwhelming victory based on the objectives. So in other words, victory over the navy, victory over the air force, victory over the missile launchers in terms of each individual military objective ahead of schedule. So that's where we are right now and again, the president has very wisely, very intelligently created maximum leverage to the United States by building up our energy independence as we engage in this conflict.\par \par \uc2 But just imagine, Laura, just think through this for a second. If Iran had long-range missiles, if Iran had thousands more missiles that they could aim at Europe and the United States and our bases, and they were as powerful as they were going to be under the Obama deal, who had a nuclear weapon and the ability to shut down the Strait of Hormuz, then the whole world would be at the mercy of this terrorist regime. President Trump alone has stopped that from happening.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, I want to get your reaction to comments from President Trump that surprised some people. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look what happened. In the last two weeks, they weren't supposed to go after all these other countries in the Middle East. Those missiles were set to go after them, so they hit Qatar, Saudi Arabia, UAE, Bahrain, Kuwait. Nobody expected that. We were shocked.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, I mean, we didn't anticipate everything. I mean he said we didn't anticipate that, which not necessarily wrong with that. But we do have military planners who spend an enormous amount of time and money gaming out these war plans, Stephen, whether how long it's going to take us to clear the Strait when we don't have mine sweeping capabilities that we used to have, we kind of slowly but surely given them away. And the fact that Iran is going to strike other countries in the region, we spent a lot on this. Why didn't they anticipate this and inform the president of those facts?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I think you're saying something very different, Laura. What the president is talking about is the fact that Iran revealed itself by pre- setting its missiles to strike at nations that, in many cases, were its friends, at least not his friends, that were non-combatants to the Middle East. And what that revealed, the president's point is, what that revealed about the Iranian regime that it underscores their blood lust, their blood thirst, how reckless they are, how dangerous they are.\par \par \uc2 What would it look like if they pursued a nuclear weapon while they had the capability to shoot missiles in which they were heading towards a far more distant targets, all across Europe, all across the Middle East, eventually into the United States. Again, while pursuing a nuclear weapon. But from a military planning standpoint, this has been exquisite, Laura. You're talking about military planning, a big part of military planning is having the defensive weapons necessary to shoot down those Iranian missiles. So what we're not talking about is, it's American planning, American imaging, American weaponry that is shooting down those Iranian drones, that is shooting down those Iranian missiles, that is killing those launchers. It's Iranian -- it's the Iranian that are being overwhelmed at every single step along the way.\par \par \uc2 The United States had exquisite intelligence that you are now seeing about their plans, their capabilities, their preparation, the locations of their resources, their launchers, their military bases, their commanders headquarters --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They're getting help from Russia. Yes, but they're getting help from Russia as well, we understand. Did we anticipate that?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: We -- of course, we anticipated that, of course, Iran would try to reach out to anybody they thought that could help them. But the bottom-line is that our capabilities are so far beyond anyone else's in the world, as we've seen with "Midnight Hammer", as we've seen with "Absolute Resolve", as we're seeing now with "Epic Fury." It's very important that nobody in the United States be gaslit by the Iranian propaganda. The IRGC has been crushed. Their leadership is dying. Their ability to project their power is waning by the day, their command structure is being ripped apart. Their infrastructure is being steadily eroded. They are being turned into a weaker and weaker and weaker power every single day. That is a story that needs to get told, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And do you believe there's anyone -- there's going to be anyone left? We went through the few people who are remaining. There's not that many, head of the justice system, if you could call it a justice system. Was there anyone remaining that you think would actually, I mean I didn't want to say be trustworthy, or could be in a position to, quote, negotiate when it's -- obviously, it's unconditional surrender. That's the only thing that we're going to -- we're going to demand or tolerate.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: The American military objective is to neuter the regime, to render them impotent. That is what President Trump is doing day-by-day, the regime is being rendered impotent in terms of being able to wield instruments of national power against the world. Ultimately, the Iranian people will have to decide for themselves what kind of future they want, but President Trump will not allow this regime, this blood lusting regime, to hold us hostage with energy blackmail, missile blackmail or nuclear blackmail, not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stephen, thank you very much. Great to see you tonight as always. And coming up, why Democrats say that Trump really wants voter I.D.? You won't believe it, or maybe you will.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Overwhelmingly, the American people support passage of key provisions of the SAVE AMERICA Act. Well, it turns out allowing non- citizens to vote, it's not popular anywhere but on Capitol Hill among Democrats. Now, why? Well, they want to rig the vote, and now, as they argue against the legislation, they're practicing the art of projection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): It's a naked attempt to rig our elections.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Donald Trump is trying to stop American citizens from voting. He wants to rig the election by picking his own voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, this is so pathetic. Only the hard Left that the Democrats become believes that elections can be rigged by ensuring that only Americans vote in them. But that's not all. The eternally sneering Senator Mark Kelly is offering his own farcical claim.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Everybody would have to re-register to vote. Think about that 80-year-old senior who doesn't have a passport or can't find their birth certificate, re-registering our entire country to vote. I mean, just think about that. How ridiculous that is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Here to discuss Missouri Senator Eric Schmitt. Senator, let's do a fact check on what Mark Kelly said this morning. Will all Americans need to re-register to vote if the SAVE AMERICA legislation passes?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): No. In fact, I was on the Senate floor dispelling these lies. Look, I'm from the "Show-Me State." We believe in common sense. We believe in the truth. And the fact is, the American people support the idea that American elections are for Americans. You know, news flash. This is something that's very obvious to most people. I can't believe we actually have to debate it. We think that we should have to show who you are when you go vote with photo I.D., so people are going to have to re- register to vote. We just want to make sure that illegal immigrants who've come here illegally don't get to vote in our elections. And this is the truth. This is the mask that's being sort of unveiled here.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, that's their coalition now. So, Chuck Schumer has talked about disenfranchising their coalition. Well, go on the floor and defend who that is. It's illegal immigrants. You want to vote. You want to vote now because you've lost the case of the American people. They want to cheat, Laura, and we should just say it out loud, that's what they want to do, and we want to protect the franchise. We want to protect the right to vote. This is a precious thing we fought for. People have died in this country for the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 You know, the right to vote isn't some like global passport you get, or some, you know, participation trophy because you're here illegally. We should stand firm on this, and I think it's really important for us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, you want to amend the SAVE AMERICA Act to include some other ornaments, which you know, I love these, but it's stuff like banning men in women's sports, banning transgender child mutilations. And while those are worthy goals, doesn't that kind of muck it up a little bit and take away from the very, very popular and very clear provisions that only focus on voting.\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: I think it's a commonsense agenda. President Trump has been very clear. He wants these five pillars in here, and they're all 80-20 or 90-10 issues. Think about it. We're protecting the right to vote. It would get rid of these mass mail in balloting scams we've seen after COVID. It would also protect women and girls from men competing in their sports, and also the transgender mutilation that we've seen. These are all very popular issues. I think it'd be telling for the Democrats to actually get on the Senate floor and argue against these things the -- I think the common bond here is a return to commonsense. That's what we heard from President Trump in his second inaugural address. That's what the 2024 election was.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Senate isn't some museum. It's not some like, you know, retirement home for proceduralism, the American people expect us to act on their defense and on their behalf, and I think that's what this package represents.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, 30 years ago, Chuck Schumer had a very different view on I.D. requirements. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Admit the truth. Everywhere people go, they're asked for a social security card. In fact, one way to prove you're a bona fide person who can have a job is to ask for a driver's license and a social security card. This is an anti-fraud amendment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, what happened to the Democrat Party? I mean what happened?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: By the way, I wish I served with that guy. Where's that guy at? Instead I served with this radical Leftist who hears footsteps from AOC every day and tries to appease this base that wants to open up our border and have illegal immigrants vote and you don't have to prove your identity. Like I don't know what happened. This is no longer the party of Harry Truman or even Bill Clinton for that matter. They've completely lost their way. Again, I think it's important for Republicans to stand up as the party for commonsense, and the Democrats have cave to this sort of radical agenda that the American people don't support.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Senator, thank you very much. And coming up, it's a parent's worst nightmare. You let your son or daughter go on spring break, maybe visit someone over in Europe, and they don't come home. A family of a University of Alabama Junior is desperate tonight for answers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: A nightmare scenario for any parent is unfolding overseas. Twenty-year-old University of Alabama student Jimmy Gracey vanished while visiting friends in Spain over his spring break. One moment he's out in a popular waterfront nightclub in Barcelona, the next he's gone. Friends say Gracey became separated from his group of friends around 3 A.M. After that, nothing. No calls, no texts. He never made it back to his Airbnb.\par \par \uc2 Gracie's father rushed out to Barcelona telling the Ingraham Angle, that he's there trying to find his son. And now reports that police suspect foul play in Gracey's disappearance. A family spokesperson told Fox that his phone was recovered during an arrest, but police aren't giving any details about the individual who was arrested, apparently had the phone in his possession.\par \par \uc2 Instead telling Fox News Digital that they're trying to pinpoint a location for Gracey based on the phone's former locations, and they're also checking cameras in the area. We'll keep an eye on this story and we're all praying that Jimmy Gracey is found. And let's all remind our kids when you're out at night, always, always have a buddy with you.\par \par \uc2 And here at home, spring break is starting to look like a lot less fun than it used to be and a lot more chaos. Wild video captured by the New York Post showed the mob attacking a man in Fort Lauderdale.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And this was the scene in Daytona Beach after thousands of people ran in panic after spring breakers thought they heard gunshots. While that wasn't the case, it was in at least four other situations. Multiple separate shootings were reported in the area with police scrambling to get control.\par \par \uc2 So, what used to be a rite of passage for college kids, not myself, but all the other kids, is now raising serious questions about safety. Here to react is Volusia County Sheriff Michael Chitwood. Sheriff, this is a difficult situation for police to deal with because Florida has traditionally welcomed spring breakers. What do you do?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CHITWOOD, VOLUSIA COUNTY, FLORIDA SHERIFF: Laura, thank you very much for having me. The big thing for us is social media. You have these influencers, who are a thousand miles away, who put out these blast Facebook posts and say, hey, let's invade Daytona Beach this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And now you have seven to 10,000 of their closest friends come down here. They take over the beach. They take over the boardwalk. There's gunshots. There's beat downs. There's drug deals going down. There's sexual assaults going down. And then the next day, they leave and they leave your community in chaos.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the dispatchers and the detectives, I understand they have identified some of these promoters of these takeover events. Where would they be prosecuted?\par \par \uc2 CHITWOOD: We've identified two. And right now, under Florida law, I can go after them civilly for expenses incurred by police, fire, EMS and sanitation. We are also working with a local private attorney who's going to gather the businesses together. They're going to file suit against these promoters.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, we're working with our prosecutors. We, down here in Florida, have an aggravated riot statute. It's kind of vague in these matters. We're going to see if we can push the end of the envelope to at least make felony charges. If not, work with the legislature to come down and improve that.\par \par \uc2 Because it's just not Daytona Beach, you just showed it went on at Fort Lauderdale. Something similar happened in Clearwater over the weekend. And obviously, Panama City in Florida is ground zero.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you, these young people that are coming here, most of them are young, they're not from this area. We have Newport News, Virginia. We have North Carolina, South Carolina, Georgia, Alabama. So, you know, we truly are being invaded.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of these folks coming in here, they're armed. We've got video in the boardwalk watching them passing guns back and forth prior to some of the shots being fired out on the main drag, A1A.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, I mean, the sheer number of people, I was thinking about you because I'm watching this, imagining your officers going out in the middle of that. I mean, they're putting their lives in danger just trying to do the crowd control. Forget breaking up fights. Forget.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you deal with it with the numbers that you have? I've seen your training and how you do your training there. It's amazing. But you've got a numbers problem.\par \par \uc2 CHITWOOD: We do. We do, Laura. I've been here 20 years now, 10 as the police chief, 10 as the sheriff. We had more deputies in that one confined area than ever in my 20 years here. And they were in cars, just what you said. They were out on foot in teams, mixing and mingling in the crowd, trying to defuse situations, making sure alcohol is dumped out, and cracking down on -- on the county ordinance violations.\par \par \uc2 But again, we were at gun there. I can tell you for a fact, we made six, or we confiscated seven firearms so far down there. And again, there's videos showing these guys racking the gun, passing it around. There probably could have been another hundred guns down there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lucky you didn't have a lot of people dead down there, but anything can happen in a situation like that. Parents, don't let your kids go to this nonsense. Hang back and have fun in safe ways. Sheriff, thank you so much. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up from chaos at the beaches, to chaos at airports and threats to the homeland. But Democrats, what are they doing? Well, they keep playing games.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: How early did you show up at the airport ahead of your flight?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Four hours. We wanted to make sure we could get through.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I didn't think that it would take this long to get through this line. It's never this bad.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is honestly the worst travel experience I've ever had in my life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, that's what happens when Democrats hold DHS and the American people hostage. And even some of them are starting to admit it. Democrat Congressman Don Beyer telling Axios, this is not forcing any change. In the meantime, we're making people hurt. The long lines, that can't make us popular.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats aren't going to do anything to change it. Maybe we're making too much of this, though. I mean, really? Yeah, the lines are bad, but are there really any serious threats to the homeland?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The intelligence community assesses that Russia, China, North Korea, Iran and Pakistan have been researching and developing an array of novel, advanced or traditional, missile delivery systems with nuclear and conventional payloads that put our homeland within range.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Nothing to worry about. Here to weigh in, Texas Congressman Brandon Gill. Congressman, thank you for joining us. If Democrats, you know, they know what's happening and people are getting really mad. And I mean, you go to the airports you get really -- be careful because people are really on a knife's edge. What do they think they're going to get from the Republican Party? They think you guys are going to crack first?\par \par \uc2 REP. BRANDON GILL (R-TX): Well, people are furious and justifiably so, because they know that we have real threats to the homeland. And despite those real threats, the Democrats are holding up funding for the Department of Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 But I think if we zoom out here a little bit, it's really clear what the Democrats are doing and why. They're doing two things at the same time here. They're defunding the Department of Homeland Security for the purpose of thwarting and hamstringing the President's immigration agenda, to stop him from deporting illegal aliens.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, they're also fighting tooth and nail against the SAVE America Act, which would stop those very same illegal aliens from voting in our elections. This whole thing is a cynical ploy, and it's very clear, it's very straightforward, to allow illegal aliens, as many as they possibly can, to vote in our elections, to impact the midterms and we're not going let them do that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So how does this get fixed? I mean, they're going to -- either the Republicans are going to cave or the Democrats are going to cave. But I guess, you know, it's got to be one of the two. So, Republicans, I imagine, are not going to cave. So, do you think the Democrats are going to say, we'll table this for now? Is that your prediction?\par \par \uc2 GILL: Well, what I hope they do is come to their senses and recognize. I mean, we've had four different domestic attacks from radical Islamic terrorists in the United States just in the past couple of weeks in four different states, four different people, but all with the same radical Islamic motive.\par \par \uc2 We're in a conflict with Iran right now. Now is obviously not the time to be playing political games with the president and with Republicans to stop our agenda.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope that Democrats come to their senses and do the right thing and fund the Department of Homeland Security, which, by the way, I think the majority of Americans would like to see, particularly those who are waiting for two hours in airport lines.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, it's a nightmare. We got breaking news. Just a moment ago, sources are saying that Joe Kent, the former director of the United States National Counterterror Center, this is from semaphore, he just resigned. He's now under FBI investigation for allegedly leaking classified information. And the investigation predates his departure.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, your reaction to that tonight. Wow.\par \par \uc2 GILL: Well, there's a lot there and I think that we need to let this investigation play out, but we shouldn't tolerate any leakers in the administration, particularly regarding classified national security interests and information. So, we'll let this play out and see where it goes. But if he was leaking, he ought to be held accountable just like anybody else would be.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, five minutes ago, he seemed to think that Iran was a big threat, and then suddenly that threat went away. It was very odd. Congressman, thank you very much. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, what Democrats think you want. You really want. Plus, when wax figures go wrong. Seen and Unseen with Raymond Arroyo is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, midterms, full swing. Democrats are favoring some unusual candidates.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, Laura, I can't see the wisdom in some of these picks. I mean, for instance, Texas's 15th congressional district, Latin Grammy-winning singer Bobby Pulido is the Democratic nominee. But more than a decade ago, Pulido released a music video for his song, Dias de Ayer, where he portrayed multiple characters, including an exhibitionist who wraps himself in a red blanket while pleasuring himself next to Oban on a plane.\par \par \uc2 Now, the campaign ads write themselves on this stuff, but all jokes aside, we reached out to this guy's team about the video. They blamed it on cultural differences, saying it's satire, but it's hilarious watching Republicans clutch their pearls over a music video.\par \par \uc2 Now, Laura, can you imagine a Republican pleasuring himself under a blanket and nobody would say a word about it in the media? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't even want to think about any of this. So, he's getting backlash. It might not go down well when you're running for a Texas congressional seat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'm a winter Texan.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right? Because it gets hot here, like real hot. So --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You're a winter Mexican. I know you're a summer Mexican.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'm a summer Mexican.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah, there you go.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah, it does. It does.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Sounds better saying I'm a winter Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So, why would Texas voters, Laura, a majority of Texas voters vote for somebody who spends most of his year in Mexico? And let's not forget, after announcing his campaign last fall, this is the guy who posted a video on X of himself urinating on Donald Trump's star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is an inauspicious and horrible way to win over a district that is 81 percent Hispanic, fairly conservative, and overwhelmingly Catholic. I don't know if these antics play down there, but we'll see.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We've now spent more time on him than I ever want to spend on my wife. What's next?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, I mean, anybody will tell you, schtick doesn't always work. You know, gimmicks don't always work. Just ask Jasmine Crockett. But look, it's an honor to have a wax figure made. All the greats have them, Laura, Leonardo DiCaprio, Meryl Streep, Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 Imagine the shock when Kevin Hart saw his wax figure displayed at a Hollywood wax museum in Pigeon Forge. Look at this. Who is that, Laura? Look at that figure.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It looks like Drake.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Looks like a young Ice T, or maybe a young Ali or something, but it ain't Kevin Hart.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He's handsome. They're both handsome men, but that doesn't look anything like him.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But Kevin Hart complained at length. He said these people, you know, they've done me wrong. What did I ever do to these people? I want to redo. And look, Laura, these things can go sideways at times. I mean, you remember Lucille Ball, that statue that was unveiled in her hometown. Lucille, we have a problem. And then there was Cristiano Ronaldo, that statue of him, where his features were all smashed together off to the side.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, you know, I found a wax figure of you the other day. And I think it's very on the note. I mean --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I like that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: -- actually, very becoming. It looks like it does favor you. I mean, you know, maybe the cheek implants we'd want to remove, but other than that, it's --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I like that. Well, I think that that would be a great tribute to me. Joan, the great Joan Rivers. She had one of the best senses of humor ever. So, Raymond -- yeah. So, Raymond, because, you know, turnabout is fair play, I submitted your photo for a wax figure.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, you did?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, and there's a couple options, I guess, they have for you and they're working on them. There's one. Looks a little -- no, that doesn't --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Very nice.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- too much. And then there's one more. Ah, you grew a mustache in this one.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, that one I'm kind of insulted by. Peewee, I could live with. This one I'm not so keen on. Don't see the likeness, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, I saw this story and I thought of you. The late Val Kilmer is going to appear in an indie film, as deep as the grave, through AI technology. The director said the role was written specifically for Kilmer, but the actor couldn't film due to serious health issues. What do you think of this, Ray?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, you know, the family, the Kilmer family is embracing this. They said, look, Val Kilmer embraced emerging technologies and we see this as a legacy, you know, extending his legacy. That's all fine.\par \par \uc2 My problem is, you have an actor here who's going to be occupying significant screen time. That's what the director said in this movie. You're not going to be getting Val Kilmer's honest choices, his emotions. It's not a Val Kilmer performance. It's an amalgamation of what a computer thinks of Val Kilmer's performances. I'm against this AI stuff.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Money is on the line.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I don't want AI, Ingraham.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, money is on the line. That's what. People are going to make money off this. Raymond, thank you very much. Great to see you and I'll see you in D.C.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. Thank you for watching. Remember, it's America now and forever. Jesse Watters Primetime next.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020260319em3i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MI Synagogue Targeted By Active Shooter; \uc2 ODU\uc2  Suspect Previously Convicted Of Helping ISIS; Iran escalates attacks across the region and targets ships near Strait of Hormuz; U.S. and Israel strike Tehran; Strait of Hormuz locked up; China accuses U.S. of 'instigating' Iran war, tries to play both sides, and supports Iran behind the scenes; Democrats vote to keep DHS closed, despite sleeper cell threats\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Kevin Corke, Dan Bongino, Nate Foy \par \uc2 7,604 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: -- behind-the-scenes content. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Breaking news tonight. Fair, balanced and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts in seven seconds.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle as always. Thank you for joining us. We are following three major stories tonight. Although, we may be witnessing a terrorist regime's last desperate moves in Iran. Terrorists are once again striking our homeland, all as Democrats continue to block DHS funding. We're going to get the latest news from the Gulf, and we're going to analyze where Iranian tax have all but stalled traffic in a critical oil route, sending shockwaves through the global economy.\par \par \uc2 But first, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is wrapping up a press conference moments ago after an armed man drove his truck into a Detroit area synagogue. Police say he drove down the hallway until an armed security guard stepped in. We're still waiting for investigators to explain what happened next. But we know that there was an exchange of gunfire and the truck itself became engulfed in flames. The suspect is dead. The only one injured was another guard who was hit by the car.\par \par \uc2 And in Virginia, a gunman with ties to \uc2 ISIS\uc2  opened fire at \uc2 Old Dominion University\uc2  today, killing one instructor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He shouted, or stated, Allahu Akbar. There were students that were in that room that subdued him and rendered him no longer alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The suspected shooter, Mohamed Jalloh was convicted in 2017. He pled guilty to providing material support to the terror group, \uc2 ISIS\uc2 . And he was released from prison in 2024. Fox Senior National Correspondent Kevin Corke is here with all the details. Kevin, I've been digging into the sentencing memoranda and everything in that case, this does not surprise me one bit. Tell us what you know.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Bush appointed judge altering what could have been a much longer sentence. We'll talk more about that, Laura. In the meantime, we continue to await new details from Norfolk, but here's what we know so far, one victim has been killed, two others have been injured after a shooting at \uc2 Old Dominion University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now the shooter has also been killed, as you heard there in the soundbite. He's been identified by some as former Virginia National Guardsman, 36- year-old, Mohamed Bailor Jalloh and he has a troubling past. In fact, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  surveillance tracked him to a meeting with a confidential source, Laura, all the way back in April of 2016 and it was at that meeting that Jalloh said he listened a lot to lectures from Anwar al-Awlaki, and that Jalloh quit his service as an Army National Guard member after listening to those lectures. In fact, according to the Feds, it was in this meeting with that confidential source that Jalloh said he thinks a lot about conducting an attack, in fact, all the time, similar in nature to the attack carried out by Nidal Hasan, who of course, carried out that vicious attack over at Fort Hood.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, in this case, Jalloh did plead guilty in October of 2016 to attempting to provide, provide that is material support to \uc2 ISIS\uc2 . Again, as you pointed out, he was released in 2024. As for today, for a period of time, students and staff were told to seek shelter, remain in place as law enforcement converged on the scene. The attack, of course, bringing to mind the devastation that happened back at V.A. Tech back in 2007. But obviously this is a much, much smaller scale, still no less terrifying for the students and those on campus and in the area.\par \par \uc2 Again, \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials confirm that Jalloh was killed by students in the classroom in which he apparently started shouting Allahu Akbar. Now, as the investigation continues, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is still urging the public to give any tips or information they think might be useful as they carry out this ongoing probe. A lot of questions still here tonight that deserve a lot more scrutiny, and perhaps we can give you some answers as we keep digging in, Laura, but for now, back to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Kevin reports are that he was stabbed by an ROTC cadet.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, he was actually stabbed by him and subdued by him. Unbelievable story tonight. Kevin, thank you very much. Here with reaction is Morgan Wright, former State Department Anti-Terror Advisor, and Stefano Ritondae, Artorias Chief Intelligence Officer. Let's start with you, Stefano, first, what jumps out at you in this case as you know it? We'll start with the Old Dominion case.\par \par \uc2 STEFANO RITONDALE, ARTORIAS CHIEF INTELLIGENCE OFFICER: Well, first, thank you so much for having me. I think the main thing that stands out to me is how this individual was still out letting the public when he was a convicted terrorist in sympathy with the Islamic State. But it's also very important to note that we are seeing the Islamic State in multiple instances already in the last past week, trying to take advantage of this situation in the Middle East and the conflict with Iran to try to radicalize individuals in the United States to conduct attacks inside the United States, likely to take advantage of the increased political tensions here and try to cause chaos and terrorism in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And so clearly, the Islamic State is looking at the situation both in the U.S. and around the world to see where they can take maximum amount of opportunities, either in New York City or, in this case, in Virginia, to try to maximize chaos and terror within the U.S. population.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well and Morgan, when I went back and looked at the sentencing petition that his attorney had filed in 2017, obviously they wanted a much lighter sentence.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN WRIGHT, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT ANTI-TERROR ADVISOR: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: His whole thing was that he was arguing that, well, I was being basically entrapped. He admitted that he was trying to help, but he claimed to be wanting to marry the individual that was the -- that was the government source, and that was kind of intimating. It was a big setup that he didn't want to go along with any plan against the West. And now all these years later, you see, we're right back at square one. What do Americans and authorities have to understand about situations such as this?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: I think fundamentally, Laura, what people misunderstand is the issue of the mindset of the jihadists about how they follow the Quran. I know a lot of people say, well, the Quran says this and that, until you study the Quran and you understand the concept of abrogation, about what has been abrogated before, Nidal Hasan acted exactly as the Quran told him to. He followed the fatwa of Osama bin Laden. We also have one of the Grand Ayatollah Shirazi issued a fatwa to said, hey, start striking. Americans. Start striking Israelis, you know, or Jews.\par \par \uc2 So when we see this combination of events between New York, between Eastern or Western Bloomfield, we see Old Dominion. We see the cyberattack, even though it's a proxy. We talk about the threats that have been talked about, the drones coming into California. This is not a coincidence. So when you look at these, I don't want to impute a motive yet, because we still need to work the case. You know, as a detective, I still want to work the case, collect the evidence, interpret the evidence in the proper context, find out the hypothesis that don't work and narrow in before we get to a narrative.\par \par \uc2 But it's clear in the case of Old Dominion, the shooter had ISIS sympathies. He had pledged his loyalty to ISIS. Laura, the part that bothers me about this, I get that there's good credit time. He wasn't eligible for the first step back because acts of terrorism aren't. But I think the laws ought to change that anybody convicted of an act of terrorism should get no credit for -- you get 15 percent, you only have to survey 5 percent of your federal sentence if you get good credit time. So I think some of that stuff should be eliminated. That might have been he -- that might have kept him in prison right now, he would not have been out because he was sentenced to 11 years. He wasn't supposed to get out till 2028.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, he also pleaded for that leniency during sentencing, writing a letter that said, since I first came to the United States, I've loved this country deeply for saving me and my family and for giving me so many