that is going on, and attacking Iran, and trying to use that as leverage to conduct radicalization and take an opportunity for even people who have previously been radicalized to conduct the United States -- attacks in the United States specifically targeting soft targets, which is very hard for law enforcement personnel to really thwart.\par \par \uc2 And so the Islamic State is going to maximize this. We're probably going to see this continue. We've already seen two attacks in the last week, and this is something that I think intelligence professionals and law enforcement agencies in the United States are going to continue to monitor, see if there's any other threats to the American people, because the Islamic State will take advantage of this regardless if they're a combatant in the Middle East or not in the spring war.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we've had a number of attacks. We've had Austin, New York, Bloomfield Hills and Norfolk, Virginia, that's just in the last month. OK, so this is, this is real stuff here. This is, this is not some fantasy of people like worrying about terrorism. It's happening. Panel, thank you very much. Coming up, more on terror in the homeland. Dan Bongino is here with reaction.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Two weeks for attacks. In Austin, Texas, a gunman wearing a sweatshirt that said, "Property of Allah" and a shirt with the Iranian flag underneath, opened fire on a popular street, killing three, hurting 14. The suspect Ndiaga Diagne was a naturalized U.S. citizen born in Senegal. In New York City, two ISIS wannabes threw IEDs into a crowd.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Both men are U.S. citizens, parents, immigrants. They traveled to the Middle East recently for months at a time, and they all emigrated from the region years ago. Today, in Norfolk, Virginia, in an ROTC classroom at Old Dominion University, as we just discussed, a convicted ISIS supporter killing one yelling Allahu Akbar during the attack. The gunman Mohamed Jalloh is again a naturalized U.S. citizen, this time from Sierra Leone. And in Bloomfield Hills, Michigan, a truck driven into a synagogue. The suspect is dead. The FBI still investigating who he is, where he's from. These stories raise many important questions about our national priorities, immigrant assimilation and the rise of violent antisemitism, and it's something the president has been warning about for many years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP (R), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The common thread linking the major Islamic terrorist attacks that have recently occurred on our soil is that they have involved immigrants or the children of immigrants. We should only admit into this country those who share our values and respect our people. Those who do not believe in our constitution or who support bigotry and hatred will not be admitted for immigration into our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, this all got worse. This all got worse under Joe Biden's open border, in just two years of Biden, there were 272 terror related encounters at the border. Those are only the ones, of course, that were caught. The truth is, we have no idea how many ISIS sympathizers got through. How many are here now? How many are naturalized citizens? We have no idea how many are being radicalized, whether by individuals or online.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER, DEPUTY WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Here's the reality, the United States admits more people than any other country on the face of the earth. We've had a serious problem in our country of terrorism, radicalization and serious problems of people joining ISIS, joining terror groups, joining al Qaeda and committing or attempting to commit acts of crime and terror against our nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We need to protect the homeland. And given what's