ts, right? You have this bucket of, say, you know, a radicalized, self-radicalized people like we've seen this, just this a couple cases this, and you indicated in the open or the last few weeks. Then you have the possibility of terror cells. The cells are easier to interdict because there's usually some communication mechanism to some foreign FTO, foreign terror organization. And we have exquisite, exquisite surveillance capabilities for non-uspers, non-U.S. persons outside of U.S. soil. Once you get here, to just put a cherry on top of this, once you get here, even if you're not an usper, U.S. person or USA (ph), once you're on U.S. soil. The rules are different. We've got to keep these people out in the first place.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: There is a process for de-naturalizing citizens. And it sounds extreme, and it is extreme, but when this individual pled guilty to providing material assistance, Dan, to ISIL, ISIS, then he's released, then he shows up in an ROTC classroom, asked, is this an ROTC classroom? Guys don't know what's going. Yes, it is. And of course, all hell breaks loose. Thank God we had that, you know, a couple of cadets. One stabbed him. Otherwise, who knows what would have happened?\par \par \uc2 BONGINO: Yes, I'm glad you brought up the de-naturalization process, because on the law enforcement side, we're going to need help from legislators. They're going to have to redo sentencing guidelines. Number one, any material support to terror charge. There are a number of federal terrorism charges. WND, material support to terror should be mandatory life, if not the federal death penalty. There should be no possibility for parole ever. You are not going to rehabilitate these people. Period. We're just going to have to chase them again.\par \par \uc2 But you brought up de-naturalization, it is an extremely laborious process. Judiciary -- it has to go through judicial order. You -- most of these cases, Laura, are fraud, are fraud through the process. They shouldn't be. If you're convicted of a terrorism charge, that should be mandatory de- naturalization, you serve your time and you're out. You should never be admitted again.\par \par \uc2 We're going to need some help, because we're just going to have to have to keep chasing these people. This catcher trying to catch me if you can game is just not going to work. It's a math problem that's untenable overtime.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Dan at the same time that we're trying to unpack all of this threats to California again, we take them. We take them at their word. We have to against the West Coast of the United States with drone attacks, perhaps sleeper cell attacks. Your thoughts on that as you're witnessing the fallout, obviously, of "Operation Epic Fury" here at home?\par \par \uc2 BONGINO: Well, on the sleeper cells, I saw a clip on the channel earlier, President Trump was pretty clear. He gets the brief every day. I mean, you know, that's not a mystery. He gets the PDB, the President's Daily Brief. He gets it every day. The president has a deeper insight into intelligence than anyone. He is the chief authority on this matter. The intelligence is provided for him to be the decision maker.\par \par \uc2 On the sleeper cell issue, there's always going to be a thread of what you indicated earlier, the you know what Rumsfeld called the unknown, unknown. We don't know who's here, if we don't know how they got in, and once Biden admitted 15 million to 20 million people, we don't even know the number. If we don't know you're here, it's very difficult to find these people.\par \par \uc2 However, I want you to keep in mind what I said earlier, and I'm not saying this is any kind of like mission accomplished, don't worry. No, worry. Anxiety is healthy here. But the really, really, I mean, this exquisite surveillance technology we have right now on non-U.S. citizens and FTOs, it is very difficult, but not impossible, but very difficult to communicate and activate a cell in the United States of people, United States, if people enter the United States and get here, excuse me, it's very hard to do that without leaving significant, huge investigative breadcrumbs. I mean, like loaves of bread, it's really difficult to do that. Usually, there's some money that changes hands. There are online chat rooms. You never know who you're talking to. There's obviously FISA, there's signals interception, there's all kinds of interception mechanisms we have, including human sources and foreign partners.\par \par \uc2 It's difficult, but not impossible to do. I worry about it, of course, but the president knows, as he said earlier in that clip, they played in the channel, hey, we're watching. We're listening. If there's a cell here, we're probably on to you. It's the lone actors that worry me more.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, because they're just here. I mean, they're here, and that some of them have been here for a long period of time, and they seem to just hate America. Dan, is it not time to, you know, some of these hate America mosques, and we know where they are. I mean, who knows what's being said behind closed doors, and we're all for freedom of religion, believe me, more than anyone I am, but we have real cauldrons of hate against America that obviously a lot of its protected speech. You can hate America, that's protected speech.\par \par \uc2 BONGINO: Yes. And Laura, listen, once you know, the record becomes public overtime. You know, nobody did more for civil liberties over this last year and in the FBI than Kash and I. We put up a number of guardrails due to weaponization that happened in the Bureau and had to do a lot of things to put up a perimeter fence around, you know, expanding some authorities. We don't think we need it.\par \par \uc2 However, what you just said is fairly obvious, too. When it comes to obvious cases of radicalization, I don't care if you're a mosque, a church, a temple or anything, if you're sitting up there calling for the death of Americans, then it should be fairly obvious that the Federal Bureau of Investigation should be able to investigate you for potentially inciting a terror attack. This isn't hard. The days of offending people's delicate sensibilities are over, and thankfully, we got a president with the cojones now to do something about it, you're 100 percent correct. The freedom of religion is sacrosanct. I get it. But in the First Amendment is that freedom of incitement to a terror attack. I don't remember reading that in the Bill of Rights anywhere, and I know this president takes this stuff very seriously and has taken the gloves off with it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dan, always great to see you, my friend. Thank you so much. And coming up, a live report from Tel Aviv, plus what it's going to take to reopen the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Iran escalating attacks across the region after it claims to have a new message, that's fine, from the missing ayatollah. He's vowing to avenge the blood of the martyrs. Fox's Nate Foy is in Tel Aviv with the latest. Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Laura. Good evening. So, yes, we're hearing from some pretty important people. Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu gave his first news conference of the war. And in it, he threatened Mojtaba Hosseini Khamenei, the new supreme leader of Iran, saying that he wouldn't issue any life insurance policies for any Iranian leaders.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned this is after Khamenei spoke earlier or at least gave a written statement earlier for the first time of the war. And he followed through on his promise to attack ships near the Strait of Hormuz. This right here is one of two ships targeted off the coast of Iraq. Another one got hit farther south near the United Arab Emirates.\par \par \uc2 Now, Khamenei didn't appear on T.V. again, fueling some speculation about his health. Instead, his written statement read in part -- quote -- "We will exact reparations from the enemy, and if it refuses, we will seize from its assets as much as we deem necessary." He also said Iran is only attacking U.S. bases in the region, but that clearly isn't true. Bahrain says that Iran attacked its fuel tanks today. You see that here as new video shows an Iranian drone smashing into oil storage tanks in Oman as well.\par \par \uc2 New air strikes are underway tonight from the U.S. and Israel in Tehran. The U.S. has now hit more than 6,000 targets. And Israel is responding to an offensive by Hezbollah last night. They're searching troops to the northern border and targeting Hezbollah leadership.\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight, U.S. Central Command came out and said that a KC-135 refueling aircraft went down in Central Iraq. It was an incident between -- Western Iraq, pardon me, an incident between two aircrafts. It's described as not happening as a result of friendly or hostile fire and recovery efforts are underway.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Nate, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)-\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Now, we're going to look very strongly at the straits. The straits are in great shape. We've knocked out all of their boats. They have some missiles, but not very many. I think we're in very good -- we're in very good shape. The main thing is we have to win this. They win it quickly, but win it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Promising words from the president. But fact is ships are still not able to get through. And the ones that are, are generally being attacked. Iran today is vowing to keep it closed. This is choking off oil to the rest of the world and driving up global cargo and freight costs. Shipping two million barrels of crude oil costs a whopping $20 million from the U.S. Gulf Coast to Asia, which is a record price. Over the last two weeks, freight rates have doubled. That affects everything we buy. So, the Strait of Hormuz shutdown is having ripple effects across the globe.\par \par \uc2 Now, to get the strait reopened, we need the help of our Gulf partners. Success is crucial because if the IRGC wins, it will have set a precedent that they own the keys to the Strait of Hormuz. They'll close it down whenever they want, for whatever reason.\par \par \uc2 Here to break it all down, to unlock the strait, how to do it, Kevin Donegan, former U.S. Navy vice admiral, and Michael Sarraille is a retired U.S. Navy SEAL. Kevin, let's start with you. What is key to reopening the strait, given what we know Iran is still capable of?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN DONEGAN, FORMER U.S. NAVY VICE ADMIRAL: Yes, Laura, I think to talk about that, we need to just put in context for a second what Iran's strategy is, and then it will make sense what we do next. You know, its clear strategy is they're playing the card that they can play, right? Their missiles have been pretty much decimated, so their ballistic missile force. So, what they have is -- what lingers is still a drone capability and this capability of mines.\par \par \uc2 But all of these is linked together. It's not just the Strait of Hormuz alone. As you know, they're -- the supreme leader called it their tools of pressure. Aren't really tools of pressure. They're really acts of terrorism, as you've seen with their attacks on hotels, their attacks on airport terminals, their attacks on these ships that we're seeing. So, if taken together, this is their way to pressure not just the U.S., but the global economy to pressurize the U.S. to stop what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 So, now, what do we have to do about the straits? In the end, Iran is going to have a lingering capability to shoot some missiles here, there, double linger the capability of drones potentially, and they'll have some lingering capability to put mines in the water because it's unsophisticated.\par \par \uc2 So, what the U.S. has to do and can do because we've done this before in the Red Sea, we've done it in the Gulf, and our allies and partners can do it, is we can't do this alone. The message has to be that we're doing this with allies and partners, Gulf allies and partners, because opening the straits isn't only in the U.S. interest, it's in the global interest.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Kevin, I get it. We know how critical this is. We know what has happened. But the point is it has to -- the logjam has to be broken. And if it's not broken soon, the -- it's going to be a cataclysmic effect on the global economy and American consumers. I know President Trump doesn't want that.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, I want to go to you on this. This is critical. This is a critical moment. And they've been -- had so much destroyed, their Navy, missile capability. But they still have a lot of help from bad actors, do they not? And they can still inflict damage that insurance companies do not want to insure against. And the $20 billion fund that we have won't be enough to cover. How do we get through?\par \par \uc2 MIKE SARRAILLE, RETIRED U.S. NAVY SEAL OFFICER: Yes, Laura, you're correct. So, you know, their force multiplier system, which is Hamas, Hezbollah, and all the militias in Iraq as well as Syria, are part of this strategy. We all know the criticality of the Strait of Hormuz, and it has always been Tehran's favorite pressure lever. So, how do we open it up? One, a little bit of patience and resolve in persistence with the kill chain prosecution that has been going on, eradicate the remaining boats that belong to the IRGC, hit the coastal defenses as well as their offensive strike capabilities along the coast.\par \par \uc2 But this is temporary. It's not permanent. And what I think people are missing as well is that this hurts one country more than it hurts the United States or the rest of the globe, and that is China. China just lost their sanctioned discounted oil, both in Venezuela as well as Iran, and they rely predominantly on imports, oil imports. So, they are -- they are screaming for the Strait of Hormuz to open up, just the military requires a little more time to continue prosecuting these targets and continue to open up the straits.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Kevin, former CIA Director Leon Panetta thinks Iran has the upper hand here, and he spoke out. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA, FORMER CIA DIRECTOR: They think that they are, in many ways, winning this war by closing the Strait of Hormuz and creating a huge disruption in oil. I just don't get the sense that the administration properly planned for what was going to be an obvious consequence to this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kevin, your reaction to that, that they think they're winning?\par \par \uc2 DONEGAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do they really think they're winning? Maybe in the short term. But really?\par \par \uc2 DONEGAN: Well, they think they're winning because to them, if the regime is there when this is over, they've won. And I don't mean the original regime because we know the original regime removed. But to say that the United States, especially CENTCOM, hasn't planned for this moment that we're in now is not true. We've had plans on the shelf, and I'm sure the plans are very up to speed on how we reopen the strait and how we escort ships.\par \par \uc2 Just to give you a sense of what that would look like, the United States and hopefully along with a lot of -- with our Gulf partners and our other European allies because the strait is so important, what you would do is create this moving sanctuary with convoys of ships that start moving through.\par \par \uc2 And we can do this. We can defend. Our ships are super well outfitted to be able to defend not just themselves but convoys of ships because we wouldn't do it just with the ships alone. We'd layer the defense that would include the ships, the ships defense systems, with helicopters, with aircraft, and all of that so that you basically have this moving sanctuary that covers the convoys as they go back and forth until Iran says they're going to stop doing this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mike, do you think it's that easy?\par \par \uc2 SARRAILLE: No. Of course, it's not that easy. But I think the CIA director's comments are actually offensive to assume that the military knew this was the center of gravity. We did. It's not a secondary line of operation. It's a primary.\par \par \uc2 But again, you know, we're going up against Iran, which is one of the fifth to sixth largest militaries. The military is doing exceptionally. There are -- you can't plan for everything. And there have to be branch plans, contingencies to things like this, and they're addressing them on the minute.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kevin, Mike, both of you, thank you so much. And speaking of China, it thinks it can play clever on this, on both sides. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Just a few weeks before the big Beijing summit with President Trump and Xi Jinping of China, well, China is accusing the U.S. of instigating the war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FU CONG, CHINESE U.N. PERMANENT REPRESENTATIVE (through translator): It was also the United States that, in total disregard of its own credibility on two occasions, joined Israel and resorted to blatant use of force against Iran during the negotiation process, which rendered the diplomatic efforts futile. The United States, as the instigator of the Iranian nuclear crisis, its conduct violates international law and the purposes and principles of the U.N. Charter. China strongly opposes such conduct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: At the same time, China is also calling on Iran to knock it off, saying they don't agree with Iran's attacks on Gulf nations. The truth is the Gulf isn't shut to all oil tankers, however, because Iran is still allowing ships loaded up with oil to pass through the strait to China. Iran has sent at least 11.7 million barrels of crude through the strait since the war began to Chinese ports. This as Iran is reportedly looking to purchase Chinese-made supersonic anti-ship missiles. It is described as a variant of China's YJ-12 system.\par \par \uc2 Here to discuss, Gordon Chang, Gatestone Institute senior fellow. So, Gordon, China is playing this game, it seems like, telling Iran to stop the attacks on the Gulf nations. They probably don't want that. China doesn't want that. But at the same time, grandstanding about the United States and threatening to sell weaponry to Iran, that could threaten ships, et cetera. What do we make of this?\par \par \uc2 GORDON CHANG, SENIOR FELLOW, GATESTONE INSTITUTE: You know, the important fact here is that Iran has a nuclear weapons program because of China. It's actually a Chinese program that has been transplanted to Iranian soil because there have been direct transfers of materials and technology from China to Iran, but also through the A.Q. Khan network of Pakistan that merchandise Chinese technology to Iran. So, they're the instigator of this problem. The United States needs to say that because we've been very, very reluctant to call China out for its proliferation.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Gordon, at that point, China has been helping Iran, as you said, behind the scenes. According to "The Wall Street Journal," Iran purchased thousands of tons of ballistic missile ingredients from China just last summer. In 2024, the DOD warned that China was providing satellite data in the past to Iranian-backed Houthi rebels. So, this has been going on for some time.\par \par \uc2 And this says President Trump is heading to Beijing. I'm personally worried about President Trump's safety going to Beijing. I mean, I know that probably sounds alarmist. I'd rather meet in a third country. I'm sorry, I don't trust him going to Beijing. I wish he wouldn't go to Beijing for this summit.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: The reality, you know, is that Beijing, China isn't enemy combatant. It is helping Iranians to kill Americans and all the things that you mentioned, plus a number of other things that China is doing. So, yes, President Trump shouldn't be going to the Chinese capital at a time of war like this. I think that that's clear. We don't want to legitimize the Chinese regime, and we certainly don't want to have the Chinese regime crow about the American president making the trip to the grand celestial court of China.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, I mean, for so many reasons, there are a lot of these Chinese dissidents in the United States, Gordon, who believe the Chinese will deploy some type of brainwave weapon. I mean, it sounds like conspiracy theory, but brainwave weapon against Trump that won't be immediately obvious when it's first deployed against him. But he's the only thing standing between China and world domination at this point, in my view. Gordon, close it out.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Yes, we know that the Chinese use some sort of sonic weapon against American diplomats in the Guangzhou consulate. That's basically the Havana Syndrome. Sure, the Chinese got it from the Russians or they gave it to the Russians. Yes, President Trump should not go to Beijing, should not go to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm extremely worried about it. Gordon, thank you very much. Great to see you tonight. And coming up, Democrats voting to keep DHS closed despite all the threats and the attacks on the homeland. Don't go.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: If Democrats' month-long refusal to fund DHS has shown you anything, it's that liberals care more about the welfare of criminal illegal aliens than the safety of the American people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)-\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): All I say to my Republican colleagues, stop the speeches that you care about TSA. Don't talk the talk. Walk the walk. Let the legislation that we are proposing go forward without blocking it, and TSA will be back to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, yes, trying to segment it off. Nice try, Chuck, but we're on to your game.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN BARRASSO (R-WY): We've had terrorist attacks right here on the homeland, two in the last two weeks. When our homeland is under attack, all warning lights are flashing red. And they want to peel apart piece by piece the Department of Homeland Security because they want to stand with illegal immigrant criminals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I've never seen John Barrasso that exercised. I like it. He should do that more often. Schumer is more concerned about ICE officers in masks than terrorists with weapons. So, that measure to advance DHS funding to a floor vote failed today, 51 to 46. Disgraceful. Every Democrat senator voted no, except John Fetterman. The partial shutdown is now dragging into its 27th day. TSA is being held hostage because Democrats want open borders, period.\par \par \uc2 Here to weigh in is Missouri Senator Eric Schmitt. Senator, do the Democrats have an argument now that they can take to the voters? They can say, look, it's Republicans who are refusing to compromise. We said yes to a carve out for TSA.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): No, voters are smarter than that. And I think it's important, Laura, to put this in context. The Democrats let in 15 million people into this country, including tens of thousands of murderers, hundreds of thousands of violent criminals and terrorists. And now, what they're doing is defunding the Department of Homeland Security amongst sleeper cells that we know of and terrorist attacks.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is insanity at the highest level. They simply just care more about defunding ICE than they do about anything else because they want open borders, they want mass amnesty, and they think they can get it if we don't actually enforce our immigration laws.\par \par \uc2 And so, Republicans should hold firm here. We voted to fund all of it. We voted to fund TSA, Coast Guard, Secret Service, all the other agencies wrapped up in this, but the Democrats have voted no because of Trump Derangement Syndrome and their obsession with open borders and mass migration.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's so disgusting at every level. I mean, Chuck Schumer, New York, 25 years after 9-11, or our fellow Americans, almost 3,000, incinerated. And we got these terror attacks, one after the other. Some thwarted, others not. And we're, like, we're playing this game? That we're the bad guy?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Right. Now, there's a few things we need to do. One, we need to make sure the border remains closed. The deportations need to continue, regardless of the Democrat whining about it. They don't think that a sovereign country should ever be able to tell people, you know, who can come and who should have to leave. We should not relent on that.\par \par \uc2 And then, also, we need to reform our legal immigration system, Laura. A lot of these people have come in illegally. A lot of American workers have been displaced by foreign workers. A lot of American students have been displaced by these visa mills at universities and tax breaks that companies get after the fact. So, we've got a lot of work to do. But we clearly have laws right now that need to be enforced. Federal law enforcement can't enforce those.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to point out one thing. The insanity of the sanctuary jurisdictions that they defend, it literally means that this monster that stabbed the woman in Virginia to death, who has 30 priors and a legal immigrant, that governor will not tell federal law enforcement or ICE when that person gets released from prison. They will not tell federal law enforcement when rapists and murders are released in communities. The Democrats have totally lost their way here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, senator, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us tonight. Do you think the new ayatollah is alive? President Trump weighed in on that with Brian Kilmeade on his radio show. We'll be playing clips of that later. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020260313em3c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with U.S. Energy Secretary \uc2 Chris Wright\uc2 ; Interview with Senators Mark Warner (D-VA) and \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  (R-LA); Iran Strikes Felt At Home As Gas Prices Rise; Second Week Of U.S. And Israel Strikes On Iran; Congress Debates Role in U.S. Military Strikes; Texas GOP Senate Primary Candidates Wait For Trump Endorsement\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Trey Yingst, Aishah Hasnie, Juan Williams, Mollie  Hemingway, Jonathan Turley \par \uc2 9,500 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Military action in the Middle East shows no sign of letting up as U.S. and Israeli forces pummel Iran, and President Trump honors those who have paid the ultimate sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): As fallen service members from Operation Epic Fury make their final return home, the Pentagon praises the progress being made on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, SECRETARY OF WAR: Our forces are executing with unmatched skill, and the mission is advancing decisively.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here at home, gas prices spiked, and stocks drop on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Energy Secretary \uc2 Chris Wright\uc2  weighs in on what the administration is doing to stabilize the markets.\par \par \uc2 And Congress rejects the War Powers resolution that could have put the brakes on the military action.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Article One of the Constitution explicitly provides Congress with the sole authority to declare war. There is nothing ambiguous.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: The War Powers resolution failed. That is the right result. We are not at war. We have no intention of being at war.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll talk with Senate Intelligence Committee Vice Chair Mark Warner, one of the Gang of Eight getting those classified briefings.\par \par \uc2 Then, fallout after President Trump fires his first cabinet secretary of his second term, just days after contentious hearings on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): The president approved ahead of time you spending $220 million running TV ads across the country in which you are featured prominently? The president --\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM, FORMER DHS SECRETARY: Yes, sir, we went through the legal processes, did it correctly, worked with OMB.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Did the president know you're going to do this?\par \par \uc2 NOEM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll get GOP Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 's reaction as the partial government shutdown over funding DHS grinds on.\par \par \uc2 Plus, midterm shakeups in Texas. Incumbent Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  heads to a runoff with the Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , both hoping for crucial endorsements.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Have you had any communication with the president?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): I'm not really comfortable talking about my conversations with the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of today's top stories.\par \par \uc2 Well, love it or hate it, Daylight Saving Time is here, giving most of the country another hour of sun in the evening, but you got one less hour of sleep last night.\par \par \uc2 Now to news, Norwegian investigators are looking to an explosion at the entrance of the U.S. embassy in Oslo overnight. No deaths were reported, only minor injuries. It comes after heightened U.S. security concerns around the world due to the strikes on Iran.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the remains of the six U.S. service members killed in Operation Epic Fury came home. The president, first lady, and Vice President Vance joined family members for the dignified transfer ceremony at Dover Air Force in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian strikes are entering a second week. And the average price of gas at the pump spiked more than 40 cents since they began.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will bring in Energy Secretary \uc2 Chris Wright\uc2  on how the administration plans to change that.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we'll get the latest on the ground in the middle with FOX News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, flames spewed from oil facilities on the outskirts of Tehran. An inferno engulfed the fuel storage sites after they were hit in Israeli strikes. These were the first attacks on Iran's oil infrastructure since the war began.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Regarding what we will do next in Iran, we have an organized plan with many surprises to destabilize the regime and enable change. We have many more targets, and I will not detail them here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: With ongoing Israeli strikes, the Iranians are firing back, launching more than a dozen ballistic missile barrages at Israel yesterday. The U.S. and Israel continue to target the launching positions of Iran and now reports indicate the regime only has 120 missile launchers left as the skies of Iran come under control of American and Israeli planes.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says he's not looking to settle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- negotiating leverage, maybe maximum, but we're not looking to settle. No. They'd like to settle. We're not looking to settle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: With the focus on Iran, Israeli strikes against the Iran-backed Lebanese militant group Hezbollah are ongoing today with new strikes reported in the Dahieh neighborhood of the capital of Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Multiple times over recent days, Iran and Hezbollah have coordinated attacks against Israel. And, Shannon, we can report to you now, the IDF telling FOX News, two Israeli soldiers were killed by Hezbollah in southern Lebanon -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv -- thank you, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss the latest on military operations in the Middle East, retired Lieutenant General Charlie Moore.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. CHARLIE MOORE (RET.), UNITED STATES AIR FORCE: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, you've done hundreds -- thousands of hours in flying combat. So, I want to talk to you about the perspective on what we've seen so far. These are the strikes the U.S. and Israel has been carrying out.\par \par \uc2 We talked with General Kellogg last week about just how massive Iran is. This is a big job for our men and women.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Oh, absolutely. I mean, when you look at the size of Iran, it's close in size of the state of Alaska, although it's about 10 percent bigger. And so, starting to cover all of the targets throughout the nation is a big task.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, we've heard from the secretary of war that we're trying to get to complete air dominance, where they -- we're able to fly the skies and do what we need to do. How does that open up different targets for us once that happens?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So, air superiority and now, we're talking about moving potentially into air supremacy opens up everything that we do. And I think what you're going to see is three big things -- mass, persistence, and expansion.\par \par \uc2 So, first, in terms of mass. Because we're able to knock down the doors and gain air superiority and now supremacy, we can start bringing our non- stealth aircraft like our B-1 bombers, our B-52s into the mix, along with all of our fighter aircraft and those that are being contributed by Israel to mass firepower over the top of Iran and hit a tremendous amount of targets.\par \par \uc2 The second part of that is persistence. It allows us to persist over the top of Iran. So, we never give them a break. There's always a threat of an attack.\par \par \uc2 It also gives us the ability to respond to any type of time-sensitive target that may present itself like an individual or ballistic missile that comes out of hiding.\par \par \uc2 And finally, it's going to allow us to expand the target space. You can see that a lot of these target areas originally began on the western part of the country. They're starting to move more towards the central, and you're going to see that fill up and you're going to see more target areas hit out to the east.\par \par \uc2 It also is an expansion of the priorities of the targets that we're hitting. Not just the nuclear program, the navy and the ballistic missiles. Now the targets that actually support the regime itself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, let's look at, you know, where they're going. Of course, this is what we're looking to degrade is their ability to come back to our men and women, our bases, our allies in the region. And they'd done a lot of outgoing.\par \par \uc2 So, how close are we, do you think, to shutting down their ability to get the missiles out?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So, we're hearing that we degraded their ballistic missiles by over 90 percent, the drones by 80 percent. As the map indicates, these are the areas they've been targeting.\par \par \uc2 This shouldn't come to any surprise to us or actually to the Iranians. Everyone knows the options where we have to bed down our forces. But we spend a lot of time in the planning phase to determine the optimum place (ph) to put our resources, to put our people and then how to prioritize our defensive systems to make sure that we can protect everybody.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to quickly hit on the Strait of Hormuz as well because that is not only a military issue, it's an economic issue as well.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yeah, certainly. So, you can see here, the ports and all the major terminals. But the Strait of Hormuz, and specifically this area right here has been a major point of leverage for the regime since 1979. So, they continually use it in a threat of attacking vessels there or shutting it down completely as a way to try to influence regional affairs, or as a way to deter the United States or Israel or any other country from attacking it.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they'd like everyone to believe that this area is shut down because of their offensive capabilities. The reality is it shut down out of an abundance of caution.\par \par \uc2 And as we attribute -- attrite all of their offensive capabilities and things they could bring to bear on the vessels that want to transit through there, I think you'll start to see that risk continue to lower, along with the fact that President Trump has offered to do direct naval escort of the oil and natural gas vessels, I think that traffic will pick up in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Quickly, any kind of timeline or guesstimates on those developments?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yeah. I think, because of how quickly we're attriting those forces, within the next couple of days to a week, you'll start to see that traffic increase.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. General Moore, thank you for your service and for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And joining us now, U.S. Energy Secretary Chris Wright.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WRIGHT, U.S. ENERGY SECRETARY: Thanks for having me, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, of course, we're just talking about the Strait of Hormuz there, and the impact that has on oil prices worldwide. The average per gallon of gas here in the U.S. is up over 40 cents since these strikes started.\par \par \uc2 What is the plan?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Yeah, the plan is to get oil and natural gas and fertilizer and all the products from the Gulf flowing from the straits before too long. And I may break a little news here. But one large tanker has already gone through the straits with no issues at all.\par \par \uc2 So, as the general just described, we're massively attriting their ability to strike with missiles and drones. And that -- that rate of attrition will increase in the coming days. So, we'll be cautious. We'll be careful, but energy will flow soon.\par \par \uc2 It's one of the reasons the president had to move on this war. Iran has just terrorized America, the neighborhood, and energy markets for 47 years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to get your take on something that "Politico" is reporting. It says Energy Secretary Chris Wright and other advisers' focus on energy policy, quote, "are getting screamed at to find some good news on bringing down prices". Quote, "Folks are scrambling for announcements and messaging to counter the narrative of these rising prices."\par \par \uc2 Fact or faction? What can you tell us on how this is playing inside the White House?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Oh, it's fiction. It's right along the line -- I read about a dozen fiction pieces from "Politico" and other news things about just stuff totally made up, whole cloth. We've been planning and talking about this, you know, for quite some time. Energy markets are massively well supplied right now.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the run-up in prices has nothing to do with any shortage of barrels of oil or natural gas. It's just fear and perception, the unknown, that this could be some long, you know, drawn out crisis, but it won't be. As you heard from the general, progress is going very fast. The world is abundantly supplied with energy, thanks to President Trump's energy dominance agenda.\par \par \uc2 The United States is a net exporter of oil, net exporter of natural gas. We're in contact with our allies. This is -- this is a disruption on the way to a much better place to end a 47-year war against America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. It was depleted quite a bit during President Biden. President Trump had said that's one of the priorities is to get it refilled. That hasn't happened yet.\par \par \uc2 House Democrat Sean Casten said this to "The Financial Times". He said, "It is not at all surprising that someone who failed to consider the political dynamics in Iran, Iran's ability to strike our allies and bases in the region or the regional stabilization that follows the collapse of the Iranian government would also fail to refill our nation's Strategic Petroleum Reserves prior to the price spikes which predictably followed".\par \par \uc2 So, what's the plan with respect to reserve?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Yes, again (ph), Representative Casten, of course, wrong on both of those points and probably fully aware he's wrong when he says that. It's just so unusual for me to see this sort of knowingly false accusations against this administration while the country is heated in a conflict right now that will make the world better not just in the Middle East but for everyone.\par \par \uc2 So, we have been refilling the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. But we can only do that with monies approved by Congress. In the Working Families Tax Cut Act, there was monies in it. We have been deploying it during a period of low oil prices to put some oil back into the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.\par \par \uc2 But yes, it shouldn't have been deleted -- depleted for the 2022 midterm elections as the Biden administration did. But, again, we've done many other moves that allow the world to remain fully supplied with oil during this relatively brief conflict.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let me ask you about one of those moves by Treasury will allow India to buy Russian oil products. And there are questions about, listen, the president has put enormous pressure on, you know, both Putin and Zelenskyy trying to get that conflict ended.\par \par \uc2 So, what about this criticism that allowing Russia to now profit again off of its oil, even if it's short-term, is going to lessen the pressure on them to get to the table and get this thing done.\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Oh, I don't think there's any change in the pressure there or the policy here. Russia's oil remains sanctioned. There's no change in policy towards Russia. But there's just a lot of tankers stacked up.\par \par \uc2 China doesn't treat its discount gas station suppliers very well. They're about to lose the second of three of them. So, there's a lot of Russian oil that's just hanging out on the water in Asia. And it's in Asia where the refineries are looking for oil.\par \par \uc2 So, we just made a pragmatic decision, instead of waiting at the gas pump for another, you know, month or two to arrive in China, let's pull that oil into Indian refineries right now because they supply not just the 1.4 billion Indians, but they export products to the region around there.\par \par \uc2 It's just a pragmatic decision. It's no change in Russia. It just speeds up some sales that were going to happen anyway.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. So, can you give folks a timeline on what we started talking about, which are those gas prices at the pump, the thing that every day to go and is a real check on their own wallet?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: And it is, Shannon. That -- this president came in on an energy dominance agenda driven for one reason, to lower prices for Americans and increase job opportunities and, therefore, push up wages.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, he is keenly focused on gasoline prices. But I -- again, I think we believe this is a small price to pay to get to a world where energy prices are returned back to where they were, and I'm talking weeks, certainly not months. This is weeks and probably an outside case.\par \par \uc2 And then you'll see this removal of this destabilization from investment and funds and peace in the Middle East. Iran will finally be defanged. And now, you can see more investment, more free flow of trade, less ability to threaten energy supplies.\par \par \uc2 And what President Trump is doing is setting up a world for years and decades ahead of lower energy prices, more stability, commerce over conflict.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Secretary Wright, we appreciate your time. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, critical votes on a war powers resolution fail on Capitol Hill and Congress appears to be no closer to ending the partial government shutdown. Democrat Senator Mark Warner and Republican Senator John Kennedy both here to weigh in live when we're back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Last week, both the House and Senate voted down War Powers resolutions. As the U.S. continues strikes alongside Israel and President Trump warrants, unconditional surrender is all he will accept from Iran.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss is Mark Warner, Vice Chair of the Senate Intel Committee and also part of that Gang of Eight that's been getting the highest-level briefings.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you back, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, last time we talked, you said this is a regime that has to go in Iran and you said that all options should be on the table. So, why now do you have conflict with the way the president has meted this out?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Listen, we have had four different answers on what the purpose of this war is. Is it regime change? Is it to eliminate their ballistic missiles? Is it to get rid of the nuclear program that the president claimed had been obliterated? I then heard recently it's the Iranian Navy.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a war of choice because I can tell you this, Shannon, there was no imminent threat to the United States. Matter of fact, there was not even an imminent threat to Israel.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But how do we define that? If this is a country and its proxies that are continually attacking us and our allies, have active assassination plots against President Trump. There was a guy convicted in federal court this week connected to that. I mean, where do you draw the line between something being imminent or not?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: There's been 47 years of this conflict with Iran. There been that pressure for much of the time. There was no imminent threat, do I believe, justify starting this war without coming to the American public and saying, here's what we're going to do, here's why we're going to do it, which he did not.\par \par \uc2 And now we're seeing -- the results, we're seeing no matter what Secretary Wright says, gas prices have gone up 47 cents. Americans are paying a $122 million dollars a day extra in gas coming right out of their pockets. That combined with the declining job numbers, that combined with the stock market crashing going into this war without.\par \par \uc2 I don't feel like the appropriate preparation or having made the case to the American public. I think we've got a lot of explaining to do, and we don't know how long Iran will last. And this the notion, you know, and I heard the general earlier, I think we are attriting some of their forces, but we still don't know whether those ballistic missiles are being totally eliminated or they're just being hidden.\par \par \uc2 And my fear is that we are running down on the munitions that intercepts the missile. We've got plenty of bombs to drop on Iran, but how long and how many more of those ballistic missiles does Iran have? And where does this end?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Isn't that one of the problems, though, because Iran, even in the talks we were having with them, said they would not discuss the missile program. And we knew that that was a growing problem, and they're looking to go far beyond the region where they've actually --\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Yeah. That would have been --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- hit a lot of our allies.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And that would have been an existential threat to Israel in about a year, is what the estimates were. But I really do feel like when we've got America's interest, when we've got dead service members, that we've not made the case, that the president has not made the case, that this was an imminent threat. And we don't know where this is going to end up.\par \par \uc2 I think on a war of choice, I think