's best military, but we are always better with allies, and unfortunately this president has literally insulted and insulted and -- insulted virtually all of our allies, put them down, and I don't think that, again, makes our country stronger, particularly as we're now seeing the ripple effects of this war with higher gas prices, with declining job numbers, and the president, who takes the biggest pride of all in the stock market, took a big tumble last week, and I'm not sure where it's going to end this week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we will see. We're tracking all of those things. I want to track this as well, though, because Democrats are so critical of what is happening now, but it's not gone unnoticed that there wasn't the same reaction to actions, say, by President Obama in the past with respect to Libya.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street Journal" notes this, when Barack Obama spent eight months on a campaign against Libya, Mr. Schumer and his fellow partisans took a pass and didn't demand that Mr. Obama seek authorization from Congress. The Democrats know that Mr. Trump has observed all the requirements of the War Powers Act of 1973, which gives him 60 days as long as he delivers regular reports to Congress. Do you think he's in compliance with that?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I do not think he's in compliance, because I believe when you have a war of choice, before you start that war of choice, you need to come to Congress and --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you say that this is differentiated from Libya?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I think when you've got this kind of pre-planned war of choice, I think it is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, if there was an authorization for use of military force, a new AUMF that came to Congress, say it was about making sure Iran couldn't have a nuclear program, basically the goals that we've been told of this particular --\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Which of the --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- conflict.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Which of the four goals are you saying?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, but --\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Is it regime change? Is it weapons?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Is it ballistic missiles? Is it the Navy or?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But couldn't it be all of those things? I mean, you're going to go after the Navy like you're going to go after the ballistic missiles. I mean, that can all be part of the same operation. They're not in conflict.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: The president has to decide, and if he's going to try to make the case of which of those have to be met before we end this war, I'd take a look at it. But what he's -- this is so -- it's been so haphazard, and it appears that there was no plan, for example, about preparing the American public for the spike in gas prices. It feels like there was not preparation, along with many of our key allies.\par \par \uc2 I think we're stronger when we do that, and if the president, he has himself said we're at war, and it'll be potentially months more. And I, again, I just don't know what will be the item that will bring this conflict to an end. And again, as the general said, I don't Iran's a country of 92 million people. Should it fall into a failed state, that would be devastating for the region. And if the president is saying now he gets to select who the next Iranian leader is going to be, I think that simply stiffens their spine.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he definitely said he wants to be part of the process and making sure that we're not doing this again in five or ten years by putting somebody in place who would not be chanting death to America, as the current leadership has been.\par \par \uc2 Quickly, before you go, do you -- you mentioned being prepared for this, the lead-up to it. Do you think that that was happening -- what was happening in Venezuela was in part preparing us for this, making sure there were other oil assets available?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, the Venezuelan oil will not be back up