d J. Trump coming straight from him.\par \par \uc2 How does that impact what you're doing there on the Hill? Because there's a lot of consternation from conservatives that the Senate doesn't seem like it's moving forward with the SAVE Act.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, the -- the -- the -- the -- the Senate is notoriously slow. Sometimes it takes us months to get nothing done.\par \par \uc2 But the SAVE Act is a priority. I'm a co-author. John -- I agree with everything the President said in his tweet. They'll have them seen it.\par \par \uc2 John Thune has promised to bring the bill to the floor. I don't know when he's going to do that. But -- but he is going to do that. And I -- I anticipate it to be in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 Will it pass? I think so. It just depends. We've got some free range. The Democrats will be against it. But we -- we've got some free-range chickens on the Republican side, and we're going to have to go gather them up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, to get to 60, though, is the thought that this would be a reconciliation bill, so you don't have to get to the 60? Is there a single Democrat? Are there several Democrats who would vote with you on the SAVE?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I would do it to reconciliation. We can short-circuit all of this, but I'm in a minority.\par \par \uc2 I have chased Senator Thune, my good friend, like he'd stole Thanksgiving to try to get him to do a reconciliation. This would be perfect for reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I also want to touch on the economy because you heard Senator Warren talk about this as well.\par \par \uc2 A bad jobs report, frankly, by any measure on Friday. And that with now, people are going to go to the pump. It's a real life check for them on their wallets every day.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street Journal" says this, "Trump's pledges to lower prices and to keep the U.S. out of foreign wars helped usher him into a second term. Yet, in advance of the '26 midterm elections, foreign interventions have dominated the White House's recent focus and cast fresh uncertainty on a fragile economy."\par \par \uc2 Our poll shows this week when people are asked about their personal situation, 57 percent of them said it's fair or poor. How do you deal with that going into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, the -- the expert economists who are -- who are predicting doom need to chill. They need to go buy an emotional support pony. Economy's fine.\par \par \uc2 I wouldn't trade Americans -- America's economy for any economy of any developed country in the world.\par \par \uc2 We had a bad job report last month. It's -- everybody knows that -- that companies are not firing people, but they're not hiring people, but our economy is -- is -- is -- is fine.\par \par \uc2 Have oil prices gone up? Well, of course. Do oil prices go up when there's a -- a war in the Middle East? Yes, duh.\par \par \uc2 The oil prices haven't gone up because there's a shortage of oil. The oil prices have gone up because you've got a bunch of oil traders out there in their Gucci loafers with their caramel frappuccinos who are -- who are bidding up the price. The prices will come back down as soon as we get out of Iran, as soon as we finish turning them into fish food, which will be pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Senator Kennedy, always good to have a colorful conversation with you. Thanks for stopping in.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. President Trump kicking off another busy week as fallout from the strikes against Iran is felt here at home. Aishah Hasnie, covering the president in Florida joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back to "Fox News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Fuel depots near Tehran are in flames today after U.S. and Israeli forces expanded their strikes on the Islamic regime, targeting energy infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 It comes as President Trump had some chilly words for one of our oldest allies on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News, White House correspondent Aishah Hasnie from Doral, Florida, with more. Hello, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 The president, yes, is slamming the U.K. for not immediately and fully supporting this mission. At the same time, he doesn't seem to be worried about reports that Russia is helping Iran target U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If they are, they're not doing a very good job because Iran is not doing too well.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice-over): President Trump responding to reports this week that Russia is giving Iran intelligence to hit U.S. forces in the Gulf. It comes, as he calls the U.K., a once-great ally as the Brits weigh sending in an aircraft carrier to the Mideast.\par \par \uc2 Trump writing on Truth Social, "That's O.K., Prime Minister Starmer. We don't need them any longer, but we will remember."\par \par \uc2 As the fighting continues, the president pausing for a moment Saturday afternoon to offer a final salute to the six U.S. service members killed in an attack in Kuwait, admitting there may be more.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Sure. I hate to do it. But it's a part of war, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice-over): Trump continues to pressure Iran to unconditionally surrender while wanting a part in picking the next leader.\par \par \uc2 In Doral, the president is shifting his focus to the Western Hemisphere, meeting with the leaders of 12 Latin American countries at the Shield of the Americas summit to strengthen U.S. influence in the region and dismantle narco-terrorists.\par \par \uc2 His former Homeland Security Secretary, Kristi Noem, now the special envoy for the mission, after being ousted and replaced by Oklahoma Senator, Markwayne Mullin, for what a source familiar calls her many unfortunate leadership failures, from the Minnesota shooting deaths of two Americans to a $200 million ad campaign and allegations of an affair.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Trump setting his sights on Cuba.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Cuba is in its last moments of life, as it was.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice-over): The Trump Justice Department reportedly eyeing potential charges against Cuban leaders over drugs.\par \par \uc2 At home, fears on Wall Street and Main Street, as the price of oil now hits $90 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: There's a lot of oil. We've got a lot of oil. Our country has a tremendous amount.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And, Shannon, the president, also squashing those reports earlier this week that he wants to send in Kurdish fighters into Iran to motivate an uprising.\par \par \uc2 He says that the Kurds are actually willing to fight, but he has ruled that out completely. He does not want to see them get hurt or complicate this matter anymore. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Aishah Hasnie in Florida. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group, Annie Linskey, White House reporter for "The Wall Street Journal." Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief of "The Federalist." Fox News senior political analyst Juan Williams. And former Bush national security official Michael Allen.\par \par \uc2 All right. I want to get to some domestic and other midterm political issues, but quickly a little round robin on how you see Iran playing out so far.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN, FORMER BUSH NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: Well, the expanding target set, I think, is the big news of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 First and foremost, the Israelis are now going after oil refineries. This is the very heart of the economic revenue that the Iranian regime has. And so this is quite remarkable. It's more pressure.\par \par \uc2 Second, we knew they were going after us to the paramilitary groups, the people that would be the police state. They're