u have a vision for what comes next? And so far, we haven't seen that.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, to Juan's point, I think it'll be interesting to see how things play out politically here.\par \par \uc2 Right now, polling seems to show almost like a proxy poll on what do you think of Trump. And you see Republicans at this point being pretty supportive of President Trump. And they do trust him to fight this war differently than his predecessors, including President George W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 It's also true that fighting Iran was not one of their top policy priorities when they elected him and had this, you know, wanted a real transformational period and a belief that America was at a precipice and needed a lot of stuff done here to save the country here.\par \par \uc2 And so the longer this war goes on, you might see changes there, even in terms of just the enthusiasm Republican voters have heading into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's talk about the midterms because all of this plays into that.\par \par \uc2 We've got this now runoff at the Republicans who are vying for the Senate nomination, including the incumbent senator down in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader John Thune would like to see it wrapped up. He says, "If Trump endorses early, it saves everybody a lot of money and 10 weeks of a spirited campaign on our side that keeps us from spending time focusing on the Democrats. If the president can weigh in, it would be enormously helpful."\par \par \uc2 Annie, also one said there is the enormous amount of money this is going to cost.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Yes, yes, yes. The -- the Democrats in Texas are in an enviable position. They have wrapped up their primary, the -- candidate who was viewed by many as the most electable candidate, advanced, whereas the Republicans are in some measure of disarray right now.\par \par \uc2 And that can be quite actually costly from a -- a money perspective, but it also can really damage either of those candidates who would emerge in a -- in a runoff.\par \par \uc2 So, you can really see why the president has said that he's going to endorse, but then you have the complication of Paxton suggesting that he won't drop out of the race when the --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: -- if the president doesn't endorse him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: So, it does show that the Republicans really might have some turbulence.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and he's offering up this deal, according to "The Hill," "Paxton said Thursday he would consider exiting the race if Senate leadership eliminated the filibuster, the 60-vote threshold needed to clear most legislation in the upper chamber, and passed a bill that would require voters to show proof of citizenship to register to vote."\par \par \uc2 Molly, that's the SAVE Act. But Thune again says we don't have the votes to get rid of the filibuster and it's to dangerous to do it.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes, I just want to point out really quickly, I'm not sure Democrats are in an enviable position. People always think, maybe we can turn Texas blue. And they just nominated someone who led the fight to trans children at the state legislature, who believes that abortuaries (ph) should be in every federal courthouse in the country and that abortionists should be federal employes. And he said that God is non-binary. This would be difficult to elect this person in San Francisco. The idea that you would put someone in Texas with these views is going to be -- it's going to be very difficult for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is true that this issue with the SAVE Act really cuts to the issue in the battle in the Republican Party in Texas. The establishment very much likes John Cornyn. He's been here for a quarter of a century and they -- they like working with him. The base is very frustrated that he's been option positional to President Trump, that he has not led the fight on issues that they care about, whether it's the border or election security. And so, they are -- they are seeming to be behind Ken Paxton. And the fact that John Cornyn couldn't wrap this up to avoid a runoff is a problem. But either way, whoever is in there is going to have no trouble, I think, beating James Talarico.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Paxton suggesting by saying he could make this deal over the SAVE Act, that Cornyn -- you know, the implication is that he doesn't support it, but Cornyn says, I support the SAVE Act. He says, as Senator Kennedy did this morning, we're going to act on it. We're going to get something done.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the polling on this, Juan, we've had this up again and again. When you look at different groups, how they feel about having I.D. for voters, Pew polling says this, white voters, 85 percent, Hispanic and Latino voters, 82 percent, black voters, 76 percent. Democrats have supported all kinds of other voting laws and things that have come through Congress, so why not this?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think that really what you're looking at here is not a matter of sort of low propensity and oftentimes minority voters who would be highly impacted by additional requirements. I think, you know, it's very interesting. I think Democrats should be making the argument, this is going to kill off mail-in voting, because you can't have mail-in voting if you're going to have strict requirements.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there is going to be some.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's very small.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean -- but it's for limited, for miliary and those who are ill and traveling, disabled, that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. Yes, but not for people who are living in a retirement home or assisted living. I think it's a -- really a difficult anti sort of vote effort. And a part of it is that the filibuster -- and I think this is what you're hearing from Senator Thune, the Senate majority leader, hey, we're in charge now, but, gee, you know, these Democrats might win one day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They'll be in charge and they'll start running over us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, quick ten seconds.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, health reasons would be included in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, I think so too.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Yes, well, I'm afraid we just need to go with pragmatism as it pertains to the race and try and hold the -- hold the Senate going forward. I'm sorry, not to go with the energy in the party, but we've got to get it done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Panel, thank you very much. Great to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 The legal debate coming up over the president's decision to strike Iran. Jonathan Turley joins us with the constitutional considerations. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): But if you're going to initiate war, you need Congress. The president not only did not come to Congress to seek a debate or vote, he acted without even notification to the vast majority of us. This is an illegal war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, that was Democratic Senator Tim Kaine on our show last week. The war powers resolutions he backed failed in the Senate this week, as did a similar measure in the House.\par \par \uc2 Joining me to discuss, constitutional law expert, Professor Jonathan Turley. He's got a brand-new book out "Rage and the Republic: The Unfinished Story of the American Revolution."\par \par \uc2 Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, CONSTITUTIONAL LAW EXPERT AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congrats on the new book.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we will get to that.\par \par \uc2 But I want to start with the War Powers Act. Listen, you have a very personal round of experience with this. I talked with Senator Warner this morning about Libya. So, give us some background on that.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, you know, the position of senior Democrats is not just hypocritical, it's now incomprehensible. You have people like Pelosi, Blumenthal, who are expressing revulsion that there was not prior consultation and authorization. These are the same people that said that President Obama did not require that authorization for an eight-month war where he attacked the capital city of a foreign country without any imminent threat in an open effort to have regime change. And they green- lighted that and said, you don't have to come to us.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's bizarre for them now to say, well, but this is a War Powers Act violation. Trump is in compliance with that act.\par \par \uc2 Look, presidents since Jefferson have been boxing in president -- boxing in Congress over their ability to start wars. I mean they have a right to initiate combat operations. That's been accepted since the beginning of the republic. Once you're in those operations, it's very hard to get that cat to walk backwards. But these members are in direct contradiction to their prior positions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, so a spokesperson for former Speaker Nancy Pelosi says, "there is an absolute distinction between the limited military operations in Libya and the broad, escalating war with Iran initiated by President Trump."\par \par \uc2 They're calling it a war of choice.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. That, I got to tell you, that dog won't hunt. I represented members challenging the Libyan war. We lost that case. And the fact is courts have deferred to presidents and to Congress and the use of their respective authorities here. But to say that Libya was in any way a limited war is perfectly bizarre. I mean we took out columns of military units. We attacked the capital city. We made it clear that we wanted regime change. What's limited about that? It went on for eight months and billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, I want to talk about your new book and how this all kind of factors in because you say it's a look at the fact that we're such a unique democracy, or republic, the way that America came together. But you also ask questions about whether we survive moving forward. How does the current events, you know, stew, factor into the discussion you have in the book?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, "Rage and the Republic" is really a book about revolutions. It looks at the American Revolution, the French Revolution, and asks why one became the world's oldest democracy and the other became a blood- letting, the terror and what we could learn from that. And the question is, can this republic survive the 21st century? Can it survive us? I think it can. I think it can survive. But we have to understand what brought us to this point. What was the distinction between those two revolutions. And we've lost that. The real crisis today is a crisis of faith. It's people who want to scrap the Constitution on its 250th anniversary of our independence. It's people who want to pack the Supreme Court. We have to address that because we're facing everything from A.I., to robotics, to global governance systems that are real existential threats. We're going to need to remember who we were then and who we are now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, quickly, let me ask you, because, listen, with the current president in the White House, his critics will say he's the one that's harming the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, that's a very interesting issue because, you know, both of us have been covering Trump in both terms. He has complied with federal courts. He has complained about it. He has attacked judges. But he has complied. As did his predecessors.\par \par \uc2 We're not in a constitutional crisis here. That's why this is really a crisis of faith. We're going to need more than ever those elements that made us unique and made this republic the first enlightenment revolution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, congratulations on the book.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a read that we should all invest some time in.\par \par \uc2 Professor, always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, you may know the story of Noah and his ark, but does it apply to your life today? That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a quick note, my podcast, a new episode, "Livin' The Bream," drops today. It's the latest installment in a series of special episode from my book "Nothing is Impossible with God." During this time of Lent and leading up to Easter. This week I talk with bestselling author John Bevere about Noah and how he was willing to follow God's plan when it really didn't make sense to the rest of the world. You may feel that way sometimes. "Livin' The Bream" wherever you like to get your podcasts and "Nothing is Impossible with God" out this Tuesday. You can preorder now or get it when it's out wherever you like to get your books.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you for the next "FOX NEWS SUNDAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020260308em3800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Says No Deal With Iran Until They Relent; Democrats Happy To Prolong DHS Shutdown Despite Iran Threat; Soft Targets For Misinformation: Jemele Hill Rips Right-Wing Cuban, Latino Comments; It's Just Insanity: Carville Demands Dems Lay Off White Male Voters; Ridiculously Good-Looking: Katie Couric Asks Newsom About His Good Looks; Plane And Simple: \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  New Policy Cracks Down On Loud Phones\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Kayleigh McEnany, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters,  Tyrus \par \uc2 8,717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And we share your sentiment of sharing our prayers for our troops. God bless our troops. God bless the United States of America. Thank you sergeant.\par \par \uc2 And thank you as well for watching us here today. It is now time for THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Shannon Bream, along with \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and Tyrus. It is 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Huge explosions rocking Beirut as Israel and the U.S. military continue to ferociously pound terror targets across the Middle East, escalating their strikes on Iranian air defenses and missile sites. President Trump posting on Truth Social, there will be no deal with Iran except unconditional surrender. And Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt outlining what that means.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: When he, as commander in chief of the U.S. armed forces, determines that Iran no longer poses a threat to the United States of America, and the goals of operation, epic fury has been fully realized, then Iran will essentially be in a place of unconditional surrender, whether they say it themselves or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The president telling NBC News that a potential U.S. ground invasion of Iran isn't needed because, "It's a waste of time. They've lost everything. They've lost their Navy. They've lost everything they can lose."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Democratic resistance to Epic Fury is just getting started. The country is split evenly on how they feel about the strikes, but AOC doesn't see it that way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): The vast majority of Americans are against a war with Iran. Two things can be true at the same time. We can acknowledge the brutal reality of the Iranian regime and their murdering of protesters and targeting of people, and we can also know for sure that a Forever War will not resolve that issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  taking a moment at \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2 's funeral earlier today to hit President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So, let me just say I predicted a lot about what's happening right now. I'm not into saying I told you so, but we did see it coming.\par \par \uc2 But what I did not predict is that we would not have \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  with us right now to help us get through this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And RIP to Mr. Jackson. All right, Jesse, a number of these Democrats just weeks ago were saying taco, the president told these protesters he was going to help, and he never did anything, but now they're angry that he's done something.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, I don't even care about these people anymore. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a joke. She couldn't find the Strait of Hormuz on a map, and AOC totally discredited herself when she was in Europe the other day, they asked her about Taiwan, and she froze. So, forget about those people. They're not serious.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat argument against the war is three things. It's illegal, not true, it's going badly, definitely not true, and it's unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the last week, we've proved that it was necessary, because every time they send a missile or a drone our way or our allies way, we have to intercept it with a FAD, a Patriot, a Hornet or a laser that costs blood and treasure.\par \par \uc2 If we had waited for the threat to be imminent five years, three years, maybe in \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's next presidential term, the Iranians would build up such a massive stockpile of drones and missiles, it would have created a shield around their nuclear program. And any operation like this would have been unthinkably bloody and catastrophic, and may not have worked, and it may have prevented, let's just go with the argument President \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  from even doing this, and then, on his watch, we would have had a nuclear Iran who would have been able to black merit the entire world.\par \par \uc2 I didn't even know this, but one-third of the world's population is within range of Iranian missiles. If we had waited, it would have been more. I'm talking from Paris to Calcutta. We did the entire world a favor.\par \par \uc2 This war was the last realistic opportunity to take this regime out, and it's going incredibly well. The drone carrier was just taken out. We have growlers jamming their electronics. We actually have attack helicopters flying over the capital of the country. Think about that. Six days ago, we were firing missiles from the Mediterranean, and now we have manned attack helicopters right across their capital, leveling their command and control posts, and they can't do a thing about it because they're blind.\par \par \uc2 The pace of U.S. operations is doubling over the last 48 hours and the pace of the Iranian offensive effectiveness has just dropped. It's plummeting.\par \par \uc2 The Israelis, they just lowered their threat level. They can come out of their bunkers now, flights are coming out of Tel Aviv now, their strategy is to cower and wait until we don't have the stomach for this and to inflict enough pain on our Arab allies hit an oil refinery or a tanker so it raises the cost of the war.\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth said it best, we have the will and the commitment and the munitions to see this thing through, and the president of the United States does not seem bothered by the timeline.\par \par \uc2 Right now you're already seeing desertions among the police, the IRGC and among regime elements. If that continues to go and you see this ethnic fracturing around the perimeter of the country, remember, it's mostly Persian, but you got Kurds, you got Arabs, you got Baluchis, you got a lot of different sects. If they start to fracture and you get an armed outside group come in and draw this -- whatever is left over of this regime out, it opens them up to air strikes. I think the rest of the country needs to look at this. We are six days in. This is going very well, and I think the United States military is the best in the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I bet the panel would probably agree on that last part, if not a lot of other things that you said.\par \par \uc2 Now, Kayleigh, I did notice that you were wincing every time he said, Governor Gavin Newsom, as President Newsom, you were a little faint there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm sorry, sorry, trying to make a point.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know how important messaging is especially in a moment like this, and that's what some of the critics are saying. Listen, the president has spoken about this. He's been very accessible the last few days, as he always is. But does he need to sort of land the plane on messaging here, because he's got trouble on the far right to a lot of the MAGA base who were worried about this.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think the effectiveness of this operation is landing the plane. I think the single most effective communication we've seen was the CENTCOM briefing yesterday that was extraordinary. They should do those often.\par \par \uc2 But as Jesse was talking, I was just looking up some facts and ensuring I was correct on them, and indeed, I was. You had President Trump when he left office, and this is something I'm going to talk to Vice President Mike Pence about tomorrow, a status quo in the Middle East that was rapidly going in a positive direction. You had Middle East peace accords. The first time in 25 years.\par \par \uc2 I was sitting there on the South Lawn when Bahrain and the UAE said, we are going to normalize relations with Israel. Two other countries came on board. I was told by people in the administration they would have been given a little more time. Saudi Arabia would have normalized relations.\par \par \uc2 Not only that, I was looking this up and confirming the last 11 months of the Trump presidency, you had not a single U.S. service member pass away in Iraq during active duty. That's an extraordinary accomplishment, and it didn't come just on its own. It came because President Trump got on the phone with the leader of the Taliban and said, if you harm the hair on the head of an American service member, you will have hell to pay, and it paid dividends.\par \par \uc2 And then you had Biden come in, and you had Abbey Gate, and you had all of these U.S. service members die at the Abbey Gate, the disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan. You had immediate resuming of talks with Iran. The posture changed and Iran grew and October 7th happened. This was the time to strike, if not now, when? Allow these maniacs to get nuclear weapons?\par \par \uc2 I mean, just today, you want another data point as to how crazy these religious fanatics were. Look at Trey Yingst's reporting that community chose not to go down into the bunker last Saturday because he was confident that nobody would have the guts to strike them.\par \par \uc2 Well, maybe you remember Biden. You don't remember President Trump because he struck you and the Ayatollah was killed immediately. Imagine if this wouldn't have happened. Imagine, God forbid, there's a Gavin Newsom presidency, and the damage that would be done to U.S. service members abroad. You sometimes need to show your strength. President Trump has done that. Iran will never have nuclear weapons, and that will be the legacy of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Jessica, to the points that both of them have made so far, Jesse and Kayleigh, this issue about imminence and the fact that proxies were, you know, fully funded, carrying out all kinds of terrible attacks, those kinds of things. We know that there were active assassination attempts that Iran hoped to carry out on President Trump. Where for you does the line come when it's imminent, not imminent?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, the right way to have you believe that it's been an imminent threat for 47 years, even though we were sending birthday cakes all around. And Jesse says, in five or 10 years, it could have gotten really bad. That would have given us a little more time to think this through. And you guys, basically listening to you should just wave that mission accomplished banner and go back to that kind of era, because you're talking as if that this is a done deal, that we are so overwhelming them that nothing can go wrong when the facts on the ground are that things are going wrong.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street Journal, New York Times, Reuters, NBC, confirming that it was U.S. airstrikes that were responsible for hitting that Iranian