he content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020260307em3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Trump Holds Cabinet Meeting As Shutdown Deadline Nears; Funding Bill Fails Key Senate Vote; Senate Democrats United Against Homeland Security Funds; Border Czar Meets With Minnesota Officials; Network Using Guides To Track Agents; Trump Administration Weighs New Iran Strike; \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  Is Being Interviewed About Possible ICE Surge Operation In Pennsylvania; Measles Outbreak in South Carolina becoming Worse; New Documentary about First Lady Melania Trump Premieres in Washington D.C.; Trump Administration Cabinet Meeting Televised; Some Speculate Department of Homeland Security \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 's Position in Trump Administration Uncertain in Wake of Minneapolis Protests; Senate Democrats Threaten Possible Government Shutdown over Department of Homeland Security Funding.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Bill Melugin, Brooke Taylor, Greg  Palkot; Jonathan Serrie, Alexandria Hoff, Reince Priebus, Juan Williams \par \uc2 7,995 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you so much. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, thank you. It was a great show.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A really great show. OK, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats defeat a bill to fund Homeland Security, setting up a possible government shutdown for the weekend. We'll bring you to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk with Pennsylvania Democratic governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  about politics, about ICE in Minnesota and around the country, and his new book.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the concerning measles outbreak in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Trump focuses on what he considers a political strength, the economy, avoids largely the Minnesota immigration crackdown, and Iran in his first Cabinet meeting of the year. This session was considerably shorter than previous meetings with no comments from several prominent members.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 With the Midterms on the horizon, President Trump is making the case the economy is booming. Officials here have long said that Americans will start to feel the fruits of their efforts come tax refund season. But new Fox polls show there is some ground to make up turning attitudes around.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are not going to go through the whole table, because the last time we had a press conference, it lasted for three hours, and some people said, he closed his eyes. Look, it got pretty boring.\par \par \uc2 But I didn't sleep. I just closed him because I wanted to get the hell out of here.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And with that, President Trump passed over a few key Cabinet officials in his first Cabinet meeting of the new year, including Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , as anticipation grows about possible U.S. intervention in Iran, and embattled DHS Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , who the president says he is standing by despite elevating his border czar.\par \par \uc2 The president, instead, put his focus on the economy.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Unprecedented. The numbers we've had on the economy and growth, you see them. And that's despite a Democrat shutdown.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): New Fox polls show voters favor Republicans on border security, national security, and immigration. But Democrats have a 14-point lead over the GOP on affordability and helping the middle class -- an early warning sign for the Midterms, despite recent efforts from the White House to spotlight falling prices.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We are bringing drug prices down by 80-90 percent.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And rising wages.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: Private sector is up. Growth was a stunning 4.7-4.8 percent in the past two quarters. Biggest back-to-back wins in a decade.\par \par \uc2 KELLY LOEFFLER, ADMINISTRATOR, UNITED STATES SMALL BUSINESS ADMINISTRATION: We are going to have seven percent GDP growth with interest rates going down, with regulation going down. These are all taxes on small businesses. And those are going away --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president says more help is on the way when \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  is replaced as federal reserve chair. The Fed held rates steady this week.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: The upside risks to inflation and the downside risks to -- risks to employment have diminished, but there -- they still exist.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Trump teeing off on Truth Social, saying, "We should have a substantially lower rate now that even this moron admits inflation is no longer a problem or threat."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The president said he expects to announce his pick for the new federal reserve chair next week. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, it appears increasingly likely that there will be a partial government shutdown this weekend. Congressional lawmakers are so far unable to come up with a funding solution for the government. The sticking point is money for Homeland Security and immigration.\par \par \uc2 And even if an agreement is reached by the U.S. Senate, the house is already out of session and would likely not be back until Sunday at the earliest.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Bill Melugin, live on Capitol Hill with the latest. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Sources tell Fox News that the White House and the Senate are getting close to a deal on this front. What that deal would see is that DHS funding bill be split away from the package of five other appropriations bills. Those five other bills have broad bipartisan support. They could pass those separately.\par \par \uc2 What would then happen with the DHS bill is they would use what's called a two-week continuing resolution to fund DHS for two weeks, while they negotiate what they want to do with this ICE money.\par \par \uc2 The problem with that is by changing anything about this package of bills, it would then have to be bounced back to the House. Well, the House isn't here this week, Bret. So, regardless of what happens with this deal, it appears we are still heading to a partial government shutdown starting tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): A partial government shutdown now seeming inevitable, after a key procedural vote on a government funding package failed in the Senate this afternoon, with every Senate Democrat voting no.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Should have decides we are going to shut down. If you look -- if you look procedurally does not have to know, the way -- that what he is done is make sure we going to have shutdown.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): At issue is a House passed package of six government funding bills, representing 78 percent of all federal funding, including DHS.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats say the DHS bill is dead on arrival because it funds ICE and doesn't reform the agency.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Well, I think the White House realizes that they made a huge mistake to let this agency go rogue.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): This is an out-of-control agency at a scale, Americans not have not seen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Senate Republicans accused their Democratic colleagues of caving to progressives.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): The Karen wing of the party wants to defund ICE, just like they wanted to defund the police. Therefore, the Democrats have got to vote against the bill.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): But even if Democrats successfully blocked the DHS bill and cause a shutdown, it will have virtually no impact on ICE, which got $75 billion in funding through 2029 via the One Big Beautiful Bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): ICE is going to be able to continue their operations. What the Democrats are really putting at risk is FEMA funding and TSA funding.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: All of this, setting up a new shutdown showdown.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): We want to avoid a government shutdown, but it's necessary to ensure that the Department of Homeland Security dramatically changes the way in which it has been conducting itself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): So, Bret, here is where we stand right now. As we hear about this potential deal between the White House and the Senate, 78 percent of the federal government is going to run out of funding by 11:59 p.m. tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Now, regarding that deal, we are told it is not set in stone yet. What's happening is senators are being asked how they feel about that deal a process called hot lining.\par \par \uc2 Now, if they were to come together and pass a package tonight, you would then need the House to come back. We just talked to Speaker Johnson a short time ago. He didn't rule out getting the house back here to Capitol Hill by Sunday, but he said that would be very, very difficult, and he warned it typically takes about 72 hours to get his conference and the whole House back here to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 So, still we are staring a shutdown in the face. Bret, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In a middle of more winter weather, here we go again.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, live on Capitol Hill. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed on a tough day for Microsoft and a big one for Meta. The Dow gained 56. The S&P 500 was off nine. The NASDAQ fell 172.\par \par \uc2 The new man in charge of the immigration enforcement effort in Minnesota says he is staying until the problem is gone.\par \par \uc2 White House borders czar Tom Homan spoke to reporters for the first time since his new assignment this morning. This comes as we look at new images of the protester killed in Saturday's confrontation with federal agents.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Brooke Taylor is in Minneapolis with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, UNITED STATES BORDER CZAR: We are not surrendering the president's mission in immigration enforcement. Let's make that clear.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE TAYLOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): White House border czar Tom Homan, says he is getting Operation Metro Surge back on track in Minnesota, focusing on targeted arrests of criminal illegal migrants and meeting with local and state officials to get on the same page.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: What we did agree upon is not to release public safety arrests back in the community.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): Homan says Minnesota Attorney General Keith Ellison agreed that local and state jails would honor ICE detainers by notifying the agency before releasing criminal illegal migrants onto the streets.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: If we get these agreements in place, it means less agents on the street.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): Homan trying to dial down the tone after the deaths of Renee Good and Alex Pretti.\par \par \uc2 New video shows Pretti confronting federal agents, spitting, then, kicking out their vehicle tail light, days before he was shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No! No!\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): A family member confirms this is Pretti in the video, where you see ICE agents take him down before later letting him go.\par \par \uc2 The Pretti family's attorney tells Fox, "Nothing that happened a full week before could possibly have justified Alex's killing."\par \par \uc2 Protesters gathering outside another Minneapolis hotel, Wednesday night, where they believed federal agents were staying.\par \par \uc2 A Fox Digital investigation found there is a far-left network using encrypted signal messages to post guides on how to track, monitor, and harass these agents.\par \par \uc2 ASRA NOMANI, FOX NEWS DIGITAL SENIOR EDITOR: This is a very professionally run organization, and Minneapolis is one front.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): Those chats reviewed by Fox News Digital show that people were already mobilized at the scene where Alex Preti was killed. But it's unclear if he was in the chat.\par \par \uc2 NOMANI: The far-left organizations that have put these surveillance operations into place of federal officers put Alex Pretti in danger. What they have done is they have turned him into a martyr.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): Meanwhile, Minneapolis Mayor Jacob Frey, sending this message from Washington, D.C. today.\par \par \uc2 JACOB FREY, MAYOR OF MINNEAPOLIS: We are on the front lines of a very important battle, and it's important that we are not silenced.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (on camera): And there has been a lot of back and forth tonight.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Ellison tells Fox News that he didn't strike any type of new deal with Homan. He says that the sheriff's office runs the jails. Ultimately, it's up to them to honor those ICE detainers. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brooke Taylor in Minneapolis. Brooke, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Another warning for Iran over how the government is treating protesters and the continued concerns about its nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is indicating another round of military action is possible, and the European Union is taking a series of actions. We get details tonight from senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As the USS Abraham Lincoln aircraft carrier group and other U.S. military assets move within striking distance of Iran, The Trump administration is getting tougher on Tehran.\par \par \uc2 New report says the White House is considering military options in response to the regime's deadly crackdown this month on protesters. That's along with demands, Iran negotiates a limit to how far its missiles can reach, a cut off of support to its deadly regional proxies, and an end to its potentially dangerous nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, UNITED STATES DEFENSE SECRETARY: They should not pursue nuclear capabilities, and we will be prepared to deliver whatever this president expects of the War Department.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Iran threatening to hit back against the U.S. if attacked.\par \par \uc2 ABBAS ARAGHCHI, FOREIGN MINISTER OF IRAN (through translator): Negotiations cannot take place under threats, and any talks must be conducted where excessive demands are set aside.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Countries in the area and beyond worried about the fallout from potential U.S. military action. That, according to the Trump administration, could be even more powerful than last June strikes on Iran's nuclear infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 DMITRY PESKOV, PRESS SECRETARY, RUSSIA (through translator): Any forceful actions could only create chaos in the region.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): White House is getting support from abroad. European Union sanctioning 15 Iranian officials for their role in protest crackdowns. The U.S. also now branding Iran's Revolutionary Guard a terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 KAJA KALLAS, FOREIGN POLICY CHIEF, EUROPEAN UNION: Those who operate through terror must be treated as terrorists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): Both the U.S. and Iran are set to conduct military exercises in the region in the next few days, a possible warm up of things to come. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot in London. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela's acting president has signed a law that will open the nation's oil sector to privatization. The measure reserve -- reverses a tenet of self-proclaimed socialist movement that has ruled the country for more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in the country's national assembly approved the overhaul of the energy industry earlier in the day, less than a month after the capture of disputed President Nicolas Maduro in a U.S. military operation in Venezuela's capital.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk with Pennsylvania Democratic governor Josh Shapiro about politics, ICE, action and his new book.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS". Tonight.\par \par \uc2 Great Britain and China signed business deals government agreements as part of the prime minister's visit to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 One deal allows British tourists and business visitors' visa free stays in China for up to 30 days.\par \par \uc2 The leaders of Britain and China are calling for a strategic partnership to deepen ties between their nations.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says he has asked Russian President Vladimir Putin not to target the Ukrainian capital and other towns for one week, as the region experiences frigid temperatures.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says Putin agreed. Russia has not confirmed that.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, Ukrainian leader Volodymyr Zelenskyy warned that Moscow is planning another large-scale attack, despite plans for further us brokered peace talks this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And Denmark's King Frederik, says he will take a trip to Greenland next month. The move comes as President Trump continues efforts to gain more control over the Danish territory. The king says he hopes to give Greenlanders a morale boost during a difficult time. Just some the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, WHITE HOUSE BORDER CZAR: I come here looking for solutions. I do not want to hear that everything has been done here has been perfect. Nothing is ever perfect.\par \par \uc2 President Trump and I, along with others in the administration, have recognized that certain improvements could and should be made.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Tom Homan is taking his marching orders from the president, and what I understood, and the reason that I believe there's an opportunity for something to do here. Because, just to be very clear, what the White House wants is for the scenes on the streets to stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Tim Walz and the border czar Tom Homan.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight to talk about that and other things Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro. His new book Where We Keep The Light: Stories From A Life Of Service is out right now. Governor, welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): Good to be with you. Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to ask you about the ICE situation. What's happening in Minnesota and across the country? You said earlier this week that you are preparing for at least the possibility of a large-scale ICE surge operation coming to Pennsylvania, you would think about it. In that case, do you support state and local authorities cooperating with ICE on their job?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: We're not a sanctuary state in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Philadelphia is a sanctuary city, but --\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: We're not a sanctuary state. And I do believe in law enforcement collaboration at all levels, local, state and federal.\par \par \uc2 But I also believe that all of your partners need to be working together on a mission that is constitutional, working together on a mission that reduces crime, that brings law and order to a community, and that protects people's constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 And respectfully, that's not what I'm seeing in Minnesota, so I'm happy to work with my partners at every level to create more safety, to build more trust.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, I made a pledge to hire 2,000 more police officers. I think we're about 1,900, we'll actually eclipse that 2,000 number. We've invested hundreds of millions of dollars in violence prevention. Violent crime is down 13 percent, fatal gun violence is down 43 percent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When you ask people, though, --\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: What we're doing is working.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Taking criminal illegal immigrants off the street. Would you honor ICE detainers, if you're not willing to hold someone longer than in a jail for ICE, what about a phone call that gives ICE the heads up that you're releasing that person? That's where a lot of the pushback is coming from.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: We want our communities to be safe, and we work with our federal partners where appropriate. As an example, if we have a violent criminal who is about to leave one of our jails, we do collaborate and communicate with our federal partners. We want our communities to be safe. What I'm seeing --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But that's ss problem with sanctuary cities and sanctuary states.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: We're not a sanctuary state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know you're not, but do you think that Governor Walz and Mayor Frey are doing the right thing?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Well, I'm not going to -- I'm not going to pine on other governors. I can tell you what I do in Pennsylvania. We work together to reduce crime. That is what we've accomplished.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Pennsylvania, to your point, is not a sanctuary state, but Philadelphia is a sanctuary city. How do you address some of the strident, some people even say dangerous rhetoric coming from people within your own party in the state, in particular, Philadelphia District Attorney Larry Krasner, who said this in front of Philadelphia City Hall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KRASNER, PHILADELPHIA DISTRICT ATTORNEY: This is a small bunch of wannabe Nazis. That's what they are. In a country of 350 million, we outnumber them.\par \par \uc2 If we have to hunt you down the way they hunted down Nazis for decades, we will find your identities. We will find you. We will achieve justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It makes some people really uncomfortable. They look at that and say, an elected official is dehumanizing law enforcement and could lead to someone getting really hurt. What's your take on that?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: That kind of rhetoric is unacceptable, it is abhorrent, and it is wrong. Period. Hard stop. End of sentence.\par \par \uc2 I think what we need, and I've called for this in Minnesota, and I practice this in Pennsylvania, is we need to bring down the rhetoric, bring down the temperature, and create calm in the community.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the president's policies have injected chaos onto the streets of Minnesota. I've been very outspoken about that. You may disagree with that statement, but I believe that to be true. I think we have seen that.\par \par \uc2 What we are trying to do in Pennsylvania is bring down the temperature and rhetoric like that is not helpful.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And in your book, you do write about it, to you know, there is a part on shared humanity. I have learned over and over again in my public and private life to be open to people of all different faiths and backgrounds, to fill your cup, to fill you up, and to understand our shared and common humanity, despite our differences at our cores, our values are the same. Our humanity is shared, and it's always there if we choose to see it. I hear what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 In the same breath, Governor, you said to NBC about J.D. Vance. You said the fact that J.D. Vance, the Vice President, couldn't bring himself to acknowledging that six million Jews were killed by Hitler and by the Nazis speaks volumes. It's not a surprise to me, however, given the way in which he has openly supported the AfD party, given the way he's openly embraced neo-Nazis and neo-Nazi political parties, given the way in which he has offered comfort, really, to the anti-Semites on the right who are infecting the Republican Party. I mean, essentially, you're calling J.D. Vance a neo- Nazi or anti-Semite.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Well, I'm not essentially calling him anything. I'm saying what I said, and I stand by that. Look --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But how can those two quotes match up the book excerpt and what you said to NBC?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Because I believe anti-Semitism is a problem on the political left, Bret and on the political right. And I think leaders need to speak and act with moral clarity, and need to call it out.\par \par \uc2 I just called it out on someone on the political left. I would like to see the vice president of the United States do the same. He should not allow for a Nick Fuentes or a Tucker Carlson or others who are promoting anti- Semitic views to be platformed. And my view is that he is seeking some short-term political gain by not criticizing them, and in the long run, I believe that makes our country less safe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were mad that it seemed like he didn't say Jews. He said, today we remember the millions of lives lost during the Holocaust, the millions of stories of individual bravery and heroism, one of the enduring lessons of one of the darkest chapters in human history, that while humans create beautiful things and aren't full of compassion. We're also capable of unspeakable brutality, and we promise never again to go down the darkest path. That's what you're responding to in NBC, and that's what you said.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I was offering a statement, not about a tweet, but of what I see as a track record. And to me, anti-Semitism is a problem in this country. You've spoken about it on your show. I'm sitting here telling you I believe it to be a problem on both sides of the political line. I'm not pointing fingers at one side ot the other. By my point is --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- the rhetoric is high and you want to moderate it and take it down, and then you say that to NBC about the vice president of the United States, I don't know. Does it match up?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: It sure does. I'm not attacking an individual personally the way you see coming from this administration, both the president and the vice president. I am pointing out that anti-Semitism is a problem, and I want to see the vice president of the United States do better.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Where do you think the future of your party is as you look forward and you may have aspirations, you've told me that you're not ready to say what those are yet, but --\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I'm running for reelection as governor of Pennsylvania, that's my aspiration.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's what I figured you'd say. But where do you think your party is, and what are the warning signs that you see for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I see a party with a lot of really capable leaders here in the Congress United States, I happen to think governors. Think very highly of governors. We've got some extraordinary governors around the country.\par \par \uc2 You reported on this show many times, the president said many times he won all seven swing states. He did. Six of the seven are governed by Democratic governors, governors who, like me, try and play between the 40s and get stuff done, good schools for their kids, safe communities, economic opportunity and affordable neighborhood to live in.\par \par \uc2 And I believe we're a party that stands up for people's rights and freedoms at a time where this administration here in Washington is eroding people's rights and freedoms.\par \par \uc2 And so, to me, I think there are a number of Democrats who are leading the way on that. There is not a singular leader in the Democratic Party the way there is obviously in the Republican Party, in Donald Trump. And that's OK. I think there's a lot of talent across the board.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're pretty critical of Kamala Harris in the book and how you were treated in this process. You think she's running for president?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I have no idea. It's a question for her. And actually I was, I thought, complimentary of her in the book, in the sense that when we had our conversation together, it was a direct and honest conversation, which I appreciated that, and it allowed me to make the decision that I did not want to be a part of that ticket. I was going to go out and work hard for it and do everything I could to ensure that Governor Walz and Vice President Harris won the election. I worked very hard to do that, as you well know and you document it.\par \par \uc2 But I appreciate the candor. I was candid back with her, and I wrote about that as one small part of a larger book.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can a Jewish Democrat who supports Israel win in your party?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I think the people who can win in my party, the people who can win in any party, are people who are going to deliver for the people that they want to represent. I think the good people of Pennsylvania are people who respect folks from all different walks of life, and I think Pennsylvania is, in many ways, really a microcosm of the rest of this great country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, an update on the alarming measles outbreak in South Carolina, and then we go to the red carpet outside the premiere of the new documentary film about First Lady Melania Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The measles outbreak in South Carolina appears to be getting worse. There have been nearly 800 cases so far. The outbreak is largely concentrated among unvaccinated children. Senior correspondent Jonathan Serrie has the latest from our southeast bureau.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Twenty-six years after measles was declared eliminated in the U.S., the nation is in jeopardy of losing that status. South Carolina health officials have recorded 89 new cases since Friday, bringing the state's total to 789 since the outbreak began in October.\par \par \uc2 DR. LINDA BELL, SOUTH CAROLINA STATE EPIDEMIOLOGIST: This is a milestone that we have reached in a relatively short period of time. It's just disconcerting to consider what our final trajectory will look like for measles.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Although the outbreak in upstate South Carolina is the largest in the nation, the CDC has confirmed measles in a total of 14 states as of its last update on Friday. Health experts say the two-dose vaccine is 97 percent effective in preventing the disease, with mostly mild side effects such as temporary soreness or rash at the injection site.\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER: We have a very effective and very safe vaccine that we've used for decades now, and around the world, in the millions upon millions of doses.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: But since the COVID pandemic, measles immunization rates have been below the 95 percent herd immunity threshold needed to limit its spread. In South Carolina, 95 percent of measles patients who know their immunization history say they did not get the measles vaccine. Although no one there has died, 18 patients had to go to the hospital. And while the most common measles symptoms, such as fever and rash, usually go away on their own in a couple of weeks, rare complications can be severe.\par \par \uc2 SCHAFFNER: One out of every 1,000 children, statistically, will develop this brain swelling encephalitis. And that can be a killer part of this infection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): And measles is one of the most contagious viruses, but it doesn't mutate as rapidly as flu or COVID. So most people who get that two-dose vaccine are protected for life without the need for additional boosters. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Trump has signed an executive order declaring a national emergency in establishing a process to impose tariffs on goods from countries that sell or otherwise provide oil to Cuba. The White House says the measure will protect U.S. national security and foreign policy from what it calls the Cuban regime's malign actions and policies.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in politics, Minnesota Democratic Senator Amy Klobuchar says she is running for governor. She's promising to take on President Trump while unifying her state that has endured, in her words, a massive fraud scandal and the federal government's immigration crackdown. She wants to succeed Tim Walz, who announced he would not seek reelection as that fraud scandal intensified.\par \par \uc2 A new documentary about the first lady, Melania Trump, premieres tonight here in Washington. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff is on the red carpet tonight with the latest. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Just about every administration A-lister will be here tonight. We're seeing everybody just beginning to trickle in. We have Ambassador Grenell over my shoulder at this point. The president and first lady, they are expected to arrive next hour. This is actually going to be the president's first time at the Trump-Kennedy Center since its renaming and at least his second time viewing the documentary, "Melania", which he calls a must-watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP, FORMER FIRST LADY: Together, with like-minded leaders, we have a voice.\par \par \uc2 Here we go again.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Debuting in theaters tomorrow, "Melania" follows the first lady for the 20 days leading up to President Trump's second inauguration. Mrs. Trump telling FOX today the intention is to give the public a rare personal glimpse inside a pivotal political moment.\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP: I'm a very private and selective person. I want to show the people my 20 days in my life. All the planning for integration, my business, my family, my philanthropy, everything.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The 104-minute documentary takes viewers from New York to Palm Beach and back to the White House. Melania is not just the subject, but the first lady also served as co-producer. Amazon MGM reportedly paid about $40 million for the rights and has spent nearly as much on the marketing, a price tag critics were quick to jump on, calling it --\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL, LATE NIGHT TALK SHOW HOST: The $75 million bribe, I mean, documentary that Amazon made for her.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The film is set to be released in over 1,400 theaters nationwide and in 30-