r in front of where the White House is, you know, at and what they want to announce, but this gets us exactly where we want to be, which is that security that we need to protect the homeland. It can't be overstated, Laura. You've talked about it, I've talked about it. How important that real estate is, whether it's our ballistic missile defense, the architecture we need for space as we continue down that road, the Arctic sea lanes that potentially open up, China, Russia, have operations off the coast of Greenland. There's Trans- Atlantic cables right off the coast of Greenland.\par \par \uc2 So, it does -- it gives us the ability to protect the homeland, the Western Hemisphere. It also, you know, is likely to lead to mineral -- rare earth and rare mineral exploration, which is really important. We're way too reliant on China. 80 percent, 90 percent of all those materials come from China for things that we need to build F-15s. That's unacceptable. So, and I think it will go a long way, the investment to solve some of the supply chain problems we have with rare earths.\par \par \uc2 So, taking a step back, look at what's happened in the last year, Panama Canal, making sure the Chinese don't control both ports, making sure narco- terrorists isn't sending criminals to this country from Venezuela. Now, with Greenland, the president is prioritizing the homeland and the Western Hemisphere, as he promised as an American foreign policy realist.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. I don't think it's a gambit, by the way, it says gambit. It's not a gambit. This is not a gamble for Donald Trump. This is a necessity. This is an urgency. And the fact that Donald Trump had the guts and the foresight and the basic knowledge with a great team in place, I got to give a lot of credit to his military top aides, to Hegseth, to Rubio, all of them, because they laid it out for him very clearly. He heard all the facts, and he said, We got to go. We got to do something here. So this is an urgent situation. It's not like a fluke. It's not like he's rolling the dice. Not a gamble. This is urgent, and we need to get it done and he's doing it. Senator.\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: 100 percent, 100 percent, and he went into Davos, into the lion's den, right? He told the Europeans what they don't want to hear, but it's the truth, which is, the world is safer when America is leading, and we finally have a president that puts America's interests first, this sort of post-cold war order that permanent Washington loves and the cocktail parties and they talk about all this stuff, President Trump doesn't care about any of that. He cares about protecting the American people. And he went into Davos and he worked a deal that will make us safer.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And you know, so we talked about China's interests. Russia's interests would be how ICBMs could basically use that air space and do enormous lethal damage to U.S. interest, to the United States. Again, it's such a large piece of -- second largest island in the world, most of it frozen, a lot of minerals underneath. But again, it's a necessity, and this Swedish Foreign Minister talking about how this is blackmail. Blackmail would be taking something just because you can take it and you want to hurt the other people. Without America, what is NATO? NATO is an acronym.\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Well, that's right, and you look at the -- since 1980, the imbalance between what we spend in other NATO countries is $22 trillion, right? So there's a rebalancing that has to happen. Europe has to step up in a much more meaningful way for their own defense, so we can focus on the Western Hemisphere in the rise of China and the Indo-Pacific. That's just the truth. That is a reality that if anybody's been paying attention, you talk about it, I talk about it, but it's amazing, the sort of stages of grief that I think a lot of our NATO allies have go through to accept that reality, this isn't 1960 anymore, right? We are moving towards 