e're going to walk into offices to get Trump thrown out of office. People didn't know what to do. They were walking in and walking out, so they decided to stay in place.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But I was going say, but the workplace without them is probably a lot more efficient. And that would assume that they're working when they're inside. When I was in Minneapolis, I'm like, what are you guys doing? I'm being paid right now. So, you know, let's just -- that's kind of --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: None of this made sense. You got to have a clear mandate if you're going to do a protest. Just do one thing, but you can't have people do contradictory things at the same time. Walk in, walk out. So, when you do that, everybody stays put. That's what happened here. A failure of even a protest. Not good. Not good. I'm mad as hell because the protest was so lousy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, say it. Say it with me. Free.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Dumb.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: D.C. Dumb. I was trying to get you to do Free D.C.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Very dumb.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But this is what I said at the Minneapolis protest I was at. I just went up to them. They got very upset. I said, you need better chants. None of these rhyme. They have no rhythm. And that's when they started screaming shame, you know, disgrace to the human race. I'm like, that still doesn't do it for me. I mean, it just doesn't.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Raymond, with all this creative power of Hollywood, they can't come up with a decent chant. Raymond, thank you, my friend. Walk in, walk out. And coming up, the one simple thing you can do to protect yourself against the flu and other respiratory viruses. Dr. Siegel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Excuse me. There's nothing worse than a cough that won't go away. And we know RSV and the flu are raging right now, but to avoid the misery, a new study says a pocketful of sunshine may help. But Angle viewers, you should have already known that because almost six years ago, our COVID medicine cabinet touted the benefits of sun and Vitamin D.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Take your Vitamin D, take your zinc, wash your hands, and let's let the science, not press releases, decide how we should proceed.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Vitamin D is a completely safe supplement and it's beneficial for keeping the immune system working reasonably well.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Let me tell you, if you take Vitamin D, this disease is nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And now this new Oxford study confirming that a daily dose of Vitamin D can help protect you from the flu. People who are Vitamin D deficient were 33 percent more likely to get admitted to the hospital with a respiratory tract infection.\par \par \uc2 Here to discuss is Dr. Marc Siegel, Fox News Senior Medical Analyst, author of The Miracles Among Us. Doctor, how much Vitamin D should we be taking?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR MEDICAL ANALYST: First of all, it's a miracle vitamin. It's a super vitamin. And with the people you just showed were right that it definitely decreased severity of COVID. It was useful, very useful during COVID as was zinc. Zinc stops viruses from replicating. And Vitamin D is a super immune vitamin. It revs up the immune system and it tells the immune system, don't attack our cells, go after foreign cells. It works together with Vitamin C.\par \par \uc2 Now, how much of it you should take. The first thing you should do is your doctor should be testing you for it. Because, Laura, 40 percent of people are Vitamin D deficient. I think it's more than that. I test every single patient for it. And I don't tell them to go out and get it in the sunshine over there, because then I worry about skin cancer.\par \par \uc2 I give them a supplement. I give a lot of my patients these supplements, and I monitor their levels. And they say I feel more energy. Their blood pressure goes down. They fight off infections better. And what you should take, to answer your question finally, is somewhere around 2,000 to 5,000 units a day.\par \par \uc2 I start with 2,000 units a day, and I recommend it for a lot of people. And it definitely decreases your risk of severe flu.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, they found that in senior centers, convalescent rehab facilities where elderly are housed, they're woefully deficient in Vitamin D. And that's a, I mean, that is devastating. I mean, that's just so simple. All elderly people need to think about taking Vitamin D. And if they can't care for themselves, their supposing caregivers at these facilities need to do it.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: I think we're also talking about a larger issue here is we go really quickly to pharmaceuticals. We really -- and I like them, but we go really quickly to talk about shots, but we don't talk about basic nutritional needs. Vitamin C also bolsters the immune system.\par \par \uc2 And you just said something else about nursing homes. We sequester people. They're in rooms. They never go out. They don't see other people. They don't commune. And, yeah, twenty minutes, a half hour, an hour of sunshine a day would be amazing for their self-esteem and for their feeling. You know that warehousing of people is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 Vitamin D is a super hormone. I want almost everybody to take it. I want everybody to ask their doctor about it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Siegel, really quickly, you and I have talked about marijuana, the scourge of marijuana for years, and now the medical marijuana lie is finally being exposed. Researchers analyzed three types of product, those with THC, CBD, and a combo THC, CBD items, found that none of these three options reduce pain any more than placebo medications. Doctor, are you surprised?\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: I just spoke to Kevin Hill about that. He was a senior author on that study from Harvard. I thought he was going to say, oh, there's a use for this. He said, no, it's a third or a fourth line agent, no use at all. A highly respected head of addiction psychiatry at Harvard agrees with us.\par \par \uc2 And we're Ivy Leaguers too, Laura, but still, I mean, your law degree from University of Virginia notwithstanding. This guy is on the right team. It's a terrible -- cannabis is terrible for you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean, Dr. Siegel, I think the president has heard a lot of false information about this. And we get trashed for it, and I don't care. It doesn't bother me one bit. Dr. Siegel, thank you very much. Jesse Watters is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020260122em1l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Faith And The Trump White House; Interview With Turning Point CEO Erika Kirk About Her Journey To Finding Strength, Grace, And A New Mission After A Season Of Unimaginable Loss; Trump Administration Targets ISIS In Nigeria; Ukraine Peace Talks Continue As Zelenskyy Meets President trump At Mar-a-Lago; Revival Events Sweep College Campuses As Gen Z Embraces Faith; Erika Kirk on Raising Her and Charlie's Kids; Astronaut Butch Wilmore Opens Up About Leaning on Religion While Stranded in Space; Zelenskyy to Meet With President Trump Today; Ukraine Reports Hundreds of Russian Drones Launched Into Kyiv; Karen Kingsbury Talks about "The Christmas Ring."\b0\par\par\uc2 Madison Scarpino, Shannon Bream, Mollie Hemingway, Lucas  Tomlinson \par \uc2 9,823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A special edition of "FOX News Sunday" as we look at the state of faith in America and around the world.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): President Trump announces strikes on \uc2 ISIS\uc2  terrorists in Nigeria amid mounting concerns over religious persecution in that country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They're killing the Christians and killing them in very large numbers. We're not going to allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We break down the next steps ahead of the president's critical week on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK, CHAIRWOMAN & CEO, TURNING POINT USA, WIFE OF CHARLIE KIRK: I keep getting the same question. How could you forgive him? How could you forgive the man who murdered your husband? The answer to me is just so simple.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Turning Point CEO Erika Kirk discusses her journey to finding strength, grace, and a new mission after a season of unimaginable loss.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 TONYA PREWETT, FOUNDER OF UNITEUS: There's an army of young people rising up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: American revival. From campuses to social media feeds, Gen Z is increasingly turning to religion to seek age-old guidance for the modern world.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Under us, a savior is born.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lights, camera, faith. A new holiday film shining a light on the true reason for the season.\par \par \uc2 All right now, on "FOX News Sunday" -- a FOX faith special.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of today's top stories.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  set to meet with President Trump at Mar-a-Lago this afternoon, looking to make progress on a potential peace deal to end the war in Ukraine. And Russia claims it's taken control of more villages in eastern Ukraine. We're going to take you live to Florida for an update a little bit later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  will meet with President Trump there to go over the next steps toward lasting peace in Gaza. Netanyahu has been expressing skepticism about phase two of the deal, which U.S. Envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  and the president's son-in-law \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  have been trying to hammer out. It calls for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to hand over weapons and for Israeli forces to pull back.\par \par \uc2 Well, Times Square New Year's Eve organizers are putting the final pieces in place for this year's crystal ball, the largest and heaviest ever. And in a twist, it's designed to make a second move this year after dropping to herald America's 250th birthday year.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we look forward to that, we wanted to take a special look today at the state of faith in our country, how it's shaping policy, family, and young people.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll hear from Turning Point CEO Erika Kirk on how she's doing everything she can to keep her husband Charlie's mission alive and thriving.\par \par \uc2 But first, FOX News correspondent Lucas Tomlinson takes a look at how faith is taking center stage in many of our country's biggest cultural and political moments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : My life was saved for a reason. I was saved by God to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Six months after an assassin's bullet nearly ended his life --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Father, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): -- President Trump returned to the White House, vowing to return the importance of faith into all aspects of American life.\par \par \uc2 He created a White House Faith Office and elevated religious advisers' roles within the administration.\par \par \uc2 The president does not shy away from releasing images of himself praying with religious leaders.\par \par \uc2 On the same day as the National Prayer Breakfast in February, he signed an executive order to address what he called anti-Christian bias and --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Bring God back into our lives.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): In addition to strong evangelical support, it was also young people who supported the president's return to office.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, TURNING POINT USA FOUNDER: Ladies and gentlemen, you're about to witness history. The 45th and 47th president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): No one more responsible than Charlie Kirk. Trump received 10 percent more of the youth vote in 2024 than he did four years prior.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I want to express my tremendous gratitude to Charlie Kirk. He's really an amazing guy. Amazing guy.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): America's faith was tested after Charlie's assassination in September. Trump said he was killed for his beliefs in freedom, God, and common sense.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Our greatest evangelist for American liberty became immortal. He's a martyr now for American freedom.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Erika now at the helm of Turning Point spoke at this year's America Fest.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: He would say this country is still worth defending and still worth sacrificing for.\par \par \uc2 So, like all of you, this is our turning point as well, especially as we move into 2026.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Thousands of students in schools now want to start New Turning Point chapters.\par \par \uc2 Two days before he was killed, President Trump made the following prediction at the Museum of the Bible.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : When faith gets weaker, our country seems to get weaker. When faith gets stronger as it is right now. We're having a very good period of time. After some rough years, good things happen for our country.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): At times, President Trump has been candid about his faith.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm not sure I'm going to be able to make heaven, but I made life a lot better for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): As part of the nation's 250th birthday celebrations next year, Trump says it will include a national prayer event on the Mall.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : To rededicate our country as one nation under God. We're not changing that. There are a lot of people would like to see it, it'll never happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, faith is certain to play a big role in those festivities as well as at Turning Point where Erika Kirk is looking to carry on Charlie's legacy.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with her in Phoenix as she was overseeing the organization's first major gathering as CEO.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wonderful to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "Fox News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So just thinking back on that day, the memorial to me, the -- I've never seen anything else like that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In Charlie Kirk, we found a great American leader.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And to have the sitting vice president, the secretary of state --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Because he carried that cross, we were freed from the sin that separated us from him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- basically gave what I think is one of the clearest, most succinct, full gospel sermons of the Christian faith --\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: So powerful.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- what did that day mean for you personally? Do you think something was sparked there or was a continuation of the idea of revival?\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: I -- I -- it was a continuation when Charlie was murdered, that was the spark. But the memorial service solidified that spark and the power that the Holy Spirit has been totally unleashed in a way that I don't think any of us could have imagined and to have hundreds and thousands of people together singing "Hallelujah", that is a slice of heaven on Earth that I'll never -- you can't -- you can't even articulate that. It was so powerful.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's one thing to memorize these verses, God is close to the broken hearted or he works all things for good, for those who love him called to according to his purpose, what they meant for evil, he meant for good.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But to live, it is something totally different.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, you've walked into a different way of experiencing those verses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: That man, that young man, I forgive him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: It's interesting because I keep getting the same question. How could you forgive him? How could you forgive the man who murdered your husband?\par \par \uc2 And the answer to me is just so simple. You trust in the Lord and when you trust in the Lord, you do it. And faith is so powerful when it's lived out.\par \par \uc2 But I -- I don't have this massive in-depth answer. God is good. And world -- the world is evil. Don't be surprised when the world acts like the world, but I know that the Lord will use everything.\par \par \uc2 But when, you know, without of a shadow of a doubt, that the Lord is your refuge, the Lord is your strength and you wake up every morning and you say, God, give me the grace. Show me the direction I'm supposed to go. Use me -- just how Charlie said on stage two years ago here at AmFest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 C. KIRK: Lord, use me. Here I am. Lord, use me as an instrument for your will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Use me Lord, here I am.\par \par \uc2 And when you surrender your life to that and that posture, everything around -- else around you is just irrelevant, because God is so good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you -- that's the second time you've said that, that God is good.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a lot of people would look at a young mom, a young widow and say, how in the world can you say, God is good? You've said that you're not angry at God. You're not saying why, why me, why this situation? And I know that grief is a journey and every minute by minute is different.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But what are your conversations like with him now about where you're at?\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: With -- with God?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Quite frankly, they're -- they're kind of the same. I've never questioned why me. I always knew that my life was not just to be lived for me. We're here for such a greater purpose and Charlie and I both knew that.\par \par \uc2 And that's why Charlie built what he built. And I talked to God, like I said, the same way. You know, God used me, God heal my heart. You know my pain, you know the depths of my pain, walk through this with me, put the people in my path that will help heal me and guide me and direct me and keep me in lockstep with your will.\par \par \uc2 I don't want anything outside of God's will. I do not. Anything outside of the jet streams of God will, God's will, do not. I do -- will not even touch it with a 10-foot pole.\par \par \uc2 But even with Charlie, too, I still talk to him. I'll be in the kitchen, randomly being like, babe, can you believe what happened today? Like, I still -- there's something powerful about communication. And the veil is so thin.\par \par \uc2 And I just know that prayer doesn't have to be this, you know, heavy religious thing. It's just, it's just communication with God. It's so simple. You just talk to God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you feel like you hear from him that he speaks back to you?\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm. But it's -- everyone's different.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Everyone hears it in their own way. Some -- some people it's this loud, booming voice. Some people, it's something really soft in your heart and other people, they get visions. I'm not going to judge how other people hear the Lord's voice.\par \par \uc2 But I do -- and for me, it's peace. When I -- when I'm talking to God and I -- I don't have a piece about something, that's not of the Lord. But when I have peace, I know it's him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned recently Nehemiah.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which I thought was so interesting because if people know the story --\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Building the world, yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. I mean, it was a man who was a Jewish exile.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he had served the king where he was working so well that when he said, I want to go back and rebuild Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to rebuild that city. The king was like, great. You can go, and I'm going to give you all these resources.\par \par \uc2 But when he got there, there were people who were not happy --\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: No.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- about what he was doing. They were making fun of him.\par \par \uc2 But it turned into something much different that they were like --\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- actively trying to sabotage him.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he stood strong and he accomplished something that couldn't have been done through human effort.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you draw from that story? Do you feel kind of like a modern-day Nehemiah?\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: In some ways, because I mean, we're carrying on Charlie's legacy and there is so much noise. I know I've said this many times before, and there will always be noise. Whenever you are over the target, there will -- the enemy -- I mean, we're in an enemy occupied territory. There will always be noise and there will always be people that'll distract you.\par \par \uc2 If myself or Charlie spent every single second going down and responding to every accusation, responding to every insult, responding to every judgment, we would get nothing done. We just wouldn't.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of -- you know, Nehemiah, he basically was saying, Lord, strengthen my hands. And so, I feel the same way. Like I'm not going to ask God to remove those people from my life. It just makes me stronger. So, Lord, instead of removing these people in this noise at the bottom of the hill, while I'm trying to build, just give me stronger shoulders, give stronger -- stronger hands to keep building, because that's always going to be there. That's the enemy. And that's -- that's life, that's life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 It's a lot to manage. It's a whole new role.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 There's some real struggles in there. I would imagine.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Of course. I mean, that's life. That you're -- you're -- but multiple things can be true at the same time. Yes, I'm grieving. Yes, alongside our great team running Turning Point USA. Yes, I'm raising my babies, and you don't separate those into different boxes. They can all coexist.\par \par \uc2 And so, yeah. I take my babies to the office. I want my kids to see what their father built. I want them to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 Maybe it's supposed to be something that inspires them and encourages them to think, okay, we live in the greatest country where you can be so many different things. You can be a mother, you can be an author, you can be a nurse. You can be a teacher in one full lifetime. You don't have to just be one thing.\par \par \uc2 And, honestly, being a mother is one of the greatest honors and privileges and job titles you could ever have, because there's something so powerful and true, authentic, biblical womanhood. And it's not something to be afraid of. It's not something that you have to be categorized as, and you don't have to fall into, you know, being a tradwife and making sourdough bread. I never fell in that category or being a boss babe, and never being around your kids.\par \par \uc2 There's a -- balance is slightly not real, but there's a way to cohesively blend your life in a way that's honoring to the Lord and what he's blessed you with, but also to what he's held you responsible for.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And community is so important, like you said.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Believers that you don't even know, there's an instant connection.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, for you, I wonder if it was always tricky or if it's impossible now to worship in community, to actually go to church?\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: It's -- it's interesting. I -- I intentionally have not -- since 9/10, I have intentionally not brought my children to a full service. I did bring them to a daytime service at one point. But when it comes to Sunday or Saturday worship, when I would go to a physical church, I chose not to bring my children. I don't need the photos of my children.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: I would like to keep some things in my life very private, and Charlie and I were very intentional about that with our kids.\par \par \uc2 We would always believe in my children. I would like to keep some things in my life very private, and Charlie and I were very intentional about that with our kids. We would always believe that it's important to protect what you love, and I don't need -- I want my babies to have a childhood. I don't need them to be dragged through the mud on social media. They're precious to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, stick around. Part two of our one-on-one sit down with Erika Kirk later this hour. She talks about Charlie's final book and its important message for increasingly fast-paced and technology dominated world, as well as the future of the organization they built together.\par \par \uc2 And much more ahead on this special edition of "Fox News Sunday," an out- of-this-world story of faith and resilience. Astronaut Butch Wilmore tells us how his church, family, and gratitude to God kept him going while he was stranded in space for months.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUTCH WILMORE, ASTRONAUT: There's millions of people around the globe that would love to be sitting in that spot, and my Lord my -- my -- he put me there. Looking out and seeing earth go by at that speed, like wow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What's the universe look like from your perch at this point?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WILLIAMS, ASTRONAUT: I got to say one of the most incredible things I've ever seen was the first time we have a module on the space station called the Cupola, which basically has windows looking down at Earth. It's almost kind of a full hemisphere that you can look out at. And I would say that the first time that I -- I got to look out at that and see the earth below me, I mean, that was just absolutely breathtaking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was our zero-gravity interview with Astronaut Chris Williams on board the International Space Station earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Now, back in April, we traveled to Texas to talk with another astronaut, Butch Wilmore, who relied a lot on his faith after his mission did not go as planned.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: Time and again, times that I think things aren't good every single time, it's like, wow, Lord, you had the best in mind.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Making the best out of a chaotic Boeing Starliner test flight, an eight-day mission turned into 286.\par \par \uc2 And hopes of heading home dashed over and over again.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: Failure is not an option. That's why we are staying here now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): For many, that would spark a crisis of faith. But for veteran astronaut Butch Wilmore, the harrowing ordeal only strengthened it.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: I can go and give so many examples from scripture of how the Lord worked providentially in situations that looked really bad. I don't -- I not only see that in scripture, but I see it in experience, in my life. And I can only see that and understand that because he gives me the ability to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Wilmore, along with fellow astronaut Suni Williams, inspired the nation with their remarkable story of resilience. Millions watched last month when they splashed down to Earth after a 17-hour journey in a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  capsule.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Splashdown, crew nine, back on Earth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Back home, Butch credits his survival to staying connected to his faith and to his church.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: Well, goodness, the word of God can continually infilling me. I need it. My pastors are the finest pastors on or off, in this case, the planet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): At Providence Baptist Church, Butch inspires not only through his many career accomplishments as an astronaut and top Navy test pilot, but through his unwavering devotion to family, faith and ministering to his church's flock as an elder.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: God's word is true, and we are here to love.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Even hundreds of miles above the Earth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Danny, what's it like to get a call and realize where it's coming from?\par \par \uc2 DANNY BIELAMOWICZ, PROVIDENCE BAPTIST CHURCH CONGREGANT: You see the space station on the TV and you think it's so far up, but it's 254 miles and you just can't imagine to be receiving that from somebody from your church, much less anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You're thinking you're going to be gone for a matter of days, but it turns into month after month after month.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you didn't let that stop the ministry and the relationships that you have.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: Technology is wonderful. I mean, I could -- there's a camera up there. I tied into every service every Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 BILLY ADKISON, PROVIDENCE BAPTIST CHURCH PATRIARCH: When he first got up there, he called me. He'd been up there four days and I'd had a little heart trouble. So, his wife told him, so he called me, and then our conversation, I said, "Well, Barry, it took you four or five years to get that thing where you get up there. We hope it don't take that long to get back."\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 ADKISON: Well, they like to come back to hold on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What did you think when month after month went by and he was still there?\par \par \uc2 ADKISON: Knowing him, I knew he'd get through. I don't know anybody that puts his whole life in God's hand anymore than he does.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): According to Providence Church patriarch, Mr. Billy, it's not only Butch that puts his life in God's hands, but sometimes the passengers in Butch's car.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You said here on earth, he's driven you around and he drives like an astronaut. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ADKISON: You better hold on.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGH)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Even more bittersweet than providing spiritual guidance from space, parenting teenagers. Butch was forced to miss much of his youngest daughter's senior year of high school, but remained a constant presence in both his kids' lives.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: I still have to be a dad from afar. I still have to be a husband from afar. And doing my dad thing is a huge responsibility. I didn't shirk my responsibilities just because I wasn't there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The church elder even finding time to put the fear of God into his daughter's boyfriend.\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: I shared some -- some thoughts with him because that's what dads of daughters do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Butch prefers, however, not to dwell on time lost, but to look forward to the joys of days ahead, especially this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You will be home for Easter this year here in your church. What does that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: Well, Easter is the culmination of the biblical narrative, right? The resurrection. To be here and share that with my home church, there's nothing better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What is it like, though, in the context of your faith to look around and see creation from a different viewpoint, to get a glimpse out into the universe and feel the goodness or the greatness of God?\par \par \uc2 WILMORE: I will say this. I did not need to go to space to learn about my Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ, to be -- that experience to draw me with that in mind, looking at the grandeur of this earth that Isaiah says that the Lord designed and made and created to be inhabited and looking at it from that vantage point gives me such an appreciation because I can close my eyes like I am now. And in my mind's eye, I can see it as if I'm there. And oh, my, it just magnifies my Lord.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a quick note, after 25 years at \uc2 NASA\uc2 , Butch retired in August. Underlining what he told us there in that interview with this retirement statement, he said, in part, "The magnificence of the cosmos mirrored the glory of its creator in ways words can scarcely convey."\par \par \uc2 Well, up next, a high stakes meeting between Ukraine's leader and President Trump today. And then personal words of wisdom from Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  on how faith guides her.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Much more to come on our "Fox Faith Special" today, but first, we want to get you back to the headlines and two key meetings set for today and tomorrow between President Trump and the leaders of Ukraine and Israel. Fox News Correspondent Madison Scarpino joins us from West Palm Beach with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Shannon. Yeah, two high stakes meetings. We're expecting President Zelenskyy to meet with Trump around 1 p.m. and there's a lot on the line as President Trump is hoping to bring this years' long war to an end.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: Tomorrow I will have a -- I hope very important and very constructive meeting with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice-over): That was Zelenskyy in Canada Saturday as the Ukrainian president seems to be pleased with the U.S. backed peace plan. The plan reportedly involves demilitarization zones and U.S. security guarantees, but President Trump is reportedly saying Zelenskyy does not have anything until he approves it. The meeting comes as Ukraine's military reports Russia launched more than 500 drones and dozens of missiles in Kyiv Saturday. Explosions booming across the capital, killing at least one person and wounding 27 others.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: This attack is again Russia's answer on our peace efforts and it's really showing that Putin doesn't want peace and we want peace.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: A defiant \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  pushing back Saturday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT: If Kyiv authorities are not willing to end the matter peacefully, we will achieve all the goals we have in the special military operation by military means.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: But Zelenskyy is not the only leader meeting with Trump this week. On Monday, we're expecting a visit from Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu. He'll reportedly give Trump an update on Iran and its ballistic missile program. And they'll also discuss the future of the Gaza peace deal.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Department of War releasing a video of that deadly Christmas day strike against \uc2 ISIS\uc2  in Nigeria. This happening in coordination with the Nigerian government. And it comes after a surge of attacks on Christians in the foreign country.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans are praising the president for taking action, but not all lawmakers are on board.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): We want to be clear that there are disturbing issues in Nigeria. But the question is, what is the America's responsibility to it?\par \par \uc2 It is the Congress that needs to be making a lot of these decisions about escalating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: And President Trump is threatening more attacks in Nigeria if the killing of Christians continue. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Madison Scarpino in Florida. Thank you so much, Madison.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group, former Biden White House special assistant and director of message planning, Meghan Hays. And Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief of "The Federalist" and a Fox News contributor. Thank you guys for being here on essentially a holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Let's start there with these strikes. Some Tomahawk missiles dropping on two \uc2 ISIS\uc2  locations in Nigeria. "National Review" writes about it this way. It says, "The Christmas strikes are yet another sign that rather than being a quasi-isolationist, like some of his most vociferous supporters, Trump is a hyperactive foreign affairs president. He makes a lot of threats and enemies completely discount them at their own peril as Nigerian militants learned on Christmas Day."\par \par \uc2 Mollie, where does this go?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's true that President Trump is not an isolationist, as some of his critics claim. He's also not a neo-con interventionist, as some people would like him to be.\par \par \uc2 He does seem to be laser-focused on what is in America's interest and -- and prosecuting those interests, but not engaging in very long drawn-out wars with boots on the ground that we saw from some previous Republican and Democrat presidents.\par \par \uc2 The issue with the Christians in Nigeria, President Trump had said quite plainly that that things needed to be done to stop the killing, the religious genocide of Christians in Nigeria.\par \par \uc2 And what we have learned from when he threatened strikes, he intend -- he tends to follow through on those if he does not get what he demands.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And he did say that the Nigerian government was working with this because when this -- when some of these accusations came out earlier in the year and -- and previous to that, they said, oh, no, no, that's not what's happening there. But he says they were in on these strikes and they welcomed the help.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, FORMER BIDEN WHITE HOUSE SPECIAL ASSISTANT: Yes. And I think there's been some conflicting reporting about the villages that are being targeted that it might not have been the correct village that with the most \uc2 ISIS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But again, going after \uc2 ISIS\uc2  is important. That is a terrorist organization and we should not be criticizing a strike to \uc2 ISIS\uc2 . They should be eradicated.\par \par \uc2 But I do agree with \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 , as you have in that package, that Congress should have a say in this and taxpayer dollars are being spent here. So, I think we do need to know more information. There just hasn't been a lot of information, I assume, because it is a holiday. The Pentagon hasn't put out their briefing and Congress hasn't been briefed yet.\par \par \uc2 But again, getting rid of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  is an important task for the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. That's one thing they can't agree on.\par \par \uc2 So, let's talk about Ukraine and Russia. Obviously, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  will be at Mar-a-Lago to meet with President Trump today.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " says this, that Zelensky told them, "He hopes to agree on a framework with President Trump for ending the war when they meet Sunday."\par \par \uc2 He also revealed, "He's willing to bring that plan up for a referendum if Russia agrees to a ceasefire of at least 60 days."\par \par \uc2 But, Meghan, certainly not a ceasefire activity what we saw from Russia on Christmas and all throughout these recent days.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Yes. I mean, let's not forget that Russia invaded a sovereign nation here and they could stop this at any time. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has no interest in peace. He continues to make that well-known over and over again.\par \par \uc2 As soon as Zelensky left, he started -- he targeted them on Christmas. He targeted Kyiv again when -- when Zelenskyy left to go to Florida. So, it's interesting here what we're actually going to see come out of this.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy has been trying to make concessions and trying to get to a peace -- a peace plan. Putin is not interested in that and Trump is being played again every step of the way. He got played in Alaska. He's getting played every step here.\par \par \uc2 So, it'll be interesting to see what shakes out of this meeting because Putin has to also participate here and he is not in -- working in good faith.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, so, what's the -- what is a win from this meeting then for ending the conflict?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, you know, this is a war that Trump feels that if he were president, it never would have started. We have seen Russia make aggressive action under so many different presidents, not Trump.\par \par \uc2 But he did say that he was going to end this war very quickly and it's been almost a year into his presidency. So, you have to imagine he's a bit frustrated that it's taken this long.\par \par \uc2 But this was always going to end in a negotiated settlement. That's how it will end. And so people -- the more people realize that and the quicker they realize that would be great.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine, you know, one and a half million casualties in this war. This is a really awful thing. And we see some pictures, but we don't really see like we saw in the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  situation that we saw so many horrific pictures. We don't get a lot of those here, but it's just as horrific and it's just as much something that people should be working to end this quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And to the Middle East, the president going to be hosting \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  also in Florida tomorrow to have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " reports this. It says, "White House officials think Netanyahu is slow walking the peace process and fear he will resume the war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . But while the Israeli Prime Minister is butting heads with Trump's team, he hopes to bring the president himself over to his more hawkish point of view."\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes. Well, the white -- we've had reporting that the White House is a bit frustrated with not seeing the resolution that they thought they had on the table here.\par \par \uc2 Israel is a wonderful ally to the United States, and that's a relationship that a lot of Americans support. It's also true that people really want to see peace in this region, and they want everybody working hard for it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. What do you think could come from this meeting tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: I don't think that Trump is going to come over to Bibi's side. I think that Bibi has to realize that he's losing standing here in the world, and Trump is trying to help him get there.\par \par \uc2 It is very similar to the same issues that the Biden administration had with Bibi trying to get peace in this region. It is a very challenging thing, but I just -- Netanyahu has to realize that time is running out for him for people to want to work with him. And he should take the olive branches of the White House is working with him to -- to get.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. We'll be waiting on big readouts from both those meetings. I want to touch on something. Somebody that we all have. And, Mollie, you've known him longer than most, but \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2 , who was a senator here, and was a college president as well. He posted this week this on X. He says, "I'll cut to the chase. Last week, I was diagnosed with metastasized -- metastasized stage four pancreatic cancer, and I'm going to die. Those who know ourselves to need a Physician, that's with a capital P -- this is a defined reference he's making -- should dang well look forward to enduring beauty and eventual fulfillment. That is, we hope in a real deliver, a rescuing God born at a real time in a real place. But the eternal city with foundations and without cancer is not yet."\par \par \uc2 I mean, Mollie, I couldn't recall reading something so heart-breaking and beautiful at the same time where he just talks about how his faith is sustaining him in the midst of this real tragedy.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes. Well, this is just horrible news that he has just received about dying from pancreatic cancer. This is a man who has two recent college graduate daughters and a son who's only 14. His wife has health problems that we've already talked about.\par \par \uc2 And for him to process this news and immediately talk to everybody about why he is not fearing death because he knows that his hope is in Christ Jesus and that he will be with him forever. That is a public witness that we have not seen very much.\par \par \uc2 And public dying is a witness to so many people that we need to see. We have so many people who want to make death this like easy thing or we want to -- you know, we see people passing bills to assist in dying, which means killing people as they go through a death process. So, this type of public witness, I think might be his best work of his entire life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And he says he's going to keep fighting, but he knows what he's facing.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: I also think it just gives so many people who are also suffering with cancer, who have family members suffering a lot of courage and grace. And he was so authentic and genuine. It was a beautiful -- you had tears in your eyes. You couldn't -- I don't even know him and you have tears in your eyes.\par \par \uc2 And it's just, you know, you wish the best for any sort of longevity that he can get from any treatment, but it's just so grateful and peaceful the - - the way he said.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And has an eternal hope. Thank you both. It's great to see both of you over the holiday and Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Back in October, I had a chance to sit down with Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 . And we talked about our new book, "Listening to the Law."\par \par \uc2 But I also asked her about how faith affects her life and her work.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: To me, my faith is fundamental to my life and it informs the decisions that I make, how I treat people, how I think about my marriage, my responsibilities to my kids. It actually helps me.\par \par \uc2 You were asking me before about how do I tune things out and not let them get to me because my -- my -- my faith really is a big part of my ability because I don't regard my job or what other people think of me as the most important fundamental thing in my life.\par \par \uc2 It does not, however, affect the way I decide cases. I mean, it -- it affects the way that I regard my job. You know, I -- I took an oath to uphold the Constitution and I took that oath on the Bible.\par \par \uc2 But it does not inform the way that I decide cases because -- and I -- I talk about this in the book, you know, we have freedom of religion under the First Amendment because America is a pluralistic country. People have many different faiths and no faith at all.\par \par \uc2 And so, I should not be imposing my faith or my view of the world through the Constitution any more than someone with a different creed or no creed at all should be imposing theirs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, straight ahead, more of my one-on-one conversation with Turning Point CEO Erika Kirk on her vision for promoting faith and freedom in future generations.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Gen Z is increasingly turning to religion to cope with the struggles of life. Our deep dive into what's happening on campuses across the country, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: For years, more seasoned believers have looked to revive faith in America by drawing young people back to the church. But now, there are new signs. Gen Z, they actually be the ones leading the way.\par \par \uc2 Seeing what's happening in this generation, watching the hunger and, you know, the movement that's taking place across our nation is so special.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): It's not exactly where you'd expect a group of college kids to be on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 No bars, no beer pong, just Bibles. But the site of thousands of students at a recent campus revival event comes as no surprise to Tonya Prewett.\par \par \uc2 TONYA PREWETT, FOUNDER, UNITEUS: I think they've searched everywhere for truth and hope and the only hope they're really going to find us in Jesus Christ.\par \par \uc2 There's an army of young people rising up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Prewett is the founder of UniteUs, an organization aimed at outreach on college campuses.\par \par \uc2 PREWETT: When I was mentoring students at \uc2 Auburn University\uc2  and I would hear stories, one girl after another saying, you know, I -- I don't want to live or the anxiety is so much I can't cope and telling stories of the different addictions and the pain. And -- and after hearing so many stories, I was like, we have to do something about this.\par \par \uc2 We baptize you in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: According to a recent study by Christian group Barna, Gen Z attends church more often than their parents, and even their grandparents.\par \par \uc2 But it's also safe to say that these are not your grandmother's church groups.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would just love to hit play and listen to somebody guide me through a prayerful meditation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The renewed interest in faith sparked in no small way by technology, a flurry of Christian influencers, podcasts, and even apps backed by some of the biggest stars in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 MARK WAHLBERG, AMERICAN ACTOR: Hey, Father, you joining Lent again this year?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Of course. Stay prayed up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Earlier this year, the Catholic Church even canonized the first millennial saint, Carlo Acutis, affectionately dubbed, the Patron Saint of the Internet.\par \par \uc2 FATHER GREGORY PINE, PRIEST AND AUTHOR: He was very devoted to worship of our Lord in the sacraments, but he was also very much online. And he saw that space as a place in which to evangelize.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Father Gregory Pine himself, a successful YouTuber --\par \par \uc2 PINE: I thought I'd set out some principles for crafting your spiritual life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- says Gen Z's growing interest in faith is also a reflection of shifting values.\par \par \uc2 PINE: We've witnessed huge growth in the number of adult converts, and especially from a younger age bracket. And a lot of that is that, you know, like in the post-Covid dispensation folks are looking for more meaningful purposeful enterprises. So, money hasn't really held up and honor, glory, fame hasn't really held up and pleasure hasn't really held up. And so they're left with a kind of experience of emptiness, but with an ongoing conviction that there is meaning and purpose to be found.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That search for purpose, of course, isn't just limited to young Christians. Rabbi Yudi Steiner, who runs the Rohr Chabad Center in \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  here in D.C., says more and more Jewish students have sought his guidance as they grapple with an increasingly divisive and sometimes violent world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is a time where to reach out and want to reengage or deepen your Jewish faith. I mean, for people -- for young people, there are real risks that many of them feel with what's going on in the world.\par \par \uc2 RABBI YUDI STEINER, DIRECTOR, ROHR CHABAD CENTER AT GWU: Look, so a good place to start is right after October 7th, the biggest massacre of the Jewish people since the Holocaust. And you would expect Jewish students to be fearful and perhaps stay away, especially with the way their peers on campus and professors sometimes and even university administrators responded to it. There's every reason for them to say, let's take a step back. The very opposite happened. People need identity and purpose. And their faith gives that to them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And how has that changed over time? Because, gosh, the technology that exists today, it creates different pressures and different pull and different destruction on young people. How have you seen that changed over these 18 years you've been ministering with students?\par \par \uc2 STEINER: You know, the standard assumption is that religion is becoming less and less relevant as modernity develops. I have found the opposite. I have found, as technology develops, there is a deeper need for more meaningful and valuable and authentic experiences.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And now to part two of our interview with Turning Point's CEO Erika Kirk. We began by talking about her late husband's final book, "Stop in the Name of God," which he called a, quote, "manifesto against the machine of the modern life."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you and Charlie did things -- I mean he talks about in the new book about being countercultural about a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK, CEO, TURNING POINT USA: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I love one of the things that he said about Shabbat envy, that he loved looking around at people who were practicing the Sabbath and saying, man, I'd love to be able to get off this hamster wheel and do that.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And he outlines that in the book about how it's not just physically resting but it's really a soul rest.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: It is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Spending time with the Lord. Regenerating there. And he really challenges people in the book.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: He does.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I love your forward, where you talk about the tyranny of distraction.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Because we all deal with that.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: We do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I think that noise only just gets louder and louder. The more we have technology, the more we have people in our ear.\par \par \uc2 So, a radical step for him to say, like, no, as busy as I am, I'm going to shut this down for 24 hours and refocus.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: And you can make it different every time. But the one thing I looked forward to the most on those days where Charlie honored the Sabbath was the note that he would write to me. And that was powerful. And he would always say, how can I serve you? How can I be a better husband? Because I was saying the same thing. It wasn't just one way. We both knew that in order to make our marriage the most amazing covenant that it was, was always asking one another, how can we improve? How can we be better? How can we serve one another? Because your marriage came first, then your kids. So, when you put the kids in front of your marriage, that's where some issues start. But Charlie and I always put each other first. I mean, obviously, you put God first. You understand my sentiment. But he was very good about, in those letters, at the end he would always say, how can I serve you? How can I be a better husband? And it's funny because I never actually took him up on that. I never said -- because he was just always so good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you say to people who don't get faith in marriage or religion in marriage or that the biblical guide where I find people will have misperceptions about submission and about a wife and what women are expected to do if they don't fully understand the scriptures, Ephesians five, and all of those things that give guidance to the husband and the wife.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Ephesians five is beautiful. Submission's not a dirty word. It's a reflection of the bride of Christ. I was -- I was blessed to be able to submit to Charlie. And it wasn't a weakness. I was -- I'm the guardian of the home. He's the head of the household. He provides the house. I make it into a home. We complement each other. We're not in competition with one another.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, what is the end goal for TP USA?\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Oh, my goodness. Well, aside from continuing the legacy and making sure that this machine that my husband built out lives all of us, it's making sure we're going to be back on campus. We're going to continue "prove me wrongs" on the quad. We're not afraid. We're going to be doing campus events at night. We have all of our tour stops with TP USA Faith, with our Make Heaven Crowded Tour that's coming up. We have, you know, all of our amazing chapters. The fact that we're having so many different governors partner with us for Club America is so humbling and powerful. But it's also showing the youth of America that your voice does matter and that you do matter because so long gen z has been looked at as dismissed or, you know, the confuse generation. And they're not. They're not. And the fact that we're having leadership pay attention and say, you know what, I get asked by my donors all the time, what are you doing for the youth? And my solution is Club America. That is humbling to hear from our leaders. And it's powerful to witness.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it's powerful to witness you walking forward, moving forward with the covering of millions of peoples and their prayers over you as well.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: God bless you (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, thank you. It's been good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: Yes, it's been (INAUDIBLE). Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you can watch our entire interview with Erika Kirk. There's much more there. We dive deep on faith, a lot of other topics too. It's all on foxnation.com, available for streaming after the show.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, many of us have been watching and rewatching our favorite Christmas movies this past week. One film is bringing a new element to the genre with a real deep focus on faith. I traveled to Tennessee to sit down with best-selling author and producer Karen Kingsbury, who hopes she can inspire unity with her newest film's message.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAREN KINGSBURY, AUTHOR, "THE CHRISTMAS RING": I think what happens in a theater, that cannot happen anywhere else, is you come in strangers, you might come in sad, you might come in distant, but you leave a community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lights, camera, faith. One new film is celebrating Christmas by returning the spotlight to the reason for the season and getting some a- list talent to do it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAREN KINGSBURY, AUTHOR, "THE CHRISTMAS RING": I've always thought a story, a full complete story is physical and intellectual and emotional, but it is also spiritual.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Feel-good romantic comedies have become a staple of the holiday season. Lovable characters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're a trio.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Relatable on-screen chemistry. And of course, a festive small-town backdrop.\par \par \uc2 But Karen Kingsbury's latest best-selling novel turned film, "The Christmas Ring," brings an added element often absent from modern holiday movies, faith.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY GRAMMER, ACTOR: Under us a Savior is born. That's the whole point of Christmas, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It was a challenge, and a gamble. I feel like anything that's sort of faith adjacent or has a faith element to it.\par \par \uc2 KINGSBURY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When I was a kid, it was mostly cheesy.\par \par \uc2 KINGSBURY: Yes. Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it's not anymore. I mean the production value is on par with any movie that you're going to see released into the theaters. And so, you're providing something that for a lot of people didn't exist before.\par \par \uc2 KINGSBURY: That is the goal. And I actually am comfortable -- I'm comfortable including it and it should feel that organic. So -- and I'm in awe of the fact that I get to do this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That goal of a central Christian message also inspired several marquee names to join the production, including iconic "Fraser" and "Cheers" star Kelsey Grammer.\par \par \uc2 GRAMMER: Sacrifice, love, all the things that we believe in, redemption and forgiveness. I mean these are all elements of it and it's finding new love and broken hearts. And that's what Jesus did, I guess, really, was to bring love back to mankind, to heal the brokenhearted.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): The film, also a love letter to the U.S. military with more than 100 active duty or retired members as background actors tells the emotional story of a Gold Star widow who finds love again while searching for her family's missing heirloom ring.\par \par \uc2 KINGSBURY: It's a beautiful love story that kind of speaks to the idea that -- of the truth that God is not the reason for the hard things and the trials and the tragedies that happen, but he is the rescue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): We caught up with Kingsbury at her native Franklin, Tennessee, where much of the film's production took place.\par \par \uc2 This area around Franklin, Tennessee, outside of Nashville, so many places like where we're sitting now, this bookstores is in the movie. How special to shoot here where you live?\par \par \uc2 KINGSBURY: Well, everything about that was just like a dream. The locations, that really what made it. Like, if you look around this bookstore, the Landmark Bookstore, you cannot imagine ever trying to recreate this in some sort of open space. Winchester's Antique Mall is where Kelsey Grammer's store was. And, I mean, it's millions of dollars in things in that store. And I said, Kim, what can I do, the owner? Can we please shoot here? We love your store. And she said, you know what, she's a Christian. She loves the Lord. She said, this is not mine. I don't have ownership over it. We welcome you. And did not charge me a penny.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Ultimately, Kingsbury hopes the faith-based film will spur much-needed holiday unity amid a difficult moment for the nation.\par \par \uc2 KINGSBURY: I think, you know, we've been through a hard year. I think it's been, you know, this -- obviously the tragedy, I think, of, you know, losing Charlie Kirk was a -- sort of a situation that brought people together. So, my hope for "The Christmas Ring" is that it will bring people together this Christmas and help us to really celebrate, not only the joy of being able to laugh and cry and honor the military together in one space, but also leave -- and you're going to smile at someone as you leave. You're going to share this experience, which is something only the theaters can do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And "The Christmas Ring" is out on streaming now if you want to keep that holiday spirit going through the new year, check it out.\par \par \uc2 And there's a lot more fantastic content from Fox Faith on Fox Nation. "Jesus Crown of Thorns" tells the story of Christ like never before.\par \par \uc2 Here's a clip of Jesus and Peter when Christ's cherished (ph) disciples is not yet fully understanding the divine mission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Never, Lord. This shall never happen to you. You are the Messiah. You can't suffer and die. We are going to triumph. And you're going to drive the Romans back to where they came from.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get behind me, Satan. You are a stumbling block to me. You do not have in mind concerns of God, but merely human concerns.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you can want the entire first two seasons of "Jesus Crown of Thorns" streaming right now on Fox Nation, where you can also watch the first two seasons of Martin Scorsese's series "The Saints," which highlights history's greatest martyrs and faith leaders from ancient times, all the way up to the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 That's it for "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" for this year. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. We'll see you --\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020251228elcs00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 : Justice Department Says More Epstein Files Coming; Congress Out For The Holidays With No Deal On \uc2 ACA\uc2  Subsidies; Interview With President Trump's Economic Director, \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 ; U.S. Conducts Retaliatory Strikes on \uc2 ISIS\uc2  Targets in Syria; U.S. Aims to Negotiate End to Russia-Ukraine War; Secretary of State Defends Venezuela Policy; Problem Solvers Caucus Co- Chairs On Looming ACA Subsidy Deadline; Trump Floats Direct payments To Americans For Healthcare, Amid ACA Subsidy Debate; Democrats Will Not Release 2024 Election Report; Erika Kirk's Conversation with \uc2 Candace Owens\uc2 ; Dead Sea Scrolls Exhibit\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Trey Yingst, Juan Williams, Marc Thiessen, Lucas  Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,427 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 President Trump aims to shore up his economic message as a growing number of Americans say they're falling behind.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You will see in your wallets and bank accounts in the new year. Our policies are boosting take-home pay at a historic pace.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Affordability takes center stage as a string of polls spell trouble for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats look to seize the moment.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Democrats in 2026, you'll be hearing from us about costs over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The president's economic director, \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , who's on the short list to lead the Fed, joins us live.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): For more than a decade, they have been hellbent on repealing the Affordable Care Act without putting forward an alternative.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Democrats do not want a solution. They want an issue in the upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: House Republicans narrowly pass their healthcare policy bill, but still no solutions on Obamacare subsidies as four Republicans break ranks and try to force a vote.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk to one of them, Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2 , along with his Problem Solvers Caucus Democratic co-chair, Tom Souzzi.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We have a regime that's illegitimate, that cooperates with Iran, that cooperates with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , that cooperates with narco trafficking. That is the threat to the national interest of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: U.S.-Venezuela tensions higher than ever after the president orders a blockade of all sanctioned oil tankers, raising new fears of armed conflict as Maduro deploys the country's navy.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would say the density scrolls are the greatest archaeological discovery ever.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A rare look at one of the most ancient artifacts in religious history as we mark this season of faith, hope, and renewal.\par \par \uc2 All right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington on this first day of winter and last day of Hanukkah.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at the headlines.\par \par \uc2 U.S. forces have seized a second oil tanker off the coast of Venezuela as part of the Operation Southern Spear effort. On social media, DHS Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  says the U.S. will, quote, "continue to pursue the illicit movement of sanctioned oil that is used to fund narco terrorism in the region."\par \par \uc2 Parts of San Francisco hit by a major power outage Saturday, knocking out electricity for more than 130,000 homes and businesses, snarling traffic and shutting down mass transit. \uc2 Pacific Gas and Electric\uc2  says a fire in a substation may have started the problem. They have been working through the night to try to restore power.\par \par \uc2 And in Sydney, thousands of people turned out for a memorial for those lost in the terror attack, targeting the Jewish community during a Hanukkah celebration on Bondi Beach a week ago. Fifteen people were killed, along with one of the attackers, and several more injured.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department says it will continue to release more of the Epstein files on a rolling basis, blaming any delays on releasing all of the files on the time-consuming process of obscuring victims' names and other identifying information.\par \par \uc2 For reaction to that much more, FOX News correspondent Lucas Tomlinson is in Florida covering the president.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, at least 16 files have disappeared from the Justice Department's website related to \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 , including a photo of President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  one day after they were posted. It's photos of a former president that's getting a lot of attention right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Newly released photos from the Justice Department show \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in a hot tub with another occupant whose face is redacted. It's not clear when or where this event took place. Other photographs show the former president with Michael Jackson and Diana Ross on a plane. Another shows this unidentified woman sitting on his lap.\par \par \uc2 Clinton has denied any wrongdoing through a spokesman. "They can release as many grainy 20-plus-year-old photos as they want. But this isn't about \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Never has, never will be."\par \par \uc2 Before the photos were released, White House Chief of Staff Susie Wiles told "Vanity Fair" she did not think there was anything incriminating about \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in the Epstein files, contrary to past statements made by President Trump. Wiles said, "The president was wrong about that".\par \par \uc2 Democrats accused the White House of stalling.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): So, he's done everything but deliver on transparency. He's dragged his feet at every juncture this year as president.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Some think Congress has been dragging its feet when it comes to healthcare reform. Lawmakers departed for the holidays without a deal.\par \par \uc2 With Obamacare subsidies set to expire at year's end, four House Republicans sided with Democrats, signing off on a discharge petition to force a vote on extending Obamacare subsidies when Congress returns next year.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD), SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: It'll be a straight three- year extension of an unsustainable, unaffordable program.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Trump plans to meet with the major insurance companies in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 In a rally in Rocky Mount, North Carolina, he also addressed affordability.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The Democrats, they're the ones that got us into the mess. They're the ones that caused the high prices. This election's about affordability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): At 2.7 percent, inflation is lower than last year. But for many Americans, the cost of living still feels too high.\par \par \uc2 President Trump will be celebrating Christmas at Mar-a-Lago and is expected to remain here for the next two weeks -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson in West Palm Beach -- merry Christmas if I don't see you before then. Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining us now from outside the White House, the president's economic director, \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  -- by the way, on the short list to lead the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . We'll get to that.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, welcome back to the show. And --\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL DIRECTOR: Great to be here, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to pick up where Lucas left off.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, he talked about this issue of people -- we're around the holidays. It's a lot of spending. They're worried about how they're going to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 I want to put up one of our polls from this week, FOX News poll. Personal financial situation. So, this is how people feel about how they're doing.\par \par \uc2 Getting behind, holding steady, falling behind at 44 percent, and that number has actually been growing.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you tell folks about when they are going to start to feel some kind of relief in their pockets?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right. Well -- well, already what we've seen is after four years under the Biden administration where real incomes went down by $3,000 for the typical family, they've gone up by $1,300 this year.\par \par \uc2 Now, it doesn't mean that, you know, Biden dig -- he really dug a big hole. Doesn't mean that we've like fixed all the problems that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  created, but we're making great progress.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I think I really liked most about the president's speeches this week, but I'm biased, I'm an economist, is he put up a lot of charts to show people like why they should truly believe that we're going to make lots of progress on affordability. So, for example, he put up a chart that showed all the electric power generation plants that we've approved that'll be in place over the next year or two. When he had a big map of the United States and showed like the -- I think it was 1,600 new power plants, which is like that's how you get electricity prices down, right?\par \par \uc2 So, you don't just talk about it, but you deliver. And in the end, what's going to happen is people are going to see lower prices. They're going to see higher real incomes just like they did last time. And that's when the - - you know, these numbers are going to turn around.\par \par \uc2 But right now, there's still like a lot of recovery from the harm that Biden caused --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: -- that we have to see in the surveys.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. I mean, I can remember having many conversations with one of your predecessors, Jared Bernstein, sitting right here --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- talking about this idea that it wasn't showing up in the polls, what they were trying to tell people fundamentally that things were turning around. And that people, you know, were essentially getting the message from the Biden administration, what you're feeling is not legit.\par \par \uc2 Do you worry that that's the message that some people are perceiving right now?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: No. Let's just remember, though that -- that my friend Jared was out here sort of saying, "Well, sure, we gave you a 10 percent inflation, but now it's 5 percent inflation, so things are getting better," but it's still 5 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're looking -- running at a three-month average of 1.6 percent core inflation, which is below the Fed's target.\par \par \uc2 But he also was sort of saying, well, yeah, sure, your incomes are down $3,000, but you should -- you shouldn't be whining about that. What we're saying is that incomes are up again. Incomes grew $6,500 out of President Trump, and they're growing almost exactly at the same pace right now that they grew when President Trump was here before.\par \par \uc2 And so -- so, they've got empty rhetoric. We've got rhetoric with substance. You know, they've got empty rhetoric. We've got charts.\par \par \uc2 I think that the American people are going to see it in their wallets. They're going to see it in their bills. And they're going to, as they did in the Trump first term, they're going to see that President Trump's policies are making them better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, speaking of charts and data, we put up another poll from FOX News this week, on what should President Trump be paying more attention to now? The first three -- here they are -- high prices, healthcare, jobs, and employment. So, clearly, that is the focus for most people.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They feel like he's had, in many cases, a real attention to international affairs. He's done excellent work with resolving conflicts. There's still more to be done.\par \par \uc2 But we see the president pivoting to this messaging now. You mentioned his speech in North Carolina the other night. He was in Pennsylvania. He's -- the speech to the nation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he's talking about the things that he knows people -- it shows up in polls and elsewhere anecdotally -- they want to hear about.\par \par \uc2 So what policy things are going to change, or is the message, "We've made the changes, they just need time to kick in"?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Well, first of all, there's so much good news in the New Year because you're going to see the biggest tax refund season of all time. The Big, Beautiful Bill was passed in July. You know, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  didn't have time to change all the forms, and so now everybody's going to get huge tax refunds if they were overtime workers, if they're seniors, if they -- you know, had tip income. All those things are going to be refunded -- those taxes this year when they file for their taxes.\par \par \uc2 And then the other thing is that we've got lots of great new ideas that we're going to put before the American people this year to make their lives better. The president mentioned in his economic speech from over here at the White House that we've got an -- like everybody in the whole cabinet working on trying to get housing to be more affordable. We're going to have a plan, a big plan to announce uh sometime soon in the New Year. That's going to be really good news for the American people who feel like it's just not affordable to buy a home anymore.\par \par \uc2 Don't forget that under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the average mortgage payment for the typical middle class family who are buying a first time -- for the first time a home went up by $14,000 a year. Fourteen thousand dollars a year because of inflation and higher interest rates. And we've got a plan that that's going to make that go almost completely in reverse.\par \par \uc2 Already, we've reduced that monthly mortgage payment by 3,000.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let me ask you about that because one of the ideas that keeps getting batted around and mentioned here in Washington and beyond is this idea of a portable mortgage. That a lot of people, if you got a 3 percent mortgage, you don't want to sell that house and go back out into the market, freeing up that housing maybe for more an entry level buyer.\par \par \uc2 Could that be part of the conversation, that portable mortgage?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yeah, we've got a list of things that we're going to present to the president. I expect -- break a little news for you. I expect that most of us are going to be down for a big chunk of the week after Christmas at Mar-a-Lago going through all the plans for next year. And definitely, we have a big list of housing ideas that have been vetted very carefully by the cabinet secretaries to present to the president in a week or two, and we'll see which ones he picks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Well, listen, if you got to have a meeting about wonky stuff, Florida is a good place in December to do it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about this issue of tariffs, too, because there's been a lot of back and forth about whether or not they're impacting consumers and how much.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street Journal" is saying this: "Businesses have been passing along some of the cost of Trump's tariffs to customers. Core commodities, excluding food and energy, were up 1.4 percent over the year, reflecting inflation from tariffs."\par \par \uc2 So, a couple of things there. First of all, do you acknowledge there is some cost to the consumer with these tariffs?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: No, I don't. I mean, think about it. Inflation was 3.7 percent when we took office. Now it's 1.4 percent. And so, it's dropped by, you know, and according to that that chart, our -- my graphic says 1.6 for core, which is like the aggregate number, but inflation has dropped precipitously under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not sure that there's a story to explain with tariffs.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is that we always believed that the reason why we have big trade deficits is that we've got foreign producers who are desperate to sell their stuff into the U.S. If we put a tariff on it, we think they're going to cut their prices and that there's not going to be much of an effect on the final cost to consumers here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And that can only be true, or it has to be true, because if you look at the 1.4 and the 1.6, those two numbers, we've seen that inflation hasn't picked up because of the tariffs. But what has picked up is revenue.\par \par \uc2 We're running surpluses the last couple of months. The deficit relative to last year is down by $600 billion or $700 billion just in one year alone. And a big part of that is the tariff.\par \par \uc2 And so, we've got four percent growth, one-point-something percent inflation, and that's the exact opposite of what the naysayers about tariffs told you would happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: And so, President Trump's pretty happy about the numbers, I can tell you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. And I know that we're all awaiting the Supreme Court decision on the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Obviously, that's going to have a major impact on the policy, whether it continues, whether you have to refund the billions that have come in. We'll cross that bridge when we get the decision from them.\par \par \uc2 But quickly, before we go, you've been on the short list for the Fed. You've said that the Fed chair and the Fed has to be independent.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's not to be some place that the president can influence. Do you think that's what the president wants, though, in a Fed chair?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, absolutely. The president feels that the Fed has gotten pretty partisan. We could go through the -- all the, you know, partisan moves that the Fed has made in the president's mind.\par \par \uc2 They're pretty clearly partisan over the last few years. You know, indeed having a big rate cut right before the election to try to help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , raising rates the minute that he was elected the last time, even before he was inaugurated and so on.\par \par \uc2 And so, he wants someone there who goes back to running the Fed the way it's supposed to be run. Look at the data and make sound independent judgments.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the data right now, we've got very low inflation, below target. We've got a supply shock, which means that you can have lots of growth without runaway inflation. And so, the president's right to say that the Fed is slow. They should have been cutting rates sooner.\par \par \uc2 And again, my old friend \uc2 Austan Goolsbee\uc2  just conceded that looking at the latest inflation numbers, he made a mistake when he voted against the rate cut last time, and that he's going to vote for more rate cuts in the future.\par \par \uc2 And so -- so, I think the Fed needs to be data dependent, independent and not partisan. And that's what the president's looking for in the next candidate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we'll look for that naming of that next individual and read out from the meetings next week in Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 Director Hasset, thank you very much. Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: It's good to see you, Shannon. Same to you. Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Coming up, the U.S. carrying out retaliatory strikes on dozens of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  targets in Syria. Trey Yingst is going to join us live from Israel, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  praising the U.S. strikes on \uc2 ISIS\uc2  targets in Syria Friday as a, quote, "declaration of vengeance," calling it "Operation Hawkeye Strike."\par \par \uc2 Fox News Chief Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst joins us from Tel Aviv with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning. We're tracking a number of foreign policy developments from Eastern Europe to the Middle East. Friday evening, President Trump ordered dozens of airstrikes against \uc2 ISIS\uc2  targets in Syria. The bombing campaign was in direct response to an \uc2 ISIS\uc2  shooting attack that left two Iowa National Guardsmen and their interpreter dead last weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENT: I ordered a massive strike on the terrorists that killed our three great patriots last week. Two soldiers, one interpreter, all great people. And it was very successful. It was precision. We hit every site flawlessly, and we are restoring peace through strength all over the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Was supportive of the American strikes, which also saw participation from the Jordanian Air Force, as reports indicate that the United States rejected an Israeli request to keep some sanctions on Syria. New information today also says that Israel's Prime Minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , plans to brief President Trump later this month on possible strikes against Iran's expanding ballistic missile program. With a focus on the Middle East, President Trump is also working to end the war between Russia and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 U.S. negotiators met Saturday with Russian officials in Miami, trying to iron out a potential peace framework.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, SECRETARY OF STATE: A negotiated settlement requires two things. Both sides to get something out of it, and both sides to give something. And we're trying to figure out what can Russia give and what do they expect to get? What can Ukraine give and what can Ukraine expect to get?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: U.S. officials speaking with "FOX News" remain confident in the Trump administration's ability to reach a ceasefire between Russia and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Trey Yingst, live in Tel Aviv. Thank you, and Merry Christmas, Trey.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group. "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " Politics Reporter, Stef Kight. Michael Duncan, host of "The Ruthless Podcast." Fox News Senior Political Analyst, Juan Williams. And Marc Thiessen, Fox News Contributor.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot there on the foreign policy plate to dive into. Let's talk about these strikes, though, on Syria in -- in, you know, vengeance, as our Secretary of War said, with respect to \uc2 ISIS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The president, when he was posting about this Friday night, said, "striking against a very strong \uc2 ISIS\uc2  stronghold." He says, "This country has a bright future. It's being led by a man who's working very hard to bring greatness back to Syria and is fully in support."\par \par \uc2 But Michael, that individual has a lot of interesting background, and where we go from here and moving that country forward is a big question.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DUNCAN, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: Yeah, it's sort of easy to forget, right, with all the other stuff going on in the world, Ukraine and Russia and Venezuela. The Middle East is still a very dangerous place, and \uc2 ISIS\uc2  still has a foothold in a lot of these areas and places like Syria, where there's young governments that are trying to respond in -- with a broad international coalition to sort of take out these forces.\par \par \uc2 But this was -- this has been going on for literally years, right? I mean, this is an older conflict than Russia and Ukraine, obviously older conflict than -- than what's happening in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, well, let's talk about Venezuela too, because we now have a second oil tanker that's been taken by us. There was a blockade there, and I thought it was interesting. This is one of the few things in D.C., it feels like, on foreign policy at least, where there is some bipartisan support. Here is Senator Dick Blumenthal. He is a Democrat on those blockades.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): There's ample justification to seize tankers that are under sanction. And in fact, I would urge that we seize all of the shadow fleet tankers that are transporting Russian oil, clearly fueling Russia's war machine against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Juan, we'd like to point out bipartisanship when it does happen here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Christmas season. You know, the thing is, I think President Trump and Secretary of State Rubio are not really interested in drug boats. They're interested in that oil. I think that's the critical point, and they're interested in regime change to get Maduro out.\par \par \uc2 The question is why. I don't think they've articulated to us, the American people, exactly what is the rationale. And the downside would be that if they are successful at getting Maduro out, and Maduro has been a malign influence in not only his own country, but in the region. If they're successful in getting him out, what does it mean for us going forward? Are we then on the hook? You know, the old Colin Powell thing, if you break it, it's yours, you know.\par \par \uc2 So, why would we want another potential forever type of war? Trump won a lot of young voters by saying that the United States would pay attention to its national interest and not get distracted overseas.\par \par \uc2 And so, the question is, where are we going here? I under -- I don't think there's any question we can oust Maduro. We can stop the sanctioned ships, but what is the long-term effort about?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and so while there's agreement on the tankers, there's not agreement on some of this other, taking out these alleged narco boats, drug boats.\par \par \uc2 Our Fox News poll this week, what about U.S. military force being used, deadly force, on drug-trafficking boats from Venezuela? Majority of people oppose it, Marc. So, what is this about?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's pretty close, but you know what? 90 percent of MAGA supporters, voters, are going to support it --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's the number the White House is looking at.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: -- according to the -- according to the Reagan -- Reagan Institute poll. So, this is very popular with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's base.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is the -- interesting thing is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not an isolationist, and the MAGA movement has never been isolationist. If you think about his first term, bombed Syria, bombed the Houthis, bombed Iran, now bombing Venezuelan gunboats, threatening to go in guns-a-blazing into Nigeria for persecution of Christians.\par \par \uc2 He's never been an isolationist. And all the polls, if you look, every year I go into that Reagan Institute poll and look at the MAGA numbers and pull them out, everything he's doing is incredibly popular with the MAGA base.\par \par \uc2 And as to Juan's question about -- about Maduro, we -- we invaded Panama and ousted Noriega and didn't own Panama. You don't have to own the country if you oust the leader. And the problem, they are concerned about drugs, because the drugs are flowing into this country, and you can't do that by playing whack-a-mole with drug boats. It's the regime. They're -- it's a socialist regime, but they're run by narco-capitalists who are all deeply involved in the drug trade. And so unless you knock the head off of that regime, you don't stop the drug flow into the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and while they're dealing with that, this weekend there is another push, yet another push to work with Russian and Ukrainian delegations to try to get some movement on what feels intractable, I think, to the White House. And Zelenskyy telling reporters back in Kyiv, just saying this format, he says now, he thinks Ukraine, America and Russia are all going to be at the same table. He doesn't know if it's going to work.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, AXIOS POLITICAL REPORTER: Yeah, so that was another idea that was floated over the weekend, something that the U.S. wants to make happen again. They want to bring all parties to the table and try to come up with some kind of a breakthrough here. But it seems that -- that, you know, the arguments are pretty stuck right now. It comes down to just how much territory Russia is going to insist that Ukraine give back to Russia that they've already had. And of course, Ukraine is not going to be willing to give up much of, if anything, at all.\par \par \uc2 These talks are continuing. This was a big weekend. We know that these negotiations have been very serious. It's something that the president and his team wants to get done, but it really does feel like we're still in kind of a stuck position right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, Secretary Rubio doing his end of year press conference, he says, maybe it's not possible right now that this is going to come together, but the effort is not going to stop.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, do not go far. We've got much more to discuss. Let's take a live look at Capitol Hill, because guess what? Who's not there? Lawmakers. They've gone home for the holidays without a fix for your expiring Obamacare subsidy.\par \par \uc2 So, what are they going to do? Well, we've got members from both sides of the aisle, Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  and \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 . They're going to be here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Why is Mike Johnson and House Republicans leaving town when there is legislation supported by a bipartisan majority of the House that would extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits for three years.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CYNTHIA LUMMIS (R-WY): We are putting a Band-Aid on a gunshot wound. The problem here is Obamacare is not affordable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So millions of Americans now bracing for higher Obamacare premiums in the new year after lawmakers left town without passing any short or long term fixes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now for a bipartisan discussion on efforts to find a compromise, co-chairs of the House Problem Solvers Caucus, Congressman \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2  and Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2 . He's actually one of the Republicans who broke with GOP leadership and signed on to a Democrat effort to extend the subsidies.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM SUOZZI (D-NY): Hey, good to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Congressman Fitzpatrick, I'll start with you. You signed -- you crossed over on this discharge petition. Four Republicans, it means it's going to force it to the floor.\par \par \uc2 It's a clean extension of the Obamacare subsidies. But do you agree with your Senate colleague who said there, \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2 , that it is like putting a Band-Aid on a gunshot wound to do that?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes, Shannon. Well, we had a far more conservative alternative that Tom and I introduced, along with Jared Golden and Mike Lawler and scores of both Democrats and Republicans that quite frankly mirrored the White House's proposal that came out.\par \par \uc2 It got -- it gave more money to individuals through health savings accounts. It had very low income caps to make sure that we were targeting middle to lower income earners.\par \par \uc2 It addressed, truly addressed the PBMs that are driving up the cost of pharmaceuticals. But that was not given a floor vote for whatever reasons. So, we were left with no other option but to send this vehicle, which is not going to be the final vehicle, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 This bill has already failed in the Senate. We know that. But since this is a tax measure, technically, it must emanate and originate out of the House. So, we just needed to get them any vehicle. The Senate will then put their stamp on it.\par \par \uc2 I promise you, this is going to end up being exactly the proposal that mirrors the bill that Tom and myself and Mike Lawler and Jared Golden introduced. So, we could have short-circuited all of this by just putting that bill on the floor.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, as we pointed out earlier, we like -- when there's something bipartisan that could be beneficial, we like to -- to highlight that it's actually happening in Washington because people think that it's not happening here.\par \par \uc2 So, I want to get to you, Congressman Suozzi. "Wall Street Journal" says this about this immediate situation with the expiring subsidies. They say it will affect roughly 1.6 million current enrollees.\par \par \uc2 "These are people with incomes above 400 percent of the federal poverty level who have been receiving subsidies. They say a family of four in Arizona making $600,000, a married couple in West Virginia making $580,000."\par \par \uc2 So, are you comfortable in extending those subsidies and telling your constituents who pay taxes that those are the families in situations they should be underwriting?\par \par \uc2 SUOZZI: You know, let me put this in real-life terms. A -- a person in my district making $130,000 a year with a family of four now pays eight and a half percent of their income for their Obamacare. That's about $10,000 or $11,000.\par \par \uc2 When these subsidies expire on January 1st, their insurance is going to go up from $10,000 or $11,000 to $30,000. So, they're not going to buy their insurance. And there can be less people insured. When there's less people in the risk pool, everybody's insurance goes up.\par \par \uc2 So, Brian really has shown a lot of courage here by trying to work across party lines. He didn't want to support this -- this particular deal. He doesn't -- we don't think that's going to become real law.\par \par \uc2 We -- the idea is to get a vehicle to the Senate so that we can work together to actually address it. He's doing what we're doing, what the people really want, working together across party lines to solve real-life problems.\par \par \uc2 Fix this short-term problem of the expiring tax credits and then deal with the long-term problem of actually addressing healthcare costs for everybody in America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. So, let's talk about the long-term issue, Congressman Fitzpatrick, what -- what is the goal there? Because when we ask people this week about their big economic concerns, inflation obviously was number one, but healthcare costs was right behind it.\par \par \uc2 So, subsidies aside, people know going to the doctor, going to the hospital, getting care, paying for drugs. I mean, one thing could wipe you out financially in this country. What do we do about the underlying problem?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes, Shannon. Over half of this country lives paycheck to paycheck. We have to always be mindful of that. Costs are going up in a number of areas, are coming down in some others, but we have to be mindful of people that are living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 And any minor increase in any one of these areas, whether it be childcare, food, fuel, transportation, housing, or in this case, healthcare, any marginal increase, we have to be very mindful, could make the difference between people being covered and not covered.\par \par \uc2 So, all we're saying is that let's take an all-of-the-above approach and have a responsible off-ramp.\par \par \uc2 Even for bad policies, Shannon, it never makes sense to have an abrupt ending and not expect or anticipate that there's going to be disruptions to the system.\par \par \uc2 So, have a responsible off-ramp, a bridge, so that we can get to the real cost drivers of healthcare, which are robust, by the way. It's PBMs, it's Association Health Plans, which we voted for on Friday.\par \par \uc2 It's -- it's -- it's raining in the out-of-control lawsuits that are forcing doctors to leave their profession.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of cost drivers to the system. This is one of them, though, but we need a responsible off-ramp here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and the president, it seems to be one of the ideas he's really getting behind is the idea of channeling money directly to consumers instead of insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to play something that he said --\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- Friday night, when he was out talking about the economy, talking about healthcare, and reforms that he hopes will happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm also taking on the gigantic health insurance companies that have gotten rich on billions of dollars of money that should go directly to the people. The money should go to the people. That's you.\par \par \uc2 So, they can buy their own health insurance, which will give far better benefits at much lower cost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Congressman Suozzi, what do you make of that? Would there be bipartisan support for that idea?\par \par \uc2 SUOZZI: I don't know about that particular idea, but the idea of taking on the insurance companies, I'm 100 percent for it, as is I know as Brian.\par \par \uc2 Take on the prescription drug companies, let's take them on. All these people that are gouging the system, making enormous profits, while the biggest issue in America right now today is affordability. People just can't make it.\par \par \uc2 So, affordability is number one, and affordability of healthcare is right at the top of that list. So, let's focus on going after the insurance companies. Let's focus on going after the prescription drug companies. Let's also work on preventative healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Let's go after -- we already went after smoking, we should do even more of that, but after obesity and lack of exercise and improper eating.\par \par \uc2 Let's go after the problems that don't get addressed for years and years and years to try and prevent people from getting very sick in the first place. Let's go after the bureaucracy that doctors have to follow with all this paperwork they've got to do on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 Let's try and make things more efficient and let's try and make people healthier.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and there's also this issue of fraud and -- and how that gets rooted out of the system because it costs everybody. I mean, "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board writing about this said, "Millions of people have underreported their incomes to qualify for bigger subsidies, and brokers are enrolling people in plans without their knowledge to earn bigger commissions."\par \par \uc2 Congressman Fitzpatrick, what do we do about that?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Yes. Well, that's -- they are right about that. And one way to address that is -- is including the bill that Tom and I introduced broadly bipartisan, and that is to deal with the so-called zero premium health insurance plans. Roughly 24 million people get them. Half of them, Shannon, never filed a claim, which tells us that half of them probably don't even know that they're insured. That's a huge problem.\par \par \uc2 So, one -- one of the many things that our bipartisan bill does, in addition to getting money to the individual, as the president said, we do have to do that. That's called health savings accounts, a big part of our proposal.\par \par \uc2 But also making sure that we -- we address the fraud that does exist in the system and it does exist. And that's why a clean extension should not be the final law. It will not be the final law. The Senate's already rejected it, but we need to get some vehicle, any vehicle to the Senate to allow the process to move forward so we can address at least this -- at least this part of the -- the healthcare equation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we know it's just one of the many big things you guys have to tackle. We're going to run out of money again. January 30th of appropriations bills are done.\par \par \uc2 I know the speaker, his birthday is that day. He does not want to shut down for his birthday. I'm sure you guys don't either.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you that you are working together. And we'll stay tuned to what you guys get accomplished. Thank you both. Merry Christmas. Happy holidays.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Merry Christmas, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SUOZZI: Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Merry Christmas, Tom.\par \par \uc2 SUOZZI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 SUOZZI: Bye, buddy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Coming up, Democrats decide to duck what could have been a scathing report card on campaign 2024, at least letting you see it publicly. But could that hurt them as they head into the midterms and beyond? That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Over the past 11 months, we have brought more positive change to Washington than any administration in the history of our country. That's true.\par \par \uc2 And there's never been anything like it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was President Trump Friday night in North Carolina, the second stop of his economy-focused tour this month.\par \par \uc2 We're back now with the panel. So, we know what his message is. He is feeling bullish on '26, although he means he doesn't know exactly what's going to happen in the midterms. None of us do.\par \par \uc2 But interestingly enough, across the aisle this week, we found out the DNC is not going to give us a look at what they think maybe could help them.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporting, "The DNC is killing its autopsy of the '24 election. Ken Martin, the chair, will keep the findings under seal. He believes that looking back so publicly and painfully at the past would prove counterproductive for the party as it tries to take back power in Congress."\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, surprise that they've decided we'll take our lessons, but we're not going to take them public.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I mean, there certainly has been a lot of surprise even among Democrats. And my colleagues spoke to several Democratic advisors who are, you know, working with other Democrats who are planning probable runs in 2028 who feel like this is an effort to maybe try to help former Vice President Harris if she decides to run again. They feel like it's very unhelpful and seems to indicate that the party is not taking seriously the lessons of last year, which, of course, have been discussed quite a bit over this year.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is why they're trying to kind of move on, move past this. But again, it kind of goes back to the problem that we saw with the party last year is this need to kind of cover up things.\par \par \uc2 That was a big concern with Democratic lawyers that they felt like they weren't being told the truth. So, I think this kind of cuts against any argument that would say the party has changed and is trying to take a different approach.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think, you know, Trump loves to look back. He loves to relay. Yes. And he spends this time. Even in that failed speech this week, he was attacking Biden. Biden -- Biden did this. It's Biden's this and Biden's that.\par \par \uc2 Americans aren't buying it. But I think the question is, should Biden have run? I don't know why we want to go back to that. Should we have had Democratic primaries and let all the candidates compete and then instead of just selecting Harris?\par \par \uc2 The question is for what? And -- and in 2025 this year, Democrats have had several wins. New Jersey, Virginia, I could go on.\par \par \uc2 And I think right now, the polls show that Democrats are in line to have tremendous victories in the upcoming midterm. So, why distract from that?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, but we also have this poll from Quinnipiac out this week. They say, "Heading into a year with the midterm elections, 18 percent of voters approve of the way that Democrats in Congress are handling their jobs."\par \par \uc2 They say that is a record low job approval rating for them.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. And 74 percent disapproves the lowest ever. Look, I'm -- I'm going to break some news. I got a bootleg copy of the -- of the autopsy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Did you?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Here's -- here's the findings. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-oh.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Don't unleash the worst foreign policy debacle in American history that -- that says people you're in -- your president's incompetent. Don't unleash the worst border crisis in American history. Don't unleash the worst for -- the -- the worst inflation in four decades. Don't cover up the president's cognitive senility until he takes the debate stage and everyone realizes he's been lying to them.\par \par \uc2 And don't replace him with the most left wing candidate in the history of the Democratic Party who couldn't complete a sentence on her own and without a single vote in a Democratic primary.\par \par \uc2 That -- that is my autopsy of the --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I feel like you could have written that. Maybe that's not the bootleg copy. (INAUDIBLE) Marc Thiessen, of your copy.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: I understand -- I understand one point --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: This is crazy.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: -- about the Democrats. They've had some wins --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: -- this year, like, no doubt. But how do you put a team on the field without a playbook? I just don't understand that.\par \par \uc2 I think to Stef's point, it speaks to a larger issue in the Democratic Party. And that is, you know, the -- the culture around Biden and -- and -- and not wanting to be self-critical in public.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans have a food fight on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  every single day before breakfast, right? And the Democrats refuse to be self-critical. And I think that's one of the big problems right now.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, wait a second. I can't believe this. Republicans never say a word about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he puts his own name on the Kennedy Center. I mean, as the guy destroys the East Wing as he attacks a dead man, Rob Reiner and Republicans, I don't want to talk about that. I don't want to mention it.\par \par \uc2 But it seems to me that you've got a president -- Americans want to hear about the economy, about affordability. And he's self-serving. And yet Republicans say, oh, no, you don't want to be self-critical. I never hear Republicans take responsibility for a president that's adrift.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I would say, Michael, I heard from a lot of Republicans on those issues this week. They are -- they have some opinions.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: I mean \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is like on a national TV circuit right now being critical of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Where do we see that in the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Just had \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  on the air right here.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's true.\par \par \uc2 OK, now, listen, let's go passed '26, let's look at '28, because this week Erika Kirk, who's now heading Turning Point USA, made news that she is going to fully back Vice President Vance in '28. She believes he needs to be the next president. And the weight of that organization going behind it.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with her in Phoenix yesterday at their big gathering to talk to her about a lot of things, but I asked her about the other big headline this week that she had had a lengthy meeting with \uc2 Candace Owens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here's what she told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you feel like you have common ground, you have some common ground with Candace? I mean you both want to honor Charlie's legacy, it seems. I mean you both say that you want to know exactly what happened. Do you feel like there was some common meeting of the mind there?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK, CEO, TURNING POINT USA: I'm not going to speak on her behalf. But what I do know is that my COO and myself, when we walked into that meeting, we were very intentional with why we were there, provided what we wanted to provide to show context and also to show we have nothing to hide, never will have anything to hide. And you just let the Lord do the rest. But I'm not going to sit and judge how anyone interpreted anything outside of myself. So, I pray for her. I pray for her family. And let the Lord handle the rest. We're building. I don't have time for the noise. I said my peace. And that's it. And we get back to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Marc, what do you make of this, these really prominent voices on the right? A lot of big figures all over the place on this.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: So, first of all, she's so incredibly gracious. And \uc2 Candace Owens\uc2  actually says she didn't drink the water in the meeting because she was afraid that some -- that it might be poisoned or something like that. I mean it -- we -- we have a -- one of the things that we have, if you look at the left, the left has a real big anti-Semitism problem. And it's in the mainstream. We have members of Congress, the academy, elsewhere. The right -- the anti-Semitic right and the -- it has been on the fringes. And they're trying to get into the tent. And we have -- I think \uc2 Ben Shapiro\uc2  said it best this week, you know, we need -- nations need borders and movements need borders. And there are certain borders that you don't cross in letting anti-Semites into the -- into this movement as -- and conspiracy theorists into this movement is a terrible idea. We need to push them out.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Well, even beyond just the anti-Semitism, which I totally agree with you, it feels like politics today has become this sort of soap opera of infighting, right? And the idea that you would take the death of Charlie Kirk --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: And turn it into some sort of macabre content farm for some of the worst people on the internet, I find disgusting. And so, I, you know, I pray for Erika, and I can't believe she has to go through all this in public. It's outrageous.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to end us on a happy note with that in the rearview mirror.\par \par \uc2 We featured Juan Williams' nephew Christopher, who is an astronaut. We had an interview from the Space Station with him a couple of weeks ago. You are here with an update. And I've got say, he's got the most epic Christmas card.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, that's --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That we're going to get this year.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think so. You know, this -- Merry Christmas to everyone. But can you imagine? One day you open the envelope for Christmas cards and here's the Christmas card that -- it's out of this world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I mean, what do you do with that? How do you say, oh, I'm going to send you a better Christmas card? No.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No. His precious wife and two girls. And he'll be away from them for the holiday season. It's an eight month commitment, but he's doing that so we can make advances. He's a brilliant guy that's doing all kinds of, you know, experiments in scientific study that will benefit, not just our country. I mean they're up there with Russian cosmonauts too.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. Yes. But I just, you know, at this moment, you know, Christmas is about family. And you just stop and you think, wow, you know, I'll be home for Christmas. No, I'll be in space.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, exactly. He will be in space.\par \par \uc2 Stef, final word to you.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I mean, that's an amazing Christmas card. I wish I had the energy to make my own, but I definitely would be in (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're not going to outer space. And this is our excuse for not sending Christmas cards this year because we couldn't top that.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Merry Christmas. Happy Hanukkah. Thank you, everybody, for being with us this year.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN: Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, for billions around the world, faith is the reason for this season. And this year the Museum of the Bible here in Washington is giving believers and nonbelievers alike, whatever faith you are, a chance of a lifetime to visit one of those famous prices artifacts in religious history. We're taking you inside the Dead Sea Scrolls, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RABBI MOSHE ENGLANDER, TORAH SCRIBE: What is the significance of the Dead Sea Scrolls? We've been doing this since we received the revelation on Mount Sinai 3.337 years ago. And it's been a legacy we've carried for 100 generations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It has been a heart-wrenching week for the Jewish community as we mark the eighth and final night of Hanukkah. It feels more important than ever to celebrate this season of hope, faith and lights.\par \par \uc2 At the Museum of the Bible here in Washington, visitors have a rare chance to connect with one of the most incredible artifacts in religious history, the Dead Sea Scrolls. It's our "Sunday Special."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: These are very special treasures.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT DUKE, CHIEF CURATOR, MUSEUM OF THE BIBLE: Yes. So, I mean, we're talking 2,000-year-old documents.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Christmas at the Museum of the Bible offers no shortage of remarkable sites. But this year, something new, or, rather, very, very old. A truly one-of-a-kind insight into the origins of the Judeo-Christian tradition, the Dead Sea Scrolls, the oldest known manuscript of the Hebrew Bible.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : I would argue, and I'm biased, but I would say the Dead Sea Scrolls are the greatest archeological discovery ever.\par \par \uc2 These texts we see here still have an ongoing reality for anyone who reads the Bible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): The Scrolls, which must be filmed in strict, lowlight conditions due to their age, were discovered by accident in 1947 by Bedouin Shepherds in a cave on the shores of the Dead Sea.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : The group that hid them in the caves was probably fleeing the Romans. Fortunately, for the history of scholarship, they didn't come back because now we have these treasures here today that probably would have just disappeared in history.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Now the property of the \uc2 Israel Antiquities Authority\uc2 , the sacred text can only be shown to the public for a few months at a time.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : We have eight documents here. But in three months they will cycle out and will go back into hibernation in Israel, in a climate-controlled, light-controlled vault for five years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This, literally, is where it all begins.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : Absolutely (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is a snapshot of Genesis.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : Yes, so, this is one of our oldest copies of the book of Genesis (INAUDIBLE). And what's more significant about this one, it actually has the first lines of the books of Genesis. It has the lines where it talks about God created the heavens and the earth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And to billions of people around the globe, these are the foundation of their lives.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): The priceless antiquities just one part of a brand-new exhibit.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Offering visitors of all faiths an unprecedented glimpse into life in the Holy Land in the time of Christ.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, what I love about modern-day discoveries as they continue.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : Totally (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And this is actually one of the newer discoveries.\par \par \uc2 DUKE\uc2 : Yes. So, in 2009, they were building a guesthouse at a catholic retreat center on the western shore of the Sea of Galilee. And on of the digging machines actually hit this stone, which stalled the project. And then they realized they needed to do an excavation because his limestone table was discovered on was the first piece of a full synagogue from the first century that was discovered.\par \par \uc2 Once it was fully excavated, and from coins and other things, they know it was in existence around the same time that Jesus and the disciples would have been going around the Sea of Galilee. I am very convinced Jesus would have been in this synagogue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): In addition to treasures of the ancient world, the museum uses state-of-the-art technology to give modern perspective on the 2,000-year-old text. For the Rabbi Moshe Englander, a resident scholar at the museum who specializes in writing the words of the Torah, it represents a culmination of his life's work.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What does it mean to you to be able to be in the presence of these documents and to see, as you said, the legacy that's gone uninterrupted?\par \par \uc2 RABBI MOSHE ENGLANDER, TORAH SCRIBE: It's a feeling of pride and privilege. And to know that I'm part of that chain of transmission and the chain has been true all the way to here and now today, and the way we practice Judaism today. What is the significance of the Dead Sea Scrolls? We've been doing this since we received the revelation on Mount Sini 3,337 years ago. And it's been a legacy we've carried for 100 generations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, as we are in a period where it's a -- it's a holiday. It's a celebration. We are amidst Hanukkah now. What does all of this do and mean to you in that holiday context as well?\par \par \uc2 ENGLANDER: It's very symbolic. As a matter of fact, we were talking about the Isaiah scrolls. Isaiah 52 says the mission of the Jewish people is to be a light unto the nations. This is a vessel of light.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That legacy of faith perhaps most evident in the exhibits ending display, a 4-ton stone from the western wall where believers from around the globe leave written prayers for their intentions and the needs of the world.\par \par \uc2 RENA OPERT, DIRECTOR OF EXHIBITS, MUSEUM OF THE BIBLE: Visitors can have the feeling of the experience, those who haven't been to Jerusalem and to the told city, of what it actually feels like to walk up to the Western Wall. They can feel the stone, first of all, and touch it and connect with it. And they can also write notes, prayers, anything they want to say. And, of course, in Jerusalem, we put them in the wall. And they're messages to God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's universal because God is listening no matter where you are, whether it's here or the Western Wall.\par \par \uc2 OPERT: That's right. We really wanted people to feel the power of what it feels like to be there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that exhibit runs through September of next year.\par \par \uc2 All right, here's something else to add to your holiday list. "Fox News Podcast" presents an audio series on the life of Jesus from Golf Stream Studios. It's now available in podcast form.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Experience the story that change the world with a 52 episode journey through scripture, brought to life by a world-class cast.\par \par \uc2 NEAL MCDONOUGH, ACTOR: You shall worship the Lord your God.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: "The Life of Jesus Podcast," hosted by Ainsley Earhardt. Listen now at foxnewspodcast.com.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You can listen on \uc2 Apple\uc2  podcast, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , foxnewspodcast.com, wherever you like to get your podcasts. Do not miss it.\par \par \uc2 And we've got a really special show for you next week focusing on faith in America. You'll see much more of my lengthy conversation with Erika Kirk. We talk about everything from her brutally honest conversations with God as she works through grief, to what she wants young woman to understand about the realities of juggling family, work and faith and how she plans to continue her husband's mission of encouraging young Americans to embrace what is important.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, somebody who's listening to our conversation, if they are interested or curious, where would you tell them to start we when it comes to trying to figure what faith is?\par \par \uc2 KIRK: One, yes, by a Bible, for sure. Make sure you surround yourself with Bible-believing Christians that will pour into you. Not just plant a seed and leave, but really water that seed and love on you and walk and do life with you. And if you're able to find a church in your community and be surrounded by believers and lean into that, get to know the pastor, get to know the men's group, the women's group, the young teens group that they have a church and really just dive straight in. Dive straight in. That's what Charlie would always do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, more of that conversation next week.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a blessed Hanukkah and Christmas. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY as we focus on faith.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020251221elcl00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Soon, Trump To Battleground Pennsylvania To Talk Economy; New York Times Op-Ed Calls Trump's Stamina Into Question; Mamdani Adds Ex-Con To Transition Team; Trump Administration Briefs Lawmakers On Boat Strikes; Charlie Kirk Honors The Sabbath In Final Book; 100 Billion Meals Challenge Fights Global Hunger\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum; Aishah Hasnie; Kellyanne Conway; Alexis McAdams,  Gillian Turner, Jack Keane \par \uc2 7,990 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Air Force One about to take off as President Trump heads in an hour or so to battleground Pennsylvania this afternoon. It could be seen in some ways as the beginning of the campaign for the midterms. He is seeking to get his message out of economic recovery, saying that he inherited a mess from President Biden, but he will say that things, he believes are on the right track. Here's a piece of an interview he did yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do want to talk about the economy, sir, here at home. And I wonder what grade you would give your economy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: A-plus.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A-plus?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, A-plus, plus, plus, plus, plus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In moments, Pennsylvania Republican Senator \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , who is traveling with the president this afternoon, joins us from that all important state in a couple of districts they will be in today, seven and eight in Pennsylvania that are very important in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But first to White House Correspondent Aishah Hasnie reporting live from Mount Pocono, Pennsylvania. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Well, tonight is a big reset for this White House. It's a brand new strategy. It's getting the president back out on the campaign trail where he's the most comfortable talking directly face-to-face with the American people and really explaining himself how his policies are making lives better, is making things more affordable for the American people.\par \par \uc2 We are learning that tonight the president will be here and he'll talk about a couple of things. He's going to talk about how he extended his 2017 tax cuts, how he passed no taxes on tips, overtime, social security deregulation, unleashing American energy. He'll talk about how gas prices are down nationwide. All the while the president will also continue to slam \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats for what they are calling the affordability crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The word affordability, I inherited a mess. I inherited a total mess. Prices were at an all-time high when I came in. Prices are coming down substantially.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, Democrats are paying very close attention, Martha, to this shift tonight from the White House. Here's Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  with some fighting words.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will throw a barrel of hay into the audience and tell them it's spun gold, but the American people know he is lying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And, Martha, both sides are fired up about where we are today because it is a big time toss-up district. Trump won this area by three points in 2020 -- 2016, excuse me, lost it by less than one in 2020 and then picked it back up again last year by just three points.\par \par \uc2 According to his chief of staff, Susie Wiles, she says they are getting Trump back on the ballot. We're going to see a lot more of these events where he is back in these swingy districts trying to keep control of the GOP House majority. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in Pennsylvania Republican Senator \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , who will travel with the president on Air Force One today. He serves on the Joint Economic Committee and has a long history in the field of finance, understands it well. Senator McCormick, great to see you. Thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DAVE MCCORMICK (R-PA): Hey, good afternoon, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, is that the grade that you would give it, A-plus, plus, plus?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, I think first of all I agree with the president 100 percent that he's inherited a basket case of an economy from the Biden administration. 22 percent increase in prices, wages didn't keep up, out of control spending. And what the president has done in the last year has made huge strides for the economy. Inflation's down about a half of what it was under the Biden administration on average and real wages are up. So -- and the other thing is that there's been huge policy advances made that are going to come into effect in 2026. That's all the tax cuts, the accelerated depreciation, the big, beautiful bill deregulation, energy dominance.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what the president's saying is, listen, things are significantly better now than they were a year ago, but there's a lot more coming and there needs to be a lot more coming because Pennsylvanians and Americans are still feeling the pinch of those disastrous Biden policies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's a quick sound -- a couple of sound bites from the president last time he was on the campaign trail in 2024 speaking to people in your home state of Pennsylvania. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Starting the day I take the oath of office, I will rapidly drive prices down and we will make America affordable again. We're going to make it affordable again.\par \par \uc2 It just went down $100.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We'll do that for you from the White House, all right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He was handing out $100 bills at a grocery store back then. His approval numbers on the economy are not super at the moment, disapprove 52 percent, although we are seeing a partisan divide on that. Republicans are a little bit more positive than Democrats, so you may be seeing some politics playing into this number as well. But when you talk to people in Pennsylvania on the ground, what do they tell you is their biggest economic personal concern right now, Senator?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, I was just in Northeast Pennsylvania for several days, a couple days ago. And what they're seeing is significant benefits in some ways. So, for example, gas prices are down. They're seeing huge job creation with all the investment that's coming into Pennsylvania as a result of the president's policies, but they're still feeling pinched. And the one thing that I think is universal is the deep concern over rising healthcare costs.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you're a family that makes $55,000 a year and what's happened over the last five years is your healthcare costs have gone up dramatically, that's a real pressure point, and it's because of the failure of the Affordable Care Act. It was supposed to deliver better quality, lower prices and access. It's failed on all three of those. And that's a big issue that's looming.\par \par \uc2 We in the Republican caucus, we just had a lunch talking about this, how to figure out how to deal with these subsidies that the Biden administration had put in place during COVID, try to make sure we protect working families, but do away with the fraud and the giveaways to people that make $500,000 a year, that's a real pinch, And then reforming healthcare, in general, given what a disaster Obamacare has been that's a broader topic.\par \par \uc2 So, I think even though they're seeing progress in the economy, they're still feeling the remnants of those terrible policies of both the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration with healthcare and the Biden administration without a control spending and overregulation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I mean, what do you say to those -- since you brought it up, what do you say to those who say, look, we're not going to extend these subsidies, a lot of this money's going straight into the pockets of the insurance companies, but who say to Republicans, where is your plan, you know? I know you said you were just discussing this issue. But where's the plan that the American people can kind of get their hands around and say, well, if I'm not going to get my subsidy, I'm going to pick option B?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, I think there's a short-term plan and a long-term plan. In the short-term, you know, we're still wrestling with how to deal with this, but I'm of the opinion that we should try to find a way to make sure that working families, those that are living paycheck to paycheck, get incremental dollars in their pocket as these subsidies roll off.\par \par \uc2 But the current plan that the Democrats have, a three-year extension of the existing subsidies, this is rife with fraud. This is the uncapped on incomes. So, these are high income people receiving these subsidies. So, the Democratic plan is really political theater. So, we're going to have to deal, in my opinion, with those people that are living paycheck to paycheck with some sort of bridge.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, we got to reform all of healthcare, because as I said, Obamacare is a disaster. We pay three times more per person than any other developed country in the world for healthcare yet bad outcomes and infant mortality and overall morbidity and 25 million people don't have access. So, this is failing.\par \par \uc2 I think we're going to need to reform the system in a way that delivers greater transparency on prices. Who's making the money? Is it these big healthcare insurance companies? Second, we need to give the consumer more control, more decision rights, that's going to drive better outcomes. And, finally, we got to have competition, cross-state competition. We got to squeeze this incredible waste out of the system, and that's going to take some real work. And while it's the Democrats' disaster that we're dealing with, we as Republicans are going to need to lead and we're going to need to find some Democrats to lead with us to get this done.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Understood. We look forward to seeing the outlines of that plan and we look forward to seeing the president and you this evening in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 How important is it that the message from the president -- you know, some people are saying that he's sort of glossing over the issue and saying it's not that bad and I inherited it all from Biden. What do you think the message needs to be tonight to make people understand that the president knows where they are on these things that you just brought up?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, the president knows his message better than anybody, but what I would say is he won Pennsylvania because of working families. Nobody has a better finger on the pulse for working families than President Trump. That's why we're bringing all these jobs back to places like Pennsylvania. That's why he's put the trade policies in place to try to create fundamental fairness. That's why he's been so focused on interest rates to bring down the cost of housing. That's why he's deregulated in energy to bring down energy prices.\par \par \uc2 So, I think he's got a great sense of how the economy affects the overall country, but particularly working families. And I suspect his message tonight will be focused on working families.\par \par \uc2 Those are the people that got us here and those are the people his policies are designed to try to help.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he has loved campaigning in Pennsylvania and was very successful there in a very tough state. So, we'll see how it goes tonight. Susie Wiles says he's going to be campaigning for the midterms with the same vigor that he campaigned in 2024. So, we're going to be watching all that very closely. Senator --\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: I don't doubt it. I don't doubt it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator for \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , thank you very much, sir, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, speaking of that, some of the media, in particular, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , calling the President Sleepy \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Remember when they tried to convince you that President Biden was the Energizer Bunny? A hot debate with Kellyanne Conway and Patrick Murphy, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If I go one day, I had one day where I didn't do a news conference, there's something wrong with the president. You people are crazy. I'll let you know when there's something wrong. There will be someday. That's going to happen to all of us. But right now, I think I'm sharper than I was 25 years ago, but who the hell does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, while the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  seems to be discovering some of the flaws of the Biden presidency these days, a lot of people noticed them a long time ago. Now, they are digging in on questioning President Trump's stamina. The Times' Frank Bruni, always a big fan of the president, cites a, quote, vertical nap that the president took he claims during a recent marathon cabinet meeting. I think it went on for over three hours, that meeting. Americans are -- he said -- he writes this, Americans are once again on a kind of presidential fitness watch, reading the tea leaves of bruises, blotches, and gaffes.\par \par \uc2 The calendar shows the vigorous schedule that President Trump kept during the course of this year. The White House insists that the president is not slowing down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Oh, I think we'll see President Trump out there, most definitely when it comes to the New Year. Of course, we have Christmas coming up in a couple of weeks, but there's nobody that loves the American public more and being out there on the road than President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, with that, we bring in Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump and Fox News contributor, Patrick Murphy, former Democratic Pennsylvania Congressman and former acting Army secretary. Great both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Patrick, you know, I think there's probably easier controversy and a contrast to draw than this one given the schedule that President Trump keeps, which is like multiple times more active than the prior president.\par \par \uc2 FMR\uc2 . REP. PATRICK MURPHY (D-PA): Yes, Martha. I'm not going to play those reindeer games. There's no doubt President Trump has energy. He's going to be obviously in Pennsylvania, my home state, where I'm calling in from right now. But, listen, he did take 