tually think that the reporters do things like this, Martha, palace intrigue, psychoanalyzing, and now physically analyzing President Trump, because they're too lazy to learn the nuances of tariff and trade policy, too lazy to learn what's in the one big, beautiful bill and how that's going to impact people starting in 2026 when they realize the fruits of that to their own and to their employer's tax liability. And I think that we've gone from all the news that's fit to print at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  to all the insults that miserable people wish to hurl. I don't think it benefits anyone.\par \par \uc2 I read the piece twice. I suffered through that and he obviously watches T.V., including Silent Life. He quoted five different people from T.V. and he quoted two other reporters who wrote pieces. The opinion piece should be exactly that. And I think people should get therapists and get better friends and maybe a pet or two instead of putting upon the rest of us every time they just want to unfurl and unhurl a mindless group of words that really have no basis and fact.\par \par \uc2 People are hurting. They want solutions. They're getting them from the president. His -- guess what? He exhausts staffers one third his age. The man will be 80 next June 14th. Show me anybody who can keep his schedule.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He has tremendous energy.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: He doesn't even need this job. Yes, it's undeniable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think anybody can deny that in any way.\par \par \uc2 Real quick, this is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  quoted in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  saying that there will be a marble bust of her in Congress, and I want to read this is also in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , okay?\par \par \uc2 She says, I understand the focus on '28 and all that, she said in an interview, but there will be a marble bust of me in Congress. I am a historic figure like any vice president of the United States ever was. Patrick?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, she was the first Democratic nominee for president to not win a popular vote in the last two decades. That's the reality of it. And I think --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is that what it's going to say underneath the marble bust?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: No, Martha, listen, I'm trying to have the Christmas spirit, Martha. I'm trying to be nice. And God bless America. How about that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, she said this. You know, this is what she -- this is the message she apparently wants to get out there a couple weeks before Christmas, that there's going to be a marble bust of her in Congress because she is a historic figure.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Historic.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just want to make sure that I got that.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: She's the queen of the self-own. Martha, she's the queen of the self-own. She doesn't even realize that she's reminding us that they spent about $2 billion in seven weeks for her to fail miserably and give us a second term of President Trump. So, you know, I became the nominee for the Democratic Party as a historic figure, and all I got to show for it was this T-shirt and this marble bust, beautiful.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, apparently, she wants to stay in the conversation and that worked today.\par \par \uc2 Patrick and Kellyanne, thank you both very much. It's great to see you both, as always. Thanks guys.\par \par \uc2 So still had this hour, Tony Robbins on his star-studded mission to change the world for the better.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He is making news again. New York City Mayor-elect Zohran Mamdani takes office on New Year's Day, just a couple -- well, it is about three weeks away, I guess. But the far left Democratic socialist is making waves very much so already with the people that he has tapped to carry out his agenda and be on his team. His transition team now includes an ex- convict who served seven years in prison for being with a crew that was robbing taxi drivers.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexis McAdams caught up with the mayor-elect to ask about this controversial appointment. Hi, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. That's the big question people want to know why did Mamdani pick a convicted felon this guy you're looking at here, who served nearly a decade behind bars to advise him on criminal justice in New York City. So, I asked him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR-ELECT ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D-NEW YORK CITY, NY): We put together a team of more than 400 New Yorkers who are on 17 different committees, and these are New Yorkers who bring with them both a fluency of the policies and politics of the city, the places that they've succeeded, the places that they've failed. And we will take all of their experiences and their analysis into account as we build a city for each and every person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And this is who we're talking about. So, Mysonne Linen is now on the transition team for incoming Mayor Mamdani. He was convicted of two armed robberies, as you mentioned, back in the late 90s. That's when he was an up and coming rap star at that point, but he was spending his time, according to the police, holding up two taxi drivers at gunpoint out in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Linen has really rebranded himself as a community activist, or he is tried to. He says he's focusing on fighting for black and brown communities across New York City. Former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  top brass say, don't buy it. They say this is just another pick that's going to erode the safety in New York City.\par \par \uc2 And it's not Mamdani's only pick that's raising eyebrows. It's also this woman, the soon to be mayor of New York City, appointed her, Zakiyah Shaakir-Ansari, to the Youth and Education Committee. Critics are saying, why did you pick her for this? Because she praised convicted New Jersey Cop Killer Assata Shakur, also known as Joanne Chesimard, calling the former member of the Black Liberation Army, someone black women could draw inspiration from. So, people are wondering why she would ever be picked to lead education for young kids.\par \par \uc2 This comes just days after Mamdani made some more news, as you mentioned, Martha releasing that video, advising illegal migrants on their rights, telling them don't cooperate with ICE unless they have a warrant. DHS says, Mamdani shouldn't do that, and it could be illegal. So, we'll keep following it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We know you will. Alexis, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, here's a live look at the Capitol right now where Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  and Secretary of State, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and CIA Director John Ratcliffe and chairman of the Joint Chiefs General Dan Caine are all briefing a select group of lawmakers about President Trump's national security blueprint and the war on narco-terrorists. General Jack Keane is standing by.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So right now there's a classified briefing going on Capitol Hill on the US military strikes targeting suspected drug boats. Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 , Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , CIA Director John Ratcliffe, and the Joint Chiefs Chairman General Dan "Raizin" Caine are all in there meeting with top lawmakers right now, very important meeting underway, as both parties sort of get read in a secure facility.\par \par \uc2 Retired General Jack Keane is standing by. But first, to senior foreign policy correspondent Gillian Turner reporting live from the State Department. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. So this briefing that's about to get underway now is really meant to give lawmakers an opportunity to iron out some of the thornier details surrounding these controversial September 2nd boat strikes. And it comes as an increasing chorus of lawmakers is now putting a tremendous pressure on the administration to release the tapes in full.\par \par \uc2 President Trump last night confirmed to Politico that he has personally seen all the footage of the boat strikes on that have went down on September 2nd. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And do you believe that that second strike was necessary?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: Well, it looked like they were trying to turn back over the boat, but I don't get involved in that. That's up to them. The admiral that did that was -- is a highly respected, as you know, a highly respected man.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So that account matches what Admiral Frank Bradley told lawmakers on Capitol Hill last week behind closed doors, according to our FOX News, sourcing he had said the two survivors of that initial strike were seen trying to recover their drug smuggling operation before they were taken out. And the President, after initially saying he wanted the video released in full, seemed to reverse course yesterday, deferring that decision to the secretary of war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Whatever he decides is OK with me. I told you, whatever \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  wants to do is OK with me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Democrats are now seizing on that reversal to double down, go all in on the President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT RYAN (D-NY): This transcends the legal implications, in my opinion, has deep moral implications, especially for those of us that served. I mean, I didn't risk my life 27 months in combat to see our American values eviscerated like this, particularly by a guy in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who dodged the draft five times.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Martha, we got a team, FOX News crew staking out this Gang of Eight classified briefing that is about to get underway. Our hope is that some of the senior officials coming out of that and some of the lawmakers will respond on camera. We will, of course, bring that to you as soon as it gets underway.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we'll head right over there if that happens. Thank you very much, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in retired four star General Jack Keane, former Army Vice Chief of Staff, now Chairman of the Institute for the Study of War, and FOX News Senior Strategic Analyst. General, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's very interesting. I mean, the people who have a lot of outcry about what happened and what's happening here with these boats seems to fall very much along party lines so far. Just a quick reaction to that, sir.\par \par \uc2 JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, I could care less about the party lines, to be frank. I'm just more focused on what the facts are. I mean, the facts are, is that for the past 25 plus years, our military has been authorized to take out terrorists because of who they are, not what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 If they're in a building having a meeting completely unarmed, or having a dinner completely unarmed, we have the right to take them out. And then, there's a chaotic situation. You can imagine, some are killed and some are wounded as a result of it. Complete chaos you're watching on a video.\par \par \uc2 Some of them are trying to help their teammates recover from wounds. Some of them are trying to run away. And we can re-attack. We're authorized to attack those survivors because of who they are. And people are just missing the point.\par \par \uc2 So you're going to see a video eventually of two survivors by a boat that are clearly not armed. It's irrelevant whether they were ever armed, to be frank. We're going to eliminate them because of who they are. We want them to stop doing what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 If they recover and survive, they can do it again. And that is what is happening here. And people are just missing the point. And we've been at this through multiple presidents over time and time again. It is authorized.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And that's why I made that caveat, because it's very -- I mean, we saw it with President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , we've seen it with prior presidents where we see similar operations, and we didn't hear the same kind of outcry. I want to play this for you from an interview on Politico that the President did about Maduro and what he thinks will happen with regard to him. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: Could we see American troops on the ground in Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't comment on that. I wouldn't say that one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So how far would you go to take Maduro out office?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't want to say that, but --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But you want to see him out?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: His days are numbered.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Can you rule out an American ground invasion?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't want to rule in or out. I don't talk about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The President also said in that interview, General Keane, that he would look to do something similar potentially with Mexico and Colombia in terms of an operation to stop the transit of these Drugs. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Well, I think it's high time that we pay some attention to this illegal transportation of drugs from multiple countries and what it's doing to our people. So we have a comprehensive strategy to do this. We're trying to shut down China from sending the precursors from which Mexico manufactures all the fentanyl drugs. And that's outrageous in itself.\par \par \uc2 We're getting some cooperation from the Mexican government, not much cooperation from the Colombian government on narcotics, and no cooperation whatsoever with Venezuela, because the government and narco traffickers are one and the same. So I think the approach is right and demonstrating force to Maduro.\par \par \uc2 I think what the President really wants here, Martha, is Maduro realize this thing is up and leave. And that's what the demonstration of military capability is all about. But he also wants him to know that if he doesn't leave, he has options here to certainly take down some of his capability, but also to remove him. Much as George H.W. Bush did with Noriega in Panama in '89 and Clinton did in Haiti in 1994 for similar reasons, as a matter of fact. Fraudulent elections, narco trafficking, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 So those options are on the table. And obviously, it's the President's decision as well as Maduro's decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed. Presidents have a lot of latitude where these kinds of operations are concerned. And as you point out, we've seen them throughout modern history. General Jack Keane, thank you very much. Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE Yes, great talking.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Erika Kirk's emotional reason for not wanting to read the final pages of Charlie Kirk's last book, that's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, FOUNDER, TURNING POINT USA: Read your Bible every single day. Fight your flesh. Your flesh is going to want to do easy and immediate things like drink and go out, and do stuff. Find your aim as a man and point everything you can towards it. And spread the good news of God and Jesus Christ in every possible way that you can, and put Jesus first in everything that you do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He was a man of faith. And today, Charlie Kirk's last lessons in the newly published book "Stop in the Name of God: Why Honoring the Sabbath will Transform Your Life." His wife, Erika, telling \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  last night that there's a reason why she has yet to finish reading the book herself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIKA KIRK, CEO, TURNING POINT USA: This is Charlie's last book so people can write books all about my husband and what he believed, and what he said, and analyze his videos. These are his words. And when you read them, you feel him come to life. It's powerful.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NATION HOST: It is very powerful. And it really caused me as I read it, again, I read the whole thing, you know, and --\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: I'll be honest, I can't read the last few pages. I'm not there yet. It's just emotionally, because when this book is done, it's done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Andrew Kolvet watched Charlie working on this book over the last year of his life. He's a very close friend, now Turning Point USA's spokesperson and the executive producer of the "Charlie Kirk Show." Andrew, welcome. Good to have you back on the program.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW KOLVET, TURNING POINT USA SPOKESPERSON: Thanks, Martha. It's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me play another soundbite from Erika about why Charlie decided to basically turn off and tune out the outside world from Friday evenings until Saturday evening every week. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 E. KIRK: The thing that was so special about our Sabbath dinners was that it wasn't about religious protocol. It was literally going to my mom's house. It was, again, bridging that generational gap where my kids don't have phones. They're not looking at iPads, they're not looking at screens.\par \par \uc2 We're sitting together and we're talking, and we're loving on each other. How was your week? What were your wins? What are you grateful for? We went around the table and talked about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, that's what we used to call normal life. And now we're sort of fighting to just carve out 24 hours of it but it has enormous impact on people's lives. Right, Andrew?\par \par \uc2 KOLVET: Well, and that's exactly right, Martha. And Charlie knew that, and he became an expert at resting. And I don't think people associate Charlie Kirk with resting or taking a break because he worked so hard. I remember one time I asked him, I said, you know, Charlie, do you ever have fun? And we used to have a joke. He said, yes, I tried it once and I hated it.\par \par \uc2 Charlie loved to work. But Charlie became convinced and then realized more and more that if he did take a Sabbath, he did take a day of rest, that he was able to accomplish more in six days with rest than he would in seven without it. And, you know, I watched him transform over the years from somebody that worked nonstop to somebody who dedicated this day with such discipline to taking time off.\par \par \uc2 And that was -- it was amazing because we saw at Turning Point, and what it was able to achieve, just go through the roof and. And all the team members then started kind of following that rhythm, and it improved our family's lives and improved our productivity. And so, this was something that worked through the organization. And it was a really amazing breakthrough for Charlie when he discovered this.\par \par \uc2 And I just think it's so apropos that Charlie, who's known as a political figure, his last book is not about politics at all. It's about God. It's about this discipline, this gift that he learned to receive, and he's giving it away to the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I don't think there are too many accidents, and I think there's a reason why this is his last book, and it will get an enormous amount of attention. I want to just read this number three guys. He writes in the book, don't be afraid to turn off your phone. You're not falling behind. You're catching up to what matters most.\par \par \uc2 The people in front of you, the presence of God, the peace you've been craving. Let the Sabbath be your weekly rebel. Let it be the time when the world's demands go silent and the eternal voice becomes audible again.\par \par \uc2 I was listening to an Advent reading the other day, and it said, you know, on the seventh day, God rested. You know, if God needed to rest, certainly the rest of us do, Andrew.\par \par \uc2 KOLVET: Yes. And it's funny, I have a section here that I had pulled aside from the book that's in front of me to read to you as well. And Charlie just says, cease striving, cease earning, cease proving, cease buying and selling and producing. This is not a suggestion. It's a divine imperative.\par \par \uc2 And so, Charlie -- if Charlie could do this, then anybody can. Because I will tell you, he was the busiest person I've ever met. He's the most driven person I'd ever met. And he knew that in order to survive and thrive, and get to that next level, he had to do it.\par \par \uc2 And I will just tell you that it's a discipline that I'm trying to do. It's a discipline that I encourage our teams to do, and I encourage everybody out there listening to put this into practice in their life. And I think if we did this as a country, Martha, that we'd see happiness improve, we'd see productivity improve, we'd see mental health improve. It's so, so important.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just -- I just want to say one last thing. I'm so proud of Erica Kirk for doing this in her husband's stead. This was Charlie's book. He was so excited to promote it and to give it to the world, and to talk about it on the "Charlie Kirk Show." And so, Erika stepping in and doing this, it's truly a brave and remarkable thing and the latest in a series of remarkable actions for America.\par \par \uc2 And she's doing a phenomenal job. And thank you for all your support for that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She sure is. And we're glad we get to have her around a bunch over the next 24 hours. Andrew Kolvet, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KOLVET: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for being here as always. So, as Andrew was saying, Erika will be here in an hour. She will be co-hosting "The Five," so stay tuned for that. And then, I'll be with her on the couch tomorrow for "Outnumbered," so stay tuned for that as well tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Tony Robbins, the mega popular life and business strategist enlists some of the biggest names in music to help with an ambitious mission to give back to those who need it most next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A child dies of hunger every 10 seconds according to the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2 . Now you can help by joining the 100 Billion Meals Challenge. You can even appear in a recording of a new song featuring Janet Jackson, Dr. Dre, Andrea Bocelli and so many other legendary artists.\par \par \uc2 The nation's number one life and business strategist, one of the most famous people on the planet, Tony Robbins, is leading this project and he joins me now. Tony, it's really great to have you on the show today. So I want to thank you for that. You knew hunger --\par \par \uc2 TONY ROBBINS, BUSINESS STRATEGIST: Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's great to have you. You knew hunger as a child. Your family was on food stamps. Tell me a little bit about this project as we jump in here.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Well, it's interesting. When I was 11 years old, we had no money and no food. When I say no food, we had crackers and peanut butter but it was Thanksgiving. And so, the level of anger and depression amongst my family was pretty rough. And somebody came to the door and delivered us an entire Thanksgiving meal.\par \par \uc2 And we don't know who did it. We guessed who might be, but we still don't know who did it from so many years ago. But it changed my life because I lived in an environment where it looked like strangers didn't care. My father said that all the time. And now a stranger cared about our family at our time of need.\par \par \uc2 So I started caring about strangers, and I decided I'd feed two families. And when I was 17, I started two, and then four, and then a million, and then 2 million. And then about 12 years ago, I thought, I'm going to provide a billion meals, which just seemed like the most amazing thing to do in the US. And I did it in eight years.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, the problem's not going away, Martha. What most people don't know is right now, around the world, not only are there 42 million Americans they're not sure where the next meal is coming from. But this year, there are normally 80 million people on the verge of starvation. This year is 385 million because of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It's actually the breadbasket for most of Africa. And so, what's happened now is they're taking food at the \uc2 UN\uc2  from people that are hungry to give it to people that are starving. So the found -- shouldn't say the founder, the head of the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2 , Governor Beasley, David Beasley, became a good friend of mine, and he got frustrated working the \uc2 UN\uc2  where you just -- there was such bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 And I said, why don't you and I personally create a strike team that does this with more precision. How many meals would it take to feed everybody that's hungry for 10 years while we're getting sustainability? And he said, I don't know, 50, 60 billion meals? I said, well, why don't we do 100 Billion Meal Challenge? He said, that's crazy.\par \par \uc2 I said, I had a billion. I wasn't a billionaire when I started, so I said, I think we can find 100 other people like me. So we've so far delivered 62 billion meals in places like the Sudan where no one else got the food in. I paid initially, get it. I got people to match me for a million people.\par \par \uc2 And as we did it, we flew them in and used drones to feed people for a month to keep them alive that were cut off from food. Same thing to give an idea, you know, out in Gaza. But now we're creating this song, the next "We are the World" as a way of bringing attention and economics to help people eat around the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to play just 10 seconds of "We Are the World" because it is -- it's feels it was such a wonderful program and you want to recreate this great vibe. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. What a beautiful project that was. And, you know, so tell me about the next verse. What is this going to feel like, Tony?\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Well, Quincy Jones was a dear friend of mine and Michael Jackson was as well. They helped create that original song. And so we put together Dr. Dre, Bruno Mars, Janet Jackson, Usher, Stevie Wonder, John Legend, Jon Bon Jovi, Andrea Bocelli, Pit Bull, I mean, it goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 And on December 16th, we're going to be in LA at the iHeart Theater, \uc2 iHeart\uc2 's our sponsor. And we are gonna make the next verse this song that's kind of the next anthem to bring people's attention because there are more people dying today than when this was done 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 And we know music goes beyond politics. It goes beyond all the things that stop human beings from helping each other. So that's our focus. And if people want to help or just experience it, you can save a life. You can go to 100billionmeals.org, 100billion.org, and when you go there, you can either watch the \uc2 Zoom\uc2  of this being made on the 16th for free or you can donate $10 and you feed 44 people, and you get a chance to push a button that allows you to take a picture with you with the stars on screen.\par \par \uc2 And if you decide to become part of our lifesavers club, it's $19.95 a month. You save a thousand lives. But you also get an opportunity to actually be in the chorus in the video background, because some of the stars won't be there in person. They're overseas, like Pit Bull is in Australia. So they're going to come in by \uc2 Zoom\uc2  and have the whole thing integrated.\par \par \uc2 But on Christmas Day the song will go out around the world. \uc2 IHeart\uc2  will carry it. TikTok obviously is carrying it and we have radio stations around the world. So we'll launch this to bring people's attention to all the starving people in the world but also empower them to participate in a way that hopefully unifies people around the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: More of that on my podcast the Untold Story. Thanks for joining us today, everybody. It's Tuesday, December 9th. We are ticking off the days until Christmas. As always, "THE STORY" goes on right here. So we will be back with you tomorrow at 3:00.\par \par \uc2 Stay tuned for Will Cain who's coming up right after this. But let's give him a shot of the Avenue of the Americas, one of the busiest and most beautiful times of year in New York City. Lots of tourists, and we will be right back here tomorrow, right by the tree.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020251210elc900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 September U.S. Boat Strike Under Scrutiny; Interview with Virginia Senator Tim Kaine and Nebraska Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 ; State of Defense; \uc2 Anduril Industries\uc2  Founder on Decision to Focus on Defense Sector; U.S. Military Leans into Next-Gen A.I. to Maintain Edge on Battlefield; Anduril Founder \uc2 Palmer Luckey\uc2  on Role of A.I. in Military Operations; The Politics Of Rising Healthcare Costs; New Reagan Presidential Library Exhibit On Cowboys; President's Road Trip To Focus On The Economy; Minnesota Benefits Fraud Scandal; \uc2 NASA\uc2  Astronaut \uc2 Chris Williams\uc2  Starts Eight Month Mission Aboard the International Space Station; Reagan Library Christmas Trees\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Karl Rove, Lucas Tomlinson, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 9,976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Live from the Reagan Presidential Library in Simi Valley, California, our in-depth look at the state of defense. I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Every boat we knock out, we save 25,000 American lives.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The White House navigates rough waters as lawmakers debate the legality of strikes on suspected drug traffickers.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): People on a boat in the middle of the Caribbean carrying cocaine are not a direct threat.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And embattled Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  faces a bombshell report over Signalgate. We'll get reaction from both sides of the aisle. Virginia Senator Tim Kaine and Nebraska Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The country is standing on the edge of a health care cliff.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN THUEN (R-SD): We want to move forward on solutions that will make healthcare not only more accessible, but more affordable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Temperature check. Senate Democrats force a longshot vote on a three-year Obamacare subsidy extension as the clock ticks and premiums rise.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  joins us exclusively with his take.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 PALMER LUCKEY\uc2 , ANDURIL FOUNDER: When it comes to life and death decision- making, it is too critical of an area to not apply the best technology.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Modern warfare. Anduril CEO \uc2 Palmer Luckey\uc2  on his company's push to transform America's strength and ability on the 21st century battlefield.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first time that I got to look out at that and see the Earth below me -- I mean, that was just absolutely breathtaking.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: View from the top. An astronaut near and dear to the FOX family on his out of this world journey.\par \par \uc2 All right now, on "FOX News Sunday" -- State of Defense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News at the Reagan Presidential Library in southern California.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of today's top stories.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Washington Saturday ruled that evidence linked to former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2 's former attorney is temporarily off limits. Prosecutors had relied on the material to indict Comey of making false statements and obstructing Congress. Daniel Richman, a friend of Comey's who briefly represented him several years ago and once worked as a special \uc2 FBI\uc2  employee, has been accused of communicating with reporters about investigations into \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hawaii's Kilauea Volcano has come alive again, spewing lava and ash fountains into the air. There have been at least eruptions since late 2024. No nearby residential areas have been seriously threatened by the eruptions so far.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says he had a long and constructive phone call with U.S. peace negotiators after the latest round of talks between the two countries wrapped up in Miami. Meanwhile, Russia launched a large-scale drone and missile attack on eight Ukrainian regions overnight, appearing to target their power stations.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll bring in Senators \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2  and Tim Kaine to discuss efforts to end the Russia Ukraine war and those continued strikes on boats off the coast of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 But first, Lucas Tomlinson joins us after his sit down with Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 , where they talked about both of those hot button headlines -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, it's great to be with you on set here at the Reagan National Defense Forum where War Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  told me, given the opportunity, he would have ordered that second strike on the suspected drug smuggling boat at the center of controversy on September 2nd.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, SECRETARY OF WAR: There aren't many people getting in boats right now running drugs, which is the whole point.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): The very first strike to begin combat operations in the Caribbean has also received the most scrutiny after two men who initially survived the initial missile strike were killed in a follow-on strike, ordered by Navy SEAL Admiral Frank Bradley, the mission commander, according to Hegseth.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: From what I understood then and what I understand now, I fully support that strike. I would have made the same call myself.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Some lawmakers call the second strike against the survivors a war crime, others a righteous one.\par \par \uc2 President Trump said he would have no problem releasing the video showing the follow-on strikes. It's not clear when that will happen.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Mr. Secretary, you will be releasing that full video?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: We are reviewing it right now.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Is that a yes or no?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: That is for -- the most important thing to me are the ongoing operations in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Last week, the top Pentagon watchdog accused Hegseth's actions in the Signalgate controversy, quote, "created a risk to operational security that could have resulted in failed U.S. mission objectives and potential harm to U.S. pilots", over his use of the end-to- end encryption app to share operational details ahead of the first bombing mission against Houthi rebels in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 Asked if he would use Signal again if he had a chance, Hegseth replied --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: I don't live -- I don't live with any regrets. It's not a healthy way to live.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Hegseth arrived in California to give the keynote address at the Reagan National Defense Forum. He outlined four pillars for a new defense strategy, prioritizing homeland and western hemisphere, China and Indo-Pacific, allied burden sharing, and revitalizing the U.S. defense industrial base.\par \par \uc2 Today, the U.S. spends roughly 3 percent of its GDP on defense, half of what was spent at the height of the Reagan buildup.\par \par \uc2 Hegseth says a budget boost could be coming.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: I think that number is going up.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Tensions also rising in Venezuela after the Trump administration designated a cartel linked to President Nicolas Maduro, a foreign terrorist organization. President Trump warned strikes could be next inside Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're going to start very soon on land.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): In his remarks, Hegseth said the Monroe Doctrine is stronger than ever. The president who came after Monroe, \uc2 John Quincy Adams\uc2 , said, "America does not go abroad seeking monsters to destroy"-- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Hey, so, Lucas, you asked him another controversial question. This one was Army or Navy?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: That's right. The big Army-Navy game is coming up next week. And I asked him who he's rooting for.\par \par \uc2 In a surprise, I think, to the audience, he said he's rooting for Navy because of the Marine Corps. It must have been his time with the Marines aboard Iwo Jima or perhaps going aboard the \uc2 Gerald R. Ford\uc2  on Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, we'll see if he goes Army next year.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Perhaps.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, now to our bipartisan foreign policy conversation earlier this weekend here at the Reagan National Defense Forum.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now is \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  member Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2 , and from the other side of the aisle, Senate Foreign Relations Committee member, Senator Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you with us at the forum.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA), SENATE FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEB FISCHER (R-NE), \uc2 SENATE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE\uc2 : Nice to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk of Venezuela. It's getting all of the headlines. One of your colleagues, Senator \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2 , said this, "The \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  has no choice but to release the complete, unedited footage of the September 2nd strike, as the president has agreed to do."\par \par \uc2 Have either of you seen it? How important do you think it is that the American people see it?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I haven't seen it, but I think it should be released. Those who have, have described it to me. I think all committee members, all senators and the public should see it, as President Trump said.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: I have not seen it yet. I know that Chairman Wicker is considering having the entire Armed Services Committee be able to review it and ask questions. And I look forward to getting back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, the people who've seen it and have come out of the briefing had very different takes. We're going to play a couple of members from opposite sides of the aisle about what they saw.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT), RANKING MEMBER, HOUSE PERMANENT SELECT COMMITTEE ON INTELLIGENCE: Any American who sees the video that I saw will see the United States military attacking shipwrecked sailors, bad guys, bad guys, but -- but attacking shipwrecked sailors.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR), ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: I saw two survivors trying to flip a boat loaded with drugs, bound for the United States, back over so they could stay in the fight. And potentially, given to all the contexts we heard, of other narco-terrorist boats in the area coming to their aid to recover their cargo and recover those narco-terrorists. (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is this going to be anything more than sort of a partisan Rorschach test, I mean, how you see what you saw?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, it sounds like it, that that's the direction we're headed right now. And that's why I think it's important that we do have the facts. But as we heard this morning here at Reagan, when the survey results were put forward, the people in this country strongly support President Trump in -- in controlling drugs coming into this country. They are strongly supporting the United States stepping up and doing these strikes.\par \par \uc2 We have seen more Americans die in the last seven years than those Americans that were lost in World War I, World War II, and Vietnam combined. This -- this is a horrible situation that we're in, in this country. The president promised he would take action. And that's what he is doing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, I know that some of the action concerns you. You've got a War Powers resolution that's privileged, by the way --\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- that you may offer up saying, this is -- if we're going to call this a war, will use certain, you know, guideposts.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congress has to get involved. It's privileged, will you force it to the floor?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I -- I will likely a week from Monday, when it becomes available.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, what I think about this is, huge questions have been raised about the legality of this operation, and not just from Democrats, but the -- there was a forced resignation of the Southern Command commander. There was the Southern Command legal adviser who said -- asked real questions about legality. The U.K. has stopped sharing intel with the United States over questions about legality.\par \par \uc2 But get this: 90-plus days, nearly 90 people killed in two dozen strikes, there hasn't been a single public hearing held by either the Armed Services Committee or the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. I have a lot of questions, but it's all based on classified information that I can't even describe to you or to the American public.\par \par \uc2 Why not let the committees actually have a public hearing about the legality, about the strategy, about the targeting criteria and so, the American public can be informed and make their minds up about whether this is a good idea?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You reference classified briefings. There have been briefings for lawmakers. Do you, Senator Fischer, think you've gotten enough information about what's going on?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: More information is always -- is always available, and that's what we need to do. But I would counter that I think it's important that we continue to have classified briefings. That's why I think it's important for the Armed Services Committee, and Tim is also a member of that committee, that we have the opportunity to have classified briefings.\par \par \uc2 There's reasons for that. And it's methods, it's personnel on how this information is -- is given to the United States. How we know these folks are there, where they come from, who's involved. And the certainty involved in that, from that information allows the military then to make these strategic strikes.\par \par \uc2 We have to be able to protect those sources and the methods that we have.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I -- I don't disagree with Deb. It's very important that we have classified hearings. But, again, to be 90-plus days into something that the president said -- has said is a war where people are being killed, where there's huge questions being raised about legality, and not have had a single public hearing in either the Foreign Relations or Armed Services Committee, it's time that we do that so that the American public can be read in and all members get a chance -- I mean, we haven't seen the videos yet. We need to see them, too.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to talk about the other big foreign policy headline, of course, is Russia-Ukraine. Talks continue through this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Knowing where we're at right now, I want to ask you both, do you have confidence that Putin actually wants peace at this point? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: No, I don't believe he does. And I don't think many of the European nations that border Russia that know his behavior very well, not just Ukraine, but Finland and Sweden and others, Poland, which had to, you know, put its air defenses on alert last night because of Russian drone incursions, they -- they don't see Putin as really wanting peace. I hope that's wrong, but that is my conclusion as of right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you hold out hope that he's actually trying to get to a finish line of peace?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: No, I don't believe he is looking for peace. It's a stalling tactic that he continues to have. I support the resolution by Senator Graham to increase sanctions to bring Russia to the table, to force them to come to the table. I thank the administration for stepping forward and trying to work out a peace that is fair for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We have to remember Russia is the aggressor here. They invaded a sovereign country. They have taken land from a sovereign nation. That has to be always at the forefront of our mind as we move forward, trying to find a peaceful reconciliation here of what's happening in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Are you confident, if that sanction -- sanctions package passes, that will actually bring this about? Because they -- there are a lot of sanctions on Russia now. This goes more broadly after people who are also doing business with Russia.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is this the thing that could bring it to now? You both have confidence in that?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: I think it'll get their attention.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: I think we'll get the attention of other countries who are doing that business with Russia, realizing that -- that they are held responsible by the United States as well.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: When you ask, you know, are we confident that this will be the ingredient -- I think we wouldn't say that, but it will get the attention. The Russian economy is suffering.\par \par \uc2 But going after those who continue to do business with Russia even despite its aggression, and Deb and I agree on this completely, that will be a significant additional element. It's a bipartisan bill, Senator Graham and Senator Blumenthal. And I think we're both co-sponsors of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm. Well, very few things get that level of support in the Senate, that level of bipartisan support. So, we'll see how that moves.\par \par \uc2 So here at the Defense Forum, they do a survey every year. This year, China is still identified as the number one foreign policy threat to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Do you both agree on that? And do you think it's more of a military threat or economic threat or the combination?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: I think it's a combination. But what we've seen in the last half dozen years or so are really the breathtaking advancements militarily when it comes to China, especially with their nuclear triad and their development on their platforms and also their weapons. They have become a peer -- a peer competitor, an adversary for us, and that is not good.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And one of the things that this forum is, you know, economic threats and military threats are not necessarily separate. You know, the theft of intellectual property around critical technologies that enable us to defend ourselves and defend our allies, that -- those threats start to blend together. And that's why China is our number one competitor and adversary right now, and for decades to come, I believe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we'll end there on a bipartisan note. Senator Kaine, Senator Fischer, thank you both for your time. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Straight ahead, the burgeoning alliance between the Pentagon and Silicon Valley as the military leans in to emerging technology like A.I. to maintain a competitive edge over America's adversaries. I sat down with \uc2 Anduril Industries\uc2  founder \uc2 Palmer Luckey\uc2  to talk about his vision for the next generation of modern warfare.\par \par \uc2 That's next as we continue our look at the state of defense from the Reagan Presidential Library.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back to our "FOX News Sunday" special, The State of Defense from the Reagan Presidential Library in Simi Valley, California. The U.S. military has been increasingly employing A.I. and other state-of-the-art tools for defense tactics and operations as our soldiers adapt to a changing field of battle.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest has been at the forefront of this technology revolution. \uc2 Anduril Industries\uc2  Founder \uc2 Palmer Luckey\uc2  joins us now. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PALMER LUCKEY\uc2 , ANDURIL INDUSTRIES FOUNDER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, from an early age, you had some really advanced technical ideas with technology. When did it become more about this military side?\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: My first company was a company called \uc2 Oculus VR\uc2 , which I started when I was 19 years old living in a camper trailer making virtual reality headsets. I sold that company. And then three years later, I started \uc2 Anduril Industries\uc2  because I wanted to get people out of the tech industry working on problems that I thought were not so important, you know, advertising, social media, entertainment, and put them to work on defense problems, national security problems, problems that really matter.\par \par \uc2 And I think I got into that because I looked around and realized that the United States was running a very dangerous experiment, which was a national divorce between our most innovative technology companies and our national security apparatus. And things have been changing for the better over the last few years. But you rewind back in time 10 years ago.\par \par \uc2 You have massive \uc2 Google\uc2  employee protests. You have companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2  pulling away from the United States and moving towards China. You have kind of this behavior across the industry. And, again, you've never seen an experiment like this in U.S. history. You've always, in U.S. history, had our most innovative technologists working hand-in-hand with our military. You can imagine how World War II or the Cold War might have gone had that not been the case. And I realized we needed to end this experiment. We need to get those people back into problems that really matter in national security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and a lot of what you work with is artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We had a show last week about A.I. and the use in military and battle and how it's changing everything. But there are a lot of concerns from people about the use of that technology where humans are sort of taken out of a lot of interaction with decisions that can lead to taking lives and serious life-and-death situations. How do we manage that?\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: Well, I'd like to point out I've been thinking about these problems for a lot longer than most of those critics. I mean, when I started Anduril, I mean, eight -- you know, eight and a half, almost nine years ago, most people thought A.I. was this crazy sci-fi thing that was always going to be in the future, never quite in the present. You know, present -- they thought it was almost like, you know, time travel or -- or, you know - - or virtual reality.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Anduril Industries\uc2 , you might notice the acronym is A.I. for a reason. I believed that A.I. was not just going to come eventually but imminently and that we were going to be able to apply it to these national security problems.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to life-and-death decision-making, I think that it is too morally fraught an area. It is too critical of an area to not apply the best technology available to you, regardless of what it is, whether it's A.I. or quantum or anything else. If you're talking about killing people, you need to be minimizing the amount of collateral damage.\par \par \uc2 You need to be as certain as you can in anything that you do. You need to be as effective as possible. And so to me, there's no moral high ground in using inferior technology, even if it allows you to say things like, we never let a robot decide who lives and who dies.\par \par \uc2 Where's the moral high ground in, for example, an anti-vehicle landmine that can't tell the difference between a school bus full of kids and a column of Russian armor? Is that really making you a better person because you've made your smart weapon into a dumb weapon? Same thing for a missile that can't differentiate between, let's say, a civilian boat and a military vessel.\par \par \uc2 Is there really a moral high ground in saying, well, at least the A.I. didn't make the decision as to which boat to strike? Again, I think that for these problem -- these matters of life and death, it's too critical a problem to not apply the best technology. Sometimes that will be A.I. Sometimes it won't be A.I. But it's really about using the best tech to make sure that you're treating these problems as serious things that I guess kind of require that level of diligence.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've talked about something that got a lot of attention. You talked about us being the world's gun store, meaning --\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- we will have the best technology available. We're not going to fight your wars, but we may create it so that you will have it to fight your own wars. But do you worry about things that you create being used by people who may not share our values?\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: Yeah, so I've often said the United States is going to stop being the world police and start being the world's gun store, meaning we need to make arms for our allies and our partners around the world. And if you're going to be a gun store, you need to do a good job of it, right? You need to be at a reasonable price. You need to keep things in stock. You can't cut people off arbitrarily. You can't say, sorry, we're not going to have any restock of that for 10 years. That's not how you do that effectively.\par \par \uc2 And there's a lot of reasons for that. I think, one, generally I don't think the United States should be sending our men and women off to die for other people's countries unless it's a matter of a last resort. But -- but also I think politically the United States is out of will on this, right? After a couple decades of misadventures in the Middle East, I don't think we have it in us to send hundreds of thousands of people to go die for someone else's country, even if it is for a good cause.\par \par \uc2 I do worry about what you're talking about, about weapons being misused or sent to countries that are going to misuse them. But I don't think I'm the right person to be making the call on when that is or what that is.\par \par \uc2 Remember, I'm a corporate executive. I make weapons and I sell them for money. My opinion inherently should be somewhat disregarded as tainted. These decisions need to be made by elected politicians, elected officials, people who can be voted into office, and then critically can be removed from office by voters.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, you have no accountability to the electorate. People will often ask me, Palmer, would you make a decision that you should sell to some country without the United States telling you that you should? Or alternately, that you would refuse to sell to some country even if the United States tells you that it's of critical importance to national security or U.S. interests?\par \par \uc2 And the point that I make to them, usually these are more -- more left- leaning people, and I say, do you really want that kind of system? You want a corporatocracy where corporate executives have de facto control of U.S. foreign military affairs? Should I, as a profit-motivated entrepreneur, be able to decide who gets weapons and who doesn't? I don't think so. I think that the right approach is for people like me to make powerful tools and then leave the decision as to who the right people are to be armed with them up to leaders who, again, are accountable through an electable process.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, I have no delusions that there will not be mistakes made. I'm sure our elected leaders will make mistakes. I'm sure that every elected leader you can think of has made some mistake, at least, you know, by your definition. But again, at least we can hold them accountable. We can make sure that things never swing out of control. And if you have a better idea for a better system, I'm all ears, but I haven't heard one yet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and these are the conversations that are so important here at the Defense Forum. You've got elected officials, entrepreneurs like you and many --\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- and folks from the defense industry and everything in between. So, thanks for taking a break from all of that to be with us.\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: Of course.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LUCKEY: Of course.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, coming up, there are just a handful of days left before health care premiums could skyrocket for millions of Americans in January. We're going to talk to the chairman of the Senate Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions Committee, \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 . He's also a physician.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to get a behind-the-scenes look at America's decades-long love of the West and Westerns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here at the Reagan Library, there's currently an exhibit called "Cowboys: History & Hollywood." And one of the things you'll see only here is this buffalo hide coat that belonged to President Teddy Roosevelt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2 , OFFICE OF MANAGEMENT AND BUDGET DIRECTOR: I think our team is working with those lawmakers, and sometimes, you know, we lead very -- very loudly, and sometimes our leadership is behind the scenes to craft something where the votes might be there for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is from my conversation with White House OMB Director \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  here at the Reagan National Defense Forum.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , chairman of the Health Committee and a key player in these health care negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Senator, great to have you with us. I was talking with the director there about how involved the White House is or isn't in these health care discussions and negotiations. Can you give us some insight into that, whether the president may be interested in signing off an extension of the \uc2 ACA\uc2  subsidies or what other policy moves the White House might endorse?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): I think the White House Republicans are very much in favor of Americans having affordable health insurance. They want Americans to have access.\par \par \uc2 And the president's given pretty clear guidelines. He wants money to go to patients and not to insurance companies. And so the kind of marching orders I've taken from the president is, how do we offer our fellow Americans who are on the Obamacare exchanges and would be paying, in some cases, $33,000 for policies that have been set up by Democrats? An ability for them to have a lower premium and have -- and have money go to the patient, not to the insurance company, so that they have an account to help pay for their medical expenses.\par \par \uc2 They -- the president gave the -- the marching orders. We're working on it, want to deliver it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It will take time. Nobody is under the illusion I think that with, you know, 10 working days or so, maybe less, left for the Senate in this year that an overhaul of the healthcare system or a long-term solutions happen before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 So, will there be some portion of this that would extend the subsidies? And I know that you are looking at this in the context of money funneling into health savings accounts. But will an extension go on while you try to figure out the policy?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Shannon, you nailed it. You can't fix everything by January 1, 2026. You nailed it. And so you got to work with what you have.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's a political decision whether or not to continue the subsidies in some extent. But if we did continue those subsidies to a certain extent, you'd either be given a 100 percent of the money to the insurance companies, in which they take 20 percent of it for deductible and co-pay or -- so only 80 percent comes back to the patient, 20 percent deductible and co-pay, or you give 100 percent to the patient. That's what the president wants to do.\par \par \uc2 So, if we do continue the subsidies, you can put that over here, 100 percent go into the patient and put it into a health savings account. They get a cheaper premium and they get first dollar coverage.\par \par \uc2 They have money in their account to pay for their daughter's sprained ankle, off the bat. That's a better thing. Let's give the American people a choice. And -- and you're right. We got to do it by next year. We can do this by next year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I want to read something from "National Review" talking about this proposal that would extend some of these subsidies, although in a different way. And whether you can get the votes together for that, from both sides of the aisle. They write this. "Republicans who are open to Cassidy's plan may be hesitant to sign on to any proposal that would keep enhanced Obamacare subsidy funding at current levels. And even if enough GOP senators warm to the plan, winning over enough Democrats to clear the filibuster 60-vote threshold for passing legislation will be tricky."\par \par \uc2 Where are you on bipartisan support for this idea?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: So, I've been talking to Democrats. There's absolute interest. I'd like to say that some of them, you know, no American wants a fellow American to not to be able to afford health insurance.\par \par \uc2 Now we may disagree to the threshold. The original Obamacare put the limit of a percentage of your income, I think at like 9.8 percent or something like that. So -- so, we may argue about that.\par \par \uc2 But -- but if we can get to a framework where they give the American people a choice, they can stay with the policy they have with a $6,000 deductible or they can go to another policy with a lower premium and money in a health savings account for them to purchase that which they do, the rest is just political decisions.\par \par \uc2 What dial do you turn here, dial you turn there in order to get your votes? I think we can do this if people are of goodwill. I say, we don't want a Republican solution, a Democratic solution. We want an American solution that works for Americans. If my Democratic friends believe that, we can do it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it sounds like what they're going to offer up this week, at least from the minority leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . He says, there's going to be a clean three-year extension of the subsidies that will come up for a vote this week on the Senate floor. Here's what he says about the political choice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Republicans have only one week to decide where they stand. Are they going to vote for this bill and bring healthcare costs down for the American people or block this bill and send premium skyrocketing on January the 1st? That's what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, in this political environment, with him laying it out with that being the choice, can you get something substantive done?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: As always, Chuck is wrong. The choice he has given is a false choice. The choice he wants to give American people is a $6,000 deductible, a system which we send $26 billion to insurance companies and they take 20 percent, they take 20 percent of it for profit and overhead.\par \par \uc2 Now, give them that choice. OK. You want a policy with a $6,000 deductible? You got it. We want to give an additional choice, one in which your premium is lower and that you have money in an account they'll pay for it.\par \par \uc2 If he wants to give people that choice, I'm OK with that. But let's give them a side-by-side choice. Both in the exchanges, the -- the patient makes the decision, not \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . Let the patient make the decision. That's the way it should be. Let the American have the power. Let the patient have the power.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Do you anticipate a vote -- a vote on what you were proposing within the next week of vote before Christmas?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I sure hope so. We're working to deliver to speaker -- Leader Thune and Speaker Johnson a plan, which I think could get 60 votes, which gives the American people the power. And they can choose a lower premium and an HSA. We're working on that. And I'll give them a piece of paper probably by e-mail tonight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I want to make sure that I get to you too about a vaccine change that as somebody who's practiced medicine for decades, you have opinions on what's happening here.\par \par \uc2 And so what we got this week was a recommended change to current policy when it comes to Hepatitis B and that vaccination.\par \par \uc2 In the past, it was recommended for all babies born here in the U.S. But there's a recommendation from the \uc2 Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 Here's some of the reporting on it. Reuters says, "The committee voted to keep the birth dose only for infants of the less than one percent of mothers who test positive for the virus." That replaces the universal suggestion from the past.\par \par \uc2 Our colleagues over at foxnews.com reported this way, "The committee voted in favor of the recommendation finding parents should exercise individual- based decision making, in consultation with the doctor."\par \par \uc2 What about that idea of giving the parents the information, the decision when it comes to this particular vaccine?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: We already do. Let's give context. When this program started, this policy started, there are about 20,000 -- let me say this. Since this policy has started, we've gone from 20,000 cases of acute hepatitis B in a child, 95 percent of the time becoming chronic. I'll explain that. Down to 20 per year. Isn't that amazing, 20,000 to 20?\par \par \uc2 And so what was made in 1991 was a recommendation, not a mandate, in which you would offer the mother the ability for her child to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Now -- now if you do that, because -- because of those who are infected at birth, 30 or 40 percent of them, should be 95 percent of them become chronic carriers.\par \par \uc2 So, what does -- what does it mean to be a chronic carrier? It means that you're at risk for end-stage liver disease, for liver cancer, for infecting other people. But if you vaccinate at birth, that never happens.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you can go and say, well, let's check the mother and see if she's positive. And if she's not, then we don't need to vaccinate the baby right off the bat.\par \par \uc2 But -- but the vaccine is safe. It's effective. And the hospital works better with the system. If the system is that every mother is offered this vaccine, and every child is offered this vaccine, not a mandate, it's a recommendation, then the insurance company pays for it is recommended, and she can elect to have it.\par \par \uc2 By the way, it is safe. There's no -- there's no side effects, and it's effective. Let's give the mama the ability to give the gift to her child, a protection against hepatitis B. She may not know her hepatitis B status.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: And the doctors may not know. But if they're all offered, it works.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We got to leave it there. It's a recommendation at this point. So, we will check and see and track this policy for potential change.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cassidy, great to have you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Now for something you're only going to see here at the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library right now. It's all about America's love affair with Westerns and the real-life men and women who inspired them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here at the Reagan Library in Simi Valley, there is a special 11- foot bronze statue of our 40th president riding his very favorite horse. And by the way, tucked inside that horse is a piece of the Berlin Wall and one of the president's belt buckles.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the library is hosting an exhibit entitled "Cowboys: History and Hollywood." It compares the real-life history of cowboys with their silver screen interpretations. You could see the Lone Ranger's original mask and neck scarf. Roy Rogers' and Dale Evans' costumes along with their taxidermied horses, Trigger and Buttermilk.\par \par \uc2 President Reagan loved Westerns and starred in six of them himself, including the "Santa Fe Trail" and the "Cattle Queen of Montana."\par \par \uc2 There's Reagan's own Willy Jeep from his beloved Sky Ranch, known by its Spanish name, Rancho del Cielo.\par \par \uc2 And the centerpiece of the exhibit is a Robinson R22 helicopter, what modern cowboys used a roundup cattle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Coming up, our special West Coast panel joins us live to talk about the president's new push on his economic message and more from my conversation with OMB Director \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  at the Reagan National Defense Forum this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And later on, my stroll through a winter wonderland of Christmas trees representing 250 years of our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2 , DIRECTOR, OFFICE OF MANAGEMENT AND BUDGET: I think they're already seen relief. And I think that has -- has been evident, the extent to which, you know, we had a nine percent inflation for the first time in 40 years and president has gotten inflation under control. Gas prices are down.\par \par \uc2 And we are hard at work trying to make housing cheaper. I think you'll see ideas from our administration later on a host of fronts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OMB Director \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  on the economy leaning hard into the administration's push to reshape the narrative on affordability after a too-close-for-comfort special election in Tennessee this past week.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group. Fox News contributor Karl Rove. Jennifer Griffin, Fox News chief national security correspondent. And Ronald Reagan Institute director, Roger Zakheim. Thank you for being up extra early with us here on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 So, let's talk about the economy. Obviously, this has been front and center. "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " says this headline that talking about President Trump plotting a travel blitz to tout his economy. "White House aides see Trump as the Republican Party's best salesman. And its best chance to reverse falling poll numbers for both the president and his party."\par \par \uc2 Karl, how does he turn the message around?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, not to say that we have the world's greatest economy and everything is good. We saw this work badly for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when he said Bidenomics is working.\par \par \uc2 What the president needs to be doing is talking about the things that he's going to do in the future, acknowledging that -- that tough steps need to be taken in order to make the economy better for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 But -- but acknowledging what he's done, but putting the emphasis on what he's going to do. And he's got some great opportunities. We saw it recently the administration was wise, for example, to say, we're not going to put a tariff on coffee.\par \par \uc2 Hawaii doesn't generate enough coffee for the rest of the country. We -- we need to import from Brazil and Colombia and other places.\par \par \uc2 Next year, by the fall of next year, the president had better do something about the cost of tennis shoes and school clothes when kids go back to school by lowering the tariffs on the countries from which we buy those things like Vietnam and Myanmar and Bangladesh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, we reference the Tennessee election. It was a blowout win for Republicans by most standards, but that was somewhere President Trump had won by 22 points, so much closer than they would have liked it to be.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" reports on this top pollster for president, \uc2 Tony Fabrizio\uc2 , "He argued it was a closer race than Republicans would have liked because they weren't talking enough about affordability issues facing voters."\par \par \uc2 He told the group of these Republican Study Committee folks. He said, "You got to stay the course and talk about the realities of the economy." Roger.\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM, THE \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  INSTITUTE DIRECTOR: Well, I think the president's going to have to go back across the country in the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's drive-thrus right here and demonstrating that he understands that it's the cost of the Big Mac that really drives people's votes coming up for the midterm, it's going to be a fight. Historically, presidents don't do well in the midterm, but being out there in the country outside the White House talking to everyday Americans is something \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's quite good at, and I think that's the recipe for success here.\par \par \uc2 The geopolitics is also important, too. Here's what I'm talking about, you know, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  was critical of the president for going out and focusing on foreign affairs too much, but there's a strong nexus between the relationship of the United States and China, for example, and the allies around China that we have, and the American economy. President Trump understands that he needs to translate it for the American people, demonstrating that our strength is actually a key way to bring down the cost of goods, tariffs on our adversaries, not on our friends.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and China had been front and center in conversations here at the Defense Forum because you guys do a survey every year. Again, China was viewed as the top political threat or foreign policy threat, and you also asked this question, how -- how large do you think the U.S. military should be? Because you've got to counter all of these different threats. At the top of the list was large enough to win separate wars against China and Russia at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Jen, that's a very heavy lift for our conversation.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: It is a heavy lift, particularly with the current defense budget top line. As we've said, and some of your guests said, the current budget is half of what the Reagan defense spending was during the buildup.\par \par \uc2 But back to the economy, the significance here is that you're seeing a divide between the president and Speaker Johnson. That could have implications for passing a defense budget in January, which if we don't get a fiscal year 2026 defense budget, you're going to have a one possible continuing resolution, which makes it very difficult to plan for the future for the Pentagon. And -- and that's what we've been discussing here at the Reagan forum.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And Roger, one of the things I talked with Director Vought yesterday about was the shipbuilding issue. And I didn't realize until we started digging into this by the White House's own numbers, only 0.2 of ships that are built in this country, in the world, are built by the U.S. Seventy four percent of them are built by China. You guys asked about how much we need to focus on this, because China can produce more than 200 ships for every one that we're producing. But it takes a lot of money and long-term investment to turn that kind of number around.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: To follow up on Jen's point, you need to have sustained funding year over year and address the new priorities that are emerging year over year. Shipbuilding needs to have that discipline. You're not going to bring manufacturing back to the United States if you're stuck in a continuing resolution, flat budgets, half of where we were during the Reagan buildup. We're not going to get there.\par \par \uc2 We heard something from Jack Keane yesterday, General Jack Keane. You know, Americans think they want the greatest military in the world. Eighty seven percent of Americans say that's what they want. That's what we want to deliver a thousand miles outside the coast of China. We are not. We are not. They outdo us there. And that's why we need to invest and have these technologies. We can secure so much of what we require for political, military and economic security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, speaking of government funding, a big scandal out of Minnesota. This is at the state level. Karl, I'm coming to you because this is 86 individuals have been charged with defrauding Medicaid and the Federal Child Nutrition Program in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 So, feeding kids. These people were accused of doing all kinds of things, siphoning this money to all kinds of different places. Kim Strassel writes over at the "Wall Street Journal," she says the system itself, the government machine is broken. It's heaving duplicative federal state programs, awash in forms and bureaucracies and ancient mainframes has already suffered Soviet style collapse. This system serves more as a cash machine for criminals than a safety net for the needy. How did we get here? Can we turn it around?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, we better because this feeding the nation scandal, that was the name of the nonprofit that -- that siphoned off perhaps as much as a billion dollars, is emblematic of what we did. We threw money at COVID in such a way that a bunch of crooks could steal a billion dollars in Minnesota and ungodly amounts of money from the programs that we set up to support small businesses.\par \par \uc2 I had a personal involvement with this. I got a letter saying you need to repay your $20,000 dollar loan made to Rovin Company never took the money out. Somebody had gone in and -- and this is rife. You talk to the federal investigators, you're looking into these programs and God knows how much money was stolen.\par \par \uc2 And we had people in this state, California and state prisons getting money during the pandemic program. And we haven't recovered it. So, it's a lesson about big government when it starts handing out money, it starts doing it recklessly.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And another piece of the "Wall Street Journal" said it's true that Minnesota's generous welfare state has become a magnet for Somali immigrants. So, the problem isn't the migration issue, but that's part of the conversation. But Jen, the immigration issue is part of the national defense conversation here.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, it's very interesting. On the eve of the forum, the national security strategy was released at 10 p.m. at night by the White House. And a principal change in that strategy from eight years ago was a shift from great power competition and discussions of China and Russia's revisionist powers. It is now a shift to the Western Hemisphere. The focus is on migration. There are swipes taken at the Europeans for -- that they will face civilizational decline if they don't focus on migration. So, a real, really interesting new national security strategy that's going to change a lot of how the Pentagon and others do things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, it gives us the overview of where this administration is headed.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much. OK, up next, shooting for the stars. \uc2 NASA\uc2  Astronaut \uc2 Chris Williams\uc2 , who, by the way, has a link to our FOX family, shares how he's living a lifelong dream aboard the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WILLIAMS\uc2 , NASA ASTRONAUT: This has been something that I've dreamed about ever since I was a little kid and worked towards, you know, my -- my whole life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to introduce you to a rising star with a connection to our FOX family. \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut \uc2 Chris Williams\uc2  joined us from the International Space Station this week as he embarks on an eight-month mission. It's our "Sunday Special."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What are the emotions when you get the notification you are going into space?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And liftoff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Holiday travel underway.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yeah, it was a roller coaster of emotions. I mean, this has been something that I've dreamed about ever since I was a little kid and worked towards, you know, my -- my whole life. And -- and it's not something I ever thought I'd have the chance to do. We have this wonderful laboratory up here. And being -- you know, being entrusted to kind of help take care and push forward the science that we're doing at it is, yeah, it's a -- it's a real honor.\par \par \uc2 It was also pretty tough because I've got little kids. So, thinking about being gone for them -- gone away from them for eight months was -- was hard. But -- but, you know, knowing -- you know, knowing all the interesting stuff we're doing up here makes it -- makes it worthwhile.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about where you are, the International Space Station. Why is the work that you're doing there important?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I mean, the International Space Station has been operating continuously for over 25 years with people on it. There's some research that can only be done here, research that involves microgravity. We've been working on things like developing drugs, working on new ways of -- of -- of forming crystals that you can really only do in microgravity.\par \par \uc2 But it's also helping us get further on. This is a great test bed for a lot of the technologies that we need for -- going to the moon with the \uc2 Artemis\uc2  program, which we're embarking on right now, and also further on to Mars.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, how do you prepare for a mission of this import and this length? I mean, physically, mentally, professionally, what goes into getting ready?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's -- it's about a two-year process from when you get assigned to a mission to -- to when -- when you fly. And you do a lot of technical training, so learning all of the systems on the space station, how to fix them, and also a lot of the science experiments that you're going to be doing. We also do things like practice spacewalking for any repairs or upgrades or experiments we might be doing outside the space station.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There is an international framework and cooperation for what you're doing there. How important is that to the success of these missions?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's absolutely essential. I mean, this -- this -- this place would not work without the cooperation of everybody involved. But I'm actually floating here in the -- in the Japanese module, the JEM, on our station. And I came up here on a -- on a spaceship with -- with two cosmonauts launched from Kazakhstan.\par \par \uc2 This research is really for the good of all, for the good of all humanity. And so, the fact that we can, you know, have our disagreements on Earth but still collaborate here to -- to work together for the benefit of all I think is really, really powerful.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm curious, and I always wonder this, how do you guys keep a schedule? How do you sleep?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We have actually a little scheduling app that kind of has our tasks for the day. But wake up at around, you know, get to work starting at around 7:30 in the morning. Then it's pretty important for our bodies to exercise.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a couple hours of exercise every day. But basically, we have a schedule where our kind of day is broken down into sort of 15-minute chunks where we have tasks that we -- that we do -- and then at the end of the day, we actually get a little bit of downtime to call our families, to call home, read a book, something like that, and -- and go to sleep.\par \par \uc2 And one thing I'll say, you know, having been up here for -- for still less than a week, boy, you sleep, at least I sleep extremely comfortably up here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Your uncle Juan wrote such a moving piece in the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " just about what this means to not only your family, but to our country, to the world, the work that you're doing, the sacrifice that you're making. But he talks about you being a little kid in your onesie pajamas with rockets and -- and planes and outer space on them.\par \par \uc2 So, this obviously is a dream. You talked about working your whole life to get to this point. What's your advice to a young person who has the same kinds of dreams that you do about pushing these boundaries and going to new places?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: For me, the path I took in life, I was always fascinated by space and exploration. And I think I realized by the time I was about 11 years old that actually being an astronaut wasn't -- you know, wasn't actually an attainable dream. But the spark of the idea of space exploration always stuck with me.\par \par \uc2 And it stuck with me -- and it inspired me to pursue a lot of the things that I -- that I did. I -- I ended up studying physics and becoming -- becoming an astronomer and then working in medicine. You know, nobody in my family before me had ever done -- done anything like this. But having that dream and that spark, I think, opens up doors that -- that are there for you, but you might not realize.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, thank you for giving us some perspective, taking time out of your mission there. We are going to stay in contact with what you are doing over the months to come.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I really appreciate you giving me the opportunity to -- to chat with you and your viewers. It's -- it's -- yeah, so thanks very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: An out-of-this-world interview. All the best to Chris. And no one ever exits an interview better than that. I'll just leave it there.\par \par \uc2 All right, as we wrap up our time here, we want to spread a little holiday cheer with a look at a special exhibit at the Library that weaves symbols of America's rich past into this year's Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Library has 26 dazzling Christmas tree displays this year, representing decades of the nation's history, from the Revolution to today. There's Betsy Ross embroidering the first American flag. Copies of the Declaration of Independence, Constitution, and Bill of Rights. The 1900s tree has the Wright Brothers' model plane and the Ford Model T.\par \par \uc2 The 1940s tree commemorates Pearl Harbor, which happened 84 years ago today. The 1970s tree includes eight track tapes and the shark from Jaws. And the 1980s tree celebrates \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , along with the Rubik's Cube and personal computers.\par \par \uc2 And finally, there's a separate special tree at the Library that is a tradition every year, honoring Gold Star families.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 2026, A.I. --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's it for us today. Thank you for joining us from the Reagan Presidential Library. I'm Shannon Bream. Enjoy your week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020251207elc700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Speaker Johnson on Military Strikes Against Alleged Drug Boats; Speaker Johnson on Delay Seating Congresswoman-Elect Adelita Grijalva; Fallout From Trump-Xi Meeting; Supreme Court to Hear Arguments on Trump Tariffs; NJ Gubernatorial Candidates make Final Pitch To Voters In Tightening Race; Future Of Democratic Party; Future of Democratic Party; House GOP Releases Report on Biden Autopen Use; Trump Gives Nigeria New Designation\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Rich Edson, Mark Meredith, C.B. Cotton, Juan  Williams, Guy Benson\\, Alexandria Hoff \par \uc2 9,662 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 State of the race, the Trump agenda gets its first temperature check from voters in critical off-year elections, and a renewed sense of urgency grips Washington as the shutdown hits a new milestone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD), MAJORITY LEADER: Do Democrats have any end date in sight?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: Republicans have spent the entire shutdown with their heads in the sand.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): America's patience grows thin one month in as air traffic control shortages spark ground delays coast to coast and funding lapses spark uncertainty for food aid recipients.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: SNAP should not be about politics, but we got to feed the people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And millions of Americans began open Obamacare enrollment this weekend with premiums spiking as COVID-era subsidies end. All as President Trump calls on Republicans to take bold action.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We'd like to see this taken care of rapidly. It's common sense. Open up the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: House Speaker Mike Johnson reacts, only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Frankly, I did a great service to the world, but because of tariffs, because of trade.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A make-or-break week for trade. The president's sweeping tariffs set to face Supreme Court scrutiny as he touts progress in easing America's trade war with China.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: On the scale of from zero to 10, with 10 being the best, I would say the meeting was a 12.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent shares his insights on the stakes for the future of trade relations.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKIE SHERRILL\uc2  (D-NJ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: New Jersey is not a red state. New Jersey is not a purple state. New Jersey is a blue state.\par \par \uc2 JACK CIATTARELLI (R), NEW JERSEY GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Our internal polling in `21 said that we had a real shot. This time, the internal polling says a whole lot better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A surprise nailbiter in the Garden State. Trump-backed \uc2 Jack Ciattarelli\uc2  narrows the gap on Democrat \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  as Dems roll out party star power to shore up the most critical races. And Republicans battle to hang on to the governor's mansion in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 All right now, on "FOX News Sunday", Democracy 2025.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 With the end of Daylight Saving Time, for most of you, we're hoping your clocks are up to date.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of today's top stories.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 L.A. Dodgers\uc2  celebrating their historic game seven World Series win against the \uc2 Toronto Blue Jays\uc2  last night in Canada, becoming the first team in 25 years to win the series in back-to-back years. The Dodgers won five to four in 11 innings.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Massachusetts authorities are searching for two suspects caught on security cameras after a, quote, "intentional explosion" early Saturday morning in a \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2  building. No one was injured in the blast.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., authorities say two men have been arrested in a knife attack near Cambridge that's left at least 10 people injured, some with critical injuries. Investigators say, quote, "There's nothing to suggest that this is a terrorist incident." Though counterterrorism police were initially looking into what happened.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is threatening to use the U.S. military in Nigeria with, quote, "guns ablazing" if the government there does not crack down on the persecution of Christians by, quote, "Islamic terrorists". In a Truth Social post Saturday, the president says he'll also immediately stop all aid and assistance to the West African nation if they do not act, quote, "fast".\par \par \uc2 All this comes as we enter day 33 of the government shutdown, which could become the longest shutdown ever this week.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will bring in House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 But first, Alex Hoff joins us from the White House where on top of everything else, we're learning about another us strike on an alleged Venezuelan drug smuggling vessel.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, this latest strike was carried out in the Caribbean Sea yesterday. Three men aboard a small vessel were killed. The administration has labeled these men as narco terrorists and says that indicate -- that intelligence indicated that this group was intending to bring illicit drugs to our shores.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  sharing this video along with the announcement of what marks the U.S.'s 15th known strike on suspected drug smugglers at sea. The post includes a promise, quote, "The department will treat them exactly how we treated al-Qaeda. We will continue to track them, map them, hunt them, and kill them."\par \par \uc2 Democrats are seeking more transparency on such actions.\par \par \uc2 The White House legal counsel says that Congress's War Powers do not apply. This comes as President Trump on his way to Florida this weekend denied reports that he had made a decision to strike military sites inside of Venezuela, calling those reports false.\par \par \uc2 Domestically, the president says his administration intends to comply with a federal judge's Friday order to tap into emergency reserve funds to temporarily fund the SNAP program.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm president, I want to help everybody.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): He wrote on Truth Social, "It will be my honor to provide the funding just like I did with military and law enforcement pay."\par \par \uc2 But the president first wants legal clarity. His administration had argued that the funds tied to an inactive program can't be released.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say the law is clear.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: The courts have been clear and the morality of it is clear. The Trump administration needs to release these benefits.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE ROLLINS\uc2 , AGRICULTURE SECRETARY: It's called a contingency fund. And by law, contingency fund can only flow when the underlying fund is flowing.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Nonprofits around the country are attempting to fill the government shutdown void. In Houston this weekend, NRG Stadium became host to a major food distribution event.\par \par \uc2 President Trump maintains Senate Democrats could easily resolve the shutdown by approving the GOP-backed clean funding bill.\par \par \uc2 With related staffing issues impacting travel, the nation's largest airlines agree.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 , \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2  CEO: Let's get a clean C.R. and get that negotiation done behind closed doors without the pressure and without putting the American workers and the American economy at risk.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): The bill lacks the 60 votes needed to pass under the Senate filibuster rule, a rule President Trump is pushing hesitant Republicans to get rid of. He calls it the "nuclear option", which is separate from the nuclear testing the president has ordered to resume making the announcement on Truth Social.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Could we just actually do foreign policy some way other than by Truth Social posts?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I know exactly what we're doing, where we're doing it, but other countries are doing it. If other countries do it, we do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Now, back to SNAP or the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, the Trump administration has until tomorrow to explain to the federal judge how exactly the administration is going to be making payments to approximately 42 million Americans who rely on the program -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Alexandria Hoff, from the White House -- Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now, House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Glad to be with you, but it's a frustrating day for sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, what are we? Day 33 now. And there are real world things that are kicking in this weekend, impact to real Americans that have been -- some -- many have been experiencing missed paychecks, those kinds of things. A broader impact this weekend.\par \par \uc2 I want to start here. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says this, "Americans blame Trump and GOP more than Democrats for shutdown", citing their poll. "Independents hold Trump and Republicans responsible for the shutdown by a two to one margin," that's according to this new poll.\par \par \uc2 So, more Americans still think you are to blame than Democrats are. What's your message to them?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, first, I take issue with that. I mean, just 24 hours before that one came out, CNN, of all places, had a big story about how Republicans are winning the shutdown battle.\par \par \uc2 But we reject all of this. This is not about winning a political contest here. Real people are losing. The American people are hurting.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have voted 14 times now, Shannon, to keep the government closed. Fourteen times they had the opportunity to have SNAP benefits float, to let air traffic controllers be paid, to take care of all these problems, and pay the troops. And they said no.\par \par \uc2 And it is a fact that no one can dispute that they shut the government down over restoring free healthcare to illegal aliens as part of their $1.5 trillion wild partisan wish list. They're playing games and real people are suffering because of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But with these numbers in mind, why do you think they will be willing to come back to the table and negotiate at some point? They think that they're winning the optics of this.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I don't know how they can believe they're winning. If it were indeed a game to them, and I think it is, because you have all the unions now coming out against them. You have the major airlines in the country.\par \par \uc2 You have every segment of the population and hardworking Americans who will be going without -- you have 42 million recipients of SNAP that are in jeopardy right now. You have women, infants, and children because the nutrition program that President Trump and the White House have heroically funded thus far is running out of money. And, of course, the troops. I mean, we've gotten them paid now for two cycles, but that money is not inexhaustible, and it's running out.\par \par \uc2 And so increasingly, the American people will be feeling the pain. And after we pass that threshold of the first of this month, it gets very real for more and more people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, the president says it's about getting rid of the filibuster, not something you have to deal with on the House side. But he's putting pressure on your Senate colleagues, saying, get rid of the 60-vote threshold. You can move most things by a majority vote in the House.\par \par \uc2 Here's part of one of his recent posts. He says, "Remember, Republicans, regardless of the Schumer shutdown, the Democrats will terminate the filibuster the first chance they get. They'll pack the Supreme Court, pick up two states, and add at least eight electoral votes. Don't be weak and stupid. Republicans, you will rue the day that you didn't terminate the filibuster. Be tough, be smart, and win."\par \par \uc2 There are some GOP senators indicating at least the tiniest open door to possibly making changes.\par \par \uc2 What's your advice to the other side of the Hill?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I think there's a lot of Democrats -- I mean, a lot of senators, Republican and Democrat, who have resisted it in the past. We, on our side, traditionally have resisted that because the worst impulses of the far-left Democrat Party. They would pack the court. They would do all the things the president has discussed there.\par \par \uc2 I think what you're seeing there is a reflection of his anger, his real frustration that the government is closed. He is a big-hearted president. He wants everybody to get their services, and the -- and the health services for veterans and SNAP benefits and all the rest.\par \par \uc2 And he's tried everything he can. He -- and he is now exhausting his ability. The courts are now saying you can't go any further.\par \par \uc2 So, he's just desperate for the government to open. He's tried everything he can. He's tried to negotiate with them. He's -- he pleaded with them, and they still dug their heels in.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a reflection of all of our desperation. We're angry about it. I think we should be.\par \par \uc2 The filibuster has traditionally been a safeguard against those worst impulses, but we'll see what the Senate does.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, of course, your counterparts, Democrats, do not think he's big-hearted or trying to help. They think he's playing -- using people as pawns that are desperate and in need of government aid and paychecks and those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 He -- it sounds like the president is willing to try to find a way to get these SNAP benefits paid out after two federal courts on Friday said, "You got to do it."\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " says this -- they say, "Trump kept SNAP funded during the 2018, 2019 shutdown by using contingency funds. Representative Salud Carbajal, a member of the House Committee on Ag, told reporters on Wednesday, 'When it suits them, they have all the funds in the world. When it doesn't, of course, there's nothing.'"\par \par \uc2 So even if the administration figures this out, there's going to be some sort of delay. Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  says Republicans are using seniors and vulnerable. They're weaponizing hunger.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's absolutely absurd. They are accusing the Republicans and the president of doing exactly what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 Remember, don't get lost in the facts. Republicans have voted 14 times to open the government. Democrats have voted 14 times to close it. Who's playing games here?\par \par \uc2 We have a clean, simple C.R., a continuing resolution. No partisan gimmicks, tricks at all. It's 24 pages in length. It would keep everything going.\par \par \uc2 The president is bending over backwards to take care of the American people. And it's the Democrats who are quite literally using the people as leverage.\par \par \uc2 I can quote you senators after House member on the Democrat side in the last two weeks who have said the quiet parts out loud, from Whitehouse to Coons to Katherine Clark, the number two Democrat in the House, they've all said that. \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , they've all said this is their leverage.\par \par \uc2 Their leverage for what? So, they can show that they're fighting President Trump to the far left because they're worried about the Marxist rise in the Democrat Party? We see it in New York City and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 And because they want -- they actually want and they've argued and they put on paper, they want illegal aliens to receive taxpayer funded benefits again. That is a fact. They've never changed it. They've never pulled back from that.\par \par \uc2 Everybody can see the stark contrast between two parties right now. And one is fighting for the people, and that's a Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, another thing that happened this weekend is open enrollment began for Obamacare. People are seeing a change in their premiums essentially if these subsidies that are set to expire at the end of the year are not changed, renewed, handled in some way.\par \par \uc2 Last time you were here, we talked about Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who had some real frustrations about this. Where is the Republican plan? I know you two had talked.\par \par \uc2 Well, she posted this on X this week. She says, "Johnson said he's got ideas and pages of policy ideas and committees of jurisdiction are working on it, but he refused to give one policy proposal to our GOP conference on our own conference call. Apparently, I have to go into a SCIF," referencing a protected intel sharing type place, "to find out the Republican healthcare plan." And there are a lot of exclamation points there.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah, I don't know what to say about that. I mean, that's absurd.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we're not going to be on a conference call explaining all of our plans and strategies for healthcare reform because they're leaked in real time. Literally, when I have a conference call with all my members, it's tweeted out by journalists. They're supposed to be private, but they're not.\par \par \uc2 And so, Marjorie knows that. She knows she can come into my office any day at any hour, and I'll lay out everything for her.\par \par \uc2 The committees of jurisdiction, the people who specialize in all of this, have been working on this around the clock for a long time. In fact, we published 60 or 70 pages of healthcare reform ideas.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, when I was the chair of the Republican Study Committee, the largest caucus in Congress, our ideas have been out there for a long time. We're formulating the final plan that will bring down premiums. There's only one party in America that is going to do that.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the Democrats are the ones that created the system that is failing us. They created Obamacare. They said it was going to bring down costs. It's done exactly the opposite. Premiums, by some estimates, are up 60 percent since Obamacare was created in 2010.\par \par \uc2 So, it's the Republican Party that is going to bring down the cost, not the Democrats. They've already proven what they're about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. I want to move to a couple of other issues. One, we've been told overnight there was another strike on a drug running -- alleged drug running boat. There were briefings this week and there were folks who were -- listen, on the Senate side, Democrats weren't even included, but there was a bipartisan briefing on the House side.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the top Democrat on the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Congressman \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2  said. He said, "The briefing was incredible; incredible for how little information was shared, how little time the briefers stayed to answer questions, and how completely absent any credible legal rationale was for the administration's unauthorized, ongoing expansion of these strikes."\par \par \uc2 There are reports the Office of Legal Counsel told lawmakers the administration is not bound by the War Powers resolution that requires congressional approval for this kind of action.\par \par \uc2 You're a constitutional lawyer. Do you think the administration is on solid legal ground with these strikes?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah, a couple of points. We had a gang of eight briefing with the Secretary of State himself about a week and a half ago, and you had Republicans and Democrats there, the top leaders on both sides in intelligence and in elected leadership. We have exquisite intelligence about these strikes, and I can't get into the classified parts, but that information is well known and is very reliable. And what the president and the administration are doing is protecting the homeland. That's one of his number one responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 I like what Secretary Hegseth said at the opening, you had his quote, "narco-terrorists" will be treated like Al-Qaeda. We have to do that. They're bringing in huge amounts, tons of illegal narcotics into the country, and it is killing Americans.\par \par \uc2 Remember, fentanyl alone was a leading cause of death for Americans age 18 to 49 in the four years of the Biden administration. We finally have a president who is strong enough to stop that and to protect the people, and one of these boats, Shannon, could kill potentially tens of thousands of Americans. This is serious business. They're taking it seriously, and Congress will continue to have oversight. We'll have more of these hearings, but thus far, the intelligence that we have is -- is very reliable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you're convinced, even if your Democratic counterparts are skeptical, you're convinced at this point?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I do know that my Democrat counterparts would come out of any hearing with the administration and say that it was inadequate, right? I mean, it's politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to ask you, too, about not yet seated representative elect Adelita Grijalva. There's now a lawsuit about this. They say there's no dispute about her election or her qualifications, and add this, "Speaker Johnson wishes to delay seating Ms. Grijalva to prevent her from signing a discharge petition that would force a vote on the release of the Epstein files and or to strengthen his hand in the ongoing budget and appropriations negotiations. Why hasn't she been sworn in? What's your response to the lawsuit?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Oh, this is so ridiculous, and we could take 30 minutes and talk about all this, but Grijalva -- Rep. Adelita Grijalva will be sworn in as soon as we get back to regular session. Remember, it's the Democrats who shut the Congress down, so we can't do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Can you not swear her in, though, when you're not operating in regular?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: There is a precedent for doing a pro forma session swearing in, but we're not engaging in regular business right now, and what I'm doing is following the Pelosi precedent. In the past, when she was speaker and you had representatives elected during special elections, special situations, when Congress was on recess or out, she would wait until the regular session to do it. I'm doing the exact same thing, but the Epstein thing is a complete red herring. It's a distraction from what's going on right now, and them causing harm to the American people with the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The Epstein estate documents are out. The Epstein files are out. 43,000 pages so far, more on the way. Subpoenas are being complied with. The House Oversight Committee, which is filled with some of the biggest guard dogs in Congress on the right and the left, Republican and Democrat, are dug in deeply.\par \par \uc2 In the last two weeks, while we've been on shutdown, they've released the flight logs, the personal financial ledgers, the daily calendars of Epstein himself. The lawyers are continuing to dig through and release all that. We're for maximum transparency, always have been. They're using this for a political issue, and it's patently obvious what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we wish you progress this week in getting this thing wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mr. Speaker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, critical week for the president's trade agenda as the legal fight over tariffs heads to the Supreme Court. A huge case Wednesday. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent joins us to preview the case and to recap what did and didn't get done with China.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Democrats deploying the party's heaviest hitter as Republicans get too close for comforts. In off-year races, will it be enough to take Dems across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (D), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: There is absolutely no effort. Republican policies have made life better for you, the people of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: It appears to most reasonable observers that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was punked on the world stage by the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  slamming the president over his negotiations with China.\par \par \uc2 So, what did and didn't get accomplished when Presidents Trump and Xi meet face to face?\par \par \uc2 Well, Secretary Scott Bessent was there. Now, he's here and joins us with details.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back, Mr. Secretary.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: Thanks, Shannon. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you make of his characterization of what happened in China?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Well, I think it's ignorant, and I think it's kind of sad he has no idea how ignorant he is because I was in the room, and I can say I was there for the president's entire trip and it was a tour de force.\par \par \uc2 President went into Malaysia, signed trade deals, peace deals with Thailand and Cambodia that everyone in the Asia region wants to be partners with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 We moved on to Japan. President and the new prime minister of Japan had great chemistry.\par \par \uc2 And then we went into Korea, and the meeting with President Xi was one of great respect and many accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, so let's talk through some of those.\par \par \uc2 First of all, there was this understanding or idea that the administration was hoping China's economy, which has been struggling, would be in a worse position even than it is.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " writing this, "China's economy has proved more resilient than Trump officials anticipated. Chinese negotiators also demonstrated a willingness to hit back that seemed to surprise members of the administration, including Bessent, who visibly bristled at Beijing's retaliation."\par \par \uc2 What do they make -- what do you make of their characterization of you?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Highly inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 And look, we saw that the Chinese manufacturing has been down for seven months in a row. So, let's do the math. The Liberation Day, April 2nd. Where are we today? November 2nd, seven months. Chinese manufacturing's been down every month.\par \par \uc2 What they've done is the U.S. put up a tariff wall, and they have sent their goods to the rest of the world. Their trade with Europe is up 10 percent but it's all discounted. They've depreciated their currency.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the Chinese economy, they -- they're in the middle of a real estate crisis. They're trying to export their way out of it. And I think what we're going to see is the rest of the world is going to join us in the tariff wall against China. The E.U. recently put up 50 percent tariffs. So, no, I didn't bristle at all.\par \par \uc2 What I was surprised was that the Chinese on October 8th tried to put in rare earth controls on the entire world and I said that it was a mistake because it was China versus the world and President Trump was the leader of the free world and we had those controls postponed for a year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about that. It's a year postponement. Do we have the ability to scale up what we need within that year in case a year from now, there are tough negotiations and China decides to threaten to cut us off again?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Well, couple of things, Shannon. One was President Trump threatened to put 100 percent additional tariffs on China which gave me and our team great negotiating leverage in Kuala Lumpur before the president's meeting in Korea. So, we had maximum leverage against the Chinese. And you know, we could threaten the tariffs again and we have plenty of things that we can hold back to.\par \par \uc2 Over the next 12 and 24 months, we're going to be moving at warp speed. We didn't get here in a year. This is a problem that has been with us for several decades. It's never been addressed with these rare earth, the rare earth magnets. The Chinese have cornered the market and unfortunately, at times, they've proved to be unreliable partners.\par \par \uc2 I think and I hope after the agreement and the goodwill between President Xi and President Trump in Korea that we can depend on them to be more reliable partners, and the rest of the world can depend on them. But you know, we can't take that chance. We don't want to decouple with China but we're going to have to derisk.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, do we trust them on the issue of fentanyl? Because there have been promises in the past about their production or control of the production of the precursors of fentanyl. I understand there's some agreement this time around. What are the teeth? Is it enforceable?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: So, President Trump in the spring put 20 percent tariffs on the -- excuse me -- on China, on fentanyl. We took half of them off as a show of good faith. The Chinese have said that they will work on stopping the flow of precursors to North America that flow into Canada into Mexico where they're made into fentanyl, killing hundreds of thousands of U.S. citizens, ruining families, ruining lives.\par \par \uc2 And what we're going to do is we're going to set up a very strict quantitative criteria and we'll revisit it in six or 12 months to see whether they've accomplished it. And my sense is the tariffs could go up or down.\par \par \uc2 I hope that the Chinese, for the sake of the American people, will live up to this, and we could see a dramatic drop in fentanyl deaths.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. To the point of tariffs, a big test at the Supreme Court this week. A case on Wednesday will be argued about what is the bulk of the president's tariffs so far.\par \par \uc2 One of the amicus briefs or front of the court briefs that was filed says this. The president's exercise of power here is either outside the scope of the power granted by Congress through IEEPA and that's the emergency powers law that the president used, or that the law is an unconstitutional delegation of power reserved exclusively to the legislative branch.\par \par \uc2 So, they argue either the president doesn't have the power to do it or if Congress gave him the power, that was an unconstitutional delegation. You've lost in the lower courts so far. How do you game out Tuesday's or Wednesday's argument?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: I'm -- I'm quite optimistic and, Shannon, what I -- what I can tell you is that because of what the Chinese did on October 8th of threatening to put export controls on any rare earth products that they produce that had 0.01 percent of rare earth produced in China, that they were going to try to control those and the president was able to push back using his IEEPA powers. If that's not use of an emergency power, the -- at an emergency time, I don't know what is.\par \par \uc2 And then we go back to the spring, this terrible fentanyl crisis. You know, it's an emergency for what's going on. And we got a solution. The Chinese, thanks to the tariffs, came to the table. So, I think just those two alone have shown us that the president has used it responsibly.\par \par \uc2 And then on the third part with the reciprocal tariffs, you know, there's something called a tipping point and these trade deficits that we've had for years, you know, 20, 30 years, especially since the China shock in the early 2000s, we were close to a breaking point and the president did not want us to get there.\par \par \uc2 You know, imagine if someone had held their hand up in 2006, 2007, and said, "We're on the verge of a housing crisis." Well, President Trump raised his hand and said, "We are on the verge of a balance of payments crisis, a trade crisis, and I'm going to use IEEPA." That is what emergency powers are for.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. If you lose, something you have to consider, "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 " reports this. Should that happen, the government could owe the bulk of the $165 billion in customs duties collected so far this year, this fiscal year, back to the companies that paid them. So, what is the plan if that happens?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Well, again, I think the "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 " analysis is wrong, and we'll cross that bridge when we come to it because many of the tariffs are also collected under 232s and Section 301s, which have nothing to do with IEEPA.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There are other tariff levers the president can use.\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, quickly, the president said he's got an apology from the Canadian prime minister about an anti-tariff ad that ran there using some old footage of President Reagan, but he says the trade talks are not back on.\par \par \uc2 How do they get back on with Canada?\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: You know, we'll -- we'll see. I don't -- I don't know about you, Shannon, but many of my worst fights have been with family members. We eventually make up, but this was very serious.\par \par \uc2 This prime minister of Ontario, who I imagine is smarting quite a bit from the \uc2 Blue Jays\uc2 ' loss last night --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: -- spent, I -- I heard, $75 million broadcasting across the U.S. border trying to sway public opinion.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we're upset about what happens during foreign interference in our elections, this is foreign interference in U.S. public policy. And I -- I agree with President Trump 100 percent, a price has got to be paid for this.\par \par \uc2 So, overtime, we'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll watch to see if they kiss and make up as family members often do.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Secretary. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BESSENT: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. All eyes are now in those key off-year races Tuesday. Could they provide an early preview of next year's midterms? We're going to take you live to our teams on the ground across those critical bellwethers, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: With just two days until the final votes are cast, our teams are on the ground ahead of Tuesday's critical off-year races.\par \par \uc2 We kick things off with Rich Edson in Norfolk, Virginia. Hey, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon. Both candidates for Virginia governor are bringing out the big names in the closing days of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  joined Democrat \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  at a rally here in Norfolk. Like Spanberger, the former president criticized the current administration over DOGE layoffs, healthcare cuts, and tariffs and the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The economy has been really good for some of Trump's billionaire pals and for finance bros.\par \par \uc2 But for ordinary families, costs haven't gone down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Addressing the crowd here ahead of \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Spanberger, democratic attorney general candidate Jay Jones, whose violent texts from 2022 upended Virginia politics.\par \par \uc2 Spanberger has condemned the text, but refused to call on Jones to drop out of the race, a point that Republicans are hammering.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R-VA): This was a great representation of the far left's embracing of violence. And I can't believe that Jay Jones was there. But you know what? \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 's for him, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's for him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  is out campaigning for Lieutenant Governor Winsome Earle-Sears to succeed him. Polls have generally shown Earle-Sears trailing Spanberger, though a tight attorney general race following news of Jones' texts.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Youngkin and President Trump held a tele-rally for the Virginia GOP ticket. Earle-Sears says Spanberger is no moderate and extreme on issues like green energy and trans students using the bathrooms of their choice.\par \par \uc2 Six weeks of early voting just concluded in Virginia yesterday with early ballot returns exceeding where they were four years ago for the last governor's race in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Now over to Hudson County, New Jersey, and my friend and colleague, Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Rich, good morning to you. Today is the last day of early voting here in New Jersey, so we're seeing both the democratic and Republican gubernatorial candidates do all they can to get out the last minute vote in a race many consider to be a potential bellwether ahead of next November's midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Fox polling of this race shows that the Democrat, likely voters, \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2 , leading Republican candidate \uc2 Jack Ciattarelli\uc2  by seven points.\par \par \uc2 Polling also showing a huge gender gap in this race with Sherrill leading by double digits among likely female voters.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Sherrill held a rally in Newark. The headliner, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , as Democrats here hope voters will send Washington a message about how people are feeling about the president's second term.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKIE SHERRILL\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NEW JERSEY GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: My fight doesn't and can't end at the border of New Jersey. We've got to take on all those hits coming from Trump and Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Because right now, the president is running a worldwide extortion racket.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The White House also closely watching what's happening in New Jersey. Fox learning, the president's political operation spending a lot of money late in this race to help Republicans. Ciattarelli was the GOP nominee four years ago. While he lost, he came somewhat close, losing by just roughly three points. This time, he believes he's got the momentum.\par \par \uc2 JACK CIATTARELLI, REPUBLICAN NEW JERSEY GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I feel great. We have 21 to compare it to. I really thought I was going to win that race. You know how close we came. This one feels very different.\par \par \uc2 Energy up and down the state is electric. The reception in the minority communities across the state has been overwhelming and positive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Both campaigns are spending a lot of time and energy talking about the cost of living. Energy costs here in the Garden State have skyrocketed just this year. As one expert put it, electricity is the new eggs.\par \par \uc2 Cost of living, of course, another major issue just across the Hudson and New York City for the upcoming mayoral race. Fox's C.B. Cotton is live in Manhattan with the latest there. C.B.\par \par \uc2 C.B. COTTON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Mark. Well, not only has former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  hit the trail in New Jersey and Virginia this weekend, he also appears to be getting on board with the idea of a shifting Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " was the first to report \uc2 Obama\uc2 's phone call to New York City democratic mayoral candidate, Zohran Mamdani.\par \par \uc2 The outlet says \uc2 Obama\uc2  praised Mamdani's campaign and offered to be a sounding board in the future. Mamdani's campaign spokesperson telling Fox, quote, "Zohran Mamdani appreciated President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's words of support and their conversation on the importance of bringing a new kind of politics to our city."\par \par \uc2 And the last weekend before Election Day, latest Fox News poll shows while Mamdani's lead has narrowed, he's still up double digits over former governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , running as an independent, and Republican candidate, Curtis Sliwa.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Mamdani courted black voters and appeared with civil rights leader, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 . The 34-year-old who was campaigned on free buses, free childcare and city run grocery stores gave a speech accusing President Trump of gutting the SNAP program, Medicare and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Cuomo spent part of yesterday with outgoing mayor, \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , who dropped out in September with low poll numbers. Cuomo thinks historic voter turnout will help him seal the deal because he says New Yorkers are scared of Mamdani's policies.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, he's tried to make inroads with conservatives, and he's urged Sliwa to exit the race, calling him a spoiler.\par \par \uc2 But Sliwa says he's not going anywhere. And he believes Republicans and those tired of city elitism are going to stick with him.\par \par \uc2 According to the city's board of elections, more than half a million ballots have been cast over eight days of in-person early voting here in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shannon, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. C.B. Cotton on the ground in New York City. Thank you very much, C.B.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group, Francesca Chambers, "USA Today" White House correspondent. The Ronald Reagan Institute director, Roger Ronald Reagan Institute director, Roger Zakheim. Juan Williams, Fox News senior political analyst. And Guy Benson, Fox News contributor and host of The Guy Benson Show. Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 You heard C.B. talking about the shift potentially within the Democrat Party. Here's something interesting Gallup has got out there. When you ask Democrats what they view positively and knowing what Mamdani's policy plat -- platforms are, 66 percent of them have a positive view of socialism. Just 42 percent have a positive view of capitalism, Guy.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. This is a party that is moving dramatically to the left. All the energy in 