s really not affecting my life. I don't like it, or I don't like the fact that they haven't been a stronger opposition to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But I think the key here is that the gravitational force in all American politics to this moment is President Trump. And what you see is that people want opposition to Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, Democratic Party doesn't rank very well when you ask about them. But right now, like example in this morning's "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," you see that the approval rating for President Trump is pretty low. I think he's down around 41 percent, 59 percent disapproval. Gallup has the same numbers.\par \par \uc2 And with that kind of action and the shutdown that Guy was talking about, and the judge's ruling on SNAP benefits, the fact that the president's been out of town, that the House has been shut down, I think people feel like the narrative is, what's going on with the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Roger, you're shaking your head though.\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM, DIRECTOR, THE \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  INSTITUTE: I think the narrative is, what's going on with the Democratic Party? The gubernatorial races are a sideshow. It's fundamentally about what the soul of Democratic Party is, and that's around Mamdani and how he will fare.\par \par \uc2 It's about what Reagan called the cult of overwhelming government. That's what he advances, this mainstream socialism. And that's just on the discussion about government and the role of government in people's lives as an assault on freedom.\par \par \uc2 We haven't gone to the fact that Mamdani talks about globalizing Intifada as some sort of rallying cry for human rights.\par \par \uc2 This is so far-left. It's shocking. It could happen in one of America's greatest cities, where 10 percent roughly of the population are Jews. I mean, it is off, and the soul of the party is at risk with this man's election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Francesca, what do you make of the fact that they -- you referenced President \uc2 Obama\uc2  now getting connected with Mamdani in a way that he's saying, you know, he looks forward to him and talking with him about policy and future kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the races that are playing out right now are full of potential 2028 contenders. They're out there. President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is out there. What is the direction of the Democratic Party when it comes to these candidates that are not exactly centrist?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : And I wrote about this over the weekend with all those 2028 hopefuls out on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 You know, there is a realization taking place in the Democratic Party right now that what would win in a state like Virginia, when you're talking about moderate Democrats, is not necessarily the same message that's either going to win nationally or in a race like the one in New York City.\par \par \uc2 What's interesting about New York City race, though, is that it is a test right now of how radioactive the idea of socialism is within the Democratic Party, and as well as nationally, as well as what could work, Shannon, in 2028. He has focused a lot on affordability and working class Americans.\par \par \uc2 When you looked to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 ' first run and how he did compared to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , to now you have it's \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  as a potential, you know, leading candidate in 2028 out there on the campaign trail with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . They do have a choice in front of them in the Democratic Party, and we'll learn, I'm sure, more as we head into 2028 what choice they'll make.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so with all of those issues out there, by the way, and the thing I noted, this welcome study that "Semafor" (ph) cited about where some people think Democrats are off base. \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , former White House spokesperson, out this week said this to "The New Yorker." She said, "I do not feel seen in the party because I think they're throwing the LGBTQ community under the bus and they're not fighting enough for migrants and immigrants."\par \par \uc2 So, guys, she thinks they could be doing more progressive work.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, TOWNHALL.COM POLITICAL EDITOR, "THE GUY BENSON SHOW" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the Democrats have shut down the government to, among other things, try to provide free health care, funded by taxpayers to illegal immigrants. So, I'm not exactly sure what she would like to see in terms of more addressing of that issue. I mean they had an open border for four years under the administration that she spoke on behalf of. And the idea that the Democrats are not pro-LGBTQ enough, it's delusional. I think there's a reason why she's getting panned (ph) on this book tour, even by her own party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a party she says now she's leaving. But, please don't leave, panel, because we have a lot more to talk about.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans urging the DOJ to probe former President Biden's use of the autopen, and calling the legitimacy of his executive actions into question. What it might mean for those 11th hour pardons, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): I think, at the end of the day, it's safe to say all of the pardons and all of the executive orders that were signed with that autopen, especially during the last three months of the Biden administration, especially on days when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was supposedly in the White House, I think they're all in political jeopardy right now.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): The so-called chairman of the House Oversight Committee, James Comer, continues to behave like malignant clown.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, new questions about the status of executive actions made in the final days of the Biden administration as Democrats and Republicans on the House Oversight Committee have both released dueling reports on the former president's autopen use, with the GOP recommending a DOJ probe of all those pardons and executive orders at the end.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 All right, here's a bit of the House Oversight report. This is by majority staff, so Republicans. They say, "as President Biden declined, his staff abused the autopen and a lax chain-of-command policy to effect executive actions that lack any documentation of whether they were in fact authorized."\par \par \uc2 Juan, do you have questions?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I have questions why we're talking about this. It's like, the Republicans and Comer, wow, they want to beat up on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's not running for anything. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's not in power. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's not dealing with the fact that Americans are concerned about inflation. They're concerned about what's going on. He wasn't over in China sending American ships back to the Chinese. He's not the one who's unable to deal with what's going on in Russia. I mean, to me, you know, you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not the one in Mar-a-Lago having Halloween parties while Americans are worried about SNAP benefits. I mean this is pure politics and it's -- it's to the point where, you know, people talk about vengeance and vendettas being executed and by the lawfare actions of Trump against his opponents. But this is -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's not in the game right now. They want to bring him back because I guess they -- they feel they beat him once.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and he did assume things that were a big use of executive power of the way out the door. So, there are questions about that. And Biden spokesperson said this week, President Biden made the decisions of his presidency. Will there be a DOJ probe? We don't know.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA: Well, President Trump has previously called for a deeper investigation into this as well. But with respect to President Biden and the report that came out, the president says, former president, President Biden, says that he did sign the executive orders. He signed all of the pardons. He signed, you know, or rather -- he authorized, rather. He authorized the signing of pardons, executive orders and legislation under his presidency. The report didn't have a smoking gun and didn't provide evidence that he didn't sign those. So, it's really his word right now against that GOP's committees.\par \par \uc2 As far as the process itself goes, there are questions about the processes that any president's including this president, uses to sign pardons. But the Constitution itself gives the president the power of the pardons. So, any reforms that they -- they might want to take, they -- you know, they would have to really figure out how to do that and also be within the constraints of the Constitution on this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, something where there was a contrast between the former and the current administration is this deal with Nigeria. They've changed the -- the Trump administration has changed back what the Biden administration unchanged, this designation as a country of particular concern. We talked about this on the show last week. Thousands of Christians being raped and slaughtered and murdered. Their -- their churches being destroyed down there.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Trump, on Truth Social, said this yesterday in part, "if the Nigerian government continues to allow the killing of Christians, the U.S.A. will immediately stop all aid and assistance to Nigeria and may very well go into that no disgraced country `guns-a-blazing' to completely wipe out the Islamic terrorists who are committing these horrible atrocities. I am hereby instructing our Department of War to prepare for possible action." "Warning, the Nigerian government better move fast."\par \par \uc2 And, Roger, Secretary Hegseth has said we are working up a plan.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: This is President Trump, Shegon (ph), making it very clear that his presidency in American is about religious freedom and protecting those facing religious persecution. And, as you noted, the numbers in Nigeria are shocking in terms of the persecution of Christians.\par \par \uc2 And I think it says a broader statement about President Trump, and his views of the world. This is not a president who wants to be restrained. This is not a president who's going to be limited to one region of the world, whether it's Indo-Pacific for the western hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 Think about over the past seven days. I mean in the Indo-Pacific, dealing with the power -- the people's Republic of China, then he's been dealing with Venezuela, now he's in Africa. People who want to restrain this president of the United States are barking up the wrong tree and it's great that the president is doing this right now and advancing religious freedom and helping those who are persecuted because of their faith.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and what I did not have on my bingo card is Nicki Minaj weighing in on this.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But after the president said he was going to designate Nigeria as a country of particular concern, she had a posting on X that said, in part, "reading this made me feel a deep sense of gratitude. Numerous countries all around the world are being affected by this horror and it's dangerous to pretend we don't notice. Thank you to the president and his team for taking this seriously."\par \par \uc2 And, Guy, now she is taking heat from people who say, you shouldn't say anything positive about this president.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Of course she is, but good for her because she's speaking out against atrocities. Christians are being slaughtered in Nigeria and Sudan by Islamists. And much of the world has had absolutely nothing to say about it.\par \par \uc2 In this country, we've had