on. And join us on Fox News Channel Tuesday at 10:00 p.m. Eastern for special coverage of election night. We've got all of your big races covered.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020251102elb200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Hopes Run High For Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  Peace Deal; Interview With House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  (D-NY) on Government Shutdown; Interview With House Speaker Mike Johnson (R-LA) on Government Shutdown and Extending Obama Care Subsidies; Lt. Gov. Winsome Earle-Sears (R-VA) on State of Jobs, Economy in VA.; Justice Barrett On Balancing Family With Career On High Court; Justice Barrett On Term Limits, Push To Change Number Of Justices On The Court; Arctic Frost Document Details \uc2 FBI\uc2  Monitoring of GOP Senators; Stalemate in Congress over Government Shutdown; Hopes for Lasting Peace in the Middle East.\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream Trey Yingst, Josh Kraushaar \par \uc2 10,401 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A groundbreaking agreement sparking new hope for lasting peace in one of the world's most embattled regions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We reached a momentous breakthrough in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): An historic step after two brutal years of war.\par \par \uc2 President Trump announcing the first phase of a ceasefire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that will send the remaining hostages home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: President Trump just made the impossible possible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This as a very different battle rages on here at home with a government shutdown stretching into a third week.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Did your boss, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , give you permission?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): He's not my boss. Why did you vote to shut the government down?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  and House Speaker Mike Johnson both joining us exclusively on negotiations as both sides dig in deep.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Open the government, Democrats. We did it in the House three weeks ago. The ball is in the court of Senate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then -- all eyes on Virginia as candidates and controversy spark headlines in the critical off-year election. We sit down exclusively with Republican gubernatorial candidate Winsome Earle-Sears on the contentious race and the implications with just one year until the midterms.\par \par \uc2 When you took office, there was a 2.7 percent unemployment rate, and that it's now 3.6. Do you quibble with the data?\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. WINSOME EARLE-SEARS (R-VA): Well, what I'm telling you is that we're creating jobs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And --\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2 , U.S. SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: The fundamental proposition that judges should not be able to be fired for their decisions I think is the most essential part of the hardwiring of judicial independence into the Constitution itself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All rise. Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  reflects on her ascent to the highest court in the land and the state of our judiciary in this unprecedented era.\par \par \uc2 All right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a FOX News alert in the Middle East as hopes are running high for a peace deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has agreed to release all remaining living or dead hostages by tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 President Trump will take part in a high-level summit in Egypt to mark an end to the war that has left thousands dead and the Gaza Strip in ruins, and to talk about the way forward.\par \par \uc2 Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst joins us from Hostage Square in Tel Aviv, Israel, just hours after a massive rally there last night -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, Shannon. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, hundreds of thousands of Israelis gathered here in Hostages Square for what they hope will be their final rally, with the families of those expected to be released by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  standing behind them and many former hostages.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's daughter \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , son-in-law \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , and special envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  addressed the crowd about this historic peace plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Steve, what does this mean to you? What does this moment mean to you?\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF\uc2 , U.S. SPECIAL ENVOY: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 YINGST: What does this moment mean to you?\par \par \uc2 WITKOFF: It's the moment of my life. A blessing in my life, and I -- and I -- and I couldn't be more thrilled to work for the greatest president out there, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . He brought them home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Anticipation is high in these final hours. The hostages are expected to be picked up by the Red Cross inside Gaza and then transferred to Israeli forces. Then they'll be reunited with family members and flown by helicopter to local hospitals for medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 The hostages are set to be exchanged for nearly 2,000 Palestinian prisoners, 142 of them are likely to be deported abroad, 100 released into the West Bank, five to East Jerusalem, and three to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Gaza Strip, Palestinians continue to return to their homes in areas formerly controlled by the \uc2 IDF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 New images show the scale of destruction within the enclave.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): What scares us most is that the war will return again, and we'll be displaced once more. We have no money, no health, no strength left to walk or speak. God willing, the war will remain halted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Tomorrow, President Trump will arrive here in Israel. He'll address the Israeli Knesset before traveling to Egypt to finalize the peace plan -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst reporting from Israel. Trey, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In other news, the small town of McEwen, Tennessee, is mourning the loss of 16 people killed when a blast leveled an explosives plant early Friday. Investigators are combing the wreckage of the Accurate Energetic Systems plant to try to determine what ignited the military grade materials. There were no survivors.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, the stalemate over the government shutdown continues on Capitol Hill with lawmakers out of town for the long holiday weekend. President Trump ordering Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  to use all available Pentagon funds to pay service members who would otherwise miss a paycheck this Wednesday. At least 4,100 furloughed federal workers were fired on Friday with promises of more layoffs to come if a deal isn't reached soon.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll be joined by House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 But first, House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of New York.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Leader, welcome to "FOX News Sunday". Good to have you this morning.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, so let's start here. On Friday, your Senate counterpart, the Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , said this, "Every day gets better for us", talking about the optics of this shutdown we're all in now.\par \par \uc2 So, what's your message to the American people? Do you agree with his view?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, House and Senate Democrats have taken a principled position in defense of the American people to protect their healthcare. We're dealing with a cost of living crisis in this country right now, and we're dealing with a health care crisis.\par \par \uc2 We have repeatedly made clear that we will sit down with anyone, anytime, anyplace, go back to the White House to have a bipartisan discussion about reopening the government, reaching a spending agreement that actually improves the quality of life of the American people, and addresses the health care crisis that threatens tens of millions of people across the country, including in rural America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But do you agree that the optics of this are, quote, "working in your favor" or good for you as a party at this point?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: This is not about party. This is about the American people.\par \par \uc2 We're dealing with a situation where as a result of the one big ugly bill that Republicans passed earlier this year, we have the largest cut to Medicaid in American history. Hospitals, nursing homes, and community-based health centers are closing all across the country. If Republicans continue to refuse to extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits, then tens of millions of people are about to experience dramatically increased premiums, copays, and deductibles that will result in health insurance costs doubling or tripling or quadrupling.\par \par \uc2 People are going to face thousands of dollars of additional costs, including in the state that is represented by Speaker Mike Johnson. In fact, in his own district, I believe premiums may increase by about 380 percent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, I want to get to that, but in a minute, first, let's talk about the continuing resolution that Democrats are voting against. It's been repeatedly voted against in the Senate. We're now, I think, eight votes into this.\par \par \uc2 So, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " describes the bill this way. They say, "Democrats have misused budget jargon to assail the Republican budget proposal is `dirty'. But the bill is considered `clean'," meaning all it does is extend current funding levels for I believe about seven weeks is what the Republicans are proposing.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats say you want to undo part of what Republicans call the Big, Beautiful Bill, that you want to add money back for things like \uc2 NPR\uc2 , and that's at the cost of funding the troops, funding air traffic controllers, funding SNAP benefits.\par \par \uc2 So, what's the message to the American people about why you won't vote for this clean measure, where you could continue to negotiate these things and said kind of holding it hostage in Republicans' verbiage to these other interests that are partisan in nature?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, Republicans control the House, the Senate, and the presidency. And unfortunately, they've made the decision to take a "my way or the highway" approach. We've repeatedly made clear we want to sit down - -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, of course, it's important to make clear, too, that they need Senate votes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: -- reopen the government and negotiate a bipartisan path forward.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They do control all those --\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: So, in --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- all of those bodies, but they do need Senate votes from Democrats and they've had some in order to move something there.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Which is why there should be a bipartisan negotiation.\par \par \uc2 Now, my friends on the other side of the aisle, they seem to believe that healthcare is an extraneous issue. We don't believe it's an extraneous issue. It's a central issue.\par \par \uc2 This is the wealthiest country in the history of the world. And people throughout this country should be able to afford to go see a doctor when they need one for themselves, their family, their children.\par \par \uc2 We're fighting for couples, which in this particular moment in time, for instance, a couple that makes $88,000 a year currently receives tax credits connected to the Affordable Care Act, pays about $8,000 a year, which is already too expensive. But if we allow the Affordable Care Act tax credits to expire, their cost will triple to $24,000 a year. That's unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, the states that are most affected are all states that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won. In fact, I think it's the top five states, are West Virginia, Wyoming, Alaska, Mississippi, and Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 So, this is not a partisan fight for us. We're fighting for everyday Americans, working-class Americans, and middle-class Americans, by the way, who are also dealing with a cost of living crisis in this country because costs are far too high. Life has gotten more expensive this year, not less, despite the Republican promise that they were going to lower costs on day one.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, a couple of things. If Republicans say, "Listen, as part of the C.R., that's not the conversation to have. That issue comes up later in the year", are you -- would you be willing to vote on a C.R. moving forward with assurances that you would address the subsidies at some point? Because a number of Republicans say they agree with you that they're going to need to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, what deal do you need right now to be a "yes" vote on a clean C.R.?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, first of all, we don't view the current bill as anything other than a partisan Republican spending bill --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But is the -- is that the issue --\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: -- that continues to gut the healthcare of the American people - -\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's at levels that you guys have already agreed to? A number of Democrats voted for this extension back in March. It's not adding or detracting anything. It's just continuing the levels of spending that currently exist.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Actually, it's not at levels that we agreed to. Democrats strongly oppose this legislation in the House because in our view, it gut veterans' healthcare, gut funding for women, infants and children programming, gut funding for affordable housing, and we know we have a housing crisis in the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, just to be clear --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Housing costs are too high.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just to be clear though --\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: And it gut funding for healthcare.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- it's not changing current, it's not -- it's not changing current levels. Nothing's getting cut.\par \par \uc2 It's at levels that you may not like, but it's about extending levels as they currently exist. Fair?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Right. We agree that current -- we -- no, we agree that current spending levels are unacceptable --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: -- and it also incorporates massive cuts that have occurred over the last several months and cuts funding for local law enforcement grants to support police officers by $500 million.\par \par \uc2 So, there's a variety of different challenges that we have with respect to this partisan Republican spending bill, but the main one is that we're facing this healthcare crisis. Open enrollment starts on November 1st.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Tens of millions of Americans are receiving notices right now that their premiums, copays, and deductibles are about to explode, which is why we need to address it.\par \par \uc2 Even \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: -- agrees that we have to address this health insurance crisis right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, let's talk about an analysis of these enhanced Obamacare subsidies and where we are because you mentioned a number of figures there. They were passed, of course, under the Biden administration in `21, extended in `22. They were about being a temporary measure to help us through the COVID emergency.\par \par \uc2 So, here's what an analysis over at "The Federalist" says. Now, this is based on data from the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , which tracks all this healthcare stuff.\par \par \uc2 They say, "If the enhanced subsidies go away, the federal government would still pay for the vast majority of premium costs. The federal government would fund, quote, `only' 78 percent of the premiums as opposed to 88 percent of the total premium under the enhanced subsidies."\par \par \uc2 So, that means taxpayers will still be footing nearly 80 percent of the cost of the premiums.\par \par \uc2 Should we be having a broader conversation about the fact that Obamacare was supposed to make everything more affordable? And yet that is the U.S. taxpayer, you and me, and everybody who goes to work out there, who is putting nearly 80 percent of the cost of these premiums, even if the subsidies expire?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: More than 70 percent of the American people agree that we need to extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits because they are making a meaningful difference in the lives of the American people. We do need to have a broader conversation about fixing our broken healthcare system, and we want to do that in a bipartisan way.\par \par \uc2 It's unacceptable in our view that legislation was passed by Republicans earlier this year that is actually triggering the closure of hospitals and nursing homes and community-based health centers throughout the country, disproportionately in rural America.\par \par \uc2 We also need to talk about the fact that hundreds of thousands of veterans rely on the Affordable Care Act tax credits in order to en -- to ensure that they have access to a doctor when they need one for themselves, for their children and for their families.\par \par \uc2 We also need to lower the high cost of living. I agree entirely -- costs are way too high. America's too expensive.\par \par \uc2 Housing costs are too high. Grocery costs are too high. Electricity bills are through the roof all across America.\par \par \uc2 And now, you've got people who are facing dramatically increased health care costs because of the refusal of some of my friends on the other side of the aisle to even have a conversation about extending the Affordable Care Act tax credits.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: That's not acceptable in this moment.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will pick up the conversation there with the speaker.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Minority Leader, thank you for your time. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, joining us now, House Speaker Mike Johnson of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 A lot to unpack there. Good to see you again this morning.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Thank you. Thank you for the fact-based questions to my colleague.\par \par \uc2 That was the art of distraction you just saw there. They -- they're trying their best to distract the American people from the simple fact that they've chosen a partisan fight so that they can prove to their Marxist rising base in the Democrat Party that they're willing to fight Trump and Republicans. There is no objective truth to everything he said there.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans have already said we were going to have thoughtful conversations, deliberation, and debate about continuing the COVID era Obamacare subsidies because they don't expire until December 31st. They're trying to pretend like that's a September issue. It never was.\par \par \uc2 The president himself offered to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  in the Oval Office when I and Leader Thune were sitting next to him that he would have that thoughtful debate, that that is for tomorrow, as soon as we get the government open so that everybody can come in and do that.\par \par \uc2 There's hundreds of ideas on how to do it. It was not appropriate or possible for that to be resolved in a continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What about his issue, though, that open people already getting notices about what they may pay? I mean, that's something that's got to be resolved before.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, and they are eating up the clock in the month of October. They shut the government down October 1st. We had always been counting on using these four weeks in October to finish up those discussions.\par \par \uc2 The great irony is it -- is they themselves that are doing it. And, Shannon, we cannot lose the simple fact that their counter-proposal to fund the government was $1.5 trillion in new spending, as you noted. They wanted to give money back for the \uc2 Corporation for Public Broadcasting\uc2  and all sorts of things, and they wanted to claw back the elimination of fraud, waste, and abuse that we took out of Medicaid to make it work better, and the $50 billion that Republicans put into the law to prop up rural hospitals. What they're saying today makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he does talk about the fact, and we know that there have been rural hospitals that have closed. That's happened here in the Virginia area and beyond, so what about that? And his contention that a lot of people will be kicked off Medicaid, and yes, people talk about waste, fraud, and abuse, but there's real concern by some analyses that there will be other people that may qualify in some way that are going to risk, you know, losing kind of health care coverage that the leader is talking about there.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: So, much to be said there. The rural hospitals that are in trouble right now are not because of Republican policies. This -- this is the legacy of what has happened under Obamacare, and you just heard the leader of the Democrats in the House acknowledge that Obamacare is unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 When they passed the new law in 2010, they promised us it was going to make health care more affordable. They put it in the name of the law. It did exactly the opposite. Premiums have risen exponentially since 2010, and they're not affordable. It was never designed to be affordable. Republicans are the party that are fixing health care.\par \par \uc2 We just demonstrated that in the one big beautiful bill, the Working Families Tax Cut. We put in reforms to Medicaid, as I mentioned, that cut out fraud, waste, and abuse. It got ineligible recipients off of the program, and it's brought down the cost and made the resources spread more broadly to the people who actually need it.\par \par \uc2 U.S. citizens who are the disabled, the elderly, and young pregnant women, those kinds of folks who are the people who are intended for that program to help. We're shoring up the program. We're fixing health care. Obamacare is what broke it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's get back to those subsidies for Obamacare. He mentioned Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , a Republican. There are other Republicans who are raising alarm. Here's what she said, in part, on X. "I'm absolutely disgusted that health insurance premiums will double if the tax credits expire this year. Not a single Republican in leadership talked to use about this or has given us a plan to help Americans deal with their health insurance premiums,' she says, again, 'doubling." So, what is the guidance from you, the leader of this party?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah, I had a thoughtful conversation with my friend \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  on the phone the other night, a couple nights ago, to -- to inform her that there are many Republicans in Congress that have been working around the clock on this. They're on the committees of jurisdiction that deal with this. Marjorie doesn't serve on those committees, so I offered to have her come into the room and be a part of that discussion, if indeed she wants to do that.\par \par \uc2 There has been a lot of work done on this. We have hundreds of ideas, literally, on the table to fix health care, to make it more affordable for the American people, to make access more available, and to increase the quality of care. There's a lot that can be done, but you have to build consensus in a large, deliberative public body like this.\par \par \uc2 And again, what they're saying right now, the reason they're talking about health care is because they're trying to create a distraction. We have voted eight times to pay the troops, pay TSA agents, Border Patrol agency, you know, everybody serving in the federal government, and to make sure these services are provided for veterans and the elderly disabled who need them so much. Eight times now, Democrats in Congress have voted to close the government. We're on day ten. It's real pain for real people, and they seem not to care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you mentioned the troops. Let's talk about that, because the president has now said he's ordering Secretary Hegseth to find Pentagon money to pay this week so that none of our active-duty folks will miss, and I think all Americans agree that that is a priority. So, Democrats say on Friday they tried to offer up a measure on the House floor that would have kept active-duty troops paid during any kind of shutdown. They do have at least one Republican signature on it, making it bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" reports this, though, the presiding Republican, Representative \uc2 Mike Bost\uc2  of Illinois, gaveled a brief pro forma session to a close before Representative Sarah Elfreth, she is the Democrat trying to do this from Maryland, could make a procedural move to try to pass the legislation by unanimous consent. "Politico" says Congressman Bost was working on your guidance, so why not allow a standalone measure for troops?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Because it's unnecessary, because we already voted to pay the troops on September 19th. We have a chart that we used at our press conference showing every time the Republicans have voted to pay the troops and the Democrats every time voted no. We did it in the House September 19th, over three weeks ago, sent it to the Senate, and now, seven different times, the Democrats in the Senate, all but three, have voted not to pay the troops.\par \par \uc2 So, the ball is in the Senate's court, the House has done its job, and that's what Chairman Bost was referring to there. That Democrat who tried to bring that resolution on the floor voted against paying the troops. Go look at the record, every single one of them, and only one Democrat in the House voted to pay the troops, and that vote happened three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, I want to get back to the optics of this, because we heard or we saw what Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said about this is getting better for them every day. I want to play a little bit of what he said about why you guys are going to get blamed for this. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: Republicans have the White House, they control the House and the Senate, so when people are laid off, when people are not paid, when people are not getting back pay, they know it's the Republicans trying to do it, even though they're using it to try and bully us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They think the polling is on their side, the American people are going to hold you responsible.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is incapable of telling the truth right now. I mean, he's been in Congress for 44 years, Shannon, I mean, almost half a century. He knows this is basic civics. Every eighth-grade civic student can -- can point out the fallacy in what he just said. It requires 60 votes in the Senate to get this through. We only have 53 Republicans. We've got to have a handful of Democrats in the Senate who apply common sense and agree to reopen the government so that we can continue all these discussions.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed out to \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , this is a clean continuing resolution. What that means for folks back home is it's exactly the same levels of spending. By the way, it's Biden-era policies and spending, which we loathe, but we're trying to get through the appropriations process to change that and fix it for the country, and they ran out of time because the end of the fiscal year, September 30th, we just need seven more weeks to finish it.\par \par \uc2 And right now, they're eating up the clock. They're doing this for political cover. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , when he voted for the exact same measure back in March, took a lot of heat from the left of his party, and he doesn't want to sustain that any longer. So, he's doing this and inflicting pain for his own selfish reasons.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we have not brokered an end to the shutdown here on Fox News Sunday this morning.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I'm a peacemaker. I wish we could.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are glad to hear from both sides. We appreciate your time, and we'll watch and wait as the American people look for a resolution. Thank you, Speaker.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All eyes now on Virginia. In an off-year election, one of the only ones this year, as Lieutenant Governor Winsome Earle-Sears and former Congresswoman \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  make their final pitches to Old Dominion voters, and controversy shakes both sides of the ticket, with early voting already well underway. I sat down exclusively with Lieutenant Governor Sears on her vision for the future of the Commonwealth. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): There should never be nude men in locker rooms.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. WINSOME EARLE-SEARS (R-VA): Oh my goodness, we finally have her saying that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, trans policy in schools, just one of the many heated issues, and the only debate that Virginia voters will get between Republican Lieutenant Governor Winsome Earle-Sears and Democrat former Congresswoman \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 . The critical November contest considered a bit of a temperature check on the state of American politics. It'll be just one year out until the midterms. We extended repeated invitations to both candidates. \uc2 Sears\uc2  accepted. So, we went to Richmond this week to discuss the state of the race and her historic campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I am one of your constituents in Virginia, so --\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Oh, you are?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As one of your constituents, I see the endless ads that are going on right now. This race feels like it's gone on and on and forever. We're now voting. And so, most of the ads, your opponent is running, talking about you being MAGA, not meaning that in a positive sense of the word, but tying you to President Trump, saying you're a rubber stamp for him. But you don't have his endorsement. So, what does that mean in Virginia?\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Well, it means we're going to keep working in Virginia. I know no other way. And would I like the president's endorsement? Of course I would. You know, he's doing really good things for America. You know, all the jobs that he's repatriating here.\par \par \uc2 And we've been part of the folks who have been getting that kind of largesse. You know, we've just announced, for example, \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  is coming to Virginia. They had 400 applications from 46 other states. And they chose, wait for it, Virginia. And what was one of the reasons? Because they said they saw so many of the other businesses moving here.\par \par \uc2 And it's because we have a great environment. They know that they will thrive here. They know that we won't force their employees to join a union against their will. And for me, that's a liberty issue. I am simply pro- worker. And then, of course, we have side pads ready for them to build.\par \par \uc2 Whereas, you know, in some of the states, maybe they don't. And then we have a good business regulation environment where, well, we're going to keep getting rid of all the regulations that are job killers. For example, we've gotten rid of 91,000 different regulations. That did nothing, nothing for businesses.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about the employment situation here in Virginia, because the truth is, a lot of federal jobs, a lot of federal workers live in Northern Virginia and throughout the Commonwealth. They've lost their jobs because of DOGE and other cutting. Your opponent has pointed to that. She said this that, "My opponent, Winsome Earle-Sears, has defended Trump's firing of Virginia workers at every turn." She says, you don't take the economic consequences of DOGE and what President Trump is doing seriously. And she points out unemployment is up under the Youngkin-Sears administration. So, how do you answer that?\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Actually, unemployment is down. That's number one. And it's down even in terms of the national unemployment numbers. But think about this. We have in Virginia created and she -- first of all, she acts as if, you know, I don't care. And of course I do. I know what it's like to lose a job. It's not anything I would wish on anyone.\par \par \uc2 So we, though, have created jobs in Virginia. We have over 250,000 job openings, if, you know, anyone would want to. And we have Virginiahasjobs.com. And we'll tee up, I call it Workforce Wednesday, so people can understand where the job fairs are, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Now, if she's talking again about, you know, all summer long, she's been saying she loves federal workers more than anyone. Well, if you love me, then prove it. And part of that is making sure that the federal workers stay in their jobs. And so, love looks like no government shutdown. That's what love looks like. And this -- she has this opportunity to tell Senators Warner and Kaine to do exactly that, go back to D.C., go and vote against the shutdown so that the federal workers will stay in their jobs. She didn't do that until I asked her last night, point blank, on the stage to do it during the debate.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, she's making political calculations. She doesn't really care. She's been using the federal workers as a political football. That's not the kind of leader we need in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She does point to data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which we hope is going to be nonpartisan and neutral, showing that when you took office, there was a 2.7 percent unemployment rate and that it's now 3.6. Do you quibble with the data?\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Well, what I'm telling you is that we're creating jobs. We have over 15,000 new business startups that never existed. So, how is it possible that, you know, we have to figure out what is the data? What are they measuring? I don't know. I don't know where they're getting the stats from. I haven't seen what your stats look like. I just know what ours looks like.\par \par \uc2 And people are at work. More people are coming into Virginia than they were leaving when we were not in office. So, the U-Hauls are stopping here, and it's because they see good things happening here.\par \par \uc2 We just announced, for example, yet another company, \uc2 Afton Scientific\uc2 , is in Virginia. They are increasing their site pad eight -- their footprint eight times what they already have. They're investing $200 million in Virginia, going to create about 200 jobs, paying about $72,000 a year. That's really good.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you something that didn't come to Virginia because of Fairfax County. We were trying to bring 800 high-paying jobs to Northern Virginia. They wouldn't do it. We couldn't get them to agree.\par \par \uc2 So, Abigail makes it --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When you say they, what --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: They, the Board of Supervisors.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In Fairfax?\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Yes. In Fairfax County, we were trying to bring 800 high- paying jobs to Virginia. They wouldn't help. So, she needs to go and talk to her friends there, because it's Democrats who sit on that board.\par \par \uc2 And so ask them, why? Is this a political game that you're playing with the people of Northern Virginia's lives?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about the impact of the Big Beautiful Bill, too, because it's another thing that you have praised and your opponent has said is a negative thing for Virginia.\par \par \uc2 One organization says this. It rips healthcare away from 350,000 Virginians, cost Virginia hospital $26 billion, and closed three rural healthcare centers in the state.\par \par \uc2 So, what is the plan for Virginians who are worried about losing access, losing coverage when it comes to healthcare, which polls as one of the top issues?\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Of course, it does. Healthcare is very important.\par \par \uc2 And so this is why I look at the politicians and what they're doing, and how dare they try to instill fear in Virginians? For example, the hospitals that are closing, they were closing long before, long before the bill passed. And that's because we are losing population in our rural areas.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, she latches onto this to say, well, this equals that. It doesn't. Her math doesn't compute.\par \par \uc2 And here's the other thing. When it comes to the bill, all the bill is saying is, you're going to make Medicaid go back to its original purpose. If you are able-bodied and you're receiving Medicaid, then you must either, according to the bill, have 80 hours of work or 80 hours of volunteer service or 80 hours of training that you're doing or some hybrid of all three of those.\par \par \uc2 I know you work longer than 80 hours a month. I do. I know many people work longer than 80 hours. So, why should you have Medicaid when you're able- bodied? This is the taxpayers who are paying for you. We're not trying to make you fail.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, when I was a homeless shelter director, I know exactly that of which I speak, because I was driving the women to their job appointments, to their job's period, or wherever it was, because I wanted them to succeed, not fail.\par \par \uc2 And if I just let them stay in the shelter and -- and just, what, just while away their time? I'm not going to help you fail. And that's what finally is happening with the bill. They're not going to let people fail.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What's your plan should you become governor if there are those who are caught in some of these loopholes or fall off of the coverage for Medicaid or other safety nets for them? What is the plan?\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Well, as I said, there are -- it's bringing those who are able-bodied. You are able-bodied and you're asking the taxpayers to foot your healthcare. Well, then why? Why? You're able-bodied.\par \par \uc2 And all we're saying is, help us to help you. That's what the bill says.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, there are 70 percent of Virginians who are on Medicaid. They're in some form of, well, whether it's volunteering or whether it's jobs, something like that. They already are doing it.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about 30 percent of the population. So, 30 percent of those who are on Medicaid are going to make decisions for themselves on whether they are going to have 80 hours of work, 80 hours of volunteerism, or some hybrid, training thereof. And so this -- this is what we're facing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I know it's been a contentious campaign. It was a very interesting debate, I think the only one that you guys are going to have.\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Interesting. Is that what you call it?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I did.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I thought it was very interesting. I know voting is underway. We'll continue to follow you on the trail.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Governor, thanks for making time for us today.\par \par \uc2 EARLE-SEARS: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, we appreciate her time. And former Congresswoman \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , welcome anytime on "Fox News Sunday." We love to chat with you too.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, my exclusive interview with Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  on her public and personal life, including if she checks out the online chatter about the court on social media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not that you would publicly run a social media account. Do you lurk at all on social media? Look at what's going on, on social media. Because that's where some of the real dumpster fires are playing.\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2 , U.S. SUPREME COURT ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: Yes. And I think I'm a much happier person for not lurking on social media at all.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The role of the Supreme Court in an era of near unprecedented political division. Well, I sat down with Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  to discuss how she tunes out the court of public opinion and about her new book, "Listening to the Law."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Justice Barrett, great to have you with us on "Fox News Sunday." Thanks for coming by.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: It's great to be here, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I really enjoyed the book. I felt at times I was back in law school.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which I think is a good thing. You could definitely see your heart as a professor, as somebody who really wants to teach and make sure that people understand. And you do a lot of work in this book trying to make people understand what the court actually does and doesn't do.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever had a level of frustration that people don't really understand what justices do?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes. I think that was one of the reasons why I wanted to write the book. I felt like I wanted to explain the court. People ask the same questions. And -- and I think people have the same questions. People don't know what's going on in the court. I love the court. I wanted to welcome people into it and introduce them to it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there's a lot of great information that I think any layperson or lawyer is going to learn new things about the court and the inner workings and how it actually plays out from start to finish with these cases.\par \par \uc2 But before you took the job, you write about the decision that your family made together. And you write in here, you say, "I just watched \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , whom I knew and respected, undergo a brutal confirmation process. And I was afraid of what I might face. Yet, it seemed cowardly to bow out because we didn't want to make the sacrifice."\par \par \uc2 You talk about your husband, Jesse, said, If we do this, we got to burn the ships. There's no turning back.\par \par \uc2 Has it ever felt too costly a choice for you?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes. I mean, it has been a sacrifice. But, you know, I'm honored to serve the country. We've never considered undoing the choice. And we don't regret it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you do talk about having to develop a thicker skin --\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- than you already had walking into this.\par \par \uc2 You say about justices, you want people to understand, because you had, I think it was a relative who came to you, and said, why don't you guys just decide what's right and wrong? Why don't you do that?\par \par \uc2 And you talk about, that's not exactly the role. You said, of justices, "They're referees, not kings, because they decide whether people have played by the rules rather than what the rules should be."\par \par \uc2 And you write later in the book, whatever the court decides, it makes enemies.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you deal with the noise left and right that you get?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I tune it out. My husband, Jesse, we joke around, that, you know, he -- he's my gatekeeper. I just tell him, I only want to know what is said about me on the news on a need-to-know basis.\par \par \uc2 If I absolutely need to know, then my assistant or my husband will tell me. But, otherwise, whether it's good or bad, I just try not to pay attention to it, because that just gets in the way of focusing on deciding cases.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, about Jesse, it's interesting. You talk about -- you both have had such demanding careers. He was a professor, he was a prosecutor in different practices and facets of the law. You have got seven kids.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you talk about how there's never been really a dividing line. They've been in your classrooms, in your offices. They've been in his courtroom. And you've kind of married these things together.\par \par \uc2 I love the story where they enjoyed his work so much as a federal prosecutor that the kids started drafting indictments against each other.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Because they now know how to do this.\par \par \uc2 How did the two of you balance two really demanding careers and seven children?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: You know, my husband is great. He does far more than his fair share. You know, the kids have gotten used to it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we lived in South Bend. It's a really livable town. It's a great place to raise kids, because everything was a short commute.\par \par \uc2 So, I was able to be at work and then pop over to the kids' school, get there in, you know, seven minutes, do something in the classroom, go back to campus. So, it made it easy to blend things.\par \par \uc2 And they're a little bit older now. I mean, we have four who are still in high school and middle school. But I think we couldn't have raised all seven in our current jobs living in Washington, where there's a lot more traffic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There is. How did those indictments go? Do the kids -- did they get into fully prosecuting cases against their siblings?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I was going to say, they -- they -- they fortunately or unfortunately never made it to trial.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, I want to ask about this. There's been a lot of talk over the years that the makeup of the court has changed. It's been at nine for a very, very long time, more than a century.\par \par \uc2 But there are in recent years more conversations about changing term limits or adding to the court.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something that former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  said about that a couple of days ago.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: People shouldn't be on the Supreme Court for 30, you know, 40 years, become disconnected from the country that they're supposed to serve. Eighteen years is enough for anybody to serve on the Supreme Court. And that has huge support.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you write in the book, the Constitution's guarantees of life tenure and salary protection for federal judges are designed to fortify a judge's resolve to stand firm against the tide of public opinion.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you make of this public conversation of some fundamental changes to the way the court works?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Well, some fundamental changes are set by the Constitution. I mean, Article III, any -- any change either has to be consistent with the Constitution or come by way of constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 But the fundamental proposition that judges should not be able to be fired for their decisions, I think, is the most essential part of the hardwiring of judicial independence into the Constitution itself. Because if judges could be fired for their decisions, then they would not be able to stand firm against the tide of public opinion or, you know, the displeasure of the other branches.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You also talk about really tough conversations in the book. And that's obviously something our country is reeling from political violence and -- and real disagreements that have crossed the line --\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- into life-and-death situations. So, are you hopeful that we'll find a way back to having really tough conversations?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I am really hopeful, because I don't think -- I feel like -- like when I talk to people, people are not happy with the level of polarization in the country and the inability of people to have conversations with those whom they disagree with.\par \par \uc2 And so I -- I guess my hope is that we are at a turning point and a corner where more people want to know how to have those conversations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you have to have them at the court. You talk about being very collegial, but there are sometimes that publicly there are some -- there's a little bit of sparring.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there's been much made of the emergency docket, so people understand its --\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You don't get full briefing and oral arguments, but you have to make a temporary decision on something. And there have been some really heated dissents from those who are on the losing end of some of those decisions.\par \par \uc2 One of them from Justice Sotomayor, joined by Justices Kagan and Jackson, said, "In the last eight months, this court's appetite to circumvent the ordinary appellate process and weigh in on important issues has grown exponentially. Its interest in explaining itself, unfortunately, has not."\par \par \uc2 So, what about her criticism that there's not full transparency on that, whether you want to call it emergency docket, shadow docket, interim docket?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, we can't decide what to call it, it seems. It's a relatively new phenomenon that it's -- or at least the amount of activity on it is relatively new.\par \par \uc2 You know, these are cases that are preliminary, and so they are not cases in which the court has had full briefing and made a final judgment. And I talk in the book about the opinion-writing process. It's -- deciding a merits case is a painstaking process. It's slow. It takes a lot of work. And when we write an opinion, it reflects our final judgment.\par \par \uc2 On the interim docket, these preliminary decisions that we make, it's not just about the merits, whether a case is right or wrong. We account for other factors as well. And if we wrote a long opinion, it might give the impression that we have finally resolved the issue. And in none of these cases have we finally resolved the issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're tracking all of them.\par \par \uc2 Quick final question. It's hard for me to believe it's been five years, essentially, that you have been on the court.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Me too. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But everybody remembers, I think, if they were following the confirmation, the process, in which you held up the notepad and you were asked, what's on it? And here's the moment. And you said, essentially, it's the letterhead of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We want to know if you kept the notepad or where it is now?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Some very kind staffers snatched it up from the table. And after I was confirmed, brought it over me -- over to me at the court. And so, I have it on display in my chambers in a shadow box.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think the book is just a fascinating discussion of what happened at the court, what its role really is. And I think it'll be very helpful for anybody who wants to know more about how the court works.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Oh, thank you, Shannon. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, Attorney General Pam Bondi's explosive testimony on Capitol Hill this week detailing a secret \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation of GOP senators during the chaotic days around January 6th code named Arctic Frost. We'll break it down, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: We learned that former \uc2 FBI\uc2  secretly investigated you and your colleagues. Why? They wanted to take President Trump off the playing field.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is Attorney General Pam Bondi testifying before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  this week regarding newly revealed details about a controversial \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation launched in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group. "Wall Street Journal" White House reporter Annie Linskey, former communications director to House Speaker Kevin McCarthy, Mark Bednar, former Biden White House special assistant and director of message planning, Meghan Hays, and editor-in-chief of the "Jewish Insider," \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Great to see all of you today.\par \par \uc2 So, let's start with Arctic Frost. We find out that several members of the Senate, Republicans, and one House member at least we know of, there was monitoring of their phone call records. Also we found out that 92 GOP connected groups, or people including late conservative Turning Point USA founder Charlie Kirk targeted as well. That's in "The New York Post."\par \par \uc2 I want to play something that Democrat Senator Chris Coons had to say about the revelation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): If true, it is something I think urgently needs more investigation.\par \par \uc2 Just on the surface of it would strike me as a significant invasion of the right of senators to conduct their jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Annie, we're just hearing about this, but there are now demands from these lawmakers for more information.\par \par \uc2 ANNIE LINSKY, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes. The senators that I've talked to about this are furious. They feel that their communications with the president should be private. They don't like the idea of one branch of government investigating a different branch of government, particularly on a sort of political topic.\par \par \uc2 And I think that this goes -- will go beyond just hearings in Congress. I think, you know, I've talked to senators who have been interested in filing civil lawsuits against the phone carriers for so easily turning over that information.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we're going to keep tracking that. But something that kind of pales in -- over everything else that's happening on Capitol Hill right now is, of course, the shutdown conversation.\par \par \uc2 We talked to the two key players in the House, the Democratic leader, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , and the speaker, Mike Johnson, moments ago. Here's a bit of what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): There's a variety of different challenges that we have with respect to this partisan Republican spending bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): It's real pain for real people, and they seem not to care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mark, we didn't make any headway on getting this resolved today.\par \par \uc2 MARK BEDNAR, FORMER COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR FOR SPEAKER MCCARTHY AND MONUMENT ADVOCACY PRINCIPAL: That's a great question. I mean, right now, does not appear that there's any off-ramps for either side. I've been a part of several shutdowns, and what's very clear is, whichever side appears least unreasonable, most sensible, they are the ones who are going to end up winning right now. And so what we heard from Leader Jeffries before, he has a lot of various demands about all sorts of spending deals that, you know, that's not the same thing as, let's just keep the government open, let's just keep negotiating.\par \par \uc2 So, based on those two interviews right now, it looks -- Speaker Johnson, he seems cool, collected, and calm that Republicans can hold out here and Democrats, they have to atone for this at some point.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Meghan, they feel good, though, about the optics. I mean, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is taking heat for saying every day gets better for us, but they really do feel like, the polling shows, and the consensus is, they're going to be able to point the finger at Republicans for this.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, FORMER BIDEN WHTIE HOUSE SPECIAL ASSISTANT AND FORMER BIDEN WHITE HOUSE DIRECTOR OF MESSAGE PLANNING: I mean, to be honest, I think that they all should get back to work and get the government open. And the fact that Johnson has his members out again for a third week, it makes one wonder why aren't they negotiating? Why aren't his caucuses here? Normally, a lot of the negotiations in a shutdown take place one layer under the leadership. If they're not in Washington, they're not negotiating.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, Democrats are here. They want to talk about getting the government open. They want to talk about lowering health care costs. So, it looks a little bit unreasonable for Republicans not even to be here to have those negotiations. And the American people do want lower health care costs, just like they want lower costs on groceries and housing. So, Democrats have a lot of strength right now in these negotiations. And I think it would be wise for the Republicans to have these negotiations, get the government open, in get health care spending lower.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Josh, you heard, I mean the speaker said, they voted. They did pass a CR. It's there. It maintains current levels for at least seven weeks. He says there's no reason to negotiate. We gave you that.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "JEWISH INSIDER" EDITOR-IN-CHIEF AND FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: And that's a very familiar argument, keep the government open. Like health care, sure, you may have your own demands, but you put that in a separate box. And I think that's a pretty effective argument. We've seen this in past shutdowns, that -- that the party that's in the minority that's making demands usually doesn't have a whole lot of political leverage or political support.\par \par \uc2 I mean the health -- Democrats do believe health care is the winning issue. That's why they're it to the -- to the brink like this. That's an open question. I get the sense that there's not a lot of urgency among the public. You look at the polling and you actually see, as we have been talking about, people blaming both sides and not a lot of urgency to get this done. I don't think we're going to see the shutdown end anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Annie, any thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Yes. I think just both sides are pointing to their own polling. And their own polling is saying that they are right. So, you just have two sides that are really dug in.\par \par \uc2 But I do think, at some point, yes, we are kind of in early days of this shutdown. And at some point, that dynamic is going to change because you can't have this go on for weeks and weeks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, so that's where we are now. We'll check in next Sunday. Maybe it will be over.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the president is getting ready to heading over to the Middle East. He's going to meet with the hostage families. We believe the - - and the hostages, we believe they are going to be released within the next 24 hours or so. He's going to speak to the Knesset and then head on to Egypt where he'll meet with a lot of the key players in what happens next, you know, restoring -- the complete destruction in Gaza and moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Here's an interesting note from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." They say Democrats are praising the Israel-Gaza peace deal, but not Trump for forging it.\par \par \uc2 So, Meghan, many of them issue statements, but they don't mention President Trump. They're excited, but they're not going to give him any credit.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: I mean, we need to give credit where credit is due. His team has worked credibly hard to get here. But I would caution that we have been here before. And I think that we need -- there's a lot more things that need to happen. Everyone is very cautiously optimistic. But I think that this summit in Egypt is really great to bring all these Arab leaders to the table. There's a lot still to figure out. So, I do think that we are -- everyone is cautiously optimistic to get these hostages home and to get peace where we needed it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're going to get about 10 seconds each for our remaining panelists.\par \par \uc2 BEDNAR: Nothing short of remarkable. President Trump deserves credit across the political spectrum. This is historic. Like, it should be exciting for America and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, I mean, just look -- \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2 , \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  spoke at Hostage Square. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the most popular figure. He was applauded very, very loudly. So, that tells you where the Israeli public is when it comes to this deal.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Yes. I mean and President Trump stood up to Israel when that needed to happen. He stood up to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the Arabs when that needed to happen. Credit goes to President Trump on this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and we all watch as this delicate dance is done, hopefully getting to what everybody has been hoping and praying for.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, panel. Great to see all of you. We'll see you again next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a look ahead to that historic moment in the Middle East as the hostages held for more than two years are finally set to return home.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We continue to watch events unfolding in the Middle East as we await the release of the hostages.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, people are returning into Gaza. They're heading up north as aid deliveries ramp up there. As you can see, part of the conversation this week the president will have is about rebuilding that area.\par \par \uc2 Stay with Fox News for coverage of all the latest developments, including that trip where the president will speak to the Knesset and then head on to Egypt for those discussions about reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next "FOX NEWS SUNDAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ 