may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Sorry, Paul.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", we are covering two big stories. President Trump's team raising the possibility that furloughed federal workers might not get back pay. This, as the government shutdown drags on, there are also concerns air traffic control staffing issues could affect flights at several major airports.\par \par \uc2 First, tonight though, a call for a congressional hearing on news, former Special Counsel Jack Smith, allegedly tracked the phones of about a dozen Republican senators.\par \par \uc2 That accusation was a key part today in this fiery hearing up on the Hill with the president's attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent, Aishah Hasnie, starts us off from the hill tonight. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 It was an incredibly contentious hearing, so much so that one Democratic lawmaker left saying that it was the most combative and troublesome hearing that he has attended in his entire career here at the Senate. This, as both sides were sparring today over who is responsible for politicizing and weaponizing the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL, FLORIDA: Operation Arctic Frost was an unconstitutional, undemocratic abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Attorney General Pam Bondi, blasting the Biden FBI over Operation Arctic Frost during the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 's annual justice department oversight hearing.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say the investigation into the January 6th Capitol riot spied on several GOP lawmakers, including senators on today's panel.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): They need to be prosecuted, and they need to face the full extent of accountability within the law.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Senator Blackburn, the weaponization against you and your colleagues should greatly concern all of us. It should concern Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And Democrats say it does.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Just on the surface of it would strike me as a significant invasion of the right of senators to conduct their jobs. So, this is something I think needs urgent power.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But they claim, the Trump administration is now politicizing the DOJ. Going after the president's enemies, while letting allies off the hook, calling into question the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's decision to close an investigation into borders czar, Tom Homan, allegedly accepting $50,000 in a cash bribe.\par \par \uc2 Homan says he did nothing criminal.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): A video or audio tape, if it exists, Mr. Holman taking $50,000 in -- bribe money from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Will you support requests by this committee to provide that tapered tapes to the committee? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: You can talk to Director Patel about that.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Democrats also quizzed Bondi about the Trump administration's decision to send National Guard troops to American cities to fight crime.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Were you consulted by the White House before they deployed National Guard troops to cities in the United States?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: You voted to shut down the government and you're sitting here our law enforcement officers are not being paid. They are out there working to protect you. I wish you loved Chicago as much as you hate President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Gillian, this is far from over. Today, Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  put the chairman on the spot, asking for a separate hearing on former Special Counsel Jack Smith, allegedly taking those phone records of those senators -- Republican senators.\par \par \uc2 Grassley says that he wants the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to conduct a thorough investigation first, and then, he will make a decision about holding a separate hearing. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, Texas National Guard troops now on the ground in Illinois. They are there, in part, to manage protesters at a suburban Chicago immigration facility.\par \par \uc2 Democratic politicians not happy about it. Senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  is in Broadview, Illinois tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Texas National Guard has arrived southwest of Chicago at an army training facility near the city of Joliet, despite emotional opposition from Chicago leadership.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON\uc2 , MAYOR OF CHICAGO: This president is determined to federalize the National Guard and to occupy our cities with the military. It's unconscionable. It's unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The Illinois National Guard deployment is still in motion, despite Democrats efforts to stop it.\par \par \uc2 Illinois Attorney General \uc2 Kwame Raoul\uc2 , asked the court for an immediate injunction to block it. U.S. District Judge April Perry declined to grant the request and gave the Trump team a deadline of tomorrow to answer questions about when the guard will arrive, where will they be sent, and what will they do, once deployed.\par \par \uc2 Department of Justice leaders visited Chicago.\par \par \uc2 TODD BLANCHE, UNITED STATES DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: I mean, at this point, it's almost laughable that the state government is standing up and saying, don't let the National Guard in.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, DIRECTOR, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: And they were screaming for the National Guard on January 6th, and now, all of a sudden, reject it when their city is literally under siege.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): That is where Juan Espinoza Martinez is now charged with solicitation of murder. Federal prosecutors say he put a bounty on the head of border patrol commander, Gregory Bovino, with a \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  that reached $2,000 for information on Bovino, $10,000 to take him out.\par \par \uc2 Following the revelation that he was the target in a murder-for-hire plot, the man in front of Operation Midway Blitz says Illinois leadership made that possible.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY BOVINO, COMMANDER, BORDER PATROL: Look at what the governor or the mayor are talking about. They are the reason that gang members feel comfortable enough to do something like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): DHS Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  has arrived at the embattled ice facility in Portland, Oregon. Last Friday, she came to the facility here in Broadview Illinois, and this is where federal agents have endured multiple clashes and threats from protesters since Operation Midway Blitz began. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  on the scene in Broadview for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is the seventh day of the government shutdown. One of the very real-world consequences now being felt by Americans is air travel.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich joins us now. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 Right now, there are ground stops and delays at airports in Nashville, Boston, Philadelphia, Atlanta, and Dallas, because of \uc2 FAA\uc2  staffing shortages, which officials say were made worse because of the government shutdown, and the impacts are just beginning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY\uc2 , UNITED STATES TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: So, if we don't have controllers, we're going to make sure the airspace is safe. So, what we do is we'll slow traffic. We'll shut down traffic.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Millions of Americans beginning to feel shut down impacts. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  citing staffing issues for ground stops and delays amid an existing shortage of air traffic controllers.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: They work six days a week. Right now, if they don't get the paychecks, how do I pay my mortgage? Do I go and take an \uc2 Uber\uc2  job? And try to make extra money to put the food on the table?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And 750,000 federal workers furloughed right now could feel a big dent in their wallets soon. Despite a federal law President Trump signed after a government shutdown in 2019 widely interpreted to require automatic back pay, the Office of Management and Budget says, not so fast.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Is it the White House's position that furloughed workers should be paid for their back pay?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I would say it depends on who we're talking about. I can tell you this, the Democrats have put a lot of people in great risk and jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): OMB's new legal analysis points to a clause that says back pay needs to be appropriated by Congress, and contradicts guidance issued by the Office of Personnel Management and the Council of Economic Advisers last month, surprising some Republicans too.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): I hope that the furloughed workers receive back pay.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The White House says Democrats could stop the bleeding by voting to reopen the government, and they confirm most people will be paid. But --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There are some people that really don't deserve to be taken care of and we'll take care of them in a different way.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And that means layoffs.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If this keeps going on, it will be substantial, and a lot of those jobs will never come back.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Meantime, the administration getting creative to fund critical programs like WIC, the nutrition assistance program for millions of low-income mothers and kids. Officials say they will keep WIC afloat with tariff revenue for a while, a novel funding plan also under consideration to bail out us soybean farmers sidelined by China amid stalled trade talks.\par \par \uc2 Another country still on the ropes, Canada. Today's meeting with the prime minister also ended without a deal announcement.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If he is a great man and you want to do a deal with Canada, why are not you?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because I want to be a great man too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The Trump administration is reportedly looking at ways to further shrink the government's balance sheet by selling off pieces of the $1.6 trillion student loan debt portfolio. It's spurring some logistical and legal concerns, and also questions about what would happen to borrower protections.\par \par \uc2 The White House referred questions to the Department of Education, which gave an out of office reply amid the shutdown. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Jacqui Heinrich, on the North Lawn for us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still no deal to reopen the federal government, the shutdowns effects now echoing across the nation miles from Washington. Here is senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Impatience with the shutdown putting more pressure on Congress.\par \par \uc2 MATT FEEMAN, AIR TRAVELER: I could expect that there would be delays because of the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice o