2 TURNER: Welcome back. New York's democratic mayoral nominee, Zohran Mamdani has taken heat tonight for a statement he made on the second anniversary of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Mamdani acknowledged that the terror attack happened, but he spent the majority of his time denouncing Israel and the U.S. Republican Congressman \uc2 Randy Fine\uc2  called the statement nauseating.\par \par \uc2 Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 ' office said it showed that Mamdani does not believe in Israel's right to exist. \uc2 Israel's foreign ministry\uc2  today said that Mamdani is now acting as a mouthpiece for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  propaganda.\par \par \uc2 Today, of course, marks the solemn second anniversary of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror massacre in Israel. Sirens blared across Israel today as residents remembered the dead and those still in captivity. Here at home, President Trump met with the family members of an American Israeli soldier who was taken hostage and ultimately killed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONEN NEUTRA, FATHER OF DECEASED HOSTAGE: We came to thank him for his leadership, for his determination to bring this conflict to an end, to bring the hostages back. We said he understands, and we acknowledge that this will be the key to start the cease fire and to bring peace instability in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Those are the parents of Omer Neutra. I had the opportunity to interview both of them on this program.\par \par \uc2 Coming this evening as well, the latest on the ongoing negotiations between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on ending the war in Gaza. Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has the latest details tonight from Tel Aviv. A warning for you that some of the images you'll see in this report are graphic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Exactly two years ago, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists stormed the border, killing young families living in small kibbutz and hunted down young people attending an outdoor music festival. The attackers killed more than 1,200 Israelis and took 251 hostage. FOX's Trey Yingst was on the scene shortly after the massacre.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: The kitchen floors are stained with blood. The soldiers here tell us they found bodies that appeared to be executed. People with hands tied behind their backs, mothers holding their small children, bodies decapitated.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Forty-eight hostages remain in Gaza, 20 thought to be alive. Aviva Siegel was held 51 days.\par \par \uc2 AVIVA SIEGEL, FORMER HOSTAGE: I'll never forget the picture of -- that I've got in my head of the terrorists telling me to come down this ladder, it's very narrow, to go underneath the ground. I'm shaking. I'll never forget his smile.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Her husband Keith was held for 484 days.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: We were tortured. We were starved. They used to eat in front of us. I lost 10 kilos. Keith came home looking like a skeleton.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Tens of thousands of Gazans have been killed in the aftermath by the Israeli military, which now controls 80 percent of the Gaza Strip. Israel says it does not target civilians. President Trump wants a quick end to the war.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: I think there's a possibility that we could have peace in the Middle East. It's something even beyond the Gaza situation.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Negotiators spent today going over withdrawal maps. The prime minister of Qatar, who has the most influence over \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , will join the talks tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): President Trump reportedly told a former American hostage and his family that the next 48 hours in the talks are critical. He sent his special envoys, \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , to Egypt to close the deal. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jennifer, it's good to have you there on the ground on this important anniversary. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IFAT KALDERON, COUSIN OF FORMER HOSTAGE: I want to call out Netanyahu because he's my prime minister. You've got to sign this agreement.\par \par \uc2 UDI GOREN, COUSIN OF DECEASED HOSTAGE: We, the western world, must make sure That this deal comes through this time all the way till the end.\par \par \uc2 VICKY COHEN, MOTHER OF HOSTAGE: We are afraid to be Disappointed again. But I have hope.\par \par \uc2 SHAY DICKMANN, COUSIN OF DECEASED HOSTAGE: We deserve it. Our neighbors. Deserve it. We want this war to end.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Tonight, we have the very special opportunity to speak to a survivor of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity and torture about his experiences and the effort he is now taking to bring the remaining captives home to their families. Eli Sharabi was held for 491 days by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists after being kidnaped on October 7th in 2023. His wife, his young daughters, were murdered by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , as was ultimately his brother. He is now the author of a tremendous new book about what happened. It's called "Hostage." He joins us now. Eli, thank you for joining us on the anniversary. It is --\par \par \uc2 ELI SHARABI, FORMER HOSTAGE: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We're at this inflection point, it seems, in the negotiations, in the prospect of ending this war. I think a lot of people feel very hopeful. But it's also a time, as you know, when many people choose to deny past atrocities committed against Jews. And so I think for the historical record, it's important for us to hear from you about your experience and what happened to you on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 SHARABI: Well, they got into our house four fours after the missiles started, and, you know, very quickly arrived to the door of the safe room, opened it, and start to shoot. We jumped on our daughters, covered them. And they stopped after 10 seconds. We immediately said to them that we have British passports. And we presume I would be kidnaped. And that's what really happened. Two of them grabbed me, and after a few minutes they took me out from the house. I just turned my head to my daughters and said to them, I'll come back to them.\par \par \uc2 And that's it. That's what I remember from that day, from it was the last time I've seen them. And from that moment they put me in the car towards. Gaza, and I've been there for 491 days, most of them in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  tunnels 50 meters underground.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And unimaginable experience for the rest of the world. I think one of the most amazing things about you, Eli, is that you write about how you decided at some point in your captivity that no matter what happened, you wanted to live, you wanted to survive the horror and the trauma and the nightmare that you were going through. You write this, "I don't want to survive just for them. I don't want to live just for them," meaning your family. "I want to live for myself, too, for me, Eli Sharabi. I want to live. I love life, I crave it, I want to breathe life, to walk free, to return to the open skies, to go back home, to find purpose."\par \par \uc2 It's incredibly poignant because it's hard to imagine being in your situation, knowing you may have lost your family members and still wanting to live in spite of it.\par \par \uc2 SHARABI: I don't think I have the privilege to do differently. For 491 days my family stopped their life and my friends to support them. And they fought, they all fought for me. And I feel that I don't have the privilege to stay all day in bed and cry.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I want to ask you about the prime minister, Prime Minister Netanyahu's message today. He wrote to the citizens of Israel, "We are in a fateful days of decision. We will continue to work to achieve all the goals of the war, the return of all the kidnaped, the elimination of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  regime, and the promise that Gaza will no longer pose a threat to Israel."\par \par \uc2 We don't know how long these negotiations are going to continue. The U.S. and Israel both hope no more than a few days. But can you really envision a future when Gaza does not pose a threat to Israel? Do you really believe that can happen?\par \par \uc2 SHARABI: I wish. I wish we'll have a new Middle East that we can, you know, live in peace with all our neighbors. I think it's most of the Israelis want this peace, but I'm most want, first of all, the 48 remain hostages. Among them is my brother, and my friend. And we're waiting to all of them.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Eli, I want to make sure that I give you a moment to tell us what you want us to know about Lianne and Noya and Yahel and your brother. How do you honor their memory? What can we do to support you?\par \par \uc2 SHARABI: Lianne used to like very much she likes to sing. And she was very, very British and proud to be British. And we raised a wonderful two daughters, Noya, that they used to work with autistic kids. And she loved every moment from that. And she learned from them a lot. And she was all heart. And Yahel, my youngest daughter, used to do loads of extreme activities like skydiving and scuba diving, and she used to play football. I always, when I remember them in every moment in my life, they for me, they're smiling and happy. And my, my brother Yossi used to surf and love every moment from that and used to live with me in, kibbutz Be'eri. And that was a big privilege for me to raise my family alongside my brother's family.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, Eli, may their memory be a blessing to you, to the entire world. Thank you for talking to us today about what you have been going through. Everybody at home, you should read his book. It's an incredibly important memoir about what happened. It 