nd she put on quite a show because she personalized things so directly.\par \par \uc2 But again, Republican or democrat, you know, just Wanting information and accountability from your public officials, from your chief law enforcement officer, the attorney general of the United States. Zero on Epstein. Zero on the indictment of the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  director. Zero on sending troops into American cities. Zero on Tom Homan allegedly taking $50,000 in bribe. Zero on the possibility of Letitia James being indicted. You just think, well, what is this? So now we don't hold our public officials to any level of accountability.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Annie, what did you think of the hearing?\par \par \uc2 ANNIE LINSKEY, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, you know, I saw this hearing very much as Attorney General Bondi performing for an audience of one who is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Bondi has a really close relationship with Trump, but she has had some struggles with Trump's base, some of whom have turned against her and have called for her to step aside.\par \par \uc2 And today, I think that she had to prove that she could be in that hearing, stand up to Democrats, mix it up, and drive a strong, at times quite personal message geared towards some, this kind of performance that Trump would like. And, by all accounts, she appears to have achieved that.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I would say that all hearings on Capitol Hill at this point are political theater for a specified, targeted audience.\par \par \uc2 M.K., I want to ask you about the shutdown, because it's raging on. There is now debate about among lawmakers, about whether or not furloughed workers are going to get back pay. Take a listen to President Trump earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is it the White House's position that furloughed workers should be paid for their backpay?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: I would say it depends on who we're talking about. I can tell you this, the Democrats have put a lot of people in great risk and jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you identified programs to eliminate under this shutdown?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, sure. I think they made a big mistake. We're able to take out billions and billions of dollars of waste, fraud, and abuse, and they've handed it to us on a silver platter.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What did you make of that exchange?\par \par \uc2 Yes. Look, Republicans met the demand of Democrat from time immemorial to give them a clear C.R. and they can come along in that clean C.R. I think the problem for Democrats is that they are negotiating with \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  who has dreamed of this since a small child, and Trump, believe it or not, is the good cop in this situation. Now that may - you mya be able to convince him to start negotiating on some things. But frankly, I think they should encourage Democrats to come along. And then you can have the discussion about the pet issues that you would like to have. And I think the American people would welcome a discussion about the fact that the Affordable Care Act in fact is not affordable, as even "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " editorial page had to admit the other day because it did not meet the promises that they sold us on many years ago.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Annie, real quick, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  on our air earlier was talking about the fact that non-essential workers could essentially all be laid off during this shutdown.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: These are some of the threats that the White House and \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  have been making. But Republicans are, especially Republicans on the Hill, are making a broader point to the White House at times that Republicans tend to be blamed for these shutdowns. That's usually what happens. They come into this shutdown on sort of on high ground. And they worry that the sense of the White House relishing in layoffs could change that dynamic and actually hurt them, es