rted an eighth house has collapsed on the Outer Banks of North Carolina in Buxton.\par \par \uc2 The illegal immigrant who has become a focal point of the fight over President Trump's crackdown has been denied asylum. A federal immigration judge has rejected the bid from Kilmar Abrego Garcia. However, he has 30 days to go to the Board of Immigration Appeals.\par \par \uc2 Abrego Garcia came to the United States illegally as a teenager. He was deported to his native El Salvador, and then returned. He was then arrested and charged with human smuggling. The Trump administration is attempting to deport him to a third country.\par \par \uc2 The former Des Moines public school superintendent made his initial appearance before a judge today on a criminal complaint charging him with being an illegal alien in possession of firearms. Ian Roberts will be -- will remain detained in federal custody pending further proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The complaint alleges that Roberts attempted to hide from ICE officers who surveilled his residence and then followed his vehicle. Roberts was found with the help of local law enforcement and a canine near his abandoned vehicle. A Glock firearm found in the jeep below the driver's seat.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ says a search of Robert's home has turned up three additional firearms. Attorney General Pam Bondi says 93 arrests have been made since a federal task force began operating in Memphis. She says that includes a Tren de Aragua gang member, a man wanted for raping a child and a suspect accused of assaulting a federal officer. Bondi says 28 illegal guns have been seized.\par \par \uc2 Well, there are new revelations tonight about then President Biden's mental health, unearthed note cards from a 2023 news conference suggest he knew what a reporter would ask him about. There are also photographic reminders of top Democrats and other visitors. Correspondent Mark Meredith has got that story for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): President Biden routinely relied on note cards while in office. Now, House Republicans say those same cards, including these examples obtained by Fox, raise new questions about the then president's mental fitness.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'll take your questions, and as usual, folks, they gave me a list of the people I'm going to call on.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): The cards, spotted on occasion by press photographers, listed people at public events, even highlighting high profile people widely known in politics, world affairs and Hollywood. The cards mention names and titles, one telling Biden, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was in the room, Denzel Washington was nearby, and that his Secretary of State was at the same event.\par \par \uc2 A former Biden staffer tells Fox the cards are standard operating procedure for briefing materials, but House Republicans believe it's part of a broader cover up. Congressman James Comer is accusing Democrats of hiding Biden's decline to abuse pardon powers.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): No one has definitively proven to anybody on the Oversight Committee that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself was the one making the decisions on these auto pens.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): Critics are also curious about one particular card showing a potential question from \uc2 ABC News\uc2 . The reporter asked a different question. But critics say the photo sparks real concerns about journalism ethics.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It also shows that the media were giving their questions to the Biden White House in order to get called on.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): In 2023, the White House denied it was getting any advanced notice.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, look, we do not have specific questions in advance. That's not something that we do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Biden staffers would ask outlets including Fox about topics of interest before news events. Previous administrations have used similar tactics.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the House Oversight Committee is writing a report about its finding on the Biden White House and John, it is expected to come out by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I spent a lot of time at the White House, and I got asked many times what's on your mind. And I would say, you'll see.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: You throw a curve ball at the last second.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Exactly. Thank you, Mark. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, are you and your family addicted to ultra processed foods? We will have a report on some of the dangers that are looming.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami, as health officials say, nearly 100 people on a Royal Caribbean cruise ship that is docked in South Florida are ill with norovirus. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  says 94 passengers and four crew members aboard the Serenade Of The Seas came down with a highly contagious virus. The outbreak was reported during a 13-day voyage from San Diego, with stops in Mexico and Costa Rica.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in New York City as two \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  regional jets collide on the taxiway at LaGuardia Airport. A statement from Delta described it as a low speed collision. The wing of one aircraft prepared to take off to Roanoke, Virginia, Wednesday night, hit the fuselage of an aircraft arriving from Charlotte, North Carolina. One flight attendant sustained minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Louisville from WDRB. One of the big stories there tonight, a local woman wins the Saint Jude's Dream Home contest. Jenny Sorrel's was picked from the 8500 tickets sold. The new home is worth $482,000. Proceeds from the contest go to benefit Saint Jude Children's Research Hospital.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We are learning new information tonight about the alleged dangers of ultra processed foods. A new study confirming alarming rates of addiction, which experts say can lead to obesity, diabetes, depression and heart disease.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff takes a look tonight. She's here with us in the studio. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, good evening, John, good to be with you. A lot of this has been talked about in the context of what we're feeding our children. The new research out of the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  finds that older Americans are the most addictive to the addictive, heavy nutrient light products known as Ultra processed foods.\par \par \uc2 The study finds that UPF addiction is far outpacing reliance on alcohol or tobacco, especially so for women, age 50 to 64 where 21 percent qualified as having a UPF addiction. That was determined with the same criteria used to diagnose substance use disorders. For men in the same age group, it was 10 percent. High consumption was then associated with poor perception of mental and physical health and feelings of isolation.\par \par \uc2 According to the study, it comes down to this population being in a developmentally sensitive stage of life during the 70s and 80s, quote", precisely when tobacco owned food manufacturers were shaping the market with addictive UPFs."\par \par \uc2 And this raises concerns for addiction levels to come. The Trump administration's MAHA report released last month underscores that nearly 70 percent of American child's calories come from UPFs, which is far more than what older generations consumed as kids.\par \par \uc2 As for why the addiction rate higher in older women versus men, researchers theorize that aggressive weight control marketing in the 80s could be to blame for that, selling women and girls on low fat, microwaveable, carb heavy products that only made things worse, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And other news out of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  today, they approved another generic form of the abortion pill. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Yes, another, because this will be the second here. It's Avita Solutions LLC, they announced this approval on their website today for a generic version of mifepristone. That's the drug used to terminate pregnancies through the 10-week mark. This approval has sparked a lot of criticism from Republicans and the pro-life community. And it comes as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is reportedly now reviewing the safety of mifepristone, so a lot going on there.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, thank you for the report and the update, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And in more news about your health tonight, growing concerns over the effects of marijuana use and gaming on young people. A new study suggests heavy use of pot by adolescents can increase their risk of developing mental problems such as schizophrenia. Correspondent Alexis McAdams has got details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Young men and frequent marijuana use is a bad combination, that's according to the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  and numerous other studies which find using too much pot puts users at higher risk for developing schizophrenia, especially among young men.\par \par \uc2 DR. YASMIN HURD, MOUNT SINAI ADDICTION INSTITUTE: We do see a very strong association with cannabis use disorder developing an addiction and with psychosis.\par \par \uc2 Irrational thinking, delusions, paranoia that does sometimes result in aggression and agitation.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: This comes after two recent shootings had one thing in common. Back in August, police say Robin Westman shot up a Catholic school in Minneapolis, killing two kids and injuring 18 others. While there is no confirmed direct link, Westman, who worked at a pot shop, reportedly blamed marijuana in part for the killing spree. The transgender shooter writing in a journal in part "Gender and weed f-ed up my head."\par \par \uc2 Also, officials say the gunman who shot up an ICE facility in Dallas, killing two detainees, was as heavy pot smoker. Friends say Joshua Jahn was obsessed with playing video games, reportedly logging thousands of hours playing mostly shooting games.\par \par \uc2 Marijuana is a lot stronger than it used to be. Back in the 60s, experts say THC potency was around two to four percent. Now many vapes at the dispensary have 90 percent THC.\par \par \uc2 HURD: The cannabis today is a completely different drug than I think a lot of people who voted for legalization around the country realized.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So it's a story we'll continue to look into, John. And President Trump said back in August his administration was going to decide within a few weeks on whether to reclassify marijuana as it a less dangerous drug so it really wouldn't have as many criminal penalties. But still, we're in October, no decision yet, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The more powerful it becomes, the more the dangers increase. Alexis McAdams for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says ruling elites in Europe are whipping up hysteria by saying a war with Moscow is near. Those comments come as there are growing questions tonight about the readiness of European nations to deal with Russia in general and the threats posed by modern weaponry in particular. Drones are becoming a serious issue. Correspondent Steve Harrigan shows us why tonight from Moscow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The 21 Russian drones that violated Polish airspace on September 10th mark the first time \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces engaged and shot down Russian assets since the war in Ukraine began, a sign, some analysts say, of what is to come.\par \par \uc2 ALINA POLYAKOVA, CENTER FOR EUROPEAN POLICY ANALYSIS: This is how the Russians do war. They test softly, a little bit. They watch for a reaction. And if the reaction is soft, then they will keep going. They will keep testing, testing, testing until they make the ultimate decision for a confrontation.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The \uc2 NATO\uc2  response was immediate. More than $1 billion in military hardware scrambled, including Polish F-16s, Dutch F-35s, a Belgium airbus tanker, and a German patriot missile system. Yet the drones were able to breach \uc2 NATO\uc2  airspace. The targets they faced turned out to be Russian Gerbera drones made of Styrofoam and plywood, unarmed, built at a cost of under $10,000 each. The penetration and response raised questions of how prepared \uc2 NATO\uc2  is for modern warfare.\par \par \uc2 POLYAKOVA: The gaps and the vulnerabilities are huge. We just saw that, I think, in very stark terms with the Russian drones that landed on Polish land, in Polish airspace. How did the alliance defend those? It was jets and missiles. This is not how you defend against a drone attack.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Repeated Russian airspace violations have given urgency to calls for a drone wall across eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 MARK RUTTE\uc2 , NATO SECRETARY GENERAL: The drone wall initiative is timely and necessary because, in the end, we cannot spend millions of euros or dollars on missiles to take out a drone, which only cost a couple of thousand dollars. So we need a drone wall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): Instead of a physical wall, plans envisage radar and jammers to track and intercept incoming drones, John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Steve Harrigan in Moscow tonight. Steve, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE, (R-LA) HOUSE MAJORITY LEADER: House Democrats and Senate Democrats need to stop holding temper tantrums.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Unfortunately, Republicans have shown zero interest in even having a conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It's the Democrats' fault. There is zero excuse for them to shut down our government right now.\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN, (D-PA): My constituents know that this has been caused by the Republicans' failure to govern. But my constituents don't care about the finger pointing. They just want us to govern.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON, (R-LA) HOUSE SPEAKER: People say, why aren't you negotiating with Schumer and Jeffries? Because I quite literally have nothing to negotiate. There is nothing I could pull out of the bill that was a Republican priority.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAKE AUCHINCLOSS, (D-MA): The Republicans have control of the White House, of the Senate, and of the House. They are in charge. They are accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The finger pointing and the debate continue over the government shutdown. Let's dig deeper on it by bringing in our panel, former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye, Tom Bevan, Real Clear Politics cofounder and president, and "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers.\par \par \uc2 I want to start off the panel with a couple reality checks. First on this idea of money for healthcare for illegal aliens. Democrats say it doesn't exist. But let's put up on the screen the clause in their continuing resolution that they wanted to get passed, which eliminates restrictions contained in the big, beautiful bill. It basically restores money for undocumented immigrants paroled into the country or here under TPS, r Restores full emergency room funding under Medicaid for illegal aliens, and even \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  admitted last night yes, there is some money for illegal aliens. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 , (D-CA): The amount of money that actually is going towards people who are undocumented is such a small portion of the Medicaid cuts or the Affordable Care Act, if at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It's such a small portion. But Francesca, it would appear to be, despite Democrats' protestations that it's not, it would be there.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": So part of the disagreement here is over the point where you are making about undocumented is that some of these immigrants who came into the country came in and then the Biden administration said that they could stay. So they are legally in the country regardless how they entered into --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: They're lawfully present is the term.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: They're lawfully present inside the United States.\par \par \uc2 Regardless, John, the White House thinks at this point that they are winning this messaging war with Democrats right now. They say that they're taking this, you know, day by day. They think historically the party seen as voting against the resolution is the one that gets blamed for the shutdown. So they are waiting to see what happens here.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " poll that came out today, there's some warning signs for the White House in that. You have a situation where Democrats are saying -- or, sorry, rather, independents are saying that they blame the president and the White House and Republicans for this shutdown. And then generally speaking among a broader electorate, you're seeing that more Americans are blaming Republicans in this particular situation than Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Does blame really matter at this point?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, COFOUNDER, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: I don't know that it does. I mean, eventually it will. I mean, if we look at shutdowns past, certainly when Republicans were -- they took the brunt of the previous shutdowns and it cost them politically. But both sides are existing in their own information bubbles on this, and both sides think they have the upper hand in the politics. And I'm sure their supporters are both in favor of what Republicans are doing and what Democrats are doing. And the question is, what are independents going to do? And how much is that really going to move the needle overall?\par \par \uc2 But I do think Republicans are winning at least this initial messaging war. And we saw, to your point, Democrats said first it's a lie, and then said, well, OK, maybe it's true a little bit but not as true as J.D. Vance is saying.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So Doug, you've always got a very balanced perspective on things. What do you think should happen here?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Look, I don't think any of this is dollars and cents. And what's interesting is we often hear that history doesn't repeat itself but it does rhyme. So let's talk about the rhyme. October 2nd, 2013, I was working in that building behind you, and we were dealing with a shutdown. We were dealing on our second day of a shutdown, just like we are now, over the word "fight." And it's because enough Republicans in the House Republican conference, led on by some Republican senator, said we need fight and defund Obamacare. We didn't have a strategy to land a punch, knock down our opponent, win the fight. We just had to show that we wanted to fight.\par \par \uc2 Where are the Democrats now? We got to fight. We got to fight \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We've got to stand up to him. If you look at the interviews today that Hakeem Jefferies did, he has no plan to land a punch, win a round, or win the fight. He says, if we stay around long enough we're going to win. That's not a strategy. You don't win politically if you don't have a strategy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The other reality check comes in the form of what President Trump was saying on Truth Social about getting together with his OMB director, \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , to talk about changing the fails of the federal government. He said, "I have a meeting today with \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , he of Project 2025 fame, to determine which of the many Democrat agencies, most of which are a political scam, he recommends to be cut and whether or not those cuts will be temporary or permanent." Remember what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said a year ago. I know nothing about Project 2025. I have no idea who is behind it. I disagree with some of the things they are saying and some of the things they are saying are absolutely ridiculous and abysmal. So I'm wondering, Francesca, did he just learn about Project 2025 in the last year, or did he know about it all along?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And it's also not clear, John, whether or not he was actually sitting there with \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  and going line by line himself and deciding what is or isn't being cut here, because, of course, prior to this the Office of Management and Budget as well as the Office of Personnel Management, they've sent out messages to these various agencies saying no, you send us plans for who you think that you should cut, and then we'll approve them.\par \par \uc2 And I will also note that one of the messages that was sent out last weekend told them oh, by the way, we might also bring people back, too, to have the minimum number required to fulfill the statutory mandate. So it's not clear how many of these, even if they were fired, would be permanent, and how much of this is a, you know, a strategy to try and get Democrats to the table.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: But Tom, this could be much different than shutdowns in the past where people got furloughed and then, for the most part, they all got brought back. People might get furloughed, then they might get laid off, and they might lose their job.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: That's right, it could be very different. And I do think that is a concern the Democrats will bring them back to the table and will eventually end this thing, probably sooner rather than later, because the Trump administration, again, feels like they have the upper hand, feels like they're driving the messaging more, and now are going to see this as an opportunity to do further what they were doing during DOGE. But, again, under different circumstances and ones that are much more politically favorable to them.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, we talked to the Interior Secretary \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  about it. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2 , (R) INTERIOR SECRETARY: It is easier to make cuts during a time when the government is shut down. And any one of us that have been in these positions that have worked in the private sector can see the amount of inefficiency in government. And if the Democrats are going to give us this chance, let's make sure we take advantage of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It's the last part of what he said there, if the Democrats are going to give us this chance to change the structure of government, let's take advantage of that. Democrats have to be looking at that saying, oh, my God, what kind of cuts did we just hand them?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: First, I'm pro-Doug. I'm always pro-Doug. But to Tom's point, I think the longer this goes on, the reality of the Democrats sort of Jedi mind-tricking themselves into fight is enough and fight is a strategy will backfire on them. Because if Trump goes through with exactly what he's talking about, \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  is talking about, \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  has just said it in that peace, then essentially they have enabled -- forget the politics -- policies that they will howl over.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, and we have seen this already with New York City, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is grabbing \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's fists and he's playing the stop hitting yourself, stop hitting yourself game. You need a strategy to win here. And I t's not clear that the Democrats have that. Fight is not an answer. It's not a strategy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: If they lose this fight and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  restructures the federal government, it will give the Democrats something to fight about next year.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: There is another vote coming up tomorrow night, so we'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We'll see if there is any defections.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: We'll see if there are any people who flip.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: They've got three right now from the other side. We'll see if they get more.\par \par \uc2 Stand by. Much more ahead. Up next, then President Biden's mental health and the discovery of some very interesting note cards.\par \par \uc2 And then a female student bucking the odds to make the team with the boys and become a starter.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'll take your questions. And as usual, folks, they gave me a list.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Was the president up for the job?\par \par \uc2 STEVE RICHETTI, FORMER BIDEN COUNSELOR: I don't want to -- of course he was.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ladies and gentlemen, they gave me a list here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Does he have to rely on those notecards?\par \par \uc2 JULIE SU, ACTING LABOR SECRETARY: No, he doesn't have notecards. In fact, he is not only laser focused on the problems that we talked about, he is funny. He is engaged. He's focused. He knows we have work to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Did she say he doesn't have notecards? Well, the National Archives released some of them. We got them. And we are back with our panel.\par \par \uc2 So here is what FOX News Digital wrote about the note cards, "Five different palm cards, which are hand sized note cards frequently used by politicians quick reminders or talking points during public events, especially while on the campaign trail, were uncovered amid an investigation. Four of the five cards obtained by FOX Digital are stamped with the disclaimer reading "President has seen."\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of presidents use note cards, or they use paper notes or whatever. But these, let's put one of them up on the screen, were unusually detailed. They had photographs of people like Denzel Washington, chef Jose Andre, \uc2 David Rubenstein\uc2  of the Kennedy Center, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , with little bio notes. Doug, is that a little weird?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: This is what staff does. It's your job as a staffer to go as detailed as possible. Maybe you don't need a picture of \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . I think we all, even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knows what she looks like. But this is what the staff's job is to do. But it also highlights, I think, a challenge that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had -- an overprotective staff. We never saw him in public for reasons that I think some of what was understandable. But what we just heard the secretary say, you know, he doesn't need anything. It turns out he did. Most politicians you do. If you act normally, sometimes you are able to have a normal outcome. This just shows how they didn't ability normal so often in trying to be so overprotective of Biden.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: One of the other note cards had Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Senate Majority Whip \uc2 Richard Durbin\uc2 , their picture on it. I mean, if you don't know who they are, you haven't been in Washington for very long. But again, with the photographs and everything, but we were talking off cameras, and maybe this isn't so unusual for people.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And President Trump is using a teleprompter, right? We just saw last week at the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: He has had notes at some events as well.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Right. And, John, you and I covered the White House together during the first Trump administration when he was coming with lists of journalists as well who he was supposed to call on and in those press conferences, too. He was very traditional in that way, and I think President Biden was, too.\par \par \uc2 What I think is quite different this time, actually, is their approach to some of these things where now you see him taking these questions in the Oval Office, so you can't come in with a list of names, right, because he is just taking questions as they are being fired off for him. He's not even having a lot of times as kind of those press conferences, right, where they even have lists of journalists. He is just calling people all across the room even when he is having those.\par \par \uc2 And traditionally we saw with other presidents with those joint press conferences with world leaders, that they would come in with lists of people they were planning to call on, too. So in many ways it highlights how different, I guess, this White House is operating even from Trump one as well as President Biden.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I will say while I have seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  use notes in the past, the way he freelances and adlibs 99.9 percent of what he does really is different than the most other president.\par \par \uc2 Tom, one of the things that really came to line though in these notes was the fact that the president, Biden, had a note card with the picture of an \uc2 ABC News\uc2  correspondent on it that had the answer to a question that they thought was going to be asked. Here it is up on the screen here. This was a talking point around an idea that they believed that he was going to be asked. The question that was actually asked was quite different than this, but it would suggest that the White House had a heads up as to what possible topics were going to be.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: It does. And there was actually another one from "The L.A. Times" that is pretty famous which was pretty specific question, actually, pretty detailed question, and was almost exactly what she asked the president. And that led people to believe that they were giving advanced notice to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Now, journalists say they don't do that, or they weren't doing that, but at the same time, it raises questions. And just, the entire note card thing itself, while other administrations, other presidents may have done it, you can't separate it from the fact that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was not only staff was overprotective, not only had issues, though, but that the administration would go out, as we just saw, and lie to the American people about this. There was a report that he didn't know who \uc2 George Clooney\uc2  was at this fundraiser. You know, those kinds of things, you can't separate that from the whole piece of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his fitness which played a part in the campaign and obviously a part in him getting out of that campaign. But the lying about it was, I think, what is most offensive to people.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, it's the cover-up. It's not the crime, right?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Always. And I can tell you, in the Capitol, certainly in the first shutdown, 2013 shutdown, we knew not what the questions were going to be but certainly what the topics would be. And what I would say to the majority leader all the time was Chad Pergram is going to ask you X. Didn't know the specific question, but we always knew the topic.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I remember, and this probably happened to you too, Francesca, that the White House would always probe for information, what's on your mind today? I remember getting the morning phone call, what are you covering today?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And if you're the White House, what you don't want to have happen, we were talking about this in the green room, is what you don't want to have where is Jackie? Is Jackie here? moment, right. So you want to have the photos and you want everybody to know who is in the room.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, and not our Jacqui, by the way. Folks, thanks for coming in, really appreciate it. Great to see you all.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I play football because I love the sport. I have loved the sport since I was a kid. And people told me I couldn't play it, so I wanted to do it anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: This one really turns the table. A Fort Lauderdale high school student defying the odds by playing on the boys' varsity, varsity, football team. Sariyah Lewis is a starting defensive tackle and relishes the opportunity to put the hurt on her opponents and prove her doubters wrong. She says she that wants her peers to see her as an equal or somebody they can depend on as a teammate. Sariyah wants to go to school for law and business, and she says she wants to become a sports attorney.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm John Roberts in Washington. Stay tuned, because THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020251003ela200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Teens Linked To Congressional Intern Death; Victim Family Mourns Loss Of Son Killed In Shooting; Hegseth: Drug Cartels Are "No Different Than Al Qaeda"; June Jobs Number In The Red For First Time Since 2020; \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Says Foreign Troops Would Be Legitimate Targets; Efforts To Find Unaccompanied Migrant Children; Specialized Technology Complicated Timeline; Latest Jobs Report Show Labor Slowdown; Putin-Trump Talks Have Yet To Produce Deal; Trump Administration Changes Name To War Department\b0\par\par\uc2 Aishah Hasnie, Chad Pergram, Lucas Tomlinson, Edward Lawrence,  Brooke Taylor, Alex Hogan, Griff Jenkins, Bret Baier \par \uc2 7,886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: All right. That does it for us. Thanks for joining us here on THE FIVE. Have a great night and weekend.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm Aishah Hasnie, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Tensions rise as the White House continues its cartel crackdown. The U.S., sending a stealth fighter squadron to the Caribbean, while Venezuelan planes buzz an American Navy ship.\par \par \uc2 Then, we take you inside the War Room where federal agents are tracking minors who entered this country illegally during the Biden administration, and then, were released to unvetted sponsors.\par \par \uc2 And our "WHATEVER HAPPENED TO" segment tonight, looks at President Trump's efforts to regain control of the Panama Canal.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Trump said a short time ago, the nation's capital, now has virtually no crime since his recent crackdown began. But tonight, a vivid illustration of the crime crisis that plagued Washington just a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 Two 17-year-olds are in custody right here in D.C. for the murder of a Capitol Hill intern. Jailen Lucas and Kelvin Thomas Jr. are now charged with killing Eric Tarpinian-Jachym. A third suspect remains at large.\par \par \uc2 The young man's parents spoke to Fox News today. His mother says officials must stop coddling those criminals. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram has more details for us tonight. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Aishah, good evening. The murder of congressional intern, Eric Tarpinian-Jachym epitomizes what Republicans say is wrong with Washington, D.C. Police charge the teenage duo with first degree murder. Authorities are prosecuting them as adults.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, UNITED STATES ATTORNEY, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: The \uc2 D.C. Council\uc2  has coddled young criminals for years.\par \par \uc2 His death is a stark reminder of how fragile life is and how violence too often visits us in the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Pirro says the suspects faced legal trouble before Tarpinian-Jachym died in June, caught in the cross fire of a drive by shooting.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA TARPINIAN-JACHYM, MOTHER: This should have never happened, and it happens all over America, and it needs to stop.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Tarpinian-Jachym attended the \uc2 University of Massachusetts Amherst\uc2 . He interned for GOP, Kansas Representative \uc2 Ron Estes\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. RON ESTES (R-KS): Our first awareness that something was wrong was, you know, Tuesday morning, when Eric didn't come to work. Our staff was calling Eric and reaching out to him, and actually, finally, one of the police officers answered the phone, his cell phone that we were calling.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Congressional Republicans are trying to write a crime bill, which may apply outside Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): That couple of the areas that we're looking at, specifically, and initially, is juvenile crime problem, which is a big thing around the country, that there is some ideas on how to address, and also, these restrictive policing policies that have done real harm in major cities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, police just announced they are searching for a third murder suspect that's 18-year-old, Naquan Antonio Lucas. There is a $50,000 reward for information leading to his arrest. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Chad Pergram, live for us from the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The U.S. tonight is ordering a squadron of a dozen stealth fighter jets to the Caribbean to conduct operations against drug cartels. Of course, this all comes as Venezuela sends a message to the Trump administration too.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson is at the White House tonight for us. Good evening to you, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 The White House has approved the deployment of 10 F-35 stealth fighter jets to Puerto Rico, after a pair of Venezuelan F-16 jets buzz an American guided missile destroyer in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 President Trump sent the following warning to Venezuela moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If they do put us in a dangerous position, they will be shut down.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): The Pentagon called the flyover of armed Venezuelan F-16s over the USS Jason Dunham, highly provocative. The flyover took place two days after U.S. forces launched a strike on a Venezuelan drug boat in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 President Trump said all 11 members of the Tren de Aragua gang on board were killed. The state department declared the group a foreign terrorist organization in February.\par \par \uc2 Republican member of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 , called the strike justified.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2  (R-OK): The boat had been warned to stop. They chose not to. They were running, clearly running, clearly running, drugs. We said, been tracking it since it left Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): Many Democrats called the strike a violation of international law.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA): They think they can get away with this. God knows what they are going to do next. So, that's it we're looking to right now is, and there is reason to believe that this was not a legal action.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): President Trump's defense secretary says the actions are justified after 400,000 Americans died from drug overdoses during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, UNITED STATES DEFENSE SECRETARY: A foreign terrorist organization poisoning your people with drugs coming from a drug cartel is no different than al-Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): At the height of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, the U.S. military carried out drone strikes in seven different countries. Iraq, Syria, Afghanistan, Libya, Pakistan, Yemen, and Somalia. Over 500 in-all killing an estimated 3500 people, including hundreds of civilians.\par \par \uc2 President Trump also made it official, changing the name of the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're going Department of War.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): This marks the second time in American history the Department of War nomenclatures has been used to describe the Cabinet overseeing the U.S. military, the first time was back in 1789. Of course, it needs congressional approval, the name change. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Lucas, tonight, we are hearing -- us about a new incoming report from \uc2 Health and Human Services\uc2  on autism. What do you know about that?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Well, Aishah, according to the Wall Street Journal, a new report from \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  and \uc2 HHS\uc2  is -- this new report is going to be on autism, and saying that a vitamin B9 deficiency and taking the pain reliever Tylenol during pregnancy could be linked to autism.\par \par \uc2 And we just received this statement from \uc2 HHS\uc2 . It says, "We are using gold standard signs to get to the bottom of America's unprecedented rise in autism rates until we release the final report, any claims about its contents are nothing more than speculation." And it's not clear when this final report will be released. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. We'll wait for it. Lucas Tomlinson, live at the White House. Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have new economic data tonight. What many are considering a disappointing August jobs report could have one possible upside. U.S. employers added just 22,000 positions last month, and that's down from 79,000 in July.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked up to 4.3 percent. The news seems to reinforce the idea, though, that an interest rate cut is coming soon.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors were jittery today, so, the Dow lost 220 points. The S&P 500 was off 21. NASDAQ fell seven.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost one-third of a percentage point. The S&P 500 was up a third. The NASDAQ was up just over one.\par \par \uc2 Our Edward Lawrence of Fox Business joins us now from the North Lawn to explain what this all means for all of us. Good evening to you, Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Yes, 22,000 jobs created in August, basically locks in the first rate cut of the year by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . When you look inside the numbers, it opens up the conversation that markets now believe that there will be a rate -- a quarter of a point rate cut in September, on September 17th, but then another cut in October, and another cut at the December meeting.\par \par \uc2 June marks the first negative number for job growth since December of 2020. Now, it adds fuel to the president's claim that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may be late on the first rate cut. Still, President Trump started getting in front of the weak jobs numbers. Last night at a dinner with the nation's tech leaders, he continued to reassure Americans about 30 minutes ago. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, one thing we have is, you know, we have the interest rates are too high. That was, you know, matter of the Fed. But the other thing is, so many different -- so many different elements are not included yet.\par \par \uc2 They have started building all of these factories and plants, including car factories. Car factories are coming back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, some economists starting to say that both the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as well as the Trump administration needs to make some moves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN LONSKI, MANAGING DIRECTOR, MOODY'S CAPITAL MARKETS GROUP: if we got a lot of this trade-related uncertainty out of the way, along with perhaps maybe a 50-basis point reduction in the federal funds rate come September 17th, monetary policy has to work alongside tariff policy in order to put this U.S. economy on a solid, convincing growth path.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, the unemployment rate still considered low at 4.3 percent. But for the first time in four years, there are more unemployed people in the U.S. than job openings. And many \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  members that Fox has talked with tell us that that's because companies paused expansion plans to wait for uncertainty to clear. They believe at the end of the year, those expansion plans will then start to continue. So, will be a good jobs boosting towards the end of this year. Back to you, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Ed Lawrence, live for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we've got new information on the so-called Russiagate scandal, in which President Trump insists that government officials tried to tie him to alleged Russian interference in the 2016 presidential election. Specifically, new insight into what then-\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  may have been thinking at the time.\par \par \uc2 Here is chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel. Good evening to you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Aishah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has obtained handwritten notes that are declassified from then-\uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2  from an October 5th, 2016 meeting among President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's national security team about how to deal with allegations of Russian interference in the 2016 national elections.\par \par \uc2 The notes are somewhat cryptic, but among the topics is the need for the I.C., the intelligence community, to do their best to lay down of what we could say publicly. Comey, at one point, paraphrases his thoughts, "What is the goal? Informing U.S. people? Disrupting Russians?\par \par \uc2 With the election about a month away, the notes appear to reinforce the view within the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration that Russian interference could have or was having an effect on the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 Comey notes quoted unnamed officials saying the Russians should have a view -- Russians have a view on who should win.\par \par \uc2 Comey also writes, "Don't we aid enemy goals as much as our own with a public attribution." Two days later, Homeland Security and the Director of National Intelligence issued a joint statement saying, "The U.S. intelligence community is confident that the Russian government directed the recent compromises of e-mails from U.S. persons and institutions, including from political organizations. These thefts and disclosures are intended to interfere with the U.S. election process."\par \par \uc2 Current Director of National Intelligence \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , in July, released records that she says reveal President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's team sought to undermine Trump's legitimacy as the newly elected president.\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: The expressed intent and what followed afterward can only be described as a years-long coup and a treasonous conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The Trump Justice Department has launched criminal probes into Comey and former CIA Director John Brennan over their role in investigating the Russiagate matter. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has no comment on the declassified hand written notes, and Comey attorney has so far not responded. Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Aisha, okay. Mike, good to see you. Thank you for that report.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, up next, a warning from Russian President Putin. He says, if European security forces set up camp in Ukraine, they are fair game.\par \par \uc2 First though, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX5 in Atlanta, as federal authorities there, say, 475 people were detained this week in what Homeland Security investigations calls, the largest single-site enforcement operation in its history. That raid took place at a Hyundai battery plant site in Southeast Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Kansas City, Kansas tonight. One of the big stories there, from FOX43. Topeka tradition takes to the skies. Numerous hot air balloon pilots are visiting the city for their 50th Annual Huff and Puff event with some new activities for the kiddos. 22 hot air balloons are expected, along with food trucks and other vendors. Looks like a good time.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Tonight, we are waiting for the Trump administration to lay out its plans for security measures in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is warning any Western troops deployed to Ukraine would be legitimate targets for Moscow to attack.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan has the latest tonight from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  setting sights on a new potential target. Foreign troops, if they're sent to Ukraine, especially amid Russia's ongoing invasion.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): These will be legitimate targets for destruction.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): This week, Ukrainian allies pledged post war support to Kyiv to prevent Russia from striking again.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT (through translator): 26 countries have committed to deploying troops as a reassurance force in Ukraine, or to being present on the ground, at sea or in the air.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): Today, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says that could mean 1000s of troops. Putin has long opposed his neighbor becoming a member of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and rejects the latest proposal, arguing Russia should be trusted.\par \par \uc2 PUTIN (through translator): If decisions are reached that lead to peace, to long term peace, then I simply do not see any sense in their presence on the territory of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): Putin's warning comes on the heels of a landmark summit in Beijing as Russia deepens ties with China India and North Korea.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he could soon speak with his Russian counterpart and is expected to lay out his administration stance on Ukrainian security in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: President Trump confirmed America is ready to take part. In the next few weeks, we'll talk with each country about what exactly they can do.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): Washington today is also phasing out some military aid to some countries bordering Russia, like Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia, all of which are part of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Overnight, Ukraine says Russia launched more than 150 drones in seven missiles. The Ukrainian military says it struck Russia's Ryazan oil refinery, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Alex Hogan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take you on an exclusive tour of the War Room where federal agents track unaccompanied children who came across the border and then were released to you unvetted sponsors during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 First though, Beyond our Borders tonight, Israel strikes a high rise building in Gaza City after an evacuation warning. The military is stepping up operations aimed at seizing control of the city of some of some of one million Palestinians. The military accuses \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists of using high rises for surveillance and planning ambushes.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Japan, at one of the big stories there and beyond. Tonight, the year's second total lunar eclipse is coming up fast, and this time, Asia will have the best seats in the cosmos. Earth's shadow will obscure this weekend's full moon, as the planet lines up perfectly between the moon and the sun. the totality will last one hour and 22 minutes. The entire show, starting with the first dusky bite of the moon will span more than five hours. Pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Tonight, we take you on an exclusive tour of the \uc2 HHS\uc2  war room as agents track unaccounted for illegal minors who were released to unvetted sponsors during the Biden administration. It is a situation officials say should have never happened. Our Correspondent Brooke Taylor is in Dallas tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GRADISON, ACTING ASSISTANT SECRETARY, \uc2 HHS\uc2 : It can be heart wrenching work to try and identify these children and make sure that we're saving lives.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE TAYLOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Fox gets an exclusive look at the effort to track down the tens of thousands of unaccompanied minors who cross the border under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FABBRICATORE, \uc2 HHS\uc2 OFFICE OF REFUGEE RESETTLEMENT\uc2 : We found kids in very deplorable conditions, very dirty conditions. We found children who have been raped.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice-over): Between January 2021 to December 2024, more than 470,000 unaccompanied migrant children entered the U.S. according to the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FABBRICATORE: Under the last administration, there was a severe lack of screen. There was no DNA testing to see if there was a familial connection. Children were released to people that said they were their parents, who were not.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice-over): DHS Office of Inspector General report revealed from 2019 to 2023, over 31,000 addresses of these children were blank, undeliverable or missing apartment numbers.\par \par \uc2 Now a team that volunteered for the mission from agencies including the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Homeland Security Investigations are analyzing records and tracing addresses to determine where these minors ended up.\par \par \uc2 FABBRICATORE:We're talking about debt bondage, where children are being made to work off debt, trafficking debt. We're talking about children that were brought into situations and then treated like sexual slaves.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice-over): More than 22,000 unaccompanied children have been found so far under this program. They also say 400 sponsors have been arrested, and sadly, they say 27 of the children were found dead.\par \par \uc2 FABBRICATORE:We will continue to knock doors as long as we can to ensure that we find every single child.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (on camera): And under the Trump administration, sponsors now are required to get DNA tested, fingerprinted, and also show proof of income. We have reached out to Biden officials, but we have not heard back for a response, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Brooke Taylor live for us in Dallas. Brooke, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's Whatever Happened To segment -- in tonight's whatever happened to segment, the U.S. plan to take control of the Panama Canal. President Trump has promised to do it, but China has something to say about that. Correspondent Griff Jenkins has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: China is operating the Panama Canal, and we didn't give it to China. We gave it to Panama, and we're taking it back.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It's been a focus for President Trump since day one, raising questions over whether he actually could take it back. So Fox News traveled south earlier this year to get a first-hand look.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: I'm standing at one of the most important places in the world, the Panama Canal. This 50-mile waterway is an engineering marvel. It is currently at the center of a high-stakes international standoff.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): Built by the U.S. in 1914 and fully handed over to Panamanian control under a neutrality treaty 25 years ago, Chinese influence has since grown.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Where are you from?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: China.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): In February, Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  visited the canal.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, SECRETARY OF STATE: We cannot continue to have the Chinese and through their companies exercising effective control of the canal area.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: That was followed by Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 , walking away with a signed security agreement with Panama.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: Our countries reaffirmed our shared commitment to protecting Panamanian sovereignty from malign influence.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): In the months since, U.S.-based asset management firm \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  struck a multi-billion dollar deal to purchase a majority stake from the Hong Kong-based C.K. Hutchinson company that runs ports along either side of the canal, giving the U.S. company control over key docking operations. But China has been moving aggressively to block the sale and refute Trump's claims of Chinese influence.\par \par \uc2 FU CONG, CHINESE AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N.\uc2 : The United States' fabrication of lies and groundless attack on China are nothing but a ploy to control the canal.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): Despite being caught in the middle, Panama is celebrating the 111th anniversary of the crucial waterway.\par \par \uc2 RICAURTE VASQUEZ MORALES, PANAMA CANAL AUTHORITY: Under Panamanian administration, the canal has doubled its capacity, expanded its locks and enhanced its security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Nearly 15,000 ships pass through the canal every year, with the U.S. accounting for the majority of container traffic. Nearly 75 percent of the cargo passing through either originated in or is destined for the U.S. We'll send it back to you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Great, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In our last segment, looking at the plane from Qatar that could replace Air Force One, we told you about the renovations that were needed to retrofit that plane. Tonight, Bret Baier looks at the planes and helicopters that carry government officials around the world.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Aisha. The most recent data from 2020 says at least 20 planes make up the executive fleet. The last time new planes were added was about 27 years ago. Those are likely to remain in service for at least another 13 years.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a look at the lengthy process to improve and update the aging executive fleet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They're not building the plane fast enough. I mean, they're actually in default, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Air Force One is used to designate any Air Force aircraft carrying the commander-in-chief. There are currently two highly customized \uc2 Boeing\uc2  aircraft, which were deployed in 1990 when George H.W. Bush was president. Those have since carried presidents Clinton, Bush.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In eight years, they never lost my baggage.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't have to take off my shoes before I get on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Trump.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We got a lot of televisions. It's a great plane, great everything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Biden.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I joke now, you know, for the first time, a long time, a plane doesn't leave until I get there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): And now President Trump again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're waiting too long from \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has got to get its act together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): The two forthcoming \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes have been plagued by delays due to the complex technology needed on board Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump has said he believes this Qatar plane is a way to fast track a plane that hasn't been updated in 35 years. What about that explanation?\par \par \uc2 JAY SOLOMON, THE FREE PRESS: I mean, if you look at it just through, you know, economics, maybe it makes sense. But I still think the fact that we're allowing a foreign country to gift something of that magnitude to a sitting president, I think it's -- it's a risk and it's not and it's -- it's not a good look.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): While the cutter jet is estimated to have a faster timeline, it still needs enhancements similar to the Air Force One program planes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What kind of updates are going to be necessary for the U.S. to use this plane as Air Force One?\par \par \uc2 SOLOMON: They've got to debug it, make sure it's, you know, there's no sort of signals intelligence risks. And I think just to make it, you know, secure against any potential military attacks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Air Force One is required to have four engines, unlike most of today's passenger planes, which have two. On board is the highest level of classified communications and external protections against foreign surveillance.\par \par \uc2 The planes are equipped with air to air refueling capabilities so they can fly as long as needed. They are built for the worst possible scenarios like nuclear war, so the president can still command military forces.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump said he expects it to fly as soon as February. Have you heard that timeline?\par \par \uc2 SOLOMON: Initially, it was supposed to be like, maybe we'll get it done by the end of the -- end of his presidency. So it looks like they really have kind of made sure this thing is going to get up in the air.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Air Force officials say it is possible to add some of the security features to the Qatari jet, but it's unlikely to have the full suite of technology in the shortened time frame.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did you talk to the CEO?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I did. I said, let's get, you know, in the meantime, I got him 160 planes to build. I said, how about the two that you're supposed to build for our country? Now they are different. They're extremely complex and I'm not going to go into it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But they're not like a normal plane. You know, it's not like building a 747.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Air Force One and much of the executive fleet have undergone modernization modifications over the years, but the age of the planes is raising concerns.\par \par \uc2 TROY MEINK, AIR FORCE SECRETARY: Even today, and regardless of the airplane, we have to operate it differently based on the threat environment that even the current or any of the future aircraft will go into.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Four modified \uc2 Boeing\uc2  757s or C-32As are the newest planes in the fleet. Those were added in 1998 and 1999. The Air Force is studying potential replacement options, but they are expected to continue flying until 2038. They are used primarily for the vice president, but also carry members of Congress and cabinet officials.\par \par \uc2 There have been several incidents involving government planes over the years. In February, Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 's plane en route to Munich was forced to return to Washington after a mechanical problem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm good. I'm good.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): In 2021, Vice President Harris's plane requested an emergency return to Joint Base Andrews as she began her first foreign trip overseas to Guatemala and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 And in 2014, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  had to switch planes after a minor mechanical problem on Air Force One. The newest aircraft are the Marine One helicopters, which President Biden rode in for the first time last year, updating those took more than two decades, and in some cases replaced helicopters flying since the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : When you fly the president of the United States, you are the best flyer. They take the best Air Force guys. These guys are phenomenal. You got to see what they look like. You know, everyone's in perfect shape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The next new major commercial aircraft is expected to be the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  777X. It is expected to enter service in 2026 after a nearly six year delay with \uc2 Lufthansa\uc2  taking the first flight.\par \par \uc2 The modernized plane is expected to have a folding wingtip, a touchscreen flight deck and wider cabin space. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the August jobs report and the administration's fight against drug cartels.\par \par \uc2 And of course, later it's Friday. That means Notable Quotables.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LORI CHAVEZ-DEREMER, LABOR SECRETARY: Twenty-two thousand jobs underperformed just a bit, but it's still in the positive. Almost a half a million jobs have been created since the President took office. It's going to take some time.\par \par \uc2 JAN HATZIUS\uc2 , GOLDMAN SACHS CHIEF ECONOMIST: The implication is that the labor market continues to be pretty slow. Hiring is very slow. Layoffs are still low, but the labor market's softening. And my main takeaway is that this seals the case for the cut in September.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, so what comes next? Let's bring in our panel. Meridith McGraw, White House reporter for the Wall Street Journal. Yemisi Egbewole, former chief of staff and advisor to the Biden White House press office. And Matthew Continetti, scholar at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  and columnist for the \uc2 Free Press\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us. Happy Friday to you. So not a great jobs report. And there was one sliver of, you know, of hope out there for folks at home, which is we could see interest rate cuts now because of this. I want to ask you what you think of that, but also the President thinks the real numbers are going to come later this year. That's what he kept talking about today. Listen to him here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The real numbers that I'm talking about are going to be whatever it is, but we'll be in a year from now with these monstrous, huge, beautiful places, the palaces of genius. And when they start opening up, you're seeing, I think you'll see job numbers that are going to be absolutely incredible. Right now, it's a lot of construction numbers, but you're going to see job numbers like our country has never seen before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, Meridith, what are you hearing because they're not changing the strategy?\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW, WALL STREET JOURNAL: No, not at all. I mean, we heard this morning from NEC director \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  that these jobs numbers were disappointing. But so far, the White House has really shrugged off the numbers. They think that this economy will show signs of improvement, like the President said, a year from now. And they're pointing to things like major investments from other companies and the longer term effects of tariffs and their immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 But we know from what we've heard from economic analysts that these are real warning signs for where the economy might be heading. But still, the - - the president and the White House has used this as a chance to renew their -- their pressure on the Fed to lower interest rates when they meet soon.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Matthew, in a way, this does put the spotlight back on the tariffs, the Trump tariffs and whether or not that was a good idea and companies just feeling jittery about it.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: I think there's some uncertainty that might be affecting the market. But I also think that when you ask most voters what they really care about, it's not so much jobs, it's affordability, it's costs. And so if I'm looking at the administration, I have some time in the administration to get the economy off of the trough that we might be in, in terms of the labor market.\par \par \uc2 And I really want to stress the ways in which the Trump administration is lowering prices for Americans and increasing their paycheck, their take- home pay. And that means relying on the taxes, on the deregulation and on the energy policies. That should be front and center for this administration.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So Yemisi, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  came out and vowed to force a vote on those Trump tariffs. Not expected to go anywhere. Of course, he's in the minority. Democrats don't really have a lot of power in this moment, other than just to watch what happens with the economy and wait, as the President says, wait to see the results.\par \par \uc2 YEMISI EGBEWOLE, FORMER BIDEN PRESS OFFICE ADVISOR: Right. Not a lot of power, but good that he spoke up. The messaging on this is really important. We know from Fox polling that 38 percent of Americans approve of the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 And so what the Democrats can do is really push ahead on this. I mean, look, Trump fired a statistician, the \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner, because he said that he didn't like the numbers. Well, that man is now fired and the numbers haven't changed. They've only gotten worse. And so I don't think Americans want to wait a year from now to see any improvement. They're talking about everyday costs. And what they want to see is relief now.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Let's talk about the other big story of the week, which was this incredible video of this Venezuelan vessel being bombed. And there's a lot of legal questions here, Matthew, the White House making the case that it has the authority to do this because these cartels are now the same as \uc2 ISIS\uc2  or al-Qaeda. That's how they see them. Here's Senator \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  making the argument from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2  (R-OK): The fact of the matter is this was in international water. The -- the boat had been warned to -- to stop. They chose not to. They were running, clearly running, clearly running drugs. We had been tracking it since it left Venezuela. And -- and this is what you get. You play stupid games. You have stupid results. And for Venezuela, the message to them is mess around and find out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What do you think of their argument?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Well, I think the president has the authority under the Constitution to face down threats to the United States. And I think if you ask most Americans, they would say that the threat posed by these cartels is huge. The devastation from drugs and overdoses over the past decade surpasses the death toll in some of our worst wars, our casualty lists.\par \par \uc2 And so I think a lot of people see this president taking decisive action to counter the threat. I heard \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  earlier in the program saying, well, what if he does this? What next? I think most Americans reply will be what he'll do next is blow up another drug shipment and the voters will approve.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I think they've warned right Venezuelans that that's what they're going to do. President Trump has said that he's not eyeing. He just said that a few moments ago. He's not eyeing a regime change. But Maduro sure thinks so. And that's what he thinks is happening here. And by the way, all of this happening as China is cozying up to India and Russia. Is that a coincidence that we're changing the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  to Department of War, and we're sort of like putting out this message that we are strong? Don't mess with us.\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: The White House has said that they're declaring war on these narco traffickers, and they really feel that the policies of the past haven't served them and that, you know, they -- they want to really take decisive action when it comes to blocking drugs and these terrorists from coming in these terrorists that they -- they call coming into the -- the country. You know, on Inauguration Day, the President signed an executive order naming Tren de Aragua and other drug cartels, foreign -- foreign terrorists.\par \par \uc2 So this is really, I think, sort of a new era in -- in going after these drug cartels. But, you know, for the -- the White House, they're clearly trying to send a message to not only the cartels, but Maduro as well.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yemisi, you know, there are Democrats that have come out and oppose this because of the legal argument. They want the President, the administration to come to Capitol Hill and get permission. This always happens. We've seen it over and over again. \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  is out there among those Democrats. But Democrats have also complained about law and order here at home. So can they take this political risk to complain about law and order abroad?\par \par \uc2 EGBEWOLE: This is a tricky situation because drug traffickers are not sympathetic creatures. And also they're trafficking fentanyl, which we know, especially in D.C., is a huge problem. I live in a neighborhood impacted by it. I think it's a very hard thing to push back against the President on this. You can raise some concern about the judicial standpoint here and due process. But overall, these are just not heroes that anybody's going to cry about.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, they're the cartels. We don't like them. I think we can agree on that.\par \par \uc2 EGBEWOLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think most people will side with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on foreign policy rather than \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes. OK, standby panel. We're going to have a lot more to get into.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday lightning round. And then the week's most memorable moment -- moments, Notable Quotables is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We have the one that I thought was going to be probably one of the easier ones, and that's with President Putin and Ukraine. But it turned out to be a little bit more difficult than I thought. And it'll get done or there'll be hell to pay.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: We've got to push him in the direction of the peace process. He's not going to voluntarily do it. We have to recognize that Putin is -- is sort of playing us with his positive discussions with the President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Interesting language there. We are back with the panel now. I'll go over to you, Matthew, first. What -- what do you think about those words that Russia has been playing with us for a while? And -- and where do you think we go from here? We still haven't seen the White House use these sanctions they've been talking about.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Right. There's been a lot of talk about what we will do or what the Europeans will do once the war is at a ceasefire. But we have to get to a ceasefire first and the question is how? And I think there are a few ways one is to take away Russia's oil revenue and I think the administration is trying to do that by expanding our supply and by tariffing India and by pressuring the Europeans to buy less oil from Russia through intermediaries, right?\par \par \uc2 But there's also the fact of the weapons which we've set up a mechanism for the Europeans to buy weapons from the United States and then give them to Ukraine. And finally though, there's the sanctions. And that's the missing piece that this administration needs to enact before I think we're going to get serious negotiations beginning.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. And I don't think we have time to talk about the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  which is now the Department of War, but I want to get to winners and losers really quickly just have a couple seconds left here. What do you think Meridith?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: Winners, I'm rooting for Amanda Anisimova in the finals tomorrow at the U.S. Open. She's really been a comeback story. She still has to -- to win her match tomorrow, but she's been a great story to watch this summer. Loser I would say the -- the fashion world with the loss of Giorgio Armani. I loved seeing all of his beautiful red carpet gowns.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Matthew?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: My winner is Paul Skenes the Pirates ace 1.98 ERA is six scoreless innings against the Dodgers and he dates Livvy Dunne, that's a winner. And my loser is Jalen Carter the Philadelphia Eagles Defensive tackle for spitting on Dak Prescott in last night's \uc2 NFL\uc2  opener unsportsmanlike.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yemisi?\par \par \uc2 EGBEWOLE: My favorite, this is my favorite one. OK. Dak spit first, but --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I'm going to have to check that tape.\par \par \uc2 EGBEWOLE: My winner Sabrina Carpenter's new album, it's pop. It's fun. It's light. I love it. And my loser is the woman who sued Cardi B. It took under an hour for the court to rule in Cardi B's favor and we got some great memes out of it, free Cardi. I love it.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, we do have time to talk about the department's -- the Department of War. One thing I want to bring up about that is some Republican senators Including \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  brought up today, you 