hey look like they have no problem. It's amazing. I want to thank you. If you want, you know, it's been a long time. If you want. If you want we could go in and we can call it a day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, and Biden's first seven months, he had one cabinet meeting. It was in, I believe April of 2021, Trump has had seven. Now, this was a -- this is one for the ages. It was record-breaking, three hours, 17 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We didn't have this problem with Biden. He was in and out very quickly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He had everything from the national guard in D.C.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Those are tough troops too. Those are not politically correct troops. We don't have politically correct anymore. We have tough guys, tough people, including some tough women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: To the engagement of the couple that everyone loves to love, Taylor Swift and Travis Kelce.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I think it's -- I think he's a great player, I think he's a great guy, and I think that she's a terrific person. So, I wish them a lot of luck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, Trump, gracious and funny. I mean, she endorsed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . It's all water off his back. The president touted a lot of the administration's accomplishments, of course, even one he admits he can't take credit for.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I did not get 250, OK. The 250th anniversary, I did not get. I'd like to claim it, if I might, but I did get that. That was whether I was president or not. It was still 250. I'd like to take credit for it. What I will take credit for is we're going to have a better celebration under Trump than you would have had under some --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Olympics and World Cup, he talked about that as well. And the president and his cabinet, they want you to understand this. They're working hard, not for consultants, not for the elites, but for you, the American people. Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  chimed in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO (R), U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: You were elected as the president of working Americans, and that's why this Labor Day is so meaningful. For me, personally, this is the most meaningful Labor Day of my life as someone with four jobs, and so -- it's true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was funny. I mean, I could have watched this all day. Think about Biden and how Harris would have been. Just keep your mind focused on that. And now, two rebrands, two flops and one awakening. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 Well, well, well, we have breaking news tonight, just in from Fox Business, we got a statement. It's from Cracker Barrel. It's doing a U-turn on its disastrous rebrand. Here's the statement. We thank our guests for sharing your voices and your love for Cracker Barrel. We said we would listen, and we have, our new logo is going away and our old timer will remain.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's good news. They didn't make any statement on their lousy interior renovations, making that all look white and bland, but we're waiting for that turn around as well. That might take a while. But it was an admission of failure and a move to stop what appeared to many to be a woke rebranding of an iconic restaurant chain. But despite their U-turn, it's worth remembering and understanding how this happened.\par \par \uc2 Now, last year, CEO \uc2 Julie Masino\uc2  rolled out her three-year plan to freshen up the restaurant chain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIE FELSS MASINO\uc2 , CEO, CRACKER BARREL: Our goal is to take what people love about Cracker Barrel and open the aperture a little bit so that more people love Cracker Barrel. So, we want to appeal to all generations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sounds nice. So, they plan to slowly but surely undermine what the brand stood for all along, which was Americana. It was a field that harkens back to a simpler time. We all know it. But like all propaganda, the goal for the time of the rebrand was to convince you that the rebrand is what people really wanted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MASINO: Not only are guests loving them, they're saying things like, lighter, brighter, fresher, feels like -- feels like my Cracker Barrel. But they're also saying that still the warm, warm hospitality. It's still the food that I love, the decor is still on the walls. They love it, and our team members love them as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Friends of mine says that -- say it looks like a veterinarian office. But Julie kept going, the hard sell kept happening in June.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MASINO: The feedback and the buzz is so good, not only from our customers, but from our team members. They want to work in a wonderful restaurant. So, we're doing everything for our guests and our team members.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was August, obviously, but the consumers we know now revolted. The stock, well, it had been struggling for a while, but the decline over the last three weeks since that new logo rollout was a disaster. Sorry for the pun, it hit the bottom of the barrel.\par \par \uc2 So, what was the motivation here? Because when you really think this through, no one in his or her right mind would have thought that going from this, featuring uncle Herschel to this, blah computer generated nothing would have appealed to core customers in Paducah, Kentucky or East Ridge, Tennessee. So what did happen? Was this a simple corporate boo boo, or was this actual hostility to traditional country culture somewhere in the corporation itself?\par \par \uc2 Online commentator Robby Starbuck pointed to Cracker Barrel board member Gilbert Davila, who was put on the board interestingly during the height of the George Floyd hysteria. Starbuck asked what qualified Davila for this board seat. Well, he owned a DEI consulting and strategy firm for 15 years that focuses on pushing DEI and DEI advertising.\par \par \uc2 Well, do you think Davila, or Davila, with his background and his views loved the idea of any rebrand or any brand celebrating country culture. From Fox Business, Davila's \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  page states that he's widely recognized for his multicultural market knowledge and his proven track record on how to best embed multicultural perspective into a corporation's entire operation. Well, that's what it certainly looks like might have happened here.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Cracker Barrel was feeling the heat, remember, and he put this woke fool on its board. So, a lot of people thought, well, it just deserved everything it was going to get for ditching what made them special, and in the process, insulting Middle America and giving into corporate group think.\par \par \uc2 Well, Trump thought, look, he saw this, and he was being magnanimous. He urged them to do a U-turn, saying Cracker Barrel should go back to the old logo and admit a mistake based on customer response, the ultimate poll, and manage the company better than ever. They got a billion dollars' worth of free publicity if they play their cards right. Very tricky to do, but a great opportunity. Well, again, Trump is right again. I mean, and they did exactly what he said to do, except for the bad renovations on the inside of the restaurants.\par \par \uc2 But let's go to the Democrat Party, because their party's rebranding effort makes Cracker Barrel look like \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2  return to \uc2 Apple\uc2  in the early 90s. They got their tushes handed to them last election. They're losing voters across multiple demographics, their customer base, because of their terrible policies. Their woke pro-criminal, pro-illegal alien, pro-trans nonsense that has led to a revolt of normal people from party ranks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRY ENTEN, CNN POLITICAL ANALYST: The Democratic brand right now has about the appeal with the American voter as the Cracker Barrel rebrand has with the American consumers, bad, bad, bad. What are you doing? Oh, my goodness gracious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, how do they turn it around? Well, at their strategy meetings, they keep showcasing what the voters rejected last time, but then they turn up the nasty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): The privilege of my lifetime would stand beside someone we know was the most qualified and would have been a fantastic president in a President Harris. We wouldn't wake up every day to a bunch of (inaudible) on TV and a bunch of nonsense. We would wake up to an adult with compassion and dignity and vision and leadership doing the work, not a man child crying about whatever is wrong with him. May his fat ankles find something today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, you actually have to be really funny to make that work. Imagine being a party that think they lost in November to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because that guy wasn't clear enough about the issues he just referenced, or because they didn't sell \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  enough, or because they weren't pro-DEI enough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: We're not shying away from diversity as a strength and equity as a goal, and inclusion being the air we breathe. That's what we should be doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, when Republicans kick off big meetings, they begin with a solemn prayer and maybe the national anthem to celebrate America. But the DNC, they want to signal to everyone that they hate the American story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let's talk about the land for a second. The DNC acknowledges and honors the Dakota (inaudible), the Dakota people who are the original stewards of the lands and waters of Minneapolis. As Democrats, I ask of every one of you to not allow land acknowledgments like these to simply be the checking of a box. Be curious. Ask questions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ask questions. OK. The Democrats are phonies on this issue, because I --you know she wants to ask, don't just check a box. Well, let's settle this once and for all. Shall we? Democrats, if you think we're living on stolen land, put your money and your property where your meaningless words are right now, and give the land back to the tribes. So, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , move out of your house and Viking Stadium, sorry, bye-bye, and all of Hennepin County and Minneapolis, Edina Country Club, Lake Minnetonka. Give it all up, liberals or shut up. Man, the Democrats, they are just simply lost. And Trump, he knows it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The new box that they've fallen into is crime. There was a consultant on one of the shows this morning, I was watching. I thought he was very good. He's a Democrat consultant, and he was screaming, no, no, don't let him do this to you. Don't let -- it's another trap. It's another trap. Trump is saying he's against crime and they're for crime. No, he's going -- it was funny. No, it's a trap. Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But they can't help themselves. Failed mayors like Chicago's \uc2 Brandon Johnson\uc2 , they throw down the race card, because that's all they can do. They don't want to address the real problems. We're going to explode that later on in the show. But their attempt to deflect questions about runaway crime, it's not working. As this CNN reporter found when interviewing Chicago residents about the supposedly improving crime rate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMEENAH HAQQUE, CHICAGO RESIDENT: First reaction was that this is a good thing. I think that the governor as well as the mayor should be on board with it, just so we can get a bit more of security in Chicago, the way that we need.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The crime has dropped since 2024. Do you feel like the city feels safer?\par \par \uc2 HAQQUE: No, not at all. No, especially with the police being defunded.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The reporter is like, can I buy a vowel, so she likes Trump's idea of federalizing some of the law enforcement to improve the city safety, and she doesn't think crime is getting better.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat rebrand odyssey is just another ride on their carousel of failure, but it's all they know. Standing in opposition to Trump and common sense. So they're simply incapable of dropping their support for trans athletes, even knowing their views are overwhelmingly unpopular, and again, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , better than anyone else out there knows how to capitalize on their spiral.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They said that's an 80-20, no, it's a 97-3. You can't have a seven foot guy playing basketball with the women, you know, it's just one of those little problems in life. And we all have our place. It's OK. I've got my place too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, Democrats don't believe they have a policy problem, not on that issue, not on any issue. They think it's just all a messaging problem. Again, this is stupid radicalism and a refusal to admit that they need to appeal, not to a fringe, not to the same liberal elites who got them into this mess, but to a core constituency, working class Americans. And you don't need to be a rebranding consultant to tell you what they want. The middle class, regular people, they want safe streets. They want decent education for their kids, and they want a real shot at prosperity. And that's what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is delivering, results. And that is the best branding of all. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 We are delighted now to be joined by our old friend Charlie Kirk, Founder and CEO of Turning Point USA. Charlie, Cracker Barrel cracked, your reaction to this breaking news?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, FOUNDER, TURNING POINT USA: Yes, they have. I'll tell you what, President Trump, he runs both the Democrat Party, because basically everything the Democrat Party says is just against Trump. So, he gets to determine the entire agenda the Democrat Party, and now he runs Cracker Barrel, all from the Oval Office, just on Truth Social.\par \par \uc2 I'll tell you what, it's an amazing test case to show the similarities between the two, because both the Democrat Party and their attempted rebrand and Cracker Barrel, it shows that when you market test in Brooklyn, you lose in Birmingham, Alabama. When you try to just always pander towards a small subset of elite opinion of out of touch actors, and then you have contempt and bitterness for the very country that you either wish that they want to buy your products from or to vote from you. So there is this very interesting connective tissue here, and it's not worth overthinking.\par \par \uc2 By the way you look at the ridiculous logo that Cracker Barrel have thought it was a good idea, that's this -- those are the same people that thought that a bunch of people in Pennsylvania were going to come out in a lot of numbers for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , like, oh, yes, which they're going to be super excited for it. That -- the -- the -- the -- the big takeaway here, though, and I think this is important, is that the middle part of the country, they do not want to see their traditions and their customs and the things that they have come to love and appreciate taken from them.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat Party has done that. So much of Trump's base are former Democrats, and they became Trumplicans, if you will, Trump-Republicans because of the working class basis. The same thing with Cracker Barrel. And the reason why they saw such a huge response is they said, no, not again. You're not going to take yet another institution, yet another restaurant or uncle Herschel from us. We've had enough stolen from us. That's why we love Trump and we want Cracker Barrel the way it should be.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But look, we don't know the exact rationale that went into this rebrand of Cracker Barrel, but it seems pretty obvious, right? I mean, I don't know if Starbuck's point on this is right. He pointed to that one DEI member of the board, but his -- certainly his background would sense -- would tell us that there was something going on here with the multicultural push. But in the process, they offended a lot of people, and just like the Democrats are doing, Charlie, on the issue of crime. You saw that African American woman saying, basically, she would welcome what Trump's doing in Chicago. But they don't care. They don't care what regular people want. They simply are ignoring it.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: No, and they're so disconnected from the regular body politic. And I love what President Trump is doing, by the way, with crime in Washington, D.C. I think this needs to be a template for the rest of the country. Someone who grew up in the suburbs of Chicago, I can tell you, Chicago desperately needs it right now, and especially other cities like San Francisco and Los Angeles, I think we need to now say, we're not going to put up with crime anymore, and that is a quality of life issue that impacts every single American.\par \par \uc2 And I'm telling there's something very real happening in the black community. They're talking, they're listening, and they're seeing that they have safer streets, they have better ways to school, and their quality of life has tangibly improved. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  deals in reality, while the Democrats are too worried about abstractions and living in the clouds.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, did you also notice and have you noticed that President Trump just seems a lot happier this time around? Right. He's been through - - run through the gauntlet, gone through so much, but he's truly enjoying this job. They didn't let him enjoy it last time around. But he's -- and it's kind of infectious. It's just -- it's fun. I mean -- I -- you know, I took him to task on the 600,000 Chinese students issue last night, and I'm still maintaining my position on that, come hell or high water, but look at how much fun he's having, and magnanimous he is as well.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: I completely agree. I was going to mention that, for three hours straight, you just kind of saw the master at work. And I think there was some point of it where the president looked around his all-star team, and his team, his roster of patriots said, boy, this is a lot different than it was, half that room in Trump 1.0 either secretly hated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , wanted some sort of 25th Amendment push, was actively working with the deep state against him back in Trump 1.0 --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: -- and either that or they weren't even good at their job, and now he has this cast of all-stars. I loved listening to it. I learned a lot hearing kind of the status report, what you saw, what the American people saw for three hours on full display, the cameras were allowed in the board of directors meeting of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , we were allowed in the room. That's why they hate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because he brings the American people. We had a seat at the table. Usually, that stuff happens in the shadows, behind the scenes. You've got a three hour uninterrupted, unedited window into how our government is running and operating. And I totally agree. President Trump is running a master class. He looks light, happy and joyful.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are hall monitoring scolds, and that's why our party has gained 4 million net new voters since 2020. Our party is as hot as a pistol as President Trump would say.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, a friend of mine at UVA, who's a freshman said that Turning Point USA group on campus is on fire, and he's joining and I'll tell you who it is later. But anyway, good job on that, because that continues to explode. Charlie Kirk, as always, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, states now risking losing federal funding if they do not put Americans first. Our exclusive interview with the Transportation Secretary, another all-star in the cabinet. \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2 , next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, can you believe this? This can't even be true, but they're telling me it is. 3 million people have signed an online petition defending that illegal alien truck driver who killed three people on that Florida highway. They want clemency or reduced charges. They argue that, well, the driver had no criminal intent. And now these same people are desperately trying to make the guy who showed no remorse at all in the courtroom look like he's the victim.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I saw sorrow and suffering in his eyes, and he is praying, he's doing serious prayer, and Ardas for the victims' families, and he is in solidarity, and he is facing mental anguish for what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, he's suffering from mental anguish. Maybe we should have a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  page, there probably already is one. Well, he got his commercial driver's license from the state of California. No shock there. He didn't speak English and he couldn't read road signs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They're endangering the lives and killing American citizens as we saw with that horrific crash in Florida.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Literally killing American citizens with their immigration policy --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: . Literally killing Americans. These are -- these are not made up statistics. This just happened. I mean, federal highway funds, that's leverage that the federal government has over the states that refuse to do the basics.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Would you consider advising that DOT and the administration pull back on federal highway funds for these states?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So, I don't want to get ahead of any administration announcements, but we are looking at a whole host of points of leverage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, well, well, that leverage may be coming in a reality. Today, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2  gave three states an ultimatum. They either fail, if they fail to enforce English requirements for commercial truck drivers, they're going to lose millions in federal funding.\par \par \uc2 Here with me exclusively, Secretary \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2 . Mr. Secretary, thank you for joining us tonight. I know the governors of these states understand the terms here. Do any of them thus far seem willing to comply?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY\uc2 , TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: So first off, for anyone to talk about the mental anguish of this defendant after killing three people, innocent people on Florida roads is outrageous, or 3 million people, Laura, that want to come to his defense. These are the same 3 million people who probably were yelling at DOT back in -- back in May, when we put out the rule that said, listen, if you can't speak the language, if you're not proficient in English, you can't understand road signs, you can't drive a big rig.\par \par \uc2 We put that out in May, and went into effect at the end of June, and three states have been unwilling to comply and work with us, Washington, California and New Mexico. And so what we've now told them said, listen, you don't comply. We're going to give you 30 days; we're going to pull back your -- your -- your -- some of your federal money that we give you. California's 30 million, Washington's 10 million, and New Mexico is 7 million. That's just step one.\par \par \uc2 We have a whole bunch of equities to play with states that don't comply with this law, because this is a safety issue. If you can't understand the road signs and you can't speak English and you're driving an 80,000 pound rig, you're a danger to everyone around you on the roads, the families, the teenagers that drive, your friends, and so we're not going to -- we're not going to put up with it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I've been hearing about this from people for like, 20 years, OK, literally for 20 years. And New Mexico told us that they believe they are in compliance. But \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's office put the blame on the Trump administration telling us that Mr. Singh got his work permit under the first administration, and now you're scrambling to shift blame after being caught Sean's nonsense announcement as a -- is as big a joke as the Trump administration itself. They're trying to play, you know, the role of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with their cutesy little response here, but your -- your reaction to that.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So first off in, with New Mexico, Laura, obviously this defendant could not speak English. We gave him a test after couldn't -- couldn't understand English, wasn't proficient and couldn't understand road signs. He was pulled over for a speeding ticket before this crash in New Mexico, and he clearly couldn't speak the language, understand the language, or understand road signs, and they let him go. They didn't take him out of service. Had they taken him out of service, we may have three Americans that would still be alive.\par \par \uc2 In regard to California, it's a requirement that when you get your \uc2 CDL\uc2 , you do have to take the test in English, and you also have a skills test. There's no way in hell this guy could have passed those tests, but for some really shady behavior in California. So, we are all in investigating, looking at this. And by the way, let Gavin come play, but he will regret the day he messes with DOT, because again, lot of equities we have with California, not just with regard to pulling money, but other things we can do with regard to CDLs, so I would -- but start to comply and look after your citizens.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Mr. Secretary, couldn't they lose their interstate right to commerce, like coming actually across state lines? I mean, if you're -- if you're in a red state, you need to -- you need to get stuff delivered from Florida all the time, a lot of agriculture and so forth. And a lot of -- we understand that this industry is dominated by foreign truck drivers, which I think is a huge problem. The administration has to do something about this, but these other states, their people's lives are being put in danger, as we saw with Florida.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So they could be barred from entering those states.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: They could be decertified, in essence. So, a California CDL couldn't be used in other states. By the way, it's even worse, Laura. Several drivers of the three states we're talking about, including California, had been pulled over in other states and been put out of service because they weren't English language proficient.\par \par \uc2 They were then subsequently pulled over in California. So, you have a red flashing light going, hey, this guy can't speak the language. In California, just let him go. And so, again, you're at the heart of the issue. We have a lot of equities, a lot to play here. We will get compliance.\par \par \uc2 And again, to your point, these truckers, they're driving all over the country. And when you have illegals that compete against American workers, when you take an American's job, number one, but you drive down the wagers of truckers all across the country because you're taking their jobs and competing with the American born citizens.\par \par \uc2 So, we're on it. We've heard the truckers and we're not going to let this one go.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. The H-1B visas, the truckers, the Chinese students, all of this stuff. This is what the base wants. They want America put first. Mr. Secretary, thank you very much for coming on tonight.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the Democrats push their favorite losing argument again. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: The Republicans are the party that wants to stop crime. We're against crime. The Democrats like crime. I don't know why. They're going to fight me like the slob of a governor you have in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Democrats, man, Trump has their backs up against the wall, doesn't he? Now all they know is failure. All their policies have failed. Otherwise, Chicago would be a shining example of what works, so with San Francisco, so with Oakland, so with Saint Louis.\par \par \uc2 But they refuse to change, as I said in the Angle. So, they minimize the crime in their own cities and how it affects regular working-class people. And like \uc2 Brandon Johnson\uc2  in Chicago, what does he do? He pulls out and throws down the dog-eared race card.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON (D), CHICAGO MAYOR: We cannot incarcerate our way out of violence. The addiction on jails and incarceration in this country, we have moved past that. It is racist. It is immoral. It is unholy, and it is not the way to drive violence down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's unholy. Who put a collar on him? Well, the funny thing here is that when Democrats fail, they want you to pay them to fail more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We have made significant progress on our own, but there is so much more that we can do if we had real support from the federal government. Our communities need more federal resources for public safety.\par \par \uc2 We are calling on the Trump administration to release the $800 million for violence prevention funds that he stole back in April. We are calling for more resources to stop the endless flow of guns into our city, and we are calling for transformational investments into affordable housing and community safety.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Translation, he wants his friends and cronies to keep getting paid to fail. Yeah. The reality is, of course, American taxpayers have been pouring hundreds of billions, probably trillions over the decades into cities like Chicago. And the results, well, you can see them for yourself.\par \par \uc2 Since the year February, Chicago has received more than $21.6 billion in federal funding, and what -- did that get the residents there? An 11 percent decrease in homicide? OK, that's good. Not great.\par \par \uc2 But Chicago still ranks among the top cities for murders in the United States. Despite all that funding, nearly 70 percent of students in grades three to eight are reading below grade level. So, is that worth the investment?\par \par \uc2 Here to discuss is Stacy Washington, host of Stacy on the Right on \uc2 SiriusXM\uc2 . Stacy, I know you're based near Saint Louis, which also has had its own crime problems over the years. You were just in D.C. Help us understand what is racist about helping people across urban America, and mostly who suffer from high crime are minorities. How was that racist?\par \par \uc2 STACY WASHINGTON, STACY ON THE RIGHT, HOST: You know, I couldn't see the racism when I was in the \uc2 Uber\uc2  speeding into D.C. for Media Row for the radio program. And, Laura, I saw national guardsmen just standing around kind of nonchalantly leaning up against our beautiful buildings in D.C., talking to black Americans in the city, just yacking it up and having a good time talking while they were keeping the city safe.\par \par \uc2 The city looks scrubbed. It looks like someone went through, like, an interior designer and cleaned everything up and put everybody in cute clothes. People are walking. They're bike riding. They're on their scooters. They look fantastic. I saw people smiling, Laura.\par \par \uc2 I go to D.C. 15 to 20 times a year during, you know, Republican administrations. And so, I see it a lot. And I have always felt like I really need to be situationally aware because the city does have a lot of crime. I had no idea there was, like, a murder every day. President Trump teaches us something new all time.\par \par \uc2 But, Laura, the city looks reborn. It's a glittering example of a capital city of a national international power. And that's what it's supposed to look like. And that's what we Republicans are into, looking good, winning, making stuff happen.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has totally remodeled the city in less than two weeks. I just -- I'm trying to figure out what \uc2 Brandon Johnson\uc2  is talking about. Of course, he needs 5,000 new officers on the street there. Of course, he needs that. And they need a new mayor. He shouldn't be doing that job because he doesn't know what he's doing about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, they go on in how we need affordable housing. He turns it into a panoply, a wish list of giveaways, again, to his friends and cronies who have been getting huge government grants and government contracts. To what end?\par \par \uc2 We have reading proficiency at horrifically low levels. We still have a terrible drug trafficking and human trafficking problem across urban America, including in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 So, while there have been some improvements around the edges, the regular people interviewed there, they're like, I don't feel safe. Maybe they feel safe in the, you know, Miracle Mile, but they don't feel safe in Englewood.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON: Well, how can they? So, Laura, part of that problem is, obviously, you know, they need better schools. But a lot of the centering of the children not being able to read or being out in the street late at night and committing crimes is because their parents don't have them in at the kitchen table doing homework for three hours a night. They're not doing a sport or a music lesson every night. They're not -- their parents aren't locked in to make sure their futures are bright. So --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stacy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON: -- it's a -- Democrat problem.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stacy, thank you very much for joining us. And coming up, exposing the H-1B visa scam. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Every policy of the Trump administration is designed to lift up the American worker, promote great paying blue-collar jobs and to rebuild the industrial bedrock of our nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, he's right about that. I mean, the only question after all the great accomplishments of the Trump administration and the early successes hitting his stride, how do the H-1B visas fit into this? This is when foreign workers are allowed to come into the United States. Because the entire thing to me is a scam.\par \par \uc2 There is no reason why we should be bringing in foreign workers when American workers are up to the task. If you believe in the free market, you got to raise wages so American workers will actually do these jobs.\par \par \uc2 Last year, nearly 400,000 applications for H-1B visa work were approved from India, China, and other countries. That's more than double the amount approved in the year 2000. So, is the American workforce really just decline that much? That's 400,000 jobs that did not go to American workers. And the companies that love foreign workers, companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , and \uc2 Meta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here to weigh in is Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Governor, your reaction to this. I mean, I know in the Trump cabinet there's a real divide on this issue, but it's an issue I fought for many, many years. What do Americans need to understand about this?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Well, I think you're right to say the H-1B it's become a total scam. These companies game the system. You know, you have some of these companies that are laying off large numbers of Americans, while they're also getting new H-1Bs and renewing H-1Bs, and a lot of times people used to say, well, you know, we're getting the cream of the crop from all around the world.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, that's not actually what H-1Bs are. Most of them are from one company or from one country, India. There's a cottage industry about how all that people make money off this system.\par \par \uc2 And look at what's happening to young people in this current job market because of artificial intelligence. You are starting to see evidence that they're having a much tougher time because of what's happening with A.I.\par \par \uc2 So, if that's going to continue to produce dislocations, why would we be importing foreign workers when we have our own people that we need to take care of?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, they want cheap labor. That's why. They want workers that will come into the United States and that have no, you know, portability of their job prospects. They have to stay as workers for \uc2 Google\uc2  or for \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , or for what risk any pick your garden variety, you know, large now, essentially, multinational corporation. That's why. I mean, they want to keep wages lower.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: No. Precisely.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Full Stop.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Hundred percent. Hundred percent. They save money. And as you said, these H-1Bs limit the H-1B worker to that one company. It's almost like a form of indenture servitude, and so we've seen them take advantage of that time and time again.\par \par \uc2 And I just think, like, the overall message from the time President Trump first ran for office, was we're going to put the American people first. And I think that he's done that in so many ways, like the border, and other things, and I think it should apply to these visa programs as well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I want to go to this other issue which set MAGA, the MAGA base on fire last night, the issue of the 600,000 visas for Chinese students that President Trump said he would be open to, allowing into the country.\par \par \uc2 Today, he said our college system would go to hell very quickly without them. This would be the most amounts of Chinese students ever admitted to the United States, almost doubling the 380,000 Chinese students that are in the country today.\par \par \uc2 My question to you is, should we be concerned if 15 percent of colleges end up going under because they can't have, you know, students from the Chinese Communist system in paying full rate?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: If you're bringing in somebody to study as a foreign student, the only wait reason that should be done is if it benefits the American people, not to subsidize colleges.\par \par \uc2 And to put it in perspective, the state of Florida, we're the third largest state in the United States, the total number of students in our entire state university system, 12 universities, is a little over 300,000, so that would mean you're almost doubling the entire population of \uc2 Florida State University\uc2  system with just Chinese students. And as we know, there is a significant percentage of the Chinese students who are -- they're engaged with the CCP.\par \par \uc2 You know, it used to be, Laura, they would say, oh, you bring the people from China, they're going to love America, they'll go back and they'll be ambassadors, and China will change, but obviously that has not happened because this has been going on for decades.\par \par \uc2 So, yeah, I think it's a -- first of all, the fact that we have 300,000 now is way out of bounds of, I think, what most people would want to see. I'd rather those slots go to American students.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'd rather have none. How's that? I'd rather have none. I think the whole thing is a bailout to colleges. And why would want to bail out colleges? We got a lot of Republican lobbyists working for top American educational institutions, and a lot of them seem to have a lot of power here. I don't know why, but that cannot continue. Governor, we appreciate it, and Florida shouldn't be having the Chinese students either.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the love story the entire world is talking about, and Jimmy Failla is obsessed with it. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We interrupt our regularly scheduled segment, What the Failla, to bring you --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's Taylor Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Don't blame me. My staff make me do this. Taylor Swift found her lover, the pop star announcing her wildest dreams came true.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Taylor Swift is engaged. This is so exciting. Oh my god. Oh my god. Oh my god. It's on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . It's on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . It's on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Oh my god. Oh my god. Oh my god. Oh my god. Oh my god. I feel like Paul Revere right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was a reporter, not Taylor Swift. Here with reaction, Jimmy Failla, host of Fox News Saturday night. Jimmy, is this the royal wedding? Our version of the royal wedding?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS SATURDAY NIGHT HOST: Yeah. But, hopefully, it's not the \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 , Prince Harry version. Let's start there. One reason to root for her today is she completely overshadowed \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 's new season on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . So that was good.\par \par \uc2 That is a -- I mean, that is a win. OK. Do I have the enthusiasm of Whitney and Sam on your staff? No. OK. But I wish them luck. I'm gracious. And as you know, marriage is a sacred institution, and odds are you're only going to do it twice.\par \par \uc2 So, as you sit here and watch these two people, OK, there's some goodwill. Trump, seemed to be gracious when he had to acknowledge it. So, there's a part of me rooting for this.\par \par \uc2 But as a guy who follows celebrity couples, nicknames are usually a dead end. You know what I mean? If you're the biggest couple in the world, Brad and Angelina were Brangelina. Tom and Katie Holmes were Tom Kat. Ben and J. Lo were miserable. Hopefully these two aren't them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I hope not. I think Trump had a great reaction. He's like, I wish him all the best. I mean, she endorsed Harris, like, who cares? He was, like, kind of winking at being, say, well, I wish him luck. Wish him luck. I thought I thought it was cool.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yes. Well, it is good. And the one thing is, OK, for all the good breakup music she's written, if she actually has a divorce album, it's going to be phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: So, I don't want her to. I'm just saying for the music people, that would be the consolation prize.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, but I have to say, the funniest thing about this, it is predictable. I think they make a cute couple, a great couple. But the funny thing about this is the reaction of the reporters. Like, the Ukraine war had ended or -- I mean, it was unbelievable. I thought, oh my gosh. What's happened? Like, I almost had a heart attack. I mean, it was so.\par \par \uc2 And if you Google Taylor Swift's name, confetti comes like on your screen. I just did that, by the way.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: That's true.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy, sorry it's so short tonight. Thank you so much. That's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. I have a Zoe message up there. Jesse Watters takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250827el8q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Democratic Party Leaders Meet In Minneapolis To Try And Plot Midterm Election Strategy; \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Testing Starship Rocket With Eventual Goal Of Bringing Humans To Mars; Democratic Mayors And Governors Resist Trump Administration Proposals To Deploy National Guard To Reduce Crime; President Trump Meet With South Korea President; President Trump Proposes U.S. Take Financial Stake In Semiconductor Manufacturer Intel; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Touts D.C. Arrests While Eyeing Chicago Streets; High-Profile Illegal Alien Again Facing Deportation; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Opens To Kim Jong Un Meeting; U.S. Becomes 10 Percent Shareholders Of Intel; Multiple Fatalities After Strike On Gaza Strip Hospital; Netanyahu Says Authorities Conducting Investigation; Mamdani Leads Fundraising In New York City Mayoral Race.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Mike Emanuel, Jonathan Serrie, Peter Doocy, Lydia Hu,  David Spunt, Jeff Paul, C.B. Cotton \par \uc2 7,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, that's it for us, everyone. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'll be there November 8th, I'm going. Thanks, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The illegal immigrant who was at the center of President Trump's crackdown may be about to be deported again. We'll bring you that story.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is obtaining partial ownership of private businesses. We'll get reaction.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's starship readies for a 10th test flight after some recent misfires.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, President Trump says Washington is probably one of the safest cities in the nation right now. The president defending his use of federal personnel in the nation's capital, saying it could happen elsewhere. He says Chicago may be next, calling it a killing field. Chicago's mayor and the governor of Illinois are strongly opposing that idea.\par \par \uc2 Today, the president signed executive orders ending cashless bail in D.C. and making flag burning punishable by a year in jail.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. President Trump is trying to stack deterrence on top of each other to bring crime down and keep it down in the nation's capital. The latest one, he is getting rid of cashless bail here in the District of Columbia. He's also so pleased with the way that things have been going here in D.C. for the last two weeks or so, that he is sizing up other cities for possible National Guard deployment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We want to go from here to other places.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): Next on the list could be Chicago, whose mayor is unimpressed by the Capitol crime crackdown.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON\uc2 , MAYOR OF CHICAGO: The president has spent hundreds of millions of dollars to what arrest nine people in D.C.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): The president was quick to correct Mayor Johnson.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Nine people? We didn't arrest nine people. We actually arrested a total of well over a thousand people.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): But President Trump plans to wait to send in the troops until Illinois Democratic Governor \uc2 JB Pritzker\uc2  asks him to.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I think until I get that request from that guy, I'm not going to do anything about it. I hate to barge in on a city and then be treated horribly by corrupt politicians and bad politicians like a guy like Pritzker. He had to spend more time in the gym, actually.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): Pritzker responded this afternoon,\par \par \uc2 GOV. JB PRITZKER (D-IL): Mr. President, do not come to Chicago. You are neither wanted here nor needed here.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): President Trump also has Maryland Democratic governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 , who some see as a possible 2028 contender, saying LOL and keep telling yourself that, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 After the latest claim in a days long back and forth about crime in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I met him at the Army-Navy game. They said, oh, there's Governor Moore, he'd love to see you. He came over to me, hugged me, shook my hand. You were there. He said, sir, you're the greatest president of my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): Despite encouraging crying data over the last two weeks, the U.S. Attorney for D.C. is concerned about keeping the district safe.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, U.S. ATTORNEY FOR DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: We have got to lower the age of criminal responsibility in Washington, D.C., the gangs and the crews are 13, 14, 15, 16, 17 years old. I can't touch them.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): For now, the president is claiming victory over those criminals.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Washington was the most dangerous place in this country, and now you know what? It's probably the safest place in our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Trump is also teasing a major change as soon as a week from now, to the name of the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 . He says he wants defense, but he also wants offense, and the president does not seem worried at all about getting the necessary congressional approval to change the name back to the Department of War, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Peter, tell us about the executive order regarding people who burn the American flag.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He signed an order that would sentence people who are convicted of burning an American flag to one year in jail without the possibility of bail. And he needs a way around Supreme Court rulings, two of them that say that burning an American flag is constitutionally protected free speech. So, the E.O. today is very carefully worded to say that the penalty will be applied to people who burn an American flag and that burning it incites violence, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll see how that goes. Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the man who became a focus of opposition to President Trump's immigration Crackdown is getting a temporary reprieve from another deportation. Kilmar Abrego Garcia was expected to be sent to Uganda following his release from a Tennessee jail Friday, but a judge has put that on hold.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt reports from Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): A crowd chanted outside the Baltimore Federal Building, supporting Kilmar Abrego Garcia.\par \par \uc2 KILMAR ABREGO GARCIA (through translator): Promise me that you will continue to pray, continue to fight, resist and love.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): Three days after being released on parole, the 30-year- old walked into a scheduled check-in with \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  before the 8:00 a.m. deadline. His lawyer says within just one minute, Abrego Garcia was taken into custody once again.\par \par \uc2 SIMON SANDOVAL-MOSHENBERG, KILMAR ABREGO GARCIA ATTORNEY: He was already on electronic monitoring from the U.S. Marshal Service and basically on house arrest. The only reason that they've chosen to take him into detention is to punish him.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): He's now being processed for possible deportation to Uganda after declining a plea deal that would have eventually sent him to Costa Rica a free man, but only on the condition of pleading guilty to federal human smuggling charges, which he denies.\par \par \uc2 Abrego Garcia entered the U.S. illegally from El Salvador in 2012 at age 16, and had been living in Maryland with his American citizen wife and children. He was eligible for deportation, but not to his home country, which is what happened as part of the Trump administration's get tough immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 When he was arrested and sent there in March, a judge determined his removal was improper, since Garcia did not have a chance to challenge it. The government disputes his claim of escaping gang violence in his home country, saying he is an MS-13 member himself. Abrego Garcia returned to the U.S. in June and was arrested again on criminal charges based on a 2022 traffic stop when he was stopped by state troopers in Tennessee who say he allegedly was transporting eight other illegal aliens.\par \par \uc2 Supporters like Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , who met with Abrego Garcia in El Salvador in person and again on Sunday, virtually argue his due process rights were nonexistent. But the Trump administration insists Abrego Garcia is a gang member and criminal and must go.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, U.S. A.G.: We've got him under control. He will no longer terrorize our country. He doesn't need to be on the streets like all these liberals want him to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Hours ago, a federal judge told government lawyers, Abrego Garcia is not to be deported before he can appeal before a judge his case to be deported in the first place. He may end up in Uganda, Bret, but it may take some time, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt live in Baltimore. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Trump met with South Korea's leader today at the White House. This comes as North Korea fires two missiles amid joint drills between the South and the U.S. Correspondent Alex Hogan has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 Str Congratulations on your election. That was a big one.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The future of the South Korea U.S. alliance in the spotlight as the newly elected South Korean President \uc2 Lee Jae Myung\uc2  visits Washington for his first in person meeting with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Lee quickly calling on Trump to establish peace on the Korean peninsula.\par \par \uc2 LEE JAE MYUNG\uc2 , SOUTH KOREAN PRESIDENT (through translator): The only person that can make progress on this issue is you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): President Trump said he'd support a meeting with North Korean leader \uc2 Kim Jong Un\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'd like to meet him this year.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): In 2019, President Trump became the first U.S. sitting president, to step foot in North Korea.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Remember when I walked across the line and everyone went crazy, especially Secret Service, but I looked in and I saw more rifles pointing at me than you could, there were a lot of rifles in that building.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): This weekend, the North Korean leader watched as his Army launched new air defense missiles, testing their combat capability. The North reacting angrily after the U.S. and South Korea began military drills just across the border.\par \par \uc2 The annual program includes field exercises, computer simulations and a nationwide civil defense drill, rehearsing air raids and evacuations.\par \par \uc2 Back in the Oval Office today, the leaders echoed ambitions of working together in both Defense and trade. President Trump has imposed 15 percent tariffs on imports from South Korea. Seoul has pledged to invest $350 billion in the U.S. Trump touted today's summit as a success.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, I think we have a deal done. They had some problems with it, but we stuck to our guns. We are going to they're going to make the deal that they agreed to make.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Tens of thousands of American troops are stationed in South Korea today, President Trump said he might inquire if the U.S. can get ownership of the land where some of the troops are based, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan in London. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's acquisition of a 10 percent stake in Intel is not expected to be the last such private sector involvement. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  and the recently acquired U.S. steel are also on the books so far.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Lydia Hu has details from New York tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Today, President Trump is pushing back on critics of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's deal with Intel, posting, "I paid zero for Intel. It is worth approximately $11 billion, all goes to the USA. Why are stupid people unhappy with that?"\par \par \uc2 That deal brokered with Intel CEO \uc2 Lip-Bu Tan\uc2 , once the target of scrutiny for his alleged ties to China, now seen in new video, sitting side by side with commerce secretary \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2 . \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: I can't be more excited for America and for Intel that we get to work together.\par \par \uc2 LIP-BU TAN, CEO, INTEL: First of all, thank you so much, Secretary, and this is big, important historical agreement that we can work together.\par \par \uc2 HU (voice-over): The government becomes a shareholder of Intel with a 10 percent stake, the equity funded by the near $8 billion from the CHIPS Act and another 3.2 billion from another previously awarded program.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  criticized the deal before it was finalized, posting, if socialism is government owning the means of production, wouldn't the government owning part of Intel be a step towards socialism? Terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 But the investment earned support from lawmakers like Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , a self-described democratic socialist and Republican Congressman Mike Lawler.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): What the president is seemingly trying to do is get a return on these tens of billions of dollars that are being invested by U.S. taxpayers -- by U.S. taxpayers, into companies like Intel to manufacture chips.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HU (on camera): And the Intel deal follows other Trump administration agreements that bring the government and private sector closer together, like \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  and AMD agreeing to pay 15 percent of A.I. chip sales to China to the government.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, there's also the government's golden share in \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's acquisition of U.S. steel. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we'll watch it. Lydia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down today, pulling back from Friday's big performance. The Dow lost 349, the S&P 500 was off 28, the Nasdaq fell 47.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an Israeli air strike in Gaza leaves several people dead, including some journalists. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York, his former Mexican cartel kingpin Ismael El Mayo Zambada pleads guilty to federal charges related to his role in the violent drug trade that for years flooded the U.S. with cocaine, heroin and other illicit substances. The long-time leader of the Sinaloa Cartel appeared two weeks after federal prosecutors said they would not seek the death penalty against somebody who was arrested in Texas last year.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in St. Louis as crews from the gas company and the police bomb and arson unit investigate a home explosion in unincorporated North St. Louis County this morning, no word yet on a cause. At least three people were rescued and transported to the hospital with other injuries reported.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Miami from WSVN, our affiliate down there, one of the big stories there tonight, the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  prepares to mark a major milestone. Officials say they have offloaded $473 million worth of cocaine and marijuana at Port Everglades. It's the biggest haul in the Coast Guard's history.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Venezuela is deploying 15,000 troops to reinforce anti-narcotics operations in two regions along its border with Colombia.\par \par \uc2 The deployment comes as tensions rise between Washington 