c2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight. Revelers parade colorful costumes through the streets of Notting Hill in West London for the annual carnival, which started in 1966. It's one of the largest festivals of its kind In Europe, celebrating Caribbean culture.\par \par \uc2 The two-day street event attracts around 2 million partiers, musicians, and dancers. About 7,000 police officers and staff are on duty each day.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Live Look at New York City there. Tonight, we look at the money race in the New York mayoral competition. Self-described Democratic socialist Zohran Mamdani is running away with it, really nearly doubling up his closest challenger, former Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . But from where Mamdani is getting his money is a big part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent, C.B. Cotton is in New York tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CB COTTON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Zohran Mamdani's campaign points to grass root support for its latest haul. Totals from New York City's Campaign Finance Board show, in a little more than a month, the Democratic socialist pulled in nearly double from individual donors, than that of his closest competitor in the polls, former Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , who is running as an Independent.\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D), MAYORAL CANDIDATE, NEW YORK CITY: I think his -- the necessity of him moving money from his state account into his account for mayor, it reflects the fact that there simply isn't the level of excitement and belief in his candidacy.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voice over): The records also show more than half of Mamdani's donations came from outside New York City, perhaps, indicating national momentum, fueling Mamdani is progressive pitch. While Mamdani remains the mayoral front runner, he has had to walk back his past takes on defunding the police. Now, saying he will maintain \uc2 NYPD\uc2 's head count and discourage use of the phrase globalize the intifada, though he has not condemned it.\par \par \uc2 Still, Cuomo suggests New York's general electorate won't stomach it at the ballot box this fall.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2  (I), MAYORAL CANDIDATE, NEW YORK CITY: New York City people are not socialists, they are not anti-business people, they are not anti- corporate people. They want jobs, they want growth.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voice over): As for what high-ranking Democrats want, it's still not clear.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): I don't think we've withheld an endorsement.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voice over): Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and House minority leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  are among those who have had conversations with the 33-year-old, only to walk away without giving their full backing.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: I've raised several of the issues with him privately, as well as publicly spoken about some of the concerns that I've had.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): Jeffries said he'll meet with Mamdani sometime this week, and the state's Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has not endorsed Mamdani yet either. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: C.B., thank you.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are beginning their summer meeting in Minneapolis tonight. It comes as the party faces an identity crisis of sorts, fund raising woes, and falling voter registrations -- registration.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are still unified, despite our differences!\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Amid week polling and lackluster fundraising, Democratic Party leadership and activist are trying to find a way forward at