eduled launch of the \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  rocket called Starship. Last night's effort was scrubbed. This tenth test flight comes as a spate of recent failures have happened. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has that story tonight. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  says it had to scrub yesterday's launch attempt because of a liquid oxygen leak on one of the ground systems. The rocket itself is apparently OK, and they will try to launch it again tonight. It is a bit of a nail-biter because three earlier test flights this year ended with the rocket breaking apart and creating fiery trails in the skies as debris reentered the atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 There are no humans on board these privately funded research and development flights, but \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is hoping to get Starship safe enough so that one day it may carry the first humans to Mars. \uc2 NASA\uc2  is studying the equally daunting physiological and psychological hurdles those first Mars explorers may face. Four volunteers at a time spend more than a year living and working inside the 1,700 square foot 3-D printed habitat located at Johnson Space Center in Houston where we had the opportunity to visit in between missions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: On the other side of the air lock is a simulated Martian surface complete with exploration equipment, even simulated Martian soil. The program participants will be wearing virtually reality goggles to make the experience even more realistic.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE BELL, \uc2 NASA\uc2 : Our president has given us a very clear directive to land the first humans on Mars, and we'd like those to be Americans. So we are working hard on those technologies and different areas of \uc2 NASA\uc2 , working very hard on the human health and performance component so that when the vehicles are ready, we're ready to send the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 S