cative tone," the president said, "the governor asked that he walk the streets of Maryland with him. I assume he is talking about out-of-control, crime-ridden Baltimore. As president I would much prefer that he clean up this crime disaster before I go there for a walk. If \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  needs help like Gavin New-scum did in L.A., I will send in the troops, which is being done in nearby D.C. and quickly clean up crime."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  director put out the latest states in the D.C. crime situation, saying as, "All federal partners are now over 1,000 arrests under the president initiative to clean up Washington, D.C., latest from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  August 24th, 25th, 26 arrests involving \uc2 FBI\uc2  personnel, six gun recoveries, five drug seizures, 86 individuals arrested by federal partners total."\par \par \uc2 We'll start there with our panel, FOX News contributor Ben Domenech, host of "Ben Big Podcast", "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, and Matthew Continetti, scholar at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , columnist for "The \uc2 Free Press\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 Matthew, it is quite interesting how this kind of keeps going. Democratic mayors and governors keep coming after the president, saying is he a dictator and he's launching these National Guard troops in D.C. as a way to talk about that authority. And the president keeps on pushing back with numbers of cleaning up the city.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Yes, President Trump has a remarkable knack for getting Democrats to defend the indefensible. And in this case it's the Democratic urban machine's record on crime. And the Democrat mayors and governors have an interest here, Bret, because they want to be fighters against Trump, because most of them are thinking of 2028 presidential campaigns. But I think the president has a real opportunity use Washington D.C. as a model city a showcase for his techniques. If he can keep the crime numbers down and continue the record of success, he is going to prove to these mayors that he can beat them at their own game.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Baltimore, if you look at that, Francesca, the numbers year-to-date, 86 homicides, 159 rapes, 1,479 robberies. If you look at Chicago year-to- date, those numbers are exponentially bigger -- 256, robberies at 3,800, shooting incidents 941. The numbers in Baltimore have gone down, Governor Moore has touted those, but, you know, you look at any of these cities, inner cities, urban areas, they are significant.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Bret, I actually interviewed the mayor of Baltimore as well as the mayor of Chicago after President Trump's original threat to potentially deploy the National Guard into those cities. And they told me that they would be looking at legal action and other potential action if he were to do it. As you noted, the fight has since escalated with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have been really torn over how to respond to this, Bret. You have some who want to talk about the statistics that show that crime has been decreasing in their states and their cities, while some of those 2028 hopefuls that you were referencing there have talked about authoritarianism and accused \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of authoritarianism-like behavior.\par \par \uc2 But this problem for Democrats in talking about it going back to the last section 2024, it has been an issue for them, Bret. When they ran, five points, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ran five points behind President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on crime. And so this is an issue that Democrats understand that they need to figure out how to talk about or to address in better ways nationally ahead of the next election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you know, and these cities are also dealing with the issue of cashless bail. And the president signing an executive order, Ben, today, dealing with D.C. and that. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What this executive order does, it charges your attorney general with identifying jurisdictions all over the country that have cashless bail policies, and then it withholds or revokes federal funds and grants that are flowing to those jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: So what area does it cover?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Potentially anywhere that has a cashless bail policy. So some of the largest cities, some of the most leftwing states.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Almost all of them, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Illinois would be a great example, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They have a great cashless bail. You don't even have to go to court sometimes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about that issue, Ben?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, I think a big difference between the first Trump term and this one is the difference between having the right posture, the right positioning, and having the right action. You know, he would, obviously, post in the past about the importance of law and order. This time around, you know, he's going beyond just complaining about Soros backed prosecutors and the regime that they represent in terms of these blue cities when it comes to an approach that is designed to take a soft approach to crime, one that they have obviously backed to the hilt across the country. And he's going to fire back at them with them weapons that he has at his disposal, namely the dial of federal funds, the pulling back of the kind of support that a lot of these institutions just assumed would always come their way automatically.\par \par \uc2 And I think the other thing that he has done is he's put Democrats in a position of basically arguing based on histograms or based on hysteria. The idea that it's authoritarian to put more cops in cities like D.C. that are running 1,000 cops short and to have safer streets because of it, that doesn't strike the actual neighborhood as authoritarian. And when it comes to the histogram side of the argument, they can argue that until they're blue in the face, but a lot of it depends on the fact that crime levels spiked so much during COVID that those declines are really a bit of an illusion. I think he has put the Democrats in a very difficult position politically here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, Democrats are meeting, trying to figure out the way forward, their leaders, their message, meeting in Minnesota. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As Democrats, I ask of every one of to you not allow land acknowledgments like these just to simply be the checking of a box. Be curious, ask questions.\par \par \uc2 KEN MARTIN, CHAIRMAN, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: I'm sick and tired of this Democratic Party bringing a pencil to a knife fight.\par \par \uc2 Let's grow a damn spine and get in this fight, Democrats!\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D) MINNESOTA: The press finds the need to talk about, oh, there is a division in the Democratic Party. There's a division in my damn house and we're still married and things are good. That's life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  there. The issues, according to "Washington Examiner", at the DNC summer meeting, "Fundraising, voter registration problem, party leaders struggle with progressive candidates. Gaza remains a tension point, and redistricting". Matthew, they are trying to straighten all of this out and come under one tent.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Good luck with that. The Democrats are at record unpopularity. The DNC has hemorrhaged cash. The party is leaderless. And the base is hurdling itself towards socialism.\par \par \uc2 The one upside for Democrats is that midterm elections favor energized bases, but even if the Democrats don't do better than expected next year, that still won't put them in a good position for 2028. This is a party that needs a compass quickly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Francesca?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: When it comes to Democrats and the off year, they are trying to figure out, Bret, what their message is heading into the 2026 midterms. We've heard some talk about fighting against the bill that Republicans in Congress passed that President Trump championed, but Democrats, whether it's crime or this or whatever it may be, are still looking for a leader.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, stand by. Up next, President Trump meets with the leader of South Korea, how his administration is getting involved in private sector acquisitions and how that's going over.\par \par \uc2 And then the search -- well, it's an interesting search in one major city, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you go back to the DMZ to meet with the North Korean leader?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: I loved it. Remember when I walked across the line and everyone went crazy? Especially Secret Service? I felt safe because I have a great relationship with Kim Jong-un.\par \par \uc2 Whatever I can do having to do with South Korea and getting people together, you should get together, right?\par \par \uc2 LEE JAE MYUNG\uc2 , SOUTH KOREAN PRESIDENT: America's role not as a keeper of peace but a maker of peace is emerging more evidently.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: South Korean president in the Oval Office talking about security and also trade. President Trump says they are close a deal or going to get a deal. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," "South Korea's leader meets with Trump. China looms large. Seoul and Washington should ensure that strategic flexibility will not undermine South Korea's security and the ally's combined ability to deter North Korea Mr. Lee's national security adviser Wi Sung-lac told reporters on Friday. The allies have found some common ground over that principle, Mr. Wi said, but officials were also wary of Mr. Trump's unpredictability."\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel. Matthew, the president's unpredictability does produce some different, you know, reactions from world leaders, but, clearly, it keeps the Asia partners on their toes.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Yes, and, in fact, this Trump administration has, I think, focused more on other regions -- Europe with the Russia-Ukraine war, the Middle East with the Gaza war -- than it has been spending time focused on the Indo-Pacific and east Asia. And I think that's because this term is Trump the peacemaker, Bret, whereas the first term was Trump the hawk, especially hawk in relation to China.\par \par \uc2 A lot is unsettled, and all I would add is that let's not forget that North Korea is an ally of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's Russia and is contributing material and soldiers to Russia's invasion of Ukraine. That's a possible pressure point as Trump considers the different cards he has to play to get Putin to live up to his stated agreement to a meeting with Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of which, Francesca, there were multiple times when the president talked to the media today, was all over the airwaves and the wires as well. This is one of the times where he talked about Ukraine and Russia and the potential for that meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: There could be very big consequences, but we will see what happens. There might be very big consequences, because this is something that has to end. This war would have never taken place, and for four years it didn't take place. So we'll see what happens over the next week or two, and at that point I will step in very strongly. And if I have to be there, I'll be there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Saying that there will be consequences if that next bilateral meeting doesn't happen. So they are still moving forward, and he still seems a little optimistic, although Putin is saying -- and the Russians are showing other things.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And it's not entirely clear at this point, Bret, what those other consequences would be. It could refer to additional tariffs or secondary sanctions, so to speak, on purchasers of Russian oil. But there are some complications for the administration with that. They are currently negotiating a trade deal with China right now. And that is -- has been delayed from earlier this month from the second deadline that they had had earlier this month, Bret. But that has been a concern the administration has stated. They are a large purchaser of Russian oil. And so that could be part of the trade deal that they're negotiating.\par \par \uc2 But we heard Secretary Rubio also say that there are other European countries that also buy oil from Russia that the administration has heard from. So as the administration considers what it does next, there are other considerations outside of Russia's war against Ukraine that they are having to make here that are also economic.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And speaking of economics, Ben, the whole issue of the government taking a piece of a private company, this is 10 percent of Intel, the president talking about that today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: I said I'd like you to give 10 percent of Intel to the United States of America. Not to me, to the United States of America. And I said if you have them as a partner, you have the United States as a partner. I think that will be a very good thing for Intel. I just made $10 billion or $11 billion for the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the president -- and the pushback coming from "The Wall Street Journal" now, "A new tax on patents. Mr. Trump accused \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  of being a socialist, but the Biden administration never nationalized companies. The Biden team did propose allowing the government to claw back some CHIPS Act money from companies that made excess profits, but this drew opposition from Congress. Why aren't Republicans pushing back on Mr. Trump's Intel deal."\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there is significant, on the conservative side, concern when \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  says, yes, this is a great idea.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Yes, and there's concern on my part, too. I don't like this kind of road that we are going down, particularly because of the potential that it sets up for future administrations. But I also think that there has been so much creep in this area when it comes to government. And the left side approach, obviously, is via regulatory pressure and all sorts of different grant-making funding that we have seen go out the door to all of these different entities that don't deserve it. I think this is, unfortunately, something of the same kind. It's not the kind of thing -- look, this is the Ring of Power from "The Lord of the Rings." You don't want to go down this road and try to use this for good. It's always going to come back and bite you in the end.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "Lord of the Rings," nice. Matthew, final word?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: The administration says they're going to be a passive partner. And so I think it's up to Republicans in Congress to make sure that remains the case. But when you look globally, Bret, what I see here the same thing I see with \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , the same thing with U.S. steel. It's President Trump improvising his way toward a strategy to get our companies working in concert with government in order to pursue the tech competition with China. We'll see how it works out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you bet. OK, panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I didn't know if I was going to win