 Some Organ Donors May Have Had Organs Removed Before They Died; President Trump Says Building of New Federal Reserve Building Overbudget; Deputy Attorney General Meets with \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  Associate Ghislaine Maxwell. Trump Visits Federal Reserve; Trump Signs Order Pressuring Cities On Homelessness; Top Aide Testifies In Mental Acuity Probe; U.S. Envoy Accuses \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Of Not Acting In Good Faith\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Nate Foy, Bryan Llenas, Jeff Paul, Mark Meredith, Juan  Williams, Rich Edson, David Spunt, Aishah Hasnie, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 7,751 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: OK, that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good job.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: You know, Shannon, sometimes I'm an itchy bear. Sometimes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you scratch that itch and you move on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you, have a good one. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The former chief of staff to President Biden, answers questions about mental acuity, forgetfulness, and ambient.\par \par \uc2 An imprisoned confidant to the late sexual predator \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  talks to a top justice department official.\par \par \uc2 And is America's organ transplant system broken? Government officials have a concerning report.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", some members of Congress are calling for more action as the DOJ creates a strike force to investigate allegations that then-President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and his administration manipulated intelligence in a conspiracy against President Trump. This comes as President Trump comes face to face with the man he has been criticizing for weeks, wrapping up a visit to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  headquarters here in Washington a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson is on the North Lawn tonight with the details. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 For months, President Trump has insulted the federal reserve chairman. Just a couple of hours ago, the two met at the Fed's headquarters here in Washington. Their meeting began with a disagreement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: 2.7 is now 3.1.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: I'm not a word --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes. It just came out.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Yes, yes. I haven't heard that from anybody at the Fed.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And it's just came out.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Well, the president, greeting the federal reserve chairman, he is previously called a, "stubborn mule" and a "stupid person". President Trump toward the Fed's headquarters, which is in the middle of a $2.5 billion renovation. Trump calls the overall cost disgraceful. The Fed blames the $700 million in overruns on design changes and inflation impacting materials, equipment, and labor.\par \par \uc2 The president today criticized Powell's job performance standing right next to him.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'd love him to lower interest rates.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Powell has argued inflation is still too high to lower rates. Down Constitution Avenue at the Justice Department, Attorney General Pam Bondi says she is created a strike force to assess allegations from Director of National Intelligence \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , that \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration officials manipulated intelligence about Russia's interference in the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 Republican senators \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , say Bondi should go further and appoint a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): In light of these new revelations that an investigation would be appropriate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Instead of him.\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: And somebody who is truly or at least as far as they can be in this town, independent, would be -- would help restore public confidence.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): This week, Gabbard declassified a 2017 House Republican Intelligence Committee report, claiming intelligence community conclusions that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  helped the 2016 Trump campaign were rushed and subjected to unusual directives from President \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: But that's why we wrote the report in 2017, you could tell then that John Brennan and other intelligence officials were in cahoots with \uc2 Obama\uc2 , to put a cloud over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's presidency.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): A spokesperson for \uc2 Obama\uc2  calls the allegations ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): As for whether the justice department will appoint a special counsel, officials say, for now, they are satisfied with Attorney General Bondi strike team that's going to look into the documents for any potential crimes. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Rich, the president also signed an executive order on homelessness today. What was that about?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Yes, he did in the oval office before he went to the Fed. It encourages states municipalities to move people from the streets who are suffering from addiction or mental illness into hospitals or treatment centers. It also will direct federal grants to jurisdictions that enforce prohibitions on open illicit drug use, urban camping, loitering, and urban squatting. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson, live in the North Lawn. Rich, thank you. We are learning tonight some of what the man who was chief of staff to President Biden has to say about his -- the president's mental health while in office.\par \par \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  testified for several hours during a closed-door interview with the House Oversight Committee today. He acknowledged the president was more forgetful, but said there was no reason to doubt his mental acuity. The panel is investigating whether there was a cover up to hide the president's condition.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the story tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Do you ever see the former president take Ambien? Did he take it in front of you?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): President Biden's former chief of staff, refusing to answer Fox's questions, but did talk to lawmakers today in a closed-door deposition with the House Oversight Committee, as they investigate the alleged cover up of Biden's cognitive decline. Sources tell Fox, Klain, who was brought back in for debate prep, said Biden appeared tired and ill before the now infamous 2024 presidential debate. But that he has no knowledge of the president taking Ambien. That comes days after \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  blamed his father's disastrous performance on that sleeping pill.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 , SECOND SON OF FORMER PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : He is 81 years old. He is tired as --\par \par \uc2 They give him Ambien to be able to sleep. He gets up on the stage and he looks like he's deer in headlights.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Republicans want to know if the president was ever taking Ambien at any other point, and if it affected his decision making?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): That is a mind-altering drug. Was that a one-time deal, or was he on Ambien every day? If so, then, that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 Sources also tell Fox, Klain said Biden was less energetic and more forgetful, often confusing names and proper nouns, which got worse over time. But he still believed Biden have the acuity to govern. Klain's five- hour long interview today a departure from the three Biden insiders who previously pled The Fifth.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): He answered every question comprehensively. Didn't avoid a single question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, some more insight from inside the room, that deposition today, our source tells us that \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  told lawmakers that former national security adviser Jake Sullivan came up to him and told him that he believed that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was less effective in 2024 compared to 2022, and that Sullivan and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  both approached him and told him that they no longer thought that Biden was politically viable. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also, "BREAKING TONIGHT", a top justice department official met today with former Epstein confidant, Ghislaine Maxwell. The session happened in a Florida prison where Maxwell is serving a sentence for conspiring with Epstein to sexually abuse minors.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has details tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Ghislaine Maxwell's attorneys praised Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche and the justice department for meeting with her, in person today, not far from that prison at a federal courthouse in Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 Now, the courthouse was just miles from where Ghislaine Maxwell is serving a 20-year sentence behind bars. The discussion lasted for several hours, and while the DOJ is not commenting on what was said, Maxwell's attorney gave us some details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARKUS, ATTORNEY TO GHISLAINE MAXWELL: He took a full day and asked a lot of questions, and Ms. Maxwell answered every single question. She never stopped -- she never invoked a privilege. She never declined to answer. She answered all the questions truthfully, honestly, and to the best of her ability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But Bret, legal experts question whether Maxwell told her version of the truth or the actual truth. We have some recent photos snapped by Fox Digital of Maxwell outside that Florida prison on a run.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO\uc2 , FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL, DEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE: I think, the reason you send someone like Blanche instead of an assistant U.S. attorney is because you want to show Maxwell that the government is serious about making some kind of deal.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell is not going to say a single word. She has no incentive to say a single word unless she gets some kind of benefit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And tonight, we are awaiting decisions from two federal judges in New York who will decide if they will release grand jury transcripts from investigations, involving both \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  and Ghislaine Maxwell's cases there.\par \par \uc2 Epstein died in prison in 2019 before ever going to trial on sex trafficking. Yesterday, a Florida judge indicated she would not release the grand jury material in Epstein solicitation of a minor case in Florida back in 2008. That judge says her hands are tied, as anything to do with a grand jury in the American court system, Bret, typically, as we know, kept close and secretive. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have a name in mind to replace the chairman.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One person.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Two people. Maybe three.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any clues?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I pick him, but I think he's not leaving the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, President Trump, visited the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  headquarters a short time ago, coming face to face with Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , who has criticized over interest rates. Let's talk about that and with someone seemed to be a top contender to be the next chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . We'll see. Former Federal Reserve Governor, \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2 . Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WARSH\uc2 , FORMER FEDERAL RESERVE GOVERNOR: Hey, Bret. Great to be with you. Great. To be on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I might as well get to the big question first. I know, you to be one of the people that the president is considering for that job to be the next fed chairman after Chairman Powell, either finishes his term or resigns.\par \par \uc2 So, the big question, if you were in the role of Fed chair at the next highly anticipated July meeting, would you cut rates?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: So, if the meeting is next week, I'd be voting for a rate cut. That's not news. That's been my view for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 I think part of the reason why interest rates are as high as they are, why first-time homebuyers are struggling to get a new mortgage is the Fed is fighting the last war. They have lost credibility because they let inflation get out of control in '21, '22, and beyond, and now, they are keeping rates higher than they need.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have been talking about a regime change.\par \par \uc2 WARSH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've been writing about it, talking about it for years. So, if you were cutting rates, would it be significant?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: Yes. So, I think you're right. Rate cuts are the place to begin. They are not the place to end. Interest rate cuts are a first step to what I've said for a decade is regime change that's necessary at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . That means regime change in how we're thinking about inflation, how we're conducting policy, how we're communicating. It's also regime change in terms of how we're supervising and regulating banks.\par \par \uc2 Just to give you one little example, the Fed right now is carrying almost a $7 trillion balance sheet, Bret. That's like emergency liquidity for Wall Street. Well, no surprise, Wall Street is doing great. Main Street is not doing as well. So, what we can do is shrink the size of the balance sheet in coordination with the Treasury, and cut interest rates on Main Street. We'll have a stronger economy as part of a comprehensive reform package at the Fed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, the Larry Kudlows, the Charles Paynes, all the folks on Fox Business, they are in this all the time. But there is a lot of people that look at the Fed and it's like a mystery. How do you explain it for the average person sitting on the couch?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: Yes. So, I'd say part of the reason it's a mystery is fed people try to keep a little bit of magic around it. Try to keep a little bit of mystique. You know, after all, the Fed is a very special institution. It is not independent of government. Its monetary policy is independent inside of government. But I think I played it. I put it simply. When the president was there to see the building today, I don't know if they showed it to him, but the Fed has a printing press. And that printing press can provide all the money in the world. It can run it hotter, it can run a cold, and that's the instrument that the Fed uses to try to provide lower or higher interest rates, easier or tighter money for Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 But the more important thing, and all that stuff that the president might have seen today, is fed credibility. The leaders of the Fed have to be credible. So, when they cut rates, long term interest rates fall.\par \par \uc2 Let me just give you one example. Last September, about 45 days before the election, Chairman Powell and his colleagues decided to an emergency rate cut. Then, about 40 days later, they cut rates even more. What happened? Long term interest rates went up? That's the Bronx cheer. That's markets in the business community saying you made a mistake. You need a credible new set of leaders and new policies. So, when we cut rates as a first step, interest rates fall. 30 fixed -- 30-year fixed rate mortgages fall with it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the president talks about a booming economy. How do you see the economy, and can rates be cut without affecting inflation?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: If and only if you have a credible central bank who's not making a series of mistakes. The \uc2 Central Bank\uc2  made mistakes in 2018, when they raised rates into a market that fell. 2020, when they came up with a new regime that brought inflation and the great mistake leading to the great inflation, and they are making another mistake now.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the short answer is credible central banks can lower interest rates, shrink the balance sheet, pull back from being in the front-page news every day, and deliver a stronger economy.\par \par \uc2 Let me just make one more point. The president has said repeatedly, we could be in a golden age of the economy. I think he's right. I think what's happening in what we call A.I., what other people would just call a technology jump, it's a huge productivity increase.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. can lead the world in a five or 10-year period. Our companies are more nimble, our companies will be more profitable, and workers can get paid more, but not if the administration has bad economic policy coming from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, you've got the latest economic figures, jobs added 147,000. 4.1 percent unemployment rate, the inflation at 0.3 percent. But you always -- also look at these big numbers of the national debt at $36 7 trillion, $1.3 trillion for the national deficit. At what point does that start to add up as a major problem for our country? Can we grow our way to solving that?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: So, the president inherited a fiscal and monetary mess. He's only been in office for six months. He's made a lot of down payments about getting that in the right direction. That's why economic growth, I think, is poised to grow. Economic growth can do a lot. Every percentage point of economic growth that we grow above what the consensus estimate is, is $5 trillion in revenue over the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Boy, that helps pay down the debt. I'll make one other point. It's not just economic growth. We have to show markets that we are serious about cutting expenses.\par \par \uc2 So, people might say, oh, how important is $2-1/2 billion building at the Federal Reserve?\par \par \uc2 But if we can show that we're putting our own house in order, then the financial markets will give us time to live -- to grow through and cut this debt over time without a crisis. And that's what the treasury secretary and fed chairman have to do together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Should the Fed have this dual mandate? Full employment and inflation, or maybe just keep inflation?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: So, there is a bunch of parliaments around the world, they all have different mandates, yet, they -- many of them have made the exact same mistakes. We don't need changes in legislation. We need changes in leadership.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Regime change.\par \par \uc2 WARSH: Regime change in everything the Fed does, so that when we get hit by new facts, the new leaders have credibility and don't have to fight the last war. They can take on the challenges in front of us, which is probably a productivity boom that's going to lead the U.S. to win the 21st Century.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You want the Job?\par \par \uc2 WARSH: Well, that's up to the president, but I am prepared for public service. I did 11 years a hard time about 11 or 12 years ago, Bret, and if the President calls, I'd be honored to serve.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: During a tough time, by the way, during the 2008 crash.\par \par \uc2 WARSH: That's why I needed a break from all this. But you know, I've been lucky to be in the private sector last 11 or 12 years. I grew up in a town a couple hundred miles away from all the bright lights in the big city. This would be my way to do public service. But again, that's not my decision to make. It's the president's.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kevin, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 WARSH: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New record closes for two of the major stock indices as investors express some trade optimism. The Dow lost 316 but the S&P 500 finished ahead four, a new high finishing mark, the NASDAQ also with a new high close, gaining 38.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what the U.S. Special Envoy is saying about today's talks to end the fighting between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists.\par \par \uc2 And later, the federal government takes a big step forward in battling one major city's sanctuary policies.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Israel is currently reviewing the response by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to the latest cease fire proposal. One Israeli source saying that response was workable, but the chief American negotiator is saying just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst tells us where things stand tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, Israeli negotiators are returning from Doha to review new cease fire terms proposed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Reports indicate the group issued new demands, including that more Palestinian prisoners be exchanged for Israeli hostages, 20 of whom are believed to be alive.\par \par \uc2 The demands prompting U.S. Special Envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2 , who is now in Italy for meetings with Qatari counterparts to bring home American mediators. Witkoff saying, in part, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  does not appear to be coordinated or acting in good faith, we will now consider alternative options to bring the hostages home and try to create a more stable environment for the people of Gaza. It is a shame that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has acted in this selfish way.\par \par \uc2 Inside Gaza, a humanitarian disaster is looming. A Palestinian baby named Aldai (ph) is placed on a scale in Gaza City. The young boy's parents were killed. Aldai's uncle worries his life is slipping away.\par \par \uc2 EID SAEED SIYAM, UNCLE (through translator): Aldai is suffering severe malnutrition. He's not accepting milk or nutrients or supplements at all, and that's all due to his very difficult health condition.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Aldai story is not unique. One million children are at risk of increasing hunger in the Gaza Strip, according to \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Disagreements between the heads of \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agencies and the Israeli military about how to distribute aid already inside the Enclave is pushing the Palestinian territory deeper into crisis.\par \par \uc2 ROSS SMITH, WORLD FOOD PROGRAM: One of the main challenges we have is that the number of delays inside Gaza once we are moving from the entry points to Gaza to our final destination points inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Tonight, Fox News obtained new video showing chaotic scenes inside Gaza as \uc2 U.N.\uc2  trucks entered, hundreds of Palestinians rushing these trucks trying to get any aid possible, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv. Trey, thanks. France is announcing it plans to recognize Palestine as a state. French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  says he will formalize that decision at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  in September. This would make France the first G7 nation to do so. The U.S. does not recognize a Palestinian state.\par \par \uc2 Multiple officials tell Fox News the Pentagon's inspector has received evidence that the U.S.'s military strike plans shared by Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  over Signal derived from a classified e-mail labeled secret, no foreign, which stands for no foreign nationals should see it. That was first reported by \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fox is also told the inspector general received the classified e-mail with markings early in its investigation, which began in April.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Hegseth team is responding. Chief Pentagon Spokesman Sean Parnell says in a statement to Fox in part, "The Department stands behind its previous statements, no classified information was shared via Signal."\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Justice Department is going after a major city tonight because of its sanctuary policy. That live shot may give it away, we'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, a plane crash in Russia's Far East leaves all 48 passengers dead, and crew also dead. Russia's emergency situations ministry says it found the burning fuselage of the Soviet designed twin turbo prop plane on a hillside south of its planned destination, about 4,400 miles east of Moscow. Cause of the crash still unknown.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Australia, one of the big stories there tonight, Australia says it will reduce restrictions on U.S. beef imports, in a move the Trump administration calls a major victory over nine -- nonscientific trade barriers. Australia's agriculture minister says relaxing the restrictions design -- is designed to keep Australia free of mad cow disease would not compromise bio security.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The Justice Department is suing New York City and Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  over the city's sanctuary policies. Correspondent Nate Foy has specifics tonight from New York. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Bret. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department says New York City's sanctuary laws violate the supremacy because of the Constitution which gives federal law precedence over state or local laws. The lawsuit argues that sanctuary laws, quote, "facilitate the release of dangerous criminals into the community by directing city employees to refuse to transfer such aliens to federal officials in a secure environment".\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit references two illegal migrants who are now accused of shooting an off-duty \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2  officer in the face in New York City Saturday night. Investigators do not believe the victim was targeted for being a CBP officer.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  previously arrested both migrants several times, but in each instance, they were released despite having active ICE detainers and deportation orders in place.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, WHITE HOUSE BORDER CZAR: Sanctuary cities are sanctuaries for criminals, hard stop, bottom line. That's what it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Attorney General Pam Bondi says tonight, quote, "If New York City won't stand up for the safety of its citizens, we will".\par \par \uc2 The office for Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  is reacting to the lawsuit as well. His spokesperson tells FOX, quote, "The mayor supports the essence of the local laws put in place by the city council, but he has also been clear they go too far when it comes to dealing with those violent criminals on our streets."\par \par \uc2 Back in April, the Justice Department moved to drop corruption charges against Mayor Adams. DOJ officials said it was in part so he could focus on immigration enforcement and crime. This week, the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  says of the 6,000 ICE detainers issued in New York City, only a handful have been honored. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another New York story. New York mayoral nominee Zohran Mamdani is facing criticism from Democrats and others over many of his past statements and positions. The self-described democratic socialist has even managed to insult the top Democrat in the House of Representatives. Correspondent Bryan Llenas shows us tonight from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI, (D) NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: If you don't clap for -- you are a Zionist. That's a joke. You don't have to clap.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's Zohran Mamdani using "Zionist" as an insulting punchline back in 2023.\par \par \uc2 MAMDANI: The struggle for Palestinian liberation was at the core of my politics and continues to be.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: These resurfaced clips causing a stir as the self-described democratic socialist has failed to condemn the phrase "globalize the intifada," seen by many as a call to violence against Jews. Only recently is Mamdani discouraging it's use following public outcry. Pennsylvania's Jewish Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , a potential 2028 Democratic presidential candidate, now speaking out against Mamdani, quote, "You have to speak and act with more clarity. And when supporters of yours say things that are blatantly antisemitic, you can't leave room for that to just sit there. You've got to condemn that."\par \par \uc2 Democratic House Minority Leader Hakeem Jefferies has yet to endorse Mamdani.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: We agreed to reconvene.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Just two months after the October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror attack on Israel, Mamdani called out Jeffries at an event he was headlining.\par \par \uc2 MAMDANI: And I must ask Congressman Jeffries how many more Palestinians must be killed before you call for a ceasefire?\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: In 2022, Mamdani even compared Jeffries to one of the most infamous segregationists in U.S. history. Quote, "\uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  got on stage at a rally and in NYC and paraphrased George Wallace. Israel today, Israel tomorrow, Israel forever."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Jeffries insists these prior jabs by Mamdani won't influence his endorsement decision. A spokesman saying, "Past political discourse is in the past. Mamdani has been vacationing in Uganda. He will meet with Jeffries again when he returns amid concerns endorsing Mamdani could hurt Democrats 'efforts to take back Congress in 2026. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, tributes are pouring in for the late wrestling icon Hulk Hogan. The bicep busting giant who turned the sport into a massive business and stretched his cultural influence far beyond the ring died today in Clearwater, Florida. National correspondent Jeff Paul looks back at Hulkamania from Los Angeles. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Police and medics in Clearwater, Florida, responded to Hulk Hogan's today on a call for cardiac arrest. Hogan was treated but later died at a nearby hospital. Authorities say there were no signs of foul play.\par \par \uc2 The official \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account for Hulk Hogan posting this picture, saying in part, "May we all take solace in the wonderful memories he left behind for the millions of fans worldwide whose lives he touched for more than four decades."\par \par \uc2 Born Terry Gene Bollea in Augusta, Georgia, in 1953, hogan began his career in wrestling in 1977, eventually signing to what would become the WWE. With his signature blonde hair, horseshoe mustache, and red and yellow outfit, Hogan would headline Wrestlemania eight times, fueling Hulkamania worldwide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is the greatest professional athlete in the world today. Look at this!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He slammed him! I don't believe it! I don't believe it!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hulk dropping the big leg! It's over!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Beyond the wrestling ring, he got into acting, appearing in movies like "Rocky 3" and eventually his own reality show with his then wife Linda and kids Brooke and Nick. But most recently, Hogan got into politics, appearing at last year's Republican Convention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HULK HOGAN, FORMER WRESTLER: And I say let Trump-a-mania, run wild, brother!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What brought you out here? Why are you here?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Bret, I get tired of remaining silent. I sat back and watched the borders collapse. I watched the economy collapse. I watched the price of gas, the price of food, everything just do an upside down flip. And when they took a shot at my hero, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I realized that I couldn't be silent anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: President Trump reacting, saying, in part, "We lost a great friend today, the Hulkster. Hulk Hogan was MAGA all the way, strong, tough, smart, but with the biggest heart." Hogan leaves believes behind his wife Sky and two kids from his previous marriage, as well as a legacy that spans generations of fans. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sad news. Jeff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, two-time Grammy award-winning musician Chuck Mangione has died. Mangione achieved international success in 1977 with his jazz flavored single "Feel So Good." He later became a voice actor on the animated TV comedy "King of the Hill". His attorney says Mangione died at his home in Rochester, New York, Tuesday. Chuck Mangione was 84.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The fertility rate in the U.S. has reached an all-time low. The federal government says the numbers for 2024 work out to fewer than 1.6 children per woman. The U.S. is once among a few developed countries with a rate that insured each generation had enough children to replace itself. That number is about 2.1 children per woman.\par \par \uc2 There are growing concerns tonight about the safety and efficacy of the U.S. organ donation system. One government report details how these horror stories are coming out of disturbing practices. Now the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2  is pledging reforms. Correspondence Mark Meredith looks at the situation tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Federal officials say far too often, organ donors being prepped for post death in surgery while they may still be alive and have a chance to survive.\par \par \uc2 DR. RAYMOND LYNCH, \uc2 HEALTH RESOURCES AND SERVICES ADMINISTRATION\uc2 : This can be fixed and the trust restored by making sure people are performing adequate exams.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: This week, the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2  told Congress these near misses are both disturbing and horrifying. The Trump administration recently reopening an investigation into one organ procurement organization, or OPO, serving Kentucky, southwest Ohio, and parts of West Virginia. It reviewed 351 cases where donations were authorized but not completed, finding 73 patients were still showing neurological signs, 28 may not have been dead when the procedure started. Officials blaming the problem on poor assessments, lack of communication, and people not properly determining how someone died.\par \par \uc2 Network for Hope, an OPO under review, says it's constantly adapting.\par \par \uc2 BARRY MASSA, NETWORK FOR HOPE CEO: We are putting in multiple changes in our processes so that these kind of things do not recur. And we are still doing our investigation into those cases.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Congress says this issue is too important to ignore.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRETT GUTHRIE\uc2 , (R-KY): We need more people to donate. We need people to be donors.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Kentucky Congressman \uc2 Brett Guthrie\uc2 's mother died while waiting for a liver transplant. Now he is among many lawmakers calling for more transparency in the donation process, with more than 100,000 people currently waiting for a transplant.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The conclusion is going to come when we have confidence in the system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Federal officials say while this problem is being looked at nationwide, the biggest vulnerabilities are occurring in smaller rural hospitals where they believe there are systematic gaps in oversight, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the meeting between a top Justice Department official and former \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  confidant Ghislaine Maxwell. Plus, President Trump at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , how about that.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 8 in Cleveland as one of three officers shot in an ambush have died. Officer Phillip Wagner and two others show yesterday afternoon. Authorities say the 28-year-old shooter was killed in an exchange of gunfire.\par \par \uc2 FOX 50 in Raleigh, North Carolina, as FOX News confirms \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairman Michael Whatley is planning to run for the open \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat in North Carolina. This comes after President Trump's daughter- in-law \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  said she will not run for that seat. FOX News also confirming former two-term North Carolina Democratic governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  is expected to launch a Senate campaign as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look in San Diego from our affiliate FOX 5, a nice night out there. One of the big stories there tonight, NASCAR announces it will hold a street race at the Coronado Naval Base in southern California as a replacement for its Chicago event that was held the last three years. The organization says it wants to celebrate the 250th anniversary of the Navy with its first ever street race on an active military base.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway, way outside from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: So we're taking a look, and it looks like it's about $3.1 billion, it went a little bit, or a lot. So the $2.7 is now 3.1. It just came out.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIR, FEDERAL RESERVE: I haven't heard that from anybody at the Fed.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It just came out. I don't know who does that.\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Are you including the renovation? You just added in a third building is what that is. That's a third building.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's the building that's being built.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was some TV this after. President Trump with Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at the newly under construction Fed building.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News contributor, host of "The Big Ben Podcast," Ben Domenech, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, and Olivia Beavers of "The Wall Street Journal".\par \par \uc2 It was just awkward, Ben.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The president likes to confront people in person and he likes to bring things and put them front of them and make them react to them. It was clearly sort of a little bit of an ambush I think you can fairly say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even with the hats.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Even with the hats. I will say, as someone who once worked for politicians, the rule is generally no hats, and the president was, I think, quick to take his off after that.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that I think is going on here that we need to appreciate is that the Fed is something that is for a lot people in this, \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2  sort of said before, a kind of a mystery box, and that their behavior is something that I think the president and his allies can fairly depict as being something that as being a drag on the economy, as perhaps working at odds with what they would like it to do and to see. And this was clearly something that was meant to send that message.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, it does seem, though, that \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  may stay until the end of his term, which is in May. But the administration may name their choice, the president may name a choice in December or January.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Correct, and the reality is, if he went ahead now, he could force the resignation. But we have seen in recent days that he has been advised against forcing Powell --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How the markets would react.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And I think he has fought a little bit against the reporting, but basically the treasury secretary has said that he doesn't think it's a good idea.\par \par \uc2 Now what you see is, I think, more theater. Again, I think, just to pick up on what Ben was talking about, the president likes to invite people into the Oval Office and school them, lecture them, even lambaste them. And in this case, I think he made Powell look like the little guy. He looked kind of ridiculous. He reminded me of Dukakis in that helmet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He is a developer, President Trump, so he feels comfortable in those hard hats, and he goes around a construction site and says this is overbudget and the whole deal. So it was about imaging.\par \par \uc2 What about on Capitol Hill, Olivia, where people stand on all this and how it sits?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Some of President Trump's allies are pushing him to get rid of \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . We talked about this on your show previously. He has brought it up with Freedom Caucus numbers, r at least that's what some of the reporting has indicated. But there are others, like you said, who think it's going to be a really bad idea. It would impact the markets. And they think just run out the clock here.\par \par \uc2 And there's also been an element, right, where the president's tariffs might be impacting cutting rates. So it's a tricky thing to juggle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's turn. Deputy attorney general meeting with the Epstein associate Ghislaine Maxwell. This is the White House press secretary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The Department of Justice has moved forward with the request to unseal that grand jury testimony. They have also moved forward with interviewing Miss Maxwell.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL, (D-CT): No ordinary person can send their Department of Justice henchmen to some kind of cover-up talking to a convicted felon in a federal prison.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: This is a much more significant interview than many of us might have anticipated. She is someone who may have a lot of information that simply has not been revealed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we did hear for the first time after this happened from Maxwell's attorney. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARKUS, MAXWELL ATTORNEY: He took a full day and asked a lot of questions. And Miss Maxwell answered every single question. She never stopped, she never invoked a privilege