a. Trump is more like gold mine to wall. But I don't think he'll be embracing the nature bathing trend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I could have been sunbathing on the beach. You have never seen a body so beautiful. Much better than Sleepy Joe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He told Juicy today that he hadn't sunbathed since he was seven. So, I guess that's out, Laura. He just doesn't have the time for it. So that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But he is -- you know, people say he's not self-deprecating but he actually is, and he's very funny about it. Like, my beautiful luscious hair, you know. He always -- you know, he does that a lot, but it's very funny.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, as you know, he keeps a tight schedule, and some of these African leaders discovered that today at this important meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I appreciate it very much. I appreciate it. Maybe we're going to have to go a little bit quicker than this because we a whole schedule. If I could just say, you know, your name and your country, it would be great.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And move it along, Alice. Alice, get to the point. It's like Edith. Edith. Edith, come on, wrap it up.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is a very important meeting. He had five African leaders there, and they were really there to help. Because we got rid of aid, he's replacing it with trade. He's trying to do an outreach that the Chinese have been doing for years, dominating the Western African ports and those rare earth minerals. And Trump even complimented them for a moment. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, thank you. Such good English, such beautiful. Where did you learn to speak so beautifully? Where -- were you educated? Where? In Liberia.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's very interesting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: There was a lot of compliments, Laura, for peace and being able to speak English. That is the official language in Liberia.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, given what we've heard from some of the protest class in the streets, the anti-ICE people, I think the praising people's English and diction is fantastic. But I think it's -- I think -- look, this is where Trump does not get enough credit.\par \par \uc2 He is trying to forge new relationships with these countries and say, look, it'll be better for you, more prosperous for you, safer for you, you'll have more freedom if you partner with us, that's good stuff. Jameson Greer of the USTR was sitting next to him.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's important. Rare earth, Laura. We need these minerals.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember, Raymond, if I see it, I can take it. Raymond Arroyo, thank you. That's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. Some cool new stuff up there. It's America Now and Forever. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250710el7900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Signs Landmark Legislation At White House; Trump To Watch Fireworks Before Heading To New Jersey; Rescue Efforts Underway Amid Fatal Texas Flooding; President Trump Informed About Flood Devastation; Non- Violent Protesters In NYC Against 4th Of July; Topics Include Iran, Gaza Ceasefire And Hostage Release; Trump Hopes To Bring Gulf Nation Into Normalization; U.S. Stockpile Concerns As Russian Air Strikes Ramp Up; Kremlin Says \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Will "Not Back Down"; Tropical Storm Watch Issued Along Southeast Coastline; Seven Still Missing After Fireworks Warehouse Explosion; Joey Chestnut Dominates Hot Dog Eating Contest; Thousands Participate In Peachtree Road Race; New Book Examines Keys To Happiness In Life And Work; Trump Bill Passage Caps Off Series Of Victories\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Mark Meredith, Danamarie McNicholl, Nate Foy,  Madeleine Rivera, Jennifer Griffin, Alex Hogan, Mike Seidel, Gillian  Turner, Jeff Paul, Bret Baier \par \uc2 8,450 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 STEVE CLEMONS, FOUNDING EDITOR AT LARGE, SEMAFOR: You know, a lot of those states supported President Trump, and a lot of those people are going to have a tougher time with Medicaid, with snap payments, with just the way they live their life.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLEMONS: You know, it's going to be phased in, but that's going to be tough on some of them. That's what we heard from \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 . \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 . I don't think they're politicking against the president. I think they are -- they are trying to raise something that to Trey and Charlie's point, they are going to have to make sure those -- this works for those people, or they are looking at some real vulnerabilities politically.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Panel, stand by. Want to go now to Mark Meredith. He has got -- he is there on the South Lawn of the White House covering this event as it unfolds. The Big, Beautiful Bill is now a law. The signing ceremony you just saw featured military flyovers, Republican allies of the president and military families there with the president and the first lady. They are going to now have a picnic. The president is touting his victory as a major tax cut for the middle class, a win for the border, as Trey just said, and national defense.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's bring in correspondent Mark Meredith, who is there at the signing. He joins us from the middle of all of it. Hey, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Gillian, I sure do. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Once again, the president had quite the setup for this bill signing, one of the most sweeping pieces of legislation we have seen come out of Congress in recent memory. It was passed by the slimmest of margins. We're still talking about a $3 trillion package, it will have an impact on pretty much all of government, but the president making it very clear tonight, he is ready to take a victory lap.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): After months of negotiations, President Trump's massive tax and policy package, what he dubbed the One Big, Beautiful Bill, is now law.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Here is a triumph of democracy, on the birthday of democracy. It's the most popular bill ever signed in the history of our country, whether you're military or anybody else, this is the most single most popular bill ever signed.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): The package passed the House, Thursday, without any Democratic support.\par \par \uc2 Two Republicans also voted against it over cost concerns. The package adds $45 billion for border wall construction and another 45 billion for detention facilities. It extends tax cuts which were set to expire, and creates new deductions on money Americans earn from Social Security benefits, overtime, and tips.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT, DIRECTOR, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: Last time, we gave you a three percent growth and $6,000 into the pockets of the typical family. We are looking at growth much higher than that and more than $10,000 going into the pockets of the typical family.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Democrats say the bill is a bust, and that new work requirements for Medicaid and food stamp recipients will leave millions of Americans scrambling for support.\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D-MD): When they learn the details of the Greed Act of 2025, they realize, this is the largest consolidation of wealth that we have seen in our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Democrats say they want the legislation to dominate their midterm messaging. The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee says it will "Make sure every battleground voter knows how vulnerable House Republicans abandon them by passing the most unpopular piece of legislation in modern American history."\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they're ready to defend the legislation as they bet Americans will see tax cuts and more border security as something we are celebrating.\par \par \uc2 REP. CLAUDIA TENNEY (R-NY): We are bolstering our support for the border. We're bolstering our support for our military, which is so important with what's been happening around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): While this bill went into a lot of aver to address taxes and the domestic economy, one of the big questions would be, of course, what happens with the tariffs? The president, say he is sending out new letters as soon as this week to more countries, letting them know he wants these trade deals to move a lot faster.\par \par \uc2 We saw the one with Vietnam get announced, but there are a lot of countries still on the list. So, we'll looking to see if the president drops any hints about where they may lead as he wraps up the celebration.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, I have to mention, I was out here five years ago for the Fourth of July of 2020. It was the pandemic. It was such a subdued atmosphere. But seeing the president out here tonight, you can see only he's feeling very good about where he is as kicks off this legislative achievement. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Try and stay cool out there as best as you can. Thank you, Mark.\par \par \uc2 Also "BREAKING TONIGHT", multiple deaths reported in Texas after heavy rain there caused flash flooding. Search teams are now using boats to conduct rescues as fast-moving water there. You can see it on your screen, threatens riverfront communities across the state.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Danamarie McNicholl joins us now with that story. Good evening. Danamarie.\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Gillian. Well, catastrophic flash flooding really came through the Guadalupe, excuse me, Guadalupe River Valley, overnight, taking people off guard, while they slept.\par \par \uc2 Now, dangerous conditions turned deadly in just under two hours. They didn't have time to evacuate. Officials say in Kerr County, sheriffs they just announced 13 fatalities. Local officials there say that there is not a flood warning in place for Kerrville. That's just a city 60 miles northwest of San Antonio. It saw almost a foot of rain before 9:00 a.m. this morning.\par \par \uc2 Now, throughout the day, the swift water rescue crews have been looking for people stranded in homes, vehicles, and even trees. Kerr County officials say an unknown number of people remained unaccounted for. Law enforcement officers, they are now working to identify people who died in the flooding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROB KELLY, JUDGE, KERR COUNTY, TEXAS: Most of them, we don't know who they are. We haven't had fingerprint them, have seen along, and one of them was completely naked. She hadn't any I.D. at all. We're trying to get the identity. We don't have it yet.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (voice over): A major concern are the children in the children's summer camps along the river valley. At one camp, around 20 young girls are still missing. That's according to the lieutenant governor of Texas. Questions remain about why camps did not evacuate yesterday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're the judge. I mean, you're the top official here in this county. Why can't you answer that? There are kids missing. These camps were in harm's way. We knew this flood is coming.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: We didn't know this flood is coming. Rest assured, no one knew this kind of flood is coming. We have floods all the time. This is the most dangerous river valley in the United States.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I agree.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: And we deal with floods on a regular basis. When it rains, we get water. We had no reason to believe this is going to be anything like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (on camera): And this is still an active situation. Officials are telling people to still shelter in place. The president is aware of what's happening, and the lieutenant governor says that they mentioned they will receive any resources they need. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Danamarie, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  now were working closely with state and local law enforcement to try and ensure a safe and secure Independence Day for folks all across the nation. Millions expected to gather in New York City for the annual fireworks display. There, it's pretty spectacular. Let's take a look now at how officials plan to keep that event safe. What we can expect from all those pyrotechnics.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in correspondent Nate Foy. Hey, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gilian, good evening. So, yes. the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  isn't aware of any specific credible threats tonight in New York or anywhere else across the country. But the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is operating in a heightened threat environment, as they have been since the October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror attack. They are specifically focused on terrorist sympathizers who are inspired by the conflict overseas.\par \par \uc2 Today, in New York, we did see nonviolent protesters light an American flag on fire. Take a look at that video from Washington Square Park as protesters chanted, F The Fourth, and "We need a new flag." Authorities say they are ready for anything. A DHS spokesperson tells Fox, "We are enhancing security measures and coordinating intelligence sharing to proactively address potential risks, including those from lone wolf actors."\par \par \uc2 Now, local police departments like the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  will rely on federal partners to take down any drone threats, and that's because it's an authority that local agencies don't have. Also, in the sky tonight, the largest fireworks show in the country. You mentioned, 80,000 firework shells will be fired from barges on the East River between Manhattan and Brooklyn, with 30 different colors. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  says 8-1/2 million tourists are visiting the city this weekend. Police are utilizing plain clothes officers, bomb sniffing dogs and marine units to keep everyone safe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN CHELL, CHIEF OF PATROL, NEW YORK POLICE DEPARTMENT: We'll be prepared like we always are, in a mission to keep people safe, and have the community come down, have a great Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: So, ahead of tonight's big fireworks show, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  shut down the Brooklyn Bridge in both directions. And to gain access to the East River waterfront, you need a city issued ticket, and you need to go through a security screening. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Nate Foy, in The Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  for us tonight. Happy Fourth to you.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge now has temporarily blocked the Trump administration from deporting eight illegal migrants to the politically unstable nation of South Sudan. This comes just a day after the Supreme Court gave the go ahead. Lawyers for these men today sought emergency legal relief. The men are currently being held in neighboring Djibouti. They are in U.S. custody. They are awaiting a final legal decision.\par \par \uc2 Well, the stock market was closed today in observance of the holiday for the shortened trading week. The Dow gained 2-3/10 of a percent. The S&P 500 added 1-1/4. NASDAQ jumped 1-7/10.\par \par \uc2 We have an update for you on a breaking news story we brought to you last night. Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  prepares to come here to Washington next week. Multiple sources confirmed to Bret Baier that the Saudi defense minister secretly visited the White House, Thursday. He talked about getting to the negotiating table to end the Gaza war, with officials there, including the president.\par \par \uc2 Also talked about getting the release of the remaining hostages. New report now says that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is showing a positive response to the latest peace proposal from Israel. Here is correspondent Madeleine Rivera.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MADELEINE RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Days after striking Iran's nuclear sites, President Trump sees an opening in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Iran does want to speak, and I think they would like to speak to me, and it's time that they do.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA (voice over): Now, it appears the Saudis want to talk to the president too. Multiple sources tell Bret Baier, Saudi Arabia's defense minister and Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's younger brother, \uc2 Khalid bin Salman\uc2  secretly met with President Trump, Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  and Special Envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  at the White House on Thursday. They talked about de-escalation with Iran, ending the war in Gaza, and negotiating the release of the remaining hostages.\par \par \uc2 The goal of the visit was to create what's been described as a peace footing in the region.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Today, we reaffirm this important bond, and we take the next steps to make our relationship closer, stronger, and more powerful than ever before.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA (voice over): President Trump often touts his relationship with Saudi Arabia, which he sees as a key broker in the Middle East. He wants the country to join the Abraham Accords. The conversation on Thursday did not exclusively focus on the possibility of normalizing ties between Saudi Arabia and Israel, but sources say the talks dealt with the steps needed to get to that point, a moment that would mark a major geopolitical shift.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, SPOKESWOMAN, STATE DEPARTMENT: His approach of diplomacy has worked in a number of areas when it comes to not just cease fires, but peace agreements, et cetera, like with the DRC and the Congo. I mean, there is an important dynamic there. But also, when it comes to the emergence of a new Middle East, after our action regarding Iran.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RIVERA (on camera): Regarding the cease fire proposal in Gaza, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says it is prepared to enter a round of negotiations to figure out how to implement the framework. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Madeleine Rivera here in Washington. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Russia unleashes a massive attack on Ukraine overnight as President Trump speaks with both country's leaders.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, forecasters issue warnings over hazardous temperatures in Eastern Europe. The extreme heat there began over a week ago now, it has stifled the western part of the continent. Multiple deaths are being blamed on the heat, which has also forced the closure of the iconic Eiffel Tower.\par \par \uc2 And this, a gas station explosion in southeastern Rome has injured at least 40 people, including 11 police officers and a firefighter. The explosion was heard across the Italian capital, sent up a huge cloud. You can see there of dark smoke and fire.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Rome says local police and firefighters rushed to the scene after receiving reports of a gas leak.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at London, one of the big stories there. Londoners getting a super view this week, as a life-like Superman figure was seen suspended at the top of the shard. The statue scanned from American actor David Corenswet. It stands at 11 feet tall.\par \par \uc2 The one day only installation was created to promote the latest Superman movie. These just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT: Welcome back. Breaking tonight, hours after a Russian air assault on Kyiv, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy says he had a, "Very important and productive phone call with President Trump." That call came just hours after the U.S. appeared to pause some shipments of crucial air defense systems.\par \par \uc2 We've got Fox team coverage for you. Correspondent Alex Hogan has details on last night's aerial assault, but we begin first with Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin with a closer look at those missile defense systems.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Trump spoke this morning with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , who thanked Trump for his support and offered to help the US with Ukrainian drone technology honed during his fight with Russia. Zelenskyy writing, "We spoke about opportunities in air defense and agreed that we will work together to strengthen protection of our skies."\par \par \uc2 Their conversation comes a day after President Trump described a frustrating phone call with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not happy about that. I'm not. I didn't make any progress with him today at all.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): As peace talks remain stalled, Ukraine has expressed concerns about dozens of Patriot missile interceptors that were already loaded onto trucks in Poland for delivery to Ukraine until Defense Secretary Hegseth halted the shipment Monday, citing concerns about U.S. stockpiles. Though President Trump says the U.S. will continue to give Ukraine weapons.\par \par \uc2 Operations in the Middle East have strained U.S. stockpiles of Patriot missile interceptors like the ones U.S. soldiers used to protect Israel and Al Udeid Air Base in Qatar from incoming Iranian ballistic missiles and SM- 3 missiles that the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  has been burning through as it shoots down Iranian and Houthi drones and missiles.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JAMES KILBY, NAVAL OPERATIONS ACTING CHIEF: We are to your point, using them at an alarming rate.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): An assessment by the Washington Examiner estimates 39 of the Army's Terminal High Altitude Area Defense, THAAD interceptors were used in Israel's defense. Yet next year's defense budget calls for just 32 THAAD interceptors to be procured after an entire year's worth of interceptors were fired in just 12 days. Only 12 THAAD interceptors were produced and procured in fiscal year 2025.\par \par \uc2 But the U.S. is not sending THAAD interceptors or SM-3 missiles to Ukraine. Those who track U.S. stockpiles say the U.S. has enough of what Ukraine needs to provide help against Russia's attacks.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. H.R. MCMASTER (RET.), FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: It is true that our defense industrial base is atrophied, but we can do this, you know, and we have co-production with some countries. For example, in Japan, they produce some of these Patriot weapon systems.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): Nothing has been emptied out. Our stockpiles are just about where they were 3-1/2 years ago. We have met a number of demands, not just in Ukraine, but in the Middle East as well. But we are not emptied out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): An analysis of the defense industrial base by the Foundation for the defense of democracies in April shows the U.S. needs to pay attention to waning stockpiles of javelin and Stinger, shoulder fired missiles, short and medium range, air to air missiles and tank shells. \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  and \uc2 Raytheon\uc2  plan to produce 740 Patriot missiles this year, ramping up to 1,100 two years from now, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Jennifer Griffin, here in the D.C. newsroom tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, Ukrainians now scrambling to try and pick up some of the pieces after Russia last night launched what was the largest air assault over Kyiv of the war so far, they struck multiple cities, including the capital. This escalation comes just a few hours after President Trump had a call with Russia's President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan has the details on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Explosions rock Ukraine's capital as Russia carries out its largest aerial attack against Ukraine since its full scale invasion. Thousands of Ukrainians in Kyiv slept in metro stations for safety or crowded into shelters, listening to the sounds of explosions above ground for more than eight hours.\par \par \uc2 At least one person is dead and more than two dozen are injured.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): Everything is destroyed. It is a big loss for me. But to be fair, I'm happy that I am alive.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Today, Dutch and German intelligence agencies warned of new evidence on the battlefield that Russia is using banned chemical weapons like dropping a choking agent from drones to drive soldiers out from the cover of the trenches.\par \par \uc2 This week, Russian forces claimed full control of the Eastern Luhansk region. Russia currently controls about one-fifth of Ukraine. While Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says Ukraine will boost its arms industry, building it up will take time.\par \par \uc2 But for now, Russian aerial attacks are not slowing. Russia launched more than 5,400 drones at Ukraine in June, a new monthly record. The Kremlin released a statement saying Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will, "Not back down."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): The Kremlin says Putin told Trump that Russia plans to agree to a third round of peace talks. No date has been set for future negotiations, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Alex Hogan in London for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up a tropical storm system is threatening the East Coast Independence Day celebrations. We're going to bring you to South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And later, mind over matter, how to find happiness in an increasingly complicated world. We've got it all for you tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. A tropical depression formed in the Atlantic late this afternoon, its impacts are now taking aim at the Carolinas. Fox Weather Storm Specialist Mike Seidel has the latest from Folly Beach, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Mike, the National Hurricane Center has issued Tropical Storm watches. Could this become a tropical storm?\par \par \uc2 MIKE SEIDEL, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: It could, Gillian. It will be a minor, a weak tropical storm, with the winds right now are 35 miles an hour, and if it becomes a tropical storm, it gets the third name on the list, and it's Chantal. We've used that name before, but it hasn't been retired, because Chantal hasn't been a big, bad hurricane.\par \par \uc2 So, here are the stats, Tropical Depression Number Three, it's just jogging north at two miles an hour. Right now, the center is 150 miles south, southeast of Charleston, South Carolina, and we're just on the beach just a few minutes about 15 miles away from Charleston, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, you see what we've got going the hurricane hunters went out there today and found that close circulation. Now, let's go to the radar satellite composite.\par \par \uc2 Just about all the rain is still well offshore. We've had a few bands of showers go in through the Carolinas and Georgia this afternoon, but it's going to be pretty much a dry evening for fireworks.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, the fireworks were canceled here yesterday because of the impending weather, and then tomorrow as the depression or storm at that point draws closer, we'll have increasing threats for rain and squally weather and heavy downpours.\par \par \uc2 But I think the beach weather tomorrow is going to be pretty good. Just heed the lifeguard warnings because the rip current risk is going to increase as well as the surf action. Right now, we've got three to four footers.\par \par \uc2 And the real impact here, Gillian, will be the rainfall. Some areas along the coast, especially north of where the center comes in, say from Kiowa up towards the Myrtle Beach area, Wilmington, Wrightsville, could see as much as 3, 4, maybe 5 inches of rain. And landfall right now, not etched in stone, but the center should come on shore sometime after midnight tomorrow night into early Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Once it goes inland, it just weakens and falls apart and just becomes kind of a wet mess. So everybody is waiting for it, but I can tell you, it can't get any better than this for the Fourth of July here at Folly Beach, South Carolina. Just mobbed, bands playing, no fireworks, but everybody is in a very festive mood despite the forecast tomorrow of rain and gusty winds.\par \par \uc2 Some of those wind gusts could top out at 35 to 40 miles an hour, Gillian. So the concern for power outages and structural damage with this system is very, very low. Gillian, Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You know -- to you as well, Mike. Good to see everybody there taking it a day at a time, enjoying it while they can.\par \par \uc2 There's also this. Seven people still missing after a warehouse full of fireworks exploded just northwest of Sacramento. The run-up to July 4th has seen multiple fireworks mishaps across the state of California.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent Jeff Paul has that update out of L.A.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): A massive explosion at a Northern California fireworks warehouse sends a fireball into the sky, and giant plumes of smoke that could be seen for miles.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Boom. The windows shaking, the house was shaking.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice-over): Days after the detonation, the cause remains under investigation, but new satellite imagery shows the massive blast zone from space. Authorities on Friday reported finding human remains and will work over the weekend to identify seven people who were previously reported unaccounted for. There's also the risk of hazardous chemicals and unexploded fireworks.\par \par \uc2 CURTIS LAWRENCE, ESPARTO FIRE PROTECTION DISTRICT CHIEF: Our objective is to make the area safe so that we can continue to bring more crews in.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice-over): Many Bay Area cities stored their Fourth of July celebrations at this very warehouse. Those shows are now postponed or canceled. \uc2 San Jose\uc2 's mayor says they're shifting to a drone show.\par \par \uc2 MATT MAHAN\uc2  (D), SAN JOSE, CA MAYOR: Due to the terrible tragedy in \uc2 Yolo County\uc2 , we had to cancel our fireworks show, but your city team pivoted quickly to find an alternative.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice-over): At least three other explosions rocked California this week. In the Los Angeles area, one man was killed after fireworks detonated in a garage. Another man was killed when fireworks stored in a home ignited, creating a fire so fierce, it took more than 100 firefighters to knock it down.\par \par \uc2 ADRIAN GONZALEZ, WITNESS: You're sleeping in your house, you know, waiting for Fourth of July, you know, to come and you just feel the boom. You come outside, your neighbor's house is on fire.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice-over): A similar incident unraveling in northern California, injuring two and causing a neighborhood evacuation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was just a lot of screaming, and so that's how we kind of knew that it was like pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on-camera): Now all fireworks are illegal here in the city of Los Angeles, but they continue to be sold out of homes and garages, and firefighters say it's those folks who often don't know how to handle or store them. Nationwide, last year more than 14,000 people were treated for fireworks-related injuries, and 11 were killed. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Wow, who knew? Not me.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, thanks so much for joining us. Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, expert advice on how to keep a positive mental attitude no matter the circumstances. Some advice we could all use.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in Atlanta. Thousands of runners there participating in the Peachtree Road Race. The 10-kilometer run said to be the largest 10K in the country. This was the 56th year for that event.\par \par \uc2 In Fox 29 in Philly, two people there have been shot and injured in what police are calling an unusual road raid shooting. It happened in a busy \uc2 Walmart\uc2  parking lot. Officials say the driver of a Jeep and a truck got into a fight regarding a parking dispute. That argument escalated. They both pulled out weapons and began shooting.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at New York. One of the big stories there tonight from Fox 5. Famed competitive eater Joey "Jaws" Chestnut reclaims his title at the \uc2 Nathan's Famous\uc2  Fourth of July Hot Dog Eating Contest. He skipped last year's battle for the coveted muster belt.\par \par \uc2 He did consume 70 and a half hot dogs and buns in 10 minutes. This falling short of his 2021 record of 76. This was Chestnut's 17th time competing in this event. He must be full.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from Special Report. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Now for a segment I'm very excited about, how would you lead your life and shape your decisions to make yourself more successful if you were a startup? That's the question behind "The Happiness Files." It's a selection of enlightening and instructive essays by \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Writer \uc2 Arthur Brooks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 My colleague Bret Baier spoke with him about how to find happiness and fulfillment even when you're going through the times of your life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We bring you a lot of troubling news on this program. Sometimes, we're covering the news. Well, tonight we talked to a man about how to stay happy during uncertain and tumultuous political times and all the times around the world.\par \par \uc2 Arthur Brooks\uc2  is a professor at \uc2 Harvard Kennedy School\uc2  and the \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2 . He teaches courses on leadership and happiness. He's also a number one New York Times bestselling author of 13 books. His new book, "The Happiness Files," comes out in August.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ARTHUR BROOKS\uc2 , HARVARD UNIVERSITY PROFESSOR: Good to see you too, Bret. How are you?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good. I'm happy, but there's a lot of tumultuous stuff happening around the world.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: There is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, for people who get inundated by this, and we all do every which way --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- what are some things to think about?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Well, you know, the truth is that the world is complicated. It's always been complicated. And you see it really up close, given the fact that you're bringing these news to people all over the world. But I know personally what makes you happy. And it's the same thing that makes everybody else happy as well.\par \par \uc2 It's your faith, your family life, your friendships, and the work that serves other people. That's the big four. That's what it comes down to.\par \par \uc2 Look, if we're trying to hook our happiness up to what's going on around the world, I don't care what the news are. It's going to be a big problem. You're leaving it in somebody else's hands.\par \par \uc2 Be paying attention to your relationship with the divine, your family life, your close friendships, and the stuff that's close to you, including the work that you're trying to do to lift other people up. That's the secret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You write in The Atlantic, "Dare to act differently and be happier. Every day, you should track a few metrics by rating variables such as positive and negative mood levels, overall life satisfaction, and your sense of connectedness with others."\par \par \uc2 You know, you're talking about kind of data --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- on happiness here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You got to pay attention to it. One of the things that people get wrong is they think that happiness is just a feeling that comes and goes. It's not true. Happiness and feelings are related, but kind of like the smell of your turkey is related to your Thanksgiving dinner, but turkey and the smell are not the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Your feelings are not your happiness. Your happiness is something more tangible. It's about your enjoyment of life, your satisfaction with your accomplishments, and the sense of meaning that you get in your life. That means you need to think about it, learn something about it, get better at it.\par \par \uc2 That's why I teach classes in the science of happiness at a big business school is because they're very metrics driven people and they can get literally more skillful in their happiness. And so can all the people watching us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So when you tell those students, you know, when you wake up in the morning, what's something you should do?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Well, I tell them to begin with, that they need to actually start their day in a healthy way. They need to start their day in a way that actually is going to put them in a mind frame of positivity. Get up, exercise, pray, do the things that you actually need to do as opposed to ruminating on the -- all the things that are going wrong in your life.\par \par \uc2 Start making a gratitude list. The things that you're thinking about that actually you are grateful for. And we all have things that we could be grateful for as well. Second, is you should consume social media and media in general, very mindfully.\par \par \uc2 There are people who are looking at bad news all day long. It's important that we know what's going on in the world, but you don't want that to be like kind of the theme song of what's going on in your life. You need to use it sparingly, by the way, like you and I do as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, you know, that thing, social media and doom scrolling on your \uc2 Instagram\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- I mean, that seems to suck happiness --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- times.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: That's highly addictive. And actually that's the way that the products have been designed. We've been productized to scroll and scroll and scroll and to use these devices. They -- look, they take us away from our real family relationships.\par \par \uc2 We're ignoring our families while we're doing that. And we're getting bad news. That's actually being delivered to us. We're glued to it. And that's the way it's supposed to work. We need to take back the meaning of our lives by actually putting down the phones and only picking them up during certain times.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So this book, "The Happiness Files," --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- it's kind of a compilation --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- of --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: 35 lessons on being happier at home and being happier at work. Is the 35 best lessons that I've learned on how each of us can actually take control of the happiness in our lives and bring it to the workplace and actually experience more of it, find the jobs that we really want, and then be happier at home, building the families that we want as well. These are my 35 biggest lessons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we have a lot of viewers. I think a lot of them are happy.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hopefully they're happier.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, we hope you're happy as well.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the all-star panel on President Trump's big, beautiful week and what is ahead. And then the top sights and sounds of the week with notable quotables. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think had probably the most successful almost six months as a president and the presidency. I think they're saying it was the best six months. And I know for a fact they're saying the last two weeks, there has never been anything like it as far as winning, winning, winning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Let's bring in our panel now.\par \par \uc2 Real Clear Politics White House Correspondent Phil Wegmann, Matt Whitlock, former National Republican Senatorial Committee Senior Adviser and Democratic Strategist, Meghan Hays, an A plus panel for us on this July 4th. Thank you guys.\par \par \uc2 Phil, what did you make of the afternoon's events at the White House?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: Well, clearly the president wants to celebrate. And I think that if you rewind and look at where he was at in his first term in 2017, you had Obamacare repeal, dying in Congress, North Korea was launching intercontinental missiles, sorry, fell all over that. And then, you know, you had the Mueller special counsel.\par \par \uc2 It's night and day now versus then. And we're not six months in, but he's been able to get the majority of his domestic policy agenda to the finish line. So love him or hate him, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is at the zenith of his political power and he's feeling himself.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It also, Meghan, might be love him or hate him, the best night of his entire presidency tonight with the signing of this bill, the fireworks on the South Lawn and welcoming military families after that successful bomber buster.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yes, absolutely. When I was in the White House, these events are extremely special. Having the military there, they're extremely fun. But I will say where it's a good night for him, it's not a good night for Republicans heading into '26.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans had to take an incredibly hard vote, this politically hard vote this week on the Big, Beautiful Bill, which will come to haunt them later on. So where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is celebrating, I'm not so sure the entire Republican Party is.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: There is peril, right, into -- in -- there's a little bit of everything in this bill, but there is political peril for Republicans coming up. They own every single facet of this now.\par \par \uc2 MATT WHITLOCK, FORMER NRSC SENIOR ADVISER: I think that they feel that they have the wind at their sails. I think there's a lot of positive things in there. I think there's really something for everyone. But President Trump tonight is trying to set the tone for them going on offense on this and making the case that, again, there is something positive in this for everyone.\par \par \uc2 If you are a family, you not only get a tax cut, but you're also getting an enhanced child tax credit. If you're a lowly worker, you have no longer taxes on your tips. There's so much there, immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 It is the culmination of his agenda now codified by Congress, but the work is ahead. They need to go on the road and sell this to the American people. And I think they feel like they've got the way to do that. And so I also think they feel very positive about the ability to make a case for this bill.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What do you think about the impact of the Democrats resistance to this bill? I mean, not just, you know, sort of rallying against it in a draft form, but the sort of knockdown, drag out stuff that ensued in the 11th hour, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  on the floor for eight hours. Did any of that add up to anything?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: Well, I mean, look, Republicans own this. They're the ones who passed it. The resistance was coming from other Republicans. Democrats left them to their fate. So it is up to Speaker Mike Johnson and Majority Leader Thune to go sell this to the American people.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot there, but I think that the defining element of getting this bill to the finish line was both of these guys realized that done is beautiful. It wasn't going to be perfect. They didn't want to see the 2017 tax cuts expire. And Trump right now is well please.\par \par \uc2 You saw him herald both of these guys over at the White House as if they were sort of the MAGA point guards. Now, we'll see, you know, what voters think. And again, Republicans don't tend to do so well when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's not on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Also big news developing, Meghan, now with Russia's war in Ukraine overnight, one of the largest drone barrages since the beginning of this war, more than 500 -- 550 drones were part of this overnight. It was just a few hours after the president spoke to Putin, a very clear signal of where the Russian president thinks President Trump's head is at.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Yes, I think this is one of the things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not too happy about. I mean, he campaigned on ending this war in 24 hours. I think that's one of the things where you realize that campaigning is much more difficult than governing. I think with the reporting that they're withholding weapons from Ukraine is really detrimental.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is getting played by Putin here, and we're going to have to see how it plays out. But we -- the United States and \uc2 NATO\uc2  has no choice but to support Ukraine in this mission and to get to a ceasefire. And Ukraine has already agreed to it. Putin is the one who's not.\par \par \uc2 He's invaded a sovereign nation. He could end this at any time, and he's choosing not to. And I think he's doing that because he knows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really doesn't have anything to stand on to get him to stop.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It's a good point that Meghan makes, that this is a president whose campaign platform centered on no foreign wars. We're now embroiled in three, six months into the term.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: President Trump's North Star on foreign policy is what's the fastest route to peace? That's why he took the actions he took with Iran and Israel. And I think that's what's beginning to make Putin really wear on his patience.\par \par \uc2 If you read reports today that said President Trump spoke to Zelenskyy about how we can support them further and take things another step, because, as Meghan said, Putin's giving him a lot of reason to turn harder and harder against Russia. And I think that that patience is wearing thin what he said at \uc2 NATO\uc2  about needing to do more to help Ukraine, to arm them, to support them. I think he's seeing the fastest path to peace is going to be continuing to support Ukraine against Russia.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Here's the president a moment ago on the South Lawn talking about the mission that he carried out two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Those beautiful planes that you saw were totally stealth. They flew right over the center of a very hostile country, and they never even saw it coming. And then they said skedaddle. They dropped the bombs. They said skedaddle. And that big sucker turned over like this on its side, and it got out of there.\par \par \uc2 Well, I'll tell you what. How'd they do that? But they can do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You know, this was an opportunity, Phil, for the president to tout what is one of the most successful military operations we are led to believe of the last decade on par with taking out, you know, \uc2 Saddam Hussein\uc2 . What do you think it was like for the military families who are welcomed on the South Lawn today?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: I think that this was an opportunity for their service to be recognized. And when the commander-in-chief of the entire country says that the nation is grateful for you, that's special. What I think the country learned about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during those strikes very specifically is there are a lot of people who read into his remarks their beliefs.\par \par \uc2 There were a lot of anti-interventionists who said, no, of course we can't get involved in Israel's war. There were others who were saying Iran is an enemy of the United States. It has been for some time. What we learned from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that he's going to be making the final decision here.\par \par \uc2 And yes, he has some North Stars, but it's a game-time decision. And in this instance, he sees this as threading the needle. He was able to do a strike and then step away and get a peace deal to the 12-day war.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, time for winners and losers. Matt, you're up first.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: My winner is Speaker Mike Johnson, who, 10 years ago, was a lowly legislator in Louisiana and through a crazy power vacuum stepped up and just shepherded one of the most consequential bills in decades through the narrowest majority in history. So huge, huge kudos to Speaker Mike Johnson as winner of the week.\par \par \uc2 Should I do loser now or come back?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes, losers.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: My loser of the week is the people of Amity Island, a fictional town who 50 years ago were terrorized by a 27-foot great white shark in "Jaws". Congratulations to Steven Spielberg on 50 years of our favorite --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That's a creative one, I like it.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: -- Fourth of July classic.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Meghan, you're up.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Winners is the professional sports -- Women's Professional Sports. The WNBA expanded -- announced an expansion into three cities, three different markets today, or this week, I'm sorry. So that's incredible for women's sports and the WNBA.\par \par \uc2 And my losers are all the puppy parents out there tonight with their little puppies and the fireworks and all the trauma that that's going to cause them.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes, it is terrible.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: America is the winner each week, every week, because obviously we're the greatest nation ever. In particular --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I like it.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: -- it is the pilots of those B2s and also the crews who kept them in there and then their families who have made sacrifices. They earned some well-deserved praise.\par \par \uc2 My loser of the week is Great Britain. 247 years -- 249 years ago, they blew a 13 lead.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: There you go. I like it.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: And they've worked today.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Those are good ones. Thank you, guys. Happy Fourth to you. Thanks for spending it with us.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 It is Friday and it is time for Notable Quotables.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Did they take the vote yet?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm telling the president that you have been misinformed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'd rather be in a North Korean prison than on the floor of the House right now.\par \par \uc2 HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2 , MINORITY LEADER, U.S. HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES: And Mr. Speaker, we want no part of it. We're going to press on --\par \par \uc2 ALL: Press on.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: -- until victory is won. I yield back.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (D), HOUSE SPEAKER: You know, it takes a lot longer to build a lie than to tell the simple truth.\par \par \uc2 LINDA MCMAHON, U.S. EDUCATION SECRETARY: You know, the university is apologizing. It is taking away the titles that were incorrectly given to men.\par \par \uc2 RILEY GAINES, OUTKICK CONTRIBUTOR: I feel vindicated. I feel like pigs are flying. I feel like hell has frozen over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's hard to root for the status quo. You know, when you come in and there's something bright and new and shiny.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you think that billionaires have a right to exist?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think that we should have billionaires.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Last thing we need is a communist. Frankly, I've heard he's a total nut job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you pleading guilty because you are guilty?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He then killed intentionally, willfully, deliberately with premeditation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He negotiated with the devil, so he has to sleep with the devil.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a great victory for Sean Combs. It's a great victory for the jury system.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Biden wanted me in here, OK? He wanted me -- it didn't work out that way.\par \par \uc2 There could be no better birthday present for America than the phenomenal victory we achieved just hours ago when Congress passed the One Big Beautiful Bill to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That made Monday feel awfully far away.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next Monday on Special Report, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is coming here to Washington. He's going to meet with President Trump at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Make sure this weekend you join Shannon Bream for Fox News Sunday. She's got Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and House Speaker Mike Johnson, as well as South Carolina Republican Senator Tim Scott and Democratic California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Please stick with Fox News coming up for our big Independence Day special. It starts right here at 8:00 p.m. Eastern, and then a special Fourth of July edition of The Five is going to start tonight at 11:00 p.m. Remember, if you can't catch us live, just set your DVR 6:00 p.m. Eastern, 3:00 p.m. out on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. We wish you a happy, safe, and healthy Independence Day. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Laura Ingraham is next.\par \par \uc2