may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KENNEDY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And we say that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So, you're telling me I can swim right after I eat?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yes, I am.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Bret, as a parent, you know not to push that fake news.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know. Alright, thank you. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you again tonight from Fox News World Headquarters in New York. A lot of news to get to, a top house Republican threatens to subpoena a former Biden aide who is now refusing to testify about the alleged cover-up of then President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Mental health.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's pick to run government news services such as Voice of America wants to tear everything down and start over. We'll bring you her testimony.\par \par \uc2 And a congresswoman who participated in that Brawl outside an immigration detention center appears in court on an assault charge.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, President Trump is expected to return from his trip to the NATO Summit within the hour, as you look live at the White House, president comes home after securing a much deeper financial commitment from alliance members. He's also suggesting the U.S. will be talking soon with Iran, and is again insisting the weekend attack on Iran's nuclear weapons sites has set that program back years. His top intelligence official says new intelligence confirms that the facilities were in fact, destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy is at The Hague again tonight with the latest. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. This just in from President Trump. He says, at 8:00 a.m. tomorrow, there will be a press conference with the defense secretary at the Pentagon. The purpose is to fight for the dignity of our great American pilots. We don't know exactly what that is about. Stay tuned 14 hours from now.\par \par \uc2 We are also hearing that there is a possibility of the U.S. pushing for a comprehensive peace plan with Iran that is coming from the special envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  earlier today. President Trump himself told us that these talks could happen as soon as next week. He believes the Iranians were brought to the table by Saturday's 30,000 pound bombs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You know, the fire and brimstone is all underground.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A source familiar tells Fox the Defense Intelligence Agency's preliminary report assessing that Iran's nuclear program was only set back a few months was based on one day's worth of intelligence reporting. Now, DNI Gabbard says new intelligence confirms what POTUS has stated numerous times, Iran's nuclear facilities have been destroyed. If the Iranians choose to rebuild, they would have to rebuild all three facilities, Natanz, Fordow, Esfahan entirely, which would likely take years to do.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: If you want to make an assessment of what happened at Fordow, you better get a big shovel and go really deep, because Iran's nuclear program is obliterated.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The White House is now limiting classified information sharing with lawmakers on Capitol Hill as an \uc2 FBI\uc2  leak investigation continues.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What consequence could the leaker face?\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: They should go to jail.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): At the NATO Summit in the Netherlands, President Trump worked to convince most partners to increase defense spending, but not Spain.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, we're going to do, we're negotiating with Spain on a trade deal. We're going to make them pay twice as much. And I'm actually serious about that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): U.S. ambassador to \uc2 NATO\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , told us what happens next.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW WHITAKER\uc2 , U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 NATO\uc2 : Spain signed up to the same pledge that everyone signed up for, and there is no Spanish out. We will have other ways for you to pay the same that everyone else is.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Leaders here seemed sympathetic to President Trump's south lawn outburst about Iran and Israel.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They don't know what the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) they're doing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: With \uc2 NATO\uc2 's Secretary General calling the president a word seldom used in diplomacy, daddy.\par \par \uc2 MARK RUTTE\uc2 , NATO SECRETARY GENERAL: Daddy has to sometimes use strong language.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, use strong language, everyone (INAUDIBLE) use a certain word.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president didn't mind.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He did it very affectionate. Hey, daddy. You're my daddy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): White House officials leave here feeling pretty good. The cease fire President Trump helped negotiate in Iran is holding. Now, he's working on ending the war in Ukraine, and he talked \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries into spending more on defense. You could call all that the daddy doctrine, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm pretty sure I've been to a lot of \uc2 NATO\uc2  summits. I've not used or heard used, daddy, that's good stuff. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The controversy over the effectiveness of U.S. strikes on Iran is also playing out in Israel tonight, the official verdict there backs up President Trump. Meanwhile, the cease fire between Israel and Iran appears intact, at least tonight. Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot is in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tel Aviv back in action as the first full day of the cease fire between Israel and Iran saw the pause holding, restrictions on work and play were lifted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Today, I feel freedom.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Talk continues here to the damage done by the U.S. strikes against Iranian nuclear facilities. Israel's Atomic Commission, calling the hits devastating, rendering the Fordow complex inoperable.\par \par \uc2 GEN. EFFIE DEFRIN, \uc2 IDF\uc2  (through translator): The assessment is that we have dealt a significant blow to the nuclear program, and I can say we have set it back for years.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): As Iran reckons with a devastating Israeli led 12 day war, there might be orchestrated cheers for the regime on the street, but 700 people were reportedly rounded up, allegedly for collaborating with Israel, three executed.\par \par \uc2 And Iran's Parliament votes to suspend cooperation with the \uc2 U.N.\uc2 's nuclear watchdog agency, the \uc2 IAEA\uc2 , a further government OK is needed as the other war in Gaza, pitting Israel against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , still rages, seven Israeli soldiers were killed when an explosive device was thrown into their vehicle. They died in the blaze that followed.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): Our hearts are with the families. We embrace them in our hearts during this unbearably difficult time.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): And in the last 24 hours, dozens of Palestinians in Gaza were killed, according to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  led health authorities, including those near aid distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): There is no safety anywhere.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Not to mention nearly 50 Israeli hostages alive and dead still held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . People here praying they won't be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): Israeli President Herzog in a visit to a cyber-defense center here came up with his own strategic vision for Israel coming out of all this war and peace. In his words, it is to live freely and securely in the Middle East without existential threats. We can only hope, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot live in Tel Aviv. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, back here at home, the Justice Department is making some adjustments to its strategy for keeping Americans safe. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  moving additional assets to areas which could be vulnerable to Iranian attacks. Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson shows us from Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): After the United States and Iran traded direct attacks over the past week in the Middle East, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is reinforcing its counterterrorism efforts here at home.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Iran, of course, is a threat. They have been a threat, and they always will be a threat to our country.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Attorney General Pam Bondi told the Senate committee today that federal law enforcement is watching for Iran inspired attacks.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: We are working hand in hand with all of our agencies to protect Americans and to keep us safe.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Officials say the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is shifting resources back to counter terrorism and cyber security. Bondi testified there are more than 1500 illegal aliens in the U.S. from Iran. Democrats question whether the Justice Department has reassigned too many resources to immigration.\par \par \uc2 SE. GARY PETERS (D-MI): How are you ensuring that there are enough resources focused on the full range of national security?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Will national security be compromised? Not at all. Senator, it will be increased. It will be enhanced.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  says it's arrested more than 15 Iranian nationals recently. DHS says many have been convicted on charges involving drugs, guns, assault and more, though no word of a specific terror plot.\par \par \uc2 ICE says it apprehended one Iranian citizen in Texas yesterday, previously convicted of multiple felonies, including distribution of heroin and intimidation or force against a witness.\par \par \uc2 After the U.S. attack on Iranian nuclear sites, DHS issued a bulletin warning that pro-Iranian or Iran government hackers are likely to initiate cyber-attacks on American networks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): All this is law enforcement agencies prepare for Independence Day events across the country, which launched the yearlong celebration of the country's 250th birthday. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the chairman of the House Oversight Committee says he will issue a subpoena to a former Biden administration official who is backing out of a commitment to testify about the then president's mental health. Chairman James Comer saying, former adviser to both President Biden and the First Lady, Jill, Biden, Anthony Bernal, is now refusing to appear for a transcribed interview.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel has specifics on that tonight. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Former Biden aides were hoping they would be covered by executive privilege and not be forced to testify, but President Trump would not assert immunity. Anthony Bernal who served as former assistant to President Biden and senior adviser to the First Lady, is refusing to appear before the House Oversight Committee tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Bernal was known as Jill Biden's so called Work husband. He was known to have much more access than other Biden aides. It was called the leader of the loyalty police.\par \par \uc2 House Oversight Chairman James Comer says he will issue a subpoena, compelling Bernal's testimony. "With no privilege left to hide behind Mr. Bernal is now running scared, desperate to bury the truth. The American people deserve answers and accountability, and the Oversight Committee will not tolerate this obstruction." Comer spoke to reporters about this investigation after another Biden insider was interviewed yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): We put a lot of staff time into this. I think the American people want to know. I think there's a huge level of curiosity in the press corps with respect to who was actually calling the shots in the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The first witness to appear was \uc2 Neera Tanden\uc2  she served as staff secretary and told the committee she was authorized to direct auto pen signatures and denies there was an effort to hide President Biden's condition. A recent Fox poll found 68 percent of voters believe the previous administration was dishonest about Mr. Biden's condition. Comer says Bernal's legal team originally said he would appear for a transcribed interview. Now it appears he will be forced to do it, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 107, the S&P 500 was flat. The NASDAQ gained 61.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Brit Hume on the controversy over the damage assessment following last weekend's us strikes on Iran's nuclear weapons facilities.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 40 in Sacramento as the Trump administration finds the California Education Department and the state's high school sports federation violated civil rights law by allowing transgender girls to compete on girls sports teams. The federal education department is proposing a resolution that would require California to bar transgenders from women's sports. It would also strip transgender athletes of records, titles and awards.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Port Canaveral, Florida from Fox 35, one of the big stories there tonight. \uc2 NASA\uc2 , \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 Axiom Space\uc2  launch a rocket early this morning, following three prior scrubbed attempts, the Axiom Mission 4 is taking a crew to the International Space Station. The SpaceX Dragon spacecraft is expected to dock in about 11 hours.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The site was obliterated, just like I said it was. And just like the pilots should be given credit for.\par \par \uc2 They came home to fake news and like, oh, gee, there was hardly any damage. Things are decimated.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, UNITED STATES DEFENSE SECRETARY: They want to spin it to try to make him look bad, based on a leak.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Was this bombing of these nuclear facilities, was it successful? We have a lot of serious questions that demand serious answers.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZACH NUNN (R-IA): Democrats here are grasping its rise. These are the same guys who, just before the weekend, said Iran probably doesn't even have a nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it was the headlines that came out over the weekend, in part from CNN and \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . "Early U.S. Intelligence assessment suggests strikes on Iran did not destroy nuclear sites, sources say."\par \par \uc2 "Strike sets back Iran's nuclear program by only a few months, U.S. report says."\par \par \uc2 It was based on a DIA early assessment with low confidence. We had it. We reported on it. Jennifer Griffin put out stuff on it, and we've covered it here on the show. The CIA Director John Ratcliffe, putting out a quote, "CIA can confirm that a body of credible intelligence indicates Iran's nuclear program has been severely damaged by the recent targeted strikes. This includes new intelligence from a historically reliable and accurate source method that several key Iranian facilities were destroyed and would have to be rebuilt over the course of years." That from the CIA director tonight.\par \par \uc2 With that, and all of this, let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 Brit, good evening. You know, there is a lot of this back and forth, but there are kind of broader issues here to look at about the strike and the fallout from it.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Bret, the best thing you can say about that preliminary report that leaked out yesterday was that it was -- it was very, very tentative, and very, very early, and it was marked as low confidence. And, you know, there is only so much you can know about underground damage from massive bombing in the 48 or 72 hours after it happens, because in order to really know, you'd have to be in there. And nobody within the reach of the \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  that I've ever heard of would be in a position to assess that.\par \par \uc2 But we have reporters working on this story, one in particular who works for CNN, who is a person who fell for the Steele dossier, hook, line, and sinker, who thought that, who wrote that the Hunter Biden laptop -- bought into the story, that, that was Russian disinformation. And so, someone I don't -- I'm not -- she wasn't working for CNN at the time. It's not clear to me how she ever got a job there, but. CNN took her anyway, and now, she is doing this kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think that the subsequent intelligence reports we've heard about are more reliable, but you know, this all has to await a longer-term situation, in which somebody is going to have to get in there and really find out. But the Iranians themselves were saying today that their nuclear program was badly damaged. So, maybe we ought to rest with that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, the Iranians -- actually, the foreign minister put out a statement. The Israelis had a statement that matched what the U.S. officials are talking about.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 David Albright\uc2 . He's a former IAE investigator. He was on today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID ALBRIGHT\uc2 , FOUNDER AND PRESIDENT, \uc2 INSTITUTE FOR SCIENCE AND INTERNATIONAL SECURITY\uc2 : We've been looking at satellite imagery during the last two weeks, and it's a pretty devastating. I mean, a lot of their above ground facilities that are critical part of a centrifuge program have been -- have been destroyed. So, I think these reports that somehow there weren't centrifuges taken out are just incorrect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But more broadly, Brit, the fact that the U.S. flew this package of B-2, bombers dropped 420,000 pounds of ordinance on nuclear sites inside Iran. Not a single shot was fired, not a single Iranian jet what got off the ground, and we got out of there, and got back, suggests that we could do it again. And that was the message, not only for Iran, for China, for Russia, for the world.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, it was a reminder of anywhere needed that the United States military is the most proficient and powerful and technically equipped military on Earth. You know, there is a sort of an attitude, Bret of view that took hold in recent years to the effect that, you know, the United States really hasn't won a war since World War II.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's nonsense. People cite, you know, the outcome in Iraq, and they cite the, you know, the first Gulf War and the fact that Saddam stayed in power. The fact is, our military did not lose those wars. In fact, the first Gulf War was a walk over, and the second Gulf War, where we took down \uc2 Saddam Hussein\uc2 , was pretty much a walk over too.\par \par \uc2 Now, you can make an argument that, yes, we won the war, we lost the peace, and maybe we did. But none of that is to take away from the extraordinary proficiency of our military forces and of the equipment that they use. The B-2 bomber being the most recent and most conspicuous example.\par \par \uc2 The United States military is very good indeed, and one can only hope that the tyrants around the world who would -- who would dare want to challenge it, would think better of it after what we've just seen over this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Brit, as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Democrats in -- here in New York City make a controversial choice for their nomination in the mayor's race. We'll have a live report. First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, a Japanese court finds a U.S. Marine guilty of sexually assaulting a woman on Okinawa.\par \par \uc2 Lance Corporal Jamel Clayton has been sentenced to seven years in prison. That case has triggered anger and safety concerns on Japan's southern island, which has a heavy American troop presence.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight -- nearby, actually, multi-billionaire \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  and Lauren Sanchez arrive in Venice ahead of their star-studded weekend wedding. The event has drawn protests by groups who view it as a sign of the growing disparity between rich and poor. Weddings going forward with their guests in Venice.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Virginia's Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  says the Housing and Urban Development Department will relocate to a new spot in Alexandria, Virginia. Commonwealth, says the move will save taxpayers several hundred-million- dollars. When we recently took SPECIAL REPORT to the current HUD headquarters in D.C., we learned of plans to move as soon as they can.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT TURNER, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT: HUD is known as the ugliest building in D.C., which is not a mantra I like. And so, we're working on this building. It's a stewardship issue, we're spending taxpayer dollars, and so, I think, we can be very efficient in a different place.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In a different place, moving everybody out.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And does that, that doesn't mean closing down HUD?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: No, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Commonwealth saying today the new building in Virginia will give the department's more than 2,700 headquarters employees, a modern mission focused work environment.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT" here in New York City, and while it's not official yet, Democrats have selected a Muslim socialist as their nominee for mayor.\par \par \uc2 Zohran Mamdani is claiming victory and a big upset over former Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy reports tonight from city hall on the man of the hour.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D), MAYORAL CANDIDATE: It's where the mayor will use their power to reject \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's fascism.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Self- described democratic socialist Zohran Mamdani is claiming victory in New York City's democratic mayoral primary. The 33-year-old state assemblyman plans big changes in the big apple.\par \par \uc2 MAMDANI: Where rent stabilized apartments are actually stabilized. Where buses are fast and free.\par \par \uc2 To stop mass ice agents from deporting our neighbors.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Mamdani promises city owned grocery stores, free childcare, and free buses paid by tax hikes on wealthy New Yorkers and businesses.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: He's a snake oil salesman. He will say and do anything to get elected. He wants to raise tax on the one percent of New Yorkers high income earners. As the mayor, you don't have the authority to do that.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Incumbent New York City Mayor Democrat Eric Edams will run as an independent in November's general election, possibly joined by former Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , who conceded to Mamdani last night but is now mulling an independent run as well.\par \par \uc2 Mamdani faces accusations of antisemitism, even from those within his own party. Congresswoman \uc2 Laura Gillen\uc2  from Long Island wrote on X, quote, "Socialist Zohran Mamdani is too extreme to lead New York City. Mr. Mamdani has called to defunded police and has demonstrated a deeply disturbing pattern of unacceptable antisemitic comments."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Tonight, President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is reacting to the Democratic primary results on Truth Social, calling Mamdani a, quote, "100 percent communist lunatic", while on X former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  has a different tone, congratulating Mamdani and wishing him well after endorsing former Governor Cuomo during the primary race. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Nate, thank you. More on this with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, congressional lawmakers in D.C. are considering what they heard earlier today from President Trump's second pick to lead the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . Correspondent Alexandria Hoff reports on the confirmation hearing for Susan Monarez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SUSAN MONAREZ, CDC DIRECTOR NOMINEE: I have not seen causal link between vaccines and autism. I think vaccines