dy as an option in certain cases. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Trump administration's effort to reform government communication agencies such as Voice of America, the person in charge wants to scrap it all and start over.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel on President Trump's big win at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit and what may come next with Israel and Iran.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Trump administration's head of global media wants to make big changes concerning what she calls deep dysfunction at government news agencies such as Voice of America and \uc2 Radio Free Europe\uc2 . Kari Lake's congressional testimony comes just days after the layoffs of hundreds of workers. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram shows us tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: VOA perhaps DOA.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, (R) U.S. GLOBAL MEDIA AGENCY SENIOR ADVISOR: It's really like a rotten piece of fish. It's best to just scrap the whole thing and start over.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Former political candidate and TV anchor Kari Lake says dead air may be better than VOA's current broadcast.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: We need to modernize. I don't think doing 1990s television is effective anymore.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Lake contends Chinese spies infiltrated the VOA's Mandarin service, and Russian spooks nearly worked at \uc2 Radio Free Europe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ANDY BARR\uc2 , (R-KY) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: This is not the voice of America. This is the voice of America's enemies.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats say curbing VOA silences America from transmitting news into enemy territory.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRAD SHERMAN\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: Our soft power is of critical importance. And I think Putin would be very happy.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But it's not just Democrats. California Republican \uc2 Young Kim\uc2  says dismantling \uc2 Radio Free Asia\uc2  cedes power to China and North Korea.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 YOUNG KIM\uc2 , (R-CA) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: So I worry about the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's ability to win in the information domain.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Lake canned 639 employees Friday and says she is just carrying out orders from the top.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: What we reviewed was an executive order, March 14th, by the man who is in charge of the executive branch --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- I want to know, I want to know --\par \par \uc2 LAKE: His name is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats say that's the problem.\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN, (D-PA) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: You are a propaganda machine for the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Congress commissioned VOA to beam news behind the Iron Curtain.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The big four have agreed to end the Berlin blockade and counterblockade.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But Republicans question its necessity. People now get their news on a phone.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT, (R-TN) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: Dadgum, it's not very efficient.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: It's a relic. It's a relic.\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: Yes, ma'am, it is. It's a cold war relic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: VOA fired dozens staff in its Persian division but restored some programs as tensions rose between Iran and Israel. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And, Chad, where are we with the big, beautiful bill? It seems like it's going to be a big, beautiful weekend.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's right. The Senate plans to start debate Friday, hopes to pass it this weekend, but House conservatives believe the bill got worse. It could be hard for the House to sync up. Also, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  went to the hospital today. He felt lightheaded, he was dehydrated. It reached 95 degrees in D.C. today. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A New Jersey Democratic congresswoman is facing federal charges tonight. LaMonica McIver is charged with assaulting federal officers during that protest last month outside of an immigration detention center. Correspondent Alexis McAdams has details tonight from Newark. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Yes, we watched McIver come out here today in Newark, New Jersey, after she pled not guilty in that federal court right behind me. She is vowing to not only get these charges dropped against her, she says, but also fighting back against the Trump administration. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LAMONICA MCIVER, (D-NJ): At the end of the day this is all about political intimidation. The Trump administration and his -- him and his colleagues or cronies, whatever you want to call them, have weaponized the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Today protesters demonstrating in support of McIver, calling for ICE to be banned from the state of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And it was that incident that led to these federal charges in the first place against the Democratic representative. You can see it on your screen. She is now indicted on three federal counts of assaulting, impeding, and interfering with federal officials.\par \par \uc2 In this body camera video, McIver appears to put her hands on federal agents as they say she forcibly grabbed one agent and then hit another. The incident happened back in May when McIver and several other Democrats showed up to an ICE detention center demanding a visit. Who else was there? That would be Newark Mayor Ras Baraka. The feds say he wouldn't leave, he wouldn't leave the property, so they stepped in to arrest him, and then McIver tried to stop them. He was charged with trespassing. Those charges were eventually dropped. Baraka, who is running for governor of New Jersey, says this was not about violating the law.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAS BARAKA, (D) NEWARK, NEW JERSEY, MAYOR: This is sheer intimidation sheer bullying. This is their opportunity to put their foot on our necks, to let people know that they are in charge.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO:\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And if convicted McIver is looking at nearly a decade behind bars. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel joins us as President Trump returns from \uc2 NATO\uc2 , plus a possible subpoena for a former Biden aide in that cover-up probe.\par \par \uc2 And a self-described Democratic socialist as mayor of the country's largest city, could it happen? Yes, it looks like it.\par \par \uc2 Then reconstructing the past out of ashes of the World Trade Center here in New York. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Statements just came out from the Israeli atomic energy commission and from Iran, as you know, that it was complete, total destruction.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2 , (D-CO) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: If there is any type of bomb or any series of airstrikes that's going to destroy a facility that somebody is hellbent on protecting, it just doesn't stand to reason.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WITKOFF\uc2 , U.S. SOCIAL ENVOY TO \uc2 MIDDLE EAST\uc2 : The reporting out there that in some ways suggest that we did not achieve the objective is just completely preposterous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of back and forth about this intel. Meantime, the president is going back to the White House from \uc2 NATO\uc2 . The headlines today, "Trump pushes back after leaked reports suggest Iran strikes had limited impact." "\uc2 NATO\uc2  commits to spending hike sought by Trump and to mutual defense." "Trump says U.S. to meet Iran next week, Putin talks ahead." The defense secretary and chairman of the joint chief also brief tomorrow morning 8:00 a.m., further investigating what exactly happened at those nuclear sites.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Lawrence Jones, co-host of FOX & FRIENDS, Harris Faulkner, anchor of "THE FAULKNER FOCUS", co-host of "OUTNUMBERED", and Jessica Tarlov, co-host of "THE FIVE". I would like to be a co-host, too. Just to fit in the group.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, there you go.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harris, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: You know what? I think it's a big deal that the president wants to trim the access that Congress will have to classified information around his decisions, particularly when it comes to military action. I mean, they are looking at moving ahead with that idea. And as much as they were pushing, they are going to get that bipartisan look in the House and in the Senate by talking with Hegseth and the Chairman Dan Caine.\par \par \uc2 But if you start to do that, you start to build power that you said you were against as Democrats. It's really confounding that putting pressure at this point, and the leaks. I mean, Democrats ought to be right out there saying yes, we want to find the leaker too. This makes us look bad, makes us look like ununified union. We don't want to, you know, to appear that way against an enemy like Iran.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessica, Jennifer Griffin matched the reporting on that intel report when it came out. She had the additional reporting about low confidence and that it was early, it was only in the first 24 hours that that report came out. And now obviously the CIA director and others, Israelis, the Iranians even weighed in on some of this intel. Does it -- does it matter right now, as we keep going back and forth about what the damages inside that, is more the message that we dropped the bombs, got in, got out, could do it, the message to Iran and the world?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": I think that both messages matter a lot. And it in no way, no matter how, quote-unquote, successful it was, diminishes what our Air Force was able to pull off. And I know that that's something that we all need to continue to emphasize, that they pulled off something absolutely spectacular and they deserve all of our respect. They risked their lives for us.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the damage that was done, I do want to know how much it was, because there is a big difference between total obliteration and six or seven months. And this back and forth with what the American intel community has been saying has been going on for a long time since \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  testified in March and directly contradicted what President Trump said. You have Mark Warner and \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  who are the ranking Democrats on the intel committees who are saying the same thing as \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 . You have the Israelis out there that are saying something different. And they are closer and probably have levels of access that we do not have. But it feels like at this point that the American president trusts the Israelis more than he trusts his own people.\par \par \uc2 And to your point, Harris, about limiting access to classified information for Congress, that's all \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. He wants to go it alone. He wants to consolidate as much executive power. He already didn't brief Democrats about what he was going to do.\par \par \uc2 (CROSS TALK)\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: -- should be working as a team right now as Americans.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But he is the one that doesn't want to work as a team, and he didn't want to produce --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me weigh in. First of all, he did brief the Democrats. Schumer got a call beforehand. They couldn't get \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 . They didn't do the gang of eight, you're right. But they did, under the War Powers Act, they met their --\par \par \uc2 JONES: But also, why would you go to air with a report that's not even flushed out, that has low confidence. And you've got to look at this not just with this leak right here, but this is the same reporter that went to air with the Russia collusion hoax. That was not true. This is the same reporter that got the 51 intelligence officials to sign off on the Hunter Biden story, saying that the laptop was disinformation. That wasn't true as well.\par \par \uc2 And so I think what the president was saying, he got some initial reports as well. Wait until all the information comes out. We've got the CIA coming out saying essentially what the president --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I read it with Brit Hume earlier. John Ratcliffe putting out that statement.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Look, a senior White House official is saying now that there's a war on leakers. That's where the war is.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think the war in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn topics to the situation on Capitol Hill and this investigation into the state of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and how they are not getting a ton of cooperation of former officials, and now an aide to the former first lady and to president Biden saying he is not going to come in.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Look, this makes Democrats look one of two things, just completely impotent or ignorant of the facts at the time contemporaneously. You had a president who was really visibly, at least shaken out of consciousness, it looked like, several times late into his presidency. So the questions were at least the last two years about whether or not he was cognitively competent enough to do the job.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats weren't asking the questions, who would have gotten the answers? I'm surprised that they can't say, well, we tried to, but they can't. And because they can't they look complicit. And if you look complicit and you know you've already lost a race, what do you do? You get quiet. So getting them to talk is going to be really difficult, because if they know the truth, they won't say it. And because they maybe don't know the truth, what does that make them look like?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, obviously they are going down the road here, Jessica, of the autopen and how it was operated, and did the president authorize it? That sort of thing, \uc2 Neera Tanden\uc2  testified to that. Does this matter big picture as we head to another election next year?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't think it matters as much as Republicans hope that it does. And the same talking points of 2024 are not going to work for 2026 and 2028. Everyone needs to do a refresh. We had a big upset here in New York last night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I want to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We might have a Democratic Socialist for a mayor. That's going to get you more votes than whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knew what he was doing and if \uc2 Neera Tanden\uc2  was using the autopen.\par \par \uc2 It's also kind of rich coming from a Republican party that flouted subpoenas for January 6th investigations. Like Jim Jordan has no leg to stand on when he talks about Democrats not showing up for their day in proverbial court.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, but the party has no legs to stand on.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We already lost. What do you want from us?\par \par \uc2 JONES: But you lied to the American people. And now that the investigation is coming out with more and more information on how they concealed the mental health of the president, then they want to say let's move on, let's not go back in the past. But no one is giving a full confession.\par \par \uc2 Now, they do tell authors of books that are making millions of dollars off of that, but no one wants to put their names on it. We have four cabinet officials in that book that said the president wasn't alert. Meanwhile, none of those four cabinet members have come up in public to tell the American people about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So let me just bring all these stories together, the Middle East and the mayor's race here. This is a soundbite from Zohran Mamdani back in December.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would Zohran Mamdani welcome \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to the city.\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI, (D) NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: No. As mayor, New York City would arrest \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . This is a city that our values are in line with international law. It's time that our actions are also.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Even though the U.S. is not a signature of the \uc2 ICC\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MAMDANI: No. It's time that we actually step up and make clear what we are willing to do to showcase the leadership that is sorely missing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So it's not official yet because they had the rank choice thing, but it will be that he, that Mamdani will have won the Democratic primary. And is it possible that he could be mayor here in New York?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Look, it's possible that he sees hot voters are in terms of getting the wind behind him for this primary race. He was all over online. I can understand it. He is saying what the people want, particularly young voters. They have not lived long enough through politics to know tat he can't do most of what he says he can do, right? So they are in the game for that.\par \par \uc2 But do you know who he didn't win were the low income voters on Tuesday night. He didn't win them. He won those earning higher incomes, 13 percent of those, the same amount, 13 percent of the lower income went for Cuomo.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , independent line, told from Democratic folks here that \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  is not going to get in to the race and the independent line, but we'll see. He was kind of hedgy on that. What do you think, Lawrence?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, we had the mayor on this morning on FOX & FRIENDS. I wouldn't be surprised if you get a big endorsement for Mayor Adams. It's literally right now, do you want a progressive party that believes in not cooperating, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  cooperating with ICE, but arresting the prime minister of a sovereign nation. That's the type of Democratic Party that we're talking right now. If moderates want to readjust their party, this is the moment to decide, because Republicans are going to run on a mayor like this if he is elected.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's going to be tough. Everyone else has to consolidate. The business community is getting together, and they are going to back whoever has the best chance. But Mamdani, and he was not my choice, tapped into something important. He talked about affordability 20 times a day. And that's something that is affecting all New Yorkers, whether you earn in that --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes, but lower income people didn't bite on that --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: From zero to $50,000, he won $50,000 to 100,000 which New York standards, sadly.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Thank you, Bret, for hanging in our house.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, thank you\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't have the whole story. So it's that fascination with what happened between 1775 and 1790 when it became part of the landfill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So I'm a sucker for history, so this Revolutionary War era ship being reconstructed for a museum exhibition. The gunboat was discovered underneath the World Trade Center site in 2010. Researchers believed it was built to defend Philadelphia in 1775 but remain puzzled on its other expeditions and why it ended up discarded. "The New York Post" reporting the restoration is expected to finish by the end of the month. How cool is that?\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250626el6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Visits The Hague For NATO Summit; Both Iran And Israel Urged To Uphold Ceasefire; Former Aide Addresses On Alleged Cover-Up; Former New York City Governor Vying To Unseat Sitting Mayor; Both Sides Rest Cases; 15 Iranian Foreign Nationals Arrested; California Official Criticizes Los Angeles Gangs For Not Confronting ICE Agents; Heat Wave Hits Parts Of U.S.; Democrats Criticize Trump Administration For Canceling Congressional Briefings On Iran Strike; Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  Calls For Impeachment Of President Trump Following Iran Strike Being Carried Out Without Congressional Approval\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Nate Foy, Peter Doocy, Trey Yingst, C.B. Cotton,  William La Jeunesse, Aishah Hasnie, Haley Meier, Martha McCallum, Brian  Kilmeade, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 7,822 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the