f warm weather today and into tonight into the overnight, and then eventually for tomorrow. Temperatures tomorrow will get a little bit better. Still going to be hot, but not quite reaching that 100-degree mark.\par \par \uc2 As far as the heat index across the I-95 Baltimore, 102 Raleigh, 107 degrees, and so that humidity in the air holds onto the heat content a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 Now, these records were not just 10 years old. They are century old records, generational type heat here. For Concord, New Hampshire, their high temperature of 100 degrees today beat a record all the way back to 1870, 1888 here for New York City. That's now the second day in a row that we have beat these records.\par \par \uc2 Now, I mentioned that relief. It is coming. What's going to happen is this high pressure slowly going to erode, pull a little bit further off towards the south and southeast and give us that much needed break. Look at Boston on Friday, 67 degrees, going to be feeling much better. And we also could see potentially a little bit of rain coming in, adding a nice little relief there too, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sounds lovely. Haley, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the Israel-Iran cease-fire and President Trump at the NATO summit.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around this country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami as Florida officials say they are building an off-the-grid facility to house Immigrations and Customs Enforcement detainees in the heart of the state's alligator alley. The 1,000-bed facility will hold criminal illegal immigrants in what is described as a virtually abandoned airport there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Oklahoma City from FOX 25. One of the big stories there tonight, the victory parade for the \uc2 NBA\uc2  champion Thunder. The team defeated the \uc2 Indiana Pacers\uc2  in game seven of the Finals Sunday night. It's the city's first major professional sports championship. Congratulations to the Thunder.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: I would like to see everything calm down as quickly as possible. Regime change takes chaos. And ideally, we don't want to see so much chaos.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARK KELLY\uc2 , (D-AZ) \uc2 SENATE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE\uc2 : We don't know with the uranium is. We don't know if we destroyed the sites. There was no plan here, there was no goals.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2 , (R-ND) \uc2 SENATE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE\uc2 : \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has always want the peace in the Middle East, not war. He has the ultimate leverage. So am I confident? I'm cautious. I'm cautiously optimistic.\par \par \uc2 BENNY GANTZ\uc2 , FORMER ISRAELI DEFENSE MINISTER: On cease-fire, especially when they start, they are kind of fragile.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO:\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The cease-fire is on between Israel and Iran. It is fragile. However, there is, as you look at the satellite images of these nuclear sites, assessment by some sources and defense intelligence that, perhaps, these sites did not get destroyed on those bombing runs, at least low confidence in one report. The defense secretary out denying that right away, saying, quote, "Based on everything we have seen, and I have seen it all, our bombing campaign obliterated Iran's ability to create nuclear weapons. The impact of those bombs is buried under a mountain rubble in Iran. So anyone who says the bombs were not devastating is just trying to undermine the president and the successful mission." Again, that's Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 . The president obviously over in \uc2 NATO\uc2  talking to world leaders.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Martha McCallum, anchor and executive editor of "THE STORY WITH MARTHA MACCALLUM," "FOX & FRIENDS" co-host Brian Kilmeade, and FOX News correspondent Bill Melugin. An august group here. Brian, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: What I think is the defense intelligence agency, one of 16 agencies, came out and said with low confidence that they don't think they got everything. They set them back a short time. I think the conventional wisdom is two to three years. But when we stopped the bombing campaign or the president stopped the bombing campaign. So I guess the \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , or agencies, the people on the ground had the air cover to do the things they were going to do, whether it was to threaten the commanders, threaten the Quds force, assassinate scientists. The minute the bombing stopped, the air cover is gone. So they won't necessarily have the freedom to go in there and see for themselves. So I don't think we're going to know for sure for a while.\par \par \uc2 But I do wonder about the timing. Why is it necessary, with the president meeting in \uc2 NATO\uc2 , to come out and put out a report that says low confidence that they really were able to be successful. I'm wondering if it's the same stain that was on his first term. That's why the president keeps such a small circle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They didn't put out a press release. It was sources and anonymous sources, and that's how some of this stuff in the intel world comes out. But there's a big pushback from the administration. The Israelis they think it was set back two, three, four years. But they are still trying to make an assessment.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I think we will see. And I think we heard from the Joint Chiefs chair the other morning at 8:00 with the defense secretary, he said it's too early to tell. The bomb damage assessment is underway. When I hear what the defense intelligence agency says, it reminds me of COVID. These intelligence agencies all had different takes on what the origins were in that case. There will be decisions that are made on this. I spoke with the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  director today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm going to play that soundbite real quick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL GROSSI\uc2 , DIRECTOR GENERAL, \uc2 INTERNATIONAL ATOMIC ENERGY AGENCY\uc2 : We do not have information of the whereabouts of this material. Iran officially told me we are going to be taking protective measures, which may or may not include moving around this material. So it is quite obvious, you are asking me about it. There is a question there, where is this. So the way to ascertain that is to allow the inspection activity to resume.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think the Iranians are going to say sure, come on in. We'll show you where we put the 900 pounds of 60 percent enriched uranium. That's not very confidence-inspiring. It is somewhere. It must be found. And it will be probably up to \uc2 Mossad\uc2  and Israeli forces to try to nail down where it is.\par \par \uc2 But one other factor here is that, A, what is absolutely essential is that President Trump went farther than any other American president in trying to end this threat. That's a fact, quantifiable fact. Destroy these facilities to a great extent, Grossi says that, absolutely. You also have a very different political situation on the ground in Iran right now, very weakened. Can they rebuild in a few months or a few years? That becomes a question of their own ability, their scientists, how much backup they have, how much defense they have around these areas. And that, I think, is really a huge part of this equation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, the markets seemed to digest this all, seemed to say oil, it's OK. They are not going to clog the Strait of Hormuz. They feel like it's heading in the right direction, perhaps, the market does.\par \par \uc2 The one thing is threat of terrorist action and amount of Iranians that are in this country. You put out those stats the other day, it was