rt of jarring realities of the chant and, yet, the friendliness has existed together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't know, the friendliness, "death to America" friendliness? I'm not sure. I'm not sure about that. I wanted to play that.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Democrats on Capitol Hill railing against President Trump. What comes next up there?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I authorized the armed forces of the United States to begin a limited military action in Libya in support of an international effort to protect Libyan civilians.\par \par \uc2 Today I authorized two operations in Iraq, targeted airstrikes to protect our American personnel and a humanitarian effort to help save thousands of Iraqi civilians.\par \par \uc2 Last night, on my orders, America's armed forces began strikes against \uc2 ISIL\uc2  targets in Syria.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's just three of many orders from President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Jonathan Turley writes this, "Dems suddenly outraged over presidential war powers Democrats were supportive when Clinton launched cruise missiles attacks under Operation Infinite Reach on two continents August 1998. Secretary of state \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  pushed for unilateral attacks during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. She dismissed the need to consult let alone secure authorization from Congress. \uc2 Obama\uc2  also defied the War Powers Resolution on Syria."\par \par \uc2 To be fair, Representative \uc2 Dennis Kucinich\uc2  of Ohio did say President \uc2 Obama\uc2  should be impeached for that action. And Congressman Tim Kaine said there should be War Powers action back then, and AUMF for \uc2 ISIS\uc2 , specifically. But there was not this pushback, Martha, that we are seeing today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It just feels very overtly political when you look at it in that light. And President \uc2 Obama\uc2  also said that it was obvious that Iran was building a nuclear plan that was not for energy, that was for weapons. And he was concerned about our personnel in the Middle East, the same concerns that he expressed right there. Basically, presidents have given up on declaring war since World War II. There have been 125 operations, none of them have sought to have approval from Congress since then. So it's a presidential power. I think it's like shoot first and ask questions later in most cases.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brian?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Libya, as you mentioned, Panama, Bush 41 didn't get permission. Grenada, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  didn't get permission. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , he had four days of strikes in Iraq. He didn't get permission. There was no movement to impeach. There was no movement to disparage. There was pretty much support. There was bipartisan support, especially in the Libya operation, which had no plan afterwards.\par \par \uc2 It's just amazing to me that Democrats haven't figured out to pick your spots. Just say not everything Trump does has to result in impeachment and excoriation. Sometimes you say listen, I back him on this. Got to find out more information. I want to get briefed a little bit later, and then we're going to find out details why he acted the way he did. And then maybe, like the biggest disappointment, as I mentioned, is someone like Senator Kelly. He's got this esteemed military career.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrat from Arizona.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Democrat from Arizona, and all he does, he is like a political pundit using his prestige just to score Democratic points, especially in a serious time like this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The president said about impeachment, make my day. And I think he sees the political benefit if they do go down that road. It's highly unlikely they will. Meantime, they're trying to get the big, beautiful bill across the finish line, likely this weekend, all the way through this weekend.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, they are. And for his biggest agenda item, mass deportations, it has a whole lot of money in there. We're talking tens of billions of dollars for over 10,000 new ICE agents. Just to put that in perspective, there's about 5,500 ICE agents nationwide right now. That would more than double their staffing. It would increase their bed detention capacity from about 50,000 up to 100,000. So you're doubling that.\par \par \uc2 And just circling back to what you guys were talking about with the whole impeachment thing, AOC calling for that. A Republican congressman texted me about that today and we were going back and forth, and he said, quote, "If Trump cured cancer, Democrats would say he is putting oncologists out of work." They just always have to find something to get angry at him about. Hence the reason why the Maryland man, they latched to him just to go against the deportation agenda. They're always finding something to lash out about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll cover it all. Panel, thanks. Great to be in New York.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Thanks for having me, appreciate it. Stay out of trouble, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's like you are \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Congress hears testimony about what the Trump administration calls the mismanagement that led to the demise of U.S. global media operations bringing news to many adversarial regions in the world. We will digest that.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and X @BretBaier. I'm going to be here in New York again tomorrow night, so we will have the fancy jib and all the nice stuff here.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250625el6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Iranian Missile Hits Hospital In Southern Israel; Trump Signaling Diplomacy With Iran; Intel Committee Warns A Strike May Be Ineffective; DNC desperate for dough; Obama's dark vision of America; Grassley says \uc2 FBI\uc2  emails reveal agents wanted to pile more charges on Trump; The left will stop at nothing to get Trump; Exposing the left's lawfare; DHS debunks Dodgers' claim of attempted ICE raid; Hochul provides $50 million for illegals to have lawyers; Rep. Maxwell Frost says, let's make everyone documented; \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  wants ICE agents arrested; Democrats hellbent on protecting criminal illegals; New York City mayor brags about a whole lot of nothing; Nutty Crockett calls Trump supporters mentally ill; Drag queens dance on Oregon House floor\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Kayleigh McEnany, Greg Palkot, Ari Fleischer, Miranda  Devine, Kevin Corke, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 7,518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: -- Special Report. Fair, balanced and still unafraid. We've got you covered, all things Israel and Iran, Trey Yingst and his team on the ground always. The Ingraham Angle is now.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  in for Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle. Thank you for watching. We begin tonight with a Fox News alert, the White House saying President Trump will make a decision within the next two weeks on whether the United States will take military action against Iran. Before we get to that, let's go to the breaking news live on the ground in Israel, where the two countries are fighting as we speak, Senior Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot in Tel Aviv with the latest. Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Kayleigh, yes, it was a day of fire and fury here in Israel as Iran, after taking a lot of beating in the past seven days, at least got a little bit of that back today. We were eye witnesses early Thursday morning to the onslaught of some 30 Iranian ballistic missiles fired at Israel. Most were shot down, but it's believed six or seven got through, making an urban mess of an area that contains Tel Aviv's Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Further down the Israeli coast in Holon, high-rise residential towers were turned into a shooting gallery for the projectiles. It was feared some residents had been caught in the rubble and hardest hit the Soroka Medical Center. It's the biggest hospital in southern Israel. The hospital building was extensively damaged, remarkably though, all of its wards had been cleared prior to the attack, Iran claimed there was an Israeli military installation nearby. Israel flatly rejected that.\par \par \uc2 As Prime Minister Netanyahu toured the rubble, Israeli officials claim that some of the Iranian missiles contain cluster bombs which spread the danger, banned in some quarters, contributed to the 240 overall injured. Netanyahu called the Iranians war tyrants. The Prime Minister also called for revenge. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  already on that mission, continuing today, to pound Iran's nuclear and military infrastructure, including a water cooled nuclear plant not currently in use. Israeli military intends to keep it that way.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, as for the task of getting rid of all of Iran's nuclear program, its nuclear infrastructure, according to Netanyahu today, while appreciative of the United States help, he asserted, Israel can get that job done on its own, tall order (ph). Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: We will find out in due time. Greg Palkot, thank you. Well, President Trump meanwhile, signaling he's still willing to cut a deal with Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I have a message directly from the President, and I quote, based on the fact that there's a substantial chance of negotiations that may or may not take place with Iran in the near future. I will make my decision whether or not to go within the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: While the President is still willing to talk, the White House is revealing that the Iranian nuclear threat is very real.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT\uc2 : Iran has all that it needs to achieve a nuclear weapon. All they need is a decision from the Supreme Leader to do that, and it would take a couple of weeks to complete the production of that weapon, which would, of course, pose an existential threat not just to Israel, but to the United States and to the entire world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Mark Dubowitz, Chief Executive of the Foundation for Defense Democracies joins me now. Mark, I'm very curious about what is going on here. You know you have Karoline Leavitt saying two weeks, within two weeks, so theoretically that could mean we still strike Fordow tomorrow. At the same time President Trump has always been loft (ph) to broadcast plans ahead of time. I think there could be some strategy here. But what do you think is going on with the broadcasting of the two weeks' timeline?\par \par \uc2 MARK DUBOWITZ, FOUNDATION FOR DEFENSE DEMOCRACIES: Yes, Kayleigh, I agree with you. I mean, I think the President last week ran one of the greatest deception operations since "Operation Overlord", when the United States fooled the Nazis into thinking that we were not going to invade at Normandy. He was involved in that. And as a result of that, the Israelis found the IRGC commanders and top personnel, literally and figuratively, in their beds last Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 So, the President could be again involved in deception. He likes to keep their enemy off balance. He's offering negotiations. He's been offering that for weeks, and the Iranians have been rejecting that and rejecting multiple U.S. offers. So he could be opening the path for Oman where the negotiations are set to take place, or opening the path for Fordow where B- 2 bombers could drop massive ordnance penetrators.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes. So there is two options there. Let's go to the second one you said about the negotiations. You know, I'm reading Politico this morning, and I read something that made me very uncomfortable, and it was this. There was a headline, meanwhile in Europe, and then they talk about Germany, France and Britain leading these negotiations with Iran. Very uncomfortable with the idea of Europe negotiating the way forward. Very much comfortable with the idea of President Trump doing so. In your opinion, what would an acceptable negotiation or end agreement look like? Because, in my mind, nothing short of Americans watching Fordow being dismantled would be sufficient.\par \par \uc2 DUBOWITZ: That's exactly right. I'll use President Trump's own words from two months ago. He said to the Iranians, you can either blow up your facilities under U.S. supervision, or someone is going to blow it up for you. And I think the President has been consistent and clear for a long time. Iran cannot have a nuclear weapon, and they cannot have the key capabilities to develop a nuclear weapon, so their program must be fully and permanently dismantled. They can have no enrichment capability or reprocessing capability that President Obama gave them and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gave them, and the President has an historic opportunity. His predecessors couldn't do it. His successors may not do it, but the President knows that he has to fully dismantle this program to stop a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: You know, I was reading through some notes that you sent, and I corresponded them with some reporting I read yesterday from The Wall Street Journal. I want you to listen to this line here, Israel has a plan for Fordow and the ability to carry it out on its own. A senior Israeli military official said without elaborating, so they're intimating, Israel might have a way to dismantle Fordow. You told our producers, Israel can set it back for months, only President Trump can destroy it permanently. So, do you believe this is a task only the United States can accomplish?\par \par \uc2 DUBOWITZ: The Israelis are full of surprises. I used to say that with respect to Israeli military capability, those who know don't say, and those who say don't know. And I think that's been borne out by the past year of incredible Israeli military and intelligence surprises. So the Israelis may have a surprise up their sleeve for Fordow and for the Supreme Leader, but it's certainly the United States. We have B-2 bombers, we have 30,000 pound massive ordnance penetrators with one plane, one bomb and one site. We could permanently set back that program. The Israelis will roll it back, but only we can -- can really end it for Fordow.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, you mentioned those surprises. And Trey Yingst was told by some of the Israeli officials, high-ranking ones, that they do have surprises up their sleeve, so I have no doubt it was, as you reminded us just last Thursday when they took out the IRGC. Really impressive. Thank you very much, Mark.\par \par \uc2 DUBOWITZ: Thanks, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, if at the end of these two weeks, President Trump decides we need to strike Iran, what exactly would that look like? You just heard about the