ght.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: But what was interesting is the groups it went up for. Hispanics, black Americans, Asians, foreign born workers got greater wage increases than everyday Americans, groups that don't often see this kind of wage growth. So, do you anticipate a Trump term too? We see these historically marginalized groups getting huge paychecks.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Of course they will. And you know what, if you look back during the really effective Trump policies of the previous administration that if you go back and look, then income inequality, which is the thing that the Far-Left folks have been telling us, is the most important metric of society that there completely is, income inequality dropped by more than it had since the Second World War, and it was precisely because President Trump is focused like a laser beam on blue collar workers and their welfare.\par \par \uc2 And I've got to tell you, I've seen you, you stick up for President Trump on shows, on Fox, and I've got to say that the things that you've been saying, I'll confirm about the meeting today. We're going over the tax bill, basically line-by-line at his desk, and he had things to say about all the nuances of the tax bill, and so he is digging into the weeds. He is getting briefed on everything. It's the complete opposite of the previous administration under President Biden. And he's doing this because he's trying to make America great again, and he's trying to, you know, make wages great again and reduce inequality and make growth break records.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: He's very much into the specifics. And one final question for you, because I say this to people, and they literally don't believe me. The Trump tax cuts, when you look at the data, if you make between $15,000 and $50,000, you got a tax cut between 16 percent and 26 percent every single group.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So, voting against the big, beautiful bill is voting against, voting for tax 