y Hochul\uc2 . She's even setting aside a whopping $50 million to help him out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We're also concerned about those that are walking out of this courthouse, taken away from their families. They don't have the attention. They don't have the lawyers.\par \par \uc2 And that's why the state of New York is providing $50 million to cover legal services where people are finding themselves in this situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: And Florida Congressman Maxwell Frost, he wants to go further.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I want to be clear. Being undocumented in this country is not a crime. My Republican colleagues who say, I don't want any undocumented people in this country, I actually agree with you.\par \par \uc2 So, let's document every single one of them to speed up the citizenship. That's how we fix this problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, here to make sense of this all is Madison Sheahan, ICE deputy director. Madison, I want to play for you a sound bite from \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 . Here is what he wants to do to some of the ICE agents that you oversee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If I was a prosecutor, the top prosecutor in my state, and these rogue agents arrested somebody like Alex Padilla, who was just exercising his first amendment right in his own office building and had him arrested, if I had a criminal statute that met the facts, I would use it.\par \par \uc2 If you have law enforcement capabilities and the facts meet those criminal statutes, you have to meet their chaos with your own strength.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Madison, first of all, we know Alex Padilla was never arrested. He was briefly detained. But what's your reaction to \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  talking about trying to arrest some of your agents?\par \par \uc2 MADISON SHEAHAN, DEPUTY ICE DIRECTOR: The men and women of ICE work every day to keep the American people safe. And it's insane to me to think that politicians are out encouraging people to cause harm to our officers, which is up over 400 percent over the last few years in trying to cause harm to these agents when they're out there doing their job.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats continue to say that nobody is above the law, and yet they will not allow law enforcement officers to do their jobs and enforce the laws that are on the books of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: You know, Madison, I saw something in Politico playbook that should give you some encouragement as to what your ICE officers are doing. And here's what it shows. The Trump administration on the issue of immigration, according to this big mega poll that was conducted, is up plus 16.\par \par \uc2 That is the approval for what your ICE agents are doing on immigration. Not only that, a quarter of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  voters approve of what's going on.\par \par \uc2 So, to your ICE agents, I just wonder, as they're being doxed, their pictures put up, being called kidnappers in L.A., do they take solace in the fact that the American people are on their side? Do they know that?\par \par \uc2 SHEAHAN: The men and women of ICE know that the American people are on their side. They know that President Trump is on their side, and Secretary Noem, and they will continue to go out arrest the worst of the worst in these communities.\par \par \uc2 And I'll continue to ask the Democrats, who are they really defending? They're defending the rapists, the gang members, the TDA, MS-13 gang members who go out and wreck havoc in the American people's communities, whether it's the rapists or traffickers.\par \par \uc2 And they continue to defend these people, but they're not what the American people want in their communities. And we're seeing that day after day as people continue to stand up and defend the men and women of ICE and allow them to do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah. And you mentioned the criminals and the rapists, and seventy five percent of ICE arrests have been just that, people with pending charges or criminal convictions. Thank you very much for bringing us the facts.\par \par \uc2 Well, still ahead, Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  brags about doing a whole lot of nothing. Plus, what's going on in Oregon? We'll tell you. Jimmy Failla is here with all that and more, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, it's time for the fun part of the night, WTF, What the Failla? And with us, the one and only, you guessed it, host of Fox News Saturday night, Jimmy Failla. Jimmy, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 Well, New York City Mayor -- hey. New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  wants everyone to come along with him because guess what, Jimmy? He gets stuff done. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Take care.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Jimmy, you're a man of the people. You stroll the streets of Manhattan. What's he getting done?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS SATURDAY NIGHT HOST: Literally, he got nothing done. This is like watching a cooking show where they don't put food in the oven at any point during the show. He billed it as like Adams is getting down and he needs to get down because Cuomo's about to be mayor, which is blowing my mind, Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, Cuomo thought MAGA stood for Make America Grope Again. And now we're about to watch Adams lose it, and it's because of dopey videos like this. Who told him that was a good idea? He just put on sport coats for five minutes. That's basically what I do all day.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: It is. Except your sports coats, they're leopard skin, and I like them a lot.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Hey, girl.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: But onto this, Jimmy. Jasmine Crockett. Hey, boy. Jasmine Crockett, she went after Trump supporters. She thinks MAGA is mentally ill. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Anybody that supports it is also sick. We've got a mental health crisis in this country because everyone, no matter how you affiliate yourself, should be against Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I love this so much.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So, evidence of the mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I just -- yes. Tell us the same.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: This is coming from the woman who was just in that choose your fighter video where all the liberals bounced around, and pretended they were in a video game. You remember that? I don't know who told her to rebrand as Cardi B's dumber sister, Cardi C-minus.\par \par \uc2 But I think her job is to make AOC look smart. Because every time they throw Crockett in front of a microphone, it elevates AOC's IQ to like a 23 or a 24. I don't know what other purpose she serves.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: You know what? I choose her as my fighter for the 2028 Democratic presidential nomination. We'll see how that works out.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Good for you.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Onto another person who needs to be educated quite literally on this topic, Whoopi Goldberg. She tried to compare living in Iran, Jimmy, to living here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think it's very different in the United States in 2025 than it is to live in Iran.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Not if you're black.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Not for everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Not if you're black.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: That is so embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Did she miss the --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I know that a black family in America was just president for eight years. OK. Iranian woman, women can't even show their face in public, let alone rule the entire country. It's so weapons grade stupid, Kayleigh. We need to bring back sliming.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember when Nickelodeon would dump green slime on people's head on TV if they did something stupid? Like the only way we're ever going to save this country is if we bring back sliming to shows like The View. The problem is they run out of slime.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes. They would indeed. Well, I've got this surprise for you, Jimmy. We cannot leave this hour without a surprise drag performance on the Oregon House floor. You'll love this. Two dancing drag queens celebrating a Democrat backed resolution honoring what other than black drag artistry. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I'm dying.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: You see it there?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: This is amazing. Well, listen, this is Oregon. So technically it's progress because the drag queens aren't in a kindergarten class right now.\par \par \uc2 But think back two clips ago to Jasmine Crockett saying the right had a mental health problem. Nobody in their right mind books a drag show in front of the state legislature except these people. So good for them. I thought it was funny and stupid.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, we powered through that delay like the leopard you are. Thank you, Jimmy.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's it for Laura Ingraham, on to Jesse Watters.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250620el6j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Xi Urges Israel And Iran To Halt Military Operations; Netanyahu Vows Revenge After Hospital Strike; Region On Edge As Conflict Enters Seventh Day; United States Bombs Could Be Used To Strike Fordow Nuclear Site; Murderer Was On The Streets Despite Deportation Order; American Freed After Nearly 600 Days In Captivity; Consumers Expected To See Higher Utility Bills; Democratic Party Financial And Leadership Challenges; Broadway Stars Give Free Concert in Times Square in Honor of Juneteenth; Trump Administration Says President will Make Decision to Intervene Militarily in Israel-Iran Conflict Within Two Weeks; New Polling Shows Mixed Attitudes of Americans towards Trump Administration Immigration Policies; Senate Republicans Investigating Possible Coverup of President Biden's Mental Decline While in Office.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Trey Yingst, Jennifer Griffin, Bill  Melugin, Mark Meredith, Nate Foy, Madison Alworth, Mark Meredith, Lauren  Green \par \uc2 7,478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. That's it for us, everyone. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Dana, I had ring-tailed lemur. Just one, exactly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you. All right.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 A huge surprise for federal agents when they arrest an illegal immigrant who is a convicted murderer. A New Jersey community welcomed -- welcomes home one of its own, just released after almost two years in terrorist captivity.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats struggling with trying to figure out an ideology, leadership, and finances turn to a familiar voice.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT," President Trump's press secretary, saying he will make a decision on whether to attack Iran within two weeks. He said to be asking questions of his advisers about so-called bunker busting bombs and their possible use against Iranian nuclear facilities. The conflict now entering a new phase earlier today when Iran's bombing of a hospital in Israel got a lot of reaction on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jaqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Today, the press secretary revealed that Iran already has all the components it needs to build a nuclear weapon. All it would take is an order from the Supreme Leader, and then that process takes about two weeks to complete. The same amount of time that the president is allowing for a decision one way or the other on U.S. military intervention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I have a message directly from the president, and I quote. "Based on the fact that there is a substantial chance of negotiations that may or may not take place with Iran in the near future, I will make my decision whether or not to go within the next two weeks."\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Trump, allowing room for diplomacy before making a call, as the White House makes clear, his decision does not hinge on any question of whether the U.S. military could eliminate the site at Fordow with bunker buster bombs. An official also telling Fox, no options have been taken off the table after reports of mixed assessments on bunker buster efficacy at the Pentagon and a report in \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  that the only sure-fire option, a tactical nuke, was not being considered.\par \par \uc2 The Iranians now heading to Geneva to meet European negotiators. Friday. No news on a White House visit yet.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT\uc2 : I am not going to get into hypotheticals, but as you heard from the president yesterday, they have expressed interest in doing so.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Chinese President Xi Jinping, offered to mediate, adding through its foreign ministry, "The parties involved in the conflict, especially Israel, should halt military operations as soon as possible." The White House leaving questions on that for the president to answer.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: There is a lot of pressure on him to finish the job with force. How is he balancing the concern that if he doesn't choose to do that, that it would send a message to Xi Jinping about the U.S. commitment to Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT\uc2 : The president is balancing a lot of viewpoints, and he is listening not just to other world leaders, but to his advisers and to people here in the country and the American people too. And I think that's what makes this president such a great leader, is his ability to listen.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Meantime, Democrats joining a pressure campaign to try to stop intervention.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): I do not believe Iran can have a bomb. The question here is, how do we deny them that bomb for the long term? I believe military strikes may get us a year, three years. It's not the solution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The White House confirmed that any deal with Iran would prohibit enrichment of uranium and preclude Iran from ever getting a nuclear weapon. The press secretary said today that Iran can and should make this deal, or they will face grave consequences. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jaqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jaqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Israel, meantime, is vowing retaliation after that Iranian strike on a major hospital. One Knesset member calls it an extreme war crime. The defense minister in Israel says the Ayatollah in Iran cannot continue to exist. Iran claims it was targeting an intelligence facility inside Israel.\par \par \uc2 Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst, live again tonight in Tel Aviv. Good evening, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: I do.\par \par \uc2 Yes. Bret, good evening. Tonight, Israel is prepared for more incoming missile fire from Iran. We do know reports indicate just 65 percent of the missiles overnight were intercepted with multiple impacts recorded in civilian areas, including at a hospital. Here is what the scene was like when we arrived at that medical facility.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Here at the Soroka Hospital in southern Israel, you can see the damage caused by these Iranian ballistic missiles. Doctors here say it's a miracle. No one was killed in the blast.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Not have Bret.\par \par \uc2 Israel's prime minister arrived on the scene later, promising a strong response to the strike and answering questions about the possibility of American involvement in the war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (through translator): President Trump will do what's best for America. I trust his judgment. He is a tremendous friend, a tremendous world leader, a tremendous friend of Israel and the Jewish people, and we will do what we have to do, and we are doing it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Netanyahu's remarks come as we're learning more about an Israeli maneuver on the first night of the war that took out Iran's top nuclear scientists. The spy craft, combined with air strikes dubbed Operation Narnia, went after 30 Iranian commanders and at least 14 nuclear scientists using classified intelligence gathered over a period of months.\par \par \uc2 The head of Israel's Intelligence Director Shlomi Binder spoke about his team's ongoing efforts to win the war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. SHLOMI BINDER, CHIEF, ISRAELI MILITARY INTELLIGENCE (through translator): We need to hunt them down wherever they flee, and you are succeeding in turning Iran, as I said earlier, from some distant place, 1500 kilometers from here, into an army that we know how to deal with. With the sense of it being a first circle threat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Israeli strikes are expected to ramp up tonight as the Israelis go after Iran's top leadership, including the country's Supreme Leader. Israel's defense minister tonight says he will have a price to pay. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst, live in Tel Aviv. Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some information now tonight about the so-called bunker busting bombs. Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin, is at the Pentagon tonight with that story. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 At the Pentagon, there is little doubt whether the GBU-57 Massive Ordnance Penetrator or MOP, could destroy Iran's Fordow nuclear facility buried 300 feet underground inside a mountain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Shown here during a test in 2018, developed by the Air Force specifically to address Iran and North Korea's underground nuclear sites, the MOP can penetrate 200-feet underground, further, if two missiles are dropped via GPS positioning into the same hole.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. TODD SAWHILL (RET), FORMER UNITED STATES CENTRAL COMMAND INTEL OFFICER: Does it have the capacity if it's employed the correct way to have the effect inside the mission space? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Retired Navy Captain Todd Sawhill oversaw the defense department's targeting operations, advising five CENTCOM commanders and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs on weapons like the MOP.\par \par \uc2 SAWHILL: You can use a multiple hit scenario so that you have multiple impacts in the same point, you'll create a large crater, spoil will be thrown out, and it allows a pathway for the next mission to go in even deeper and farther, so that successive hits on the same point will allow increased penetration and increased lethality.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): All eyes now on Whiteman Air Force Base in Missouri, home to the B-2 stealth bomber the US is the only military in the world with the capability to strike this buried program with its B-2 bombers, which can fly at 50,000 feet, carrying two massive, 30,000-pound bunker buster bombs. There are two entrances to Fordow.\par \par \uc2 SAWHILL: If they wanted to go after the portal entrances, it's likely that they would use to be twos for each portal entrance, so it could drop two bombs per portal.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The B-2s fly with a significant air escort. It's a 30 hour round trip to the Mideast from Whiteman that requires a massive air refueling bridge. That's why the Pentagon ordered dozens of refuelers to Europe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Iran still has missiles that can reach U.S. bases and ships, and has vowed to respond if the U.S. strikes. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have new Fox polling tonight about the immigration and deportation issue. 70 percent of registered voters surveyed say legal immigrants who come to the U.S. help the country: 22 percent disagree. 56 percent favor deporting illegal immigrants. 40 percent oppose.\par \par \uc2 46 percent approve of the job by immigration and custom enforcement. 52 percent disapprove. In this survey, 83 percent of Republicans surveyed approve of ICE's performance. 84 percent of Democrats disapprove, showing that split.\par \par \uc2 Tonight though, an amazing Fox News exclusive about the latest effects of the Biden border policy. Our crew was embedded with federal agents as they arrested a convicted murderer from El Salvador. But that's just the beginning of this story.\par \par \uc2 Here is correspondent Bill Melugin, reporting from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's a cargo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Only Fox News was with ICE in Los Angeles, as they moved in to take down a high- priority aggravated felon. His name is Alexander Palacios Guevara, and DHS says he's a Salvadoran illegal alien and Surenos gang member, with convictions in L.A. County for murder and attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have an immigration law.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Guevara has had a deportation order since February 2024 and had been living freely in Los Angeles County, a sanctuary jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Now, that this convicted murderer is in federal custody. He is coming here to this ICE facility where he is going to be booked. They are going to take his fingerprints, take his biometrics. He'll be placed in a cell.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): But as we were reporting on what would happen next with Guevara, he interrupted us to repeatedly say he shouldn't be deported because he has a special form of relief called CAT.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: ICE says you have a deportation order since February 2024, sir.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER PALACIOS GUEVARA, SALVADORAN NATIONAL AND CONVICTED MURDERER: No, I just got CAT on December the 18th of last year by the BIA. You can call my lawyer.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): It turns out his claim is true. ICE wasn't aware at the time of his arrest, but in the final days of the Biden administration on December 18th, the BIA or Board of Immigration Appeals under the Biden Justice Department granted Guevara a relief known as the CAT, meaning deferred removal under the Convention Against Torture. It means Guevara can't be deported to El Salvador because he fears being killed or tortured.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Are you a convicted murderer, sir?\par \par \uc2 GUEVARA: I am free.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Are you a convicted murderer?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, we learned that ICE had to briefly release this man from their custody, then, they quickly re-arrested him the very next day. He remains in their custody now. ICE sent a statement to Fox News about this, saying that this criminal alien should have never been roaming the streets of Los Angeles in the first place. They went on to say that this Biden-era decision to protect him from deportation to his home country is, "highly unusual". ICE tells us this guy is going to stay in their custody right now, while they try to find another country they can deport him to, other than El Salvador.\par \par \uc2 We did reach out to his immigration attorney multiple times over the last couple of days, asking specifically, who is this guy afraid of? Who does he think is going to torture him? But that attorney never responded to us. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was an amazing story. Bill Melugin in Los Angeles. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: U.S. stock markets were closed today for the Juneteenth federal holiday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a happy homecoming for an American Israeli captured by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  during the October 2023 massacre. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's president orders defense and security units to step up their monitoring and intelligence efforts in response to China's new military activities. Taiwan and Japan have been tracking the movement of two Chinese aircraft carriers conducting simultaneous operations in the Pacific for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Erick makes landfall in southern Mexico. The hurricane has maximum sustained winds of 125 miles per hour. The storm was downgraded slightly before coming ashore from a powerful Category 4 to a Category 3.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Seoul, South Korea, courtesy of \uc2 EarthCam\uc2 , one of the big stories there tonight. South Korea's last circus marks its centennial. The Dongchoon Circus troupe had more than 200 artists, acrobats, and other staff at its peak in the 1960s. Much has changed. Now, it performs at a big top just south of Seoul. Officials say their business is still doing relatively well.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has signed an executive order to keep TikTok running in the U.S. for another 90 days. It will give his administration more time to broker a deal to bring the social media platform under American ownership. It's the third time the president has extended that deadline.\par \par \uc2 A welcome ceremony was held this afternoon for an American Israeli man just released after being captured by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  during the October 2023 massacre. Correspondent Nate Foy shows us tonight from Tenafly, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: For Israeli American Edan Alexander, this homecoming follows nearly 600 days in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FOX, TENAFLY, NEW JERSEY RESIDENT: It's really a bright day when everything else is very difficult and very tough.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Edan's longtime rabbi tells Fox News today is surreal.\par \par \uc2 MORDECHAI SHAIN, ALEXANDER FAMILY RABBI: I'm just going to hug and just my tears will just come out, and nothing more. There's nothing more you need to say.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Edan left Tenafly, New Jersey shortly after graduating high school, volunteering to serve in the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 . Now he returns a grown man with a harrowing story of survival in the face of terror.\par \par \uc2 SHAIN: We don't even know how much torture he went. He didn't even tell us a little of it.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists took Edan and 250 others hostage during its October 7th attack. Edan's parents pushed hard for his freedom. The Trump administration negotiated his release last month.\par \par \uc2 EDAN ALEXANDER, FREED HOSTAGE: Mr. President, oh my God.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Edan, I'm just really honored, and your parents are incredible. I saw your mother. She was pushing me around a little bit. She was putting a lot of pressure on me.\par \par \uc2 YAEL ALEXANDER, MOTHER OF EDAN ALEXANDER: Like a good mom. Like a Jewish mom.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Thousands celebrated his release back home, as Edan began his recovery from brutal conditions.\par \par \uc2 ADI ALEXANDER, FATHER OF EDAN ALEXANDER: He's a six foot something tall and at the lowest he was 121 pounds. So it was -- it was a disaster, but he's much, much better now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, many people here today call the Edan's homecoming a miracle. It's something that they prayed about every single day after he was taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As for what comes next, Edan's rabbi tells Fox that he looks forward to returning to somewhat of a more private and normal life and spending time with his family, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, another example of how artificial intelligence may already be affecting your life, even if you know nothing about A.I. It turns out the energy required by this evolving technology could mean higher cooling bills this summer. Here's Fox Business correspondent Madison Alworth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Data centers, the backbone of A.I. use a lot of energy, and their energy demands are growing at the same time, we are seeing higher temperatures, electricity shortages and an aging electric grid.\par \par \uc2 According to a government estimate, electricity bills will be about four percent higher this summer than last. That's well above the overall 2.4 percent inflation rate.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 National Energy Assistance Directors Association\uc2  thinks the number is even higher, closer to a seven percent increase in cost, making this potentially the most expensive summer to stay cool in 12 years.\par \par \uc2 Energy economists say if nothing is done to address data center energy costs, the problem will only get worse.\par \par \uc2 MARK WOLFE\uc2 , NATIONAL ENERGY ASSISTANCE DIRECTORS' ASSOCIATION: Their consumption puts more pressure on the grid, and some people have argued that their rates are not appropriate. They should be higher, because if they're not sufficient to cover their costs of electricity, then consumers pay more.\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (voice over): And some consumers are already paying a lot more. In Illinois, \uc2 Ameren\uc2  customers are seeing their electricity bills rise by 50 percent. In Pennsylvania, \uc2 PPL\uc2  customers are paying 16 percent more as of this month. In Maryland, electric bills are climbing $15.00-18.00 a month. All states that have data centers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH (on camera): The Energy Department is forecasting that data center energy demand will triple by 2028. States are incentivizing companies to come in and build data centers in their areas, but experts say that that needs to be paired with energy development, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison, thank you. New details about just how bad things are getting for the Democratic Party as they're trying to figure