: Well, we all remember the No King protests against \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's deportations. No, we don't because there weren't any. The left tolerated that.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think just as President Trump promised to deal with Iran's nuclear program, this is an unambiguous promise. I think most people agree controlling the border is good. The complexity comes, and I know you've got this in your poll as well, when we get to the issue of people who came here illegally but are part of their communities and work in our economy. That's a tough one. And that's a tough one, I will say it's a tough one for me. I can see both side of this. So if I were polled on that question, I'm not sure how I would answer it. It would probably depend on how the pollster phrased it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, exactly. Hugh, and one of those polls, the view of deporting illegal immigrants, favor 56 percent, oppose 40 percent. But to that point, you know, how you feel about it, to Mark's point, you know, it's the people that came over during the Biden years, the open doors in recent months, in recent years, before that, how long. There's all kinds of questions.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I am with Mark. I am a border hawk and regularization dove. Migrants -- I lived in California for almost 30 years. Migrants are a feature, not a bug. People have been there for a very long time. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , by the way, he has told me on the air on my program, he is open to taking care of the Dreamers. I think he is open to taking care of everyone who is in the country and living a good, solid, productive life because that helps American productivity.\par \par \uc2 I noted that Karoline Leavitt said "criminal" again today. The emphasis has been, and I think it should remain, on criminal migrants who came in during the Biden flood, and it was a flood. And unless and until sanctuary cities cooperate with ICE, we're not going to get those criminals without also getting the hardworking migrant who has been here for 25 years. It's a tragedy, but I think most Americans have this same mixed feeling that Mark and I have.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Meredith, it's Juneteenth. We have this video from Juneteenth at the White House that went viral of President Biden, if you remember that. That raised a lot of questions and talked about his mental, cognitive abilities, how he was kind of staring into the light there. There is now this Senate hearing dealing with all of this, who knew what when. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA): Tell the truth and run, or do you have a greater obligation to your boss?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, luckily I didn't have that problem.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL) SENATE MINORITY WHIP: Apparently armchair diagnosing former President Biden is more important than the issues of grave concern.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): The ceremonial song hail to the chief was effectively replaced with hail to the pen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking about the autopen there. There is a poll question in our poll about, do you, previous White House, was it transparent about Biden's mental fitness? And 68 percent said no. You can also see using the autopen, making key decisions, covering up his decline. Thoughts on this and where it's going?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: Well, I think one thing that was worth pointing out in that poll is that there is a difference between people wanting to know about how President Biden's mental decline may have impacted decision-making, may have impacted the use of the autopen, versus the cover-up. And I thought that was sort of an interesting data point in there.\par \par \uc2 But we continue to hear new reporting come out from new books, new revelations about what was going on behind the scenes. And in the Senate hearing, Republicans are trying to get to the bottom of it, but Democrats did not seem to be o