rvices II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, THE CLAY TRAVIS AND BUCK SEXTON SHOW: We're going after New York City, where huge numbers of illegal immigrants are. Can you imagine if he showed up at the Dodgers day and did an ICE raid in reaction to this?\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Freak out in sports media, Clay, to exceed anything you've ever done. Appreciate you, Clay. Thanks for watching us today. We'll now turn you over to THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino, along with Kennedy, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 And it is the fourth day of the deadly conflict between Israel and Iran. President Trump is talking to the press at the G7 and we are monitoring that for any big news.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let's go to \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 . He can give us a quick catch up from Tel Aviv. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Dana. Iran is now interested in de-escalation and a return to the negotiating table with the condition that the U.S. stays out of the fight. The U.S. for its part is now sending the USS Nimitz carrier strike group to the region ahead of schedule.\par \par \uc2 There was a strike during a live broadcast of Iranian state T.V. and what I can show you now is video of the aftermath of the state T.V. building in Tehran on fire.\par \par \uc2 Israel, for its part, did send a warning into the area, warning people to evacuate ahead of that strike, state T.V. is now back on the air, broadcasting from a backup location.\par \par \uc2 Israel's Prime Minister says Israeli jets can now do whatever they want in the skies over Tehran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): The Israeli Air Force controls the skies over Tehran. This changes the entire campaign. We are on the way to achieving our two goals, eliminating the nuclear threat and eliminating the missile threat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: We have new video out of Iranian F-14 on the ground being taken out by Israeli air strikes. Iran purchased those F-14s before the Islamic Revolution in 1979.\par \par \uc2 We also have satellite images of missile production and nuclear facilities before and after Israeli air strikes. Israel says one-third of Iran's missile launchers have now been destroyed.\par \par \uc2 We keep getting warnings out of Iranian state T.V. and the Iranian Revolutionary Guard that drone strikes and missile strikes are inbound to Israel, but thus far, they haven't materialized. It doesn't mean they won't, but we usually get credible warnings from Israel's Home Front Command, and we haven't had that yet this evening, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 , thank you. Stay safe and we will continue on with this.\par \par \uc2 The big headline today, is Iran ready to wave the white flag and cry uncle. The Wall Street Journal is reporting that Iran is seeking talks with the U.S. and Israel to end the hostilities. President Trump out with world leaders at the G7 in Canada, and once again imploring Iran to make a deal before it's too late.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, have you seen, have you heard any signals or seen any messages from intermediaries that Iran wishes to deescalate the conflict?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, they'd like to talk, but they should have done that before. I had 60 days, and they had 60 days, and on the 61st day, I said, we don't have a deal. They have to make a deal, and it's painful for both parties.\par \par \uc2 But I'd say Iran is not winning this war, and they should talk, and they should talk immediately, before it's too late.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse, President Trump has been consistent for 10 years that Iran should not be allowed to have a nuclear weapon and letting Israel do what it thinks it needs to do to protect itself.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Right. So, we're pretty involved already. We green lit the operation. Obviously, we're intercepting all the missiles that Iran's sending over, and we have shared intelligence and are doing refueling flights. The question is, how much more involved are we going to get?\par \par \uc2 And so, a few scenarios. One, if the Iranians attack U.S. assets, we'll have to get involved. The question is whether we're going to give or use bunker-busting bombs to completely eliminate the Iranian nuclear program, or if we'll authorize regime change strikes against the leadership.\par \par \uc2 And I think the balance that Trump's trying to strike is you can't allow the Iranians to have a nuclear weapon. It's been almost a hundred years of U.S. policy, Non Proliferation. You can't get blackmailed by rogue states. But also, you can't have regime change wars, and those have unintended consequences.\par \par \uc2 So, if you were to have a decapitation strike against the Iranian leadership, what comes next? Because it's a theocracy, and you have 30,000 radical Muslim clerics that are still there, you can't kill all of them.\par \par \uc2 So, does it break down into militia factions where they all just kill each other? Do we install somebody? Do people come from all over the region with guns? Is there an insurgency? No one knows.\par \par \uc2 And so, the Israelis have their own agenda. They want this guy gone now. Does that agenda jive with our agenda? Maybe not, because they're not going to be the ones to manage the chaos. We're going to have to manage the chaos, and they know that, and that's fine.\par \par \uc2 But we've already overthrown Iranian leader before, we installed the Shah, and he was a great ally for many years, and he pumped oil, and he was fantastic against the Soviets, but he was so unpopular, he was overthrown by these even more fundamental radical people who were still dealing with today.\par \par \uc2 The big question is, is the Iranian nuke deal still possible? The Israelis say no. They call them fake talks. They think they're going nowhere, and we're getting strung along.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump disagrees. He thinks there can be a deal that was put together, and at this point, the Iranians have no leverage, and so it looks like they're kind of begging to come back to the negotiating table, and Donald Trump isn't ready to do the deal yet. I think he's going to let the Israeli military continue to soften up Iranian targets as they finally limp back to the negotiation table and swear off enrichment.\par \par \uc2 And that would be a great outcome if they swear off enrichment. But I don't know if that's even possible, because the Iranians saw what happened to Gaddafi. Remember, after Saddam was toppled, Gaddafi was like, all right, we'll get rid of the nukes, and the next thing you know, Hillary (ph) and the Europeans came in and took him out.\par \par \uc2 So, very delicate situation. The next couple days will be pretty serious.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And the IAEA , which I guess it's basically the international organization that tech checks on nuclear enrichment, just last week, they said there that Iran is in total noncompliance, Harold.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, the Israelis said we underestimated our enemy on October 7th, and we can't afford to do it again, because they're closer than ever to a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 So, now the stage is set. In the meantime, though, we still have we -- Israel has 53 hostages still in Gaza,\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, good to be back around the table. Hope everyone had a productive and happy Father's Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 Jesse, you framed it fairly well. I think the only thing I would add to what you -- what you eloquently said is two things.\par \par \uc2 First, we're going to be confronted as a nation very soon with whether or not we should provide the wherewithal and the resources. We're the ones with ammunition and that bunker-busting bomb that you talked about that would be used to blow up a half a mile under earth there the mountain that we've identified, that the Israelis have identified.\par \par \uc2 I'm a believer that we should if the Israelis believe that's what's needed to put an end to this.\par \par \uc2 I listened to Mr. Netanyahu. I'm not always a fan of but I listened to him on Sunday and had a chance to react to him on Bret Baier's show. Bret did a really good interview.\par \par \uc2 I thought Mr. Netanyahu was convincing. He made two points there. He said, first, the slumber of democracy. He invoked Churchill, talking about, when democracies get lazy, they get content. This is when these kinds of evil things happen. We have a moment here to stop this.\par \par \uc2 He also spoke to the Iranian people and spoke to the Persian people and said, look, the Israelis and the Persian people have had a great relationship over many centuries. There's no reason going forward if we get past this ugly and unfortunate chapter that we can't do the same going forward.\par \par \uc2 Now, I understand, Dana, some of the concerns that Democrat, Republican politicians, including some supporters of President Trump, have about broadening this war, because the real risk here is the escalation of the war.\par \par \uc2 Jack Keane said something I thought very telling over the weekend as well, and even repeated on our network today. He said that the Army, Navy and Air Force of the Iranians have been diminished and weakened, and that the leadership of their army, navy and air force are diminished and poor as well. And if we're thinking about the moment in a way to put an end to this, this might be the moment to do it.\par \par \uc2 I remember when there were those who were saying we should have supplied more support and more resources for the Ukrainians against the Russians. President Trump often says that, had he been president, that never would have happened. Russia would have never invaded Ukraine. And I assume he meant that he would have provided more resources and tools for the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 I think we're at that moment now, Dana, with this Iranian-Israeli conflict. I don't want a broadening of the conflict. I don't want an escalation. I think the president of Turkey and others are right. We are -- we're in a precipice.\par \par \uc2 But what I don't want to have happen going forward is this risk and threat that the Iranians pose, and what you o -- what you led into this question about, they have lied about their preparation, their ability to develop and detonate a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 I don't want that. And if, even if the AIEA is wrong, I'm willing to take the risk. If we have this moment now, we should destroy this capability of the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Kennedy, I feel like President Trump doesn't get credit for something else. That's a key part of the story, but it's a smaller part of the story, and it's that first of all, Israel did degrade the capabilities of Hezbollah and Hamas , and then you have President Trump in the Middle East just two weeks ago, making friends, making allies, and recognizing the new Syrian government, to the point that yesterday, not when this started four days ago, Lebanon said, we're staying out of it.\par \par \uc2 And then Syria yesterday, you can use our airspace. And if you just hold that thought, because we have a new sound from President Trump. He was just asked about the possibility of regime change in Iran, and we could listen to that, and then we'll come back around to Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP : I want to see no nuclear weapon in Iran, and we're well on our way to making sure that happens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And short comment, Kennedy, I wasn't sure how long that would be, your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 LISA KENNEDY MONTGOMERY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Short thought. Yes, I think he shares that sentiment with the entire Western world. No one wants Iran to have a nuclear weapon, because, you know, this is not the era of detente, of Mutually Assured Destruction, where, you know there were countries stockpiling nuclear weapons for defensive reasons.\par \par \uc2 Iran has talked about going on the offense. And part of the existence of this regime is the desire to annihilate Israel, you know, which is right next door.\par \par \uc2 Think about it, what if Canada talked? What if the reason for being for Canada was to destroy and obliterate the United States?\par \par \uc2 I was recently on a Holocaust tour in Poland and we visited the extermination camps. And you know, that took years for Nazi Germany to exterminate six million Jews and three million Catholic poles. Iran wants to do what Hitler started with one bomb, and that's the fear.\par \par \uc2 So, Israel has started this, and now what the United States has to ask itself, are we going to be the ones who finish it? Because it will, if you're talking about the main enriching site for uranium, the Fordow site, that will take the kind of bunker buster that Israel does not have.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to a former Navy SEAL Senior Chief, and he said, yes, Israel does have bunker-busting bombs, but not the massive type, where it will take a pair with a B2 bomber in order to completely obliterate that facility. That is a possibility if the United States wants to get involved.\par \par \uc2 Iran does not want the U.S. to get involved, because they know it is not only the end of their nuclear program, but also their petroleum program as well. So, what does Iran have left?\par \par \uc2 What they're clinging on to now is the desperate hope that they will, with some of their other Middle East partners, be able to move forward capitalistic in a way that takes them out of petroleum but into the economic future. They can't do that as an authoritarian, theocratic regime relying solely on oil.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Greg Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: This is a tough one. You know, I -- is this -- is Trump's actions a violation of the Trump doctrine, which is America First? I think that's the -- that's the debate that's kind of raging among the right.\par \par \uc2 But you elected Trump because you trust him. He's been consistent on everything, pretty much everything, here and there. He's directionally consistent. I don't remember him being for any wars. He was very -- he was outspoken about most of them.\par \par \uc2 So, I think to say, oh, now he's a neocon, is unfair. You know, he wouldn't do this unless he thought it had to be done. So, you kind of have to give him the benefit of the doubt, which it cannot do with Iran.\par \par \uc2 Iran is like the T-1000, you know, in the second Terminator movie. You knock it down, you knock down its nuclear capabilities, and it reconstitute itself.\par \par \uc2 And so, your only hope is that you make the time it takes to reconstitute longer and longer. You're not kicking the can down the road, you're kicking Tehran down the road, and it's like whether or not you want to do it now, you know, you have to weigh the benefits.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to sound wishy washy, but you know it's all about frame of reference. You know, Israel has the Holocaust. That is their frame of reference. This is not going to happen again. You know, if Israel was around before, there wouldn't have been a holocaust.\par \par \uc2 But then, the other frame of reference is Libya, Iraq, Afghanistan, we were promised an end to that. So somehow you've got to, like, start fresh with this and try to push the back story of history off on the side.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that's possible you know, but you know, if you're going to trust somebody, I would trust Trump and not Iran. That's where I'm at.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: In addition, I would trust the Middle East allies that he met with, who are pretty quiet about all of this happening and not unhappy about it, as reported, apparently, OK.\par \par \uc2 Up next, anti-ICE, chaos erupting again in Los Angeles as President Trump launches a new immigration crackdown in Democrat-led cities.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: Well, it's a very incendiary party. Well, how's this for California love? Los Angeles seeing another round of mostly peaceful fires as President Trump Announces plans to flood other Democrat-run cities, New York and Chicago, with new larger waves of ICE agents. Here's 47 to elaborate on that plan. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You did a post last night where you said you want ICE to really target Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP : I want them to focus on the cities, because the cities are where you really have what's called sanctuary cities, and that's where the people are.\par \par \uc2 I look at New York. I look at Chicago. I mean, you got a really bad governor in Chicago and a bad mayor, but the governor is probably the worst in the country.\par \par \uc2 Biden allowed 21 million people to come into our country. Of that, vast numbers of those people were murderers, killers, people from gangs, people from jails, they emptied their jails out into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Most of those people are in the cities, all blue cities, all Democrat-run cities, and they think they're going to use them to vote. It's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: All right. So Jesse, it feels like both these sides, the blue state mayors of sanctuary cities and the president with his immigration agenda, they have been on a collision course with disaster. Could these riots have been averted, maybe earlier, maybe a few years ago?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Of course. I mean, they say they want the criminals out, but then they shelter them in the sanctuary cities, and they say they're not voting. But anytime you try to pass voter I.D., they're like, oh, no, you can't, it's racist. And then they say, oh well, you know, these people aren't here to take American jobs. And they're like, oh, you can't deport him. Who's going to wash my lettuce?\par \par \uc2 Nothing the Democrats are saying is consistent. Now, Donald Trump has to target the cities, because that's where they're clustered, but he also has to go after the job sites, because that's going to get a lot of attention, and it's going to make bosses not want to hire illegals.\par \par \uc2 They just did a raid at a site in Oklahoma. I think it was a meat processing plant. They swept up a bunch of illegals, and the next week they posted job openings, and they got overwhelmed with resumes from Americans, because Americans will do these jobs if you just pay them a fair wage.\par \par \uc2 Remember, during the pandemic, we used to do segments about how this was like a worker's market, and all these wages were going up, and these companies couldn't find anybody and like, you could have your choice where you wanted to work and what benefits. You know what happened? Biden let in 20 million illegals, and it just dropped the wages to the dirt.\par \par \uc2 And then it's like economic treason to do that. So you have to really make sure American wages go up so they can buy cable T.V. news subscriptions, because that's what this is really all about.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: All right, so Harold, in Texas, they don't have sanctuary cities, but they do -- they do have a lot of immigrants coming over the border. It's a lot easier to access some of the bigger cities, like San Antonio and Houston, but they don't have to call themselves sanctuary city, and they're more cooperative with law enforcement. So, why can't California adopt the Texas model?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I don't know. They probably should.\par \par \uc2 Couple things here, I am for -- I'm a part of the majority of Americans, overwhelming majority of Americans who want to deport people who have come to this country illegally, who are committing crimes.\par \par \uc2 The data shows, thus far, that less than 10 percent of the people we've deported so far have criminal records. Now they have a criminal record because they're here illegally, but have a criminal record.\par \par \uc2 The president, I think, often says and rightly says, that these countries have released the worst of the worst in their countries, and these people have come here. And we have seen awful, just tragic, ugly crimes, fatal crimes, committed by -- committed by illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 That's where, and I think a lot of the country throughout the election, one of the reasons I think the president won, because people thought he was going to -- that would be the model first, number one. I hope they continue to do that. And I think even his -- the president's own secretary of agriculture has come forward to say that we've got to think about the lettuce pickers. We've got to think about those who are -- who are farmers in our country, who presumably are from red states.\par \par \uc2 Now, last, I don't like the Red State, Blue State thing. I don't care where you are. If you are an illegal immigrant here and you're committing a crime, you shouldn't be treated differently if you -- if you do it in a red state versus a blue state.\par \par \uc2 And I think the president, I understand what he's saying, and I get the politics of it, but I would just encourage him not to talk about blue state this and red state that. If there are people here who are here illegally, regardless of where they are, and they're committing crimes, you're going to continue to garner, generate big, big support, even from Democrats, to get these people out of here.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are questions about what's happening in Los Angeles, should the military be there, how it should be done? But I am one Democrat who firmly believes that Mr. Homan ought to do exactly what he said from the outset, which was to go after the worst first, you do that, you will continue to enjoy broad, broad support.\par \par \uc2 But when he started going after workers, people who are working on farms, people who are working in restaurants, people who are working in retail stores. That's where I think the president is going to face some resistance. And the numbers suggest that that indeed is happening.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: And Karen Bass, she's another politician who could have handled this differently, and you know now she's complaining that the president won't pick up her phone, and she's surprised by some of the ICE raids. But to Harold's point, doesn't 80 percent of the country want the worst to be taken out first?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And I think the White House is smart to keep the focus on the worst first efforts. And I don't even think President Trump needs to say blue cities run by Democrats. You could just say in America's largest cities where the -- where illegal immigrants and gang members are gathered, that's where we're going to focus first.\par \par \uc2 And that -- but however, I say that, and then at the same time I think, but why shouldn't he point out the leadership, because it's what people have voted for that have landed themselves in these situations, in these cities. And we have a mayor's race going on right now in the city where there's a hot and heavy debate about what is the direction of the country going to be.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think -- you know, the president is so transparent. You can't pick up any -- you pick up any media organization, he has been willing to talk to everybody this week about immigration, about Iran, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 And then you have Gavin Newsom . So, he was on the New York Times podcast, it's called The Daily last week, and he was defending those who are here in the country illegally, but who haven't gotten in trouble since, OK?\par \par \uc2 But this is the real dividing line, which is, do you think coming to the -- into the country without permission and without doing it legally, does that mean you have broken a law?\par \par \uc2 And yes, I think fundamentally, two plus two is four, then that's how you get to that point. But that's why I think this worst first thing makes a lot of sense, change and fixes takes time. People -- anytime there's something new and something disruptive, it is going to get a lot of headlines, but you also have the media being super surprised at the numbers of people who have self-deported.\par \par \uc2 But part of this is important about the magnet. What's the magnet in California? You got free health care. California, you can do anything you want in California, that's why they have such a problem.\par \par \uc2 And taking away that magnet means that maybe they leave and we help correct some of this problem without as much disruption as people are seeing.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: Yes. And to Dana's point, Greg, in California, you pay really high taxes. I mean, you don't pay high taxes. So, the fire equipment works, and so the reservoirs are filled. It's so people who come here illegally can get free health care and free college education. When will the state of California revolt against this?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You can't have free stuff and no border. I think we can all agree that borders are important.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: There we go.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Great. I'm done.\par \par \uc2 The problem is we all didn't agree that borders were important. We just experienced a systematic removal of our border under people who claim to be the Biden administration that we're still not sure who they are, and so now they're criticizing the process of removal?\par \par \uc2 No, we don't have to listen to you. I can listen to Harold because Harold, you talked about the need for a border. But a lot of these people, they didn't, and now, you know, you -- this attack over process camouflages the rejection of the goal, you know? So now the adults are in charge, and they're rescuing a country. It's like Trump boarded a ship and is sealing a gaping hole.\par \par \uc2 If we had listened to the people making the biggest noise about deportation and process, we'd have no country left. This is an intervention. We have to do this, you know, this -- if this border crisis had to happen, this would be a different discussion, but now we're doing this. It's dramatic, it's forceful, but we couldn't get the Democrats to listen, and now they're engaged.\par \par \uc2 But they're not engaged over the victims of illegal aliens or the missing sex trafficked kids. They're upset that someone won't wipe their ass, or in Jerry Nadler 's case, people won't wipe their ass.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: He's a -- he's a little dumpling that Jerry Nadler (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 Up next, the left loses it at Trump's military parade. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: A real split-screen moment in America over the weekend. President Trump holding a big beautiful military parade celebrating the 250th anniversary of the Army, while the No Kings anti-Trump protests brought out millions of cat ladies and Woodstock-era boomers.\par \par \uc2 This lady was running around in whatever the heck this is. And these children are smashing the snot out of a Trump pinata. The Democrat speakers just as unhinged.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN , PRESIDENT. AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: Together we build a future of opportunity and justice for all.\par \par \uc2 MARK RUFFALO, ACTOR: When I look at my phone or the news of the world around me, we're holding on for dear life. We have a king in his court and his beige henchmen and they're trampling on our rights.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, CORRESPONDENT, NBC NEWS: They're coming from the heart. These are people that are speaking their truth. And it's not about insulting the president, it's about letting people know who they are and where they come from.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): We have a supreme constitution. We don't have a supreme leader. We have no dictators here. We have no kings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Greg, how would you assess the No Kings Protest this weekend?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I thought it was a raging success. No kings, you know. But it's -- it is amazing that they create -- it's again another delusion that they create in their heads that doesn't exist. We do not have kings here. If they had been -- if we had kings, you wouldn't have had to march because you're cowards. So, they're protesting something that doesn't exist. So, it's easy then to claim mercy.\par \par \uc2 Now, look at Canada. They had a no king's day, but they changed the name to No Tyrants.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, because they have one.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because they have a king. There is courage. There is courage. Oh, it would have meant something if you had no king's day because you have a King Charles . But no you didn't want to piss off the king, so you changed it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, what a contrast. We have a military parade where 250 years ago we got rid of a king. And on the same day, you have this astroturf cosplaying circus of people pretending that there's a king, hallucinatory drama. Meanwhile, you know, in D.C. they're actually commemorating getting rid of a real king.\par \par \uc2 Imagine all the time wasted, all of these old people that are so scared, so scared they have to go and protest. They have -- maybe their family isn't talking to them. I don't know what's going on. But they go I think they're doing something. It's sad.\par \par 