was busy. I salute and believe that the United States Army is the greatest fighting force to ever exist and the freedoms that I enjoy because of it.\par \par \uc2 I also happen to agree with the premise of what they talked about, the no kings. I don't want a king either. Now, I might not have done it quite the way they did it, but anybody watching, this is what democracy is built on the hopes and aspirations of people. So, I don't mind this. I just think it's a testament to the strength of the country that we can coexist.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Harold, do you not want Bigfoot? Should we ever protest against Bigfoot?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: If that's what you want to do, I'm your pal. We can absolutely go protest.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Kennedy, if Barack Obama had held a military parade, Fox wouldn't have had a problem with that, right?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: No. We would have aired it. And hopefully, it would have been just as spectacular because the Army was just as spectacular when Barack Obama was president. So, I was watching this and it was actually a wonderful walk through history and the history -- the military history of our country. It doesn't make you a wararmonger to to look at that and go, oh, wow, you know, look how our technology and uniforms have changed over 250 years.\par \par \uc2 My grandfather was an Army captain during World War I. My beloved partner is a Wester Pointer, as I told Dana today on her show, and he also went to Ranger School. and they highlighted West Point and they highlighted the Rangers. And that was a really cool thing for me to see as a civilian. He's also a combat veteran.\par \par \uc2 And you know, our country is safe because of people like him. And it was not about Trump. And they're not going to do this for the Air Force because the Air Force is not as cool as the Army.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I want to hear more about your partner in the commercial break.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: He's great.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Up next, is Sean Penn too chicken to meet with President Trump?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Hollywood's human ashtray Sean Penn telling Bill Maher he would not sit down for dinner with Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN, ACTOR: The only reason I would not accept an invitation is because I see -- I see no -- it's a -- it's a long flight. And I see no --\par \par \uc2 BILL MAHER, COMEDIAN: Really? You'll meet with (BLEEP) --\par \par \uc2 PENN: I don't think --\par \par \uc2 MAHER: -- Castro and Hugo Chavez, but not the President of the United States?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Yes. I saw good results come out of some of those things that I just personally wouldn't trust anything that was said in the room, including the personality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, Jesse, I was watching that and I just got like hypnotized by -- what's his name again? Sean Penn's shoes. Do we have a picture of the shoes there? Check those out.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What the --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Aren't those amazing? I think this is a -- I think he's trying to set some kind of fashion trend.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Or is that -- he has a hole in his shoe and he's wrapped tape around it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Is that what I'm looking at right there?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, exactly. I think he -- is he down on his luck or is this --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's like when you're worth a hundred million and you wear shredded jeans.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Really?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, I used to think Sean Penn was a traitor when I was younger. And he would go interview Chavez and Castro and I was like, oh, how dare you? But then I listened to an interview he did and he seemed intellectually curious about these people and wanted to see for himself, wanted to get past the propaganda, so I respected that.\par \par \uc2 But then when Maher hit him between the eyes and said, you won't even meet with the president but you meet with these tyrants. He had nothing. I mean, I would interview Tampon and I've invited Tampon on the show and he refuses. That's why I am the most tolerant host on cable.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know I'm not a doctor, Dana, but I look at this angle and he definitely has to go to a doctor. There is a swollen node on his neck which is unmistakable. Again, I'm not saying I'm a doctor. But I have saved many lives just by watching TV.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It is sort of weird to watch a show where you've got like vodka and cigarettes and shoes that look like somebody's homeless. I think he's afraid to have dinner with Trump because he's afraid he would be charmed and he doesn't want --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He doesn't want to take the risk of losing his identity as a never- Trumper.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You -- I think he hit the nail on the head. It's exactly why they --they're giving crap to Maher because he "was charmed" when he really wasn't.\par \par \uc2 Kennedy, I don't know, man. I'm watching this -- it's -- I'm a little worried about the guy.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: He looks like Randi Weingarten and Nick Nolte had a very old baby.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: A very old baby who's quite unwell. Yes, I mean, I've known Sean Penn for years. And he doesn't look good, but also like, he's beholden to the progressives.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: If he loses them, he has nothing politically. And the way he looks, he's really given up on himself and acting. So, yes -- but he still has to play this popularity contest. But the smartest thing for him would be to find one avenue, even if it's a very small lane where he and the President agree --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: -- and really capitalize on that because Trump is a kind of person where he would acquiesce --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY: -- if that would make the world or the country a better place. But you know, he's too much of an egotistical or narcissist to do that, which means he'll never be bono.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, Harold, I look at Bill Maher and Sean Penn in the same picture, and Maher is older than him, but looks maybe 10 to 15 years younger. Much like you, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Thank you. I appreciate that lead in. And I'm -- you know, we talk about this a lot. Being around this table I often say at the start "it's good to be back around the table" because I value and respect the chance to be able to sit, disagree, listen, be stimulated, hopefully persuade a little bit, and even be persuaded myself.\par \par \uc2 During the Civil Rights era, Civil Rights leaders, Black leaders who were being denied full participation in America were being hanged, who were being arrested, being denied basic opportunities, would still sit down with the very people who were doing that denying to try to make the country better.\par \par \uc2 It's famous -- a famous famous story that Tip O'Neill and Speaker -- Tip O'Neill and President Reagan who had very different politics, very different upbringings, very different outlooks on life, would often get together to try to find ways to make the country better. We are so divided as a country today that we find ourselves at a strategic disadvantage not only in trying to figure out and solve problems at home, but even how we present around the world.\par \par \uc2 I was glad to hear Bill Maher counter Sean Penn like he did. He did it in his own way and basically suggested maybe you should take a look at your own pods. You may dislike Trump so much, but if you can do these things, how can you not be -- meet with a person that your fellow countrymen elected as president. And it goes both ways.\par \par \uc2 So, I was excited to see this. I hope they both learn from it. And I hope - - I hope we make progress as a result.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Back with what Dana said. If you're very confident in what you believe in, you should have no problems meeting with somebody. He's not confident in what he believes in.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But he's very comfortable meeting with Castro.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There you go.\par \par \uc2 Up next, 10 years since Trump's golden escalator ride.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: When Mexico sends its people, they're not sending their best. They're bringing drugs, they're bringing crime, they're rapists, and some I assume are good people.\par \par \uc2 I will build a great great wall on our Southern Border and I will have Mexico pay for that wall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: OK. 10 years ago today, President Trump rode down that golden escalator and announced his bid for the White House and changed the course of history.\par \par \uc2 Dana, as you -- as you think about that --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: -- and watch that visual what, there's got to be a lot going on here.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I saw him on the way here.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I was in a car on the way here and I remember turning the corner on 57th Street going, what a remarkable life. If you think about it. Like, what he has been able to do in 10 years to become a political icon, somebody who won unbelievably, and then came very close to winning a second time but basically had to deal with COVID, and then comes back to win again. And to basically say, I told you so. It is really a remarkable thing.\par \par \uc2 And I liked what Maggie Haberman of the New York Times said in an interview she did about this period, this 10-year period, is separating the signal from the noise. And one thing for me in the second term