is not all there. I mean that. I wasn't saying that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is not all there, you just said.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president says the opposite is true.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: He doesn't remember the call. He doesn't remember. I never spoke. And then, I produced a phone call. I had it. It took me about two minutes. I said, well, here is the call.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Anti-Trump protests are being organized in every state to draw coverage away from this weekend's military parade in Washington, D.C., celebrating the Army's anniversary, which also falls on the president's birthday. The protesters will march under the banner, No Kings.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What do they call it? No King?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No kings.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't feel like a king. I have to go through hell to get stuff approved.\par \par \uc2 A king would say, I'm not going to get this -- a king would have never had the California mandate to even be talking to him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): A judge actually just compared President Trump to a king a few minutes ago in a California courtroom, while hearing arguments about California's lawsuit to stop the National Guard from being deployed there. U.S. District Court Judge \uc2 Charles Breyer\uc2  said, how is that different than what a monarchist does? That's not where we live. This country was founded in response to a monarchy. Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Peter Doocy for us at the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles Mayor \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , says the curfew in parts of downtown L.A. will go on as long as they are needed. Meanwhile, the demonstrations have expanded across the country.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Los Angeles for us tonight. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: