ining. We could see them on the streets here tomorrow. Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Steve Harrigan, live for us. Thank you, Steve.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up in just a couple of minutes here, Democratic governors come to Capitol Hill to defend their sanctuary state policies to Republicans. Griff Jenkins will have that story next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): You are not advocating on behalf of these victims. You are shielding illegals. Even in your opening statement, you prioritized putting illegals first.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is not true. That is not true.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Rapes of young girls, beatings of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  cops?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And take a look at this. A second day of encouraging information about inflation sent stocks up. The Dow gained 102. The S&P 500 finished ahead 23. The NASDAQ rose 47.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a sole survivor in a horrific plane crash in India. We will have a report from there.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. The death toll rises to 57 from flooding in one of South Africa's poorest provinces. Officials say a lack of resources is paralyzing rescue efforts. Heavy rain caused a river to burst its banks in the pre-dawn hours of Tuesday, and then, floods swept away people, along with parts of their homes and their cars.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Biarritz, France. One of the big stories there tonight, is the helium-powered hot air balloon that lit up the French capital skyline during the 2024 Olympics makes a dramatic comeback to the gardens there. It is now called, the Paris Cauldron. It will stay in the gardens from June 21st to September 14th for a total of three years. That, of course, leads up to the 2028 Games in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Those are just some of the stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says his country has repatriated a new group of prisoners from Russian captivity. Video released by his office shows the released soldiers embracing their comrades and tearfully greeting their families. The exchange was conducted under an agreement reached in Turkey, and it brought home some soldiers who had been held for more than three years.\par \par \uc2 A passenger jet with more than 240 people aboard crashed this morning in India. It is believed that there was only one survivor. Several people on the ground were killed as well.\par \par \uc2 Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Searching for answers in India to the worst global aviation disaster in a decade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): I saw the crashed plane, many bodies lying.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): 242 on board, passengers, along with Indians, including British, Portuguese, Canadian, children and infants and a 12 person crew, all now confirmed dead, except for one Brit, who made it out alive.\par \par \uc2 It happened in the western Indian city of Ahmedabad, an \uc2 Air India\uc2 Boeing\uc2  787-8 Dreamliner leaving from the airport there, bound for London. Shortly after takeoff, a plane full of fuel issued a mayday warning, it reached 600 feet and then descended into a residential area, crashing in flames.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It looks like whether the plane was losing power and or the flaps weren't deployed, it is a low speed impact.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): There won't be certainty about the cause of the crash, at least until the plane's black boxes are found and analyzed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are going to go to the depth of why this incident has happened.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Among those injured on the ground, 