ity Correspondent Jennifer Griffin reports tonight from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Concern on Capitol Hill as lawmakers asked Defense Secretary Hegseth about the possibility that Israel could strike Iran if nuclear talks fail, after President Trump indicated such a strike is possible.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I don't want to say imminent, but it looks like it's something that could very well happen.\par \par \uc2 Look, it's very simple, not complicated. Iran cannot have a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Secretary Hegseth authorized the voluntary departure of U.S. service member dependents from across the Middle East, especially Bahrain, UAE and Kuwait.\par \par \uc2 REP. BRIAN MAST (R-FL): There's no question that it sends a message to the Ayatollah that the United States of America is preparing for what could happen.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The head of the \uc2 IDF\uc2  reportedly told Prime Minister Netanyahu that Israel cannot strike without coordinating with the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Will you stand up today and make it clear to Netanyahu that that escalation, what he's threatening just yesterday, is not in the American interest, that he should not be dragging America into war with Iran?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: What I know is that \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  is going to put his country first, and we're going to put our country first and we're positioned properly in the region.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Democrats pressed Hegseth about a DOD inspector general report that will determine whether Hegseth shared classified information on Signal ahead of U.S. military strikes in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): Do you plan to take any accountability for that?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: As I pointed out at the --\par \par \uc2 MOULTON: But you said tha