mocratic nomination in New York City's mayoral race will hold a debate, and it will feature former New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . This evening, we will hear from one of the women who accused Cuomo of sexual harassment. She talked with correspondent Bryan Llenas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY BOYLAN\uc2 , FORMER CUOMO AIDE: This is about as broad abuse of power. His abuse of women is just one manifestation of that. We cannot accept this abuser back to office.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, a former aide is warning New York City not to elect \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  mayor. \uc2 Lindsey Boylan\uc2  was the first of at least 11 women to accuse the former governor of sexual assault, leading to his resignation in 2021.\par \par \uc2 BOYLAN: The only reason why more women didn't come forward is because they were afraid.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Do you think there are many more women?\par \par \uc2 BOYLAN: I know there are many more women. I know.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Today, Cuomo is the frontrunner in the Democratic mayoral primary. His political ads tout him as the most experienced candidate to take on President Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trump is at the city gates. We need someone experienced to slam them shut.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: New York Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  is trying to slam the gates on Cuomo, endorsing Democratic Socialist Zohran Mamdani.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): And make sure you do not rank \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  on your ballot. Don't do it, folks.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: The 33-year-old socialist has surpassed in Cuomo in some polls. Mamdani wants to defund the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , and Jewish leaders accuse him of being antisemitic. Mamdani denies the accusation, but he refuses to recognize Israel as a Jewish state and supports pro-Palestinian protesters.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic and political establishment is now falling in line behind Cuomo. As for the allegations, Cuomo says he regrets resigning.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: Knowing thought facts, there was no factual basis to resign. It was all political, which is what I said from day one.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: His accusers say otherwise.\par \par \uc2 BOYLAN: I do not want this man running the city that my daughter lives in. I refuse to not fight against that. It's just unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): A state attorney general's investigation found the sexual harassment allegations against Cuomo credible. Tonight, a Cuomo spokesperson is calling Lindsey not credible, and also a bully, saying that she's essentially embarking on a revenge tour. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: We'll be watching this one. Bryan Llenas for us, thank you, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 Health and Human Services Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is reacting tonight to newly released documents about the assassination of his father, then Senator \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy\uc2 . The secretary tells Martha MacCallum the CIA documents that were just revealed, revealed details that he did not know about his father.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR., HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY: I didn't know that my father, during his trip to the Soviet Union in 1955 when he was a Senate aide, that he had been reporting back to the CIA. My mother was on that trip. She joined him in Saint Petersburg, and she actually was working for the CIA at that time, and she was wearing a carnation in her lapel that was attached to a little bubble where she could take pictures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Those documents also note that the CIA wanted to know about artwork in Saint Petersburg the Russians may have taken from Germany during World War II.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the incident involving California Senator \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2  and Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 ALEX PADILLA\uc2 , (D-CA): Senator Padilla. I have questions for the secretary.\par \par \uc2 This is how this administration responds to a senator with a question? You can only imagine what they are doing to farm workers, to cooks.\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: It wasn't becoming a U.S. senator or a public official, and perhaps he wanted the scene. But I think the American people are sick of those kinds of actions.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: This is not what democracies do. It's despicable. It's disgusting. It is so un-American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, let's bring in our panel, FOX News contributor Ben Domenech, Olivia Beavers of "The Wall Street Journal", and Democratic strategist Meghan Hays. And good evening to all of you. Thanks for joining us on a very busy evening of news.\par \par \uc2 Let's play the video -- unexpectedly. Let's play the video here that I think everyone is going to be analyzing the next couple days, including us here on the panel. And there are a couple different angles here, but we're going to look at it from this angle here. It looks like the senator shows up to this press conference unannounced, uninvited, begins interrupting, asking questions. And the Secret Service that's assigned to \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  removes him and cuffs him and puts him on the ground. Seems like a lot went wrong here.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Well, I think certainly this was a situation that went wrong from the perspective of a lot of different sides here. But one in particular that I just have to point out is I think that the senator wanted something to happen here. There's a reason why you don't start this situation by identifying yourself. There is a reason why you don't have in advance a conversation saying, hey, I'm going to be here at the same time \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  is here. I want to sit down and have a conversation with her about what's going on in California.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I don't think he knew she was going to be there.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: He claims that he didn't know that she just happened to be in the building at the same time for a press conference. He interrupts the press conference. You know, he does so without announcing who he actually is. And Secret Service, I think, is on -- quite frankly, they are on a little bit of alert here because of the way that ICE agents have been targeted, the way that the president himself has been targeted, the way that they feel the obligation to protect their principle.\par \par \uc2 And so I actually don't judge the agents in this situation for reacting the way they did to someone who they clearly did not recognize, who was not wearing anything identifying him.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: He was not wearing his Senate pin.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: And he was not wearing his Senate pin. And a little bit about that, Aishah, as you know. If you wear a pin that is round or similarly shaped as a man in the early days of a new Congress, you will frequently get called representative or congressman, as I have been on multiple occasions. They didn't realize who he was.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And maybe they just didn't know who he was. By the way, we did reach out to the senator's office to see if he had a comment and wanted to come on the show. They did not call us back. I just wanted to point that out. We tried.\par \par \uc2 Meghan, Secretary Noem today called this political theater, a lot of folks from the administration are calling it that. I want to ask you about that, because Democrats, this party has been trying to say that we need more fighters out there. We need people to go after this Trump administration, encouraging brawlers to primary incumbents. Are we going to see more of this? Is this the kind of thing we're going to continue to see, more disruptions and moments that wind up like this?\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Look, if you talk to the Democratic leaders in some of these blue states, they have reached out to the administration. They have reached out to DHS and \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , and they are not getting calls back. So if he had been in a federal building and go through security and all the other thing and knew that \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  was going to be there once he was waiting for a different briefing, he is taking the opportunity to ask a question. I don't fault him for doing that. This is a complete overreaction.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What do you think he thought was going to happen?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: I think he thought he was going to ask a question and there would an exchange back and forth, and he would leave. That is normally what would happen in the situation with protesters. I saw it when I worked for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . When he would get protested at events, the same thing would happen. He'd have a back and forth discussion and then he'd meet with them afterwards. And that is what happened after he was forcibly taken out and then handcuffed outside, which we have all seen the video of that as well.\par \par \uc2 I just think the American people deserve better than both of this, than all of this on both sides. The American people want different. This is not -- this is going to end in a really bad way, and I think that we need to figure out how to de-escalate this on both sides.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Olivia, I want to follow up on that, what she said. It's going to end in a really bad way. Obviously, Democrats on Capitol Hill were enraged today. There was a lot of tension between House Republicans, House Democrats, and then also in the over in the Senate. And they are demanding investigations although they are in the minority.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": They are in the minority, which means they are going to have a hard hill upwards. And Republicans, not in the Senate, but when you ask some Republicans, they said he was disruptive. It was his fault. Democrats argued that he did identify himself as a senator, and so there is a lot of confusion about what the agents knew and how they handled it.\par \par \uc2 But some Republican senators were also taken aback. I think there is something about an image of a U.S. senator being pushed to the ground and handcuffed that did not sit well with colleagues of his. And so, I don't know that you are going to an investigation. You had Mike Johnson saying that what Padilla was doing was censure-worthy as House Democrats passed by him and shouted you're lying. So, it definitely did not seem to de-escalate what is already a very escalating situation in L.A.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So today President Trump actually talked about how he might change his deportation policy for a select group of people. Watch this exchange with Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: -- change your mind about targeting in California farmers and people in the hotel and leisure business?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Our farmers are being hurt badly by -- they have very good workers that have worked for them for 20 years. They are not citizens, but they have turned out to be, you know, great. And we're going to have to do something about that. We can't take farmers and take all their people and send them back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Ben, these are the kinds of people that these protesters and Democrats are talking about trying to save from deportation.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, what they have been trying to highlight, of course, is people getting arrested who are waiting in line at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and the like. But I think the real reason that this administration has had the early experience with this that they have is that they decided to lean into the far more popular decision to arrest people who had criminal activity, either in the past or here in America, that could be something beyond just being here illegally. That's why they have had the kind of success politically in most polls that have you seen to this point.\par \par \uc2 The backlash that I think is coming is from these more sympathetic cases. That's what the Democrats are going to lean into. And how the White House responds to it, the president, quite frankly, there sounded more moderate than he has in the past when it comes to immigration enforcement. It will be interesting to see whether that leads so some sort of change in policy. At the same time, if \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  and others who are demanding more deportations faster from an effort that they believe has not gone fast enough.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Meghan, this should be welcoming, welcoming news for Democrats, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Right, and also the polling that came out today. Trump is upside down on immigration, and people are not thinking of it favorably. But to your point about policy change, this is what we need Congress to act on, both Democrats and Republicans. This has been an issue for 60 years. You cannot legislate immigration through executive order. It's not going to work. It did not work for the Biden administration. It will not work for the Trump administration. It gets too extreme each time. Congress needs to do something, and they need to work together to do it.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: And they won't.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Olivia, who is smirking right now. Congress getting to work seems, you know, far-fetched.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Republicans not too long ago thought they had a deal and agreement to move forward. Trump came out against it, said it didn't go far enough on immigration. It's really tough, it seems, these years for immigration legislation to move forward in a way that addresses some of the --\par \par \uc2 HAYS: A hundred percent. These people work for the American people, and if the American people start voting in people work for them, they can get something done.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: If you want to put this back to the ballot box test again in the midterms, I think you will have a similar experience that you had last time around.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: He is right about that. There are a lot of Americans out there that are OK with the current deportation policies.\par \par \uc2 Panel, stand by. We will have you back in just a few seconds here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Israel, Iran, and a possible strike on the Islamic Republic's nuclear facilities.\par \par \uc2 And then a look at last night's congressional baseball game.\par \par \uc2 But first, this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Wow, it was on June 12th of 1987 that president \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  challenged Soviet Leader \uc2 Mikhail Gorbachev\uc2  to tear down the Berlin wall which separated free West Germany from the communist controlled East Germany. Two-and-a-half years later the walls did come down, triggering the unification of Germany and the eventual dissolution of the Soviet Union. What a moment there.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How imminent is an Israeli strike on Iran?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Well, I don't want to say imminent, but it looks like it's something that could very well happen. Look, it's very simple, not complicated. Iran cannot have a nuclear weapon. Other than that, I want them to be successful. I want them to be -- we'll help them be successful. We'll trade with them. We'll do whatever is necessary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The president today warning of a potential strike on Iran by Israel. Things are getting very tense in that part of the region. We bring back our panel now to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Ben, this is how "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " puts it today on how serious this is getting. They write this, "The U.S. is concerned Israel's air defenses would not be able to handle an Iranian response involving hundreds of missiles. Such an attack, Witkoff told the group, could cause massive casualties and damage."\par \par \uc2 So, what does this White House -- what does this president and \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  need to do in the next few days here, these critical days to prevent this, and can they?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, I think they can. But I also think that there is a good degree of this that is a real test for \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  and his future within this administration. Just given how much the -- so much of his brief has been expanded during the early days of this administration. He certainly has been vocal about his own perspective on this, one that puts him alongside certain others within this administration, and it is one that is split in terms of their views on the nature of response to an Iran threat that is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 We have seen the lobbyists from outside, you know, people who are trying to influence what they do. But I really think that at this moment, you know, the president seems to be in the middle, kind of seeing things go back and forth in front of him. He ultimately is going to be the decider about what type of course we pursue and whether we are willing to take the risks that are involved in any kind of strike.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: He is in the middle of deal-making. He thinks he might be able to get this done before things get out of control in that region. This is Congressman Crow today criticizing the president's policies on Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2 , (D-CO) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: This administration rolled back the JCPOA, the deal that actually had us doing inspections, had international inspectors going in and checking that prevented Iran from having a nuclear program. They wiped that deal off the board. They have pursued what they said is a maximum pressure policy, which has completely failed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Meghan, what if there is a deal? What if there is a deal and this all goes away?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: That would be great. But, to his point, it's really ironic because this is very similar to the deal he already pulled out of once. So now we are just renegotiating what we already had. But Israel needs us -- needs the United States, I'm sorry, to move forward. And I think Trump is going to have to make a decision whether or not he wants to support that or whether he wants to continue down the deal path. And how long that deal will take I think is where we are at right now.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Olivia, it's very interesting what is happening within the GOP. There are two factions, those that are very MAGA loyalists that are trying to avoid any kind of war, any kind of tension or violence in that region, and then there are those that are Iran hawks, those that sort of almost are pressuring the president to green light, to OK an Israeli strike on Iran. What are you hearing on Capitol Hill between these two?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Like you said, the fissures in this sort of space in the GOP had been there and growing for a while. You had the isolations who want the U.S. to pull back. They don't want them to be sort of, being the dominant force determining -- getting involved in foreign wars. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , those are some of the names that come to mind.\par \par \uc2 And then you have the hawks who are very intense about charging after Iran and their proxy groups that have created terrorism issues and come after U.S. troops. That's Tom Cotton, those are some of the veterans that you have seen.\par \par \uc2 And this split also has been in the administration as welling. But there's different sides pushing the president to handle this differently, and we're going to see which one wins out.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Ben, who is the president listening to?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I think that the president has someone closest to him in his ear all the time about this, and that's J.D. Vance. And I think that his view is much more aligned with the Witkoff-ian view about the potential for a deal as opposed to aligned with the traditional hawks as it come to the Iran issue.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: We will continue watching it. Panel, thanks for joining us today on a very newsy day. We appreciate it. Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Pretty much played everywhere last year. Catcher. Known for making great contact. Oh! Robbed at third, what a play!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Republicans continuing their winning streak last night, beating Democrats 13 to two in that annual congressional baseball game at Nationals park. It was a beautiful night last night. Our own Chad Pergram and Kevin Corke, they were calling all of the action from the booth. Republicans have now won the past five games, four of them were blowouts. More than 30,000 fans filled the park, and that's what matters, because the game raised nearly $2.8 million for local charities. That is why they play. Great game.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Whatever Happened To?" segment returns on the attacks targeting \uc2 Tesla\uc2  vehicles and facilities.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if you can't catch us live, you can set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. eastern, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250613el6c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Small Business Administrator Kelly Loeffler\b0\par\par\uc2 Will Cain \par \uc2 1,166 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Will Cain Show\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It`s the moment everyone is talking about, Democrat Senator \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2  of California barging into DHS Secretary Kristi Noem`s press conference this afternoon and making a total scene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ALEX PADILLA (D-CA): Sir, hands off. Hands off.\par \par \uc2 I`m Senator \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2 . I have questions for the secretary, because the fact of the matter is, a half-a-dozen violent criminals that you`re rotating on your -- on your -- hands off!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On the ground. On the ground. On the ground. Hands behind your back. Hands behind your back.\par \par \uc2 PADILLA: If you would let me -- my hands go, I would put them behind my back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right. All right. All right. Cool.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey, move the hand. Lay flat. Lay flat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Other hand, sir. Other hand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s no recording allowed out here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right, I did not know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s no recording allowed out here, per \uc2 FBI\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You know when a man barges in unannounced and interrupts and start blurting out questions, this is the rational, expected response that you would get. It doesn`t matter that it`s a senator.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that`s not the way you conduct oversight, Senator.\par \par \uc2 But here is what Senator Padilla had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PADILLA: If this is how this administration responds to a senator with a question, if this is how the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  responds to a senator with a question, you can only imagine what they`re doing to farmworkers, to cooks, to day laborers out in the Los Angeles community and throughout California and throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 We will hold this administration accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: But this wasn`t a Q&A, and that wasn`t a question designed to get an answer. It was a stunt designed to create a scene.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  expressing much of that same sentiment you just heard -- quoting -- "If they can handcuff a U.S. senator for asking a question, imagine what they will do to you."\par \par \uc2 Noem`s response is, if you wanted to, if you wanted to talk, you could have asked.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM, U.S. HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: I don`t even know the senator. He did not request a meeting with me or to speak with me. So when I leave here, I will have a conversation with him and visit and find out really what his concerns were.\par \par \uc2 I think everybody in America would agree that that wasn`t appropriate, that if you wanted to have a civil discussion, especially as a leader, a public official, that you would reach out and try to have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Noem keeping a word, meeting with the senator for about 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Padilla expressed his concerns about ICE operations and the two opened a line of communication. Seems like that could have been the way it had gone from the beginning without the show.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is former Georgia Senator and current Small Business Administrator Kelly Loeffler.\par \par \uc2 Administrator, thank you for being with us here today.\par \par \uc2 What is your response? You saw that just unfold earlier today. You by now perhaps have seen the video and seen the reaction on both sides.\par \par \uc2 KELLY LOEFFLER, ADMINISTRATOR, SMALL BUSINESS ADMINISTRATION: Well, this is political theater at its worst.\par \par \uc2 And it`s in sharp contrast to what I just saw with my former Senate colleagues. I just came from a meeting with the Republican Caucus, where they were trying to solve problems for the American people, talking about cutting taxes for hardworking Americans, for small businesses, for protecting the entitlement programs for those who need it, for securing our border to prevent problems like this in the first place.\par \par \uc2 This political theater is classic playbook of \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . The American people voted strongly against it November 5. And they have also said that they want this tax cut passed. They want border security. They want opportunity for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 And the Democrats are busy fighting for illegal criminal aliens, while Republicans are fighting for the American people, for small businesses and hardworking Americans that deserve this tax cut, while Democrats vote against it.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, speaking of small businesses, we have begun to hear out, hear from some of the small businesses in Los Angeles who have been experiencing these riots.\par \par \uc2 I want to share with you a little bit of sound from small business owners who have suffered, suffered in L.A.\par \par \uc2 Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is so ridiculous. This doesn`t look like this is for -- they`re protesting for ICE or anything. This is that they`re doing just for the looting the stores and everything.\par \par \uc2 I saw them break into the Apple Store. They break into the Adidas store. This is not they`re doing protests.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very bad. I`m not a big guy. I`m not a big fish.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re sick and tired of it. We have no one in charge. I would go so far as to say we have children in charge of Los Angeles right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: So, Administrator, you hear there from words of the small businesses in L.A.\par \par \uc2 I understand that you have some figures to share with us today about how much damage has been done.\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Will, we estimate on the ground, our disaster response team at the SBA, that $1 billion of damage has been done to businesses in that area where those violent riots are taking place.\par \par \uc2 Governor Newsom has made a strategic decision at every step of this process to stand with criminal illegals, instead of standing with ICE, instead of standing with our brave men and women and police officers, instead of standing with small businesses. He`s refused to ask for a disaster declaration that would support the recovery of these small businesses you just heard from who`ve been looted, who`ve been vandalized.\par \par \uc2 And we have got to make sure that we hold Governor Newsom accountable for - - and make sure his residents know he`s not fighting for them.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, a billion dollars, that`s a stunning figure, and it`s sad for all these small businesses.\par \par \uc2 But, Administrator Loeffler we appreciate you being with us here today. Thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Thank you, will.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020250612el6c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Small Business Administrator Kelly Loeffler\b0\par\par\uc2 Will Cain \par \uc2 1,226 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Will Cain Show\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  not a fan of the big, beautiful bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, DEPARTMENT OF GOVERNMENT EFFICIENCY: Disappointed to see the massive spending bill, frankly, which increases the budget deficit, not decrease it, and undermines the work that the DOGE team is doing.\par \par \uc2 I think a bill can be big or it can be beautiful.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MUSK: But I don`t know if it could be both.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: The head of DOGE miffed that his spending cuts weren`t included in President Trump`s massive domestic policy package that passed in the House, saying it will only increase our deficit.\par \par \uc2 Musk and DOGE going through the federal government books with a fine- toothed comb, including the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2 , DOGE identifying over $630 million in fraudulent loans there, so what happens now?\par \par \uc2 To help answer that question, joining us here in Dallas is Small Business Administrator and former Georgia Senator Kelly Loeffler.\par \par \uc2 Administrator, great to have you on the show.\par \par \uc2 KELLY LOEFFLER, ADMINISTRATOR, SMALL BUSINESS ADMINISTRATION: Great to be with you, Will. Congrats on this incredible show.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Thank you. Congratulations on your new position.\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: We`re curious how something like that works. So DOGE comes in, finds $630 million in inappropriate loans. Then what happens at the SBA?\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Well, we get to work. We`re investigating fraud.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had shifted it under the rug and wanted to forgive fraudulent loans. We`re getting back to holding people accountable for fraud in this agency, and that`s certainly something that`s just part of what DOGE is helping with.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just fraud. It`s waste and abuse, and we found billions at this agency, which, by the way, will be made permanent. We are getting rid of $3 billion in wasteful contracts. And what I`m excited about is, we can do that. We can make the DOGE cuts permanent, and we can have the big, beautiful bill, which is really brilliant in terms of what it does, because it cuts $1.6 trillion in wasteful spending, in mandatory spending, and then we will also have the DOGE cuts on top of it.\par \par \uc2 And we know that President Trump supports both getting rid of the mandatory spending, as well as the DOGE cuts, which are so important.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: But it`s good to hear from you in your situation, and perhaps a couple of other departments, that DOGE cuts can come in addition to the legislation.\par \par \uc2 So even though they weren`t codified in the big, beautiful bill, that doesn`t mean those cuts aren`t happening at the SBA.\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: They`re happening. They`re ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m a former businessperson. I was in the private sector for 30 years. I know how to run a business. We`re going to run the SBA like a business and do what any small business person would do, which is, we`re going to do more with less. We owe that to the American taxpayer. We owe it to small businesses to refocus on small business.\par \par \uc2 This agency had gotten away from focusing on Main Street, and focusing on DEI, Green New Deal, and we`re back. This agency, it`s a new day, and we`re focused back on small businesses.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, and you`re visiting Main Street while you`re here in Texas. I know you were over in Fort Worth visiting manufacturing plants and other small businesses.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I had Dave Ramsey, who`s very close to small businesses, on the program. And I asked him. I took that opportunity to say, many people are talking about tariffs, and they`re worried about the impact on small businesses. If I open up The Wall Street Journal, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , that`s where they feel like tariffs will be felt first and the most, small businesses that are having trouble importing goods they need.\par \par \uc2 Are you finding that as you connect with small businesses?\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Well, small business finally has a champion in the White House in President Trump. He is standing up for small businesses and for the workers across America.\par \par \uc2 We lost five million jobs, 70,000 factories over the last three decades. President Trump believes in Main Street. He believes in the American worker and industry, and he`s bringing that back. And what I`m finding as I go across the country, small businesses, manufacturers, specifically, they want those tax cuts made permanent.\par \par \uc2 They want a skilled work force. They want deregulation. They want the entire Trump agenda of pro-growth initiatives. They`re going to turn this country around like we have never seen in modern history.\par \par \uc2 And so small businesses are grateful. They feel the wind in their sails from President Trump. And we`re going to continue to push that at this SBA that we can make things in America again, despite the liberal media narrative.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I hope that`s true because we both know the small businesses are the backbone of this American economy.\par \par \uc2 I`m glad to have you on. We are about to, right after you leave, do a segment on what`s been going on in the WNBA. It`s a fascinating story. It involves race. It involves their biggest stars when it comes to Caitlin Clark and Angel Reese.\par \par \uc2 And the WNBA just came back with an investigation into allegations of their fans chanting racist things at various players. The investigation revealed no evidence that that ever took place. But you have a deep connection to the WNBA. You have been a part of some controversies when it comes to the WNBA.\par \par \uc2 I`m just curious about your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Well, Will, I was a team owner for a decade. And it`s -- the WNBA continues to step on a rake when it comes to Caitlin Clark, who is their billion-dollar player. I mean, she`s increased ratings beyond all expectations.\par \par \uc2 It`s incredible to see what she`s done, but now to the point of they`re not only going after Caitlin Clark. They`re going after the fans of the team that she plays for in that investigation. And, look, fans want sports to be about sports. They want sportsmanship. They want to see the thrill of victory, the agony of defeat.\par \par \uc2 And this league continues to kneecap itself when it comes to really promoting what could be a great sport and reverting back to social justice issues that were proven to be an abject failure for the league in 2020 and continue to be.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 All right, Administrator Loeffler, it`s great to see you here in Texas. We wish you the best of luck at the SBA.\par \par \uc2 LOEFFLER: Thank you so much. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020250528el5s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Judge Blocks Trump's Ban On Harvard Students Visas; Trump Threatens Tariffs On \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Samsung\uc2 ; ICE Agents Foil Judge Dugan's Dirty Deed; Virginia Trans Policy Faces Outrage Over New Video; New Video Shows Trans Student Films Boys In Locker Room; MAHA Versus The Media; CNN Gets Schooled On Real Science; RFK Jr. Brings Real Science Back; Obama Bro Says He Wanted Biden To Win; David Hogg Says Crockett Is The Leader The Democrats Need; Chicago Mayor Brags About Hiring Black Staff; Report Shows Widespread CCP Infiltration At Stanford; Stanford Has Gotten $183 Million From China; Report Says China Gave $175 Billion To U.S. Schools In 2024.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Lisa Boothe, Christina Coleman, Nicole Saphier \par \uc2 7,850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: We put a lot of stuff in there. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts now.\par \par \uc2 LISA BOOTHE, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Lisa Boothe in for Laura Ingraham, and this is a special edition of The Ingraham Angle. Thanks for joining us tonight. Tonight, MAHA battles with the liberal media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., SECRETARY, U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: One of the big mistakes that you and many other -- your media colleagues made during COVID -- COVID is to try to convince the American people that they should trust the experts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Plus, can you hear me now?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's a phone call. Do you mind. It's only a congressman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: It's only a congressman. That was awesome. All right, and an Obama bro reveals well, he covered for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the candidate, I want him to (inaudible) win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: But first, the robed resistance getting a workout. A federal judge today pausing the Trump administration's move to cancel Harvard's student visa program. This coming after Harvard sued last night over the policy. Obama appointee judge Allison Burroughs granted its request for a temporary restraining order.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Billions of dollars has been paid to Harvard. How ridiculous is that? Billions. And they have $52 billion as an endowment. They have $52 billion and this country is paying billions and billions of dollars and then give student loans, and they have to pay back the loan, so Harvard is going to have to change its ways.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why did you not want the best and brightest from around the world to --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- come to Harvard.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I do. But a lot of the people need remedial math. Did you see that? Where the students can't add two and two, and they go to Harvard? How can somebody that can't add or has very basic skills, how do they get into Harvard? Why are they there? And then you see the same people picketing and screaming at the United States and screaming at, you know, they're antisemitic, or they're something. We don't want troublemakers here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: I mean, isn't that just common sense, good old common sense. Now, this is all part of the President's crackdown on antisemitism on college campuses and other schools, better take notice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTI NOEM, SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Harvard brought these consequences upon themselves. They have promoted and allowed violent activity on campus. They have allowed antisemitism participation with CCP and Chinese infiltration and influence on their campus, and they haven't protected their students. This should be a warning to every other university to get your act together. Get your act together, because we are coming to make sure that these programs that you are facilitating in an environment where students can learn, where they're safe and that they're not discriminated against based on their race or their religion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: You heard her, get your act together. All right with us now, William Jacobson, law professor at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 ; and \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2 , Vice President of Domestic Policy. Now, both are graduates of Harvard Law. So it makes this interesting. You know, Roger, I want to start with you, so the federal government has broad authority over immigration and visa programs. So why did this judge issue this temporary restraining order?\par \par \uc2 ROGER SEVERINO\uc2 , THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION: Well, it's Trump derangement syndrome. We have this over and over again where district court judges want to take over the United States immigration system, the United States foreign policy, what's next? National Defense? And they just are snapping too, to -- it's really because Trump is trying to enforce a law here. Students are here as guests from abroad. If they come here to not to learn, especially to do anti-American activity and especially antisemitic violence, they do not belong here, and that's what this case is about.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: No, absolutely. And Professor, I want to get you on this, because it's not just these judges that have Trump derangement syndrome. It's also the Democrats in Congress as well. So, senators Markey and Blumenthal, well, they're very concerned about what will happen to poor little Harvard. Let's watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY (D-MA): This is authoritarianism on steroids. This is an attempt to intimidate universities across our nation. We have to fight every single day against this dictator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Very serious escalation of pressure on Harvard of the most pernicious kind, because what they're doing essentially is using antisemitism as a pretext to crack down on Harvard's academic independence and freedom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: So, Professor, is this authoritarianism on steroids, or is he just standing up for Jewish students?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM JACOBSON, HARVARD LAW SCHOOL GRADUATE: Well, I think he's standing up for our country more than anything, and Jewish students and other students on campus. The campuses have turned into riot zones, the campuses have turned into takeovers, and a lot of it is fomented by the faculty and by outside groups. And Harvard, notoriously has failed to react to it. We remember how disastrous the prior President was in congressional hearings.\par \par \uc2 And so, there's a huge cultural gap that people are looking at in the country and saying, you know, Harvard has turned into a bubble, which really, in many ways, opposes the country. And, you know, there was a book written in 1978 called Harvard hates America. And it's still true, and people sense it. And people say Harvard can do whatever it wants, but we don't have to pay for it. That's the issue.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Roger, I want to get you on this as well, so I know that you were the Director of the HHS's Office for Civil Rights during the first Trump administration. So, now investigative findings from that office alleged that Columbia's handling of student on student harassment of Jewish students over the past 19 months violated Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964. \uc2 HHS\uc2  alleged that Columbia continually failed to protect Jewish students by allowing the hostile environment created by some of its students to continue.\par \par \uc2 So, what legal obligations does \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  have under civil rights laws to protect its students and prevent harassment?\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: If you receive federal funds, you can't discriminate, and you certainly can't create Jewish Exclusion Zones, as we saw with UCLA. A federal judge found that very recently. This is ridiculous. Campus after campus has declared no go zones for Jewish students, and in the first Trump administration, we started the antisemitism efforts. This has been going on for quite some time. This is long overdue, and now you had the tragic murder of two people in D.C., because of their Jewish association, right?\par \par \uc2 This is what happens when you have this fomenting of unrest, of dissension, calls for violence on these campus, you start radicalizing people. We should not be funding these institutions. Harvard could do just fine with this $52 billion endowment. There shouldn't be the woke ideology being funded by the American taxpayer anymore, because people are being hurt, and in this case, antisemitic violence. In other cases, Asian students and white students were being barred from a quota system that Harvard imposed on admissions.\par \par \uc2 And now, the American people have said enough is enough with President Trump, and they're taking action.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Right. You know, Professor, as Roger pointed out, in the backdrop of all these antisemitic protests and of that horrific murder of those two Israeli embassy workers that he mentioned, we see this activist Rebecca Jones speaking out in favor of what happened two nights ago. Now, she was upset that the murdered man posted online in support of his own country. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He was a monster representing a foreign, adversarial government currently conducting a holocaust, and they want us to feel sorry for him. Even if I hadn't looked at his profile, I would have said that representatives of a genocidal country should not feel safe in America, because we're supposed to stand in opposition to that. And now, having seen it, good (inaudible) riddance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Now, Professor, where is the line between free speech, hate speech, and incitement?\par \par \uc2 JACOBSON: Well, people are allowed to say ugly things. They're allowed to say hateful things, as long as they don't act on it in a physical way. But what we've seen on the campuses is not speech. We've seen actions. We've seen intimidation. We've seen checkpoints. We've seen building takeovers. We've seen trashing of buildings. We've seen threats, and of course, in D.C., we just saw an assassination.\par \par \uc2 People need to understand that when they attack the Jewish students, when they attack antisemitism -- when they engage in antisemitism, that is a reflection of a much deeper problem on the campuses. The guy from Columbia (ph) who's trying to be deported and he's fighting it; he ran a group whose stated goal was the destruction of Western civilization. In almost every one of these protests, when they burn an Israeli flag, they also burn an American flag. So what you're seeing on campuses at its core is anti- Americanism, anti-Westernism, which is playing out as antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Well, you know, and of course, when these students call for the global Intifada, or from the river to the sea. That's what they wanted. What happened in Washington, D.C.? Professor and Roger, thank you so much. We appreciate your insight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We want to make our cars. It's a phone call, do you mind. OK. It's only a congressman. It's a different congressman. They're all congratulating us. It's lucky, it is OK. Let's go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: I'm obsessed with that. He's just like us. Also, this happened to me during COVID when I was on TV. I'm sure everyone experienced that. All right. Well, so iPhone users, this one's for you. President Trump threatening \uc2 Apple\uc2  with a 25 percent tariff for anything not made in the United States. But don't get too excited, you green text message. Android users, you might be on the hook too.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On \uc2 Apple\uc2 , you said this morning that if they don't make their iPhones in the U.S., you're going to hit them with a 25 percent tariff.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you have the power to tariff one single company? And why would you want to put an American company in that way?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It would be more. It would be also \uc2 Samsung\uc2  and anybody that makes that product. Otherwise it wouldn't be fair. So, anybody that makes that product, and that'll start on, I guess, the end of June, when they build their plant here, there's no tariff, so they're going to be building plants here. But I had an understanding with him that he wouldn't be doing this. He said he's going to India to build plants. I said, that's OK to go to India, but you're not going to sell into here without tariffs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Joining me now is John Carney, Breitbart Economics and Finance Editor. John, let's start with the most important question, who do you think was calling the president?\par \par \uc2 JOHN CARNEY, BREITBART ECONOMICS EDITOR: You know, maybe it was \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  pranking him, being like, OK, you're going to raise my tariff. I'm going to call you at the most embarrassing moment you can.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: So first it was \uc2 Apple\uc2 , you know, and then later he sort of grouped in more smartphone companies. Why \uc2 Apple\uc2  initially do you think with that, that Truth post earlier today?\par \par \uc2 CARNEY: I think it really was exactly what President Trump said. He feels like he has a good relationship with \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 . He felt the trade when it turned out that \uc2 Apple\uc2  had no plans to build in the United States. No plans to make iPhones in the United States, that if they got out of China, maybe they'd go to India, but they hadn't even really seriously considered, can we build this in the U.S.? And so, Trump is saying, look, we -- it's not like India or China has some natural ability to do better at making iPhones. We can make those here, and we should be making them here. And so, if you're not going to do it, that's fine. You'll just have to pay a much higher tariff.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: You know, the President was also asked about tariffs on the EU today. Let's watch this clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you looking for a deal in nine days? Will you be able to do that, sir?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm not looking for a deal. I mean, we've set the deal. It's at 50 percent, but again, there is no tariff if they build their plant here. Now, if somebody comes in and wants to build a plant here, I can talk to them about a little bit of a delay.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: John, what do you think he wants from the EU?\par \par \uc2 CARNEY: So the EU is a, basically a protectionist club. It has free trade within the EU, but everybody else faces a, almost unimaginably complex web of non-tariff barriers and tariffs. What he wants them to do is figure out why and how they keep out U.S. products and start letting them in. Europe has not been negotiating in good faith. They didn't when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president in his first term. They're not doing it now. He's saying to -- and part of it is a structural problem. There are so many different countries in Europe. To get them all on the same page, negotiating in the same way is very difficult.\par \par \uc2 So, he's saying to them, get your act together. If you don't get your act together, if you don't come to us with a good trade deal, you're going to all face a 50 percent tariff. Stop messing around with each other. Come here, and give us a good deal, and then the tariff will come down. Or if you can't do that, by the way, if you are a European manufacturer and your country can't get its act together, the second solution is, build your plant in the U.S. You don't have to export from Europe. You can just build it here in the U.S., and you'll avoid all of those tariffs.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: And the President certainly holding everyone accountable. John, we appreciate you. Thank you. Ahead, brand new video reveals how an activist judge try to sneak an illegal immigrant out of court. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Another example of the robed resistance, but now caught on camera. The Angle brought you the story as it happened, an activist judge in Milwaukee allegedly helping an illegal alien accused of assault evade arrest by ICE agents. The new footage released by Milwaukee County in response to an open records request shows two plainclothes ICE agents waiting outside the judge's courtroom to identify an arrest, Eduardo Flores-Ruiz, Judge Hannah Dugan got word of their presence and came out to confront them.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to the complaint, the agents and judge disagreed over the type of warrant needed for the arrest, so Judge Dugan sent them to the Chief Judge's office, then she went further down the hallway where more plainclothes agents were waiting, and sent them to the same -- or same office.\par \par \uc2 At this point, the complaint says the judge escorted Flores-Ruiz and his attorney through the jury door into a non-public area of the courthouse. The illegal alien is then seen on camera walking through the hallway and out the jury door. But guess what? The judge didn't find all the ICE agents. They follow Flores-Ruiz all the way outside. And he takes off running, but they nabbed him, and Judge Dugan's nasty scheme failed.\par \par \uc2 She's now facing federal charges for obstruction of proceedings and for concealing a person to prevent arrest. Here to react to this crazy story is Jonathan Fahey, former Acting ICE Director; and Rich Zeoli, host of The Rich Zeoli Show. You know, Jonathan, let's start with this. So the judge here pleaded not guilty, but she's on camera, so how is that going to work for her, you tell me/\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN FAHEY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, yes, that standard typically for defendant to plead not guilty. But these -- the video really shows some appalling behavior on a part -- part of this judge. And what's really amazing about this, and it doesn't get probably enough attention, is this person was facing three assault charges in which two people were hospitalized as a result of this, so this judge walked him out the door knowing he wasn't coming back to her court or anywhere else, because he knew he had an ICE warrant out for him.\par \par \uc2 So, it's really absolutely outrageous that she did this. And what's even more outrageous is the Democratic Party, by and large, is supporting her illegal behavior. And it's really another example of the Democratic Party, this judge, in particular, putting the safety of their citizens behind the interests of illegal aliens. But the whole thing is appalling, and her defense is probably going to be, she's going to file -- she filed a judicial immunity motion, but that's ultimately going to fail and she's really got to face the consequences of her actions, I believe.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: You know, and Rich, just to reiterate, you know, it's important to note that she may have even obstructed justice to the victims of Flores- Ruiz's three misdemeanor battery charges, she allegedly chose not to hold a hearing for him and kept his victims awaiting in the courthouse. So, I can't wrap my head around this. So, the judge here -- you have victims, alleged victims of domestic abuse, waiting in the courtroom to get their justice. But then she chooses the illegal alien that she sees as a victim. So, how do Democrats identify who a victim is these days.\par \par \uc2 RICH ZEOLI, HOST OF THE RICH ZEOLI SHOW: Yes, well, the Democrats are always on the side of the illegal alien in every instance, right? They don't care about the victims. We saw this with the Laken Riley Act. They just don't care. But this obstruction by this judge, very similar to the Democrats in Newark, New Jersey, right? Obstructing ICE agents, shoving ICE agents. The mayor of Newark wanted to get arrested. Lisa, they're doing this. They want this confrontation. They want to be the resistance and these judges think what, I have a black robe, I can do whatever I want. I'm a member of Congress; I can do whatever I want.\par \par \uc2 No, this is their whole idea. They want to be famous, and they're getting famous, right? They're getting all this media attention. They're making money. They're raising money off of this. The reality is, the Democrat Party, if they're going to die on this Hill, they're going to keep dying, because the American people do not want to stand with criminal illegal aliens. They want to stand with the victims and their families, and they want justice, and they see this is what it is, Lisa, political theater.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: And just to tee this up for the audience at home, this is so bad that the illegal alien allegedly strangled one of the victims. That's how bad this domestic abuse, you know, was and took place.\par \par \uc2 All right, so Jonathan, the judge randomly assigned to Judge Dugan's case is Lynn Adelman. In 2020, he wrote in the Harvard Law Review, ripping into the hard Right Supreme Court. And he said Trump has the temperament of an autocrat. And that Trump is disinclined to buck the wealthy individuals and corporations who control his party. So, I feel like all of this with both of these judges just undercut this broader concern from conservatives that these judges are so biased and incapable of doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 FAHEY: Yes, it really does raise that concern when a judge makes those sorts of public statements, it does show, can they really be fair and impartial if they're so politically motivated and I think that's a concern in this case, particularly with the judicial immunity motion that that this judge has filed. Fortunately for the Trump administration, if this judge rules against him on that motion, they'll be able to appeal it to a higher court and ultimately they're going to prevail, because no serious court is going to say a judge has immunity to obstruct justice and sneak criminal defendants, violent criminal defendants, out the courthouse door. It's an utterly absurd motion, and that's really her core defense at this point.\par \par \uc2 But I think the overall point is really scary is the Democrats have gone from defaming law enforcement, defaming ICE to now essentially sanctioning violence in Newark, and sanctuary obstructing justice here in Milwaukee, and it really is a troubling trend. And just in the last -- just this year, the assaults on ICE agents have gone up, I think, 413 percent and really a lot of the rhetoric contributes to this.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Yes, and Rich, to Jonathan's point, I mean speaking of these Democrats protecting criminal illegals, Congressman Glenn Ivey of Maryland will travel to El Salvador to check on Kilmar Abrego Garcia, and in a lengthy statement he said this, or his office said this, that he is going to meet with leaders, officials, and hopefully my constituent, this President and administration needs to prove their assertions under oath and in court.\par \par \uc2 Last time I checked an illegal alien is not a constituent. Am I missing something here?\par \par \uc2 ZEOLI: Yes. No, you're not missing anything. And the question I have is it going to be Margarita or Skinny Margarita with Kilmar Abrego Garcia. These democrats look like fools, Lisa. They're going down to El Salvador to try to bring back to America a criminal, and we have enough criminals in this country. The purpose of immigration, as you know, is to bring in people who America needs. We don't need more wife beaters, we don't need more domestic abusers, we don't need more criminals. We have plenty. And the American people want these people gone.\par \par \uc2 The fact that a Democrat would choose to travel to another country to try to get a citizen of another country back into this country who happens to be a criminal shows you just how crazy the Left has become. I mean, they are so out of touch with the American people, who, in their right mind, would sit around at a bar and go, yes, you know what? This is definitely what you guys should do, go and bring a criminal back to the United States of America. We applaud you. We stand with you. They are so completely out of touch the American people.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: For the record, I would do a Skinny Margarita --\par \par \uc2 ZEOLI: Would you do a Skinny, you don't need.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: It's almost summertime, you know, you've got a -- got to say trim. I would not have it with a, you know, alleged MS-13 guy in El Salvador.\par \par \uc2 ZEOLI: Fair point, but (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: I would have with ICE agent, with you guys, after show. Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 ZEOLI: All right, just checking.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Yes. All right. We covered the important things. We did, then we got a little off the road. All right, Jonathan and Rich, thank you so much. Appreciate your time. All right, coming up, RFK Jr. schools the liberal media. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Welcome back. Now an update to a story the Angle brought you earlier this month. Three high school boys under investigation after complaining about a biological girl, who identifies as a boy, being in their locker room and filming them. Yes. You heard me right. The boys are being investigated, not the girl who violated their space. You can't make this stuff up.\par \par \uc2 So, we've just got our hands on that video, and Fox's Christina Coleman is here to break it all down. Hi, Christina. What do we need to know?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi Lisa. Yes. The trans identifying student recorded the encounter. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: The district allows students to use restrooms and locker rooms that align with their gender identity. The attorney representing the three boys does not believe they did anything wrong.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We don't see anything, even on the face of this that is legitimate. Just the fact that they recognize that there's a girl in their bath -- in their locker room, was apparently enough to get them investigated for sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Tensions definitely ran high during a school board meeting, Tuesday, regarding this matter. The mom of one of the boys under investigation pointed out that there's a policy that prohibits recording video in the locker rooms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The disappointing piece of this is this person is still allowed to use the locker room. And, you know, to me, after an egregious offense of recording people in the locker room, I feel like you should lose your privileges.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: The governor and state's attorney general are investigating how Loudoun County Public Schools handles this situation. In a statement, the school district said it would not investigate students based on personal beliefs alone, quote, "provided those expressions do not violate policies prohibiting hate speech, discriminatory language, threats, or other forms of harmful or disruptive conduct." Lisa?\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: And, Christina, Loudoun County is no stranger to this type of controversy relating to transgender issues, are they?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: No. They are not. You know, we're definitely familiar with the case that occurred last year. And, again, I mean, when you look at this video, you hear the audio there. It sounds as those -- as though those boys are just recognizing the fact that there is a biological female in their locker room, and they were reacting to it.\par \par \uc2 But, again, this has landed them in the center of investigation in a county that has drawn national attention over this matter. And so here we are again, and we will see how it all plays out.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Same school, same county. Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Crazy world we live in. So, \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary RFK Jr. is making America healthy again by getting to the root of American sickness, and part of that is by calling out liberal media's lies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: One of the big mistakes that you and many other, your media colleagues made during COVID is to try to convince the American people that they should trust the experts. Trusting the experts is not a feature of science. It's not a feature of democracy. It's a feature of religion and totalitarianism. The -- what we should do is trust the science.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The message was to trust the science and what studies were finding and what works.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That was a bad message. Try -- no, it was trust the experts. That's what they -- that's what you kept telling us. Trust the experts.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I don't know that I ever said that. It shows -- actually --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: -- they were saying it every day.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We took the briefings live every day with all of the experts such as Dr. Fauci --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I can tell you what President Trump did right and what President Biden did wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Joining me now is Dr. Nicole Saphier, Fox News contributor and my friend, that wasn't in the prompter. I added that. Dr. Saphier, I love having you on. I appreciate you making the time tonight.\par \par \uc2 So, we got really close during COVID. We bonded over the insanity that was going on in the country, and we wanted common sense to prevail. It finally has, but it's taken a while. I get what the secretary is saying in this interview. But what we saw during COVID is that sometimes both the experts and the science can be wrong. So, who do we trust in that environment?\par \par \uc2 DR. NICOLE SAPHIER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, so first of all, Lisa -- and yes, you're my friend as well. I think it's really important to say that that's the most important thing that we get in this entire message. I think it's really important to say that the experts were wrong undoubtedly, but science is never actually wrong. Science is just ever evolving. It's not a monolith.\par \par \uc2 And so, especially when it came to COVID, we were getting information by the -- not by the day, but by the hour, maybe even by the minute, and it was actually very difficult to report from that.\par \par \uc2 But here's the biggest hiccup that happened during COVID. You had two different things happening. You had people like me who we had no idea what was going on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, initially, I was saying, you know, maybe we should be wiping down packages. Maybe we should be staying home. Maybe we should be doing some of these things that the public health experts at the CDC and the \uc2 NIH\uc2  and even the \uc2 WHO\uc2  were saying.\par \par \uc2 But after a very short amount of time, things started to get a little tricky. It didn't seem like anything was changing, although we were getting more information. We knew it was a coronavirus, which is a respiratory virus, which means that contract transmission, like on packages is not really a thing.\par \par \uc2 So, all of a sudden, maybe I wipe down packages for 48 hours, and then I was like, OK, guys. Never mind. We shouldn't be wiping down packages. This doesn't make any sense.\par \par \uc2 But the problem you had -- so people like me who has no problem admitting when they're wrong, because let me tell you, I can -- I'm wrong a good amount of the time. I mean, that's just what being human is all about.\par \par \uc2 But the good thing about someone who is in the health field, who is a person of science like myself is, is that as data comes in, as research evolves, we then change our recommendations. And one of the biggest problems or missteps that we saw during the COVID pandemic was that you had these other talking heads or these other so-called experts that even as the data evolved, they did not change their recommendations.\par \par \uc2 I mean, one of the most blatant examples is \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , former CDC director under President Biden. Before she became CDC director, in emails that she was sending to local school districts before she became the CDC director, when they were asking about spacing with kids and how far-apart they should be, she was saying three-feet's probably enough.\par \par \uc2 Well, as soon as she became CDC director, she just hopped on board with whatever the talking points were where, OK, now we're doing six feet and therefore, because the teachers union were so involved in that, so intricately involved, they're like, never mind. Kids can't come to school.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: In public school districts because we can't do the six feet. So, it wasn't about science. It was just about public perception. And then --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Yeah. And to your point, there was also -- I mean, early on in the pandemic, we had those antibody studies from both Stanford. I think there was one done at \uc2 Miami University\uc2 , as well as here in New York, showing us that a broader portion of the population had COVID, which would mean that the fatality rate was much less than what they were telling us, and we still didn't adapt our strategies.\par \par \uc2 Or you can fast forward to the vaccines and there was a, you know, there was a bunch of -- a cluster of cases in Provincetown, Massachusetts, I believe it was July of 2021, and 75 percent of the cases were vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Yet they still push the fact that the vaccines would stop the spread of COVID despite the CDC being fully aware of these breakthrough cases in Provincetown, Massachusetts. So, I think that was a mutual frustration. To your point, even when we knew better, they still lied to us. They didn't adjust policy.\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: Lisa, you have actually become a brilliant journalist throughout COVID, and you absolutely hit the nail on the head. So, when we talk about science, we talk about ever evolving science, science can be flawed. Research can be flawed, especially when it's produced by the industry. We had a lot of industry produced science that came out of COVID.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that they continued to do was they continued to not give us the big