ity\uc2 . What could they be looking for and how dangerous is this, Gordon?\par \par \uc2 GORDAN CHANG, GATESTONE INSTITUTE SENIOR FELLOW: They're looking for a couple things, Laura. First of all, they would like to know about research at Stanford. They want to know where priorities are, what's going to be done next, and of course the fruits of that research. But they also want information so that they can coerce students in the future.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, you know, generally what they want to do is make sure that students stay in line while they're in the U.S. So, there's all sorts of things of monitoring and surveilling that the Ministry of State Security and Chinese consular officials actually engage in.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I know the Trump administration is all, you know, into making a deal with China and, you know, we'll see how that all develops ultimately when all the details are come out. But this stands as a real threat, I think, to American security.\par \par \uc2 We have about, what, 400,000 Chinese students in the United States right now studying. Are all of them approved in some way, shape, or form, Gordon, by the CCP?\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Well, certainly the regime knows who wants to come and study for the U.S., and they look into their background. So, someone who the regime doesn't want here is very unlikely to get to the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, this is very carefully vetted on the part in Beijing. And they're going to continue to do this because we allow them to. So, this is going to be an issue where, not just at Stanford, but at all institutions where you find Chinese students.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: According to recent data, Gordon, from the Department of Education, China has given Stanford at least a $183 million since 2005 in the form of gifts and other contracts, and everyone has to remember that universities are only required to report foreign gifts and contracts of more than $250,000. OK?\par \par \uc2 So, China has poured, you know, in 2024 alone, a $175 billion, with a B, into U.S. schools. Gordon, that is a staggering, staggering figure.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: It is. And we know that many administrations have not enforced those federal reporting rules. It was only really in the first Trump administration when \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  was secretary of education that they actually started to go after, for instance, the Ivy League schools that had completely violated their obligations on reporting.\par \par \uc2 You know, we need to drop that threshold much lower, and we need to start looking at all of this money coming in, because, obviously, it does redirect the way that these schools treat students.\par \par \uc2 And they allow the Chinese consular officials and Ministry of State security agents to monitor them, and they also allow the chapters of the Chinese Students and Scholars Association to surveil students.\par \par \uc2 So, there's a control mechanism in place that we permit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Important segment. It's not just China either. It's also countries like Qatar. Well, that's it for us tonight. I'm Lisa Boothe in for Laura Ingraham.\par \par \uc2 If you're not sick of me yet, you can catch me on Monday on Fox & Friends starting at 6 A.M. But if mornings aren't your thing, tune it Outnumbered at noon eastern. I'll be back on the couch. But until then, enjoy your Memorial Day weekend. But more importantly, remember and honor those who have paid the ultimate sacrifice defending our country.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching this special edition of the Ingraham Angle. Jesse Watters Primetime takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250524el5n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Budget: White House Proposal Trims Non-Defense Spending; Musical Chairs: Last Day For Waltz As National Security Advisor; Celebrating 250 Years: Trump To Host Military Parade For \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  Birthday; Waste Management: Musk And DOGE Team Reflect On First 100 Days; Spending Cuts: Trump Moves To End Federal Funding For \uc2 NPR\uc2  And \uc2 PBS\uc2 ; Pressure Putin: Estonian Spy Chief On Ending Ukraine War; White House Effort To Defuse India-Pakistan Conflict; Acting ICE Director On Framing Of Migrant Arrests; Video Released Showing 2022 Encounter Between Tennessee Highway Patrol Troopers And Kilmar Abrego Garcia; Vice President J.D. Vance Discusses Lack Of Leadership In Democratic Party; April U.S. Jobs Report Indicates 177,000 Positions Added And 4.2 Percent Unemployment Rate; Trump Administration Announces Budget Proposal For 2026\b0\par\par\uc2 Aishah Hasnie, Matt Finn, Chad Pergram, Rich Edson, Lucas  Tomlinson, Bill Melugin, Greg Palkot, Brooke Taylor \par \uc2 7,987 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: And good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Aishah Hasnie in for Bret Baier tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox News gets the first national T.V. interview with President Trump's Director of \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2 . That's ICE.\par \par \uc2 Another Fox exclusive tonight. \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally, Estonia's intelligence and defense chiefs talk to us about the growing threat from Russia.\par \par \uc2 And another exclusive, Vice President J.D. Vance sitting down with our Bret Baier. And yes, he addresses the 2028 presidential race.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, we are getting an early look at some of the highlights from President Trump's 2026 budget plan. It includes deep cuts to foreign aid and increased spending on the border and national security. Meanwhile, the Trump Administration is going on the economic offensive tonight. The jobs report for April better than expected. The President is touting what he calls the lowest gas prices in years, cheaper groceries, and reduced mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 Stocks also climbed back to liberation day levels. After the surprise jobs report, the Dow was up for a ninth straight day, surging to 564. The S&P 500 finished ahead 83, extending a 9-day winning streak. That is the longest in 20 years. The Nasdaq jumped 267. For the week, the Dow is up three percentage points. The S&P 500 gained almost three. NASDAQ finishing off ahead three and a half.\par \par \uc2 We've got Fox Team coverage tonight. Rich Edson at the White House with some big cuts on the table from the President and his \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2 . Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill. He will tell us what is in and out of the President's budget. But we begin tonight with Correspondent Matt Finn who is with the President in West Palm Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Matt, good evening.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Aishah, President Trump kept a low profile here in Florida today. He's scheduled to be hosting an \uc2 RNC\uc2  fundraiser at Mar-a-Lago right now. He arrived here late last night after speaking at a commencement for the University of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're in the Golden Age. You watch the Golden Age.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): President Trump's 100-day mark this week boosted by a better-than-expected jobs report today. 177,000 jobs were added last month, higher than the 135,000 expected. However, revisions to February and March numbers show 58,000 fewer jobs were created than initially reported. Unemployment remained steady at 4.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 LORI CHAVEZ-DEREMER, LABOR SECRETARY: And with President Trump at the helm, I'm telling you, he mentioned to the American people over and over again that the Golden Age was coming. I will tell you the Golden Age is here.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): One of President Trump's frequent Golden Age promises is lower prices. The President posting "Gasoline just broke $1.98 a gallon, lowest in years. Groceries and eggs down." Today's national average according to AAA is $3.18 a gallon, down about 50 cents from this time last year, but some states are still averaging more than $4.00 per gallon.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's proposed $1.7 trillion budget for next year promises a cut of $163 billion in non-defense federal spending.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2 , DIRECTOR, OFFICE OF MANAGEMENT AND BUDGET: We went agency by agency. We wanted to preserve the things that the President wanted to fund. So, we protect transportation and infrastructure. We protect homeland security and give them a major increase.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): But next year's economy is not guaranteed to be golden amid Trump's tariffs. The Administration insists the tariffs are hurting countries like China which will force negotiations but critics are not convinced these.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH (D-VT): These tariffs are going to be seen by historians as the biggest economic blunder in a 100 years. Our farmers who import fertilizer from Canada, they're getting hammered. Our manufacturers that have to get steel and aluminum, they're getting hammered.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And today is scheduled to be \uc2 Mike Waltz\uc2 's last day as National Security Adviser. Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is expected to temporarily hold that position for up to six months. Both Rubio and Deputy Chief of Staff \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  are reportedly in the running to fill that spot permanently. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Matt, everybody's already talking about next month marking the 250th anniversary of the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 . And we've learned that President Trump plans to throw a big parade to celebrate. What do you know about that?\par \par \uc2 FINN: Well, Aishah, that parade is scheduled for June 14th which happens to be the President's birthday as well. It is described as having more than 6,000 soldiers, hundreds of vehicles, and aircraft, a spectacular fireworks display, and a day-long festival. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. Matt Finn live for us in West Palm Beach, Florida. Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, some think that President Trump's new budget is yet another declaration of war on the status quo and no surprise here. Democrats not happy with the skinny budget. But some Republicans have issues too. Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill with that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Staggering cuts, President Trump demanding Congress slash an eyepopping 20 percent of annual spending. Republicans embrace the plan.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE HARIDOPOLOS (R-FL): We're going to move towards a long-term balanced budget. I like how we're thinking long-term instead of short-term.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): The package eliminating dollars from every department except the V.A. and Transportation. Space programs are safe too. Still lawmakers must greenlight the plan. The House Freedom Caucus declared "This is how you break the swamp."\par \par \uc2 The package knives housing programs by 40 percent and axes as the Labor and Interior Departments by 30 percent. Pentagon money is essentially flat. \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  Chairman Roger Wicker torched the outline accusing the budget office of trying to "shred to the bone the nation's military." He insists on more spending.\par \par \uc2 The budget left out Medicare and Medicaid. They could face changes in the so-called Big Beautiful Bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Hospitals will close. Nursing homes will shut down. And people will die.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Even some Republicans are skeptical of how the GOP will handle Medicare and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): If you want to be in the minority forever, then go ahead and do Medicaid cuts. That would be catastrophically stupid.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): The White House gave Congressional leaders a wish list as the GOP tries to wrap up the measure. Tax credits for EVs are out.\par \par \uc2 REP. BRYAN STEIL (R-WI): I don't have a problem if somebody wants to go buy an electric vehicle. I just don't think hardworking Americans should be subsidizing that.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): The goal is to pass the bill in the House by Memorial Day, but some Republicans doubt that timeline.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): No, there's no way because, again, unfortunately President Trump chose the One Big Beautiful Bill. What he should have done is the multiple-step process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The House could consider the bill in two weeks. One senior source tells Fox "We will stay until we pass it." Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And they probably will. Chad Pergram live for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, President Trump is going after public broadcasting tonight. He signed an executive order slashing taxpayer funds for \uc2 National Public Radio\uc2  and the Public Broadcasting System. He is also taking aim at the tax- exempt status for one of the country's most prestigious universities. The President of Harvard says that would be highly illegal.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes as we get ready for a big change at the \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2 . Here is Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Fraud in flagrante delicto, OK. We're talking -- at scale.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and the \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2  are showing and defending their work across the first 100 days of the second Trump Administration. At the Department of Education --\par \par \uc2 STEVE DAVIS, DOGE: They found that money was being used to rent out Caesar's Palace for parties, rent out stadiums, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): The Interamerican Foundation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They get $50 million a year, Congressional money, to give grants. These are things like, you know, Alpaca farming in Peru.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): And more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Improving the marketability of peas in Guatemala.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Really?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fruit jam.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): All part of DOGE claims the department has saved $ 160 billion. Musk says he's going to spend more time at \uc2 Tesla\uc2  where sales in the company's stock price are down since inauguration day. His special government employee status is scheduled to end May 30th. Musk says DOGE needs to be a long-term enterprise.\par \par \uc2 MUSK: There's a lot of work required to restart the waste and fraud.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): Democrats accuse DOGE of inflating its savings, sloppy work and cutting vital government services, medical research, and aid. They say spending is up this year and that Musk has failed to deliver at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): We heard from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that he was going to cut the Defense budget? And what happened? We're adding $150 billion to the Defense budget.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): There are more cuts coming. These directly from the White House. President Trump signed an executive order instructing the \uc2 Corporation for Public Broadcasting\uc2  to cease federal funding for \uc2 NPR\uc2  and \uc2 PBS\uc2 , saying neither entity presents a fair, accurate, or unbiased portrayal of current events to taxpaying citizens. \uc2 PBS\uc2  says it's exploring all its options. \uc2 NPR\uc2  says it "will challenge this executive order using all means available."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The President is also escalating his fight with \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 . He says his administration will revoke Harvard's tax-exempt status. Harvard called the move unprecedented, unlawful, and says it will have grave consequences for higher education in America. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Rich, thank you. The Trump Administration is asking the Supreme Court to clear the way for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2  to be able to access Social Security systems containing personal data on millions of Americans. The emergency appeal comes after a judge in Maryland restricted the team's access under federal privacy laws. The administration says DOGE needs to get in to cut waste in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continue our reporting from Estonia, one of America's allies in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Their Defense Minister and Intelligence Director sitting down with our Lucas Tomlinson about how they will counter threats from Russia. Here's Lucas from Tallinn, Estonia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAUPO ROSIN, FOREIGN INTELLIGENCE SERVICE, ESTONIA: Russia's goal is to weaken the U.S.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): As Russia continues its war on Ukraine civilians, peace talks appear to be going nowhere. Last night on SPECIAL REPORT, Vice President J.D. Vance told Bret he was cautiously optimistic. But if neither side compromises --\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE-PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's not going to end anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 ROSIN: I would say we can put more pressure on Russia to force Russia onto the negotiation table.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Including more sanctions on Russia's so-called shadow fleet. Over half of Russia's oil exports go through the Finnish Gulf in the Baltic Sea off the coast of Estonia.\par \par \uc2 ROSIN: Russians understand only the language of force and power.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Some Americans have grown frustrated about supporting Ukraine. What is your message to them?\par \par \uc2 ROSIN: In the Russian mindset, this is also a war against the Americans. If Ukraine loses, in Russia's perspective, this is a defeat for U.S.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): In a few days, \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces from 10 countries including the United States will arrive in Estonia for a month of training dubbed Operation Hedgehog held every five years. Estonia's Defense Minister described the upcoming war games.\par \par \uc2 HANNO PEVKUR, DEFENSE MINISTER, ESTONIA: This is the biggest \uc2 NATO\uc2  exercise this year. It will have 16,000 troops all around eastern flank of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Estonia has close defense ties with the United States. The Defense Minister showed us this memorial for Estonian soldiers killed in action. Many alongside U.S. Marines in Afghanistan's Helmand Province.\par \par \uc2 PEVKUR: When I take proportionally, then Estonia has lost more soldiers in Iraq and in Afghanistan than United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Estonia's spy chief says over the last 10 years, Russia's been playing hockey and the West cannot get away with figure skating. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Lucas Tomlinson in Estonia for us. Thanks, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what the Trump Administration is doing to try to ease tensions between India and Pakistan, two nuclear nations. First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 9 in the Twin Cities as court records there indicate the alleged gunman in Tuesday's mass shooting was a member of a Minneapolis street gang. They say James Duane Ortley fired while sitting in the car with victims shooting all five in the head.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Seattle reports seven people dead there after a tour van and pickup truck collide near Yellowstone National Park. Look at that. Idaho State Police say the accident happened last night near Henrys Lake State Park in Eastern Idaho. The cause of the crash under investigation.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2  in Louisville. And of course, the big story there tonight from our affiliate WDRB. Final preps underway for tomorrow's big Kentucky Derby race The 151st Run for the Roses takes place at \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2 , post time 6:57 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Syria's President is denouncing a second Israeli air strike this week, calling it a dangerous escalation trying to destabilize the country. It is now asking the international community for support.\par \par \uc2 Israel's Air Force struck near Syria's presidential palace in Damascus overnight, it came after a warning to Syrian authorities not to march towards villages that are inhabited by members of a minority sect in the south.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Trump administration is trying to defuse tensions between two nuclear nations. Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has called senior officials in India and Pakistan in an effort to mitigate the crisis that followed last week's deadly attack in Kashmir.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Vice President J.D. Vance spoke with Bret Baier about the situation. Here's the latest now from Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Tensions are growing between India and Pakistan. An attack last week in the Indian-controlled mainly Muslim Kashmir region not far from the part Pakistan runs, left 26 mostly Hindus killed.\par \par \uc2 Gunmen asking victims, what religion they were before shooting. India accused Pakistan. Pakistan denied responsibility. India reducing diplomatic, trade and transport links. There have been exchanges of gunfire. Pakistan claims India is set to attack.\par \par \uc2 ATTAULLAH TARAR, PAKISTANI INFORMATION MINISTER: India has apparently decided to tread the dangerous path of irrationality and confrontation.\par \par \uc2 AMIT SHAH, INDIAN INTERIOR MINISTER (through translator): If someone thinks that by carrying out a cowardly act, that this is a big victory, then understand, no one will be spared.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Both countries have nuclear weapons. President Trump described the incident as deeply disturbing. Offering his support to Indian Prime Minister Modi.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State, Rubio and Defense Secretary Hegseth are in touch with their counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Vice President J.D. Vance, who met Modi just before the attack, said this to Bret Baier last night.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our hope here is that India responds to this terrorist attack in a way that doesn't lead to a broader regional conflict, and we hope, frankly, that Pakistan cooperates with India to make sure that the terrorists are hunted down and dealt with.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Adding to the geopolitical stakes, while the U.S. is aligned with India, Pakistan has been reaching out to China. Analysts say the nuclear option is not likely yet, but military action could be.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL RUBIN\uc2 , AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: India is going to respond militarily because with the type of provocation, with the type of attack which they suffered, their job is to protect their own people and also to signal that there is going to be a high cost for any future terrorism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): On the trade deal front with India, Vance also told Fox good negotiations are underway and that Modi drives a hard bargain, a lot on his hands now, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Greg Palkot for us. Thank you, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the first T.V. interview with the acting director of ICE about the first 100 days of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Bret talks to Vice President J.D. Vance about the 2028 race.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  loses his appeal challenging the \uc2 British government\uc2 's decision to take away his publicly funded security. The move came after Harry stepped away from the royal family and moved to the U.S. The Court of Appeals were ruling unanimously that Harry had not been treated unfairly.\par \par \uc2 The body of a truck driver whose vehicle fell into a sinkhole near Tokyo three months ago now recovered, the 74-year-old driver was trapped in the cabin of that truck. Experts say the sinkhole caused by corrosion in sewer pipes.\par \par \uc2 And take a look, a live look at Rome right now, the big story there, of course, preparation speeding up now for the Conclave, as Cardinals gather to pick the next pope. Firefighters were seen on the roof of the Sistine Chapel installing the chimney which will signal the outcome of the voting.\par \par \uc2 Remember, black smoke means a failed vote. White smoke means that they do have a pope. The Conclave begins on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Those are just some of the stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The top official at \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  says he is happy with the progress during the first 100 days of the Trump administration, but acting ICE Director Todd Lyons is not happy with opposition to his mission. Correspondent Bill Melugin reports from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)(BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD LYONS, ACTING ICE DIRECTOR: How can anyone argue with you when you're deporting violent gang members, sex offenders, fentanyl traffickers?\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Acting ICE Director Todd Lyons, giving his first national T.V. interview to Fox News and going after sanctuary cities, which refused to work with ICE.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: The more they're going to double down or triple down, we're going to ramp it up more. We're going to make sure that we're doing all we can do to bring our law enforcement resources to that jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): Lyons is tasked with carrying out President Trump's mass deportation agenda.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're tracking down the illegal alien criminals. That agenda got a shot in the arm in Florida last week with Operation Tidal Wave. ICE partnered with Florida law enforcement to net 1,120 arrests of illegal aliens.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: That's been our longest sustained operation. It's only going to keep going. We're going to use this model. We're going to go across the nation, states, municipalities that want to work with us. We're going to capitalize on that.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Lyons also sounded off on narratives surrounding ICE targets, including the so-called Maryland man Kilmar Abrego Garcia. Lyons says media and politicians portraying innocent victims are often very wrong.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: What's frustrating for me is you see a lot of people that will go towards one narrative without actually learning and taking the time to learn the criminal history, learn the actual facts behind a case.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): And as for the 66,000 ICE deportations in the first 100 days --\par \par \uc2 LYONS: That's some great numbers. We can do more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Aishah, a lot of people don't know this, but ICE only has about 5,500 deportation officers across the country. And now House Republicans are looking at giving them the funding to hire an additional 10,000 ICE personnel. Lyons says that would be an immediate game changer. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Bill Melugin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We are getting our first look tonight at video showing a 2022 encounter between Tennessee highway patrol troopers and the illegal immigrant from El Salvador living in Maryland whose controversial deportation has caused a firestorm among Democrats. Correspondent Brooke Taylor shows us tonight from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hello. How are you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good. And you?\par \par \uc2 BROOKE TAYLOR, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Newly released body camera footage shows the moment that Kilmar Abrego Garcia is pulled over by a Tennessee highway patrol trooper in 2022. He was initially pulled over for speeding, but it eventually led troopers to believe he was involved in human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 KILMAR ABREGO GARCIA: The problem with my license right now is suspended, right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's suspended?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Suspended.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Eight other passengers with no I.D.s packed into the SUV.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How many rows you have got in here? Four? Four seats, four rows of seats?\par \par \uc2 He's hauling these people for money. He's got a -- hauler is what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Abrego Garcia told troopers he wasn't doing anything illegal and they were returning from a construction project. Sources tell FOX that ICE was called but they didn't show up. It's not clear why. Abrego Garcia was only given a citation for driving with an expired license. All this comes as "USA Today" obtained and audio recording of Abrego Garcia's wife begging a judge for a protection order against her husband in 2020.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER VASQUEZ, KILMAR ABREGO GARCIA'S WIFE: When he hurt me, he grabbed me from the hair and then he slapped me. And the neighbor, he didn't know what to do, he didn't know what to react.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: A week later Jennifer Vasquez asked the judge to rescind the protection so Abrego Garcia could attend his son's birthday party. She has chosen to stand by her husband and recently spoke at a May Day rally in D.C.\par \par \uc2 VASQUEZ: My husband was illegally detained, abducted, and disappeared.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Several Democratic lawmakers have visited El Salvador to push for Abrego Garcia's release, including senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2 , (D-MD): I have repeatedly said that we're not vouching for Kilmar Abrego Garcia. We are vouching for his constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (on camera): And the Trump administration is responding to this tonight, sending a statement saying, quote, "The hole that Democrats and the fake news media dug themselves into by demanding the return of a wife- beating, MS-12 terrorist illegal alien just keeps getting deeper." Aishah?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Brooke Taylor live for us. Brooke, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have more from Bret Baier's sit-down interview with Vice President \uc2 J.D. Vance\uc2 . They spoke yesterday in South Carolina, and the conversation turned to 2028 and who might be on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You talk a lot about policy, but just the politics. And I know it's very Washington to talk about politics. But when the president says 2028 and he has the Trump 2028 hat, what do you think? Are you thinking, is this real? Are you thinking about that? What is your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENT: So I was at the Oval Office a couple days ago, and there were some Trump 2028 hats floating around. And I put one on. And I said Mr. President, you took a photo of this. This would go super viral. And we joked about it and laughed.\par \par \uc2 Look, what I've said, Bret, is I think the president and I are going to be working for years to come. I think that we have a rare opportunity over these next three years to actually undo a lot of damage that was caused by not just the Biden administration, by 40 years of failed policy, but I think build a true American renaissance. That's what we're excited about. That's what I'm going to try to do.\par \par \uc2 And if we succeed, then the politics will take care of themselves. And if we don't succeed, the politics will take care of themselves that way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you don't really think he's running in 2028, do you?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I'll let the president speak to his political future. I think the president -- one thing I'll say about the president of the United States is he loves to have a good time with the American media. And I really do think that if we take care of business over the next three years, we're going to be in a great position politically whoever ultimately runs as the president of the United States in 2028.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On the Democratic side, do you think \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , AOC, is the new leader? Could it be Vance-AOC in 2028?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: President AOC, the stuff of nightmares, Bret. Thank you. You've ruined my sleep for this evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who is the leader of the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I don't know. Honestly, I think --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think about it?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think to the extend that Democrats have a leader, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  is the leader of the Democratic Party --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Because they're against --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Because they're just against everything that he does. Who could possibly disagree with rebuilding American manufacturing and ensuring the steel workers have higher wages and better jobs? But any time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does anything, they have this emotional response they have to be against it. Who could disagree with deporting millions of illegal aliens, many of whom are violent criminals? But they do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They have.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: They have. And I think that what they do is whatever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does something, they react negatively to it. They don't have real leadership. And I think it's why I'm not too concerned about 2028, because if I wake up in a fantasy world where the Democrats actually have constructive ideas about how to govern this country, that will actually be a good thing. But in the world that we actually live in where they're just anti-Trump about everything, the politics of 2028 will take care of themselves.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know the president talk about polls, and he's not a fan of how the polls are done. But the approval ratings for you, for the president, if you look at the polls, not just one but a span of them, they're not great 100 days in. They're great on border security. But on the economy and overall approval, you know, it's a little underwater. And how do you look at that? What do you think that is from?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, so I'm a firm believer, Bret, that in the modern era, in 2025, opinion pollsters just don't know how to capture most Americans. They don't answer the phones. If they do answer the phone, they hang up immediately.\par \par \uc2 I saw this in the 2024 election where remember, I lived through this even more personally than you did, where every mainstream media pollster said \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to win the popular vote, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to win six of seven, maybe even seven of seven battleground states. And there's one pollster I really trust. His name is \uc2 Tony Fabrizio\uc2 . I said, Tony, what's actually going on? And he said unless I stopped knowing how to do my thing, we're going to win seven of seven battleground states and you guys are going to win the national popular vote.\par \par \uc2 You have got to be able to go to people where they are. I think that, again, you do a good job, you worry about the polls later. The politics will take care of itself. For the reasons I just said, I really am skeptical of the polls, Bret. I also just don't really care that much about the polls, even if they were accurate, because if we do a good job, if people see the results, then in three years people will be talking about what a great job \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance did. That's all I really care about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Some breaking news to get to right now. A federal judge has just struck down President Trump's executive order that targets law firm \uc2 Perkins Coie\uc2 . It's part of President Trump's efforts to go after law firms that have handled legal challenges to his actions, represented political adversaries, or employed lawyers investigating him. That little bit of breaking news just coming in right now.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Trump's 2026 budget, and then later the return of the Friday Lightning Round.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LORI CHAVEZ-DEREMER, LABOR SECRETARY: With the investment of these large companies back into American workforce and the American manufacturing sector, transportation, healthcare, we're seeing the jobs tick up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH, (D-VT) SENATE FINANCE COMMITTEE: These tariffs are going to be seen by historians as the biggest economic blunder in 100 years. Our manufacturers that have to get steel and aluminum, they are getting hammered.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MIRAN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISER CHAIRMAN: I don't think that they were right about tariffs in President Trump's first term in which the tariffs left no lasting market on the economy but generated tons of revenue. I think they're wrong again now. And today's data proved it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, let's bring in our panel tonight, Matthew Continetti, scholar at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , Real Clear Politics White House correspondent Philip Wegmann, and Sabrina Singh, former Pentagon deputy press secretary.\par \par \uc2 OK, here we go. Happy Friday, everyone. What a week. It was the first 100 days. That's over. Now we're on to the next 100. A pretty good jobs report, a good headline to end the week for the Trump administration -- 177,000 positions added, 4.2 percent unemployment rate.\par \par \uc2 And this is how "Axios" saw it. They said "Job market shrugs off trade war for now. There's little evidence of trade war pain in the job market, which kept chugging along last month with strong hiring and low levels of joblessness." So Matthew, pretty great week. How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Well, if you think about why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected, what were the two big issues? Immigration and inflation/economy. Immigration, he shut down the border, something that earlier presidents said couldn't be done. He did it even before the 100 days was over. And now we're arguing about how we deport people, not about the people coming in illegally.\par \par \uc2 And on inflation and the economy, there's been a lot of sturm and drang about the liberation day and the tariffs. But this first read of the data on inflation, on gas prices, egg prices, now jobs, the S&P 500 having a great week, great run, 20-year run closing today, things are going pretty well for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in these first 100 days.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Something that he did before he left for West Palm Beach was drop this skinny budget on congressional Democrats and Republicans. And Democrats obviously weren't going to like it very much. But Republicans, some of them out there also have a problem with it, including Armed Services Committee Chairman Roger Wicker, who wasn't too happy with the numbers that the defense spending is at. It's kind of stagnant. And this is what he said going after the OMB, is that "President Trump successfully campaigned on a peace through strength agenda. But his advisers at the Office of Management and Budget were apparently not listening." And then he says, "Their intent is to shred to the bone our military capabilities and our support to service members." Sabrina, what do you make of this budget?\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, DEPUTY PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Yes, I think this budget is incredibly controversial for both sides. And so it's interesting that you're seeing a bipartisan call of, like, this might not get our chambers, because even though there is an increase to defense spending, there are significant cuts to programs that military service members and their families depend on, many millions of Americans depend on as well all around the country.\par \par \uc2 And so when they're looking at cutting $163 billion, I think was the figure that was cited, you're also looking at programs like Medicare and Social Security. These are programs that are widely popular among Americans all over the country that people depend on it. And kids also depend on Medicaid, something that's also going to be cut. So I think there's going to be a very strong look on both sides of the aisle at this budget. At the end of the day, you want to see our men and women protected in uniform, but there has to be protections also for families all across the country.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Philip, it's like no surprise that he's taken an axe to government funding and he's trying to make this as skinny as possible. They're calling it the skinny budget, $163 billion in cuts to nondefense spending. Congress is not used to this. This is going to be a little bit of a headache when he comes back.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: And all of us here know that these White House budgets are basically press releases with a lot of math. That said, I still think that this is significant, because what is OMB Director \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2  doing? He's essentially looking at the DOGE team and saying hold my beer. I can cut spending, too. Maybe this doesn't get through, but that 22 percent cut to nondefense discretionary, I think it signals to the rest of the country and to Congress that they're serious about going after what they see as waste, fraud, and abuse. And now after the White House thinks that they've done their part, they're signaling to those members of Congress who are very eager to get their picture taken with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that now they need to deliver as well.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Matthew, how does the White House deal with that, when there are Republicans that will talk about wanting to make deep cuts but then there are others that don't want to codify some of these DOGE cuts?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think the president put it well earlier this week. He said the first 100 days was about executive power. All the executive order, we get new executive orders every day. But the second hundred days is about congress. And so this is going to be where he has to leverage his political capital to get the House of Representatives, extremely narrow majority, to get the Senate, which is kind of divided over defense, as we just saw, divided over Medicaid spending. There's no Medicaid cuts in this budget. There's no entitlement cuts in this budget. They're going to have to argue over that. And I think he's in a position within the Republican Party that he's going to be able to get that one big, beautiful bill by the end of the summer.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, we've got a lot more to talk about coming up. I want to get to all of our favorite topics. Up next, the Friday Lightning Round, India versus Pakistan, Vice President Vance on 2028 politics, plus this week's Winners and Losers. And then, of course, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On the Democratic side, do you think \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , AOC, is the new leader? Could it be Vance-AOC in 2028?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: President AOC, the stuff of nightmares, Bret. Thank you. You've ruined my sleep for this evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who is the leader of the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: They don't have real leadership. And I think it's why I'm not too concerned about 2028. In the world that we actually live in where they're just anti-Trump about everything, the politics of 2028 will take care of themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, we are back with our panel now. It's lightning round time. So Matthew, I'll go to you first. 2028, who do you think is going to be on the ballot? And what do you think of what Vance said, politics will just take care of itself?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I have a feeling Vance will be on the Republican ballot.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You think?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I have a pretty strong feeling about that. I have no idea who will be on the Democratic ballot. And you just think about it, the Democrats are a leaderless party. And they're not doing themselves any good. Who are the faces they put forward? \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , AOC, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I don't know if they're putting them forward. They've just come out.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: President Biden is coming back next for an interview on "The View". They're exactly the same faces that the public turned against last year. So if they want a chance in 2028, they need a fresh face.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Sabrina, real quick, yes. Jump in.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I would say that, well, here's what you're seeing. There's a diversity of faces out there, and we're unifying behind a message.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: They're not diverse at all. They say exactly the same thing.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I will say, the Trump administration is handing us some wins here. His numbers are down when it comes to the economy. And even though, yes, there was some wins with some expansion of jobs, the economy is shrinking. The volatility in the stock market, the tariffs are going to be a tax on --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  talked about his ability to code talk to white men. It didn't work out for him then and it won't work out for them now.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I think it's actually good that we're having a diverse range of voices out there.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, we'll see where it goes. Philip?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: 2028 is sooner than we expected. I think what the vice president proved in that interaction with Bret Baier is that he is Trump's hillbilly apprentice. He is someone who speaks fluent MAGA but also someone who is taking very seriously this new populism and nationalism. And President Obama, he's going to be the vanguard of the new right.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, back to Sabrina real quick on Trump -- sorry, not Trump, India and Pakistan. And what do you think is going to happen with that?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I think it's good that you're seeing Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  engaging both sides. Ultimately what you want to see here is a de- escalation of tensions. And of course, ultimately what you want to see is accountability. And that needs to happen, but you don't want to see both sides keep ratcheting up their rhetoric. So it's good that \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is engaging, and hopefully we see a calming.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Two nuclear nations, we don't want anything to happen.\par \par \uc2 OK, back to you. We're going to talk about Winners and Losers. You had a lot of time to think about this. It was a really whacky week.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think about it all the time. I spent all week thinking about it. My winner this week, my winner is Bobby Wood Jr. of the \uc2 Kansas City Royals\uc2 . He's on a 22-game hitting streak. It's awesome. And we'll see if he can continue it tonight when they play the \uc2 Orioles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And my loser of the week is the \uc2 Los Angeles Lakers\uc2 . LeBron James was getting Luka Doncic, it was going to be a huge trade, a megadeal. And they got out of the first round of the playoffs, yesterday. So they lose the week.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Very noncontroversial Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: In sports what I said is very controversial.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Sports, stick to sports. Sabrina?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: My winner would probably be the movie "Sinners". It's outperforming the box office. It's going into its third week. It's a genre that you don't usually see performing at this rate. So congratulations to the movie.\par \par \uc2 And my loser would be \uc2 Bill Belichick\uc2  because I didn't even realize he was writing a memoir. But that hasn't been the topic of conversation anymore. And certainly someone that's been on the other side, you never want to be the story as someone staffing an interview. And they've gotten themselves into a bit of a mess.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I suspect he might think that he's the winner in all of this.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I'm sure it will help with sales.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: He's got a pretty good looking girlfriend. And she might think she's a winner, too. Philip, winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I'd echo that \uc2 Bill Belichick\uc2  is very a loser as an \uc2 Indianapolis Colts\uc2  fan. My winners this week are whoever writes \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 's memoirs, because with all of these jobs, that writer is going to have a ton of material to draw from.\par \par \uc2 My loser are the Harvard administrators who have to spend the weekend doing homework figuring out how to keep their tax exempt status now that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going after them.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, can I just one more quick thing going back to 2028. You said Vance for sure on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I don't think we have a Democratic nominee yet. I'm OK with that.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, and who did you say?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: It's a mystery on the left. But I think it's likely Vance. But \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , a lot of jobs right now.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I think it might be Vance and maybe a Trump. I think there has to be a Trump on the ticket. Maybe a \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 ? I think there has to be a Trump on the ticket for maybe a Trump endorsement. You think he endorses?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: In 2028.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: He will choose the next nominee of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, and when do you think we'll have some nominees, some big faces come out?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I don't think that's going to shake out for -- we have to get through the midterms.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: After the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: And then you'll start to see that field emerge. But right now we're having a very nice, healthy conversation both publicly and privately.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I'm with you there.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: All right, it is Friday. And that means it is time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Why was \uc2 Mike Waltz\uc2  let go?\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENT: I think you can make a good argument that it's a promotion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a real acknowledgement finally on the part of the president that Signal-gate was a disaster.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And there's more shoes to fall off this centipede.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am glad to announce the signing of today's historic economic partnership agreement.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Who saw that video a couple weeks ago? The one of the elephants at the San Diego Zoo? What a powerful metaphor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My kids know more about trade than kids should know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are over the shock of the American betrayal. But we should never forget the lessons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Even my hat has a hat.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: They all hated me in my first time, and now they're kissing my ass.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When we survive this shameful episode of American history, we will relegate their portraits to the museum halls reserved for tyrants and traitors.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Mom, I'm going to do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 Washington Commanders\uc2  will be returning to the district.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Amazing day for the city, amazing day for or franchise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We also appreciate the endorsement for the tush push.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: All right, Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the debate over how to care for transgender minors, therapy or surgery?\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests will be House Budget Committee Chairman \uc2 Jodey Arrington\uc2 , House Ways and Means Committee Chairman Jason Smith, House Intelligence Committee Ranking Member \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2 , and Democratic National Communication Chair Ken Martin.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  sits down with \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  on "My View". You can catch that at 9:00 p.m. eastern.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if you can't catch us live, you can set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. eastern, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Aishah Hasnie in for Bret Baier tonight in Washington, and THE INGRAHAM ANGLE with Laura Ingraham is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250503el5200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 DOGE Efforts to Cut Government Fraud, Spending; Some Industries Throw Support Behind Trump Tariffs; Sen. Coons on Trump Tariffs' Impact on the Economy; Worst Week on Wall Street Since 2020; Political Impact of Trump Tariffs; Trump Tariffs Halt Progress on TikTok Deal; AG Bondi On Supreme Court's handling Of Executive Power Cases; Federal Judge Orders Return Of Man Mistakenly Deported To El Salvador; Severe Storms In Midwest And South; Secretary Of State Suspends All South Sudan Visas; Israeli PM To Meet With President Trump Tomorrow; Trump's Sweeping Tariffs Take Effect; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  (R-OK); Interview with Sen. \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  (D-DE); White House Efforts to End Russia-Ukraine War; U.S.-Russia Relations under Trump Administration; Severe Flash Flooding in Memphis, Tennessee\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Marie Harf, Josh Kraushaar, Lucas Tomlinson,  Madison Scarpino \par \uc2 10,123 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 The White House defends its aggressive tariffs saying the outcome will ultimately pay off for U.S. workers and the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: April 2nd, 2025, will forever be remembered as the day American industry was reborn.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The administration says the short-term pain will bring long-term gain.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK, COMMERCE SECRETARY: Their tariffs are the tip of the iceberg. It's going to create a renaissance of manufacturing. It's going to be the employment of American workers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But not everyone in Washington agrees.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is using tariffs in the dumbest way imaginable.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): They're not only bad economically. They're bad politically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll get reaction from both sides of the aisle with Senators \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  and \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: These gangs are organized. They are terrorists. They are living among us because they think they can get away with this and they cannot.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Justice Department ramps up its prosecution of violent gangs.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: If you are a gang member living in this country, I'd self-deport right now because we're coming after you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're joined by Attorney General Pam Bondi to talk about that and the administration's use of a wartime law to deport alleged gang members.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're changing the way that Congress works, making sure that -- that moms and parents have a voice.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A possible path out of a fight over proxy voting for new parent members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our Sunday panel about the new twist and push to negotiate now that the president has weighed in.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of your headlines.\par \par \uc2 At least 16 people have died, thousands have been displaced by severe storms causing widespread flooding and power outages across several states in the Midwest and across the South. More heavy rain is predicted for parts of those regions today. We're going to get a live report on the ground in Tennessee later on in the show.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  releasing a statement Saturday announcing the suspension of all visas for citizens of South Sudan, accusing the African country of not accepting the return of its citizens being deported from the U.S., quote, in a timely manner.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is said to meet with President Trump here in Washington tomorrow. The two leaders are expected to talk about the war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza and to negotiate over those new 17 percent tariffs imposed on Israeli imports to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get reaction to the fallout from the tariffs from Senators \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  of Oklahoma, and \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 But first, we turn to Lucas Tomlinson in West Palm Beach, Florida, where he is covering the president.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, the stock market has lost nearly $10 trillion since President Trump returned to the White House, over $6 trillion alone since the new tariffs were announced. Trump's advice to Americans, hang tough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Now, they'll do anything for us.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): At the stroke of midnight Saturday, 10 percent tariffs went into effect on most of the world, including nations where the U.S. has a trade surplus, using the International Emergency Powers Act of 1977.\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. allies are outraged.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY ALBANESE, AUSTRALIAN PRIME MINISTER: This is not the act of a friend.\par \par \uc2 MARK CARNEY\uc2 , CANADIAN PRIME MINISTER: We must respond with both purpose and force.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: A member of Trump's cabinet defended the use of emergency powers, to build up America's industrial base.\par \par \uc2 KELLY LOEFFLER\uc2 , ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 U.S. SMALL BUSINESS ADMINISTRATION\uc2 : We can't even make screws and nuts and bolts in this country anymore.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The Senate's top Democrat says history is not on Trump's side.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We all know that the Great Depression was caused by a trade war like this.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Even some Republicans appear dubious.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): I'm not a fan of jacking up taxes on American consumers. So my hope is these tariffs are short-lived.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The head of the federal reserve issued the following warning.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , FEDERAL RESERVE CHAIRMAN: While tariffs are highly likely to generate at least a temporary rise in inflation, it's also possible that that the effects could be more persistent.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Trump responded, this would be a perfect time for Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Using another obscure act, the Trump administration has continued deporting what it says are violent gang members to El Salvador, something U.S. District Judge \uc2 James Boasberg\uc2  says he wants stopped and has accused the Justice Department of violating his ruling. Some have questioned the power of federal judges issuing nationwide injunctions.\par \par \uc2 And in a victory for the White House, the Supreme Court ruled the Trump administration could block teacher training grants that include DEI programs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Judge Boasberg is now weighing whether or not to hold Trump officials in contempt -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, joining us first, Oklahoma Senator \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2  (R-OK): Thanks for having me on, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, you've seen the headlines. They are in papers across the country, across the world, talking about the markets tanking, about all of the trouble that has been sparked by these tariff wars.\par \par \uc2 So, the president has taken a victory lap when the markets are good. He has taken credit for that. But they've taken a brutal beating this week.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" says this: Trade wars are easy to start but they can be hard to stop once the retaliation gets rolling. Mr. Trump started this war, and he owns whatever happens next to prices, jobs and economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree he owns what's happening now, Senator?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: No, what -- well, what we have wrong here, Shannon, is this isn't a trade war. This is balancing our -- our economy with countries that have taken advantage of us. There's countries for decades that have got rich over the backs of the workers here in -- in America.\par \par \uc2 What's happened is we've lost manufacturing jobs where because of our unfreed -- unfree -- unfair trade deals that have left America, went to other countries, building their products and turning it back around and selling it to our economy. The countries need access to our economy because we're the largest and the greatest economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 The problem is it's costing us manufacturing jobs. It is time for someone to stand up and I applaud President Trump for having a backbone and being the first president to ever stand up and say, hold on a second. It's not about today's economy. It's about the future of America's economy going down the road.\par \par \uc2 We know the trade deficits have spread. They've only got worse year after year after year and at some point, someone had to do something about it. And President Trump is finally doing that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And the reality is, it has rattled the markets. It is affecting people's 401(k)s. Millions of Americans have those.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's not just the economic issue though. One of your GOP colleagues is warning about the potential political impact.\par \par \uc2 Here's \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): Tariffs have also led to political decimation. When McKinley most famously put tariffs on in 1890, they lost 50 percent of their seats in the next election. When Smoot and Hawley put on their tariff in the early 1930s, we lost the House and the Senate for 60 years.\par \par \uc2 So, they're not only bad economically. They're bad politically.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you worry about that short term some of these special elections and things going on now, but more broadly, long-term -- midterms 2026, which generally are not good for the president in power?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Well, here's what you have, is you have a president in the White House as a businessperson, not a career like -- career politician. I would trust President Trump in his handling of the economy way before I would trust -- trust Senator Paul on this.\par \par \uc2 The truth is we have to do something about it now. We know, Shannon, it has cost us jobs year after year after year, and these countries have taken advantage of us.\par \par \uc2 What the president is saying here is he's not wanting to start a trade war. He's simply wanting to even the playing field. Reciprocal tariffs isn't just about the tariffs, but it's also access to our economy I say this all the time.\par \par \uc2 But Japan charges us zero percent tariffs on going on selling U.S. auto vehicles in Japan, but we can't sell them because the access to the economy is closed. So, it's not just tariffs, it's also the rules that prevent us from able to do business in their country like we allow them to do business in our country. And President Trump has been talking about this since literally the '80s, way before he was even considering running for president. And so, he understands what's going on.\par \par \uc2 And when are we going to fix it? If we're not going to fix it now, Shannon, when are we going to have the backbone in this country to fix it? And now is the time to do it and I trust President Trump and his leadership in doing so.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you've been a consistent critic of China as has President Trump, and yet we know that there is a fallout --\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- from what is going on with the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" reports that they're actually benefiting Xi Jinping.\par \par \uc2 Here's what they write: Mr. Trump has taken an axe to the economic cords that were binding the rest of the world into an economic and strategic block to rival Beijing and at precisely the moment many countries were finally starting to re-evaluate their economic relationships with China.\par \par \uc2 China's already announced some retaliation, limits on their export of rare earth minerals that we don't have in abundance. We use them in defense. We use them in MRI machines.\par \par \uc2 What is the off-ramp now with something like China where we are a major trading partner and we need each other's goods?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Well, we actually do have rare earth minerals here in the United States. The problem is, is because the environmental movement that we have, we can't mine them.\par \par \uc2 As far as access to economy, China has to have access to our economy. Over 40 percent of the goods that are manufactured in China are sold here inside the United States.\par \par \uc2 We have a huge trade deficit. We don't have to have access to China's economy to keep our economy moving. The reason why the \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2  exists is because just a short -- a few years ago, 75 percent of the manufacturing goods in China was sold inside the United States.\par \par \uc2 What China is trying to do is diversify themselves by forcing other countries with the \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2 , do business with them by -- by getting them in debt, and forcing them to trade with them. The \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2  is -- is a very big problem we have around the world and you see countries that are pushing back because of the pressure that China is putting on them once they get them in debt.\par \par \uc2 So, you have two different things colliding here, once again what we're trying to do -- what the Republicans are trying to do working with President Trump is balance the unfair trade indifference that is going on and the countries are taking advantage of us. And this -- as I said before, Shannon, this is the perfect opportunity to do it because we have a businessperson that understands a market like we've never had before in the White House and that's President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Well, he seems to have a high level of tolerance for the risk for the pain. We'll see how long this takes as this plays out.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you all passed a budget early Saturday morning, the framework for budget out of the Senate. The Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget says it would add nearly $6 trillion to primary deficits and adds this: Such a bill would be historically unprecedented in its fiscal irresponsibility, adding several times more debt than any reconciliation package or other recent piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 Now, a long list of Democrats, including our next guest, Senator \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2 , say you are doing this to give billionaires tax cuts at the cost of cutting things like Medicaid and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 Are there cuts to those programs in this Senate framework?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: No one in Medicaid, Medicare, and Social Security is losing any of their benefits that are due to them. What we're trying to do is cut the waste and fraud out of any of those programs. President Trump has been rock-solid on saying we're not cutting benefits. It's just talking points.\par \par \uc2 And as far as the Democrats saying we're trying to give tax cuts to the billionaires and millionaires, we're extending the current policy that the American people are working underneath right now. So, if we're just extending it, we're not changing it.\par \par \uc2 We have two options here, Shannon. Either it expires at the end of this year and if it does, then every American regardless of your working class, regardless of your status will receive a $4 trillion tax increase across the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, everybody will -- will -- will burden that -- will carry that cross. If we extend it, then the current tax policy stays in place.\par \par \uc2 And what we are trying to do in the Senate, working with the White House and working with our friends in the House is saying we don't want the tax policy to expire. We want to extend it and make it permanent, so businesses that are investing in us, families that are planning their taxes can understand that they don't have to worry about if it's going to expire in one year, two years, one month or eight years. It's going to be permanent for long term.\par \par \uc2 So how is it that, I would ask my Democrats, are we giving billionaires and millionaires tax breaks when we're extending the current tax policy that they worked underneath the last four years with the disastrous economy that Biden had us in.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. We got to go, but I really quickly want to ask you about this. The House Budget chair on the other side of the hill, \uc2 Jodey Arrington\uc2 , Republican, obviously, said the Senate framework is unserious and disappointing.\par \par \uc2 Quickly, how do you guys get on the same page?\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Well, President Trump, the White House or -- and the House and the Senate, we're working every day together. We're very close. We want to deliver this one big, beautiful, glamorous bill that President Trump has been talking about for the American people, so we can put the DOGE cuts in there, we can get fiscal responsibility under control, and we can look towards to balance our budget.\par \par \uc2 And so, Jodey is a friend. We will work together and we will work the differences out.\par \par \uc2 The good thing is, it's a thoughtful conversation that both chambers, just like our Founding Fathers wanted it to have a different opinion, eventually, we'll come together underneath the leadership of President Trump and deliver the promises that we made to the American people. It was the reason why we're in power because of their trust in our leadership.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We'll see how those differences get worked out across the Hill.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 MULLIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining me now, Delaware Democrat Senator \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Always great to have you back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS COONS\uc2  (D-DE): Thank you, Shannon. Great to be on with you this morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, while you guys wrangle over the budget and there were many amendments and fights as this thing went through the night, we also have this against the backdrop of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and of DOGE trying to cut what they say is waste in government and -- and I know that you have strong feelings about that.\par \par \uc2 So, I want to play something from Steve Davis. He's one of the executives who have left his family, left his business for a time to do this for free, to come try to root these things out.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he said about why he and these other executives are doing it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE DAVIS, DOGE CHIEF OPERATING OFFICER: The reason I'm here which is probably for many is that I think the goal is incredibly inspiring. I think most of the taxpayers in the country would agree that in order to have the count -- the country going bankrupt would be a very bad thing and therefore the country going not bankrupt is a good thing, that all of us are willing to kind of put our lives on hold in order to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He's literally a rocket scientist. He's got a Ph.D. in economics. Why wouldn't we want people like that at the table trying to help?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, I have no objection to folks who are bringing their skills and talents to try and strengthen and modernize the federal government.\par \par \uc2 The thing that I've been speaking out against is the way they're being untruthful. Frankly, lying about the amount of waste, fraud, and abuse in Social Security. President Trump's own acting Social Security Administrator, a man named Lee Dudek, just recently testified that less than one-tenth of one percent of all the Social Security payments made are erroneous or false or made in error.\par \par \uc2 And President Trump, when he spoke to Congress, went on at great length about how many people are on the Social Security rolls who are 200 years old, 250 years old. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has called Social Security a Ponzi scheme. And the truth is, nobody who is in the Social Security system of an age over 115 gets any payments.\par \par \uc2 And as Lee Dudek said, there are very, very, very few false or erroneous or wasteful payments. And so what I hear from Delawareans about, Shannon, is their alarm about these suggestions that somehow Social Security has to be cut because it's full of waste, fraud, and abuse.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, you heard that --\par \par \uc2 COONS: So if talented people are coming to help find waste, fraud, and abuse, I'd like them to be honest about where it exists and to work in a bipartisan way to address those issues.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, would you sit down with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 ? We would be happy to broker a conversation. I know that you have real problems with things that you've said that haven't been truthful, but do you see, I mean, no one is losing more money doing this than \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . Would you sit down in a room with him to say, here are my ideas, here's where I think you're getting it wrong, where can we find common ground? Because I know you work across the aisle and have strong friendships across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 COONS: I do. I do, and look, I was proud to join a bipartisan bill this week led by Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Maria Cantwell\uc2 . Seven senior Republicans joined this bill to pull back the power to impose tariffs unilaterally. President Trump announced tariffs this week on almost every country on Earth, including many with which we have a trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 What my colleague Senator Mullin just said about how President Trump is rebalancing trade and he's going after those that have a trade surplus with us doesn't account for the fact that he's slapping tariffs on every country, including our closest partners and allies. That's going to raise prices for middle Americans. They're going to pay more for fruits and vegetables, gas, cars, furniture, clothing. It's going to make America less affordable, not more affordable, which was a key campaign promise of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and I do want to get back to tariffs, but before we leave DOGE, one more thing there, because you talked about Social Security, and I get what you're saying. There are -- we're told, millions of people with those, you know, 200-plus-aged old people. You're saying they're not getting payments.\par \par \uc2 COONS: But they're not getting payments, correct.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They may be there. So there's another executive who is working to go through this with DOGE as well, and he's put together numbers showing that there are 1.3 million people who are in the country illegally that are receiving some level of Medicaid. If that number holds true, is that a concern to you that you believe we should be cutting?\par \par \uc2 COONS: I'd be happy to look at that and to see what data they have. The other issue that I'm going to push back on my colleague Senator Mullin about is the House budget bill includes $800 billion in Medicaid cuts. And so as the House and the Senate --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But again, they say that's not just services. They say they hope that will be in fraud and waste, but the President and the Speaker have committed to not cutting actual benefits for anyone. So fair to say that? That's been their representation.\par \par \uc2 COONS: It would be great if they didn't cut benefits, but I don't believe the math works. I don't think you can find $800 billion in just waste, fraud, and abuse in Medicaid and not cut people's benefits.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's go back to tariffs because you were talking about that. There are a number of American industries and businesses who say they're actually happy about what's happening. They know there's going to be short- term pain, but they say they've been throttled by what's been happening with foreign tariffs, including from some of our closest allies. Here are some of those businesses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN PANNEBECKER, AUTO WORKERS FOR TRUMP FOUNDER: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  members, and I brought 20 of them with me. They're sitting right over here. We support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's policies on tariffs 100%. So, Mr. President, we can't thank you enough.\par \par \uc2 ETHAN LANE, \uc2 NATIONAL CATTLEMEN'S BEEF ASSOCIATION\uc2 : Our hope here is that the president can use this tool to square up the deck and get us a little bit of a better deal with some of these trading partners so that we're not balancing the backs of these different trade balances on U.S. farmers and ranchers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so some autoworkers, some farmers, some ranchers. What do you say to them? They think that this is going to actually help them, that they've been at a very unfair disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 COONS: I would say to them that targeted tariffs that are clearly focused on a few countries where we have bad trade practices, deep trade imbalances, can be smart economic policy and can help protect American farmers and ranchers and American manufacturers. That's not what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing. He's slapping massive tariffs on almost every country in the world, and it's hard to explain or justify some of the tariffs he's imposing.\par \par \uc2 In the coming weeks and months, when millions of Americans see their 401(k)s dissolve, the stock market lost 10% just in the last two days of last week. And when hundreds of millions of Americans pay more for everything that they need for daily living, the groceries that they buy and the food that they put on the table and the cost of housing, I think they're going to be upset. And I think we're going to see long-term, broad economic damage, not for a few targeted industries or companies, but across the entire American landscape.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And if that happens, there may be a midterm price to pay for Republicans. So we will see.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Yes, it will.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But before you go, I want to make sure we get you to respond to this. You're a longtime defender of President Biden. You were co-chair of his national campaign. This week, we're learning something coming out in a new book. They have a lot of quotes from former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  talking about how much the president deteriorated during his term. He was tired, confused, didn't understand the arguments that President Trump was making on issues like inflation that he was, quote, "out of touch."\par \par \uc2 The "Daily Beast" says this. "Klain's new comments suggest that the longtime political advisor and other members of Biden's team were increasingly aware of the president's mental and physical deterioration, even as they continued to project outward appearances of confidence in his abilities." You co-chaired his campaign. Does that include you?\par \par \uc2 COONS: I was shocked at what I saw on the debate stage, the performance President Trump and President Biden had debating each other. I had frankly never seen what that quote suggests about President Biden's abilities. But frankly, Shannon, I haven't taken the time to read whatever's come out about this. I've been very busy this past week. As you mentioned, we stayed up all night Friday night debating and voting against the DOGE cuts, against the Republican budget bill, and speaking out against the tariffs. I'm happy to come back and do a future interview about whatever quotes come out in future books.\par \par \uc2 But right now, I'm focused a lot less on retired private citizen \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and a lot more on how President Trump's tariffs are going to hurt middle Americans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Senator Coons, come back soon. Always appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Attorney General Pam Bondi joins us exclusively to talk about the Trump administration's big win at the Supreme Court Friday night.\par \par \uc2 But up next, the popular social media app TikTok gets a reprieve as talks over selling the Chinese-owned company break down. Our Sunday panel is here to take a closer look at how the President's tough tariffs may be affecting that deal.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: Why would he do something like this? Because the Republican Party is in the vice grip of a group of very greedy billionaires. They have a billionaire bubble. And they say, yeah, all this money from tariffs could help make our taxes lower. It's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  blasting President Trump's wide-ranging tariff rollout this week, a move that sent the markets plummeting. It's time to talk about it with our Sunday group, "Axios," "Politico," Politics Reporter, Stef Kight, Marie Harf, Fox News Contributor and former State Department Spokesperson, Heritage President, Kevin Roberts, and \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , Fox News Radio Political Analyst and Jewish Insider Editor-in-Chief.\par \par \uc2 So let's continue our tariff conversation. "Wall Street Journal" writes this Friday, ". the shocks threaten to undermine Trump's central appeal in last year's election. His vow to bring down prices is in jeopardy. The market fallout affects not only active investors, but also millions of Americans with 401(k) retirement accounts.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, you heard Senator Coons talking about this. That was the promise. I'm going to make things better for you economically and right now, that's the opposite of what's happening.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ROBERTS, HERITAGE PRESIDENT: Well, we're a few days into this. No one wants the market turbulence, but I would expect an economic quarter or two of that. I think once the reciprocal tariffs, and I would emphasize reciprocity on this, would be paired with deregulation, which the administration is doing a good job of, and fiscal restraint and tax cuts, that by the end of this calendar year, you would begin to see, especially from the standpoint of the everyday hardworking American, that benefit.\par \par \uc2 I think it's really important for us to look at this from the lens of the everyday American, as opposed to looking at this through the lens of Washington.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Marie, do you give it objectively a little bit of time? How much time until GOP has to pivot? There are already Republicans who've signed onto this measure that \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  mentioned, that there are seven of them at last count, and others publicly talking about joining. That would give them some power to override some of these tariffs.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: That's right, and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  called this the largest peacetime tax hike in U.S. history. Noted liberal \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , right? Well, noted not fan of maybe President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But, look, Republicans are very nervous about this. We know, if you look at economists across the political spectrum, small businesses will close because of these tariffs. The American people will pay more for almost everything.\par \par \uc2 Other countries are furious at us, slapping even greater tariffs on us. These are not reciprocal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But some coming to the table.\par \par \uc2 HARF: These are not based on economic reality. This is a myth that these will create economic growth, and for a president who campaigned on average Americans paying less, we know that tariffs do the opposite. Some conservative estimates say that American families will pay up to $5,000 more a year.\par \par \uc2 Don't look at your retirement account right now. So, I think the American people are willing to give this administration a little bit of time to see if these tariffs do what they say they will do. But economists, Republican, Democratic economists, people across the political spectrum, absolutely believe that these will do the opposite. That Americans will suffer. They will have less access to goods. They will pay more for what they have access to.\par \par \uc2 And you can see Republicans across the country getting very nervous about this. This is a tax hike on the American people. And the math coming out of the White House just makes no sense. It is punitive, and it is not going to do what they say it's going to do. And there will be political consequences for it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We shall see. Only time, truly, is going to tell on this. But one of the things that may have been impacted by the tariffs is this TikTok deal the President was really hoping to get done.\par \par \uc2 The "AP" reports Beijing hits the brakes on the deal Thursday after Trump announced wide-ranging tariffs. ByteDance. Representatives called the White House to indicate that China would no longer approve the deal until there could be negotiations about trade and tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , JEWISH INSIDER EDITOR-IN-CHIEF: Yeah, look, there's a battle between sort of the national security hawks in the Republican Party. Keep in mind that the ban on TikTok passed overwhelmingly in Congress.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: And the President is now saying he can indefinitely extend the negotiating timeframe, which is not written in the law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No, that's not the way the Supreme Court saw the law.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yeah, so there's going to be a big political test. There's a lot of concern. We know TikTok is run by the Chinese communists. It's been seen by a lot of lawmakers on Capitol Hill as a tool to spread anti-American propaganda. There was hope that there could be a deal to bring it under American control. It looks like the tariffs have stalled that process.\par \par \uc2 But, look, this is going to end up becoming a clash between the people who worry about America's national security. There's a reason it had so much political support the TikTok ban did. Trump is -- I think Trump likes TikTok because he views it as helping get him elected. There's a political self-interest at play. But, boy, there are a lot of national security officials in Washington that are very concerned that this is getting kicked down the road. And there's certainly a lot of legal implications to play as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, what President Trump posted on Truth Social on Friday in part that he thought the tariffs were working to get this deal done. He says, we hope to continue working in good faith with China, who I understand is not very happy about our reciprocal tariffs, but this proves that tariffs are the most powerful economic tool and very important to our national security.\par \par \uc2 Do they come to the table and negotiate in a way that benefits us because they want this TikTok deal?\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICS REPORTER, AXIOS: I mean, that's the big question, right? And it's -- we seem to see that the tariffs are going to have wide- ranging impacts on how the U.S. deals with foreign countries and ways outside of trade, potentially. You look at the TikTok deal seeming to fall apart a little bit. It shows that nations may not be willing to come to the table because of these tariffs and the impact that it has on their economies.\par \par \uc2 They may be less willing. They may have more leverage now to get their way out of these deals as opposed to Trump having the leverage that he thinks he has in some of these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, speaking of leverage, I want to touch on this point. There's this been the showdown internally with the GOP about allowing new members of Congress who have new babies to vote by proxy so they can stay out.\par \par \uc2 There are those who are very worried about this, think that it's going to be abused. The president said on Air Force One, I think it's good if you want to stay home with a baby and call on your vote.\par \par \uc2 Well, after that, Speaker Mike Johnson, who has opposed this idea, tweeted the next day and said, thank you, real \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , for your leadership, quote, Mike, you have my proxy on proxy voting.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, how does this work itself out? Because everybody wants what the president's got, which is his approval on whether this happens or not.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ROBERTS, PRESIDENT, THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION: Well, you have two goods that are colliding. One, of course, is the beautiful witness to motherhood that the female members of Congress are providing.\par \par \uc2 But also this sticky thing called the Constitution. Article One, Section 5, as you know well, and you simply can't have proxy voting in Congress. The Speaker has been 100 percent right about that.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, with the president way again, I think there's going to be some compromise. There's going to have to be limited usage of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Quickly, Marie. Does the GOP risk missing a moment, but they could appeal to the moms out there?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Well, we will see. They've done a lot of things in the past few months that I think are politically very questionable. So we will see how this plays out. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his administration has found many ways to circumvent parts of the Constitution that they do not like.\par \par \uc2 It will be interesting if on this one that would help working mothers, they suddenly are going back to the exact letter of the law. We will see.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, panel, do not go far. We've got much more for you later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our exclusive interview with Attorney General Pam Bondi. She's here live on the legal pushback from federal judges over the Trump administration's efforts to deport suspected gang members. She's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN\uc2 , NATIONAL CONSTITUTION CENTER, CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER: The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  issues an unambiguous order and the president defies it. I think everyone agrees or shouldn't be. That's a definition that I would embrace. That would be a constitutional crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So that's \uc2 Jeffrey Rosen\uc2  of the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 , weighing in about this debate, how or when the U.S. ever gets to the point of an actual constitutional crisis. It's a phrase that someone in Washington and beyond like to throw around amidst a host of legal challenges to the new administration.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the person at the helm of most of those legal fights. Attorney General Pam Bondi, welcome to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: Thank you. I'm so happy to be here with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Glad to have you. So let's start there.\par \par \uc2 Is there any scenario in which the president gets an order or a final judgment from the Supreme Court in which he doesn't agree with it and he doesn't comply?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Yes. The president is going to comply with the law. He was overwhelmingly elected by an overwhelming majority of the United States citizens to be our commander in chief. And that's what he's been doing.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, just since January 20th, we've had over 170 lawsuits filed against us. That should be the constitutional crisis right there. Fifty injunctions, they're popping up every single day, trying to control his executive power, trying to control where he believes our tax dollars should be allocated.\par \par \uc2 And saying he won by an overwhelming majority is so important because that's what the American people want, what President Trump campaigned on and what he won on. And he's implementing that agenda at a rapid speed. None of us can keep up with him every single day. And so it's just we're going after all of these lawsuits. We're defending them all. We just got a great win. And we'll continue to fight every day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You did. Friday night, five to four at the Supreme Court. You got a win with respect to cuts at the Department of Education.\par \par \uc2 But the court did go against you on issues of foreign aid being paid out. How do you think these issues of executive power, when they get to the court, ultimately on the merits, will go? Because you know the court has told executives know when it comes to things like student loans or recess appointments.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Sure. I think we're going to be very successful on all these. So the one you just brought up, the DEI, that was Department of Education. So we represent all the various agencies. And all of these 170 lawsuits have to do with all the agencies across the board.\par \par \uc2 This particular one, though, was very important because the court said that President Trump can control where our tax dollars go and what they're allocated for. And we do not have to spend them on DEI.\par \par \uc2 And that's what was happening. So the ramifications of that are huge for our country. And that impacts every single agency to give you an example of a case, \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 . So we had its military readiness. And that's one of the president's top agendas as is Pete Hegseth.\par \par \uc2 So in the district court, a district court in D.C. ruled against us on military readiness, meaning gender dysphoria, ruled against us. We appealed that to the circuit court and we won.\par \par \uc2 The second we won, within minutes, Shannon, in California, they filed an identical lawsuit and we lost. So that's what we're up against.\par \par \uc2 It's basically a game of whack-a-mole with these district court judges around the country who have a tremendous amount of power. They believe they do. But that's why we're appealing all of these cases, of course, up to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. They're very busy. So you have several things that are sitting there now. So while you had the win on Friday night, a lower court judge on Friday night ruled against you with respect to, and I'm going to read this because I want to make sure I get his name right.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Kilmar Armando Abrego Garcia, who is somebody who was living in Maryland. He was under an order not to be deported because of fears or danger that he may face back in El Salvador.\par \par \uc2 The government has admitted, there was an error in deporting him. But this judge has said, you've got a deadline midnight Monday to get him back from El Salvador.\par \par \uc2 There have been allegations that he is a gang member, that is involved in human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 This is what the judge said. That's just chatter in my view. I haven't been given any evidence in a court of law when someone has accused of membership in such a violent and predatory organization. It comes in the form of an indictment, a complaint, a criminal proceeding that has robust process so we can assess the fact.\par \par \uc2 So, would it be helpful in making your case, not only to the court, but in the court of public opinion to show what you have explaining these allegations against him?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Yes. And to explain to you, ICE has testified, members of ICE, that he is an MS-13 gang member. And the defense, his lawyers, have argued, well, he should be here because he was studying to be an electrician.\par \par \uc2 Well, let me give you a comparison. I was just in our home state of Florida this week where we arrested three MS-13 gang members. These three, it was a cold case, 10 years old. They were charged with violently, violently stabbing an individual over a hundred times as part of their gang initiation.\par \par \uc2 One was arrested in Fort Lauderdale. One was arrested. He fled to Minnesota. Great police work, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , all of the agencies together. Border patrol even caught him. One there. And then the other, ready for this, was a drywall hanger in the villages in Florida. Hanging drywall.\par \par \uc2 So the argument that because these people are living among us, these illegal aliens from El Salvador, means they're not part of a gang, that's how they're hiding. That's how they're succeeding.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the days all run together, last week, we were in half an hour from here, from where we're sitting right now in Virginia, and arrested another extremely violent gang member. He was the leader for the East Coast, one of the top three leaders for our entire country. And this guy was living in Virginia in a nice neighborhood. There were wreaths on the door. There were children's bicycles outside his neighbor's home. That's so frightening. And that's how they insulate themselves.\par \par \uc2 So we have to rely on what ICE says. We have to rely on what Homeland Security says. They're our clients. And I firmly believe in the work they are doing and we're going to make America safe again. That was President Trump's directive to all of us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Abrego Garcia, that was not charged with any of these things, stabbings, murders, anything.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He was never charged or convicted with anything. So, is there a missed opportunity there for the government with this order to bring him back? Even if you could, if you did, would he then face charges here?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Well, yes. And our jails are full of people. We want to send these people out of our country when possible.\par \par \uc2 When we have charges of violent murder, we're going to keep -- the ones that are here, we're going to keep them here. We're going to charge them. We're going to prosecute them. The one in Florida, I just mentioned, we have the victims' families in court with us. And that gives them closure.\par \par \uc2 But the best thing to do is to get these people out of our country. And when that first plane left and landed in El Salvador, there were 261 reasons why Americans were safer.\par \par \uc2 We have to look out for the safety of our country, first and foremost. And that is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's directive and we're going to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there a reason that the attorney who argued this case on a Friday has been put on suspension? Is it because you don't think he's zealously advocated enough? Did he mishandle the case?\par \par \uc2 I mean, he had to admit, as has been done in this pleading, the government made a mistake in deporting this guy.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: So it's a pending matter right now. He was put on administrative leave by Todd Blanche on Saturday. And I firmly said on day one, I issued a memo that you are to vigorously advocate on behalf of the United States. Our client in this matter was Homeland Security -- is Homeland Security. He did not argue. He shouldn't have taken the case. He shouldn't have argued it, if that's what he was going to do. He's on administrative leave now.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, you have to vigorously argue on behalf of your client. As lawyers, we know that. That would be a defense attorney walking in, conceiving something in a criminal matter. That would never happen in this country. And so he's on administrative leave now and we'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I want to try to get to some other cases. The death penalty, this week, you announced that you want the prosecution to seek that with Luigi Mangione, accused of gunning down UnitedHealthcare CEO, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , in Midtown Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" reacted to that with this line, how Trump loses Gen Z.\par \par \uc2 I was in an airport a couple of weeks ago with a young woman that had on a sweatshirt with his face on it. And he was portrayed as a religious icon. There was a halo, kind of like a saint.\par \par \uc2 What's your take on how the younger generation sees him as a bit of a folk hero and "POLITICO's" assessment that it may cost you politically?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Shannon, the president's directive was very clear. We are to seek the death penalty when possible.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it hadn't been done in four years. If there was -- I was a capital prosecutor, I tried death penalty cases throughout my career. If there was ever a death case, this is one. This guy is charged with hunting down a CEO, a father of two, a married man, hunting him down and executing him. Yes, I feel like these young people have lost their way. I was receiving death threats for seeking the death penalty on someone who is charged with - with an execution of a CEO.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue to do the right thing. We're not going to be deterred by political motives. You know, I've seen a protester walking down the street here "Free Luigi." I mean this guy's charged with a violent crime, and we're going to seek the death penalty whenever possible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, another headline, there's chatter about President Trump seeking a third term. He has said, quote, "There are methods." Is this just one of his epic trolls, or does he, or do you believe there is a method, short of amending the Constitution, by which he could seek a third term?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: President Trump has served one full term. He's on his second full term. He's a very smart man. And - and we - I wish we could have him for 20 years as our president. But I think he's going to be finished probably after this term.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Probably?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Well, the Constitution would - we'd have to look at the Constitution for that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It would be a heavy lift.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: It'd be a heavy lift.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And whether you get two-thirds of the Senate and House to go along with you, but is that concession that that's really the only way to do it?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: It'd be a heavy lift. It'd be a heavy lift.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Attorney General, we thank you for your time. We hope you'll come back soon.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, our panel back with a growing look at tensions between the U.S. and Russia as skeptics doubt \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has any intentions of reaching a peace deal. We'll discuss that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRILL DMITRIEV\uc2 , RUSSIAN ENVOY: There's no question that President Trump team not only stopped World War III from happening, but also had already achieved sizable progress on Ukraine resolution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's Russian Envoy \uc2 Kirill Dmitriev\uc2  after meeting with the Trump administration about finding a path to end the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We are back with our panel.\par \par \uc2 Josh, word this work that the - that the president was, quote, "pissed" at President Putin. And there are growing concerns about whether Putin wants the same thing that Americans want.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "JEWISH INSIDER EDITOR-IN-CHIEF AND FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, it doesn't look like it. I mean we were supposedly in a ceasefire moment and yet Russia continues to fire missiles at Kyiv, at Ukrainian cities, killing children. It looks like Russia is having its way. And I think it's encouraging that the president is seemingly kind of aware of that and is trying to sound a little bit tougher. But, boy, he is - I don't believe there were any tariffs imposed on Russia. I think that could have been a good moment to actually show that we're willing to put some punitive measures on Russia. And I think the big test is, when are we going to impose - you know, to have the Russian envoy into this country. I mean that's a change from the previous administration. I think the administration could use a page from the Reagan playbook, trust but verify, and perhaps put some more tougher measures against Russia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and we have this where the secretary of state has just been over in Brussels. There have been all these conversations with allies and other people who clearly have a stake in what's going on between Russia and Ukraine. The AP reports this Friday. "European NATO allies in Canada on Friday said they are willing to ramp up defense spending but are cool on American demands for the size of their military budgets, particularly given U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's readiness to draw closer to Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Stef, that's the way much of the world views their relationship.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, "AXIOS" POLITICS REPORTER AND "AXIOS HILL LEADERS" NEWSLETTER CO-AUTHOR: Right. And now we're hearing that - that the president is upset with Putin. But earlier we've really just seen his ire taken out on - on Ukrainian President Zelenskyy, and that whole fallout from the meeting earlier this year, the way Trump panel that has made a lot of our allies worried about the U.S.' dedication to actually ensuring that Ukraine is protected, Trump's willingness to push back on Putin.\par \par \uc2 And even though we're hearing about this frustration with Russia, we're still not seeing the kind of response that Trump had towards Ukraine when he was upset with Zelenskyy. We're not seeing that same kind of language, and we also aren't seeing, you know, any real follow-through or any real actions, to Josh's point, we're not seeing sanctions. Trump has floated this idea of sanctions on Russian oil. We'll see whether he actually moves word with that.\par \par \uc2 But President Putin is actually acting in the - in the way that Zelenskyy warned Trump about, that he's not someone to be trusted, he's not willing to come to the table, he's not really willing to end the war. He's not interested in that. So, we'll see if Trump, you know, ends up reading the room and reads the actions Putin is taking and actually changes his strategy there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Kevin, we do know, there are a host of sanctions on Russia. There have been questions about actual enforcements of them across administrations.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ROBERTS, HERITAGE PRESIDENT: Let's - that is correct. And we need to remember, the only reason we're even having this conversation about bringing this war to a conclusion is because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I would speculate that the reason that tariffs have not been put on Russia from last week's announcement is because President Trump and Secretary Rubio are trying to maximize the leverage they have over them. And it may be that part of that leverage, Shannon, is doing a better job than the Biden administration of enforcing those sanctions.\par \par \uc2 I still expect this war to come to a conclusion in a way that's satisfactory for the everyday American, most importantly. But to the point about the Europeans, I just have to say, if they want to stay in the American defense umbrella, they need to pull their own weight and stop wagging their finger about us spending our money that's hard-earned by the everyday American. And I'm really grateful to the Trump administration for resetting that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What you say about that, Marie? I mean you worked at the State Department. You know how tricky it is to try to navigate these very complex international situation.\par \par \uc2 MARIA HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The Europeans don't believe that an American defense umbrella exists anymore. We see European NATO allies who are canceling contracts for American planes, for other weapons they were going to buy from American manufacturers, because they do not trust us anymore. And I don't think there is any plan on the table that Russia will accept that will - that will preserve Ukraine's territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Putin sending missiles, sending bombs to Kyiv, to Zelenskyy's hometown, right? It is very clear what the Russians are doing here. And President Trump, who's talked about using tariffs as negotiating on a whole host of issues, put tariffs on uninhabited islands, and did not put them on Russia. He is - the administration is deporting at least one that we know of Russian national activists who's opposed Putin.\par \par \uc2 So, this is part of a broader strategy. A part of a broader close knitting of ties between Putin and Trump that makes a lot of Republicans, or at least used to, incredibly nervous. Negotiations are challenging, right? Ukraine will likely have to give something up. The question is, how much Trump will push them to give up in the service of Vladmir Putin.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well we've got to get everyone back to the table actually having a conversation.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So that's been in fits and starts. We'll see what this week brings.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 OK, up next, millions of Americans are dealing with this, the aftermath of deadly storms that have been going on for days. We're going to take you live on the ground in Nashville for an update.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Destructive storms and floods have been ripping through parts of the South and Midwest for days, killing at least 16 people.\par \par \uc2 Madison Scarpino is live in Nashville, Tennessee, with more on the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Out of all of the deaths that we know about so far, ten of those happened here in Tennessee. And while the Nashville area was mostly spared over the last 24 hours, the severe storms hit other parts of the states very hard. Torrential rain stalled over Memphis yesterday, causing severe flash flooding. Water swamping parts of Interstate 40. There were several water rescues in the city according to officials.\par \par \uc2 And just north of Memphis, in Dyersburg, Tennessee, people dealt with severe - several tornado warnings. Many residents weren't taking any chances after days of daunting weather and were crowding into tornado shelters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's just been so many tornadoes lately, it's just scary. Yes, devastation. We've had a lot of trees, big trees, and limbs down. So, I have to go pick up my momma and two sisters to bring up here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: Over in Arkansas, Little Rock had its first ever flash flood emergency yesterday. The state also had intense lightning and nearly 80- mile-an-hour winds. Little Rock police found a five-year-old dead in a home because of this weather.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, officials forced people in multiple towns to leave their homes yesterday because waterways were overflowing. At least one building was seen floating down the Kentucky River. Authorities had to use boats to access several homes surrounded by floodwater to help with evacuations. And the severe weather hitting the south and Midwest thankfully is set to calm down today but, Shannon, it's going to be a long cleanup process for those dealing with the impacts across several states.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Madison, thank you very much for the updates.\par \par \uc2 And a quick note, a new episode of my podcast "Livin the Bream" drops today. I talk with \uc2 NBA\uc2  player Jonathan Isaac about his faith and clothing gear. He says he's aimed at spreading a positive message in a culture that doesn't always value that. "Livin the Bream," wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 And a heads up, something special coming up. FOX NEWS SUNDAY from the heartland airs next Sunday. We are hitting the road. We're talking to farmers about the impact of recent tariffs and other moves by this White House, and special guests. Tune in next Sunday. We'll see you then.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020250406el4600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Dem Policies Have Hollowed Out The Middle Class; Trump Is Making The Middle Class Great Again; Commuters Haunted By Crime Fears; IRS To Lay Off More Than 20,000 Employees; Eight Killed In Tornadoes And Flash Flooding This Week; Rage Culture Rising Among Teens; Teens Are Committing Crimes All Over U.S.; \uc2 Disney\uc2  Remakes Turn Happiness To Horror; Screamboat Makes Steamboat Willie A Horror Flick; Kamala's Word Of The Day: Courage\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Kevin Corke, Raymond Arroyo, Jason Chaffetz \par \uc2 7,295 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight, and all this week, has been busy news week. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Make a great weekend. The Ingraham Angle is now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle. Thank you, as always, for joining us. Now, if you have been checking your 401(k), maybe obsessively over the last few days, it's understandable that the steep decline has you worried. The market slide is real, but it is also temporary and the people I trust are right, when they urge you to hang tight, do not panic sell. But of course, the Democrats, who we know are incapable of raising the standard of living of middle class Americans, their only response is to fuel the panic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): We've seen close to 8 percent or 9 percent of the stock market drop. That means a lot of people's retirement plans are going out the window.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a bloodbath in the markets again today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): For our families, for small businesses, for small farmers in my state, this is devastating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And of course, they were quick to dismiss at the same time that gang busters job number that came out today which beat expectations at 228,000 jobs created in March. Remember, that's without the tax cut that's coming.\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm here tonight to tell you when you hear the usual suspects predict the worst or claim that Trump's approach is ruinous. Well, remember this. These were many of the same people who told Americans to be patient when China began to devour our manufacturing sector. So why would we ever take economic advice from them? They told us that China would become more open, more free, less of an adversary if we allowed them to join the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  back in 2000 and they certainly waved off concerns regarding American industries, they weren't going to shrink, who cares they said.\par \par \uc2 I mean, after all, it's only going to be what made in China Happy Meal toys and \uc2 Nike\uc2 's maybe. Well, you fast forward today, and here's where things stand. America has gone from being the dominant exporter of steel to number four in exporters behind China, Canada and others. And now, if we do nothing, China will dominate in everything from tools, computers, solar panels, shipbuilding, drones, satellites and yes, of course, pharmaceuticals.\par \par \uc2 Now don't even get me started on what China is doing with artificial intelligence, quantum computing and biotech. So Trump's economic reset, it comes after 30 plus years of disastrous economic policy that made a small subset of America very, very rich, while the middle class fell further behind.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN PANNEBECKER, AUTO WORKERS FOR TRUMP LEADER: Nobody was crying these crocodile tears when they were closing the Mound Road Engine plant that Chrysler had in Macomb County or the Romeo Engine plant in Romeo, Michigan, now that we're trying to bring these products back, these components and these vehicles back to the United States, all of a sudden, all the so- called experts are saying it's going to plunge us into recession and be terrible for the economy. If they're crying, I think we're doing something right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As for the Republicans who are giving up on Trump's policies after two days, be careful who your anti-tariff bedfellows are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): It's the first time we have seen some Republicans buck \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They've all too often gone along with him, even when they know his policies are dead wrong. So I welcome it, and I hope it could pass. It's a bipartisan bill and it shows you how bad these tariffs are when even Republicans who have been so afraid to break with Trump, some of the leading Republicans are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Anyway, that dinosaur, he gives us a really bad case of whiplash on the tariffs issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: My views on trade are probably closer to President Trump's than they were to President Obama's or President Bush's. I think our trade laws have hurt working people. The tariffs, if they wanted to do something about auto should have been much more pinpointed and isolated than this massive assault on American families, all to help the billionaires.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. At one point today, it was so weird, he just started reading on live TV.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How worried are you that all of this could push the U.S. economy into a recession?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I'm very worried about it, Dana. This is one of the most disastrous and poorly thought out policies that the Trump administration has done thus far. What it does is it makes everybody just worry about spending money. Businesses hold back on spending, on training, on hiring people, and it's so poorly done. Do you know, Dana, this policy taxes penguins, but not Putin?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Penguins, but not Putin. I don't even know what that man's saying. So look, I'm here tonight to tell you ignore the Dow Doomsdayers. Remember, these are the people who get to trade in the market, even though they have a lot of effect on the market with their legislation. I'm not listening to any of these people. You have to think beyond the short run. I think the CEO of United seems to get it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT KIRBY\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 : To me, what's happening there is, there is a genuine desire from the administration to actually create more careers like we create here at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  for middle class Americans, to strengthen the middle class here in the country. And I think that's a laudable goal. You may disagree fairly with the way we get there and the tactics to get there, but the goal of creating great middle class careers, I think, is a laudable one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, the United CEO is bullish on America, good stuff. So when the dust settles, we are going to once again have a dynamic economy where companies and their workers who make things in America will have advantages. It certainly will be more fair. So people who build cars, people who forge steel, will get paid more, and hedge fund managers, they're going to do just fine as well.\par \par \uc2 A friend today put it this way, would Joe six pack rather have $62 oil or a temporary stock market loss, because the drop in oil prices and the 10 year Treasury that ends up immediate as a tax cut for Main Street.\par \par \uc2 So at the end of this, we're going to have a stronger and a sounder economy. We won't be barely keeping ourselves afloat as we have been for years borrowing trillions at a time. Right now, the media is covering this because it's easier, like a tariff story, but it's not. It is much bigger than that. Soon there will be new tax policies, new regulatory policies and then better schools, less crime and fewer foreign workers to lower American wages.\par \par \uc2 Now that may upset the globalists who love keeping wages low and love outsourcing jobs to Vietnam and elsewhere, but it is music to the ears of all those Americans who saw their factories close, and their families ripped apart in economic despair. The working class vote, I think, is going to determine the 2028 election. And the future, I think it belongs to the working people who swung the last election.\par \par \uc2 The Dow is going to come back, the NASDAQ too. But if we kept going the way we were going, America as an independent, sovereign country would be finished. Trump's moves are bold, yes, but they're not calamitous. Calamitous is the word that describes how the uniparty ran our economic policy for the past 30 years. They were wrong then, and they're wrong now.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's 25 percent auto tariffs is going to drive up the price of foreign vehicles. So if you're in the market for a new car, we've got a simple solution. Buy American. Joining me now, \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , President and CEO of the great \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2 . Jim, thank you so much for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now we're seeing a difference in how automakers are responding to these tariffs. GM told Fox Business it's ramping up production in the U.S., several 100 temporary workers. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , though laying off 900, which way is Ford going?\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, FORD: Well, thank you, Laura, for having me on. First of all, we build more vehicles in the U.S. than any other brand. We export more and we have more factory workers. So we never left the U.S., and our plan is really simple to offer employee pricing, basically the price I pay or anyone at the company pays for all Americans. We believe this is a moment, afford to differentiate itself for us to really highlight the American car industry at a moment when it's being discussed in media like yourself, Laura, and we think this is our opportunity to gain some business.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jim, the Chinese auto company, BYD, is dominating the market now in Australia, one of our strong allies. So, President Trump was obviously very annoyed by the fact that our cars don't get into the markets, even in countries that are allies, military allies, economic partners. So will these tariffs, in your view, help make that situation more competitive abroad for Ford?\par \par \uc2 FARLEY: Well, we're really thrilled to gauge in the White House in a serious discussion about fair trade. And the Chinese are now the largest exporter of automobiles in our industry, and that's very recent phenomenon. For example, over 20 percent of the vehicles sold in Mexico are built in China. So this is an incredibly important topic. We're very grateful for the President's leadership and the chance to work with his administration to compete on a fair playing field, which these tariffs, especially those coming from China and also other trading partners, as you mentioned, to really put us on an equal footing so we can compete in a home market in a fair way.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jim, we talked about this at the open of the show, but it's not just the tariffs that put American manufacturers at an unfair disadvantage. It's the massive subsidies, the market manipulation, the stealing of intellectual property. There's so many different things that give these industries in other countries, whether its steel or aluminum, automobile, pharmaceuticals. So this is a broad -- broader look at a big problem that's developed really over the last 30 plus years, is it not?\par \par \uc2 FARLEY: I've been in the industry for 40 years, Laura. I've seen this play out for four decades, whether it's non-tariff barriers or tax policy in those countries. You know, we -- we see all of the fair and unfair ways that our competition place globally. They know how important auto is to jobs in their country, and they're going to do everything they can to help those -- those competitors. And it's high time for us in our country to get serious. Now, we have to work out the details with the administration, and we're doing that, but I'm very encouraged that of that we will have a better chance for a fair and square fight, for the biggest fight we've ever seen in the auto industry in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jim, what about the auto parts that Ford needs to assemble the most vehicles in the United States, as you said, lot of those are made in other countries. Will these tariffs have an effect on you in that regard, and then hike prices up?\par \par \uc2 FARLEY: I'm really glad you asked this question, because this is really these -- the essential point of working closely with our leaders in D.C. on the details, because the details matter greatly. We have thousands of parts in our American made vehicles, and we want to keep them as affordable as possible. Some of those parts like wiring looms and others are especially kind of work, and as we work through this with administration, we're optimistic that we can find the right balance between affordability and a strong industry where we can add jobs to the industry In the coming months and years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jim, thank you so much for joining us on this very busy day and evening. Thanks so much. We'll check in with you as all this plays out. And the Trump administration is pressuring blue cities to crack down on the crime plaguing commuters. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy is here, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, people should not be frightened while commuting to and from work, whether they're riding busses in their own vehicles, whether they're traveling to get groceries, dropping off children, yet in big cities across the country, that's a reality. In Chicago, a man was attacked by a group of seven while he was riding the Red Line train. They cut his hand and robbed him. In Philly, a 17 year old was arrested for murdering another teen on the SEPTA bus. And in New York City this week, a crazed lunatic allegedly shoved someone onto the subway tracks. Two teens were stabbed on the subway platform. So crime on mass transit, it seems at least to be out of control, and that's why these blue cities are facing pressure from the Trump administration to crack down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: I'm here with the mayor because I do care about the city. I do want the city to be to function well. I want the \uc2 MTA\uc2  to function really well, and that's why the mayor and I are here together to partner up and figure out how we can work together to improve the ridership for those who take the subway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy. Mr. Secretary, thank you so much for joining us. I know Mayor Adams; you've worked with him on this. He made this big show, the subway being safe, and he says crime is down, but I know from so many friends of mine, mostly women who are on the subway regularly, some on a daily basis, they're still gripping their purses, carrying pepper spray whenever they ride it. What's the truth?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So the truth is, transit across America is dangerous, right? And these liberals, liberal mayors, they want everyone out of cars into trains, but they made the trains unsafe, so no one really wants to ride them. Listen, I know a lot of people in New York who they're OK ride in transit or the subway during the day, but if it's in the morning or if it's at night, even big men don't want to ride the subway at those times of night. And so the mayor has indicated they've made some progress. I've got other stats that will say they haven't, but the mayor has sent more law enforcement officers into the subway system.\par \par \uc2 But this is really a failure of the governor of New York, right? She's someone who wants to invest more in the \uc2 MTA\uc2 , which is the transit system in New York, and she really doesn't do anything to look at the bottom line of how they spend their money, how much of it goes to security, how much is to make it beautiful and safe. They have homelessness and crazy people that live and reside inside of subways, and so it's not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 And so we at the Department of Transportation, we actually partner with a lot of the transit systems around the country. We give them a lot of federal taxpayer money to help them run their systems. And our position is, if you don't clean up your act, we're not going to invest in you. If you do it well and you have beautiful trains that are on time, that are safe, we'll make those investments as taxpayers. But if you don't, we're going to pull the money, and we'll put it somewhere else. And again, they have a choice. I was telling the --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: -- transit authority and the governor, Laura, we need to send DOGE into the \uc2 MTA\uc2  and actually look at how they spend their money, look at the fraud, the waste and the abuse, pull it out and start to make these projects efficient and cost effective.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, again, this is a national story, because this is happening, and this is our taxpayer dollars in blue cities across America. But I want to move on to another issue, Mr. Secretary, which is what seems to be happening in the sometimes not so friendly skies. Passengers on board an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  plane evacuated in Augusta, Georgia, from the aircraft's wings. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Smelled like the brakes were burning or something. The cabin was filled with smoke, and we climbed out on the wing. Looks like everybody's safe and welcome to Augusta.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mr. Secretary, it seems like there is a lot of near mid-air collisions or close calls in Reagan airport, obviously the horrible nightmare disaster there. But what can you tell the American public about the safety of aviation today.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Listen, it's very simple. Flying on an airplane is the safest mode of transportation by far, bar none. It is the safest. And I think since DCA, Laura, it was the first time in 16 years, we had a major air disaster, and I think there's now a lot more focus on aviation. So if you know, smoke fills a cabin, and we land and people go out on the wing, it gets a lot more attention. But there are not more incidents right now in aviation than there were last year or in the last five years. Actually, there are less and so that doesn't mean, though, we don't look at what were the mistakes of the past. I mean, we had the prior administration. I mean, they had transit equity day, as there were near misses in the air, they were focused on changing the name of cockpit to flight deck, and, you know, airmen to aeronaut.\par \par \uc2 I mean, stupid things like that. They weren't doing the work of actually keeping people safe. And so, now I'm left with a situation where we actually have to fix it. We have to upgrade our system, make it brand new. We need more air traffic controllers. We have to retrain our air traffic controllers. It's a really big job, and maybe because it's such a big job, the last administration chose not to do it. It's not that they didn't know that it needed, needed a whole redo, but I'm going to do it.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has seen our plan to build a brand new state-of-the-art system. He's bought in. We're going to Congress to get the money. And listen, I think if we do this right, Laura, I don't know how much you fly, but I'm sick of delayed flights and --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A lot.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: -- canceled flights. It's happening way too much. And so if we get a brand new air traffic control system, it's not just safer, it is actually more efficient. So there will be less delays and less cancelations. People will move through the air in a more seamless fashion, and more people fly today than ever. And so we need this brand new system that's not 30 years old using floppy disks. It's actually brand new, the best technology that we can buy. And by the way, the air traffic controllers, they deserve to use brand new technology. We're the greatest country in the world --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, you bet.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: -- why shouldn't we have the best technology in our towers? And no one has done it, but this administration has committed. I'm committed to making sure that happens.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Mr. Secretary, as always, thank you very much for joining us tonight. And ahead a culture that seems at times full of rage, especially among our young people. Dr Drew Pinsky is here to investigate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This is a Fox News update. I'm Kevin Corke in Washington. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  is cutting about a quarter of its workforce that according to an email sent to employees there. The layoff's expected to affect more than 20,000 people at the agency. That email also said about 75 percent of the agency's civil rights office will be affected. Mass layoff's coming during the \uc2 IRS\uc2 's busiest time of the year with the April 15 tax filing deadline quickly approaching.\par \par \uc2 A relentless barrage of tornadoes and flash flooding in the central U.S. now being blamed for at least eight deaths. A boy in Frankfort, Kentucky, the state's capital, the latest victim after reportedly being swept away by floodwaters. The violent storms have also injured dozens of people and destroyed homes throughout the Southeast from Arkansas to Missouri, Tennessee through Kentucky, Illinois, and Indiana. A very tough time as you take a look at those rising waters.\par \par \uc2 I'm Kevin Corke in Washington. Now back to the Ingraham Angle.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Teens seemingly raging for no reason. We see it all over the country. In Virginia last Saturday night, the Pentagon City mall was shut down after multiple brawls broke out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Among those charged, two girls, ages 13 and 15, they were assaulting police officers. In New York City, a wild mob smashed the windows of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  police cruisers in the middle of the night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (POLICE SIRENS)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And at least two 18-year-olds were nabbed for that. Then the killings. A 17-year-old arrested in Florida accused of killing a mom, a mom of four, as she sat at home on her couch. A 14-year-old suspected gang member is being tried as an adult in the ambush of a New Jersey police officer. That's happening.\par \par \uc2 And of course, last night, we told you about the teen charged with the murder of Austin Metcalf, stabbed in the heart after a dispute over a seat at a high school track meet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I received a phone call from one of the coaches that said my son had been stabbed, and jumped in my vehicle and drove to the stadium and ran up there. And when I saw him on the gurney, he wasn't breathing. I knew he was gone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We don't know the motives for any of these crimes, but we do know that it seems like -- these young people feel that they're entitled or justified in carrying out these acts of violence. Maybe that's what society wanted them to believe.\par \par \uc2 Think about how people reacted after Luigi Mangione was arrested. He was celebrated. It is being celebrated as a hero. A lot of this started with the BLM riots in 2020. Remember that summer, it taught our young people that if you feel wronged, you feel like someone owes you something, then demand reparations. If you don't get those, torch buildings. Sometimes it ends up killing someone.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Dr. Drew Pinsky, host of Ask Dr. Drew on Rumble. Dr. Drew, I mean, these are all individual incidents, no doubt, but how can we start to change the way young people are reacting in the moment to things?\par \par \uc2 DR. DREW PINSKY, HOST OF ASK DR. DREW ON RUMBLE: Well, by the time they get to this point, I'm not sure we can do a lot other than the deployment of law enforcement, but the first thing it seems to me we should be doing is not glorifying violence.\par \par \uc2 Violence has become some sort of entertainment, something that is sort of sought after. And no doubt, the cameras that everybody is holding is giving us the opportunity to see more violence than we've ever seen before, and it seems worse than it has ever been before.\par \par \uc2 There's no doubt always throughout history, the young male has been the main perpetrator of violence. So, obviously, we get to focus on young males, getting them the right sort of activities to participate in. And the extraordinary thing of the current moment is that we're seeing as many women, females involved with this.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is one of the things that consistently comes out in the literature, you ask how we're going to change the behavior, is availability of dads. So, dad must be in the life of the child and dad must not be physically abusive. Those two things are the ingredients necessary to reduce some of this crazy behavior.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Drew, the suspect in the murder of Austin Metcalf reportedly admitted to killing Austin. And the NBC Dallas outlet says that he told police, yeah, I did it. And again, there's bizarre commentary online from people trying to justify -- I mean, some of it, they're making it racial, but the bottom line is someone had a seat, and it was in the wrong place, and I don't know what was said, but it ends up with someone dead.\par \par \uc2 PINSKY: It's sad. It's really truly sad. And the people that are engaging in the pathological practice of something called Hybristophilia. Hybristophilia is the love and glorification of criminality, of criminals, people that fall in love with people in prison, that sort of thing. That's a pathology. And we have elevated that to a media standard where justification of murder is somehow something people can contemplate.\par \par \uc2 And let's be clear. That's delusional. That's delusional thinking. And hysteria and delusion has been alive and well in this country, since Trump's derangement started, frankly. That's where I first saw signs of it. And then COVID, it reached a pitch that was unpredictable. Who could have seen that coming?\par \par \uc2 And now we're seeing it continue through these violent expressions and justification of violence and murder in ways that when people wake up from this, they're going to feel deeply ashamed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. I think you hit on it, like a lack of connection, fathers. If a father isn't present, someone else who is a person of authority and trust and respect who can help raise young people. But a lot of these young people are running around at night with no supervision, apparently no rules.\par \par \uc2 PINSKY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And there is a sense of entitlement, like you have something, I should have it too. I'm going to take it whether you like it or not. And if I don't get it, I'm going to punch you in the face. I didn't see that growing up. It's something very new.\par \par \uc2 PINSKY: No. It's new. It's new. It's new. And if you look at essentially every religious scripture on earth, they're injunctions against this emotion. It's the most destructive of human emotions, and that emotion is envy. Envy is not jealousy. Envy is you have something I don't have, not -- I'm going to work hard and get it or it makes me feel bad that you have it. I have to destroy you and bring you down to my level.\par \par \uc2 Envy is something that all religions have recognized as one of the most destructive impulses that humans have, and we must talk about it again and address it where it occurs. It's part of the dark triad. It's a terrible, destructive emotion, and it's underlying exactly what you're talking about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. I mean, I think it goes back to what you and I have talked about before, Dr. Drew, the ability to have differences of opinion or maybe disagreements, whether it's about a pro-life issue or it's about a seat at a, you know, at a track meet and just say, hey, brother, you know, sit with us for a while and chat or, you know, I'll go over and sit in your tent. I mean, you got to teach people the way to deescalate these situations.\par \par \uc2 PINSKY: Well, it's as simple as the golden rule. Right? It's as simple as that. It's all these simple things we've completely lost track of. Grandma knew, and guess what? They were right.\par \par \uc2 So, you wouldn't want somebody to treat you as an object. You wouldn't want somebody to wield a knife towards you. So go ahead and not do that to somebody else.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, it's shocking. Dr. Drew, as always, thank you so much. And Snow White wasn't the only twisted remake we have to endure this year, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets tangled once again. How? Where? In her own syntax. Friday Follies with Raymond Arroyo is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday, and that means it's time for Friday Follies. And for that, we turn to Fox News contributor, Raymond Arroyo. Hey, Raymond. After the disaster that was Snow White, oh my goodness. I hope we were done with the remakes, but there are more.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Laura. When intellectual property ages sufficiently, it falls into public domain. And anybody can capitalize on those titles or the storylines that we've known throughout our lives and make their own new version.\par \par \uc2 Well, Mickey Mouse is now headed into troubled waters. Remember Steamboat Willie, the 1928 classic cartoon? Well, it hit public domain this year, Laura, and now we have the second horror remake of this film in twelve months.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Welcome aboard to Staten Island, America.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We're all going to die.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We said that last week.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And the week before.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I heard a story about a monster that lurked in the underbelly of the ferry. Steamboat's wind.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Billy's world, it's the happiest place on Earth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, why are they --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I thought you were joking.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No. I wish I were. And this is the second horror remake of this movie. But it tells you something about a culture. You did that segment on rage with Dr. Drew. When a culture and filmmakers are taking things that were once in the domain of childhood, innocent childhood touchstones, and perverting them and turning them into these darkly neurotic murderous escapades, it tells us something about where we are.\par \par \uc2 We can't remake a sweet new version of something. We have to pervert it and make it murderous and turn it into a slasher. There's something wrong in the culture, and that's what's fueling all of these instances, sadly, that we're seeing all over the country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, it's certainly a factor, but just normalizing sadistic violence and it's also just bad. There's no drama, there's no writing, there's no acting. It's just \uc2 CGI\uc2  stupidity. And I hope these films just crater and maybe we'll get back to start making real films again.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But they're capitalizing on innovative and wonderful intellectual property that now they can commandeer. They did the same thing with Peter Pan and Winnie the Pooh. Remember we did this? They transformed those into slashers and blood-fests. And, you know, it's all quite sick.\par \par \uc2 It's almost as sick as what \uc2 Disney\uc2  did to its own intellectual property with Snow White. I mean, that movie lost hundreds of millions of dollars at the box office, Laura, prompting them this week to cancel the live action remake of Tangled. That was the Rapunzel cartoon that kids love.\par \par \uc2 Well, I know you're weeping all the way to the tower on that one, but, you know, it's probably a worse move.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Great news.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So, Laura, I have a little good news. The live action Minnie Mouse reboot is continuing. They're going to relaunch Minnie Mouse. I heard they found a big star.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is Minnie going to be a transgender act -- oh, come on. That's ridiculous. Michelle looks cool. That's kind of a cool -- I think it's kind of a cool -- I'm no one to talk about hair. I'm always -- you know, it's -- the hair is a struggle for we, women. OK? So that's something different. That's all right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, you mentioned Tangled. Yeah. Did you mention Tangled? Did you see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's, she came out, I don't know, the other day at this Leading Women Defined Summit. What a weird -- that's a weird title. Leading Women Defined Summit. A little tangled there.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That was it. Well, she did -- she was a little lost in her own verbiage, which brings us to Kamala's word of the day, Laura. Courage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: When a few stand with the courage, that is the courage exhibited by the leaders in this room every day. To stand, to have the courage to say, I feel fear. The courage to say what is happening is wrong. The courage to say that there is a way that we must chart to get through this. Courage is contagious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: My lord. It's repetition masquerading as coherence, Laura, or leadership. I mean, what is that? How can she get up at this moment when the Democrats are desperately looking for a leader? And I just don't understand how \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  remains near the top of those polls for the leader that Democrats at this point trust.\par \par \uc2 Some polls have her up at 25 percent. Does that sound like credible leadership to you?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Please stay in these last few months in our country. And it understandably creates a great sense of fear for many things that we knew would happen. Many thing -- I'm not here to say I told you so.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The joy, Laura. More of the joy. She's got a big problem though in California with Xavier Becerra running for California governor. Now he made that big announcement, believe it or not, that he's going to run for governor of California. And Kamala hasn't made a move yet, so we don't quite know what she's going to do.\par \par \uc2 But if this is the new and improved \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she better call Oprah and drop 5 million on her, do one of those town halls again.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's horrible.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ahead, speaking of, a deranged Democrat claims that Trump is going to round him up. Can't round one person up. The latest from the left- wing loons next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. If it's possible, the left is becoming even more deranged since Trump took office. It's almost hard to keep it up -- keep up, but we're going to try.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Jason Chaffetz, Fox News contributor, and Alex Marlow, editor-in-chief over at Breitbart. OK, guys, one of the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  protest organizers is now feeling a little bit of the backlash and trying to play all innocent. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  called the organization, quote, "criminals," claiming that you're supporting the vandalism of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  dealerships. How do you respond to this? What are your concerns about the administration's response?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Look, it's bogus. Obviously, we've been organizing for the better part of a decade, and nonviolent protest has been a core foundational part of what we do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Core foundational part of their mission, Jason, nonviolent. They were -- some of the people seem inspired by this or using Molotov cocktails, shooting guns, obviously doing enormous property damage and terrorizing people.\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah. They're doing it in mass. This isn't a coincidence. I still think law enforcement needs to follow the money to find out who's being, you know, paid to do what. But these lunatics have gone off the rails deciding that destroying public property, you know, a $60,000 vehicle, is somehow, you know, they have these bumper stickers that say, you know, coexist, and they have this attitude that we're going to be all inclusive and all this.\par \par \uc2 But, no, if \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , heaven forbid, tries to put our financial footing back in order, they just lose their minds and decide, oh, we're going to get rid of an emission-less car, for goodness sake. Like, what good is that going to do? These people are nuts.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Public property, private property, Alex, it doesn't seem to matter. As we talked about earlier with, Dr. Drew, this rage culture that is fueled by this -- it is big money that's funding these efforts to try to shut down and discourage, I thank \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . This has gotten way out of hand and serious, and I know people who own Teslas who are actually afraid now to drive them. They're afraid.\par \par \uc2 ALEX MARLOW, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, BREITBART: Right. And one narrative here that I think people are really missing is this is a Democrat civil war. Think about this. Who owns Teslas? It's typically liberals. All the demographics show this.\par \par \uc2 So, you have organized well-funded left-wing violence that is targeting other liberals. And the liberals, no one's standing up and saying, hey, wait a minute. Why are you going after us? It's all because they hate \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  so much. They hate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  so much that anything that looks like maybe it will intimidate them to slow down the cutting of the government.\par \par \uc2 To get these people who have banked on electric vehicles, saving the world, that all of a sudden, overnight, now they want to destroy them, they want to torch them. It just shows you how they're complete lunatics, totally deranged, and shouldn't be trusted with anything.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And one of the anti-Trumpers, Jason, is spinning a new conspiracy theory. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We live in a country where you can get snatched up off the street by writing an article in a newspaper. And as a person who writes articles in newspapers, this is something that has really worried me and really changed and affected how I think about my job and my life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jason, he's just being rounded up, I guess, with the Tren de Aragua people and sent over to the super max in Salvador.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah. He forgot to mention the big part, which is people that are here illegally, you know, committing crimes, doing those types of things. Nobody is attacking the First Amendment. In fact, it was the Democrats that went after the First Amendment. They were the ones that were all about censorship.\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they were the ones that wanted to have the thought police out there. And this is where they lost, I think, the traditional Democrat who really did believe in the First Amendment. They've all come over to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 They -- you know, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  wasn't always a conservative Republican. They lost guys like that. They all came over to the right. And they lost the traditional working-class person who believed in the first amendment. They're all with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at this point.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Alex, I mean, honestly, they've spent more time obsessing about that one person who may have been on the wrong list and got sent over to El Salvador who, you know, the government believes is an MS-13 allied person.\par \par \uc2 But the left has spent more time rallying to his cause than they ever have, the parents of all the victims of these horrific illegal alien, criminals across the country. Very quickly on that.\par \par \uc2 MARLOW: Oh, yeah. These judges and Jamie Raskin out there, one of the Democrats saying we need to turn around military planes because there could be one guy who's merely an illegal alien who may have missed some court appearances. I could go along with the dozens and dozens of violent gang members. These people are completely lost.\par \par \uc2 And I'll tell you, on that guy, who is -- the other guy was selling a book. Trump's not going after that guy. Like, he's just trying to get attention and that's -- you know, we'll give it to him in the TV and black cast. But it's fine. Let's make fun of him. Sounds great.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you saying that people who sell book like to say inflammatory things to promote them? Oh, my goodness. Jason, Alex, good to see both of you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right. That's it for us tonight. Remember, follow me all weekend long on social media, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and X. It's America now and forever. Don't panic. Calm down. Everything is going to be OK. The economy is going to be the way it should have been for the last 30 years. It's going to take a little time. Jesse Watters is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250405el4400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Declares America's Economic Independence; The Global Rip-Off Ends Today; Two Florida Races, Two Wins For GOP; Reining In Radical Judges; Nationwide Injunctions Thwart Trump; Rep. Crockett Lashes Out At Bondi, Musk; Jasmine Crockett Admits To Being A DEI Hire; Booker Gets By With A Little Help From His Friends; Alec Baldwin: We're In A Pre-Civil War Era; DOGE Hits \uc2 HHS\uc2 ; MAHA Advisor Defends \uc2 HHS\uc2  DOGE Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Maria Bartiromo, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 8,081 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight from the White House tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle is next.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight, liberating us from decline. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 The scene at the White House today was something to behold. You had steel workers, pipe fitters, auto workers and mechanics, the people who make American industry run. They were gathered in the Rose Garden to watch President Trump announce and sign his executive order that aims to make trade fair again, and we were there to watch it all unfold.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My fellow Americans, this is Liberation Day, waiting for a long time. April 2, 2025, will forever be remembered as the day American industry was reborn, the day America's destiny was reclaimed, and the day that we began to make America wealthy again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And President Trump announced reciprocal tariffs on virtually every major exporting country in the world, calculated by adding the foreign nation's tariffs, plus the value of any trade barriers that they have in place, kind of ingenious. And then in turn, we charge them half of that amount.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is one of the most important days, in my opinion, in American history. It's our declaration of economic independence. For years, hardworking American citizens were forced to sit on the sidelines as other nations got rich and powerful, much of it at our expense, but now it's our turn to prosper, and in so doing, use trillions and trillions of dollars to reduce our taxes and pay down our national debt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He also addressed what I call the myth perceptions about tariffs peddled by his critics. Now, how many times have you heard that a tariff is a tax? But of course, that's facile thinking. Even if foreign manufacturers try to pass on some of their price hikes to consumers, you've got to consider the broader picture.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Jobs and factories will come roaring back into our country, and you see it happening already. We will supercharge our domestic industrial base. We will pry open foreign markets and break down foreign trade barriers, and ultimately, more production at home will mean stronger competition and lower prices for consumers. This will be, indeed, the golden age of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And I have to give a great shoutout to my great friend, Pat Buchanan, who back in decades ago, but also recently in 2019 put it best. He said, a tariff is a tax, but its purpose is not just to raise revenue, but to make a nation economically independent of others, and to bring its citizens to rely upon each other, rather than on foreign entities.\par \par \uc2 Again, the word independent is really key. Now, when I was a little kid, almost nothing we bought from toys to TVs were made outside the United States. I still have my Raggedy Ann and Andy dolls, believe it or not, I'm going to give them to my, my kids, kids someday, they were all made in the USA, and even clothes. My class spirit shirt from college made in the USA.\par \par \uc2 But then globalization meant that we made fewer things, lost millions of jobs. Also, we could buy more of everything from electronics to pet products at a lower per piece cost. And if we learn nothing, nothing else from the COVID nightmare, it should be that we need to re-shore the manufacturing of our critical supply chain. President talked about this today, PPE, antibiotics, other pharmaceuticals, many of them made in China.\par \par \uc2 Now, another myth that Trump is starting -- that Trump is like starting a big trade war. Well, the truth is, we've been in the midst of a trade war for almost 50 years now and losing it badly. The trade deficits speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: 81 percent of the cars in South Korea are made in South Korea, 94 percent of the cars in Japan are made in Japan. \uc2 Toyota\uc2  sells 1 million foreign made automobiles into the United States. And \uc2 General Motors\uc2  sells almost none. Ford sells very little. None of our companies are allowed to go into other countries. Such horrendous imbalances have devastated our industrial base and put our national security at risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You bet it has. A lot of Americans don't even know those facts. It was very educational to listen to the President today. And for all the tough talk on the global scene, key countries are already beginning to see the light.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUG FORD\uc2 , ONTARIO PREMIER: Let's sit down and discuss this, because it's just going to hurt American jobs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But do you think it's fair that you have tariffs on a whole number of products.\par \par \uc2 FORD: OK. And we'd be willing to take those off tomorrow, if he took all the tariffs off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Does President Trump know that you'd be prepared to do that?\par \par \uc2 FORD: He knows, and Secretary Lutnick knows that we're willing to take these tariffs off like in the next minute, if he said he's taking their tariffs off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, we're waiting. Now, the bottom-line is any economic hit we collectively take is temporary and should not be seen in isolation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : \uc2 Apple\uc2  is going to spend $500 billion. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , a hot company is investing hundreds of billions of dollars. \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , great company, $55 billion. \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , 27 billion. Meta, CMA, CGM, $20 billion. \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 GE Aerospace\uc2 , Honda, \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , Hyundai, are all putting in billions and billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, one of my favorite moments of the day came early on when Trump brought up one of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers to speak.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have watched my entire life; I have watched plant after plant after plant in Detroit and in the Metro Detroit area close. There are now plants sitting idle. There are now plants that are underutilized, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's policies are going to bring product back into those underutilized plants. There's going to be new investment. There's going to be new plants built. In six months or a year, we're going to begin to see the benefits. I can't wait to see what's happening three or four years down the road. Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now that was just a beautiful moment. The \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  was founded in 1995, 30 years ago. We allowed China to join the \uc2 WTO\uc2  in 2000. So the free traders purists have had their way for most of that time and we know how it worked out. 5 million manufacturing jobs lost as the outsourcers and the foreign companies got rich. Trump said this today, look, I campaigned on this, the entire 2024 campaign. He told the voters that this is what he was planning to do, to make America great again by ending the great foreign rip-off of America. And now, he deserves a chance to implement this program and demonstrate to the voters that it can work.\par \par \uc2 So Republicans, I know you're watching, don't even think about blocking this on Capitol Hill. And the courts, they should not be enjoining this either. Let's see how the markets settle out over the next six months. Let's see how other countries and their major companies react.\par \par \uc2 Five American presidents helped dig us into this trade deficit hole that we currently find ourselves in, and only one, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , had the guts to try to dig us out. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, man who was right in front row there, Kevin Hassett, Director of the National Economic Council. Kevin, for Americans watching and might have missed what happened today and don't quite understand the nuances of the different percentages and how you calculate the tariffs. What's your message to them?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , DIRECTOR, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: I think that the first message, which I think it was great that the Angle led with, is that if you go back to 2000 when China entered the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 , everybody told us how wonderful it was going to be because we're going to get all these cheap goods and everybody's welfare was going to be so much better. You know, if you go back and look at the eight years of President Bush and eight years of President Obama, do you know that over those 16 years that the typical family only made $80 cumulatively, so their increase in welfare was $5.50 a year, about over the 15 years after China entered the \uc2 WTO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So there was no gain in real income, because while we're getting the cheap goods, we were losing jobs, destroying cities, destroying towns and so on. And President Trump announced, in a very historic action today that that's going to end, that we're going to create jobs in America. We're going to make stuff again here in the U.S. We're going to make real wages go up. We're never, ever, ever going to see like the Bush, Obama years, where we get $5 a year of increased income, because China's taking all the business away from us --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, real median income, when you look at the line, the graphs --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: It's just flat.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Flat. And I mentioned, when I growing up in the, you know, as a little kid in the 60s, and in the -- into the 70s, you just didn't have anything was made in China, because they weren't a member of the \uc2 WTO\uc2 . Very few things really made overseas. And then it was a tsunami of foreign imports. But not all are fans of this. GOP Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  said that the tariffs could cause a recession and have a major political impact. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): Tariffs have also led to political decimation when McKinley, most famously put tariffs on in 1890, they lost 50 percent of their seats in the next election. When Haw Smoot put on their tariff in the early 1930s, we lost the House and the Senate for 60 years. So they're not only bad economically, they're bad politically.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Your response to that.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Look, the fact is that President Trump has been saying that he supports tariffs because he has the intuition of what we talked about, how those incomes are not gaining because we're losing the jobs abroad. He's been saying that for 40 years, and he just won a landslide election, really, in the U.S. So the idea that it's bad politics for tariffs. Well, while the voters who voted for Trump should have heard that, and the bottom-line is, the reason it's good politics is people know what happened to their communities, just like you remember what it was like when we were growing up.\par \par \uc2 And President Trump is going to fix it with this policy. It is absolutely an incredible day in the history of American economic history, because we're finally stopping the trade war. We've actually -- it's not we're starting a trade war. We're stopping the trade war because we're going to once again build everything here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- the way the tariffs are calculated. Now there was the chart that Howard Lutnick, the Commerce Secretary, brought up, and it was different amounts for different countries and one thing that is very important is for people to understand why it's higher for certain countries. Explain that.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right. So the bottom-line is that some countries will sell lots of stuff into our country, but they won't let us sell stuff back to them. And one of the ways they do that is a -- with a really, really high tariff. But another way is they say, hey, we don't like the seed that you planted, or we don't like the way that you're, you know, feeding your cows. And then we're not allowed to sell farm and agricultural products or manufactured products abroad because of all their non-tariff barriers.\par \par \uc2 So think about it, when we got this free trade deal, free trade deal with South Korea, they agreed to let U.S. cars into South Korea. How many U.S. cars have you ever seen in South Korea? Well, because they have all these non-tariff barriers. And so what we did at the White House, with the help of the USTR is we estimated the economic impact of all those non-tariff barriers, and that's how we got these reciprocal numbers.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What is the likelihood that a lot of these countries, Korea, India, he mentioned India. And India is notorious, sorry, cheater on trade deals that they come to you and come to the White House and the President and say, let's make a deal here.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Well, I think that's up to the president, but I could tell you the President's phone is ringing off the hook, but it doesn't mean that he's going to change, because there's so much that they're doing with their value added taxes and their non-tariff barriers that they're not -- like do you think the EU is going to change all their regulations that harm American workers? Not fast, for sure. And so I don't expect that there's going to be a massive reduction in this action. But you know, everybody's talking to the President right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, again, we're going to see scenes and other networks of reporters at like the \uc2 Toyota\uc2  dealership or the \uc2 Mazda\uc2  dealership, and say, now you're going to pay more for your cars. What do you think? And people, you know, they'll get people on camera saying, I don't want to pay more for my cars -- for our cars. Is it necessarily the case that those increase in cost -- those increase in costs are always passed on to the consumer?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: No, absolutely not. And the way to think about it is that what happens is that China is just shipping stuff to us, because it's one of the ways they keep political stability, is they create jobs for their folks as they almost will ship it no matter what. And so they're going to have to cut their prices and pay the tariff, and exactly how much prices go up in the U.S. It's very, very uncertain. And I don't think that the thing though, even if it went up like \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  had an estimate of maybe about half a percent, 1 percent, the bottom-line is that if you think about the tax cuts and the no tax on tips, and the no tax on overtime and the other stuff that we're extending that the benefit for American workers from all that vastly exceeds even our critics estimates (inaudible) --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, one of the things that the USTR told me in the Rose Garden after the ceremony was that the de minimis exception to tariffs is gone.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That $800 if you send it from China, it's under $800 you're not subject to the tariffs. That was a big rip-off, and we were losing --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- a lot of money. That's gone, correct.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right. It's gone. And not only that --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's huge.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: -- but because of the postal treaties, it was cheaper to ship stuff from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , from China to the U.S. than from the --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean that was --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: -- town next door. And we've stopped that. And so again, it's America First. American workers first. And last time we started doing this recall that real wages went up about $6,500 in three years before COVID. And what President Trump learned from that is that he was right about these policies, and now he's doubling down, and I expect that real incomes are going to once again skyrocket, as opposed to the $5.50 a year you got after --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: -- China entered the \uc2 WTO\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: December 2019, go back and look at economic you know, stories about --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: It was through the roof.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- everybody was so optimistic and positive, we will see how this plays out. But I was very, very excited to see this all unfold, and especially for the workers who are at that Rose Garden. It was unbelievable. Kevin, thank you --\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- so much. And Democrats who didn't care about the economy under Biden at all are now claiming, of course, that the tariffs are going to hit Americans where it hurts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is set to unleash the most reckless tsunami of tariffs; Americans have seen in over half a century. Prices will rise on virtually every kind of product.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's just a sales tax, and it's going to be ordinary families who pay that tax at the kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): Americans will not, by this move, be liberated from high prices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's funny that they're all obsessed about this, because when Biden actually kept Trump's tariffs on China and announced other tariffs, we were told the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will American consumers bear the cost of these tariffs?\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF THE TREASURY: I don't believe that American consumers will see any meaningful increase in the prices that they face.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, joining me now, \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2 , host of Sunday Morning Futures here on Fox News and Mornings with Maria on Fox Business. Maria, you know they scold these other countries for placing tariffs on us, but they don't want to do anything about it. And when Trump actually has the guts to take this on, which is a -- that's what he campaigned on, they're going crazy.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BARTIROMO\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS NETWORK ANCHOR: Yes. Thanks so much Laura for having me. I think it's rich when you see the Democrats start complaining about inflation, when in fact, inflation was not a problem after they spent $7 trillion in the Biden years and sent inflation to 40-year highs. Look, there are a couple of things about this announcement today that I like a lot. In fact, I like the idea that there is a delay in some of the tariffs on the bad actors, you were just talking to Kevin Hassett about some of the more significant tariffs on what President Trump deems as the bad actor countries. You've got a 24 percent tariff on Japan and a 20 percent 