etting going. He campaigned on everything we just talked about with -- I love that, reindustrializing the United States to independence. Just reindustrializing -- bring back those jobs, bring back economic prosperity.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  is out there saying he knows what the Republicans are going to do next. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Republicans are going to try to distance themselves now from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , it's not going to work. It's too late. You're attached at the hip and you're going to feel the consequences of it, just like you did in Wisconsin last night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I thought he was going to say, just like \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . What of that?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, of course, Hakeem doesn't want to talk about is what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the Democrats did for this past four years. And if we're going to get inflation under control, obviously we got to go and slash the spending that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the Democrats put in place. It's that simple. So not only are there some cuts that are put forward in both the House and the Senate, but we have to be smart with how we do it. We can use things such as AI, supply chain optimization, streamlining services to make tens, if not hundreds of billions of dollars a reduction. You bring down the spending. That's how you make things more affordable and that's what I think President Trump and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and the leaders in the House are going to do here.\par \par \uc2 Also Laura, I think we got to go big on some of these things. We talk about taking the corporate tax rate down to 15. We talk about making the capital expensing permanent. Actually, let's go and make it retroactive back to January 20th, like what the President said in his address to Congress. We got to do some of these big things, but I think today, with what President Trump did was the first step in how he turned this economy around, I thought it was big move on the -- in the Rose Garden at the White House and I loved it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was bold, but that's what he campaigned on. I've been for tariffs since Pat Buchanan in 1992, OK? Like I was pushing for this, but you know, we did the \uc2 WTO\uc2 , and it was a complete disaster. We all know what happened. Now this story about Musk getting back to this, I find fascinating, because somebody is talking to Politico, because they reported that Trump told his inner circle that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  will soon leave. But of course, the White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt says the scoop is garbage. He was always going to be a temporary advisor to the White House, wasn't he?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, I think so. But you also got to remember that when you're taking on the swamp, the swamp fights back. And I think a lot of folks at home need to keep that in mind. The reason why \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is being attacked is because he's doing some really great things, because President Trump is saying, get out there and don't just take the scalpel, take the cleaver. Let's go and have some real cuts. But when you do that, you shake up the system.\par \par \uc2 We saw some of this in the first term, where the swamp would start fighting back. I think \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is doing a lot of good. I think the administration and the people they've put together are good. But ultimately, President Trump has the vision for how we go and turn this around. We've been failed by leaders for both parties for the past couple of decades. Finally, we have somebody who's going to get it turned around.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think the abortion issue and putting that Susan Crawford out there, oh, she's just a moderate. She's a mom like you. That's -- that hurt the Republicans in Wisconsin. And \uc2 Brad Schimel\uc2  is good guy, smart guy. He wasn't particularly dynamic. I think that hurt them as well. Jason, thank you so much. Good to see you.\