he Senate floor, which was billed as a filibuster, though it really was not. He wasn't blocking any bills, so it wasn't a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 He just invoked his privilege and took time on the Senate floor, and he was helped many times by a lot of his friends.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Would the Senator of New Jersey yield for a question.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Let me think about this for a second.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You take your time thinking about it, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'm going to pause because I know you have another question, and I'm going to yield to the question. I want to go a little bit past this, and then I'm going to deal with some of the biological urgencies I'm feeling.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You're still making sense, and your sentences are still properly structured, and it's starting to feel like a superhuman thing.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This was effective. This was calculated, coordinated, and disciplined. He did with --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That is what fighting looks like if you want results.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You'd swear he liberated Berlin, Laura, from this coverage. I mean, my goodness. All he did was hold forth on the floor and find stall tactic to rest throughout the day. Every time he turned around, somebody was asking a question which went on for fifteen minutes, and then he'd read Langston Hughes or something.\par \par \uc2 This is not an act of heroism. It's an act of desperation, and I don't know how this is leadership.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't recall there being so ebullient over Ted Cruz reigning greeting green eggs and ham during the Obamacare filibuster. They were ridiculing him for that. And I kind of -- I found it all very entertaining. And he tried to make a last stand for Obama, you know, to, you know, repeal Obamacare and it didn't ultimately work, but they were just ridiculing him. But now it's like -- he's -- it's a Peloponnesian War and he's the great victor.\par \par \uc2 All right, finally, Ray, I would guess that man of history, Alec Baldwin, he caught Ken Burns in Civil War series and he sees some parallels.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Something led my hand to pick up the remote and boy, you can see now that we are in a pre-civil war culture now. We are in a very similar state now in a pre-civil war culture, in a pre-civil war environment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Perhaps he's confusing the country, Laura, with his home. Have you seen an episode of the Baldwins? I mean, between hilarious demands and those wild children, it's like a tiny civil war. And look, Alec did shoot off the opening shot, I guess. So, surrender Alec, surrender.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tragic. No, it was a tragic outcome there. But, I mean, he's just whipped, right? He's just whipped. He just seems like he's just -- he's out of gas. I mean, he's out of gas.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He's in the room alone watching Ken Burns. That series only dropped in 1990, so he's really a man of the moment, but --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Anything to get away --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: -- worst things he could watch.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Anything to get away from Hilaria, who is not hilarious. Raymond, thank you. All right. MAHA calls out big pharma.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It is insane for you to insinuate that the thing standing between us and better health is more government bureaucrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The RFK Jr. advisor who said that, Calley Means is here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: DOGE is hitting \uc2 HHS\uc2 . Secretary \uc2 Robert F Kennedy Jr.\uc2  laying off 10,000 employees this week and Big Pharma is big mad about it. But MAHA is not backing down. Top RFK Jr. advisor, Calley Means, took on the media and the lobbyists today at Politico's healthcare summit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: When you turn on CNBC, it's just a nonstop, it's a nonstop infomercial for pharma. It's a Sky Rizzi commercial followed by \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2  saying how Bobby is killing people followed by a breathless coverage of the measles outbreak and no mention of the mental health crisis and --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: -- a bunch of \uc2 HHS\uc2  solve that problem.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It is insane. It is insane for you to insinuate that the thing standing between us and better health is more government bureaucrats. Like, that is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That is what everyone's saying. It is absolutely insane to insinuate that it's some crime.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's not all bureaucrats. It's a lot of scientists. It's researchers. It's people who are making sure that --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Those scientists fundamentally have overseen, just demonstrably, a record of utter failure. Of utter failure. It would be insane. Is that funny?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Not true.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Is that not -- is that not true? I'd love to hear how -- so what metrics would you guys point to for how the scientific community, how the four times more per capita on healthcare spending that we spend in America versus other countries has produced innovation?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, the man you just heard, Calley Means, MAHA senior advisor. You walked into the lion's den, my friend. Thanks for joining us. Life expectancy on the downslope, obesity, heart disease, certain types of cancers skyrocketing. Where's the great benefits?\par \par \uc2 CALLEY MEANS, SENIOR ADVISOR FOR MAHA: We spend four times more than Italy on health care cost per capita and live seven years less. Out of 212 countries measured, we're 212 in cancer rates. We are the highest rates of diabetes, obesity, heart disease among children in the world right now.\par \par \uc2 And when I walked in there today, I felt the weight of MAHA moms behind me, the millions of MAHA moms who gave President Trump and gave \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  this mandate to solve this. And what would be the -- anything other than the first step than to fire the people who've overseen this devastation and put in amazing new people like Marty Makary, Jay Bhattacharya, and Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 But the media, the stenographers in the mainstream media call this an attack on science. This is an attack on interests that are profiting from kids being sick. This is a victory for MAHA moms. Of course, they should be making changes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Democratic Congressman Jake Auchincloss had a message for you at that summit. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think what we just heard explains and crystallizes the arrogance of these bros that have descended on Washington D.C. I mean, these professional podcasters, professional podcasters stand in front of a bunch of career scientists and say, what has science ever done? I don't know. Science eradicated measles until two months ago. We will not take a lecture from people who are based in conspiracy and quackery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It reminds me of what happened during COVID when I was talking with, you know, my medicine cabinet about hydroxychloroquine and Ivermectin, and I was called all sorts of names at \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Well, I mean, all these articles. The experts know how to handle things.\par \par \uc2 MEANS: Your kids are getting sick. Fifty percent of teens are overweight or obese. Thirty eight percent of teens have pre diabetes, but shut up and listen to the experts. And listen to that particular congressperson who should have been wearing a NASCAR jacket with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Merck, and all the other pharma companies.\par \par \uc2 He's one of the largest recipients. \uc2 Google\uc2  his name. There's many articles. He literally just reads the talking points. Right before that, I saw him prepping literal pharma talking points with his staffer. We should not be trusting paid off lawmakers. We should not be trusting scientists who profit from kids being sick. Of course, we need change.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you this from being with Marty Makary, Jay Bhattacharya, and \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , their mandate for President Trump is to get more money to scientists to do real scientific inquiry. Right now, the \uc2 NIH\uc2 , ninety percent of their budget goes to pharma R and D.\par \par \uc2 We are going to figure out why kids are getting sick. President Trump signed a momentous executive order. There's going to be a report in a month, and we are going to turn the tide of this devastating chronic disease virus.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is that include harms caused by the vaccine, even though the vaccine was developed, Operation Warp Speed, under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 MEANS: You're going to be hearing good news after good news of exploring the taboo subjects, of taking a holistic approach to vaccines, to overprescription pharmaceuticals, to our sedentary lifestyle, to our food.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I got to get up and start moving. Just as you said that, I felt like I've been sitting for a long period of time.\par \par \uc2 MEANS: It isn't about lecturing people. Seventy percent of our child's diet right now in the United States is ultra processed food. That's 15 percent in Japan. We are recommending this stuff to kids, and we're going to fix it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Great to see you, Calley. Thanks for going in there.\par \par \uc2 MEANS: And Happy Liberation Day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Happy Liberation Day. That's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. Some \uc2 Instagram\uc2  posts from today in the Rose Garden. Jesse Watters has a great show. He takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2