ho are imbibing too much. All right, I have to get you to tee off on \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  who's not going away. He is going to, it looks like, make another run at it because he's the man to get the job done for the Nation. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Look, I never had an ambition to be president or vice president. I was honored to be asked. If I feel like I can serve, I will. And if nationally, that's what it looks like. If I think I could offer something or if there's, there's a piece there that would do it. I would certainly consider that. If it would be that I have the skill set, I'll do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Twisted Tim, 2028. Ned, Real quick.\par \par \uc2 RYUN: Do it. Come on \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , do it. Pick Jasmine Crockett as your V.P. Your slogan should be a tampon. Tampon in every boy's bathroom. And I have to tell you, Laura, I don't think they could match his jazz hands or how he prances onto the stage. But, you know, in all seriousness, it's completely delusional.\par \par \uc2 But it does show you that Democrats are a little short on the bench. If \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is even thinking about running for president, dear God.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, well. He and Chris Murphy. Maybe he has the woke mind virus. I'm teasing you, Ned. Ned, love to see you, as always. Thank you so much. All right, that's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on Insta. We have some Rocky Balboa moment.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250304el3300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With White House Deputy Chief of Staff Stephen Miller\b0\par\par\uc2 Will Cain \par \uc2 1,433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROWE, FOUNDER, MIKEROWEWORKS FOUNDATION: While I`m rooting for the president, I would ask Howard Lutnick, I would ask all of them, Elon, I`m asking them now, what is the plan for the work force? Please don`t put your head in the sand with regard to that question, because, look, it`s -- like I said, it`s a hard question to ask because it`s an unpleasant question to answer.\par \par \uc2 But if our country doesn`t have the will, if the work force isn`t there, then part of unraveling this puzzle is to make a persuasive national effort to support the very jobs the president is hoping to create, as well as a long list of jobs that are wide open right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Awesome conversation earlier today with Mike Rowe on the job gap today in the United States of America, in that there are some seven million jobs that remain unfilled today, not just a skills gap, but a willingness gap as well, of American men, primarily men, seemingly disinterested or unwilling to fill the jobs that are open today, many blue-collar jobs.\par \par \uc2 He said, it`s something to think about if we talk about reshoring and bringing all of these businesses back to America through tariffs, onshoring various companies. We need to figure out our work force.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the White House deputy chief of staff, Stephen Miller.\par \par \uc2 Stephen, it`s always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Great to be here. Thank you, Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You`re always smart on this.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about that, by the way? It`s great to bring jobs back to America, to bring companies and manufacturing back to America, but there is something going on with the American work force and young men choosing to accept the jobs that are looking to be offered to them.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, there`s a very important point on this that I`m very glad to get the opportunity to share today, which is that mass migration drives Americans and U.S.-born workers out of the work force, oftentimes in a permanent way, through alienation, social networking, and crowding out effects.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you a great example. If you went to New York City in the 1980s, it was overwhelmingly likely that your cab driver, when you showed up, would be a New Yorker, a U.S.-born, a New York-born cab driver. In fact, I think only about 80 percent of cabbies in the 80s were foreign-born.\par \par \uc2 So you would show up as a tourist, you come in from Omaha, you get a cab driver, it`s a U.S.-born cab driver. He would tell you about his favorite sites, his favorite restaurants, favorite things to see. There`s a good chance today that, of course, if you went to New York City, that the cab driver would be foreign-born.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s not an attack or an insult or a disparagement of that cab driver. But as immigration changed and we brought in immigration networks to fill up all of those jobs, it created social networks that crowded Americans out of that. And they no longer -- many Americans no longer saw that as a job in which they would feel like that`s part of their social network.\par \par \uc2 You see this in profession after profession, where so many jobs -- I mean, again, look at, for example, entry-level jobs in many fast-food restaurants. In areas that have very high amounts of immigration, it is very often the case that those workers are indeed foreign-born.\par \par \uc2 If you go to a part of the country where there`s very little immigration, you will often see high school graduates -- or -- sorry -- high school students, U.S.-born, doing those jobs to earn extra money.\par \par \uc2 What elites don`t understand is that unfettered migration changes the nature of the work force in a very permanent way. And so people will point to a job and they will say, well, we need immigration for that job because it`s all foreign workers doing it.\par \par \uc2 Look at a picture of the \uc2 Empire State Building\uc2  being built in the 1920s. Who are the people sitting on top of those beams high up above the city? Those are just American workers. So we have changed the nature of these industries.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes, this is -- Mike Rowe talked about this as well. He mentioned, like, look at the public image that we have created through pop culture of the plumber, right, guy with his pants down, halfway down his butt.\par \par \uc2 Well, plumbing is an honorable job and it`s a necessary job that society needs, along with electricians and welders. And look how that is sold P.R.- wise to the young American man.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Now, that being said, let me play devil`s advocate. I know you will have the answer. When I`m listening to you explain your first answer, I can hear the rebuttal. Oh, these people are doing jobs Americans don`t want to do or won`t do.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: No, it`s -- look, every -- first of all, every industry that built this country, that dug out the Panama Canal, that built the Interstate Highway System, that built the skyscrapers, those were American workers.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: American workers were the plumbers, the electricians. But it`s your point.\par \par \uc2 The elites said that these jobs weren`t jobs that are good for Americans. They downgraded and degraded these professions and said that we need to have cheap labor foreign workers to do these jobs, which, of course, drives down the wages for so many of these positions.\par \par \uc2 These are skilled craftsmen jobs. These are jobs that have always been a historic route to the middle class. So we drove down wages, we made these professions less attractive, we drove down conditions, and then were surprised at the result.\par \par \uc2 So, as you reform welfare, as you change incentive structures, but also shut down migration pipelines, and then you promote the dignity of these jobs, and you create an economy that works for American workers, that means you end the outsourcing, you end the offshoring, you make these jobs more economically attractive, you make it so they pay better so you can raise a family if you want to on a single income job.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And if you do all of these things, then you`re going to change how the work force operates in this country.\par \par \uc2 That`s all part of the Trump agenda.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: And I think that`s illustrated today, Stephen, by a topic I would have talked with you about, which is the president making English the official language of the country.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Which, by the way, you and I just chased this conversation where our interests went, so I ran out of time to hit on the news today.\par \par \uc2 But I think that fits, Stephen. I think the president making English the official language of the country is tied to what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 If I can say one thing on this because it`s so historic, we have been talking about this for decades, for generations. President Trump has finally did what people have been saying they want year after year, that we have one unifying national language that promotes assimilation, it promotes integration, it promotes national identity, two words we shouldn`t be ashamed of.\par \par \uc2 National identity matters. We are an English-speaking nation. President Trump once again made history and Americans can celebrate.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I think that`s to be celebrated. National identity matters. Be proud to be American.\par \par \uc2 Stephen, you are welcome to come on any day you would like to talk about anything you want and jumping into the conversation we are on to.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Because it`s always smart.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Stephen Miller.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020250303el330002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Israel Halts Aid From Entering Gaza Strip; Wildfires Lead To Evacuations In The Carolinas; Ukrainian President Meeting With European Leaders; Interview with Director of National Intelligence \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 ; Sen. \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2  on State of U.S.-Ukraine Relations; Trump, Zelenskyy Tensions Reach Boiling Point in Oval Office Clash; Battle Over Budget As March 14 Deadline Looms; DOJ Moves To Enforce Key Priorities Of Trump Admin; Lawsuits Question Authority of Musk and DOGE; Trump Administration's Mass Job Cuts Challenged; DOJ Dismisses Biden-era DEI Laws Against Police, Fire Departments; Supreme Court to Weigh in on Trump's Foreign Aid Freeze; Mexico Blames U.S. Gun Companies for Weapons Ending up in Cartels' Hands; \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  Receiving Treatment for Pneumonia\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Richard Fowler, Lucas Tomlinson, Lauren Green \par \uc2 10,306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Fallout after tensions boil over between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy with the path towards peace now unclear.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You're gambling with the lives of millions of people. You're gambling with World War III. You're gambling with World War III.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: What security guarantees can we have? I'm not pushing. We are not pressuring. We're just asking. We are just sharing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The Oval Office clash reverberating across Europe but well-received in Russia. We'll talk exclusively with the Director of National Intelligence, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , in her first Sunday show sit-down, and with the Democrat on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , about this stunning sequence of events.\par \par \uc2 And Mexico sues U.S. gun makers all the way to the us Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Mexican government should be bringing Mexico criminals to Mexican justice in Mexican courtrooms.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at your top headlines.\par \par \uc2 Israel has stopped all aid supplies from entering the Gaza Strip citing \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's refusal to accept an outline for continued peace talks and the failure to release the remaining Israeli hostages. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has released its own statement condemning the move.\par \par \uc2 High winds and dry conditions are fueling wildfires in the Carolinas, leading to evacuations in several communities. Firefighters did get some help overnight as the winds died down.\par \par \uc2 And Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is meeting with European leaders in the U.K. today. Britain, France and Ukraine have agreed to work on a ceasefire plan that they will then present to the United States. In a moment, we'll talk with the director of national intelligence, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Lucas Tomlinson. He's covering the president in Florida, and he's at Mar-a-Lago this morning.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, after that contentious meeting in the Oval Office, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  received a warmer reception in London than he did in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER, U.K. PRIME MINISTER: You have full backing across the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Zelenskyy received a warm welcome in London. Before arriving at 10 Downing Street, Zelenskyy showed his appreciation for the U.S., posting on X: We are very grateful to the United States for all the support. I'm thankful to President Trump, Congress for their bipartisan support and American people.\par \par \uc2 Gratitude the Trump administration thought was lacking in Washington. Zelenskyy arrived at the White House to sign a mineral rights deal. But 40 minutes into an Oval Office meeting with President Trump, it went off the rails. Vice President J.D. Vance jumped in.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Mr. President, Mr. President, with respect I think it's disrespectful for you to come into the Oval Office and try to litigate this in front of the American media.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: A fiery exchange in the Oval Office unlike any in history caught on camera.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy left the White House a short time later after Trump asked him to leave. The planned press conference and lunch cancelled, the mineral rights deal unsigned.\par \par \uc2 Before leaving for Palm Beach, Trump was asked when Zelenskyy will return to the White House.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, he says he wants it now, he wants to come back right now, but I can't do that.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Hours later, Zelenskyy appeared on "Special Report" with Bret Baier. Many in the Trump administration wanted an apology, Zelenskyy did not offer one.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR, "SPECIAL REPORT": So I'm not hearing from you, Mr. President, a thought that you owe the president an apology.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: No, I respect president and I respect American people and if I don't know -- if -- I think that we have to be very open and very honest, and I'm not sure that we did something bad. The fallout split along party lines even among Ukraine's biggest supporters.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Complete utter disaster. He either needs to resign or he needs to change.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): It was a planned ambush designed to embarrass President Zelenskyy in order to benefit \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): This weekend, President Trump will be working on his speech to Congress Tuesday night -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We'll have full coverage.\par \par \uc2 Lucas, thank you very much\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, the director of National Intelligence, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what is the intelligence community's assessment of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his future ambitions?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Well, I want to focus, first of all, on President Trump's goal and the reality that's on the ground, and that's the thing that seems to be getting lost in -- in what we're seeing in President Zelenskyy's statements in the White House and the European leaders' response to this.\par \par \uc2 President Trump recognizes the urgent need to end this war after three long bloody years and has proven that he is the only person that can do this.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy has different aims in mind. He has said that he wants to end this war but he will only accept an end apparently that leads to what he views as Ukraine's victory, even if it comes at an incredibly high cost of potentially World War III or even a nuclear war.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is committed to peace and to freedom. We're seeing this big divergence here between his position and his commitment to these values and the interests of the American people and the interests of President Zelenskyy and these European leaders.\par \par \uc2 This is an issue that has to be resolved. I know President Trump is committed to doing this and that's really where the next step needs to lead.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. So what is that next step? Because one of the criticisms of the Biden administration is that they hadn't talked to Putin for years. President Trump is doing that, he's having that conversation. But now, it seems the relationship with Zelenskyy is broken, at least temporarily. How does that get back on track? Because he's got to be part of the conversation as well.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Of course, and President Trump has made very clear that in his previous conversations with both Putin and Zelenskyy, that he wants to engage with both. He wants to engage in honest and real negotiations.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, what happened in the White House after close to an hour of conversation when President Zelenskyy directly challenged President Trump and Vice President Vance in front of the media and the American people, he really showed his lack of interest in any real good faith negotiations.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, there were a lot of conversations that were happening through different emissaries both for Zelenskyy and for President Trump, leading up to his visit there to the Oval Office and President Zelenskyy's immediate escalation there was -- was frankly quite a surprise. This has created a huge riff in the relationship.\par \par \uc2 President Zelensky, as you pointed out in the clips that you played, doesn't believe that that he did anything wrong, that there was no issue with what he brought up in, in the Oval Office with President Trump. There's -- there's going to have to be a rebuilding of any kind of interest in good faith negotiations I think before President Trump is going to be willing to re-engage on this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: One of the things that might have gotten lost in the shuffle as people were trying to listen and now they're talking over each other, we've all seen the clips there from the Oval Office is Zelenskyy was saying we did have ceasefires. I have signed onto other agreements and yet Putin crossed those lines. He said he blew up the ceasefires. He killed my people.\par \par \uc2 Does he not have a valid point that there should be real -- you know, understanding his frustrations and skepticism that Putin is actually going to be a reliable partner in whatever deal comes together?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Really, there's -- there's two viable paths here. One is that this war continues on, as it has for over three years, more and more Ukrainians will lose their lives, more and more of the country of Ukraine will be decimated and destroyed in this what will continue to be essentially a war of attrition. And to what end? To what outcome?\par \par \uc2 President Trump sees this reality. So the alternative to this which he is so intent on which he talked about continuously throughout his campaign that the American people voted for is his commitment to ending this war, and bringing about peace. That requires bringing interested parties to the table to conduct these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Of course, both sides are going to have their arguments and their interests that they are fighting for. This is the reality of how these negotiations always occur during times of war. Neither side is going to be happy very likely with the outcome, but ultimately, it needs to lead to peace.\par \par \uc2 And that's what President Trump is so committed to because he recognizes that the longer this goes on, not only are more Ukrainians losing their lives but it increases the potential of this escalation towards World War III that he warned against as he sat there with Zelenskyy, saying that he's gambling with World War III, and that's not a cost that President Trump is willing to accept.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to put something up. A recent Quinnipiac poll just days ago, this is before the incident in the Oval Office, if that's what we want to call it, and it asked the American people, do you think the U.S. should trust Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ? Eighty-one percent of them said no.\par \par \uc2 You know it's hard to get 81 percent of Americans to agree on anything but they're united on that.\par \par \uc2 So why does this administration trust Putin?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: I think President Trump goes in with the cynical eye as he does, I imagine, with any deal that he's made. It's not about blind trust at all. It's about looking at the factors that are on the table, negotiating with all interested parties, and then for President Trump ultimately, he's going to look out for what's in the best interest of the American people, our ability to live in a peaceful and prosperous way in our country, and uphold the values of freedom that we hold dear.\par \par \uc2 So -- so I think again, President Trump has a very realist view of the picture and what's at stake, which is why he's so committed to working to bring about an end to this war, and, frankly, why he's proven that as -- as this war has been waged over the last three years, he's the only one that has the ability to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, our next guest coming up, Democratic congressman -- excuse me, now senator, was congressman, Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , says that he is very worried about what he saw on Friday, what it communicates.\par \par \uc2 He says: The people who are celebrating are Russia and Putin, Xi and China, people who don't have America's best interests at heart.\par \par \uc2 And we do have over the weekend a number of reactions out of Russia. You've got Russian Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  saying President Trump's the one using common sense and that we like the way he's acting. You've got the Kremlin spokesperson Dmitry Peskov saying what the U.S. is doing is changing the game and it largely coincides with our vision.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of Russia celebrating and saying what the U.S. is doing lines up with their vision?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: They're going to say what their position is.\par \par \uc2 What we should pay attention to here in the United States of America is the American people. So many people across the country celebrating the strong leadership that President Trump and Vice President Vance demonstrated in the Oval Office, something we have not seen over the last four years of the Biden administration where we finally have a president who's going to stand up strongly and fiercely and unwaveringly for the cause of peace and freedom for the American people.\par \par \uc2 I know that that is who President Trump has on his heart and on his mind as he is working towards bringing about a negotiated end to this war.\par \par \uc2 I think those who are criticizing his effort in this way are showing that they are not committed to peace. And in the case of many of those European countries, that they're not committed to the cause and values of freedom.\par \par \uc2 Even though they speak of this, we heard very clearly during Vice President Vance's speech in Munich, different examples of how these European partners and longtime allies in many cases are actually implementing policies that undermine democracy, that shows that they don't actually believe in the voices of the people being heard and implementing anti-freedom policies. We're seeing this in the United Kingdom. We're seeing this in Germany. We saw it with the tossing out of the elections in Romania.\par \par \uc2 So there's something fundamentally deeper here that shows a huge difference and divergence between the values that President Trump and Vice President Vance are fighting for, the values that are enshrined in our Constitution, the interests of the American people in our peace and freedom and national security, versus those of many of these European countries who are coming to Zelensky's side as he walked out of the White House, saying basically that they are going to support him in continuing this war and they don't stand with us around these fundamental values of freedom.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and they also though think that they're in more alignment and they should be with the U.S. when it comes to those freedoms and things you talk about. Certainly, those are not things that you would say that Russia or Putin celebrates or bestows on his own people.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: That's correct. I -- I would not make that claim and it's clear that that's not the case, nor does President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But that's not really what we're talking about here. We're talking about many of these European countries and Zelenskyy himself who claimed to be standing and fighting for the cause of freedom and democracy when we actually look at what's happening in reality in these countries, as well as with Zelenskyy's government in Ukraine is the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 You have the cancelling of elections in Ukraine. You have political parties being silenced or even criminalized or thrown in prison. You have the freedom of religion, churches being shut down. You have political opposition being silenced. You have total government control of the media.\par \par \uc2 We could go down a whole laundry list of issues that are against the values of democracy and freedom.\par \par \uc2 So it really begs the question as vice -- Vice President Vance said again in Munich, it's clear that they're standing against Putin. Obviously, that's clear, but what are they actually really fighting for and are they aligned with the values that they claim to hold in agreement with us, the values that President Trump and Vice President Vance are standing for, and those are the values of freedom, of peace and true security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. So the way that you describe Ukraine in the way that most Americans and most of the world understands Russia -- are you saying it's essentially choosing between the lesser of two evils?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: What I'm focused on is what President Trump is focused on, which is peace -- seeing the world through a very clear picture and making sure that the president is having honest and good faith negotiation with both of these countries to bring about an end to this war. That is what it's about, that lives are at stake. And, again, President Trump will make his decisions and will stay very focused on what is in the best interest of the American people and ensuring that future of peace and freedom.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. And we'll stay tuned to see where the reset is on the Ukrainian side. Director Gabbard, thank you for stopping in.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we're going to get reaction from a member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, Democrat \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 . He's live next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Zelenskyy's got a chip on his shoulder. Every European leader applauds him for standing up to Trump. All I can say is a missed opportunity. He doesn't even begin to explain it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, that was Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , clearly frustrated in the wake of that chaotic Oval Office meeting between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy, leaving the future of U.S.-Ukraine relations perhaps more uncertain than ever.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to get reaction from a member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, Democrat \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 . He's live next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Zelenskyy's got a chip on his shoulder. Every European leader applauds him for standing up to Trump. All I can say is a missed opportunity. He doesn't even begin to explain it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, that was Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , clearly frustrated in the wake of that chaotic Oval Office meeting between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy, leaving the future of U.S.-Ukraine relations perhaps more uncertain than ever.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Maryland Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , who sits on the Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Welcome. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): Shannon, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so this didn't happen suddenly on Friday. We know that there have been growing tensions. There have been a number of Trump administration officials who've sat down, who've met with Zelenskyy or his inner circle, and have said they've been very heated meetings.\par \par \uc2 There have been attempts to come to some kind of agreement on a number of things. They feel sometimes they've come out of these meetings, and they've been misrepresented or misconstrued what was actually discussed there. I want to play something from Secretary Rubio that he said Friday night about all this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, SECRETARY OF STATE: And when you see efforts to impede it, when you deliberate, when you tell someone, don't say, let's not talk about these things, let's not go in this direction, because it makes it harder for us to engage, and they insist on doing it anyway. You start to wonder. You start to wonder. I don't like to impugn people's motives, but you start to wonder what's behind it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You voted for Secretary Rubio. Do you have confidence in him and the way that he's trying to manage this?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I did vote for Senator Rubio to be Secretary of State. I regret that vote, because as a member of the Senate, Secretary Rubio was somebody who stood up for American values, American principles. He acknowledged that Russia was the aggressor against Ukraine. He realized that it wasn't Zelenskyy who was the dictator. And now he's simply taking his directions to the State Department from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and essentially parroting the president's position, which I understand, but it's very different than what Senator Rubio used to talk about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so we've got Secretary Bessent. He described the meeting that he had with President Zelenskyy in Kyiv. He said they wanted to get this deal done on the rare earth minerals. They wanted to show a bond and come out of that meeting united against Russia. He said it was a very tough 45-minute meeting, very loud decibel level. He wanted no daylight between the two. He says, he refused, Zelenskyy, to sign that and he said, I'll sign in Munich. Didn't sign in Munich. Came here on Friday, which we thought was going to be a celebration of signing this deal. And Secretary Bessent said it was one of the biggest own goals in history. That's how we saw the meeting going down.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I saw the meeting as a despicable display of bullying by the President of the United States and the Vice President of the United States against somebody whose country is at war with Putin, a brutal assault by Putin, who's lost thousands and thousands of Ukrainians, who wants peace more than anybody, but he also wants to make sure that it's a durable, sustainable peace.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned this mineral deal. The deal that was on the table at the White House, just to be clear, was very different than what Secretary Bessent first put on the table, both in Ukraine and at Munich.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Was that in part because Zelenskyy and his team, they had input, I mean they wanted changes to this deal?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Right, but just to be clear, what happened in Munich was the President's people essentially tried to extort the Zelenskyy and the Ukrainian people to give up half of their mineral supplies in order for past U.S. support and to continue ongoing U.S. support. It was extortion pure and simple. It would be like in the middle of World War II, you know, FDR saying to Churchill, we're not going to help you against the Nazis and Hitler unless you give up half of your mineral resources and half of your coal.\par \par \uc2 So it did change, but to suggest that, you know, Zelenskyy was wrong to be rejecting what was essentially extortion earlier on is a -- is a warped version of what actually happened.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think that the U.S. should get some return on the billions in taxpayer dollar investment in the way that Europe has, structuring these as loans?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Well, Europe has provided a lot in grants. They've provided some in concessionary loans. I'm fine if the United States wants to include some of its support in the form of concessionary loans. I'm sure Zelenskyy would support that. I mean, it was Zelenskyy's idea originally to include some kind of deal on minerals to provide for reconstruction. But what happened is that idea got transformed originally into an idea where it was -- it was really extortion. It was like, give us half of this stuff for what we've already done.\par \par \uc2 And then to see what happened at the White House, I will say, was embarrassing because, you know, Zelenskyy has talked about the sacrifice of his people. He's also been grateful to the United States. Whenever I meet with him and I met with him with others before his meeting at the White House, he always expresses gratitude. But you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not recognize sacrifice for this greater principle. After all, it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who said of American combat troops who died for our country that they were suckers and losers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, to that point, you know that there are 17 people who've been on the record who were in those meetings and on that trip who said those words were never said. So that's 17 people saying it didn't happen. It is a popular line to use. You can understand, though, maybe that this administration has got real levels of frustration with this and the way that things have gone down.\par \par \uc2 It's not just them. You were part of this meeting. You said bipartisan senators meeting with Mr. Zelenskyy before he went to the White House. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was also one of them. He seemed super frustrated after this meeting that the way he had talked with Mr. Zelenskyy about how to approach Mr. Trump had not maybe gone in one ear and out the other. Here is a bit of his reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I busted my (bleep) to try to help Ukraine to make sure we helped them win a war that would are -- but, you know, get this war over in a way that we don't have further aggression. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's the man to do it. I told him this morning, I told him, don't take the bait. Don't let the media or anybody else get you into an argument with President Trump. What he's doing today is resetting the relationship. You should be grateful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A lot of folks here in Washington are thinking if you've lost \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  on this, who's been incredibly supportive of President Zelenskyy and of continuing aid to Ukraine, you may be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : First, Shannon, I would just say it was General Kelly, a Marine Corps general, who made that statement about what Trump had said about U.S. service people. I choose to believe General Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Look, I -- Senator Graham has been a big supporter of the Ukrainian people. This was clearly an example where he decided to preserve his relationship with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think he was not genuinely frustrated? I mean, you were part of that meeting that morning, and it sounded like he was trying to prep him for success.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I was part of that meeting. And in that meeting, we saw President Zelenskyy again express gratitude to the American people. And he was sitting through this meeting, President Zelenskyy, for a long time and faced a barrage of misrepresentations, misstatements.\par \par \uc2 I actually thought he handled himself very well under the circumstances. I don't think we should expect foreign leaders to have to come into the Oval Office and play dear leader like the president's cabinet officials and others may be doing. I don't think that that is what we should expect of them.\par \par \uc2 He had to listen to a litany of lies and misinformation. And I thought he did it with incredible calm. And then, of course, you saw the President, the Vice President, just turn on him and bully him. And that spectacle, as you played earlier today, is being warmly received and embraced by our adversaries, by Putin and the Russians. And you can be sure President Xi is keeping one eye on what's happening out of Ukraine as he keeps another eye on Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 This was a total disaster for those of us who believe in American foreign policy, where we respect our allies and our friends and we stand up for democracy and freedom around the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is definitely a change in tone from the last administration. And we'll see how it plays out as we hope that these conversations come back to the table.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always good to see you. Thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 All right. Outrage after the Department of Justice releases documents from the Jeffrey Epstein case, but maybe not for the reasons you might imagine.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel on that and the possibility of a government shutdown just days away from the federal government running out of money, they're next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're looking for peace. We're not looking for somebody that's going to sign up a strong power and then not make peace because they feel emboldened and that's what I saw happening.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: It will be difficult for us. That's why I'm here. That's why we speak about the future negotiations. It will be difficult without your support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Future of U.S. support for Ukraine in question after an extraordinary Oval Office dust up between Presidents Trump and his Ukrainian counterpart, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Time now to discuss with our Sunday group, "USA Today" Washington bureau chief, Susan Page. Richard Fowler, Fox News contributor and Forbes contributing writer. Former \uc2 RNC\uc2  communications director, Doug Heye. And Hans Nichols, "Axios" congressional reporter.\par \par \uc2 I think we're all in agreement. We've never seen anything like we saw Friday in the Oval, but now we're onto the aftermath of it. We know that Zelenskyy is meeting with a number of European leaders today.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says this, European diplomats have acknowledged that while Europe could try to match U.S. funding if needed, they would have a harder time substituting the full range of weapons that have been provided to Kyiv, especially key capabilities in which they are lagging such as air defenses.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Susan, that begs the whole question, can this move forward a resolution to this conflict without active U.S. involvement?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : It won't be the same. Europe was not able to make up the whole role that the United States is playing. And they have to make a decision about how far they go along with a U.S. piece that's acceptable to the United States and could be imposed, that way that also doesn't just leave Ukraine out in the cold.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we've been at -- many of those Oval Office meetings, between a president and a leader or foreign leader, nothing like this has ever happened before. The world changed this week, coupled with the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  vote that the United States cast with Russia and against our European allies on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The alignment of the United States changed this week. And the U.S. role in the world has changed. And we're going to look back at this week as epic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Hans, how much -- and that was the third world leader that was in the oval office this week. I mean, it's been a week. But, Hans, how much of what this administration is saying about Putin, about Russia, about Zelenskyy is posturing for the sake of negotiating, keeping Putin at the table and moving him toward a resolution?\par \par \uc2 HANS NICHOLS, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, AXIOS: You know, in diplomacy, there's always the public part and the private part. And I think what was remarkable about this week is that the private part was public.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: So, yes, we've never seen anything like that happen. But that's not to say nothing like that has ever happened behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: And when you look to sort of the core of your question is, where is the Trump administration on finding a final peace deal? We know where they want to end up, which is to have some sort of end of fighting and -- but not security guarantees and then the access to the minerals.\par \par \uc2 But the details of how they get there are very messy and they're not -- I mean, you can say a lot about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but he always isn't very clear on what he -- how he wants to from path A to path B, where he wants to get, how he wants to do it.\par \par \uc2 And he's being almost intentionally ambiguous about his path. Because that's how he negotiates. And I'm not in a position to adjudicate on how good of a negotiator \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is. But a lot of his staff and a lot of his allies say, he's the best in the business.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. He loves to get a deal done. With that in mind, though, "Wall Street Journal" editorial board says this, their headline, Putin Wins the Zelenskyy -- Trump-Zelenskyy Oval Office Spectacle. It said, Mr. Trump does not want to be the president who abandoned Ukraine to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  with all the bloodshed and damage to U.S. interests that would result. Mr. Vance won't like to run for president in such a world either.\par \par \uc2 They were asking about why the vice president jumped in the way he did, but clearly, he thought there was some measure of disrespect.\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, FORMER RNC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Sure. And I think, you know, this is a situation where everybody didn't really adjudicate themselves in the best way possible. Zelenskyy should have known that he was walking into this. I don't think he was ambushed, as some were saying.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, the conversations that the European leaders have been having over the past couple weeks have been focused on what this question is. And obviously, I saw you this week at the British embassy. The week before I was in London, every conversation I had was -- whether it was with a Tory, a Lib Dem, somebody from Labour, what is the posture of the United States moving forward?\par \par \uc2 And I go back to when \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was in the Oval Office, one of the first things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did was point out, hey, we have a bust of Winston Churchill. It's back in the Oval Office. Well, Winston Churchill, we know, said, we will fight on the beaches. We will fight on the fields. We will never surrender. That is the kind of language that we usually expect from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think, ultimately, Zelenskyy has one card here that he can still play, and it's the visual of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he goes to Ukraine. We saw how that played very, very well for Boris Johnson, the times that he went.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  understands visuals very well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Some type of Reagan-esque moment, if that is possible, in Kyiv would be a real strong signal from Trump and to Putin.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I think that it's going to take a lot, it would take a lot, Richard, to get there because I'm not sure they're even communicating at this point.\par \par \uc2 But to President Trump's point, he is having a conversation with Putin that wasn't happening during the Biden administration. And how do you resolve it if you're not at the table with the key players?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: This is actually I agree with the sitting president is that we do need to be talking to everybody at the table. With that being said, the ideal of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going to Kyiv, I think is highly unlikely.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to play down Susan's point because I do think the ideal of this meeting being historic and unprecedented and all the edges that follow is actually very important in this moment.\par \par \uc2 What we heard from the United -- on the \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign minister talking about that we need a new world order, we need a new world leader. That moment to me was very, very, very -- it was -- it cost pause, but it was also very telling.\par \par \uc2 Europe is sort of saying, if the U.S. is not going to lead in this moment against somebody that we can all agree is our adversary, Russia and Putin under Russia's control, then there's other problems that's at stake. How do we do joint military engagements? How do we do joint intelligence deals in a world in which the United States is walking away from alliances they've had since the beginning of our founding, i.e. France and the Europeans?\par \par \uc2 And so I think this is a very telling moment for the United States. And I think it is a shift in the world order. And I think there's a lot of countries, many of our long-time allies saying, how do we navigate?\par \par \uc2 And I think also with that being said, many of our foes, including China, saying, how do we navigate in a moment where the United States is walking back in an attempt to save America first? Is this also an America last? Because the world is shifting.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and he is definitely up into the world order, but a lot of people would say that's his strength. These leaders do not know exactly what to expect from President Trump, and that's part of his negotiation style.\par \par \uc2 It certainly worked when you were talking with people like the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and others who were really concerned about what he might actually do.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure that we touch on this, though, a couple of things. There are other things going on here domestically that we are going to run out of money, apparently, in a couple of weeks. There is this massive budget deal on a collision course.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Hans. Where do we go on this? Because the two frameworks that we have right now are both Republican-led --\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But they have serious differences and President Trump's going to have to get involved. It would seem at some point in trying to negotiate these.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: And we see the level of which Trump had to get involved just to get this first incremental House deal across the line, which is a lot of phone calls and a lot of leverage.\par \par \uc2 If there's going to be any sort of big tax and spending package of the scope that the president and these Republican allies are going to talk about, that president is going to get much more involved, he wants it. It's central to his agenda. He wants to deliver tax cuts. There's a sense inside the White House that they need to have an economic boost and have Americans starting to feel better about it.\par \par \uc2 So that's the strategy. Again, how they get there, it's going to be difficult. The change in the last 48, 72 hours is that there's less talk about a budget reconciliation deal and more talk about a potential government shutdown, which I'll leave for the rest of the table to solve.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Doug, you've got a resolution?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: I've lived through government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: They are not fun. And by the way, Congress is working throughout them. They're not on vacation or anything. But this comes down to, I think, ultimately \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's leadership, right? We have a very small majority in the House, so small that \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  can't have her confirmation vote because they need her vote --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: -- in the House. Obviously, a small one in the Senate as well.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the one who's going to have to get these House Republicans and Senate Republicans in line together to move forward because ultimately, regardless of party, we always know the House and the Senate don't agree on things in any normal circumstance.\par \par \uc2 This is not a normal circumstance. And it leads to a lot of questions of what would the DOGE effort then be if we're operating in a shutdown?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Another thing that made headlines for, not the reasons that we thought it was going to make headlines, potentially, is the Epstein revelations this week, which were not revelations.\par \par \uc2 Foxnews.com has this headline, Conservatives explode at botched Epstein document rollout, quote, complete disappointment. Richard.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: It is utter complete disappointment. I'm not really sure what they were expecting. I don't think we'll see these Epstein's files come out for all the reasons, even though we should.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it's important to sort of talk about this government shutdown, because I think that's a real thing. And I think we've got to take it outside the beltway for just a second. And when I say I think about somebody like Maria Alvarez, who's somebody who works at HUD, she lives in Virginia. Right? She's worked there for 10 years, helping the American navigate our complicated federal housing system.\par \par \uc2 She's worried about her job right now, like many federal employees are, right? And when you think about, one, a government shutdown, which could make many of them not have money. But two, they're also worried about mass firings that could come this week because of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who are basically pushing the line on federal executive power, while Congress sort of sits and sort of, I guess, applauds this.\par \par \uc2 This is a real, real concern for a lot of the American people. And I think there's a lot of blame going on Musk and Trump, but at this moment, I also blame the \uc2 American Congress\uc2 . When are you going to say, this is our job to allocate how money is spent? This is our job to write federal statues and determine how agencies are operated.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to hold that job, whether we're Democrats or Republicans, because the Constitution has given this to us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Susan, what about that? Because we're going to hear more of these personal anecdotes and stories of real people who are on the other end of these decisions. Do Republicans risk pushing this too far?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes. You know, the problem for President Trump is that this is beyond the power of an executive order to fund the government. And so the issue with a shutdown may be not whether it happens. I think that is more likely than not now, but who gets blamed when it happens?\par \par \uc2 And if Republicans control the White House, the House, and the Senate, how do they avoid holding responsibility for the government running out of money?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I feel like they get blamed even when they're not running the House and the Senate and the White House. Democrats have been really good at messaging on these shutdowns. So we'll see. But hopefully, the next time we see each other, we will not be sitting in the midst of a shutdown. Panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 OK. Courtroom drama. The Trump administration facing nearly 100 lawsuits now as it aims to impose sweeping changes to the federal government.\par \par \uc2 And as we right now await action from the Supreme Court, our legal panel is going to join us to break down the most critical cases, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Since the president began his second term with a flurry of executive action and sweeping cuts to the federal budget and workforce, the legal challenges against him have been piling up. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 A couple of these have already reached to the Supreme Court and we're waiting on action there, but it hasn't all been defense. The administration is on offense too as it warns it's going to take action against states and localities that did not or will not comply with the president's directives.\par \par \uc2 Our legal panel is here to break down the merits of some of the most critical cases. Tom Dupree, former Principal Deputy Assistant U.S. Attorney General. And Manhattan Institute Senior Fellow, Ilya Shapiro. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER PRINCIPAL DEPUTY ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So let's start there. The DOJ on offense, they are going after states and localities that are not complying with some of these executive orders, whether it's sanctuary cities, whether it's something like the president's statement and executive order on women's sports.\par \par \uc2 Ilya, there's outgoing, not just incoming in the legal fights.\par \par \uc2 ILYA SHAPIRO, SENIOR FELLOW, MANHATTAN INSTITUTE: Well, they're lawyered up. They're aggressive. They used the transition well.\par \par \uc2 And look, states, if you take federal funds, there are strings attached, including civil rights protections, including enforcing federal law. So if you don't like it, don't take the money.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we saw this play out with the president and the governor of Maine, and she said, we'll see you in court.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Oh, absolutely. And look, I think the Trump administration is much more comfortable playing offense and trying to enforce their policy agenda through litigation than having to defend all their actions in the federal court.\par \par \uc2 And what we saw with the Maine governor, I think, is just the first in what is going to be a long series of blue state governors pushing back against the Trump administration's agenda saying, we'll see you in court, and you know the Trump team is ready for a legal battle.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They have been gearing up.\par \par \uc2 OK, next in our countdown, these arguments about DOGE and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and whether they have the authority to do what they're doing. "Wall Street Journal" had an opinion piece and said, listen, Congress could solve this. They could put a line into the reconciliation package of the budget conferring this authority over onto the administration. The piece says, "the plaintiffs claim that Mr. Trump is violating statutes or exercising authority Congress hasn't granted him. Such claims would be made mood if Congress gave the president express power to take these actions."\par \par \uc2 Would it work?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I think it would work if Congress took those actions. But I think it is a very, very long shot, to put it charitably, that Congress is actually going to legislate in a lot of these areas.\par \par \uc2 And look, the administration has made clear, they are going to move as fast as they can, as hard as they can, on as many fronts as they can. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  - and the courts may push back. They may put in guardrails. They may slow down the administration. But I think, at the end of the day, it's going to be largely speed bumps and the courts are not going to prevent the Trump administration from trying to dramatically reduce the size of the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, there's been a mix of wins and losses.\par \par \uc2 ILYA SHAPIRO, MANHATTAN INSTITUTE SENOR FELLOW AND MANHATTAN INSTITUTE DIRECTOR OF CONSTITUTIONAL STUDIES: While there may be some grey areas, most of what DOGE is doing has authority in the sense that there is actually an Obama era U.S. Digital Service that Trump renamed the U.S. DOGE Service. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and all of the Musketeers are special government employees pursuant to statute. They're not just kind of floating out there as - on some whim. And they're looking at waste, fraud, and abuse. They can't, you know, shut down the entire Department of Education. That would take legislation. But they can certainly cancel certain contracts, reevaluate certain things. And there shouldn't even be standings to challenge because the president has until the end of the fiscal year to spend the money.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so next on our list, it kind of works in with this, is the firing of federal employees and doing this in a mass, you know, email or whatever it is. The Office of Personnel Management here in D.C. has sent out some of these emails, and they've done firing of thousands of probationary employees. Well, a judge has ruled on this. Judge \uc2 William Alsup\uc2  said this on Friday. He said, "no statute - anywhere, ever - has granted OPM," Office of Personnel Management, "the authority to direct the termination of employees in other agencies." He goes on to write, "Congress' statutory scheme grants to each agency head the authority to manage their own affairs, including the hiring and firing of employees."\par \par \uc2 Ilya, does that suggest if these agencies did this on a more individual basis, at least within their silo, could it work?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I think so. This is kind of ticky-tack. You know, you haven't crossed the t's dotted the i's.\par \par \uc2 There could be bigger challenges, again, proactive Trump administration litigation against some of these civil service rules tying the president's hands. Union contracts in the - in the public sector might violate executive control over - over the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to get to number two on our list because, Tom, this is something that you flagged. It didn't get a lot of attention this week, but this idea that the Justice Department, now under Attorney General Bondi, has said a number of local fire and police departments that were being sued because they were using standardized tests, that there was an argument they were having to discriminatory results, that they were facing legal trouble over that.\par \par \uc2 Now, on to the Biden administration. The assistant AG, Kristen Clark at the time, said, "discriminatory barriers that deny qualified black and female applicants the opportunity to be police officers violate civil rights and undermine public safety efforts."\par \par \uc2 The AG says, these are standard tests. We're not going to sue you over that when the results don't go according to a quota or plan.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Exactly. And, look, this is one of the clearest possible signals that there is a new sheriff in town at the Justice Department. The civil rights division is walking back a lot of these lawsuits that were filed under Biden. And to your point, what they are saying is the fact that these police departments and fire departments are using standardized tests that may result in disparate outcomes and not achieve the diversity results that they were hoping for, that doesn't amount to intentional discrimination. They're walking these lawsuits back, they're closing the door on the litigation, they're saying, let the police and fire departments make their hiring decisions, and they will. We're going to focus on different priorities, such as enforcement of, I'm assuming, religious civil rights and other civil liberties that may not have been as enforce as strictly in the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and, Ilya, every administration is going to have its own priorities and they're going to want their to-do list when they get a new AG.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: I mean police departments, fire department, didn't the Supreme Court already rule on this like 15 years ago in the Ricci case?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, I covered it. I remember it very well. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: So, universities as well. If they are engaged in massive resistance to - or just shuffling around the same edu-crats rather than shutting down those civil rights violating programs, they're gong to - there's going to be hell to pay.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's get to the Supreme Court. This is our number one case that we're watching because this could literally come at any time. A lower court judge has ordered the Trump administration to pay out a lot of money in federal foreign aid. They are - they don't want to do it. The administration says, give us time. We're - we're reviewing a lot of these decisions.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  - this got appealed all the way up to the Supreme Court - he put a pause Wednesday night because there was a midnight deadline. He said, let us look at this before we make a decision. So, we're waiting for that.\par \par \uc2 When the Trump administration filed with the Supreme Court to get that lower court order thrown out, they said, "what the government cannot do is pay arbitrarily determined demands on an arbitrary timeline of the district court's choosing or according to extra-contractual rules that the court has devised." So, they're saying, this lower court judge can't force us to pay the money.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, the Trump administration is saying, this lower court judge overstepped his bounds. And look, I think the Supreme Court has a very good chance they are going to come to the rescue of the administration on this, for two reasons. One is that they may say that the district judge infringed on the president's power to conduct foreign affairs. The other thing they may do, and this was - we saw in that quote, is that they may say, look, this is an arbitrary timeline. The district judge basically said, you know, pay within 72 hours, I direct the money to go out. The U.S. came to the court and said, we can't do it that fast. It's a physical impossibility.\par \par \uc2 So, my sense is the Supreme Court may be receptive to that and may say, look, even if you have to pay some of these, we're going to let you do it on the schedule that you, the administration, requested, not what the district court has ordered.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, these groups that are suing on the other side, they say, "forcing thousands of American businesses and nonprofits to suspend their work," halting disbursements for work already done, work that had already been reviewed, it's - "the government is plunging respondents into financial turmoil." They say the money's got to go out.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Well, the question isn't whether businesses or anybody else, foreign organizations, NGOs are affected. The question is, whether the president has authority and discretion to review these contracts. And it's pretty clear that he does. Most of what DOGE is doing and, you know, it's not saying, Congress, you spent this, we're not going to do it. They're saying, you know, this particular thing, we don't like. And the - the - ultimately the Supreme Court - you know, for \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  to jump in alone, that's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is. And we stand by. And I will let you know when he makes a decision.\par \par \uc2 Ilya and Tom, thank you very much for lending us your expertise. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, this week, by the way, a novel case lands at the Supreme Court. Mexico is suing several U.S.-based gun manufacturers saying they are to blame for growing violence south of the border. The companies say they produce a lawful product and they're not responsible for Mexico's crime crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Smith and Weston, Beretta, Colt, Glock, all among the defendants in a $10 billion-dollar lawsuit filed by Mexico alleging these U.S. companies knowingly allowed guns to end up in the hands of dangerous cartel members in Mexico, who then used them to terrorize and kill.\par \par \uc2 Larry Keane\uc2 , is senior vice president to general counsel for the National Shootings Sports Foundation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you know what Mexico says. There were notifications that there were entities that were letting these distributors, letting these manufacturers know, this is where your weapons are ending up. That they had some form of notice.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KEANE\uc2 , NATIONAL SPORTS SHOOTING FOUNDATION GENERAL COUNSEL: Generalized foreseeability does not give rise, it's not proximate cause. There's no legal duty.\par \par \uc2 Baseball bat manufacturers know that some of their products will be misused in assaults. Knife manufacturers know that some of their products will be misused to stab people. That doesn't give rise to liability.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But what happens if the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , after hearing arguments next week, allows Mexico to pursue a legal remedy in the U.S. court system? Gun rights advocates like Keane are worried.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: It would mean the destruction of the Second Amendment in the United States. It would bankrupt the industry.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Congress passed the Protection of Lawful Commerce in Arms Act in 2005 to prevent gun companies from being held liable for misuse of their products. But Mexico argues the law, quote, "provides no protection for businesses engaged in unlawful commerce and arms." And alleges that's what the manufacturers have been doing, by selling to dealers Mexico claim the manufacturers knew were preferred by the cartels.\par \par \uc2 A group of blue state attorneys general is backing Mexico, saying the law is meant only to shield "blameless manufacturers and sellers" but that it doesn't equate to blanket absolute immunity against any and all liability.\par \par \uc2 But another coalition led by Republican Montana Attorney General Austin Knudsen argues it isn't the manufacturer's fault that gun crime is raging out of control in Mexico, and points the finger elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN KNUDSEN, MONTANA ATTORNEY GENERAL: This is happening primarily because we provide a lot of firearms to the Mexican government, to the Mexican army, and a lot of those guns go missing and end up in the cartels' hands. This is - this is a Mexico problem, not an American problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll see what the justices think. That case is scheduled for an hour-long argument on Tuesday with a decision due by the end of June.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will take you live to Rome for an update on \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  as he struggles to overcome a life-threatening health crisis. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is resting today as he undergoes treatment for double pneumonia. Fox News chief religion correspondent Lauren Green is in Rome with more.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like he's tweeting as well, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT: He's tweeting a little bit. A lot of that tweet he had is a reflection of his Angeles prayer that he released earlier today.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  still slowly improving. You know, Friday's respiratory crisis appears to be a rather isolated event from which he recovered rather quickly.\par \par \uc2 As you said, the Vatican - the latest out of the Vatican is that he had a good night. He woke up. He had breakfast and continued to read newspapers, as always. Doctors at Gemelli Hospital continue to treat the 88-year-old pope for double pneumonia, alternating between two types of oxygen therapy, air through a nose tube and a more intense mechanical mask. Vatican sources say the pope is in good spirits, eating solid food and able to walk with some assistance.\par \par \uc2 Today marks the third Sunday that the pope has not delivered his Angeles prayer in person. He released it in writing earlier today. In it he thanked doctors and staff for their attentive care, and for the faithful for their prayers, saying in part, "I feel in my heart the blessing that is hidden within frailty because it is precisely in these moments that we learn even more to trust in the Lord."\par \par \uc2 Last night, that continuing rosary vigil, this time inside St. Peter's Basilica because of the rain.\par \par \uc2 While the pope continues to direct Vatican business from the hospitals, it hasn't stopped the rumor mill from speculating that he could resign if his frail health becomes an obstacle. One papal historian reacts - rejects that scenario.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUSTEN IVEREIGH, POPE FRANCIS BIOGRAPHER: He's spoken various times about this. He's saying that for him the papacy is for life. It's really important in a world where there's a kind of cult of health and youth. But here in the church, we say, no, there is a mission which God gives you, which is for life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Doctors say the pope's prognosis is still guarded, that he is still not out of danger.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lauren, thank you very much for the update.\par \par \uc2 And a quick note, my new episode of my podcast "Livin' the Bream" drops today. I talk with Joel Rosenberg about his newest book, "The Beijing Betrayal." It is a thriller that looks at the terror connections and foreign policy challenges ranging from the Middle East to the Asia-Pacific region. Check it out wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 And don't forget, we've got special coverage of the president's joint address to Congress on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Have a wonderful week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020250302el3200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Clarifies Federal Job Justification E-Mail; U.S. And Ukraine Agree To Framework Of Deal; Ukraine Locals Plea For More Aid From America; House Democrats Stand Firm Against Budget Resolution; \uc2 FAA\uc2  And \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Investigating; Senate Confirms Driscoll As Army Secretary; Around 90 Illegal Migrants Arrested Near Houston; ICE Operations Leads To Arrests Of Illegal Immigrants In Texas; Activists In Southern California Post Information About ICE Agents While Interfering With Their Operations; Nonpartisan \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  Outlines Ways Federal Government Can Save Money; Trump Administration Says Deal With Ukraine And \uc2 Rare Earth Minerals\uc2  Near; Department Of Government Efficiency Sends Second Email To Federal Employees Asking What They Did Last Week; House Speaker Claims To Have Enough Votes To Pass Large Spending Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Alex Hogan, Aishah Hasnie, Mike  Tobin, Jennifer Griffin, Brooke Taylor, Jonathan Hunt, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 7,784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: There you go. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Jesse, did Harold guess hot tea?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, all right, just checking. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The House prepares to vote on the Republican budget resolution with uncertainty over how many in the GOP might defect.\par \par \uc2 Activists against immigration reform try to disrupt operations aimed at rounding up illegals, and one of their tactics could be dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And another near disaster in the aviation sector, a private plane gets in the path of a commercial jet about to land, prompting reaction from the transportation secretary.\par \par \uc2 But first breaking tonight, speaking to reporters from the Oval Office, President Trump says the directive for federal workers to detail what they did at work last week is, "somewhat voluntary, but could result in their firing depending on how their boss handles it."\par \par \uc2 Adding a bit more confusion to a process that administration officials say is being handled individually by each department and agency head. Last night was the deadline for federal employees to reply to the initial e- mail, all an effort to see who is working and what they're doing for the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. As all that was happening, also separately, a federal judge left a temporary restraining order in place, siding with non-profits, continuing to block a government wide funding freeze. This case stemmed from a rescinded memo from the Office of Management and Budget. Despite the short term losses on that front, DOGE is posting big successes, 65 billion in savings, 2200 contracts terminated. But it hasn't gone off without a hitch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Only hours after the Office of Personnel Management told agency heads they need not fire workers who don't respond to the e- mail asking for five things they accomplished last week, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  posted the opposite. Subject to the discretion of the president, they will be given another chance. Failure to respond a second time will result in termination.\par \par \uc2 The second time in as many days, Musk announced a consequence on X that wasn't communicated elsewhere. OPM maintained agencies would determine appropriate consequences despite messaging from other officials.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You need to be able to answer the question. If you couldn't answer it yesterday, you have until today.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt clarifying.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The secretaries are responsible for their specific workforce, and we advise federal workers, unless your agency has dictated you not to, to respond to this e-mail.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But dismissing confusion.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Do you think the judge should hire a spokesperson, just so that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  doesn't put out tweets that then confuse federal workers?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Are my press briefings, not good enough for you, Jacqui?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president following up with this.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you don't answer, I guess you get fired.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The deadline for answers and which agencies are participating still unclear. This answer also mighty today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who is the DOGE administrator?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: So, the president tasked \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to oversee the DOGE effort.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you tell us who the administrator of DOGE is?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Again, I've been asked to answer this question. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is overseeing DOGE.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The White House later confirming Amy Gleason is the Acting Administrator of DOGE, but in the Oval Office, clarity coming together on President Trump's deal to get U.S. access to Ukraine's rare earth minerals.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I hear that he's coming on Friday. Certainly, it's OK with me. If we'd like to, we're saying, look, we want to be secured. We want to get that money back.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president also pushing back after Russia denied Trump's claim yesterday that the Kremlin would accept European peace keeping troops.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We need peace keeping too, a form of peacekeeping that's acceptable to everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH The president was asked in the Oval Office What Ukraine gets out of this deal, and in his answer, he said it includes military equipment. And we've asked the White House to clarify, because they've insisted that this deal does not include any commitment for military support, and he could just be talking about past aid. We'll let you know when we get the answer, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 According to multiple sources, the U.S. and Ukraine have reportedly agreed on a framework of a deal involving critical minerals. We're awaiting more details about that agreement.\par \par \uc2 But what happened at the United Nations had some hard feelings wasn't really received well in Kyiv or in Europe, a final deal, though, could be signed -- could be signed as early as Friday.\par \par \uc2 The major shift, as I mentioned, came at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  Monday, when the U.S. voted against resolution calling out Moscow's aggression and demanding an immediate withdrawal of Russian troops. The U.S. then abstained from voting on its own resolution after the Europeans amended it to make clear Russia was the aggressor in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now, throughout the negotiations, President Zelenskyy has been steadfast in his security demands. Let's take a deeper look at the potential deal that would give the U.S. access to the war torn country's vast critical mineral reserves. Those are essential and they're essential metals. There's also other materials used in a broad range of products.\par \par \uc2 For example, the dark blue here shows Ukraine's titanium reserves. Titanium is a key component in the surgical applications in aircraft. The ground beneath Ukraine is believed to hold about five percent of the world's mineral resources. By comparison, the U.S. covers roughly 12 times as much area, but is estimated to only have one or two percent of the world's mineral resources.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Trump says the deal valued at up to $500 billion would compensate the U.S. for nearly $183 billion in aid and military assistance during the war. This is the real number. He has used the number 350, this is what it's all added up. As so far, Kyiv has pushed back, saying any agreement like this must come with security guarantees, something French president \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  discussed with me last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT: The U.S. will have the deal on mineral, critical minerals and rare earths and stone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you're fine with that?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes. But if we -- if we leave Ukraine alone, we did it in the past. So, we need something more substantial. So, we have to negotiate with the U.S., with the allies, with Ukraine, which type of security guarantee makes sense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, even if a final agreement is reached, it's not quite taking a shovel and digging money out of the ground. Developing Ukraine's resources requires significant capital investments in a market dominated by the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 We've done stories about this. Rare earth elements are a subset of critical minerals. China is well ahead of all other countries in rare earth mine production, extracting 270,000 metric tons in 2024. The U.S. was second, with 45,000 metric tons. Burma actually was third at 31,000.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data indicates China accounted for almost half the world's rare earth reserves in 2024 and that is a major reason why the U.S. is eager for access to Ukraine's resources.\par \par \uc2 Now, rare earth elements are vital to national security. They are used in batteries, in phones, in computers, cars, wind turbines, medical tests, nuclear reactors, glasses, wastewater treatment, among other things. You can see it plugs into a lot of different things.\par \par \uc2 Another looming challenge here is that a large portion of these deposits in Ukraine are in Russian occupied areas. Estimates place up to 40 percent of Ukraine's metal resources under Russian control. This is all in the red right here.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the largest rare earth deposit is right here in the gray. It spans the front line of the highly contested Donetsk region.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says his country is open to economic cooperation with the U.S. on rare earth minerals, and he added a deal could be reached on these Russian occupied areas of Ukraine. But what that deal looks like and control, those are all things that are developing. The rapidly evolving negotiations continue as Russia unleashed another night of heavy attacks on the country.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan is in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): For soldiers in the cold trenches on Ukraine's eastern front, it's the start of a fourth year at war. Russia for another night unleashed more than 200 attack drones across Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Give us weapon because I want my children to live.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Ukrainian civilians are begging Americans not to give up on their fight.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA (voice over): Democracy was always a priority in America in its values and its fight with terrorism.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): While Ukrainian officials are optimistic about the potential minerals deal.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSIY GONCHARENKO, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT MEMBER: I'm absolutely in support of having American companies here and working together.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Some politicians are unhappy about President Trump's calls for Ukraine to reimburse the U.S. for military aid.\par \par \uc2 GONCHARENKO: The United States decided what to give us. It was not asked to choose what to receive. So you just can't now say, OK, now this is after three years the bill for this.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Today, the White House says it's critical this happens.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: This is to recoup American tax dollars that have been funding Ukraine's national defense.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): The future of the conflict also weighing heavily on those who have seen action on the front lines and paid a high price.\par \par \uc2 SERGIY SHYKUTO, WOUNDED UKRAINIAN SOLDIER (through translator): I'm trying to adapt.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Sergei broke his spine and injured his leg one year into the war. He's recovering, but concerned about what a potential cease fire could mean for the country he fought to protect.\par \par \uc2 SHYKUTO (through translator): It hurts to think about it what awaits us in the future, because as far as I understand now, there are no security guarantees for us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Tonight, air raid sirens are blaring once again in Kyiv with a new wave of Russian attacks. Reports indicate that a drone has set fire to house just outside of Kyiv, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan live in Kyiv. Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, House Republicans are expected to vote tonight as you look live on the House floor on their budget resolution, the bill would kick start President Trump's so called, big, beautiful bill. But not all Republicans are for it, and unified Democrats are obviously trying to block it.\par \par \uc2 Senior International Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is live on Capitol Hill tonight with the latest. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. We are keeping a close eye on the House floor right now because this bill is about to come up. It is the second in a three vote series, and as you mentioned, Speaker Johnson dealing with some very, very slim majority here. He is dealing with budget hawks demanding massive cuts, and then also moderates who are afraid those massive cuts will hurt their districts.\par \par \uc2 Right now, if the numbers shake out the way that we are thinking, Speaker Johnson can only lose three of his own, and that is because he won't get any help from the Democrats tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): House Democrats will not provide a single vote to this reckless Republican budget. Not one.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): House Democrats all over the Capitol today protesting a critical vote tonight that would unlock President Trump's agenda. The GOP budget resolution opens the door to $300 billion in border and defense spending. It also includes an extension to the 2017 Trump tax cuts and a $4 trillion debt ceiling increase.\par \par \uc2 Republicans hope to cut two trillion in spending, and Democrats say they will do it by slashing Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRENDAN BOYLE\uc2  (D-PA): The math is quite clear. They have to, as a minimum, cut hundreds of billions of dollars from Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): GOP leadership, though, says that's a lie.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): The word Medicaid is not even in this bill. This bill doesn't even mention the word Medicaid a single time.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But first they have to persuade their own.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NICOLE MALLIOTAKIS\uc2  (R-NY): We have to make sure that leadership includes those of us who have large Medicaid populations in that process.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Democrats are also ripping apart the Trump tax cuts, warning that those will only benefit billionaires.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): Average Americans got breadcrumbs. They got a couple weeks of groceries and tax cuts, while the wealthiest got the lion's share of that tax scam.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The nonpartisan \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2  though states that all income levels would pay less if the tax cuts were extended, even if higher earners see bigger gains.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, that majority, that slim majority just got a little bit slimmer. We believe that speaker Johnson can now only lose two Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Now, Fox just told a group of House Republicans went over the White House today to speak with President Trump. President Trump putting in his own personal factor here, reaching out to some of these Republicans to try to flip them. We will see if that Trump factor pulled through tonight as they are about to vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Down to the wire. Aishah Hasnie live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a near miss at Chicago airport. We'll show you the dramatic video and the fallout.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the China threat takes center stage on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT", another scare in the aviation world today, two planes, you see there, nearly collided at Chicago's Midway Airport, prompting reaction from officials.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin has details Live from Chicago. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  and \uc2 FAA\uc2  are now investigating this near miss at Chicago's Midway Airport. Video show Southwest Flight 2504 inbound from Omaha. Coming in for a landing, but just before the wheel's touchdown, a private twin jet, \uc2 Flexjet\uc2  560 is seen crossing the runway. The way Southwest puts it, the crew performed a precautionary go around.\par \par \uc2 We've got audio of the conversation with the \uc2 Flexjet\uc2  and the tower.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (via telephone): \uc2 Flexjet\uc2  560, turn left on runway four left. Cross runway three-one left and hold short of runway three-one center.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (via telephone): All right left on two -- on four left, cross the two-two or one-three center. \uc2 Flexjet\uc2  560.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Flexjet\uc2  560, negative. Cross runway three-one left, hold short runway three-one center.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Cross three-one left, hold short three-one center.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): So, you know, here the tower, tell the \uc2 Flexjet\uc2  to cross runway three-one left. That's one of the thinner runways to the bottom left of your screen. Then, hold before crossing three-one center. Three-one. Center is the big runway in the middle, where Southwest Flight 2504 supposed to land.\par \par \uc2 The pilot doesn't seem to get it the first time, but acknowledges the second. Still, that video shows the private twin jet in the runway as the big 737, is coming in.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BOYD, AVIATION EXPERT: The instructions given to that small jet were not listened to by the pilot. As a result, we had a near miss.\par \par \uc2 In this case, it's not a systemic issue. It's really a human issue. There are systemic issues that add to