counts of then-Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 , as she weighed new export controls on semiconductors.\par \par \uc2 And last summer, Chinese hackers breached the GOP e-mail system before the Republican National Convention.\par \par \uc2 RUSH DOSHI, ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF SECURITY STUDIES, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY'S SCHOOL OF FOREIGN SERVICE: China is the most capable cyber actor the U.S. faces.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Perhaps, the most serious recent breach was when Chinese state sponsored hackers known as Salt Typhoon, penetrated nine U.S. telecommunications firms, giving Beijing access to Americans text messages and phone conversations.\par \par \uc2 Those Chinese hackers remain inside this sensitive critical infrastructure, according to cyber experts.\par \par \uc2 DOSHI: The unclassified systems are compromised, whether at the state department, treasury, or the U.S. trade rep, which gives China insight into what we're doing, an advantage in negotiation. We should move that into a more secure system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: China's President Xi, called \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , on Monday, the third anniversary of Russia's invasion of Ukraine, vowing China and Russia, "cannot be moved away from one another", pushing back against the Trump administration's efforts to drive a wedge between the two powers by negotiating directly with Putin. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Senate has confirmed President Trump's pick to oversee the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 . Lawmakers voted 66 to 28 to confirm Iraq war veteran and adviser to Vice President Vance, Dan Driscoll. Vice President Vance, swore in Driscoll this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Driscoll served in the Army for 3-1/2 years, after leaving the military attended Yale Law School. That's where he met his friend, J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Up next, immigration enforcement in Texas, how agents rounded up nearly 100 illegal immigrants in just one day.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is back to taking care of some business matters as he undergoes treatment for a respiratory infection at a Rome hospital.\par \par \uc2 Vatican says the pontiff met with his secretary of state yesterday and approved a handful of decrees.\par \par \uc2 The collapse of elevated parts of a highway under construction kills four workers in South Korea. Six others were hurt there. Cause of that collapse not immediately known.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight. Britain's prime minister pledges to raise defense spending to 2-1/2 percent of gross domestic product by 2027.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , on his way to the U.S., says the Europe -- this says "Europe is in a new era of insecurity that requires a generational response." That announcement coming two days before he meets with President Trump at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Almost 100 illegal immigrants are off the streets tonight in one Texas community. Correspondent Brooke Taylor takes us along on the mission from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE TAYLOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In one of the largest operations, yet, under the Trump administration, borders czar Tom Homan, tells Fox News, around 90 illegal migrants were arrested in and around Colony Ridge, a community outside of Houston.\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, FORMER ACTING DIRECTOR, IMMIGRATION AND CUSTOMS ENFORCEMENT: There's actually a large-scale criminal investigation. I'm not going to get into the details of that investigation, but today was phase one.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Early Monday morning, Homeland Security Investigations, special agents, and Texas DPS troops, conducted a targeted operation in the largely Hispanic community.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There were like police on the \uc2 Family Dollar\uc2 , and they were taking some people, and my mom, she got scared.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Homan, though, says the majority of those arrested were criminal migrants.\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, WHITE HOUSE BORDER CZAR: The last number I saw was like 69 criminals.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Among those, a three-time previously deported criminal illegal alien from El Salvador. According to ICE, he had a criminal arrest warrant for alleged repeated sexual abuse of a minor. Homan says some of the illegal migrants had warrants and were arrested at worksites. Others were picked up during traffic stops conducted by Texas DPS.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: Our priority is, right now is criminal threats, public safety threats, and national security threats. But I also said if you are in the country illegally, you are not off the table.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Colony Ridge has been a problematic area for a while, according to Homan. Earlier this month, three illegal migrants were arrested near the community after deputies say they found around 350 pounds of meth.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: We took a lot of criminals off the streets, and we made that community much safer because of the actions of the law enforcement today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (on camera): And border czar Tom Homan there calling this operation a massive success, though he says this is just phase one, so more of these mass arrests expected soon, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brooke Taylor live in Dallas. Brooke, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick says the Trump administration is revamping its U.S. investor visa. President Trump says the U.S. is increasing the funds required to secure what is being called a gold card.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: We're going to be putting a price on that card of about $5 billion. And that's going to give you green card privileges, plus it's going to be a wrap to citizenship, and wealthy people will be coming into our country by buying this card. They will be wealthy, and they will be successful, and they will be spending a lot of money and paying a lot of taxes and employing a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll talk about that and more. We'll be coming to you live from the Commerce Department tomorrow night. We will have an exclusive interview with Secretary Howard Lutnick and show you Commerce H.Q.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Seattle is blocking President Trump's effort to halt the nation's refugee admissions system. The ruling came in a lawsuit brought by major refugee aid groups. They argued the president's executive order suspending the federal refugee resettlement program runs afoul of the system Congress created for moving refugees into the U.S. Lawyers for the administration argue the order was well within the president's authority to deny entry to foreigners whose admission would be detrimental to the country.\par \par \uc2 There is some resistance to the immigrant raids and deportations, illegal immigrant raids. Activists in southern California are posting information about agents and also interfering with their operations. Chief correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2  has that part of the story tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Across the country, law enforcement officials have watched as activists have staged large anti-ICE protests. They have expressed frustration at leaks about upcoming raids, giving time for activists to show up, as they did here in Aurora, Colorado, and warn potential targets.\par \par \uc2 But the latest form of protest is prompting outright anger among officials. Fliers apparently posted in the Los Angeles area claiming to show the identities and phone numbers of some of the agents involved in raids, and reading in 