c policy address at the Australian embassy in Washington. He says interest rate volatility, sticky inflation, and reliance on public sector for job growth have hobbled the U.S. economy despite positive top line gross domestic product growth and low unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today over tariff uncertainties. The Dow gained 160, the S&P 500 was off 28, the Nasdaq fell 261.\par \par \uc2 A nonpartisan government watchdog group is identifying federal programs it says are most vulnerable to waste, fraud, and abuse. This is aside from DOGE. This has been around for a long time. Correspondent Mark Meredith takes a look from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I will withdraw --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The member is --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- calling him grifter in chief.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As lawmakers fight each other amid a new era of cost cutting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Disband for a second --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- just a few weeks ago --\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Today the nonpartisan \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  outlined 38 ways it thinks the feds can save money and still serve taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 GENE DODARO, U.S. COMPTROLLER GENERAL: Last six years there has been over $150 billion every year in improper payment. That's not even the complete number.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The GAO says Congress should eliminate billions of dollars in fraud tied to entitlement like Social Security and Medicare, also adopt new technology instead of spending more money repairing decades old computer systems. It also suggests the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  should change its business model, and ensure Americans pay the \uc2 IRS\uc2  what they owe.\par \par \uc2 For the first time the GAO says the feds should also overhaul emergency management. Last year there were 27 disasters, costing at least $1 billion each in damages.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: Most Americans do care about making government more efficient.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But Democrats claim the Trump administration's approach, specifically \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's DOGE effort, is both disrespectful and illegal.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAVE MIN, (D-CA): The Constitution is clear. Congress and only Congress has the power to make laws and appropriate money.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Republicans say cuts are long overdue.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: When you talk to taxpayers, they're kind of like, yes, people should go back to work and, yes, we are spending money on stupid things that should stop.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: And cutting spending is catching on with similar task forces set up beyond the beltway.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL): I'm pleased to announce that we are launching a comprehensive initiative to continue to streamline our government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But tonight DOGE here in Washington is facing a new setback. More than 20 DOGE employees who are on the technical side of things have resigned from their post today, saying they no longer support the \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  effort and that they believe it could end up hurting the American people. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith live on Capitol Hill. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on today's executive orders from the president, and the fight over DOGE.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 13 in Seattle with the clean-up after a powerful storm hit the region Monday night. Significant damage, thousands were without power this morning. Several roads were blocked because of downed trees.\par \par \uc2 FOX 6 in Milwaukee, with criticism for Wisconsin's Democratic governor over a proposal to replace terms such as "mother" and "biological father" with "inseminated person" and "natural parent." It is part of the budget bill from Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 . He says he is, quote, "pretty P.O.-ed" by the fierce backlash.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Memphis. One of the big stories there tonight -- nice sunset -- from FOX 13. A Missouri woman pleads guilty to a federal charge accusing her of concocting a brazen plot to defraud Elvis Presley's family. Lisa Jeanine Findley tried to auction off the Graceland mansion and the property in Memphis last year.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: It's a very big deal. Look, it could be $1 trillion deal, it could be whatever. But it's rare earths and other things.\par \par \uc2 We need to have a deal with Russia. Otherwise, it's going to continue. But now when Americans put up their money, the taxpayer money and the president approves it, we are getting our money back in some form. But it could go on for a while or it could be settled quickly. I think it's going to be settled quickly. I spoke with President Putin. I think he wants to settle it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we are getting closer. All parties are talking, and this Ukraine rare earth minerals deal, whatever a general framework looks like, seems like it's coming together, may come together in a couple days. I laid that out for you earlier about what that deal may look like. Some back and forth over it after the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  action the other day.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, start there, Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast and former chief of staff to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Democratic campaign consultant \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2 , and Annie Linskey, she is White House reporter for "The Wall Street Journal".\par \par \uc2 Josh, the president is saying this a good deal for the U.S., brings back money. Ukraine, if they agree to this deal, is probably doing it for the security element of U.S. companies being on the ground.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: Yes, no question. I don't think we'd even having this conversation if not for President Trump winning the election in November, obviously. And I'm always astounded at how many people mis-assess Trump's foreign policy. Everyone, he's an isolationist, or he's pro-Putin, or whatever.\par \par \uc2 No. He asks basically one question, what's in it for us? And I think what you are seeing with this deal in particular is you are getting some guarantees, some real financial, potentially gigantic financial benefits as it relates to the continued security of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That \uc2 U.N.\uc2  action and the U.S. not voting to say that Russia was the aggressor in Ukraine did ruffle some feathers in Kyiv and Europe, and may have kind of slowed down this deal getting locked in, Annie. But the president answered that the other day, or he was asked that and said it's self-explanatory why I'm not doing that.\par \par \uc2 ANNIE LINSKEY, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes. His response was he is looking forward, not backward. And that's the kind of very different approach that Josh is referring to that this president is taking when it comes to the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, we're used to this conflict being framed as good versus evil, autocracy versus democracy. That was the approach that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took, and he was very proud of that approach. He rallied European support behind Ukraine. But three years into the conflict, the United States is in a very different position. And it does feel like, in D.C. at least, it feels like there might be movement towards stopping the fighting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kevin, the security guarantee, I talked about that with Macron. They are willing to do troops. I think the U.K. Prime Minister is willing to do that. I'll talk to him on Thursday. What the U.S. security guarantee looks like is a little bit hazier.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC CAMPAIGN CONSULTANT: Yes, we're still trying to figure out the contours of that, obviously. We'll know more on Friday. But what has been released so far is language that says free, sovereign, and secure Ukraine as part of this arrangement. So what that entails, boots on the ground, more military assistance, what have you, we don't know yet. But that is a good step forward in cementing more that relationship with Ukraine. I give the president, speaking as a Democrat, credit for negotiating that. He obviously sent the new treasury secretary over there to do that. Any time we are tying our hands closer to Ukraine, the Ukrainian people in this conflict, I think is a win for both countries.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's turn to DOGE and the heartburn over now the second email. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Elon has implemented this at his private companies. It has proven successful.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, O'LEARY VENTURES: If they are not answering after two times requested, are they dead? There is nothing wrong with this request. In the private sector, I do this every day.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: Americans don't like the harmful chaos that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and DOGE have unleashed. They don't want to see a rich billionaire treat federal workers with immense disrespect.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It really is remarkable that you have the Democratic Party is saying this is the hill that we are going to go die on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So here is the original email, "What did you do last week?"\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, now we are on the second iteration. The president says it's somewhat voluntary, but you could get fired depending on if your boss is into this or not. Basically, what the administration is saying is every department agency is dealing with it separately, but it's an effort to be a bed check. Are you really working? What did you do?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Consistently one of the most effective things that President Trump and his entire team have done for years now is create an argument that, regardless of its practicality, highlights an absurdity of which the vast majority of the American people completely identify with. What is it that you say you do here, is not exactly --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think it was a scene in "The Office," where the guy cannot come up with what he did. And he just walks away.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Not a particularly complicated question, one that many Americans grapple with. But yet this has highlighted this discussion that they are trying to talk about, which is there's a big, bloated federal bureaucracy here. It would be nice if we did something about it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And so Kevin, fighting that on the other side, Democrats are giving these big speeches, and it's offensive. But yet, five things you did doesn't seem that tough.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Yes, I think Democrats need to strike this middle ground. We can't be in defense of a bloated federal budget. We've got to be supportive of where we can cut, where it makes sense with fraud and abuse. And you've seen that line kind of drawn by Hakeem Jefferies appointing members to the DOGE subcommittee in the House, a willingness for Democrats to say, OK, let's come to the table. But Democrats have to be invited to that table. We have recommendations that have fallen on deaf ears, and you are seeing this unilateral action from this DOGE committee, whether the special status of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the temporary status of the administrator of this DOGE committee, more questions than answers of the operation of this. Democrats are willing to play. But we just want a seat at the table and a place to make our voices heard.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And maybe it's not as chaotic as --\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- is what you are saying.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , well respected on both sides, had this to say about this whole separation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 , \uc2 JPMORGAN CHASE\uc2  CEO: The government is inefficient, not very competent, and it needs a lot of work. And so you and I, it's not just waste and fraud. It's outcomes. Why are we spending money on these things? Are we getting what we deserve? What should we change? So I think doing that needs to be done. It's not just about the deficit. It's about building the right policies, procedures, and the government we deserve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And, you know, as far as the approval ratings, if you look at the new Quinnipiac poll out, you see President Trump hovering right around 50, a little bit less than he was. But Musk really taking the heat, and maybe that's part of the deal. He's the shield here.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Yes. And I think that is sort of part of the way that this arrangement is working. Musk is doing some of the really politically difficult work. And you're just seeing him take all sorts of incoming, and he very much is a shield to Trump.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I thought was really interesting that happened today was when Karoline Leavitt was asked about who was going to decide which of these workers is fired? And there is more of a movement towards the Senate approved cabinet secretaries being the ones to make that final evaluation. And that's a little bit of a debate that's been going on in the last few days, and you see that got a little bit of clarity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", "Crunch time for Mike Johnson and the GOP's big, beautiful bill." Set to put a budget resolution up to the floor tonight. It's facing potential revolt. And you've got a lot of back and forth. However, Johnson just in the past few minutes, Josh, has said that he has the votes to pass the one big, beautiful bill. We'll see. Scalise has said that, too, in the last few minutes. And so we could see House Republicans come around this effort.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: That would be an astonishing accomplishment. I think most of us who have watched, and you watch the margins down to a two-vote miss. The chances of you succeeding the first time are very rare. And I figured this would take a couple machinations before we got there. If they did it the first time, that is a huge accomplishment. It is the ultimate shirts and skins part of this episode, though. If you can't support the Republican budget, where are you going to go from there? I like where we are headed, and if, in fact, they pass that, that's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats obviously making a stand, saying that this includes Medicaid cuts. The speaker pushing back against that, Medicaid is not even in the budget blueprint. But it's clear this is not a bipartisan effort.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there is a little dance that the Republicans are doing. They have to cut $880 billion, rather, from under the jurisdiction of the Energy and Commerce Committee. That's Medicaid cuts. You know, 41 million children in this country rely on Medicaid directly for their health insurance. It's going to decimate rural healthcare. You are seeing pushback in all of these district town halls that we're seeing from Georgia to Washington state to Illinois. Some uncertainty with Republicans. You played the clip earlier in the show, Bret, with the \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2 , you know, real fierce about implications for Medicare and Medicaid cuts in her district.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a gift to Democrats. You saw a unified Democrats on the steps of the Capitol led by Hakeem Jefferies, and we'll let this process play out. Take a step back. That's one of the nice things not having to govern.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go, yes, minority.\par \par \uc2 Last thing, "The New York Post" has it, 1,000 plus questions answered by President Trump in the first month. That is -- President Biden, his first month had 141 questions answered. I mean, 1,000 plus. No wonder it feels like seven years.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: I know. I mean, just hearing that statistic, I'm living it, Bret, so I believe it. There's no part of me that has any doubt in a data, because, I mean, if you just think about it. Today, the president -- there is a press briefing, and then the president brought the pool, including "The Wall Street Journal" into the Oval Office for 45 minutes. So, he is taking a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's really everywhere. Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Someday you'll be here as the president, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Somebody in this group has a chance. Have a good time. Have a great tour. And you're going to give them a special tour, OK? You're going to get a special tour. Have fun, everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump surprised the group touring the White House today, first such tour since he took office in January. He credited the first lady, Melania Trump, whom he says has been working hard to make sure the tour is perfect now. As he walked away, the crowd chanted "USA", so that's pretty cool if you get a tour and there's the president.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will be live at the Commerce Department. We'll talk exclusively with Secretary Howard Lutnick. We'll show you around Commerce H.Q.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, we will interview British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  as he meets with President Trump at the White House. We'll be over at the Blair House again. Another world leader.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and X @BretBaier, behind the scenes content. We'll give you some of that from the Blair House.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250226el2p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Clarifies \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  E-Mail To Federal Employees; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  Hold Wide-Ranged Talks; Assessing Responsibilities As War Enters Fourth Year; Analysis Of Exclusive Interview With Macron; U.S. Votes Against U.N. Resolution Naming Russia As Aggressor In War With Ukraine; Doctors In Rome Say Pope Francis Showing Improvement After Respiratory Crisis; Some Federal Government Departments Tells Employees Not to Respond to DOGE Email Asking Them To List Five Things They Did at Their Job in Previous Week.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Brit Hume, Harold Ford Jr., Marc  Thiessen, Katie Pavlich, Jeff Paul \par \uc2 7,418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Is there a goat? All right, that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Dana, thank you. Welcome to the Blair House, right across from the White House, home to foreign dignitaries when they're in town. One is today, French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  here with us. We'll talk with him in just a minute about the day's events, his meetings with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For a wrap up of that, first, we turn to senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret, President Trump said today his focus is on achieving a cease fire ASAP, adding that Russia would benefit from a deal to end the war. But how that happens is unclear.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has insisted on security guarantees, holding up signing a rare earth minerals deal with the U.S. in exchange for that, officials said today the deal is at the one yard line. Meantime, Europe is trying to figure out what all this means for them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've spoken to President Putin, and my people are dealing with him constantly, and his people in particular, and they want to do something. That's what I do, I do deals, my whole life is deals.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Trump expressing hope the Russia-Ukraine war is nearing a close as plans for Europe to play a primary role in peace keeping come together, but Europe has not been party to the talks. Macron giving Trump this advice.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT: Our predecessors negotiated peace with President Putin, but because of the lack of guarantees, and especially security guarantees, President Putin violated this piece. Having deterrence capacities is the only way to be sure it will be respected. And I insisted on that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Trump declining to call Putin a dictator today, or explain why the U.S. opposed two \uc2 U.N.\uc2  resolutions calling out Russia as the aggressor, implying it would be counterproductive.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I would rather not explain it now, but it's sort of self-evident, I think.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Closer to home, the White House also confirming federal agencies need not fire workers who failed to respond to the OPM e- mail asking to list five things they accomplished last week. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said failure to reply would be taken as resignation, and the president seemed to confirm.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What he's doing is saying, are you actually working? And then if you don't answer like you're sort of semi fired, or you're fired because a lot of people are not answering because they don't even exist.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Officials tell Fox, the Office of Personnel Management is not taking a one size fits all approach, instead working with individual agencies to get the information they need. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy indicated the five things e-mail is a good approach.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: This is an easy task. It happens in the private sector all the time, and so I wanted our employees to actually engage and talk about what they did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Meantime, the three year anniversary of the war is nearing a close without a statement from the \uc2 G7\uc2  allies, the U.S. apparently in conflict with Europeans over language that would call out Russia as the aggressor, just as we saw in those \uc2 U.N.\uc2  resolutions today, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, we welcome French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 . Mr. President, thank you for the time.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Thank you. I'm very happy to be here with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, you had a lot of time with President Trump today. You answered a lot of questions, both in the Oval Office and at that press briefing. How would you characterize this meeting today, and what was your basic message to President Trump?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: For me, it was a very decisive meeting, and I decided to offer President Trump to come here. It was not scheduled for weeks. We decided last week, given the sensitivity of this moment and because of the ongoing negotiation between the U.S. and Russia, because of all the discussions we had amongst Europeans, I wanted to come here first to convey a message of friendship and the importance of the history and the relation between France and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And second to say, OK, we want peace. We agree. Today is clearly the anniversary of the beginning of the aggression war of Russia and Ukraine. They invited -- they invaded Ukraine three years ago, we want peace.\par \par \uc2 And I think the initiative of President Trump is a very positive one, but my message was to say, be careful, because we need something substantial for Ukraine, but for the security of Europe and France.\par \par \uc2 And second, let's work together on the future to preserve security guarantees, meaning to be sure that this peace will be respected by Russia.\par \par \uc2 And more than that, I wanted to discuss about the bilateral relation and our future, France and the U.S., Europe and the U.S., and how to have peace, how to have more prosperity, how to have more trade and investment.\par \par \uc2 So, it was a very strategic and decisive meeting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk about the security guarantee and what you see there. But first, numerous times today, President Trump expressed confidence that this war could, really, in reality, end in a few weeks. Do you share that confidence?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I hope so. And I think everybody has in mind all the losses and casualties and what the Ukrainian people live during the past three years.\par \par \uc2 And I mean, they've been very brave, and they just protected their territory and their sovereignty. And I think the arrival of President Trump is a game changer, and I think he has the deterrence capacity of the U.S. to re-engage with Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's feasible. My concern is that we have to go fast, but we need something first, a truce, which could be assessed and checked and full-fledged negotiation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, a cease fire?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes, but be careful, because in 2014 we had a cease fire with Russia. And I can tell you by experience, because I was in charge with Germany to follow up the cease fire in its respect, it was violated every time by Russia, and we didn't react, all of us.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you as well by experience, because there was one meeting between President Putin and President Zelenskyy in history, one, it was in Paris in December 2019. And because there was a lack of credibility of our collective deterrence, in February 2022, Putin decided to invade Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, this is why I think it's feasible to have a truce at least and start negotiating for sustainable peace. But we need something substantial For Ukraine, for the Europeans and security for the U.S. and its credibility.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, can you clarify your stance on sending French peacekeeping troops to Ukraine. What are you willing to commit to get to that cease fire, or whatever you want to call it, the stopping of the war?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: For me, the sequence should be the following: negotiation between U.S. and Russia, and U.S. and Ukraine. And I think President Trump announced today's willingness to have short term meeting with President Zelenskyy is super important.\par \par \uc2 First, you have a truce. I think it's -- it could be done in the weeks to come. Truce on air, sea, infrastructures. If it is not respected, it would be best evidence of the fact that Russia is not serious.\par \par \uc2 During this period of time, we negotiate on security guarantees, land and territories, and it will be the responsibility of the Ukrainian president, reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. will have the deal on mineral, critical minerals and rare earths and so on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fine with that?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes, and I think it's very important, because it's one of the best way to have the U.S. commitment in the Ukrainian sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 So, we will negotiate, and at the end of this period, you have a peace treaty and a peace agreement.\par \par \uc2 The day after how to make sure that Russia will not violate this treaty. There is no consensus for \uc2 NATO\uc2  and having Ukraine joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 . But if we - - if we leave Ukraine alone, we did it in the past.\par \par \uc2 So, we need something more substantial. So, we have to negotiate with the U.S., with the allies, with Ukraine, which type of security guarantee makes sense.\par \par \uc2 It could be, first, increase the capacities of Ukraine to maintain a strong army. Today they have one million soldiers, at least we have 800,000 soldiers, something like that, but to be credible vis-a-vis Russia.\par \par \uc2 Second, if this is requested, we work very hard together with the U.K. Prime Minister to have a French-U.K. proposal to say we are ready to send troops, not to go to the front line, not to go in confrontation, but to be in some locations being defined by the treaty as a presence to maintain this peace and our collective credibility with the U.S. back up and the U.S. back stop.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, did you talk about that today?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: President Trump spoke as well, spoke very clearly about that, and it means we are ready to take our fair share of the burden. It's in Europe, we are engaged.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with 20 -- I mean 30 leaders, Europe -- European leaders and allies, a lot of them are ready to be part of such a deal, such a security guarantees in any way.\par \par \uc2 But all of them say, OK, but we -- if we were attacked, let's imagine one second that Russia could violate this treaty. What will happen? They need this message from the U.S. solidarity. And I think this is very important. And this is what we worked in details today, and this is what your president very clearly announced this morning.\par \par \uc2 For me, this is what we have to negotiate and finalize, if we manage to do so with, clearly, the agreement of Ukraine for what its sovereignty, and with the agreement of the Europeans, as this is European security, and the agreement of the U.S. for this solidarity and this backstop. I think we have a deal.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Were you worried that you weren't at the table as these negotiations were going on between the U.S. and Russia?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I'm relaxed. I mean, it's normal when the U.S. discussed with Russia. I mean, I'm not at the table.\par \par \uc2 Look, when I discuss with you, Russia is not at the table. When I discuss with China, you are not at the table. Come on.\par \par \uc2 I'm just -- we were concerned to have a discussion on Ukraine without Ukraine or European security, without the Europeans. But you have a lot of other topics between the U.S. and Russia. And I mean, it's normal and it's positive to re-engage.\par \par \uc2 My point is just, let's work very, very closely together. Why? I mean, because we've always been together in this very -- in hard time. Lafayette, here we are, and as a very beginning of your nation during the First World War, the Second World War. And this morning, I had a meeting with an incredible U.S. veteran. You were here and we were here.\par \par \uc2 And today, what's happening in Ukraine, I want you and U.S. people to be clearly aware of that ,it's not just about Ukraine, it's about European security. So, this is why it's important we work together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One year ago, I traveled to the front lines of Ukraine with this crew, actually, to interview President Zelenskyy, and came away that he was determined. Have you given advice to Zelenskyy on managing his relationship with President Trump?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: No, I never give advice. I try to help and to accompany, and I think it's -- I mean, President Zelenskyy, honestly, is a very brave leader and I think none of us could imagine having three years of war against Russia.\par \par \uc2 And clearly he has a strong leadership and a very capable leadership, and three years ago, did you think that Ukraine would resist like they resisted? Nobody thought that. Let's be honest, they did an incredible job.\par \par \uc2 And not -- we should not forget that three years ago, Russia announced a special operation for three weeks. Three years later, they took some kilometers, but not even the whole Donbas.\par \par \uc2 Three years later, they pushed Sweden and Finland to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and the whole Baltic Sea is full of \uc2 NATO\uc2  members. Three years later, they almost damaged our economy. I mean, they did damage our economy and lose a lot of young people.\par \par \uc2 So, come on, we should not ever underestimate the Ukrainian leadership. But I think it's important for all of us to help President Zelenskyy to have a solid and sustainable peace, because since the beginning of the war, probably one million people were killed or wounded. Since the beginning of war, thousands of soldiers were captured by Russians, and thousands of children were taken, we have to fix that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump has said he wants \uc2 NATO\uc2  now. \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies to spend five percent of their GDP on defense. Your country, if you look at the graph, sits at about two percent. Will you boost defense spending in this environment?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: This is what I started. So, first, since I -- since I was elected in 2017, I passed two laws of planification, and at the end of these two laws, we would have double our budget in France. As you mentioned, we are two percent, we have to do much more. Your president is right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it's a tough economic environment in France.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: It is totally true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Political environment. Your coalition is frayed.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes, but what I did, and this is my job, it's to convince the majority of French people and the majority of parties to step up our efforts, and it's not because you ask us to do so and have more burden sharing. This is our interest and why, and this is important, and this is something you have to realize as well, because Russia is still a threat. Russia spends 10 percent -- 10 percent of its GDP in defense, and it's close to us.\par \par \uc2 And Russia is aggressive vis-a-vis Europeans. We have every week, cyberattacks. We had terrorist attacks. They have a sort of hybrid war vis a vis European. So, we have to step up our expenditures.\par \par \uc2 So, in France, we will deliver increases, additional ones. I'm building consensus, and I will do my job, and the political forces are all very aware of the situation.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, we have to do much more at the European scale. I convened a meeting beginning of this week, and we will have a European Council in the days to come, sixth of March, and we will have some special announcement by the commission to increase as well, short term financing.\par \par \uc2 And what we have to do? We did innovative financing to have much more common investment as Europeans. Why? Because we have to take our part of the burden, and we have to invest much more in our own security.\par \par \uc2 First, to decrease your own burden, but second, because this is our interest.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Were you surprised President Trump won in November of 24th?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: No, because we saw the dynamics the months before, and especially after the terrible attacks he had. And I know the man. I know the man and his determination.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, you invited him to Bastille Day.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In that parade, clearly, you got along then very closely. In fact, the all the press was writing about a bromance between the two of you. What has happened since then? Have you changed, does his change? Has he changed? What do -- how do you describe the relationship?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Right. Super complicated to qualify ourselves. But no, I think we have friendship and trust. I think sometimes we agree, sometimes we disagree, but we have very honest discussion.\par \par \uc2 And he knows that I'm always very respectful, but I'm always very direct. And I try to argue, and I try -- and I try to convince him. And I --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was that today's dynamic?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trying rank exchange.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: No, but I think we have a very strong convergence today on Ukraine for peace. I hope, and I believe, I convinced him on the fact that we have to be careful not to have a cease fire without any guarantee, but truth negotiation and full-fledged peace.\par \par \uc2 I hope I did convince him on the very -- the importance of the U.S. role in the security guarantee and your backstop.\par \par \uc2 And I presented the French U.K. plan and what the European can do. And I hope I did convince him on trade and tariffs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which was what? He wants to do reciprocal.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I told -- no, I told him -- I told him, it's very important for you not to launch new tariffs. That is not a tariff, and I argue it, which is true.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That thing is reciprocal. If you tariff us, we're going to tariff you.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I agree, but we don't tariff you. So, we don't move. So, if my -- that my points, and it's very important for the -- first, we are super intertwined. Our economy are totally intertwined.\par \par \uc2 $1,500 billion of exchange products and goods and digital services. Can you imagine? There is no other economies like Europe and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Second, we didn't tariff the U.S. We want to cut red tape. For earth as Europeans, and it will simplify the life of your companies, fine. But I think the solution is this very moment is not to increase tariffs. Why? Because the emergency is to increase our defense and security expenditures. How do you want us to increase security and defense expenditures if we are at trade war?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think you made a breakthrough today?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: And second, what is your priority? I understood your priority is to put tariff on China, because President Trump said they are unfair with us. Come on, you cannot have a trade war with China and Europe at the same time.\par \par \uc2 I hope I convinced him, but it's very important for you to understand that all in all, the transatlantic relation is that very important moment, and the top priority is to work together very closely, to have a good, solid, and sustainable peace in full transparency.\par \par \uc2 Second, it's very important for the Europeans to spend more on defense and security, because it will be good for us and our security good for you to decrease your involvement.\par \par \uc2 And third, we don't need a trade war. We need more prosperity together. I announced $120 billion investment on A.I. in France. A lot of U.S. companies and U.S. investors are part of that.\par \par \uc2 France is the fifth largest investor in the U.S. We have to work together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. President, we really appreciate the time. We look forward to talking to you again, because there's a lot of hot spots around the world. Iran, China, Ukraine, Russia.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I agree. I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I want to thank you for this, this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I want to thank the White House for the hospitality and Blair House and the whole team of Blair House.\par \par \uc2 And I want to invite you in Paris. You should come to see Notre Dame de Paris. Your president came in December.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: And follow up this discussion. But thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll do it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACRON: And for the leadership of your president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that, we'll hit a break. And then on the other side, we have headlines, other stories today. Plus, analysis of this interview and all that happened today. Keep it here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Numerous times today, President Trump expressed confidence that this war could really, in reality, end in a few weeks. Do you share that confidence?\par \par \uc2 MACRON: I hope so. And I think everybody has in mind all the losses and casualties, and what the Ukrainian people live during the past three years. And I mean, they have been very brave. And they just protected their territory and their sovereignty. And I think the arrival of President Trump is a game changer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: French president, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , over at the Blair House. We've come over here to the North Lawn of the White House. His motorcade just leaving now, heading back to Paris.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel. Fox News Chief Political Analyst, Britt Hume; Harold Ford Jr, former Tennessee congressman, co-host of "THE FIVE". Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, and \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist and \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  senior fellow, Marc Thiessen.\par \par \uc2 Brit, your thoughts what you heard from the interview?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I thought it was pretty clear, Bret. And it was clear in your interview with him, even than it was in the multiple news conferences that the two leaders had today, that Macron is here because he is afraid that the president, in his rush to get a deal to stop the fighting in Ukraine, will do so on terms that would open the door to Russia simply re invading and just, you know, a matter of a short period of time, or even a few years, and that it -- and what's needed are security guarantees strong enough to deter that, and that he was -- he is -- he's concerned that without them -- that it will be a bad deal, it will stain everybody involved, and certainly will be a threat to the rest of Europe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Harold, I was struck by how much he really talked about the positivity out of this meeting, but also the warnings to Brit's point about Putin and the long-term effects in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: First of all, you did a great job in the interview, eliciting so many -- so many of the things that Brit just mentioned. I think Brit nailed it.\par \par \uc2 It's mineral rights and economic opportunities for the United States in the mind of President Trump and its long-term security guarantees, not just for Ukraine, as Brit said, but for Europe.\par \par \uc2 I listened closely, as he said at the end there, Bret, that he was concerned, wanted us to understand that three years ago, we all thought that this would be a two-week invasion and that the Russians would walk away victorious.\par \par \uc2 The fighting, the bravery, the fortitude that the Ukrainians have shown, men, women, and children that have died. I hear President Trump talk a lot about the casualties. These have all been Ukrainian casualties, and these people don't want to give up. But the president won this election, and people want to see some closing to this effort. I don't think any American who voted for President Trump was looking for capitulation to Russia, but they are certainly looking for a way to bring about an ending.\par \par \uc2 And I would agree with Brit wholeheartedly. I think President Macron's visit here to United States today was to say, think about Europe, Mr. President, as you go forward with some sort of end agreement to this war.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was interesting, Marc. He said that he was for the rare earth minerals deal that the U.S. is going to strike with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm told that, that deal is pretty much done, and waiting for Zelenskyy to come here and officially sign it.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, in part, because it gets buy in, U.S. buy in into Ukraine on the ground.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. Yes, exactly. It is that the minerals deal is actually more important than the peace deal. When that mineral deals is signed, you -- Russia has lost the war. Because Putin's goal was to conquer Ukraine, and three years later, the end result is the U.S. is going into business with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We are investing, literally, not figuratively, billions and billions of dollars in a deal with the Ukrainians. If Russia takes over Ukraine, Putin is not going to pay us hundreds of billions of dollars for the weapons that we gave Ukraine to fight his troops.\par \par \uc2 The only way that we get the hundreds of billions of dollars that Trump is negotiating right now is if Ukraine remains a free, sovereign, independent nation with whom we're doing business, and when this deal is struck, I mean, I know for Trump has said, there -- he's going to have the earth movers and the tractors going in, people saying there is not -- they're not U.S. troops involved. He is sending something better, billions of dollars U.S. direct investment. U.S. bulldozers, U.S. earth movers to dig up those minerals, and you need a free -- you need free trade, you need ports, you need to export them, and you need peace in order to do that, it has be lasting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Katie, I talked to him about his situation in France. He wants to raise defense spending. But he has got an economic problem. He has got a political problem. "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 " writes it this way, "His ratings dropped to record low. His popularity dropped to 21 percent in January, down three points from the previous month, taking the score below the levels reached during the Yellow Vest protests that began in 2018." He acknowledged that but said that he is committed to increasing -- they have put in about $5 billion into Ukraine, compared to about $200 billion for the U.S.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, America and Russia are at the table, and they are moving very quickly. And when you ask the question about whether the French president is willing to send French peacekeepers, French troops to Ukraine to get these security guarantees that he keeps talking about, he didn't thoroughly answer that question. And one of the moving parts here is what is Europe's responsibility? Back in 2014, when Russia invaded Ukraine, President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , running for president at the time for the first time, was warning about Europe not paying their two percent to hold up their end of the deal with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , an agreement that they all bought into. And then he spent much of his first term trying to make sure that they were paying enough to deter Russia with another invasion.\par \par \uc2 Lo and behold, 2021 rolls around, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who didn't have many expectations for increased defense spending, Russia rolls into Ukraine, and here we are today with the three year anniversary of horrific bloodshed. And the frontline hasn't moved much over the course of the past two years.\par \par \uc2 And so the question is, how is Europe going to step up to the plate and do what they should to make sure that Russia doesn't move past Ukraine, doesn't invade other countries? And as you say, there are domestic policy issues that for a very long time, Europe has placed social programs, socialist style economies ahead of defense spending and taken for granted the United States would back them up even if they weren't holding up their end of the deal. And so that's where they are on that issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, listen, the leaders are coming here, Brit. U.K. prime minister arrives, is going to be here on Thursday. We are hoping to talk to him as well. And you look at this, today at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2 , the United Nations, the U.S. voting against the resolution that named Russia an aggressor, trying to tone down the resolution that marked the third anniversary of the start of this war. Critics said this has never happened. The U.S. is never on that side. The president saying that this is about the negotiation, essentially, and questions about that today. Your take, finally, on that and were this beginning gambit goes?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I'm not excited that the U.S. did that today. On the other hand, if you are negotiating with Russia in the middle of a delicate period, perhaps understandable that you would not vote for a resolution condemning Russia. But as for what we've been talking with respect to Europe, Katie is right that we don't have any hard guarantees from these European nations about the commitment of troops and the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 But Europe is making more of an effort, I think it's clear, and said it would be, these nations said they would be making more of an effort in their own defense. And after all, if they're looking at the United States, which is not going to continue to bare this burden, these European nations are going to have to. And if they are worried about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and the possibility of another invasion, I think they will end up bolstering defenses on their own, much as those two Scandinavian nations joined \uc2 NATO\uc2  after the invasion of Ukraine to bolster their defense posture as well. so I think we can look ahead with some hope there will be a much stronger defense posture in Europe than there has been.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, stand by. We'll come back for final thoughts at the back end of the show.\par \par \uc2 There is other news today here in the U.S. and around the world. Up next, a live update on the health of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  from Rome.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here are some other stories making headlines tonight. A federal judge will not issue a temporary restraining order against the White House over its limitation access for the Associated Press. President Trump is citing the A.P.'s refusal to refer to what is now being called the Gulf of America. The judge, however, is urging the administration to reconsider its ban.\par \par \uc2 Embattled New York City Mayor Eric Adams says midtown's Roosevelt Hotel migrant intake center and emergency shelter will soon shut down operations. Officials say it's no longer necessary and migrants entering the city can register elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The new chairman and CEO at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  says the company plans to lay off 1,100 corporate employees around the world. \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  sending a letter to employees today saying workers will be informed about layoffs by midday tomorrow. He says \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is also eliminating several hundred open and unfilled positions.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow was up 33. The S&P 500 lost 30. The Nasdaq plunged 237.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, doctors in Rome say \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is showing slight improvement this evening. He remains in critical condition after experiencing a respiratory crisis Saturday. National correspondent Jeff Paul has the latest tonight live from Rome. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, yes, Bret, compared to some of the other updates we have been receiving from the Vatican in previous days, tonight is much more upbeat. It sounds like \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is back doing some work, even making a phone call to a church in Gaza, something he has been doing since the start of the war there. But even with this slight improvement in his condition, a lot of folks getting together tonight at Saint Peter's Square for a rosary prayer. The gathering is being led by Cardinal \uc2 Pietro Parolin\uc2 , second to the Pope and the Vatican's secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  spent the last 11 days in the hospital battling double pneumonia and a serious respiratory infection. And while he is breathing on his own, he is still receiving some oxygen, although less today than in previous ones.\par \par \uc2 The Vatican says doctors also detected a slight kidney insufficiency but added it's no longer concerning, and that is coming as a bit of relief for Catholics who have been showing up at both Saint Peter's Square and Gemelli Hospital pulling for the Pope's recovery.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIA VOZLV, ROME RESIDENT: We are praying. We Ukrainians are also praying a lot for the Pope because the Pope has helped Ukraine a lot. We pray that the Pope will return, recover, and be well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now, the Pope is said to be in good spirits. He is also eating. He is alert, and the Vatican says he is not in pain, something they have said in previous days with their updates. But we should mention that his recovery will be a bit touch and go just given his age and his past respiratory issues. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul live in Rome. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll turn here to the White House, the panel returns. And we'll analyze some more of the day's news.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York as three people are dead, two injured, one missing after a boat capsizes near Staten Island. It happened around noon Sunday about 10 miles southeast of the island's eastern shore. First responders recovered five people from the boat, four of whom were unresponsive.\par \par \uc2 KHON in Honolulu. The owners of the residence that exploded in a frenzy of fireworks on New Year's Day area taken into custody. A total of 12 people have been arrested over that incident. The explosion has killed six people so far, including a toddler. Several victims remain hospitalized tonight.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Orlando from FOX 35, our affiliate down there. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  prepares to launch Falcon 9 rocket from Cape Canaveral, Florida. It will carry 21 Starlink satellites, including 13 with direct-to-cell capabilities into low earth orbit. Liftoff is scheduled for 11:26 p.m. eastern tonight.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Some of the agency heads instructed their employees not to respond because they are waiting on further guidance, but \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's tweet said a failure to respond would be taken as a resignation. So there has been a disconnect in communication. Are you concerned at all about that?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: No, no. No. That was done in a friendly manner. I think everyone thought it was ingenious idea. We have find out who these people are. Who are they? And we said, if you don't respond, we assume you are not around.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump answering a question for our own Jacqui Heinrich about DOGE and the emails that went out to federal employees telling them to respond with what they did, or five things they did last week.\par \par \uc2 We're back with our panel, Brit, Katie, Harold, and Marc. Harold, what about that and the president's response and how it's playing across not only the federal government but across the country?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I think a couple things. Two main things. One, I think the country is in support of any effort to root out fraud and waste and to create more efficiencies in government.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, I think the country is trusting those in charge, particularly the president and Mr. Musk, to not root out the kinds of things that make their lives better, that makes our country safer. I was pleased to see a judge, a federal judge, say to the DOGE that we're not going to allow some access to some of these agencies in respect or out of respect for federal privacy laws on behalf of people.\par \par \uc2 But I think the president is walking a fine line. On one side, we support what he's doing. But on the other side, don't take away those essentials. Don't compromise our security. And whatever you do, don't compromise our privacy in the process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Brit, his point is, listen, there are people who are not working, there are people who may not even be people who are getting checks. That's what the president said today. He said of the agencies that said don't answer the email are the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the State Department, people that are doing confidential things, the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , that they don't want to get out there what they are doing the past week. But your thoughts about the whole thing?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think the disagreement whether or how to respond to this email that was sent by the DOGE team may be much to do about not much. What the key question really is here, what is it going to uncover? Is it really going to turn out to be the case that there are zombie federal workers, people who are either don't exist, as the president suggested, or people who are just simply not doing any work, and thus should obviously be disposed of? And if so, how many?\par \par \uc2 And it's been hinted that there are lots of them. And that the federal payroll, people don't come to the office. Some of them may not be doing any work and all of that. If that turns out to be true, that will be a big deal and a big win for the president and his team for uncovering it. If not, people will look at that and say, well, that was a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Katie, there is a DOGE website. They are putting more and more stuff on there that they're finding. The question is if it's all sinking in and adding up.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: It's certainly adding up. And when you look at the response to a simple question from the executive branch leadership of what did you do last week, not even what did you do yesterday in one day, for the week, you have the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , the union, coming out with a full length letter trying to argue that this is an inappropriate question to be asked. And DOGE is showing the big difference between the private sector and the federal government, and the outrage over having to answer an email is another good example of doing just that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marc, you know, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " has this piece out, "Saying No to Musk," that these cabinet officials and other high level Trump appointees have balked at the directive is how he had write it. You heard the president's explanation of that. Is this -- where is this public relations-wise? There is overwhelmingly a lot of support for going after waste, fraud, and abuse.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I think the big problem is that too many people are trying to look at an unconventional presidency through a conventional lens. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not doing things the way people normally do them in Washington. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is doing something very simple. He sent a request for proof of life. Everybody who is on that email receipt is getting a federal paycheck. Are you there? Are you alive? Can you just respond? He said he doesn't even care what they respond. He just wants them to respond and show that they are there.\par \par \uc2 There are literally people, Kelly Loeffler on her second day at the SBA showed at the SBA and did a video touring all the empty cubicles. There's nobody there. They don't even have family pictures up on the wall. It's not like they left and were coming home the next day. There is literally empty cubicle by the mile at the SBA. We have people who have jobs and have left Washington and are not doing their jobs. Let's find out and get rid of them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly Harold, it's tough politically on the Democratic side to push against that, isn't it, if they are listing that, if they are saying what they are finding? Isn't it tough?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I don't think there's any doubt. I do think we -- I think you nailed it in terms of are people not there, are they there. I think at the end of the day people want government work. And if people are not working they shouldn't be on the payroll. I think some I heard today say that one of the reasons they are not responding to DOGE is that they don't believe these people are the right people they should be responding to. But there is no question, waste, fraud, and abuse, and inefficiency in government, there are no advocates for that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks for rolling with the punches today. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a man who is very definition of an overachiever.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to the White House. Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS EDELL, MARATHON RUNNER: It really is actually more of a psychological game at a certain point where you are almost in a grappling match with your own head trying to prove to yourself how capable you are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen to this, a San Francisco man said he was searching for added meaning in his life when he decided to hit the ground running literally. Chris Edell managed to run seven marathons in seven days on seven continents. His journey started with a marathon in Antarctica, included stops in Australia, Spain, Brazil. He says he had the battle frigid temperatures, intense heat, humidity in some areas, jetlag. To avoid food poisoning he ate Chicken McNuggets and Gatorade. He also says he actually would do the whole thing again. No, thank you. Good job.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, a House hearing on emerging global threats and putting America's national security first.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You never know which world leader will show up here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250225el2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 DOGE Sniffs Out More Waste, Fraud And Abuse; Clay Travis Calls Out Liberal Men For Being Weak; 