em. Two of them, one was the cultural issue around transgender and little boys and little girls playing sports together and bathroom sharing.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, for some reason, it didn't even -- it didn't down upon them that that could be something that would resonate with voters.\par \par \uc2 And two, the border issue Democrats, I know we're going to deal with in the next -- the next segment. The Democrats should understand all the images that people are seeing across the country, on our network and local networks across the country, with people being deported who are criminals, this worst, first policy, that may be the best thing he does in the thing that will bring and help keep that coalition he won with together, more than anything else, at least in his short term, it will give him some leeway if he makes some mistakes in other -- in other areas.\par \par \uc2 But this is what happens the first three, four, five days, people are very excited, and I would agree with you, Dana, I don't say that to push back on your proposition, which is, how can you be against the things he's talking about? Who does not want more jobs in America, more high paying jobs in America, safer America?\par \par \uc2 The question is, can you do all of those things by executive order? Can you do all those things just by putting your hand on the table? Do you have to go over it or putting your fists on the table? Do you go over and try to work with people to try to get it done? I hope he gets it done, whichever way he has to do it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse, do you feel like the people of California are relieved Trump's coming tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, I just want to know is smelt salty? Is it like an anchovy?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, it's little, but it's not really salty, not at all. That's why you slap it in the egg and the flour and you put salt, pepper.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You got to make it taste like something else.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Is that the thing on California they're protecting?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So yes, greasy needs to turn on the faucet. That's common sense.\par \par \uc2 And Trump's just solving all the problems Biden created. Did you know that Mexico is building tents for Mexicans? Mexico is like, what do we do with all these Mexicans? It's going to be a homecoming, Dana. Maybe we could have a king and a queen.\par \par \uc2 Bad hombres are getting iced across the nation. You guys just touched on it about basic economics. You lower the price of energy. It lowers consumer prices. It lowers inflation. The Fed drops rates, mortgage rates follow, and then young couples can buy homes and have lots of babies and live the American dream.\par \par \uc2 Did you know the price of oil was $110 under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Now it's at 77. We're back to work. We're not collecting checks in our pajamas anymore. DEI is gone, and I know Harold knows this better than anybody. The S&P just hit fresh highs.\par \par \uc2 This A.I. investment. It's not our money, it's their money. We're not printing it to then pick winners and losers and hand it out to Intel. We're just having them do all the heavy lifting for us.\par \par \uc2 And he's doing it with a stroke of a pen. Remember Biden said, I need Congress. I can't do anything without Congress. No, he needed a little common sense, and he needed some caffeine, and it's fun to watch the Democrats and the media completely broken and just getting rolled over.\par \par \uc2 Thank God. Look at what this government is capable of. I'm just -- I'm invigorated. I am invigorated, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You got to look at the ratings. Like, nobody is watching those other channels because they're like you guys lied to us for four years, so we're not watching you anymore. They're going away.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: RuPaul's Drag Race is beating CNN.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, it's quality programming.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Are we beating RuPaul's Drag Race?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. But you know what, Jake Tapper and Jim Acosta now going to do it in drag just to compete.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Then I would watch Jim Acosta.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know, it's a stunt. Wait till you see what we dress up Greg as tomorrow. Right up next, criminal migrants aren't taking President Trump's immigration crackdown very well. Watch this guy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm not going back to Haiti. (EXPLETIVE DELETED) Trump. You feel me? Yo, Biden forever, bro. Thank Obama for everything that he did for me, bro.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Bill Melugin got an exclusive look at Trump's ICE raids.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: President Trump's deportation force is moving at breakneck speed, and Bill Melugin getting an exclusive look at elite ICE agents in the sanctuary city of Boston, focusing on the worst offenders who crossed the border. An MS-13 gang member wanted for murder. An illegal from Honduras who raped this victim while holding a gun in her mouth. And this feisty Haitian gangbanger with 17 past criminal convictions. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm not going back to Haiti. (BLEEP) Trump. You feel me? Yo, Biden forever, bro! Thank Obama for everything that he did for me, bro.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Harold, I wouldn't want to go back to Haiti either, but you got to go.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I think the worst -- again, just to repeat, I think the worst first policy is the right thing. I think people are going to give the President a lot of latitude and I could be patient with him in terms of if he goes -- if he's -- if he is too aggressive, I think people will be forgiving.\par \par \uc2 I think people want to see illegals in this country who have committed crimes deported. I think people want to see their neighborhoods made safer. I think when you think about the costs associated with it -- there are whole range of things. So, I think the President is going to continue -- President Trump is going to continue to get a lot of support of this. I think as we get going a little bit here, I think you saw out in the State of Washington, a Reagan-appointed judge, federal judge there, struck down President Trump's executive order around Birthright Citizenship.\par \par \uc2 The President may be on to something there but you'll probably -- he probably won't be --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: He won't be able to do it by executive order. No lawyer would think -- if you want to change the Constitution, we can do it. But this right here he will -- he will have support for. Some of the other things I think will invite some -- invite some tension, but every American wants to see criminals, certainly if they're not here legally, deported and I'm one of them.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How bad of a look is it for Barack and Biden where Haitian gangbangers with 17 convictions are singing their praises?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I'm so happy he did it because Barack and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  earned it. They earned those accolades from this 17-time convicted Haitian gangbanger who is saying F the President of the United States. Look, it's about time. They were -- they were not only blamed by, you know, the Republicans but blamed even by this individual.\par \par \uc2 CNN apparently, and I hate to even mention, they made a statement. They said, America is now more hawkish on illegal immigration as opposed to the way they were in 2016. No. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was prescient. In 2016, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was concerned about immigration, they said he was a xenophobe. They said he was a racist. But he knew what was at risk. He knew what was coming. And we weren't ready. We had to suffer for eight years before we got to the point where America said you know what, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is right.\par \par \uc2 And this is what Biden wanted, and this is what Obama wanted, and they never told us why. We know why. It's about power. It's about the Democrat Party. Because if there were a real refugee problem, first of all, they'd be showing up -- the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  would be showing up. They'd be showing up for their asylum hearings.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is this. They did not care about the American people. They were more concerned about power and now we've got ICE agents begging to get back to do their job. We've got people in apartment buildings yelling thank you for getting these individuals out of this country and out of their neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't remember, Greg, in the last years ever seeing video of an ICE arrest. We just saw borders open and people waltzing through with their entire family.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, it is -- it's interesting -- and I think you -- the media -- we should be very careful not to let the media frame this as a crackdown. It's not a crackdown. It's a correction.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is how -- this is how normal life is. Illegal criminals in your country, you arrest them. And the idea that like somehow -- you know, it's great. I love this guy -- 17 convictions. If only Biden was here to pardon him, you know, he -- this is a blow to MSNBC because I think he was next in line for \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 's spot.\par \par \uc2 Mexico by the way is assembling these huge structures, right, to receive illegals and they are worried that they won't suddenly be able to house them or care for them. But they're also worried they may be sounding like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And this is -- this is what is brilliant. It is again, I said it in the A block, or was it B, free money on the table. Let's get rid of the worst first.\par \par \uc2 The Dems are like hey, why didn't we think of this? Well, you stopped thinking. You replace thinking with feeling. And you were wondering why they did this? It was because they were dictated by activists and moronic allies known as the media.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: From a public relation standpoint --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I love that question.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very clever for the administration to embed Melugin with ICE.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Look what I wrote, embed. Oh my gosh. We're all thinking the same way. So, I think --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thinking about Melugin in bed? Geez, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's what you were thinking. That's what you were thinking. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I believe there are more -- you can fact check me. Whatever. But I do believe that there are more \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers than there are ICE agents in the country. So, what they are able to do, they have to be super effective and they knew exactly what they were doing today. But I thought was amazing was how terrible the visuals were for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here you have all these criminals who were recently let out of jail, and they're just released back into the population. And that's the sanctuary? That was incredible to me. The video is very important. Do you remember when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tried to stop drones from flying over the border? He tried to get the \uc2 FAA\uc2  to stop it --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- because they didn't want to see the visuals of what was happening especially when the Haitian migrants were coming over. And we fought back, Fox News did, and they had to back down. So, now I think it's so important for the Trump team and for ICE to figure out a way.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin, take him along. Great. Take the reporters that are willing to go. What's interesting is that Bill Melugin, and Griff Jenkins, and all the people at Fox News who were at the border covering that story were the only ones there. The story of a lifetime. You could win a Pulitzer.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's so true.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, I'm glad that they're taking Bill Melugin, but what about these other entities? Why don't you go along and see -- and tell your viewers and tell your listeners exactly what is happening there?\par \par \uc2 President Trump was defined by false videos in the first term when it came to immigration especially on family separation, to the point that one of the big publicly funded radio stations had to backtrack and say actually, sorry, that video of the mom and the daughter separate, that wasn't true. And so, I think that they are very clever and they are thinking ahead that they have to show what they are doing. They cannot rely on other people to do it for them.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Should I embed with ICE?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I would like you to.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: How about \uc2 ISIS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And then Bill Melugin.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: An embed with \uc2 ISIS\uc2 , Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You embed with \uc2 ISIS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the story of my Liberal parents freaking out over their MAGA sons.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is diving into the struggles of Liberal parents who are having a hard time connecting with their Trump-loving kids. The headline saying, "When your son goes MAGA." The article adding, "Plenty see their sons embrace of Mr. Trump as an expected act of rebellion or a choice made by an independent young adult that they should respect. For others it has felt like a painful rejection of the values they have tried to instill in their children."\par \par \uc2 Now, Jesse, I couldn't start this without calling on you first. Are you an embarrassment to your mother? And does she think that there should be tampons in the boy's bathroom?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No and no, I think. She -- I tried to tell her, Judge, that as a conservative, she raised me to have common sense, a work ethic, respect for all individuals. That message hasn't sunk into her. She thinks I'm a liar. And she keeps texting me about me not telling the truth. And it's just a matter of opinion, mom. You're reading the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , you're getting one side, I've read all of the newspapers. I know everything about everything.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm a professional newsman. You don't know enough about things way I do.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You're (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right. And so, she'll catch up. And she has caught up. It hasn't been as hateful as of late.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Harold, liking the President to many in your party is considered an act of rebellion. Does that make sense?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No, it does not make sense. I think in the political climate we live in today, people think that that is what allows you to hold on to whatever your political view, political position, political party affiliation might be. My -- a buddy of mine, one of my best friends, gave me the best piece of advice about this many years ago. His son was having doubts about being a Democrat. He's a Democrat and his son was saying, who was a voting age, I don't think Democrats are speaking to me and speaking to the issues.\par \par \uc2 And he said to him his father's, a big Democrat, he said to him three things. He said number one, I don't care what you do, but here are the three things you have to do if you're going to do that, whatever you want to do. Don't be a hater. Two, be able to defend your positions with clarity, humility, and substance. And three, that humility should allow you to say when you're right about something or when you're wrong about something and to learn from it.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope my kids are Democrats. If they're not, I'm going to apply these three things to them. And my dad and I who -- my dad is probably a little more to the left of me than I am, than my politics, and we have these conversations fairly for the regular. I hope he's not watching because --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Greg, Harold is so reasonable. But you were with Men's Health, that magazine. Don't you think it's healthy for young men and young people to admire masculine men?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, yes. But that's not why I was there, no matter what anybody says. Sure I went to the photo shoots, and I kind of hung around longer than I should. But anyway, I'm not allowed in a few gyms. Look, you know the article was titled, "When your son goes MAGA." The real title should be when your teacher hates Trump. because this -- that causes your son to go MAGA when your teacher goes Trump.\par \par \uc2 Rebelling against teachers who didn't persuade and only demanded was what created this. Suddenly Trump became cool and dangerous, a political and social pirate ship. And the Dem started to look like an emasculated dinghy because all the annoying freaks, your guidance counselor, your -- I don't know -- math teacher, your parents started to all sound the same.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't just the lockstep that you rebel against. It was the compliance. You were to accept their notions without explanation. You know, Kamala is brilliant, Trump is Satan. My pronouns are they or them. It wasn't the beliefs, it was the compliance. And if you're an individual mind, and I think the advice that you were talking about was good. If you want to think for yourself, then no one can compel you what to believe. They can't just say it is.\par \par \uc2 So, I will just end on this. I often asked this question by young folks when I do live shows. They'll say, what do I -- I'm the only Conservative among Liberals and they always gang up on me -- on me when I disagree with them. And I say, ask them one simple question. Why is it so important for me to agree with you? Why does it bother you that I won't obey?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Why should you care? That usually hits the heart of their insecurities. Why do you need everyone to agree with you? That usually destroys them, and they don't know what to say.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I usually give them different advice.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Of course you do.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I say sleep with their girlfriends.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, excellent.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, God.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You never lose after that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. All right --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that's a good idea. I really think that's smart.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: As long as --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Or their moms. Sleep with their girlfriends or their moms.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, my gosh. Stop that, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They'll never come back from something like that.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Let me put that down as a--\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, no, no.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: -- as a fourth piece of advice.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Consensually.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Even then, stop it. Dana?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Consensually.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Dana, we're going to -- we're going to pass -- you know what, Jesse? Don't talk anymore. You can't talk for the rest of the show.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do I still get paid?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, you probably will.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Fine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Dana, isn't the Republican Party really more like a party --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, they're having fun.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- where even the celebs don't charge to perform?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that's changed. Now, and I do think if you go back and forth, I remember I have a friend who started Nixon watches. And one of the reasons they called it Nixon is because it was like for the skateboarders and the surfers, and they wanted to rebel against their parents. The parents hated Nixon. So, it does go back and forth.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing that President Trump was able to do this time around is to tap into the new ways that people are communicating. And you have a lot of these new podcast or long form ways, people on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , and they're funny and they're having a good time. And they're thinking, well, wait. Yes, I want opportunity. They don't -- they hate the DEI stuff and they see President Trump getting rid of that.\par \par \uc2 But I also noticed a friend of mine said I was watching the inauguration, and all the \uc2 YouTube\uc2  stars were there and they couldn't believe it. And it's -- what's interesting is with politics being downstream of culture, but now it's like it's all the same. It's intermesh and people are having fun in amongst the politics.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Why do they hate Cynthia so much?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Who Cynthia?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Cynthia Nixon. She was one of my favorites on Sex in the City.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Cynthia.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Anyway --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: She's cute. And on your point, Dana, I mean, kudos to \uc2 Barron Trump\uc2  on that too.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Trump's return to the office order.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Welcome back. Federal employees are pushing back against President Trump's 30-day order to get back to the office five days a week. Many dread the daily grind of traffic, fighting for parking, or desk space, arguing that they've already made -- that they've already proven they can get the job done remotely.\par \par \uc2 Judge, you think they should get back to work?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I think I'm looking for a succinct answer.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. OK. They absolutely should get back to work. Biden signed an executive order. He wanted them to be able to work remotely until 2029. But if you got the boss working day and night since he was sworn in, I think they can show up at the office.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Primetime, your thoughts on showing up to work?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We were just in D.C. and we saw these beautiful Federal buildings, Department of Commerce, \uc2 EPA\uc2 , they're empty. So, if they don't want to go back in, they should have a fire sale and we should beable to buy them and then make them into luxury condos.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Dana, you've talked about this. This is a way we can -- DOGE should look at this to figure out way to save money.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, I mean, agree on the point about the empty building should be sold or repurposed. I do think that there should be some discretion given to managers if you have a great employee. And they can only come in three days a week, not the five. Like, the discretion for a manager I think is important.\par \par \uc2 But I would get back to work. And the City of D.C. has been begging the federal government to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's good for business.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Exclamation Point, your thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This reminds me of Jim Acosta. Apparently, he threatened to quit CNN if they move him to midnight. It's like, dude, that's the point. We're trying to get you to leave.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Rigght.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're not in a position to negotiate, people. If you work for the government, you got to work for the government. If you're on death row, feel free to complain about the temperature of your last meal. You're still going to fry. They just want to get him out of here. Go.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: And that's that. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OMT. Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I've been waiting to show you all this. I had a lot of fun at the inauguration for all sorts of reasons. But also, I got to sing with Dierks Bentley on stage at the \uc2 Free Press\uc2 , Uber, X party. And the song was Country Road. I'm not playing the audio because Kate was singing so loud that she wanted it. Just kidding. We all love TikTok, Kate. And he was -- he was happy to have her there.\par \par \uc2 Perino on Politics with Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. That's your favorite Bentley. It's an EV Bentley. All right, tonight, we have Jim Norton, Charly Arnolt, Kat Timpf, and Tyrus. That's tonight at 10:00. Quickly friends.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Animals are great! Animals are great! Animals are great!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We haven't done this in a while. Harold loves this because it's not in his face. You know, there's nothing better than enjoying a corn on the cob upside down with your mouth, Jesse. Here is Juno the sloth enjoying his corn on the cob at the Cincinnati Zoo upside down. 15 -- they sleep up to 15 hours a day hanging upside down. That's it for me. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: A Japanese aquarium has shut down last month for a six-month renovation. And they noticed that this sunfish here was not well. He wasn't eating. So, they came up with this creative solution. They put people smiling. They put cutouts of people smiling and the sunfish was mesmerized by this. He got happier and he start eating. A little companionship goes a long way and cheering you up. I love that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Harold makes friends.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Best of luck to Ben Shelton and Madison Keys tonight at the Australian Open.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That fish likes his corn on the cob upside down too.\par \par \uc2 Fox Nation celebrates January Comedy Month. We have Tyler Fischer. They call him a comedic genius. "NO PARDON NEEDED." Go check it out on Fox Nation.\par \par \uc2 And Johnny went to the balls. Here's a little look.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY BELISARIO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PRODUCER: I heard there were some illegal migrants trying to get into this event tonight.\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, BORDER CZAR: Where? Show me.\par \par \uc2 BELISARIO: You'll take care of it?\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: I'll take care of it right now.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's Johnny tonight at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, 10 seconds, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I don't --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Eight seconds.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I can't do it in 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Six seconds.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's about three African elephants.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Four seconds. Three seconds.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Missy, Kimba, Lucky, LouLou, and Jambo.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We're done.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020250124el1n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Supports \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  For House Speaker; Security Preparations Continue For New York City New Year's Celebrations; Republicans Debate Immigration Visas; Biden Regrets Decision to Drop Out; South Korea Plane Crash; Interview With Rep. Tim Burchett (R-TN); Remembering \uc2 Jimmy Carter\b0\par\par\uc2 Alexandria Hoff, Steve Bender, Alveda King, Greg Palkot, Jonathan  Serrie, Nate Foy, Matt Finn, Lucas Tomlinson, Edward Lawrence \par \uc2 7,865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  throwing his support behind Republican Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2 . Ahead of what's expected to be a close vote on Friday, is it enough to get Republicans on board?\par \par \uc2 We will ask Tennessee Congressman Tim Burchett about it in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, remembering the 39th president of the United States \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , who passed at the age of 100. The tributes and messages of gratitude rushing in from his predecessors here in the U.S. and leaders around the world. The longest living U.S. president, Carter died nearly two years after entering hospice care.\par \par \uc2 Now multiple observances are set to get under way. Welcome, everyone. I'm Edward Lawrence, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We begin with Jonathan Serrie in Carter's hometown of Plains, Georgia, with more on this -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Well-wishers have begun to arrive here in Plains, Georgia, the town where former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  was born and where he always embraced the rural and very religious way of life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KITTY THOMAS, GEORGIA RESIDENT: Our American traditions and values, we need to bring that back, and it's going to be hard, but we can do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Carter won the presidency following \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 's Watergate scandal in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 He worked to restore faith in America's institutions and negotiated a lasting peace settlement between Egypt and Israel. But he had to contend with ongoing inflation and energy crisis and American diplomats taken hostage in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Even those who did not always agree with him politically praise Mr. Carter's humanitarian initiatives both during and post-presidency, as well as his deeply held Christian faith that guided his charitable spirit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BUDDY CARTER (R-GA): We're part of the Bible Belt down here in Georgia, particularly in South Georgia. And I would submit to you that \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  may be remembered more for his faith than for his politics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: On his TRUTH Social platform, president-elect Trump writes about Mr. Carter: "While I strongly disagreed with him philosophically and politically, I also realized that he truly loved and respected our country and all it stands for. He worked hard to make America a better place. And, for that, I give him my highest respect."\par \par \uc2 President Biden has designated January 9, 2025, as a national day of mourning. The Atlanta Journal-Constitution reports Mr. Biden is expected to deliver the eulogy at a ceremony in Washington on that day. Additional ceremonies are being planned in Atlanta and here in Plains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What I find extraordinary about \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , though, there are millions of people all around the world, all over the world, feel they lost a friend as well, even though they never met him.\par \par \uc2 And that's because \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  lived a life measured not by words, but by his deeds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: The former president's legacy lives on in the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2 , the international nonprofit organization he created in partnership with \uc2 Emory University\uc2  to help eradicate disease, promote fair elections, and negotiate peace.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Carter, who was 100 when he died, will be buried next to his beloved wife, Rosalynn, his wife of 77 years. They have a family plot under a willow tree just outside of the residence where they lived together Ed, back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thank you, Jonathan Serrie.\par \par \uc2 Now, Alveda King offering her deepest condolences, posting a photo of Carter and her grandfather, Dr. Martin Luther King Sr., who were close friends.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from Atlanta is Alveda King, daughter of civil rights activist A.D. King and the niece of reverend \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr.\par \par \uc2 So, first, thank you for being here. How do you think President Carter will be remembered?\par \par \uc2 DR. ALVEDA KING, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I am so glad to join you in remembering a family friend, President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He and my grandfather, Reverend Dr. Martin Luther King Sr., were very, very good friends, and I will remember and celebrate his life. He was a man of integrity, a man of faith and prayer, good husband, good family man, and he often counseled members of our family.\par \par \uc2 And so it's wonderful. I was a state legislator while his era -- during his political era, State Representative Alveda King, House District 28. So I knew him politically. Many of the pictures that you are seeing now, I was right there at those events.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 So, Alveda, this was a president who arguably did more for his legacy after he left office, his work through the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2 , his work for Habitat for Humanity. What does it tell you about a person who's already served in the Navy, he served as a state senator, as you talked about, and as governor of Georgia?\par \par \uc2 What does that say about that person that he kept going?\par \par \uc2 KING: He was a man of integrity, very thorough.\par \par \uc2 He was one of the few presidents that read the 500-page documents and went through them and wanted to make the world better. He was so phenomenal. I didn't agree with him in the latter years with his political aspirations, because we were different. I'm conservative and he was more of a liberal, but just a wonderful man who cared about humanity.\par \par \uc2 And he understood that we're one blood, one human race. He and my grandfather, Daddy King, did a lot of racial reconciliation work. And they just said, hey, we're the same blood, so we have to get along. And I just remember him as a man of integrity, yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, President Carter became an activist through civil rights movement with the Democratic Party as a Georgia state senator. He also pardoned all the Vietnam War draft dodgers on his second day in office.\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And President Carter wasn't afraid to stand up to big issues, was he?\par \par \uc2 KING: He was never afraid to do the convictions of his faith and his heart. He's to be remembered for that and admired for that, yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, so the former president, during his civil -- during the civil rights movement, he was there at the same time as \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr.\uc2 , but they never met. Why do you think that was?\par \par \uc2 KING: Well, we lived in the times prior to the 1960s and '70s, on into the 80s, and the South was and still is a little different than other parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 And so there was no reason because there was no conflict or confrontation that caused them to come together. My grandfather did have that experience, and they hit some of those very serious issues head on together.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And President Carter won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2002 for his work through the \uc2 Carter Center\uc2 , which has projects in 80 countries, including helping to monitor fair elections and leading efforts to treat rare tropical diseases. He was pretty selfless after he left office, wasn't he?\par \par \uc2 KING: I called him the humanitarian president, and that's the way he should be remembered, who cared about humanity, cared about all people. And he demonstrated that over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Thank you, Alveda. I appreciate your time on this. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you so much. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: God bless you too.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is a Carter family friend who lives in Plains, Georgia, and performed at Carter's 100th birthday celebration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, with us now is the singer-songwriter and Carter family friend Andrew Greer.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being here. The nation is mourning along with you.\par \par \uc2 What is it about President Carter that you admired?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GREER, CARTER FAMILY FRIEND: I think President Carter's dedication, his commitment, his kind of dogged determination to carry out peace, I know in an interview when he was asked what does he want to be remembered as, he said, a man of peace.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we know Edward in our current climate of just the state of the world, peace is a tricky thing. It's not simple to pursue. So President Carter's pursuit of peace is something that has deeply impacted who I am as a person and the way I want to live my life.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Is there something that comes to mind that most Americans didn't know about the former president since you did know him?\par \par \uc2 GREER: Well, I think his determination, which I think a lot of people knew him for his steely glance while he was president, but his punctuality and his impatience with unpunctuality, which I am a late person.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GREER: I remember one time being at church at Maranatha, and I happened to be playing the piano that Sunday for our regular pianist, Jan Williams, and I was supposed to be playing the prelude to the service at that point in time, but I was kneeling by his pew talking to him.\par \par \uc2 And he interrupts me at some point and said: "Why aren't you playing the piano?"\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GREER: So, I went and played the piano. Yes, Mr. President, happy to do that.\par \par \uc2 But I think his humility, no matter how much we know about that, no matter how much we see that in his life, I think it goes layers beyond what we actually saw on the surface. His humility is what makes him unique as a modern American.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, President Carter gets a lot of criticism for his policies which led to an oil shortage in the U.S. and mainly because the Arab oil- producing countries started an embargo of the U.S. at a time that President Carter was trying to transition to solar energy.\par \par \uc2 Did those criticisms weigh on the former president, do you think?\par \par \uc2 GREER: I'm sure they did. I didn't know the former president at that time. I wasn't born at that time.\par \par \uc2 But I am certain that they did then. Of course, the history books have shown that. But I think that is, again, a part of the determination and the pursuit of peace and of love around the world that both President and Mrs. Carter were committed to, because they didn't let that deter them.\par \par \uc2 Failure was not a deterrent for them, but, rather, a motivator for them. And I think that's -- when you're trying to do good in the world, when you're trying to be an example, when you're trying to treat people equally, to extend human rights to all mankind. That's something that's going to come with success and failure. And their failure was fuel for their fire and their passion for people.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's an incredible example for us today. That perfection is not the point. People is the point. And I think, for the Carter, especially President Carter, that's true.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, if it doesn't work, keep trying and trying and trying again. He kept going at it.\par \par \uc2 What about in Plains, Georgia,? What is the town, what are you guys doing to honor the former president?\par \par \uc2 GREER: Well, of course, we're getting ready. You can see it behind me. We have been getting ready since last night.\par \par \uc2 But plans have been in place for years now. That's true of any former president, but especially one that lives to 100 years old. And so, really, it's just tidying up the town. Plains is Plains. Plains is not going to change overnight. It has not changed for a couple of hundred years.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what continually brought President and Mrs. Carter back to this place to inspire them for all their international pursuits. And so we don't want to change the place up. We want to really give him his last send-off from home from a place he would recognize.\par \par \uc2 So we're tidying up. We're getting the church ready. We're getting the town ready. And, certainly, we're hanging up some special ribbons and bows and signs that we love him. And, as people pass through 280, they know that we love him. But we want to make it feel like home.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And we have heard that Plains, Georgia, is a place that kept him grounded, even throughout his presidential career, but then also after.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Greer, I appreciate it. Thank you very much for remembering the former president for us. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GREER: Thank you for remembering...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, before we move on, taking a look at how Wall Street is paying tribute to the late president. U.S. markets will be closed on January 9 for a national day of mourning.\par \par \uc2 Both the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  and the Nasdaq, they opened today with a moment of silence.\par \par \uc2 We will have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  throwing his support behind House Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  today ahead of Friday's vote for the gavel, a move which could sway some on the fence.\par \par \uc2 We will ask Tennessee Republican Congressman Tim Burchett about that in a moment, but first to FOX News' Alexandria Hoff live with the posturing from Washington -- Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Well, Johnson supporters had hoped that the endorsement by the president- elect would help draw in the unanimous support that the House speaker needs. And it might, but it hasn't yet.\par \par \uc2 The president-elect wrote in part -- quote -- "Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  is a good, hardworking religious man who will do the right thing and we will continue to win. Mike has my complete and total endorsement."\par \par \uc2 Now, Johnson can only afford to lose one vote. But there's more than one Republican feeling that he showed weakness in the funding battle earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Here's Congressman Andy Biggs:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): I haven't publicly or privately committed yet. I do want to speak with the speaker just to see what his plans are because there are some issues that I think need to be worked out specifically dealing with the budget issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Congressman \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  is among those not enticed by the endorsement. There are still a handful of them.\par \par \uc2 He wrote -- quote -- "I respect and support President Trump, but his endorsement of \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  is going to work out about as well as his endorsement of Speaker \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Now, in a follow-up post to that on X, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  half agreed, adding: "You might be right, but let's see how it goes."\par \par \uc2 A majority of Republicans are, though, frustrated by their colleagues potentially jeopardizing the timeline of Republican priorities. If a speaker isn't elected on the 3rd, then the election can't be certified.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): We can't get anything done unless we have a speaker, including certifying President Trump's election on January 6. So to waste time over a nonsensical intramural food fight is a joke.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Congressman Jim Jordan agreed with the president-elect, adding: "Time to do what we said we would do."\par \par \uc2 And Speaker Johnson, he did thank president-elect Trump for his support today, saying: "The American people demand and deserve no time wasted" -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, now that president-elect Trump is backing Speaker Johnson, let's read the room.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee Republican Congressman Tim Burchett, thank you, first of all, for being here.\par \par \uc2 You have said that you supported the House speaker because no one else is officially running. And now that president-elect Trump is behind him, is your mind solidified? Is he the guy?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT (R-TN): That does weigh heavily on me, but, ultimately, I will make up my mind Friday or so when we do the votes. I will pray about it.\par \par \uc2 My wife heard that I was going to be on FOX News, so she made me wear all my new Christmas stuff. She said she was tired of me looking like a hobo when I'd come on news with you all. So I'm trying to up my image.\par \par \uc2 But, no, I will make up my mind some time between now and then. I will talk to the speaker soon. I might talk to the president. But President Trump is very compelling, though. As I have said earlier, he's the largest dog in the pound. There's nobody bigger than him. And I don't think too many people want to tangle with him, especially on this.\par \par \uc2 And our members taking shots across the bow at some of us, that's just what the news does. The legacy media, they try to tear the Republican Party down, but I'm hoping for some big things out of us this year.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, so your wife has great taste. She did a good job with the clothes. I have to say that first.\par \par \uc2 This is exactly what president-elect Trump...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 This is exactly what president-elect Trump said on TRUTH Social. I want to read this, so everyone can hear it: "Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  is a good, hardworking, religious man. He will do the right thing and we will continue to win. Mike has my complete and total endorsement."\par \par \uc2 There's no ambiguity. Do you think now the House speaker then will have enough votes on Friday? What do you believe?\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: I think he ultimately will. I think that the die has been cast pretty much.\par \par \uc2 But, as I have stated, I'm going to -- I will make up my mind on Friday. And, like, I will talk to Mike some and maybe President Trump even and discuss some ideas and issues that I have.\par \par \uc2 But Mike's been a good friend to me. And there's nobody more honest that I have ever dealt with in Washington. I mean, he is a fine Christian man. He's not a religious man. He's a Christian man. And he doesn't cheat on his wife. And I find that very appealing in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's good.\par \par \uc2 So let's look beyond the House speaker vote. The House is going to have an issue with the debt ceiling coming up. The debate to fund the government comes up again before March 14 deadline.\par \par \uc2 So I spoke with Representative Kat Cammack about suspending the debt ceiling. And listen to what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK (R-FL): One, it's irresponsible. Two, it's not fair. And, again, it is kicking the can down the road.\par \par \uc2 So in no shape way, form or fashion have I ever supported a debt ceiling being lifted, because it makes very little sense that we campaign on cutting the size, scope of government, cut spending, but then we say, hey, let's get rid of the credit card limit in order to cut spending?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, what do you think about that, Republicans as the party of cutting spending, so just cut the spending and not reach the debt ceiling? What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: I think it's a refreshing view.\par \par \uc2 I don't think I have ever voted for a debt ceiling increase. You're seeing more money going up in debt than I will earn this year by the time this -- I finish this thought even. But every 100 days, we had another trillion dollars to the debt. We better start taking it serious.\par \par \uc2 As I told -- as I said on X, I tweeted at the @TimBurchett account, had over a million views, that the -- that the problem is going to be with the votes in that room. It wasn't with Elon or Vivek. It was going to be the members having enough guts to do what's right and make those cuts. That remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 We do this every time. Everybody goes home and preaches to their people and throws out the red meat. Then they come back to Washington and they sell our country down the river. I'm hoping that will change, and I hope Trump cleans it up, because we have got to do something.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Do you feel like cuts can be made? I mean, it's one thing to identify the waste. It's another thing to actually do it. I mean, do you think there's an appetite for that in the Republican -- for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 But you got so many people that are embedded in these things and they're compromised. Let's just be honest. They have got investments. They have got family members. They have a wife and/or a girlfriend that works for some contractor or some lobbyist on K Street or some organization that's going to get some funding.\par \par \uc2 And we will tell the -- we will wink at the Senate and we will say, we're going to make all these cuts. And then the Senate will say, we're going to make all these cuts. And guess what? They don't line up. And then we come back and don't do anything.\par \par \uc2 And that's the trick they're going to play. Let's just cut and keep cutting. We talked about cutting the Department of Education, and everybody freaks out. We're not talking about cutting the money to the states. Send the money to the states, let them be accountable for it, instead of Washington, D.C. It's not a one-size-fits-all. There's not one Washington bureaucrat has ever taught a kid in Corryton, Tennessee, how to read, write or do arithmetic.\par \par \uc2 And we could cut -- they're worse than the mob. I mean, for goodness' sakes, God only takes 10 percent with tithe. Washington takes a whole lot more off the top, and all they're doing is borrowing from our great- grandchildren. And we know better.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, we're -- Congressman, we're going to have to see how this all plays out. In the first month, I guess, the president, it seems like -- or president-elect seems like he's going to be pretty fast.\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Representative Tim Burchett from the great state of Tennessee, I appreciate it. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 BURCHETT: Thank you, brother. Pray for our country.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Security preparations under way in New York City ahead of tomorrow night's New Year's ball drop. We will take you there live.\par \par \uc2 First, though, to the investigation as U.S. officials rush to South Korea to find the cause of this deadly crash caught on camera -- the latest with the investigation and if it was a bird strike or what it could have been.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: The Treasury Department informing the Senate Banking Committee several workstations were targeted in a China-based cyberattack earlier this month. It contacted law enforcement partners upon learning of the hack.\par \par \uc2 More as we get it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: To South Korea, where U.S. officials joined the investigation into what caused a passenger jet to crash-land, killing 179 people.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot live in London.\par \par \uc2 So, Greg, what do we know about this?\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Edward investigators are just beginning to pick up the pieces, but there are some clues behind this terrible disaster.\par \par \uc2 A warning: Some of the video you are about to see is of a graphic nature. The South Korea airline \uc2 Jeju Air\uc2 's \uc2 Boeing\uc2  plane on its way from Bangkok made a very hard landing at an airport in the southwestern part of the country Sunday, its landing gear, engines and other systems not working properly.\par \par \uc2 The plane ran off the runway, crashing into a concrete barrier, exploding and breaking up. The 179 people killed included all of the passengers. As distraught families gathered at the memorial today, they learned it could be days before all the victims would be identified and the remains returned to their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 As for the cause of the crash, Edward, South Korean officials say the airport warned the approaching plane of a possible bird strike. Shortly after, the plane's pilot issued a mayday call and confirmed it had been hit by birds. But experts say that shouldn't alone have caused this catastrophic incident.\par \par \uc2 The plane's data and voice black boxes have been recovered. \uc2 NTSB\uc2  inspectors are on the way to assist in South Korea's probe. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is also helping. The company has been plagued by safety and other issues in recent years. But the 737-800 model is considered safe and widely used.\par \par \uc2 Still, Seoul says it will be looking at that aircraft type and all the country's aviation operations. One more thing, Edward. South Korea is going through a political upheaval right now. A president and an acting president has been impeached in the last couple of weeks. Experts are worried the government can't handle this national disaster.\par \par \uc2 Ed, by the way, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  shares off a bit more than 2 percent today -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, juggling a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Greg Palkot, in London.\par \par \uc2 So, moving closer to home, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  reporting President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  regrets dropping from the presidential race and thinks he could have won reelection.\par \par \uc2 To Lucas Tomlinson in Saint Croix, where Biden's stewing over this while on vacation -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS PENTAGON PRODUCER: Well, good afternoon, Edward.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's approval rating right now is hovering at about 39 percent. And despite that, as you just mentioned, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  reports, President Biden has told aides he regrets dropping out of the race.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to that report -- quote -- "Biden and some of his aides still believe he should have stayed in the race, despite the rocky debate performance and low poll numbers that prompted Democrats to pressure him to drop out. Biden and these aides have told people in recent days that he could have defeated Trump."\par \par \uc2 Now, yesterday on the Sunday talk shows, one CBS News reporter says this is the following most underreported story of the year:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: Undercovered and reported, that would be, to me, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's obvious cognitive decline that became undeniable in the televised debate.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: At the presidential debate with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CRAWFORD: We should have much more forcefully questioned whether he was fit for office for another four years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Now, that's not all. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  also reports Biden has another regret as well.\par \par \uc2 Quote: "Biden has also said he should have picked someone other than \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  as attorney general," complaining about the Justice Department's slowness under Garland in prosecuting Trump and its aggressiveness in prosecuting Biden's son Hunter.\par \par \uc2 Now, newly released photos show Biden and his son shaking hands and posing with Chinese businessmen during a 2013 trip to Beijing when Biden was vice president. Biden has long denied any involvement in his son's overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 The White House reacting to the new photos -- quote -- "This trip, which occurred over a decade ago, is old news. House Republicans already wasted years and millions of taxpayer dollars proving that the president was not in business with his son. It's past time to call it a day."\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is here in Saint Croix with his father. They were both spotted together walking out of church yesterday. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's lawyer, Abbe Lowell, says this is also an old news story -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Lucas, appreciate it from Saint Croix.\par \par \uc2 So, president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting behind \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  on an important issue, but the internal debate within the GOP has already been ignited. Where's this heading?\par \par \uc2 Plus, security officials preparing for the ball drop in Times Square, but will Mother Nature cooperate? That forecast ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, president-elect Trump aligning with DOGE leaders \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  over the H-1B visa program, but critics argue it puts foreign workers over U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Matt Finn has the details from West Palm Beach, Florida -- Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: President-elect Trump and his team are holding some sensitive meetings here in West Palm Beach to get everyone on the same page for the first 100 days, but these H-1B visas sparking a little bit of discourse inside the Trump world, with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  leading the charge in their favor.\par \par \uc2 He says he will go toward defending them, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  weighing in as well. President-elect Trump essentially settling the matter for now, telling The New York Post he's in favor of H-1B visas.\par \par \uc2 Now, the visas historically have allowed qualified foreign workers into the U.S. for specialty jobs, an estimated 85,000 to 200,000-plus per year. However, some Republicans and Democrats are telling FOX News that the H-1B program is broken, that these visas are being given away to foreign workers, taking away entry-level jobs from Americans.\par \par \uc2 And heading into the first 100 days, immigration all around a major topic. A judge ordered the Biden administration to stop auctioning off large portions of President Trump's southern border wall. The Trump administration, of course, is looking to do a 180 on Biden border policies.\par \par \uc2 Congress has struggled for years to pass bipartisan reform. However, some Democrats have signaled they may be willing to work together.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. AMI BERA (D-CA): Let's secure our borders, but let's think about immigration slightly differently as an economic issue. And, again, you might find there are some Democrats like myself that are willing to work with Republicans if they don't go too far.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: The northern border also a focus here. President-elect Trump has been threatening 25 percent tariffs against the Canadians if they don't fix what he calls a flow of illegal drugs and immigration into our country -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Matt Finn, appreciate it in West Pal Beach. Everybody seems to be in a better location than I am, West Palm Beach, Saint Croix. Appreciate it, Matt Finn. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So let's see if this is something Republicans should be squabbling over.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to break it down, economist Steve Moore and political journalist Tiana Lowe Doescher. So, Steve, as an economist, legal immigration is something the U.S. needs. Now, we know that tech giants in Silicon Valley believe that American workers don't have the skill that some of the foreign workers have when it comes to doing jobs with advanced computing.\par \par \uc2 What do you think? Or do they?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, first of all, you have got it exactly right, Edward, that legal immigrants, those who come in lawfully with a visa, for the most part, are great economic contributors to our economy.\par \par \uc2 They're one of the reasons we're the most prosperous country in the world. We take so many of the best and brightest and hardest-working people from every country in the world. And we truly are a melting pot. This program -- I'm with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  on this one, on this H-1B program. This is a program that brings in scientists, engineers, computer programmers.\par \par \uc2 The employers have to certify with the Labor Department that they could not find a qualified American to do these jobs. Now, granted, there are some abuses in the program, Edward. I will agree to that, and I think some of those things have to be mended, but the program itself is very important for America's technological prowess, and we should continue with it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Tiana, this poses an interesting issue for president-elect Trump. He's a president who won, America first as his slogan. It did not say America first unless it has to do with foreign-born workers in high tech.\par \par \uc2 TIANA LOWE DOESCHER, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 And, also, let's remember \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in particular. When he came to this country, he has created with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  alone 120,000 jobs. Those legal immigrants that come in through this program are a net benefit for native- born Americans, right?\par \par \uc2 And just consider the fact that there is a maximum cap of 85,000 of these H-1B visa jobs per year. That works to be a little more than a quarter- million jobs over the course of four years. In that same time, 10 million illegal immigrants have illegally crossed into the country under the reign of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So we're really missing the forest for the trees here. While Republicans are infighting over this program that is broadly popular, that creates a massive amount of wealth, that creates American jobs, we're ignoring the 30 times more immigrants that came to this country illegally, and that must remain the focus.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, I don't know about ignoring. I mean, there is a focus on to make sure -- I mean, we all know, and the reason president-elect Trump or one of the reasons he was elected is because of what happened at the southern border. So they want to stop that.\par \par \uc2 Steve, I want to ask you this. We're in discussions right now with Canada about avoiding tariffs. The president-elect says he will impose them. Now, we have a House speaker that is not locked in. Does the GOP need an internal rift over American versus foreign workers right now?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well they are important to our economy.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And we wouldn't -- probably wouldn't have a Silicon Valley today and the technological -- we totally dominate the technological sector, in no small part because of so many of those businesses in Silicon Valley were started by immigrants.\par \par \uc2 And so, look, I think -- but I think there's...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right, but does it take the eye off the ball?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: What's that?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But does it take the eye off the ball of what needs to be done?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, what needs to be done is, we have to secure the border first, no question. I don't think there's any argument within the party about that, because Americans are tired of a million illegal immigrants coming over the border every year.\par \par \uc2 And then -- but the polls are pretty clear. Americans do see the benefits of people who come in legally, come in to work for a better life. We don't want immigrants coming in and going on welfare, but we want immigrants to contribute, as they always have.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, Tiana, what about that House speaker vote? The president- elect came out today posting on TRUTH Social that: "Speaker \uc2 Mike Johnson\uc2  is a good hardworking, religious man. He will do the right thing and we will continue to win. Mike has my complete and total endorsement."\par \par \uc2 But there seems to be some -- there was some chaos around the continuing resolution a few weeks ago. The lack of an immediate statement supporting Johnson, did this open the door for this kind of debate that they're having right now with the Republicans, leaving this up in the air a little bit?\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: This is president-elect Trump's warning to the Republican Caucus. They need to remember the mandate. And instead of fighting each other, fight for his agenda, because let's just consider the math, your Edward.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  does not take his seat on Friday, as he said he is not, Republicans will only have 218 votes. That means if \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2 , the Kentucky Congressman, votes against Johnson's speakership, as he said that he will, Republicans can only lose one other vote. And that's it.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if they don't get the speakership vote detailed, this will also delay confirming Trump's own election to the presidency. And so everyone understands that the House majority is a razor-thin margin, but also, in terms of how much time Trump has, he gets that every moment as precious.\par \par \uc2 Every day he can use to pass his agenda is necessary.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: So the longer the infighting continues, the slower his ability to succeed in pushing that agenda forward is then resulting.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right, get going, yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And he wants to move quickly.\par \par \uc2 So, Tiana and Steve, we're going to have to leave it right there. I appreciate it. Thanks for the debate on this.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thanks, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, to Times Square, courtesy of EarthCam, where they are getting ready to ring in the new year tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy with a preview of his story on those preparations -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Edward.\par \par \uc2 So, one million people are set to ring in the new year here at Times Square tomorrow. What does that have to do with the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war in the Middle East? The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  commissioner is going to explain for herself next.\par \par \uc2 Keep it locked.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: One million people expected to descend on Times Square tomorrow to watch the ball drop, and New York City officials are getting ready.\par \par \uc2 To Nate Foy in Times Square to show us all the security preps -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Hey, Edward.\par \par \uc2 So, there was just a round of applause because, just while we were in commercial break, they played the national anthem, which is how the party is going to start for those one million people who will flood Times Square tomorrow at about 6:00 to celebrate the new year.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  shared today that they are ready for this. They're sparing no expense. They got the drones in the sky. They have teams on the ground as well. And the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  commissioner, Jessica Tisch, said they're ready for protesters, including those of the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TISCH, NEW YORK CITY POLICE COMMISSIONER: At this time, there are no specific, credible threats to The Times Square New Year's Eve celebration. However, we have been operating in a heightened threat environment since October 7, and we remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: So, New York State Police, National Guard, and the New York homeland security team will all be working together tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 One threat they haven't had to deal with before is the emergence of the Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  said today they have made progress cracking down on the gang, and that it's not a threat specific to New Year's Eve.\par \par \uc2 New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  had this message today:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: Security is everyone's responsibility. So, if you see something, say something, but, most importantly, do something. Notify any uniform personnel that is in the area.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Edward, organizers are also preparing for the fun stuff.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. They made sure the 12,000-pound crystal ball drops the way that it's supposed to. It did. It went well, and so did a test of confetti. Take a look at this. They did a confetti drop on Sunday morning, and, tomorrow night, 3,000 pounds of confetti will fall here at Times Square.\par \par \uc2 So, again, they're testing the audio, the speaker system with the national anthem. Again, as we're reporting right now, the anticipation is building. Unfortunately, there is some rain in the forecast for tomorrow, but, other than that, people are excited, and for those attending, it should be a good time, Edward.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, that's -- he sounds good too. Sounds good in the background.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy live in Times Square.\par \par \uc2 Now, we will talk about that rain. The weather could put a wet blanket on plans for New Year's Eve.\par \par \uc2 FOX Weather meteorologist Steve Bender's here. And I don't know if we really want to hear what his forecast for Times Square.\par \par \uc2 But what do you have?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BENDER, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Yes, Edward, it's difficult to pass along this forecast. A million people are going to be at Times Square.\par \par \uc2 As Nate had mentioned, we have rain locked in, not only right leading up to the event, but beyond the New Year's Event too. So, looking at what we typically see, we're at 47 degrees. It's not going to be too warm. It's not going to be very cold, but it will land into that rain.\par \par \uc2 How does it build? On the exclusive \uc2 FOX\uc2  model, a light drizzle comes in about 7:00 p.m. Right from 10:00 p.m. on, that's when we actually have thunderstorms that start to push through. Something else with that, umbrellas are not allowed in Times Square for the big event and the ball drop.\par \par \uc2 By 6:00 a.m., we're clearing out, but this does linger on for hours ahead of time and after the ball drops. Temperatures will be mild, actually very comfortable to be outside for many that are going to be there for at least eight, 10 hours standing around.\par \par \uc2 The difficulty is, when that moment happens, will it just be confetti dropping, or will it be rain and lightning? And as we enter into 2025, the two active spots, the Northeast and the Pacific Northwest. So, Edward, it's going to be difficult tomorrow, but a lot of people are still going to be packed into Times Square.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thanks, Steve. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, FOX Weather is your winter weather H.Q., always watching the latest forecasts that could affect your travel plans. Stream live weather and news any time on any device.\par \par \uc2 Now, back to the tributes pouring in after the passing of President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  on how corporate America is remembering him today. Plus, his work with Habitat for Humanity will never be forgotten. One of its former directors on the legacy Carter hammered home is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Tributes pouring in remembering President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Delta posting a video of him on a flight from Atlanta to Washington, the former president walking down the aisle of the plane, shaking the hands of each and every passenger. And United posting a photo during Carter's campaign years, when he flew around in an aircraft chartered by United which he dubbed Peanut One.\par \par \uc2 So it's what Carter did after leaving the White House, the humanitarian work that many are focusing on today, especially the time he spent with thousands of homes for Habitat for Humanity alongside his wife, Rosalynn.\par \par \uc2 Bob DeRocker is a former executive director for the organization -- or Rob DeRocker, I should say.\par \par \uc2 So, Rob, what did President Carter's presence at Habitat for Humanity mean for it?\par \par \uc2 ROB DEROCKER, FORMER EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, HABITAT FOR HUMANITY: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 Well, Habitat -- good to see you, Edward, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Habitat was relatively unknown, almost completely unknown prior to his coming to New York and working on that project. What it did was, number one, it put it on the global map immediately. All three morning shows -- and this was back in '84, when you only had three morning shows -- they all did two-minute segments, and it was the front page of all the newspapers and whatnot.\par \par \uc2 So it did that, but it also helped legitimize the few projects that were around the world at the time, both in the United States and elsewhere. And where these project managers had been struggling to get funding, to get volunteers...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEROCKER: ... to get cooperation from city governments, that all suddenly came about as a result of the visibility of that project.\par \par \uc2 And we knew that 19 units was not going to settle the housing crisis on East Sixth Street, let alone Manhattan, New York City or the world. But it was the symbol and the visibility that it brought about for that program.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Rob, yes, I wanted to ask you, what was the reaction from other volunteers who worked on homes and may have not have known that he was on their team until someone told them?\par \par \uc2 DEROCKER: Yes, until they see him on the morning shows.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEROCKER: Well, it was astonishment, I think pride, that suddenly they were legitimized in doing what they were doing.\par \par \uc2 So, again, it was just a -- it was a rocket -- it was rocket fuel for the ministry.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, so the stock market is closed on January 9 as a day of mourning. The court -- the Supreme Court will be closed. National Archives has a special exhibit on display, including the Camp David Accords, the Egyptian-Israeli peace treaty of 1973.\par \par \uc2 How should Habitat for Humanity honor this man? We only have about 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 DEROCKER: Well, I think they're planning a memorial service. The new directors in Habitat in New York are planning a memorial service. I don't know if it's January 9, but it is at some point at that original building that they worked on, which was -- you know, that's still going strong.\par \par \uc2 And they -- they're doing great work all over New York and, of course, throughout the world.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: All right. Right.\par \par \uc2 Rob DeRocker, we have come to the end of the show. I appreciate it. Thank you for your insight.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  dead at the age of 100.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020241231ekcu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Deadly Plane Crash In South Korea; Trump Addresses Immigration Issues At Northern And Southern Borders; Interview with Sen. \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  (R-MO); Trump Plans Crack Down on Illegal Immigration; Manchin Slams State of Modern Democratic Party as "Toxic"; Musk, Ramaswamy Ignite Debate Around H-1B Visas; Some Dems Seeking Common Ground with DOGE; Biden Vetoes Bill To Expand Federal Judiciary; Investors, Economists Increasingly Optimistic On Second Trump Team; Impact Of Trump Agenda On Markets, Econ Outlook; The Winners and Losers from 2024; Hanukkah at the Museum of the Bible\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Matt Finn, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 9,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 As President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  prepares an ambitious agenda for his first 100 days, a rift is brewing between his big tech supporters and the MAGA base.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm looking in a situation where we don't have a lot of Americans who are willing to fill those jobs are capable of filling those jobs.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): USA STEM graduates of which there is a surplus right now for jobs, can't get those jobs because they're competing with H- 1B visas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Fallout over immigration after \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  defend work visas for highly skilled workers despite push back from the America First crowd.\par \par \uc2 We'll get reaction from Republican Senator \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then -- President Biden takes new actions on the environment and limiting the number of new judges President Trump will be able to nominate as the end of the Biden era approaches. We'll talk with House Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, the new Congress is sworn in on Friday and right out of the gate, the fate of Speaker Johnson hangs in the balance.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN CRENSHAW (R-TX): It's really not clear who else would be in the waiting there.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM BURCHETT (R-TN): I think President Trump behind the scenes will ultimately decide who the next speaker will be.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our Sunday panel on the leadership fight on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from "FOX News Sunday" in Washington on this last Sunday of 2024.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a FOX News alert.\par \par \uc2 South Korea suffering its deadliest passenger plane disaster ever on its soil this morning. We want to warn you, the video you're about to see is disturbing. A \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 800 jet used by a low budget airliner crash landed, veered off the runway and slammed into a concrete wall bursting into flames, 181 people were on board, only two people are known to have survived.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are sifting through the charred remains, trying to determine the cause of the crash. The airport control tower had issued a bird strike warning moments before the pilots declared a mayday.\par \par \uc2 In the Middle East, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  was scheduled to undergo surgery to remove an enlarged prostate today after he was diagnosed with an infection.\par \par \uc2 And here at home, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  -- \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  successfully launched a Falcon 9 rocket carrying four micro geo satellites from Cape Canaveral in Florida early this morning. That follows a successful launch Saturday in California.\par \par \uc2 And the new Congress is set to be sworn in Friday right here in Washington. In a moment, we will talk with Senator \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  about that and much more.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we got team coverage. Lucas Tomlinson in St. Croix covering President Biden and Matt Finn in West Palm Beach, Florida, following President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, Matt, we begin with you.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, here in West Palm Beach, the high-profile meetings are happening to hammer out critical issues and get everyone in alignment for Trump's first 100 days. But late this week, a bit of a clash arose over an immigration issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): After a few days of flare up inside the Trump world over H-1B visas, the president-elect has settled the matter saying he will continue to support the visas. MAGA leader \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  led the charge, fiercely defending the visas, writing in part: The reason I'm in America is because of H-1B. I will go to war on this issue.\par \par \uc2 But some MAGA supporters say the H-1Bs which historically allowed highly skilled workers into the U.S. are taking American jobs.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENNINGS, POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: I think what a lot of people in the party want to do is eliminate fraud in this H-1B program.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Another brewing topic, the wildly popular TikTok app that has more than 150 million users in the U.S. Trump who previously tried to ban the video app in the U.S. because it's owned by a Chinese company linked to the CCP is now asking the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  not to shut it down. The U.S. issued a January 19th deadline for TikTok to divest from its Chinese-owned parent company or be banned here. But now Trump says he wants to make the final decision on TikTok's fate.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson telling FOX: As the incoming chief executive, President Trump is the right constitutional actor to resolve the dispute through political means.\par \par \uc2 On immigration, Canadian cabinet members visited West Palm Beach on Friday for talks with Trump's camp. Trump is threatening 25 percent tariffs against Canada if it doesn't stem what he calls a flow of drugs and illegal migrants into our country. Trump's incoming borders are says they've already begun to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, TRUMP BORDER CZAR: I've talked to the foreign minister of Canada, you know, and I've been out to Arizona talking to the sheriffs out there. So we're not waiting for January 20th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN: This week, a judge ordered the Biden administration to stop auctioning off pieces of the Trump era border wall. President-elect Trump calls that decision a major win for America -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Matt Finn in Florida, thank you very much, Matt.\par \par \uc2 We go now to Lucas Tomlinson in St. Croix, the U.S. Virgin Islands where President Biden is on vacation.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden and his family have been enjoying a leisurely vacation here in St. Croix. Yesterday, the president was spotted leaving church alongside his recently pardoned son Hunter, President Biden spoke briefly to the press.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Should Putin take responsibility for the plane crash?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Apparently, he did, but I haven't spoken to him yet or my team.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): The White House has taken steps to so-called Trump proof Biden's agenda, including setting new ambitious climate goals, something he dared his successor to scrap.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's going to be politically costly and economically unsound, the next president to disrupt or cut.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Other parts of the Biden agenda have taken a hit. A Texas judge ruled Friday the Biden administration cannot sell unused border wall material. The incoming president called the decision a major crucial win for America.\par \par \uc2 President-elect Trump's border czar criticized the White House for selling components needed to finish the wall.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: They wait four years just before we take control to sell a main piece of our plan to secure that border.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Biden has told aides to spend as much as possible from the four massive spending packages he helped push through Congress. The president also vetoed a bill that would have added 66 new federal judges in a sign he wants to deny the Trump administration the ability to make more appointments.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports Biden has looked back on his time in office with some regrets. Quote: Biden has also said he should have picked someone other than \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  as attorney general, complaining about the Justice Department's slowness under Garland in prosecuting Trump and its aggressiveness in prosecuting Biden's son Hunter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): President Biden also regrets dropping out of the presidential race. That's also according to that "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " report, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Lucas Tomlinson reporting from St. Croix -- Lucas, thank you very much\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Missouri Senator \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to the show, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So let's start here with this fight of the H-1B visas, which has gotten really ugly on social media. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  obviously supports them, says the tech companies need them. He was an H-1B visa holder himself at one point.\par \par \uc2 In the middle of this fight, he's referred to people within the GOP who are hateful, unrepentant racists. In another post on X, he said this: Take a big step back and blank yourself in the face. I will go to war on this issue the likes of which you cannot possibly comprehend.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of this, we've got longtime Trump ally \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  who says the whole thing is a scam that's hurting American workers and he adds this: We are going to win this. They are going to surrender. They are not going to continue to destroy American citizens and American engineering by this scam, and this is a total let me repeat if you didn't get it. It's a total and complete scam.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Trump brought together a coalition of all kinds of groups and people and thinkers to win. How does he now manage them in the middle of a fight like this that's gone very public?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Well, first of all, I think it's important to acknowledge, this is a very important debate to have, and it's happening on X right now, which is a free and open platform. It's something that I fought for when I was attorney general of Missouri. We sued the Biden administration for their censorship regime.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think this is an important thoughtful debate that's happening, but the context that we need to I think keep in mind here is that the American workers have been left behind by this economy. Many factory jobs have been sent overseas and I think the abuses of the H-1B program have been evident, where you have sort of the sons and daughters of those factory workers lost their jobs, got white collar jobs as accountants and they are, you know, training their replacements, the foreign workers who are undercutting their wages (ph).\par \par \uc2 So I think the solution here, President Trump has actually articulated this in 2020 is to reform that system and, you know, get rid of the abuses, make it merit-based, and make sure that we're not undercutting wages, and having, you know, Americans replace their our -- train their foreign replacements.\par \par \uc2 And also keep in mind here that the big debate here, and the big issue that's coming is actually the border, and we have 15 million people here, Shannon, that are here illegally that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats have let in through an open borders policy, these people who were writing white papers for liberal think tanks, were writing policy for the United States of America and now, we have crime -- we have fentanyl, we have a national security risk.\par \par \uc2 So the first order business I think for the Trump administration is the deportations that are going to begin on day one, the executive orders that are coming. I do think President Trump has talked about reforming this system, the H-1B system. But the broader issue here is mass migration that has undercut jobs, wages and in increased housing costs.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's where the focus is going to be. That's what the mandate President Trump got was to secure our southern border and you heard Tom Homan talk about how he's going to go do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So I want to get to immigration more specifically, but before we leave this H-1B visa issue, "The New York Post" had a conversation with President Trump yesterday, and here's what they say.\par \par \uc2 They say he supports immigration visas for highly skilled workers appearing to side with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , quote, I have many H-1B visas on my properties. I've been a big believer -- a believer in H-1B. I have used it many times. It's a great program.\par \par \uc2 He's been critical of it in the past and to be fair, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " seems to question whether he was talking about these H-1B visas because they say he's used H-2B visas and H-2A on a lot of his Trump properties, but they didn't see use of the H-1B visa. So I'm not sure that that's been clarified yet, but he's been critical about this.\par \par \uc2 You talk about undercutting the American worker and that seems to be the biggest argument against that, is that people who are brought over as foreign workers are necessarily supplanting American workers who may be qualified, may want these jobs but the foreign workers are paid less.\par \par \uc2 So I want to talk through some possible legislative changes to this because the group that is the Federation for American Immigration has talked about things they'd want to see changed that possibly you and Congress could tackle -- not allowing the H-1B visa to be a pathway to citizenship, preventing employers from paying those foreign workers less than Americans in the same jobs, requiring proof from employers that they first sought and offered these positions to qualified Americans and that they want tougher enforcement mechanisms investigations and random audits.\par \par \uc2 Is that the kind of thing you think that your colleagues whether just you as Republicans or in a bipartisan way could get behind?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Well, I think there's been a lot of abuse and that's what's driving, Shannon, a lot of this discussion right now is the abuse that's happened with this where you see qualified Americans being denied a lot of these jobs because a company can go out and hire cheaper labor from a foreign country, right? That is really the abuse.\par \par \uc2 And so, the reforms that President Trump put forward in which by the way were cancelled by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- canceled by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- had to do with right now, it's a lottery. There's really no merit to this. It's just a lottery.\par \par \uc2 So you see companies trying to gain the system. IT companies sometimes putting 30,000 applications in, hoping to get 10,000 from a random lottery. That is a system I think that needs to be reformed. President Trump's talked about that.\par \par \uc2 And then also undercutting wages by bringing in somebody, you know, under a loophole for example, you can't have it be the same employer, but it's a consulting firm.\par \par \uc2 So that -- those are the kinds of things and I think the starting point that President Trump articulated when he wanted to reform this very broken system back in 2020 that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  nixed. He came in under the auspices of undoing everything that was President Trump, including the 94 executive orders he issued getting rid of "Remain in Mexico," getting -- you know, stopping the construction of the border wall, all the things that President Trump was trying to do to shore up the border and make sure we were putting American workers first.\par \par \uc2 And I think you're going to continue to see that that's the mandate that President Trump got. It's what he talked about on the -- on the campaign trail is we have to.\par \par \uc2 And, Shannon, one last point here -- this is a longer term conversation, too. We have to invest in American workers.\par \par \uc2 I think what people get frustrated by is they see $175 billion going to Ukraine, they see millions of dollars going to other countries to teach them about DEI, meanwhile our education system is completely failed in many ways, and there hasn't been a focus on the American worker.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's -- this multi-ethnic working-class party, the Republican Party is now, led by President Trump. I think there's a lot of ground to be made up here in putting American workers first.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. And so the point the broader conversation about immigration, the president has talked about putting a stop to illegal immigration, getting control of the southern border, but also deporting potentially millions of people who are already here.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something that your colleague, Senator \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2 , says about actually getting those people out of the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ALEX PADILLA (D-CA): So the next time we hear Republicans say that they support Trump's plan to deport waves -- waves of undocumented immigrants, let's be clear about what comes with that. Higher prices for American families, they're supporting the forced removal of millions of hardworking community members, 80 percent of whom have lived here 10 years or more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what do you say to critics who say this is going to tear apart existing families and it's actually going to hurt the U.S. economy by taking millions of workers out of the mix?\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: Well, I would point out to my friend from California that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  deported 17 million people, okay?\par \par \uc2 So the idea of deporting people here who are here illegally is not a new concept. In fact, the policy and the law of the United States of America since, you know, for 200 years is if you come here illegally, you are detained. If you don't have a valid reason, like asylum, and by the way, 9 out of 10 asylum claims are bogus, then you are deported. That is how we have always operated. Only recently, quite frankly, in the last four years when the radical left has taken over the White House, and these people do not believe in borders. They think they're arbitrary lines on a map, that we're all world citizens and everybody should be able to come here. They could put up in a luxury hotel in New York at the expense of taxpayers. They look the other way when an illegal immigrant sets a woman on fire at a subway. This is all part, and by the way, you don't need to be a citizen to be counted in the census.\par \par \uc2 This was the goal for them all along. The truth is, we have laws in this country, they're just not being enforced. So President Trump's going to come in, he's going to enforce those laws, bring back Remain in Mexico, which creates a disincentive for the cartels to traffic people because they're waiting -- you know, the waiting room is in Mexico, not here in the United States, where they're just released into the interior, never to be seen again, and given a court date 10 years from now.\par \par \uc2 That's what's happening right now, Shannon. That's the system that's totally broken. So President Trump is actually just going to enforce existing laws and deport people who are here illegally.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that's coming on day one. I think you're going to see a flurry of executive orders to that point, and the American people want that. You see that among Hispanic voters, you see that among black voters, you see that among white voters. Every socioeconomic group supported that. The exit polling supports that. That's why President Trump won. He was very clear about this, and I think it's a big important part about his mandate that he received.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and we saw that in our Fox News voter analysis, as you mentioned as well. We know there's an ambitious agenda ahead for those first 100 days. We know there are confirmation battles to come that you'll be in the middle of as well.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, we hope you'll come back. In the meantime, Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: We'll do it. Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, up next, we're going to look ahead to the new Congress from the other side of the aisle. Congressmen \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , a Democrat who represents Silicon Valley, weighs in on this fight over importing high-tech workers as the America First Coalition vows to fight back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (I-WV): I am not a Democrat. In the form of what the Democratic Party has turned itself into, the national brand? Absolutely not. The brand got so bad, the "D-brand" has been so maligned from the standpoint of, it's just, it's toxic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Retiring West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  with some tough talk for the Democrat Party as he wraps up a decades-long career as one of the strongest centrist voices in the upper chamber.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to react, California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . Welcome back. Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Thank you, Shannon. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to get to that because you have also been vocal about what the party needs to do as sort of some introspection post-election. But I got to get to you because you represent Silicon Valley on this H-1B visa issue that I talked about with Senator Schmitt. It's gotten really ugly on social media, and part of that has gotten into culture.\par \par \uc2 I want to read part of what \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  posted on X in talking about this. He says, "A key part of it comes down to the c-word: Culture. Our American culture has venerated mediocrity over excellence for way too long. A culture that celebrates the prom queen over the math Olympiad champ, or the jock over the valedictorian, will not produce the best engineers."\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , former ambassador, responded to that saying, "There's nothing wrong with American workers or American culture. All you have to do is look at the border and see how many want what we have. We should be investing and prioritizing in Americans, not foreign workers."\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this culture argument and the need to invest more in American workers, American students, to make sure they are up to taking these jobs and there won't be as great a need to look overseas?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, American culture is extraordinary, and I respectfully disagree with Vivek. You know, I was a valedictorian in my high school, and they respected valedictorians and people who were exceptional at football and music. And what makes American culture extraordinary is that we celebrate the talent and genius and skills of people of diverse backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 There's a reason that America leads in Nobel laureates in physics and in medicine and in chemistry. If there was some problem with the culture, we wouldn't be the world's greatest economy of $30 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But one of the points that I think Elon and others are making is what makes America as exceptional is that we are a magnet for the world's talent. It's great that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  comes here. It's great that Jensen, who started \uc2 NVIDIA\uc2 , is doing it here and not in Taiwan. We made a mistake when we lost Morris Chang, who was an immigrant here, educated at MIT. He leaves the United States, and guess what? He starts Taiwan Semiconductors, and now we don't have semiconductors in the United States. So we want the best talent coming here while reforming the abuses of the H-1B program.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about that, because critics will say these aren't always people with specialized, genius-level technology. There are people who are doing jobs that could go to Americans. \uc2 Neil Patel\uc2  of "The Daily Caller" wrote a really lengthy post. People can check it out on X. I'm just going to read part of it. He says that big tech is the only group that actually benefits here. It's not the foreign worker. It's not the American worker. He says, "The system keeps many of these workers in a legal limbo often for decades. Workers tied down like this are less able to negotiate salary and benefits. That's the main reason big tech loves hiring H-1Bs. American workers, who then have to compete with these foreign workers, are thereby hurt by unfair labor conditions."\par \par \uc2 He says, "The system is broken. It needs major reform." And if you don't see that, either you don't understand what's happening or you're not being truthful. So let's talk about where you think there is room for reform.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, Neil is largely right on the need for reform. I have a bipartisan bill, which we introduced with former Representative Dave Brat. Today, \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , \uc2 Frank Pallone\uc2 , and I are on it.\par \par \uc2 And basically, it would say you can't underpay these H-1B folks coming in. And you shouldn't have lower-skilled things that could be done by American workers. You shouldn't have that kind of category in the United States. It shouldn't be for accountants or entry-level I.T. jobs. It should really be for exceptional talent. And we can have that balance. And I hope President Trump actually looks at that bipartisan bill that would reform this.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Senator Schmitt said that America's towns have been hollowed out. And he's absolutely right. And workers have gotten a raw deal because jobs have been offshored. Let's just be honest about this H-1B program. We're talking about 85,000 people. Sometimes it goes up to 200,000 because there are too many exemptions.\par \par \uc2 But the real challenge in America is, how did we lose steel? How did we lose aluminum? How did we allow for deindustrialization? You want to bring back new jobs, you need to have the investment in reindustrialization of America and places left out. And that's what we should be talking about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so with Elon and Vivek are not talking about this H-1B visa program and getting into these epic fights on social media, they're now about the Department of Government Efficiency, or DOGE as people are calling it. Where do you think you or other Democrats could work with them on cutting government waste?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, I've gotten some criticism on my side because I said I'm open to working to cut programs that are not competitive. For example, the defense budget, 56% of discretionary budget, it's dominated by five primes. And Musk is saying that maybe we can disrupt that, have new competition so that we actually have a better national security strategy without all of the waste.\par \par \uc2 If there are real proposals to do that, then Democrats should work with him. If there are actually duplicative agencies that aren't efficient on taxpayer dollars, then we should work with him.\par \par \uc2 What I will not do is support any cuts to Social Security, any cuts to Medicare, any cuts to veteran benefits, any cuts to education programs that parents rely on. But there is no doubt that there's waste and where there is waste, we should work at putting the country first.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's get back to your reflections. You wrote some tough truths to your party after the loss in November of the White House, House and Senate. You said essentially they weren't listening to people where they are suffering pain. You said you've got to get to common sense on things like crime and safety and those kinds of issues. How has your analysis been received internally? Do you feel like Democrats have a path forward?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Absolutely we have a path forward, but we need generational change. Look, people are upset with the system. They're upset that too many billionaires are spending money on our elections. They're upset that politicians offshore jobs and lost industry for decades. They're upset that health care is being denied to too many Americans. And while this is going on, you have some of the people in our party have been in Congress for 20, 30 years and they're not change agents.\par \par \uc2 We need a new generation to step up. We need people who were part of the failed status quo to step aside. And I think the vibrancy of the House Democratic Caucus where you have new generational leaders and some in the Senate is what's going to revitalize this party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So there was a piece in the "Hill" as you get ready to come back here swearing in the new Congress, got to elect a speaker, do all these things. This piece in the "Hill" by two attorneys said that Congress needs to block President Trump from being certified as the winner. They said, "Democrats need to take a stand against electoral college votes for a person disqualified by the Constitution from holding office." And they have arguments about that. "No less is required by their oath to support and defend the Constitution."\par \par \uc2 Now in 2016, a number of Democrats did push back against certifying the first win by President Trump. What do you expect this time around?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: We're going to certify the election. I plan to show up to the inauguration. It's not about President Trump. It's about honoring the American people. And we've got to get past this thing that everything has to be partisan.\par \par \uc2 You know, when I put forward a proposal of common sense and H-1B reform, I had people on my side say, why are you doing that, Ro? Let the Republicans fight. Let them destroy each other fighting. That's the wrong approach. We're Americans first. We've got to be solution-oriented. The American people just elected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I worked my heart out to oppose him. I went to every state, but we lost the election. We respect the American people. And now let's think about how we have a better message going forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let me get you to respond to Jen Rubin, who writes an opinion column over at the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." She says Democrats should not try to find common ground with Trump. She says "A depressingly high number of elected Democrats are declaring their intent to find, quote, 'common ground' with President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his,' as she calls it, 'crackpot Cabinet picks. Their naive, tone-deaf declarations epitomize an infatuation with bipartisanship for bipartisanship's sake." She says you shouldn't be doing it. Your response?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: We need to look at it issue by issue. No one is saying that we should work with Republicans or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if there's any proposal to cut Social Security or if there is a proposal to politicize the Justice Department or if there is a proposal to abolish the Department of Education. Of course, Democrats will oppose that with our values.\par \par \uc2 But if the President has actually a good idea to bring manufacturing back or to cut wasteful defense spending or to make our food system safer and get the additives out of our food, why wouldn't we want to work on things that are good for the American people? This is the part of politics that folks are sick about that we're always looking at things from a partisan lens as opposed to what's good for America. And what they want is change agents on the Republican side and the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. Our numbers showed people desperately want to change in big numbers, more than 80% saying they want complete upheaval or significant change. So there's definitely a theme there.\par \par \uc2 All right, Congressman, we'll see you back here in the New Year. Thank you always for your time and Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you. Happy New Year to you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, disorder in the court. That's what many now fear after President Biden vetoed a bill that would have added 66 new federal judges to an understaffed judiciary. Our panel reacts to critics' claims that the move is blatantly partisan and it's aimed at hindering President Trump, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN, SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, THE GRIO: It's simple. The president, this president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , did not want to give President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a chance to add more conservativism into our courts, bottom line.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So President Biden vetoing bipartisan legislation with just weeks left in office, that measure would have added 66 judges to what lawmakers, in a bipartisan way, had argued is a desperately understaffed federal court system. Critics call the move blatantly political.\par \par \uc2 Let's see what our panel thinks. Time for our Sunday group. "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers. "Axios" political -- politics reporter and co-author of the "Axios" Hill letters -- Leaders newsletter. I will get this right, Stef Kight. Philippe Reines, former Deputy Assistant Secretary of State and Senior Advisor to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . And syndicated columnist, Cal Thomas. Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 OK. So let's start with this bill. President Biden said this about why he vetoed it. He said it would create new judgeships in states where senators have sought to hold open existing judicial vacancies. Those efforts to hold open vacancies suggest that concerns about judicial economy and caseload are not the true motivating force behind passage of this bill now, Francesca.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : This has turned into an issue where both parties are accusing the other one of playing politics. Before the election, the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  passed this unanimously, but then the House didn't take it up for a vote until after the election. So now you have Democrats saying that their House Republicans only wanted to vote for it because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had won the election and he would get to appoint a significant number of these judges into the role, whereas you have Republicans accusing Democrats of playing politics because House Democrats didn't want to vote for it now and then Biden ended up issuing a veto of this. So all around, you have lawmakers saying that the other side is at fault for this issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2 , Democrat senator, said this, he's one of the big sponsors of it. I'm disappointed by the outcome for my own state and for the federal judges throughout the country struggling under the burden of ever-higher caseloads. I've worked on this bill for years and thanks to tireless bipartisan effort with Senator Young it made it to the president's desk.\par \par \uc2 So, Cal, he's saying, we wanted to get this done in a bipartisan way, whoever was going to be the next president.\par \par \uc2 CAL THOMAS, SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: Well, he was right, of course. But look at what Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did at the beginning of the Biden administration. They had this clandestine meeting and came up with a plan to create as many federal judgeships with judges of a liberal constitutional view as possible.\par \par \uc2 Now we have this bill that passed, as you said, unanimously, supported by not only \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  but Senator \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  of Indiana, and now it's flipped on the other side. And who knew that Washington played politics? It's just amazing to me.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats, for years, really beginning since prayer in the public schools and bible reading and Roe versus Wade have used the federal judges in this country to get policies enacted that they knew they couldn't get through congress so both sides play this game and it's too bad.\par \par \uc2 But this deal was a real deal and I think \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  and Senator Young deserve a lot of credit even though it's now been vetoed by the president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. Well, I mean these judicial nominations and confirmations last way beyond any presidential term or two. So they have a ton of impact.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley weighed on -- weighed in on this as well. He called it the move by Biden a disgrace. He says, our courts are overwhelmed by dockets that leave parties without any resolution for years.\par \par \uc2 In 2004, the number of cases in district court pending for more than three years was 18,280. This year, there are nearly 82,000 cases, Stef, that had been pending for more than three years.\par \par \uc2 It takes a lot to get these cases moving. Do you foresee maybe this effort being taken up again under a Trump first -- well, it's his second term, but a new term for him?\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICS REPORTER, AXIOS: I could see this be taken up again. You know, judges are a very important issue for both parties as we've discussed. You know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was the one who kind of wrote the playbook for how to push through as many judges as possible in a single term. And \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  only recently surpassed that record in a single term just a week or two ago.\par \par \uc2 These are important positions. Like you said, they are long term. They're not just for four years. And both parties have an interest in getting these through.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at this actual piece of legislation, it was crafted in a bipartisan way, not giving all 66 of these new judges to any particular administration to fill. It would have spread it out over multiple administrations, giving both parties chances at filling some of those.\par \par \uc2 So I could see movement on this. Obviously, this is not necessarily the number one priority for Trump. He has a list of policies that he wants to push for, the border, you know, energy policies, et cetera. So this may not be one of the first things we see get done, but it's clear that there's real bipartisan support, and especially when you look at the Senate, they're going to need bipartisan issues that they can push through.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Philippe?\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPE REINES, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, I mean, first to Cal's point, I think that Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , 10 years into his term, would probably --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 REINES: -- disagree about.\par \par \uc2 But I do think, with all respect to the senators, it was naive to think you can remove politics from this topic in particular, judgeships.\par \par \uc2 If we need another 66 or whatever it is, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can add it to his day one agenda, which I know is already crowded with about 100 other things, but he's controlling Congress and the White House, they can sign that in.\par \par \uc2 Not to stand on ceremony, Francesca alluded to it. The point, the theory behind removing the politics of it was to get the House and the Senate to approve this in August. Mike Johnson didn't. I don't know if that's because he didn't have the confidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would win.\par \par \uc2 But in any event, this was not -- apparently this is not the way to get politics out of judgeships. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has got another bite of the apple in about a month.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 OK. So speaking of confidence, this is the headline from "Axios," the Trump effect, CEO and investors bullish on global economy survey finds. They say President-elect Trump's election victory has produced soaring confidence in the global economy, according to a new survey, an outlook that outweighs concerns about tariffs, trade barriers and geopolitical tensions.\par \par \uc2 And, Cal, that's very different than some of the predictions we heard about his impact pre-election, should he win.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Yes. Well, of course, I remember \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2 , the now former columnist for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," predicting that if Trump were elected in 2016, there would be a depression. Well, never mind.\par \par \uc2 I think the confidence is warranted if Trump maintains the tax cuts, but mostly if Ramaswamy and Musk get that $2 trillion in spending reduction. But even that is only a drop in the bucket in a $36 trillion debt, where interest on that debt this year alone is $1 trillion. We got to cut the spending. No nation has ever been able to survive with this level of debt.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And, Stef, it's Republicans and Democrats who have contributed to that spending over the years.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: It's true and it tends to kind of switch which party cares more about cutting spending depending on who has power at the time. We have already seen that fight kind of play out in Congress already where we have on one hand conservatives in the House demanding that there be significant spending cuts included in any kind of spending deal.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the president-elect is asking for the debt ceiling to be eliminated. We're already seeing that tension within the Republican Party play out. And I'm sure we've not seen the end of that fight moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So as to these predictions, "Axios" had Goldman back in September, pre-election saying that if he won again, it would result in a half percentage point drag on GDP in the second half of 2025. They went on to note such predictions did not age well during Trump's first term when the economy experienced steady growth, historically low unemployment and benign inflation, even amid trade wars.\par \par \uc2 This is now \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , post-election this time around. The U.S. economy is poised to beat expectations in 2025, Philippe.\par \par \uc2 REINES: Well, look, I mean, I hope that, you know, naysayers who believe that things are going down are wrong, including anything that I said about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to ruin the economy. I'm part of the economy. It's not in my best interest.\par \par \uc2 But this is anticipatory. And, you know, I'm sure if the Fortune 500 CEOs of Washington, of course they feel good. They feel good about tax cuts that are coming their way that are going to benefit them.\par \par \uc2 But I wrote down a few things. Ten of the last 11 recessions dating back to 1953 have been Republicans. The GDP has risen 3.9 percent under Democrats versus 2.4 percent under Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 has risen 11.2 percent versus 6.9 percent under Republicans. My favorite, unemployment. Seventy-two percent of the jobs since World War II have been under democratic presidents. Seventy million over seven presidents.\par \par \uc2 Now, these numbers of what's happened, this is history. This is not people guessing. The American economy has done better under Democrats for whatever reason, which we could probably debate. Cal might not agree with me about why.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: A lot of (INAUDIBLE) too.\par \par \uc2 REINES: But they have done better. If it's about CEOs looking forward to their tax cuts, or if it's about what Democrats have done for America, I will go for this every single time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. But quickly, Francesca, voters felt like the economy was not working under this administration.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: But there's quite a few unknowns in the Trump administration of what he -- of what he will end up doing. He said that he'll put tariffs on a number of countries, but those are believed to be a negotiating tactic. How many of those will he actually enact? We talk about the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. In what order will he pursue these policies?\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers want to do the border and immigration first before they even get to that. Will he cut corporate tax, the corporate tax rate for companies that produce their manufacturing in the United States of America?\par \par \uc2 So there's so many question marks about what order and combination of the economic policies in terms of what the economic analysis is.\par \par \uc2 REINES: I can tell you one thing he won't do is, I mean, he said that he would end inflation on day one. And I'm no economist --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 REINES: But that's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. But non-economist Philippe as well.\par \par \uc2 But we got a lot more to talk about with the panel, so don't go far. We're going to get to your winners and losers as we say goodbye to '24 and your predictions for '25. Maybe on the economy from Philippe, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY HARRIS (R-MD): Before the last couple of weeks I was, you know, in this corner. But now I'm - you know, we should - we should consider, what's the best path forward.\par \par \uc2 We do need to consider whether if we're going to advance Mr. Trump's agenda whether the current leadership is what we need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, House Freedom Caucus Chair Andy Harris on whether current House Speaker Mike Johnson survives the upcoming speaker vote. And he's not the only Republican voicing concerns and hesitation.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 So, we wait to see whether Speaker Johnson is a big winner are loser in this year, or in the new year. But we want to get to your political winners and losers of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Would you like to start us off, Philippe, with your winner?\par \par \uc2 REINES: I think the state of Israel. They basically start out as, quote, mowing the lawn. And they just basically paved a parking lot over \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Iran itself. And this might sound boring, but they did us, the United States, a lot of good. These are the two worst terrorist organizations in the - in the Middle East. And they do Iran's bidding. Iran themselves looked kind of stupid shooting 300 missiles off and 300 being shot down. And I think that's great.\par \par \uc2 The flipside of that is, Iran's the biggest loser. We've had, you know, Iran, North Korea, Russia are our three, you know, axis of whatever these days. Russia is looking pretty bad. They've been marred in the same war for three years. And the United States is basically winning two proxy wars without losing a single life and a fair amount of low-cost to our Treasury. So -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and to - and to watch how Israel patiently scripted some of those attacks years in advance was a pretty stunning thing to watch. OK, so, and your loser is Iran.\par \par \uc2 All right, Stef, your winner, \uc2 John Thune\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: My winner was \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who, you know, pretty shortly after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  announced that he would be stepping down from his role as Senate Republican leader, Thune became the considered favorite. But he did survive, you know, two challenges from the other Johns, also \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who got a lot of momentum at the end from people very close to the president-elect. There were concerns that Trump may weigh in against \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , but he seems to have at least made amends well enough that they appear to be on good terms. He's moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And, right now, three seats feels quite roomy in Congress and he's going to have a little bit of wiggle room when he has the majority come January. And so I - you know, it's rare that the leading contender actually ends up winning these days. And so, the fact that he was - he managed to keep the party in line, win (INAUDIBLE) at the end of the day makes I think makes him a winner.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and then he was given an interesting slate of people to get through the confirmation process as - as the prize for winning.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: He was. So, he's avoiding - though the \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  fiasco ended pretty quickly. So he, at least, won't have to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That one's done. OK, your loser is the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes, a little bit of a cop-out, but they've had a rough year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Philippe's face when I said that, he was not very excited about that.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: They've had a rough year, you know, from the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  health concern, the age concerns, the fact, you know, we all watched the debate. And even at the beginning of the year, they were coming off of record illegal border crossings and grappling with how they're going to respond to this issue. That was becoming the issue in the election cycle. They never really got a firm handle on how he respond to that issue politically. They had the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  debate. They have the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  accension, only to have her lose resoundingly in every single battleground state. And now you're seeing this - this tension and the vision play out on television screens with progressives saying that they're the ones who the party should be listening to, and moderates saying, no, we need to continue moving right and embrace more of these more conservative policies to win over the American people. And it's just very hard to see who their leader is right now moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Cal, your winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Well, I think the biggest loser, to play off Stef's Democratic Party is the media. Same thing. Sorry to repeat myself. They covered up for Biden's mental acuity decline, they covered up on the - on the Hunter Biden laptop, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and other social media covered up on "The New York Post" story about the Hunter Biden laptop, the 51 intelligence agencies who, the Russia, Russia, Russia things, signed off saying it was true. The media followed all of this. And I think it's a disgrace to the profession that I've been a part of for a half a century. And I wish we'd get some new blood in the media to actually practice journalism instead of practicing politics.\par \par \uc2 Now my winner is outgoing Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia. A true independent who puts his country first, his party second and himself third. I think he's gone out with great class and dignity. And I salute him for that. I wish we had more of him. Maybe it will cause me to rethink my position on cloning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Or running yourself.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Or running your - no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Francesca, winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, my - my runner-up actually on the winner was the Dakotas, but you took \uc2 John Thune\uc2  there. Well, because they have had a real run of it in Trump's upcoming cabinet. You look at \uc2 John Thune\uc2 's new position, as well as the fact that you have \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  and \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  getting positions.\par \par \uc2 But I did go with Florida, Shannon, partially in honor of you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you. You go with my home state.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: I know - I know that you would - that you would appreciate the Florida - the Florida love here.\par \par \uc2 But, no, but, really, when you look at the people that he's been picking, you've got Susie Wiles as White House chief of staff, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , the Florida senator, as secretary of State, his incoming national security advisor, Michael Waltz, is also from Florida. You also have \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2 , who's now going to be the chair of the House foreign affairs committee. So, a lot of Florida politicians (INAUDIBLE) under - under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REINES: Don't forget \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Retired.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, actually, I did forget Pam Bondi. Pam Bondi, who's (INAUDIBLE) for attorney general.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Pam Bondi, you're right.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: So, lots of love for - for Florida in the incoming Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And then, as the loser, not to be to trite, as well, Stephanie, I picked \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I covered the White House, covered the Harris campaign. She - not because she lost the election per say, but she was handed a losing campaign. She had three months to try and turn it around. Regardless of the things that she did during that campaign to contribute it, whether it be the comments that she made on "The View" and not distancing herself from Biden, or not focusing on the economy, as voters felt closer to the end of the election, she - she was handed a three-month time frame in order to be able to try and do a lot of things and she but couldn't get it done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we literally have seconds left, so I want you each to give me a one sentence on your prediction for 2025. We'll work back around.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Executive orders.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lots of them from Trump.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Lots of executive orders from Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Taylor Swift and Travis Kelce get engaged, and maybe married.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: And maybe married as soon as the lawyers can finish the prenup.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, that one may take a while. Details, details.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I Love it.\par \par \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's 2028 star starts to rise.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh. He's right around the corner.\par \par \uc2 REINES: 2025 looks a lot like 2017.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Meaning?\par \par \uc2 REINES: Meaning, you know, a lot of dysfunction in 2017. If you don't learn from history, you repeat it.\par \par \uc2 But, more importantly, I think that your ratings continue to skyrocket. We continue to -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, guys, (INAUDIBLE) with continued appearances from all of you.\par \par \uc2 REINES: "Livin the Bream."\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: I hope it looks like 2024 in terms of the Chiefs' Super Bowl championship.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We've got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: So do I. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Panel, thank you very much. Happy New Year to all of you.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight marks the fifth night of Hanukkah. We're going to show you how the Museum of the Bible here in D.C. is sharing a message of light and honoring the remarkable history and tradition of the Jewish people, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Today marks the fifth nights of Hanukkah. And last week we showed you how the Museum of the Bible here in Washington is highlighting the history of faith this season. That includes priceless artifacts that tell the story of the unwavering resilience of the Jewish people. It is our Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RABBI MOSHE ENGLANDER, TORAH SCRIBE: I'm a - just a little link in that unbroken chain going all the way back 3,336 years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): You might be surprised to learn that one of the many educational resources at the Museum of the Bible is the rabbi, who specializes in writing the sacred words of the Torah.\par \par \uc2 ENGLANDER: The reason we are still writing it by hand is because I, as a Torah scribe, have to have a thought process that every letter that I'm writing is the word of God himself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But inside these walls the history of religious tradition and its impact through the ages is an experience that's shared across many faiths, particularly during this festive time of year.\par \par \uc2 ENGLANDER: We also have to understand also that the foundation is the same for everybody who embraces the Bible. The foundation is the same. And it all started right there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it takes us through so much of what the Jewish people suffered, where they triumphed. I mean Exodus, all these different things, and - and leads us to the season we're now celebrating, Hanukkah, as well.\par \par \uc2 ENGLANDER: Correct. And they're all tied in together.\par \par \uc2 The Torah is the DNA of the world and the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): The museum's collection of Judaic exhibits includes rare editions of the Hebrew Bible, along with its crown jewel, of the newly unveiled Afghan Liturgical Quire, or prayer book, which dates all the way back to the 8th century.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT DUKE, CHIEF CURATOR, MUSEUM OF THE BIBLE: It is the earliest Jewish prayer book in a book format that was found in the modern city of Afghanistan, but it really is this Jewish treasurer. Those of the Jewish faith that have come here are just stunned and they're amazed that this is - this treasure is here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Next year, another treasured artifacts of the Jewish faith is set to make its home at the museum, the Dead Sea Scrolls. We got a look at the ancient manuscripts when FOX NEWS SUNDAY visited the Reagan Library earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 DAVID TRULIO, PRESIDENT, REAGAN PRESIDENTIAL FOUNDATION AND INSTITUTE: The scrolls themselves are the oldest known manuscript of the Hebrew Bible, or what Christians might call the Old Testament. This is the Psalm scroll. It is one of the most intact of the scrolls. And you can see, there it is in its completeness, only slight damage on the fridges. This contains psalms that didn't make it to the Book of Psalms but speak about King David.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): For now, visitors can experience the rich history of Judaism through the museum's thousands of current exhibits just in time for Hanukkah.\par \par \uc2 DUKE: Hanukkah is about the - the freedom that the Maccabees (ph) brought when they brought new light back to the temple. And in some ways we're in a world right now that needs new light, but even this can give hope that maybe someday we can get back to that concept of light being all around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Absolutely remarkable pieces of history over there at the Museum of the Bible.\par \par \uc2 Happy Hanukkah to everyone who is celebrating tonight and beyond.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream, wishing you and yours a happy and healthy 2025. It's going to be great.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you next week and next year for FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020241229ekct00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Strike; Stock Market Outlook for 2025?; President Biden Drops Student Loan Bailout Plans; Drone Drama; New York City Subway Murder Investigation; Holiday Travel Rush\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Acuna, Gary Kaltbaum, Nate Foy, Paul Mauro, Alexis McAdams,  Jeff Flock, Danamarie McNicholl, Kiyana Lewis, Edward Lawrence \par \uc2 8,238 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Get there way earlier than you think you need to, because there's probably going to be a long line.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Obviously, it's Christmas. You have got children. What's the strategy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We don't have one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Making sure you're on time. I think that's the best, because you never know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Last night, they told us that we're going somewhere, like, special and a surprise. It was a present for...\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The holidays.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we don't know where we're going, but they say it's really cool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It's really cool. The travel rush is on, millions of Americans making their way to holiday destinations by plane and by car.\par \par \uc2 And we're on top of all of it with FOX News team coverage. Danamarie McNicholl is in Atlanta on how fliers are facing, FOX Business' Jeff Flock in Pennsylvania on the Turnpike on how drivers are dealing, and FOX Weather's Kiyana Lewis on how winter storms could be impacting those travel plans.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. This is "Your World." I'm Edward Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to this, Danamarie McNicholl at \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So what's it look like there?\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Edward.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, the good weather here in Atlanta and short \uc2 TSA\uc2  lines has led to a relatively smooth experience for most travelers, but, for people in Texas, that's not the same story. Let's take a look at the MiseryMap, as we call it.\par \par \uc2 You can see across the nation around 3,900 delays with a majority of them actually coming from both Dallas Love Field and Dallas-Fort Worth airports, where, there, the average delay is almost four hours. They faced a double whammy.\par \par \uc2 First, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  issued a four-hour-long nationwide ground stop because of a vendor technology issue and then a second ground stop just this afternoon because of thunderstorms. One traveler we spoke to here was preparing for the worst today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Atlanta's airport has a reputation, from what I have heard, for somebody that is not -- that's not familiar with the airport and on the other side, it's a big airport.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I missed my flight last night. Wasn't by fault.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: Well, this year, we finally surpassed pre-COVID travel numbers. For the first time, there are more people on the road and in the air than in 2019 -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thanks, Danamarie.\par \par \uc2 So it's not just the airports that are seeing that surge, the nation's highways also jammed. But there's a silver lining.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Business' Jeff Flock reporting from along the Pennsylvania Turnpike.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Well, Edward, we did have some weather earlier today on the Jersey and Pennsylvania turnpikes, snow, sleet, freezing rain, but as maybe you can tell right now, all clear.\par \par \uc2 If you look out the windchill, 12 lanes of the Jersey Turnpike and all of them moving at or close to posted speeds, as the traffic guys tend to say. And that is good news, and particularly interesting news, given the fact that 107 million people are said to be traveling more than 50 miles this Christmas holiday.\par \par \uc2 That is very near to an all-time record, pre-pandemic record. And it is an all-time record when it comes to air travel, almost eight million people, and when it comes to travel on, well, trains, buses and cruise ships, which are increasingly popular.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of increasingly popular, that's an increase of last year, over last year by about 2.5 percent when it comes to cars, almost 5 percent when it comes to air travel, and almost 10 percent when it comes to other modes of travel, particularly cruise ships.\par \par \uc2 And despite the costs, costs are up too, but, according to AAA, people all too happy to spend whatever it takes to get where they want to go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are 64,000 higher travelers versus 2019 this holiday season. And I think it's because people have made a conscious decision that they are going to do those bucket list trips. They are going to go see their families.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: The other piece of good news when it comes to traveling on the road, gas prices, not too high, not too low, stable, though. And so you know what you're dealing with, $3.04 the average gallon of regular today. That's about what it was a week ago, about what it was a month ago, and, well, not far off what it was a year ago, so stability when it comes to gas prices and clear sailing when it comes to the roads.\par \par \uc2 I say goodbye to you and a merry Christmas from the Jersey Turnpike with 12 lanes of traffic moving smoothly -- Edward, back to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Moving smoothly in his green shirt and red tie.\par \par \uc2 Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 So, the Northeast seeing a bit of snow earlier today. We got ice pellets here in the D.C. area. Let's see what this -- or if this signals a white Christmas anywhere.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Weather's Kiyana Lewis.\par \par \uc2 So, Kiyana, where will Santa be dealing with that snow?\par \par \uc2 KIYANA LEWIS, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Hey, Edward.\par \par \uc2 I think he will definitely be seeing the snow a little farther to the north of New York. Could we still classify this as a snow white Christmas? I think we have a good chance. We got about an inch of snow earlier today. And if we keep that inch of snow, along with the inch of snow we had from this weekend on the ground, we could classify that as a white Christmas.\par \par \uc2 So we will have to go ahead and see. Most of the active weather has gone ahead and cleared the coastline. As I mentioned, back towards the New England region is where we have really seen some of the heaviest totals, eight inches in areas like Vermont, almost a foot in other areas like Maine here, Parkman, for example, a foot of snow, out towards Wellington, about 10 inches there.\par \par \uc2 So they certainly do have a white Christmas across the board. But here in the New York area and the New York region, all the way down to D.C., where you are, Edward, I think that it's going to be slim pickings. New York for sure may see it, D.C. not so much.\par \par \uc2 We got some icy weather that slowed things down on the roadways and even up in the airways earlier in the day. Now we're starting to see that pick back up. Here's where we do see that better chance of a white Christmas, again, across the northern tier of the country, all the way out across the Upper Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Christmas Day forecast looks like this. We do have a lot of sunshine and a lot of areas across the country, New York City sunshine, with temperatures back into the mid-30s, a little bit of rain through the midsections of the country. Snow back out across the parts of the West Coast, where we have an atmospheric river that has been just pummeling the coastline.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, across the Northern Rockies, back towards, say, the upper tier of the country and back into the Northern Plains, we have seen some good chances of snow, Cascades down through the Sierra Nevada too getting in on all of that snow, making for another opportunity of a white Christmas.\par \par \uc2 We will see some rain back into the forecast after the holiday heading into Thursday and the weekend. And that may slow folks down as they're heading home from their Christmas destinations. All that snow, though, it really starts to pile up. We could see two feet of snow in some areas, especially all the way down towards, say, the Sierra Nevada heading into the weekend - - Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, Kiyana, I will take the 64 degrees in Los Angeles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: I will tell you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 So, if you're headed out to the airport, what are some of the do's and don'ts?\par \par \uc2 Now, with me now is travel expert \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 TSA\uc2  says that the number one delay slowing screening at security checkpoints is the prohibited items in carry-on bags. Now, we all know about that forgotten water bottle. But, Gabe, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  says that one of the don'ts is wrapped gifts.\par \par \uc2 Why?\par \par \uc2 GABE SAGLIE, TRAVEL EXPERT: That's right.\par \par \uc2 Yes, because if it looks suspicious, Ed, they're going to open it up. They're going to unwrap. All those minutes you spent wrapping that beautiful gift are going to be gone. They're going to completely open that gift in front of everybody else.\par \par \uc2 So the idea is to opt for gift bags, opt for boxes, or just wait to wrap those gifts on the other side of your flight. But, actually, that happened. The best idea is to just not wrap your gifts if you're taking them on the plane.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, another one's what I alluded to, liquids in your carry-on bag. You can carry some, but the problem is a lot of that, right?\par \par \uc2 So, Gabe, I know you love the eggnog. The \uc2 TSA\uc2  says that they have rules about food, so grandma's fruitcake might be OK as a carry-on. But the line is at spreadable foods, right, when you talk about liquids?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Yes. If it can be sprayed, squeezed, or spilled, you have got to check it. Or it's got to fit in one of those 3.4-ounce containers. So even 3.4 ounces of eggnog, Ed, you can take with you on the plane. So that's a little treat, just sort of anticipating what waits for you on the other side of your flight.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: But, yes, those -- that 3/1/1 rule, the liquids in the plastic baggy rule, if you haven't flown in a little while, what we're seeing obviously during the holiday season, this is the only time that many folks are flying. Maybe they haven't flown since Christmas of last year.\par \par \uc2 So it's a good reminder that 3/1/1 rule is in effect, one relatively small- sized baggy and no liquids inside a container that's more than 3.4 ounces.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Gabe, this is the first holiday season since the Transportation Department rule requiring airlines to give automatic cash refunds for canceled or significantly delayed flights.\par \par \uc2 But, in many cases, the consumers this year had to ask for those refunds. What do people need to know about this?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: So, yes, that's a set of rules put forth in April, and that applies to things like your checked luggage, your Wi-Fi on board. If it doesn't work, you're entitled to a refund there. Seats that you upgraded because you wanted to be a little bit further up in the cabin, but mostly certainly for those flights, if they get canceled.\par \par \uc2 Even if you bought a nonrefundable ticket, and even if it's due to weather, you're now entitled to a cash refund. Now, preemptively, obviously, these airlines are going to offer you an attractive credit. They're going to try to get you on that next flight, but you do have the right to say, you know what, I don't want to travel any longer. If you cancel my flight, you are entitled to that refund.\par \par \uc2 The tricky thing, of course, Ed, is, during the holidays, we are looking to get to that dinner or back home after that holiday celebration.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: So it's a little tougher to simply say, no, I'm sticking around for an extra couple of days.\par \par \uc2 But that rule is in effect now. And, in fact, I think a lot of folks are going to be learning about it during this holiday season because it's been put into effect already, as we know, today.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 What's the definition of significant delay then? Is it an hour, two hours, three hours? What is it?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: So, with delays, it's -- we're looking at three hours if it's domestic flights, six or more hours if it's international flights.\par \par \uc2 When it -- for checked baggage, if your bags are delayed by more than 12 hours after you have landed or 15 to 30 hours after you have landed from an international flight, you're entitled to those checked fees -- checked bag fees to be refunded to you, even if your bags eventually get to you outside of those windows.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2 , good tips. Thank you very much. Appreciate it. And watching out for our money too.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Merry Christmas, Ed. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 So, and to a group that isn't going anywhere, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers not budging as that strike is expanding. What they're demanding and the implications coming up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we're learning more about the immigrant accused of setting a woman on fire on a New York City subway, including why he was hospitalized today.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams with a preview.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this story is horrendous, especially (AUDIO GAP) latest information this migrant who police say lit a woman on fire and then fanned those flames with his jacket -- coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: The Guatemalan migrant arrested for setting a sleeping woman on fire on a New York subway train arraigned today.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn now to FOX News' Alexis McAdams with the latest -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Hey, Ed.\par \par \uc2 This is a horrible story. I mean, people watching at home are probably thinking, what else is going to happen on these subways in New York City? The migrant accused of lighting a woman on fire and watching her burn alive was arrested with that lighter in his pocket, I'm told. And he was in court this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 You're looking at him here. It's Sebastian Zapeta. He's now charged with first-degree murder and arson, the migrant sitting in front of a judge today out in Brooklyn after investigators say he used that lighter to start a woman on fire while she slept on a New York City subway the other day. Authorities have not yet identified the woman. She appeared to be homeless, but I'm told she was so badly burned.\par \par \uc2 That's why it's taking a while, because it's so difficult to identify her. Now, Trump's new border czar has been talking about this case because Governor Kathy Hochul of New York posted these pictures the other day, Edward, talking about how crime in the city's largest transit system and the nation's largest transit system is down, but people don't feel that way.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : This is a senseless act of violence for this innocent woman to be killed at the hands of a criminal illegal immigrant. It really shows how the current border policies, catch-and-release, has really impacted the entire country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, Zapeta is originally from Guatemala. Investigators tell us the 33-year-old was in the country illegally, listen to this, crossing into the United States at least two times.\par \par \uc2 He was deported back to Guatemala in 2018, but he came back again. Police say the address he gave was to an addiction center out in New York City. This comes as The New York Post is reporting that Zapeta often smoked K2, that synthetic marijuana sprayed with who knows what.\par \par \uc2 A man who lived with him at the shelter telling The Post that he would drink and then -- quote -- "lose it all the time." Zapeta remains in custody. He is going to be back in court again soon. As you mentioned, Edward, he was in the hospital today at some point, we were told, but he was back in court as well. So we will keep an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Alexis McAdams, thank you. A horrible story.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, the suspect reportedly telling police, yes, again, he has no memory of that attack.\par \par \uc2 So let's turn to retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  inspector Paul Mauro.\par \par \uc2 Paul, are you buying that?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, he very well might not. There are videos out there circulating of him prior to the event where he seems to be semi-coherent.\par \par \uc2 But let's be clear, that's not going to be a defense to murder. It's certainly not going to stand up in court, no matter what his public defender feels. And I don't think he's really going to get much purchase on that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So this man crossed illegally into the U.S. in June of 2018. Within days, in the first Trump administration, they deported him back. CBP believes that he got back into the country as a got-away. Now, he shouldn't even be here, right?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Yes, I mean, there are multiple points of failure in this.\par \par \uc2 And, first of all, you go right to it, which is the southern border, obviously. That's the initial one. Then the second one, I would argue, is the shelter system in New York City, whose largess is legendary. He's living in there. He's apparently reportedly a heavy substance abuser. K2 is a very virulent drug.\par \par \uc2 It's not marijuana. It is a synthetic that is completely unpredictable and can really cause all kinds of psychoses. But then there's the subway piece. New York City's subway system and bus system, the \uc2 MTA\uc2 , lost $750 million last year to fare beaters.\par \par \uc2 Nobody believes this guy paid his fare. It used to be a lot harder to get on the subway if you were going to beat the fare and the subways as a result were much safer. These days, people just walk in and you see transients living there going in and out from the shelters, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And so that's another point of failure. He shouldn't have not only been in the country. He shouldn't have been in New York City and he shouldn't have been on the New York City subway system.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So it's interesting. On Sunday, I asked Tom Homan about the got- aways. Listen to what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: How concerned are you that someone's already come across the border who wants to do us harm?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS HOMAN, INCOMING BORDER CZAR: I'm very concerned. We got over two million known got-aways, got-aways. These are people that pay more to get away, people that didn't want to be fingerprinted, people that didn't want to be vetted. That should scare the hell out of everybody. It does me.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how many terrorists have crossed this border, but someday we're going to find out, and it's going to be a bad day for America. That's why I'm glad President Trump's been in the Oval Office very soon.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: How do you find those people?\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: That's law enforcement-sensitive, but we will find them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So as a retired law enforcement officer, do you share his fears over the got-aways and the harm that they might cause?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Well, certainly.\par \par \uc2 One of the things you do in law enforcement to contextualize what you're dealing with is, you look at the prior incidents, OK? You look at the record that the perpetrator may have. You run the fingerprints after an arrest. You look at the phone calls. You -- there's various things you do to see what we got here.\par \par \uc2 And you lose all of that when you have somebody who's a clean skin, as they say. You know absolutely nothing about them. For all we know, there are people running around in New York City who are serial murderers. In fact, it's likely, considering the numbers, and when you consider the fact that we're not getting the best and the brightest from places like Venezuela, who are only so -- too happy to get rid of Tren de Aragua and other career criminals.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, of course, I am concerned. And that is going to be the problem, which is locating these people, figuring out what you got and figuring out a lever to get them out. Homan seems to have his arms around it, but he's going to run into a lot of headwinds, especially in a sanctuary town like New York.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: All right, so in about 20 seconds that we have left, I wanted -- the related video to this shows this crime that happened in New York City shows witnesses stood and watching, but didn't move to help the sleeping woman.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian Angels found her, \uc2 Curtis Sliwa\uc2  says. This is the Daniel Penny effect and \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has made it so people are frozen when they see crimes. What's your take in 20 seconds on that?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: He's not wrong.\par \par \uc2 And when you consider the fact that the police department's head count is significantly down, the cops that are working transit are working on overtime, 12-hour tours, in some cases mandatory, there's just not enough coverage to keep all the subway systems safe. And it's -- listen, you can never have a cop in every car.\par \par \uc2 And so if you're going to abdicate the good samaritan effect, where people will step in and help their fellow New Yorkers, we're in more trouble than we even had been. And you really hope that that's not the case, but just consider how bad it would have been had Daniel Penny been convicted.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, and you can multiply that by a number of other cities that we have talked about here across the nation.\par \par \uc2 Paul Mauro, I appreciate it. Thank you for your insight on this. And merry Christmas to you.\par \par \uc2 So, with no answers and not much being done over the mysterious drones, several state agencies are now taking matters into their own hands, right, Nate Foy?\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that's absolutely right, Edward.\par \par \uc2 And, tonight, a lot of people will be looking into the sky, hoping to see Santa Claus. Here in New Jersey, people will be looking for drones. Why State Police say all you need is your phone to figure out if your eyes are playing tricks on you -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Ho, ho, yes, Santa coming early for a rally on Wall Street, the Dow surging almost 400 points heading into the holiday, stocks poised to close out a big year. Can we see a repeat in 2025?\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, disappointment growing over the Biden administration's response to these mysterious drones. Now several states are taking matters into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy digging deeper in New Jersey -- in Jersey City, New Jersey, I should say, with the latest.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Hey, Edward.\par \par \uc2 So New Jersey State Police tell FOX, they told FOX this morning that they are no closer to solving this drone mystery now than they were in the middle of last month. So that's the bad news. The good news is for the people here in New Jersey, reported drone sightings are going down to just one per night over the past several nights compared to 150 per night not long ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COL. PATRICK CALLAHAN, NEW JERSEY STATE POLICE: I'm glad that the temperature is kind of come down on the issue, but I still think it let us know in law enforcement that we have a little ways to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: So New Jersey State Police say that numbers are going down partially because of apps that help residents distinguish between a drone and a plane.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey also hosted 20 law enforcement agencies from various states and Canada to discuss how best to deal with drone threats. They're advocating for congressional action to increase state and local counterdrone authority. Right now, that is a federal authority.\par \par \uc2 Now, temporary flight restrictions continue over critical infrastructure in New Jersey and New York, while the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is pushing for these restrictions to become the new normal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WEINER, DEPUTY COMMISSIONER, NYPD COUNTERTERRORISM: We think that there should be flight restrictions, permanent flight restrictions around critical infrastructure and other sensitive locations. It would make things safer for everyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: So, right now, those restrictions mean that drone pilots can't fly within one mile of critical infrastructure sites like this power station that we're outside tonight in Jersey City. If pilots violate that, they can be detained and their drones can be shot down.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Nate, I want to ask you, because of all the media attention though, does law enforcement believe maybe some of this is those folks that we're doing it laying low a little bit?\par \par \uc2 FOY: I'm sorry, Edward. Say that one more time, the last part about frustration.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Could this be a factor of them laying low? Now that there's so much heat so to speak on this topic, whoever was doing these drones, could they just have backed off a little bit?\par \par \uc2 FOY: OK, I think I got it. And forgive me if I'm answering a different question here, Edward.\par \par \uc2 But I did speak to the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  and New Jersey State Police and their theory is that there was a small number of possibly unauthorized drones that were flown in New Jersey in the middle of last month and then after that, as sort of a domino effect, people who are hobbyists who own drones flew their own drones up in the air and were looking for these suspicious drones.\par \par \uc2 And that's part of why we saw so many of the drones after that sort of suspicious part of the middle of last month, and then now they're advocating for the residents to use these apps, which are sort of telling people that they're actually seeing a plane very often, rather than a drone, which is a big reason why the reported drone sightings are going down so significantly.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, Nate, drones chasing drones.\par \par \uc2 I appreciate it. Nate Foy, live in Jersey City, New Jersey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FOY: You got it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So President Biden facing backlash after tossing out two student loan bailout plans.\par \par \uc2 And, later, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista strike shutting down 170 stores. What it could end up meaning for your coffee addiction.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Former President Clinton discharged from the hospital earlier today after being treated for the flu. He was admitted yesterday for testing and observation after developing a fever.\par \par \uc2 Now, his spokesperson says the former president and his family are deeply grateful for the care provided by the team at \uc2 MedStar Georgetown University Hospital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will be watching for any more developments on that story.\par \par \uc2 So, now to the White House and now to the president currently in office. He scraps two major student loan bailout plans weeks before the Trump administration heads into office. Now, Republican Louisiana Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  says this move shows Biden's student debt cancellation push was a dishonest attempt to buy votes.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read on this.\par \par \uc2 Politico reporter Tiana Lowe Doescher and The Hill national correspondent Julia Manchester are with me now.\par \par \uc2 I appreciate it, ladies.\par \par \uc2 So, Julia, I'm going to get to you.\par \par \uc2 So the courts -- what do you think? What do you make of this? The courts had said over and over that these plans to get student loan bailout, it just -- they weren't legal. So was this just inevitable?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: I think, in many ways it was, Edward.\par \par \uc2 You had the issue of the courts and the ongoing legal challenges this move was facing, but also, look, in just a few weeks, we're going to have president-elect Trump taking office and this action or these actions would likely be reversed. So I think this is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  essentially backing off this because there were questions as to how far this would ultimately go.\par \par \uc2 But going to Senator Cassidy's post on X from earlier, I think it's interesting, because we saw the Biden campaign, then the Harris campaign really talked up this idea of student loan debt forgiveness in an effort to appeal to younger Americans, to student voters, et cetera. It didn't really seem to work out the way they wanted it to.\par \par \uc2 But, clearly, I think today there is a lot of pushback from those on the left and student loan debt relief activists who were hoping for obviously a very different outcome.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, Tiana, I want to ask you about that. Still, $180 billion, taxpayer dollars, was used to forgive student debt. And, again, this was an attempt by the administration, as some critics have said, to get that younger vote.\par \par \uc2 It just -- it really didn't work, given the numbers that we saw for president-elect Trump.\par \par \uc2 TIANA LOWE DOESCHER, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: It's an incredibly cynical ploy that clearly, worse than just didn't work, it backfired.\par \par \uc2 And it's why you saw a disproportionate working-class share and a nonwhite share of voters go for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in large part because we have never spent hundreds of billions of dollars in 2024 on public education, let alone the $1 trillion that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  promised.\par \par \uc2 And if you're going up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the restoration of norms and grievances, what you don't go is, you don't go to the Supreme Court and say, we are going to ignore your ruling and saying that Congress still retains power of the purse, which remains true. And the only person that engaged in the delusion that it didn't was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  up until today.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So I want to broaden this out, Julia, for you a little bit.\par \par \uc2 We had the student loan bailout plan. That didn't work. You had the economic plan, which spiked inflation. You had the Biden energy policy, which put a jump in gas prices. You had Biden's focus on E.V. mandates that caused Ford to lose $4 billion in 2023.\par \par \uc2 So where does this -- I'm not even talking about foreign policy. Where does this president then rank in history?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Well, I think right now, when you look at his approval ratings, they aren't that high.\par \par \uc2 But I think, ultimately, this takes time. It takes decades -- years, decades to figure out where a president ranks in terms of history. You look at someone like \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , for example, who was very unpopular when he left office in 2009. Now he is seen as a pretty beloved figure.\par \par \uc2 But I think in the immediate aftermath of this election of course, Americans wanted a change. They chose \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The Biden administration didn't seem to be doing it for them. And we have not only saw that change at the top of the ticket at the presidential level, but we saw Republicans take control, obviously, of the House and Senate and do very well in state legislative races across the country.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is definitely telling of how much Americans really just wanted to try something new, how the status quo wasn't working for them. We will see how that rates \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in terms of history. But I think the pardoning of his son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , as well as questions surrounding his health and fitness for office going back earlier this year, that's not going to do him any favors going forward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, it's interesting. And most of his presidency, 70-plus percent of people thought the country was going in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 So, Tiana, I want to change subject a little bit. Former President Clinton released from the hospital today, but a fever put him in the hospital. He's 78. Are we already worried about his health?\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all born in the exact same year. We have been governed by the Boomers. And I think the big mistake is to view it as like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was an aberration, when actually he was a return to the norm.\par \par \uc2 Like, the Obama era of someone who was Gen X being the president of the country, that was the anomaly. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , we, of course, all wish him wish him well, but he did exemplify what motivated a lot of the Democratic base in both the Bernie Sanders revolution and in terms of the resistance against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the era of corruption and whatnot.\par \par \uc2 And did the Democrats ever do anything to reconcile themselves from that legacy? I don't think so. And especially having a pseudo-primary to reelect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  probably did not work.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And anointing the vice president, yes, that probably won't help. We're going to see how history remembers these presidents.\par \par \uc2 So, Tiana and Julia, I appreciate it. Thank you for your insight on this. So merry Christmas and happy holidays to both of you guys.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, now Santa making an early stop on Wall Street today, the gift of green. His rally, combined with Trump enthusiasm, may be keeping that green going in 2025. We will debate that.\par \par \uc2 We're also keeping an eye on Santa and his reindeer as they span the globe, bringing the gifts of holiday, joy to kids around the world. We will be tracking Santa's movements throughout the night. There you see him on the screen. I think Rudolph is leading that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: What's better than the gift of green? Stocks rallying ahead of the holidays, getting a good jump on the potential Santa Claus rally, which is the trading period that we're in now until the first two days of the new year.\par \par \uc2 So it's been a banner year in 2024, and let's see if the new president means that 2025 will also be up.\par \par \uc2 So we will talk to our market pros, Gary Kaltbaum and Frances Newton Stacy.\par \par \uc2 So, Gary, I want to talk to you first. I want to ask you, stocks are up again today. With the Dow up this year somewhere around 5,500 points, is the Santa rally taking place broadly this year?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Finally, today, I can tell you, before today, the broad market has been very, very weak. I can list a dozen sectors that have been getting in trouble, whether it be housing or oils.\par \par \uc2 The big stuff, like the big-cap tech and stuff, is what's been leading, and that was on the move again today. And I got to tell you, after last Wednesday's 1,000-point drop, I was worried about whether we're going to get an end-of-year rally. But it looks like we're getting going right now, as the broad market, I think, probably putting a good low today.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, quickly, Gary, so is the boost then -- is there coming a boost because of the optimism of president-elect Trump's business-friendly policy?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: I think the regulatory environment getting 100 times better is going to be huge,and the fact that we went from losing the Trump tax cuts to more than likely keeping them and more to come.\par \par \uc2 The business and the consumer keeping more of their money is always a good thing for the profits and the economy.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Frances, traditionally markets have risen 77 percent in this Santa rally period. Since December 17 going into today, the S&P fell 2 percent, though.\par \par \uc2 But, this year, we have seen a lot of those losses thanks to, or this period, thanks to Fed Chairman \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 's news conference talking about the timing of the rate cuts and the Fed signaling there will be two interest rate cuts next year, not four, like they had forecasted last September.\par \par \uc2 So are the markets too focused on the Federal Reserve? Because earnings have been pretty good overall this year.\par \par \uc2 FRANCES NEWTON STACY, DIRECTOR OF STRATEGY, OPTIMAL CAPITAL: Yes, there are two catalysts. As you say, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was one for sure, but it hurt the bond market far more than it hurt the stock market.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the rise in the 10-year has been trying to kind of compensate for potentially easy money coming into the new year with the fiscal bill being signed. Of course, we have promises from DOGE and lots of rumors about cuts, and we will just have to see how that works out. We don't have any numbers to actually price in as market participants.\par \par \uc2 I'm very, I'm very excited about this administration coming in. I'm very excited to see what Elon is going to do with these fiscal cuts. Obviously, he is probably the smartest guy in the world and can think with the mechanical things.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that has kept a recession off our backs with the Fed tightening has been the fiscal spending. So, if they're going to drastically cut the fiscal spending, mechanically, we have to replace it with something. Volatility is down. Haven assets are down.\par \par \uc2 So the fear in the market is down. The U.S. dollar with the incoming administration is strong. And barring any bad news, I see a Santa Claus rally continuing.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Huh, Santa Claus/Trump rally.\par \par \uc2 So, Gary, what sectors are you watching the first half of the Trump administration then as he makes these policy changes?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Well, technology and financials always lead. And if they continue to be in good stead, I think we're in good stead.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to repeat something. There's a heck of a lot of areas that have been pretty much left in the dust. And I would continue to avoid them. And all I can tell you right now, top 10 stocks in the S&P is about 40 percent of the S&P and 56 percent of the Nasdaq 100. And they're all acting well from \uc2 Apple\uc2  on down.\par \par \uc2 If that continues, that will definitely be the place to be. But Frances nailed something big time, the 10-year yield. And I talk about that all the time versus talking about the Fed too much. If the 10-year yield starts going above 5 and more while the Fed's down at 4.25, that means they missed the mark.\par \par \uc2 And then I think the market gets a little bit angry that they may have to raise rates to catch up. And if that ends up being the case, we will hit some headwinds with an expensive market at this juncture.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, Frances, we know that 10-year is what they base the mortgage prices -- or the price of mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 So, Frances, what policies do you think we need to follow and see if they pass from president-elect Trump to keep these markets going?\par \par \uc2 NEWTON STACY: For this, it's going to be all about fiscal.\par \par \uc2 So remember when the Fed tightens, they're taking liquidity out of the system. The fiscal spending from the Biden administration has been so high that it has been reliquefying the economy.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 NEWTON STACY: That can be a good thing, in the sense that it keeps things liquid, but it can be a bad thing because it's all debt, right?\par \par \uc2 And so when Trump gets into office, what he, Elon and Vivek actually do with the fiscal spending is going to determine whether we have an inflationary threat, disinflation, or potentially, if they cut it too much, we could have deflation and then the bond market would sell off.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, Gary, Frances, I appreciate it. We're going to have to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 And we will see. We had the Grace Commission under the Reagan administration. Nothing really came of it. Well, now we have social media, we will have to see what the end result is.\par \par \uc2 Gary, Frances, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, now to the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  strike shutting down around 170 stores in a battle over pay -- what this could mean for the cost of your cappuccino next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  strike expanding in its fifth day, closing around 170 locations here in the U.S., as workers continue to battle for more pay with less than a day to Christmas.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Alicia Acuna is live in Denver.\par \par \uc2 What's brewing?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Edward. Well, not coffee, that's for sure.\par \par \uc2 This Denver location has been closed for the past two days. It's one of about 500 stores that's represented by Starbucks Workers United. And they started their walkout on Friday. The union began organizing stores back in 2021. And they have been trying to get a contract for workers since then.\par \par \uc2 But negotiations that started in February with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  are now at a standstill. The union represents more than 10,000 baristas and shift supervisors throughout the United States and is calling for increased pay and benefits and ending what it considers unfair labor practices. They also want additional staffing and more predictable schedules.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE RANDALL, \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  DENVER SHIFT SUPERVISOR: We are not asking for anything that is unreasonable. We are asking for a living wage. While we're here, it's hard to actually get our jobs done, because we're asked to do the work of a person-and-a-half. And it's just not sustainable and so it leads to a lot of burnout.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Ninety-seven percent of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores are not unionized, so the majority of locations remain open for business.\par \par \uc2 We're told \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  offered 1.5 percent pay increase. Members called that number insulting, while \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  says the union's demands of pay increases upwards of 60 percent are not sustainable. Now, Edward, there are more than 10,000 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  locations across the United States. This impacted, according to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , only 170.\par \par \uc2 And the union workers say they're back to work tomorrow, so the strike ends today -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, we're going to see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 Appreciate it, Alicia. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas already make an average of $18 an hour, but if you add in benefits like health care, free college tuition and paid family leave, it's actually closer to $30 an hour.\par \par \uc2 So what does it mean for the cost of your latte if workers get everything that they want?\par \par \uc2 Consumer expert Vera Gibbons joins me on that now.\par \par \uc2 Vera, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 VERA GIBBONS, CONSUMER ANALYST: I think the benefit package is pretty -- pretty darn good. I mean, that's one of the main draws to working at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , is the wages and all the benefits that come with the territory.\par \par \uc2 So it's interesting to actually see this strike actually happen, but it makes sense that it would be happening now, because you have got a lot of eyeballs on the situation over the holidays, a very busy time for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . And I think it's about just putting the company in the spotlight and sort of like putting corporate greed in the spotlight and showing that they want more.\par \par \uc2 And, now, I would imagine that they would try to settle things sooner, rather than later. But as your reporter said, only a very small portion of stores have been affected.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, in the D.C. area, I can tell you it's $6.50 for a Frappuccino.\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: At some point, this will be the habit that people cut first to save money, right?\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: Well, you didn't factor in the fact that you probably tip on that latte or that cappuccino.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: I mean, that's another thing on top of the benefits and the nice pay packages that they get. You know, if you don't tip -- I mean, that's pretty much mandatory in today's environment.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: So I would not expect to see the latte or cappuccino prices rise because of this strike. I mean, they brought in a new CEO not long ago to turn the company around, and he's trying to attract employees, retain the employees.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we did see a little uptick in 2024. But 2025, I think he's trying to stabilize prices, streamline the menu and keep everyone happy.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 So I want to change subject to with you on this. So now that we have lived through the price increases of 20.5 percent under the Biden administration, people have dipped into their savings and there's an expectation that President Trump can reduce prices.\par \par \uc2 How should people then catch up on their retirement savings?\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: Oh, retirement savings, well, technically, it's never too late to actually start saving for retirement. A lot of people are way behind on this front, but, obviously, the age-old advice of maxing out your 401(k), at least contributing enough to capture the company match, that's sort of an all-weather strategy.\par \par \uc2 Some people are also tapping their home equity, which actually may make sense for some people now, because home values have risen so much over the past couple of years. That's an option too. And then, while it is tempting to take your Social Security benefits early, it's in your best interest to actually delay taking those benefits.\par \par \uc2 For every year up to the age of 70 that you delay taking those benefits, your payout increases by 8 percent. And it's very hard to find that kind of a -- that kind of a percentage increase anywhere really without assuming a high level of risk.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. That's really interesting.\par \par \uc2 So I want to get you to take on this, the changing car habits. The Wall Street Journal is reporting that smaller cars like Honda Civic and Nissan Sentra have taken off this year, with sales rising 23 percent. Meanwhile, SUV sales have declined 2.3 percent compared to 2020.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: That kind of makes sense to me because car prices now are ridiculous. I mean, for like an average car, it's like $45,000. So, I think people are thinking, hmm, how can we actually save money on our transportation?\par \par \uc2 So the small compact, subcompact cars that were once reserved for like college kids and entry-level drivers are now becoming very much in fashion for the average American, because it's not only car costs that are up. I mean, insurance costs are up...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: ... something like 26 percent year over year. Maintenance costs, repair costs are up. Gas prices, while they are coming down to about $3 gallon, that's still pretty high.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: So, people are thinking of creative ways to actually work...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GIBBONS: ... their budgets.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, Vera, we'