ng trafficked as well.\par \par \uc2 And so, it was not easy, but I had just supernatural experiences with God. And there was a day that he told me, you need to quit this industry and I would bless your life like no man ever could, because the life that I have for you will overflow with love and peace and joy.\par \par \uc2 And so, I really just took a step of faith, not knowing where I would go next. And I quit the industry behind, and I had called my mother to help me to get away from the pimp. And I've been out for over a decade. I have two beautiful children. I'm married to a wonderful man.\par \par \uc2 And now we help people who are struggling with addictions to pornography. We also help industry workers who want to -- who want to exit the industry. We help them get out as well. And we also try our best to protect children from stumbling onto things like pornography.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's so important. As we hear everybody talking about the needs to protect the rights of sex workers, I mean, that's just normalizing sex work. And you hear a lot of politicians on the left do that. That's not really helping them. That's just keeping them in a business that degrades them.\par \par \uc2 But I want to read a portion of the Texas brief from the case. In 2006, just over 25 percent of children aged 13 or younger were exposed to online pornography. But by 2022, that number more than doubled to 54 percent. Smartphones were the way most people ended up accessing pornography.\par \par \uc2 And in Australia, a new study, one in three teens learn about sex from violent pornography. Peter, very quickly, how important is age verification to try to stop this madness? And all the damage that's being done to young men and women.\par \par \uc2 GENTALA: Absolutely, Laura. Yeah, age verification is the solution. It's being used widely in Europe. And the only thing that's really holding it up now in the United States is lawsuits like this one brought by the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and 