cross the I-5 corridor, farther south across the San Francisco Bay Area. So, this was something that happened offshore to your point, but it was felt well inland across parts of California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. So, because of the proximity to the United States coastline, I don't think there was necessarily enough time for the \uc2 USGS\uc2  and for the tsunami warning center to actually measure a tsunami had it occurred. Sometimes these happen way out there and there's time to watch this moving in. This was super close. You saw something just like 40 miles offshore.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: And I was looking at -- here it is, too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: The depth of it was about eight miles down into the Pacific Ocean. So, it was a fairly shallow earthquake. But still, to that point, a lot of folks did report that they felt it onshore, on land in California, and even up into Southern Oregon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. So, back in weather command, we were looking at some of the projected arrival times for a potential tsunami. Again, we want to be clear, the tsunami warning has been canceled. There is no tsunami threat now to the West Coast. But we were watching some of the projected potential arrival times and we watched these pass, thankfully.\par \par \uc2 Being said, what are we looking at here? That's --\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Some of the waves are coming in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, that's courtesy of our affiliate out in San Francisco, yes, the Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Yes. So, you know, at least California, they took all the proper precautions. The local transportation there did shut down, suspended the local zoo, San Francisco Zoo, a big zoo, did take the animals and they brought staff to higher ground. So, they're taking precaution for that possibility of a tsunami. But, thankfully, that has let up as of now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. So, again, to be clear, we do want to report the tsunami warning has been canceled longer. In the bulletin, they've reported that please don't return to some of these areas, immediate coastline, like the beaches, because law enforcement and crews want to get out, survey all this and make sure this was a major earthquake that struck just offshore of California, just about 40 miles off to the west, northwest of Petrolia, a magnitude 7. 0.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Right. And as we mentioned, on the scale, that would be considered a major. Here's the scale for you. So, the earthquake magnitudes, where we stand right now was at 7.0 offshore. But had this been onshore in California, had this been in a highly populated area, like San Francisco, or even Eureka, we could have saw major damages.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. What we were watching as this played out over the past hour or so, because of its proximity to the coastline, this could have been a scenario where had this produced a tsunami, it would have been on shore quickly. So that's why the tsunami warning center got out ahead of this. A lot of folks reported feeling this. You look at some of the wave heights. We could see that with the buoy count, a little bit of a blip. But, thankfully, this was not a widespread tsunami produced.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Very much so. And I was looking at the amount of time that it would take in between an earthquake and a tsunami would take up to 30 minutes to see a possible tsunami. Thankfully, they have let up on those warnings. That's no longer going to be the possibility or even the case at this point. But even the waves have gotten up five feet as of now in some of these areas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. So, if you're just joining us, this was the strongest earthquake to strike this area going all the way back to 2005. So, we're talking about almost 20 years. It's up into that strong criteria. There was a lot of unsettled feelings when you get that alert. That push notification on your phone and it says tsunami warning, but, again, the alert has been canceled.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Yes. And I think we're getting in on some new video as well that's getting pushed out through this afternoon. So, take a look at this. This is some of the images around the areas that may have felt that shaking through this afternoon. We know that it happened about an hour ago. But as we have video of some of the homes in the area, they were feeling the shaking, they were reporting it. And they're now showing us some of the images there on the ground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It looks like that's the video, Kiyana. This is in the pool. And we're looking at, we're waiting on this. This is from Northern California.\par \par \uc2 [15:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's a still shot there. There's the video. Yes. So, that's obviously sloshing around, Kiyana. And to an extent, that's the concern that could happen on a much --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. Thank you to Fox News Weather. They're watching all of this very closely and assessing the damage. It was about 40 miles out into the ocean. And you can see people out there looking at what's going on and one guy out there on a surfboard in the ocean just off of San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 So, let's go to Chief Correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2  reporting live for us from Los Angeles on the developments here. Hi, John.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Hey Martha. So the good news here is that the tsunami warning for coastal California, Northern California and indeed the Oregon coastline has now been officially canceled. There is no danger of tsunamis hitting any of that area, including San Francisco, which you're looking at on the live pictures there. San Francisco, of course, is around 250 to 270 miles south of where this earthquake struck.\par \par \uc2 Now, the earthquake itself just about an hour and 20 minutes ago. It was a 7.0. That is a big earthquake by any measure. It was also relatively shallow. We're now being told about eight miles. That's pretty shallow for an earthquake. And the significance of that is the shallower the earthquake, less distance there is for the shockwaves to travel to the surface. If it's deeper, they tend to dissipate a little before they get to the surface. A shallow earthquake means a lot more people are going to feel it. There is potential for a lot more damage.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are not getting many reports of extensive damage done up in the Eureka area. It's not a heavily populated area, but we have seen a tweet from State Senator Mike McGuire who says that the state is sending immediate assistance to the area, that area being Humboldt and Del Norte Counties to assist with emergency operations. And according to the state senator, Mike McGuire, there are reports of some homes that have come off their foundations.\par \par \uc2 Now, it might be a minute before we get any video of the damage done there. But, again, Martha, 7.0 is a very significant earthquake. And as you heard our colleagues at Fox Weather saying, it's the biggest earthquake we've had in California since something like 2005, so in nearly a 20 years.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that it hasn't created a tsunami. The good news is that it was in a less populated area of the state. But, obviously, we await to see what the effects are in the immediate area, the immediate area being the city of Eureka, not far from the Oregon border, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Jonathan, thank you. It's a part of the country that is always sort of on the edge of this kind of experience. And, you know, the fault that goes through California is very active and people there, I think, are generally kind of semi-prepared for these kinds of events, right?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes, we are always prepared for these kind of events, or at least we're always supposed to be. You know, my wife, for instance, drives around with an earthquake kit in the back of her car. I think 