nto the shell casings, made sure that the shell casings were found on the -- or on the live rounds and made sure that they were found in that area.\par \par \uc2 He also really wanted to get up close and personal to this victim. He could have just, if you think about it, he could have just shot him and backed away. Most people when they shoot someone, they want to back away and get out of there.\par \par \uc2 He was under that -- he had obviously surveilled the location previously, so he knew there were cameras up there. And he seemed to be in the corner of the area where the cameras would in fact capture him. But instead he chose to fire the one round, cripple him, and the victim goes down and then he walks up on him, almost as if he wanted him to see him And then he fired additional rounds or one round.\par \par \uc2 But he walks right under the light in an area that he knew was being captured on film.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So he was also -- I'm sorry, Maureen. I want to bring up this picture of him smiling at the hostel, no mask over his face. And all I can think, he looks like a young man. I mean, all I can think when I look at this is, if this person is in my family, I mean, he's easily identifiable.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are people, there have to be people across this country are looking at that and saying, oh, that's, you know, and naming him.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Absolutely. And if you look at the initial photos where he has the mask on, you can see a little bit better how large his nose is, how much it protrudes from his face, almost like, you know, Pinocchio or whatever. But he -- you can see right there on that one that his nose is really quite large.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you put those two things together, people do know who this person is. And when you consider the fact that there's a reward out for him, this guy is going to be in handcuffs in no time. That burner phone that he dropped -- that there's just too many mistakes, that's what I mean by an over communicator.\par \par \uc2 He left his cell phone, he left candy wrappers, he left a water bottle. He wrote on the shell casings, this isn't your normal killer.\par \par \uc2 This is someone -- this is very personal to them. And they wanted people to know -- but do they want -- is this a red herring? Does he really want people to think that, you know, someone died as a result of his actions, or is it something entirely different?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Maureen, thank you very much. Somebody who's looking at this image knows exactly who this person is. And I would encourage them to call the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2  because he will be found. And if you know anything, you need to get that information to them as quickly as you can for him and for everyone else.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Maureen. It's always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So nearly five years post-pandemic, a shocking number of federal workers still don't go back to work. And they just locked in a deal that they can keep that going till 2029. David Asman and Clay Travis on the DOGE plan to step on that big time, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Cutting waste up here is kind of like going to heaven. You know, everybody wants to do it, but nobody's quite ready to make the trip.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So just a couple of minutes ago, Aishah Hasnie, our reporter on the Hill, had a quick exchange with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who's been pretty quiet all day, the DOGE duo visiting DC. Let's watch what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie, Fox News Correspondent: Elon, do you want to see Democrats here?\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Thank you to Aishah. So a report from DOGE Senate Caucus Leader 