there do, which is say, OK, you have to show up five days a week., And if you don't, then you can leave.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: It's actually a way to call your overall employment ratio without having to fire people and buy people out. Because I don't know, the last time you were in DC, I went to college there. We got a studio.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yesterday, yes.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: You were there yesterday. OK. When you walk around in the lunch hour, there's no one at any. It's like a ghost town on the streets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, like Agriculture Department, just as an example, it's almost a city block.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you look up at windows and windows, what is everyone doing there now? We know they're not even there.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I have to say, even when they were there, I grew up in DC and I used to, when I was in high school, we used to have a game where we'd walk through these big Department of Agriculture, for example, Department of Commerce. There were -- most of the people, most of the workers there were just biding their time.\par \par \uc2 They were just, you know, work on the phone, talking to their girlfriends or their wives or whomever. I mean, the point is whether these entire departments, like Commerce Department, like the Department of Education, those -- that's really low hanging fruit, I would say, for the DOGE brothers, you know.\par \par \uc2 For both Elon and Vivek, they have scouted out the biggest wasteful departments in general and are going to try to hit them now. It's been tried before with the Grace Commission back in the '80s and there was the Simpson Bowles Commission. They weren't superstars like these guys. These guys are superstars. EV, this is an EV mandate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you know what, they're also working at the behest of President Trump, which is also a different game changing environment, Clay.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: And remember what Elon did with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? He fired 75 percent of the employees and the business actually is more efficient and effective now than it was before. Do any of us doubt that if 75 percent of federal employees were gone tomorrow, in terms of being on the payroll that we all have to pay for, that the government would severely be curtailed in terms of its ability to work?\par \par \uc2 I don't. I think you could knock out 75 percent of federal workers and most of us would have no idea. And the government would go right on the same way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think it's very smart. Elon talking about two year severance packages.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like, you know, we understand that this will be new and you'll have to figure out what you're doing next, so we're going to give you two years of severance. And because he is such a shrewd businessperson, he knows like that we're still going to come out way, way, way ahead if we give all of you people two years.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: By the way, there's also some of the low hanging fruit, obviously is fraud and waste. Right now you have the biggest spending period. I think in the history of federal government where the Biden administration is just shoveling billions and billions of dollars out as fast, very often to losers.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you have this CHIPS Act, which was a losing act to begin with. They're sending $8 billion to Intel, which is a losing company right now. That's corporate welfare, folks. That's your tax dollars, middle class tax dollars going into big corporations that are wasting a lot of money. $8 billion to Intel alone, intel, by the way, just taken off of the Dow Jones Industrial.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: You also are pointing out something that I think is wildly important in terms of the size of the federal government. We spent more money on COVID than we did per capita to win World War II. We dialed back spending in a big way. If you go look at federal spending after we won World War II, this COVID spending has now been embedded as the default basis of --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: It supposed to be temporary and now it was being pulled back but Biden kept putting it back in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Those spending is ever temporary.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is locked in. It is never temporary.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Like the Godfather, they pull keep pulling this back in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Every one of these agencies, just find 2 percent. Give me 2 percent, 2 percent, 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: That would add up to a trillion, I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It sure would. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always great to have you on. Thank you so much for being here. So from Musk and Ramaswamy to -- on the Hill to Zuckerberg dining at Mar-a- Lago, tech titans are now jockeying to be inside Trump's tent, as Axios puts this. And now billionaire \uc2 Amazon\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  says he too is optimistic about a second Trump term.\par \par \uc2 Remember the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , democracy dies in darkness, so a little bit different tone now. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports. Grady?\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. And \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  specifically says he's looking forward to what President Elect Trump is going to do around deregulation. And he says he's willing to get involved, too.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFF BEZOS\uc2 , AMAZON CEO: I'm actually very optimistic this time around that we're going to see. I'm very hopeful about this. He seems to have a lot of energy around reducing regulation. And my point of view, if I can help him do that, I'm going to help him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Bezos made those comments at a New York Times DealBook Summit event yesterday. He and Trump, though, have had a contentious relationship over the years, particularly during Trump's first term. Trump said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  at one point didn't pay its fair share of taxes and he allegedly blocked the company's multibillion dollar cloud computing deal with the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Trump also regularly criticized the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , which of course Bezos owns. But now Bezos says Trump has changed, describing him as "calmer, more confident and more settled." It's worth pointing out, though, the tech mogul and his businesses have a lot to gain from a good relationship with Trump.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs could increase the cost of products on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . And \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  competes for federal contracts, Martha, so this could be a smart business move by Bezos as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I think everybody wants to see the country be successful at this moment in time. And if it helps their business, that's OK, too. Thank you very much, Grady. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So tempers flaring today at this hearing on Secret Service Security lapses and steps to being taken to protect President-elect Trump's life. There's the iconic image from Butler, Pennsylvania on July 13th. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT FALLON (R-TX): I'm an elected member of Congress and I'm asking you a serious question. And you were --\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING DIRECTOR OF THE SECRET SERVICE: I am a public servant who has served this nation on our country's darkest day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, a combative moment on Capitol Hill earlier today where the task force investigating the first attempt on Trump's life in Butler, Pennsylvania, pressed acting Secret Service Director Ronald Rowe on what is being done to prevent the failures of that day. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. I have covered a lot of hearings over the years and I have never seen a verbal rumble quite like this one. Now look at this, Pat Fallon said Ronald Rowe got in this picture at ground zero on 911 out of vanity. Fallon says Rowe wants to become the full time Secret Service director so he stood close to President Biden, Vice President Harris and President-elect Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Why are there? Because you wanted to be visible, because you (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ROWE: You are out of line, congressman. You are out of line.\par \par \uc2 FALLON: -- president (inaudible) of his life because you are put -- you put those agents out of position.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Rowe would not say whether he wants the job on a full time basis when I pressed him in the hallway.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Have things gotten better protecting the president-elect and others? Will you stay on the Secret Service director for new administration?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Fallon said Rowe's position near the trio pushed aside other protective agents. Fallon says this demonstrates that the Secret Service did not improve after the assassination attempt on Mr. Trump's life in July. Bipartisan members of the panel investigating the assassination attempt are frustrated that they never got to examine the body of Thomas Crooks. The report is due next week. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Significant, important. Thank you very much, Chad Pergram. Sometimes people get pretty hot under the collar. And that was an extraordinary exchange that we saw today in Congress.\par \par \uc2 So that's "The Story" for this Thursday, December 5th, and "The Story" goes on. We look forward to seeing you tonight at 8:00 for FOX Nation's sixth annual Patriot Awards. It is a very special night ahead. Tune in.\par \par \uc2 Some surprise guests are going to be there and I think you're going to want to be watching. We'll see you there tonight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241206ekc500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 The Future of A .I.?; Airline Executives Grilled on Capitol Hill; Drone Invasion?; Interview With Ontario, Canada, Premier \uc2 Doug Ford\uc2  ; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  (R -NC); Gunman Kills UnitedHealthcare CEO\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Foy, Kelly Saberi, Charles Payne, Paul Mauro, Chris Swecker,  Hillary Vaughn, Alexis McAdams, Maureen O'Connell, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Will Cain Show\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SIRENS BLARING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hear the three shots. I was parking down there in the car. I hear three shots, yes, and then, when I looked, there's a man down.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TISCH, NEW YORK CITY POLICE COMMISSIONER: Fifty-year-old \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  , the CEO of UnitedHealthcare, was shot and killed in what appears at this early stage of our investigation to be a brazen, targeted attack.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH KENNY, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  CHIEF OF DETECTIVES: The shooter steps onto the sidewalk from behind the car. He ignores numerous other pedestrians, approaches the victim from behind and shoots him in the back. The shooter then walks toward the victim and continues to shoot. It appears that the gun malfunctions, as he clears the jam and begins to fire again.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: Particularly the business executives, that, again, this was not what appears to be a just a random act of violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Armed and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 You are looking live at Midtown Manhattan, where a massive, I mean massive, manhunt is under way as we speak after the CEO of UnitedHealthcare was shot and killed outside a New York City hotel in what police are calling a brazen, targeted shooting.\par \par \uc2 What we are learning about the suspect who is still on the loose.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it with Alexis McAdams just around the block from our studios here, where all this went down early this morning.\par \par \uc2 Alexis , what did have learned?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's been hours, right, since this happened. I mean, it happened around 6:45, when New Yorkers were just waking up, including this CEO, OK, who was walking outside of that hotel because he had big business meetings here. And he was shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 Sources telling me on the ground, Neil, in New York that this was a targeted attack and they say the gunman was highly skilled. You can see it there on your screen. That's this news surveillance video we just got into the news room, the shooter walking up, pulling out a gun and opening fire.\par \par \uc2 He shoots the UnitedHealthcare CEO, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  , at least two times. But we froze that video because it's really difficult to watch. He shot him once in the back and another time in his leg. He kept walking toward him too as he was fighting for his life on the ground, and he shot him again.\par \par \uc2 This is who investigators are looking for right now on your screen, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  releasing these photos that show the suspect. Part of his face is covered. These are taken at some point today, I'm told, in New York City. Investigators say this guy walked off after that shooting, hopped on a bike and rode away toward Central Park.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENNY: The shooter appears to be a light-skinned male. He's wearing a light brown or cream-colored jacket, a black face mask, black and white sneakers, and a very distinctive gray backpack.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: This is who was shot and killed today. He was in New York City for business. His poor wife and two young kids, Neil, found out about this tragic news while they were at home in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 That's UnitedHealthcare CEO \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  . He was known as a stand-up guy by people who knew him. He was very successful in the business world, only 50 years old and was supposed to speak at the investors day conference today at that \uc2 Hilton Hotel\uc2  in Midtown. That's when he was targeted in this attack.\par \par \uc2 He had only been in town since Monday. A New York City councilman telling me a lot of people here in his district, which is Midtown, are on edge. Back out here live, you can see it's still an active investigation. We were in that area near the Hilton. I saw those little shell casings still on the ground. We're talking about three shots that were fired, and then since he unjammed his gun so quickly, he had three other shells too that dropped, so now all evidence in this case.\par \par \uc2 Where is this guy? We don't know just yet. So if anyone has information, you're asked to call Crime Stoppers, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You touched on Alexis , but is wife talking about threatening phone calls that he had been receiving, do we know anything more about that?\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: She said it was something to do to one of the reporters who got her on the phone, which this was also right after it happened. So she was trying to still piece this all together.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: She said it was something that pertained to his specific job. So we know he was in the health care -- in insurance. So was this somebody who was disgruntled? Was this somebody that knew him at a personal level, a professional level? They don't know.\par \par \uc2 But all you could tell from that video is this guy was waiting too, Neil, for like five to six minutes at least in -- across the street waiting for him to come out near the Hilton. And then he shot him. So he knew exactly who his target was, but motive, not sure just yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just incredible. All right, Alexis , great reporting today. Keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 We should say that he was the head of UnitedHealthcare, the insurance arm here, by far the largest part of \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  , the parent company. We still don't know if he had security on hand at that hour. It does not look like any were noticed, again, in a lot of the videos and pictures that have been taken since.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Paul Mauro, what he makes of this, the retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  inspector, attorney. We have also got Chris Swecker, the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  assistant director.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Paul, to you first. We don't know piecing this together a whole lot, but what we do know from images and videos that have become available, that the shooter was very calm and focused, that he had a silencer on his weapon -- that's stuff you don't find necessarily everywhere -- and that he was in no hurry after the shooting. What do we make of all that?\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I would just say a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 Right now, everything is speculation, obviously. We don't have a whole lot to work with. We have more than you and I had earlier today. The video shows that it is, in fact, a silencer that seems to fit the gun. It's not some homemade thing made out of a bottle or something, which argues he has access to that kind of thing, which is very rare and de facto illegal.\par \par \uc2 Also, it looks like the bike that he rode away from is not a city bike. It's an e-bike. That closes down that avenue of investigation. We know that he goes dark inside of Central Park. That gives him egress from the park in a whole bunch of areas, and it actually gives him access to the subway as well. He seems to have planned this.\par \par \uc2 He took a route that clearly was preconceived. And, right now, you got to say to yourself, the longer this goes on, the more unlikely a street level solve is going to be, and more likely that we're going to get a solve coming out of the victim's life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, we know he had a mask of some sort. He could have easily tossed a lot of that as he made his way, I think, when all was said and done, Chris, to Central Park, which is about eight blocks up from where all of this went down.\par \par \uc2 And then he could just get lost out of sight for the rest of the time, right?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SWECKER, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR: Is that for me, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it is. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that may have been by design to get into Central Park. I mean, there's video -- wall-to-wall video until you get to Central Park, so you get pretty much reconstructed his movements. And then it's -- then there's the foliage in the park and I'm not sure how the video set up there, but it's going to be a lot more sporadic.\par \par \uc2 But I will say, Neil, I was the head of security at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  after I retired, and we had, among many other things, executive protection to deal with. And CEOs are very high-profile, particularly this one. They face a specific -- they face a peculiar type of threat.\par \par \uc2 I mean, anybody who's disgruntled in the company, any disgruntled customer, he's the focal point -- he or she is the focal point for any threats. And we have handled many of those, sometimes getting restraining orders. So it's interesting to see that he had no executive protection.\par \par \uc2 Somebody knew exactly where he was going to be and when and handled it fairly professionally with a pretty professional shooting position and cleared his gun pretty professionally, which is -- not everybody can do that under stress.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it does raise questions about how that information might have been shared, Paul, because this was around 6:45 in the morning. How would the shooter know that the CEO was going to go into that building to set up a conference that was planned for later that morning with some shareholders and company officials?\par \par \uc2 And you do then start to raise questions, was he given a heads-up? Were there others aware that this was happening? And he didn't waste a lot of time either before or after. What are you to -- what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Well, he's certainly very calm.\par \par \uc2 I would argue that he walked that route previously. He knew where to go and how to go he had the bike waiting for him. So I'm sure they're looking to pull video from before the event...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: ... see if you have somebody who matches his gait and his body type to see if you can I.D. who this person was.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is that, as we just learned, the victim was in town since Monday? And so once again that goes to the idea that the convention is the entry point, because if he's been in town since Monday, he's presumably been out and around town. You would have had many better opportunities, certainly less public opportunities, than 54th and Sixth, near the tree lighting right....\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: ... one of the public and heavily traveled areas of the city.\par \par \uc2 So, very clearly, I think that one of the hard data points you can work off of here is this convention that he was going to seems to be the jumping-off point. And I'm sure the detectives at backtracking from there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it is interesting.\par \par \uc2 I don't know, to Frankie (ph), if we can show this. We're right on Avenue of the Americas, Sixth Avenue, about an avenue away from where all those went down this morning. And it is a crowded city for the tree lighting at Rockefeller Center tonight.\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, the backdrop of this was this planned big event, when we're told upwards of a million make their way into Manhattan up for this. Again, this is a live shot going on right now outside the crime scene. But, again, it's a block away from where I am right now and then the crowds that are building for this event.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder, Chris, the police that authorities continued and did not disrupt plans for the tree lighting, because I would assume this did look and does look like a targeted hit. Otherwise, might they have recommended hold off on this or not?\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: No , from the start they seem very convinced that this was indeed targeted.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: It was a very specific hit, if you will.\par \par \uc2 So I don't see them canceling an event like that. I'm sure they're going to be in a state of hypervigilance. They will be looking for someone that fits his description. It's interesting your photo you just showed about half of his face.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: And five years ago facial recognition might not have been ready for prime time, but it's getting better. And there's enough of his face there that you can -- you might get enough of his features to be able to get some close facial recognition off of some of the video if it's good digital video.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's just one avenue. There's many avenues and a lot of gumshoe worked to do here, but just video is going to play -- it has and probably has already played a significant role.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. It's hard to see it in this banner covering up the bottom part of the shot, but you can clearly make out his hands. They were not covered. They were not gloved and any of that. Obviously, this was a shooting and that would be disruptive.\par \par \uc2 But there's a lot you can make of that, Paul. What do you make of it? You add the silencer. You add this calm approach to this, how he was right after the shooting. I believe at least three shots were fired and then he walked off between this alleyway between 54th and 55th Streets, again, just a few blocks north of where I sit right now.\par \par \uc2 So put all that together and ultimately trying to find this guy. Is he still in the city, do you suspect?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: You know, unfortunately, he gets out of Central Park and he gets to the subway, you jump on the E Train and he's at JFK in 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 And so obviously that would be the nightmare scenario. One of the things I think we should recognize and maybe take off the table a little bit here is this idea of a -- quote, unquote -- "hit man." That's exceedingly rare.\par \par \uc2 What's also exceedingly rare is stranger-on-stranger murders, all right? It's like single digits in New York City, even the city of this size. So it argues that obviously there's a real motive here to target a hit, et cetera. But I would go away from this idea that it's a hit man hired out of Soldier of Fortune magazine or something.\par \par \uc2 I think that even if there is a level of professionalism here, it argues that he's familiar with the gun and know how the thing itself works. He's shooting as he walks. He does manage to jam it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: There's some talk that maybe he used a device that is in addition to the suppressor that does a -- sort of limits the action of the slide, which also makes it even quieter.\par \par \uc2 There's no reporting of the gun spotter activation going off. So all of this is speculation at this point. But, as I said, chances are now you're going to get something out of the victim's life that's going to lead us in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it very closely. Final word on that.\par \par \uc2 Gentleman, I want to thank you both, Paul and Chris. Great help here trying to make sense of this incredible tragedy right here in Midtown Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 I want to take you to Washington, D.C., right now, meanwhile, where, of course, the hunt goes on to approve and ultimately get Cabinet officials approved for \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  , but, in the meantime, the Treasury secretary picked making some news today.\par \par \uc2 We have the latest now from Hillary Vaughn.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, we just had a chance to ask Trump's pick for Treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, a few questions on his way into meetings here on Capitol Hill. We will tell you after the break how that went.\par \par \uc2 But also here on the Hill, Trump's pick for secretary of defense, \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  , is up here fighting for support to keep his nomination alive. We will have an update on all of that after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Can you say Dow 45000?\par \par \uc2 Well, I just did. That's good enough for a record, a gain of better than 308 points, while the Dow was sprinting ahead, because, remember, it was lagging the S &P and the Nasdaq. That also hit records today, so that hat trick a unique event buoyed by a key jobs report, an ADP report that kind of takes a look at the service sector.\par \par \uc2 Some people say it's a good harbinger as steady as she goes growth for the economy without being too inflationary for the big jobs report due out on Friday. But say what you will, and people go back and forth on this. Up, up and away the markets continue to go.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, focusing on Washington, D.C., right now, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trying to get his Cabinet through. There are some bumps along the way. We will talk to Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  about that shortly.\par \par \uc2 First to Hillary Vaughn on what she was hearing from a particular Treasury candidate -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, starting with Trump's pick for defense secretary, \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  , he said that Trump gave him some key advice when he chose him. According to Hegseth, Trump told him -- quote -- "You have to be tough as 'blank,'" something I can't say on TV.\par \par \uc2 So, needless to say, Hegseth is not backing down from this fight, despite unproven accusations swirling related to his drinking and treatment of women, which Hegseth denies and points out are from anonymous sources.\par \par \uc2 He is here on Capitol Hill to make the case face-to-face to every senator that he has qualified for the job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY NOMINEE: I spoke to the president- elect this morning. He said: "Keep going. Keep fighting. Find your own way."\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: So, you're in this all the way?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Why would I back down? I have been always been fighter. I'm here for the war fighters. This is a personal passion for me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But while Hegseth fights on, there are reports indicating Trump might be mulling a possible plan B if Hegseth can't win enough support to bring his nomination off life support.\par \par \uc2 Sources telling FOX that Trump is considering Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as a backup. Trump and DeSantis, according to sources, spoke in person about the idea yesterday. Meanwhile, Trump's pick for Treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, is also here on the Hill for meetings today with senators. He's going to face some questions about Trump's economic plans from tariffs to Bessent's controversial idea of creating a shadow Fed chair.\par \par \uc2 Bessent says that that shadow Fed chair could make \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  basically irrelevant without having to fire Powell. We tried to ask Bessent about that on his way into his meeting today.\par \par \uc2 So, Neil, some Republicans are a little skeptical about this idea. When I tried to ask Bessent about it, he said he's just looking forward to the meetings today. So, we will see how that all shakes out, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I notice that these guys no longer stop to say, oh, my God, it's Hillary Vaughn. They're just prepared for it. They're ready for you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, kudos to you there, but they're not at least running away. I mean, they're just, oh, here she is. Shock of shocks, right?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Not bad. Not bad at all.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hillary, you're the best.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn following all of that,\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  of North Carolina kind enough to join us now. Get his take on how this process is going.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Let me get your take on the \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  defense nomination. Is it your sense that enough of your colleagues don't want to vote for him that his nomination is looking unlikely?\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R -NC): No , I wouldn't say that.\par \par \uc2 I think, first off, we're early in the process. The contacts that are most important now are the members that are on the committee. And Pete and his advisers are just going to have to take a good, hard look at the path on committee and then the path on the floor.\par \par \uc2 I'm really focused on the -- my committees of jurisdiction right now. So I want to talk about Pam Bondi. I want to talk about Kash Patel.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: We will be talking about some of the financial regulators. But I'm staying in those lanes. That's where I'm actually drilling down in the detail.\par \par \uc2 And then we will see. As Pete moves through the process, I'm sure we will have an opportunity to discuss the merits of his nomination at some point in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We're told that president-elect Trump has reached out to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida at a break-the-glass moment if something were to go wrong with the Hegseth nomination. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, I think I'm in the same position with any of the other nominees.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I was on Senate Armed Services until I moved on to Finance Committee in the last Congress. So I'd have been giving you detailed answers to these when I was on the committee of jurisdiction. But I trust Roger Wicker and my colleagues in Senate Armed Services to be on the front lines for those SAS nominations.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. That's politely punting on this question, as you're right to do.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator, let me get -- talk a little bit about the Pam Bondi nomination for attorney general, obviously, maybe an easier fit or maybe an easier approval process