 Well, I think it's unfortunate, considering Canada does $1.3 trillion of trade, more than China, Japan, U.K. and France combined. Ontario alone does $500 billion of two-way trade.\par \par \uc2 And I might add it's equally split down the center. And we're a big auto province here. And parts can go back and forth across the border up to eight times, so the supply chain is very connected.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was making a joke with Trudeau, in which he said, you could always become a state and those problems go away.\par \par \uc2 But what did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 FORD : Well, the president has a good sense of humor. And it's a funny comment. I guess he's still upset that in 1812 we burnt down your White House and he's holding a grudge after 212 years.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORD : But I understand it's -- he's a funny guy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, but I am curious.\par \par \uc2 For whatever people's views on this subject and the individual personalities, the president-elect says that tariffs are the sweetest word to him, they're very effective, they can be used as a cudgel to get what you want out of those who you feel are taking advantage of you.\par \par \uc2 But you can also go too far with these things. And other countries reciprocate with tariffs of their own and it builds on itself. Do you think that this could really explode into a global recession if it gets out of hand?\par \par \uc2 FORD : Well, I hope not, Neil, because you can't compare Canada with any other country in the entire world when it comes to the U.S .\par \par \uc2 We're part of the family, per se. We're the number one trading partner, Ontario alone, to 17 states. We're number two to 11 others. Nine million Americans wake up every single morning to produce products that get shipped up to our province, Ontario. And I think it's a great partnership. It's a great friendship that's gone on for many, many generations.\par \par \uc2 And we want to keep it that way. We're so much stronger when we stick together. I heard the other day, Neil, I think it was yesterday, China came out and talked about the critical minerals. Oh, they're going to cut the U.S . off. Well, who has the critical minerals? Ontario has the critical minerals.\par \par \uc2 And I don't want to give them to anyone, but I will tell you one thing. I want to give them to the U.S . to support them. They need our energy, Americans. We ship 4.3 million barrels every single day down to the U.S . Ontario sends energy, electricity down to New York state, down to Michigan to keep your factories going, keeping the lights on in New York.\par \par \uc2 That's what we have to offer and much more. So we look forward to a mutually rewarding relationship moving forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it very closely.\par \par \uc2 Doug Ford\uc2  , Ontario, Canada, premier.\par \par \uc2 Premier, always good seeing you or hearing you again. It's been a while.\par \par \uc2 FORD : Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Doug Ford\uc2  , the Canada premier.\par \par \uc2 These tariffs are going to be in effect, we are told, with Canada and Mexico. It could extend through much of Asia. So, China is a front-and- center country, but it won't be limited to that, as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  targets what he says are unfair trading practices on the part of friend and foe alike on the global stage. We will see if it does prove to be nearly the cudgel he does get concessions from or something worse.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I was catching my buddy Charles Payne talking to Sam Altman, sort of like the father of A.I., if you will. And one thing that struck me very interesting in that chat is when he was relaying the fact that a lot of people, he was telling Charles this, are going to lose their jobs, a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 Charles is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: People will lose jobs, and there will be this gulf. And do you feel any sort of responsibility or how do you deal with that yourself?\par \par \uc2 SAM ALTMAN\uc2  , CEO, \uc2 OPENAI\uc2  : People will lose jobs. Many new jobs will be created, I think much better jobs.\par \par \uc2 We feel a responsibility to educate society as we see it. We will be right about some things, wrong about others, and to be as good as we can at being stewards of this technology. But not everyone's going to like all of the impacts, but this is coming.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: This is like -- this is a scientific achievement of humanity that is going to get embedded in everything we do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, on that happy note here, you had Sam Altman, the \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO, was saying -- and we cut it off where he said, for television anchors particularly, it's doomsday.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, it was a really fascinating exchange. And Charles is such a great interviewer, but he listened and got a lot out of this guy.\par \par \uc2 Charles, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Charles, of course, the host of "Making Money" on FOX Business and much, much more.\par \par \uc2 I guess there's nothing FOX News Alert there. I get it. People will lose jobs, but he seemed to kind of step back and say, maybe more than we thought. My interpretation was maybe a lot more than we thought. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I agree 1000 percent.\par \par \uc2 The thing is, Neil -- and I told him, I'm a natural Luddite, right? And when Nat Love was around and they were breaking up those weaving machines, I would have been in there with them, right? But we know, historically, all of these evolutions, these industrial revolutions, net-net, have always created new jobs, better-paying jobs, which he alluded to.\par \par \uc2 But it's harder and harder to conceive that with this particular technology. Think about this. Five, 10 years ago, all the rage was teach people how to code. Everyone needs to learn how to code, teach girls how to code, teach people in the disadvantaged communities how to code.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That's the way out of it. A major tech company two weeks ago reported the earnings and said, guess what, 25 percent of our coding was done by A.I.\par \par \uc2 So that was the magic pill. What happens next? A.I. is a threat that, yes, sure, some amazing jobs are going to be created and people who master it will be better at their jobs, but there's going to be a big, big point where a lot of folks will be disadvantaged.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I always think too, for those who want to jump on an investment bandwagon, anything connected with A.I., whether you're talking about \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  , the chips that really power us to that, and all the companies, \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  , buying this stuff from them, billions and billions of dollars, do you ever get a sense we're getting ahead of our skis here, that this is -- yes, it's a great promise tomorrow, but are we sort of overplaying?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I know Wall Street feels that way though, Neil, and you hear many experts on Wall Street say that, but, today, in today's session, the number one stock was a company called \uc2 Salesforce.com\uc2  , a software company.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That stock was down 16 percent earlier this year because they were getting shut out of the A.I. game.\par \par \uc2 Last night, the CEO laid it on thick, said we have an A.I. agent that's going like gangbusters. Yesterday, the number one stock of every stock in the market was a small company called Credo, A.I. once again. Last week, a little retailer, a bath and salts kind of company, stock exploded. They said we were able to integrate technology, A.I.\par \par \uc2 And so companies that are