lly. I mean, you dressed up for it. It's a pity Sam did not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That was his best sweat suit, though.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What is it with all these guys? The ties, jackets, we don't bother, right?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's the whole deal.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We're old.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we are old. Thank you, my friend. And I'm older than you.\par \par \uc2 All right, Charles Payne, he is one of the nicest people on the planet. Also, he works hard on the show. He's always walking and running around. My staff tries to get me to do that. I say, no, no, I like it sitting down.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY MADDREY, CHIEF OF DEPARTMENT, NEW YORK CITY POLICE DEPARTMENT: With an incident like this, of course, we put a heavy deployment into Manhattan. We put additional resources out. We have the drones up. We have aviation out. We have canine out. When an incident like this happens, we don't spare any expense. We put all of our resources out there to support the chief of detective's mission and to keep New Yorkers safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so New Yorkers are safe, that this was a targeted attack on the CEO of UnitedHealthcare. He was killed this morning, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  , only 50 years old, two young kids.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of open questions as to what happened and why and where is the guy who calmly left the scene after shooting him dead?\par \par \uc2 Maureen O'Connell a lot more on this and some good perspective, the retired \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent.\par \par \uc2 Maureen, where is the shooter now, you suspect?\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O'CONNELL, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Well, I think he went into the park, which was a really good strategic move, and that would fall under the category of this was a hit, for sure, and he meant to do so.\par \par \uc2 There were all kinds of things in place that lended to be that. But, as far as it being a hired hit man, there are things that go against that theory. But, right now, I'd say he went into the park. And, in that backpack, he probably had a change of clothing. He changed his clothing and he went about his way.\par \par \uc2 The problem he's going to have is, from what we have seen of the surveillance photographs of him, he's got a rather distinctive nose and facial structure just from what we saw. And I'm more confident that the -- I'm very confident that if anyone can solve this, it's the fine men and women in the \uc2 New York Police Department\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting. I looked at the timeline on all of this, Maureen. The shooter shows up a little bit before 6:40 in the morning, right? So he obviously knew that Thompson was going to be crossing the street from his hotel to get to this hotel where this company event, this business event was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 And he didn't stay there very long. He timed it. I just -- so someone had to clue him in when he would be there. And I only mention that to you, Maureen, because the CEO was there since Monday. Well, what are we to make of all of that?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: You're right.\par \par \uc2 When you think about it, he's staying at the hotel across the street. How did the guy know that?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: He had to enter in through the front door. So that's the choke point he's looking at. And these are all the things that fall under the category of inside job, had to have all kinds of information, which I would agree with when it came to this particular piece of information.\par \par \uc2 However, when you look at the actual video of the shooting, he could be using what Paul Mauro alluded to earlier, which is a Hush Puppy, which is a suppression, a noise suppression device, a silencer that's put on this gun.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: But his first round goes into his leg. He's only a few feet away -- a few feet behind the intended victim. And he shoots him in the leg, which is not indicative of a pro.\par \par \uc2 And he also leaves three live rounds on the sidewalk, as well as three shell casings. Now, with -- he was just walking along like he was taking his dog out. He could have picked up the rounds, or at least the live rounds.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Because when a round cycles through a weapon, oftentimes, the fingerprints in large part are burned off the shell casing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: However, live rounds, even if he's using gloves in his workshop, it could be you could find DNA and/or fingerprints on those.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: So I think those live rounds are going to be very interesting. And I also -- one of the...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Maureen, I'm jumping on you rudely.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: OK.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020241205ekc40002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Leading On The World Stage; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Giving Away All Our Money; DOGE Caucus Taking Shape In The House; Democrats Turn To Downplaying Trump's Historic Victory; NYC Mayor Says He Will Work With Trump And Homan; Daniel Penny Faces Up To 15 Years In Prison; Jury To Decide Penny's Fate In Chokehold Case; Senate Democrats Re-elect Schumer As Leader; Workers Abuse Company Credit Cards; Poll Shows That Gen Z Considers $587k Annually As Successful\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Katie Pavlich, Jeanine Pirro, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 7,985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the "Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. Trump's early power flex. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 All right, tonight, the Trump transition is abuzz with activity. Not only has he moved with lightning speed to fill out his cabinet and other senior positions, he's preparing for a triumphant return to Europe on Saturday for the reopening of Notre Dame. Now, remember, it was badly damaged by a catastrophic fire back in 2019 and many doubted it could ever really be restored. So, it's fitting that Macron invited Trump to the ceremony, because not long ago, that's what all the Euro and American elites thought about him and the populist movement that they were damaged beyond repair.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, it's a fitting end to an era that has been characterized by lawlessness.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC ANCHOR: It may just be the last dying gasp of Trumpism. They're dead. They just don't know it politically. We have seen time and again. 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2022, voters reject Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It never gets old. All right, now let's never forget their drumbeat, which was meant to demoralize Trump supporters. Now, after the 2020 election, intellectuals with impressive credentials told the world not to worry that Trump would eventually fade away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DORIS KEARNS GOODWIN, AUTHOR, LEADERSHIP IN TURBULENT TIMES: When he's out of power, and that oxygen isn't there day-by-day, I think there will be a diminution of the power that he holds over the other people in his party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thanks. Doris. Other academics were sure the grown-ups in the GOP would sideline Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIALL FERGUSON\uc2 , SENIOR FELLOW, STANFORD'S HOOVER INSTITUTION: I think what we're seeing at the moment is the Republican Party establishment summering (ph) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all the way out of the White House. And I think they will then make sure that whatever happens, he is not the nominee in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Never will be the nominee again ever. Well, same deal over in Europe, where even pre-COVID, they tried to wish the appeal of populism away, noting \uc2 Le Pen\uc2 's setbacks, and later that year, the Trump effect was supposed to transform Europe, but it hasn't. That was that title, or they cited the COVID-19 pandemic, has COVID killed, killed off populism in Europe? All of it was wrong.\par \par \uc2 Just like the war hawks were wrong a few months ago, trying to scare voters about Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMB. MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: First of all, the world is scared to death of the return of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and it goes back to the 1930s. They worry that we're just going to quit on the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Doesn't it all go back to the 1930s? They're out of ideas. So, right, Trump, the peacemaker was going to be the threat to the world. Now it's entirely fitting that while Trump's fielding invites and calls from world leaders, Biden is in Africa. Is this what we call a guided tour. And his sense of humor is no better than his sense of direction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We, Biden's are like poor relatives. We show up when we're invited, stay longer than we should, eat all your food and don't know when to go home. But you've been very, very generous and hospitable. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Guy on the right in the back fell asleep. Now, he said they've been generous in Africa. Wait, Biden was busy celebrating Giving Tuesday with our money.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm announcing over $1 billion in new humanitarian support for African displaced from homes by historic droughts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now this seemed to be part compensation for slavery, part for climate change, with the usual bad writing, bad jokes and bad delivery.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As you know, I'm in the final weeks of my presidency. You don't have to clap for that. You can, if you want. Because although I don't know exactly what the future will hold, I know the future runs through Angola, through Africa. I mean it sincerely. I'm not kidding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: God's honest truth, Dr Jill. Now, I watched most of this tragic address, and its apologetic patronizing tone was yet another reminder of why Trump won. Now, although he's all about strategic partnerships, Trump is, with other countries when they benefit the United States, it's never done with cringy obsequiousness. No, instead, Trump operates from a position of strength.\par \par \uc2 Remember, for years before he ran for President, he bemoaned how politicians let America get ripped off and taken advantage of, by other countries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 , TV PERSONALITY: What would you do differently, Donald?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND CURRENT PRESIDENTIAL-ELECT: I'd make our allies, forgetting about the enemies. The enemies you can't talk to so easily. I'd make our allies pay their fair share. We're a debtor nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, boy, he was right then, and he's right now. Why shouldn't we be America First? As we saw during COVID, China put China first. Same in Germany when German leaders banned the export of its PPE to protect its citizens first. I don't forget any of this. They've always had a strong Trump like protectionist strain in Europe. Look at their trade policy, slapping exports and other restrictions on American dairy agriculture, wine and cars, all meant to protect European farmers and automobile manufacturers. It's Europe first.\par \par \uc2 Now, why should we blame them? We use our leverage. They use theirs. What's the point of having countries if they don't put their own people first? That's always been the point of Trump and other people who agree with them. But listening to Biden today, it sounded more like Angola first. Countries with their hands out love having a weak American president like Biden in office. Remember they were positively giddy in 2020.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FEDERICA MOGHERINI\uc2 , FORMER EUROPEAN UNION FOREIGN POLICY CHIEF: It's a great feeling. It's a feeling of relief to see America back as of January, we will feel much less lonely than we have felt in this last four years. It has been very difficult four years and yes, the feeling of hostility has been there, in particular towards the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , because it was very clear to me that President Trump didn't particularly appreciate or like multilateral experiments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Multilateral experiments. Yes, you bet he did not like them, because we always got the short end of the stick. Now with Joe, though, this is true, what's happening is the free writing never ends, and because elites at home and abroad believe we are a fundamentally evil country, existentially racist, they hate Trump's America First approach. Punishment is what they prefer for our middle class, not prosperity. But the truth is, open borders and unfair trade have been a disaster for the average working person. And from Syracuse, New York to Malmo, Sweden, it's taken a wrecking ball to countries, cultures, safety and harmony. Populism has not failed. Globalization has failed.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the French government is on the verge of collapse, yet Argentina is coming back under Trump loving \uc2 Javier Milei\uc2 , the populist Italian Prime Minister \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2  is a rising star in Europe. The Euro elites and American Left may not be happy tonight, but the working people whom they look down upon, whose concerns they ignored for decades may finally have a fighting chance, and that's the Angle on this Giving Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Elbridge Colby, former Deputy Assistant Secretary of State under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Missouri Senator Eric Schmitt. Elbridge, let's start with you. What should Trump's first order of business be?\par \par \uc2 ELBRIDGE COLBY, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, great to be with you, Laura, I think you hit the nail on the head. I think one of the key things that he's laid out is trying to end the war in Ukraine, but overall, bringing back the kind of leverage in getting our allies to do more of their fair share, and trying to get peace in other parts of the world and prevent war. And I think you're absolutely right. You've seen that. You've seen Xi Jinping, according to Wall Street Journal, is trying to get a phone call. Sheinbaum and Trudeau, the worst of the Lefty progressive faddish types is also, you know, sort of catering to, I think, the kind of the strength that President Trump brings, the clarity, the understanding of leverage, and the willingness to use it for the American people.\par \par \uc2 So you're already seeing these results, and it can't happen soon enough, because, as we've discussed, Biden on the way out the door, he's not just giving money away in Angola. He's deepening the war in Ukraine when it's not even going to work, and when our weapons stockpiles, by their own admission, are already you know, bare.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator Schmitt, $725 million greenlit yesterday, giving Monday. It's giving every day for the Biden administration. But as funny as funny as it can be, this is not funny, because people are going to end up more people dead because of these weapons that we're sending over there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): It's the last gasp, I think, of a failed foreign policy that's going on now for generations, this Wilsonian adventurism that the Biden administration is deeply involved with, almost sort of baiting Putin to, you know, overreact to extend a war that we know has to come to an end that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has pledged on the campaign trail to end peacefully, right. And return us to a sense of American realism, where our foreign policy is based on what is America's core national interest? How do we protect the homeland? He's already talking about an Iron Dome for America, and also understanding our real threats in the Indo Pacific with China and so our European allies, it's time for them to step up in a more meaningful way. We've subsidized the welfare state in Europe for decades now, and --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Free riders. I'm sorry, they're free riding us. They have for decades. I mean, when did World War II end just like 75, 80 years ago, and we're still picking up the lion share of the security burden.\par \par \uc2 SCHMITT: And their industrial base has 