e mug shot?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything you're going to be able to see live on TV. They're going to be mug shots. They're going to be fingerprints.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O'DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be fingerprinted. He will be photographed and end up with a mug shot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A picture that will live in infamy, the first mug shot of an American president slashed across the front pages this morning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, he turned that into a huge coup for himself, because he's smarter than they are. Of course, what about all the taxpayer dollars that were spent on these fraudulent cases? We'll never get it back, and we'll never really know how much was spent. Newsweek estimated that what about 50 million or so was spent by just Jack Smith's team. That's an understatement.\par \par \uc2 So what we saw was a serious, concerted effort to use all the resources of the federal government to put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in prison for the rest of his life, and it was all because they wanted to prevent you, the people, from having your say, and, of course, him from being president.\par \par \uc2 So history is going to remember Jack Smith and \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  and the judges who let these cases get as far as they did, as modern day Keystone cops who chased Trump around, hounding him, trying to keep him off the campaign trail because they hated his agenda. They hate everything he stands for. So it's a mistake, though, to write them off as all incompetence. Their teams were backed by some of the smartest and toughest, meanest lawyers out there, they work nights and weekends and through holidays all to put him behind bars and subvert this election.\par \par \uc2 The only reason \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not in jail now is because of you, the voters, and of course, Trump himself. Imagine if Trump wasn't Trump, forget about it. Most people would have folded like a $20 lawn chair. He fought like his freedom depended on it, but mostly because our freedom depended on it. If Harris had won this sick weaponization of our justice system would have been validated, at least for now. It's on ice.\par \par \uc2 But despite these setbacks, the Angle does not believe the Democrats have given up on this lawfare strategy. In a statement reacting to the dismissal, Adam Schiff today ripped the system, saying, if Democrats, you know, basically saying that the system itself failed, failed the people, failed to do what it was supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 And I'm telling you, if the Democrats somehow managed to take the House in 2026 and they could, it is highly probable that they will do everything in their power, stay with me here, to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again. You think I'm kidding? Well, we'll see.\par \par \uc2 And if Democrats win in 2028, I mean they're just going to resume the case that we're talking about tonight, and they'll probably try to go after Vance as well and anyone else connected to Trump. This is why the DOJ has to be cleaned out. I think Pam Bondi can do it. The Democrats and their Never Trump poodles have done enormous damage to these institutions, and they will do it all again. They do not care. There should be, however, consequences where appropriate, so that this never happens again. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2 , Executive Vice President of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Eric, I'm going to get to that Trump, your dad's posting on Truth Social about the tariffs in regard to that migrant caravan in a moment. But how is your dad feeling tonight, knowing that, at least for now, this Jack Smith case is over.\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, THE TRUMP ORGANIZATION: Yes, well, Laura, the irony of Jack Smith is, I mean, he brought this case to try and make sure my father never got elected. And I actually think it was Jack Smith who might go down as one of the very reasons my father got elected. Right? It's kind of the law of unintended consequences. But I think the only thing I disagree with you on this case is dead. The case got dismissed. The case will not get revived. By the time my father is out of office, the statute of limitations will be long gone. You can't have double jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Laura, this case is dead. It's not coming back, and that man on the screen right there will go down as a national embarrassment. He will go down as a figure who literally tried to subvert democracy, who tried to put a president in jail for no reason to benefit his political class. He's an embarrassment to the DOJ. He's an embarrassment to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He's an embarrassment to law enforcement. He's an embarrassment to this nation, and I promise you, history will not reflect kindly on this guy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, I'm telling you, Eric, the same people who thought they could turn the 14th Amendment into a piece of silly putty and kind of just twist it and change it to mean something else, they will try to pause the statute of limitations. I'm telling you this -- I don't put anything past them yet in a normal person's mind, that's what would happen. But these people are sick. I actually believe they're sick and twisted.\par \par \uc2 Eric, I want to talk about your father's posting on Truth Social because we've seen an epic run in the stock market since his election again to the presidency. I'm a big tariff person. Everybody knows that. I've been very pro tariff, my entire career, but this series of caravans, and apparently there will be another one building we understand tonight, coming to the United States, Mexico knows they're coming and is not doing anything to stop it? Tariffs, the answer.\par \par \uc2 E. TRUMP: Absolutely, Laura, I've talked about fentanyl every single day of the campaign trail. Is something that affects me great. I have four friends who have lost children based on fentanyl. I took my son, my daughter in the football game yesterday, the Dolphins game, and they were sitting on my lap, and the guy next to me turns around looking at my son and said, a couple months ago, I lost my son. They killed him. They killed him with fentanyl. I lost my little boy. Cherish every single moment that you have, and he's literally looking at my son sitting on my lap at his first \uc2 NFL\uc2  football game. And it broke my heart.\par \par \uc2 When my father sent out that tweet, you want to allow drugs to come through our southern border. We're going to tariff you. You want to allow them come through Canada. We're going to tariff you. China, if you're going to allow this stuff to get sold in our country, largely produced in China, we're going to add another 10 percent to your chair. We are going to cost your countries, your economies. We're going to cost your businesses billions, hundreds of billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 If you think you're going to -- you're going to poison Americans. It's not going to happen. You're not going to destroy our youth; you're not going to destroy our society. You're not going to destroy our families. You're not going to do it. It's going to cost you. I'm going to put an end to it.\par \par \uc2 Laura, when my father sent that 20 minutes ago, I literally cheered out loud. There's nothing more twisted and perverse than what the cartels have done, what China has done, and you know what the Biden and Harris have literally been absent. They don't even talk about it. They haven't even talked about the fentanyl crisis that is killing so many people. Laura, it's 320,000 people. 320,000 kids died --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But this was their strategy to flood the country --\par \par \uc2 E. TRUMP: You have police departments --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- with work, with new people.\par \par \uc2 E. TRUMP: Laura, you have police departments in this country that are -- you have police departments in this country that are running out of Narcan. That's how bad the problem is, right? They can't keep it in stock. People are dying by the thousands, and this is exactly why the American people voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They want a guy who will take this kind of action. They want a guy who will literally be very transparent before he's even in office, he's calling these countries out. I'm telling you; he's going to -- he's going to stop the problem. And this is exactly how you use tariffs. This is exactly how you use --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I have a feeling Mexico will find a way to stop those migrant caravans coming across the board. Eric, really quickly, the report, report by John Solomon today about Boris Epshteyn, an advisor to your father, longtime advisor, internal investigation by the Trump transition, when they heard allegations from Scott Bessent and others that Boris Epshteyn was allegedly we have not confirmed this was trying to shake down people for access, for contracts, for money. Any comments on this tonight?\par \par \uc2 E. TRUMP: Yes, listen, I've known Boris for years, and I've never known him to be anything but a good human being. So, that said, I will tell you, my father has been incredibly clear. You do not, you do not do that in under any circumstance, and believe me, there will be repercussions if somebody was so, I've never seen that side of him. I've known him for a very long time, and I certainly hope the reporting is false, and I can also tell you if it's true, you know, the person will probably no longer be around.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Eric, thank you very much tonight. And joining me now, David Schoen, a former Trump impeachment lawyer. David, we'll get back to that a moment. But Jack Smith has left the door open. It seems to try to charge Trump after he leaves office. But to Eric's point, doesn't -- wouldn't the statute of limitations have run by then, or is there a way for them to argue that it should be basically suspended?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHOEN, FORMER TRUMP IMPEACHMENT ATTORNEY: Well, first of all, I hope that Eric is right, and as a practical matter, it won't ever be brought, but I'm afraid your skepticism is also right. There is a provision in the 2000 -- October 16, 2000, \uc2 OLC\uc2  memo that suggests that on footnotes 33 to 35 there could be an equitable tolling of the statute of limitations in the extraordinary case, it would be an abuse here. I think the Department of Justice can act to make sure that doesn't happen. I think Congress happen. I think Congress can also.\par \par \uc2 I think there's an invitation to Congress in that -- in those footnotes, for that to happen. But you know, you said earlier how the Smith's team worked day and night. You've never seen anything like this. It's even worse than that quite frankly, I've never seen anything like the shadow prosecution team that operated here, people like Andrew Weissman, Norm Eisen, Joyce Vance, Danya Perry, they wrote model prosecution memos in fact in the D.C. case, suggesting that many of them suggested that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be charged with insurrection. That was even too much for Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, even though the case is dismissed. Smith, as were noting here, could still try to damage Trump with this, speaking of your buddy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There definitely will be a report, because the Special Counsel rules require Jack Smith to write a report. There's also a report that initially is given by the Special Counsel to the Attorney General. In this case, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , in secret. The Special Counsel has no power himself to make it public, but \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, David, could at this point Jack Smith be spiteful enough to try to file a report and or leak elements of what would have been a report