eds to succeed?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think the American public cares who does the background checks. What the American public cares about is to see the mandate that they voted in delivered upon. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I think the American public has got great concerns about how weaponized it's become.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief at The Federalist, and Mary Katharine Ham, both are Fox News Contributors. Mollie, what is this really all about?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE FEDERALIST: Well, there are two different issues here. One is that you do want to vet people who are going to be in the administration. The President-elect has an interest in making sure that these people are good. The Senate has an advise and consent role from the U.S. Constitution, but the idea that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  gets to exert veto power over the President's nominees after the last eight years of what they've done. You might remember the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . You know \uc2 James Comey\uc2  ran the Russia collusion hoax. They sent agents over to entrap the National Security Advisor. They leaked like sibs with any information they had.\par \par \uc2 It's a complete -- completely corrupted department. There are still good people there, but there, but overall, it's a very corrupt agency. And the Department of Justice, of course, like you just talked about throughout the top part of the show was engaged in trying to imprison and bankrupt \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the idea that these are people who could in any way that whether a candidate is good or not, that they would be trusted to do that, it's just, it's, it's laughable.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Mary Katharine, I mean, you got Daniel Goldman from New York, who was always known as just the font of objectivity and unbiased analysis. He was speaking out about the Senate's role here. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BE