a ton of headaches going to get to folks. We have already ongoing systems. This is storm system number two that's impacting the West Coast. This is today.\par \par \uc2 And then storm system number one across the Great Lakes, primarily this first round going to be just that rain. It'll be through the Northeast by the time you get into Wednesday, or even Thanksgiving Day, but then storm system number two poses a threat. Now, we already have winter storm watches and warnings that are in place for the Rockies, portions of the Northern Rockies as well.\par \par \uc2 All that activity is going to head farther off towards the East Coast. The Great Lakes, that could also mean some instances for icing as well. We're right at that temperature profile. We're not quite at 32 degrees for some spots for a long duration. And that's where we see some issues.\par \par \uc2 For Wednesday, which actually is one of the busiest travel days of the entire year, AAA estimating 80 million people-plus traveling at least 50 miles away from home, whether that be on the car or jumping on a flight, a lot of rain, not only for the Northeast and the Great Lakes, but also extending into the Southeast as well, as that cold front associated with that low for storm system number two is going to be long-stretching.\par \par \uc2 So even in the south, you might want to pack a couple of layers if you're traveling to see some family members. Might be a little bit surprising by how chilly it can be. One airport that is going to be one to watch going to be Atlanta, the biggest airport in the country, and the rain is going to be widespread.\par \par \uc2 The biggest issues for travel, especially for airlines, going to be wind and rain. Now, a little bit of rain, nothing too bad to contend with, but any kind of delay when you see this amount of people is going to create this backlog and further other folks' flights.\par \par \uc2 Now, for airlines, it's people traveling into the smaller feeder airports that are the first to get delayed and canceled. So at least keep that in mind if you're traveling to maybe a road less traveled by for some of your spots.\par \par \uc2 Going into Black Friday, the Friday after Thanksgiving, more of that snowy mix, especially for the Great Lakes, where we could be looking at a lake- effect snow event, and maybe an abundance for some spots,the biggest lake- effect snow event we have seen yet this year, but we're only ramping up the winter storm season.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Haley trying to see if this holds off.\par \par \uc2 So for this holiday season, FOX Weather is the best gift you can give. Well, you know this. Scan the code to download the app for free or find us on your favorite connected device FOX Weather is your winter storm H.Q., always free, always on.\par \par \uc2 So nearly 80 million people, as you just heard, expected to travel this holiday week, that could be a new record, as a strike by airport workers in North Carolina throws a wrench into things.\par \par \uc2 Now, Steve Harrigan is at Hartsfield-Jackson Airport in Atlanta, where Haley said that's a place to watch.\par \par \uc2 Steve, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So far, so good here. It's 70 degrees and sunny in Atlanta. Of course, that could change.\par \par \uc2 There was an incident earlier today at Boston's Logan Airport. Two planes touched wings on the ground. An \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  plane was being towed into the gate, made contact with a \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  plane that was parked. No one was injured. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is investigating how this happened.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, TSA gearing up for its busiest week of the year, prepared to screen 18 million passengers who are going to be flying during the Thanksgiving week. That's up 5 percent from one year ago, the busiest days likely to be Wednesday and Sunday. The goal for TSA here in Atlanta is to get travelers through the TSA screening process in under 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 But some veteran travelers we spoke to this morning say they don't plan on taking any chances.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come here as early as possible, sit around, grab something to eat, and wait. At least I have that less stress to worry about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: And you mentioned the strike in Charlotte. This is airport service workers.\par \par \uc2 They're going out on strike for higher wages. This is, of course, a hub for \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , so that could also produce major delays -- Edward, back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thanks, Steve.\par \par \uc2 Administration expected to screen, as you said, more than 18 million people over a seven-day holiday stretch, with a record three million expected on the return home on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 So joining us now is TSA Administrator David Pekoske. He joins us from Reagan National Airport.\par \par \uc2 Now, David, first, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 And, originally, I want to get into this first, but you were appointed by President Trump, reappointed by President Biden. So you must be doing something right. Now, before we get into this, your term is up in 2027. Have you heard from the Trump transition team about staying on?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEKOSKE, ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 TRANSPORTATION SECURITY ADMINISTRATION\uc2 : No, we really haven't had much communication with the transition team just yet, but I expect we will over the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: All right.\par \par \uc2 So how do you handle 18 million people over a seven-day holiday stretch without huge wait times?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: Well, this is our Super Bowl. It's the busiest time of the year for us. Our busiest day almost always in a given year is the Sunday following Thanksgiving. We expect that to be the same this year.\par \par \uc2 So the busiest days are going to be tomorrow, Wednesday, and then Sunday, like you mentioned, about three million passengers.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, and you know how this goes. You have to be on top of it every time. Are there enough TSA officers to safely screen everyone in a timely manner?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: No, we have a work force that's bigger than it's ever been. So we have enough officers to handle the volumes that we project.\par \par \uc2 We just encourage people to give themselves a little bit of extra time. And if you're a standard passenger, about 30 minutes or less, you should expect in a wait. But if you're a PreCheck passenger, you can rely on that being 10 minutes or less across the entire system.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So are there certain areas of the country then you anticipate a larger surge of travelers over other areas in the country?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: Well, generally, across the country, we expect the major hub airports to have the larger surge. And that's where you will see it's going to be most crowded in the airport environment.\par \par \uc2 But we're staffed to maintain those wait time standards the same across the entire country.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So what about the strike in North Carolina? Is that going to impact what you're doing?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: It won't impact what we're doing, but we work really closely with the airports and with the air carriers to make sure that we're all coordinated to be able to handle any unexpected things that happen during the course of a given day.\par \par \uc2 And so we will just monitor how this strike action continues and staff and adjust accordingly.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: David, I want to throw this at you.\par \par \uc2 We have been talking about the amount of people who have crossed the southern border illegally; 382 of those people encountered were on the terror watch list and they were stopped over the past four years. The homeland security commission -- homeland security commission in the House says about two million people were got-aways in that time.\par \par \uc2 So with this many people we're talking about and we don't know who they are, how do you ensure that no one is getting on a plane who has been on that terror watch list or means to do harm?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: Well, first, we verify the identity of travelers before they even begin the screening process. So the very first thing we do is verify identity.\par \par \uc2 And if we have any questions with respect to a passenger's identity, they always receive additional screening. And so that's a way that we mitigate that risk. And then we have a very technologically advanced screening system and expert officers in all of our checkpoints to make sure that travelers as they travel and they get on board their aircraft are going to be safe and secure and safely get to their destination.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Have you done tests leading up to this to test the security?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: Oh, we test our system all the time. There are tests that occur throughout the entire year, both on a national level and a local level.\par \par \uc2 And what that does is, it helps us identify, if there are any vulnerabilities that are developing, to quickly close those vulnerabilities.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, how important -- you talk about this being the Super Bowl.\par \par \uc2 How important is it -- well, we know it's important to get it right, but how important is it for you guys to do it in a timely fashion? I mean, you have seen the lines in years past through the airports. How important is that speed in terms of the -- and in terms of the accuracy?\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: Well, we don't measure the length of a line per se. We measure the wait in terms of time.\par \par \uc2 And so we do have those standards, so for a standard passenger, non- PreCheck, 30 minutes or less, for a PreCheck passenger, 10 minutes or less. I check that data all the time and all of the TSA senior leadership. If we exceed those standards in any checkpoint across any given day across 433 airports in the country, we get notified of that so we can adjust as quickly as we can to bring additional staff to those airports.\par \par \uc2 But I would expect that we will maintain those wait time standards across the entire holiday period.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: David Pekoske, thank you for your time there at National -- Reagan National Airport. And, hopefully, things go as smoothly as you have laid out.\par \par \uc2 Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PEKOSKE: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, special counsel Jack Smith moving to dismiss the election interference and classified document cases against president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To David Spunt in Washington digging deeper into the moves today -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Edward, this was not unexpected, but to see it after more than two years of work, it is quite something.\par \par \uc2 Special counsel Jack Smith in 21 pages is asking federal judges to dismiss both cases. Smith was supposed to go to trial back in March in the D.C. election interference case. It was not meant to be once the Supreme Court got involved. Once \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the election, it was game over for Smith.\par \par \uc2 We reported right after the election Smith would drop the case in D.C. and step down to special counsel before Trump is inaugurated. Why? Because the Justice Department cannot prosecute a sitting president.\par \par \uc2 He writes: "It has long been the position of the Department of Justice at the United States Constitution forbids the federal indictment and subsequent criminal prosecution of a sitting president. But the department and the country have never faced the circumstance here where a federal indictment against a private citizen has been returned by a grand jury and a criminal prosecution is already under way when the defendant is elected president."\par \par \uc2 A similar situation in the classified documents case on appeal after the judge dismissed that case earlier this summer. Smith is asking the appeals court to also dismiss this case in Florida,given the fact that Trump won the election. He's not asking to dismiss the case against Trump's two co- defendants in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Now, Smith does go on to note that the outcome is not based on merits or strength of the case against Trump, meaning he believes he had the evidence to convict Trump in both courts of law, but, again, he won't prosecute a sitting president.\par \par \uc2 Trump posting on social media: "These cases, like all the other cases I have been forced to go through, are empty and lawless and should never have been brought."\par \par \uc2 We now wait rulings from the circuit court judges in Atlanta and Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2  in D.C., who are all expected to dismiss both cases. After all, Jack Smith is asking them to do so, and, of course, the defense agrees they should too -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: David Spunt, appreciate it from Washington, on top of everything there. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, forget senators. President-elect Trump's Treasury pick is getting a green light from investors. And we're on it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a warning for Americans' enemies from Trump's top man on the National Security Council. Why some here say the message from \uc2 Mike Waltz\uc2  cannot be shared enough.\par \par \uc2 And the border crisis front and center today, as incoming border czar Tom Homan prepares to hit Eagle Pass, Texas, tomorrow. Bill Melugin will tell us what he's learning about the changes that are yet to come.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: FOX on top of stocks on the way up with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his Treasury pick, Scott Bessent, on the way in, the Dow closing at an all-time high, second day in a row.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Business' Hillary Vaughn at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Why do they like him so much, Hillary Vaughn?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Well, because he is one of their own, Scott Bessent has a long to-do list and not a lot of time to do it. He is going to be the person that helps get president-elect Trump's economic agenda through, from cutting taxes to increasing energy production and gutting government waste and getting America's debt under control.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OMEED MALIK, FOUNDER AND PRESIDENT, 1789 CAPITAL: Scott is definitely someone that the market likes. He's going to be on it with the monetary policy, cutting, creating pro-growth policies, and obviously is on the president's agenda with tariffs as well. That's all going to make the markets rip.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: On the top of the to-do list, Bessent says he's going to work to cut taxes on tips, overtime, and Social Security, while working to make President Trump's tax cuts of 2017 permanent.\par \par \uc2 He also has a 3-3-3 approach to the economy with goals to cut the budget deficit down to 3 percent of GDP by 2028, push economic growth to 3 percent through a pro-business tax policy, and push the U.S. to produce three million barrels of oil per day.\par \par \uc2 Republicans on Capitol Hill are cheering Trump's pick, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  saying of Bessent -- quote -- "No one understands the America first approach to reviving our economy, getting a handle on our debt, and enforcing our trade laws better than Scott Bessent. President Trump's economic agenda is in good hands with Scott Bessent."\par \par \uc2 But some Democrats don't like his ties to Wall Street. Senator Elizabeth Warren saying -- quote -- "I do not know if Mr. Bessent will transfer his loyalty from Wall Street investors to America's workers, but I am willing to work with anyone to advance the interests of working families."\par \par \uc2 But even some Democrats admit Bessent's up for the job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON FURMAN\uc2 , FORMER CHAIR, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: He's serious. He's mainstream. He's smart. The open question is, will he -- how will he be advising the president and will the president listen to him? Because, ultimately, all these economic decisions are the president's call.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Trump's energy plans are already taking shape. According to Reuters, the transition team is already prepping a massive energy package. The plan includes increasing export permits for new LNG projects and increasing oil drilling offshore and on federal land.\par \par \uc2 This energy package, they plan to roll out within days of president-elect Trump taking office -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Thanks, Hillary, at the White House.\par \par \uc2 It appears that most Americans approve of president-elect Trump's transition plans and are looking forward to what his team can do, especially when it comes to the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 So let's get the read from Bill McGurn at The Wall Street Journal and Taylor Riggs, co-host of "The Big Money Show" on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 So Taylor, ladies first and the fact that you work at FOX Business. President-elect Trump moved quickly with his Cabinet. He's gearing up for a series of quick actions, getting his vision to move forward. How long do you think Americans will give him to see change before the mood sours if he doesn't deliver?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR RIGGS, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: You know, I asked that question to \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2  over at \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2  and formerly on Trump's sort of economic team.\par \par \uc2 And he thinks that a lot of things can happen day one, particularly with the great pick. And I agree with some of the commentary that you came into this on Scott Bessent. There's a lot of executive orders he can do on day one to help start the drilling machine, right, to start boosting economic growth,a lot of that we're seeing in A.I. and productivity.\par \par \uc2 So I do think that, instead of 100 days, \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2  was saying, he needs a 100-hour agenda because he's so laser-focused on really delivering for the American people. I think Scott Bessent is certainly a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And we will see if the numbers move that fast.\par \par \uc2 So, Bill, similar question to you. Americans seem to be saying, let's turn the page from the last four years, but what's the grace period to see results?\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I agree with Taylor. I think it could be very swift.\par \par \uc2 For example, on energy, an important component of the economy, our policies are so backward now. We subsidize electric vehicles that no one wants to buy at market price. We penalize fossil fuels that people need to power their cars, everything. He can move fast just with common sense on energy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that could have an effect. Mostly what I'm encouraged by is the word growth, that tax cuts and so forth. The one area that's kind of a discordant note is tariffs, which are not really pro-growth. But if he uses them as what he said, a negotiating tool, OK, we will see what happens then.\par \par \uc2 But the rest of the agenda is geared to having growth. And we had the growth the last time he's in office. He has to do it again.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, so Trump's pick for Treasury secretary, Scott Bessent liking a 3-by-3-by-3 plan, calling cuts to the deficit to 3 percent -- cutting the deficit to 3 percent of GDP, using taxes to spur 3 percent growth, and producing an additional three million barrels of oil every day in America.\par \par \uc2 So with president-elect Trump reportedly looking at a wide range of energy package to boost production, as Taylor talked about, Taylor, I have talked with a number of people who believe that energy is the key. You reduce the price of oil, thus the price of gas comes down, and will almost immediately impact inflation.\par \par \uc2 So do you think these will actually lower prices? Because inflation and prices can be two different things. What do you think, Taylor?\par \par \uc2 RIGGS: Yes, so that's been one of my bigger questions, is, can you have deflation without a recession? And a lot of people think that actually you can, right?\par \par \uc2 If you lower oil prices, oil goes into almost everything we think of. Our duct tape, you name it, right, every product that we have has petroleum- based products. So my big question was, can you get deflation, which is actually a drop in prices, not just disinflation, a slowing rate of growth on prices rising, without a recession?\par \par \uc2 And actually a few economists, I think E.J. Antoni over at \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2 , is one of those, right, who thinks that we can do it. If you start drilling more, it brings down the cost of everything, and I think that's what people are really excited for.\par \par \uc2 I think you certainly saw a little bit of hint in that -- in the bond market today. The bond market had a huge sigh of relief today as you're sort of seeing Scott Bessent normalizing sort of what a Treasury secretary could be and really common sense, as Bill mentioned.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Bill, the House speaker's office told me that extending the tax cuts will be among the first bills that they will take up. Do you think the other tax proposals will go into that bill, or are they going to tackle this after in the first two years?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: I don't know. It's a good sign that he's making a priority of the tax cuts. You want to prioritize the growth component, because everyone gets bogged down in the rest.\par \par \uc2 I think, look, there are decisions to be made. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made a lot of promises on cutting taxes, like, for example, on tips and so forth. And the revenue is not really there to do that all. I think the logic of that points to a flat tax. We're going to lower it for everyone and make it cleaner and simpler.\par \par \uc2 That's the direction I hope they go, rather than gunking up the code with even more provisions.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, that might be really tough to get through Congress.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes, it would.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Maybe the flat tax would work. We have heard it talked about, but that's going to be an uphill battle, don't you think?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes, I think it would be a huge battle, but I think it's worth at least raising the question. I think most Americans support it.\par \par \uc2 They're tired of a tax cut -- code that's so complicated. I lived in Hong Kong for 10 years. My -- the tax code was a flat tax. You could fill out the tax form in a half-an-hour, and I think people like that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, we have heard it talked about for years. We will have to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Bill, Taylor, I appreciate it. Out of time on this. Great debate. Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 So, Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  telling Neil why he's pushing to get rid of the Education Department. But with programs like student loans, how would it work?\par \par \uc2 But first to Bill Melugin at the border on a big visit and some big changes coming this way -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Edward, incoming Trump administration border czar Tom Homan is going to be here in Eagle Pass tomorrow with Texas Governor Greg Abbott. He will be shaking hands, saying thank you to the troopers down here, trying to secure the border, showing he's going to be a partner, not an adversary to this government.\par \par \uc2 And coming up after the break, we're going to show you remarkable footage of dozens of unaccompanied children who showed up right here yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Flyers looking at more than 7,000 delays and cancellations in the past 48 hours, and now storms are threatening to drive those numbers higher. The FOX Weather app will help you out, and we will keep you covered.\par \par \uc2 Back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: To the border and a big visit coming tomorrow in Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 That's where Bill Melugin is standing by.\par \par \uc2 Bill, Tom Homan's wasting no time, and he's technically not even on the job yet.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Not wasting any time at all. He wants to come down here and show his support for the state of Texas and show that they're going to have a partner in the Trump White House, not an adversary.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration was destroying this razor wire you see behind us. Tom Homan's probably going to tell him, hey, put more razor wire in. And they need it out here. We will show you why. Take a look at this video. This was yesterday right here in Eagle Pass, a group of 200 illegal immigrants that crossed over, of that, 60 unaccompanied children in that group.\par \par \uc2 We're talking little kids, little toddlers traveling completely alone, no parents or guardians, trafficked across the border by cartels and human smugglers, some of them with little pieces of paper or phone numbers about where they want to go to in the United States.\par \par \uc2 There was a 2-year-old girl in that group, completely alone, who had one of those pieces of paper and talked to a Texas Trooper. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And then take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 Last week here in Eagle Pass, Texas DPS arrested this group of men from Afghanistan. They are considered special interest aliens. They will have national security concerns, meaning DHS will make it a priority to give them some extra vetting.\par \par \uc2 And then take a look at this video. This is here in Eagle Pass, Texas DPS running what they call mass migration drills. Basically, they want to show a posture of deterrence to any migrant caravans or any migrants who are planning on rushing the border before Trump gets in office. The idea being, don't try it. You're not going to be welcomed here. You're going to be turned back.\par \par \uc2 And Texas Governor Greg Abbott says he is thrilled to be working with Tom Homan come January. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): We have had people who were antagonistic to Texas securing the border. Now we have allies. It's going to be Texas working collaboratively with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And Homan is expected to visit with the troops and the troopers here in Eagle Pass tomorrow at noon local time. Again, he will be joined with Texas Governor Greg Abbott. We will be here.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thanks, Bill. We're looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 So, and as Trump's border enforcer is set to visit Eagle Pass tomorrow and his administration prepares to crack down on border security, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and many Democrats are calling on Biden to counter Trump's deportation agenda.\par \par \uc2 To Peter Doocy at the White House, where there's bound to be pushback -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And a White House spokesman just told us that is not going to happen. The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  wants them to start shutting down ICE facilities with bad records. A White House spokesperson is telling me they will not do that.\par \par \uc2 They want the Senate to continue focusing on that bipartisan bill, which there's no indication they're going to get to that between now and the end of President Biden's term. We are also hearing, with a look ahead to the next administration, that the Trump White House could either withhold federal funds from jurisdictions that don't comply with the mass deportation orders or they could prosecute leaders who are interfering with those federal orders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LISA MCCLAIN (R-MI): There is not going to be a red carpet welcoming illegal immigrants into this country. We need to start cleaning up our streets for our American citizens. And it starts with sending those criminals back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Part of that \uc2 ACLU\uc2  request that we just found out is rebuffed is for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to act to limit mass detention of immigrants by closing \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  detention facilities with egregious records of human rights violations and abuses and further halt detention expansion.\par \par \uc2 We are awaiting -- we do know also some influential Democrats are urging members of their own party not to go down that road.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): One area where we kind of lost ourselves was the border. I have tried to describe. It's you have up to 300,000 people, encounters per month, and I put that in perspective with Pennsylvania. Like, that's the size of Pittsburgh showing up in one month. It's like, what's going to happen for those folks?\par \par \uc2 If we want them -- the American dream for those people, how can we possibly deliver that? Where do they go?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And changes are coming, but not immediately. February one is going to be the vote on the next DNC chairperson. Until then, the party remains kind of leaderless -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Peter Doocy on the North Lawn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President-elect Trump's national security pick issuing a new warning for America's adversaries after meeting with the Biden team. You will hear it next.\par \par \uc2 And as the nation's debt tops $36 trillion, some say Trump's tax cuts are in trouble, but others say we need them now more than ever. Which is it?\par \par \uc2 Former CBO Director Doug Holtz-Eakin Doug is here on it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, if you haven't already seen a check out FOX Nation's hit series "Martin Scorsese Presents: The Saints." The first two episodes are now available on the channel. And catch every episode every Sunday through December 8.\par \par \uc2 Now this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE WALTZ\uc2  (R-FL): Jake and I, Jake Sullivan and I, have had discussions. We have met.\par \par \uc2 For our adversaries out there that think this is a time of opportunity, that they can play one administration off the other, they're wrong. And we are hand in glove. We are one team with the United States in this transition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Trump's incoming national security adviser, \uc2 Mike Waltz\uc2 , meeting with his predecessor, Jake Sullivan, and delivering a stern warning to America's adversaries.\par \par \uc2 Former CIA station chief in Moscow Dan Hoffman joining me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Dan, do you think China, Russia, Iran care about this warning?\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I think they take it seriously, that, if there were to be some sort of a provocation, especially from China -- we have been concerned in the past over some Chinese provocation in Taiwan -- that Democrats and Republicans, the Biden -- the outgoing Biden administration, the incoming Trump administration, stands ready to work together to deal with that threat.\par \par \uc2 I thought that was a great message from national security adviser-designate Michael Waltz, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Probably an important one to deliver at this moment.\par \par \uc2 So, during the first Trump administration, he changed the narrative on China by focusing on that. He brought China to the top of mind around the globe. How concerned do you think the Chinese are that their shenanigans will no longer be tolerated and they might even have consequences?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Well, I think the Trump administration actually built pretty good bipartisan consensus on the threat that we face from China.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration, for the most part, carried that through. I think the Trump administration is prepared to augment the pressure on China, and that will be a good thing. But, again, \uc2 Mike Waltz\uc2 's statement about Democrats and Republicans working together and how no one should seek to take advantage of this lame-duck period, it's really important going forward when we try to craft legislation that will require bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 This is the right tone for the national security adviser to set. It also helps for confirmation hearings that we're on one page when it comes to national security. It's the kind of message, I think, that you will hear from secretary of state-designate \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to get to Russia in a second, but what do you think of how publicly smooth President Biden's administration is making this transition?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN\uc2 : So far, the transition has been smooth. I thought the meeting between President Biden and president-elect Trump went as well as one could have hoped for.\par \par \uc2 I think there's a lot going on behind the scenes. I can tell you from my experience at CIA it puts a premium on our intelligence community. When I was at CIA, my colleagues and I briefed Democrats and Republicans on Capitol Hill. We briefed incoming and outgoing presidents without a hint of partisanship.\par \par \uc2 We will tell our boss, that's the president in some cases, what he needs to know, especially when it's not what he wants to hear. And that's where I think the national security adviser really relies on our nonpartisan intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: You spent much of your career focusing on Russia. And President Trump says he will end the invasion of Ukraine quickly. How do you believe he will do that?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I will tell you, I spent my career primarily focused on the Middle East, on Russia, and South Asia, three places in crisis right now.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I would just emphasize that the terrorist -- the threat with the shortest fuse is terrorism. We have got a lot of \uc2 ISIS\uc2  and Al Qaeda operatives enjoying ungoverned space in Afghanistan, the likes of which we haven't seen since before 9/11. We have got the war in Ukraine. We have got Israel fighting a multifront war against Iran and its terrorist proxies.\par \par \uc2 The world is definitely a lot less safe now. The Biden administration is leaving us a little worse off, a lot worse off in foreign policy than the way they found it. And those will be wickedly complex challenges for the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And just in 15 seconds or so, do you believe that the energy policy, bringing oil prices down, will conquer some of those issues globally?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN\uc2 : I think the important thing is to try to bring everything together for a whole-of-government approach.\par \par \uc2 If that's the goal and to stress Iran and Russia, those oil producers, then that may indeed make some sense.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Dan Hoffman, I appreciate it. Thanks for your insight on this. We will obviously call on you in the future. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 So, the Menendez brothers case back in court. A release from prison could be one possible outcome.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hunt following it all from Van Nuys, California -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Erik and Lyle Menendez and their supporters were hoping they might be free for the holidays. We will tell you what a judge said after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And it's now official, Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2  granting the dismissal of the election case against president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after special counsel Jack Smith moved to drop the case earlier today.\par \par \uc2 So, meantime, Erik and Lyle Menendez's case back in court, the latest step in their bid for release. FOX's\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hunt is in Van Nuys, California.\par \par \uc2 So what happened, Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Ed, well, Erik and Lyle Menendez and their supporters had hoped a decision might be made today or very that would essentially free the brothers for the holidays, more than three decades after they admitted murdering their parents and were sentenced to 30 -- to life in prison without the possibility of parole.\par \par \uc2 But, today, a judge quashed those hopes. They will not be home for the holidays. He has rescheduled, the judge, the resentencing hearing for January 30. So, for the moment, the two boys, who murdered their parents back in 1989, will remain behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Their attorney, though, hopes that ultimately they will achieve their aim of getting the Menendez brothers released. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK GERAGOS\uc2 , ATTORNEY FOR MENENDEZ BROTHERS: By January 30 or 31, we're hoping that by the end of that or some time sooner that we will, in fact, get the brothers released.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, the delay in the resentencing hearing will also give the incoming L.A. County district attorney, Nathan Hochman, more time to study the case. His predecessor, George Gascon, had asked for the resentencing, implying that he believed that the boys should be released because of new evidence, which he said, George Gascon, backed up their original claims that they only acted to kill their parents after years of sexual abuse by their father.\par \par \uc2 But the incoming DA, Nathan Hochman, says he wants to study every facet of this case before he decides what he will recommend to the judge. Listen here to Nathan Hochman.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HOCHMAN, INCOMING LOS ANGELES COUNTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY: I'm going to be doing that thorough review of the facts and the law, as apparently the judge will as well.\par \par \uc2 It involves reviewing thousands of pages of prison records, thousands of pages of trial transcripts from two monthslong trials.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So Erik and Lyle Menendez will remain behind bars for Thanksgiving. They will remain behind bars for Christmas. They now have to wait until January 30 to find out if a judge will resentence them, and even then it would have to go before the Parole Board.\par \par \uc2 So it could be months before they get out, if indeed that is the final outcome -- Ed.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Jonathan Hunt, appreciate it, in Van Nuys, California.\par \par \uc2 So, as the nation's debt tops 36 trillion, we know DOGE is going to target spending. But others say this is no time for a tax cut. Digging deeper into the tax battle next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE ROUNDS (R-SD): Look, there's a better way to do it. Get the bureaucrats out of this thing. They haven't educated a single student, but folks back home have.\par \par \uc2 And so let's put some money back in for the folks back home. Let's put a lot of it into block grants. Allow the states to actually make those decisions. And where we can save a billion dollars, which is what we think we might be able to save by eliminating the department, that's a good step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 And, at the same time, we get the bureaucrats out of schools in our communities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So South Dakota Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  telling Neil about his proposal to get rid of the Department of Education, something that president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also called for.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Mark Meredith joining us to dig deeper in how this all can be done -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Edward, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 You're right. The president-elect has vowed to dismantle or completely shut down the Department of Education, but he won't be able to do it without Congress getting on board. The department goes back to 1979, created by Congress under then-President Carter. So to remove it, Congress would have to agree to close it.\par \par \uc2 And even with the strong Republican majority, you need 60 votes in the Senate to kill it. That's a big threshold. Instead, advocates for closure say they believe Trump's nominee to lead the department, former Small Business Administrator Linda McMahon, should try to limit its power, even transfer major functions like student loans to other government agencies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEAL MCCLUSKEY, CATO CENTER FOR EDUCATIONAL FREEDOM: And that collection would almost certainly be overseen by the Department of Treasury, possibly the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . But there are lots of departments in the federal government whose focus is finance.\par \par \uc2 And that is what student loans are. So I don't think there's any chance that somehow student borrowers are left orphaned because nobody is in charge of student loans anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Even Trump's former education secretary is calling for the department to be overhauled. She argues it should follow the individual states to regulate education.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETSY DEVOS, FORMER U.S. EDUCATION SECRETARY: There's a whole host of Republican governors who have led on issues around K-12 education in particular and in other cases higher education. They have great backgrounds and could do a great job in carrying out the policies of the second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But some Democrats call the idea insane. They're vowing to fight back.\par \par \uc2 But, Edward, this is great. The \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , certainly not a fan of Trump, say they are willing to work with McMahon in her likely new role -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Mark Meredith, thank you in Washington.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in someone who can help us sort all of this out, former CBO Director Doug Holtz-Eakin joining me now.\par \par \uc2 So, on paper, you cut out a huge department and it adds a lot of savings. In practicality, how hard is it to get rid of a department?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Well, the Department of Education was -- the first proposal eliminating in the Reagan administration. So you now know how hard it is. It's still there and it's still underperforming.\par \par \uc2 And so the first step would be to get it to perform adequately. I think the most recent experience with the financial aid form, three years in the revisions, go to roll it out, disaster. So let's get something that works. And I think Secretary McMahon can probably do that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: What do you think about starving it, that idea of sort of just taking the programs away from it bit by bit?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, certainly, the case for student loans is pretty strong. I mean, the student loan portfolios have been mismanaged for a long, long time.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Education is the largest lender in the planet now. It's really quite stunning. And it's not built to do financial management. So move that and put real education issues as the only thing that it actually does.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to change gears a little bit. I want to ask you about the nation's debt, north of $36 trillion now.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And some critics are saying that the Trump tax cuts expiring won't help that, they'd get in the way. The president-elect believes it'll spark growth.\par \par \uc2 Which way -- how do you fix this? How do you tackle this?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So we actually have two problems. We're growing too slowly as a nation. We need more rapid growth, and the debt's part of that problem. But a big tax increase is not going to help, $5 trillion over the next 10 years. So you don't want to just do that.\par \par \uc2 The other problem is the debt. We have that problem. The best thing to do would be to get a real pro-growth tax reform that means something. That means you don't jack up rates. If you're going to raise revenue, you broaden the base. That's what they did in 2017. Do more of that. And if you can, cut spending so that the whole package is deficit-neutral.\par \par \uc2 If you do that, Edward, then you can make the tax cuts permanent, and there's nothing more powerful than permanent policy.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So you think the growth, but also maybe the cuts from DOGE, if everybody's talking about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , what -- the cuts that they're making, will that play into...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: I think these are the cuts that Congress has to make, because they're going to have one bill and they can put tax policy in it. They can put spending policy in it. And they should do both in a way that doesn't raise deficits and so they can make it permanent.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So with 30 seconds that we have here, I want to ask you about the president-elect's pick of \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , going to be the director of OMB.\par \par \uc2 He's got to be working with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and some of these folks to cut programs and cut -- find the waste. How difficult is that job?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: That is one of the most powerful jobs, quietly, in any administration. OMB is an important agency, has tremendous reach into the agencies.\par \par \uc2 That is in fact the only route for the DOGE to affect the government. They make recommendations to OMB. So all eyes will be on \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2  when he gets them.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Do you think that there could be enough savings cut out of the government to reduce our deficit to zero? It's almost $2 trillion now.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: I don't see an effort like DOGE being successful in doing that, because it's just like the Department of Education.\par \par \uc2 Congress created most of the spending, and we need Congress to actually pull it back.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Congress is the one that has got to fix that.\par \par \uc2 And is this something that can be done in two years?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Two years would be an enormous reach. If they can make progress in two years, I'd be thrilled.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, I appreciate it. Thank you, Doug Holtz-Eakin, for being here.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: This has been a very interesting debate, something -- we're going to see this play out, right?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: We have got a huge debt here. It's crushing.\par \par \uc2 Now, I do want to say that President Biden just announced that he is -- or the White House announced that he is going to go to the inauguration of former President -- or president-elect Trump's inauguration. So that's going to be interesting news, breaking news here.\par \par \uc2 But thanks for joining us here on "Your World: Cavuto." We will be here all week.\par \par \uc2 But you know what's coming up next? I do. It's "The Five".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020241125ekbp0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Left Has Been Targeting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  For Years; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Returned To MSG Like A Conquering Hero; Democrats Are Good At Finding Extra Votes; Government efficiency is needed at the Pentagon; The Pentagon fails 7th audit in a row; Adding fuel to fire, Russia sends major warning to U.S.; Biden letting Ukraine strike Russia with U.S. missiles; Elon and Vivek to force government employees back to office; Mika and Joe's trip to Mar-a-Lago; Liberal women take out their anger on men\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: -- the Orlando pride Saturday in the championship match, we wanted to bring you that. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on Special Report, Fox News exclusive Trey Yingst interviews Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  about all that's happening. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 06:00 p.m. in the East, 03:00 p.m. on the West Coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @bretbaier and on X. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. Fair, balanced, and unafraid.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. Thank you so much for joining us. President Trump filling out his cabinet at a record pace, announcing another key pick tonight, and it's someone you're very familiar with, including on the Angle. Sean Duffy has been named Transportation Secretary. He started his career as a D.A. in Wisconsin, then he served in Congress for eight years coming in during the Tea Party wave. More on all of that later.\par \par \uc2 Plus, it's legends night on the Angle. Hulk Hogan and Brett Favre will both be here in moments. I cannot wait. But first, \uc2 Red Hat\uc2 's rule, that's the focus of tonight's Angle. All right, since Trump won the presidency now less than two weeks ago, the political shift in America is finally beginning to sink in. Now, of course, we all know the victory was seismic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- of voters who put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back in office looks nothing like a traditional Republican voter set, and these shifts that the Obama electorate looks -- went toward \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Trump electorate looks more like the Obama electorate now. I think that's a really surprising takeaway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You think? Very surprising. Well, not really to us, but even some of his old enemies are crawling back to make peace, to salvage what's left of their credibility.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALYSSA FARAH GRIFFIN, ABC NEWS: I would sit down with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I would interview \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I'll go a step further, I hope good people work for him. We need to stop demonizing people because they supported a man who just became President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can't demonize people. Is anyone laughing as hard as I am? I think they should all just start wearing MAGA hats when they go on the air. Now, what this confirms, of course, is what we've told you for years. They never believed he was a fascist or even an insurrectionist. It was all either a delusion or just a lie. More on that later.\par \par \uc2 But there's also a cultural shift happening. Now, think about this, what was it six months ago or so, this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was facing every day, yes, outside a cold Manhattan courtroom with liberal judge Juan Merchan presiding, and before that, liberals were gleeful when Trump was hit with another huge judgment in a civil defamation case filed by this woman. But it wasn't just the legal wins that were buffeting Trump, but the cultural wins as well. All the Left-wing loons were ecstatic. They thought surely Trump would never recover.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DE NIRO, ACTOR: Do you think Trump ever threw a punch himself or took one, this guy who ran and hid in the White House bunker when there were protesters outside. No way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But when Trump returned to New York on Saturday night for the big UFC fight between Jon Jones and Stipe Miocic, it was like none of that other stuff had ever happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: USA, USA. Let's go.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: USA, USA, USA, USA.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was his second appearance at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  in just four weeks, and this time, it was not as a candidate, but almost as a conquering hero.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It is wild. So, Trump with Musk, Dana White and Trump's new buddy podcasting giant Joe Rogan, they were all there. Then the moment that sealed the deal. Now, while Jon Jones had beaten his opponent in the ring, Jones acknowledged that Trump had beaten something a lot more powerful, the D.C. machine.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, where before people were silent and keeping their opinions to themselves, never put a MAGA sign outside your house or worried about that. It's suddenly OK to be a Trump supporter, and it's even OK to have some fun with it. Yes, meanwhile, over at the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , although Taylor Swift didn't make it to the Chiefs Buffalo game yesterday, we wonder what she thought about some of the end zone celebrations. It's infectious.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Again, there's a new sense of optimism in the air. You feel it, and just the sense that people are breathing a sigh of relief. In Axios, this headline, "no more hidden Trump voters. Trump voters in deep blue cities are no longer keeping it to themselves." So pay attention liberal weenies, as more Americans start coming out of the political closet because they saw Trump as the guy who punched through, the reason America now has a fighting chance, it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm voting for Harris.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I actually voted for Obama.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You did?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That was the last time I ever voted for somebody that's not Trump. Yes. So --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So what changed?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My level of thinking. Me not thinking that the police are above me and white men are above me, and I'm all good about like, yo, we can learn about history and all that stuff, but don't tell me, or don't, don't try to put it in a way that is like our country is racist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, of course, some people are still not quite getting it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARC CAPUTO, POLITICAL REPORTER, THE BULWARK NATIONAL: Really looks like ancient Rome here. This is sort of the conquering Republican Caesar who is going into the Coliseum, and everyone is cheering, and he's got his political gladiators with him. That appearance isn't just about him enjoying the applause. He's sending a message to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For sure.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Like not only are you entertained, but these are my people, and are you willing to fight, because here's who I have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't know if any of them had any trouble with when Kamala walked into her celebrity fest, but news flash, after what they put him through, what do you expect Trump to do? Shrink from the scene. My gosh, he's a New Yorker. Should he say, pretty please work with me? No way. They tried to kill him, and he won. Things can and will change, but right now, Trump has leverage and political capital, and he intends to use it.\par \par \uc2 Strength and perseverance against all odds and independent fighting spirit, authenticity. Well, Trump embodies all of it. That's why so many young people like him. And the cultural figures who want to remain relevant beyond their little bubbles should pay attention. Try opening your ears, your eyes, learning more about why former Democrats join the Trump movement, why young people are suddenly following politics again, why young African American men see the Republican Party in an entirely new light. And what about the Hispanic vote? What about it?\par \par \uc2 And why pro-athletes are no longer afraid of having a little Trump fun. Now the cultural Left applauded, remember, Colin Kaepernick taking a knee and lectured America on race. Surely they can find a way to love the Trump dance. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Hulk Hogan, \uc2 WWE\uc2  legend. Hulk, so great to see you. I was at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  when you came out in October during the campaign. Why are so many athletes thrilled, it seems, by Trump's win?\par \par \uc2 HULK HOGAN, FORMER WWE WORLD CHAMPION: Laura Ingraham mania, I still got my Trump pump on and let me stake my claim. Let me mark my territory. I was the first one, if you were there to do the Trump dance, baby.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I did see it.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I had that place going in the Garden, and now it's catching on. Everybody is dancing like Trump, because we 