d they've been wrong about everything from Russia all the way down the line to trying to impeach him, bankrupt him, put him out of business. I mean, give me -- trying to kill him. Give me a break. Don't even listen to that stuff.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can you do the Trump dance for us, please? I want to see you do it.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Got to put the lips up, go.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You got it. You got the guns still, Hulk, I love it. I've got to pull the camera back. OK.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Hey, let me tell you something. Let me tell you something. You might not be a fan of sports, but we are a fan of yours.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, back at you. Oh, Hulk, it's so good to have you on. I loved watching you at the Garden and the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . You brought the house down. Unbelievable stuff. Come back soon.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Love you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, the Trump dance sweeping the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , but those celebrations, are they short lived? There are new concerns that the league could be cracking down on them. A USA Today reporter says that right after he talked to Raiders tight end Brock Bowers about his Trump touchdown dance, the team abruptly ended his post-game availability.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Brett Favre, NFL Hall of Fame quarterback. Brett, fantastic to see you. I don't know how you follow Hulk Hogan, man, but you're going to have to figure it out. Brett. Why do you believe the \uc2 NFL\uc2  players are doing the little dance? What's going on there?\par \par \uc2 BRETT FAVRE, NFL HALL OF FAME QUARTERBACK: You know, I think people have had enough. And they're, you know, they're coming out of the woodworks right and left. And when I woke up Election Day and saw the landslide victory, it really put, put where I think everyone's perspective is on this country right to the forefront. I mean, we were in desperate need of President Trump to take back over and get this country back where it needs to be. I mean, we were, we were definitely going in the wrong direction. And I think our youth, when I say youth, \uc2 NFL\uc2  players, college players, high school players see it. You know, when I was 22, 25, 30 years old, I never thought about politics. It was never an issue. There was never a focus on politics like it was this year. And I think people just saw it for what it was that we were in desperate need of help, and that that help is President Trump.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Brett, don't you also think that some of this is almost an athlete's competitive spirit, like when you're counted out, you're injured, no one thinks you can come back again and you throw the perfect pass, or you go for the perfect pattern and you catch the perfect ball, and again, no one thought you could do it, but you do it. That that as someone who played some college sports, that's to me, what I see, that connection between athlete and him, maybe that's overblown.\par \par \uc2 FAVRE: Well, Laura, you're right. I don't think a lot of people gave President Trump a chance of winning. I know I did. I was very hopeful that he would, that he would win, like, like most of us, which you just didn't really know, based on what's happened in the past, you really didn't know. And so again, when I woke up that morning, and I'm sure a lot of people felt the same way. It was, like, you have got to be kidding. Not only did he win, he just dominated. And I just, I just think that we -- you know, as I said, we were in desperate need of --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do you think, Brett, sorry to interrupt, but do you think the \uc2 NFL\uc2  should be cracking down on players doing the little Trump dance in the end zone, because some believe they are.\par \par \uc2 FAVRE: Well, I think back to -- back in the -- just over the years, I mean the Ickey shuffle. I don't know if you remember that. Ickey Woods with the \uc2 Cincinnati Bengals\uc2  had that Ickey shuffle. And I could go on and on. I don't remember anyone being fine