stopia.\par \par \uc2 And even worse, Laura, she goes out there and she advocates for these progressive policies that are going to shove those policies down the rest of Americans' throats while she flees. So, she's a fraud and that's why you shouldn't listen to these Hollywood people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lisa read the whole Marie Claire article.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: I'm a nerd. I'm a nerd. I did it for you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm sorry. Yeah, you did it. We have to cut this short. I'm so sorry. We're going to have you back on next time for much longer. Ben and Lisa, thank you, as always.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. Jesse Watters, next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241115ekbe00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fallout Over Former \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Chief Of Staff Comments; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Holds Rally And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Participates In Town Hall; Foreign Attempt To Impact U.S. Presidential Election; North Korean Troops Deployed To Russia; Interview With Defense Secretary After Zelenskyy Summit; Austin Says United States Committed; Austin: Goal Is To Take Away Houthi Capability; Iran-Backed Group Threatening Global Commerce; State of Georgia Seeing Record Early Voting Turnout; Striking Workers At \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Vote New Contract And To End Walkout; Former Trump Chief Of Staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  Says Trump Admires Dictators And Fits Definition Of Fascist; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Avoids Answering Question On When She Knew President Biden Not Fit To Run For Reelection.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, David Spunt, Mike  Emanuel, Jennifer Griffin, Jonathan Serrie, Grady Trimble, Guy Benson \par \uc2 7,865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, I like some more of that. Jesse, pass the candy.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hey, Bret!\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Greg. So, in your face, Harold, is not an everyday thing when he's on the show?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Truly when he's on it's going to become a new book in a new series on Fox Nation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I see, I figured.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Hosted by Harold Melvin & the Blue Notes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. confirms 3,000 North Korean troops in Russia. Washington says they are fair game if they join the fight against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Record numbers of early voters in the crucial swing state of Georgia, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is there right now, we'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 And growing concern over foreign involvement in the U.S. election. You may be shocked by which nation is now being called out.\par \par \uc2 But first, does it qualify as an October surprise comments by a former chief of staff for then President Trump making headlines less than two weeks ahead of the election. Former general, former chief of staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  says, President Trump -- former president would rule like a dictator, has no understanding of the Constitution and meets the definition of a fascist. Kelly has made disparaging remarks about his former boss before, but these come, obviously at a particularly crucial time.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying today, Kelly's comments revealed Trump for who he really is, the Trump campaign pushing back. We're learning tonight, Harris will make a major closing argument on the Ellipse here in Washington Tuesday, one week before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel leads us off tonight with this. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Former Chief of Staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  has done two highly critical interviews of former President Trump. The interview is blowing up less than two weeks before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2  (RET.), FORMER TRUMP CHIEF OF STAFF: Certainly the former president is in the far right area. Certainly an authoritarian. Admires people who are dictators, he has said that, so he certainly falls into the general definition of fascist for sure.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): In an interview with The Atlantic, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  recounted how Trump told him he wanted generals that were loyal like Hitler's. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was quick to jump on Kelly's criticism.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that because he does not want a military that is loyal to the United States Constitution. He wants a military that is loyal to him.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Retired Army Brigadier General \uc2 Anthony Tata\uc2  says, Kelly's use of fascism is a cheap shot and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. ANTHONY TATA (RET.) FORMER DEFENSE UNDER SECRETARY: I am shocked that General Kelly has made these comparisons of Trump to Hitler when he knows they're not true. He knows that he's enabling a Harris-Walz ticket that would continue to help the Middle East unravel and drive our enemies together.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): The Atlantic also referenced Mr. Trump inviting the family of 20-year-old Army Private Vanessa Guillen, who was murdered at a military base in Texas, to the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 At one point, the president offered to pay for her funeral. Then Atlantic sources suggest, Trump blew up when he heard the price tag would be $60,000. A former Pentagon chief of staff says that is not true.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, FORMER TRUMP PENTAGON OFFICIAL: He said, if the military didn't pick up the tab, he would in its entirety. That's the commander in chief.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Guillen sister accused the Atlantic of exploiting her sister's death for politics, and said, "President Trump did nothing but show respect to my family and Vanessa. In fact, I voted for President Trump today."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): \uc2 Nick Ayers\uc2 , a former chief of staff to then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  called Kelly's commentary, "Patently false".\par \par \uc2 So, some are asking about two weeks before Election Day, why an interview about incidents, reportedly for more than four years ago are coming out just before the election, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and we're seeing more of that. You had the former chief of staff -- commander in chief -- the chairman of the Joint Chiefs \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  quoted in a Bob Woodward book saying something similar, but this is the first audio we've heard on the record.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: That's right. \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  speaking in his own words. We can hear his voice, his points of emphasis, and makes you wonder if he may come on camera before long to talk more about what he believes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vice President Harris participates in a CNN town hall later tonight, former President Trump is trying to nail down the 16 electoral college votes from Georgia, hosting a faith town hall in Zebulon earlier this afternoon, and in about an hour, he appears at a rally in the Atlanta suburb of Duluth. That is where Senior National Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is right now. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 That's right. The front row former president isn't slowing down out on the campaign trail. In fact, tonight he'll be joined on stage with country music star Jason Aldean as both campaigns now bring out the star power out on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have to make sure too big to rig. We want to make it too big to rig.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Former President Trump getting out the vote in Georgia, holding a town hall on faith with Lieutenant Governor Burt Jones, taking in the record number of Georgians voting early in person.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Over two million early votes as of tonight.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's a record.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is a record. It is a record.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump is now up in the state. According to a new Atlanta Journal Constitution poll, 47 to 43 within the margin of error. But according to that same poll, eight percent of respondents said they are still undecided. The Trump campaign staying cautious in the final two weeks.\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: We have to play as if we're down by one point. You play a prevent defense, the only thing it does is prevent you from winning.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump's Prime Time rally lineup tonight includes former Democrat and now former independent \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, FORMER REPRESENTATIVE OF HAWAII: I'm proud to stand here with you today President Trump, and announce that I'm joining the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Also on the stage tonight, RFK Jr. and Turning Point's Charlie Kirk, who is also helping with voter outreach.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign teasing, but not announcing yet big names for when the former president rallies at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please welcome Bruce Springsteen.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Democrats also getting out the vote, with the help from the boss Bruce Springsteen, who will play a concert series across battleground states, starting with a rally in Atlanta on Thursday, headlined by Vice President Harris and former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, Trump heads to the swing states of Arizona and Nevada next and in Nevada, excuse me -- in Nevada and Las Vegas, particularly, Bret, he's targeting the Asian American community, a key voting block in that state, and really as he continues to court minority voters, people who might feel left behind by the Democratic Party, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah, thank you. The nation's top intelligence officials are anticipating foreign attempts to influence next month's election. They specifically point to Russia, China and Iran.\par \par \uc2 But there is one source you might find surprising. Correspondent David Spunt tells us what that is tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. We're talking about the United Kingdom, of all places, and that special relationship between the U.S. and U.K. under the microscope even more following this Trump campaign allegation. The formal complaint filed with the Federal Election Commissioner, \uc2 FEC\uc2  alleges that roughly 100 members of the Labor Party in the U.K. came to several states, Nevada, North Carolina, Pennsylvania and Virginia to help the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 And the complaint Trump's Deputy General Counsel calls the situation, "Blatant foreign interference." And alleges U.K. money is in the Harris campaign, but Labor Party officials working directly for British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said there's nothing wrong with volunteering in the United States. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER, BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: People are free to do that. It's not against the law here. It's not against the law in the United States, but it certainly wasn't organized by the Labor Party itself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: U.S. election laws are clear, although foreign nationals may not make contributions or expenditures, including advances of personal funds in connection with any federal, state or local election, an individual who is a foreign national may participate in campaign activities as an uncompensated volunteer.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Bret, the intelligence community is focused not on the U.K. so much, but Russia definitely for election interference. Officials say a viral video circulating on social media last week that purported to show one of \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's former students in high school accusing him of wrongdoing is fake.\par \par \uc2 Intelligence officials say the supposed person may actually be artificial intelligence. The real student came forward and said his name was wrongly used and the allegations are false, full stop.\par \par \uc2 We expect to see more of this kind of stuff happening unfortunately leading up to the election on both sides.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to be talking to some senior officials about all of that, specifically the computer side in the coming days. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, the U.S. says 3,000 North Korean troops have deployed to Russia. They are training at several locations, we're told. The Biden administration calling the move very serious and warning those forces will be fair game if they go into combat inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The White House says if Putin has to turn to North Korea for manpower, it would show his army is weak. At least 3,000 North Korean soldiers are training at military sites in Russia after arriving by ship this month. But officials maintain they don't know why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Well, I think they're not tourists. Deterrence has been lost everywhere.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The White House says if North Korea joins Russia's fight against Ukraine, it could have significant impacts in Europe and the Indo Pacific, and there's looming concern Russia could help North Korea develop its nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: What does Kim Jong-un think he's getting out of this? Maybe it's technology. Maybe it's capabilities. We don't have a good sense of that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But for now, the Biden administration is just monitoring.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Why not green light the long range missiles for Ukraine's use, which is Zelenskyy's number one ask as you're sounding the alarm?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: For one thing, Jacqui, we don't exactly know what these guys are going to do.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: What else could they be there for?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): A U.S. official tells Fox, the intelligence community and Pentagon recommended against allowing Ukraine to strike targets inside Russia with U.S. made long range missiles, citing greater cost than benefit, including limited targets in range, limited supplies, and concerns Russia would view it as escalation, something that's marked Biden's handling of the war.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Why are you waiting on Putin to make the first move, Sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What a stupid question.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden refused to impose preemptive sanctions as Russia staged troops on the Ukrainian border in 2022 and hesitated to approve each military capability the U.S. later provided. Zelenskyy exasperated at the developments.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): North Korea can intervene in the war in Europe, then the pressure on this regime is definitely not strong enough.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Officials say Biden has not ruled out a future change in U.S. policy, or given Zelenskyy a definitive no yet.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: If these North Korean soldiers decide to join the fight against Ukraine, they will become legitimate military targets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Right now, the U.S. is discussing more sanctions as a response, we are told. But House Intelligence Committee Chairman Mike Turner says North Korea joining the fight against Ukraine should be a red line for Biden, and he should both green light those long range missile strikes within Russia, and also consider direct U.S. military action against the North Korean troops, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Rough day for tech stocks sent the Dow to its worst day since early September. Industrial Average losing 410, the S&P 500 was down 54, the NASDAQ plunged 296.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Jennifer Griffin with the second part of her exclusive interview with Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 . First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around this country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in Atlanta, as the suspect in the Winder, Georgia high school shooting pleads not guilty and demands a jury trial. 14-year-old Colt Gray is accused of killing four people, wounding nine others September 4th. He was indicted on 55 counts by a grand jury last week.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Miami from WSVN, our affiliate down there. One of the big stories there tonight, the baseball hit by \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2  star Shohei Ohtani for his 50th home run this season is auctioned off for a record $4,392,000 that Homer came in Miami. Ohtani became the only player in Major League history to have 50 home runs and 50 stolen bases in the same season.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is confirming the death of another top official. The terror group says, Hashem Safieddine was killed in an Israeli air strike. He was widely expected to be the group's next leader. Yesterday, Israel said it had killed Safieddine in a strike earlier this month in Beirut's southern suburbs.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , says Israel needs to pursue what he calls an enduring strategic success in Gaza, after its tactical victories against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The top American diplomat urging Israel to seek a deal that would end the war and bring back dozens of hostages. Blinken spoke before traveling from Israel to Saudi Arabia. He is on his 11th visit to the region since the war began.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, part two of chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin's exclusive interview with defense secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 . She is in Rome again tonight. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. We have new information about what was and was not in those classified documents that forced the Pentagon to launch a leak investigation. According to a senior U.S. defense official, they tell -- told Fox, these are not Israeli war plans for Iran that were leaked.\par \par \uc2 The documents describe satellite images of Israeli war planes leaving hangars practicing air refueling last week for potential long-range bombing runs an indication the planes could be used to target Iran. No information about the targets Israel plans to strike were leaked.\par \par \uc2 Here in Rome, Defense Secretary Austin met the pope, who described the death toll in Ukraine as terrible, and called the Palestinian suffering inhumane.\par \par \uc2 In part two of our exclusive interview with Austin, we discussed his concerns about the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: There is been growing frustration by you and others in the administration that during the past year, Israel has not been careful enough about civilian casualties in Gaza and now in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has been blindsided at times, with Israel not telling it in advance about striking certain targets, and that has threatened to spread the war as well as draw the U.S. into a direct conflict with Iran. Is Netanyahu a reliable ally?\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: I won't comment on the prime minister.\par \par \uc2 What I will tell you is that our commitment is that we're going to continue to support Israel in its right to defend itself. Protecting civilians in a battle space and achieving military objectives are not mutually exclusive.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and Lebanese, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has made this this tough fight in using civilians as shields, putting their military command posts underneath hospitals and schools and mosques. You know, our expectation is that we're doing everything we can. The Israelis are doing everything they can to protect civilian life.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: You and Secretary Blinken wrote a letter to your Israeli counterpart, suggesting if Israel, in the next 30 days, did not open humanitarian corridors into Gaza, that you would withhold future weapons shipments. Why put this in writing, and are you serious this time?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Jen, I won't comment on correspondence. What I will tell you is that my concern on providing humanitarian assistance to the Palestinians has been there from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Did you and President Biden encourage the Israelis not to strike Iran's nuclear sites or oil facilities? Why not strike those targets?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Israel chooses to do in its targeting is going to be their choice. From my perspective, if you are conducting a military strike, it ought to be against military targets. That's a -- that's a military gas speaking.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: It's almost exactly a year since the USS Carney shot down dozens of Iranian backed Houthi drones and missiles. You spent the last year playing defense against these Houthi attacks. 90 percent of global shipping has had to be rerouted at great cost to the consumer. Do you regret not going on offense sooner?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Jen, we have been striking the Houthis on a near daily basis over the last many months, and our goal is to take away as much capability from the Houthis as possible. We will continue to do things to take away their capability on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And the cost of shooting down those Houthi drones and missiles, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  has been shooting multi-million-dollar interceptors at what are sometimes just thousands of dollars of drones and missiles. Is it part of the Houthi strategy to deplete U.S. military stockpiles and bankrupt your budget?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: I can't speak to the Houthi strategy. I'll tell you what mine is. Mine is to make sure that the Houthis are not allowed to shut down the global commerce that's moving through the Red Sea and the strait.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Austin has returned to the United States, and we've just learned that he spoke to his Israeli counterpart, Yoav Gallant today. He said that he welcomed more humanitarian aid getting into Gaza, but he also said that he has deep concerns about Israeli air strikes that may have targeted Lebanese armed forces. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live in Rome. Jennifer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go to the touch screen for a closer look at the presidential contest in Georgia. First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's interior minister says an attack on the premise -- premises of a state-run aerospace and defense company has left five people dead, several injured. Not clear who may be behind that attack.\par \par \uc2 In apparent retaliation, Turkey's Air Force struck Kurdish targets in Syria and Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Torrential rain set off by an approaching Tropical Storm swamps the eastern Philippines with widespread flooding.\par \par \uc2 Officials say at least nine people have been killed. The government shut down public schools and government offices, except those urgently in need for disaster response on the entire main island of Luzon.\par \par \uc2 And officials in Hong Kong say they have discovered dinosaur fossils in the city, for the first time. The remains were found on a remote, uninhabited island that's part of a geopark.\par \par \uc2 Experts believe them to be from a large dinosaur, and it's about 40 -- 145 million years to 66 million years ago. Long time.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. In tonight's "FIFTY RACES AND 50 DAYS", the presidential campaigns barnstorm the toss-up state of Georgia. 16 electoral votes here could make the difference in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is now leading Vice President Harris in Georgia in the Real Clear Politics average of polls. Before today, both candidates have made five visits to the Peach State since President Biden withdrew from the race, July 21st. Vice President Harris has traveled to Atlanta for three of those trips. She will need to boost the turnout in the heavily blue city and its surrounding areas.\par \par \uc2 Now, one critical spot actually right around Atlanta is Cobb County, as we zero in there. It covers portions of the Atlanta suburbs to the north and east. It's been a key driver of gains for Democrats the last two cycles but hasn't always been that way. 2000, 2004, look at \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  winning there by 23 and 25 points. \uc2 John McCain\uc2  beat former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  by about nine points in 2008. \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  wins by 12 points in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you look at Cobb County, we go back to 2016, that's a flip, because former President Trump lost to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  by two points in 2016. As you go on to 2020, it's even a bigger spread. Vice President Biden then wins by more than 14 points four years later. If Vice President Harris wants to continue the blue shift, she will need to keep turnout high, particularly among black voters. They make up 27 percent of the registered voters in Cobb County.\par \par \uc2 OK, 13 days away from Election Day. Many people have already cast a ballot, or they intend to before November 5th. One in four Georgia voters has already turned in their ballot. Georgia has become a hotbed of election reform activity since 2020. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie reports from Decatur tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Georgia is having continuing records every day, it seems, with early voting turnout.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This afternoon, Georgia surpassed 2 million early ballots, more than 28 percent of the state's active voters, more than doubling early votes at this phase of the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 GERALDINE WASHINGTON, GEORGIA VOTER: I always vote early in Georgia. I don't want to be caught up on the day of. Get in and out easy.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Knowing who voted will help the campaigns tweak their ground games in this battleground state. But experts say the numbers alone reveal little about who is ahead because Georgia's open primary system does not register voters by party.\par \par \uc2 ANDRA GELLESPIE, EMORY UNIVERSITY PROFESSOR: People will pay attention to demographics in the state because there is racial polarization in terms of party identification in voting. But the truth is we are not going to know for sure who voted for whom until we start to see those results start to come in after 7:00 p.m. on November 5th.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: The Georgia Supreme Court has blocked the Republican controlled state election board from implementing several new procedures during the current election cycle, including one that would have required election night hand counts to verify the number of ballots collected. But other election integrity efforts are proceeding, including the Georgia secretary of state's embrace of new technology to audit voter rolls for non-U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 , GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE OFFICE: The 20 individuals we found as non-citizens who were canceled were canceled based on individualized information from those people. What it is, is they signed affidavits get out of jury duty, basically saying I am a non-citizen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): And Georgia election officials have reported those 20 names to district attorneys for possible prosecution. Additionally, the audit flagged 156 potential non-citizens that the secretary of state's office wants to further investigation. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is warning officials with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's political action committee that his $1 million sweepstakes to entice voters to register in certain swing states may violate federal law. The department recently received a letter from attorneys and public officials across the country requesting a review of payments made by Musk's America PAC to voters in Pennsylvania and other states. Those who signed a petition to support First and Second Amendment freedoms may win prizes or money. You must be registered to vote in specific states in order to sign the petition. No response yet from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is losing serious money as striking workers vote on an agreement to end their walkout. Today the company reports a third quarter hit of around $6 billion. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports tonight from Arlington, Virginia. Good evening, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's new CEO \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  is promising to right the ship after years of financial and safety struggles. One of the company's short-term challenges could come to an end today. Factory workers who have been on strike for around five weeks are voting as we speak on whether to approve a new contract. It includes a 35 percent bump in pay over the four years of the contract and better retirement benefits.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot riding on this union vote today. An industry group for Aerospace company says in a letter to President Biden, "A protracted strike may have grave national security implications causing delays in military aircraft production and maintenance that could compromise our defense readiness at a critical time in the world."\par \par \uc2 And the company itself, as you noted, is strapped for cash in part because of the walkout. It reported a loss of almost $6 billion in the third quarter. In his first earnings call since taking helm of the company, Ortberg says it needs to change its culture and stabilize the business. He acknowledged the problems at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  which hasn't had a profitable year since 2018.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY ORTBERG\uc2 , BOEING CEO: The trust in our company has eroded. We're saddled with too much debt. We've had serious lapses in our performance across the company which has disappointed many of our customers.\par \par \uc2 But by the same token, we have great opportunities ahead. Our company backlog is roughly a half-a-trillion dollars.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has announced plans to cut around 17,000 jobs both nonunion and union, but not those on strike. Even with the slimmed downed workforce, the company says it expects to keep losing money through next year. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady Trimble in Arlington. Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Top state officials in Louisiana are suing Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas and others while asking for an immediate stop to the release of immigrant detainees until they are medically deemed healthy. The state's attorney general filed a lawsuit citing a patient from China who crossed the southern U.S. border with what is being called a rare aggressive and drug resistant form of tuberculosis which carries high mortality rates. Louisiana officials estimate about 200 detainees have come in contact with that patient and countless other non-detainees have as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the presidential race with fewer than two weeks left.\par \par \uc2 And as you take a live look at the setting sun in Washington, D.C., courtesy of Earth Cam, nice sunset. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2 , FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF FOR PRESIDENT TRUMP: He's certainly an authoritarian, admires people who are dictators -- he has said that. So, he certainly falls into the general definition of fascist, for sure.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We know what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. He wants unchecked power. The question in 13 days will be, what do the American people want?\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. \uc2 ANTHONY TATA\uc2  (RET), FORMER DEFENSE UNDER SECRETARY: It's very alarming that we have a retired four star general out there with that dog whistle. The fact that it's coming out two weeks before the election smacks of personal revenge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, former chief of staff, former general, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  is speaking out. You heard the audio there. We'll play a little bit more in just a bit from this interview with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and all that he is saying about his former boss. Add that to a couple of articles that have come out, including one in "The Atlantic."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel about the impact, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Chris Ryan, host of "New Hampshire Today with Chris Ryan" on WGIR on iHeart Radio, and Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign has put out a statement. I think the former president just posted on Truth Social. We'll and get that up in a second as well. This is the statement from the campaign. "\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a stone cold loser who is increasingly desperate because she is flailing and her campaign is in shambles. That is why she continues to peddle outright lies and falsehoods that are easily disproven."\par \par \uc2 It's getting a lot of attention, obviously. This is his former chief of staff, Guy, who is saying he had affinity for Hitler, mentioned his generals, and also that he fits the definition of a fascist.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Yes, so \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  is a serious person. He had the proximity and influence with Trump to be his chief of staff, so what he has to say will have some impact, I think, on some voters.\par \par \uc2 I do wonder how much of that is baked in, because this particular revelation, if we want to call that today, we've actually read this before. It was in a "New Yorker" story in 2022. It was in a book that came out earlier this year. So for voters who may be hearing this for the first time, maybe it makes some new impact. But I think a lot of this is just sort of what the American people understand. There are allegations about Trump when it comes to this, and there are other people, also serious people with proximity to former President Trump pushing back pretty forcefully on at least the categorization or characterization that we are hearing from Kelly and others.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It meets, Leslie, another article from "The Atlantic." We'll just put up a picture of the former president as he gets ready for this rally in Georgia. This is his Truth Social, "Thank you for your support against the total degenerate named \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  who made up a story out of pure Trump derangement syndrome hatred. This guy had two qualities which don't work well together. He was tough and dumb. The problem his toughness morphed into weakness because he became Jell-o with time. The story about the soldiers was a lie as are numerous other stories he told. Even though I shouldn't be wasting my time with him, I always feel it's necessary to hit back in pursuit of the truth. \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  is a lowlife and a bad general whose advice in the White House I no longer sought and I told him to move on. His wife once told me at Camp David John admires you tremendously. When he leaves the military he will only speak well of you. I said thank you."\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the former general has an exemplary record. He was chosen as chief of staff and at Homeland Security Secretary.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yes, very respected in the military, in political circles, and just as an outstanding human being, and by both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is not surprising, what have you just read from the former president. And when you look at how close this race is, it doesn't surprise his, and it doesn't matter to his supporters. And for people on my side of the aisle, that makes us drop our jaw when he makes a comment about anything in admiration of Hitler.\par \par \uc2 When he does the name-calling, which just seems so juvenile, whether it's "New-scum", my governor of California, or not being able to pronounce the current vice president's name Kamala. So I'm not surprised that he would say this. Anybody who says anything negative about him, even if they were within his inner circle, he comes back and insults them. That seems to be his M.O. And sadly, his supporters are OK with that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I said earlier, I was asked about the state of the race in regards to this, and I said it's hard to judge, Chris, because not diminishing the story itself, but possibly looking at how much impact it has, as we say in the political parlance, moving the needle with a lot of people that have come to the choice that they look back to the Trump years, how they felt about it, and they look back at the past three-and-a-half years and how they felt about it. That's what I was saying earlier. Does it move things?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RYAN, TALK RADIO HOST: I don't think it does. I think that what \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  says has validity. I think it has substance. I think it has gravity. But I also don't think that it's going to change anything in regards to this race, other than this -- \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to shift the race back on to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . She does not have a closing argument other than the fact that she is, in her view, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and provides more stability than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And her close has to be a focus on him. For her, we are wondering what her administration is going to look like, what she would do as president of the United States. She does not have clarity in regard to her messaging.\par \par \uc2 Trump has been able to have success with independent voters because it is clear what he would do. And for whatever reason, to Leslie's point, independent voters and leaning voters don't seem to really care about these things. Whether we agree with that or not, that's the reality. They are not moving based upon this. And as both Guy and you said earlier, these are not new. These are things that have been brought up before. The difference, obviously, is the audio, which is significant.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The audio. And here's another little piece, and we'll also have General \uc2 Tata\uc2 's response here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KELLY\uc2 , FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF FOR PRESIDENT TRUMP: He commented more than once that Hitler did some good things, too. And, of course, if you know history, and again, I think he is lacking in that, but if you know what history -- you know what Hitler was all about, you'd be pretty hard to make an argument that he did anything good."\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. \uc2 ANTHONY TATA\uc2  (RET), FORMER DEFENSE UNDER SECRETARY: To compare him to Hitler is highly irresponsible. Particularly in this day and age for a retired four star Marine general to get out there and make this comparison again is beyond the pale.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, the timing factors in. Former aide \uc2 Nick Ayers\uc2  was inside that White House, put on X, "I've avoided commenting on intra-staff leaks or rumors or even lies as it relates to my time at the White House. But General Kelly's comments regarding President Trump are too egregious to ignore. I was with each of them more than most, and his commentary is patently false."\par \par \uc2 So you're going to have this fight over what was said, who said it, who do you believe.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: And on this front, it's a report about a purported conversation that happened years ago that has been reported out previously. Something that is new today, you mentioned "The Atlantic" story which broke last night. There is a component of it that describes Trump allegedly saying something about a deceased soldier who was killed on a military base. He had met with this slain soldier's family, and he apparently used profanity and referred to her in a very pejorative way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Paying for the funeral.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right, paying for the funeral. That source in that "Atlantic" story is a single anonymous person. And people who were in the room for that at the time are on the record denying it entirely, categorically. The family of the slain soldier has come out rejecting it and backing former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I'm still a little bit astonished. Having gone to journalism school -- I will confess that -- maybe day one they taught us the sort of standards for what you can run and what is considered quality sourcing. A single anonymous source is not anywhere close to that bar. And it was printed at "The Atlantic." And it led the CBS Evening News last night minutes later. I find that, honestly, astounding.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the sister of the deceased woman said, "Wow, I don't appreciate how you are exploiting my sister's death for politics. Hurtful and disrespectful to the important changes she made for servicemembers. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did nothing but show respect to my family and Vanessa. In fact, I voted for President Trump today." She posted that. I believe she is going to be on Laura's show in a bit.\par \par \uc2 Either way, it's going to be a big story in coming days all over the place, and whether it effects things, even along the margins, is really the question.\par \par \uc2 Up next, more with the panel on the presidential race rhetoric. And then making musical history 23 years ago today.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Kamala, I love you. You always have my back. You are really amazing.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I do.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You are the best partner I could imagine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You met with him at least once a week for three-and-a-half. You didn't have any concerns?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think the American people have a concern about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And the American people trust you in these moments, even when it's maybe uncomfortable?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I speak with not only sincerity but with a real firsthand account.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that's the same question, essentially, asked a different way by NBC. We're back with the panel. Leslie, there's a couple of those instances where the vice president on "The View," Stephen Colbert, and with me was asked, what would do you differently? How would you turn the page? And there wasn't really an answer for all three of them. And then there is this answer about when did you know that something was wrong with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Here you are today. NBC asked it differently. There really wasn't an answer. So, what about that?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I don't think there is going to be an answer because, one, she is -- it would be her opinion. Two, it is her current boss. You don't throw your boss, especially when you have been chosen not only to be on the ticket with him but when he stepped aside and he ordained you, in a sense, to be the nominee of this party. And in addition to that, you just don't throw your boss under the bus. She is also not in a position, able to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 With regard to distancing herself or changes, there have been changes. I mean, the things that she wants to do with regard to home health aides and things like that, and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But that doesn't come --\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: That's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's plan. That's her plan.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- that doesn't come as part of the answer, usually, right away. It seems like --\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I would agree with you there. That would have been my answer out of the box. I'm able to be critical of anybody on my side of the aisle. But at the same time, this -- when you have something working, and people laugh at Bidenomics, but the Nobel economists don't, and world that's reporting about the American economy, how strong it is and how much stronger it's becoming, if something is working, if it's not broken you don't fix it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's how Americans feel about it, though, Chris. In New Hampshire, President Biden was there. Why is he campaigning in New Hampshire? Is it tighter there?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I have no idea. I will be really honest. He was not helpful for him to be there. The only news that came out of it was when he said that he would lock \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up, and we should lock him up. He clarified and said politically. That is stomping completely on everything that your party is trying to stand for in regard to being the party of democracy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what Harris needs to do down the stretch of the campaign, Leslie mentioned behind the scenes and nuance. There is a lot of different policy positions than Biden. But people don't see that. Trump is a master of clarity. You know what he is going to do, how he is going to do it, and why he is going to do it. Harris, it's very murky.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New Hampshire is not getting tighter.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: No. It will be an eight, nine percent win for Harris.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, the polls overall, RCP average, 49.1 to 48.5. But if you look at this day in history, you look back 2016, 2020, 47.9 to 42, 50.8 to 42.8. Obviously 2020 came down to the wire, 2016 was a Trump win.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: So if we see even a fraction of the polling error in Trump's favor this year as we saw those last two cycles, he will win. One question is, are we going to see that again, right? And if so, how big would the error be? Is it possible it could go in the other direction? Are there still Trump voters who are as shy as they used to be, or are they out in the open?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What does James Carville see what we don't see?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I don't know. I think James Carville, who seems pretty bullish on Harris's chances, might have something up his sleeve. He's a wily guy. He has been around a long time. On the other hand, he might be trying to rally the troops just to keep morale somewhat up, because that is a pretty beaten down group at the moment, looking at that time exactly the type of polling you are talking about. To me the polling is interesting. More interesting is the behavior of these campaigns. She does not look confident.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I think I know why.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I have got to run.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I think I know why.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really quick.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Women are going to be that silent vote. We're going to see what happened in the midterms in Ohio on such larger scale, and that's what he is talking about, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We shall see. I've got do.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Are they overcorrecting for the last couple cycles?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's the question. Got to run.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, today's throwback.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE JOBS\uc2 , FORMER APPLE CEO: But the biggest thing about iPod is it holds 1,000 songs. This is a quantum leap because for most people, it's their entire music library.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you believe this? This is 23 years ago today. Music listening, changing forever. \uc2 Apple\uc2  releases the iPod, 1,000 songs fit into this device, the size of a deck of cards. It cost $399. Sleekly made. Less than an inch thick. Think about that. \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 , 23 years ago, and how much has changed since then. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Vice President Harris turns to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and the Boss to campaign in Atlanta. Former President Trump hits the trail as well. We have got them all covered.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241024ekan00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O`Brien\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: And where this strike might be going or maybe not going, with us right now is Sean O`Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, joining us from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Sean, it`s always good to see you. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SEAN O`BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: Great to see you as well, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This was all renegotiated, generous provisions here, up to 35 percent pay raises and the like. This is significantly better than the first deal offered. How do you think it will go? What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, I don`t know the ins and outs of it, but I know that the members are passionate about getting the best deal they possibly can.\par \par \uc2 And it`s good to see that a CEO recognizes that they have got trust issues. And I believe, as a result of them being on strike, that it signifies that there has been a total disregard for those machinists. And hopefully they got everything they wanted and move forward. You get a trillion-dollar backlog.\par \par \uc2 They need those workers, they need our members and they need the machinists back to work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would you vote yea on this?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I don`t know the particulars of the contract. I try not to speak about things, like some other people do when they don`t have all the facts, but hopefully they got the deal that`s going to work for them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you could never make it as a TV anchor then, Sean.\par \par \uc2 But I`m kidding. I`m kidding, of course.\par \par \uc2 Let me get your take on where this whole battle politically stands. You made a lot of news by not endorsing either \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but you made it very clear that your members might strongly feel the other way for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So I know I asked you this at the time of that decision not to make a decision behind one candidate or the other.\par \par \uc2 Do you regret that, that you weren`t clearer following your workers` wishes, and that is to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, we don`t regret our decision. We`re a pretty autonomous international union. As a general executive board, both candidates weren`t specific on a lot of issues that were important to us. So we let the local unions and the joint councils, which are regional councils, make their decision.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we do. The autonomy is very well-respected in this organization.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, not by \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` running mate. He had this to say about union leaders who didn`t make a commitment for the Democratic ticket. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: These are folks that are directly responsible to their members.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART, HOST, "THE DAILY SHOW": Right.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And, look, their members, some of them split off. The bottom line is, how can you be with a guy who wants to bust a union, is not there to make collective bargaining a right, not to make health care there? So...\par \par \uc2 STEWART: But that is the cognitive distance that I don`t quite understand. So you`re talking about unions, blue-collar unions.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Some of those leaders did not show the courage they needed to.\par \par \uc2 STEWART: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: A vast majority of union leaders showed the courage. They`re backing us.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think he`s saying that you didn`t show courage.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, that`s kind of a knucklehead statement.\par \par \uc2 I show courage every single day. Our organization shows courage every day. And to make statements, it`s kind of disrespectful to our members and the process. He`s talking like an educator, like he is in a classroom teaching a lesson.\par \par \uc2 The lesson that needs to be taught is that our members don`t need to be educated. Our members are educated. And that`s the problem with this party right now is, they are looking down on our members, saying they need to be educated.\par \par \uc2 What I would do if I was him is actually start talking to our members and find out what the actual problem is. Instead of pointing fingers and looking for excuses, find out what the problem is and create a solution so you gain that support back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I will be careful how I quote you here, Sean, but you had said of the Democratic Party "It has effed us over for over 40 years."\par \par \uc2 But over that time, your union has consistently and almost always back Democrats.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, was this like a -- did this suddenly dawn on you and said, we`re sick of it, I`m sick of it? What happened?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, I think -- Neil, I think you know my demeanor. I`m a pretty chill person.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re attacking me personally and the organization for listening to our members, you do get fired up. And then, like anything else, we look back and we look back at who passed trucking deregulation, who introduced it. We look at who passed \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 . We look at bankruptcy reform that never happened.\par \par \uc2 A lot of our agenda wasn`t put forth. Look, Joe Biden`s been the most pro- union president. I have said it and I will keep saying it again. However, one issue doesn`t define our support and/or our members` support.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, you look back over 40 years, and remember this. For the last 16 -- 16 out of the 20 years, the Democratic Party, and I`m a Democrat, have been in control. We didn`t get Employee Free Choice Act when we had a full Senate, Congress, and also a Democratic president.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lot of things that could have been done that haven`t been done. And you look right now, people are publicly saying in media forums like CNN and \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  that big tech is the -- they`re the Democratic supporters. And big tech is threatening a lot of our jobs with automation and A.I.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So big tech is the problem? You said that the Democratic Party is, in fact, bought and paid for by big tech companies. That didn`t endear you to many Democratic operatives.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Look, I`m not worried about Democratic operatives. I`m worried about people doing the right thing. And instead of portraying that for the working people, whether you`re a Democrat or a Republican, actually prove it by showing it.\par \par \uc2 Something is wrong. And I have stated this time and time again. If 60 percent of our members are leaning Republican, then it`s up to the Republicans to figure out why they`re voting that way -- Democrats to figure out why our members are voting that way, instead of pointing fingers and looking for excuses.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, whoever wins this election, they have got to win over working-class America. If you`re a Democrat, you have got to win back the support. If you`re a Republican portraying yourself you want to support working people, you`re going to have to prove it.\par \par \uc2 And I`m not going to sweat people coming at us with knucklehead comments or anything else. We have got to focus on fixing this country. There`s a divide down the middle, and let`s not put people in the middle of political parties.\par \par \uc2 Let`s fix the problem and work together.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so the way it stands now, we have \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going at it. Are you saying, when you talked about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a few minutes ago, if he were the nominee now, would you have committed the union support behind him?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If who was the nominee?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  were the nominee.\par \par \uc2 No, I would be in the same spot, I believe. A lot of our decision was based upon how these answers were answered around our roundtable and the surveys of our members. And, look, we have got a lot of issues ahead of us as working-class America, and all candidates weren`t as strong as they should have been on our issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Have you talked to him since that decision, nondecision, whatever you want to call it?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I have not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I have not talked to her.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I have not talked to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That says a lot there, right?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, I don`t think I`m on too many Christmas cards list this year. But...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, let`s say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins back the presidency. How will that go, you think, for you, for your members?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, the one thing that we`re going to do regardless of who wins the president`s election is demand and hopefully get a meeting and make certain that each and every candidate who has promised to be the working people`s president, that we hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 And there`s been a lot of documented potential support on our issues that are important to us. And what`s being said and what`s being done, we want to hold them accountable. So, it doesn`t matter who`s in there. We`re going to try and push our agenda forward either way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of your members like the idea of tariffs on foreign goods. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plans a lot of tariffs on a lot of foreign goods, particularly Chinese goods. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, look, I think a lot of the trade agreements in the past that we have spoke about previously, like \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 , we lost a lot of good American jobs. We lost a lot of good union jobs. We lost manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 I think everybody, regardless, especially if you`re in the working class, we want to see manufacturing come back. We want to see creating middle- class jobs. So I`m not certain about the tariffs, that`s going to solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 Let`s solve the problem of bringing manufacturing back to the United States, giving people the opportunity to have careers and produce products in this country. I mean, think about the pandemic. There was such a strain on supply chain because a lot of the products that we needed weren`t built in this country.\par \par \uc2 And then you look at the strike that happened a couple of weeks ago with the longshoremen. And, thankfully, that didn`t go on long. But, again, if we produced a lot of these products, we`d be in a far better spot of creating jobs and also keeping supply chain moving.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Sean, it`s always a pleasure talking to you.\par \par \uc2 There was no nasty language here, right? So that shows progress, right?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No. No, you know I never use nasty language with you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean O`Brien, thank you very much. Always good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 He speaks his mind. And we do respect that here and that he frequently comes on FOX to state that.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020241023ekan00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Continues To Dominate Retail Politics Battle; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Relying On Star Power To Attract Voters; That The Game Plan: Cringe Walz Video Tris To Link Trump To Project 2025; Not Deranged To Fear This: Lib Hosts Defending Having Trump Derangement Syndrome; Bird Blunder: AOC: Knock On Eagles Fans' Door During Eagles Game\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,273 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: -- and my colleague, John Roberts, weekdays, 1:00 p.m. Eastern Time for "AMERICA REPORTS".\par \par \uc2 real quick, Dow check, as we are an hour out now from the close, it was a down day on Wall Street, a drop of 344 points for the Dow as there are continued growing concerns over where this market goes next, still sitting at record highs though.\par \par \uc2 All right, that does it for us. Thanks again for joining us. THE FIVE starts right now.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Well, the king of retail politics, he's at it again while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  accuses \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of being exhausted, the former president, he was busy schmoozing voters and charging through three unique campaign pit stops in the past couple of days. There was a barber shop in the Bronx with "FOX & FRIENDS", a Steelers football game and a McDonald's in the battleground of Pennsylvania. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting on an apron and getting to work on the fries while disputing Kamala's claims who have worked at the Golden Arches back in the '80s.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm looking for a job, and I've always wanted to work at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , but I never did.\par \par \uc2 Never touched by a human hand, nice and clean.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Mr. President. You made it possible for people like us to meet you. I mean, thank you so much for all you are doing.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Worked for 15 minutes more than Kamala.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you going to put this in your resume?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I mean, she never worked here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Democrats in the media having a bit of a McMeltdown (ph). Not big fans of the former president working in the deep fryer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She actually worked in a McDonald's. She didn't go and pander and disrespect McDonald's workers by standing there in your red tie and take a picture.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who worked briefly as a fry cooker without a hair net, I might add.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He is so inept at pretending to be a real person that he really, literally cannot operate the frying machine as normal worker. This just shows what an ultimate fraud he is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I still found these images to be jarring.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's no logic to it. It's a stump. He's engaged in some really bizarre types of activities during this campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Trump also tried his charm out at a barber shop in the Bronx with Lawrence Jones on "FOX & FRIENDS".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You guys are the same as me. It's the same stuff. We were born the same way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want to say, I'm a registered Democrat. I voted for you the first time. Voted for you the second time.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And I'm going to vote for you again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Are you better?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, taxes continue to go up, and sometimes you feel like you're in a losing battle.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I know this place with the taxes and tax certiorari. All I can do is give you a great economy, and you're going to have to get your rents up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think he won the news cycle over the weekend, Greg, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, no, I think you know who won the media because I love -- I love the hacks who had to point out that Trump really doesn't work at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . I just read on some tool named Ron Philip Howski (ph) said it was staged. It was staged.\par \par \uc2 Because, you know, you'd expect the president has been shot at twice to go into an actual McDonald's and start working there. You dope. I use the right phrase then.\par \par \uc2 We understand he didn't actually fill out an application that doubles as a placemat at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . We also know that he didn't say that he worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . Somebody else did, and the reporters, instead of investigating that claim, they decide to, like, pull their hair out over this.\par \par \uc2 The irony is, if they had actually done their job the first time, this incredible opportunity to troll Harris would not have taken place. Kind of reminds me of how the media is now treating Trump as, "unstable and exhausted", when they didn't do the same for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who actually was and is unstable and exhausted.\par \par \uc2 Again, they had the opportunity for the investigative story, this giant story before them. You have a president that is incapacitated. They don't do it, and then they wait and they do it on Trump. It's comical.\par \par \uc2 I still remember. It wasn't long ago, a couple months ago, the dams were getting their mojo back. They're trying to convince everybody that that lemon that they drove out of the lot was a restored E.V. Bentley, turns out it's exploding Pinto.\par \par \uc2 The whole fantasy was desired to keep the polls close so they could keep the fund raising up. The problem is, the fundraising did go up, but she made a lot of money, but she's as flat as an irritated jellyfish. There's nothing happening there. So they have all the psyops of the media now comparing to Hitler, to Mussolini, and Stalin, no Mao, where's the diversity? So, they're all returning to the place they were at that he's a danger to society.\par \par \uc2 But then you -- it goes against everything you see at the barbershops and \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . Yes, he won.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And Judge, when you're on a campaign, if your opponent does something that's like, pretty great. I mean, at some point you just go, well done.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, well, they can't do that because Trump is Hitler and he is now fascist. So, but you know, the amazing part of this whole thing with \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's is that there was no more powerful way to say that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wasn't being truthful about working at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting on that apron, going in there and doing what he does best, and that is engaging with people, whether it's at a barber shop, McDonald's or just showing up at a Steelers Jets game, where the fans -- where the crowds go crazy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he can communicate. He can relate. He can talk. All she's got are platitudes. She can't engage in that communication, which was evident Thursday night at the Al Smith dinner.\par \par \uc2 I mean, where, you know, in a race this close, you would think you'd want to, you know, engage a few Catholics, but instead, she put out a video that was totally disrespectful of Catholics.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is he's walking the walk while she talks to talk.\par \par \uc2 But what is so incredible is that the media is that -- doesn't even care if she worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . They're not interesting -- interested in checking it out. They just want to make fun of him.\par \par \uc2 And finally, they called it a political stunt. I mean, as if Kamala at the border wall after they cleaned it all up, that was a political stunt. Or how about \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , you know, a good old hunter who can't even load his own shotgun. I mean, it's kind of crazy.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the exhaustion, you know, when she said, Trump is exhausted, and then he just out worked her all weekend.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: How do you see it, Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks for having me back. How are you?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Look, I think a couple of things when I thought it was a good move for him to go to the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's. I do think there's a juxtaposition between going to the McDonald's and actually connecting with the folks who work at the McDonald's. There was once a time in the 1980s when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . She did it in a semester while she was at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , where, you know, that's what folks did. They went home from college, worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , made some extra money to pay for books.\par \par \uc2 I think in today's day and age, there's a lot of working families that depend on a McDonald's salary to make ends meet, to pay the mortgage, to pay the rent. And I think, you know, in 2019 \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was on the strike lines at with \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's workers demanding higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Also in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he was going to raise the minimum wage when he was president for -- in the four years, he didn't do that.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think there's a juxtaposition of him doing this photo op versus actually standing with workers at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . And I think this comes at a unique time in the country where we've seen both red states and blue states alike, voters in these states go to the ballot box and say, we want to raise the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, voters voted to raise the minimum wage. In Arizona, voters voted to raise minimum wage. In Florida, voter vote -- voters voted to raise minimum wage. In Arkansas, voters voted to raise minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 So, it's about time in this country that our lawmakers catch up with that and we finally raised the federal minimum wage, which is what the Harris campaign is advocating for.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, California did raise the minimum wage, and how many fast food workers lost their jobs and all those restaurants closed?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I don't have that exact number now, Dana, but I'll have my staff get it for you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I liked how his hair matched the fries and how his tie matched the catch up. It was like with the apron. It was like a Norman Rockwell painting. Just gives you a warm feeling inside, or the color scheme was kind of like an Andy Warhol painting, you know, like --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's really what you're going for?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Political pop art. Think about it, the mug shot fight, fight, fight and this, the three most iconic images of the campaign, and he owns them all.\par \par \uc2 And these images have been now baked into Americans brands very much like a warm apple pie.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I love those.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And so, Trump is America. Good luck running against America.\par \par \uc2 She had an opportunity to do this. She could have gone to a \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . She could have gone to a football game. She could have gone to a barber shop. Why didn't she?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: She went into church. She was in church all weekend.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: She's had more than one day to go, Richard. There's a WNBA finals going on. There's been five games. She's gone to zero. Why didn't she go there? Why didn't she go to, like an SNL taping, do it once.\par \par \uc2 She didn't go to Al Smith, she's not even in a barber or a hair salon. If she wants to connect, there's lots of ways to do it, but he's one step ahead of her every single time.\par \par \uc2 She's doing Bret, he already did it. She's doing a town hall. He already did it with CNN. He did it in the spring, and he was just as tough on Trump as he was on her.\par \par \uc2 She now doesn't have the joy. He's DJing. He's working the fries. She's angry. She's screaming. He's actually more middle-class than she is, and that's all she talks about.\par \par \uc2 Now, yes, they're saying this was staged. Like, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's driving through Pennsylvania and sees the Golden Arches and like, pull over. That's not how it works.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if you want to know stage, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's entire four years was stage, Richard.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: By the way, there was no -- \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's even admitted it, there's no record they have Kamala working.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, thank you for saying that. You know, but that's the thing, no one in the media cares to address the fact that there was zero evidence.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What's the proof?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: The manager of the McDonald she worked at said she worked there.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Really?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's a friend of hers?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Another outlet said that --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  says there's no record.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: NBC, there's -- it was confirmed by NBC in the 1980 in the summer that she worked at a McDonald's between in 198 -- in the 1980s she worked at a McDonald's between the summer of \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's like in 1980s we didn't have records. It was not the 1880s.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: There was a record of her working there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: When Biden was working at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Come on, that is fine.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That was good.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was perfecting his Friday game, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was serving up for campaign message with a bunch of Hollywood celebs. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: 15 days to go and Kamala momentum is in the toilet. V.P. Harris look into Hollywood to rescue her stalled-out campaign.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, she was rolling out the star power with Lizzo, that's her on the left, Usher and Stevie Wonder.\par \par \uc2 And later this week, she got the democratic dream team of Barry and Michelle O., but there's one big name missing in this coconut party. The Harris campaign is said to be abandoning \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the final weeks before Election Day, with no current plans for Kamala to campaign with the big guy, but she is hitting up \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  to whine about Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: A role model of what it means to be committed to certain standards, including international rules and norms, but also standards of decorum.\par \par \uc2 And what you see in my opponent, a former president of the United States, really is it demeans the office.\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, MSNBC HOST: Do you think some of the resistance of some men, Black and white is misogynist?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I have the support of countless Black men who are in elected positions. I am very clear I must earn, earn the vote of everyone, regardless of their race or gender.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, Judge, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  is a staunch defender of decorum and misogyny. I would call him the Rosa Parks of hate crime hoaxes. Forget his dedicated defense of Tawana Brawley in 1987 which launched a whole litany all the way up to Jussie Smollett. So, in a sense, he really is a forerunner.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You are so right, Greg, I must tell you, your knowledge of history as it relates to \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  is really incredible.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Unparalleled.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it is unparalleled.\par \par \uc2 You know, 