ly compared to where most of our citizens were pre- pandemic, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in charge. And it's hugely stressful for parents and single people alike.\par \par \uc2 But if you believe that we can do better, that our country can do better, that we can have higher wages for the middle class and a higher standard of living for Americans, then you're going to support Trump, and you'll ignore the incessant propaganda from now until November 5th, and it is heating it up. We'll get into that a little bit later on.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember when they said that Trump was going to start all those wars, when he'd crashed the economy? 2015, 2016. Well, of course, he did the opposite. Back then, just as now, they're trying to scare you all away from embracing a more America focused economic and foreign policy. But historians are going to judge this campaign, I think, 100 years from now, as an inflection point in American history.\par \par \uc2 If Trump is reelected, this will be seen as a time when Americans took their country back again from the incompetent, bloated, corrupt forces that have run us into the ground for decades, and that they returned it and turned us to a period when we still believe we're a noble nation with an amazing history, not perfect, but still amazing, and that we can still do great things.\par \par \uc2 Musk believes that with his whole heart, Trump and Vance obviously believe that which is why they're running. But the question is, do enough voters believe it, or have they gotten so beaten down and so beaten up and so intimidated that they have given up? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 From the national pride and an amazement about \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the enthusiasm and the patriotism and love that you're seeing at all these Trump rallies, including in California over the weekend, I'm betting that enough Americans still believe the best is yet to come. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Victor Davis Hanson, Senior Fellow at the Hoover Institute. Victor, they're really, really annoyed that they don't have every billionaire on their side who wants to maintain the type of status quo we have in a state like California today, and they're going to do everything they can to try to stop this and they are.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 HOOVER INSTITUTION\uc2 : They are, and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  represents everything they're not. He's -- I mean, he's a renaissance man of the 21st century, Laura. He reinvented social media. He's saving \uc2 NASA\uc2  from itself. He created the whole EV industry, basically single- handedly. Let's not even get into the AI and the tunnels and all the other things he does. So, he's a renaissance man. He's a Da Vinci of our age, and they hate that.\par \par \uc2 This election is starting to come down to the people who lecture us and say, you can't do things, can't do this, can't do that. Got to think this way, must be this way, to the people are getting tired of being lectured. They said, we can do stuff, and we're going to do stuff. And one side says you should be ashamed of who you are. Your constitution was flawed. It got worse. Now, it's even the worst of all. You should be guilty of who you are. And the other side say, you know, I'm tired of that. Our civilization has nothing to apologize for. We're better than any other alternative, and we don't have to be perfect to be good.\par \par \uc2 So, I think people were getting to an inflection point like you pointed out, and you can feel it. You can feel it in the country that people are getting tired of this. And I think we're going to see something like we did in 1980 when people said, I'm tired of \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  lecturing us, and Reagan said, we're not going to be lectured anymore. We can still -- we're still Americans. The best is ahead, and I think --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We'll --\par \par \uc2 HANSON: -- point again.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We'll cue up the sound in 