ney\uc2 . It's terrifying to watch him do that. I would not have felt comfortable in the foo with him after watching that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No. Well, again, they're trying to convince men that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , who is so bizarre, is the manly guy that you need to embrace for the 21st century. And meanwhile, you got --\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: I did not get that watching that video.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. J.D. Vance is meanwhile like going on all these shows and like making mincemeat of all these hosts. And that's cool brains are really cool, that to me. I think that's the coolest thing ever.\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: He just -- and for him to -- he tries to act like an everyman. He's out there with the Beretta A400, that's a pretty pricey gun. That's a pretty pricey shotgun. I mean it's, you know, a couple thousand dollars. So that, his bougie gun that he doesn't use. I mean that just, to me, it was screaming privilege.\par \par \uc2 He's out there in his brand-new brush pants and his brand new reflective and he's out there not a speck of dirt on him. You know he's got his nice hunting dogs, bird dog out there. He's got his nice Italian gun.\par \par \uc2 You know, Beretta is a nice gun. But again, this is a guy who's like I'm a coach. I'm an everyman. I'm, you know, your regular average knucklehead. But I don't know average knuckleheads who have like that kind of a nice shotgun. I just don't. I mean, he's just everything about him is fake.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, the scary thing, millions of unregistered voters who are hunters, that's who they're going after.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, they got the -- you should be registered and you should not be voting for that duo. Remember, though, Dana, that Kamala has a gun too.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What kind of gun do you own, and when and why did you get it?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I have a Glock. And I've had it for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Have you ever fired it?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes, of course, I have. At a shooting range. Yes, of course, I have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's not believable either. I want to see the video for shooting and shooting range.\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: I doubt that she -- I don't know. I kind of doubt that she has one, considering that she tried to, she wanted to ban those guns in California. I mean, they're considered an unsafe gun in California. She led the charge against that.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, she wanted to ban -- she wanted to make pistols national firearm NFA items. So, I wish that -- I wish that she would have been asked, you know, by Whitaker about that. What generation do you want and why do you want to ban them?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, we're ending on a bang, Dana. Wish we had more time. We'll have you back soon. Thank you so much. And remember, it's America now and forever. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241015ekae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Campaign In Pennsylvania; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Tries To Gain Support From Black Male Voters; Four Dead In Strike On \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Location; Family-Owned Business Destroyed By Hurricane Milton; Father And Son Recount Harrowing Rescue; Road To White House Could Go Through Pennsylvania; Former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  Draws Controversy Over Comments On Immigration During Campaign Event For \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Granting More Interviews Including To Fox News; Afghan National Accused Of Plotting Election Day Terror Attack; New Polls Continue To Show Close Presidential Race; Republican Vice President Candidate J.D. Vance Answers Question About Venezuelan Gangs Taking Over Apartment Complexes in U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Brit Hume, Chad Pergram, Guy Benson, Greg Palkot,  Peter Doocy, Bryan Llenas, Mark Meredith, Jonathan Serrie, Madison  Scarpino \par \uc2 8,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Dogs and humans can get matching Crocs together, Greg, October 23rd.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hey. You know, I think in your face, Harold, is my new favorite segment.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you, Greg. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Cleaning up from hurricanes Milton and Helene, we will have survivor stories from Florida and North Carolina. We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 Why Republican lawmakers are upset over the handling of the suspect in the alleged Election Day terror plot.\par \par \uc2 Israel hits hard at terrorist enemies, but also takes a hit on its own personnel. We'll go live to Israel in a bit.\par \par \uc2 But first, both presidential candidates are in Pennsylvania tonight, the crucial state of Pennsylvania, the Commonwealth. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about to speak live there at a town hall meeting in Montgomery County, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris will hold an event in Erie, Pennsylvania, the appearances in one of the crucial states. Most crucial really comes as we near the three week mark in the countdown to Election Day. We will be there later this week, Wednesday for our exclusive interview with Vice President Harris. It's her first interview on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas starts us off tonight from Oaks, Pennsylvania tonight, the site of the Trump event. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Brrr, good evening. It's shaping up to be Pennsylvania's closest presidential election ever. This hour, former President Trump will hold a town hall here in Philadelphia's suburbs for the first time this campaign, and the focus will be the economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: I think that President Trump is getting gaining momentum in Pennsylvania, and I think I am too.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Pennsylvania's Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  has cut Democratic Senator Bob Casey's lead in half, according to the latest New York Times poll, while former President Trump and Vice President Harris remain in a tossup race.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Trump campaign balking at Harris' demands the former president release his medical records.\par \par \uc2 TIM MURTAUGH, \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  2024 SENIOR ADVISER: People care about the economy and the border. They don't care about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  waving around a note from her doctor, President Trump is perfectly healthy.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Trump continues to focus on the economy, the most important issue to Pennsylvania voters. The former president blasting high inflation, promising tax cuts, and polls show the message is resonating.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The prices have skyrocketed, especially in Pennsylvania right now. You can't afford even groceries here.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Trump is up 11 points on the issue of the economy in Pennsylvania, doubling in the last month. The former president's path to victory in Pennsylvania includes chipping away at Harris's support in Philadelphia's blue suburbs, where she has a double digit lead.\par \par \uc2 In Bellwether Erie County, Republicans say there is enthusiasm on the ground.\par \par \uc2 TOM EDDY, ERIE COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA GOP PARTY CHAIR: I see a significant amount of energy happening right now. We go through probably 50 to 100 yard signs a day.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Over 405,000 mail in ballots have been returned in Pennsylvania, but so far, three times as many are from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't feel confident when you have mail in voting. \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  had a commission on it. Nobody does mail in voting.\par \par \uc2 You look at other countries, France, they go to paper ballots.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BARTIROMO\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: But you don't want to discourage people from mail in voting?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I don't. Look, we have to win the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): In that same interview with \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2 , former President Trump says he believes the border is an even bigger issue than the economy.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, the Border Patrol Union, the National Border Patrol Council, endorsed Trump, Trump promising to hire 10,000 new Border Patrol agents, give them 10 percent raises and $10,000 signing bonuses, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas in Pennsylvania. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  also in the Commonwealth, tonight, she will deliver remarks at a rally in about an hour and a half in Erie. Correspondent Mark Meredith is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, Vice President Harris is back in Pennsylvania trying to shore up support among Black men, as New polling shows more of them are considering voting for former President Trump compared to four years prior.\par \par \uc2 In a new interview, Harris says she will address Black Americans' needs on day one.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My policies include what I know is going to have a profound impact on Black folks around economic policy, but everyone will benefit from them.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): That new policy platform includes a pledge to offer more government loans to Black entrepreneurs and address health inequities.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  says it's too soon to tell if it will sway his state.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): We just have to acknowledge that it's going to be very close, and it's going to be hand to hand combat for every vote in counties all across Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Last week, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  urged Black Americans to stay engaged.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I understand people feeling frustrated and feeling we can do better.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Today in South Georgia, it was former President Clinton's turn.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I even had one guy with a MAGA cap asked to take a picture. It made me feel good.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): But it was Clinton's comments on Sunday, which Republicans, including the Trump campaign want to go viral. Clinton said the death of 22-year-old Laken Riley was likely preventable. The Georgia nursing student was murdered in February, allegedly by an illegal immigrant from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Well, if they had all been properly vetted, that probably wouldn't have happened. But if they all properly vetted, that doesn't happen, and America is not having enough babies to keep our populations up, so we need immigrants that have been vetted to do work.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Clinton did not blame Harris for Riley's death. Instead, he says it's Trump who is playing politics with border security after urging Congress to block immigration reform legislation.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: We didn't design divide people from their children, and we did total vetting before people got in.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump killed the bill. The bill was written -- being written by senior Republicans in the Senate, and he killed the bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): The vice president should have a fairly large crowd, which takes the stage behind me here in about an hour or so. She's got multiple events scheduled this week. She's going to be in states where they really think they're going to defend their territory, the so called blue wall, making multiple stops in Wisconsin, Michigan, and yes, right here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Bret, tomorrow, she's got a big radio interview with Charlamagne tha God in Michigan, an indication she really is trying to keep the Black vote turnout high on Democrat side. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith in Erie. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, there are said to be growing tensions between the Harris campaign and President Biden's team. Some on the Harris team apparently feel the White House has not been doing enough to coordinate the president's message with the nominee's message.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. When President Biden dropped out of the race, he said it was time to come together to beat Trump. Three months later, there are some signs that he and the V.P. he endorsed are coming apart.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, WHITE HOUSE SPOKESMAN: She is running as her own candidate. She is her own candidate.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Lately, President Biden has sounded frustrated about being upstaged by his vice president.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The White House insists, while ensuring that all critical White House functions are fully staffed, we have made significant changes to guarantee the vice president's team has all of the support and resources that they need.\par \par \uc2 But according to Axios, Biden's team wants Harris to win the election, but many senior Biden aides remain wounded by the president being pushed out of his reelection bid.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He's definitely not used to being a supporting role in election that he intended to be running in.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): After Biden dropped out, his close allies were sidelined for advisers like \uc2 Obama\uc2  alum David Plouffe, he's the one surveying early voting results and telling New York magazine we like what we're seeing so far. In most states, we're seeing a lot more first time or irregular voting Democrats than Republicans, and Trump is very reliant on first time and irregular voters.\par \par \uc2 Trump allies are hoping to exploit their opponent's personal problems.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's no argument between the Trump and Vance teams.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The Harris campaign declined to clear the air today twice.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: I think first and foremost, when you think about tensions between a president the vice president, you've got to think about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: To answer my question, is there tension now between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, look, I think that when Vice President Harris is out there on the stump and she's answering voter questions and she's answering questions from reporters like Bret and yourself every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): He could have said no, there are no tensions. He did not say that, but there are some signs that the Biden family may be willing to set aside differences to help Harris win.\par \par \uc2 The first lady has been out there campaigning for Harris for the last few days in battleground states, and the first lady is the one who a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporter described in her book this year as the family's self- appointed grudge holder, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks led the way on Wall Street. The Dow, gaining 201 for its first close above 43,000, the S&P 500 finished ahead 45 and again, a new record close there, the NASDAQ jumping 160.\par \par \uc2 A new warning tonight for U.S. citizens to get out of Lebanon as Israel maintains its offensive against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists there.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. is giving Israel some help as tensions with Iran escalate and Israel continues to deliver and absorb more blows.\par \par \uc2 We get the latest tonight from senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot in Haifa, Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Death and destruction all around the Mideast after Israel claimed it was striking a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  location in a Gaza Hospital, a fire at a next door shelter was set ablaze, leaving four dead, 40 injured, including children.\par \par \uc2 AQUNI KHATTAB, GAZA RESIDENT (through translator): We woke up to the sound of a huge explosion. We thought it was Judgment Day.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): As more horrors of a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  attack on an Israeli military base are revealed. Four soldiers were killed, all 19 years old, dozens injured, some seriously.\par \par \uc2 Casualties were Medevac out after a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  drone packed with explosives, flying low from Lebanon penetrated the camp undetected.\par \par \uc2 Touring the scene, Israeli Defense Minister Yoav Gallant said, "We pledge to learn from this strike"\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, sirens in Tel Aviv as three \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rockets entered central Israeli airspace, then were knocked down as Israel weighs retaliation for the Iranian ballistic missile barrage earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon confirms it is sending a THAAD, or Terminal High Altitude Area Defense battery to Israel. The sophisticated gear will be operated by 100 U.S. soldiers, the first ground deployment to Israel of American troops during this war.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why did you decide to give permission for the THAADs to be deployed in Israel?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: To defend Israel.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): As Israel's war against \uc2 Hezbollah in Lebanon\uc2  pushes across the border and air strikes pound including Beirut, there is new word from the U.S. Embassy strongly encouraging American citizens to leave now as evacuation flights will not continue indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): And back to that threatened Israeli attack on Iran, according to a just out report from \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , quoting two unnamed Officials, Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu has told President Biden he is willing to strike military rather than oil or nuclear facilities in Iran. It is suggested that would be a more limited counter strike to avoid full scale war. More to come, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot live in Haifa. Greg, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll check in on hurricane cleanup with live reports from Florida and North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, China uses a record 125 aircraft along with an aircraft carrier and ships in a large scale military exercise surrounding Taiwan and its outlying islands. The drills simulated the ceiling off of key ports there. The move comes four days after Taiwan celebrated the founding of its government. Its president declared Taiwan will resist Chinese annexation or encroachment.\par \par \uc2 Canada and India each expel six diplomats as part of an escalating dispute over the June 2023 assassination of a Sikh activist in Canada. Canada says police uncovered evidence of a targeted campaign against its citizens by agents of the Indian government. India says it voluntarily withdrew its diplomats.\par \par \uc2 Archeologists in Denmark make a landmark discovery that could hold important clues to the Viking era. They found a burial ground containing about 50 exceptionally well preserved skeletons. Experts say the relics were preserved thanks to favorable soil chemistry, and could present clues to the past.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Presence along Florida's Gulf Coast, cleaning up after back-to-back hurricanes left devastation in their region. And this evening, we look at a fourth-generation family business in the Tampa area that has been especially hard hit this time around.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie is in Tampa tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In the wake of Hurricane Milton, the Tampa headquarters, a Pilgrim Permocoat, looks more like a dollhouse.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT FORLONG, PRESIDENT, PILGRIM PERMOCOAT: We've been through a lot of little or smaller hurricanes, obviously, and with very little damage, that building has held amazingly well for all these years. And we were so lucky this time.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (voice over): The family-owned construction products business has operated out of this former cigar factory since 1956.\par \par \uc2 DANA HEAD, VICE PRESIDENT, PILGRIM PERMOCOAT: I've been at this building since a little girl, just roller skating around. And, you know, I have a lot of fond memories. And it was just -- it was really hard seeing it like that.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (voice over): Milton caused around $50 billion in damage statewide, according to President Biden, who visited the Tampa Bay area, Sunday to meet with first responders.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is all a team effort, folks. We made a big difference, and it saved lives. But there is much more to do. We're going to do everything we can to get power back in your home. Not only helping you recover, but to help you build back stronger.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (voice over): Police have been facilitating the return of fuel to empty pumps and intervening when tempers flare.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Getting warned like the rest of us!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (voice over): Florida Gulf Coast residents are uneasy about the timing of Milton, which made its October 9 landfall less than two weeks after Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHAW, VICE PRESIDENT, PILGRIM, PERMOCOAT: I guess it's kind of part of living here now. We're going to have storms and stuff's going to get ruined, but everything can be rebuilt. So, as long as everyone sticks together and keeps a positive mindset, I think it's, you know, it's the only way to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): A great attitude. Utility crews have restored power to about 3 million homes and businesses statewide. According to poweroutage.us, just under 300,000 customers remain without electricity. Utilities hope to have them back online by late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And according to GasBuddy, while more than 80 percent of Florida gas stations now have fuel here in Tampa, only half have gas to sell. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Serrie, live in Tampa. Jonathan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We are still hearing harrowing stories of survival in North Carolina, in the mountains, following the unexpected onslaught from Hurricane Helene. This evening, one family shares how they narrowly escaped floodwaters rushing into their home.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Madison Scarpino reports from Asheville tonight. Good evening, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MULTIMEDIA REPORTER: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear how many people are still missing, and the death toll from Helene now stands at more than 250 people. Most of those deaths are here in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BURGIN, RESIDENT, SWANNANOA, NORTH CAROLINA: Every time I come here, I feel really anxious.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): Father and son, Michael and Tony Burgin are trying to salvage anything they can after escaping their house that was nearly submerged in Helene's floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 BURGIN: That's where we were in the attic.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): They say, when the water started rising in their Swannanoa home, 17 days ago, they decided to go up to their attic.\par \par \uc2 TONY BURGIN, RESIDENT, SWANNANOA, NORTH CAROLINA: The water was rising so quick, we really had no time to even like think about what we were going to do next. And so, we made the decision to get out and swim through our house, basically.\par \par \uc2 M. BURGIN: It became clear that we have to get through a window.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): And to that's what they did. Busting through a window and climbing on the roof, a stranger on a kayak eventually rescued the family and their dogs.\par \par \uc2 T. BURGIN: It was horrible. It was -- it was insane.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 T. BURGIN: It was -- because incredibly chaotic. Yes.\par \par \uc2 M. BURGIN: Yes, we were definitely panicking.\par \par \uc2 I think if we had done the same thing 10 times, I don't think we would have made it out of there 10 times, you know. I think, we are lucky to be alive.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): But it's not just people's homes taking a hit from Helene. Businesses across the region rely on tourists, and owners say this time of year is when they would normally make the bulk of their money.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN KING, OWNER, 12 BONES SMOKEHOUSE: There are so many areas that are decimated and won't be open for business for, you know, until sometime next year, at best.\par \par \uc2 KEITH LAGUNA, OWNER, SAINT BRIGHID'S: Three weeks ago, things were great, you know, we were preparing for our busiest season of the year. Just like most everyone in Asheville.\par \par \uc2 HALLE HIRSCH, OWNER, SAINT BRIGHID'S: We started this business when it was a dirt floor and we had a vision and a dream. Now, it's back to being a dirt floor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (on camera): And Bret, when natural disasters like this happen, people often take advantage of the situation. In Watauga County, another hard-hit area, the sheriff's office confirms that there have been several looting incidents and two arrests so far.\par \par \uc2 We're waiting to hear if there had been any similar incidents here in Buncombe County. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go to the touchscreen a look at Pennsylvania's importance to the presidential race. It also features tonight's 50 races in 50 days, as we promised.\par \par \uc2 Plus, analysis from Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 With 22 days to go, the Trump and Harris campaigns focusing on voter outreach in key battleground states and beyond.\par \par \uc2 The former president wrapping up his western swing over the weekend with events in California, Nevada, and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 First Lady Jill Biden also in Arizona, campaigning for Harris on abortion access.\par \par \uc2 J.D. Vance made his pitch on manufacturing in Pennsylvania, as Vice President Harris visited people impacted by Hurricane Helene in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 We reported earlier, former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  also hitting the trail for Harris, reaching out to rural voters in Georgia, but those comments he made there about immigration and the border have become a lightning rod already.\par \par \uc2 Now, former President Trump and the vice president are both campaigning in Pennsylvania today. The former president participating in a town hall in suburban Philadelphia, less than one week after his most recent trip to that crucial battleground State.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Harris is holding a rally on the other side of the state, in Erie. Polls in Pennsylvania have been deadlocked, always within the margin of error. The current average of polling has the former president ahead of the Democratic nominee by three-tenths of a point. It is tight.\par \par \uc2 I will be speaking with Vice President Harris in Pennsylvania, Wednesday. You will see that all here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Also, in Pennsylvania, you will find today's 50 races and 50 days spotlight.\par \par \uc2 The latest Fox Power Rankings find the House context in the Pennsylvania, Eighth District is a toss-up. All of these yellow races in the House are toss ups.\par \par \uc2 Incumbent \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  faces Republican challenger Trump-backed Rob Bresnahan. He has been a supporter of unions, a traditionally progressive position.\par \par \uc2 Cartwright is considered a moderate Democrat. In 2020 he resisted calls from the left-wing of his party to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you look at 2016, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  lost that district by eight points. But 2020, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  narrowly wins the district. It's redrawn in 2020. The three points there when he went on to win Pennsylvania and the presidency.\par \par \uc2 Now, the road to the White House, we've said it time and time, likely goes right through the battleground of Pennsylvania. And the eighth district race could serve as a bellwether on November 5th. We'll be watching it. Just one of the races we will be watching on election night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, U.S. FIRST LADY: We will vote for a new generation of leaders. We will choose a new way forward.\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: There are seven states where the election is too close to call. We could win them all.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: And we could lose them all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you could. Surrogates for Vice President Harris on the campaign trail as we prepare for the final three weeks of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening. You know, you hear there former President Clinton, and I referenced and we heard earlier in the show that soundbite in Georgia where he talks about not vetting illegal immigrants and the death of Laken Riley. And boy, it seemed like that was a major stumble or a gift to the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: You would think so, wouldn't you? But that's the one thing about surrogates, Bret, is it's good to have them, especially really famous ones like former presidents, but you can't necessarily control what they are going to say. And of course, you notice in that soundbite Clinton went on to talk about how well he and his administration had vetted incoming immigrants whose presence is needed because we will need -- may need the laborers and the workers to fill out the population as our birth rate is not necessarily keeping up. So you can't control what they're going to say, and sometimes they say things that help the other side. But they are protecting their own reputation is what happened there, I think.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just to remind folks how much of the Trump campaign and the former president has come back to this issue of this woman who dies in Georgia, Laken Riley, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  let in the savage monster who murdered Laken Riley. Laken's blood is on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's hands, and she was in charge of the border. There are many, many people like that.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  should not be asking for your vote. She should be begging Laken Riley's family for forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Anyway, it's just a focus, and obviously the former president kind of stepping into all of that with that comment. Where do you see the race today? Today the former president said immigration may be a bigger issue than the economy. But, as you look at it and these polls around the country, where do you see it?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, Bret, you know, the polls really haven't moved very much. This has been kind of a dead heat race across the board, including the battleground states, almost from the time of the conventions and beyond even that.\par \par \uc2 But the behavior of the candidates may tell us something, particularly the behavior of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her campaign where it's clear now, especially as an example, your interview upcoming with her, that the strategy of hiding her or only having her do interviews with happy talk programs is being abandoned. Not completely, but to some extent, the "60 Minutes" interview is an example, your upcoming interview is another. And I think they've realized that hiding the candidate, particularly one not so very well-known as she is, isn't working. And they are trying now, at great risk, it seems to me, to put her out more and to allow her to subject herself to more probing questions than she might get on some of these other shows.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, she hasn't done all that well with the happy talk shows. She was on "The View" the other day and booted a question about what would be different in her administration from the Biden administration. And asked it again later on a late night comedy show, she booted it again. So, I think that's -- if you were managing her campaign, I don't think you would be tempted to put her out for tougher interviews, particularly when you think how badly the "60 Minutes" interview went, unless you think you are losing ground and it's your only hope.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Let me just take a second and be clear. There were no concessions for this interview, no stipulations. We're given roughly 30 minutes, but the timing was the concession. They are giving from us 5:00 p.m. to roughly 5:30. My show starts at 6:00. We'll do it live outside Philadelphia. So that's why there's a pre-tape. We planned to run the whole thing as live without commercial breaks. We were working the logistics today. And no one has the questions, just me. And I plan to be the same as I have been, which is tough but fair to candidates who come on, as you did before me on this show.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, thank you, Bret. I'm sure it will be just that way. And I will be watching with great interest as I think will millions of other people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why some congressional lawmakers are so upset over the arrest of a suspect allegedly plotting a terror attack on Election Day. We will bring that you story.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel on the ever tightening race for the White House, where we are at this minute.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a great shot of the sunset here. This is on the mall. Just to give you a perspective, this is from the Washington Monument, a camera. You are looking down at the World War II monument. In the distance, there is the reflecting pool, and then you can see the Lincoln Memorial and the sunset in D.C. Just wanted to give you perspective on what you are looking at.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, acting Labor Secretary Julie Su has gone to Seattle to meet with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union representing about 33,000 striking workers. Her intervention comes days after the plane maker announces it will send 60 day notice in mid-November to staff being made redundant as part of a plan to cut 17,000 jobs and take $5 billion in charges. The strike is now in its fifth week at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  is facing a new wave of lawsuits tonight. The entertainment mogul is accused of raping women, sexually assaulting men, and molesting a 16-year-old boy. The accusers are part of what their lawyers say is a group of more than 100 alleged victims who are in the process of taking action against Combs in the wake of his sex trafficking arrest last month.\par \par \uc2 Up on Capitol Hill, there's considerable frustration tonight over the case of an Afghan national accused of plotting an Election Day terror attack. The suspect was previously vetted and cleared to do security work with the CIA. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is denying briefings to top Republicans despite some of them representing the district in which this suspect resides. Here is senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: An Election Day conspiracy foiled. But Republicans say the Biden administration's bungled Afghanistan withdrawal may have cracked the door to an alleged terror plot.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2 , (R-OK) \uc2 SENATE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE\uc2 : When you evacuate people that quick, and then you bring them to the United States, the vetting process isn't really what it should be.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The feds busted Nasir Tawhidi in Senator Mullin's home state of Oklahoma. He appears to have entered the U.S. from Afghanistan on some sort of specialized vista with less rigorous standards.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS, (R-FL): We knew that there was no real vetting process of Afghan nationals coming into the United States.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The suspect was vetted previously for contract work with the CIA. Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas defended how the government handled this case.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: If we obtain information subsequently that suggests the individual could be of danger, we take appropriate law enforcement action. That is exactly what we did in this case.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  personally briefed both Oklahoma senators before the arrest. But Oklahoma House members, like \uc2 Tom Cole\uc2  and \uc2 Stephanie Bice\uc2  were never briefed. Bice asking what the administration has to hide. Public details of the terror plot are limited. It's unclear whether the suspect allegedly cooked up the plan in Afghanistan or \uc2 ISIS\uc2  radicalized him once he got here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2 , (R-OK) SENATE HOMELAND SECURITY COMMITTEE: This is real. And we have people that are trying to be able to kill us that are within our own country. we are living on borrowed time through this moment.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: While authorities diffused one plot, There is worry about the potential for others.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL) HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: I fear this is the tip of the iceberg for what we're facing in terms of terrorist threats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Terrorism resonates in Oklahoma City. It bears the scars of the federal office building bombing in 1995. The prosecutor in that case, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest developments on the campaign trail. First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in New York, covering the New Jersey transit commuter train striking a chunk of the tree on the tracks. The train operator was killed, actually, and nearly two dozen people injured. Authorities are trying to determine how the tree ended up on the tracks.\par \par \uc2 FOX 2 in San Francisco as a Navy fleet week performance ends with a parachutist crashing into two spectators. One of the six jumpers on the leapfrog team missed the preplanned landing mark, came down on a mother and a teenager. Authorities say the two suffered minor injuries. The parachutist was not hurt.\par \par \uc2 And these are live looks at Corpus Christi Texas from FOX 38 and Cape Canaveral Florida from FOX 35, two affiliates. The big story there tonight, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  -- have you seen this video -- pulls off the boldest test flight yet of its enormous starship rocket, the first stage booster flying back onto the launch pad after blasting off. The launch tower's monstrous metal arms catching the descending stainless steel booster, gripping it tightly, dangling well above the ground. I think I watched this video seven times. The jubilant \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  called it science fiction without the fiction part.\par \par \uc2 And a mission to explore Jupiter's moon Europa beginning with a launch from the Kennedy Space Center. The Europa Clipper will travel almost 2 billion miles to reach the moon in 2030. It will investigate whether Europa could support human life.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We're going to usher in a new golden age of American success for the citizens of every race, religion, color, and creed.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This man is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 My policies include what I know is going to have a profound impact on black folks.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: She has got all the money, and he's got the momentum. You would rather be him.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D-PA): We just have to acknowledge that it's going to be very close and it's going to be hand-to-hand combat for every vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it is tight. We have talked about it, talked about it with Brit Hume. We mentioned African American men and the effort to get to get black men. If you look at a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll, presidential support among black voters in general 2016, Democrats 2020, and then you see 2024. And a big shift there as far as support for former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Two new national head-to-head polls, NBC and ABC-Ipsos, and there you see it. It tells the story on a national level. And these are registered voters.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  senior fellow Marc Thiessen, and Meghan Hays, former assistant to President Biden and director of Message Planning.\par \par \uc2 OK, Marc, obviously, the vice president choosing to come on FOX on Wednesday in Pennsylvania. Took a lot of back and forth, but she decided to make that call, and we're excited to ask her some tough but fair questions on a host of issues. Why do you think she is making that call?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": Because I think the race is tightening, and she was ahead before and now she's not. That doesn't mean that she is not going to win the race and that it's not tight. But it's moving in Trump's favor right now.\par \par \uc2 Look, if you look at the "New York Times"-Siena poll, they found that about 18 percent of the electorate in the key swing states is not -- either undecided or leaning Trump or Harris but still could change their mind. And they are people that don't -- when they ask them, what do you don't like about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Only three percent say they care about abortion, only three percent say they care about the criminal charges. Seven percent say threat to democracy. And 43 percent say character and temperament are what is stopping them from committing to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, he needs to go after those votes. He needs to convince those people. These are people who like his policies, but they don't like him. But they also don't like Harris. And so they are kind of stuck. And so he needs to convince those people, give them -- create a permission structure that allows them to vote in their self-interest, because they all know their lives were better under Trump than they are right now. But they're struggling with the decision to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If we look, Meghan, at the RCP average of battleground states, there's a lot of red on the board. Now it's slim, one point, two points, but the former president is up. Sometimes it's less than one point. When Democrats look at that, do they say ground game? What do they say when they look at this race?\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I think they are looking at the ground game. I think that's one thing that she does have an advantage over Trump. She does have an incredible structure in place already. She has the money to be out there knocking on doors every day and reaching these voters. I also think that's part of the reason she is coming on FOX. She is going to reach voters where they are, and she's reaching a different set of voters than some of her podcast and other interviews were reaching. And it's smart of them to go after every vote.\par \par \uc2 This is an election where this is very slim on the margin, and every vote is going to matter. And she needs to convince voters, just like permission structure for Trump. She needs to create a permission structure for Republicans to also vote for her.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She has some surrogates out. Obviously, she had former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in Philadelphia, and that caused a little stir when he was talking down to young black men, saying you can't vote for anybody else. You got to vote for her. Now you have another former president today causing his own stir. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: She is the only candidate who has actually endorsed a bill that would hold down immigration in any given year a certain point. Trump killed the bill. You got a case in Georgia, not very long ago, didn't you? They made an ad about it, about a young woman who had been killed by an immigrant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Yes, well, if they had all been properly vetted, that probably wouldn't have happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Laken Riley. If they had all been properly vetted it might not have happened.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Yes. It wouldn't have happened. Of course, you can't properly vet millions and millions of people who come into the country, who flood across the border. And so \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  gets some credit for mentioning that case, which is largely ignored, conspicuously by a lot of the people in his party. But it really causes a problem for the argument that Harris is trying to make. He mentioned the bill, which was an election year gambit, to give them a talking point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which, by the way, six Democrats voted against.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Correct. But even if you set that completely aside, that was cooked up in 2024. The man accused of killing Laken Riley entered the country illegally in 2022. A few days later, literally, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  went on national television and said the border is secure. She denied a crisis was even happening and said the border was secure. Of course, it was not. That man went on to commit additional crimes in other sanctuary jurisdictions before allegedly killing Laken Riley. So even with the caveat from \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  with the timing of the bill, it doesn't actually excuse what happened there. And that might be a good question for someone to ask her if they get an opportunity at some point.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you want to write this stuff down? Just write it down on this napkin right here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, just kidding. You were saying earlier today that you thought it was surprising that "The View" and Stephen Colbert answers about how you're different than Biden or how it would be different, that you're surprised that Trump campaign hasn't done more with that.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes. NBC's poll, which is not a good one for her overall, shows her slightly leading on the change question. Here she is in a 15-seconds sound bite saying she would not have done a single thing differently than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over the last four years. That is the closing argument for the Trump campaign out of her own mouth. That was six days ago. We haven't seen that ad in swing states yet. And I wonder, is it happening? What are they waiting for?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marc, the former president says some things on the trail that obviously raised some eyebrows on a lot of different circles and a lot of different topics. But it seems like a lot of the media is focusing on the Harris campaign and the back and forth, the Harris-Biden split. There's a big article in "Axios" about tensions between Harris and Biden. Is it that they're numb to some of the things that the former president says that are sensational, or what do you think it is?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, I think, yes, I think there is the Trump outrage -- there is a limit to Trump outrage even in the media. But I mean the reality is the fact that they ever focusing on those things is good for Trump. What he ought to be doing is focusing on winning those 18 percent swing voters who are going to decide the election, because his base is going to walk over hot coals to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 These voters, and the idea that she is winning the change question. She is the sitting vice president. A sitting vice president, except for George H.W. Bush, hasn't won in 188 years in this country. And George H.W. Bush won because people wanted another Reagan term. Nobody wants another Biden term or else he would be the nominee right now. So the idea -- and she is running on new way forward he? He ought to be hammering her on that and reaching out to those swing voters and trying to convince them to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is J.D. Vance on ABC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The incidents were limited to a handful of apartment conflicts -- apartment complexes, and the mayor said our dedicated police officers have acted on those concerns, a handful of problems.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Only, Martha -- do you hear yourself? Only a handful of apartment complexes in America were taken over by Venezuelan gangs? And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the problem and not \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' open border? Americans are so fed up with what's going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He is definitely going to places and being confronted by guess questions and doing, arguably, a pretty good job pushing back.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Yes, absolutely. And I think on this, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is staying on message for the first time in talking about immigration regardless of how insane his things are and how untrue they are, he is staying on message, and I think that is what appeals to his base. And I think the fear that he is instilling in people is a motivating factor for some of these independent voters who are still deciding who to vote for. So where he is, we all find it offensive, or people aren't covering it as much. It is resonating with his base and it could be resonating with these independent voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, this is live in Oaks, Pennsylvania. That is South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  doing this town hall with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But to J.D. Vance's point, the question was only a few apartment complexes have been taken over by Venezuelan gangs.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Let's just assume that Martha Raddatz is 100 percent correct about that and Trump has overblown the problem. Even within that framework, we are talking about violent overseas illegal immigrant gangs taking over apartment complexes in the United States of America, and to fact-check that like it's a Trump rhetoric problem rather than a facts on the ground problem I think is an issue. And J.D. Vance saying do you hear yourself was the perfect response.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meghan, do you hear positives after this past week? What are the positives that Democrats are looking at?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: I think that they are looking towards the inflation numbers are coming down. The unemployment numbers are still low. The economy is moving in the right section direction. I think her being out there more is creating a little bit of agita for people and they're getting a little anxious that she isn't necessarily addressing the people who are in the base. Like now she put out a policy for black men. So I think that people are still looking to that. But I do think that the momentum is still at her back, and I think that the money she is raising and the $1 billion that she announced is going to help her in November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We shall see. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day, we love this story. The wait is finally over. Two three-year-old giant pandas, Bao Li and Qing Bao, have left China on a cargo jet dubbed the \uc2 Panda Express\uc2 . They are headed for the Smithsonian National Zoo here in Washington. The zoo announcing that in May, that two panda bears would arrive by the end of 2024. This comes a year after the national zoo last pandas left to return to China. Bao Li is actually the son for Bao Bao for you following at home with a whiteboard, born at the D.C. zoo in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the latest on a mysterious fleet of drones that entered restricted airspace more than two weeks ago. We'll tell you. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. We've got you covered.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241015ekae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Mideast Tensions; Defense Department Supporting Hurricane Response Efforts; Dockworkers Strike Suspended; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Campaigns in Michigan; Interview With Canton, North Carolina, Mayor Zeb Smathers; Trump Campaigns With Georgia Governor; Strong September Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Tobin, Jennifer Griffin, Bill Melugin, Madison Scarpino,  Lucas Tomlinson, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, following a lot of developments at 4:00 p.m. this hour.\par \par \uc2 Of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has just wrapped up an event in Evans, Georgia, right now with Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , obviously, that state hard-hit by the fallout from Hurricane Helene and the recovery now that is expected to cost upwards of $35 billion and has already left more than 220 Americans dead.\par \par \uc2 We're also following up, if you can look at the corner of your screen, on the Dow today, a big run-up because jobs were aplenty in the latest period. We're going to be exploring how that big jobs issue not only helped people at the corner of Wall and Broad, but had a lot of people thinking that the economy has achieved a soft landing, and this is leading to maybe big campaign issue, perhaps bigger than we know.\par \par \uc2 It could cut a couple of different ways. Some people who are arguing that they don't feel it, don't see it, so it doesn't matter if we keep showing it. But this jobs report that showed 254,000 Americans found jobs was close to double what we thought would appeal -- would appear today.\par \par \uc2 And that is a big reason why you're getting all of this attention to the economy on this day with the backdrop of all of these worries.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Lucas Tomlinson in Evans, Georgia, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 So, what can you tell us, my friend?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON PRODUCER: Well, Neil, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just finished speaking here outside of Augusta, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 He was joined with Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , the first time these two have been together in nearly four years. And former President Trump said the governor is doing a great job. He said that this, of course, is the worst storm that this country has seen since Katrina.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  said 33 people have been killed. Locals on the ground have said this is an apocalypse situation, a lot of damage. The president thanked the governor for his hard work. He also thanked \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  for providing all the Starlink terminals.\par \par \uc2 And the president had a story saying that when he was on the phone with Elon, and he was already getting congratulations from people about Starlink and said he works pretty fast. Of course, the president, the former president is off to North Carolina right after this.\par \par \uc2 But the significance here is former President Trump coming to Georgia for the second time, of course, Georgia a key state if the former president is going to be returning to the White House, his second trip here to Georgia in just the past few days.\par \par \uc2 I had a chance to ask the former president a few questions. I asked him if the White House was slow to respond, Neil. The president said yes. He also took FEMA to task for not providing more money for people. I also asked him if he's worried about voters being disenfranchised this election, especially those that have been in the hardest-hit areas. Will they be able to get their votes in there?\par \par \uc2 The former president said he's not worried about voters right now. He came here to address the people, to tell the Georgia governor he's doing a great job. He also met members of the Georgia National Guard and first responders before he spoke.\par \par \uc2 And that is the situation here outside Augusta. It's also notable that Augusta National Chairman \uc2 Fred Ridley\uc2  donated $5 million to the community here, of course, Augusta the national home to the Masters, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for all of that, Lucas Tomlinson.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Madison square. She is in Swannanoa, North Carolina. I hope I'm pronouncing it correctly.\par \par \uc2 And, Madison, of course, that was the hardest and remains the hardest-hit state of all that were hit, the half-dozen that were hit, and still a lot of folks without power and a lot of people we still not heard from, period, right?\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 There -- North Carolina, as you said, has the most of the 225 deaths that are confirmed, but, unfortunately, officials do expect that number to go way up because of all of those people still missing. But here in Swannanoa, I mean, it's pretty much destruction every corner that you look.\par \par \uc2 We're on the side of someone's house right now. You can see cars just completely destroyed. If you look to the side over here, you can see a deck flipped up on its side,just tons of personal items here too. And when it comes to the federal assistance, which has been talked about a lot, Neil, when you speak to people in Swannanoa, some will say that they haven't seen or heard from FEMA.\par \par \uc2 But then other people will tell us that they already got that knock on their door from the feds and they applied for that assistance. But right now, people aren't relying on it. We're hearing so many incredible stories of neighbors helping each other out. And that includes a man named John, who went out with another man named Pat on kayaks into the flooding to try and rescue people. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ARNDT, SWANNANOA, NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT: It was just -- it was chaos. It was chaos. Buildings were floating by, crashing into the backside of these houses. Everybody was down there helping us get the people out of the water, and they were sheltering under Pat's roof and giving them blankets.\par \par \uc2 I don't think anyone around here has any spare blankets or jackets anymore, because they're all out in the water and on rooftops and stuff. But it was intense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: And I wanted to show you this image, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We're still on the side of someone's house. And in the mud, you can see so many personal items, like I said, like this travel notebook. People -- we read through it, and it was just a girl talking about her travels. She went to Spain. She went to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 So it's items like those that people may never get back. But, again, that is just not people's top priority right now. The number one concern is still clean water. And, of course, in mountain communities like this, a lot of people have wells, but they're being advised not to use any well water either right now because of the contamination -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Madison, thank you very much. Great reporting on this, Madison.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Zeb Smathers, right now, the Canton, North Carolina mayor. He's been very, very busy.\par \par \uc2 Mayor, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us how things look by you?\par \par \uc2 ZEB SMATHERS, MAYOR OF CANTON, NORTH CAROLINA: Well, I mean, we're making progress.\par \par \uc2 Here in Canton in our city limits, we here restored the majority of power and water to our citizens. But this is the West North Carolina community. I mean, this has been apocalyptic for us in our town, but this has spread across the region.\par \par \uc2 And every place, no matter your hometown, your cove, your community, you have been affected by this, not just now, but going forward. We have to be able to advocate no matter where you call home, that you get the resources, you get the respect you deserve, because when you call these mountains home, you understand you're a much larger family.\par \par \uc2 And I have heard from so many people across the U.S. that have so many great memories here in West North Carolina. But the first thing, we have to be here for our community. We're still doing search-and-rescue across the mountains and slowly putting one foot in front of the other.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, we're told that a good number of people are still without power. I have heard roughly a million. Whether that's all in your states, I don't know. But that's a lot of people who can't be reached and, furthermore, a lot of people who can't reach help. So how do you handle it?\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: Well, I mean, that was the biggest issue.\par \par \uc2 In the beginning of the storm, we lost cellular power.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: We had an absolute network collapse across. That has gotten better. I commend all the workers out there putting our telecommunications together.\par \par \uc2 Again, Starlink played an important role in doing this. But, again, this is a question that I will ask once we catch our breath why this happened. Again, this was across the entire mountain region. And we know there's going to be another natural disaster in our country. And I pray, I pray that no town like Canton is going to have to go through or a region the inability not just to communicate with emergency communications, but loved ones being able to place phone calls days later and still not know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: You're afraid of what you don't know. And every citizen, no matter who you are or where you live, depends on our cell phones and communication to be able to say, hey, mom, are you OK?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. No, you're quite right, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 And it was a week ago that this storm hit land in Florida as a Category 4 storm. Very few would have envisioned that it could do the damage it's done in your state, and it stands out in your state. So maybe we have to rethink how we judge the severity of some of these storms and hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 We usually rate them by what their category is, Category 4 and all. But it turns out that this storm, much like we saw in New Jersey with big storms that just wallop the state, they weren't even registered as hurricanes. They were just worried as giant masses of winds and flooding that cause and wreak the havoc it has in your state.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that and how authorities in Washington have to reassess how they, FEMA and others, deal with that?\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: Well, I mean, no place does better than Canton.\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, we experienced a tropical storm threat, and we were told this was a once-in-a-lifetime storm. And so when you're told that and you make decisions on that, that's a different mind-set.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: Here we are three years later in an absolutely worse storm than we had just three years ago. I'm going to the same businesses.\par \par \uc2 My sister lost her home three years ago. She's lost it again. I mean, I had an expectation three years ago, hey, this is once-in-a-lifetime. Let's go forward. We're going to get you back open.\par \par \uc2 And now I'm going to the same businesses and these small businesses that make up such an important part of West North Carolina and saying, look, if you want to open, we will support you 100 percent. And I hope you do. But if you have had enough and just three years ago, I understand that too.\par \par \uc2 We have to realize that the game has changed, that a mayor in a town that's roughly 3,000 feet above sea level has to monitor tropical forecasts as if Canton was 30 speaks volumes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It really does, yes.\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: So, again, leadership requires making decisions in the world we live in, not the world we wish. And it seems that the mountains of North Carolina are now directly in Hurricane Alley.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. No, you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 And it did remind me of the Superstorm Sandy in New Jersey, sir, because that was not even a hurricane. And -- but, of course, between just the heavy rain and storm surges that were experienced and the fact that it hung around so long, it did the damage it did.\par \par \uc2 And when I look at where you are in the map in the southwest corner of North Carolina, you're a long way from the shore. And so people have to, to your point, reassess what is risky and damaged, because the whole area around you needs a whole lot of cleaning up, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 SMATHERS: No, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and I know that we're up to the task. And right now, I mean, again, we're seeing the best of North Carolina, Democrats, Republicans, small towns, large cities coming together. And I think in the world that we live in now, people need to see that there's so much goodness, so much prayer, and the grit and grace, not just in Canton, but the entire West North Carolina is met with love across the country.\par \par \uc2 And, again, at the same time, we're combating misinformation out there. I mean, there are so many rumors and innuendo, that we're trying to concentrate people on the volunteers, where that it can get help, help from the feds, the state. We just got to focus on it and not get distracted, because this is a -- this is not a sprint. This is a marathon.\par \par \uc2 And we got to make sure people know about West North Carolina and what we have experienced, not just weeks, months, but years to come. These people deserve it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. You're right about all of that.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Zeb Smathers, thank you very much, sir. My best for you and your constituents and everyone in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in the meantime, was in Evans, Georgia, today also looking at the damage. There's some substantial damage, at that. He was with Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , and he did manage to take some questions from reporters while he was there. Take a peek.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's great. No, no, no, it's great. No, we work together. We have always worked together very well, very really well.\par \par \uc2 It's top. Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, of course, they are. It's been a terrible response from the White House. They're missing a billion dollars that was used for another purpose and nobody's seen anything like that.\par \par \uc2 No, from that standpoint, it's been terrible.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Say it.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm not thinking about voters right now. I'm thinking about lives. And to be honest, it's much bigger than anything else, but we're thinking about lives, a lot of lives lost, a lot of people missing.\par \par \uc2 And that's what I'd be focused on right now.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, it's not. It's -- inflation has devastated our economy. It's one of the big problems we have.\par \par \uc2 And on jobs, we have -- the illegals have taken more jobs than anybody else. You have illegals coming in and they're taking the jobs. And when you look at the numbers from that standpoint, it's a shame. We have to get the jobs for the people that have lived here for a long time and they're great, great citizens of our country.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at the job numbers, they just came out and then you look at who's getting the jobs and it's been very unfortunate from that standpoint. You have illegal. You have the migrants. You're reading about it.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of the money that was supposed to go to Georgia and supposed to go to North Carolina and all of the others is going and has gone already, it's been gone for people that came into the country illegally, and nobody's ever seen anything like that. That's a shame.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't know anything about what he said. I only can hope that it's going to be free and fair. And I think in this state it will be. And I hope in every state it will be. And I think we're going to do very well.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, we're focused on this. We're not focused on the election.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I won it easily in 2016. We actually were very close last time. And right now, the recent poll came up we're up seven. So I think we're pretty far up, and we seem to be going up and she seems to be going down.\par \par \uc2 So I hope we're going to keep it that way.\par \par \uc2 Ladies and gentlemen, thank you very much,. I appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know what polling he is referring to where he says he is up seven points.\par \par \uc2 And one other reference I want to refer to, when he was talking about more illegals are accounting for these jobs than anyone else, I don't know where he is getting that. In the latest employment report out today, 254,000 jobs were gained, says nothing about annotations for illegals getting those jobs, nor would it.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is, both private payrolls and across virtually all sectors say barely any movement at all in manufacturing. The job gains were double what were thought. Furthermore, the numbers for August and July were revised upward 55,000 and 17,000 respectively.\par \par \uc2 Now, people can quibble with that data, as they often do, and this big revision that the government had in showing that 818,000 fewer jobs over the last year than were earlier reported. The Labor Department came out with that, as it does every year, in revised figures when they take everything into account, and, even with that, millions of more jobs gained.\par \par \uc2 Now, I only stress that because the jobs are what the jobs are. Some are revised. And, indeed, on a monthly basis, they can be updated. But these grew, and there was no annotation of any of these disproportionately going to illegals. That would be a tricky feat to pull off. Just want to say that.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I always like to say on this show we're about balls and strikes and showing the numbers the way they are.\par \par \uc2 The non-farm payrolls out today showed 254,000 Americans found jobs last month, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just saying that many of them, maybe more of them, are going to illegals than anyone else.\par \par \uc2 Leaving that aside, it is not unusual for the opposition party, in this case, to seize on what was a strong report like this and say it's either made up or bad or there are problems with it. That is not unusual. It is not unusual as well to hear the party in power, and that's why you saw \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  step up to the podium at the White House today and actually take questions, do a little bit of bragging about this jobs report in the context of settling a three-day long port worker strike.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are a lot of issues with that. It has yet to be resolved, but the trend, the administration says, is its friend. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Republicans will say, as the opposition party would, that it's not really as good as it seems.\par \par \uc2 My job is simply to look at the data and tell you that that was a damn good employment report, bottom line, end of story, no debate.\par \par \uc2 But, but again, it could be seen in context of, could it be better?\par \par \uc2 Hans Nichols, Axios reporter, a very good one, at that, what did you think, first of all, of how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  addressed that and how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  addressed that, Hans?\par \par \uc2 HANS NICHOLS, AXIOS: Well, for Biden, it was a victory lap, which was a pretty easy one to do, because as you mentioned, I won't swear, but I think we can safely say that this is a very good jobs report, and Biden did a victory lap.\par \par \uc2 Every time they get a good report, good economic numbers inside the White House, they go straight to the cameras. They don't go straight because you have to wait an hour after there's market-moving news before you can actually speak to the cameras or make any public pronouncements.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: It won't surprise you, Neil, that sometimes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would tweet inside of that hour window, but no one was coming after him for that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: And this has really been the challenge of the Biden campaign, the administration, the White House, the Harris campaign is convincing the voters and convincing consumers that the economy is much better than they think it is.\par \par \uc2 And that dynamic might not change with another good number. Trump downplayed it. And I heard you talking about this earlier, but I'm unaware of anything in the \uc2 BLS\uc2  data that gives you anything on whether or not you're a native-born American or an immigrant or a recent arrival.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: There's just nothing in the data. Now, there's a big debate on how many immigrants are getting jobs here, but that's not in this data. You have got to wait for census data for that. You have got to wait for other household surveys.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is studying this. It's a big open question. I mean, Trump is within his rights to sort of bring it up. But you and I and no one in the White House can actually tell us how many people that are working in this economy are here without previous or official work authorization, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And that's the only point I'm trying to say here, first of all, to say that whatever job gains we have are to illegals getting the jobs, that is just preposterous at its first sense.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're right. I mean, these numbers vary widely. And, of course, it was the Labor Department itself that sort of reset things a couple of months ago, as it's obligated to do every year when they come out with new numbers that did show 818,000 fewer people found work. Millions more still did find work, but 818,000 fewer.\par \par \uc2 But what I worry about in this back-and-forth over this, and both sides can play it to a degree, and I understand that, is, you do have to kind of stick to some reasonable facts here. This isn't some whistle-blower who released these updated figures.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This was an obligation the part of the Labor Department to refresh them. So there was no sinister plot here. It was just what it is.\par \par \uc2 Now, on these latest numbers, even the Labor Department will report the last couple of months were stronger than we thought. You could read all you want into that. But I guess I'm stepping back from this, because you raised a very good point at the beginning. If you don't feel it or feel like we're doing that well, you're not buying it.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that is the state of mind of many voters who do not buy this recovery, do not buy the soft landing, do not buy the Wall Street reaction, or celebrate Wall Street doing well.\par \par \uc2 That's the challenge for the White House, right?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, look, what the White House will say is, listen to what consumers are doing, not what consumers are saying. And among the reports that I find interesting is when you have retail sales coming out, and retail sales have stayed healthy.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's a two-speed economy a little bit here, Neil, and that is that wealthy Americans are still spending a lot and you're seeing a little bit of retrenchment, if not a lot, in some sectors among Americans at the bottom end of the income spectrum.\par \par \uc2 But if you just look at what -- and this is the White House's point. If you look at what consumers are doing, they're investing in new businesses, they're creating small businesses, they are indicating they're more confident about the economy than they're telling pollsters. Now, that said, and the easiest answer here is that people really don't like inflation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: And that's why voters are sour on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's economy. And as J.D. Vance tried to do the other night, make voters or remind voters, it's also \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' economy as well.\par \par \uc2 But the numbers are trending -- and, like, I -- we are entirely agreement. If you have \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers, you have to trust \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers. I'm not saying there are not ever any quirks in the data.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: But these are trusted numbers that they put a great deal of effort into getting right. And they're the best snapshot at the time of what we have going on in the economy.\par \par \uc2 And those numbers indicate this is a healthier job market than we thought even two weeks ago. It's not to say there isn't any softness in the labor market, but right now it looks pretty healthy, and that's something the White House clearly wants.\par \par \uc2 And just do the reverse. Imagine this was a miserable job report. It would be a really easy call to make politically that this was going to be bad for the Harris campaign. But it's not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 Hans, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Hans Nichols.\par \par \uc2 We are going to explore how the Harris campaign is reacting to all of this as well, because give credit to President Biden. He took on reporters to talk about this head on. He did that effectively today. Why isn't she?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that port strike is settled, but not completely so. It looks like the striking longshoremen are going to get a big pay raise, but as for that modernization thing, not resolved yet -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It got to be Trump. It's for the whole city. They all voting Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All my friends that are Trumpers only think about themselves. And we have to think about the whole world. And I really like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it's time for history to be made. I just think that's going to be a woman's president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's split, people on both sides. It's going to be a close one. The economy and the border was much stronger when Trump was president. He is crazy, though. But I prefer him over Kamala.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there you have it, a smattering of voters in the state of Michigan how they feel going into a race that's very, very tight there. Some call this a must-win state for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin has more from Flint -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is trying to court working-class voters and union members while she is here in Michigan. She's going to be giving a speech here in Flint within the next couple of hours. We're told her plane just touched down here in Flint. But as we're waiting for her, take a look at a few hours ago.\par \par \uc2 This was when she was in Detroit giving another rally. While she was on the ground in Detroit, she talked about a few different topics. She said she's glad the dockworkers strike is over. She said that the administration got a great jobs report this morning, but she feels the cost of living and groceries are still too high.\par \par \uc2 Then she went after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , saying he is not union-friendly. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (D) AND U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is a man who has been a union buster his entire career, who has called union leaders -- quote -- "dues-sucking people."\par \par \uc2 And as president, he did not lift a finger to save the pensions of millions of American workers. We did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Now, yesterday, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had an unlikely ally, you could say, who was on stage with her at a campaign event in Wisconsin. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 Former Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was campaigning with Harris, the two of them apparently finding some common ground in their opposition to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I tell you I have never voted for a Democrat. But, this year, I am proudly casting my vote for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  really is a leader who puts country above party and above self, a true patriot. And it is my profound honor, my profound honor to have your support.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is out on the campaign trail today with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  lost for Congress. She was terrible. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is a stupid war hawk. All she wants to do is shoot missiles at people. I think they hurt each other. I think they're so bad, both of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Now, here in Michigan, the Harris campaign is also trying to court Muslim America voters. Michigan is home to a very large pro- Palestinian population, as well as a large Lebanese population.\par \par \uc2 It's also home to the Uncommitted Movement, which has pledged they're not going to vote for any Democrats until the war in Gaza is over. And, Neil, that could prove to be damaging in a race as tight as it is right now. They need every single vote. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's a tight one. Thank you very much, Bill Melugin, on all of that.\par \par \uc2 I told you about the port workers strike. They were all on strike three days running, better than 45,000 along the East Coast and Gulf Coast. Well, it is settled for the time being. They got a big pay raise. We will tell you all about that.\par \par \uc2 But when it came to the modernization of ports and bringing them up to A.I. standards and all that, that's not quite done, not even close -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, Santa's relieved. Maybe you are too. It looked like the possibility of delayed packages this Christmas because of the port worker strike that was expected to go into weeks or months.\par \par \uc2 It's resolved, kind of, after three days. Looks like they're going to get a 62 percent pay raise over the next six -- I think six years. So the fact is, it's going to be about 10 percent a year when you average it all out. Nothing about the modernization of plants and all the other things.\par \par \uc2 But Douglas Holtz-Eakin with us right now, the former CBO director.\par \par \uc2 Doug, a lot of people are relieved to see that part of it. We could crunch the numbers on whether they are going to be inflationary and all of that. But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Well, I think for them to settle the wage part of this and go back to work for a couple of months is a great boon for the economy.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of estimates about the economic loss, half-a-billion-to-$5 billion-a-day range. Certainly, the delays would have started to mount. A week of strike probably adds up to six weeks of delays in shipping. And so there was some real pain in the future,maybe some increases in prices. I'd say perishable foods would have been a real issue. And food price inflation has been one of the Achilles' heels recently.\par \par \uc2 So taking all that off the table is good news for the economy, no question about it, and makes the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's job a lot, lot easier.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, a lot of people optimistic that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  doesn't have to worry about that right now. We will see.\par \par \uc2 But we know that they're going to sort of regroup on this.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it could be just right before January's inauguration of the new president. So how do you think that's going to go?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, January should be exciting.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: We're going to have the debt ceiling come back January 1, 2025. I will see you on January 2. We will see how they're doing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So we're going to immediately have to deal with a debt ceiling debate.\par \par \uc2 And the issue of automation is a real problem. The U.S. is way behind the world in the efficiency of its ports. We don't have a single port that's in the top 50 worldwide. And so it is essential that the operators and owners be able to modernize these ports and have them move cargo more efficiently.\par \par \uc2 That, by the way, would lower prices, on average, as much as anything. And that would be good for consumers. But the union is dead set against that. If the numbers I read today are right, there are 45,000 people in the union, but only 25,000 jobs. So they're not even all working now, even though they're getting paid.\par \par \uc2 And they want to continue it that way or even make it more that way. So that's not a recipe for efficiency or for lower costs and prices in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm told, Doug, that the turning event was when the West Coast port workers who had settled their contract issues a year ago...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... said that they would not unload ships that might have been diverted to the West Coast. And that kind of cinched it, that that -- that they were not going to play that middleman role. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: I think there's something to that.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, the retailers knowing that the holiday season was at risk ordered earlier and they ordered through the West Coast, had it transshipped across the country. And the West Coast longshoremen said, look, we're going to slow down and we're going to do this in sympathy of our East Coast and Gulf Coast fellow longshoremen.\par \par \uc2 That and White House pressure -- the White House put a lot of pressure on the owners to settle this. And so it's a big win for the White House. They take the economic disruption off the table right before the election, and they get a big win for the unions, where they have been self-professed the most pro-union White House in American history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Always great catching up with you. Thank you very much, Douglas Holtz- Eakin, of course, the former \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  director. He was always warning about debt under Republican and Democratic presidents. It keeps piling up and up and up and up.\par \par \uc2 All right in the meantime here updating you on rescue efforts still going on in these six states and the helicopter rescues and what have you. It looks like a war zone, probably because, increasingly, it seems that way, and now the Pentagon to the rescue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Western North Carolina people are tough. They're going to bounce back from this. It may take us -- it's going to take multiple years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You hear people say it just happens so fast, and you don't believe it. But it just happened so fast. Things just started -- the water just started rising from beneath the house, it seemed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it continues in all points Western North Carolina, where we're increasingly seeing helicopters. A lot of times, it looks like a war zone there. We were looking at a lot of this video earlier today, and a lot of them courtesy the Pentagon, where a lot of soldiers are stepping in to help in this effort.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin has more on that side of the story.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, I spoke to representatives of the U.S. army this morning about the status of the 1,000 active-duty troops that Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  authorized earlier this week to help the National Guard and FEMA in North Carolina, members of the 18th Airborne Corps at Fort liberty, Formerly Fort Bragg.\par \par \uc2 I'm told the 20th Engineering Brigade left Fort Liberty yesterday and will be embedded with FEMA and the state National Guard in Western North Carolina. The 20th Engineering Brigade is bringing a tactical water purification system that I'm told could bring clean water to the affected areas in 24 to 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 The 18th Airborne Corps, which is our top ready unit, is based there right in North Carolina. It has sent six Chinook helicopters, which are operating out of the Asheville Airport, and are delivering food and water. Thirty Black Hawk helicopters also left fort liberty to help reach hard to reach mountain areas. Their expertise in logistics is crucial for reaching mountain communities that are still isolated.\par \par \uc2 The National Guard has also deployed 6,700 troops from several states to help with recovery. What they need right now is stronger command-and- control on the ground. That is missing. Many of the isolated areas most cut off, where people still have no access to food and clean water, look from the air like terrain you would expect to see in Afghanistan, which is very familiar to the 82nd Airborne.\par \par \uc2 There is a push for the North Carolina governor to follow the model used during Hurricane Katrina and request that an active-duty general, instead of the state National Guard, be put in charge of the command-and-control and logistics on the ground to help the thousands of state National Guard and already there and FEMA.\par \par \uc2 That is what happened during Hurricane Katrina, when Lieutenant General Russel Honore was put in charge of the rescue and recovery efforts, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Jennifer Griffin, on that.\par \par \uc2 We will keep you posted, again, on this ongoing rescue effort. It seems to be focused, especially with the military presence, for the most part, in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, updating you on waiting for Israel's response to the Iran missile attack. We do note today that Iran's foreign minister was in Lebanon, had some interesting things to say -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We are still waiting for Israel's response to that Iranian missile attack. No word yet on exactly what might be calculating the equation, what the Israelis do.\par \par \uc2 But Mike Tobin and Haifa Israel has some thoughts on all of that. He's been reporting on all of this.\par \par \uc2 Mike, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, the world is waiting with you to see how Israel responds to this unprecedented missile attack from Iran.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden has already discouraged Israel publicly from striking at the nuclear facilities. And, today, he discouraged striking at the oil infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If I were in their shoes, I'd be thinking about other alternatives than striking oil fields.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: As reliable as the air defenses like Iron Dome have proven to be, they're not 100 percent. Today, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  confirmed two soldiers were killed when a drone was fired from Iraq. It hit a base in the Golan Heights. Two dozen were wounded.\par \par \uc2 Israel says 250 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters have been killed by Israeli forces operating in the south of Lebanon. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  claims the south is saturated with weapons and tunnels that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  intended to use to attack in the north. A spokesman says ground operations will continue until Israelis living in the north of the country can safely return to their homes.\par \par \uc2 The fight is on multiple fronts. Last night, the Israeli air force fired into the West Bank town of Tulkarm. The target was a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader in Tulkarm and a cell apparently getting ready to strike 18 people were killed. This is the first time Israeli fighter jets have fired into the West Bank since the second Palestinian intifada -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that, Mike Tobin.\par \par \uc2 Want to get what could be at stake here with the Lieutenant General David Deptula, \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 General, we already had a clear idea, at least from President Biden, that he wouldn't be keen on the Israelis targeting Iranian nuclear facilities. He seemed to extend that or clarify today to going necessarily after oil facilities. We do know that the Iranians have been moving some tankers around and are preparing for just that type of an attack. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. DAVID DEPTULA (RET.), \uc2 U.S. AIR FORCE\uc2 : Well, Neil, for deterrence to work and for the current administration's credibility, the U.S. needs to show what happens when it says don't and Iran ignores that admonition.\par \par \uc2 Now, just as you said the president's urged Israel not to strike its nuclear weapons development efforts and then he came out today and said he doesn't want to strike or doesn't want Israel to strike their oil infrastructure. He ought to be passing these perspectives in private, as taking both these positions make the U.S. look weak and deterred by Iran's leadership.\par \par \uc2 The administration needs to support Israel in responding to Iran's unprecedented aggression. And this attack cannot go unanswered. Frankly, now's the time to take the opportunity to cut the head off of the Middle Eastern snake and the U.S. should support and assist Israel in this endeavor, because, quite frankly, that would also garner the support of the Iranian people in doing so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, General, then the question becomes -- let's say you do go after oil either refineries or tankers. The immediate response likely would be a sharp hike in oil and gas-related prices.\par \par \uc2 Already, this week, oil is up close to 10 percent largely on these concerns that this will fester. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: Well, once again, look, the economic impact has to be taken into consideration, and I'm sure that's part of what the administration is considering, as well as we have heard the issues of concerns with escalation.\par \par \uc2 But, frankly, I think we should fear appeasement of the Iranian mullahs more than escalation. Iran's already escalated with its massive ballistic missile attack. And the other concern the administration has to take into account is their current approach may actually encourage autocrats like President Xi and others to take advantage of the administration's hesitancy to strike decisively.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I have been reading The Jerusalem Post a lot of lately, General, and there's an expression that some have there, editorially, that this is the opportunity for Israel to finally address these nuclear facilities, these -- they're hard to get to, as you have reminded me many times, sir. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But that is the pressure on \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  as well. And I'm sure he probably might concur with a lot of that. What is the risk in doing that?\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: Well, my perspective is, there's actually more risk in not doing that than the risk in potential escalation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: I mean, what else is Iran going to do? This is why I say this is an opportunity for Israel, and the United States should also completely support them in this effort, because it is an opportunity to prevent Iran or at least put them back in the context of their development of nuclear weapons, which would be a cataclysmic outcome for Israel, if in fact they're allowed to development.\par \par \uc2 And that kind of an attack would set back Iran's nuclear program. And it makes a lot of logical sense to do so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General, what did you make of Iran's foreign minister? He popped up in Lebanon today, and that did not stop Israel from continuing to pound \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  positions in Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 So it just seemed like weird timing. If that was the hope to get Israel to cease and desist, it didn't. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: Well, you know, just like you said, it didn't deter Israel from acting.\par \par \uc2 And they're very determined to see through their military operations. And, quite frankly, I think they're at the leading edge of the opportunity to put Iran and its proxies in its place. And that's why I say these are opportunities that present themselves for Israel, because, the sooner the world is free of the scourge of the Iranian mullahs and its proxies, the sooner the people subjugated by them can be free and the world can become a better place.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, no condemnation of Israel by Saudi Arabia as yet. What did you make of that? Super quick.\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: Well, it's sort of like what we talked about a couple days ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: The Arab states view Iran just as significant a threat as Israel does.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Got it.\par \par \uc2 DEPTULA: So, it's...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well said, General. I apologize for that.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020241004eka4000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 FEMA Is Running Out Of Money; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Ignore Americans In Need; Sources: Compromise Reached In Port Strike; Democrats in panic mode over Michigan; Another debate win for the GOP; GOP Senate candidate crushes incumbent in debate; Thousands of noncitizens removed from voter rolls; Kamala crumbles in new interview; Harris doesn't understand your financial struggles\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Mary Katharine Ham \par \uc2 7,467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: -- controversy after \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  released photos allegedly showing Lawler wearing blackface in college as part of a Michael Jackson Halloween costume. Lawler did not dispute the photo's authenticity, but said in a statement to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the costume was intended to truly be the sincerest form of flattery and a genuine homage to my musical hero. Now, he's fighting to win reelection in one of 19 Republican held congressional districts that President Biden won in 2020. We'll bring that to you.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on Special Report, Chief Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst joins us to discuss his new book, "Black Saturday", also about the situation on the ground as it expands in Lebanon. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. A lot of breaking news tonight. The Ingraham Angle starts now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. A lot to get to. Jam packed show Breaking just moments ago, the port strike temporarily suspended until after the election. How convenient? More on that in moments. But first, the stranding of America, that's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 All right, did Joe and Kamala care more about the millions of illegals coming across our border than the people struggling in the storm ravaged southeast. Over the past four years, Democrats led by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Kamala racked up over $7.9 trillion in spending, sending our national debt to over $35.6 trillion, but somehow, after all that spending, it turns out we don't have the money we need to rescue and help our fellow Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We haven't seen FEMA, we haven't seen Red Cross, we haven't seen Hearts with Hands. We've seen no agencies come through here. Most people drive through with their cameras out filming us like we're animals in a zoo, and don't stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When this is allowed to happen, how do we even qualify as a superpower anymore? How can the people running things be this heartless and this incompetent, ensuring that we have money and supplies set aside for emergencies is a basic duty of government officials. So, we expect that when we allow our fellow citizens, we help them when they're under siege by a natural disaster like this, that our top officials will be fully engaged.\par \par \uc2 But as hurricane victims are waiting for relief, Kamala was rallying supporters in Vegas. Six days after the lights went out and the flood waters rose in Georgia, she did finally do a drop by.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Federal relief and assistance that we have been providing has included FEMA providing $750 for folks who need immediate needs being met, such as food, baby formula and the like.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Imagine losing your house, your transportation, basic necessities, and women looking for diapers. Earlier today, I was watching - - most stores, by the way, in the area, are wiped out, and all you're getting right now is a six -- it was a $750 check she referenced from the federal government. The government you've been sending taxes to for your entire life. Yes, and where are they going to buy that baby food, Kamala?\par \par \uc2 Of course, the man who allowed the migrant invasion into the U.S. is trying to pass the buck by saying, well, we're out of bucks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: We are meeting the immediate needs with the money that we have. We are expecting another hurricane hitting. We do not have the funds. FEMA does not have the funds to make it through the season.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's funny, they seem to have no hesitation about setting aside massive amounts of money for their foreign priorities.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If you want to see how sick and distorted \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's priorities are, just consider FEMA. Kamala spent all her FEMA money, billions of dollars on housing f