2 , not like last time for Biden and Harris at all.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't know if that means anything, but they certainly don't have the signage up.\par \par \uc2 CAO: No, they don't. Look, Northern Virginia has a lot of immigrants. I came here as a refugee from Vietnam. We ran away during the, when the communists overtook Saigon and this country gave us everything. And so, I paid back with 25 years of service in Navy special operations. As a Navy diver I recovered \uc2 John F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  and his plane. And then as an EOD tech, I defused bombs for our SEAL teams and special forces teams, because I wanted to pay back every dime of that American dream.\par \par \uc2 And this is what all the immigrants in Northern Virginia feel as well, that they're turning this country into what we ran away from.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and Hung, you're -- we don't have time to play it, but I'm going to post this on my \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Your answer on whether you're in favor of mass deportation of illegal immigrants, you know, will you get them all out or whatever it was. It was brilliant.\par \par \uc2 It was a -- I think the moderator was like, you know, drooling on himself at the end of that. It was a great moment. About five people sent it to me. Thank you so much. Hung Cao, Senate candidate in Virginia. Bye, bye, Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 All right, Democrats just made it easier than ever to commit voter fraud. How are they doing it now? I'll tell you next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What do you make of all of this smoke and mirrors that's going on about non-citizen voting?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Look, this is one more dog whistle to the Republican MAGA base.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: They're pushing the latest version of the big lie by fusing voter fraud paranoia with anti-immigration hysteria.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: They push a false narrative about non-citizens supposedly voting illegally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Supposedly. Well, of course, in Texas alone, more than 6,500 non-citizens have been removed from the voter rolls just over the last three years. And 1,900 of them, according to Governor Abbott, had a voting history. Maybe MSNBC should check it out.\par \par \uc2 Well, this summer, Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  announced that he was removing 6,300 non-citizens from his voter rolls. In Alabama, more than 3,000 non-citizens were also purged. The DOJ is actually suing them, however, for doing this so close to the election. In other words, they want non-citizens on the voter rolls. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 Well, Democrats are working overtime to prevent Republicans from trying to ensure that your vote is not canceled out by the vote of an illegal immigrant. In California, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  just banned voter ID requirements in his state. Why would he do that? Oh, so much for being the defenders of democracy and rules and norms.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Texas Congressman Chip Roy. Congressman, this just in from \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 's America First Legal. They're saying that there are 200,000, 218,000 registered voters in Arizona who have never provided proof of citizenship. Now for context, the margin of victory in 2020 in Arizona was only 10,457 votes. So, they sued to obtain the list of voters.\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe they just didn't show their driver's licenses. That could be possible. But could be 10,000 non-citizens trying to vote, yes?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): Well, it certainly could be. And congratulations to \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  and those guys for taking on this fight. As you know, as the author of the SAVE Act, we passed it out to the House of Representatives. It died in \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's Senate. That bill would have made very clear that you need documentary proof of citizenship to register to vote in elections. It would have cleaned up voter rules -- voter rolls and required states to do that.\par \par \uc2 But let me say this, Democrats constantly say that you don't need these reforms because it's already illegal to vote in the United States if you're non-citizen. Well, if that's true, then why is it that, as you pointed out earlier, Ken Paxson, the Attorney General of Texas, and our Secretary of State are having to fight the federal government to get the information necessary to ensure there are non-citizens on the voter rolls?\par \par \uc2 Why is it that, as you said, the Department of Justice is suing the state of Alabama to prevent Alabama from ensuring that non-citizens are not on the voter rolls. This is the reality. They want non-citizens to vote. You know that and I know that.\par \par \uc2 The only logical conclusion on why a country like this, of the United States, a free and strong country, would allow murderers, 13,000, rapists, 15,000, all of these criminals to be dumped onto the streets of the United States is because our democratic colleagues, our radical progressive democratic colleagues want to have that kind of political power by allowing people onto our streets and putting non-citizens on the voter rolls.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman, are you concerned tonight that the people affected by Hurricane Helene and a lot of those red counties are not going to have proper access to voting or mail-in ballots? I mean, the postal service isn't even working, obviously, in those areas. And I haven't heard any of the democracy defenders speak on their behalf.\par \par \uc2 ROY: Well, this is actually a really important issue. As you know, something like $600 million under the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and FEMA was used to give money to illegal aliens. Meanwhile, we're not getting money to the affected citizens of Western North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wouldn't let that happen. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be there for the people of Western North Carolina. President Biden, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , they're MIA. And on voting, there are 1.3 million registered voters in that part, in those counties. A large block of them are Republicans. You know Democrats don't want them to vote.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, they're going to use it for chicanery and using mail-in ballots. And there are already articles written in The Federalist today, our friend Mollie Hemingway tweeted it out earlier, with concerns about what they're going to do.\par \par \uc2 So, we need President Trump in the White House to make sure we've got actual voting integrity. And between now and then, we need it to be too big to rig. Take everybody out and vote, so we can beat the Democrats in November.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, thank you very much. And everyone should be allowed to vote who is a legal citizen of this country, even if you're hit by a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 Kamala crumbles in a new interview about the economy. Dave Ramsey, oh, what a treat. He's here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Former President Trump says that you've had four years as vice president to do all the things you've promised in the campaign, but haven't done it. Is he right?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: The former president is really becoming quite desperate and has really been offering a lot of misstatements and misinformation. And perhaps it's because he wants to distract from the fact that he has offered no plan for the American people. My plan is about building an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh my God. There we go with the opportunity economy again, that she's been hiding from the American people for the last three and a half years. Joining me now, Dave Ramsey, personal finance expert, host of the Ramsey Show. Dave, it's always wonderful to see you.\par \par \uc2 But she talks about, like, well, he doesn't have a plan and he doesn't understand what -- but you talk to folks every day in your show and they ask for your advice. Do people think things are working now as they should? And what are their financial struggles?\par \par \uc2 DAVE RAMSEY, HOST, THE RAMSEY SHOW: No, I think they realize exactly where we stand. And like you are aware, we sat down with President Trump last week. And the question I ask him, you know, in the first part of your term, if you get in, in the first 90 days, people are concerned about $7 eggs, 7 percent interest rates, house prices outpacing their wages. They're concerned about $5 gas. I think the economy is the number one issue in the election.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and the stories, Dave, about families, parents having to dig into their 401Ks and their retirement plans. I was listening to your show, this is a couple months ago, and someone called in about that. What do they do today?\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: I mean, people are doing all kinds of different things. Some are coping better than others. Some are more willing to cut lifestyle in order to use some common sense in their budgets. But -- and most of the people on our show are not waiting on someone in the White House to fix their life. They're well aware that their life is their responsibility and what happens in their house is more important.\par \par \uc2 But the White House can set the table, the administration can set the table and let the economy run, and give people an opportunity to go prosper.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What did you -- how did you find President Trump in your interview?\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: You know, we sat down, did a long form interview. We posted it yesterday morning. We've had about 2 million people watch it in the last 24 hours. And I found him to be just real calm, really, really knowledgeable.\par \par \uc2 Dropped all the numbers and they were all right on the different issues on taxation and on energy and on the economy, because that's what I was hitting him on was that stuff. But it was just two guys sitting and having a conversation.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, did \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , did you get a chance to interview her or?\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: We reached out to both camps, and the Harris camp told us that we're under consideration.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, well. Dave, I have to say of all the shows that you've done over the years, and you won the Marconi, one of the great radio hosts, that will be the tour de force. So, I'm looking forward to your sitting down.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  can explain, you know, the personal financial struggles of Americans and how her policies of the opportunity economy will make it all get better. Dave Ramsey always says, take control of your own finances, live within your means, which is what Congress needs to do. Dave, thank you so much. Congrats on all the success.\par \par \uc2 All right. That is it for us tonight. Remember, it is America now and forever. We have a special trip planned on Saturday, and a big interview. We'll tell you about it tomorrow night. Jesse, next.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241004eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Union Seeks Higher Wages And Automation Restrictions; Strike Threatening Critical Supply Chains; Major Hurricane In Atlantic Amid Helene Rescue Efforts; Biden Tours Areas In Florida And Georgia; Trump Calls Biden Disaster Response A Disgrace; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  On Kamala Harris White House Chances Amid Trio Of Crises; U.S. Support For Embattled Ally; Israel And Iran Signal Potential For Escalation; Cheney Campaigning With Harris In Wisconsin; Israel Vows Major Response To Iran Missiles Attack, Trump Faces Criticism For Comments On 2020 Iran Attack; AP: Dockworkers Agree To Delay Walkabout Until January 15; Trump And Harris Campaigns Rally Support On The Trail; Melania Signals Support For Abortion in New York; Three Former Officers Convicted And Two Acquitted; Democrat Effort To Flip New York House Seat; Alleged Photos Of Lawyer In Halloween Blackface\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Brooke Taylor, Steve Harrigan, Bill Melugin, Grady  Trimble, Jacqui Heinrich, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 7,407 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm sorry, but we're out of time.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We have to talk to Bret now.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Greg, you never want to ring affair -- fire.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, you don't.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Good evening, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Israel is expanding its ground operation into Lebanon, warning some communities to evacuate, and for the second night in a row, massive explosions just within the past few minutes inside Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, as some residents return home in North Carolina, another huge storm threatens to strike the East Coast. We'll bring you the radar and where it's headed.\par \par \uc2 And a report from the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  watchdog claims current vetting practices for migrants could be creating risks for U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", officials are warning the strike at ports along the east and Gulf coasts of the U.S. could impact hurricane relief. President Biden saying today, progress is being made in negotiations, but some governors, they are not having it. With states with major ports, they are saying it's crucial for the union to reach an agreement and soon.\par \par \uc2 The comments come as the number of vessels stuck near 50 tonight, with the backlog estimated to double by the end of the week. Correspondent Brooke Taylor is at the \uc2 Port of Houston\uc2  again tonight with the latest. Good evening, Brooke.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE TAYLOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 You have the governors in New York and New Jersey who are siding with the dock workers.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Florida, you have DeSantis telling the White House to step in and do it fast. But there is one thing that all of these governors can agree on, Bret, that's that the strike needs to end soon, before families at home feel the impact.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): As tens of thousands of dock workers are on strike, shutting down major ports on the east and Gulf coasts, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has a plan to reopen his state's ports, saying it is unacceptable to leave Hurricane Helene victims without essential supplies.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The Florida National Guard and Florida State Guard will be deployed to critical ports affected to maintain order and if possible, resume operations.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): Meanwhile, on the west coast, the president of the local Longshoreman Union in San Francisco is standing strong with his fellow union workers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID GONZALES, \uc2 INTERNATIONAL LONGSHORE AND WAREHOUSE UNION\uc2 : We are not going to move any of your cargo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes!\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): The national union refuses to comment if all West Coast ports are on board. The striking workers along the east coast and Gulf of Mexico have two major demands: higher wages and a total ban on automation.\par \par \uc2 DENNIS DAGGETT, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, INTERNATIONAL LONGSHOREMEN'S ASSOCIATION: Stay strong, guys. Stay strong. We got it.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): ILA executive vice president Dennis Daggett, talking to picketers in New Jersey today.\par \par \uc2 DAGGETT: We fully understand your value and your worth and what you did for this country during covid.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: But consumers and business owners worry their demands are going to run the economy to the ground, with experts saying it's costing up to $4.5 billion a day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They want more pay, but it trickles down and it affects everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I feel bad for the customers. The prices are tremendously high.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): With both sides at a standstill and the world watching. President Biden, who has said he is not intervening, had this to say, today.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we are making progress on that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How much progress, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We'll find out soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (on camera): And if you at home wondering what products are going to be affected by this, I am here to tell you stop panic buying that toilet paper. Experts say the majority of toilet paper is made here in the United States, but come next week, you might start to see shortages of things like fresh fruit, bananas, auto parts, and alcohol. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Brooke, we'll follow it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another hurricane could be headed for the East Coast. Hurricane Kirk is now a category forms -- 4 storm, could cause life threatening surf and rip current conditions this weekend, as you see the radar there.\par \par \uc2 It comes amid the long road to recovery for Hurricane Helene. Victims, residents in North Carolina have started to return to assess the damage, but many roads are still impassable.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan reports just outside Asheville tonight with the latest. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Brett. The death toll now stands at 215.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our town is gone, virtually. That's our town is just gone.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: As the roads slowly open up, the true scope of Helene's destruction is revealed in western North Carolina, mountain towns like Swannanoa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is like what you see on television from Cat 5 hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 The more we looked, the more we realized, when are we coming back from this.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Before and after images of the devastation are evoking comparisons to the great flood of 1916.\par \par \uc2 The Swannanoa River here rising 26 feet, more than five feet above its previous high set more than 100 years ago. It will be hard to recover from 10-foot-high flood waters that covered what's left of the town in mud. Fresh water and power are in limited supply, and cell service almost nonexistent.\par \par \uc2 For some areas, so too is any sign of help.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WELLS, RESIDENT OF CANTON: We haven't seen FEMA, we haven't seen Red Cross, we haven't seen Hearts with Hands, we've seen no agencies come through here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Some residents taking desperate measures to rescue others, including one who stole a kayak.\par \par \uc2 I yelled at him, and he said, sorry, man, people are drowning. So, I grabbed my kayak, and my life jacket, followed him. Some people were in their attics, and we could see their fingers sticking out through the attic vents. They were screaming.\par \par \uc2 So, we started going around, rescuing people.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): President Biden was back on the ground in hard hit communities in Florida and Georgia today, again, promising to foot the recovery bill for several months, following the deadliest hurricane to hit the U.S. since Katrina.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I promise you we have your back. We are going to stay until you're restored.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): They are waiting. A woman here whose house was completely destroyed, signed up for a FEMA emergency payment of $750. It will be direct deposit after 10 days. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan, on the ground in North Carolina. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , says he will visit North Carolina next week to assess the hurricane damage. The former president held a rally in Saginaw, Michigan earlier today, and also spoke with correspondent Bill Melugin about those recovery efforts and some of the other top stories this evening.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. We spoke with former President Trump backstage before his rally here in Saginaw today.\par \par \uc2 There is a whole lot going on in the world right now. We covered a variety of topics with him, including the federal response to Hurricane Helene. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: We're up to more than 200 people dead from Hurricane Helene. I'm sure you saw yesterday. DHS says FEMA doesn't have enough money to last hurricane season. What's your reaction to hearing that?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, they are doing a very bad job. They have given over a billion dollars to illegal migrants that came in, and now, they have no money for North Carolina, for Georgia, for South Carolina, and Alabama, Tennessee, and Florida. I think that's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 This is a country that's being run by fools.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Biden says that Israel should not target Iranian nuke facilities. What are your thoughts on that? What should Israel respond?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I mean, that's the craziest thing I've ever heard. That's the biggest risk we have.\par \par \uc2 The biggest risk we have is nuclear. I mean, to make the statement, please leave their nuclear alone. I would tell you that that's not the right answer. That was the craziest answer, because, you know what, soon, they are going to have nuclear weapons, and then, you are going to have problems.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: What's your reaction to Melania's memoir about championing abortion rights and reproductive freedom? When you read it, what were your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We spoke about it, and I said, you have to write what you believe. I'm not going to tell you what to do. You have to write what you believe. She is very beloved. People love our former first lady. I can tell you that.\par \par \uc2 But I said, you have to stick with your heart. I've said that to everybody, you have to go with your heart. There are some people that are very, very far right on the issue, meaning without exceptions. And there are other people that view it a little bit differently than that.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is out on the campaign trail today with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  lost for Congress. She was terrible. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is a stupid war hawk. All she wants to do is shoot missiles at people. I really think it hurts. I think, frankly, if Kamala -- I think they hurt each other. I think they are so bad, both of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Former President Trump also told us he believes the dock workers strike would not have happened if he were sitting in the Oval Office right now. He told us he would have gotten all the stakeholders on the phone and done everything possible to try and avoid this strike.\par \par \uc2 Bret, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, live in Saginaw, Michigan. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", former President Trump's lawyers have requested a delay to file a response to Special Counsel Jack Smith's immunity filing. We brought you that last night, 165 pages.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Judge Chutkan agreed to allow Trump's team to respond on November 7th, two days after election day.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hopes to appeal to Republican voters in her Wisconsin event tonight. Her event is being held in a traditionally conservative location, and joining her, as you heard just there, is a member of a traditionally conservative family.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In Ripon, Wisconsin, known as the birthplace of the Republican Party, turned supporter, former congresswoman, Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , a vocal Harris detractor in a 2020 interview on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): She is the epitome of a San Francisco liberal. She is even farther to the left than Barack Obama was. She seems to be farther to the left than \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): After the January 6th Capitol riot, Cheney made it her mission to block former President Trump from getting back into the White House. On paper, the Republicans views appear to be diametrically opposed to the vice presidents, including on abortion.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: It cannot be for the people, if you will not protect our babies.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When Congress passes a bill to restore reproductive freedom, I will proudly sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): On immigration, Cheney in 2021, attacked Harris for not securing the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Here she is discussing national security, a hundred days after President Biden and Vice President Harris took office.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: The president and the administration have demonstrated an inability to really understand and recognize how important it is to demonstrate strength.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): Cheney sided with Trump on nearly 93 percent of her votes in Congress, but now she is backing Harris.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign, blasting Cheney as, "A stone cold loser who's so desperate for relevance and attention, she has debased herself by campaigning with a weak, failed and dangerously liberal \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The latest Marquette poll shows Harris with a four-point lead over Trump here in Wisconsin. That's within the margin of error, so, this is expected to be an extremely tight race in a state that's seen as a must win for the Democrats if they want to secure the blue wall and the White House. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady Trimble, live in Ripon, Wisconsin. Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, Israel targeting a suburb of southern Beirut in airstrikes, massive explosions, as you see here, could be seen as planes landed at the nearby airport.\par \par \uc2 Western nations are preparing contingency plans for large scale evacuations of their citizens from Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  organizing additional flights for Americans to flee that country. It comes as Israel's military issues evacuation orders in southern Lebanon, signaling an expansion of its ground operation there.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin reports on the situation from Haifa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): More airstrikes pushing closer to the heart of Lebanon's capital.\par \par \uc2 Israel says 15 targets were weapons production, weapons storage, and intelligence headquarters for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): We heard the explosion at night. We came now and saw everything flattened to the ground. There is nothing left.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The \uc2 Lebanese health ministry\uc2  says an additional 9,000 people were killed, bringing the total killed by airstrikes to nearly 2,000 over the past year, the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  helped 250 Americans living in Lebanon escape the bombing.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2 , SPOKESMAN, \uc2 UNITED STATES STATE DEPARTMENT\uc2 : We are organizing additional flights to allow more us, citizens and their eligible family members, to depart Lebanon now.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: The \uc2 IDF\uc2  continues to operate on the ground in South Lebanon, with infantry and reservists joining the fight.\par \par \uc2 Civilians in 70 villages were told to evacuate north as Israel destroys weapons and tunnels.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI, CHIEF OF THE GENERAL STAFF, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): We will not allow \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to establish itself in these places in the future.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fired back for the first time on Wednesday, nine Israeli soldiers have been killed. Israel claims to have killed 15 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters.\par \par \uc2 A segment of a missile fired at Israel and intercepted now lays in the desert west of the Dead Sea and has become an attraction.\par \par \uc2 With fears that Israel's response to the missile attack could spark a direct war with Iran, President Biden is talking sanctions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What kind of sanctions are you considering?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'll tell them before I tell you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you support Israel striking Iran's oil facilities, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are discussing that. I think -- I think that would be a little -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And live pictures now in the aftermath of those airstrikes. Why reports say the target was Hashem Safieddine. And anticipated to be the successor to \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 . The \uc2 Lebanese Health Ministry\uc2  says 37 people were killed in airstrikes today. Israel also struck in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 The target is said to be the leader of a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cell in the town of Tulkarm in the West Bank. 14 people are reportedly killed. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin in Haifa. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  juggles her official duties with her presidential campaign commitments -- we take you to the trail.\par \par \uc2 And later, are issues forum looks at the conflict in the Middle East. We hear from Democratic Delaware Senator Chris Coons and Republican Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks finished lower ahead of Friday's jobs report, analysts say the port strike and hurricane Helene recovery could distort the labor market picture in the year in the near term. The Dow losing 185 today, the S&P 500 fell 10, the NASDAQ lost seven.\par \par \uc2 Several major stories have already moved markets and could have further economic repercussions. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is working to maintain her campaign strategy while continuing her vice presidential responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich reports on that balancing act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It always gets this close. She's going to do fine.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden projecting confidence as a deadly storm, a massive strike and a war in the Middle East put his V.P. in the incumbent spotlight, shattering her pitch as the agent of change.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: If you're one point apart, these kind of things could have a huge consequence on the election.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The trio of crises pulling Harris' attention off the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She's not being a, you know, a right now. She's actually being the vice president, and that's a challenge.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Although her team sees an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, WHITE HOUSE SPOKESMAN: I think the vice president doesn't see these issues through the political prism. I think she sees them through the leadership prism.\par \par \uc2 Do we want someone who is serious or do we want someone who is unserious?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Critics warn the administration's refusal to intervene in the Longshoreman strike could magnify the storm's impacts at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): We're losing over $5 billion a day, and costs are going to start going up even higher on families who are already struggling under the Biden-Harris inflation economy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): If we need to send anything overseas, you know, think about vehicles armor. It's not going to go by ship, is it? So, it's impacting our projection of strength as well.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): What is on display is the contrast in approach from each side.\par \par \uc2 REP. CARLOS GIMENEZ (R-FL): The national security of the United States, those people in those states that need those supplies to recover, that should be the priority, not politics.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is saying that workers who strike should be fired. You know, we know where he stands when it comes to labor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Despite Biden and Harris' efforts to court union support. Today, the International Association of Firefighters announced that it will not be endorsing a candidate. They were among the first unions to endorse \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  four years ago. It's a snub for Harris, especially after her campaign mocked J.D. years ago. It's a snub for Harris, especially after her campaign mocked J.D. Vance when he was booed at the IAFF conference last month, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our issues forum with Democratic Senator Chris Coons and Republican Senator Tom Cotton, focusing on their candidate's plan for the Middle East and what's happening now.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York, where the man accused of choking a homeless person on the New York subway appears in court for a preliminary hearing. Former Marine Daniel Penny has pleaded not guilty to manslaughter and homicide charges.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chestnut Hill, Massachusetts, one of the big stories there tonight from Fox 25, a court will decide whether a beaver raised by rescuers will remain in captivity or release -- be released into the wild. Massachusetts wildlife officials have ordered the release of Navy. The workers at Newhouse wildlife rescue filed a case to stop the beaver's release, arguing she would rather be around people and has no interest in bonding with her own species. So, there you go.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: They reversed all the sanctions that President Trump out in place that were crippling Iran's economy to go and support terrorism around the world. Iran's made about $100 billion, Lawrence, selling their oil on world markets. They're funding terrorism. \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , October 7th, came out of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Houthis that are attacking our troops, and Biden just sits back and does nothing. Kamala does nothing. Let them defend themselves. Israel has a right to self-defense, whatever it is, including nuclear sites.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As the conflict in the Middle East escalates, foreign policy has become a central argument on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's Issues Forum, we will hear in a moment from Republican Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton on the Trump campaign's platform in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Delaware Senator Chris Coons. He's the co-chair of the Harris presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS COON (D-DE), SENATOR FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE: Great to be with you again, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You heard the House majority leader there talking there, Congressman Scalise, about what he believes and many Republicans say has empowered Iran to be in the position to funnel money to its proxies and caused what we saw not only in April but just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 COON: Well, let's be clear. President Biden and Vice President Harris have stood strongly by Israel's side following the hateful, heinous attacks of October 7 by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . President Biden has imposed more than 600 sanctions individually and against groups to constrain Iran and its regional group of proxies. And on April 14, pulled together a coalition of the United States, United Kingdom, France, UAE, Jordan, Saudi Arabia to partner with Israel to defend them against a barrage of 300 drones and missiles from Iran.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Vice President Harris stand strongly with Israel and will continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Senator, President Biden has expressed a reluctance, if you will, to have Israel go forward, continue attacks. It's been a lot of calls for, obviously, a ceasefire, but also to not escalate, in not so many words. It seems like the Israeli Prime Minister has some wind behind his back and is changing the dynamic with the threats he sees to Israel. Do you think the U.S. should be limiting what they're doing, considering the amount of success they've had in recent days?\par \par \uc2 COON: Well, let's be clear. The recent killing of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  was met with a strong, forceful statement of support, a recognition that Nasrallah had American blood on his hands for his role in the killing of hundreds of U.S. Marines now decades ago. But he's also been involved in killing thousands of innocent people throughout the region, Lebanese, Syrians, and Israelis. And tonight, as the Jewish people have celebrated Rosh Hashanah. It's important that we reemphasize that both President Biden and Vice President Harris are clear eyed, that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  are terrorist organizations, that this coming weekend, which will be the one year anniversary of the conflict in Gaza and of the horrific and ongoing practice by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of holding hostage far too many individuals.\par \par \uc2 They are not restraining Israel. They're not preventing Israel from its actions against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but they are urging caution about the possibility of a regional war should there be an all-out exchange between Iran and Israel.\par \par \uc2 As you know, just this past weekend, 180 missiles were launched by Iran at Israel. All of them were intercepted in no small part because of our ongoing close defense and security cooperation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, Senator, what do you say to the Republican critics and maybe some inside the military who question whether Vice President Harris would make a good commander-in-chief? They point back to the debate and answer here and what she said just the other day. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: As of today, there is not one member of the United States military who is in active duty in a combat zone in any war zone around the world the first time this century. Iran is not only a threat to Israel, Iran is also a threat to American personnel in the region, American interests and innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously talking about American personnel in the region. Changing that answer from the debate.\par \par \uc2 COON: We have 40,000 us troops in the region, and that's in no small part because President Biden and Vice President Harris stepped forward and decisively reallocated U.S. military resources to the Middle East following the October 7 attack. They have provided the support both regionally and on the ground that has been needed to make sure that there are the military resources, the intelligence resources to defend Israel against the ongoing attacks of Iran and its proxies. And you asked the question, am I confident that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would be a strong commander-in-chief?\par \par \uc2 I am. Her service on the intelligence committee while she was in the Senate and her service as vice president makes her, in my view, the second most qualified leader in terms of foreign policy and national security, second only to President Biden given his 50 years of service in the senate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She just got that answer.\par \par \uc2 COON: And as vice president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Answer wrong at the debate, right?\par \par \uc2 COON: Well, we are not currently in a declared war. The United States is not in a declared war against a major foe like the Vietnam War, the Korean War, the Second World War. So to that extent, we don't have troops in combat in a declared war. But, of course, any day of the week, we have troops deployed all around the world. And one of the things about our being in a dangerous world and having an important role in supporting our partners and allies around the world is that at any time, one of those members of the American armed forces could be targeted.\par \par \uc2 President Trump, when he was in charge, when he was president, destabilized and weakened our critical global alliances by being unpredictable, disrespectful, in some cases, actually picking fights with our closest partners and allies, whether in the Indo Pacific or in \uc2 NATO\uc2  and in Europe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 COON: I think President Biden has strengthened \uc2 NATO\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, Republican --\par \par \uc2 COON: -- strengthened our alliances. And Vice President Harris will continue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Republicans said that the enemies were kept on their toes because they didn't know what Trump was going to do. And I'm going to ask Senator Cotton about that. Senator Coons, we appreciate your time as always.\par \par \uc2 COON: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES REPRESENTATIVE: They are the ones who defended our capital, our democracy, and our lives. Many of them fought a bloody, hours-long battle on the west front of the capital. Go watch the video of that battle. It is sickening.\par \par \uc2 They are the ones who held the line and prevented far worse from happening that day. They are the true profiles encouraged. Do not let anyone lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former Congressman woman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in Wisconsin endorsing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , talking there about January 6. For the Republican side, let's bring in Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  often held the same kind of beliefs on foreign policy that you do. The fact that she's endorsing Vice President Harris, what do you think that means? Big picture?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR), TRUMP CAMPAIGN SURROGATE: Well, Brett, just four years ago, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  publicly said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was unfit to be the commander in chief would be dangerous for America and dangerous for the world. And I don't think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has changed in the last four years. I think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has changed.\par \par \uc2 I know \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hasn't changed. You just mentioned that she served on the Senate Intelligence Committee, Senator Coons. I served with her there for four years. I can tell you she was just as naive and reckless in private on that committee as she is as the vice president and as a presidential candidate. At every turn, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have made the world more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You listened to Senator Coons there defending the Harris record, how they're handling the Middle East. What's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, what Senator Coons described is not a record I'm familiar with, Bret. From the very beginning of Israel's fight against bloodthirsty Iranian-backed terrorists a year ago, President Biden and Vice President Harris has put more pressure on Israel than they put on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  or \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  or Iran. You know, Senator Coon said that they can. Or they celebrated the killing of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist mastermind. That was the very same day they both called for a ceasefire against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Once again, they're putting pressure on Israel not to mount a vigorous retaliatory strike against Iran. After Iran has launched almost 200 ballistic missiles at innocent civilian populations in Israel. They imposed a de facto arms embargo on many categories of weapons earlier this year. They've insisted that they're not helping Israel with intelligence sharing against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even today, the Biden administration took the side of the anti-Semitic United nations over Israel. That's the opposite of what we should be doing, which is what President Trump did four years backing Israel to the hilt and letting Israel win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's a question that the former president was asked about Iran and his dealings with Iran in Wisconsin on Tuesday. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you believe that you should have been tougher on Iran after they had launched ballistic missiles in 2020 on U.S. forces in Iraq, leaving more than 100 us soldiers injured?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So first of all, injured. What does injured mean? Injured means. You mean because they had a headache because the bombs never hit the fort. So just so you understand, there was nobody ever tougher in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, many organizations, Reuters had this headline February 10. More than 100 U.S. troops diagnosed with brain injuries from Iran attack. And there's criticism about that answer and about the policy. Your response to that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Sure. Well, Bret, President Trump is right. There hasn't been anyone since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  who's been tougher on Iran. President Trump ordered the killing of Iran's terrorist mastermind. By the end of the Trump administration, Iran was down to just a few billion dollars less than foreign currency. President Trump is contrasting the Americans who have died on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's watch, who have been killed by Iranian-backed terrorists throughout the Middle East, or the Americans who were killed in \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's attack on Israel, or who are still held hostage to this day. Nothing like that happened on President Trump's watch, in part because Iran was scared of America when President Trump was in the White House. Right now, they think they can push around and walk all over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, last thing, very quickly, just crossing the wires. This is AP. A source, the union for 45,000 us dockworkers agree to suspend the strike until January 15 to provide some time to negotiate a new contract. That's a long time. This brings an end, at least for the temporary time, to these port strikes that could have been crippling. Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, I don't know the details of the agreement, but it's welcome news. We don't want to have even more inflation and shortages of goods piled on top of what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  brought us to. So I hope they can reach a long-term agreement that is both satisfactory to the workers and to the port owners and operators.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Okay, Senator Cotton, thanks. Thanks for dealing with the breaking news as well. We appreciate it, in our issues forum.\par \par \uc2 We'll have the panel. Coming up next on the campaign trail, the latest breaking news and the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, we're getting reports union workers have reached an agreement to suspend their strike until January, telling our Alexis Adams on the dock there that the strike is over. One of the delegates saying that the ILA union notified all workers that an agreement has been reached and that port workers must report back to work tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We're being told that a final deal will have to be struck by January 15th, giving a lot of time here. Otherwise, the proposal for a 62% wage increase goes off the table. But it seems like for the interim, that this standoff with ships off the coasts of these ports will come to an end, which is a big deal for the economy. Let's bring in an abbreviated panel here with Byron York, Chief Political Correspondent, the Washington Examiner, and Julia Manchester, National Politics Reporter for The Hill.\par \par \uc2 Byron, a big deal. We don't have all the details yet, but seems like a win for the union at 62% wage increase if it all locks in at that. But more importantly, it doesn't affect the economy directly. It doesn't seem before November 5th.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, WASHINGTON EXAMINER: And it's a big deal for the campaign, too, as well, because I think this was shaping up this longshoremen strike, which could easily have lasted until election day, as a really major disruptive force and a sense, something that gives the voters a sense of chaos under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who, by the way, were on the side of the strikers. So in addition to this terrible sort of slow-motion tragedy unfolding after Hurricane Helene and the administration's sluggish response to that, a devastating strike would just be about the worst thing politically that could happen. And now it looks like it's just gone away.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. Julia, thoughts on this? Politically? Really?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, THE HILL: Yeah. Look, I mean, I'm sure that Democrats are somewhat breathing a sigh of relief. You had Republicans including former President Trump, really tying Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to this strike. And, of course, this was happening as we saw the devastation across much of the southeast from Hurricane Helene. So there was a lot of anxiety, obviously, as to how that could impact recovery efforts or if the strike and the devastation caused in the southeast could cause more of an economic impact, but certainly a sigh of relief for Democrats. You know, a lot of Republicans I've chatted to really in the run-up tonight's breaking news, Bret, have essentially said they were confused. Just why did President Biden sort of not get involved in this or get involved at least publicly for political purposes?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's head to the campaign trail. Some sights and sound from that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If lion Kamala gets four more years instead of a golden age, America will instead be plunged into a dark age.\par \par \uc2 TIM WALZ: Character matters. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has it. Her vision is about bringing us together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People are being reminded about the danger that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  poses to our country.\par \par \uc2 STEVE SCALISE (R-LA), HOUSE MAJORITY LEADER: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's first day was 1400 days ago. And look at the results. The world's on fire. Americans can't drive to go to the grocery store, let alone put food on the table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it seems like there's a lot happening day to day, but the polls are exactly where they've been. If you look at the real clear politics, average, head to head, it's basically where it's been, you know, within the margin of error here, two points. And as you look at the battleground states, it's, you know, swings day to day one way or another. But for the most part, it is really tight, especially in the seven states.\par \par \uc2 Byron, tonight we took live in Ripon, Wisconsin, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , former congresswoman, conservative, endorsing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  largely. She said in her speech about January 6 and the former president's behavior during that day. Does that move the needle on Republicans or disaffected Republicans at all?\par \par \uc2 YORK: No, I think the one thing we can say is that given all the things that have happened in this campaign, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 's endorsement of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is probably not going to move the needle at all. Because, as you were saying, what has been remarkable about this is that all of these things that have happened, both conventions, an assassination attempt, all of these things that have happened have not really changed this race too much. You have these two really big dug-in sides on either the Democratic and the Republican side. And you have a small layer of voters in the middle who still apparently haven't decided which way to go. And that's what the whole next 33 days fight is about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to play a sound byte from the former First Lady, Julia, about the issue of abortion. It's a full screen. I'm sorry. I'm sorry about that. I thought it was a sound byte.\par \par \uc2 And this is former First Lady Melania Trump. Why should anyone other than the woman herself have the power to determine what she does with her own body? "A woman's fundamental right of individual liberty to her own life grants her the authority to terminate her pregnancy as she wishes."\par \par \uc2 The former President was asked about that by Bill Belugian. He said she has her own thoughts. I told her to say what she believes. How do you think that affects the issue of abortion when it comes to former President Trump, Republicans, does it?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I think it does because these comments are coming on the heels of comments from Senator JD Vance, President Trump's running mate at the camp, at the -- excuse me, the Vice Presidential debate, where he essentially said that Republicans have to regain the trust of voters on this issue. And during that debate, we also saw former President Trump himself post a truth social that he would absolutely veto a national abortion ban, which Democrats say, you know, he would, you know, pass the national abortion ban so him pushing back on those claims.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think what you're seeing here, and although the former First Lady certainly went farther than Trump advanced it. But you're seeing the Trump campaign and Republicans trying to run to the center on this issue or trying to soften their stance on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Look, this has been an issue that has, you know, negatively impacted Republicans in past elections. In 2022, obviously, in the aftermath of overturning Roe versus Wade, we saw that this, you know, the red wave were supposed to see was in a way blunted because Democrats were focusing so much on abortion. We saw it also last year in Virginia's off-year 2023 legislative election so Republicans very cognizant of the fact that this is a tough issue for them. Polling also reflects that.\par \par \uc2 There was a \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  poll released last month that showed 54% or 53% of Americans said they \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  more on that issue, 34% say the same thing about Trump. So a tough issue. This is them trying to regain their footing on it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Byron, quickly, I heard a lot of pundits say this hurts the former president. But to Julia's point, if anything, it seems like it softens and maybe shores up that he wouldn't pass or sign a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, you know, those are both kind of persuasive arguments. But listen, I think the bottom line is what Mrs. Trump said right there sounds a lot more like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than it does the Republican ticket. And this is something that -- that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had just a lot of trouble dealing with. And it's not really going to get any better because he did provide the judges and the energy and the presidential power that led to the repeal of Roe versus Wade. And when he says, well, we wanted to give it to the states, that's what everybody wanted. Actually, it's not what everybody wanted. It was what conservative constitutionalists wanted. But that is not -- that does nothing satisfy the majority of Americans who feel that abortion should be legal in some cases at least. So what Mrs. Trump said really sounds a lot more like a Democrat than a Republican.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Byron, Julia, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More breaking news tonight. Three former Memphis police officers have been convicted of charges of witness tampering and the 2023 killing of Tyree Nichols. Two were acquitted of federal civil rights violations in the case. His death sparked national protests and calls for changes in policing. Up next, we'll take you on the 50 races in 50 days and we promise to get one in every time. We will.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: 50 races in 50 days with Vice President Harris widely favored to win New York in the presidential election. Democrats are hoping to ride that expected momentum there to flip a pair of House races.\par \par \uc2 In the 17th district, incumbent Republican Congressman Mike Lawler faces former democratic representative Mondara Jones, who was called a progressive, a rising star until he got beat. Jones represented the district from 2021 to 2023 due to redistricting. He ran in New York's 10th district in 2022, losing in the primary to representative Dan Goldman. Now he's back to challenge the first-term Lawler, who finds himself today embroiled in a controversy after the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  released photos allegedly showing Lawler wearing blackface in college as part of a Michael Jackson Halloween costume.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241004eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Campaigns In Battleground Michigan; Dockworkers Strike Over Pay, Automation; Melania Trump Defends Right To Choose; Suspects In Texas Girl Murder May Have Gang Ties\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, William La Jeunesse, Jonathan Hunt, Chad Pergram;  David Marcus; Marc Thiessen; Juan Williams; Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 8,762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Sandra, John, thank you. Good to see you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum, and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump about to take the stage in Battleground, Michigan, where he will likely have a lot to say about Special Counsel Jack Smith's newly unsealed court filing surrounding the 2020 election case. The former president slamming the, quote, Department of Injustice and the timing of all this just 33 days before the election.\par \par \uc2 We are also monitoring President Biden's visit to hurricane-ravaged Georgia this hour and day three of the dock worker strike, threatening higher prices and shortages across the U.S. Marc Thiessen, Juan Williams and columnist David Marcus, who says the Biden administration appears, quoting, rudderless in the face of all that's going on.\par \par \uc2 But, first, as we await former President Trump, a closer look at the Hurricane Helene recovery effort and warning that federal money is drying up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We are meeting the immediate needs with the money that we have. We are expecting another hurricane hitting. We do not have the funds. FEMA does not have the funds to make it through the season.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Congress approving more than $600 million for a FEMA program providing resources to illegal migrants last year, and some Republicans say Washington does not have its priorities straight.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram with the back story on Capitol Hill. Chad, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. Here's the question. Why is there money to help communities deal with illegal immigrants, a man-made disaster, but when it comes to a natural disaster, FEMA's coffers could bleed dry? Some Republicans are mad at DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everyone should be waking up this morning outraged by that comment.\par \par \uc2 This is not something that has just happened recently. And now, right now, in Florida, North Carolina, Tennessee, Georgia, all of these states are hurting because of this truly natural disaster, not a Biden-Harris-made disaster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: GOP Florida Representative Cory Mills helped distribute generators and other aid to people in the disaster zone. He asked why Congress spends money to help those here illegally compared to a natural disaster, or even sending money abroad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORY MILLS (R-FL): It's our responsibility to put Americans first, whether you're in Florida's seventh district in my district or you're anywhere in the union. I think that it's important for us to prioritize Americans to restore hope and let them know they're not forgotten.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But DHS has different accounts to help communities cope with the migrant crisis and handle the hurricane. Congress is now on track to approve additional money for FEMA later this fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY (R-TN): Nobody has seen anything like this in recorded history. The amount of damage, the flooding is just it's just hard to imagine. We've got to have resources quickly. We've got whole communities that are cut off bridges that are out, roadways that are out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The hurricane season runs until the end of November. House Speaker Mike Johnson concedes that Congress must address Helene, but he's worried about the national debt. Extra spending for war, terrorism, and natural disasters help drive the debt. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Chad Pergram live for us on Capitol Hill, Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Fox News Contributor Marc Thiessen, Fox News Senior Political Analyst Juan Williams, and columnist David Marcus, who writes for Fox today, quoting, the southeast is underwater, the Middle East is on fire, ports are closed, where is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 It's kind of amazing, David, let's begin with you, because it really is a compelling article, and the theme throughout this whole thing, whether it's Israel or whether it's the port strike, you name it, is that there has been a lack of a timely response by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARCUS, FOX NEWS COLUMNIST: Yes. After \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  left the race, I was all over the country talking to various voters and I would ask them, do you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in charge? I don't even ask anymore because almost to a person they said, no, many of them would just start laughing.\par \par \uc2 I would say to Democrats, though, isn't this a problem that we don't have a functioning president and say, well, it's okay. He's got a great team, he's got experts. And the Democratic voters I talked to are comfortable with this notion of a president by committee. It doesn't matter who's in the Oval Office. The big wheel just keeps on turning.\par \par \uc2 Republican voters are horrified by this. They want one single accountable person actually making the decisions and they blame a lot of the recent failures on the fact that right now we don't seem to have that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And it's interesting, Marc Thiessen, because he makes a good point. You know, a lot of the Democrats we talked to are also fine with this power by committee. But it's interesting when you move on to subjects like Israel, The Wall Street Journal points out this morning quoting here the thinking best -- talking about Biden protecting Iran's nuclear program, the thinking is that has led to the collapse of U.S. deterrence on Mr. Biden's watch. Enemies rely on him to come to their defense by restraining allies from responding to aggression. Mr. Biden didn't even wait to keep Iran guessing. I mean, Iran, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2  all know that Biden will keep telling Israel to stop instead of telling Israel to win.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I mean, the one area where I disagree with David is that one competent person, if it wasn't \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , would be \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And Republicans ought to be careful what they wish for, because I don't want \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to spend a single minute on the Oval Office behind the Resolute Desk, not even a couple of months, because she's just as bad as Biden is, if not worse.\par \par \uc2 And, look, it's absolutely true. He's hanging out on the beach while we have the worst hurricane since Katrina. We have Iran firing missiles at Israel, which it has never done before until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was president. It had always attacked Israel through proxies.\par \par \uc2 Now, they've twice attacked Israel directly with weapons and Biden is terrified of escalation. I interviewed President Trump at Mar-a-Lago the other day, and one of the things that became clear in discussing this whole issue with him is that the difference between Biden and Trump is that Biden fears escalation. Trump makes our enemies fear escalation. After he gave a red line to the Iranians and said, if you kill a single American in that region, I'm going to -- you're going to pay a price, not your proxies, you. And when they cross the red line, he killed \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And then he told them, I've picked out 52 targets inside Iran in honor of the 52 American hostages. And if you cross the line again, I'm going to strike Iran proper. And they backed down because they feared escalation more than he did. Our president right now fears escalation more than the Iranians, which is why we have escalation.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And it's a fair point, Juan, because you talk to -- as we do every day, you talk to American Jews, you talk to some of these victims of Helene, and they're not exactly giving the Biden-Harris administration high marks.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I don't understand much of this conversation because in the Middle East, I don't know what you would want the American administration to do beyond signal clear support for Israel, which is what the Biden administration has done. And in terms of escalation, we have 40,000 American soldiers and troops on the ground or in the seas in the region.\par \par \uc2 So, escalation could involve our people getting killed. We don't want it. We want peace and we want some kind of solution to the Middle East tensions. That's why you would encourage us -- I think encourage people to look for some resolution.\par \par \uc2 And with regard to the hurricane, Trace, you know, I don't think this is a -- it's a fair choice. It's a false choice. In fact, to say, oh, money's that were going to immigrants, and it's about $600 million, not even a dollop of the billions that we give just recent, just last week, we gave billions more, $20 billion to FEMA to help with hurricane recovery. You know, Trump likes to blame immigrants for everything, from crime to infectious disease.\par \par \uc2 And now for hurricanes, I mean, this is so transparent. You can see right through it. It's a political argument, not realistic in terms of what the president can do.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David, I say I've got 30 seconds for you if you want to respond.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Yes. Look, you can't have a situation where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  goes down to Appalachia and says that people who had their houses destroyed get $750 while New York City is handing out ATM cards where illegal immigrants are getting $350 a week. It's patently absurd. It's not a political question. It's a moral question and it's wrong.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. With that, we've got to go. Marc Thiessen, Juan Williams, David Marcus, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Well, we will bring you to Michigan as soon as former President Trump takes the stage. Meantime, a grim milestone one week after Hurricane Helene tore through parts of the southeast, the death toll now surpassing 200 deaths. And while President Biden pledges millions of dollars, thousands of victims are still without access to water, electricity, and in many cases, a place to live.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it's very disappointing, you know, that there's been such a delay in getting the help that this community needs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're lucky to be alive. And we know a lot of reporters keep asking about, you know, body counts and death. And the real truth is they're still looking for everyone. You know, they're in the river. They're in the trees. We don't know the scale yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, President Biden in route to Georgia right now for another survey of the destruction from Hurricane Helene. After visits to Florida and North Carolina, former President Trump says he plans to visit North Carolina next week. No word on an exact date yet. At least 200 people now dead in six states.\par \par \uc2 One of the most heartbreaking stories, the mother who lost her parents and her seven-year-old son, Megan Drye, took this photo on their roof in Asheville, North Carolina. Just before it collapsed. Only Megan survived. She paid tribute to her son, Micah, in an emotional interview with Fox weather anchors Stephen Morgan and Marissa Torres.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN DRYE, LOST SON AND PARENTS IN FLOOD: I'm so proud of my son because in his last moments, he wasn't screaming for me. He was screaming, Jesus, save me, Jesus, I hear you, Jesus, I'm calling upon you. And his wildest dreams and everything that he wanted to be was a superhero. And that was his goal in life. And instead he's my hero. He was the smartest, bravest, hopeful, great friend, great son. I couldn't have asked for a better son and he was so happy up until the very end when he was screaming for Jesus. And in that moment, I think he found joy.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA TORRES, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: You know, the innocence of Children is actually quite spectacular. Oh my gosh, I'm so sorry.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MORGAN, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, it is, Marissa being a mother too, Megan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it really is a heart-rending account. Let's bring in Brian Trascher, vice president of the United Cajun Navy. He's standing by. Some of the volunteers involved in the group's rescue efforts say the damage done is of biblical correspondence. Very quickly, let's check in to with Lucas Tomlinson, who's live in Valdosta, Georgia. Lucas?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Trace, good afternoon. We just saw Marine One flying overhead with President Biden aboard. He'll be on the ground at Moody Air Force Base here in Valdosta, Georgia, shortly. The death toll from Hurricane Helene across the southeast now over 200, making this the second deadliest hurricane to hit the U.S. mainland in the past 55 years.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden has just ordered a thousand active duty troops to join in the recovery and search and rescue efforts, as well as help clean up the destruction. Some of those troops, of course, from Moody Air Force Base, home of the pararescue jumpers, the most elite search and rescue forces in the world.\par \par \uc2 Now, when President Biden arrived in Tallahassee this morning in Florida, he was greeted by the mayor and state officials. Governor DeSantis was not among them. And here in Georgia, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  does not plan to meet the president when he arrives here.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden just toured Keaton Beach about an hour and a half southeast of Tallahassee along the Gulf Coast. At least 19 people in Florida have been killed due to Hurricane Helene, and at least 33 have been killed here in Georgia. Biden also toured Perry, Florida, toured the damage by air aboard Marine One.\par \par \uc2 Now, here in Georgia, Trace, 1,400 members of the National Guard are helping their communities clear the roads and evacuate the stranded. Three counties in Georgia, Atkinson, Jefferson, Montgomery, are still 90 percent without power.\par \par \uc2 As I mentioned, President Biden should be on the ground at Moody Air Force Base here in Valdosta, Georgia shortly. He'll be touring the damage and then is expected to give remarks. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Lucas Thomason live for us, Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 United Cajun Navy Vice President Brian Trascher joins us now on the phone from North Carolina with an update on the group's relief efforts. Brian, thank you for coming on. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 You know, the people we talked to who are victims of Helene say, in a lot of cases in a lot of these towns, they are the first responders because there's so much debris, so much destruction that the actual first responders and outside help are having a difficult time getting in. What are you finding, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN TRASCHER, VICE PRESIDENT, UNITED CAJUN NAVY: So, thanks for having me on, Trace. And, you know, like always, we go in, we try to be the first shrimp boots on the ground because we know that, you know, the federal government's not a first responder. They're a second responder and it takes a while to get that cavalry going.\par \par \uc2 But we started off, we based air operations in Hickory, North Carolina, and we had over 30 aircraft participate in missions, flying supplies and we've been evac'ing people out of the damaged areas. And just earlier today, I flew a helicopter into Burnsville, which is a town hit very hard. And there were people there from neighboring towns, like Bakersville and Rome Mountain, who found their way to Burnsville because it was the closest sort of like civilization they could find.\par \par \uc2 And they need medical attention. They said nobody's been there, nobody's touched them. And so we're just trying to send our teams further and further towards the Tennessee line. It seems like it gets worse the further you go that way.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's interesting. You say that, you know, the government is always second responders. I want to play this sound bite from Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . He had this to say, Brian. I'll get back to you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): I know there's a lot of people that are frustrated out there and I can certainly appreciate that and Marty and I felt that when we were on the road yesterday, but also told them this is unprecedented that we're dealing with, previously the worst storm Georgia power had ever dealt with. They had 1,700 poles that needed to be replaced.\par \par \uc2 Currently, there are over 8,000 that have to be replaced.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The people that we talked to, Brian, are frustrated because the response, the federal response has not been as robust as they were hoping.\par \par \uc2 TRASCHER: Well, you know, I went through Katrina. I'm from New Orleans. You'll know that. And it was the same song and dance. You know, the feds, you know, they can do a lot. Once they get there, it's a question of when will they get there. And that's why organizations like the United Cajun Navy, you know, we feel are important to get there first. Because we don't have -- look, we don't have to ask our lawyers for permission to do anything. We just need them to show up at our bond hearing. That's our motto.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. What do you think the priority is right now, Brian? I've got to go but is this a water, a clean water thing? Is that the primary objective right now for these people?\par \par \uc2 TRASCHER: Yes, clean water, medical supplies, the fire stations are overwhelmed and we need -- they need oxygen, they need fuel. And we deployed several hundred Starlink units. They need communications and they need them now. So, keep the Starlinks coming. That's been the glimmer of hope. This whole thing is Starlink.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Brian Trascher, best of luck to you and your fellow members of the Cajun Navy. Thank you for the help. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, former President Trump taking the stage at a campaign rally in battleground Michigan, currently leaning Democrat. That's according to our latest power rankings. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: In this beautiful state with thousands of proud, hardworking American patriots, which is exactly what you are. Early mail-in voting is underway now in Michigan, so if you want, go out and get that ballot, return it as soon as possible, and if not, get a ballot, just go and vote. You got to vote. We got to win this election. Our country is being destroyed by these lunatics. And while you're at it, get as many other Trump supporters as you can, and they'll do the same.\par \par \uc2 33 days from now, we are going to win the state of Michigan. We are going to defeat Lying \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , one of the worst candidates ever. You never had a candidate like that. And how about the vice president? That's going to be president someday? I don't think so. I don't think so. Did you see the debate? I don't think that's going to be president. He called himself knucklehead. We don't want a knucklehead as a president. And we're going to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 And I'm asking every citizen all across our land to join me in launching a new golden age for America. As catastrophic as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- you know, I never used the name Harris. Nobody knows what the hell I'm talking about. I say, you know, Harris is doing a lousy job. Who's Harris? So, we usually just say Lying Kamala. It's much better, more accurate.\par \par \uc2 But as bad as Kamala's failures have been, we have a plan to fix it and we're going to fix it very fast. It's going to go very quick. Can you imagine what she's doing to our country on the borders? Can you imagine? With everything else is bad. Can you imagine what they're doing? Why? Why are they doing it? They're killing our country.\par \par \uc2 We can soon have soaring income, skyrocketing wealth, millions and millions of new jobs and a booming middle class. You know, we had the best four-year period of any president in history and that's despite the China virus coming in, despite the China virus. Instead of a manufacturing recession, we can have a manufacturing renaissance, which is what we want, especially here, especially in this time.\par \par \uc2 We can live in cities and towns that are safe and clean and borders that are sealed and secure, wouldn't that be nice? And America can once again be strong and confident and free. I mean, how would you like to live like they're living where thousands and thousands of criminals are put into your town or your city? And all of a sudden your whole life changes, your whole life changes. You see what's happened with Springfield and \uc2 Aurora\uc2 . You see what happened, Springfield, Ohio. You see what's happening.\par \par \uc2 Would you like to have that happen to you? Good. I was just curious who the hell can do that? Who can do it? They inject 32,000 people from a foreign land that has lots of problems, but they eject 30 -- and your 50,000 people and now you have in a month you have you've almost doubled the size.\par \par \uc2 And it's a disaster what's going on in Aurora, where you have Venezuela and street gangs. They went into the same business as me, but they take their real estate with guns. I had to go borrow money from banks. They're in the real estate business.\par \par \uc2 They go in and the governor there in Colorado, Aurora, Colorado, the governor is petrified. He doesn't know what to do. He's never seen anything like it. Actually, nobody has seen anything like it when you get right down to it.\par \par \uc2 But we could be a nation that dreams big again. We can be a nation that builds things again. We can reclaim our pride, our dignity, our glorious American destiny, but only if we make a clean break from the failures of the past four years. They've been the worst four years. When you add everything up, the inflation, the lack of respect, the Afghanistan disaster, which was the most embarrassing period of time in our history, where we were just humiliated, and you add it all up and all we're going to do is a very simple thing. We're going to make our country so incredible and so fast. You only have one thing to do on November 5th. We're going to vote for a person named \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . Have you ever heard of him? Have you ever heard him?\par \par \uc2 Nice hat.\par \par \uc2 But if Lying Kamala gets in, if she gets -- can you imagine another four years like we've lived the last four? Think of it. People are dying in North Carolina. They're dying all over those five, six states. They're dying and they're getting no help from our federal government because they have no money because their money's been spent on people that should not be in our country.\par \par \uc2 If Lying Kamala gets four more years instead of a golden age, America will instead be plunged into a dark age. Your family finances will be permanently destroyed. Your borders will be gone forever. They already are when you think of it, right? We don't have any borders. You're a border state now, you know that? Welcome. Congratulations. Tens of millions more illegal aliens will invade our cities and towns. Medicare and Social Security will buckle and collapse from the weight of all of these people becoming proud members of our Social Security System. Isn't that nice?\par \par \uc2 Remember, for four years I took care of it. I didn't raise the age five years. You know what that means? That means, darling I thought I was going to have Social Security. I thought I was going to retire a little early. But they've raised the age by five or six or seven years. That's what they're going to do to you. I won't, because I would have done it already if I was going to do it. But they're going to do it. They're going to do it.\par \par \uc2 And they're really destroying the whole system in the world. We'll be thrown into chaos and conflict like never before, although we're pretty close to that right now. And we're also very close to a third world war. And we have incompetent people running our country. We don't want to be -- we don't want to have them -- did you see where Biden said today when they asked about what about Israel, what are they doing? And he said, well, I don't know. He didn't even know where the hell he was.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's all these politicians now. I think I was the only one that ever went to the planes and the helicopters and talked, right? Now, they all sort of stop and they talk, but he has to do a good job of it. He talks -- but he said, well, they'll have to do. I don't want them to do anything, Israel, but as long as they don't destroy the nuclear weapons on the other side.\par \par \uc2 I said, did he say that? Now, isn't it supposed to be the opposite, Judge? We got some of the biggest judges in the country right here. But isn't it supposed to be the opposite? No, as long as you do destroy the nuclear weapons, right, he said. He's the worst foreign policy president in history, and everybody knew that from the beginning. You know, he said, as long as they don't destroy the nuclear, and I'm still trying to figure that one out. Nobody can figure it out. He can't figure it out either, come to think of it.\par \par \uc2 If you want to avoid this miserable fate for America, Michigan and the entire Midwest will have to go out and you have to get -- we have to be too big to rig, you know that, too big to rig. Turn out and vote in record numbers, too big to rig.\par \par \uc2 You know, last time, last election, we did great in 2016. A lot of people don't know we did much better in 2020. We won. We won. We did win. It was a rigged election.\par \par \uc2 It was a rigged election. You have to tell \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- that's why I'm doing it again. If I thought I lost, I wouldn't be doing this again.\par \par \uc2 You know where I'd be right now, on the beaches of Monte Carlo, maybe, or someplace, be having a nice life. But if I had my choice of being here with you today or being on some magnificent beach with the waves hitting me in the face, I would take you every single time because we're gonna make our country great. Every single time.\par \par \uc2 It's a very energetic crowd, this crow. And who else can fill up? Only Maga (inaudible). But who ever fills big places like this at 3:00 in the afternoon? We're going to be meeting. We're going to think of it.\par \par \uc2 I just left Wisconsin four days ago. Five days ago, we had a -- sort of a rally. It wasn't supposed to be 60,000 people showed up in Wisconsin. But the White House wouldn't give us proper security coverage so we end up in a room of about 900 people.\par \par \uc2 You have 60,000 people, 50,000 to 60,000 people outside waiting. But they don't want that because they don't want crowds. What they are doing is such a disgrace to our country. And so to those people in Wisconsin that didn't make it, I think they love us and I think they're going to vote.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we're up in all of the polls. We're up in every swing state, doing great. They had a honeymoon period. You know, they had like a honeymoon, I didn't know what a honeymoon period. They kept talking about the honeymoon. Because I beat Biden, we were up 21 points by their numbers, and they went to him.\par \par \uc2 Crazy \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , she's crazy as a bedbug. Crazy Nancy, but others, too, they went to him and they said, Joe, you're out. He said, I'm not. I'm not getting out. And they said, no, you're out. We're going to use the 25th amendment because you're fricking crazy, man.\par \par \uc2 And he eventually agreed to get out, which took about, you know, 24 hours. Remember, he said he would only get out if he was forced to by God. And that didn't work out. That didn't work out. So they said, you're getting out because you're going to lose. You're going to lose by a lot.\par \par \uc2 Right after the debate, they went to him, right? I don't know, did I do a good job or a bad job? They said, you should have taken it a little bit easy. But all of a sudden, we start hearing rumors, right, that he's getting out.\par \par \uc2 And then we heard they, you know, have the standard list of people, Democrats and Gavin New-scum and lots of people. He destroyed California. She destroyed California, by the way. She destroyed the greatest city in our country 20 years ago, San Francisco. And then she became AG.\par \par \uc2 She destroyed the state of California along with New-scum. But she had every bit -- did you ever hear of him, Gavin New-scum? He's a terrible governor, done a lousy job.\par \par \uc2 And you have such advantages, you know, you do have great weather. You have the beautiful ocean. You have everything. But you still need somebody that knows what they're doing. And she destroyed, think of it, she destroyed San Francisco when she was the DA, the crime started to be rampant. It was the best city, the best.\par \par \uc2 And then she destroyed California. And now she wants to destroy the United States of America. We're not going to let her go.\par \par \uc2 So you have to tell Lying Kamala that we've had enough. Kamala, we can't take you anymore. We're not going to stand for it anymore. Kamala, you're fired. Get out. You're fired. You're fired. Get the hell out of here, right?\par \par \uc2 Starting on day one of my new administration, we will end inflation and we will make America affordable again. It's not affordable. They kill you. They kill you with their spending, a combination of spending and their stupid energy policies.\par \par \uc2 They said we're going to get away from the Trump energy policies. We were energy independent. Your numbers were the lowest they were in 30 years. We're going to get lower numbers. By the way, we are going to from January 20, that's the day you take office. One year from January 20, your energy bills will be one-half what they are right now. Fifty percent, 5-0, they'll be one-half.\par \par \uc2 Because we're sitting on more liquid gold than any other country in the world, including Saudi Arabia and including Russia. We have more liquid gold right under our feet and we don't use it. We go to Venezuela to get their tar. And then we clean it up, you know where? We go to a very good -- I just left there, actually. We go to Houston, Texas where they're the only plant that will clean their tar.\par \par \uc2 It's tar. Instead of using our stuff, which is pure, as pure as there is. And it's so crazy what we're doing in this country. It's just crazy. But we have more liquid gold. You know, I did a thing called ANWR that's in Alaska. You know about it a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Reagan tried to get it. Everybody tried to get it. They couldn't do it. I got it.\par \par \uc2 In their first week, they terminated it. It's larger, perhaps as big as, but maybe larger than Saudi Arabia. Think of that. We would have supplied the entire Asian continent. We would have supplied Asia. We would have supplied everybody. But we'll have it redone very quickly because they didn't kill it like they wanted to, because they didn't kill it in Congress.\par \par \uc2 I actually got it approved in Congress as part of the best -- as part of the biggest tax cuts in history for this country, I got that approved in Congress. We got ANWR. So they didn't kill it in Congress, and I don't think they ever could. So we'll get it back very quickly. It's going to be back very fast.\par \par \uc2 But think of that, the biggest sight probably anywhere in the world, you never know exactly, but pretty much anywhere in the world, in Alaska. And it would have been great for Alaska, but it would have also been great for, you know, so big would have been great for our country. But we'll have it approved very quickly.\par \par \uc2 But they ended it in the first week. But they approved the Russian pipeline. I had that disapproved. I said, you're not going to build, you know, when they say, I was nice to Russia, I terminated their pipeline. I mean, it's the biggest thing they've ever done.\par \par \uc2 They had a pipeline going to Germany and all over Europe. I said, nope, you're not going to build it. You're not going to build it, Vladimir. I'm sorry, Vladimir. You're not building it, Vladimir. No, no, no, because it didn't make sense.\par \par \uc2 We're defending them with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , right? So we're defending them, all the countries over in Europe. They're not paying their bills. They're delinquent. You know, they're delinquent. They're not paying their bills. But we're defending them with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and yet they're paying billions of dollars to the people that we're defending, the country that we're defending them against Russia.\par \par \uc2 So we defend them. And I said, you know, Angela, I met with \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 . I said, Angela, this doesn't make sense. We're defending you from Russia, but you're paying Russia billions of dollars a month. Somehow that's not working for me, Angela. And I terminated their pipeline right in the middle of that construction.\par \par \uc2 You think Putin was happy? I don't think so. You think Biden would have done that?\par \par \uc2 When Biden came in -- when Biden came in, one of his first acts was to unterminate. It was to let him build their pipeline. That wasn't good. And by the way, if he didn't, you wouldn't have had the war in Ukraine. You wouldn't have had it anyway if I was president.\par \par \uc2 If I was president, you wouldn't have had that. You wouldn't have had October 7th with Israel. Iran, as you know, was broke.\par \par \uc2 They had no money for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They had no money for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . They had no money for anything. They would have made a deal. And they would have been happy. They would have made a deal.\par \par \uc2 And now look at what's happening over there. The whole world is blowing up. And the biggest threat we have is World War II, and we have incompetent people. And she's more incompetent than Biden.\par \par \uc2 And remember, that was a coup. That wasn't like -- that was a coup of an American --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, there you go. That's former President Trump in Saginaw, Michigan, holding a rally there with a big crowd turnout. Meantime, Vice President Harris railing against companies making record profits in the dockworker battle that could hit hard for millions of consumers. Senior National Correspondent William La Jeunesse is live at the port of Los Angeles with a preview. William?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Tracy, you know, East Coast longshoremen are worried that robots are going to take their port union jobs. Well, what happened when longshoremen lost that fight here on the West Coast? That's story coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, vice president and -- or President Biden and Vice President Harris calling for a fair deal for dockworkers as the port strike hits day three, threatening to cause weeks of supply chain backlogs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: There's a consortium of basically six owners that control all the ports from all the way to the East Coast down around the gulf. And they've made incredible profits, over 800 percent profit since the pandemic. And the owners are making tens of millions of dollars in this. The last thing they need is to profit off of this. It's time to then sit at the table and get this strike done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And Vice President Harris saying, quoting here, "This strike is about fairness. Foreign-owned shipping companies have made record profits and executive compensation has grown. The longshoremen who play a vital role transporting essential goods across America deserve a fair share of these record profits."\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse is live at the port of Los Angeles. William?\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Well, Tracy, this strike is not just about pay, a major sticking point is automation. The union worried that humans are going to be replaced by robots up in those cranes and down on the wharf. So that's why you need to take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 This is the difference between the East and the West Coast longshoremen contracts. On the left, the East Coast bans "any equipment devoid of human interaction," and they want it to stay that way. The West Coast Union fought this battle years ago and they lost, by contrast. This contract says fully mechanized and robotic operated marine terminals are allowed. And that's where the world is going.\par \par \uc2 China's largest port, you got four guys at computers handling about two dozen automated cranes delivering containers to automatic vehicles, and then to another automatic crane, putting those on trucks and trains. Now, upfront, it is expensive but it's more efficient and productive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GERALDINE KNATZ, USC PROFESSOR AND MARITIME EXPERT: So the terminal operators started, you know, basically started to see a return on their investment after five to six years, some of it longer, because of the cost of implementing the automated equipment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Study show that the semi-autonomous terminals here in Long Beach and LA processed containers about two times faster than a conventional terminal, but they also took about 600 jobs. Now, as for diverting traffic here to LA from the East Coast, two things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, once a ship is under sail, by and large it's committed to the itinerary. That cargo is going to go off Baltimore, New York. That's where it's going to sit.\par \par \uc2 However, we do have room here, right? There's about 20 percent excess capacity. That's room for about 40 more container ships per month here in LA Long Beach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA, PORT OF LOS ANGELES EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: If a shipper decided tomorrow they wanted to move to cargo to Los Angeles, we'd see that box arrive in about a month.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So if we're going to see that volume shift, Trace, we're probably going to see those numbers come out next week. As for the robots, they are on their way. Some will argue that the increase in productivity leads to more jobs. But the East Coast longshoremen don't believe it. They don't want to see robots coming to the port. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: William La Jeunesse live for us in the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 . William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2  is Chief Policy Officer for the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  and warns the strike could cause weeks of supply chain backlogs. Neil, great to have you on the show. We're in day three of this strike. If it goes a week, if it goes two weeks, what does it start to look like, Neil?\par \par \uc2 NEIL BRADLEY\uc2 , CHIEF POLICY OFFICER, US CHAMBER OF COMMERCE: Well, it starts to look like empty shelves in grocery stores. That's some of the first impact we'll see. We don't grow bananas here in the United States, we bring them in mainly through the East and Gulf Coast ports.\par \par \uc2 And those are perishable items. You can't really store those for a long time. So we're going to start to see shortages in grocery stores and restaurants. That's going to compound into retail stores.\par \par \uc2 The ones that are going to be the first hit are actually small businesses. Small businesses really can't afford to stockpile inventory months and months ahead of time. So small businesses that rely on this holiday sales season may find that they actually don't have any inventory to put up for sale.\par \par \uc2 And so, if this keeps going, we're talking about really profound negative consequences for businesses and consumers all across the country.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: William just talking about the fact that a lot of this has been the focus of, you know, wages, but there's also a lot of technology involved in these debates. \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  said this, and I want to get your take afterwards. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The union has a point here, an important point. We got clobbered on inflation. There's been an affordability crisis for years. We haven't had a raise. The company's making good money.\par \par \uc2 The rank and file have a right to come at this. They probably deserve a pretty good raise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Do they, Neil?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, of course they deserve a raise. And let's be clear, the container ship operators in the ports offered a 50 percent pay raise just to keep the union at the negotiating table. Not as a final deal, just to keep them at the table.\par \par \uc2 And the union bosses said, nope, not good enough. We would rather strike. And the union president made clear his strategy. He said he wanted to cripple, "cripple" the US economy, and he's doing that to gain leverage. I don't think any of us find it acceptable that one union boss can try to cripple the United States economy as a means of trying to achieve their ends.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Meantime, the President, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , says he does not believe in Taft-Hartley. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you intervene in the dockworkers strike if they go on strike on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's collective arguing. I don't believe in Taft-Hartley.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And even if it was implemented, the union bosses say they would just slow things down. I mean, instead of unloading 30, they'd unload eight. And so that's kind of the thing here. Should the President be more stringent with the union bosses themselves, Neil?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: He absolutely should. Listen, President Biden's predecessors have used Taft-Hartley on 33 separate occasions, including 14 times where we had port or maritime operations suspended just like we do now.\par \par \uc2 And here's the other thing. It's bipartisan. President Biden's six Democratic predecessors who had this law available to them, four of them used it in situations just like this.\par \par \uc2 And so, there's no foolproof plan here but the president should absolutely be invoking Taft-Hartley. He should force these parties back to the table. He should get the workers back unloading this produce and the essential goods that we need for hurricane recovery, for our store shelves and to support our economy.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I got 30 seconds. You know, you said first when I asked you that the thing you're going to see first is empty store shelves. And then you say produce. Is it the produce on the store shelves that we are going to see disappear first?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: That's what you're going to see first because you really can't store that up ahead of time. It relies on real time delivery. And these are things that -- we can't supply bananas. We don't have the climate to grow bananas in the United States.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: And so, those are the types of things that will disappear first.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2 , thanks for coming on the show. We appreciate it, sir.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, President Biden also weighing in on Israel's possible response to this week's missile attack from Iran. Israel launching more strikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets in Lebanon today, and revealing it killed the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  government chief in Gaza three months ago. President Biden taking questions from reporters as he left the White House today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The hostages in Gaza, do you have any updates on this?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Not right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you support Israel striking Iran's oil facilities, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're in discussion with that. I think that would be a little -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And still ahead, former first lady makes sure there's no confusion about where she stands on abortion. And stunning new details possibly linking the suspects accused of brutally murdering twelve year old Jocelyn Nungaray and the violent Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS NUNGARAY, JOCELYN NUNGARAY'S MOTHER: I come to you all as a grieving mother to please help make this country make a change for the greater good. This country needs to make a change. We need to properly secure our borders. We need to be able to have that security knowing we are safe in our own country. And most importantly, we need to protect our children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Days after Senator JD Vance said the Republican Party needed to regain trust on abortion, former first lady Melania Trump dropping a new video to carve out a clear stand on the hot button issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP, FORMER FIRST LADY: Individual freedom is a fundamental principle that I safeguard. Without a doubt, there is no room for compromise when it comes to this essential right that all women possess from birth, individual freedom. What does my body, my choice really mean?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: An excerpt from Melania Trump's upcoming memoir reportedly says restricting a woman's right to choose is the same as denying her control over her body. Former President Trump says he'd veto any federal abortion ban if reelected and is leaving it up to the states to decide on abortion access.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, disturbing reports today that the suspects in the murder of twelve year old Texas girl Jocelyn Nungaray, who are here in the US illegally, may have ties to the notorious Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua. This is Jocelyn's mother, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NUNGARAY: I have to go through the rest of my life with my son always asking for his sister. And I just really, really, really want everybody to please take into consideration and how important border control is, because we're losing very innocent people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The Chief Correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2  following the story from here in Los Angeles. Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. Jocelyn Nungaray was sexually assaulted, her hands and ankles tied, and she was then strangled to death, according to prosecutors. These two men, Franklin Pena and Johan Martinez-Rangel, have been charged with her murder and both are being held on a $10 million bond. And investigators now believe both are connected to the notorious Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua.\par \par \uc2 Evidence of those ties was allegedly found on their TikTok accounts. According to a newly released search warrant, the evidence includes emojis commonly used by the gang, such as a ninja cross swords and an icon of a classical building, as well as tattoos and hand signals. Nungaray's mother says she's not surprised by the alleged gang links.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NUNGARAY: Individuals like that do not have a heart. They are nothing but monsters who are predators. And those are the kind of individuals that we so openly let in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, just last month, Texas Governor Greg Abbott designated TDA, as the gang is known, as a foreign terrorist organization, and Texas law enforcement vowed to do everything in its power to crack down on gang members.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE MCCRAW\uc2 , \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2  DIRECTOR: Tren de Aragua, TDA, gangsters are like cockroaches. They multiply quickly. Small intrusions into communities become infestations if not aggressively pursued.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, both suspects in the murder, as you mentioned, Trace, entered the US illegally across our southern border.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And a lot of people are wondering, Jonathan, because you look at this in the totality and how many of these people are now in Aurora, Colorado, etcetera, is how they got in, how they got in unchecked because, because now they're starting to do some checks. As you said, some of those symbols are being checked.\par \par \uc2 So the big question is, over the past several years, how they kept getting in unchecked?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right. And more and more of them we're finding out about, and more we are finding out that they crossed illegally between ports of entry. There are -- Border Patrol agents are doing the best they can at those ports of entry. But it's in between as we know, it's open season.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Very quickly. Is there evidence that these members of Tren de Aragua in this country were actually in prisons in Venezuela before they were released?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: We do not have that information. We simply don't know the background of these particular two. But I'm sure investigators are looking into it very closely.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2 , Jonathan, thank you. Well, that is "The Story" of Thursday, October 3, 2024. But as always, "The Story" goes on. And I will see you back here for Fox News at Night, that's 8:00 in the West Coast, 11:00 in the East.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we are going to be talking to a man who actually voted to impeach former President Trump. But now he says he will vote former President Trump because he thinks what Democrats call the threat to democracy is actually worse now than it was then. I'm Trace Gallagher. I'll see you back here tonight. "Your World with Neil Cavuto" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241004eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Now, J.D. Vance In Battleground Michigan; Thousands Of Dockworkers Strike For Second Day; Israel Says, Retaliation Against Iran Will Be Painful; Walz And Vance Spar Over Abortion At VP Debate; 170 Dead, Hundreds Still Missing After Helene; Devastated North Carolina Communities In Need Of Food And Water; Breast Cancer Cases Rise As Deaths Decrease\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jeff Flock, Larry Kudlow, Trey Yingst, Tomi  Lahren, Steve Harrigan, Gerri Willis \par \uc2 9,204 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: It's because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her allies are trying to silence the American people rather than engage with them.\par \par \uc2 When you try to censor your fellow citizens, when you try to shut them up, you breed division and hatred. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I are going to bring this country back together by encouraging the free exchange of ideas and this idea that our leaders ought to persuade their fellow citizens, not try to shut them up, which is what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to do. Open this country up, open up the dialogue and open up the debate. And that is how you bring American citizens back together in these divided times.\par \par \uc2 But on the second question about what do you do when you disagree with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? The answer is, first of all, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is at the top of the Republican ticket. He will be the next president of the United States. I've got two responsibilities. I've got two responsibilities. The first is to support the president in enacting an agenda that I believe is going to lead to rising take home pay and lower inflation for American citizens. That's number one. But, of course, if I disagree with the president, it is my responsibility to talk to him about it and to offer honest counsel in private.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I love about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that he takes advice and feedback from everybody. As much as -- you know, it's funny when the president -- the only conversation that he and I ever had about whether I might become his vice presidential nominee was the Saturday before the convention. If you remember, that was the Saturday where he was shot in Bucks County, Pennsylvania. I saw him that morning in Florida. We ended our conversation. He hopped in a plane and flew to Pennsylvania where, of course, an assassin nearly took his life.\par \par \uc2 And you know what the president told me? He said that he talked to everybody about who his vice presidential nominee should be. And I think that's one of the great things is he's willing to take feedback. Of course, he makes the final decision, but he's willing to take inputs from everybody. And he even said that he was talking to the Mar-a-Lago gardener about who the vice presidential nominee should be. And I said, well, sir, what the hell did the Mar-a-Lago gardener say, because this affects my life? I'm very curious what these people you're talking to are saying about this very important decision.\par \par \uc2 But I think that it's one of the great things about his leadership style is he is in charge, he is in command, but he makes decisions after getting as much information as possible and that's how the country should be led.\par \par \uc2 You know, it actually raises an important question, you know, Robert, and I know, of course, the journalist who's asking this question, look, one of the things that people in D.C. say about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and it's Democrats that say this, it's people she's served with in the Senate, and it's also Republicans, and, of course, they say this in private, but they'll say \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is very insecure. She doesn't like to be challenged. If you don't like to be challenged and you don't like to engage in open debate, you should be nowhere near the Oval Office of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 Next question.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Thank you, Charlie Langton from Fox News, WBJ here.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Hi, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: I talked to a lot of people here. They also said you were very cordial last night in the debate. And the message got through. But compare your style to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's style, where he was a little aggressive. Do you think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's message sometimes is lost because of his aggressive style?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: No, Charlie. Look, I think, first of all, there is a media caricature out there of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that is so different from the man that I've come to know. And I think that, you know, when he called me last night, you know what he said? He asked me how my wife was doing. And he said, you know, you just had a hell of a debate, I'm sure that you were nervous. Yes, you had every right to be nervous, but go and have a nice meal with your wife and go and enjoy this moment. Like that's the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that I know.\par \par \uc2 People say -- now, look, the other thing I love about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I think it's true of all of us, we can be so devoted to and so grateful to this country and its incredible opportunities, but then also be kind of pissed off that the leadership is failing this great nation. There's no contract -- there's no tension in saying on the one hand, we've got the greatest country in the world, and on the other hand, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is doing everything she can to screw it up.\par \par \uc2 And I love that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can do both of those things at the same time. He can go out there and talk to the American people and be warm and engaged. He can talk to a normal American citizen about their struggles in paying for groceries and housing under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' policies.\par \par \uc2 And then he can go on a debate stage and tell \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that she ought to stop screwing up and she ought to stop talking about solving the country's problems, she ought to go and actually do it. That's the kind of president we need. That's the kind of leader that we need. And I think his message gets through. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Next question.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Hey, Senator, Craig Mauger from the Detroit News newspaper here. You were talking about your opposition to E.V. mandates. My question is, do you think the government should have any role in incentivizing, providing financial assistance to battery plants, to electric vehicle plants? Michigan is poised to benefit from some of these investments. Will you allow those to go forward? Will you try to interfere with those? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, look I think there's certainly a role, and I believe this, I know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes this, in encouraging innovation, but there's a difference between promoting innovation and sending hundreds of billions of dollars to favored industries that make their products in China. These are two very, very different things.\par \par \uc2 And here's -- the problem with a lot of these E.V. mandates is that this money was supposed to go to American workers, but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' \uc2 IRS\uc2  found every way possible to allow that E.V. subsidy to go to manufacturers in China and not in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 And here's the other problem with it. It's not just auto jobs that we're worried about here. Think about all the people who make carburetors. Think about all the people who make transmissions. There's an entire supply chain in the auto industry that China dominates when it comes to E.V.s and America dominates when it comes to gas powered cars.\par \par \uc2 So, before we go and subsidize the E.V. industry, let's open up the American supply chain for that industry so that American workers and American consumers benefit. All of the minerals, all of the rare earth materials, a lot of the battery technology, a lot of the supply chain for these electric vehicles is made in China.\par \par \uc2 And with the one hand, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to make it harder to build that supply chain in the United States of America. And with the other hand, she wants to send money to an industry that's built in China. It's disgraceful and we got to stop doing it. Invest in American workers. Not in Chinese workers and Chinese firms.\par \par \uc2 And I know we unfortunately have to go to our next stop in the great state of Michigan. So, I'll take one more question and then I'll offer a final observation and then we'll hit the road.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Hi, Senator, Will Jones from Local 4.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Hey, Will.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: I spoke with some elected officials or election officials last week about their concern about people who are trying to undermine our elections. Why didn't you answer the question last night during the debate about who won the 2020 presidential election?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, look, here's the simple reason. The media is obsessed with talking about the election of four years ago. I'm focused on the election of 33 days from now because I want to throw \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  out of office and get back to common sense economic policies.\par \par \uc2 But I also think you can believe that America needs to have secure and free elections, but also talk about the fact that just a couple of weeks ago, Democrats in the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  blocked a piece of legislation that would have ensured illegal aliens don't vote in our elections. If you believe in American democracy, if you believe in our constitutional republic, you should be trying to strengthen American election integrity and not weaken it.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to talk about election integrity because I believe that every vote ought to count, but only the legally cast votes. And that's why we fight for election integrity. And that's why we care so much about it.\par \par \uc2 Now --\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Very important place for the next 33 days, that's for sure, and that is Auburn Hills, Michigan. And you're watching the vice presidential GOP candidate speaking. J.D. Vance had a very good night last night on the debate stage and folks around the Trump team are feeling good about their pick in J.D. Vance and how he performed last night, certainly.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a lot going on in the country right now that whoever is president for the next four years is going to have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, President Biden has to deal with this situation. Thousands of dock workers are now on strike. It is day two. They're demanding very large pay hikes, and they want to ban on automation, as a battle between union workers and companies threatens to drive up prices for consumers who are going to end up being stuck in the middle.\par \par \uc2 This is the head of the union. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAROLD DAGGETT, PRESIDENT, ILA: People never gave a (BLEEP) about us until now, when they finally realized that the chain is being broke now. Cars won't come in, food won't come in, clothing won't come in. You know how many people depend on our jobs? Half the world. And it's time for them, and time for Washington to put so much pressure on them to take care of us, because we took care of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay, that is the message. \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  is standing by. First, let's go quickly to Fox Business correspondent Jeff Flock. He's at the Port of Newark in New Jersey, where it's a quite an active scene. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, hello to you. If you thought that was strident comments from the president of the International Longshoremen's Union. Well, it's backed up by what you see here behind me. I'll tell you, I've been through a lot of strikes in my time. These guys don't do it very often. The last one was 1977 when \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  was in a junior high, I think.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you when they do it, they seem to do it right and they do it loudly. And they are getting support in a lot of places, high places, like from the president and his administration, as well as, you know, surprisingly, to me, the truckers. Take a look at some pictures we have from earlier today. The truckers, you know, stand to be out of a job once the last of this freight gets delivered. If there's nothing coming in, they don't take anything anywhere. They don't make any money, but they had a big caravan out here today at the port of Newark.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of stop on the way, I leave you with a map that shows you how many ships are now making their way toward the ports that are now shut down by these folks, 120,000 -- 129 ships with thousands of containers aboard. I don't know what they're going to do. Some of them are already anchored offshore.\par \par \uc2 I leave the last word perhaps to these folks, as you mentioned, no automation. That's one of the key factors in this strike as well. In addition to the money, it sounds like a lot of money, but these guys say, you know, they work through the pandemic and the companies made hundreds of billions with a B dollars in profits, and they'd like to see some of that money,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jeff. Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now, as promised, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 , anchor of Kudlow on \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2 , which is coming up shortly. His upcoming interview with former President Trump is going to air this Friday on Kudlow. So, you don't want to miss that. Larry, it is great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha. Make great pleasure. Just one little addition, 1977, I was actually the chief economist of a very large brokerage firm in New York City.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed you are. And you're watching all this play out.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I was not in junior high. You were not in junior high.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He was just trying to make you very youthful at that time. But so, so I want to play this. This is another sound bite from Harold Daggett, who is the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  president. And here's what he's talking about in terms of what their demands are. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAGGETT: First week, be all over the news every night, boom, boom. Second week, guys who sell cars can't sell cars because the cars ain't coming in off the ships. They get laid off. Third week, malls start closing down. They can't get the goods from China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What are we headed for here, Larry?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Well, look, I want to say, I mean, Wall Street and I see editorials and newspapers, everyone's beating up on the labor union here. I happen to think that this 77 percent demand is too high. Over six years, that would be close to 13 percent increase in wages every year over six years. I happen to think that's too high. But they've been clobbered by the inflation of the past three and a half-plus years. As you know, cost of living up 20 percent, and when you get through individual groceries and insurance and other things could be 30, 40 percent. They've been clobbered by that.\par \par \uc2 And the company who runs all this, USMX, they have had $400 billion in profits in the last three years. Now, I love profits. Don't get me wrong. I'm a free market capitalist. I'm just saying that the union has a point here, an important point. We got clobbered on inflation. There's been an affordability crisis for years. We haven't had a raise. The company's making good money, okay. Good money is good money. Again, I love profits, but they have a point.\par \par \uc2 I think 77 percent is too high.\par \par \uc2 The offer from the company was 50 percent. That would be 8 percent a year. The thing is no one knows who's going to win this election right now. No one knows what the future will be for inflation and the cost of living and groceries and mortgage rates and car loan rates and insurance rates. No one knows. And you're seeing a bunch of unions around the country that strikes a lot of places.\par \par \uc2 Now, to some extent, I think Mr. Biden is to blame here. He's encouraging that. But I don't think that's as important as the fact that inflation is a huge tax and it's robbed them of kitchen table necessities, and you're seeing them fight back. The rhetoric coming out of the leadership, I know, but the rank and file have a right to come at this. They probably deserve a pretty good raise.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I was disappointed that there was no question on this last night because I think it's a question that's very reflective of how a candidate feels because they are stuck between the consumer and the union. So, I think it would have been really interesting to hear them speak on this.\par \par \uc2 And I know you have to go, but just very quickly, this issue of automation, Larry, they're fighting back against automation. Other international ports are highly automated.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, how do you -- this isn't the last time we're going to hear about this kind of question.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Now, there's a whole bunch of issues here. I'm not sure on how far -- there's going to be additional automation. You are right. Europe is much more automated. I believe Asia is too. The question is how fast and by what means and by what compensation, right? Because if you put people out of work and they've been there for 20, 30, 40 years, you got to pay them.\par \par \uc2 I'm not so -- look, the last time we had a dock strike -- I know I'm supposed to rap, but I'm going to say this. The last time we had a dock strike was not 1977. It was 2002. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was president, okay? It lasted 11 days, because more than that, you know, if you get into a week, two weeks, three weeks, four weeks, you're going to chop off GDP, you're going to really wreck the economy. The economy is not that sturdy in the first place and they'll take some actions.\par \par \uc2 But, nonetheless, vegetables are rotting. Equipment can't get in. We could lose a half a point of GDP depending on how far it goes. But I just want to say again, they have a point. Inflation has robbed them of a lot in the last three and a half years. They have a point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm glad you're here, Larry.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I'm supposed to take the Wall Street position and beat them up? No, I'm not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No. That's why I like talking to you, because I never know exactly where it's going to come down and I appreciate your opinion a lot. So, thank you for being here. And Larry's shows coming up on Fox Business at 4:00 four.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I have to run up and get there,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Run up and get there. Larry, thank you very much for stopping by. Good to see you, really good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden chose the line after vowing an iron clad backing of Israel and issuing a veiled threat of don't to Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Would you support an attack on Iran's nuclear sites by Israel?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: The answer is no. And I think there's things -- we'll be discussing with the Israelis what they're going to do, but then all seven of us agree that they have a right to respond, that they should respond in proportion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll talk about that with retired Four \uc2 Star Charge\uc2  General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , former commander of U.S. forces in the Middle East, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Back to the war in the Middle East, brand new video has been released of U.S. missile destroyers thwarting Iran's massive attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Immense power there. That's the USS Bulkeley. The world is waiting for what comes next in this high pressure conflict. Israel says that they are planning a painful response to the barrage of missiles that we saw, over 200 ballistic missiles, into Israel yesterday, President Biden is urging once again, a proportional response.\par \par \uc2 Retired Four Star General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , former commander of U.S. Forces in the Middle East, joins me in just a moment. First to Chief Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst, live in Haifa, Israel, with an exclusive first- time look inside Israel's military operations in Lebanon. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. As Israeli forces started their ground operation into southern Lebanon, we were with a commando unit that approached a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  position. Here's what we saw during that embed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Now, Israeli forces are conducting a ground invasion into Southern Lebanon. We're embedded with an elite commando unit that just arrived at this \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  position carved into the side of a mountain. It's in tunnels and rooms like this the Israelis say they are finding Iranian weapons that were smuggled first to Syria and then into Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 To get to this \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  position, we had to cross minefields and hike across an uneven terrain, pocked with craters, a result of the Israeli bombardment of Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 All of this began on October 8th, when \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  followed \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and started attacking Northern Israel in the hours after the October 7th massacre. Now, Israel says they are conducting these limited raids across the border to destroy these \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  positions and allow Israeli civilians to return to their homes in the northern part of the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Just to give you an idea of how sensitive this military embed was, we are waiting to air our full report from Southern Lebanon until next hour when those forces are out of this area. We know the Israeli ground and air campaign against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  continues. Hundreds of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters have been killed in the process along with hundreds of civilians. And tonight, we can report that eight Israeli soldiers were killed in battles against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  earlier today. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a very hot war situation. Thank you very much, Trey Yingst, for joining us and for that extraordinary reporting from inside the tunnels in Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired Four Star General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , the former commander of U.S. Forces in the Middle East, now executive director of the \uc2 University of South Florida\uc2 's Global and National Security Institute. And he is the author of The Melting Point. General McKenzie, great to have you with us. Thank you so much for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what goes through your mind as you listen Trey's report and you watch the events of the past couple of days where \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is under tremendous pressure, Iran is also under tremendous pressure, having lost major leadership, and now we are at one of those perhaps tipping points in the Middle East. What is your take?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET.), FORMER CENTCOM COMMANDER: Well, Martha, I think actually the people that are under the most stress are the Iranians. They've watched their principal ally, \uc2 Lebanese Hezbollah\uc2 , be decapitated. They've watched significant attrition of their military capabilities. And now the Israelis are methodically clearing north of the border so that their Israeli citizens can return to the northern tier of kibbutzes cities and villages in Northern Israel.\par \par \uc2 All of this is bad news for Iran. Last night, they launched a major -- a second major ballistic missile attack on Israel, by any objective measure, that attack too failed. So now Iran has really delivered a maximum effort against Israel. They can't count on \uc2 Lebanese Hezbollah\uc2  to assist them in further attacks because of the decapitation and the ongoing Israeli military operations up there.\par \par \uc2 So, the people whose backs are really against the wall right now waiting for the Israeli response is Iranian senior leadership. They're not in a good place right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Two questions on that. The first one is about \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and what has happened to them. And were you surprised that they were able to launch over 200 missiles? And I think we're still learning about potential casualties in Israel but an extraordinary situation where - - I mean, isn't it humiliating to them that none of these missiles or the majority of them were not able to penetrate or to do any damage, or significant damage?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I think it's very humiliating to them. The crown jewels of Iran actually are its ballistic missiles, its drones and its land attack cruise missiles. Now, in the attack of last night, they chose not to use drones or land attack cruise missiles. My theory would be in order to gain surprise against Israel because of the 12 to 14-minute time of flight. However, we're able to discern when the Iranians begin to deploy their missiles. We know ahead of time it's very difficult for Iran to gain surprise when they bring their missile force into the field.\par \par \uc2 So we were able to respond. The Israelis did a very good job. We certainly assisted them. And I agree with you. I think that attack was largely ineffective, and it's got to be profoundly embarrassing to the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 But one last point I'd make, Martha. The Iranians have a boundless ability to lie to themselves within their own chain of command about how successful or unsuccessful their attacks are. So, you don't know -- what we would want to know is how is that attack actually being reported to the supreme leader in Tehran? We might be surprised at what he's learning about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, he's got to be a little nervous about his own future at this point because he watched the leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  be taken out in Tehran in a building there when he was at a ceremony and then Nasrallah, and he has to be wondering if his days are numbered as well.\par \par \uc2 With regard to what President Biden said about discouraging an Israeli attack on nuclear sites within Iran, I mean, that is the ultimate threat that faces Israel. And do they see this as a window that cannot be missed to mitigate that threat and to try to take out those sites, General?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, Martha, I probably know as much about the Iranian nuclear program and the Iranian nuclear target as anybody in the world. That's a very large order to take out that program. If the Israelis wanted to do it, it would require vast expenditure of resources. It wouldn't be able to be done very quickly. It would be a long, hard slog. Can they do it? Possibly.\par \par \uc2 I would think actually, just speaking as a military observer, there are other more significant targets that you might want to undertake 1st, as you calibrate your response to an attack against Israel that caused very few casualties and failed, and actually had the effect, as I've noted earlier, of pushing Iran further and further against the wall and into a very lonely corner.\par \par \uc2 So, the nuclear program is certainly a target. There are other targets they could undertake. They might even choose to use restraint, although I would note that the restraint they used after the 13 April attack on Israel by Iran, Israelis fought back a couple of nights later with a very carefully sculpted, technologically impressive small attack that didn't prevent the Iranians from coming back with the big attack of yesterday.\par \par \uc2 So, the Israelis have probably learned that lesson. So, I suspect the Israeli response will be larger, probably much larger than what we saw in their response in April as to who is going to go against all of the initiative, and I mean, this absolutely all of the initiative is with Israel right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: General McKenzie, you're a wealth of information and have spent so much time in the region, and we're going to have you on speed dial, I think, over the next several weeks. And we so appreciate you spending time with us, sir. Thank you very much, general.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Harris and Walz ahead on the issue of abortion, but they seem to be peddling some fear in some cases when it comes to where the Trump- Vance ticket actually stands. There's a lot of information that gets thrown out there and we're going to do some fact checking on it, digging in with Tomi Lahren and Patrick Murphy next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: You talk about his moderate position, his moderate position has been adopted over the course of this campaign, for purposes of this campaign, after he put the justices on the Supreme Court who overturned Roe, who have created a crisis for women who live in 20 states in this country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENNINGS, CNN CONTRIBUTOR: His position is moderate, and it is in line with what I think most America.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: Do you believe him when he said -- when he said that women should be --\par \par \uc2 JENNINGS: And I think Harris and Walz have proven they cannot discuss even a single restriction that they want.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Now, of course, they have said time and time and time again they want a national ban on abortion. So as this comes down to the final days, let's be very clear where we're headed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We're going to break this down because they have not said that they want a national ban. Former President Trump debunked that claim during last night's vice presidential debate, saying that not only does he not support a national ban on abortion, he has not ever supported that. But said that he would veto a federal abortion ban if it came to his desk. He said, under any circumstances.\par \par \uc2 So here is how the hot button issue played out last night on both sides. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM WALZ (D), MINNESOTA GOVERNOR AND VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: Their Project 2025 is going to have a registry of pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O'DONNELL, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: Will you create a federal pregnancy monitoring agency?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: No, Norah. Certainly we won't. And I'll statute that you signed into law. It says that a doctor, who presides over an abortion where the baby survives, the doctor is under no obligation to provide life saving care to a baby who survives a botched late term abortion. that is, I think, whether it's not pro-choice or pro- abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. That was an interesting moment there, because there's not a lot of discussion about restriction or where a restriction should be in some of these states, including Minnesota. So here are the facts. Minnesota's abortion law does not limit the abortion procedure at any stage, all the way up to nine months. It does not limit it in terms of accessibility. And as for Project 2025, there is no mention of a pregnancy registry.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump has disavowed \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 's 900 plus page document. He says he has not read it. And there is a Republican platform that is quite clear on the issue of abortion. Democrats have their platform, which is also quite clear on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Tomi Lahren, host of Tomi Lahren is fearless on OutKick and Fox News Contributor and Patrick Murphy, former Pennsylvania congressman and the first Iraq veteran in Congress. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Patrick, why does \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  keep saying that they want a national ban? We heard the same thing from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and President Trump has not supported a national ban. He supported the Supreme Court to make a decision, and they made that decision, and he was supportive of that.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY (D), FORMER PENNSYLVANIA CONGRESSMAN: I think, Martha, because I think people are frustrated because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in this case, says one thing and does another. I mean, he's the one that said publicly on air that women should be punished if they have an abortion. He's the one that said, hey, I'm going to nominate the Supreme Court justices who testified under oath that the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , their confirmation hearing saying, hey, Roe v Wade has set a law and then, within years, overturned Roe v Wade in a Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 And these are tough decisions. You know, I'm Catholic, Martha, but, you know, I think that's what people are frustrated about, when people say one thing and do another. And as far as disavowing that, prior to 2025, I mean, JD Vance wrote the forward to it as, you know, and, you know, you just can act like, you know, you're an ostrich and buried your head in the sand and act like you don't know what's going on. That's the blueprint.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, what's quite clear, Tomi, is that the Supreme Court, of course, he picked conservative judges because he's a conservative. Democrat president picked judges that they think will uphold their view of the Supreme Court, and we've seen how it plays out. All the states make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, JD Vance pointed out last night, he's also Catholic and he said, you know, I live in Ohio but I -- and -- but Ohio did not go the way I wanted it to go, he said, in a referendum vote. It was a more broad interpretation of the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 But, Tomi, Democrats can't -- they can't let this issue slip away, right? I mean, they have to make sure that people fear this kind of national ban is a huge part of what they are running on.\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: And some would argue, including myself, that it's one of the only things that they are able to run on because of the list of things that are important to voters, this is the only one where they have a substantial lead. But I think that Senator JD Vance did an excellent job last night of calming fears, because there were many swing voters that were watching that, particularly women who would like to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for economic purposes, for immigration purposes, but they were a little weary of the abortion question and issue.\par \par \uc2 But JD Vance effectively disarmed all of those arguments by making it explicitly clear that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would not sign a national abortion ban. He made that very clear. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  followed that up by making it clear on social media. So now the left, of course, is going to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would sign it, because that's all that they have in the toolbox.\par \par \uc2 But I think it was very effective for JD Vance to acknowledge that the Republican Party has had an issue messaging on this particular topic, and he understands that frustration. But he called fears last night and he did it in a compassionate way. A lot of Republicans have been unable to do that. He did it wonderfully.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He said, you know, that Republicans have to be better at talking about this issue. Patrick, so you, I mean, we're clear on the fact that this decision, put it back to the states to decide, and they have been doing that. And they will continue to do that, the people closest to the issue in their own states.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: which is a frustrating thing, Martha, because he says, I'm not for a national band. But yet, more than one in three American women right now don't have reproductive choice anymore. That is a ban.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Governor Walz was so eloquent last night when he talked about Amanda (ph) and Nicole (ph) and Amber (ph). Amanda (ph) and Nicole (ph) almost died. Amber literally died because of this. I mean, this is why people are fired up.\par \par \uc2 And JD Vance -- and God bless him, he talks about we have to earn their trust. Well, they stop taking away their rights. As you know, under our system of government, we have federalism, meaning you have minimal rights under the Constitution and the Bill of Rights, minimal rights. States can give you more rights if they want. He is chipping away and chipping away at that, and that's why people --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But every time we've seen referendums, Patrick.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: -- are frustrated with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We've seen referendums in states, and they have -- majority of them have gone towards preserving abortion rights in states. So, Tomi, when people have the right to decide in their states, they are because of the issues that you raised and the way a lot of women feel in this country, they are preserving those rights.\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: Right. Empower the voters to make those decisions. And you might not agree with it, some of these decisions are going to come down and conservatives are going to be angry, and some liberals are going to be angry.\par \par \uc2 But I'd also like to point out that a lot of states have restrictions on things, legislation, regulations that some folks might not like. I had to leave the state of California because I didn't like the way voters in that state decided on a number of issues, so I left and I came to Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 So the voters ultimately have to decide. Not everybody is going to be happy, but that is up for the voters to decide. And if national opinion really is trending towards a more pro-choice attitude, then the states will reflect that. Let it play out and, again, stop fear mongering on a national abortion ban, because that's simply not the case and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and JD Vance could not have been clearer last night on that issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, one of the things, Patrick, that we never get when we're asked -- when the candidates are asked this is, what restriction would you approve of as a Democrat? You know is, is it -- is nine months your, you know, the line that you think should be upheld, or is there any restriction at all that Democrats --\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, and that's why I should codify it under Roe v Wade, under federal law, which means that women have a right to choose their reproductive freedoms when -- until the baby is viable, right? Until the fetus is viable, right? So you have that right now.\par \par \uc2 They took that away. They gave it to the states. And you have states like Alabama, as you know, banned IVF, right? So that's why I think people are confused. And they're confused because they're like, wait time out. I thought the Republican Party used to be a limited government, less government type of organization. You can't pick and choose what part of the Constitution --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, it's a state's issue, that's why. It's a state's issue. I mean, that's the court decided.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Women have a right to choose in America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. And they can vote in their state the way that they feel and over -- upholding the vote, upholding those rules in their states.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: And that's why they don't trust \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Martha. That's why they don't trust --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Inaudible) an opportunity to vote. I just want everybody on both sides to understand the parameters of what we're discussing in a clearly presented way. So Patrick Murphy, Tomi Lahren, thank you both. Good to have you both here today.\par \par \uc2 So President Biden and Vice President Harris deploying to the southeast after being pressed on by some on a lack of leadership as they saw it, in response to the devastating Hurricane Helene, which is another issue that we really heard almost nothing about last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the hurricane, Mr. President, why weren't you and Vice President Harris here in Washington commanding this, this weekend?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I was commanding. I was on the phone for at least two hours yesterday and the day before as well. I command on a telephone, called my security --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it not important for the country to see?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Republican Congressman Cory Mills, who is on a rescue mission in North Carolina, there are still hundreds of people, by most estimates, who are missing in this situation. It's a terrifying situation unfolding on our soil, and he says that FEMA, so far, is nowhere to be found. We'll talk to him next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden en route to North Carolina right now after getting a bird's eye view of the destruction that has been left by Hurricane Helene. Soon, Vice President Harris will also be there to survey the damage in Georgia. The death toll is now topping 170 people as the long journey to recover those who are still missing, which is said to be potentially in the hundreds, and then to rebuild. Just beginning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have people on oxygen that are completely out now. We have babies up there with no formula, nothing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is like a Katrina on steroids.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We've lost friends. There's been landslides. The day that happened, it was so bad. Our children thought we were dead. And we had to hang on to boulders, trees and go through Roy Creek to get out but we're alive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We tried to find our friends, with (inaudible). We don't know --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- if they're dead or alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just absolutely heartbreaking. I mean, these people are going through the most horrific moments in their lives, and thank God those two people survived, hanging onto boulders in a mudslide. In moments, I'll speak with Florida Congressman Cory Mills. He is once again on the front lines of this disaster. He does this all the time when needed. First to senior correspondent Steve Harrigan in Maggie Valley, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 What a heartbreaking story we just heard, Steve. And I know there are many, many more of them out there.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: All across Western North Carolina, Martha, and because many communities like this one are still cut off one weekend, that death toll stands at 179. But people here say it's going to keep going up. That's because the only way to find out if someone is dead or alive here, someone has to go by foot door to door.\par \par \uc2 They use walkie-talkies here to communicate, hand radios to get news, and everything is paid in cash. And when you talk to people and say, what help have you gotten in the past week from any outside agency, most often, the answer is nothing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're not getting the amount of support that we need right now. There are way more people impacted, way more people missing, definitely -- unfortunately, way more people that are going to be found dead in the coming days as far as I believe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: And drinking water is the big challenge. Many of the wells here have been contaminated, and they can't reopen the schools until there's running water that could be weeks away. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Incredible. Steve, thank you very much for your coverage. Joining me now from Asheville, North Carolina is Florida Congressman Cory Mills. He's been on the ground there for days, helping to deliver much needed supplies to those hard hit areas and evacuate some who are stranded. As you remember, he helped rescue Americans from warzones in Afghanistan, also Israel and in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 Cory Mills, congressman, welcome. Thank you for what you do for people in these situations. Tell us what you're observing there and what we all need to understand.\par \par \uc2 REP. CORY MILLS (R-FL): (Inaudible) getting the necessary needs in here. That was that, you know, the people were wanting. And so, we've gone into - - we delivered now about five tons worth of supplies, about roughly 10,000. We've actually transported about 12,000 pounds between the two helicopters that we brought in (inaudible) which is only places you can get in is by rotary-wing helicopter.\par \par \uc2 So we've gone into Broad River, South Toe. We've gone into Banner Elk, Asheville, Burnsville. We've gone into Holler (ph). We've continued to try and go to at least five or six different locations, either conducting search and rescue operations to drop star, to do health and welfare checks on family members who's gone missing, or even just doing distribution for Starlink systems so that we can get our missing persons report down.\par \par \uc2 Because a lot of this people just haven't heard from their family members for two and three and four days. We were able to successfully evacuate someone from Bailey, North Carolina to Johnsonville yesterday, 83-year-old man yesterday was his birthday, showing signs of heart attack or hypertension. So we're able to medevac him and get him out, but also delivering insulin oxygen bottles.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people, it's not just food and water and non perishables, but it's medications that they're short on. So trying to fulfill those needs until the federal government can get up and do what they need to be doing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, congressman, we missed -- you got cut off a little bit at the top. With, you know, are -- you're working with your private organization that you're part of, and what do you see in terms of federal help happening? Tell us the difference.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: So that's really where the missing pieces of the federal piece. And again, let's go ahead and point out the obvious, which is that FEMA had utilized a large majority of the personnel who should be here taking care of this, to be set and set -- replaced or repurposed, if you will, for immigration resettlement, when we need to be focusing on Americans and what's going on, whether it be in Maui Palestine, Ohio, Florida, North Carolina or Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 But I'm actually working with great organizations like Mercury One, charity for Glenn Beck, Universal Air, as well as for Sentinel Foundation and so many others. This is a group project where Americans are coming together to answer the call for Americans when the federal government fails to act.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're hearing one of the most horrifying details is that, it could be hundreds of people who are still missing. And we don't know if they are dead or alive, or if they're hanging onto a boulder like that woman just described, which was so heartbreaking. What is your sense? Do you have any sense of that and that number?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Well, again, we've got about an estimate of around a non-confirmed report of roughly 1,000 to 1,200 people still being listed as a missing person. A lot of that, I will tell you, as we get Starlink in place, and there's so many units here that's allowing individuals to start reaching out to their family members in other states, I think that missing person report will come down, and then we'll be able to really conceive (inaudible) find (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, as you can tell, it's tough to maintain communication, and obviously it's easy to understand why. I just wanted to give him an extra second there to see if maybe we could get Congressman Mills back. I give him so much credit, because he leaves his job and he goes into these places, in shoppers.\par \par \uc2 He's fearless. He went into Haiti, in the worst of circumstances, into Ukraine. And he is part of these teams of, in many cases, former veterans who want to continue to help people, because that's what they like to do, and they want to be where they are needed.\par \par \uc2 And it's an extraordinary story of American heroism. Of course, you know, he doesn't want to be -- take credit for that but I do think that it's -- I just want people to understand the selflessness. And I have covered these hurricanes and difficult situations in the past.\par \par \uc2 I'm always amazed by the people who show up with, you know, huge campers and just make barbecue for everybody on this -- on who's there, you know, the first responders. Just people who literally leave their lives and go to where they are needed and help out. And it's very moving and very extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 So I ask all of you to, you know, look up these organizations, whether you want to do Red Cross, which Fox is doing, of course. And Congressman Mills just named a bunch of other organizations, Sentinel was one of them that, that you, you know, feel the best about, that you believe was is going to get your money where it needs to go. And these people also need time and volunteers, so what an effort.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue to cover this story very closely. And our thanks to Congressman Mills, he's back at work, no doubt, doing what he's there for.\par \par \uc2 So we want to bring you this as well, an alarming new health trend that is emerging as we mark Breast Cancer Awareness Month. The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  says that deaths from breast cancer have declined, but warns that new diagnoses are steadily rising, especially among women under 50, leading to both a physical and financial toll. Fox Business Correspondent and breast cancer survivor Gerri Willis is with me now.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, you have documented, you know, very graciously and publicly, your own battle with cancer, which I think has enlightened so many people. What did you learn in this work that you're doing now?\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, let me just say that study you just referenced shows that 1.4 percent more women were diagnosed with breast cancer who are under 50 this past year than the previous year. That sounds like nothing, but let me tell you, it's the biggest increase they've ever seen. So the numbers are really surprising.\par \par \uc2 The thing that we're learning about right now is that there's a financial burden that goes with cancer, and guess what? It doesn't only create economic hardship, it also results in worse outcomes for those in treatment. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY\uc2  Those individuals will have effects of their cancer therapy that they carry with them throughout their entire life, not only a different risk of cancer but also financial implications. Someone who has had a cancer diagnosis will, over their lifetime, have a much higher cost of cancer of -- of health care than someone who has not had a cancer diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Three in five people who are diagnosed with cancer are faced with financial hardships like taking unpaid leave, losing health insurance or even losing a job. Simple things like traveling for treatment can make care unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 Sharon, pardon me, Shannon Barrett was recently declared free of cancer. She was treated in New York City, right here. She stayed at Hope Lodge, that's a facility that the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  offers free to patients. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BARRETT, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: Women need to focus on their treatment and their recovery and the medicine. We need a community to help every breast cancer survivor get the treatment they need, and they are entitled to world class medicine. And that should not be held back because of any kind of financial burden.\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Look, bottom line, doing what you can to make sure your wallet isn't devastated by cancer means getting screened. Treatment costs increase with the severity of the diagnosis. One year treatment costs for a woman diagnosed with stage one cancer is $5,700. But if the diagnosis is stage two, that cost increases 17 times.\par \par \uc2 Look, the best way to protect yourself, get tested. It is October Breast Cancer Awareness Month, get your mammogram.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I will be doing that this month. As you know, we have a history of breast cancer in my family, and there are so many great organizations out there. I would just name the Connie Dwyer Breast Cancer Foundation, which helps people who need help with financing these treatments. There's a lot of help out there for people who need it and thank you so much for this reporting --\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Thank you, Martha, for always --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Crosstalk) this week, all right. So that is "The Story" for this Wednesday. Thank you very much, Gerri. "The Story" goes on as you know, so I look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow. Thanks for sharing your afternoon with me. And "Your World" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241003eka200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Gov. \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  (R-VA)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,100 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Honored to have with us right now Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  of the beautiful state of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 To show you how prescient the governor was, it was back on September 26 -- and, Governor, correct me if I`m wrong -- that you had written a letter to President Biden and Vice President Harris, urging them to do everything they could to prevent the longshoremen`s union strike from happening, and that would require swift action and a swift resolution to avoid a strike.\par \par \uc2 That didn`t happen. So what do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R-VA): No. No, it didn`t.\par \par \uc2 And it`s now causing real economic heartache and damage, not just in Virginia, but across the whole country. And, of course, we have anywhere between 5,000 and 6,000 port workers, plus truckers who are now not working.\par \par \uc2 We have 60 vessels off the coast that aren`t unloading or picking up. And that`s just Virginia. And the economic devastation that this strike can cause is $4 billion to $5 billion a day, and as you have just heard, up to a week of supply chain interruption for every day of striking.\par \par \uc2 Let me just put that into context. The critical supply chains that are being interrupted, everything from pharmaceuticals to food to the supplies for our manufacturing industry, are seeing a potential disruption that we haven`t seen since the COVID outbreak.\par \par \uc2 This is real. We`re going to see inflation as a result of it. And we`re going to see Americans, and particularly in the Southeast, further hurt by this strike, when in fact we`re already suffering from the dramatic impacts of Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  can fix this. They should have fixed it ahead of time. We in fact finished our negotiations at the local level between our local workers and our local shippers back in the summertime. There was no progress made at the national level over the course of the summer into the fall.\par \par \uc2 They should have convened everybody and kept them in a room until this was resolved. They now have authority under Taft-Hartley to get everybody back to work and have a cooling-off period and then to reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 But we cannot have this kind of supply chain interruption in Virginia, on the East Coast and Gulf Coast or for America. And it is going to be economically devastating if they don`t get it resolved quickly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it doesn`t look like it could be.\par \par \uc2 That Taft-Hartley, that`s an act, a 1947 act that is meant to address crises like these. And it gives both sides about 80 days in this case to sort out.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... whatever differences there might be and force workers back to the ports during that time to continue working as they`re presumably working on a settlement.\par \par \uc2 But the back-and-forth on this is that this is going to add, the price pressure is going to add to the possibility of folks getting packages delayed, if not denied. Even Santa Claus is upset about this, I understand, Governor, because a lot of his stuff is on those ships. So where does this go?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yes, well, first of all, it is a leadership gut check for the president and the vice president.\par \par \uc2 And if Biden and Harris can`t get together and get this done, I think it`s just another moment for Americans to see that failure in leadership does cause chaos. We have seen failure in leadership in building an economy and they unleashed inflation everyone.\par \par \uc2 We have seen failure in leadership at the border that has turned every state into a border state. We have seen failure in leadership internationally, which has resulted in Iran attacking Israel yesterday, chaos in China and Taiwan, and, of course, war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And now we`re seeing failure in leadership unleashing further economic hardship on America that is so unnecessary. It`s time to lead. And we need the Biden/Harris administration to step up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Governor, I`d be remiss about it and talk about the devastation your state has experienced from Hurricane Helene, storm Helene, whatever you want to call it.\par \par \uc2 It`s pretty substantial. I believe earlier you had said a number of communities have simply washed away. What`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yes, so, of course, it has been absolutely devastating to the entire Southeast and in Southwest Virginia particularly.\par \par \uc2 We had tragic life lost with two Virginians, but we had 70 heroic rescues by our first responders. Today, we are still working hard to get electricity restored, cell phone service record -- restored food and water to everyone, and to make sure that people have the basic necessities of a warm place to sleep, a warm meal, a shower, and, of course, access to all the medical care that they need.\par \par \uc2 This is a big step. We were granted an emergency declaration from the federal government over the weekend and then a major emergency declaration late yesterday into this morning. And so between our state resources and the federal resources and the incredible efforts at the local level, we are doing everything we can to help people recover.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be a long recovery. Our hearts are breaking for what we`re seeing in North Carolina and Georgia particularly, as I know there are so many people that are still yet identified. Our search-and-rescue teams are heading out to help, but this is a massive, massive storm that caused intense damage.\par \par \uc2 And this is a time for us to come together, just like Virginians have, in order to help one another.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: The character of a community is shown during times of duress, and the character of Virginians has really brought me to my knees in thankfulness for what they have demonstrated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor Youngkin, very good having you on. Thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 To the governor`s point, 170 people are dead, many more missing.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020241002eka20008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Iran Fires At Least 180 Missiles Into Israel; U.S. Role In Protecting Israel From Iran Attack; \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Is Being Interviewed About The Middle East Escalation; Workers Demand Higher Wages; White House Ponders Consequences For Attack On Israel; Worker Strike Impact On Economy; Former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  Turns 100; Vice Presidential Debate Between Walz And Vance\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Mike Tobin, Gillian Turner, Jennifer Griffin, Grady  Trimble, Larry Kudlow, Peter Doocy, Steve Harrigan, Aishah Hasnie, Jacqui  Heinrich, Martha MacCallum, Harold Ford Jr., Bill Hemmer \par \uc2 7,948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, the singing, the singing to the show. I like it.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier from Fox News World Headquarters in New York. A big show tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, rescue crews in North Carolina continue to search through -- sort through the catastrophe and the damage in search of residents who may still be stranded in the aftermath of Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 Analysts warn the damaging economic impacts throughout the country from this massive port strike that are happening right now across the east coast in the Gulf of Mexico, we'll examine that.\par \par \uc2 And the vice presidential nominees prepare to go head to head, Republican J.D. Vance, Democrat \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , will make their case for their campaigns on the national debate stage in just three hours. You can see it here.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, Iran escalates aggression in the Middle East, targeting all parts of Israel in the largest ballistic missile attack in history. Iranian officials tonight warn if Israel retaliates, Tehran's response would be even more crushing, and that if the U.S. assists American bases in the Middle East will become targets.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  says Iran made a big mistake tonight, and "It will pay for it." Defense officials here in the U.S. say \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  guided missile destroyers have already helped the Israelis, also blocked some of those incoming Iranian missiles, and that forces are prepared to provide additional defensive support.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight. Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon with the U.S. response to the Iranian threats.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin though starts us off in Haifa, at one of the sites targeted in the Iranian missile attacks. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Bret, in the aftermath of this unprecedented missile attack, Israeli leaders are ignoring warnings about escalation. Defense Minister Yoav Gallant said, Iran has not learned a lesson. Those who attack Israel pay a heavy price.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Sirens sound with explosions in the Sky. Iran attacking Israel directly with at least 180 missiles, with a combination of Israeli defenses and allies in the region, most were intercepted. A few penetrated the center of the country.\par \par \uc2 Israelis scrambled for cover. The cycle of violence is at full speed.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): Iran made a grave mistake this evening, and it will pay for it.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Iran's leader tweeted in Hebrew, the blows of the uprising front will become stronger and more painful to the worn and rotting body of the Zionist regime.\par \par \uc2 Shortly before the rocket attacks, an attack from within, a mass shooting in Jaffa, the old city near Tel Aviv. Two attackers, at least one M16 style rifle and a knife. They killed seven in cold blood, wounded at least eight according to police, the attackers were killed.\par \par \uc2 This after Israeli forces handed Iran and its proxies brutal defeats and humiliations, from the sabotaged exploding electronics to the assassinations of enemies like \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  political chief \uc2 Ismail Haniyeh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Israel operates at will after a brief incursion into South Lebanon last night, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  revealed that special forces have been making raids into Lebanon since November, 70 secret raids. They've been in Lebanon for 200 nights, unchallenged by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's feared Radwan Forces. From warrants of tunnels on the Lebanese border, Israel showed a sample of anti-tank missiles, machine guns, mortars, IEDs and rifles, all of it staged and ready for an October 7th style attack from the north.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. NADAV SHOSHANI, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2 : This plan was ready, according to our intelligence, to be activated within a few hours, six hours from their decision to activate this plan storming Israel with thousands of Radwan is the special forces of their terror organization.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Amid all this rising tension, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  has sent messages into the southern suburbs of Beirut telling people to evacuate, something they do before more airstrikes come in, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin live in Haifa. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials, meantime, say U.S. forces worked successfully alongside Israel to defend against Iran's attack. Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon live tonight with the latest. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The Pentagon saw signs that Iran was preparing for a ballistic missile attack when it began moving missile launchers following the killing of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  last Friday. President Biden said there was intensive planning between the U.S. and Israel as they anticipated an attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The attack appears to have been defeated and ineffective, and this is testament to Israeli military capability and U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake, the United States is fully, fully, fully supportive of Israel.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): All of the nearly 200 ballistic missiles were fired from Iranian territory.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: This is totally unacceptable, and the entire world should condemn it.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The Pentagon says two US warships in the eastern Mediterranean, the USS Cole and USS Bulkeley, fired a dozen missile interceptors in defense of Israel. It's notable that the Cole was attacked by Al-Qaeda terrorists, with suspected help from Iran at a port in Yemen 24 years ago, a precursor to 9/11. The Pentagon and State Department said the U.S. was not given a heads up by Iran in advance of today's attack.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PAT RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It's something that we've been monitoring. Certainly, this was a significant attack, probably about twice the size in terms of scope of what we saw earlier.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): A U.S. official tells Fox News, Iran has been at a state of readiness to launch an attack on Israel within 12 to 24 hours since early August, following the July 31st assassination of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader \uc2 Ismail Haniyeh\uc2  in Tehran, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: This is a significant escalation by Iran a significant event, and it is equally significant that we were able to step up with Israel and create a situation in which no one was killed.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: We're still playing defense with these guys. It makes no sense whatsoever. 200 ballistic missiles is significant, and I don't see how the Israelis can ignore that and just go punch back lightly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Joining us tonight to talk about the situation in the Middle East, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , she's former presidential candidate, former U.S. ambassador to the \uc2 U.N.\uc2 , former South Carolina governor, and host of a self-titled podcast, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Live. Thanks so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 , FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N.\uc2 : Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really consequential day, if you think about it, for Israel and what happens next. How do you see this?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: First of all, very different than the Iranian strike that we saw in April. Think about basically how that happened.\par \par \uc2 In April, Iran did strikes, first time they ever did it directly, but they did drones first, which was very much giving notice to everybody that this was getting ready to happen. Didn't really want any damage, just wanted to send a signal.\par \par \uc2 This time, well over 150 ballistic missiles, no warning, only 12 minutes for them to get there. Iran wanted this to hurt. Let's be clear, they've been caught flat footed, for a long time they said, these are their proxies. They said that they had nothing to do with it, but these are all mobsters, whether it's the Houthis, whether it's \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , whether it's \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , whether it's what's happening in Syria or Iraq, and the head mob boss is Iran, and Iran is flat footed, and right now they're trying to play in any way that they can.\par \par \uc2 But don't -- make no mistake, they're not encouraged by this. This was reactionary, and they're just trying to stop the bleeding.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've dealt with the Israelis a lot. You dealt with them here at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  If you were to guess what they do next, what do you think it would be?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: They're not going to stop, and they shouldn't stop. I think what you're seeing is, you know, it's funny to see Jake Sullivan or even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  say, oh, we were with Israel the whole way. No, Israel did this in spite of them. Israel was tired of them saying, pull back. They were tired of saying, deescalate. They were tired of everything else.\par \par \uc2 They saw what \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is getting ready to do, and they knocked him out before they had a chance to do it. Now, the U.S. is stepping up to be beside them. But make no mistake, Israel does not trust the U.S. Israel does not trust Biden and Harris because they haven't been there.\par \par \uc2 What I think is going to happen next is Israel is going to continue to make sure that the North is safe enough for the 60,000 Israelis to come back. You're going to see them start to hit the Houthis over in Yemen. They look at this. This is the time to go ahead and take out all the threats while they can.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting, the Secretary of State, \uc2 Anthony Blinken\uc2 , had an op-ed published today in Foreign Affairs saying America's strategy of renewal, the Biden administration's strategy has put the United States in a much stronger geopolitical position today than it was four years ago.\par \par \uc2 Can you definitively say that's not true? And I know you endorsed a former president. Can you definitively say that their policy would be better for the Middle East than the Biden-Harris policy?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Blinken knows it's not true, but Harris and Biden need to know it's not true. They are the reason that this all happened from the very beginning, going to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at it, they haven't even been a good friend to Israel. They've constantly lectured them. They've constantly told them what they can and can't do. They've done the same thing with Ukraine, not letting Ukraine use the missiles to go into Russia. All of these things have led to distrust with our friends.\par \par \uc2 And so, you look at the time during Trump, there were no wars. There were no conflicts, and the reason is, at least our allies knew where we stood. With Biden and Harris, they never know where we stand.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned the April attacks, and before that, there was a lot of warning from the administration with one word, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have one word, don't.\par \par \uc2 Don't, don't, don't.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's the message to Iran?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Don't.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Israel may be now launching a limited operation into Lebanon. Are you aware of that? Are you comfortable with their plans?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm more aware than you might know, and I'm comfortable with them stopping. We should have a cease fire now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was the message recently, but since then, you know, all the don'ts, since that happened, the Iranians launched that barrage on Israel in April, accelerated uranium enrichment program. They charged -- they charged with hacking the U.S. presidential election, both campaigns, and then sending information from the Trump campaign to the Biden-Harris campaign, and then U.S. Intel revealing this Iranian plot to assassinate the former president, let alone other officials in the former administration.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Which I was one of them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So, how do you see that geopolitically? You know, and is this a campaign issue that you think the former president is going to hit on?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: This is a massive campaign issue. And first of all, it's not just Iran doing that. Look at the aggression coming from Russia, from China, from North Korea. All of them know that the U.S. is weak, and that's playing out.\par \par \uc2 But what else did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  do? While he said, don't to our -- you know, to our allies on what they could and couldn't do, and he said the words, don't to Iran. He lifted the sanctions on Iran. He allowed the money to flow. He basically continued to dictate to our allies what they couldn't, couldn't do. I'm glad Israel did. I'm glad Israel went forward and said, enough with Biden and Harris. We're going to protect ourselves, because we can't count on them to help us do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, politics, there were a lot of Republicans who looked to you and said, maybe that would have been a good choice for V.P.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, J.D. Vance steps into head to head. What advice would you give him? You've been critical of some of the things he said in the past. What would you tell him tonight?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Keep your eye on the ball. Know who your audience is. Your audience is not the Republican primary base. Your audience is suburban women, college, educated, independents and conservative Democrats. They are the ones that are going to side this general election. That's who you focus on for the next 35 days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you very much for coming in. We'll check out the podcast.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Almost half of the U.S. ocean shipping operations have halted tonight. This is a huge economic story. Port workers are demanding increased wages as this importers warn that despite -- this dispute will increase prices for consumers across the board.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Grady Trimble reports from the Port of Baltimore on where the negotiations stand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): From Texas to Maryland and New Jersey to Maine, a massive and potentially costly strike is on as 45,000 workers at three dozen east and Gulf Coast ports walk off the job.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  is demanding higher pay and less reliance on automated machinery. The union's fiery president fully aware of the havoc the walkout is causing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People never gave a (EXPLETIVE DELETED) about us until now. Cars won't come in, food won't come in, clothing won't come in. You know how many people depend on our jobs? Half the world.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and other critics are pointing out Daggett's union salary is more than $700,000 a year, according to a Department of Labor filing from 2023.\par \par \uc2 The two sides don't appear to be at the bargaining table today, industry groups for retailers, manufacturers and wholesalers are calling on the Biden-Harris administration to reverse course and invoke the Taft-Hartley Act, forcing workers back to their jobs while negotiations take place.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOPLIN, PRESIDENT AND CEO, WHOLESALER-DISTRIBUTORS ASSOCIATION: As we're getting this close to the election, it sounds like they don't want to make their friends in big labor unhappy, but in the meantime, the rest of America is going to be unhappy.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): Former Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , a Republican who has the local union's endorsement in his Senate race, standing with picketing workers Tuesday, refusing to criticize the administration.\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 , FORMER MARYLAND GOVERNOR: I don't think it should be a Republican Democrat thing. I think we should all be just trying to see if we can't get these things resolved, because it's going to be important to the economy.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Should they invoke the Taft-Hartley Act?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN I'm not sure at this point if that's necessary or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): By some estimates, there's up to $5 billion at stake for the U.S. economy each day this strike lasts.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, businesses are scrambling to find other ways to move their products into and out of the United States, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices jumped today as stocks fell today on escalating tensions in the Middle East. The Dow losing 173. The S&P 500 dropped. 54. The NASDAQ fell 279.\par \par \uc2 Up next, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  joins us to discuss the market reaction to Iran's missile attack on Israel. And what you just heard that port workers strike across the country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: There is no surprise that Iran desperately wants \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to be president. If I win, we will have peace in the world again, a guarantee we will have peace in the world again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What do you believe the consequences for Iran should be?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That remains to be seen. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's talk about the market reaction to all of today's news. Let's bring in Fox Business anchor, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 . Larry, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Thank you, Bret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: First, on the Iran strikes in Israel. We saw a bump, obviously, in oil prices, the market took a little dip. Long term, what does that do for --\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: It's hard to know, because there was a bump up in Brent crude, European crude, which is directly tied to the Middle East. West Texas had a bump and then gave it back. It's been a weak oil market.\par \par \uc2 We are producing here. We should be producing more, but we're running 13 million barrels a day. The Saudis are sitting on a pile of oil reserves. They have been very judicious about handling it. I don't think they want to blow up in oil prices, or, for that matter, the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I have a thought. I think these Gulf states, particularly the Saudis, they are very pleased that Israel is doing what it's doing. They are very pleased that Israel is taking out \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and its leaders, and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and its leaders, and Iran may have lobbed a bunch of weapons. I understand that through bombs today, but I don't think they have the staying power. I wish the United States would be stronger behind Israel and this whole venture.\par \par \uc2 They are going to come a time -- I don't know if you saw Gerry Baker's article in the Wall Street Journal today. He's dead, right? I said the same thing on the air. There is going to come a time when, literally, the rest of the world is going to thank Israel for preserving democracy and preserving freedom, and eventually, preserving peace in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Let's turn to the U.S. and the impact of this port workers strike, and what it potentially means. Let's just say, they don't get a deal. It doesn't sound like they're close.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it takes a week -- two weeks. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Well, if it goes on for a bunch of weeks, it will be very harmful, very damaging to the economy. It's not going to be catastrophic, but you could lose -- you could lose a half a point to a point of real GDP, if it goes on, let's say for a month. Just for argument's sake. These things are very hard to model. And I've seen various Wall Streeters try to put pencil to paper on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And they are being aggressive. And there are some people coming after the Longshoremen. But the union, you know, saying, I will cripple you if you don't get to the table. What's --\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: You know, coming after the terrible, terrible hurricane, Helene. I mean, it's an untimely strike, but these things always are.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think the ILA is aiming too high. OK, way too high. I mean, the latest, 77 percent over six years. That's almost 13 percent a year increase in wages plus various other pension and contribution items. I know the head of the union makes a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the U.S. maritime allies run these ports, they made $400 billion in the last three years.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Was a B because of inflation.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: B, in part, because of inflation, in part because of the comeback from covid. That is as much -- I think it's slightly more money than they made in their entire history, going back to the late 1950s.\par \par \uc2 And again, the union is asking too much, OK? But remember, we've had 20 percent increase in the cost of living, in the last 3-1/2 plus years. OK, that has hurt these working folks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Real wages have fallen. This is throughout the economy, so, they are shooting for the sky. But who knows what the future inflation rate is going to be? Once you let that genie out of the box, you never -- it's very difficult to put it in. And the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  had to raise interest rates sky high. Now, they are slowly going to bring them back down.\par \par \uc2 I can say this. I can't blame the workers 100 percent. I read editorials, I have friends on Wall Street, all unions are bad, unions are bad. Yes, but they got hosed in the last several years with respect to the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Beating wages and their families in their kitchen table. So, they are fighting. I expect them to fight. I don't think this is going to go on forever.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Because, at some point, it's bad politics for Mr. Biden, is bad politics for the workforce as well. I don't think it will go on forever.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And for Vice President Harris, a lot of factors impacting the economy, as often happens, but this happens to be October.\par \par \uc2 Larry, as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Thank you for having me. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the Biden administration's words of warning to Iran have fallen on deaf ears. Peter Doocy explains why that may be.\par \par \uc2 But before we go to break, we'd like to wish former president, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  a happy 100th birthday. He is the first President ever to live a full century. Carter has been in hospice care for the past 19 months at his home in Plains, Georgia. His hometown, will host a concert to celebrate his birthday tonight. The White House is celebrating with a large letter sign wishing the 39th president happy 100th birthday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We return now to our top story at the bottom of the hour, State Department officials say they did not get notified by Iran of its plans to target Israel. President Biden saying intensive planning between the US and Israeli militaries helped in defending against the missile barrage tonight. Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy looks at how the Biden administration plans to address increasing Iranian aggression and how they've dealt with it in the past.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: At my direction, United States military actively supported the defense of Israel.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The President and Vice President monitored from the Situation Room.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), US VICE PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I fully support President Biden's order for the US military to shoot down Iranian missiles.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As advice they gave in April --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, message to Iran in this moment.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Don't.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And last October --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And what's the message to Iran?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Don't.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: -- was ignored.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a significant escalation by Iran.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Officials aren't ready to say what kind of Israeli response they'd support.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's an active discussion right now. We got to get all the data straight.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Officials also still won't explain why the administration's Iran envoy, \uc2 Robert Malley\uc2 , had his security clearance revoked last year.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Three of Rob malley's top advisers, his is inner circle, his -- that he relied on, were Iranian operatives.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That was October 2023. And in September of 2024 --\par \par \uc2 SEN. JAMES RISCH (R-ID): I still have no response from the administration to my letters regarding basic questions about the suspension of Special Envoy \uc2 Robert Malley\uc2 's security clearance.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Since taking office, the Biden administration has unfrozen billions of Iranian dollars for humanitarian reasons, but White House officials insist they've maintained a muscular posture.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have not lifted any single sanction, if anything, as I said moments ago, we increase pressure.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As the President deals with complex problems from Iran, from Halim (ph) and from a port strike, officials are asking people to have faith.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I hope the American people have confidence in this President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As President Biden prepares to phone Prime Minister Netanyahu about next steps in Israeli retaliation, remember that near the top of the list of things President Biden says he wants to accomplish before retiring in January, is bringing peace and security to the Middle East. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, as Peter just mentioned, President Biden will visit Asheville, North Carolina tomorrow. He says his top priority is to assure communities get the help and support they need as quickly as possible. Emergency crews in North Carolina are working around the clock to clean the roads, restore power and also reach those still stranded by the storm.\par \par \uc2 It's not the only state residents who have been able to leave their homes are still without essential items. Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Maggie Valley, North Carolina tonight with the latest. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. This is a scene that's being repeated across Western North Carolina. You've got a hundred houses up on the mountain, and the only way in or out is across this bridge, a bridge that's been down now for five days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: As flood waters slowly recede in the southeast, there is growing frustration as people lack the basic necessities, while government and volunteer groups bring in supplies by air, truck, even horses and mules. Residents say it can't come fast enough.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN CAPPELLINI, ASHEVILLE, NC RESIDENT: It's just been really hard to try to find services. If we can go and if we do go, where to go. But right now, I know that gas is -- there's a gas shortage.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Another big need, clean water.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DAVIS, ASHEVILLE, NC RESIDENT BUSINESS OWNER: You can't flush your toilets and you don't have enough water to drink. You might be able to do OK without electricity for a few more days.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Meteorologists say the storm dropped more than 40 trillion gallons of rain, enough to fill more than 60 million Olympic-sized swimming pools. The waters so powerful homes were knocked from their foundations. One North Carolina mother lost her son and parents after they sought refuge on their roof.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN DRYE, STORM VICTIM: I thought we were OK still, until the house broke apart and until everything started to collapse.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: And it's not just North Carolina seeing catastrophic damage, devastation in Georgia and Tennessee, where caskets were seen floating away. Fox Weather's Jane Minar is in the eastern part of the state.\par \par \uc2 JANE MINAR, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Here in trade, Tennessee, rural mountain community, the power of the Rome Creek overwhelming these homes as unfortunately, it rose 20 to 30 feet in a matter of hours.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The death toll now stands at least 159. One woman who died on the mountain during the storm, her body had to be taken across this broken bridge by hand. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan live in North Carolina. Steve, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Next up, we head to the touchscreen for a look at our latest round of issues, Power Rankings, the Fox News Power Rankings, plus a pair of live reports from the debate spin room. Countdown is on.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIR: I think what the governor needs to do is to continue to talk about what he and the vision that he and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have for the American people. Let's talk about the opportunity economy --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WHATLEY, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: Are we going to have a strong southern border? Are we going to have a strong economy? JD Vance is very ready to go explain and articulate why America needs to be strong and what we need to do to get there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to "Special Report." We have new Fox Power Rankings tonight on the top issues ahead of the vice presidential debate as you look at this election. Now, the top three issues, Vice President Harris is far ahead, out in front on the issue of abortion, 17 points as you see there.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, however, on these other two huge issues, immigration by 11 points, the US economy by 9. If you think about the US economy, it is by far the issue, a huge factor for November's election, the top issue for voters, as you can see, overwhelmingly over the other two.\par \par \uc2 We look across the states, a number of the key battleground states in yellow here, the top issue is the economy. And a lot of voters have soured on the economy, Pennsylvania 19 electoral votes, North Carolina 16, Georgia 16, Arizona 11. There's 62 electoral votes just in those four state and let's take a look at how they view the US economy. Pennsylvania, 70 percent of voters there say it's only fair or poor. That's in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 If we go down to North Carolina again, the number 70 percent of voters, likely voters, say the economy only fair or poor. Down in Georgia, the issue of immigration and border overwhelmingly for the former president. Economy, it has gotten a little tighter but still an advantage for the former president. There you see abortion. In Arizona, it is roughly the same that spread on immigration and the economy, and abortion the other way.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are some silver linings for Vice President Harris. She does lead on issues outside of the top three, health care, climate change, even the slight lead in bringing needed change. Democrats and Republicans have positive views largely of their respective candidates and their running mates. However, Independents, that's what this is. Independents have a more favorable view of Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  than they do of Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2 , and that's a seven point difference.\par \par \uc2 Voters are just not as familiar with the vice presidential nominees, and obviously they'll have a chance to change all of that. Tonight, we have Fox team coverage at the site of the debate here in New York. Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is following preparations for Team Vance, but we start with White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich on what to expect from the Democratic side. Good evening. Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Bret. The Harris campaign might be trying to downplay the importance of this debate, with high profile Democrats staying away from the spin room. But DNC chair, Jamie Harrison will be here. He's signaling that Walz tonight will try to put Trump in the room.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: I think you're going to see a stark contrast between representative of a party that wants to take us back. And then you're going to see someone who is good, who is genuine, who is that that good, funny uncle that you like to hang out with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This is the first major test for Walz since the DNC. He's faced no questions about controversial bills he signed as Minnesota's governor, like granting driver's licenses to illegal immigrants or making Minnesota a sanctuary state for children seeking transgender surgeries.\par \par \uc2 The campaign has kept him away from the media circuit, except for one joint interview with Vice President Harris, where he squirmed under questions about misrepresenting his military record. And today, the campaigns ducked our questions about newly unearthed falsehoods, exaggerating how many times he's been to China, and this claim.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM WALZ (D), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: I was in Hong Kong on June 4, 1989 when, of course, Tiananmen Square happened.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Newspaper reports indicate Walz was actually in Nebraska and didn't go to China for at least two months after the protests ended. But Walz repeated those stories in Congressional hearings as well.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  has been playing Vance in his debate prep. And according to CNN, Walz is nervous even warning the vice president that he's a bad debater, and tonight he doesn't want to let her down.\par \par \uc2 Well, that could be a standard play to try to lower expectations. We're seeing a different strategy from Team Trump on the other side of the room, where we find Aishah Hasnie. Aishah?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Jacqui. You're absolutely right. So the Trump-Vance ticket is actually trying to raise expectations for Governor Walz tonight, saying that he's a very good debater. He's going to be very good on the debate stage. More on that in their strategy in just a second.\par \par \uc2 But first, this might be the first reaction we hear from Senator Vance about what happened with Iran today, the Iranian attack on Israel. And it might sound something like this from former President Trump today on the campaign trail in Wisconsin. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER US PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They are very close to global catastrophe. We have a nonexistent president and a nonexistent vice president who should be in charge, but nobody knows what's going on. That's why Israel was under attack just a little while ago because they don't respect our country anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So as of yesterday, President Trump wasn't giving any debate advice to his running mate, but said that the debate would be "rigged" against him. Vance was all smiles and waved as he landed in New York yesterday. And according to his debate prep team, he is prepared to "wipe the floor" with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now they say that Senator Vance has done more than a hundred interviews since becoming the vice presidential nominee, so he is ready to go tonight. The strategy tonight, Bret, is to go after Walz military record, his time in Congress. What the Vance team says are Walz's far left policies as governor, and really draw this stark contrast between that ticket's stance on illegal immigration, the economy and, of course, their own stance.\par \par \uc2 And Vance will have a lot of surrogates here in the spin room tonight. Take a look. And that includes the guy who is credited for getting him on the ticket. That's Don Jr. He will be here in the spin room tonight, as well as his senate colleagues, including Senator Tom Cotton. More from them later on. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: JD Vance as well. Aishah, thank you. Jacqui before her, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Well, in JD Vance's home state of Ohio, Republicans are hoping to snatch of victory from a Democrat who has not lost in more than 40 years. Ninth District incumbent Marcia Kaptur is up against Republican challenger Derek Merrin, a member of Ohio Statehouse.\par \par \uc2 At 78 years old, Kaptur is the longest serving woman in Congress. She has cruised to reelection in most campaigns, but Trump carried this Ohio district by about three points in the 2020 general election. Trump endorsed Merrin as a self described constitutional conservative. He criticized Kaptur's record saying she introduced just five bills during her entire tenure in Congress. Kaptur touts herself as an independent voice for northern Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Our Fox Power Rankings has this as one of the 22 toss-ups in November in the House in the fight for control of that chamber. Up next, Harold Ford Jr, Martha McCallum and Bill Hemmer join our all star panel here in New York on Iran's attack on Israel, plus setting the table for tonight's debate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Iran is not only a threat to Israel, Iran is also a threat to American personnel in the region, American interests and innocent civilians across the region who suffer at the hands of Iran-based and backed terrorist proxies. We will never hesitate to take whatever action is necessary to defend us forces and interest.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I've been talking about World War III for a long time, and I don't want to make predictions because the predictions always come true. We're not going to make, but they are very close to global catastrophe. We have a nonexistent president and a nonexistent vice president who should be in charge, but nobody knows what's going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The two major candidates there reacting to Iran firing ballistic missiles, the largest ballistic missile strike ever on Israel, 181 of them. And as you saw it happen, all of the alerts went across Israel. And you saw the map of people getting to bomb shelters. It was filled with those red dots all across the state of Israel. Fortunately, only one so far has been killed. We haven't got full readout until the morning hours.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel. Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee congressman, co-host of "The Five," Bill Hemmer, co-anchor of "America's Newsroom," and Martha MacCallum, anchor and executive editor of "The Story with Martha MacCallum." All right. Bill, it was a big day?\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, big day. Another big day, actually, if you think about Friday and taking out \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and all its leadership. I think for who knows what happens next, but there is a window of opportunity if Netanyahu was to go forward and hit Iran its nuclear weapons program.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the Iranians have had many people in the Middle East fight and die for their cause. When it comes time for the Iranians to do the same, they seem to back off all the time. Today might have been another indication of that backing off.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Martha, the vice president was very muscular in what she said about supporting Israel and going against the aggression of Iran. But, boy, as you look at the Biden administration, you look at the Obama administration and how their policy towards Iran has developed over the years, there's a lot of criticism for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. I mean, both the Obama administration and the Biden administration had very cool relationships with Netanyahu. And I think that it's not -- it shouldn't go unnoticed that he has taken advantage of this time period.\par \par \uc2 Which is Biden is a lame duck. We don't see him much. He has scolded the Israeli government and Netanyahu for months and months. Harris is out on the campaign trail most of the time. And then, you see this pager attack, and you see the hit on Nasrallah.\par \par \uc2 He's unpopular to a great extent at home and unpopular with this leadership. And I think he's in a moment where he says, you know what, I'm going to do what I have to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS HOST: You know, thanks for having me. You know, he's gotten a lot of the blame for October 7th because there was an intelligence breakdown. I agree with Martha and I agree with what Bill, this may be a moment. He's unpopularity at home. He's not popular with me either, but I understand when you've asked the Israelis to stand down, or ask the Israelis to perhaps only search for a ceasefire, only search for a way to bring into hostilities, they've been attacked the Israelis (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 So I get where he is. And if he has the window, I think he may take it. I was pleased to see the Jordanians help us today. We obviously the US naval ships help shoot down some of those missiles, but we should not kid ourselves the stabilization this will cause in that region. Everything from higher energy prices and perhaps the committing of our own troops, these are things that we as Americans, as we talk about this, we have to understand what the implications could be for us as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, one of those \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyers was the USS Cole, which was struck 24 years ago, almost this week, pretty --\par \par \uc2 FORD: October 12.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Let's turn to setting the table for tonight. A lot of focus. Obviously, you don't really focus on the VPs that much, but this one seems like it's going to get more attention.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: You know, I -- it's a real time debate in a real time world with real time events. We just think about the last 72 hours or so. I went back and watched the debate from 2022. I think \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is highly capable. I think he's fluent. He knew all the issues in Minnesota. We'll see how it is in the national stage.\par \par \uc2 I thought Tim Ryan was a really good sparring partner for JD Vance, and I thought they were both actually quite effective. We'll see how it goes tonight. I think Vance has to go after the Harris-Biden record in a way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  failed during his debate.\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has pushed a lot of progressive policies through as governor, and, you know, the field is ripe for picking when you think about free college for migrants and free driver's licenses for migrants. There's a bunch of stories out there as to whether or not I was telling the truth about China and Hong Kong from years ago in Tiananmen Square, which kind of couples with the football coach story and kind of couples with some of the stories about the DUI. So there's a lot out there.\par \par \uc2 One last thing, I'd say it's open mics at \uc2 CBS\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: -- so they're going to be able to, you know, to go at each other. And I think the question is, who goes low first?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we look at these polls, Martha, and every day just goes back and forth. Quinnipiac is out with a new poll. The former president really hates Quinnipiac. Usually he really likes this one.\par \par \uc2 This one is Georgia up six, and North Carolina up two. So we thought to put that on the board. But you see this swing in the swing states, and it's really tight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And I think that these events, they do I think lean into where Vance is, because it's a strength argument. When you look at what's happening with the ports, when you look at Helene, which is an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 People are still -- we don't know where a lot of these people are at this point. They don't have power, they don't have water, all of that. And foreign policy with what happened today, Bret, we've heard almost nothing about foreign policy throughout the course of this election. And events have a way of getting in your face.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It will a lead tonight, I guarantee you.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes. The ideas and the issues that has been reset to Martha's point, this foreign policy will lead this debate. And you will get an opportunity, the American people get an opportunity to see if these two guys, if they can succeed, the people are on top of the ticket at a moment of crisis.\par \par \uc2 I hope he's cheerful. I hope they both are cheerful less contingents. I hope both are optimistic and less ominous. I think whomever does that best and couples it with issues is going to have the better night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I could sit here and talk to you guys all night. We had a lot to squeeze in this show, but we'll see you later.\par \par \uc2 Please stay tuned for complete coverage of tonight's vice presidential debate. Laura Ingraham keeps it going, 7:00 PM Eastern Time, Jesse Watters at 8:00, Martha MacCallum and I will host special coverage starting at 8:20.\par \par \uc2 We will simulcast the debate at 9:00. You can see right here on Fox, followed by analysis by our veteran panel. \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  takes over from the debate spin room and "Fox News at Night" rounds out the coverage tonight.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on "Special Report," we'll have reaction, plus chief foreign affairs correspondent Trey Yingst joins us to discuss his new book and the latest from the ground. All that's happening there. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR 6:00 PM in the East, 3:00 PM on the West Coast. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night.\par \par \uc2 That's it for this "Special Report," fair, balanced and still unafraid. Remember special debate coverage tonight and the " Ingraham Angle" starts next. I want to keep talking here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241002eka100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Iran Unleashes 200-Missile Barrage At Jewish State; Donald Trump Hammers Kamala Harris Over Iran's Attack On Israel; J.D. Vance And \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  Ready To Battle Overnight; Coach On Defense: Tonight: Walz To Answer For Numerous Falsehoods; 45K-Plus Union Dockworkers Strike On Kamala's Watch; I Was Not Sleeping So Well: Harris: I Marinated Pork Roast When Biden Dropped Out\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 7,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: -- these crosscurrents and developments. A sell if that looks substantial, but in the scheme is actually quite tiny, about point four percent in the Dow.\par \par \uc2 Again, the fear being that a war could erupt, but right now the markets are betting it won't be nearly the worry that it is right now, we shall see. THE FIVE is now.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Fox News alert, we're just hours away from the huge vice presidential debate between \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and J.D. Vance, but first, the world's on the brink of war with Joe and Kamala at the helm. Israel is planning their next steps after getting attacked by Iran, the terrorist-supporting regime, firing over a hundred missiles at the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 Joe and Kamala trying to act like they're on top of things after Iran attacked Israel. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The United States military actively supported the defense of Israel, and we're still assessing the impact.\par \par \uc2 But based on what we know now, the attack appears to have been defeated and ineffective, and this is testament to Israeli military capability and U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Let's go right to Trey Yingst in Israel. Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Jesse, this was a significant attack that took place across Israel earlier today, the Iranians firing more than 180 ballistic missiles at this country. Some of them were intercepted by Israel's arrow three missile defense system, but others slammed into the ground below.\par \par \uc2 We also understand that the Israeli cabinet is meeting tonight to determine what their response will be to this attack that sent millions of people to bomb shelters. We could see here from the city of Haifa in the northern part of Israel as these missile defense systems were active, they were picking off the ballistic missiles that were soaring through the air.\par \par \uc2 And again, this is significant because it is the first time, according to Iranian state television, at the Iranians use the Fattah for hypersonic ballistic missile.\par \par \uc2 Back in April, when Iran attacked Israel, they didn't used hypersonic ballistic missiles of this caliber, the latest that they have in their arsenal.\par \par \uc2 And so, as we look forward, there are major questions about how the Israelis will respond. But Fox News has talked with Israeli officials tonight in Tel Aviv, and they indicate it is not a question of if Israel will respond, but rather when and where, guys.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Trey, thank you. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows exactly who's to blame for Joe and Kamala's latest foreign policy disaster. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The world right now is spiraling out of control.\par \par \uc2 When I was president, Iran was in total check. The Iranians were starved for cash. Iran was on the verge of bankruptcy. They had no money left. They had no money for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They had no money for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the people they're fighting now, they would have been willing to make any deal. You could have made any deal, but Kamala flooded them with American cash.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, Judge Jeanine, this is -- this is -- this is tough now. Right now in October, the world seems like it's falling apart.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, it's not just this. It's the longshoreman, it's the economy, it's -- everything's going on.\par \par \uc2 But you know, what's interesting about this is you recall that Iran, as Trey Yingst just referenced had -- they shot some missiles over in April, and that was in response to Israel apparently bombing an embassy in Syria, the Iranian embassy in Syria.\par \par \uc2 This is a little more serious, but make no mistake, Iran is -- wants to be the funder of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the Houthis. They want them to go out and do the fighting. They'll fund them, they'll train them, they'll teach them how to fight. But the blood is only going in one direction.\par \par \uc2 So, Iran doesn't really have the capability or capacity to do the kind of damage that everyone thinks they can. And even their own people in Iran are starting to be frustrated with that country.\par \par \uc2 I think what's going on is that they are sensing that Israel has the upper hand, given the fact that Nasrallah was taken down, and the upper echelon of the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the pagers, the walkie-talkies, 3,000 killed and injured, I should say.\par \par \uc2 And now, it appears that, you know, Israel is on the march in Lebanon as well they should be, but all the United States says is ceasefire. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last night, ceasefire now, that was the quote.\par \par \uc2 And the truth is that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , which is even worse and more sophisticated than \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , you know, violated a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  resolution from 2006 like almost immediately and the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  and everybody's cool with it.\par \par \uc2 But as soon as Israel tries to defend itself that there's a problem, JCPOA, they got the money. We didn't monitor it. They told us that they were going to spend it whatever way they wanted. Iran is selling oil on the open market, and so as a result, we are funding terrorism.\par \par \uc2 And until that ends, or until somebody wins, you know, we talk about diplomacy, but at some point, maybe somebody has to win here.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Jessica Tarlov.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I feel, and I hope that Israel is on the precipice of winning this. It's been an incredible couple of weeks, and frankly, an incredible few months, going back to April, when they did manage to hit that embassy, I think that \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 , for however people feel about him, generally speaking, has garnered a lot of goodwill and praise for the type of leader he is for this, the kind of statecraft that this took, the kind of spy craft that it took, and feels as though the \uc2 IDF\uc2  is the only force that could have pulled something like this off.\par \par \uc2 Iran looks weak in this, I understand it is very serious. I was speaking to a friend whose family lives in Israel. Said they are all in bomb shelters. There is not one person that is out walking around right now, but Iran has been humiliated because of what Israel has pulled off in terms of destructing \uc2 Hamas\uc2  then with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  as well, and they needed to do something.\par \par \uc2 To the point that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has just said, ceasefire now, that's not true. The goal is a ceasefire obviously, but he said firmly, we stand with Israel. They are using our missiles to kill these terrorists. They are firing off of our aircraft carriers. We are part of this fight with them.\par \par \uc2 And the narrative that Iran was completely contained and did nothing while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, the Iranians fired missiles at U.S. bases and over a hundred service members had traumatic brain injury, and that was when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 So, this narrative does not hold up. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Kamala are not responsible for what has happened since October 7th. There obviously were questions about what intelligence failures there were, but that lays at the feet of \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 , not at the U.S. president and the U.S. vice president.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I want to make one thing clear, you said it was a lie. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, yesterday, ceasefire now.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You said that's all he says, and I said it --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wait a minute. Wait a minute. He said ceasefire now last night. That's what I said.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And then -- but that's not what you said, you said, all \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says is ceasefire. My point --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He said ceasefire on October 8th, come on.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: One of the differences, Dana, is that under the Trump administration, the Iranians never took Americans hostage like they have seen now in Israel through \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So, the irony of some of this is that Biden wants to talk about his capabilities in diplomacy, and he's made a lot of international phone calls. Talk about like the world, like there's a lot of things going on. It's not just in the Middle East, but of course, we know in Europe as well, and elsewhere, including Africa, all these places, and it's complicated, and there's a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 But he wants to be given praise for the diplomacy because he was a Senate Foreign Relations chairman, and he knows all the leaders, and he can call them, but he doesn't want to be judged on the outcomes.\par \par \uc2 And what Biden has been saying to Netanyahu over this past year is OK, enough. Take the win.\par \par \uc2 And I think what Netanyahu should say to Biden tonight is, no sir, take the win. Let's go. Let's press up the advantage. We have gotten -- we have helped Western civilization get to a point that you could be on offense again against radical Islamic terrorism. So, join the side that you're on, and let's do this together.\par \par \uc2 So, one thing about tonight in the vice presidential debate, I do expect for this to come up, and just one thing is because this election is so close. I was talking to \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2  on the show yesterday at "NEWSROOM" and in Pennsylvania, for example, Jewish Americans could make up a difference if the race is that close, and you are a Jewish American, who I interviewed one last week, who said, I'm a single issue voter, and my issue is Israel. Does Israel continue to exist.\par \par \uc2 I think that they'll be listening to hear what Walz and Vance say about what they would do if they were ever to become commander-in-chief, or if they can articulate what Harris and Trump would do.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: GG.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, well, this is stuff that's been going on since I was a young and it doesn't matter whose president really. It's just stuff happens.\par \par \uc2 But I feel in this case, it's good not to listen to Joe in this, Israel has no reason to. You know, on October 8th, they went from wanting to deciding, and the decision was to exact justice for an atrocity. And we should understand that, if anybody were to say, hey, you should exercise restraint. To me after something like that, I would be polite, but it would have absolutely no impact on what I plan to do.\par \par \uc2 If anything, I'm amazed at Israel's restraint, and I'm impressed by their precision and methodical response. I mean, Iran is you know -- Israel's playing chess and Iran is playing Yahtzee. Compare their responses. You know, Iran is lobbying these missiles onto a country's capital, and Israel took out the entire command of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  with little to no collateral damage, and this is going to be talked about for ages to come. Even Hollywood couldn't make this up.\par \par \uc2 And I'm sure Israel already planned for this, has something -- you know, they know what Iran's going to do.\par \par \uc2 But Israel, you have to understand. And I said this before that you kind of have to like, let Israel do Israel. They think like warriors because they have -- they have to. It's not their choice. We don't think like that anymore, because we're surrounded by oceans and we're blessed with wealth and power.\par \par \uc2 You know, imagine if we use the Israeli beeper and phone strategy on fentanyl dealers. But we don't, we don't, you know, we're not allowed to. That's in a way, you know, I admire. I watch Israel's clear-eyed purpose in protecting their country. And I guess I'm jealous.\par \par \uc2 I'm jealous when I look at our borders, when I look at our crime, and I see how this progressive mentality has brought us here, and how they are so steadfast in letting that mentality, you know, corrupt their dedication to protecting their homeland.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well said, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow, you sure are really being nice to them.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I didn't mean that but well said.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  ready to throw down in tonight's debate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: We are just hours away from the high-stakes vice presidential showdown between J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , but watch out, J.D., Team Walz seems to be laying a trap. They're acting all nervous that the governor will blow it and even taking to the airwaves to lower expectations. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just think we need to lower expectations a little bit, because then we can all be pleasantly surprised if I'm wrong and that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  just mops the floor with him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trying to pin him as extreme, out of touch and all of that. I think that's where he's going to go really, really hard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If it's joyful warrior versus grumpy young guy trying to represent grumpy old guy, I think it would be a win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is getting in on the pre-debate fun by taking this shot at \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You'll see it tomorrow with J.D. It will be stacked. He's going up against a moron, a total moron. How she picked him is unbelievable, and I think it's a big factor. There's something wrong with that guy. He's sick.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, let me ask you this, Jessica, you've got a guy who was a six term congressman. He's in his second term as governor. He's run for major office nine times, now he's running for vice president, and they really want to lower expectations, as though he's not up to this?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Everybody wants to lower expectations for all their candidates. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  basically won 2016 because we lowered expectations. He showed up and didn't like vomit on himself. And everyone was like, oh, this will be fine. And then he got to be president. It's a completely normal practice.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  calling \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  a moron, and over the weekend, he said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was born mentally challenged -- disabled, challenged or disabled. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became that, but Kamala was that way from birth.\par \par \uc2 I'm not -- you know, I'm not a Pollyanna (ph). I can take it, the mean commentary that does not signify to the American public, that you can govern for all Americans, or that you're not a little child.\par \par \uc2 So, as far as tonight goes, J.D. Vance has already made history. He's the least popular V.P. candidate that we've ever had going into this.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , I understand will be pressed about what happened during the summer after George Floyd was murdered. But he has a really good record to defend. He won reelection in 2022 by a bigger margin than he won the first time. Minnesota is the sixth-best state in the country to do business. He got a $3.7 billion surplus. He cut taxes for middle-class Minnesotans.\par \par \uc2 He can talk about all of those things. The foreign policy part will definitely be interesting, especially with Israel at the top of the agenda. And I really want him to press J.D. Vance on why he voted against the aid package to Israel.\par \par \uc2 This is not the same thing as having \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up there, who says, give Israel whatever they want. J.D. Vance is an isolationist, someone voting against aid to Israel, one of our great allies, as well as to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Dana, some of the articles have characterized Walz as a bit manic, a guy who literally shuts down when he -- when he gets most stressed. How is this guy supposed to handle the most difficult job, one of the most difficult in the world? Or does he just open the windows and smell the burning rubber?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think because he's only he -- in the CNN interview, the one interview he's done, and the CNN interview, he said 700 words total. So, I don't think J.D. Vance needs to win a popularity contest, but I do think that he has to continue to introduce himself to America tonight, his biggest audience.\par \par \uc2 But same for \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , except for people in Minnesota, nobody really knows who he is, so it's not a popularity contest, but Vance's mission basically tonight is he's not going to try to necessarily make people like him. He has to take the fight to the Harris policy agenda and hit some of those things that President Trump didn't hit in his debate, so that all the good vibes and the joy can be exposed.\par \par \uc2 One thing that I thought Walz has done that I've never understood is he immediately out of the gate made fun of J.D. Vance for going to Yale and for becoming a venture capitalist.\par \par \uc2 And he said, no one that I've taught has ever done that. As if -- as if J.D. Vance was born with a silver spoon in his mouth, which we all read the book. Like, that's obviously not the case. It was a very strange and weird way to attack J.D. Vance. So, I'm looking forward to tonight.\par \par \uc2 I do think the other thing that's strange is that I think Walz has a more difficult position tonight, because, like when I was press secretary, if somebody asked me, what does George Bush think about this? Like I knew, how did I know? One, because I probably agreed. Two, I was with him all the time. And three, he never wavered. He didn't change his position. So I always knew what it was.\par \par \uc2 Imagine you're \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  tonight, and you're thinking, wait, is she for that now, or did she -- is she against that now? I can't remember. And he could get caught in something that could be very embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What do you think about that, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'd like to respond to what Jessica said. They've called \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a Nazi so many times he got shot.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: J.D. Vance also called him a Nazi.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And now you're complaining about being called dumb.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm not complaining. I'm saying it's a --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You're complaining. I sensed complaint.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I said I can take it. I said it's not a smart way to try to win an election.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK. Well, maybe getting your opponent shot and putting them in jail is not a smart idea either.\par \par \uc2 Tonight is a battle for men Judge Jeanine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right? How sexy is that? They're going to be fighting over us, because this is really what it's all about. The Democrats have to stop the bleeding with men, and the Republicans need to run up the score with men.\par \par \uc2 And Walz is going to come out. He's going to try to act like Tim Allen (ph), but he's really Bernie Sanders, and he's going to have the same exact strategy as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had, which is attack Trump and don't talk about any solutions, because he doesn't have any solutions, and just make J.D. look weird and try to make himself look normal.\par \par \uc2 He cannot make any mistakes, I agree with Dana. Either of them can't make a mistake, because this race is so tight. Any ridiculous thing that either one of these guys says about tampons or cats is just going to get blasted out everywhere, and that's all everyone is going to talk about.\par \par \uc2 But he does have a challenge, because he has to defend himself. He has to defend Kamala and he has to defend \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 J.D. Vance has to do what Trump wasn't fully able to do, which is make Harris and Walz own the Biden legacy, and the mics are hot, so he can just turn to this guy and say, you said Bidenomics was working. How or why are we hiring you guys after you're in power now? You're supposed to fix the problems you created. That's what he should do.\par \par \uc2 And if he's smart, he should attack the media. And since he's such a great storyteller, I'd like to see him say, this is where we were. This is where we are now, and this is where we take you. That's what he needs to do. And I think he'll be very effective.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Greg, they say that he also has a very short fuse. How does -- how does J.D. tap into that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know. I think you just talk about the issues, but I do, I agree with Jesse that men don't care about joy, right? Being gay does not lower the inflation or lower the crime rates, and I use the word gay in its original sense, happy.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Trump's right. The odds are stacked against him. No matter what happens, Walz will be the winner. The only reason Trump was considered the winner against Biden is that the media couldn't spin \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's collapse.\par \par \uc2 I do believe that Walz will probably come after Vance over the cats and dogs stuff, and I think Vance could easily just parlay that go. You know, you're right. I'm probably better at stuff like crime, immigration, inflation, drug addiction, issues of war and service, I'll give you the -- I'll give you the victory on cat rhetoric, OK, but at least, at the very least, my stories came from citizens. They weren't made up. They weren't lies to falsely attribute valor to my past or pad my resume, about, you know, going here, going there.\par \par \uc2 And then, he just moves from there to immigration, right? Talk about, you know, why is it that all the policies of his party only go in one direction, illegals in, not out, sanctuary cities, the asylum laws, that Remain in Mexico. These were all designed to get an end result, which is now we have rapists and murderers by the thousands in the United States.\par \par \uc2 If he tries to fact check that, that's very simple, he will say, but that's an accumulation over decades, and he can obviously say, so you're telling me, somebody in the '80 -- from the 1980s or '90s is included in that number, it's obviously weighted towards the present when you look at Venezuela's low crime rate right now, and we have Venezuelan gangs, if it were a consistent number, we would know that, but it's not.\par \par \uc2 So, we know that they're talking about the relevant time right now. So, I think that's -- if he just sticks to the specific policies, not you know, I think if you're on the fence, and I can't believe anybody's on the fence right now, but if you're on the fence, that's pretty amazing. Get some preparation, H (ph). You're going to -- you're probably going to hear like, oh, this is -- the Democrats are a bunch of extremists and the right -- the Republicans, that's going to wash over you.\par \par \uc2 So, you just have to go, what is the consensus between everybody? Low crime, low inflation, a strong border. What more could you want? And you just look at and you go, but the problem with Walz is it's been the wrong side of every one of those.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, right. Up next, don't miss our special coverage of tonight's vice presidential debate. We've got a must see primetime lineup, and that's ahead of our simulcast of the debate itself, starting at 9:00 p.m. After that, it's \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  live from the spin room.\par \par \uc2 And up next, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  can't go a wall on this one. Kamala's running mate will have to answer for the many fibs and falsehoods and exaggeration.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The assistant coach will be on defense at tonight's high-stakes debate. Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has been ducking and dodging questions over what critics say is a growing list of lies and falsehoods, everything from embellishing his military record to what spice he puts on his tacos.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE-PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Those weapons of war that I carried in war.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Command sergeant major and served with his battalion at Operating Enduring Freedom in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Like, I have white guy tacos.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: Do you put any flavor in it?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, there was -- on the military thing, Greg. There was a statement from the Harris Campaign saying yes, embellished but it's OK. But he's never had to answer it. Do you think J.D. Vance who did serve would --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I mean, the contrast between Vance's honest