eaking tonight, officials say hundreds could be dead in the aftermath of Hurricane Helene, but confirming those numbers is proving difficult. Millions of people are without power and cell service.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, the lack of communication services have prompted hundreds of missing persons reports, with many others still stranded in their homes due to high flood waters.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Asheville, North Carolina tonight, where he's been surveying the devastation. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, John, the mayor of Asheville put it simply. She said, we need everything.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything is just --\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Wiped out and washed away.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our whole house, bro.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Roads and bridges, homes and businesses all erased by the extended power of hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY HARRISON, ASHEVILLE, NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT: It's been an incredibly apocalyptic weekend for all of us here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): More than two feet of rain socking parts of western North Carolina, causing the worst flooding here in a century.\par \par \uc2 The village of Chimney Rock gone, city of Asheville, underwater, historic Biltmore Village caked in mud, once picturesque Lake Lure filled with debris, residents say the rural town of Swannanoa is unrecognizable.\par \par \uc2 JARED QUINN, SWANNANOA, NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT: It's a lot to process. You can barely recognize some areas, and you're trying to figure out where am I?\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Not only are thousands left homeless, but struggling for supplies, waiting for hours for clean water, food and gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't know what they're going to do to get us out of here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): People are cut off from the outside world, with roads closed, no power and cell service, leaving many unable to get in touch with loved ones.\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MANHEIMER, MAYOR OF ASHEVILLE, NORTH CAROLINA: It is a dire situation. We are still in an active crisis situation.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): North Carolina's Governor activated more than 500 soldiers and airmen from the National Guard, and is asking people to stay off the roads as search teams look for those still missing.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): Everyone is pulling together to meet the needs of people in Western North Carolina, but we know there's a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Helene left behind a 500 mile path of destruction spanning six states. It could take weeks to years to recover, while Florida's governor says his state has everything it needs, residents of places like Treasure Island are still inundated with sand and water.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My heart breaks for this entire community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The death toll continues to rise. It now stands at more than 120, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And it's raining again. Steve Harrigan, for us. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he will visit North Carolina on Wednesday to survey the damage in Asheville. He and Vice President Harris say they are prepared to help communities impacted by Hurricane Helene. It comes amid criticism that they were both absent from Washington as many people began to assess the damage.\par \par \uc2 Our chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel looks at the federal response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want them to know we're not leaving until the job is done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Holy smokes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In the wake of Hurricane Helene