axes very simply and she is raising taxes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So, John, this debate is going to be different here in New York City. CBS News says the candidates will be responsible for fact checking each other. But former President Trump saying today that debate will be rigged. So we're going to have to see exactly what happens then that debate stage, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Going to be a smackdown in New York. Alexis McAdams for us. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At least a dozen veterans affairs staffers have been accused of accessing the medical records of both of the vice presidential nominees. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports investigators notified the Trump-Vance and Harris- Walz campaigns. The employees are under criminal investigation for potentially violating federal health privacy laws.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a preview of a new film shining a light on human trafficking and child labor. But as we head to break, basketball hall of Famer Dikembe Mutombo has died. The \uc2 NBA\uc2  announced his death from brain cancer. He is remembered as one of the best defensive players in \uc2 NBA\uc2  history and a longtime global ambassador for the game. Dikembe Mutombo was 58, and singer, songwriter and actor Kris Kristofferson has died.\par \par \uc2 The Texas native was known for his country and rock and roll songs, Sunday morning coming down and for the good times. He also starred alongside Barbra Streisand in the 1976 film a Star Is Born. A family spokeswoman says Kristofferson died peacefully at his home in Maui. He was 88 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Immigration officials say they have arrested a Salvadoran national on Martha's Vineyard who was wanted by authorities in his home country for human trafficking charges. The 50-year-old is a got-a-way who entered the U.S. at an unknown date. He was deported at least one other time in June of 2011.\par \par \uc2 A new film now available on streaming services is shining a light on human trafficking and child labor. Bret Baier speaks with the director and the executive producer. Brett.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, John. A new movie being described as a gripping tale of resilience and hope debuts on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Video and \uc2 Apple\uc2 . It's called "City of Dreams" and it's about child trafficking. The film follows one boy's harrowing journey as he is sent from Mexico to a sweatshop in Los Angeles. Author and speaker Tony Robbins. His executive producer, Mohit Ramchandani, is the writer and director. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Tony, just give us the elevator pitch on this movie because I think it's really compelling. Just even looking at the trailer.\par \par \uc2 TONY ROBBINS, AUTHOR AND SPEAKER: It's really -- it's a movie you want to see just because it's a thriller will keep you on the edge of your seat. It's based on a series of true stories that have been combined together. And it's really -- it's about a boy that gets trafficked. He thinks he's coming to go to a soccer camp and maybe work play for the galaxy in LA, and he gets put into a sweatshop.\par \par \uc2 And so it's about slave labor, but it's hard to describe. It's beyond your imagination what these kids go through. And on the other side of it, though, it's a hero's journey, because this young man finds a way, against all odds, to free himself and free others. And then it's really called action, because right now, there are 12 million children in the world that are enslaved. It's the fastest growing criminal enterprise in the world, $150 billion.\par \par \uc2 But people have no idea what's happening when they hear 10, 11 million people coming across the border and we hear that government has told us we've lost 325,000 children. We know where they are. Well, this is where some of them are ending up.\par \par \uc2 Film has the power to move people emotionally. If you think about it, we have slavery in this country forever. What changed? It was Uncle Tom's Cabin, and it made people say, no more. And even \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  read the book. I think about the impact that book had to change slavery. I think this film is a move in that direction.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me play a clip from City of Dreams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's good, it's good. It's the right decision for you whole family. You think you're going to run out onto the pitch tomorrow? No, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What kind of business you in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The busy kind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is real life. You know, it is gripping, but it is -- it transitions over to what is really happening today.\par \par \uc2 MO RAMCHANDANI, "CITY OF DREAMS" WRITER AND DIRECTOR: Yes, I mean, it was very shocking for me. I'm originally from India, and, you know, this stuff is happening India and China and third world countries. But I had read a case 10 years ago, about 72 immigrants trapped in a residential location in Almonte, California, and I couldn't believe it.\par \par \uc2 What's been even more shocking is, as I was making the movie, between 2019, 2020, 2021, you know, the cases just got worse. I connected with a guy at the Labor Department who runs the Wage and Hour Division for the entire west coast, and he started sending me cases.\par \par \uc2 And you know, eventually the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  last year did an expose about how 300,000 children have gone missing in our country, and many of them have ended up in the supply chains of some of the largest corporations in America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to play some of the movie testimonials. Some people, you know, Tony, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE, ACTOR AND FILMMAKER: Every now and then, you come across a film that is actually truly important.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN SHEEN, EMMY WINNING ACTOR: Exposes in no uncertain terms the harsh, brutal, and devastating reality for numerous innocent victims of human trafficking right here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 PAT RILEY, PRESIDENT, \uc2 MIAMI HEAT\uc2 : It's a masterpiece about one of the most insidious problems that we have in the world and in our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So it's not a partisan thing, Tony. I mean, it gets characterized as somehow left and right, but it really isn't.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: They think this is focusing on the issues around the border today, and it's not about that. It's about our children. Our children is not a left or right thing, you know, and we need to know what's going on. And the first step is awareness and getting enough emotion. This film will impact you. I mean, it's an incredible film on its own, but it's a film with a mission.\par \par \uc2 RAMCHANDANI: I'm showing you from a boy's point of view, what happens when you're trafficked across the border and you're. You're sold into slavery. And when I was making the film, it wasn't a big political issue.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I started this journey in 2019. Somebody said to me, you know, Mo, I know you tried to make this fairy tale like Slumdog Millionaire type story, but you happen to be pointing at the biggest vulnerability of one of the two political parties in America today in a year that they're trying to get elected.\par \par \uc2 And I was like, well, that wasn't my intention. My intention was to tell a heroic story of a kid who didn't have a voice. And by the end of the movie, he gets his voice back and he shows us what we need to do in order to clean up our country.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: So people will love it at Six Ethan film and hopefully will be inspired to participate in some way to put an end to this. Our children really need our help. This is really insane.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is pretty crazy. It does cross party lines, and it shouldn't be an issue one way or another. The movie itself talks for itself. I mean, it's really, really powerful.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it tracks what we're dealing with real time. Here's the \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  hearing on human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANY BIGGS (R-AZ) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: Children are not for sale. Many children, especially those crossing the border as unaccompanied minors, are not safe.\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN (D-PA) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: Child labor and sex trafficking to the production and distribution of child sexual abuse materials online harassment and sex torture. Perpetrators of these repulsive crimes hide within our society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's Democrats and Republicans, Tony. And it's real time, and it's happening now. So the movie is current?\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Yes, I think so, too. I just hope people will get a chance to see it, let themselves be moved, show it to their family, show it to their friends. You can see it now. It's available on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , as you said, so you don't have to go to the movie theater to see it. But it's an important film to see.\par \par \uc2 And my wife and I personally have been involved in this trafficking issue for almost eight years. And with myself and some of our friends, we've saved over 71,000 children. And many of these kids, by the way, become social workers to go help other kids get out of this situation.\par \par \uc2 Because you can imagine the trauma they've been through, turning this around is such an important issue. And I really appreciate, Bret, you having us on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Tony, Mo, we really appreciate the time. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RAMCHANDANI: Yes. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: John, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Bret, a very important movie indeed. Up next, our panel on the political storm over the response to Hurricane Helene. And is there another political storm brewing over a looming port workers strike? And then it's a long way to take a dip and stand at the top of the podium.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MANHEIMER (D) ASHVILE, NORTH CAROLINA MAYOR: This is a catastrophic situation that we have in Asheville, an unprecedented storm event.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The federal government is not being responsive. The vice president, she's out someplace campaigning. The governor's doing a good job, but he's having a hard time getting the president on the phone.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: My first responsibility is to get all the help needed, those impacted areas.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: And I plan to be on the ground as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 GREG MURPHY (R) NORTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: I mean, this could flip an election. If those people are not able to get out and vote, heck, they're looking for shelter and food and medicine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A tragic situation that the White House homeland security advisor says could result in the deaths of some 600 people. But because it's in two very important swing states, politics inevitably creeps into this disaster.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com. And host of the Guy Benson Show on Fox News Radio. \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is the Fox News radio political analyst. And USA Today White House correspondent Francesca Chambers is here with us.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, start us off here, because it's Georgia and North Carolina and a disaster, and everybody wants to get the upper hand on this politics again inevitably creeps in.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : And the White House is trying to keep politics out of it today, they said, and now President Biden does say that he will go to North Carolina later this week. But as you were noting in the intro, he has said that he didn't want to go and be on the ground already. The White House said that because they didn't want him to be in the way. You covered the White House as well.\par \par \uc2 This is always the bait for presidents is how quickly do you go to a disaster area. You don't want to be accused of not going quickly enough, but you also don't want to put a strain on local resources. And you basically heard the same thing from the vice president today.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, well, Trump said he wanted to go to North Carolina, but logistically, now was not the right time. So that's why he went to Valdosta, Georgia. But in terms of being where you're supposed to be, Biden and Harris have both come under criticism for he was at Rehoboth, she was out west doing fundraisers when all of this went down. Peter Doocy asked \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  about it at the White House briefing. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: People in western North Carolina were drowning in their houses this weekend. Others were losing everything. President Biden was at his beach house and Vice President Harris was hosting political fundraisers on the west coast. Is there a reason that they could not be here?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president did exactly what a president in this moment needs to do, which is directing his team to take action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Now, he pushed back against that, saying, look, I was on the phone the entire time, which he likely wasn't, but do you have to be at the White House?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, "THE GUY BENSON SHOW": Well, first he said he was on the phone for 2 hours, and then the entire time he was at the beach. And I think if it were a republican president, we would be getting a lot of attention, a lot of scrutiny and conversation in the media about the president being yet again at the beach for yet another weekend with some very horrible things happening to Americans. Whether you care about the politics and think of them as voters, these are people in need of help.\par \par \uc2 And I think there would be some criticism of this more, I'd say direct and high decimal criticism if the parties were reversed. And look, the vice president, who's now the party frontrunner here, she's the torch carrier for the Democrats. She was at this glitzy fundraiser. She was with Alanis Morissette and Demi Lovato. They had beef Wellington in lobster rolls, I'm told, which sounds great. But as Peter asked, people were drowning. Optics actually do matter in politics.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, she had a military lunch on the way back aboard Air Force Two, the White House put out a picture of her talking to Criswell, the FEMA director, in a photo that looked slightly reminiscent of the one on the right, which was George Bush taking a look at Hurricane Katrina from Air Force One, Josh, back in 2005.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Optics matter. Perception matters when you have natural disasters. And look, the scope and scale of the destruction of Hurricane Helene. I think was much worse than the White House realized. It took a little, and frankly, I think a lot of folks were slow on the uptick to understand how damaging this hurricane was.\par \par \uc2 But the images matter. And that showing that you have empathy and you care more about their well-being than the politics of the moment is really significant. And look, if it turns out that the recovery is slow, these are two of the most important swing states on the map. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  running neck and neck in North Carolina and Georgia. Every constituency matters. This could make a difference in a close election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: But there's something else that could potentially hinder commerce in all of the swing states. If we go into a port strike tonight. Biden was asked if he would invoke Taft Hartley to stop a port strike from happening. Watch this exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mister president, will you intervene in the dock workers strike if they go on strike on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's collective bargaining. I don't believe in Taft Hartley.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Collective bargaining. I don't believe in Taft Hartley. Obviously, he's concerned about the unions, but there are millions of other folks out there who may not be able to get what they need to get, particularly in time for Christmas. And he's going to leave his vice president with that.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And the White House actually backed him up on that again today, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  saying from the podium that he would not intervene and then raise collective bargaining again, raised the issue that he's been, as they say, the most pro-union president in history and from the White House podium and said that they again wanted to take politics out of this as well for the election.\par \par \uc2 But with the economy being the number one issue in poll after poll, it is certainly something that were this to happen and that's still quite speculative at this point, could definitely be something that could have an effect on the election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So how many Long Sherman are there compared to how many consumers are there at large?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right. I mean, that's a fair question. And I think, I'm sure the Harris campaign, just purely politics, they want no part of a strike here. They want no part of a protracted strike. This could be really problematic for the economy and messy. They want this resolved.\par \par \uc2 They're trying to sort of bring back some of the labor vote because we've seen that hemorrhaging and the Trump campaign gaining support there. So they're in a bit of a bind politically, but they would love for this to be resolved already yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: You got to wonder, didn't the Biden administration learn the first time around when the ports were having real problems with supply chain?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, I mean, there's a lot of political risk. Biden has trumpeted his relationship with unions as a major theme of his presidency, but he didn't get the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  or Harris didn't get the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  endorsement. And it sounds like he's not willing to kind of work the phones and actually try to cut a last minute deal given his close relationships with a lot of these blue collar unions.\par \par \uc2 So this has a lot of political ramifications for Harris. If the economy takes a hit right before the election, would not be a good signal.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: It is important to note that there are other members of his administration, the White House said today, other senior White House officials who are on the phones while Biden himself has not gotten involved. They're on the phones with both sides at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Want to go over this real quick because we understand that Israel has begun a limited ground incursion into southern Lebanon. This is what the Wall Street Journal Board said about the takeout of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  last week. Israel strike Friday against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  justified defensive act against an enemy backed by Iran and bent on the Jewish state's destruction.\par \par \uc2 It's also a lesson for the Biden administration in how to deter an enemy using military force instead of hopeless pleading for restraint and deescalation. Guy is Israel really kind of showing the way here?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes, through strength. It's what people in that neighborhood understand. And they have brought a world of hurt down on a lot of their enemies. And frankly, our enemies watching this wishy washy sort of tsking of the Israelis coming out of Washington and the Biden Harris administration is frankly embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 What America should be saying to Israel is, thank you, well done. Keep going and we will help as we can because the people that they're taking out not only want to destroy the Jewish state, they are stained with blood of Americans. So this is a great service Israel is performing for the world.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So Josh, Biden was asked, what do you think about what Israel is doing? It says, I'm looking forward to a ceasefire. Michael Oren, who is the former Israeli ambassador to the U.S., was on my show earlier today and said this is an opportunity to completely changed the whole face of the Middle east. Why stop now?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: It's something of a muddled message from both Biden and the Harris campaign. Look, this is a moment of celebration. And if you look at the statements by both the president and the vice president, they celebrated the death of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader. But at the same time, they're like Israel. We don't want you to go too far. We don't want you to finish the job against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, we've interviewed a lot of Republicans and Democrats who have cheered Israel's operation. A little bit unusual for the White House not to get behind it fully. It's a real political opportunity to show that they're fully behind the Israelis.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, well, we'll see where this goes as this operation begins. Gang, thanks very much. Francesca, great to see you again. It's been a long time. All right.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, the crowd was definitely a bit louder for me at the end. I could feel them behind me as much as they were Kivo (ph) at the start, but it was fun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Cliff divers from around the world gathered in Turkey to showcase their best stunts. Women jump from 69 feet, men from 89. Look at this. Into the Mediterranean Sea for the top title at the Cliff Diving World Series. Winners included a man from Great Britain and a woman from Australia. Somehow I don't know how they do it, but they all made it look very, very easy.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on Special Report, a preview of the vice presidential debate between democratic nominee \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance. You can watch the debate simulcast in Fox News. We'll also have special coverage before and after the debate --\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241001ek9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Port Worker Strike?; Israel Continues Strikes in Lebanon; Trump Visits Georgia; North Carolina Authorities Update Status of Hurricane Recovery Efforts\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeff Flock, Arnon Mishkin, Mike Tobin, Steve Bender, Alexandria  Hoff, Steve Harrigan, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,504 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ROY COOPER\uc2  (D-NC): Owners who are making their hotels available to first responders. We went out to Baptists on Mission, and water, food being served to people.\par \par \uc2 So, everyone is pulling together to meet the needs of people in Western North Carolina, but we know there's a long way to go. And we're here today to talk a little bit about that. So, first, I want to recognize Will Ray, the head, the director of North Carolina Emergency Management.\par \par \uc2 Will.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM RAY, NORTH CAROLINA EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT DIRECTOR: Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon, everyone. As the governor said, we're here with FEMA today to really take an opportunity to survey the damage not just here in Buncombe County, but in the Western counties that have been so significantly impacted by this event.\par \par \uc2 We currently have 15 county EOCs that remain open with 31 states of emergency in place. Of the 92 search-and-rescue assets that the governor mentioned, three of those are federal teams, 22 are from other states, and 54 are organic to North Carolina, and really cannot overstate the importance of partnership both here in North Carolina, as well as the support that we have had from our colleagues in other states, as well as the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Today, we are continuing to ramp up pushes of commodities, particularly water and MREs, as well as fuel and other resources, into the impacted communities. We are increasing the number of assets we have on the road and in the air to be able to make those moves, as well as working with FEMA to be able to sustain those levels over the next couple of weeks as we look over the horizon at what the duration of this event and the recovery could be.\par \par \uc2 We currently have three feeding sites open. Those are in Buncombe, McDowell and Watauga. We did visit one of them earlier today, as the governor mentioned. Currently, over 7,000 North Carolinians have already taken an opportunity to register for FEMA individual assistance and there is already money flowing into people's pockets, again, to start getting them off on the right foot and assist them in their recovery.\par \par \uc2 We have a state medical support shelter that is also open to assist both with decompression of our health care facilities, but also long-term cares that may have had damage and may need to move patients into a safer and secure location. And we're also standing up a second location that can be scaled up significantly should we -- be needed.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, our confirmed fatalities for this event have increased to 33 over the day and in into the evening yesterday. As the governor said, we are seeing an increase in capacity and capability in the communications sector across Western North Carolina. We still have a ways to go, but we have 16 911 centers that are still currently degraded with fallback plans in place.\par \par \uc2 That's an improvement from what we have seen over the last couple of days. Lastly, we have 121 systems that currently have boil-water advisories. And of those, 33, like we have discussed before, are in need of their testing to clear those advisories. And we have 36 systems that currently have no power.\par \par \uc2 We're working with our federal partners, as well as our partners here at the state, on getting the right resources in to be able to bring these critical water systems back online.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, I would just say thank you again to the county, the city, Mission, all the partners here in this jurisdiction that are doing such incredible work to care for the needs of this community and of Western North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: We have been grateful to have the FEMA administrator with us all day, and she's going to be here for a while. She's gotten to see firsthand what's happening here in North Carolina and has gotten to hear some needs directly from people.\par \par \uc2 So, FEMA Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Administrator.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , FEMA ADMINISTRATOR: Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon, everybody.\par \par \uc2 First, I just wanted to start by saying, on behalf of the Biden/Harris administration, our deepest sympathies go out to those who have lost loved ones, those who have incurred damages as a result of this storm. And at the president's direction, we are going to be here as long as it takes to finish this response and continue through the recovery.\par \par \uc2 As you heard this -- from the governor, this is a historic storm, with catastrophic impacts across many communities. But I want the people of North Carolina to know that we have the resources here in North Carolina to help with this response, to begin the recovery. We will continue to send additional resources in. So far, we have over 1,200 personnel from across the federal family supporting the response efforts.\par \par \uc2 That includes things like our search-and-rescue teams, our medical teams from Health and Human Services, the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  that are doing assessments of water systems and power infrastructure, and we have our incident management assistance teams that are embedded at the state emergency operations center, as well as in the county or the local communities, so we can have seamless communication with those on the ground in the impacted areas, as well as with the state, so we can move those resources as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 I was able to see some of the devastation today while I was with the governor. And we're already starting to think ahead about the resources that might be needed as the recovery is going to continue or even to provide some rest and relief for the responders that have been working so hard day in and day out since this storm passed.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be bringing in additional DOD medical teams to help support some of the long-term sustainable needs to run some of the medical facilities. We will continue to bring in additional search-and-rescue resources as needed. We're going to continue to also bring in the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  to start planning -- they have already started planning, but more resources for the debris removal, as this is going to have a significant debris removal mission.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest thing that I can say right now is, again, at the direction of the president, he has asked me to make sure that we have every federal resource available to support the people of North Carolina and thinking about those resources in perhaps a way that we wouldn't traditionally use them.\par \par \uc2 And he has directed me to stay as long as it takes to ensure that all of our federal family are here supporting North Carolina and we're meeting the needs that the governor has identified. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you, Administrator.\par \par \uc2 Our North Carolina National Guard has played a significant role both in search-and-rescue and in the delivery of commodities. I'm going to recognize the adjutant general of the North Carolina National Guard, Major General Todd Hunt.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. TODD HUNT, NORTH CAROLINA NATIONAL GUARD: Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 And, first of all, I want to thank the National Guardsmen for their dedication and service for the state of North Carolina. I'd also like to thank the Asheville Airport for being our partner in helping distribute commodities through our airlift, which you see behind me.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the National Guard, we have over almost 800 people on duty for this specific event. We have -- our primary responsibility and our primary focus right now is still search-and-rescue or recovery and then a relocation followed by commodities.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we brought in 100,000 gallons -- 100,000 pounds of water and commodities on a C-17. We have also done 100,000 pounds through our helicopter lifts that we have. We continue to service the area. We have hit 18 -- I'm sorry -- eight counties within the region already with commodities. And we're going to continue to push soldiers into the region to help the citizens of the Western North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: One of the major issues we have is ingress and egress and making sure that we clear roads. We still have some communities that are cut off.\par \par \uc2 Our \uc2 North Carolina Department of Transportation\uc2  has been working hard to clear roads and to get roads back open. I'm going to call on our secretary of transportation, Joey Hopkins.\par \par \uc2 JOEY HOPKINS, NORTH CAROLINA SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon, everyone. First of all, our hearts go out to everyone here in Western North Carolina. We have employees that are directly impacted by this,as well as everybody else that has friends and families.\par \par \uc2 We have at this time over 300 roads closed due to damage, landslides, rockslides, fallen trees, mud on the road. Some roads are breached. Some bridges are gone. Right now, I-40 in the western part of the state is probably the worst damage we have. There's about four -- a four-mile section where we lost the Eastbound 40.\par \par \uc2 We are hopeful that we can open 40 on the east side of Asheville in the coming days. Right now, that is open, but it's only open to people responding to the storm. It's not open to the public at this time.\par \par \uc2 The damage is severe and we're continuing to tell folks, if you don't have a reason to be in North Carolina, do not travel on the roads of Western North Carolina. We do not want you here if you don't live here and you're not helping with the storm. So that will continue to be our message pushing forward in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 If you have been on the map apps, they are also sending that same message out. So we will continue saying that. This is an all-hands-on-deck type of event. We have over 1,600 employees working now. We have over 73 contract crews helping us also. And that number is growing every day. We have crews coming from other states.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, Florida is sending crews up here to help us, and they're also bringing some assets, like temporary bridges, to help us make temporary repairs. So we're doing that.\par \par \uc2 And I do want to say again that many of our crews that are working, they have not been home. They don't know what their own damage is. And due to communication issues, they have not been able to find out from their own families how they're doing.\par \par \uc2 So I'm very thankful and grateful for their dedication as public servants in the state of North Carolina, and my hearts and thoughts and prayers go out to them too.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to help, we have also secured a location for housing for our employees because some of the hotels either don't have power or water or the ones that do don't have rooms. So that will be set up in the next day or so for about 250 people, and we're working on that.\par \par \uc2 And I also want to echo what the governor and others have said. I want to thank our many partners, along with our staff, that helped to alert people, that helped to respond to storms and downed trees. That goes from the North Carolina National Guard to the state Highway Patrol, local law enforcement, first responders, and even private citizens are helping clear trees.\par \par \uc2 We learned today that Mission Hospital has also helped to clear trees from roads, so their employees can get to the hospital to help serve the critical care needs of our people in Western North Carolina. And, lastly, I want to thank our federal partners at FEMA and FHWA.\par \par \uc2 You can see FEMA boots on the ground here, and we're grateful for that and grateful for their assistance. I have been in contact with our FHWA division administrator and also the acting FHWA administrator too. And I'm grateful for their help.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the most catastrophic events we have ever seen in North Carolina. And I just ask for your patience as we continue to try to solve the transportation issues here in North Carolina. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: We're also concerned about our health care facilities, our hospitals, our nursing homes. We have with us today Secretary of the Department of Health and Human Services \uc2 Kody Kinsley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KODY KINSLEY\uc2 , NORTH CAROLINA HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY: Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 As Governor Cooper said, we have been focused at the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  on ensuring the stability of our health care system here in the region. We have 22 acute care hospitals in Western North Carolina. As of last night, all had had their power restored back onto the grid.\par \par \uc2 All of them had generator power during the storms, so were able to continue operations and supporting their communities. And we have been in direct, near hourly contact with those facilities to support a number of resource requests.\par \par \uc2 And with support from the North Carolina National Air Guard and emergency management, we have been able to get food, water, fuel and other supplies to those health care facilities to make sure that they stay operating.\par \par \uc2 Today, we went to Mission Hospital to see firsthand the heroic efforts of the individuals of that hospital, over 1,000 staff that have been sleeping there every day to ensure they could continue operations in supporting their community.\par \par \uc2 We continue to work to bring them resources. They had their power back on Saturday night. And I know we're all focused working very hard to restore water resources in that community, but meanwhile bringing in temporary solutions to support water there.\par \par \uc2 We're also very focused on our long-term care community and facilities, skilled nursing homes, adult care homes. And we're proactively working with our insurers to identify individuals that are at home, on oxygen, and other medical supplies to make sure we can proactively reach out to them, support emergency management to bring resources to them.\par \par \uc2 We have activated our patient transfer system so that hospitals all across the state are stepping up to bring individuals in need of critical care out of hospitals in Western North Carolina if they can't get the care immediately.\par \par \uc2 Our entire department has rallied every resource to make the difference. We're so grateful for the work of FEMA, who immediately brought forth the disaster medical assistance team on the ground at Mission Hospital. And we are in this for the long haul and into recovery.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: We also have with us the secretary Department of Public Safety Eddie Buffaloe, our head of the Highway Patrol, Freddy Johnson. And Congressman Chuck Edwards is here as well.\par \par \uc2 We will be glad to take your questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Well, first, we knew that this was going to be a significant event, and local officials told people to get out of low-lying areas.\par \par \uc2 We also know there are going to be a significant number of fatalities here. The devastation was beyond belief. And even when you prepare for something like this, this is something that's never happened before in Western North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Search-and-rescue teams are continuing to work. We even talked with some people who are still hoping to get and talk with their people. And as phone service comes back up, we will know a little more. I'm going to ask Will Ray to respond specifically to see if he knows that number.\par \par \uc2 You know of a number of people. OK. We don't know the number that has been requested. But we ask people to call 211 if they're trying to get a hold of somebody. Don't call 911 right now on that kind of issue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Next question (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You mentioned progress being made on cell service out (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 COOPER: So we're getting reports from \uc2 AT&T\uc2  and \uc2 Verizon\uc2 . Their initial problems were significant, in that landslides cut main fiber lines for them.\par \par \uc2 We have asked them and pushed them to bring in their deployable assets. So more of them are getting into the area. And we're getting reports that more and more have cell service.\par \par \uc2 Will, would you want to add something to that?\par \par \uc2 RAY: I would say progress is the key. We know we still have a lot of work to do, again, but I think we are pushing our commercial providers, as well as other partners across the commercial space, to bring in mobile assets of a variety of types to be able to provide to critical sites, to local EOCs, to some of our public safety answering points or other critical facilities.\par \par \uc2 Again, they are very integral to what we do. They are a huge piece of it. We know communications is important for everybody. And so we're going to continue to push and encourage there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Next question right here.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Governor.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What do you say to the residents who feel that the local and federal response wasn't fast enough following this storm?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: So, first, people are working around the clock to provide help to them right now, food, water, working hard to make sure that we have got shelters open all across this area, and working to get power back o.\par \par \uc2 When you have a situation where communities are completely cut off, when first responders can't even get in because water is still there and rivers are rising right now as we speak, a lot of work is done. What we want to tell people is that more help is on the way and help is continuing.\par \par \uc2 This is our main mission right now. hand this is a massive coordinated effort to help this area, both in the short term and in the long term.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Next question right here.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Yes I'm going to let Director Ray answer that issue.\par \par \uc2 RAY: Certainly.\par \par \uc2 So I think, as we think about the size and scale of event, that's why we have taken the approach that we have, especially for distribution of water and food. So where we are able to get ground routes in reliably and safely, we are using those routes.\par \par \uc2 There's -- as Secretary Hopkins mentioned, there is a ground route in through on I-40. There is a ground route in via I-26. We know there are many areas of the western part of the state that are still -- that still have major access challenges, which is why we have put in place the operation you see here of moving -- using aircraft to move commodities into those impacted areas.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we are trying to surge as much of the commodities into the entire impacted region. Yesterday, as an example, either via ground or by air, 25 -- 30 counties had commodities delivered to them yesterday. We know that we're not going to be able to meet right now out of the gate the universal need that everyone has.\par \par \uc2 That's why we are surging up both our distribution. So we're putting additional drivers on to be able to get more trucks on the road to get commodities in faster. We're continuing to increase the number of aircraft that are operating and we're working with our partners in FEMA to increase the amount of commodities that we're getting pushed from them every day.\par \par \uc2 Currently, we're on a cycle now of 40 trailers a day of food and water, in addition to what we're procuring on the open market or have in our warehouses, that we are working to distribute into the entire impacted area across North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 RAY: I'm sorry. Say that one more time.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 RAY: So I think the impact to our water systems across the state, especially here in Buncombe and Asheville, are significant.\par \par \uc2 As the administrator said, we're using some of our partnerships with the U.S. Army Corps and FEMA to get some teams on the ground to determine, what is the long-range solution to get these water systems back online, as well as plan some of the mid-range solutions to get the hospital, other critical infrastructure back online so that folks do have running water?\par \par \uc2 I think we are -- and back to the previous question, I will say we have continued to deliver water both here to Buncombe County, to support Buncombe, as well as the city of Asheville. We know there is more need there. We know there is more than they need in both food and water, and we will continue to surge those resources in to be able to support that local jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 KINSLEY: Right now, Mission Hospital is bringing in upwards of 20 trucks a day of water to support the hospital's operations, in addition to package sterile water, so they can continue operations.\par \par \uc2 We know it's a huge challenge for the hospital. And we're working actively with them to determine other potential strategies. As Director Ray said, midterm solutions that will get running pressurized water into the facility as soon as possible is a top priority for us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Next question. (OFF-MIKE) Great.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's the time we have got.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, you have been listening to a briefing by North Carolina officials.\par \par \uc2 The Helene death toll has topped about 100 across some six states, a third of that essentially coming from North Carolina alone and specifically the town of Asheville, North Carolina, an idyllic, beautiful community battered and looking like a war zone today.\par \par \uc2 Help is on the way. And the president, we're told, is making sure that help does get on the way. The former president also doing his part to show support for victims of this incredible storm. He was in the Valdosta, Georgia, a little bit earlier.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Steve Harrigan, though, who is in Asheville, North Carolina. That town has a long way to go to come back -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Certainly does, Neil, conditions here pretty rough, but some signs of encouragement from those North Carolina officials, on, the one hand talking about help on the way, citing some statistics.\par \par \uc2 They said at least 100,000 pounds of aid brought in by cargo plane today, also another 100,000 pounds of aid, food, water, generators, brought in by helicopter as well. So help is on the way, but some really disconcerting facts there as well when you really listen to that press conference, 16 911 call centers in Western North Carolina still not functioning.\par \par \uc2 That means if you had electric power to charge your phone, which most people here don't, if you had a cell phone signal, which most people here don't, there would be no one to call. No one would be on the other end of that phone. That's got to worry some people.\par \par \uc2 As far as running water goes, it could be weeks until there's running water back here. And I think the big overall concern is that this storm hit so far away, 500 miles away. You really get the sense that not only officials, but a lot of locals were taken by surprise by the enormity of this destruction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The water was like right at my feet level. And then I woke up my roommate, and I grabbed all my things. And by the time we were - - 20 minutes later, we were leaving. It was maybe six inches, a foot high.\par \par \uc2 So we went through the window. And it just kept rising. By the time I made, it back it was maybe to my waist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: You hear the reports about everything being brought in by air.\par \par \uc2 In large part, that's because so many roads are down, three of the major four highways that come into Asheville down, 300 roads down across the state overall, so really tough to get in supplies when roads are either washed out or blocked -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Steve, you have covered so many disasters and war zones and hurricanes, tornadoes. But what made this so unique in the case of Helene is, when this first started and looked like it was going to be a threat to Florida, less so going north, and we look at the six states of devastation, particularly in North Carolina, or that one North Carolina town would account for roughly a third of the fatalities, I don't think anyone saw that coming.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: No.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when can you think of another storm where day six was far worse, far more deadly, far more destructive than day one? This happened 500 miles away. I covered the storm in Florida. I thought it was over. I was at home. And they said, oh, you got to go, because it's much worse actually now, six days later.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wild stuff.\par \par \uc2 Steve, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 I want to update you on this excessive rain risk you have heard for places like Raleigh, North Carolina, and Richmond, Virginia, parts of Tennessee, half the state of South Carolina. So you could see why six states have all but claimed disaster territories and seeking disaster relief.\par \par \uc2 Five of the six have already done so. So we will keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 Georgia was among those hard-hit as well, and that's where former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  visited earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Alexandria Hoff more from Valdosta -- Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the former president said he came here to Valdosta to show solidarity, a signal of solidarity with all those living in Georgia who are struggling and all victims who are living in Helene's wrath or wake right now.\par \par \uc2 He also said he brought supplies with him like water and gasoline and did so in partnership with Franklin Graham's relief organization, \uc2 Samaritan's Purse\uc2 . The former president held a moment of silence for lives lost and gave remarks in the rubble of an antique mall in historic Valdosta. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: As you know, our country is in the final weeks of a hard-fought national election. But in a time like this, when a crisis hits, when our fellow citizens cry out in need, none of that matters. We're not talking about politics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Maybe not in that very moment, but Trump has been critical of the Biden administration's response, saying this after landing in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The governor is doing a very good job. He's having a hard time getting the president on the phone. I guess they're not being responsive. The federal government is not being responsive, but they're having a very hard time getting the president on the phone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But Governor Brian Kemp said earlier that he did speak with President Biden yesterday after initially missing his call. As of this morning, he said that he and Vice President Harris were playing phone tag.\par \par \uc2 Next hour, the vice president, she will receive a briefing from FEMA on the impacts of Hurricane Helene and the federal response that's taking place. She had canceled scheduled campaign events out West to return to Washington, D.C. That's something else that former President Trump had been critical of, that she and President Biden were not in Washington while this storm made landfall.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump also said today that he talked with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  about getting Starlink capabilities to those who are still struggling with communicating with their loved ones right now, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, there's got to be a way to fix that, Alexandria. If you can't communicate with anyone, maybe Starlink or something that you can hook up with people via satellite is the way to do it. But it's so sad.\par \par \uc2 Alexandria, great coverage as always.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Steve Bender. And don't say Steve didn't warn you about all of this and what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 And we're not out of the woods yet, my friend. So, Steve, what are we looking at here?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BENDER, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Well, Neil, we have so much activity out in the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, it's deep out into the Atlantic. We will go through some of those named storms, check them off the list. And then we will talk about that area to watch, which is of concern. There's Kirk, right, the newly named storm, likely to become a very powerful storm, Cat 3 potential, but notice the steering mechanism takes it deep out into the Atlantic, just like Joyce and Isaac, so no threat to land.\par \par \uc2 Now let's address that area to watch. This is still in that same atmosphere pattern that spawned Helene. It's the Central American gyre, this area in orange showing us that potential. Here's the good news, Neil, the latest model trends slowing this down and decreasing that likelihood of actually development.\par \par \uc2 So, when we look at the time frame, areas in orange Friday through Sunday. So we're looking at this weekend, the Bay of Campeche, the Southern Gulf. That's where we could potentially see a named storm, if not, just widespread showers setting up.\par \par \uc2 The concern, though, is Friday through early next week, these are the same areas that took the historic landfall from Helene as it came in, 140 miles per hour. So spots like Cedar Key could get a very heavy rain, Apalachicola, those bays, still very heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 I know there's extensive coverage on the Carolinas, Tennessee, Georgia, even Southern Virginia, rightfully so, but still major damage in Florida, and with rain on the way, something that we will be watching closely here at FOX Weather and making sure that we talk about well in advance, so people can prep for that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Steve, what's remarkable about this -- and you and I have touched on it when -- in prior discussions of this storm -- is, it caused the most damage when it wasn't really of hurricane status anymore, barely tropical storm status.\par \par \uc2 And it reminded me some years back in New Jersey and the New York metropolitan area with the -- with what Superstorm Sandy did. That was a nor'easter.\par \par \uc2 BENDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, as you were reminding viewers, we pay attention to the magnitude or the severity of the storm and what category it falls under.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, that matters very little. If you have got two-foot storm surges, as you reported, you have got serious problems.\par \par \uc2 BENDER: Yes, Neil, the category is only the wind, right? That's one threat out of many.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BENDER: Inland flooding that we experience in the Carolinas and Tennessee, that is actually the deadliest aspect of storms.\par \par \uc2 So that's the concern with this is when you have tropical moisture from the Gulf, and, by the way, the Western Atlantic aided in some of that moisture hitting the Eastern Appalachian Mountains. That allows that rain rate to be excessive.\par \par \uc2 And we know, Neil, when the rain falls in the mountains, it comes rushing down into the valleys. And, oftentimes, that's where you have those beautiful communities like Chimney Rock, Asheville, Boone, and so many others that we're first getting or now getting into to tell those stories and try to help with that recovery effort.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I want to thank you and your colleagues, Steve, at FOX Weather. You guys, just yeoman's work and great help as well.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we're getting word that Israel is now warning residents in Beirut suburbs to evacuate because of imminent strikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets.\par \par \uc2 We have got Mike Tobin in Haifa with more -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, the new phase of this war is under way. We're live in the north of Israel, and we will bring you all the details.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Through hurricanes and wars and a growing global threat, look at this, on the day, on the week, on the overall month and quarter, all the major averages advancing. They worry not.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Israel seems to be indicating an attack could be imminent right now in Lebanon and has warned individuals in that area to clear out and protect themselves, because they're not done going after \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , or, for that matter, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , a key \uc2 Hamas\uc2  official also killed today in multiple raids.\par \par \uc2 We get the latest right now from Mike Tobin in Haifa -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: And, Neil, the artillery fire started really about two hours ago.\par \par \uc2 And, generally, what you get with the artillery fire, what we have seen the Israelis do in the past is they soften the battlefield. What I mean, by softening the battlefield is, they blast an area. And anything that isn't a combatant will generally get out of the way.\par \par \uc2 And there you're looking at some video of the ground forces that have been massing there. Two brigades of reservists have been called up, a complement of armor with those Merkava 4 tanks in the foreground, also artillery and armored personnel carriers.\par \par \uc2 They presumably are going in on the ground now. We do have some live rems (ph). I'd like to get those to you. But we have -- one thing I should note is -- there's that live rem. And that is in the town -- it looks like it's showing Beirut, but it should be a live rem of the town of Metula.\par \par \uc2 And what we have been able to see are flashes on the Lebanese side of the border, indication of the artillery or different types of strikes that are going in, and also some of the defiant rockets coming out of the south of Lebanon, as the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters are still digging in.\par \par \uc2 What we're getting from U.S. officials who've been speaking to their Israeli counterparts is that this is not going to be like the 2006 invasion into Lebanon that lasted for 34 days. This is said to be a limited ground incursion that will target \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  infrastructure and is supposed to wrap up shortly after that.\par \par \uc2 But it is a significant chapter, a new chapter in this conflict. For the first time since the rocket fire started almost a year ago out of Lebanon, Israeli ground forces are at least on the verge of going in, if they're not already in on the ground -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin, be safe.\par \par \uc2 As Mike alluded to, all of this happening very close to the one-year anniversary of the attack on Israel by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . That claimed more than 1,200 lives. And of all the stories and people I had the opportunity to talk with over that roughly one year, I was on the air live when that horror was unfolding out of the Middle East on that Saturday, none moved me more than this story and this woman and her family and her mission.\par \par \uc2 Liz Hirsh Naftali is her name. The book is "Saving Abigail: The True Story of the Abduction and Rescue of a Three-Year-Old Hostage."\par \par \uc2 I'm honored to have you, Liz, and thank you, on this day of all days, to be talking to you. But I actually think it's very important that we are. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 LIZ HIRSH NAFTALI, AUTHOR, "SAVING ABIGAIL: THE TRUE STORY OF THE ABDUCTION AND RESCUE OF A THREE-YEAR-OLD HOSTAGE": I'm OK. I mean, it's been, it's almost a year. So it's really rough.\par \par \uc2 And with everything that's going on today, it makes it even harder because we still have hostages. And we know that these are humans and these are people that need to come home.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, there are 96, some say 97, by last count. Bottom line, we don't know for sure all their whereabouts, but fears that their lives might very well be in danger.\par \par \uc2 I always think of your beautiful great niece, Abigail, and what she had to endure that day almost a year ago.\par \par \uc2 HIRSH NAFTALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Both her parents killed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . She's taken hostage. And lo and behold, 50-some-odd days later, she's freed thanks to your efforts. How the heck did that happen?\par \par \uc2 HIRSH NAFTALI: Well, it's -- it was a lot of work from a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I realized when we learned that Abigail had actually survived -- because, originally, she was in her father's arms when he was gunned down and murdered by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  trying to escape. And her brother and sister, who were running next to their father, after they had all three kids seen their mother murdered, he was murdered and she was under him.\par \par \uc2 And the 6- and 9-year-old thought she had been killed, so they ran and locked themselves in this closet for 14 hours before they could be rescued. So when we learned that Abigail had actually crawled out and gone to a neighbor and had been with the neighbor and her three children and then kidnapped, and I learned that, I'd already come back to America.\par \par \uc2 I was in Israel on October 7. And it was the worst day, one of the worst -- I mean, probably the worst day I can imagine in my life and the worst day for Israel and for Abigail and for our family.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things I realized was, when I came back -- we thought in the beginning this was going to end. You just thought these hostages would be released. And what we learned is that wasn't going to happen. And so it just became like this call to action.\par \par \uc2 And I found myself in a position, and I write about in the book, where this woman from North Carolina, Sophia (ph), just called me and said, I'm going to D.C. Come and tell your story.\par \par \uc2 And that was the beginning of this movement of so many people telling their stories so that people could understand that we all had to come together to make this happen. And, thankfully and miraculously, 105 women and children were released at the end of November. And Abigail was on the third day, like you said, one of those people that was -- little kids that was released.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, she was 3 years old at the time, 4 now. Does she remember any of that day, Liz? I know she has a 6-year-old brother, 11- year-old sister. I'm sure they do. But what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 HIRSH NAFTALI: Look, she knows that she was kidnapped. She knows she was in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 She knows who she was with. She knows who was guarding her. She's 4 years old. So we do not pry, and we sort of are waiting for her to talk about it. But she's very, very smart. And she knows exactly what happened that day. And her 6- and 9-year-old brother and sister know what happened.\par \par \uc2 And these are children that, one year later, thank God, they are safe. But they don't have a mom and a dad, and they don't have a home. They live with their aunt and uncle. And they have a beautiful, beautiful family now with their other three cousins. But they know what happened to them. They know the terror of what happened on October 7.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I can't imagine myself, Liz -- and you put new definition in great-aunt, by the way. But when you saw and got -- appreciated the gravity of what happened that day, I believe you were doing a walk, and were going to do your exercises when all these sirens are going off, and one after another.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, you have realized the magnitude of what's going on. Little did you know it would affect your family. But here we are a year later. The war still rages. It could be expanding right now with these strikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . How do you feel about going after these terrorist groups the way Israel is right now and the way it has with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HIRSH NAFTALI: Well, there's two parts to this. One, these are terror groups, and they have no -- they cannot exist.\par \par \uc2 And Israel has to protect itself, and Israel has a right to defend itself, and I believe that these terrorists who are responsible for the murder of Abigail's family, 1,200 people -- and, as we know, in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's case, it's common that they are the responsible for over 40 years of murdering Americans, people all over the world, their own people, and Israelis.\par \par \uc2 So that is something that must be dealt with. And I'm not an expert, but I support that they need to be eradicated. But in the same breath, I also know that we have over 100 hostages. Seven of them are Americans. And they have to -- we have to get a plan, a diplomatic plan for their release.\par \par \uc2 And so while there is this war going on, and I understand it -- and, by the way, it will also have to end with some sort of diplomacy, because that's how wars end, and that's how people can move forward. I focus right now and I ask everybody to understand that there are hostages, real people, children, young people, boys, men, women, grandparents, and, I said, seven Americans.\par \par \uc2 And they need to be released. So, in this moment, I feel like it's been a year, and there have been many opportunities, and I really call upon the leadership in Israel, I call about -- I call on Prime Minister Netanyahu and his coalition and all of the people who can make this deal happen, because no matter how much Israel must protect itself, and I support that, these people need to come back for Israel, for their families to move on, and for Israel to move on, and for it to heal.\par \par \uc2 Because until they are returned, October 7 lays very heavy on the souls and the hearts of the Israeli people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: These anti-Israeli protests still pop up and continue around the world. I don't know if we will see more as a result of these Israeli actions against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . How do you feel about those?\par \par \uc2 HIRSH NAFTALI: Well, one of the reasons that I wrote this book was because I wanted people to know the truth.\par \par \uc2 I needed people in America, people around the world, to know really what happened. And Abigail's story, as you said, is just so -- like, it's so -- it's horrific, and it's also one of those stories where it's like there's resilience and this hope.\par \par \uc2 And so, when I wrote this book, my goal was to basically say, this is what happens. So those people that are denying and protesting, understand that, on October 6, there was a cease-fire. \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , a terrorist organization, broke that in the most deadly way by 3,000 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror -- fighters coming in and killing and rampaging and raping and kidnapping.\par \par \uc2 So I needed this story to be told. But I also want to remind everybody that's listening, the minute these hostages are released, then we have an opportunity also for the fighting to stop.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HIRSH NAFTALI: And then we have an opportunity for the people in Gaza who are suffering to be taken care of.\par \par \uc2 So, when you ask that question, I think that people don't really understand that we're all in this together to bring home these hostages to help the people in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 Liz, you're a remarkable woman. You have a remarkable great-niece, and you're a remarkable great-aunt.\par \par \uc2 And this is a remarkable book. And I defy anyone who has a chance to read it -- it's only about 200 pages. If you finish that with a dry eye, then there could be something wrong with you, "Saving Abigail: The True Story of the Abduction and Rescue of a Three-Year-Old Hostage."\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, on top of all the other calamities this day, now talk that tonight, at midnight, we could see a dockworkers strike that could affect tens of thousands of workers and mean everything that you have been waiting for or want to get in time for Christmas is going to be late, delayed, or well, not at all. So, even Santa's a little bummed out about that.\par \par \uc2 To Jeff Flock right now on what's at stake. He's in Newark, New Jersey -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Neil, I have breaking news for you.\par \par \uc2 A source is now telling the \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2  that the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  has just rejected the latest offer from the shippers and has not made a counteroffer. That makes it a virtual certainty that there will be a strike tonight at midnight.\par \par \uc2 Behind me, you see the frantic pace that has been carried out here near the ports around the country today, frankly, as the shippers try to get the containers off the ports, because if they're trapped there when the strike happens, they might be there for a long time.\par \par \uc2 We have got pictures of the line of trucks that was trying to get into the port to get these truck -- these containers out. I want to give a quick word to Thomas Villacampa, who's a V.P. of IMC Logistics.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS VILLACAMPA, IMC LOGISTICS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: What's going to happen?\par \par \uc2 VILLACAMPA: Tough day ahead. We're anxious for both parties to come together, put a resolution to this and keep things moving, you know?\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: The -- Neil, the U.S. Commerce -- U.S. Chamber of Commerce, I should say, sending a letter to President Biden today: "It would be unconscionable to allow a contract dispute to inflict such a shock to our economy. Taft-Hartley would provide time for both parties in negotiation to reach a deal."\par \par \uc2 That's the labor law that the president could invoke to bring the parties together and stop a strike. It doesn't look like that's going to happen, Neil. And so I leave you with a picture of what's going on now, but what might not be going on for much longer -- sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jeff, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Delayed goods, canceled goods mean more expensive goods. A worry for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as interest rates are coming down? I raised that with Austan Goolsbee, the Chicago Federal Reserve president, earlier. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PRESIDENT, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF CHICAGO: This seems like it fits into my old Chicago motto. I always tell you, Neil, there is no bad weather. There is only bad clothing. And things happen that we're going to have to take into account and deal with by how they affect the conditions.\par \par \uc2 We have gone through in the last year or two a big strike in the auto industry. We have gone through the port collision with the bridge that affected shipping. We went through, in the pandemic, as you know, major problems through -- with shipping and the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 And anything that's a negative supply shock, in our language, that's going to raise the cost of doing business or lead to shortages is something we're just going to have to deal with. And the impacts are never good.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I guess, if it comes to a strike, once one is on, it could be weekslong. And it could go -- to your fears, Austan, it could raise prices.\par \par \uc2 And that could get in the way of this recovery that you said showed a golden path a little more than a few days ago, when you said that. Are you worried about that recovery now?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, look, Central Bank's job is to be worried about everything. And there shouldn't be anything that you can come up with that we haven't at least thought of that it's a possible complication for our plan.\par \par \uc2 That said, we set an interest rate very high more than a year before and held it there. And now we have started reducing that rate because conditions have gotten more back to normal. I mean, if you look at the new inflation numbers, they're coming in relatively pretty close to target.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the unemployment rate and the job market, we have gotten back to a steady state, kind of a sustainable level that's close to what we think of as full employment. And if you think of -- in my mind, I think of it a little bit like the bathtub.\par \par \uc2 You want the water to be the right temperature. If it's too hot, you got to add cold water. But once the bath is the temperature you want it, you got to reduce the amount of cold water you're adding. And so that's why, as I look out over this next year, interest rates coming down is going to be the order of the day, because you want the rates to be normal, just like the other conditions getting back to normal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I just wonder. That cold water, do you need a quarter-point of that, or do you need a half-point of that to sort of deal with it at the next meeting?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Depends what the conditions are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: And the more you're seeing these disruptions -- I'm still pretty hopeful that, if you see a lot of the evidence on just GDP growth and how fast the economy is growing, you're seeing some of the private sector forecasts and the \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2 's GDP now upping their estimates of what this quarter's GDP growth is going to be.\par \par \uc2 But there's some cautionary indicators on the job market side.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: And just the way monetary policy works, as you know, you can't wait for it to happen before you start trying to get yourself into position to react, because there's lags in both the data and how long it takes...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I understand that, Austan, but that sounds like a quarter- point cut. If you had your druthers now, it would be a quarter-point?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I would say the most important thing is not, for one meeting, is it one cut or two cuts at that meeting.\par \par \uc2 The most important thing is, this is a process over a year or more that we're trying to get the rates down to normal to keep conditions at this -- at these decent dual mandate situation that inflation and unemployment are around the target.\par \par \uc2 So, it's got to come down a lot more than 25 basis points over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: It's going to be a lot of cuts.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the statement of economic projections, you see us pretty widely viewed as going to be appropriate by almost everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so what he's saying there, a voting member of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , that interest rates will continue coming down, bumps along the way, including a port strike.\par \par \uc2 I want to raise all of this with Arnon Mishkin. He's our FOX News Decision Desk director.\par \par \uc2 And, Arnon, lo and behold, we have this vice presidential debate tomorrow, this issue, this potential strike, and its jarring effects potentially on the economy, it's going to come up.\par \par \uc2 ARNON MISHKIN, FOX NEWS DECISION DESK DIRECTOR: It will definitely come up, particularly if the strike happens tonight at midnight, which is what they just said.\par \par \uc2 And I think it'll be an excellent tool or excellent opportunity for J.D. Vance to attack \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  for the Biden administration or the Biden/Harris administration's management of the economy.\par \par \uc2 That will be an opportunity. And the question is -- assuming that happens, the question is, how does Walz pivot? How does Walz respond to it? How does Walz bring it back to the issues that Walz wanted to talk about on -- in the debate?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, they will both be reaching out to union workers as well, especially in light of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  deciding not to officially back either candidate, although a lot of the worker bees do like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and a lot of the heads seem to, for the moment, like the history of unions, to really like the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So where do you see this sorting out in a V.P. debate?\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: Well, I think that that's going to be the question, because I think one of the -- J.D. Vance is an excellent debater.\par \par \uc2 He's had enormous experience in recent years, particularly from the Ohio Senate race. And he's done a great job, I think, every Sunday. He practices for this debate every Sunday, because he's on almost every one of the network talk shows talking about...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's true.\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: ... boosting the Trump/Vance ticket. And I think that that's great experience.\par \par \uc2 Tim -- and, plus that, he's a Yale-educated lawyer. He's no slouch. And I think that my prediction, for sure, is that, if you read the transcripts of the debate tomorrow, tomorrow night's debate, you're going to think J.D. Vance wins.\par \par \uc2 Who wins the television show, though, I don't know, because, while Vance is very articulate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has proven to be of a very folksy manner. I have watched some of his debates that he's had running for Congress and running for the governorship of Minnesota. He's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 And he knows -- he's -- if you think about the two charismatic leaders of American politics recently, the two most charismatic guys are \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has been trained, if you...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And likability -- likability matters, to your point.\par \par \uc2 But I did want to get in this notion of we don't usually pay as much attention to V.P. debates as we should. But, of course, we can remember Lloyd Bentsen and Dan Quayle. That was a memorable one. We can remember \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  and her past with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But do they affect the race? Does a vice presidential debate turn the presidential election?\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: Well, I would argue that we have had six presidential elections this century since 2000, and in three of them, I'd argue that the V.P. debate was almost a defining moment.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 , the former defense secretary...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: ... basically beat Joe Lieberman incredibly effectively, and the entire frame of the pollings after that changed, and to Bush's favor.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, real quickly...\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: In 2012 -- not against Palin.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... real quickly, you think that this could move the needle, potentially, when the needle's like right in the middle, right?\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: Absolutely, particularly because they're both going after the other person's strengths.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MISHKIN: Vance -- I mean, Walz is there to attack -- or to get more support in rural America, which is Trump's strength, and J.D. Vance has to get one of Trump's weaknesses -- or boost one of Trump's weaknesses, which is those sort of more moderate Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will see.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240930ek9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Interview Senate Foreign Relations Committee Member, Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE); Interview With Former Secretary Of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 ; \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Confirms Death of Leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  Following IDF Strike; Iran Condemns Israeli Attacks in Lebanon; Harris Supports Getting Rid of Senate Filibuster to Pass Abortion Rights Bill; Harris Blames Trump for Tanking Border Bill; Final Weeks of Campaign 2024; Battle for Control of the Senate; ICE: More Than 425K Migrants Convicted Of Crimes That Are Not Being Detained; Fox Poll: Trump Leads Harris On Immigration, Border y At Least 15 Points In AZ, GA, NC, PA; U.S. Dockworkers On Verge Of Massive Strike; One Man Show Shares the Life and Legacy of Lincoln\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Mike Pompeo, Mark Meredith, Rich Edson, Mollie  Hemingway, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 9,641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Israel takes out the head of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and a top Iranian leader in Lebanon. Iran now vowing revenge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HAGARI, IDF SPOKESPERSON: \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is dragging the state of Lebanon and the entire region into a wider escalation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Islamic Republic of Iran will be on the side of Lebanon and resistance by all means.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Fury from Iran after the Israeli airstrikes on the main \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leadership compound in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Senate Foreign Relations Committee member Chris Coons joins us on what may happen ext in the volatile region.\par \par \uc2 Then, J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  about to go head-to-head in their only scheduled faceoff before Election Day. Both VP candidates set to debate Tuesday just weeks before Election Day and with voting already underway in more than 20 states.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk with Trump-Vance surrogate, Senator Katie Britt.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: There are consequential issues at stake in this election and one is the security of our border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Three years after her last visit, Vice President Harris travels to the border trying to close the gap with Trump on immigration.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel weighs in on that and the shocking new number showing there are more than 13,000 non-citizens convicted of homicide apparently roaming free in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BAKULA AS ABE LINCOLN: You can't be serious. You think I could make a formidable candidate for the presidency?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll take you behind the scenes with Scott Bakula playing Lincoln at the theater where an assassin took the life of our 16th president. It's a message for our divided times.\par \par \uc2 Do you see parallels now?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Oh, the parallels are too many to count.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a quick look at your headlines:\par \par \uc2 The Southeast is cleaning up behind the widespread path of destruction from Hurricane Helene. More than 60 people are dead from Florida to Virginia, and that death toll is expected to climb. Early estimates put the damage between $15 billion and $26 billion. Several dams across the region are threatening to break from all the record flooding.\par \par \uc2 If you can help, please visit redcross.org/foxforward to find out how you can donate money to the recovery effort. Or we've got a QR code there on your screen, you can hold up your phone and scan that.\par \par \uc2 More than two and a half million people are still without power. Georgia and the Carolinas particularly hard hit, millions of Americans still displaced and out of their homes. Some of the remnants of Helene are making their way up the East Coast today with more chances of local flooding in many areas.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the Middle East, the Israeli military announcing it has killed yet another top ranking \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  official in an airstrike, capping off a deadly week in Lebanon for the Iran-backed terror group which lost its leader, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , in an Israeli strike in Beirut Friday.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get reaction from Senator Chris Coons who sits on the Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Trey Yingst in Haifa, Israel.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 24 hours, hundreds of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  positions have been targeted across Lebanon, strong explosions rocked the skyline of Beirut this weekend, targeting specifically the stronghold of Dahieh, that was hit with Israeli airstrikes, taking out another \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander Nabil Kaouk, this after Israel confirmed Saturday it killed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This has now given the region a new phase and unpredictable chapter in that same strike a deputy commander in Iran's IRGC was also killed, prompting more fears that Iran could decide to directly retaliate for the killings. Israeli forces remain on high alert for a possible response but a senior defense official told FOX News over the weekend that despite the continued rocket fire from Lebanon, the belief is that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is too disorganized at the moment to launch a serious counterattack of their own.\par \par \uc2 This comes as reports today indicate that Israel is conducting limited minor ground operations into Lebanon in preparation for an expected ground invasion. Thousands of Israeli troops are currently staging on Israel's northern border as President Biden calls for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, is a ground incursion in Lebanon inevitable?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Time for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Will you respond to the missile attacks on the U.S. warships on the Red Sea?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're responding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: While the war unravels further, Lebanese civilians continue to evacuate southern Lebanon and southern Beirut, resulting in the displacement of families in the heart of the Lebanese capital. Some of Lebanon's most vulnerable residents with nowhere to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DALAL DAHER, LEBANESE RESIDENT FROM SOUTHERN BEIRUT (through translator): We are here because in our area, there are strikes and bombings and yesterday's strikes were unbelievable. We had fled before and then went back to our homes.\par \par \uc2 But then the bombing got more and more intense, so we came here waiting for Netanyahu to stop the bombing, so we can go back home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The United States is clearly looking for a diplomatic off-ramp here to avoid a broader regional war across the Middle East and prevent Israel from entering Lebanon on the ground -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst reporting from Israel -- thank you very much, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Delaware Democratic Senator Chris Coons, member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back, Senator. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thanks, Shannon. It's always good to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to start here with what Prime Minister Netanyahu said at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  just days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: As long as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  chooses the path of war, Israel has no choice and Israel has every right to remove this threat and return our citizens to their homes safely, and that's exactly what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He went on to imagine -- ask Americans to imagine what would happen if towns like El Paso and San Diego border towns were taking incoming fire, turned into complete ghost towns, he says. American officials would not stand for that for a single day.\par \par \uc2 And yet this administration continues to call for a ceasefire. You know, incoming has been happening since October 8th.\par \par \uc2 Why should we ask them to stand down? Do you think that's the right thing to do?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, let's be clear, Shannon, Vice President Harris put out a forceful statement as did President Biden that I joined in, saying that \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , the leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , is a murderous terrorist who had American blood on his hands from the attack on the American Marine barracks back in the 1980s and he's killed thousands of innocent civilians, Lebanese, Syrian, as well as Israeli.\par \par \uc2 And as you said in your question, they have been raining rockets and missiles onto northern Israel since October 7th.\par \par \uc2 So I think Israel is justified in their strikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The call for a ceasefire is really because of a concern that a ground invasion of southern Lebanon by the \uc2 IDF\uc2  might well turn into a long and grinding war of attrition and concerns that Iran may well now retaliate. But President Biden and Vice President Harris are clear that we will stand by our ally Israel and defend them against attacks by Iranian proxies or direct attacks by Iran.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, one of your GOP colleagues, Senator Ron Johnson, sees it differently. He says that Israel doesn't have the ability to fully trust us at this time.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he said yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): They always seem to want to throttle back Israel's ability and desire to defend itself. I think it's the very sad fact that Israel doesn't trust the United States under the leadership of President Biden and Vice President Harris. They just can't trust us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They don't have to ask our permission but there are things they're doing we're finding out when they're actually in play or after the fact.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of your colleague's assessment there that they can't trust us?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Ron's statement is just nonsense.\par \par \uc2 No one has been stronger in coming to the defense of Israel, especially after the hateful attacks by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on October 7th than President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . In fact, in the first ever direct attack on Israel by Iran, the Biden-Harris administration coordinated a response by several of our close allies that ensure that none of the 300 missiles and drones that Iran launched at Israel hit any significant target or caused any major damage.\par \par \uc2 We have stood by Israel faithfully and loyally throughout the entire period that there are repeated attacks by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the territory of Israel and by the Houthis on commercial shipping in the Red Sea.\par \par \uc2 So they should trust us. There have been some moments of tension because they don't inform us of what they're doing before they do. There is some question of course about the number of civilian deaths in Gaza as that war has ground on.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that there should be any question about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  standing strongly by Israel and their right to defend themselves from attack -- just nonsense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to turn to another foreign hotpot which obviously is Ukraine and Russia. President Zelenskyy here in town asking for things this week meeting with the candidates, meeting with the current administration. What he really wants is the ability to use Western weapons deeper into Russia.\par \par \uc2 Putin out today, spokesman for the Kremlin saying they're considering changing their policy and their guidelines when it comes to the use of nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 Is that a threat? Is it bluffing? What do you think that the administration should do with respect to allowing those weapons for further use into Russia?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, look, we need to be careful because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has shown that he's willing to take risks and gambles. Most of the world did not expect him to engage in the full broad spectrum invasion of Ukraine back in 2022, and President Biden and Vice President Harris have masterfully assembled a global coalition of 50 countries to defend Ukraine, to support Ukraine in their fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 One of the more alarming things that President Trump and his running mate J.D. Vance have said is that they will somehow bring this war to an end in a day, and J.D. Vance in the Senate has questioned whether we should continue to support Ukraine's fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important that we give them the weapons, the tools, and the freedom to use those weapons in order to win this war against Russia.\par \par \uc2 But I respect that President Biden gets more detailed briefings than I do on the potential risks of escalation and when President Putin makes threats to potentially use tactical nuclear weapons, he has a very tough balancing act to strike.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, did -- were you encouraged by the meeting between President Trump and President Zelenskyy because they came out of it and seemed that they had met -- you know, had some kind of meeting of the minds, that they heard each other, I mean, President Trump said, you know, it hasn't changed where he is overall on trying to get this thing ended.\par \par \uc2 But even President Zelenskyy, he talked with our Griff Jenkins, too, sounded like he felt like it was a good meeting.\par \par \uc2 COONS: I'm glad they met. It's important that there be conversations between President Zelenskyy and both Republican and Democratic leaders of our nation, and candidates to be the next president of our nation.\par \par \uc2 But in the clip that you're showing right now, President Trump said, I'm a very good friend of Putin's and you could see Zelenskyy sort of stiffen up and go, well, I hope you're a better friend of Ukraine than you are a friend of Russia under Putin. So it was a strikingly awkward moment.\par \par \uc2 Putin is a war criminal. Putin has carried out horrific attacks in Ukraine. We should not have a president of the United States saying that he is seeking to be a better friend of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Well, I want to ask you before you go quickly about our own border because we got numbers from ICE late in the week that showed a picture that was pretty shocking for most Americans, that there are hundreds of thousands of non-citizens here who have already been convicted of a crime and are not in ICE custody. That includes, by their numbers, more than 13,000 people convicted of homicide.\par \par \uc2 Were those numbers a shock to you? And what can and should we be doing about -- people, you know, get upset talking about mass deportations -- but targeting people that have been convicted criminals, murderers, rapists, should we not have an effort to get that done?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Shannon, thanks for a chance to talk to that, because that's a misleading number. Those 13,000 convicted of homicide, they may not be in ICE custody but most of them are in prison, state, federal or local prisons, and will be deported as soon as they finish serving their prison terms.\par \par \uc2 Under the Biden-Harris administration, they have prioritized the deportation of those who've committed violent crimes and have in fact deported them at a 50 percent higher rate than under former President Trump who didn't make that a key priority.\par \par \uc2 If we had been able to pass the tough bipartisan border security deal that my friends Senator Lankford and Murphy and Sinema negotiated, we would have $20 billion more being invested in deportations and be prioritizing the deportation of violent criminals, something all of us should want.\par \par \uc2 That bill didn't pass only because former President Trump called his allies in the Senate and said don't pass it. He wanted an issue, not a solution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well, as you know --\par \par \uc2 COONS: Vice President Harris has made it clear she would sign that bill into law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As you know, many of your Republican colleagues said they would not have voted for it anyway because they felt it contained many magnets to bring people here, work permits, and other things, and codified a flow that they already think is out of control. And they ask questions about if we're not enforcing current law, why would we enforce that bill if it was passed.\par \par \uc2 We'll see. If she becomes president, if you guys hold the Senate, maybe it comes up for another vote and we'll be watching that.\par \par \uc2 Senator Coons --\par \par \uc2 COONS: It will and it'll make us stronger. Thank you very much, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining us now, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Secretary, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what the senator had to say about the fact that the Biden-Harris administration has been full-throated and unequivocal in supporting Israel?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Look, Senator Coons is a nice man, but he's just living in a fantasy world.\par \par \uc2 The most important people who make the determination about whether America is providing enough support, frankly, are the Iranians, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and they have felt free to fire missiles into Israel, kill 1,200 Israelis, continuing to hold Americans hostage.\par \par \uc2 We don't even talk about that anymore. There are still hostages held by the Iranian regime in Gaza today.\par \par \uc2 Instead of talking about a ceasefire, which is what Secretary Austin spoke about, what Secretary Blinken speaks about, we should be saying plain and clear, finish the job.\par \par \uc2 Nasrallah would be alive today if the Biden administration ceasefire had gone into effect. The entire \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership would be alive today if the Israelis had agreed to the ceasefire that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had demanded and that the Iranians had demanded and the United States had supported.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake about it, we know history. We know the history from 1967. We know the history from 1973.\par \par \uc2 There was only one thing that will protect Israel and make the Middle East more prosperous and peaceful, and that is the demonstration of power and imposing costs on Israel's adversaries.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so there were reports in "Axios" earlier this week that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  had asked Iran to directly strike Israel. They didn't want to do that because their president was here speaking at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Well, now there are questions about if \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is disabled, does Iran now move forward with that? There's a quote by Trita Parsi, the Executive VP of the Quincy Institute here in Washington, talking about this saying, "If it becomes clear to Iran that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  actually cannot defend itself following the bombing in Beirut, then the Iranian justification for staying out of the war has collapsed. At that point, Iran's credibility with the rest of its partners in the access of regional militant groups will collapse, will risk collapsing if Tehran does not react." Do you worry about where this has pushed Iran to and what happens next?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Shannon, Trita Parsi has been an Iranian sympathizer for a long time. He works closely alongside some of the leaders inside of the country. The way to convince Iran to stay out of this conflict is to demonstrate that the Americans and the Israelis together can hold Iranian leadership to account, impose real costs on them.\par \par \uc2 When they see the leadership of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  destroyed, when they see \uc2 Hamas\uc2  dismantled, when they see the United States prepared to actually support that, and they see our Gulf partners recognize that they too can trust the United States of America, that's how you keep Iran at bay. It's how you push back against what, frankly, the Biden-Harris administration has allowed, the continuation of the Iranian nuclear program to build. There's only one way. You have to put pressure on them. You have to deny them resources. You can't hand them billions of dollars and then tell the Israelis to stand down