eorgia, he's got a little bit of a problem.\par \par \uc2 There he is down by three points to the Vice President. And part of that is a gender gap with women who say they do not trust you or him, the GOP, on issues of abortion or IVF. This week, Ohio Senate candidate Bernie Moreno adding fuel to that fire with some comments he made there.\par \par \uc2 Why is this messaging on that issue in particular not working? How can the GOP, is it possible to fix it? Because women just don't trust you guys on that one.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Look, we are going to continue, I think, to be able to grow in the polls here. And as we're able to talk about these issues and get our message out to voters, they'll see where President Trump stands on this. Look, there's been no stronger supporter of IVF than President Trump.\par \par \uc2 If you think about the situation that occurred in Alabama, President Trump came out immediately and said that he strongly supported IVF and he encouraged the Alabama legislature to act. They acted within six days of that, Shannon. And our Republican legislature and our Republican governor signed IVF protection into law.\par \par \uc2 We've continued to talk about this. Senator Ted Cruz and I led a letter with all 49 Republican senators signing on saying they strongly support nationwide access to IVF. And the truth is Democrats continue to use this issue as a scare tactic because IVF is legal and accessible in all 50 states. And President Trump has made it perfectly clear that he'll continue to make sure that that's the case.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you voted against a measure, though, that was offered up by Democrats on IVF in the Senate there. And they continue to point to that to say Republicans aren't supportive of protecting IVF. Why did you vote no?\par \par \uc2 BRITT: So, Shannon, I'm so glad you broke that and brought this up. This is politics at its worst. If you think about what \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is doing, instead of putting funding bills