t possible that Republicans are overconfident? Is this possibly another midterm 2022 when that red wave did not materialize the way that you all had hoped? Because Democrats say when it comes to this issue of abortion, which is on a lot of state ballots as well, they feel like it's going to put them over the top and they're going to maintain control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Look, I was actually with Steve Daines last night, who is the Chair of the NRSC, senator there from Montana. He is working diligently to ensure that we make sure that we take back the Senate. And of course, we hope to do that in a big fashion, but he is being diligent in every single sector.\par \par \uc2 We're taking nothing for granted. Even in places where we're ahead, we are working like we are five points behind. Because Shannon, we believe that the future of our country depends on it.\par \par \uc2 When you see \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  saying and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying they will break the filibuster to do radical things, and you know, whether it's taking gas cap powered cars off of the road, whether it is banning fracking when we know that we not only need to be energy independent, we need to return to the energy dominance of President Trump, where we look at our border. We know what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 Look, President Trump had the most secure border of all time. And now we look at what we have under Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's borders are. We have over 425,000 convicted criminals on the non-detained docket for ICE.\par \par \uc2 Look, I ran these numbers last year. They're up over 1.5 million from the numbers we had last year on the non-detained docket. Of that, we know to your earlier point when you opened the show, 13,000 convicted murderers. Do you understand that? Convicted murderers roaming our streets. We have 16,000 convicted, you know, people that are convicted of sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 Our country is not stronger. It is not safer. We've heard already on the show, literally, the world is on fire. Under President Trump, we actually got to see what peace through strength meant.\par \par \uc2 Putin never invaded Ukraine under President Trump. We see the Middle East. President Trump was able to institute the Abraham Accords, moving the embassy to Jerusalem. There has been no more pro-Israel president than President Trump. The world was a more stable place. Our border was more secure. Our dollar went further.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, as women look and look at the next generation and they want to leave this country better for their children.\par \par \uc2 And when you watch this debate on Tuesday night and you see \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  be ask the question, are we better off than we are four years ago? He, just like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , will not be able --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: -- to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me --\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Because the answer to that is no.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let me just say a couple things. You heard your colleague, Senator Coons, he said of those numbers of the people who are free here in the country, he says many of them are incarcerated. We've asked DHS for clarification on those numbers as well.\par \par \uc2 But I very quickly want to give you a chance to respond to what he said about Ukraine, that President Trump doesn't have the best interests of those folks at heart.\par \par \uc2 I know there was a meeting this week with President Zelenskyy, but there are many who worry about the Trump position, the Vance position, that it will include ceding some territory to Putin as a way to end this and that being, you know, something that the Ukrainian people do not want to accept. Quick comment from you on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Look, there is a reason that President Putin only invaded Ukraine under \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden, and then again, under Biden-Harris.\par \par \uc2 The world respects President Trump. And I believe that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does as well. And so I know that President Trump met with Zelenskyy. He understands the importance of standing firmly with our allies.\par \par \uc2 And you look at what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , slow-walking resources to Ukraine, and their disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan, disastrous withdrawal, has created a ripple effect across this globe where people do not respect us.\par \par \uc2 The moment that we -- President Trump gets back in office, people will respect the United States and know what we mean, what we say, and we say what we mean. He will protect and stand firmly with our servicemen and women, and he will back the blue.\par \par \uc2 We will have law and order once again, safer streets. And we will have a more peaceful globe, guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Well, about five weeks to go, we'll see what the American people decide.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Thousands of U.S. port workers could walk off the job as early as Tuesday, throwing a wrench into our economy just weeks before the election. Our Sunday panel is here to debate the political fallout if dockworkers make good on their threats to strike.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And as president, I won't only bring back the border security bill that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tanked, I will do more to secure our border.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're like a dumping ground. Over 647,570 migrant criminals are in our country.\par \par \uc2 You can't have her as your president. She doesn't know what the hell she's doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Vice President Harris and President Trump each making the case that they are the one best equipped to handle the border crisis. It's time now for our Sunday group. See what they think.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers. Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief of "The Federalist." Former 2020 Biden-Harris surrogate, \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2 . And Mario Parker, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  managing editor for \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. So we've heard these numbers. DHS is taking issue with them. But if we're talking about people who are released into this country, or at least from ICE custody, on the issue of homicide, the ICE letter that had all these numbers in it said this, most non-citizens who are convicted of homicide are typically not eligible for release from ICE custody, but in limited circumstances, ICE officers may use their discretion in making custody determinations and release non-citizens with conditions.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, that sounds like an acknowledgment that some of these people convicted of homicide are free in the country.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE FEDERALIST: Right. And even Senator Coons was saying that only a majority of people might be in state prisons or other facilities.\par \par \uc2 What we've seen in the last four years is an absolute explosion on the non- detained list. From 3.2 million illegal crossers in 2020 to 7.4 million, I think it is now. This is a result of just millions upon millions of people crossing into the border -- crossing the border. And yes, we have. And this could be from prior to the Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: But we have 13,000 convicted murderers, nearly 16,000 convicted rapists, 56,000 people convicted of dangerous drug crimes. Whether that happened in a different country or this country, this is clearly not a cohesive immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 And it's clearly a result of the Biden administration going out of its way on day one. Biden and Harris worked to remove all of the protections that the Trump administration had painstakingly provided.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says that she opposes migrant detention facilities. She doesn't support the wall. She compared ICE to the KKK and thinks they should be abolished. She wants free healthcare for illegal border crossers. That includes, in her mind, sex change surgeries paid for by taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a radically different position than the Trump administration, which sought to control the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and border patrol reaction to her going down there to visit in Douglas, Arizona on Friday was, Francesca, they called it a slap in the face, not only to border patrol, but to Americans as well. They said she not only ignored the problem, in their estimation, she helped create it.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : So Vice President Harris is trying to reframe the debate away from the issues that Mollie is talking about in areas that are more in her background, that are more in her court. She wants to talk about fentanyl. And she also wants to talk about transnational crime. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Because the Biden administration cracked down on China, they say, when it comes to fentanyl precursors in the country. When she talks about her record as attorney general on transnational crime, it allows her to talk about going after drugs, guns, as well as the trafficking of people.\par \par \uc2 Why did she go to Arizona, though? When you look at the polling and Fox's own polling, she's down two points to Trump in the state of Arizona. In the USA Today/\uc2 Suffolk University\uc2  poll, that was six points. This is all in the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 But importantly, look at the issues. And that Fox poll, 15 points down in Arizona when it comes to the issue of immigration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And let me put some of this up because we've got several key battleground states that we did this polling and asked this question about who's better to control and help with the border.\par \par \uc2 Arizona, Trump's up 15. Georgia, he's up 15. North Carolina, up 15. Mario, in Pennsylvania, up 17 on that issue.\par \par \uc2 MARIO PARKER, BLOOMBERG MANAGING EDITOR FOR U.S. GOVERNMENT: Yes. And what you're saying again is Harris trying to shore up these vulnerabilities. This was a week where you saw her do that, both on the economic part where she released a report, but also with a visit to the border.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been hitting her and not visiting the border for some time, right? We remember her first major misstep as vice president in 2021 when she gave the interview and she said, well, I haven't been to Europe either.\par \par \uc2 She's not going to over -- there's no world where she's going to overtake \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  among voters on the border, which you're seeing her do though is embrace a different tactic to say that we do need enforcement on the border, a sharp departure from what she ran on in 2019, and a sharp departure from how Democrats have talked about just rejiggering the entire system.\par \par \uc2 If she can cut into some of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's lead, again, she won't overtake him. The hope is that with this game of inches in the polls, that maybe she at least gets another two points or three points that could carry her electorate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. That can make a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, she may have another headache on her hands if there's this huge dockworker strike, which the White House is saying they're staying out of it. It could come as early as Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" says this, intervening to stop the strike could sour labor voters on Harris, who's trying to shore up support from blue collar workers who could decide the election in critical swing states.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, we've all heard the dire predictions about no vegetables, no bananas, no car parts. If this dockworker strike happens, it could be a massive impact and a hit on the economy.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: It certainly can. And that's what most economists are saying, that this would be one of the most dangerous economic situations to play out that we've seen since COVID.\par \par \uc2 This is affecting 70,000 workers in contract negotiations right now. That contract expires, of course, on Tuesday, which is, you know, five weeks out from the election, another two months out from the Christmas season, which could be really dangerous for this economy.\par \par \uc2 But certainly, I think you're seeing an administration want to support both sides in this negotiation. The vast majority of organized labor is behind the vice president for her commitment to standing with workers. She's one of the first vice presidents and only vice presidents, along with the president, to stand on a picket line with those striking workers. But certainly, this process will play out in the next 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and "POLITICO" goes on to say, if Trump tries to make hay of this, they say Democrats should try to use his track record on labor policy, along with his recent comments to Musk about firing striking workers to blunt his attempts to curry favor with working class voters.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes. Well, the best possible situation we can get here if Biden and Harris intervene is the status quo. We will be able to keep getting things into ports. This is not an economic success story. It does just kind of highlight for people all the problems that this economy has had over the last several years with inflation being such a problem and people being unable to buy homes.\par \par \uc2 And so it just reminds people, oh, during the Trump administration, the economy was firing on all cylinders. During this Biden-Harris administration, it has been rough. And even if they resolve the poor workers' strike, it's still just going to be how it should be.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We got to go, but I want a show of hands. Who thinks the vice presidential debate Tuesday moves the needle at all on the overall polling and ticket?\par \par \uc2 But we're all going to still watch it. Right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, no. Actually, it depends.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You do. OK.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: I'll say, you don't want anybody to hurt their main candidate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's more about doing no harm this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 OK. Panel, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President Zelenskyy hedged his bets this week, meeting with both presidential contenders here in Washington, as the two sides may have very different ideas about how this whole thing with Russia should end.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: I have a message for the tyrants of Tehran. If you strike us, we will strike you. There is no place - there is no place in Iran that the long arm of Israel cannot reach.\par \par \uc2 MASOUD PEZESHKIAN, PRESIDENT OF IRAN (through translator): We want peace for all and seek no war or quarrel with anyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  and Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian, dueling speeches there at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  amid growing tensions in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 And, Francesca, a lot of the reporting has been that the White House we knew for months has been very frustrated with Netanyahu, but it really hit it seemed a new high this week when they decide to go after \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  directly in Lebanon and not get on board with this talk of a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Yes, so let's go back.\par \par \uc2 The White House was under the impression that this 21-day ceasefire proposal that they laid out with the E.U. and nine other nations, that Israel was on board with it then. Netanyahu originally comes out, rejects the proposal, and we've seen them continue to strike ever since. The White House's view on this is that the politics inside of Israel for Netanyahu are very complicated for him, and that's why you're seeing these differences play out in public.\par \par \uc2 But moving forward now with these recent strikes, the White House did not know about this yesterday until it was already under the way. And the questions that U.S. officials now have are, what happens next with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 ? Right now they're reeling, they're trying to figure out, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  that is, what happens next. And U.S. officials don't think they're in the mood right now to talk about a ceasefire. What they are trying to do in the U.S. is lay the groundwork for when that conversation can happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, "The New York Post," its editorial board, has been very critical of the way this administration has handled this, saying, "If Biden-Harris had any true compassion, any desire for real peace and not just a three-week joke of a ceasefire for the benefit of anti-Israel voters, they'd encourage Israel to finish off the terrorists and maybe even help. Instead, the Pentagon says the U.S. military won't provide intelligence or other support for Israeli operations in Lebanon."\par \par \uc2 Kevin.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Well, certainly I don't believe that's the case. You know, Secretary Austin has had no less than three phone conversations just over the weekend with his counterpart, with the \uc2 IDF\uc2 . So that support for Israeli forces remains steadfast and lockstep. You saw that double down at \uc2 UNGA\uc2  last week with the president's remarks in support of Israel and on the sidelines, obviously, in support of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And you compare that to the language coming out of the Trump campaign in terms of our support for allies in remaining steadfast. So there's a contrast, I think, in terms of the world stage and in terms of that messaging, but there is no daylight between this administration and it support of the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, "THE FEDERALIST" EDITOR-IN-CHIEF AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I could not agree more that there is a major contrast between the Trump administration and the Biden administration, the Biden-Harris administration, on these issues. You'll remember during the Trump administration you had peace breaking out all over the world. You had peace breaking out in the Middle East with - with economic agreements between Israel and all of its neighbors, and isolating Iran, and you did not have Putin invading Ukraine. With this - elections have consequences. And with this Biden-Harris administration, you've got tremendous war and you've got a risk of World War III breaking out.\par \par \uc2 The - the situation in Israel is one thing. The situation in Ukraine has been a disaster for the people of Ukraine. You have a million casualties. You've lost so much of the population. You have the economic catastrophe. And there are no statesmen in D.C. talking about how to end this conflict with the least damage possible to Ukraine, except for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and - and, listen, Zelenskyy was here this week. He met with both Harris and Trump. He met with the current administration. I would have loved to have been a fly on the wall, Mario. That meeting between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But they came out seeming like they'd had some kind of meaningful conversation.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: It looked OK, right? The body language looked OK at best. But just speaking with folks that are close to the former president, I mean they were offended, right, of how this all unfolded. For Zelenskyy to visit Pennsylvania, a battleground state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had just been campaigning in, signing artillery on a government plane, no Republicans in the delegation, alongside Josh Shapiro, I mean that was just a tough one. So, tensions are - remain, but at least there is somewhat of a detente.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and it does seem that for now bipartisan support, there's enough of it on The Hill, more weapons, more money flowing that way.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Thank God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we'll see whether the White House decides to allow further use of the weapons they have sent.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a behind the scenes look with a new play here in the nation's capital actor Scott Bakula becomes \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , bringing the legacy of our 16th president to light at the very sight of his death.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You may know something about the history of what happened here at Ford's Theatre, but there's plenty you don't know about the life of President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 . Coming up we'll take you inside a new production here that's got some surprises.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's been nearly 160 years since an assassin's bullet cut \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 's life short. Now, as America finds itself in yet another era of stark, political divisions, actor Scott Bakula is bringing the 16th president's legacy and message back into the spotlight as he portrays Lincoln in a one-man show at Ford's Theatre, the site where he was mortally wounded.\par \par \uc2 It's our "Sunday Special."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BAKULA, ACTOR, "MISTER LINCOLN": Well, I have to be honest, Jesse, I must admit that the taste is in my mouth a little to become president. But there are other men far more capable than I am.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Actor Scott Bakula is known for taking audiences back in time, with his hit science-fiction series "Quantum Leap." But this fall the longtime veteran as stage and screen shares a very different glimpse into the past with "Mister Lincoln," and intimate one-man play that pulls back the curtain on the triumphs, tragedies, and decisions that shaped one of history's most reviewed leaders, in our nation's most divided moment.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You are a seasoned, award-winning actor, but how do you tackle something like this?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: It's daunting. Not just the fact of doing a one-man show, by doing Lincoln. The play goes from all different aspects and - and approaches in his life and times in his life. If you were memorizing the Gettysburg Address, that's one kind of work because that's something that I kind of - in your head you kind of know. But if I'm talking to his - to Mary Lincoln, that's a different kind of memorization.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Adding to the emotional weight of Bakula's onstage performance is the play's venue, Ford's Theatre, the very sight of Lincoln's assassination.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The gravity of being in this place is partially because of the history that was made here.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes, that's the Lincoln Box. That is a sacred space up there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you mentioned, it is going to be referenced in the show?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes, very much so. It's -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's acknowledgment of it.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes. Right - almost right from the get-go. And I think it was important to do that because otherwise it just looms over the - over the top - over the whole show. And the show isn't about that.\par \par \uc2 You don't know what got us there, and you don't what happened to that man before.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it sounds like people will, obviously, be entertained, but they're going to be educated at the same time.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes. Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Educating audiences on what led to such a pivotal moment in our nation's history is a critical part of the theatre's mission. The venue features a one-of-a-kind museum where dozens of artifacts, including the small derringer pistol used by John Wilkes Booth offer a unique view into a country torn apart by war. And Lincoln's ultimately fatal struggle to put it back together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is so much more than a theatre here.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TETREAULT, DIRECTOR, FORD'S THEATRE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's really special.\par \par \uc2 TETREAULT: It's a working theatre, but it's also a history place. It's a museum. And to be able to see, you know, this is the knife that John Wilkes Booth held up and yelled, "sic semper tyrannis" 160 years ago, we have that here!\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But despite its tragic past, theatre director Paul Tetreault says political violence isn't the main takeaway from this historic space.\par \par \uc2 TETREAULT: We use as assassination as an entry point, but what we really want to talk about is Lincoln's legacy. And you look back at Lincoln and you think, you know, he was just a man.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That humanity, along with Lincoln's unshakable resolve in the face of war, political division, and personal heartache, is what the play's leading man hopes the audience discovers about the 16th president.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: I get surprised every day. I didn't know that he had loaded his cabinet with people that he ran against, that were - so he had voices in there all the time constantly that were - had the other side of an opinion, or - or - or not necessarily had his back. I didn't know that when he lost his second son in - that he was heard at night howling in pain. You could hear him outside the White House. He dealt with so much death in his life, and figured out how to keep going because he believed in something and he believed he had a mission. And - and he stayed with it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Of course, to many, the mission of uniting a divided America seems just as daunting in 2024 as 1864.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, of course, we think of President Lincoln, and - and just how deep those divisions, how split this country was. Do you see parallels now?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Oh, the parallels are - are - are too many to count. We are in a time now where it seems a lot of people feel like there's no going back or whatever. All the rhetoric. And we can't reach across the aisle. We can't - we can't solve this problem. And the civil war was pretty serious. You can't get worse than that.\par \par \uc2 But I think, at the end of the day, we have to say that - and this play is about that, but that there is hope, because we're here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There is hope.\par \par \uc2 "Mister Lincoln" plays at Ford's Theatre until October 13th. If you happen to find yourself here in the nation's capital, you can get more information and tickets to the show at - the museum as well, at fords.org.\par \par \uc2 And just a quick note, my podcast, "Livin' the Bream" drops today. This week I sat down with Fox News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst to discuss his brand-new book "Black Saturday." Trey and his team have been covering the October 7th massacre and all the follow for nearly a year now. "Black Saturday" takes you inside the conflict with the stories of real people across the region. It is available starting Tuesday. You can preorder now. Get the book anywhere you like to get your books, and the podcast where you like to get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 And a quick programming note. Tun into Fox News Democracy `24 special coverage of the CBS News vice presidential debate simulcast. Fox News coverage on the channel begins Tuesday, October 1st, at 8:00 p.m. Eastern. And I'll be here on all of your Fox local stations 9:00 p.m. for coverage.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you back here for the next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par