m copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It is great to have you on a Friday, and maybe they could do a greased peloton.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, my gosh, it's genius.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, great idea.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. All right. See you then.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Bye.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The number -- the numbers are shocking. We will tell you about an almost half million illegal immigrants already convicted of crimes roaming the streets of the U.S. Those numbers just out.\par \par \uc2 New presidential polls we have tonight from another pair of crucial swing states. We'll bring that to you. And Israel goes after the top terrorists in the brutal \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  organization. The question this minute, did they get him?\par \par \uc2 But first, residents of Florida, Georgia, and other parts of the southeast assessing the damage from Hurricane Helene. It made landfalls a devastatingly powerful Category 4 storm. Dozens are dead in four states. Hundreds of thousands are still without power tonight.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Crystal River, Florida tonight with the latest. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The death toll has been rising all afternoon. Right now, it stands at least 40 people killed by this storm.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Roaring ashore as a Category 4 hurricane, Helene slammed into Florida's Big Bend region, leaving behind death and destruction.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my goodness, that building there is going to come apart.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Seventh most powerful storm to hit the state made landfall with winds as high as 140 miles an hour, and up to 15 feet of storm surge.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDATE: It's a real gut punch to those communities, and I know a lot of people are reeling right now.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): It also set homes on fire and turned roads into rivers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Anybody in your house!\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): First responders headed out in boats to rescue those trapped by rising floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was trying to make it to my mama's house. I'm not able to get any further.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Many of those rescues still underway in hard hit, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): We are also trying to get the multiple structures right now that we know have individuals inside that we're unable to communicate with.\par \par \uc2 We'll have to literally cut our way in the situations like this.\par \par \uc2 BOB VAN DILLEN, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: I'm going to go see if I can help this lady out of --\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Near Atlanta, Fox weather's Bob Van Dillen jumped into action when he heard a woman screaming for help, as she was trapped inside her car. The woman's husband was full of appreciation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VAN DILLEN: OK, anytime.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Strong winds and waters also pushed boats into people's front yards and collapsed homes and businesses, knocking out power for millions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our whole entire home is completely ruined. The couches, the beds, the clothing.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): A bird's eye view from the Fox flight team showed significant flooding in Florida, while Georgia, the Carolinas, and Tennessee are seeing historic rains.\par \par \uc2 In Unicoi County, dozens are stranded on the roof of the county hospital, while others were taken away in rescue boats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The main highways have all been cleared, but it might take another day or two for these small business owners using brooms and squeegees to get all the water out. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan, live in Crystal River, Florida. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 If you would like to help those impacted by Hurricane Helene, you can donate right now at redcross.org/foxforward. Or you can take your phone and your camera there and scan the Q.R. code on your screen right now.\par \par \uc2 The race for the White House, we have new Fox polling tonight from a pair of crucial battleground states. Vice President Harris leads former President Trump by two points among registered voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 But this is key. The race is tied among likely voters in Pennsylvania. The registration -- registered voters believe the former president would do a better job on the border by a 17-point margin. He leads Harris in the economy by six. The Democrat has a 21-point advantage with the abortion issue in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, Harris has a two-point lead among registered voters. Again, this is key, the former president is up one-point with likely voters.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump has a 15-point advantage on the border with registered voters in North Carolina, and up seven on the economy. Harris is up 16 on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 There are new staggering numbers tonight, illustrating the dangers posed by the border crisis, \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2 , says the Biden- Harris policy along the border has allowed almost a half million convicted criminals to enter the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 These people are free to roam the U.S. that includes more than 13,000 convicted of homicide, almost 16,000 convicted of rape.\par \par \uc2 Again, these numbers from ICE. Those numbers emerged just hours before Vice President Harris will make remarks near the southern border. She will be in Douglas, Arizona, her first trip to that region since her nomination.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. Aishah Hasnie is traveling with the Trump campaign as the former president is blaming Harris and Biden for the border crisis. But we begin with correspondent Bill Melugin in Douglas, Arizona right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Greg, good evening to you. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is officially on the ground here in Douglas, Arizona. Her motorcade is actually passing behind me right now as we speak. She is expected to head down to the local port of entry, where she is going to meet with CBP officers to talk about fentanyl seizures.\par \par \uc2 Then, she is expected to meet with Border Patrol somewhere along the border wall. She will then speak at a campaign event tonight, as her critics are saying, this trip is nothing but politics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , making her first visit to the southern border in more than three years on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The V.P., arriving in Douglas, Arizona with Democrat Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , who the local sheriff says urged her to make the visit.\par \par \uc2 Harris' trip comes as ICE releases stunning new data on noncitizen criminals. In a letter to Texas Congressman Tony Gonzales, the acting ICE director, reveals there are currently more than 13,000 noncitizens with homicide convictions and nearly 16,000 noncitizens with rape and sexual assault convictions who are roaming the United States as part of ICE's non- detained docket.\par \par \uc2 That docket is made up of illegal immigrants who have been caught and released at the border pending future court dates, as well as illegal immigrants who have already been ordered deported by an immigration judge but are still in the U.S. out of federal custody.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): My stomach turned, you know, my heart sank to see the numbers on paper. It just goes to show of how real this threat is.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Harris has previously supported decriminalizing illegal crossings and giving taxpayer funded health care to illegal immigrants. She called the border wall a vanity project and pledged to end the use of ICE detainers.\par \par \uc2 Now, she has walked back some of her prior positions and says she would immediately sign the bipartisan border deal. She is expected to accuse Trump of killing that deal when she speaks tonight.\par \par \uc2 The former president said today, given the startling new ICE data on migrant criminals, Harris shouldn't even show her face at the border.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And nobody who's allowed this to happen to our country is fit to be the president of the United States. I mean that too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is expected to speak here tonight at about 7:45 p.m. Eastern. It's unclear if she's going to take any questions from the media. We certainly hope she will. But she's getting blasted by the Border Patrol union for this visit.\par \par \uc2 They are calling her visit essentially a political stunt, saying, she spent years not just ignoring the border crisis, but helping create it in the first place. They say her visit is a slap in the face to Americans and border patrol agents just weeks away from an election. And their question for her is, where have you been these last few years?\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, along the border wall there in Douglas, Arizona. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is about to appear at a town hall in Warren, Michigan, as you look live there. Earlier today, the former president said, if elected, he can end the war in Ukraine before taking office. Something he said before.\par \par \uc2 The Republican nominee also met with Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  this morning in New York.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Warren, Michigan right now.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We had a very good meeting, very productive meeting.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President Trump walking side by side with Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  today in Trump Tower, vowing to strike a deal to end the Russian war in Ukraine before he's inaugurated. But admitting, he and Zelenskyy have differing ideas on how to do that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And I have my own ideas, and I'm sure the president definitely has his own ideas, but has to be fair.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Whether fair, means ceding land, the pair did not elaborate. But the talk was a thawing of icy relations between the two that began when Zelenskyy told The New Yorker, "Trump doesn't really know how to stop the war, even if he might think he knows how."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did he say anything today to change your view of it?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): No, I said that I think that we understand much more better than everybody, really, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What's going on in Ukraine, how to stop him. It's difficult to understand. Yes, with all our respect between United States and Ukraine, but we are on the big distance. So, without this experience, you can't really understand how to stop Putin. And that what I wanted to share to president.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The GOP nominee, shifting back to domestic policy out on the campaign trail. \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  in battleground Michigan, where some voters are relying on food pantries to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are seeing a 30 percent increase.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: At a stop in walker, Trump promising supporters he'll reignite the auto industry and bring in foreign jobs.\par \par \uc2 American workers will no longer be worried about losing your jobs to foreign nations. Foreign nations will be worried about losing their jobs to America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, tonight, the Harris campaign is out with a brand-new digital ad, particularly, about this visit to Warren, Michigan, slamming Trump's 2016 promise to auto workers here that he would try to keep their jobs and add jobs to the area.\par \par \uc2 The ad instead highlighting the layoffs that took place here in Warren in 2019 when a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant shut down. But obviously, did not stop the thousands of people that you see behind me who are ready to see the former president tonight. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, live in Warren, Michigan. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", Israel strikes the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terror organization's central headquarters in Beirut, trying to take out the group's leader. The question is, tonight, did they?\par \par \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has responded with rocket attacks targeting Israeli citizens. President Biden has directed the Pentagon to assess and adjust U.S. force posture in the region. He also wants to make sure U.S. embassies are protected. The Iranian president reportedly has had an emergency meeting tonight.\par \par \uc2 This comes as Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  delivers a fiery address to the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  this morning. Many delegates walked out of that speech. Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst reports tonight from Haifa, Israel, with the very latest as of this hour. Good evening, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Israeli fighter jets are striking the Lebanese capital of Beirut once again tonight, after warnings were sent to civilians in certain buildings to evacuate immediately. The Israelis say they are storing \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  weapons inside those buildings. We are also getting reports of dozens of other airstrikes across southern Lebanon at this moment.\par \par \uc2 It comes after the Israeli prime minister addressed the world today from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The assembly will hear an address by His Excellency, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): As the Israeli prime minister walked to the podium, he was met with both applause and protest. Netanyahu gave a defiant speech amid calls for a ceasefire in the Middle East, while he hinted at further offensive action.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL: As long as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  chooses the path of war, Israel has no choice, and Israel has every right to remove this threat and return our citizens to their homes safely.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: 24 minutes after Netanyahu wrapped up his remarks, the Israeli military announced it had resumed airstrikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . And later, massive strikes targeted the Lebanese capital of Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has learned the target of those strikes was \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 . The Israelis have not confirmed he was killed. President Biden was asked about the strike today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, do you -- do you support Israel's decision to try to kill Nasrallah?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Still gathering information. I can tell you, United States had no knowledge of or participation in the idea of action.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): As war expands along Israel's northern front, the Iran-backed group is increasing the range of its rocket fire targeting the cities of Haifa and Tiberius today.\par \par \uc2 Israel's defense minister, Yoav Gallant issued new threats to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Friday, while visiting troops in northern Israel who are preparing for the possibility of a ground invasion into Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, MINISTER OF DEFENSE, ISRAEL (through translator): We have been waiting for this opportunity for a long time, including me personally, not only in the last year, for many years.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Across the border, residents of southern Lebanon continue to flee north and into neighboring Syria. Hospitals are struggling to keep up with the rising death and injury toll among the population, as international bodies warn of a catastrophic situation for Lebanese civilians.\par \par \uc2 DR. MOHAMED DAWOUD, SURGEON: The elements of steadfastness have become almost nonexistent. It's possible that soon, we won't be able to treat our patients.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Israel's Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is returning from the United States a day early amid fears about possible \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  retaliation, following the targeting of leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 . Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, really quickly, we should know if Nasrallah was targeted and killed. One would think there would be some kind of public -- that they would have to bury him within 24 hours, there would be some acknowledgement at some point. Right?\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Absolutely. In the coming hours or days, we will have an answer to this open question. And if he was indeed killed in these strikes, Bret, it is an unprecedented moment in this conflict. And certainly, could lead to further destabilization across the Middle East. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Pretty amazing that all that went down as the Israeli prime minister was speaking at the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst in Haifa. Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a federal grand jury indicts Iranian nationals accused of hacking former President Trump's campaign.\par \par \uc2 And as we look live at the border, Vice President Harris is touring there along the border in Douglas, Arizona. We will listen in heading to break as she walks along the border wall.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Live look at New York City there. New York City Mayor Eric Adams is pleading not guilty to federal corruption charges. He appeared at an arraignment today in Manhattan. Adams is accused of accepting overseas travel and illegal campaign donations from people looking to buy his influence. Adams says he is innocent and does not plan to resign.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today, the Dow gained 138 for a new record closed. The S&P 500 lost seven. The NASDAQ dropped 71. For the week, the Dow and the S&P 500 were both up six tenths of a percentage point. The NASDAQ gained about one.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a federal grand jury has indicted several Iranians accused of hacking former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign. There are also reports the Islamic Republic might be planning to do something much worse. Iran is denying the allegations.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has details tonight from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: There are few actors in this world that pose as grave a threat to the national security the United States, as does Iran, a state sponsor of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Department of Justice charging three men with ties to Iran with hacking former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign and turning over some of the material to media organizations and people they believed were with the Biden re-election campaign, a tactic known as hack and leak.\par \par \uc2 The indictment alleges the men were employed by Iran's Revolutionary Guard and used aliases to gain access to trump campaign e-mail accounts. Part of what the Biden administration says is an ongoing Cyber Campaign. The goal:\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: To stoke discord, erode confidence in the U.S. electoral process and advance its malign activities.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Iran's \uc2 U.N.\uc2  mission calls the allegations unsubstantiated and says Tehran is not interfering in U.S. elections. But the alleged hack is just the latest example of Iran targeting not only the Trump campaign, but the candidate himself.\par \par \uc2 Multiple law enforcement officials tell Fox News, the former president receives a steady stream of threats originating in Iran, and they increased after Trump as commander in chief, ordered the drone killing of Iranian General \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2  in January 2020.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : The \uc2 FBI\uc2  would like to send a message to the government of Iran. You and your hackers can't hide behind your keyboards. If you try to meddle in our elections. We're going to hold you accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, all three men live in Iran. It's unlikely they'll ever see a jail cell in the United States. I asked the Attorney General about this. He said the Justice Department has a long memory and will spare no resource when trying to pursue these three, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt live at the Justice Department. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a live report on the damage in Atlanta from Hurricane Helene. Take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 11 in Los Angeles as large groups of people target 7- Eleven stores in what police call flash mob robberies. Officers say at least 14 incidents have been reported since July. Surveillance video shows dozens of people overwhelming store crews and throwing items at workers.\par \par \uc2 Fox 29 in Philadelphia, where the city's progressive District Attorney scores a legal win in an effort to impeach him. The Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled state lawmakers improperly extended the effort to impeach \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  over two different legislative sessions. Republicans say Krasner failed to prosecute some crimes and did not enforce bail for some suspects. Krasner has remained in office and has not been charged with a crime.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dallas from our affiliate Fox 4, one of the big stories there tonight, the Texas State Fair opens up for the start of its three week run. A parade was held earlier today, and an opening ceremony will happen later this evening. It's a big deal down in the big state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have Weather Alert now to our top story at the bottom of the hour, Hurricane Helene has left flooding, property damage and power outages in its wake, not only in Florida, but in Georgia throughout the Mid Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Madison Scarpino is in Atlanta tonight, where the system made a bit of history today. Good evening, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Good evening, Bret. Today, Atlanta faced its first ever flash flood emergency. And according to Georgia weather officials, the amount of rain in the city reached record levels. In just the last 48 hours, we saw 11.12 inches of rain, beating the previous two-day record from over 130 years ago which was 9.59 inches.\par \par \uc2 Now, all that rain created dangerous flooding, shutting down roads and even damaging at least one road in forest park near the Atlanta airport. And there were dozens of water rescues. Atlanta Fire and Rescue officials tell us their most high-risk rescue happened when two adults, an infant, and their pet dog got stranded on the roof of their car because of the quickly rising water. Crews had to use multiple agencies and a boat to save them. And we spoke to one woman who says her brother ended up stuck in the flash floods overnight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So my brother is in his house, has been there since last night, said he didn't want to leave. I called 911 for him and they're coming to get him. I told them they would need a boat. They asked how high the water was, and it's certainly above his head. So I said they were going to need a boat. So we hope they bring a boat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: We have update, that man did make it out of the house. He is safe. But for some people the cleanup process is going to take while. We spoke to a man, Bret, who lives in an apartment complex that got pretty flooded, and he describes his furniture as, quote, "being sloshed around everywhere." And tonight, there is still almost 900,000 in Georgia without power. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Again, if you would like to help those impacted by hurricane Helene, you can donate now at RedCross.org/FOXforward, or again, scan the QR code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 Here we go. We're keeping that list going. Tonight's 50 races in 50 days, Republicans try to flip a House seat that has been a Democratic bastion for more than 120 years. You heard that right. President Biden carried North Carolina first district in the 2020 presidential election by fewer than two points. Now Republican nominee and retired Army Colonel Laurie Buckhout aims to turn the tide in the mostly rural district against Democratic incumbent Don Davis. The retired Air Force captain won the seat in 2022 by almost five points. The first term congressman has broken with the Biden administration in the past, including voting with Republicans on a resolution condemning Vice President Harris's role as border czar.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DON DAVIS, (D-NC): I have spoken to Border Patrol agents they all tell me they are overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: With both campaigns focusing on the economy, new FOX polls signal trouble, perhaps, for Democrats there. Just 30 percent of North Carolina registered voters say the economy is either in excellent or good condition, a far cry from former President Trump's term in November of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Historically, a Democratic stronghold, you would have to go back to William McKinley's presidency to find a non-Democrat representing this district. Could the Trump-endorsed Buckhout finally change her party's luck in this district and help keep the House?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURIE BUCKHOUT, (R-NC) U.S. CONGRESSIONAL NOMINEE: I speak for the folks in my district that they are sick of what is going on. They are sick of the open border. They are sick of the economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So with just 39 days until Election Day, we will follow this critical toss-up race in the House.\par \par \uc2 The Wisconsin Supreme Court says \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 's name will remain on the presidential ballot. The ruling upholds a lower court's decision that candidates can only be removed from the ballot if they die. The decision comes after more than 400,000 absentee ballots have already been sent to voters in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Since his successful run for the presidency in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has reshaped America politics. There's no doubt about it. He's transformed the Republican Party. And in our new series, "The Unauthorized History of Political Parties", we look at the effect the former president has had on the GOP and what it may mean this November and beyond. Here's an excerpt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came along and said the two parties are letting you down. The Democrats are letting in millions of immigrants because they want to convert them to Democrats and win elections. But the reason the Republicans don't care is they like that your wages are going down. They like hiring these immigrants. It's an attack on both political parties.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We've lost 60,000 factories.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trump is the first one to come out and say something that hadn't been said by the political leadership, but that also was on the tip of so many Americans' tongues.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We were a respected country. Now the whole world laughs at us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As the two political parties prepare for this November's election, one thing is clear -- our two parties are always evolving, prodded by events, shaped by personalities, and galvanized into action by third parties and independent candidates.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're always changing. We're always evolving with the times. The political parties are us, and we are them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you want to learn more about why and how both parties keep changing, watch that new series, "The Unauthorized History of Political Parties." It is streaming now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 A new movie about child trafficking premiers on Prime Video and \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV today. "City of Dreams" follows one boy's harrowing journey as he is sent from Mexico to a sweatshop in Los Angeles. Author and speaker Tony Robbins is the executive producer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Jesus! Jesus! Jesus!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's good, it's good. It's the right decision for your whole family.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You think you're going to run out onto the pitch tomorrow? No, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What kind of business are you in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The busy kind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is real life. You know, it is gripping but it is -- it transitions over to what is really happening today.\par \par \uc2 TONY ROBBINS, "CITY OF DREAMS" EXECUTIVE PRODUCER: The film has the power to move people emotionally. If you think about it, we had slavery in this country forever. What changed it was "Uncle Tom's Cabin." It made people say no more. And even \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  read the book. Think about the impact that book had to change slavery. I think this film is a move in that direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will have additional comments from Tony Robbins plus writer and director Mo Ramchandani on Monday's SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the latest FOX battleground polling, plus former President Trump meet's Ukraine's leader. And later, it is Friday. "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking right this minute, you are looking live there at southern Lebanon. We have seen some explosions there, some pretty big explosions. You can kind of make out some of the smoke there in the distance. We have seen that develop over the past few minutes as Israeli forces are striking into southern Lebanon. It comes after a day of many strikes, and one targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  command.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Israeli prime minister today was at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: In this swamp of antisemitic bile, there is automatic majority willing to demonize the Jewish state on anything.\par \par \uc2 Israel will win this battle. We'll win this battle because we don't have a choice.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAKE AUCHINCLOSS, (D-MA): The United Nations has decades of a shameful double standard regarding Israel behind it. And so some of that rhetoric is justified.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RYAN ZINKE\uc2 , (R-MT) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: I stand by Israel on this. If you participate in such horrific acts, we're going to find you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So the headlines, you can imagine, all over the map about \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 . "Netanyahu strikes defiant tone at \uc2 U.N.\uc2 " "Netanyahu at \uc2 U.N.\uc2  vows that Israel will keep degrading \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  until its objectives are met," and "Netanyahu sows doubt about ceasefire with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in \uc2 U.N.\uc2  speech."\par \par \uc2 Meantime that targeting I mentioned was going after the head of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 . We don't have any confirmation that that bombing has taken him out, but we can tell you that the Israeli prime minister left the U.S. quicker than we thought he was going to. The Iranian leadership has called an emergency meeting. President Biden and Vice President Harris have been briefed about the situation. And now we wait to see exactly what is happening on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," and Bill McGurn, columnist for "The Wall Street Journal." Again, Bill, it's early, but clearly Israel is striking after what we have seen with the pagers, the exploding radios, the strikes that we have seen over recent days, and now this command control strike that we are told was going after \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, COLUMNIST, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes. I mean, the strike is in Beirut, not only targeting the commander but wiping out the entire building. It's going to be controversial, but I agree with the prime minister. I think Israel doesn't have a choice. And in stark opposition to those who think a ceasefire is going to be a solution with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  still holding hostages, I believe if they have eliminated Nasrallah, the prospects for peace in the region, actually in the long term, go up. And I think we need to remember he not only is involved in deaths of Americans and Israelis, but a lot of Arabs, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it's pretty clear Israel is on the offensive and they are taking a very aggressive posture.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Another explosion there, by the way.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, they are being very aggressive in going after \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and they are aggressive in Gaza and the West Bank. They're really pushing this. The question tonight is about Iranian restraint in terms of response. I think one of the reasons that Netanyahu flew back, he was supposed to spend the weekend in New York having spoken at the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2  today. He was supposed to spend the weekend in New York, and even with the sabbath is flying back to Israel.\par \par \uc2 I think there's fear that Iranian retaliation will spread possibly missiles in Tel Aviv, and he wants to be on the ground and in position as a commander there, you know, as the president to say what's going on and where is this going to go. The fear is that this is going to spread rapidly. And part of that spread, necessarily, will involve the United States and what posture we take. The United States today said they didn't know, were unaware that Israel was going to do -- take such offensive action.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which in and of itself is telling. Mollie, as we look live there, we can see explosions and counter explosions. Just for people's knowledge, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has a network of tunnels. You thought \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had tunnels under Gaza. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has exponential miles of tunneling and hiding. And they have had their leadership go underground, and they have obviously a lot more sophisticated weaponry and missiles that they have been using in the hundreds and thousands on Israel in the past weeks.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Yes, totally apart from what you think about the underlying political issues, what we have witnessed in the last couple of weeks is one of the most impressive military campaigns in history. Whether it was the pager situation which took out so many fighters relative to civilian casualties, whether it was the other explosives, the entire leadership structure of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been taken out here. This is something that's going to be written about in history books for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 And yes, there is concern about Iran because Iran has been using \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as proxies in their efforts to destroy Israel. But it's just been under a year since October 7th. So much time had to be spent on the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  portion of things while \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was exploiting the situation. And now that Israel is able to fight back here, it really is impressive, and it shows what happens when you want to win a war rather than spend a lot of time spending money on war and not actually achieving your objectives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, no matter what you think about the Israeli prime minister, in this environment, when he is facing all kinds of threats from Iran and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , he delivers a fiery speech. He had a warning for Iran today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: I have a message for the tyrants of Tehran. If you strike us, we will strike you. There is no place in Iran, that the long arm of Israel cannot reach. For too long, the world has appeased Iran. He turns a blind eye to its internal repression. It turns a blind eye to its external aggression. That appeasement must end.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some of that speech, Bill, quickly, seemed like it was aimed at U.S. foreign policy under the Biden-Harris administration when it comes to Iran.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes, it definitely was. They have been in odds largely over that problem. But to Mollie's point, this strike, they now killed a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander in Lebanon. Before that, they killed a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  operative inside Tehran. So, and they have these pagers that blew up. So they must be feeling very insecure in Iran. I don't think Iran wants full scare war because I don't think they'll come out very well at the end.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, well, we're going monitor this and breaking news from Lebanon, from Beirut. Trey Yingst on the ground with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest FOX battleground polling, plus former President Trump meets Ukraine's leader, and Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Folks have plenty enough problems that they need their president to focus on instead of a wall.\par \par \uc2 This whole thing about the border, we've been to the border. We've been to the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You haven't been to the border.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And I haven't been to Europe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're going to have 2 million people cross this border for the first time ever. You're confident this border is secure?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We have a secure border.\par \par \uc2 My value around what we need to do to secure our border, that value has not changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Vice President Harris making a trip to the southern border, in fact, right at this hour she is down there in Douglas, Arizona, along the border wall, talking to officials down there about what to do. We have new battleground state polls. As you take a look at the four polls over the last couple of days, these are likely voters. And, again, Arizona 51-48, Georgia, you see Vice President Harris with the lead. North Carolina, slight lead for the former president, and tied, as it has been, in Pennsylvania. One interesting point from Arizona, the breakout on the issue of immigration, overwhelming support for the former president, also on the economy, and obviously, as we have seen in every state, abortion flips the other way for the vice president.\par \par \uc2 We are back with our panel. Mollie, anything surprising on any of these numbers? And what about the vice president's trip?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, what we are seeing with so many of these polls is that it's essentially a tied race. And that's interesting because 2016, 2020 we were told that Trump had no shot of winning. He, of course, won in 2016, narrowly lost in 2020, and so these tied numbers are indicative of just how flawed of a candidate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 And I think this border trip today happened at the first possible time for her given the other news that came out, that her administration allowed nearly half-a-million criminals, many of them violent criminals, to just be in the country and roaming freely, including 16,000 convicted murderers and 13,000 rapists -- or maybe it was the other way around, 13,000 murderers and 16,000 convicted rapists. This is something that would be ending a presidential campaign in any sane country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Juan, to Mollie's point, Democrats when the former president said that on the stump for months and months, even years, about convicted felons and murderers, they would laugh him off the stage, saying he doesn't have that detail. ICE director's letter to Representative Gonzalez saying exactly as Mollie said, 425,000 total non-detained non-citizens who have been convicted of a crime are now inside the U.S. And you see the convicted of homicide, convicted of sexual abuse. These are ICE numbers, and it's kind of shocking. It backs up the former president's statement.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, these people came in over time. And it's shocking. We can compare it to how many murderers and rapists we have who are native born. But the point of her trip to the border.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, these are people who came across the border illegally.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Correct, but we don't know the time period, when they came and when they entered, and we can't compare it to anything. Those numbers are out of context. I think what you have to understand is the purpose of her visit today, and I think it's a smart move, is to go down there and say there's good news. The good news is we've had the lowest level of crossings in four years according to the data coming last month. And it's in large part because her administration, the Biden administration, changed the asylum rules so that people who cross illegally are no longer eligible for asylum. That's tough. And this week she focused on the economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, this happened three-and-a-half years --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: She says she has a gun. She is going as a centrist candidate, introducing herself to voters against people who want to demonize her and make her look like she is some terrible person.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, she has had a lot of policies on immigration. We showed some of them from 2019. But these numbers are going down. We have reported about the exact numbers that Juan is talking about. But it happened three-and-a- half years after the administration, people on the border will argue, let in millions and millions and millions of people, Bill McGurn.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: I think this is a disaster for her, because can you argue, as Juan does, that's why she is behind on immigration. People see these numbers that correlate with the crimes that they have been reading about, some of them very brutal, and they don't like it. And whether she is border czar or root causes czar, she had responsibility for the border. And, you know, this is what we have today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. All right, guys, we're going to do Winners and Losers next time. I wanted to squeeze it all in with all the breaking news. Have great weekend.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, that sound means it's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Who am I introducing next? Who's next?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who ordered the burger with extra cheese? Whose next?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And I try and get some proper hamburgers.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Gone is the day of everyone thinking they could actually live the American dream.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We can't afford four more years of this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let's extinguish him for good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In extinguish, you mean vote him out?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP, FORMER FIRST LADY: They are only fueling a toxic atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is a cultural rot within the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You do anything to harm this person, we are going to blow your largest cities and the country itself to smithereens.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Our enemies seek not only to destroy us. They seek to destroy our common civilization.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think he should terminate the ambassador and move forward with somebody who understands the appropriate lines for our allies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't want to involve us to election period of the United States.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think we need this mayor to step aside.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: I look forward to defending myself.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Some things are more important than staying in power.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And I think some people think we must hate each other's guts. It's just not true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This man was a living redemption story. He encountered darkness and met it with love.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Johnny Cash there in the Statuary Hall.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, a preview of the vice presidential debate, that happens October 1st. Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests are FEMA administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  and Alabama Senator Katie Britt, plus Harris-Walz national co-chair Senator Chris Coons.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to watch Ainsley Earhardt's interview with former first lady Melania Trump. That runs 10:00 p.m. eastern Sunday. And tomorrow at noon, Griff Jenkins has an exclusive interview with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . That's a lot to watch.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and all this week. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts --\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240928ek9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Polls Remain Close in Presidential Race between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Continues to State There Will be No More Debates with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; Democratic Senate Decision in Maryland Angela Alsobrooks Reportedly Benefited Illegally from Tax Breaks She Didn't Earn; President Biden Hosts World Leaders in Delaware. Israelis Urge Civilians Evacuations Near \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Sites; U.S. Sending Additional Troops To Middle East; New Poll Shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Leading In Sunbelt States; Ryan Routh Faces Life In Prison; Debate Over Precedent Set By Pager Attacks; Shifting Pennsylvania Voter Registration Trends; Some Republicans Consider Changing Nebraska Process.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Mark Meredith, Aishah Hasnie, Jacqui Heinrich,  Danamarie McNicholl, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 7,870 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Have a great night everyone, that's it for us.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I mean, next to Tyrus's concert. All right, Dana, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the Biden-Harris administration is sending what it calls a small number of additional U.S. troops to the Middle East. This comes as the Israeli offensive against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists in Lebanon is expanding tonight. Hundreds of airstrikes have been launched with Israel warning residents of southern Lebanon to stay away from \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's weapons storage sites.\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's health ministry\uc2  says more than 490 people have been killed so far in the most devastating barrage since the two fought 18 years ago.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight with the Middle East on the brink. Jacqui Heinrich at the White House with what the administration is saying about the growing conflict in the region, but we begin with senior correspondent Mike Tobin live tonight in Tel Aviv. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: And Bret, after almost a year in which Israel says some 9,000 missiles, rockets and drones were fired into Israel's north, the Air Force responded, striking at some 1300 targets in the South of Lebanon, as the defense minister put it, to demolish what \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  built over the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Lebanon says this was the most deadly day since Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  went to war in 2006 as Israel rained air strikes down on southern Lebanon, attempting to stop \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket fire, which has menaced and threatened Israeli civilians for almost a year.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  spoke directly to the Lebanese people.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: For too long, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been using you as human shields. It placed rockets in your living rooms and missiles in your garage.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): To prove that point, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  released video spokespeople say show a home used to store a missile complete with an opening for the weapon to be launched and air strikes followed by secondary explosions as stored weapons detonated.\par \par \uc2 Israel took the step of sending electronic communications to tens of thousands of Lebanese civilians, warning them to get away from stored \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  weapons.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI, ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES SPOKESPERSON: We advise civilians from Lebanese villages located in and next to buildings and areas used by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  for military purpose, such as those used to store weapons.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Panicked Lebanese civilians created a traffic jam, fleeing north to escape the bombing. Lebanese officials say the collateral damage from the Israeli air strikes include two ambulance crews blown up, hundreds dead, more than a thousand injured.\par \par \uc2 IMRAN RIZA, U.N. DEPUTY LEBANON COORDINATOR: What we've seen over the last week has been dramatic. Has been dramatic. The consequences on civilians has been huge.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Through it all, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  continued to fire rockets into Israel.\par \par \uc2 CHAIM ELHARAR, KIRYAT BIALIK, ISRAEL RESIDENT: I feel more afraid and you know, I don't know what happened tomorrow, after tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And word tonight that Israel targeted the number three in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's chain of command Ali Karaki. Israel's Defense Minister Yoav Gallant made the public statement that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  now sits alone at the top. But that may have been premature. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  says Karaki is alive and moved to a protected space, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin live in Tel Aviv. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is trying to get all sides to take a step back as fighting in the Middle East intensifies. Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that part of the story live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The Pentagon is calling or sending, rather, what it calls a small number of additional U.S. troops to the Middle East, out of an abundance of caution to support the 40,000 troops already in that region, amid growing concerns of a wider war as volleys of attacks between Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  grow hotter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've been briefed on the latest developments in Israel and Lebanon. My team is in constant contact with their counterparts, and we're working to deescalate in a way that allows people to return to their home safely.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden meeting with his Emirati counterpart in the oval office before heading to New York for the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 , where the Secretary General has made clear Israel is under scrutiny following pager explosions targeting \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO GUTERRES\uc2 , U.N. SECURITY-GENERAL: But the logic of this kind of devices is for a preemptive strike in a major military operation to dismantle the communications capacity of your adversary.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden will speak for the U.S. position, the V.P. not planning to attend amid questions about how she would handle future U.S. policy. Harris instead hitting battleground states with an economic pitch after an NBC poll showed Trump ahead by nine points on the issue.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's about what we can do more to invest in the aspirations, the ambitions and the dreams of the American people, while addressing the challenges.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But the Details are sparse. Even Reuters the newswire exclusively announcing the plans reports, "None of the sources would provide specific details on the expected new policies, and the Harris campaign would not comment on any new proposals."\par \par \uc2 And the Harris campaign is trying to reframe several missed opportunities to articulate whether Americans are, in fact, better off than they were four years ago.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, WHITE HOUSE SPOKESMAN: I think when people are running for president, they want to see, what are you going to do for me, it's not a retrospective question. It's actually a forward looking question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Harris is keeping her foot in the door for the foreign policy discussion, despite her absence on the world stage at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  she has a separate meeting with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  on her calendar this week, she's also touting the endorsement of 700 current and former national security officials who say that she defends America's democratic ideals and Trump endangers them, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The former president will appear shortly near the spot of the first assassination attempt against him in July. He's currently speaking in containing (ph) Pennsylvania. Trump will be in Indiana, Pennsylvania, 50 miles from Butler to talk about his inflation and food prices policies.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, he spoke to farmers and other producers in Smithton, Pennsylvania. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is with the Trump campaign in Indiana, Pennsylvania tonight, we're covering Pennsylvania all over Aishah, good evening.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. And in regards to the second assassination attempt in Florida, the former president now wants Florida to take over that investigation. Accusing the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the DOJ, of mishandling and downplaying that case.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have reached out to those agencies for a response to those accusations. Meanwhile, former president, yes, indeed, back in this battleground state to try to flip it as it could once again decide who goes to the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My first call, I'm going to call up President Xi. I'm going to say you have to honor the deal you made.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Tonight, former President Trump promising to force China to buy $50 billion in U.S. agriculture exports as he vows to stand up for American farmers.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're just being ripped off by other countries, and this administration has allowed it to happen.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Today's campaign stop comes as the GOP nominee pitches himself as the best candidate to bring down food prices in the battleground state.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign sees its path to victory through Pennsylvania, Georgia and North Carolina, and tonight, brand new New York Times Sienna polling has trump up in North Carolina and two other Sunbelt states.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you heard from President Trump at all?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But his endorsed candidate for governor, Lieutenant Governor Mark Robinson, not by Trump's side at a rally over the weekend after allegations surfaced that he wrote disturbing comments on a porn website over a decade ago, Robinson denies that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't not believe him. I don't believe him. I just think that you have to let these things sometimes play out in the court of public opinion.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump has not yet pulled his endorsement, but some top Republicans are creating distance.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2  pulling the plug on future ads for him in that state, and several of his top aides stepping down over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, back here in Pennsylvania, some state Republicans are now celebrating a small victory. Here in Luzerne County, a competitive county that sits right next to President Biden's birthplace of Scranton, just registered more Republicans than Democrats, something that hasn't happened in decades, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live in Pennsylvania. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We're learning new information tonight about the man accused of trying to shoot former President Trump eight days ago at his Florida golf course. Ryan Routh appeared in a federal courtroom today. This comes as the Justice Department releases a letter he allegedly wrote in the event his assassination attempt failed, we're also hearing about a phone call the suspect made from