obable cause hearing where we could learn more about this government's massive investigation, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Danamarie, thank you. Stocks were up today as traders extend the post Fed rate cut rally. The Dow gaining 61, the S&P 500 finished ahead 16. The NASDAQ rose 26.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a former congressman tells us how software is changing the way wars are fought around the world.\par \par \uc2 First, we go around the world, Beyond our Borders tonight. The Soyuz Capsule carrying two Russians and one American from the International Space Station lands in Kazakhstan. The astronauts returned after 374 days aboard the space station.\par \par \uc2 Friday, they broke the record for the longest continuous stay there. Also in the capsule was American Tracy Dyson, who was in the space station for six months.\par \par \uc2 And French athlete Tom Pages combines motocross and paragliding to produce the world's first ride and fly in the French Alps, the five times X game gold medalist spent more than two years working on the spectacular stunt with good video as well.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, more news from Washington after this, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: China continues to behave aggressively, testing us all across the region. And it's true across the scope of our relationship, including on economic and technology issues. At the same time, we believe intense competition requires intense diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 We see this engagement as important for conflict prevention and crisis management amidst our strategic competition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden (INAUDIBLE) talking to other leaders there over the weekend about China.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a look at how software is changing the way we fight wars, and specifically with China. Just today, the Biden administration proposed banning Chinese software and hardware and connected with connection with autonomous vehicles in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Our guest wrote about how software is reshaping the battlefield in a Wall Street Journal op-ed over the weekend. Former Wisconsin Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  joins us this evening. He chaired the select committee on communist China's -- Chinese Communist Party. And now works for \uc2 Palantir\uc2  technologies as their head of defense. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , FORMER REPRESENTATIVE, WISCONSIN: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Back in the mix here. You wrote this as a warning. Why?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We know for a fact that if our allies have the capability to do something like this, our adversaries are going to exploit every opportunity to do damage to America and the free world more broadly. We've had a series of incidents that go back to the revelation that the Chinese have infected our critical infrastructure with malware that they could weaponize in the midst of a war.\par \par \uc2 That suggests we don't know our own vulnerabilities with the requisite level of clarity. Imagine, for example, we were to get into a conflict with China over Taiwan, and they launched a preemptive cyber strike that disabled water utilities in the Midwest or prevented Americans from getting access to energy. Imagine the chaos that would cause.\par \par \uc2 Or if they exploited the vulnerabilities inherent in the fact that Chinese smart cranes dominate ports across the world. What they could do to international commerce? So, on the modern battlefield, we need to not only know our adversary, but know ourselves, and map our supply chain in great detail.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And you write about this that "Look at the damage done by exploding pagers" -- speaking about the Israelis there. "Then, imagine the chaos caused by haywire power grids, or the economic consequences of frozen ports. We should focus on mitigating the main risk to our way of life by building a free world technology stack, trusted allied vendors and chips to servers to software."\par \par \uc2 So, you're talking right there about supply chains. You're talking about changing the dynamic. Can this administration, do it? You see, you were on that committee. You chaired it. Do you sense that that's an urgency? I know you worked with your ranking member about it.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, I think there is a great deal of bipartisan support for a tougher strategy on China. The fundamental problem with this administration is they continue to pursue this idea that for some -- that somehow our interests align with the Chinese Communist Party when it comes to responsible use of critical technology like artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 I think that is naive. I think that's foolish. I think the only interest Xi Jinping has when it comes to A.I. is in dominating the technology and using it for dystopian, totalitarian purposes.\par \par \uc2 And I think, in light of the pager attack, in light of the weaponization of supply chains, more broadly, we're going to see a bifurcation of the world into a totalitarian tech stack and a free world tech stack.\par \par \uc2 Our job is to make sure that the latter prevails over the former. And step one in doing that is recognizing that the CCP is not a responsible actor when it comes to technology or climate change or fentanyl cooperation. That's a fundamental first step we need to take.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you worried since the handover in the Democratic Party to Vice President Harris and President Biden stepping aside as the nominee that the mental capacity question is a problem as president, as you look around the world and world leaders look at us?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'm worried that we don't have strong leadership in the White House, and I'm worried that our foremost adversary, the Chinese Communist Party will take advantage of the transition period, in particular, between the election day and when a new president is sworn in, and that we could see the risk of an invasion of Taiwan increase in the short term.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, I'm just worried about the fact that we're not attacking this problem with a sense of urgency. If our goal is actually to prevent World War III, we would be working much more aggressively -- we would be taking intelligent risks. Look, The Times of Israel reported today that the whole pager operation was cooked up by a highly talented female intelligence operative in her 20s.\par \par \uc2 Ask yourself, could something like that happen in the American national security establishment today? I doubt it, because we've come too risk averse. The defense industrial base has become too ossified. We need to leverage our asymmetric advantage, which is our ability to produce world weapons grade software and apply it to revitalizing our entire defense industrial base.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's fascinating, something that the country has to deal with. We appreciate you coming in. Former Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 . You're getting used to that?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'm very happy being more anonymous and spending more time with my family.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We like to hear from you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take a closer look at the tight voter registration numbers and one of the most important states. You heard a little bit about that earlier in the presidential election and later, pollsters Alex Castellanos and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  give us their thoughts on where we stand right now, this minute in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 We heard Aisha reporting earlier about registration for Republicans in one particular county, Luzerne County in Pennsylvania. They are happy with that. They should be happy all across the state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 It is a big story. If we go back to 2016, you see that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins by a little more than 44,000 votes. We go to 2020 in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wins by a little more than 81,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 But let's compare and contrast the registrations in all of these years. Democrats had 892,000 more Democrats than Republicans registered in 2016. 666,000 more Democrats than the Republicans in 2020. And look at this. These are the registrations. Now, 338,000 more Democrats than Republicans, but that number has been almost cut in half.\par \par \uc2 If you go back to the map and we go back to 2016, again, the spread, 44,000 for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's win. That is at a place where Democrats had 554,000 more Democrats registered than are registered now in the state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 That is a huge story that we're going to be watching to see how that affects the vote on election night.\par \par \uc2 Another big story that we're watching is the Electoral College. The path to 270. Again, takes 270 electoral votes to win. The gray states are our toss ups, let's say, for the sake of argument that the vice president wins across the Rust Belt, Wisconsin, Michigan, and Pennsylvania. Let's say, and there are some new polls indicating that former President Trump has some strength in the Sun Belt, and he wins North Carolina, Georgia, Arizona, Nevada, 269, to 268.\par \par \uc2 That brings us to the congressional district states. There is Maine, which traditionally splits, and Nebraska with three. Traditionally, around Omaha, that is a democratic place. One electoral vote goes there.\par \par \uc2 However, there is an effort to try to get Nebraska to be winner take all on its electoral votes. It would have to be the state legislature, specifically the State Senate, that blocks a filibuster, and now one of those senators -- Republican senators, State Senator \uc2 Mike McDonnell\uc2 , came out with a statement today.\par \par \uc2 Saying, "After deep consideration, it is clear to me right now, 43 days from election day, it is not the moment to make this change. I will not change my long-held position and will oppose any attempted changes to our electoral college system before the 2024 election."\par \par \uc2 Back to the map, if it stays like this and this scenario played out, the traditional outcome would be that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would pick up that and get to 270 and the White House.\par \par \uc2 Trump allies, including Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , are still fighting for this winner take all option.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): If they change the law in Nebraska, it won't be on the -- on the phone in the middle of the night, it will be through a democratic process. The entire federal delegation of Nebraska, House members and two senators want this change. To my friends in Nebraska, that one electoral vote could be the difference between Harris being president or not and she is a disaster for Nebraska and the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: State Senator McDonnell might be the one who stands in the way of all that. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a closer look at what the polls are telling us tonight about the presidential election. With us tonight, political consultant, cofounder and chairman of Purple Strategies, Alex Castellanos, and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former advisor and poster for President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . He's the CEO of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2  Chairman of the \uc2 Harris Poll\uc2 . Gentlemen, great to have you back. Of those things I just talked about, that registration in Pennsylvania is the real deal.\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: It reminds us that, despite the everyday vagaries of the campaign, oh, this happened today, that happened yesterday, that underneath this, a lot of Americans are thinking, things were better for me four years ago. I was safer. My economy was better, and things might be better for me for years from now if I elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There's a lot of this election that's already over.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, I referenced the sunbelt polls, and that's coming from "The New York Times"-Siena poll out in which the former president, 50-45 in Arizona, 49-45 in Georgia, 49-47 in North Carolina. And as I also mentioned, the vice president is showing some strength in Wisconsin, Michigan, and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER BILL CLINTON ADVISER: Yes, so I think you are really seeing these kinds of splits here where the Electoral College is shifting around. People think that everything is given. If you go back, Texas used to be Democratic, California used to be Republican. You have a real fight over in the Midwest here. It could split in half really and this election. And I think that Trump is picking up better with Latino voters, and that's why he is doing better in the sunbelt. And so I think that that's a change.\par \par \uc2 Now, I also think the state-by-state polls are out of sync with some of the national polls we're seeing. They can't be both accurate. If it's this close in these states, she is not winning by four points nationally.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does the Lieutenant Governor Robinson thing in North Carolina affect North Carolina for Trump?\par \par \uc2 PENN: It could. I tend to doubt it because I think the presidential race leads above the governor's race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's the thing we're not thinking of?\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: In North Carolina, usually things below you on the ballot don't hurt the guy above. But the Democrats are doing some pretty enough spots. I think you hit one of the biggest things is the change in registration, which I think is driven by culture. The reason you are seeing Trump talk about transgender surgeries paid for by taxpayers for prisoners, the reason you're seeing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  all of a sudden saying, oh, I've got a gun, and if anybody tries to break into my house and interview me, I'll shoot them. It is because they understand that cultures upstream of the economy and every other issue. And that's the thing to watch.\par \par \uc2 Those registration numbers tell us that there is a big cultural boom for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  underneath all of this. and I think that may be the powerful thing that drives this election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's also powerful that they are making choices about where they go, Mark, and where they don't go. The Al Smith dinner coming out, Jim Gaffigan posted out the invite in which it says President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and after that went out we heard from the former president that he doesn't want to do another debate. He said, I guess the next and final time we will see these two kids together will be at the 79th Al Smith Memorial Dinner on October 17th. That dinner traditionally is two candidates ribbing each other. Take a look back at Hillary Clinton and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I have no doubt that Hillary is going to laugh quite a bit tonight, sometimes even at an appropriate moment.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Donald wanted me drug tested before last night's debate.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: And look, I've got to tell you, I am so flattered that Donald thought I used some sort of performance enhancer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's fun, Mark. I have been there a few times. But the vice president says she is not going.\par \par \uc2 PENN: I have been there as well. I think her positioning that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is unacceptable and a threat to democracy means it's very hard for her to go to a forum and just joke with him because I think that gives him the respect of a colleague running for president that she doesn't want to give him.\par \par \uc2 I think it's a bad thing that she's not going. And I think Trump's decision on the debate basically does make sense at this point. It's too risky for him in the final days. He thinks there is a natural arc here where she has peaked. And now she is going to really have to figure out ways to get another point or two which are declining.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are very few moments where you see Americans or get to talk to Americans in that big number of 70 million roughly together.\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: In a debate, yes. And again, they get to test each other. We learn more about not only what they're for but who they are. So it's a shame to see them pass up that opportunity. But if you're \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and you think about the great strong women leaders who have been elected, whether prime minister of England, \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 , they are strong, tough women, serious people. And \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  didn't cackle or laugh a lot. And I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is concerned that maybe going into this environment, on top of the good reasons Mark mentioned, dignifying her opponent, she may not have wanted to put herself in a position where she was taken less seriously. She seems to be doing a pretty good job of that already.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is the thing -- obviously the press wants more interviews. We always want more interviews. We always want more access. But is that whole dynamic of limiting her exposure to tough interviews, Mark, is it having an effect at all in the electorate, how they think about the race?\par \par \uc2 PENN: I think it is both a strength and a vulnerability for her. It's a strength because her image makers and herself are able to project an image here of being likable, of being for some popular policies, of never having to address what she really supported before. And it's a vulnerability because 20 or 30 percent, particularly independent voters who want to make up their mind, realize they are not getting those things to make up their mind. And if the Trump campaign really effectively attacked it, then, of course, it would be a vulnerability. I think it's more of a strength today than a vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So has this race come down to how Democrats paint \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a risk and his strength as a risk versus the risk that Republicans say is the empty vessel that you don't know what she is really going to do, remember 2019?\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: The risk of the unknown, the risk that she is really the woke candidate she is trying to get away from. It's tough, getting tougher for her to maintain that basement strategy like Biden used, because she is beginning to adopt Trump's agenda, right? Middle class tax cuts, fracking, what's the latest one? Oh, plastic straws, she is for that, too, and again, guns. So how do you say you are not going back if you're going back to your opponent's agenda?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gentlemen, thanks so much, as always.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our 50 races in 50 days segment focuses on a major twist in the Maryland Senate race. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel will give us their thoughts about where things stand.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Officials in Birmingham, Alabama, are pleading for the public's help locating suspects in Saturday night's shooting that left four people dead and more than a dozen injured. Police described the attack as a targeted hit by multiple shooters who opened fire on a popular night spot in the city's Five Points South district. Authorities say they are going through tips and the public, $100,000 being offered for any information.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says violent crime dropped in the U.S. last year. The Bureau says overall violent crime was down three percent in the new report. It says murders and nonnegligent manslaughter dropped nearly 12 percent. Critics say the report is not accurate because it does not include big cities. The National Crime Victimization Survey found the violent crime victimization rate rose from 16 per 1,000 people in 2020 to 22.5 in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The House, meantime, is trying to figure out how to pass the interim spending bill to get the government funded this week and throughout the year, until the end of the year. There was a weekend agreement to avoid a shutdown. But House Speaker Mike Johnson needs a coalition of Democrats and Republicans to pass that bill. Johnson denies that this is the same play former Speaker Kevin McCarthy ran last year to avert a shutdown. GOP Representative Anna Paulina Luna says conservatives are, quote, "getting rolled right and left."\par \par \uc2 Fifty races in 50 days. There's a significant development tonight in the race to become Maryland's next U.S. Senator. It's our spotlight contest in our series. It comes as the balance of power in the Senate as you see there sits at 51 Democrats and independents who caucus with the Democrats, and 49 Republicans. Democratic nominee in Maryland Angela Alsobrooks reportedly benefited illegally from tax breaks she did not earn. That could open things up for popular Republican former governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  whose potential victory would be huge for the GOP effort to take control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Here's correspondent Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANGELA ALSOBROOKS, (D-MD) SENATE CANDIDATE: Are you all fired up?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, Maryland Democratic Senate candidate Angela Alsobrooks is apologizing after journalists uncovered that 53-year-old claimed multiple property tax credits she should not have qualified for. The campaign telling FOX, Alsobrooks, quote, "has reached out to the District of Columbia to resolve the issue and make any necessary payment." It's unclear how much money Homestead Exemptions, which reduces a property owner's tax liability, may have saved her over several years on two homes. But her opponent --\par \par \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2 , (R-MD) SENATE CANDIDATE: Thank you, Maryland!\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: -- former Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , says voters deserve answers. His campaign telling FOX, "It's deeply disturbing that Angela Alsobrooks thinks the rules don't apply to her. She campaigns on raising taxes while failing to pay her own and taking advantage of tax credits reserved for the poor and elderly."\par \par \uc2 Polling shows the race between Hogan and Alsobrooks remains a dead heat, a surprise to some top Democrats after President Biden won Maryland by 30 points four years ago.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 GARY PETERS\uc2 , (D-MI) DEMOCRATIC SENATORIAL CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE: So it's just a matter of running a campaign and making sure people understand that she is a top-quality candidate.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Alsobrooks is also on the attack, launching ads like these.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am a diehard lifelong Republican.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Accusing Hogan of being a secret supporter of Former President Trump. But Hogan told FOX this summer he is not even voting for Trump this November, leaving voters to look beyond the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I don't think it's going to have much to do with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is not competing in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Hogan continues to distance himself from the former president as he tries to appeal to Maryland's moderate voters. He's also touting the support of West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  who recently left the Democratic Party. Hogan and Alsobrooks, Bret, they are going to debate just once, though, before the election. A lot of interest in this race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Definitely. Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The latest installment of my documentary series "The Unauthorized Histories" is now on FOX Nation. It's a five-part at the history of political parties in America. The programs trace the rise of our two-party system and how the parties have helped shape our politics and our nation. We have an excerpt tonight dealing with the origins of the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I was a Democrat most of my life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Democratic government is not practically possible without political parties. And so if our parties are not healthy, our democracy is not healthy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In many ways, the history of political parties is the history of America.\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN DELANO ROOSEVELT, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I pledge myself to a new deal for the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As Americans prepare to cast their votes, they face a choice between candidates, principals, and parties.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Political parties are always changing because they are always interested in creating majorities.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And while for almost all of American history, our politics has been dominated by two major parties, third parties have played an important role.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The way to think about the independent candidates is not whether they are going to win, but's it's to think about which party listens to them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One of the things you see in history is that no party calls to unanimity on any group of American voters forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The history of -- "The Unauthorized History of Political Parties" is available right now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the presidential campaign, and President Biden goes to the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  with the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We're going to defeat \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And we are going to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The problem, I think, on the other side is that there is a devaluation of who the American people are.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE ALVAREZ, TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: We know that swing state voters absolutely agree with President Trump. We know that President Trump is ready to lead like he did during his four years in the White House.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, SPOKESMAN, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN: Vice President Harris is narrowing that gap. She is focused on making sure that people's lives continue to get better and don't go backwards like they would with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The state of the race right now as we look at the map of where candidates are going this week that usually tells a story. And this does. Former President Trump in Pennsylvania today as well as Vice President Harris. Then you've got North Carolina, you've got Georgia. The former president going to Minnesota. And then Vice President Harris in Nevada and also Arizona.\par \par \uc2 As we look at the new battleground state polls. You can see how tight this is. This is the Real Clear Politics average of all the polls. And Trump with a lead in Arizona. And you can see, it's all within one, two points all over the place. And if you look at the national head-to-head, it is the average of recent polls, there you see 49.4 to 47.2. That is where we stand at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Democratic strategist \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2  Chief, and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. Katie, it does seem like we portrayed this race as rustbelt and sunbelt. And they may be splitting. And it may be happening before our eyes, a little bit of separation for both of them.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It looks like a national race. These candidates are not focused on one area. They are focused on all over. And when you look and dive deeper into the polling, it's interesting to see which coalitions each candidate is trying to lock up. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is headed to Arizona where she is losing Hispanic voters. And that is an issue with illegal immigration she has tried to run away with.\par \par \uc2 Also, I think that you are looking at the numbers in terms of independents who have not decided who they are voting for in all of the swing states. No matter which belt you are looking at, their still top issue remains the economy. And they are not satisfied with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her answers of saying just trust me and what I'm going to do versus how you felt and what your life was like four years ago under Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kevin, the vice president and her team accepting an invitation for a second debate, saying, "The American people deserve another opportunity to see Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  debate before they cast their ballots," although they're casting ballots now. "It would be unprecedented in modern history for there to be just one general election debate."\par \par \uc2 Former president saying there have been two because Biden and Harris. And he says it's too late in his mind. I wonder, for the people in the Democratic side that I talked to, say why would she do that? She had the win in her mind and in a lot pundits minds? Are they seeing something that we are not seeing as far as the need for a second presidential debate?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC CAMPAIGN CONSULTANT: Yes, Bret, I think it's a great question. I think the need is there in terms of reintroducing herself to large portions of this population. No other event is going to be on the same scale as that first event between the vice president and the former president that saw 70 million viewers tune in. She needs that moment once again. Again, these stops on the campaign trail, these rallies are all critically important, the interviews that she is doing. But on a large scale you have the vice presidential debate next week. But beyond that, there aren't that kind of large viewing events. And she is eager to take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  certainly again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: May change. We can't say that the former president doesn't change his mind. He has changed his mind before. But it seems like he is pretty definitive at this point, Byron.\par \par \uc2 YORK: It does. And she needs it because nothing has really moved the needle of lot in this whole campaign. Our FOX News national poll came out last week. Harris up 50-48. The month before that, August, same poll, Trump up 50-49. And before that, which was before the big switch in early July, Trump was up 49-48. Nobody below 48, nobody above 50. So you have got this tiny band of possibly persuadable voters in there, and you have two hunkered down, locked in groups on the other side, and both candidates will want to see if there is some way they can chip away at the other side.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Usually, obviously, Katie, elections come down to the U.S. economy and how people feel around the kitchen table. But "Wall Street Journal" editorial board, "Biden leaves his successor a world of disorder. President Biden will address the \uc2 United Nations\uc2  on Tuesday in what is likely to be his last big moment on the world stage. It is a far more dangerous world than Mr. Biden inherited, far less congenial for U.S. interests, human freedom, and democracy. Addressing this gathering storm will be difficult and dangerous. Whoever wins the White House will have to abandon the failed policy of the Biden years lest we end up careening into a global conflict with catastrophic consequence." Speaking of which, the A.P. writes this headline, "U.S. sending more troops to the Middle East as violence rises between Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, it shows that the comments from the vice president during the first debate, that there are no U.S. troops in combat zones, remains to be untrue, and that has still not been corrected by the hosts of the first debate. The argument against a second Trump presidency has always been from the left that we don't like chaos, we don't want things to be crazy. Well, the world is in a very, very dangerous place. You have this new war on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in the north in Lebanon, the north of Israel. The Biden administration continues to argue that we don't want to escalate. But the real issue is Iran, and this administration has really done nothing to stop them from fomenting additional escalation into something that the next president will certainly have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden, Kevin, had world leaders in Delaware, not the White House, in Delaware. And his critics say he does not instill confidence. These are not deepfakes or cheap fakes. This is him talking with those leaders in front of reporters this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to thank you all for being here. And now, who am I introducing next? Who is next?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It Prime Minister Modi was next from India. And so, there are people like the former Congressman Gallagher who says that's a potential problem.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: That quad meeting was important. I think it was meaningful to all the leaders invited back to his residence there at his high school. But certainly, I think you are going to see the president in these last few months of the administration focus on what is tangible around the world, right, double down in his speech tomorrow about alliances and the importance of alliances, working towards a Middle East peace, of course, made much more difficult by what is going on right now in that region. Obviously, you have the \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  there, you have the \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  on its way, to your earlier question to Katie about troop movements in their region.\par \par \uc2 But these are tangible things that a president, a lame duck president can do on the international level when he can't really get anything done on the domestic level. But certainly you're going to still have some of those gaps he's very famous for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's quite something to watch, because there are hotspots all over the world. And we're watching them all. Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, or "Common Ground" segment with Senators \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Katie Britt on bipartisan efforts to protect children from the harms of social media. That will be one to watch.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240924ek9n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Port Strike Imminent?; Interview With Rep. Mike Kelly (R-PA); Suspected Trump Golf Course Gunman Remains in Custody; Harris and Trump Make Competing Economic Pitches; Israel Strikes Lebanon\b0\par\par\uc2 Katrina Campins, Max Gorden, Jennifer Griffin, Mike Tobin,  Danamarie McNicholl, Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie; Sandra Smith \par \uc2 9,705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Strike after strike, Israel blasting multiple \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets today, as fears of a wider war escalate by the minute.\par \par \uc2 More on this growing threat in a moment, but first to the presidential race here and one key issue in the Keystone State, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  targeting food prices in Pennsylvania, as he and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are locked in a fierce battle there.\par \par \uc2 Harris expected to be back in the state this week to make a new economic pitch. So who has the best pitch to win over voters and consumers?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to Trump's latest pitch.\par \par \uc2 For that, we go to Aishah Hasnie. She's in Indiana, Pennsylvania, for us at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Sandra. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the economy and inflation, very important not only to voters, but also to American farmers, specifically the farmers here in Pennsylvania. So Trump just met with them, held a big roundtable with them and listened to their woes and their complaints about what's been happening in terms of competition.\par \par \uc2 He said that Americans are being ripped off by China and he is going to deal with that day one. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So the first thing I'd do is, I would probably, my first call, I'm going to call up President Xi. I'm going to say you have to honor the deal you made. We made a deal. You buy $50 billion worth of American farm product.\par \par \uc2 And I guarantee you he will buy it 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, Trump is headed here to Indiana, Pennsylvania, next to talk about inflation.\par \par \uc2 This is an issue, Sandra, that's affecting red and blue voters in this state. In fact, Pennsylvania experienced the highest grocery inflation rate in the nation in 2023. We're talking up 8 percent year over year. That's according to Consumer Affairs.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Trump appears very focused on reaching voters in person the trail, rather than on the debate stage. Even as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has agreed now to an October 23 debate, Trump tells FOX News that is simply a bad idea because people will already have begun voting.\par \par \uc2 Now, he may not need another debate if he can break away in these polls. A brand-new New York Times/Siena poll is doing well for him. It's got Trump up two to five points in key Sun Belt battleground states. In fact, J.D. Vance, his running mate, is in North Carolina today, and Trump heads back this week as well.\par \par \uc2 Trump was there over the weekend, although without his endorsed candidate for governor. That's Lieutenant Governor Mark Robinson, who is facing quite the scandal in that state, allegations about writing disturbing things on a porn Web site over a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 He denies that, but he just lost some of his top staff over the weekend. Again, remember the Trump campaign strategy to the White House, their path to victory includes North Carolina, includes Pennsylvania and Georgia, and that is where we will see Trump in the next coming days.\par \par \uc2 Again, remarks here in Indiana, Pennsylvania, though, start about 7:00. A lot of voters here eager to know how he's going to bring down food prices, because that is such a problem for voters here in Pennsylvania and really across the country -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, we're just hours away from that.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie there in Indiana County, Pennsylvania, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be back in the state on Wednesday, where she will be reportedly announcing new economic proposals.\par \par \uc2 For the latest on that, we turn to our own Jacqui Heinrich. She's at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 So what do we know, Jacqui?\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Sandra, an NBC poll this weekend that was broadly positive for Harris showed her trailing Trump by nine points on the economy and eight on dealing with the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 And it seems that her campaign is getting the message. They're now planning another economic policy announcement for later on this week. But even Reuters, the newswire that got an exclusive about it, didn't have too many details. This is what they wrote -- quote -- "None of the sources would provide specific details on the expected new policies. And the Harris campaign would not comment on any new proposals."\par \par \uc2 Here's how the V.P. previewed it yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (D) AND U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I have named it an opportunity economy, which really, in short form, it's about what we can do more to invest in the aspirations, the ambitions and the dreams of the American people, while addressing the challenges that they face, whether it be the high price of groceries or the difficulty in being able to acquire homeownership.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Harris has still not held a formal news conference, and the few interviews she has done were by and large with nontraditional news outlets, like this one with Wired posted online Friday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: How was \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' life as a child?\par \par \uc2 I had the blessing of having a great childhood. I grew up in a neighborhood full of families, adults who were everything from construction workers to teachers, people who were proud of their lawn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This strategy, focusing more on mobilizing than persuading voters, has left many wanting more detail on her policy plans, but it appears so far she is leaning more into the contrast with Trump, even though she has struggled many times now to answer whether Americans are in fact better off than they were four years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, HARRIS/WALZ CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Well, I think, when people are running for president, they want to see what are you going to do for me? And it's not a retrospective question. It's actually a forward-looking question.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the choice on the ballot in November, it's either me or it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And my plan is actually to take on these things and to work for you and to try to help you bring those costs down, whereas \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is pushing the same old trickle-down agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Harris' new plans are expected to be announced in Pittsburgh on Wednesday, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, good stuff.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich live at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Inflation is a top issue in battleground Pennsylvania, as both candidates are targeting it. So whose plan will actually tackle it? We will speak with the Democratic strategist on that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, to investor \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kevin, it's a pleasure to have you on the program tonight.\par \par \uc2 So who is it? Who's better for the economy?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , "SHARK TANK": I don't know yet because I don't have any details from Harris. She has been all sugar, no protein. That's it. And it's become a really big sticking point with investors.\par \par \uc2 We have got to remember we have the largest economy on Earth. Over 85 percent of our jobs are created by the private sector. Business investors like me and leaders have to deal with whosoever in the White House in January. So whoever ends up on the 6th of November as the new mandate, we will have to deal with them.\par \par \uc2 So we need data. We need policy. And I'm getting a little critical now because I don't have any. So I need answers around taxation. For example, if you tell me the taxes are going to be 28 percent, plus state taxes on corporations' headquarters, I will have to worry that they move to Ireland again because we did that to them once and that didn't work out for us very well.\par \par \uc2 I really need to understand the idea of price controls. That didn't work in the '70s. It doesn't work anywhere else on Earth. And this whole idea of taxing unrealized gains, that -- you have got to remember the number one export of America is the American dream.\par \par \uc2 This is a 200-year-old economy that's based on supporting entrepreneurship. That destroys capital formation. Those ideas are beyond nonstarters. They're crazy. And so you have to get more details on that.\par \par \uc2 And I'd like her to sit down with a non-celebrity interviewer, a real reporter that asks her questions and follow-on questions. This kumbaya stuff with pictures of kids and everything, it's great. And I get it. And I'm not criticizing her for it because it's working for her in the polls.\par \par \uc2 But now we need details, and we don't have any.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And to that point, it would probably behoove her to be challenged on it a bit so that she can actually address voters and their concerns.\par \par \uc2 You could talk about inflation coming down or slowing from where it was, but prices have not. And standing there in Pennsylvania, she's going to have to explain how, under her watch, there in Pennsylvania, they have lived through some of the highest inflation in the entire country when you look state to state.\par \par \uc2 And right now, they're paying about 26 cents more for gasoline in that state than they are at the national average. So prices are still high there. But what about this notion she's pushing and her campaign is pushing that this is just the same old \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , OK? He's just pushing the same old trickle-down economics. We have heard this before.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: Dangerous strategy.\par \par \uc2 People in Pennsylvania and Michigan and all the other battleground states remember what they paid for eggs, bread and protein in the beginning of Trump's mandate. He's going to hammer hard on that. I really think she's got to start delivering some numbers, because, look, it's really all about the battleground states now.\par \par \uc2 We're less than 50 days away. And those people are going to make the decision of who the next president of the United States is. And I have employees in all those states. They're not feeling too good about what they're paying for just to live.\par \par \uc2 So if you go into a state like Michigan and talk to or in Pennsylvania and talk to people at the kitchen table about how they're feeling about what they're buying, whether it be energy, housing or food or protein, they're not loving it right now.\par \par \uc2 And so you really need to have a plan with some details that speaks to their decision-making. And, look, all this fair and love and war and politics, I totally get it. And I talk to her advisers directly to try and glean some information out of them.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not going to disclose who they are because I want access to them. And they keep telling me, don't worry, be happy. The crazy stuff she's talking about will never happen, but she's got to stay crazy on it because it helps from her base.\par \par \uc2 And I'm saying, this stuff's too crazy not to speak to.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'LEARY: She's got to deal with it. And it's time to actually disclose it.\par \par \uc2 She needs to talk to you. She needs to have a press conference. It would be unprecedented in modern times for a presidential candidate not to speak to the press. And no more celebrity interviews. I'm done with that. That's a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, voters really need to hear what the plan is. And the price gouging thing, I mean, I spoke to some economists that would typically lean left. They did not let that rollout and where it was going. So we will see what her new pitch is.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, great to have you. Thank you, \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, the read now from Democratic strategist Kelly Hyman.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 That didn't go over so well, the calling out corporate America and all those companies that are price gouging. It seems maybe she's trying to put something new out there. Are we going to hear something new from her campaign on how she's going to fix these sky-high prices?\par \par \uc2 KELLY HYMAN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, she has laid out a plan already, and it's basically talking about lowering costs to American people.\par \par \uc2 It's important for us to remember that she grew up middle class. And she talks about -- in that agenda, she talks about lowering grocery prices and the cost of that, besides other things, to American people.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I want to chat with you more, if you could.\par \par \uc2 Right now, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is taking questions. We're going to head there now live. He's taking a question, answering right now. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We are going to do a real job. We need the support of Pennsylvania. It's a very important state. And she is not going to be good to Pennsylvania, between pricing and energy.\par \par \uc2 They started off knocking the hell out of the energy that I had. I had it going at a level. We were number three and probably number four. And by the time I left, we were far and away number one, ahead of Saudi Arabia, ahead of Russia. And then they cut when they came in. They started cutting.\par \par \uc2 And then prices started going through the roof at the beginning. And what they did is, let's go back to what Trump was doing. We got to go back, and they opened up the stuff that they were cutting and it was a similar amount. But what they don't say is that we would be at least three to four times more production of energy right now, and your prices would never be what they are, because you can't compete when you have to pay that much for energy.\par \par \uc2 So they didn't do their job. And, by the way, if they won, you could forget about it. They would go -- they want to go to all-electric cars. How's that going to work? They don't go far. They're too expensive. They're made in China.\par \par \uc2 And on day one, I end the all-electric mandate. And I'm a fan of electric cars. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , frankly, gave me the most beautiful endorsement. He endorses me all the time. He says, this is the guy -- this is the only guy that's going to save our country. He's been very generous. And I think he makes an unbelievable product.\par \par \uc2 But you have to be able to also sell gasoline-powered cars. We have gasoline. And they want to use something that we don't have, but China has, because for the batteries