 \par \uc2 SMITH: We have heard that, Congressman Kelly. What do you think? Do you feel that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is stonewalling in this probe?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I don't think so much that they're stonewalling. They're being very careful to reveal whatever it is happened that day and what their involvement in that was.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think that's understandable. At the end of the day, that's not going to be acceptable. But, again, we're talking about the most elite groups that we have protecting us. And, sometimes, you have to be a little bit careful about what you reveal, because it's not just revealed to the task force. It's revealed to the world.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we have we have members with great talents when it comes to the military, what they have done in the past, and how they're really locating in on this and very -- studying it in every situation.\par \par \uc2 But I'm more interested in, what was the start of it, not what happened that day, but what was the start of it? What was it that we didn't know when we started that day? And then since then, what have we determined that we will never let happen again? It's a little deeper dive than that.\par \par \uc2 Listen, a lot of people have opinions on this. I can't go through a day without somebody telling me, this is what happened that day, and you need to you need to get on top of it. And my answer is always the same. That's interesting. So you were there?\par \par \uc2 And they said, me? No, I was never there. And I think -- but this is the type of investigation that's going to take place. People are going to pick up on bits and pieces and say, you know what, this is what I think. And they're honest in their opinions.\par \par \uc2 That's not the problem. Our goal is at the end of the time, when we're done with everything at the American people have the faith and trust and confidence that all of these different agencies are doing the best they can every single day, and that, that they have a bad day, why did they have that day -- that bad day?\par \par \uc2 And then what can we do to make sure it never happens again? That's the whole crux of what it is we need to find out.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, well, that is a lot. And we know you're up to the task, Congressman. Thank you very much for joining us. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, to the Mideast and fears of further escalation, Israel unleashing another series of strikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets in Lebanon to its north. And this could go on for some time.\par \par \uc2 To Mike Tobin now. He is live from Tel Aviv. He's got the latest from there for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And, Sandra, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  chief of staff has just given a name to this air-to-ground campaign in Southern Lebanon, calling it Operation Northern Arrows, as the \uc2 IDF\uc2  defense minister says they have spent the last day demolishing what \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  built up over the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Israel says they hit over 1,300 targets in Southern Lebanon. The numbers from the Health Ministry in Lebanon keep changing, but the latest figure we have is that 492 people have been killed, 1,645 have been injured, but, again, those numbers change.\par \par \uc2 Israel has been sending text message warning civilians to get away from \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  weapons storage and launchers. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  has released video, making the point that their targets are rockets and other weapons ready to launch. The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says a cruise missile in a home could be seen with an opening there ready to launch.\par \par \uc2 They also released video purporting to show an airstrike followed by secondary explosions of stored weapons. Israel's prime minister sent a message directly to the people of Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: For too long, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been using you as human shields. It placed rockets in your living rooms and missiles in your garage.\par \par \uc2 Those rockets and missiles are aimed directly at our cities, directly at our citizens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Israel's Home Front Command has told people not to travel in the north of the country and to avoid gathering in groups.\par \par \uc2 School was canceled on the Israeli side of the border and the Lebanese side of the border as well. Despite the Israeli air defenses, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was able to get some rockets off and get some rockets through the defenses. At least three struck down on the Israeli side, but minimal injuries on the Israeli side.\par \par \uc2 Israel says since, October 8, some 9,000 missiles, rockets and drones have been fired into the north of Israel. This air campaign is intended to cripple \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's ability to do that again, but Lebanese civilians are caught in the crossfire -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Mike Tobin live there in Tel Aviv for us.\par \par \uc2 Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon, meanwhile, says a small number of additional troops are heading to the Middle East, given these escalating tensions.\par \par \uc2 To Jennifer Griffin now. She's at the Pentagon. She has the latest from there for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Fears have increased that Israel is on the brink of initiating a ground war. But so far they have not moved enough soldiers north to the Lebanese border to do so. But they have called up a special forces unit, Unit 98, which just left Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Israel is striking from the air, benefiting from the fact \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  does not have an air force. Today, we learned from Pentagon Press Secretary Major General Pat Ryder that out of an abundance of caution the U.S. is sending a small number of additional military personnel to the Middle East to augment those already there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Don't believe that escalating this military conflict is in their best interest. It's certainly not going to be in the best interest of all those people that Prime Minister Netanyahu says he wants to be able to send back home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The U.S. has kept 40,000 troops in the Middle East since last year and at least a dozen warships and four Air Force fighter jet squadrons spread across the region.\par \par \uc2 Normally, the U.S. has about 34,000 troops in the Middle East, but that spiked to 50,000 a few weeks ago, when the U.S. expected Iran to strike Israel. Two \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  destroyers are in the Red Sea. The USS Georgia guided missile submarine remains near Israel. There are six U.S. warships in the Eastern Mediterranean Sea, including the USS Wasp amphibious assault ship with the 26th Marine Expeditionary Unit aboard.\par \par \uc2 And three Navy destroyers are in the Med. The USS \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  and its three destroyers are in the Gulf of Oman. About a half-dozen of the F-a-18 fighter jets from the \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  have been moved to a land base, we're told. Defense Secretary Austin also ordered a squadron of F-22s to the region.\par \par \uc2 So far, Iran has not moved to strike Israel as its main proxy, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , gets pounded in Lebanon by Israeli warplanes -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon for us.\par \par \uc2 Jen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A major port strike along the East Coast and the Gulf closing in. What will it mean for the American consumer already feeling tapped out?\par \par \uc2 To Max Gorden in California dealing with the far-reaching impacts.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Max.\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, West Coast ports are already seeing a surge in cargo as shippers divert goods. We'll have the latest on this developing story coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: One week away now from a potential strike at ports along the Gulf and East Coast, possibly costing the economy billions each day. And both sides don't seem any closer to a deal.\par \par \uc2 To Max Gorden now. He is coming to us from San Pedro, California, as shippers are already rerouting to the West Coast ahead of this possible strike, Max?\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Now, this potential strike on the East Coast and Gulf Coast is already having a major impact out here in the West, as we're seeing a spike in goods coming through West Coast ports, this as shippers prepare for this potential shutdown at Gulf Coast and East Coast ports.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Port of Long Beach\uc2 , back in August, they saw their busiest month in their 113-year history, volume jumping nearly 34 percent compared to August of 2023. The neighboring port of Los Angeles reported an August volume jump of 16 percent compared to their year prior, the busiest nonpandemic month ever at that port.\par \par \uc2 Increased cargo volume on the West Coast has led to congestion at Western ports. Truckers are reporting long wait times and pull chassis shortages. The potential labor action comes at a critical time of year for retailers right before the holidays.\par \par \uc2 Customers of \uc2 CMA CGM\uc2 , \uc2 Maersk\uc2  and other large container carriers have been rushing in stocks of Halloween and Christmas items. Manufacturers have also been loading up on solar panels and other goods targeted for potential tariff increases.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the clock is ticking. The \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2 , ILA, says that it's 45,000 dockworkers at three dozen U.S. ports up and down the East Coast and Gulf will stop work if they don't have a new contract by October 1.\par \par \uc2 The ports where these dockworkers are employed collectively handle about half of the country's seaborne imports. Today, the United States Maritime Alliance, USMX, which represents the employers, released a statement saying: "Despite additional attempts by USMX to engage with the ILA and resume bargaining, we have been unable to schedule a meeting to continue negotiations on a new master contract. We remain prepared to bargain at any time, but both sides must come to the table if we're going to reach a deal. And there is no indication that the ILA is interested in negotiating at this time."\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile, the ILA has responded. They said they have met with the employers, but simply they are stuck over compensation. If this strike does happen beginning on October 1, goods will still flow in through the West Coast, but they will likely be more expensive and will likely see delays because of all this -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Thank you very much, Max.\par \par \uc2 So how will this impact consumers already feeling the pinch? Let's get the read on all of it from economist and former director of the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  Doug Holtz-Eakin.\par \par \uc2 Doug, I'm so happy you're here.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Because I feel like people have seen this building. They're waiting for what happens next. I mean, where do you see this going and what really could be the impact that all of us feel at home?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, what you see first is exactly what you heard described, which is you try to reroute the shipments to the West Coast ports, so that you don't get the interruptions, but that is more expensive, longer ship than times to the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 You can't do that for everything. So that becomes an issue. In the past, where typically this was West Coast port issue, we have seen some short- term disruptions. Those are usually pretty survivable because people can use their inventories for a while and then replenish them. They can move their activities back in time and the economy doesn't get hurt too much.\par \par \uc2 But you can't do that forever. And, ultimately, these are very damaging key arteries and they're choosing to strike at exactly the time when the Christmas and other holiday shipments are going to take place. So they have timed this to be the most disruptive, and that's going to take its toll, billions of dollars a day typically in lost economic activity.\par \par \uc2 How do we go forward? Well, the last time, a president stepped in. It was 2002. Then-President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  invoked what's known as the Taft- Hartley Act, and can send the owners and the longshoremen back to the table for an 80-day cooling off period and force them to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 And so I think the bullseye is very quickly going to be on President Biden, the most pro-union president in history by his own description. And people are going to wonder, is he going to settle this thing prior to the holidays and during an election season, or is he going to let the union disrupt the U.S. economy at that time?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I mean, I think people are home are thinking, wait, hold on. They keep telling me inflation is going down, which it's not. It's slowed. But prices are still going up, but the prices have not come down.\par \par \uc2 So you see this building and you're thinking, gosh, I mean, how much more could that add to our gas prices, our grocery bills, our everyday costs? They just keep going up. It just sounds horrible to think that this could - - this could actually happen.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The final thought on the politics of the moment and the timing of this. You just touched on it a bit.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But, obviously, presidential election coming up, we see the date of the contract coming up. We know that there's going to be some politics at play. Real quick final thought on that.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Politics are -- this is higher prices. It would surprise me if the White House would tolerate that at this point in the election season.\par \par \uc2 I'd expect President Biden to step in, send them back to the table for the 80-day cooling off period and dodge this until after the election.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I knew I'd get a prediction from you.\par \par \uc2 Doug, always good to have you on the show. We will hope for the best.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OK, the Fed unleashing rate cuts, but will that unlock the American dream for priced-out homebuyers?\par \par \uc2 Realtor Katrina Campins is taking a special look at that for FOX Business and she will join us live next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The Federal Reserve cutting, some welcome relief to potential homebuyers who are currently priced out of the market. So will the ray cut get more Americans to sign on the dotted line?\par \par \uc2 Let's talk to Katrina Campins, FOX News real estate contributor and host of "Mansion Global" on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 We are so thankful that you are here.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, you will be able to catch her all week on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 So you guys are running this amazing special, "Open House: Unlocking the American Dream." It's a special series of reports helping you to tackle the housing market.\par \par \uc2 I feel like people are talking about this everywhere I go.\par \par \uc2 KATRINA CAMPINS, HOST, "MANSION GLOBAL": They are.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The housing market. When are things going to start moving? When are these people going to put their house on the market so people can get into their starter homes or whatever it may be? So what's the read? What's the scoop?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: And, Sandra, to your point, everybody's talking about it because it's such an important issue because still Americans note that buying a home is the largest aspect of achieving the American dream, regardless of age.\par \par \uc2 And it's such a hot topic within the election, more so than, in my opinion, it's ever been because affordability is an issue. But not only is affordability an issue. It's affecting a broader area of Americans, right?\par \par \uc2 So it's not only first-time homebuyers, but you're also -- affordability is affecting the middle class, because they're spending over 50 percent of their money on rent. So we're hearing more about it because housing continues to be a bigger issue and because we don't have enough supply in the market, plus interest rates, to your point.\par \par \uc2 We are -- we do have hope now because interest rates are lower, right? But it's not going to be dramatic. We're not going to have instant gratification. You and I were discussing. Like, everybody wants instant gratification.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: It's not going to happen that way. It's going to be slow.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I look at -- I just pulled these stats when we were talking the economy a moment ago, and I look at shelter costs, OK?\par \par \uc2 This is homes and rents up 23 percent over the past 3.5 years. I mean, if you were in a home to start 3.5 years ago, you're looking at the value of your home up significantly and that could be a wonderful thing. But if you are looking to buy right now, it is tough out there.\par \par \uc2 So we're starting to see interest rates come down. What else has to happen to have homes become more affordable for people?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: We need more supply, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: And we keep on hearing about that. But the reason that we need more supply is because we don't have existing homeowners placing their properties on the market because they're locked into 2 or 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The main driver in this market has been new construction, right? So builders are just trying to build as many homes as possible. But what's so interesting to me is about 30 percent of the cost of a new home is due to regulations, which is why it's so incredibly important to pay attention doing this regulation.\par \par \uc2 Even though, again, regulations are more on the state and local level, the president can provide incentives, tax incentives, and so forth to be able to help first-time homebuyers and can definitely help inflation, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Not to mention all the raw commodity prices, all the resources that go into a home. Tough to find skilled labor. There's so many reasons that homebuyers or homebuilders have not been incentivized to build those new homes.\par \par \uc2 So to your point, roll back some of those regulations, get them back out there. Are we hearing that?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Getting them back out there. And, also, one thing that's really important is to release some of this federal land, which I think both candidates have mentioned, and have low regulation in those areas to be able to provide more affordable housing, right?\par \par \uc2 So we need more housing in the market. And every area is different. It's so incredibly difficult to just give a blanket statement to say real estate is doing greater. It's not, because, in Florida, for instance, it's a completely different animal than it is in other parts of the nation.\par \par \uc2 And so it really depends on the market that you're in and that local economy.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Is the stigma with renting sort of going away?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Because I know for -- sometimes, it's like, well, you have got to go buy a house. Well, no, there's some people who actually choose that that's the right thing for them. Maybe they want the mobility, right? Maybe they don't want -- they don't want to have to get into a house, only to sell it a few years later.\par \par \uc2 For some people, that is truly a choice. What's sort of the psychology in the market right now?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Well, I think renting has become more acceptable for so many people, right, because of life issues. But then also, you may be waiting for interest rates to continue to go down, because if everybody continues to tell you, interest rates are going to go down, they're going to go down, you're going to wait, right? You're not going to buy something if you think the price of it is going to go down.\par \par \uc2 You're going to wait for that opportunity. And also, a lot of these new builders are building these amazing rental communities that have everything in it. You don't have to worry about anything and your landlord's responsible for everything at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 Because let's face it, you have to be ready for the costs involved with homeownership, insurance, property taxes and repairs. So, homeownership is definitely a huge aspect of the American dream, but you have to be responsible when it comes to making those decisions.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: They're to instill that, like, new, new, new, new, white, white, white. You want the white glove service, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you for the information. All good stuff. I learned something. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 So, Neil will be back tomorrow. And you can catch me alongside \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  on "America Reports" every weekday at 1:00 p.m. Eastern time. Thank you so much for joining us here. It was a jam-packed news hour. I learned a lot as well. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 For now, here is the team on "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240923ek9n0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With John Driskell Hopkins; \uc2 Apple\uc2  iPhone 16 Hits Shelves; Secret Service Updates Trump Assassination Attempt Investigation; Looming Port Strike?; Three Mile Island Nuclear Plant to Restart; Harris Campaign Focuses on Abortion Rights; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Targets Jewish Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 David Spunt, Jeff Flock, Susan Li, Lydia Hu, Jonathan Serrie,  Aishah Hasnie, Edward Lawrence \par \uc2 7,456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Forty-six days to go. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  making abortion the issue in Atlanta today before heading to Wisconsin. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , meanwhile, makes a play for the Jewish vote, but the election could hinge on an entirely different issue. More on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Edward Lawrence, in for Neil Cavuto. This is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX News team coverage with Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta with the Harris campaign and Aishah Hasnie in Washington the latest with the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 We begin with you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Vice President Harris wrapped up her remarks just moments ago here in the Atlanta suburb of Cobb County.\par \par \uc2 It's a once-conservative stronghold that voted blue in the last two presidential elections,obviously, the vice president hoping to repeat that at an event focused on abortion rights, highlighting the deaths of two Georgia women who, according to a \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  investigation, were denied or delayed access to potentially lifesaving medical procedures as a result of state abortion restrictions that Harris blames on her opponent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (D) AND U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, he hand-selected three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , the court of Thurgood and RBG, with the intention that they would overturn the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And, as he intended, they did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: This event in Georgia follows a forum last night in Michigan hosted by \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , in which the vice president heard directly from the family of Amber Nicole Thurman, a young Georgia woman who reportedly died after waiting 20 hours for doctors to perform a D&C to treat complications from abortion pills she had been prescribed out of state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want you all to know, Amber was not a statistic. She was loved by a family, a strong family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: And abortion opponents, though, insists that you should not blame state laws, but the abortion procedures and medications themselves.\par \par \uc2 Harris after wrapping up this event has returned to her plane and she is heading to Wisconsin for an evening rally in Madison -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 So now to Aishah Hasnie in D.C., where the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been courting the Jewish vote and taking aim at Iran -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, sort of going off the battleground path for a moment here to court the Jewish vote in America last night, and a lot of emotional moments in front of these voters, including this one right here where he vowed that he would bring the October 7 hostages home. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: That's a great group of people, and that's a lot of people too. We're going to get them out. We're going to get them out.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: Bring them home! Bring them home!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The former president also commenting for the first time about Iran stealing sensitive information from his campaign and offering it to the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Iran has spied on my campaign -- think of this -- and given the information to Kamala. What's that all about? That alone should get me elected. If Kamala is reelected, Iran will quickly obtain nuclear weapons. They're already about ready to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Also, last night, Trump promised to ban refugee resettlements in the Gaza Strip and cut any federal funding to universities that allow antisemitism to reign on their campuses.\par \par \uc2 At the end of this, though, Edward, Trump complained about the lack of Jewish support he's been getting and told the crowd that if they're voting for Harris, they should have their heads examined, adding that they would be to blame if he lost.\par \par \uc2 And that got quite the reaction from second gentleman Douglas Emhoff, who wrote on X: "Last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again fanned the flames of antisemitism by trafficking in tropes, blaming and scapegoating Jews."\par \par \uc2 The former president doesn't have any public events on his schedule for today. He's expected to get back on the trail, though, in North Carolina, a critical state for him. It's a big Saturday rally, as his surrogates, though, fan the country tonight holding a lot of town halls -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, those candidates all over the place.\par \par \uc2 Those are important issues, Aishah, but what if I told you these are the issues most voters are concerned with? Sixty-six percent say grocery prices are a major problem for their family in a new FOX News poll, up from 62 percent in May; 60 percent say housing costs are a major problem, up from 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 How this affects people currently in power, we need to help unraveling this.\par \par \uc2 So, with me now, GOP pollster \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , The Hill national reporter Julia Manchester, and Democratic strategist Doug Schoen.\par \par \uc2 So, Doug, I'm going to start with you on this, age before beauty, if you will.\par \par \uc2 What effect will this feeling reflected in the polls have on the election?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: It works to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's advantage. The Biden/Harris team has a low rating on the economy. The poll numbers you cite, Edward, suggest that people are very upset with high grocery and housing, and I can add gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 But Trump has to make the case that he has a better record from his presidency, but most importantly has an agenda of change to give people hope about the future. He's yet to do that, so for that to be fully realized as an electoral issue, the president, the former president, has to make the case that his economic agenda is superior to that of the vice president.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's an interesting point.\par \par \uc2 So, Lee, trying to break through on that, the two major issues favor former President Trump. I mean, 39 percent in the new polling we have from FOX News -- 39 percent of registered voters see higher prices as a motivating factor in voting; 32 percent say it's immigration, and the former president has a 10-point advantage in handling immigration and a five-point advantage in handling the economy.\par \par \uc2 So why is this race so closely, Lee?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Well, I think, frankly, it should be more than a five-point spread.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing right now is he's running really hard on immigration and less so on the economy, especially since \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has taken over. And I think he needs to make that reverse, because he should be ahead by 10, 15, even 20 points on the economy. He used to be.\par \par \uc2 He's actually lost ground on this. And I think it's not because she's running such a smart economic plan. I think it's because he's not talking enough about it. When he was back at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , he was talking about making America great again, making America wealthy again, making America safe again. And he was very, very focused on issues that matter most to the American people.\par \par \uc2 He's been a little bit on his heels. I think he needs to get more forward- looking again.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And it's interesting.\par \par \uc2 With the economy, with immigration, Julia, how does the Harris campaign then message all of this?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: Well, look, the Harris campaign has been pointing on the economy. For example, they point back to the Trump's handling of the coronavirus pandemic and the economy that was taking place during that time.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, that was abnormal. There's pushback on that. But that's what the Harris campaign has been saying. And they have been talking about the Biden administration's recovery efforts coming out of COVID and talking about, for example, unemployment continuing to drop.\par \par \uc2 On immigration, though, I think that's a bit of an easier issue for them right now. And it's a bit ironic because we know that, a few weeks ago, the Harris campaign was more on the defensive about immigration. We know that, under the Biden administration, we have seen that has been a tough issue for Harris, the situation at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 But -- and I think this ties into what Lee was just saying earlier -- with former President Trump's comments at the debate talking about the unfounded claims about Haitian immigrants in Springfield, Ohio, a lot of Republicans have told me that served as a distraction.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Instead of talking about those claims, why don't you talk about, for example, what would you do about the immigration system and how it's impacted communities like Springfield, Ohio?\par \par \uc2 So I think there's a frustration with the messaging there. And it's been easy for the Harris campaign to sort of go on the offensive on that issue.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, interesting.\par \par \uc2 So, overall prices -- bringing it back around to the economy, overall prices under Biden are up about 20 percent. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  asked about that at an event that was hosted by \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 . Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We really would love to know what your plan is to help lower the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am taking on, for example, price gouging, which is when most companies and corporations are good, but for those bad ones, they take advantage of people, especially during a pandemic or extreme weather, and they end up jacking up prices.\par \par \uc2 And there needs to be accountability and consequence for that. So I'm going to be taking on price gouging.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So none of the price gouging investigations, though, have amounted really to anything.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you look at the \uc2 FTC\uc2  were looking at oil companies about two years ago when gas prices were close to $5 a gallon and nothing came of that. So, Lee, what about help right now? What are you hearing about that?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, what's really interesting is when I have been talking to voters, they have not been satisfied with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' answers on the economy.\par \par \uc2 They say they do not understand what it is that she's really going to do that are going to bring the cost of living down. They understand a lot of her plans to spend a lot of money, but they're still saying they don't think that she's going to address the root causes or even gets it. And I think her first response here to go after price gouging is just a real miss.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of people are still waiting to hear what it is, because the economy right now -- they will say the economy right now is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' economy. They want to know what is she going to do different, and she has not outlined it. I don't think that this price gouging thing, as much as it sounds good, really addresses that root concern that people have.\par \par \uc2 And many people are really afraid of price gouging being really about price controls and other things as well.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So the other big story this week, the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  and its lack of endorsement for either candidate, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president Sean O'Brien telling Neil that both parties should not take the union vote for granted. And yesterday, on the show, Sandra Smith spoke with union members, one backing Trump, the other backing Harris.\par \par \uc2 Here's what they both had to say. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: That poll making waves. Did it surprise you at all or is that what you hear as a member, overwhelming support for former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 J.W. PAINE, TEAMSTERS MEMBER: Actually, my -- I was surprised it was that low. I think it was really around 68 percent. But it really surprises me that we're not supporting the one that actually overwhelmingly won this race. And that's sad.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CURBEAM, CHAIR, TEAMSTERS NATIONAL BLACK CAUCUS: And we got to look at what the vice president has done for the labor movement. She has stood on picket lines with union members. She walked the picket line with union members. She has supported our pension plan, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Doug, how do you think this union vote is going to play out, especially in some of those key battleground states?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I think there's a real question, as Lee was suggesting, whether the populism of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will have any resonance. The polling with \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  certainly suggests that it isn't reaching a majority there.\par \par \uc2 And the question is whether Trump can take his advocacy of no sales -- no tax on tips and a limitation on interest on credit cards and turn it into an economic plan for working people. They have supported him in the past. The non-college-educated male vote has been the mainstay of his support.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: The real question is can he maximize that vote with an economic plan?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I wanted to get one quick one in here for Julia. In 20 seconds that we have left here, President Biden has changed it up to have his first Cabinet meeting in about 11 months today. And the vice president was not there in person. She was on the phone.\par \par \uc2 She's part of this administration, and the look in that Cabinet meeting was that maybe he wasn't quite in charge of the administration. Does that rub off on the vice president when you have a look like that, Julia?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: You know, I think you're going to certainly see Republicans try to point that out, but Democrats and Harris' supporters would say, look, she is running for president. She is running to extend the Biden/Harris agenda into another administration.\par \par \uc2 So I think we might see those attacks, but, ultimately, I don't think it's anything new necessarily from what we have seen before in terms of those attacks on Biden and Harris and that dynamic.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Julia, Lee, Doug, appreciate it. Thanks for the thoughtful insights.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, the Fed cuts and investors buy, the Dow closing the week at record highs.\par \par \uc2 We will go down to the floor of the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  and see if this week's rally has some legs.\par \par \uc2 Plus, does this sound familiar?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There was an accident today at a nuclear power plant near Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. It's a bad one, one of the worst.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Forty-five years ago, a partial meltdown of the Three Mile Island nuclear plant gripping the nation. That was then, the plant reopening now, but not for the reasons you might think.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Record high for the Dow and a big week for your money, the boost sparked by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cutting interest rates for the first time in four years.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's talk this out with New York Stock Exchange trader and Wall Street legend Peter Tuchman.\par \par \uc2 Peter, great to talk to you today.\par \par \uc2 So the markets wanted it and they got a half-a-percent cut. We saw a huge rally yesterday, mixed things today. Where does the market open on Monday? The sugar high still going?\par \par \uc2 PETER TUCHMAN, NYSE TRADER: Well, you know what, look, I mean, it's hard for me to really predict that.\par \par \uc2 I think everybody was caught by surprise by the 50-basis-point cut, but the market tells us what it thinks of something that's so radical and sort of off the beaten path. And I think the market is really thrilled with it.\par \par \uc2 As you know, everybody benefits from cutting this interest rate at this point. And I think possibly the 50-basis-point was part of the July story and part of the August story.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: In my opinion, if you sort of overanalyze it a little bit, it really felt that maybe the Fed missed an opportunity in July. And that's why we had sort of those two weeks in the beginning of July.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: The August 5 massive sell-off may have been a missed opportunity on their part. This was well played.\par \par \uc2 I think the market responded really nice to it. You have to realize that today was actually a very constructive day. We had the S&P 500 rebalance. We had the largest option expiration date in history; $5.2 trillion went in and out of the market. For me, I trade the market on closing balances.\par \par \uc2 We had $8 billion for sale at one point and we flipped $8 billion to buy. So we had an absolute, an incredible amount of buy interest in this market.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: So I think that continues in a big way going forward surely into the next -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. No, no. So I do want to get this in. I mean, so the Fed made the deeper cut.\par \par \uc2 Will the markets, do you think, try and push for another half-percent at the next meeting?\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: I think that we got the impression from \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  when he spoke the other day on Wednesday that we're going to probably get two 25-basis- point cuts, one in November and maybe one in December, to close out the year with 1 total basis point move.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what the market needs. We have been anticipating -- you have to realize the market, the market can handle virtually anything except expectations that are not fulfilled. They have been dangling this in front of us since January. January, we were looking at seven basis points -- seven cuts during the year and then maybe none.\par \par \uc2 And so this one was -- that's why the reception by the market on Thursday was so positive. And I think with two, maybe two more 25-basis-point cuts through the end of the year are going to be super positively received.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: You have so much institutional knowledge there, such a legend. Can you explain what the cheaper money does for the markets, for those who may not know?\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: You know what, I don't know if that's the answer to this question.\par \par \uc2 I think that there's so many new players in the market right now who are actually trading the market. You have got 50 million-plus new retail traders that have come in post-COVID, right? You have got huge institutional money, huge hedge fund money. A lot of people are playing ETFs and are playing in the option market.\par \par \uc2 So there's this enthusiasm around the market. There are still people sitting around with billions and billions of dollars in cash who have not been convinced that this market was not ready for a massive sell-off or a recession or a depression, all the things that the media has sort of been throwing at us over the last number of months.\par \par \uc2 So I think that finally when they get some clearance and the confidence is solid, I think it's going to unleash what we saw on way on Thursday, which was just a solid bull market. And I think there's a lot of money still to be spent in the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's good, and good information. Thank you, Peter, right there in the trenches of all of it. Thank you for all of the insight on this. Just amazing. Great to talk to you again. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So a nuclear -- yes.\par \par \uc2 A nuclear accident made Three Mile Island infamous. Now the tech industry might need the power plant more than ever. Find out why.\par \par \uc2 And could a possible port strike have holiday shoppers striking out?\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu is in Bayonne, New Jersey with the bumpy details -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hey there, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Billions of dollars worth of goods are traded inside those containers on board those vessels every single day. What exactly is inside those containers? And what does a strike mean for those goods? We're going to get into all of that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Secret Service facing continued scrutiny after those assassination attempts on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , its acting director taking questions from the media today. Did we get any answers?\par \par \uc2 We're going to be back in about 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: A strike at major ports along the Gulf and East Coast could begin in less than two weeks, possibly costing the economy billions each day and putting a major freeze on the holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 To Lydia Hu live in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 So, Lydia, are we really concerned already about the holiday shopping season with this?\par \par \uc2 HU: Yes, we sure are.\par \par \uc2 Retailers are certainly raising concerns about the holidays shopping season, but we're also keeping an eye out for concerns about other types of products and industries. Experts say, if there were to be a strike, the public at large would first notice shortages and delays on things like autos and agricultural products like food.\par \par \uc2 And we can take a look at what imports are most brought into the ports that will be impacted by a strike if it happens on October 1 to see what other product categories could be impacted. In New York and New Jersey, we're seeing cars and semiconductors and furniture as top imports here.\par \par \uc2 Down in Savannah, the top imports include bulldozers, batteries and car parts. And, in Houston, TV equipment, air conditioners and commodities like iron and steel, all of these products could get snarled in a strike if that were to come on October 1.\par \par \uc2 Some industries, as I mentioned, are sounding the alarm. The Auto Car Association warns a strike will create a shortage of car parts. Think delays on your auto repairs. The \uc2 American Chemistry Council\uc2  concerned about chemicals needed for health care and household products and also semiconductor chips.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2  is warning that a strike will push prices up for restaurant operators on top of what we know are already high food prices and labor costs. And you asked about the holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 I had a chance to speak with the CEO of Balsam Hill. That's an artificial tree company. He says he's worried about having all the inventory he needs for this holiday season and he's redirecting some of his container ships and his orders from the East Coast over to the West Coast. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAC HARMAN, CEO, BALSAM HILL: It's something that keeps our supply chain team and me up at night. And I'm worried in particular that the government's not necessarily going to intervene because it's in the middle of an election year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: As of right now, there are no further negotiations scheduled between the employers and the port workers. The deadline for this contract negotiation, it expires September 30. So we have got our eye on that October 1 for a possible strike -- Edward, back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Hey, Lydia. Tick, tick, tick.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is Steve Moore, economist.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Steve, so what effect, Steve, could the strike have on prices overall?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, God forbid that we don't have Christmas trees for Christmas. I mean, that would be like the Grinch.\par \par \uc2 Look, this would be very negative for the economy, no question about it. So much of the merchandise that we buy goes through those ports, Ed, as you know. And so this could mean not just shortages, but also, I hate to say it, higher prices of these services and goods if they're not available.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So this is a tough spot for President Biden and Vice President Harris. They're the union administration, as they said.\par \par \uc2 So they have to be in support of the union and support of the strike should they go on strike. Could this be the October surprise because of the effect on the economy?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, look, I don't think the Biden administration wants a strike right now. You're quite right. It would just be a black eye for the administration.\par \par \uc2 But I also don't think it's appropriate, frankly, for an administration, whether it's Republican or a Democratic presidency, to weigh in and take a side one way or the other in these disputes. I mean, let them work it out, let the management and the labor unions work it out. And, usually, that's the best outcome.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, I want to shift on prices. We heard the Federal Reserve chairman come the closest I have ever heard him say mission accomplished when it comes to inflation.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But one of the Federal Reserve members, Board of Governor \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: ... said that she preferred the quarter-of-a-percent cut to the half-percent cut, mainly because of core inflation, without food and energy prices, that sticky inflation. And she's worried about reigniting it.\par \par \uc2 Are we flirting with reigniting it?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Maybe, maybe. And I think that was a mistake. I think that Fed got some egg in their face yesterday, 45 or so days before the most important election in our lifetime to press its thumb on the scale.\par \par \uc2 I could have lived with a 25-basis-point, but as The Wall Street Journal called this, a jumbo rate cut, and it certainly was. I think it caught a lot of people by surprise. And I think the question a lot of Americans would ask is, look, it's an important election. You know, their next meeting, by the way, is the day after the election.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So, some people are just saying, why couldn't they have waited?\par \par \uc2 And the Fed, as you know, is always saying, oh, we have to maintain our politically independent -- political independence. I don't think they look so politically independent here.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, the image issue.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to switch gears on this. The \uc2 Tax Foundation\uc2  found that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' plan could cost the economy 800,000 jobs, mainly because of the pullback that companies would have to pay the taxes, the increased taxes that she wants to give. What's your take on that?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, it's a catastrophic tax increase.\par \par \uc2 And the Trump campaign -- and I work with the Trump people, just for full disclosure. We have got to really expose to the American people how dangerous this tax increase would be. I mean, you're talking about doubling the capital gains tax. You're talking about giving the United States the highest business tax rate in the world, taxing unrealized capital gains.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I think that estimate -- I know those folks at \uc2 Tax Foundation\uc2  - - I think it's a conservative estimate. You have -- some people on Wall Street are saying, if you put that tax increase in place, it could cause a 30 to 35 percent decline in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So I would just quickly, in the last 20 seconds that we have getting into this...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to say, the former -- if former President Trump wins, and you see the 20 percent increase, we have seen 20 percent increases in prices under the Harris/Biden administration, can former President Trump actually lower prices?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, we had no inflation when Trump was president. His average inflation rate was 1.9 percent, 6.5 percent under Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . So just look at their records, folks.\par \par \uc2 You have a guy who's been in the White House. We had a booming economy. Let's get that back, and, by the way, no inflation, 1.5 percent inflation when he left office. You remember that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. No, he's got a record that he's running on.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Steve Moore, economist. I appreciate it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So now to Pennsylvania, and Three Mile Island, the site of the country's worst nuclear accident, set to reopen in a deal to power tech giant, Microsoft A.I. and their needs there.\par \par \uc2 Digging -- Jeff Flock digging deeper into this developing story -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Before there was Chernobyl, before there was Fukushima, there was this, Three Mile Island.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps on the left side of your screen, you see the cooling towers from reactor two. That's the one that melted down in March of 1979, spewing radioactivity into the environment around us, causing the evacuations of thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the screen would be reactor one. That wasn't affected, was restarted, but then shut down again in 2019 because it wasn't making any money. But now with money from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , which has a voracious appetite for power because of artificial intelligence, they have agreed to buy the power from that reactor if it's restarted for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 And so it will be. By 2028, it should be up and running again,nuclear power again coming from Three Mile Island, nuclear power advocates saying nuclear is a lot safer now than it was in 1979.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIANN EDWARDS, THE NUCLEAR COMPANY: I mean, just like our phones, right, there's an evolution, a generational upgrade with every technology, and so the restart of Three Mile Island is a huge market signal, because we have reactors that are currently shut down that can be restarted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Among them the Palisades power plant in Covert, Michigan, which just got a $1.5 billion federal loan to restart, becoming the first U.S. nuclear power plant to be restarted after being shut down.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , they say they are seeking "to enable a decarbonized grid for our company, our customers and the world. This will bring a significant supply of net new reliable carbon free electricity to the power grid, recognizing the importance of nuclear energy."\par \par \uc2 It also answers the question from some critics about artificial intelligence and that voracious need for power, taking power away from the grid and perhaps driving demand up and costs for local consumers. This is a new addition to the grid. And \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Constellation Energy\uc2  that operates the plant say that's the future.\par \par \uc2 At Three Mile Island, I'm Jeff Flock with the \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, Jeff, feed the A.I. beast.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Apple\uc2  releasing its highly anticipated iPhone 16 today, but with an artificial intelligence holdup, how that affects sales coming up.\par \par \uc2 And the Secret Service still facing lots of questions after two assassination attempts on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the answers we got today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING U.S. SECRET SERVICE DIRECTOR: And as a result of these failures, what has become clear to me is we need a shift in paradigm in how we conduct our protective operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, acting Secret Service Director Ronald Rowe Jr. taking questions today on the security failures that led to the July 13 assassination attempt on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOX News' David Spunt is live at the Justice Department with this investigation -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 More than two months after that tragedy in Butler, Pennsylvania, on July 13, and acting Secret Service Director \uc2 Ron Rowe\uc2  today said at a news conference that the Secret Service is now in what they call the penalty phase, but basically says that Secret Service employees will face consequences. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: These employees will be held accountable and this agency has among the most robust table of penalties in the entirety of the federal government. And these penalties will be administered according to our disciplinary process. Due to federal regulations, I cannot get into personnel matters as they are proceeding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Edward, last month five Secret Service employees were placed on administrative desk duty following that shooting in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 The Service has been working on an internal report for two months. It's almost complete, finishing touches, but, today, a summary was released. Some of the highlights include -- quote -- "A constant theme gathered from state and local law enforcement personnel who helped secure the Butler rally was the presence of communications deficiencies."\par \par \uc2 The building where Thomas Crooks, the 20-year-old shooter, took his shots, not in the secure perimeter. It was also not on the minds, apparently, of certain Secret Service personnel that day.\par \par \uc2 The report's summary continues: "There was a lack of detailed knowledge by Service personnel regarding the state or local law enforcement presence that would be present in and around the AGR complex." That's the building where Crooks was. "The different radio frequencies used at the Butler farm show venue were not conducive for quickly sharing real-time information."\par \par \uc2 Roe's said before important information was shared that day on text chains and text messages, instead of old-fashioned radio frequencies that they have used in the past. Meanwhile, on Capitol Hill today, the House passed unanimously 400 people -- 405 people in the chamber voted to zero to make sure that candidates, presidential candidates, get the exact same protection as sitting presidents.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service director told me that they have been getting the same protection as President Biden since July 13. But Congress wants to make sure that this is enshrined as a law so it continues in the years and decades to come, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, David Spunt, thank you live at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 So, let's turn to the expert on this, the retired Secret Service Agent in Charge Frank Loveridge.\par \par \uc2 Now, Frank, first of all, thank you for your Service in supporting this country.\par \par \uc2 What did you think about the answers we got today?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LOVERIDGE, FORMER SECRET SERVICE AGENT: Well, Edward, thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 One of my concerns was, we never addressed the whistle-blower allegations that were made to Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 's office raising concerns. You want to dispute those, you want to refute them if you can, because, if not, they become reality.\par \par \uc2 The one question that was asked of him was, did you deny any assets to the former President Trump's advance detail? And the way that question was phrased, according to whistle-blowers, it should have been phrased, were you aware of the war room or the manpower room telling any former President Trump advance ages not to ask for additional assets?\par \par \uc2 That should have been the question that was asked, and we needed an answer to that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, no, I want to -- and I want to get this in there. That is a good point. What about the talk of complacency within the Secret Service that could have contributed to the failures in this assassination attempt? What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: Well, obviously, the protective plan was in writing on the Special Operations Division tactical survey, on the post-assignment log. We know where everyone was supposed to be.\par \par \uc2 The question is, why didn't someone go around on the perimeter, as a side agent should, and make sure that all of our assets were located where they were supposed to be? I think Acting Director \uc2 Ron Rowe\uc2  acknowledged that failure, and that was a huge failure.\par \par \uc2 That was complacency.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Frank Loveridge, I appreciate it. Again, thank you for your Service on this and insight related to this. This is something we're going to keep on, because now we have had a situation, two assassination attempts. It's just mind-boggling that we are talking about multiple attempts. Appreciate it. Thank you for your service there.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Apple\uc2 's iPhone 16 hitting shelves today, but it's not as originally billed.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Susan Li, what are you seeing there?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: If you build it, they will come. \uc2 Apple\uc2 's hoping that its next-generation A.I.-powered iPhone 16 will be a hit. But will it draw in the crowds for the world's biggest company?\par \par \uc2 I'm Susan Li outside \uc2 Apple\uc2 's flagship store in New York. I will get you more right after this short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: The new \uc2 Apple\uc2  iPhone 16 hitting store shelves today. A slow A.I. rollout could be impacting shopper turnout.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Susan Li is live from the \uc2 Apple\uc2 's flagship store on New York's Fifth Avenue.\par \par \uc2 Now, Susan, is this a big miss for \uc2 Apple\uc2 , I mean, billing A.I. throughout this iPhone and then not delivering that today?\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, we still have long lineups, because those die-hard fans lined up bright and early.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, those next-generation A.I.-powered iPhone 16s did go on sale today, except, of course, without the actual A.I. software. And that's delayed until next month. But we had \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  on hand to open the doors and greet the customers at the iconic cube here on Fifth Avenue, eager fans that didn't seem to mind the wait until next month for those A.I. features.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, every year, I'm changing the phone. Before, it was like I was waiting one month, two months. But, today, I don't know. I just came here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm interested on the new \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch and also the new AirPods.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Apple Intelligence. I'm just waiting on it to come out and I'm going to use it a bunch. I love the use of A.I., so this is going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes, so I should show you that the lineups here are dwindling this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And last year, we had lineups all the way down the block, but two reasons for this could be possibly that A.I. software delay and also in 2024 most people do get their iPhones now delivered straight to their door.\par \par \uc2 Now, Wall Street analysts, some say that the A.I. software delay means that the iPhone 16 isn't selling as well as the 15. But then some other Wall Street predictors say that this actually lengthens out the sales cycle for the iPhone 16 this year.\par \par \uc2 Now, one selling point remains, and that's those generous telco subsidies, trade-ins for free iPhone, also up to $1,000 back for the Pro models if you sign those longer dated contracts. Now, \uc2 Apple\uc2  kept the prices the same this year and that's to entice the upgraders. So the base model still starts at $799.\par \par \uc2 Remember, back in 2007, the first ever iPhone cost you $489. So if you inflation-adjust that, that might seem pricey in today's cost. But I will tell you this, that \uc2 Apple\uc2  has sold 2.5 billion devices, created $3.5 trillion dollars in value. So, yes, I think they have sold pretty well over those years -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, people might also be going to dinner.\par \par \uc2 All right, Susan Li, thank you right there in New York.\par \par \uc2 So, you might know him from the Zac Brown Band, but John Driskell Hopkins wants to raise awareness for his work on a different project. The Grammy Award-winning musician is here and he's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Zac Brown Band founding member John Driskell Hopkins was diagnosed with \uc2 ALS\uc2  in 2021 after dealing with symptoms on stage.\par \par \uc2 Through his battle with the neurological disease, the Grammy Award-winning musician continues to perform. He's taking part in a benefit concert tonight to raise awareness and funding for ALS research.\par \par \uc2 John Driskell Hopkins joins me right now.\par \par \uc2 So what do you want to know -- what do you want people to know about this fight, John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS, MUSICIAN: I want people to know that \uc2 ALS\uc2  is not incurable. \uc2 ALS\uc2  is massively underfunded.\par \par \uc2 And we are working so hard to get the proper research and the proper funding into the hands that can do the most with it. And we have done amazing things so far, but we have so far to go.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So I have to ask -- I have to ask, how hard is it to perform at the level you do when you're dealing with issues from this disease?\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: I'm very fortunate in that my progression is slow.\par \par \uc2 ALS\uc2  is typically a three-to-five-year life expectancy from diagnosis. And in late December this year, I will be at three years. So, some of us last longer than others. And my hands still work, but they're slower. And you can hear my speech is labored.\par \par \uc2 But I'm still singing it, so I'm still bringing it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And do the -- what's the reaction for the other band members? Talk about the support that you're getting a little bit.\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Incredible support from the Zac Brown Band. Every day, these guys have my back, and gal. And I couldn't ask for a more supportive group of friends that are absolutely extended family to me.\par \par \uc2 And they're there to pick me up when I fall, and they're there to hold my arm when I stumble. And as long as I'm able to perform with excellence, I will be out on the road with Zac Brown Band.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: How can people help the cause that you're raising money for today?\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Well, you can quickly go to hoponacure.org. That's the 501(c)(3) that my wife and I funded in May of '22.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of this year, we will have supported over $3 million dollars in research and funded from Hop On A Cure. There are a million places to go if your heart is more into the caregiving. There are certainly great organizations to support there.\par \par \uc2 But we believe that we can knock this out and that we are going to do that through more research.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, John, in the last 30 seconds that we have, tell me, where are scientists today? Are they making progress on it?\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: We have made a lot of progress. And we're looking at things like head trauma, mitochondria, microbiome. This is all motor neuron disease.\par \par \uc2 And we have to figure out what happens to the motor neurons, and then we will have a lot more vision to defeat ALS, Alzheimer's, Parkinson's. It's all one big motor neuron dilemma.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Exactly. And, John, they need money for that. And the benefit concert that you're doing tonight is going to help. And folks who are watching this can also help.\par \par \uc2 John Driskell Hopkins, thank you for your time today and the battle that you're going through. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, be sure to tune in tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to tune in tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern for "Cavuto Live" right here on FOX News.\par \par \uc2 For now, you know what's coming up? We all do. It's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240920ek9k0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Michael McCaul (R-TX); Interview With Rep. Anthony D'Esposito (R-NY); \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Campaigns in New York; Federal Reserve Cuts Interest Rates By 50 Basis Points; Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O'Brien\b0\par\par\uc2 Kelly O'Grady, Bryan Llenas, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Today is the day. I am not talking about the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . I am talking about the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  now saying it will not -- repeat -- not make an endorsement in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Exactly why? We're going to ask \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president Sean O'Brien in an exclusive chat moments away.\par \par \uc2 First, the Fed cutting interest rates by half-a-point, the first rate cut we have seen now in more than four years, stocks bouncing around on the news, originally up almost 400 points, ending the day down about 100 points.\par \par \uc2 Now, for consumers, it means lower borrowing costs for everything from home loans to credit cards. It could also have an impact on the upcoming presidential race. And we are on top of it, on top of all of it.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 First to the Fed and that surprising cut, not that it was a cut, but that it was a big one, a half-a-point.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady has more -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, that's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 If I were a betting person I would have lost money today. I had 25. But, look, the temperature coming out of today is that the Fed feels confident enough about its 2 percent inflation target, but clearly wants to ensure the labor market doesn't cool off anymore.\par \par \uc2 Now, 50 basis points, it's a big deal, especially because Fed chair Powell has been so measured in his messaging leading up to this. Now, a lot of investors, they were happy. They wanted the Fed to get aggressive out of the gate.\par \par \uc2 But I will highlight we have only gotten a 50-basis-point cut recently in dire times. So I'm talking 9/11. I'm talking COVID. And so there's another camp that worries a larger cut could indicate the soft landing is at risk and we are in more economic trouble than we originally thought.\par \par \uc2 Now, during Powell's presser, he maintained he doesn't see signs of a recession or slowdown. He indicated even that we could get a couple more cuts by the end of the year, but Powell has emphasized they will take it meeting by meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , FEDERAL RESERVE CHAIRMAN: Meeting by meeting. As I mentioned, we -- there's no sense that the committee feels it's in a rush to do this. We made a good, strong start to this, and that's really, frankly, a sign of our confidence, confidence that inflation is coming down toward 2 percent on a sustainable basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Now, investors always listen to the language used in those pressers. Will we have an aggressive or a cautious cycle?\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, markets seemed really confused. We ended down over 100 points, but we were all over the place during the day. President Biden, though, he took a victory lap.\par \par \uc2 He said this: "We just reached an important moment. Inflation and interest rates are falling while the economy remains strong. The critics said it couldn't happen, but our policies are lowering costs and creating jobs."\par \par \uc2 One word of caution here, though, you cut too quickly, inflation could spike back up to the levels that we have seen in recent years. And, Neil, of course, just because the rate of inflation is down doesn't mean costs are actually down. So this is going to continue to be a really delicate balance with inflation and the labor market, all with an election less than two months away, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that's the weird part of it, right, Kelly? And, again, one of the arguments for only a quarter-point cut, to be gradualistic about it and not look like you're doing anyone's bidding.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure this was an apolitical move, but what are you hearing on that front?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: So, actually, Fed Chair Powell, he did get asked specifically about this, whether there was any political pressure.\par \par \uc2 And he said, we look at the economy. The economy, the data that's coming in is telling me that this is the right decision. So they're saying no, but, of course, there is a lot of speculation, you saw President Biden come out, take that victory lap, that there could have been some pressure. He certainly wants to keep his job if V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does get in come November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Kelly, great job, as always, here. We will try to sort it out.\par \par \uc2 Danielle DiMartino Booth joins us right now, the former Dallas Federal Reserve adviser.\par \par \uc2 Danielle, what did you make of the half-point size, the size of this cut, and now?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DIMARTINO BOOTH, FORMER FEDERAL RESERVE ADVISER: Well, Neil, I was actually pretty pleased with it.\par \par \uc2 It's been since Paul Volcker himself was in office that any Fed chair has defied the market, that he defied Wall Street today. Out of the 114 economists and analysts queried going into this meeting, only nine were calling for a larger 50-basis-point, half-a-percentage point rate cut.\par \par \uc2 And like Paul Volcker, big feet to step into, but definitely Powell's hero, he defied Wall Street and did what he thought was best, based on what's happening in the economy. Several meetings ago, he was asked whether September was too close to the election and he said, I am not being political. Every single meeting is and will be live.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Danielle, we have been on a trend for lower rates that the market, maybe in anticipation of this or not exactly this move, market rates have been sliding. Mortgage rates, for example, are in an out of two- year lows right now.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if it's had any corresponding impact on the housing arena. Certainly, housing is more expensive than ever. But what do you see happening on that front?\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: Well, Neil, we got fresh data out this morning, and we have seen purchase applications rise about 17 percent off their lows.\par \par \uc2 But they really have been trading in a very tight range, and we have not seen as much of a reaction as you would think. If you would say, hey, Danielle, mortgage rates are two-year low, I would say, wow, we should be seeing some serious activity.\par \par \uc2 Where we're seeing most activity right now, Neil, is in people refinancing and taking cash out of their homes to make budgets -- to make their budgets meet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you're so good at this. You follow it from your days at the Fed, but a lot of people, when they see a cut of this magnitude, they start worrying, what does the Fed see that I don't?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you get the typical kind of numbers that are out, some not great, others OK, but certainly not in the midst of what appears to be a big slowdown or a recession. But, again, it could be in the eye of the beholder. I grant you that.\par \par \uc2 But how do you think that sort of resonates with people? Because that seemed to be the seesaw the markets are on.\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: And, boy, what a seesaw it's been, I mean, red, green, red, green all afternoon long since this happened.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: I mean, 2001, 2007, these are the other precedents when the Fed did start with a larger rate cut than what would have been considered to be normal.\par \par \uc2 And these were indeed precursors to a time when the U.S. economy fell into recession. Now, \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials fully anticipate that the unemployment rate is going to rise from its current 4.2 percent to 4.4 percent by the end of the year. That would be one full percentage point off of its lows.\par \par \uc2 Historically speaking, that tends to mean that the United States is in recession and that the unemployment rate is going to continue marching higher from here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Danielle, real quickly on that. When I look at these predictions of where rates go, the consensus seems to be around 3.25, 3.5 percent for this overnight bank lender rate called federal funds a year from now, in other words, about two points lower than where we are now.\par \par \uc2 Does that jibe with you?\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: Neil, I think we're going to see actually more easing than that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DIMARTINO BOOTH: The markets got priced in three rate cuts through the end of the year as of the close of this meeting. So I think we're going to see a Fed being pushed to continue to be aggressive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Danielle, thank you. Great job, as always.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Tim Anderson right now, New York Stock Exchange trader, joining us from the floor of the New York Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Tim, were you surprised by this?\par \par \uc2 TIM ANDERSON, MANAGING DIRECTOR, TJM INVESTMENTS: You know, I was looking for 25-basis-point cut, and I think that it was pretty much a jump ball in terms of what people were expecting.\par \par \uc2 And so I'm a little bit surprised by it, but I'm not totally 100 percent against it. I think that Powell pretty much said at one point in response to a question, look, if we had gotten the July unemployment numbers before the last data, we might have gone in July.\par \par \uc2 That's as close to the Fed as the Fed is ever going to get to saying, yes, we wish we would have made a cut the last time. So I think this is a little bit to make up for that. And I think that markets were pretty muted after that.\par \par \uc2 Sure, we had a 100-point move up and down, but I, mean, the Dow's above 40000.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: So, these 100-, 150-point, 200-point moves back and forth really are just at the margin. And, very significantly, I will say the old closing high from July 15 is -- on the S&P 500 is 5667.\par \par \uc2 We got above that for a very brief period of time. We closed about 50 points below it. Now, Neil, you and I have both been doing this for a long time. There's plenty of times when in between 2:00 and 4:00 on the day of the Fed announced that the market has a strong move one direction, and the next day no-follow through at all...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: ... and it completely goes the other way. That's not what we had today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: We have got like a mixed decision. The jury's still out. We want to go home and think about it. We want to see what -- how it kind of settles in overnight.\par \par \uc2 And then we will take another look tomorrow. But there are a lot of -- I will just say that there are a lot of crosscurrents the remainder of the week, because we have got a quadruple expiration Friday, combined with an S&P rebalancing. So we're going to have a fair amount of individual stock volatility and very high volume, particularly on Friday of this week.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you are an encyclopedia. But just to bring a lot of folks up to speed, you're talking about a lot of technical indicators that could happen, stock -- and stock futures and contracts, options, contracts that expire all at the same time.\par \par \uc2 That can usually lead to some spooky stuff here ahead of October, but September has been a crazy month for the markets. They seem to think that the soft landing, though, is achieved and that we're not getting anything more worrisome out of companies when they make their quarterly earnings announcements and give us guidance.\par \par \uc2 Some, like \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , Lowe's, maybe \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  will say, yes, the consumer is retrenching a little bit. But you are getting a sense from them that, if it was panic city, they're not panicking. Are you?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: No, they're not panicking by any stretch the imagination and we had a lot of very cautious comments on the consumer from a week-and-a-half ago, some comments from \uc2 Ally Financial\uc2  that they were worried, really worried about some consumer auto loan default rates or people being behind.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: But we did not get that reinforced by other people in the same business making the same thing. So three or four days later, a lot of comments were, this might be very specific just to \uc2 Ally\uc2  and not really a in industry-wide or a consumer-wide thing.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, retail sales have slowed a little bit, although, this week, the numbers were certainly in line, maybe just a touch better. We have to see still what happen -- we're two weeks from the end of the quarter. It's typically a volatile time of the year.\par \par \uc2 I don't doubt that we might have some more volatility. But I think we need more conviction to get through those old highs on the S&P and go to a new level. That might be really hinging on Q3 earnings reports a month from now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Well said, my friend.\par \par \uc2 And you're right. You might say that we have been around long enough to cover these gyrations, but you're the only one who's not aging. I seem to be aging a lot faster. So we will see what happens there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, my friend. Great having you.\par \par \uc2 All right, by the way, we should posit it here that there is this sentiment that's building here that we're going to see one interest rate cut after another at virtually every Fed meeting going. I don't know if anyone is in that -- they have this thing called a dot plot, where they sort of break apart that from every Fed voting member what he or she thinks will happen.\par \par \uc2 And from that, we glean this idea that a year from now we're going to be two full percentage points lower. Now, you hear these rates on short-term rates. This affects the federal funds rate. It's an overnight bank lending rate, but it's the one puppy that the Federal Reserve controls. And it has knocked that down for the first time in four years.\par \par \uc2 Now, I should also pause it here that market rates, things like the 10-year note or bond or whatever you want to call it, that is something the markets trade on and base for themselves oftentimes because of what they see the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  doing or not doing. And their expectations is that the economy will be steady as she goes and interest rates will remain fairly low.\par \par \uc2 Now, they slightly backed up today and that 10-year, which is pegged to a lot of mortgages and refinancing and home loans and the like, to say nothing of auto loans, that has come down from a high of little more than 5 percent a little more than two months ago. So you can see that the markets were kind of leading the Fed's way.\par \par \uc2 Where this goes is anyone's guess, but we're following that. No doubt, of course, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is following this. Wonder what he thinks of the half-point size of that cut here. Some have worried that that might be a political move on the part of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Maybe he will talk about that tonight when he is in New York, and Long Island, New York, more to the point.\par \par \uc2 That is where you will find our Bryan Llenas.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump choosing a very important campaign day to be not in a battleground state, but in New York. We will tell you why next. And we will also have fresh reaction to that major news coming from the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  members who voted in support of the former president next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Want to take you to Uniondeal -- Uniondale, New York, I should say, on Long Island, New York, right now.\par \par \uc2 That's a Democratic region, although that particular area, judging from the crowds that have been there for quite some time, it is a throng and then some, looking forward to hear from him on a day we're learning that the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  holding off on endorsing any presidential candidate and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has gone ahead and cut rates, but a lot more than thought, about a half-point cut.\par \par \uc2 So he will no doubt be weighing in on those developments.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas is there right now with more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, people have been lining up here in Uniondale in Nassau County since Tuesday afternoon to try to get a seat inside of the Nassau Coliseum. There's some 16,000-plus folks that will be inside of this stadium tonight. The campaign says 60,000-plus asked to get a ticket. So, obviously, it's going to be a big rally.\par \par \uc2 The big question is, why did he choose New York, when there are other battleground states? This is not a battleground state. It is decidedly a blue, Democratic state. And the answer to that is from the campaign, one, New York, is the largest media market in the country. So this will replay itself in those battleground states.\par \par \uc2 And, number two, there are three competitive congressional races in New York that flipped from blue to red in 2022. And they are -- Republicans are trying to hold those seats. New York could decide which party gets full control of the House.\par \par \uc2 And one of those congressmen is Anthony D'Esposito. He is the representative here for the Fourth Congressional District, which includes Uniondale as well.\par \par \uc2 You are fighting for your seat. How important is it to have former President Trump come out here and not go to a typical battleground state?\par \par \uc2 REP. ANTHONY D'ESPOSITO (R-NY): So, I think it's great. The excitement is electric here at the Nassau Coliseum.\par \par \uc2 And, listen, it is very clear perhaps New York is not a battleground state. But what there is there is, is a battleground right here on Long Island. And when President Trump wins on election night, he is going to need a House majority. And that House majority runs through the Empire State.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Got to get your reaction the breaking news out of the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 , the rank-and-file voting overwhelmingly, 60 percent, for former President Trump here, over Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that decision, particularly not just their vote, but the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 's decision not to formally endorse a candidate for the first time since 1996?\par \par \uc2 D'ESPOSITO: I think it tells you that President Trump is the person in this race who appeals to the hardworking blue-collar American people.\par \par \uc2 He is the person who's fighting to secure our borders, to make things cheaper at the supermarket, to make life more affordable. And those at the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  saw exactly that.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Last quick question.\par \par \uc2 Economy, immigration, those two things are the most important here in New York?\par \par \uc2 D'ESPOSITO: Economy, immigration, and, of course, crime. We're very close to New York City.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, because of the migrant situation in New York City, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is one of the finest in the world, my alma mater, but they're doing what they can with the resources that they have.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Congressman, thank you, Anthony D'Esposito. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, there you have it, some fresh reaction there to the big news. Tonight, former President Trump will take the stage at 7:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that, Bryan Llenas.\par \par \uc2 We have got Congressman Michael McCaul with us right now, the Texas Republican kind enough to weigh in on all these fast-moving developments.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you make of this \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  thing? We will be talking to Sean O'Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president. The membership seems to really like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but the official position is no endorsement at all.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I think it's great news for the Republican Party. We're sort of outreaching to the working-class man.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think a lot of what we stand for does appeal to the working class. And Trump, if anything, if he wants any labor union, I would think he would by far be more popular than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I just think that our value systems, standing for the common man, if you will, that's where Trump, I think, is expanding the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So where do things stand right now? A lot of people are questioning why the former president is in New York. It's a traditionally blue state. But maybe one of his guests pointed out that this is all about picking off some vulnerable Democratic seats and maybe through New York building on that House majority that you enjoy.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: I think it's strategically done. And I think this is -- New York and California are going to decide the majority -- whether we have the majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 And so I'm very honored that President Trump is going to New York to help these candidates, because it's going to make or break our majority or if we have a stronger majority, if you will.\par \par \uc2 Also, I think, as your broadcaster said, this will get a lot of play in other battleground states as well, the speech that he's giving tonight. So I think, from a campaign standpoint, it is a very smart, smart move. It's going to help our Republican candidates in New York. And it'll get played all over the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I know, Congressman, you're not a Fed watcher or really a Fed critic at all. You respect their independence, but some of your colleagues were surprised by the half-point cut the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  announced and sensed that it might have been politically driven to help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is supposed to be independent. I hope that's still the case.\par \par \uc2 Now, a cut in interest rates, as you know, it will spur the economy. It will spur investment and make for a stronger economy. As an average American, I'm glad to see that happen. But I hope politics weren't a play at this one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great seeing you again. Thank you for jostling your schedule to be with us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 All right, the aforementioned \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  the big news of the day, along with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cut in interest rates today. Sean O'Brien, the head of that union, on why it ultimately decided not to endorse any presidential candidate. He's here. He's next with us, and only us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, Sean O'Brien always calls them as he sees them, so he's been very blunt about trying to make a way to speak to Democrats, speak to Republicans. He only got a chance to speak to the Republican Convention, not the Democrats, but, so far, no endorsement.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have been looking forward to this interview for some time, especially given the last-minute drama.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about a chat with Sean O'Brien. He is the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president. We have talked to him many times over the years. And he speaks his mind.\par \par \uc2 That's why I'm a little surprised by this news today that the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  will not be officially endorsing a presidential candidate. So I thought to myself, Neil, when you talk to Sean, ask him how a guy who was invited to speak before Republicans at their convention, but didn't get the same honor at the Democratic Convention, could decide not to support the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 And here he is, Sean O'Brien.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 Why not? Why not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: Hello, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very good.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, listen, we interviewed both candidates, and we were seeking commitments from both candidates. And we couldn't get solid commitments on our core issues, like the PRO Act, like vetoing national right-to-work, like staying out of labor disputes and not trying to force any contracts on us, like what happened to our brothers and sisters in the rail industry.\par \par \uc2 We didn't get solid commitments from either candidate, which was a major factor in our decision as a general executive board not to endorse any candidate.\par \par \uc2 However, we did a lot of polling, as you know, and the polling leaned heavily towards former President Trump. So that tells you...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very, very heavily, very heavily, Sean.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering, do you look disconnected from your members?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: No, we're definitely not disconnected from our members, because, look, we did extensive polling. That was important.\par \par \uc2 Our goal as leaders, as union leaders, as leader of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , to provide the ability for people to have a voice and to provide credible information. And we cannot dictate to our members. Our members are sophisticated, they're going to make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 But this is a bigger problem. The Democrats have always taken for granted that they're going to get our vote no matter what. And the Republicans fancy themselves as the working parties, working people's party. I think right now both sides have to take a step back and really reevaluate what their commitment is to working people.\par \par \uc2 And that's what we did in this process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but it does make me wonder, when two out of three of your members prefer \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- now, it's a telephone survey, I grant that.\par \par \uc2 But that trend has been around for a while, where members tend to favor more the Republican candidate than the Democratic candidate. But that's a pretty overwhelming shout-out right there. So did that come into your thinking, or no?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We evaluated everything. We evaluated the straw poll, which was done in person, which, as you know, when you have an audience...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: ... you can influence that audience. We figured that was going to lean heavily towards President Biden, and it didn't lean heavily towards him.\par \par \uc2 And then we did the poll, where 40,000 of our members participated in that. That's a 60 percent poll, the other 58 percent poll that you see. And then we did the research-based polls.\par \par \uc2 And two out of those three polls leaned Republican. So, again, I will get back to, there's a lot of work that got to be done. Our members are paying attention. And, again, no endorsement, I think sends a message to both parties that, if they truly want to support working people, they have to reevaluate it and understand that nothing is given; it is earned.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I don't mean to disrespect here, but no endorsement also means you can have it both ways and you're not taking a stand.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: No, we're not looking to have it both ways at all. I take a stand every day. I call balls and strikes.\par \par \uc2 There's some great Democrats that supported us. President Biden passed pension legislation that fixed our pensions. But the reality of it is, again, I have said this time and time again, we fix -- they fix problems that were created a long time ago. Trucking deregulation cost 400,000 Teamster jobs. Companies went into bankruptcy, which destroyed pension funds.\par \par \uc2 So, although they fixed the problem, some of us created those problems that had to be fixed. And, look, it's different times. Our members are different. A lot of people -- there's a lot of social issues out there where sometimes our members are focused clearly on the social issues.\par \par \uc2 Not taking a position is not having it both ways. This country is divided down the middle. It's my goal, just like we have done with our union, is to bring this together, work bipartisan and get some stuff done, like the PRO Act, like bankruptcy reform and other issues that affect our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, did this turn in any way, Sean -- I think you were critical of President Trump after his interview where he was going back and forth, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and others, about when you can fire, get rid of workers, and that he admired that ability.\par \par \uc2 And you were quite vehement about it. And I just thought that might have represented a turning point.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes, I mean, obviously, words have consequences, right? Statements have consequences.\par \par \uc2 And I will -- I called former President Trump out, just like I would call anybody else that tries to retaliate or make fun of people being terminated for exercising their right to strike. But when we asked both candidates in the presidential forum roundtables about supporting our railway workers and workers that are under the RLA to strike and not interfere, we couldn't get strong commitments from either candidate.\par \par \uc2 So that was a major factor in our decision-making. But back to your point, unacceptable for anybody to fire workers for exercising their right and/or gloat about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, let me...\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: It's no secret I'm not a big fan of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. That, I do remember.\par \par \uc2 But let me then step back and get your thoughts on that relationship, because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  apparently might have a great sway in a potential Trump administration, and even when it comes to looking at the government books and looking at how the government spends its money. Did that weigh in?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes, I mean, look, we can't look to see ghosts.\par \par \uc2 I have got 1.3 million of the strongest people in the world, and that's our rank-and-file members. And although there may be a difference of opinions or there may be criticisms, if we have to go to fight with corporate America and the Elon Musks of the world, I like my chances.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, so let me just get a sense of where you go from here, because your colleagues at other unions, by and large, have been going hook, line and sinker for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I know your members, when polled on this -- and I know these can be erratic polls. They're not scientific polls. I know how they go -- liked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a lot more than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Is that true?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I think they polled consistently in our polls. I can't speak for anything else. But they polled about 25 percent to 30 percent popularity without members consistently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I'm sorry. Compared to President Trump, though, he scored higher, did he not?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: He scored higher. He did, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does not?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: No, she does not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they're not psyched about her. They're more psyched about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I don't think they're psyched about both candidates. There's issues that are specific to what our members want and need.\par \par \uc2 And this polling wasn't just a vote. There were issues and questions posed within this polling that our members asked. So I think this process -- and I guess I will get back to, people are sick of it. People are sick of the name-calling. People are sick of tearing each other apart because they support one candidate versus the other.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's just people are fed up and they want it over with. I want it over with, to be honest with you. We do great work. We negotiate the strongest contracts,. We're organizing thousands of members each month. That's what our focus needs to be on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , they're strong Democrat. They're going for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Service workers, maybe because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the original proponent of getting rid of the tax on tips, seems to lean his way.\par \par \uc2 Is this an opportunity missed? Do you think you made a statement by not making a statement one way or the other for either candidate?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: It's not an opportunity missed, as far as we're concerned. It's an opportunity to obviously highlight that there's a problem politically with union members.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I polled my members. I released the data. I don't interfere in any other unions or their policies or procedures, but I can only speak for the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . We're going to be transparent. And I can't speak -- if there's going to be criticisms from my peers in the labor movement, so be it.\par \par \uc2 Our mission still stays the same. We're going to be transparent and inclusive of the rank-and-file members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you didn't get a chance to speak to Democrats at their convention. You did to Republicans at their convention.\par \par \uc2 Does any of that stick in your craw a little bit that you guys, I went the extra mile, and you turned me down?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: You know, I don't -- my feelings don't get hurt easily, and they weren't hurt because I didn't get invited to the DNC.\par \par \uc2 I went to the \uc2 RNC\uc2  with one agenda, and that was to highlight how important the American worker and the Teamster member is to this country. It wasn't an endorsement of either party. And if I had the opportunity to speak at the DNC, the message would have been the same.\par \par \uc2 Look, it's about fighting corporate America, fighting corporate greed, and holding politicians accountable. At the end of the day I have been told recently by Democrats and Republicans everything they do for us. And I appreciate it. But just like anything else, that's their job. That's what we elect them to do, and that's what they should be focused on.\par \par \uc2 They shouldn't be telling us what they have done for us in the past. They should be focused on what they're going to do for us in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I'm reflecting on that one telephone poll. I don't mean to belabor it, Sean, but you're going to have a lot of angry members, I would assume. How are you dealing with it?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We're going to have some angry members, there's no doubt.\par \par \uc2 But we're going to have some happy members as well. Remember, our union is different. We're 50/50 right down the middle. So, either way, we're going to take criticism. And this is a small portion of what our job is as union leaders in the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . Don't forget, we have got to organize members.\par \par \uc2 We have got to negotiate the strongest contracts. What we have done, we have set industry standards. So at the end of the day, we're going to continue to fulfill the mission that we have been doing for 2.5 years under our leadership.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, very quickly on this, I think you have to go back to around 1996 when your union didn't commit to someone. Is this a rarity then? Will you, four years from now, be less inclined to repeat a no-support statement?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, in 1996, obviously, that was a result of \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 , \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 . So that was a great reason.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Now we took a position...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was president, right.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: But this should be an eye-opener for 2028. If people want the support of the most powerful union in North America, whether you're a Democrat or Republican, start doing some things to support our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean O'Brien, a real pleasure. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president speaking his mind. We will see the fallout from this, because it is coming in fast and crazy as I was talking to Sean. But I can't repeat some of these things. It's a family show.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you just heard from Sean O'Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, indicating today that, even though he met with both candidates for the presidency of the United States, his union will officially not be endorsing either of those candidates.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we're keeping an eye on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is in a bar, I believe, in Downtown New York called the PubKey, in Downtown Manhattan. Maybe we can play some sound here, guys, and see if he says anything.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Scan here. Scan this Q.R. code here with this phone, if you'll take that from him.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's that one.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right. And fantastic, it went through!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Great.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, it did go through. All right, we're done!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Perfect!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first transaction by a president on the Bitcoin protocol, history.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know what that means? (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's history. It's history in the making.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everybody. I have got to go back and see this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come on, let's go.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Come on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right this way.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you. Thank you, everybody.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Sir, what's your reaction to the Fed cutting interest rates today?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I guess it shows the economy is very bad to cut it by that much, assuming they're not just playing politics. The economy would be very bad or they're playing politics, one or the other. But it was a big cut.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, any reaction to the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' decision not to endorse?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, they were -- it's a great honor, they're not going to endorse the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 That a big thing. And this is the first time in, I guess, 50-60 years that that's happened. Democrats automatically have the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . They took a vote and I guess I was 60 percent or more. And that's a great honor. I mean, it's really -- I have had a lot of \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  work for me, a lot of the concrete trucks have built all these buildings that you see in New York City, they're \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , exclusively \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . And I have to say, they've done a great job.\par \par \uc2 But the vote of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  themselves was very high for me. And the leadership, Sean O'Brien and the group, who are great people, they said, we can't endorse the Democrats. So I think this is the first time in many decades that they haven't endorsed Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... difference in those big industrial states, you think? Think it'll make a difference?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think it will. Yes, I think so. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  carry a lot of weight. The Democrats cannot believe they didn't -- you know, look, this is -- it was always automatic that Democrats get the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . And they said we won't endorse the Democrats this year. So that was an honor for me.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. Trump, there is a lot of concern about your vulnerability as you -- as you golf. Are you going to continue to golf? And have you talked with the Secret Service about upping security?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, we'll look at it and we'll do what the Secret Service wants. You don't want to ever be curtailed because of, you know, the crazy people out there, the bad people, crazy people, whatever you want to call them. So we'll let the Secret Service determine that. If they think things are safe, you do it, like coming to a place like this, it's got to be safe, got to be safe.\par \par \uc2 We're going now to Nassau Coliseum where, I mean, it has literally been packed. It's been -- you know, they have over 60,000 people for 15,000 or 16,000 seats. So I look forward to that. But it has always got to be safe.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: We got some great pictures.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You just don't want them dictating to you where you should go and what you should do.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you feel kind of hemmed in by the -- by all the new security?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I don't. I really don't. I just have to lead my life. And I have to make America great again. That's what it's all about.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... about Harris not having biological children. Do you think that that's something that Harris...\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : About who?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Governor Sanders yesterday had a dig about Harris not having biological children. She's a stepmother, but doesn't have biological children. Do you think that's something she should be attacked for?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I just don't know anything about it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We're still monitoring this. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is at a bar called PubKey. It's a Manhattan Bitcoin bar. It opened a couple of years ago in December in Greenwich Village in New York. It took over a basement level space that I believe, Washington Place, 85 Washington Place down there.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, the Bitcoin community is very fond of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He has been advocating even a Bitcoin-like currency ETFs around that. So he is very pro this new, some call it a rogue currency.\par \par \uc2 But let's listen in a little bit more. He might be responding to it.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, I like that.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Hello.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : How are you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hello.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's been a while, huh?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you feel safe, Mr. President? Are you looking at any private security in addition after your...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I feel very safe.\par \par \uc2 I have the top-of-the-line people. They needed more people and now they have them. They've been given that. and I guess it's presidential level. And based on the poll numbers, I guess you have to do that. We're leading in just about every poll. And we have to be safe. But we really have to be safe for the country. That's the most important thing to me, we have to be safe for the country. A lot of progress is going to be made, and it's going to be made fast.\par \par \uc2 But the Secret Service, I think they're doing a very good job. They just needed more people.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And what's the message...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And they've been given.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What's your message for New York?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I love New York. I always loved New York.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to give New York a big play. You know, I leave here, I mean, you see the crowds, and it's so friendly. I love New York. New York has a lot of problems and we're going to make New York great again also. We're going to bring it back. And we're going to hopefully win. We -- I think we have a real chance of winning with hundreds of thousands of people coming from prisons and jails and from mental institutions and dumped into New York.\par \par \uc2 The people don't want that. And we'll work with your mayor and your governor and we're going to do a lot of great things for New York. We want to do that. And we have to bring it back. We can't let this happen to New York. It's so unfair. You have hundreds of thousands of people dumped into the city and the state. You -- you just can't do that. Nothing -- no place can sustain that.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Would that mean a lot to you since it's your home state?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I would love to win New York. It hasn't been won in many, many decades by a Republican. But this time it should be won by -- this is a Republican that loves it, that lived here for many, many years. And, you know, I'm all about New York. We have to bring New York back. And we're going to.\par \par \uc2 We -- hopefully we're going to win New York. I mean, we're here because we want to win New York. And I think that's going to happen, OK?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: President Trump, we -- as Bitcoin fanatics here in New York, we love this bar.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just wanted your impression. What's your impression of PubKey?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think it's a great place. I just made the first transaction in Bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Everybody, that's a crypto guy, crypto maniac, as I call them, because they really are.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: We're a little wild, right?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They believe.\par \par \uc2 Everybody, whether it's Bitcoin, but crypto, get out and vote, because if you vote, we cannot lose. We cannot lose. We want to get everybody that agrees with you people, what you're doing. And a lot of people, there's a big lot of people. And, you know, they've been treating you very badly at the SEC and such. And we're going to treat you very fairly because you have to get out and vote, OK?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're going to make it -- yes, we'll make it -- we'll make crypto...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And it's pretty easy to use, right? It was fairly easy to make that payment?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It was very easy, yes. No, it goes quickly and beautifully.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bitcoin is a fortune, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's going to be great. It's the beginning of a new era, actually. I mean, if you think about it, it's -- what they've done is so amazing that -- over a short period of time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We appreciate that you see that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And you look at the values, and look at the kind of numbers you're talking about, bigger than the biggest corporations in the world put together, many of them. So it's quite something.\par \par \uc2 Want me to sign that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That would great if you could.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the menu, we have some -- it's National Cheeseburger Day, evidently.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, I love them.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So we got some burgers and Diet Cokes, if you want to.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I want more of this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Best burger in the city.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And David's doing a good job everybody, right? David, does everybody know David?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's doing a good job. He got this whole thing started. I will sign that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A menu? Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Would you like one? Do you want a hamburger?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These burgers are killer. They really are.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They're good.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's great, man. How's that? Nice?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's perfect. Amazing. And one more right here would be fantastic, if you don't mind, and then we can -- we have some gifts for you up front.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Good. I want Bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : OK. You got it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. Appreciate your open-mindedness. Thank you so much for coming here.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, it's great. It's a new -- it's a new era.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Paradigm.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's what's happening. It's amazing to see what has happened in such a short period of time. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Who wants a hamburger?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : These suckers are good too, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is a crypto burger.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Actually -- actually, you should name them. Name them Bitcoin and a Crypto.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Many of them, you can name.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Think you're going to be in good shape back there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Do you want one? Huh? Do you want one?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She's been great.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I just saw Greg Gutfeld. You have to watch it tonight, Greg Gut -- how many people with them? Doug, you with them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's cool, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's done a good job. Does everyone know Pulitzer Prize winner? Greg is one of the greats. Look at him, he -- he's embarrassed. He's embarrassed. OK. Here you go.\par \par \uc2 Doug, you're great.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You going to have one?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Those -- it makes me look thin. That's all I can...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Have a good time, everybody!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're going now to Nassau Coliseum.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Safe travels.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Packed house.\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Thank you, very much, everybody.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Sir, you're...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... wireless explosions in Lebanon?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think it's crazy. I have never seen anything like it. Nobody has.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sir, this is...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nice to see you again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you very much. I'm glad you guys could...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you, everybody.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. You have been watching and listening to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He is at this bar in downtown New York. It is called PubKey. That is short for a Public Key, which is a special way, a cryptographic code that's used to encrypt information, make it not discernible and traceable when you use this so-called rogue currency.\par \par \uc2 You might have heard the former president briefly mention the SEC, the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 , no big fans of this new technology. It's not so new anymore. Bitcoin itself has been stuck at around $60,000 a coin or so.\par \par \uc2 Didn't get much movement today with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . But that's not the point here, just to say that the president is a big advocate of all of this. He thinks that this type of currency and the fact that the United States all but invented it. We should be taking advantage of that.\par \par \uc2 He did refer to this a big event he has planned in Uniondale, New York, on Long Island. Better than 60,000, 70,000 were waiting outside. Some got there as early as yesterday morning just to get a seat. He also acknowledged that today is National Burger Day. Think of that. You have National Cheeseburger Day, more to the point.\par \par \uc2 You have also got the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cutting interest rates. He did make a reference to that where he wondered if it might -- I don't want to misparaphrase him -- might have been politically motivated, that some had expressed that concern that a quarter-point cut might have been in the offing, but did we need a half-point cut.\par \par \uc2 Hans Nichols is with us right now, the Axios report, as we keep an eye on this.\par \par \uc2 Hans, what do you make? This has been a crazy day for all of this.\par \par \uc2 HANS NICHOLS, AXIOS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, first, if I can, with Sean O'Brien and the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  not officially backing anyone, which, to the former president's view, is a win for him. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Yes. Yes, it's exactly what I thought. The president just did a victory lap on a non-endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Now, some of the state chapters of \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are apparently endorsing for Harris, but -- and the Harris campaign is pushing that. But what you just saw the president there is claim that this is a big win, even though there's a non-endorsement. And we will see whether or not that actually translates into votes.\par \par \uc2 And he's clearly trying to tap into the energy, to sort of, put in a pun, on crypto. And that's something the president and many of his top advisers are committed to. And he wants to harness that energy and, as he said, potentially win New York.\par \par \uc2 Now, I'm not going to call New York for the president. I'm not going to call it a swing state. But he's in New York. And it does have implications downballot, because the road to the majority for either party, at least here in the House of Representatives, where I am, runs through New York.\par \par \uc2 And both parties understand that -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And you can think there are a couple of vulnerable Democratic seats which Republicans at least in New York hope they can pick up to increase that very narrow margin they have in the House. I don't know how likely that is.\par \par \uc2 But on the O'Brien news and what he does now by not supporting a candidate, that he did send a strong signal to Democrats here that you can't take our support for granted. How do you think this all falls out?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Well, the unions, especially the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , have always kind of - - and the building trades in general -- have always had their members be more sort of towards Trump than the leadership.\par \par \uc2 And this is the first time we have sort of seen it come back into balance, where the leadership is officially staying neutral. Now, there's a pull out that I'm sure you saw as well that had the more of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  members actually supported Trump.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if too many \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  members are waiting for a permission structure from their leadership on who they're going to vote for. But that's what they got, right? They have got -- it's no longer sort of verboten to vote for a Republican or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' side.\par \par \uc2 And they now have -- they now have the green light from their leadership to go ahead and do that. That's how Trump's trying to spin it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Like, you and I both know he would have rather had the full- throated endorsement -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But to your point, I mean, you talk about the New York and the uphill fight to make that state vote Republican, but it did for \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  twice.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we know there have been some surprises in the past. So, obviously, he is trying by reaching out to unions, sending a signal here. Some of that is resonating.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Yes, and don't forget that the sort of the -- both on the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' side and the crypto side that there are members all across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: So he can make a -- visit a crypto bar. And, yes, it's a localized Manhattan bar, but that -- just in the same way that crypto is so decentralized, his message then will be sort of diffused throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 And that's clearly a play that the Trump campaign wants to make, because -- and there's also a bit of fund-raising aspect here. They want to gather some of that crypto money, because, as you know, there's a lot of crypto money out there -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There is a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 Hans, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Just a reminder, the former president will be with Greg Gutfeld tonight. That should be interesting, as should "The Five" right now.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240919ek9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O`Brien\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 2,223 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I have been looking forward to this interview for some time, especially given the last-minute drama.\par \par \uc2 I`m talking about a chat with Sean O`Brien. He is the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president. We have talked to him many times over the years. And he speaks his mind.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I`m a little surprised by this news today that the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  will not be officially endorsing a presidential candidate. So I thought to myself, Neil, when you talk to Sean, ask him how a guy who was invited to speak before Republicans at their convention, but didn`t get the same honor at the Democratic Convention, could decide not to support the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 And here he is, Sean O`Brien.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 Why not? Why not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SEAN O`BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: Hello, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very good.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, listen, we interviewed both candidates, and we were seeking commitments from both candidates. And we couldn`t get solid commitments on our core issues, like the PRO Act, like vetoing national right-to-work, like staying out of labor disputes and not trying to force any contracts on us, like what happened to our brothers and sisters in the rail industry.\par \par \uc2 We didn`t get solid commitments from either candidate, which was a major factor in our decision as a general executive board not to endorse any candidate.\par \par \uc2 However, we did a lot of polling, as you know, and the polling leaned heavily towards former President Trump. So that tells you...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very, very heavily, very heavily, Sean.\par \par \uc2 And I`m just wondering, do you look disconnected from your members?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, we`re definitely not disconnected from our members, because, look, we did extensive polling. That was important.\par \par \uc2 Our goal as leaders, as union leaders, as leader of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , to provide the ability for people to have a voice and to provide credible information. And we cannot dictate to our members. Our members are sophisticated, they`re going to make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 But this is a bigger problem. The Democrats have always taken for granted that they`re going to get our vote no matter what. And the Republicans fancy themselves as the working parties, working people`s party. I think right now both sides have to take a step back and really reevaluate what their commitment is to working people.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we did in this process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but it does make me wonder, when two out of three of your members prefer \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- now, it`s a telephone survey, I grant that.\par \par \uc2 But that trend has been around for a while, where members tend to favor more the Republican candidate than the Democratic candidate. But that`s a pretty overwhelming shout-out right there. So did that come into your thinking, or no?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: We evaluated everything. We evaluated the straw poll, which was done in person, which, as you know, when you have an audience...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: ... you can influence that audience. We figured that was going to lean heavily towards President Biden, and it didn`t lean heavily towards him.\par \par \uc2 And then we did the poll, where 40,000 of our members participated in that. That`s a 60 percent poll, the other 58 percent poll that you see. And then we did the research-based polls.\par \par \uc2 And two out of those three polls leaned Republican. So, again, I will get back to, there`s a lot of work that got to be done. Our members are paying attention. And, again, no endorsement, I think sends a message to both parties that, if they truly want to support working people, they have to reevaluate it and understand that nothing is given; it is earned.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I don`t mean to disrespect here, but no endorsement also means you can have it both ways and you`re not taking a stand.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, we`re not looking to have it both ways at all. I take a stand every day. I call balls and strikes.\par \par \uc2 There`s some great Democrats that supported us. President Biden passed pension legislation that fixed our pensions. But the reality of it is, again, I have said this time and time again, we fix -- they fix problems that were created a long time ago. Trucking deregulation cost 400,000 Teamster jobs. Companies went into bankruptcy, which destroyed pension funds.\par \par \uc2 So, although they fixed the problem, some of us created those problems that had to be fixed. And, look, it`s different times. Our members are different. A lot of people -- there`s a lot of social issues out there where sometimes our members are focused clearly on the social issues.\par \par \uc2 Not taking a position is not having it both ways. This country is divided down the middle. It`s my goal, just like we have done with our union, is to bring this together, work bipartisan and get some stuff done, like the PRO Act, like bankruptcy reform and other issues that affect our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, did this turn in any way, Sean -- I think you were critical of President Trump after his interview where he was going back and forth, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and others, about when you can fire, get rid of workers, and that he admired that ability.\par \par \uc2 And you were quite vehement about it. And I just thought that might have represented a turning point.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, I mean, obviously, words have consequences, right? Statements have consequences.\par \par \uc2 And I will -- I called former President Trump out, just like I would call anybody else that tries to retaliate or make fun of people being terminated for exercising their right to strike. But when we asked both candidates in the presidential forum roundtables about supporting our railway workers and workers that are under the RLA to strike and not interfere, we couldn`t get strong commitments from either candidate.\par \par \uc2 So that was a major factor in our decision-making. But back to your point, unacceptable for anybody to fire workers for exercising their right and/or gloat about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, let me...\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: It`s no secret I`m not a big fan of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. That, I do remember.\par \par \uc2 But let me then step back and get your thoughts on that relationship, because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  apparently might have a great sway in a potential Trump administration, and even when it comes to looking at the government books and looking at how the government spends its money. Did that weigh in?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, I mean, look, we can`t look to see ghosts.\par \par \uc2 I have got 1.3 million of the strongest people in the world, and that`s our rank-and-file members. And although there may be a difference of opinions or there may be criticisms, if we have to go to fight with corporate America and the Elon Musks of the world, I like my chances.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, so let me just get a sense of where you go from here, because your colleagues at other unions, by and large, have been going hook, line and sinker for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I know your members, when polled on this -- and I know these can be erratic polls. They`re not scientific polls. I know how they go -- liked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a lot more than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Is that true?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I think they polled consistently in our polls. I can`t speak for anything else. But they polled about 25 percent to 30 percent popularity without members consistently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I`m sorry. Compared to President Trump, though, he scored higher, did he not?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: He scored higher. He did, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does not?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, she does not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they`re not psyched about her. They`re more psyched about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I don`t think they`re psyched about both candidates. There`s issues that are specific to what our members want and need.\par \par \uc2 And this polling wasn`t just a vote. There were issues and questions posed within this polling that our members asked. So I think this process -- and I guess I will get back to, people are sick of it. People are sick of the name-calling. People are sick of tearing each other apart because they support one candidate versus the other.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s just people are fed up and they want it over with. I want it over with, to be honest with you. We do great work. We negotiate the strongest contracts,. We`re organizing thousands of members each month. That`s what our focus needs to be on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , they`re strong Democrat. They`re going for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Service workers, maybe because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the original proponent of getting rid of the tax on tips, seems to lean his way.\par \par \uc2 Is this an opportunity missed? Do you think you made a statement by not making a statement one way or the other for either candidate?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: It`s not an opportunity missed, as far as we`re concerned. It`s an opportunity to obviously highlight that there`s a problem politically with union members.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I polled my members. I released the data. I don`t interfere in any other unions or their policies or procedures, but I can only speak for the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . We`re going to be transparent. And I can`t speak -- if there`s going to be criticisms from my peers in the labor movement, so be it.\par \par \uc2 Our mission still stays the same. We`re going to be transparent and inclusive of the rank-and-file members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you didn`t get a chance to speak to Democrats at their convention. You did to Republicans at their convention.\par \par \uc2 Does any of that stick in your craw a little bit that you guys, I went the extra mile, and you turned me down?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: You know, I don`t -- my feelings don`t get hurt easily, and they weren`t hurt because I didn`t get invited to the DNC.\par \par \uc2 I went to the \uc2 RNC\uc2  with one agenda, and that was to highlight how important the American worker and the Teamster member is to this country. It wasn`t an endorsement of either party. And if I had the opportunity to speak at the DNC, the message would have been the same.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s about fighting corporate America, fighting corporate greed, and holding politicians accountable. At the end of the day I have been told recently by Democrats and Republicans everything they do for us. And I appreciate it. But just like anything else, that`s their job. That`s what we elect them to do, and that`s what they should be focused on.\par \par \uc2 They shouldn`t be telling us what they have done for us in the past. They should be focused on what they`re going to do for us in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I`m reflecting on that one telephone poll. I don`t mean to belabor it, Sean, but you`re going to have a lot of angry members, I would assume. How are you dealing with it?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: We`re going to have some angry members, there`s no doubt.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to have some happy members as well. Remember, our union is different. We`re 50/50 right down the middle. So, either way, we`re going to take criticism. And this is a small portion of what our job is as union leaders in the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . Don`t forget, we have got to organize members.\par \par \uc2 We have got to negotiate the strongest contracts. What we have done, we have set industry standards. So at the end of the day, we`re going to continue to fulfill the mission that we have been doing for 2.5 years under our leadership.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, very quickly on this, I think you have to go back to around 1996 when your union didn`t commit to someone. Is this a rarity then? Will you, four years from now, be less inclined to repeat a no-support statement?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, in 1996, obviously, that was a result of \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 , \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 . So that was a great reason.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Now we took a position...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was president, right.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: But this should be an eye-opener for 2028. If people want the support of the most powerful union in North America, whether you`re a Democrat or Republican, start doing some things to support our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean O`Brien, a real pleasure. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president speaking his mind. We will see the fallout from this, because it is coming in fast and crazy as I was talking to Sean. But I can`t repeat some of these things. It`s a family show.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240918ek9i0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 President Biden Meets With U.K. Prime Minister; \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Strike; Interview With Harris/Walz Campaign Adviser Ian Sams; Interview With Former U.S. Commerce Secretary \uc2 Wilbur Ross\b0\par\par\uc2 Jacqui Heinrich, Dan Springer, Aishah Hasnie, Bryan Llenas, Neil  Cavuto \par \uc2 9,217 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (D) AND U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I am feeling very good about Pennsylvania because there are a lot of people in Pennsylvania who deserve to be seen and heard. That's why I'm here in Johnstown.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm looking to the future. We're going to make our country bigger, better, greater than ever before. We're going to turn it around.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, don't look now, as if you have to, but the 2024 showdown is heating up.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are not letting up, not one bit, Trump hitting the trail in Western states, as Harris is back on the ground in the Keystone State.\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX team coverage with Aishah Hasnie on Trump heading to Nevada with a new tax break pitch, Bryan Llenas on Harris heading to Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania, with her new way forward pitch.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to Harris spokesman Ian Sams why she chose to expand that pitch in a certain state. I will give you a hint. It's Pennsylvania. All that and former Trump Commerce Secretary \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2 , what he says are the fuss about all of these former Trump officials who are not keen on their former boss getting back to his old job.\par \par \uc2 Lots to cover, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Let's get right to it.\par \par \uc2 We want to go to Aishah right now in Rancho Palos Verdes, California, with how things went down for the former president today.\par \par \uc2 It was a combination speech and a presser afterwards, but he got into a lot, didn't he?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: He did, as he always does.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon to you, Neil. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 The former president remaining defiant about his performance at this week's debate, saying that he won and that he doesn't have any reason to do another debate with his opponent, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But after this press conference wrapped up, he was walking away and I was able to grab him and sort of pull him aside and ask him one more time, what would it take for you to do one more debate?\par \par \uc2 And he left the door cracked open this time. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What would it take to do another debate? What would you need and want?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It would be -- I wouldn't need anything. I could do it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Would you do it on FOX...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But I have done two debates. Everything was crooked in the second one. I did great with the debates. And I think they have answered everything.\par \par \uc2 But maybe if I got in the right mood. I don't know. Right now, I'm leading. And I'm leading in every single poll on the debates. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So there you heard it there, "Maybe if I got in the right mood. I don't know right now. I'm leading."\par \par \uc2 So that was his last answer before he walked away. Now, in this press conference, he took a lot of time basically attacking his opponent's home state here in California, the policies, the Democrats' policies. He also, Neil, took a lot of questions about what influence right-wing social media personality Laura Loomer has on the campaign.\par \par \uc2 She's been traveling with him on the trail this week and getting some blowback from Republicans, conservatives, for a tweet saying that the White House would smell like curry if Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  won.\par \par \uc2 Well, Trump told us that Loomer brings a sort of spirit to the campaign, did not condemn her comments, but said this to us. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't control Laura. Laura has to say what she wants. She's a free spirit. I don't know what she said, but I will go take a look and I will put out a statement later on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, so he is now headed to a big fund-raiser in the Bay Area, as he needs to fund-raise because he's well behind Harris in campaign cash. She reported almost $50 million in the first 24 hours following that debate, and she raised almost three times as much as him in August, leading him by about $100 million cash on hand.\par \par \uc2 And then he heads to battleground, Nevada, Las Vegas, where he will hold a big rally tonight -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Aishah, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Fair and balanced. Let's go to Bryan Llenas with team Harris right now in Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania -- to you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Well, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she landed this afternoon in Pennsylvania. She will be making two stops in rural parts of the state. She started in Johnstown, Pennsylvania, before she comes over to Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign believes that if they can lessen the margin of victory in rural parts of Pennsylvania, which is the vast majority of the counties here, that can make all the difference in the race come November.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, in Johnstown, Harris shook hands with folks at the airport as she got off of the plane and she met with voters. She also visited a local business. She was then asked, how is she feeling about the race in Pennsylvania, where polls show it's tied?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am feeling very good about Pennsylvania because there are a lot of people in Pennsylvania who deserve to be seen and heard. That's why I'm here in Johnstown.\par \par \uc2 And I will be continuing to travel around the state to make sure that I'm listening as much as we are talking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, Harris says she wants another debate. She's trying to use her debate performance to build momentum.\par \par \uc2 The campaign in the last couple of days has released three new ads using clips from the debate on abortion, leadership and her call for national unity.\par \par \uc2 Now, a new national Reuters/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll post-debate shows that Harris' lead among 1,400 registered voters has increased to five points, 47 to 42, over former President Trump. This is slightly higher than the four-point list she had in this poll before the debate.\par \par \uc2 The economy remains the number one issue here in Pennsylvania. And to that end, the Harris campaign is pushing their new plan for rural America. It promises major federal investment in rural infrastructure, lower energy costs and lower grocery costs.\par \par \uc2 But the latest Congressional Republican Joint Economic Committee report shows costs are up 19 percent in Pennsylvania under the Biden/Harris administration. This means Pennsylvanians are spending $984 more a month on food, energy, housing compared to 2021.\par \par \uc2 The latest polling also shows, Neil, that Harris is trailing Trump on the issue of the economy here in Pennsylvania by eight points. At this rally, I should say they are giving out stickers that say "Tax the ultra-rich." This campaign spending double the amount of money they are spending in any other state to try to convince voters that she is best on the economy, some $76 million promised in future advertisements in the commonwealth -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas with team Harris on that.\par \par \uc2 Ian Sams is joining us right now, the Harris-Walz campaign senior spokesman.\par \par \uc2 Good to finally be able to talk to you, Ian. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, HARRIS/WALZ CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Thanks, Neil. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you a little bit about what our Aishah Hasnie might have gotten out of President Trump, maybe a small crack opening in the door to another debate.\par \par \uc2 Is it your belief that, despite his not wanting one, maybe he is hinting at one?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, I thought his exact wording was pretty interesting. He said, if he's in the right mood, he might do a debate.\par \par \uc2 He seems awfully moody. I don't know if he's just working through his feelings after Tuesday night. But, as the vice president has said, the American people deserve a second debate. They deserve to see the choice in this election between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and a proposed vision that brings the country forward and turns the page on the chaotic division of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and is offering real plans, whether it's grocery costs, housing costs, things your reporter just talked about, that she's put forward to take those things on.\par \par \uc2 Or do we go back to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his show that he likes to put on that is very dependent on his mood, but with an agenda that's pretty scary, more tax cuts for those at the very top, taking away our freedoms, whether it's reproductive freedoms, voting rights, things like that?\par \par \uc2 That's the choice that people get to see on stage in a debate. And so she thinks that the American people deserve another one. And I guess we will see if President Trump's mood changes to allow him to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, there does -- you talk about the tax cuts that the former president wants to extend.\par \par \uc2 Of course, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  isn't a stranger to offering incentives as well for tax relief. Maybe it will extend not only to service workers and income, tips and all that. It could go to Social Security and the rest. Do you guys worry back and forth that you're driving us into debt with plans like that, both sides?, $5 trillion for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , close to $2 trillion for you guys?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, you just kind of hit the nail on the head.\par \par \uc2 Independent economists have come out and said that President Trump would be way worse for the deficit and debt than President Harris would be. And so I think that, when it comes to people who...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's still trillions of dollars, right? It's still trillions of dollars, right?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: When it comes to people who care about the debt and deficit, I think that they want to see that people are willing to raise the revenues to pay for their plans.\par \par \uc2 And I think, with Vice President Harris, she's put forward a suite of proposals to make sure that we have the right revenues to help pay for some of her proposals, for example, talking about making sure that billionaires pay a minimum tax, talking about quadrupling the taxes on stock buybacks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we don't get there, right? Right? Isn't that the problem, where we don't really get there? She's still over a trillion shy.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know these things really oftentimes on the left and the right aren't worth the paper they're printed on because things change. I get that. But it's not as if the debt is going to get smaller under President Harris.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, under the Biden and Harris administration, where she's been proud to serve as vice president, we have reduced the deficit.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that there are ways where we can make good progress in this.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, the deficit has come down. The debt, which is a combination of deficits, has not. It's increased by trillions.\par \par \uc2 But go ahead. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: That's OK.\par \par \uc2 And then, if we can put forward a suite of proposals that help in -- foster real economic growth, that's also going to help. And when you look at the two candidates who are running, for example, Vice President Harris has put forward just a couple weeks ago a real plan for growing small businesses in this country.\par \par \uc2 Right now, if you want to start a small business in America, it costs about 40,000 bucks to get off the ground. But our tax code only allows a deduction for those people of $5,000. She wants to make it $50,000, so that we can actually help those small businesses grow and they can create jobs, and that can create revenues in local communities.\par \par \uc2 These are the kinds of investments in real working America that she's proposing, whereas, again, President Trump and independent economists, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  and Nobel Prize-winning economists have said that his plan would be an inflation bomb. You know, he's offering plans that are completely all over the place and would actually explode this issue and make it worse, because, as we know, when he was president last time, the debt increased significantly more than it did under Democratic presidents.\par \par \uc2 And so I think, if you're a voter out there in this country who cares about the debt and deficit, there's only one candidate who even remotely comes close to offering forward a plan to take that on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you will be surprised the other side says just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 But, Ian, let me ask you this. And I'm just curious, because I hadn't had a chance to talk to you post the debate. I have made no secret of the fact that I thought your candidate won hands down, in fact, convincingly so.\par \par \uc2 But you do hear from the Trump people that she got, your candidate got deferential treatment. She was not interrupted or fact-checked nearly as much as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was. And I have heard some of your surrogates and her surrogates say, well, that's because his whoppers were bigger.\par \par \uc2 I'm just curious, were you surprised the moderators did not interrupt her or fact-check her to the degree they did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the end? I mean, you go into this meticulously planning things, right? That had to surprise you somewhat.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, the funny thing about fact-checking is, it is only really is relevant when you're telling lies.\par \par \uc2 And the vice president wasn't really telling lies on stage, and President Trump was. And so, in order to be able to fact-check, you have to have...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, there were some -- there were some mini-whoppers in there. There were -- no, no, you're right. There were some mini-whoppers in there, compared to allowing the killing of infants after they're born and going back to the cat and dog eating thing. Those are whoppers. You're quite right.\par \par \uc2 But there were some areas where she stretched it, and no one bothered to go after her about, no combat going on around the world involving U.S. servicemen, or what was going on in the preference for dealing with what she was doing at the border and her obviously obfuscation around that.\par \par \uc2 But it just didn't seem balanced in that respect.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, it's also quite telling of a candidate who's complaining about the moderators how he performed in the debate.\par \par \uc2 I think ABC did a good job. I think that they ran a tidy debate. I think that it allowed the American people to see the clear choice between the two candidates. And, boy, oh, boy, did they get to see a choice between someone who was focused on the issues that they actually care about and someone who was only focused on himself.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What did you think of that, Ian?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but did that surprise you, Ian, that he did take the bait, and that it was planned that you would throw this out there, that she would throw out the crowd sizes at his rallies, knowing that would irk him?\par \par \uc2 And maybe the only surprise was, despite the fact that he was advised not to lose his cool, he did. Did you expect that to happen?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Look, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's team and himself were signaling that he might be a more disciplined candidate. And I think it's just not in his nature.\par \par \uc2 He's uncontrollable. And so he clearly is provoked when he's called out for his own record and his own lies. He's also provoked when the vice president decides to provide the truth to the American people about some of his bombastic claims and hyperbolic claims, including about his crowd sizes.\par \par \uc2 And so these are the kinds of things that we're used to seeing from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's not really focused on real issues that people care about. He's only focused about himself and wanting to talk about himself and defend himself and lash out at people who criticize him.\par \par \uc2 And I think it really is emblematic of what we would see if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets elected to the White House again, which is someone who's going to be completely unrestrained, especially now that the Supreme Court has said he can get away with whatever he wants.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's not quite what the Supreme Court said.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you a little bit about the whole crowd size thing. I mean, the former president might stretch certain truths a little far. But when it comes to crowds and his rallies, he does pack them in. I think you would have to admit that. And his crowds are significantly bigger than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' crowds.\par \par \uc2 Would you acknowledge at least that much?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: I'm not sure if you have been watching crowds and rallies over the last couple of months. And I don't think that's the case.\par \par \uc2 I think we have seen a lot of people who are wandering off in the middle of his 45-minute-long rants about electric sharks and Hannibal Lecter, whereas the vice president's packing people into arenas.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see some people leaving. And I do see some people even when I watch these things. I would hardly say it's a significant number, any more than those who might wander out of a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  rally.\par \par \uc2 I guess I'm wondering, would you at least, on that, say what you will of the former president, he can pack them in?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: He used to. He's a faded image of his former self.\par \par \uc2 I think, in 2016, he had a lot more excitement, a lot more people showing up. I think now we're on season nine of this show, and maybe they have lost the plot a little bit and I think would prefer a new set of characters.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, maybe we can argue over numbers and all of that, but he doesn't seem to have any problem getting people to come up to venues hours, sometimes a day ahead of an event.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, what is your take on the candidate now? I know she has spoken, I believe, to a Philadelphia reporter today, so that seemed to be the first sign that maybe she's engaging in more interviews. But the reputation or the rap she has right now is she doesn't want to do that, and maybe that's the campaign strategy, to avoid exchanges like that.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering why. Given her debate performance, she seemed pretty on message, whether people liked that message or not. She was articulate, fast on her feet, very prosecutorial. So she seems to be able to handle herself, so why not more media interviews?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: It's a funny question, because, as you just said, she literally did a media interview about an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 And so she talked to one of the top anchors in the top station in Philadelphia. Obviously, Pennsylvania is a critical battleground state...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: ... decided by just 80,000 votes four years ago. You're going to see the vice president continue to talk to the press, continue to engage with the media and do interviews.\par \par \uc2 It's part of this campaign. I think that people are tuning in now that we have gotten through the first debate to really see what these candidates have to offer. She's going to continue to engage with the media. I think it's interesting, because President Trump, for example, you know, I know he likes to hang out in prime time on this network and do Twitter Spaces with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But you don't see him very often sitting down, taking tough questions from mainstream reporters like yourself. And so there's a real...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, he just did a press conference today. He just did a press conference today. So he does embrace and go with the press and all of that. And he's open to these extemporaneous exchanges with the media.\par \par \uc2 Now, many people might criticize how he goes about that, but he does it a lot more than your candidate, right?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Oh, she also just about 20 minutes ago stood in -- outside of -- inside of a store and took questions from the reporters who were with her.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, this is part of a campaign. She's going to continue to engage with the media and press. President Trump, I guess, will continue sort of doing 45-minute-long rants at a podium with reporters sitting there wondering what he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 But we will continue to engage with the media and share her vision for the country and why she's running and what her priorities would be as president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I know -- and you alluded to it in the beginning, Ian, and I don't want to misquote you, but, obviously, her strategy is to make sure the rich pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 I believe there's this idea of a minimum tax for the very wealthy, for corporations. But I am curious what you think, what she thinks is a fair share. And getting outside the billionaire community, because they're rarefied air, we used to have the top income tax rate of 90 percent in this country. Then it was in the 70 percent. Then it was in the 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 It's been dancing in and around 37 percent, 39 percent, around 40 percent. That's just the federal top rate. And that apparently is not a fair share, in your eyes. So could I ask you, what is?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, we have a situation in this country where you have corporations and certain