 CAVUTO: Well, Ian, we certainly appreciate your coming here and answering those questions, trying to give us a better idea where you're coming from.\par \par \uc2 Be well. Very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: I appreciate you having me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Ian Sams on all of that.\par \par \uc2 We have got a lot more coming up. Why \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2  is weighing in what his former boss had to say at that debate and his concerns about a debate he warned the former president about, we're told.\par \par \uc2 The former commerce secretary is coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, fair and balanced. We talked to a top Harris campaign spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 It is time to go to the former Commerce Secretary of the United States \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2 . He has a great book out, but I did have the opportunity to ask him about his boss' performance in that big debate. He was prescient on some of his warnings for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILBUR ROSS\uc2 , FORMER U.S. COMMERCE SECRETARY: Debates are actually more about style than about substance, and particularly when the moderators in the debate were very one-sided against Trump and for Harris.\par \par \uc2 You could see it in the way they fact-checked everything he said, very little that she said. But presidency is not mostly about style. Presidency is about substance. And the substance of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, we had very low inflation when he was president.\par \par \uc2 We had very low unemployment when he was president. And we had relative peace throughout the world when he was president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he never did any of that, right, Will? But he never did.\par \par \uc2 And it -- you're very prescient in a very low-key way, which I have always admired about you. There's no histrionics or drama to you.\par \par \uc2 ROSS: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I caught a