 And this Wharton study that's been done says that his plan is going to add $5 trillion to it, hers a little under, I believe, $1.5 trillion. Now, those numbers probably aren't worth the paper they're printed on.\par \par \uc2 But neither candidate seems to be very worried about the debt. Does that bother you?\par \par \uc2 ROSS: Well, I know from my personal experience with Trump that he understands that the way to get the economy to be able to live with debt burden is rapid growth.\par \par \uc2 And that's what he's aiming toward. Her programs, taxing successful people, taxing successful businesses, is not the way to stimulate. But the worst thing is, there will be more and more regulation. And when we were in office, when businessmen would come to see me, they were much more grateful for regulatory reform than they were even for the tax cuts that President Trump put in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2 , the former commerce secretary of the United States. We don't know what role he might play in a potential returning Trump administration. Probably too early to talk about that sort of stuff, but it is out there.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the fallout from a lot of this and who's right when it comes to all of these spending initiatives and plans. Well, count \uc2 Steve Ballmer\uc2 . The former CEO of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , one of the world's richest men, is worried about both sides spending money and driving us into debt, and no one seems to care. He's worried about that.\par \par \uc2 Ken Fisher\uc2 , though, has a different view on things. He's not going to get knee-deep in debt and the matters like that. He is likely to talk about the markets continuing their incredible surge, five straight days now for the S&P 500, and going up, up, up and away.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the conditions that he predicted when everyone was talking recession have panned out. \uc2 Ken Fisher\uc2  on that, \uc2 Steve Ballmer\uc2  on debt and the campaign race and a lot more -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, looking right at the White House.\par \par \uc2 That is where the present president of the United States lives, by the way. We're going to be seeing him making a statement. It's a tape playback, as they like to call it, between him and the U.K. prime minister, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 . When that happens, of course, we will roll it along to you.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich is there with more.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Western diplomatic sources confirmed to FOX that part of the discussion between President Biden and U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  will be whether to green-light Ukraine's request to use Western-made long-range missiles on targets deep inside of Russia as Ukraine faces potentially a new wave of destruction after the State Department says that Iran supplied Russia with short-range missiles.\par \par \uc2 Biden would need to sign off on Ukraine using British Storm Shadow missiles for expanded strikes because the components are made in the U.S. Kyiv has also asked to use French SCALP missiles and U.S. ATACMS. But U.S. officials have signaled openness to a policy change, while also insisting there's nothing to announce right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: As what Russia is doing has changed, as the battlefield has changed, we have adapted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The U.S. has resisted lifting restrictions, as Putin warns the U.S. and its allies that such a decision would put \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries at war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 But this is nothing new. And on every capability the U.S. has ultimately supplied Ukraine, the answer was first a no on HIMARS, on ATACMS, on Patriots, and on fighter jets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The vice president was promoting what this administration has done in terms of giving Ukraine what it needs. But there's plenty of criticism outside of that to say that it's also effectively blocking a victory by slow-walking these decisions.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I think if you were to ask those 50 nations, if you were to ask \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders, if you were to ask President Zelenskyy itself -- himself their thoughts on the president's leadership, I don't think they would have the words that you just said to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: FOX is also told, though, that this decision isn't the entire focus of this meeting.\par \par \uc2 The two countries have been broadly discussing how to best position Ukraine, both in terms of security guarantees, future funding, and potentially a future settled negotiation or whatever Ukraine wants to do in terms of ending this war, especially as everyone heads to \uc2 UNGA\uc2 , the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 . They want to have some concrete proposals in hand they can get other allies to sign off on, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui, great job, as always.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Again, when we get that tape playback, as I like to call it -- these kids are so hip with these terms. Tape playback, that's been out there for decades.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, I want to get to Brigadier General Rob Spalding on all of this, B2 stealth bomber pilot, the former \uc2 White House National Security Council\uc2  senior director for strategic panel -- planning, and I should say "War Without Rules," bestselling book.\par \par \uc2 General, it's good to have you. I know this is veering from maybe the obvious of the time being, but it still seems weird to me, General. We have this kind of weird -- this environment where the present commander in chief, makes these events happen, and this time with the prime minister 