 he sure seemed like he had command of the situation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you -- one thing you hear from critics of the ongoing war in Ukraine and our support for Ukraine is that we make the situation worse. When we allowed weapons to be used on Russian soil from Ukraine, that opened up a Pandora's box.\par \par \uc2 And yet that is what's happening, and that is what is explaining \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's response, that you started this, not me. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 SPALDING: Well, what I say is that clip you showed, where the press was saying, well, what about victory?\par \par \uc2 And I think this is a problem. We don't really have a concept of where this thing ends. Go back to the Korean War. Again, we were concerned about a nuclear war with the Soviet Union. MacArthur said, hey, I want to use nukes. And Truman basically fired him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPALDING: And if you look at the conduct of the war from then on out, there was a lot of control by the White House on what we were willing to do within the Korean War.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's a lot of complaints about that. But at the same time, we didn't have a nuclear war. I don't get the feeling that we have the same level of control of the implications of what might happen here in this situation, which if you look at Ukraine and Russia, North Korea and South Korea have very similar situations.\par \par \uc2 They both look very similar. I would bet you, and as I have said this from the beginning, we're going to look at some -- at some point having the cease-fire along a demilitarized zone, just like we have in Korea still today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow. Now, that provides another series of problems and worries.\par \par \uc2 But, General, thank you very much. Good catching up with you. And thank you again for your incredible service to this country.\par \par \uc2 I always say that because I always mean that. A lot of people appreciate that, no matter where they are in 