 the future, because we're looking for a career job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: A lot of workers told us \uc2 Boeing\uc2  could afford to pay them more, pointing to the golden parachute they gave former CEO \uc2 Dave Calhoun\uc2  as he was pushed out, $33 million.\par \par \uc2 And new CEO \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  is making $22 million in cash and stock. Before the vote, Ortberg practically begged workers not to strike, saying the timing is awful, with the company trying to rebound from multiple crises admittedly brought on by poor management.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's sales are down 15 percent. The company lost $2.2 billion last year and is $60 billion in debt. But workers were unswayed. So now production of seven planes is halted. Four are commercial aircraft, including the important 737 MAX and the 777. Three are military planes, including the hugely important KC-46 refueling tanker.\par \par \uc2 And it all matters to the broader economy, Neil, because, as you know, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is about 1 percent of the GDP with $70 billion in sales. It affects about a million jobs, all told, across the country. The last strike at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was in 2008. That one lasted almost two full months.\par \par \uc2 We just will see how long this one goes -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, and you educated me on this thing earlier, Dan, that this strike is despite the 25 percent pay hike over four years that was apparently exactly what ended the last one back in 2008, right?\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Well, that was -- what I said was 25 percent is exactly what the \uc2 UAW\uc2  got last year from the Big Three after being out on strike...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: ... a strike which organized labor said was a big victory. So \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers turned down the exact same amount that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  was able to get for the workers last year in the Big Three strike.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I got you.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: So they want 40 percent, though, and they think that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  can afford that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I'm wondering. I mean, those are generous terms, a generous deal, if it were to come to pass, and they want more. They must be aware of the company's bad publicity, that this is probably not an ideal moment.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, they do see big gains in the part of labor elsewhere. But isn't \uc2 Boeing\uc2  a bit of a different case?\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Well, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is in a bad situation right now with their debt at junk bond status, basically.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: They have got to rebound from the disaster, the door plug blowout in January, and the congressional hearings that came after that.\par \par \uc2 And they're still under federal investigation for that. They're still rebounding in a sense from the 737 MAX 8 planes that crashed in 2018 and 2019. They're still trying to rebuild confidence in their customers. And so it's a bad time for the company.\par \par \uc2 And Ortberg is going to be very involved in these negotiations. They have already put out a statement that they want to get back to the bargaining table as quickly as possible. They have offered a lot. They offered an extra vacation day. They offered better health care coverage, lower costs on that, so $4,000 a year more to their 401(k) program, a $3,000 signing bonus, essentially, to ratify right away.\par \par \uc2 So they think they have offered everything that they need to, but obviously not, because that thing went down in flames in that vote yesterday, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and overwhelmingly so, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Dan, thanks for bringing us up to speed.\par \par \uc2 Dan Springer on all of that.\par \par \uc2 And we should posit here that the stock, of course, has been buffered by this for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, I will take you to that readout with President Biden and U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Good idea? What do you say to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's threat of war, sir? It's a serious threat.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: All right, you got to be quiet. I'm going to make a statement here, OK?\par \par \uc2 All right, anyway, Mr. Prime Minister, welcome. Welcome back to the White House. I have often said there's no issue of global consequence where the United States and Great Britain can't work together and haven't worked together.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to discuss some of these things right now. We're going to do first Ukraine. I want to thank you for the U.K.'s leadership on this front. The United States is committed to standing with you to help Ukraine as it defends against Russia's onslaught of aggression.\par \par \uc2 I have been clear that Putin will not -- make it clear that Putin will not prevail in this war and the people of Ukraine will prevail.\par \par \uc2 Second, Middle East.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER, BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Today, we will discuss our work to secure a deal in the Middle East to release the remaining hostages that are being held help by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , bring an immediate cease-fire, increase the flow of humanitarian aid to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 And, thirdly, we're going to be talking about the Indo-Pacific. No region is more important to our future. And I'm that, under our AUKUS partnership with Australia and -- with Australia, the last three years, we have made the region more free, open, and secure.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Prime Minister, thank you again for being here. And I look forward to our conversation.\par \par \uc2 STARMER: Mr. President, thank you very much for those words. Thank you for the invitation to be back here just two months after our last meeting here.\par \par \uc2 And it's really important to us, great allies, that special relationship to have this time to talk about the global issues you have just identified, starting, of course, with Ukraine, where I think the next few weeks and months could be crucial and very, very important that we support Ukraine in this vital war of freedom.\par \par \uc2 And so I look forward to discussing that with you. The Middle East, of course, again, vital over the coming weeks and months and elsewhere in the world. I think that, historically, we have shown the strength of our relationship, that we are strategically aligned. We have common cause on these global issues.\par \par \uc2 And, therefore, it's very important us -- for us to have this opportunity to talk them through, not just as a matter of tactics, but also the wider strategy that underpins them. So, thank you for the invitation. It's pretty good to be here again.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What do you say to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's threat of war, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't think much about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, press. Thank you, press. Thank you, press. Thank you, press.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that seemed to go well.\par \par \uc2 I often find, like, when journalists ask questions all at the same time and shout them, you can't make it out. There are some people who have that ability to distinguish who's asking and actually ascertain who's doing the talking. I am not one of those people.\par \par \uc2 So I really can't say what came of that. But we do know that the British prime minister, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , and President Biden are, they say, on the same page when it comes to dealing with Ukraine and obviously these latest threats from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , where he is talking about nuclear weapons and their potential use again.\par \par \uc2 Now, the details of some of those reports on Putin came out. And oil, which had been slip sliding away on concerns the global economy was softening after a brief run-up, that quickly spiked. I think by day's end, we were up a little bit -- just checking myself here -- about 20 cents a barrel, just over $69 a barrel, still relatively soft compared to the $80 or plus so that we were at for a little while, I'm dating myself, more than a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, this is an ongoing worry. But, again, if you were worried about whether the markets are still getting rattled by this, well, they never have been. By and large, stocks are running ahead with it today. We were in record territory for a good chunk of the day, the Dow itself moving up about 297 points, all the major averages ahead.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, and you probably get sick of hearing it, a lot of this has been built on this growing sense of interest rates coming down and that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates, and then, given a couple of week economic numbers that we got as the morning ensued, some people hoping to see a half-point cut on the part of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  next week, September 18, when they wrap up that meeting.\par \par \uc2 It would be the first such reversal in rates in the better part of four years. But the betting in something called the Fed fund's futures markets - - I didn't mean to get wonky here -- but the fact of the matter is, that people trade off of this stuff.\par \par \uc2 And it used to be, a little more than 24 hours ago, about a 15 to 20 percent expectation of anything bigger than a quarter-point cut. The latest move is a more than 50 percent chance of a half-point cut. That could be interpreted a couple of different ways. It might scare the bejesus out a lot of people that the Fed knows something that we don't, or it might just be justified, given some softening, just some softening numbers.\par \par \uc2 We will explore that in great detail tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time on my Saturday show.\par \par \uc2 Right now, here...\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240914ek9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pakistani Charged For Plotting New York Terror Attack; Trump New York Criminal Trial Sentencing Delayed; Trump Mocks Harris Debate Preparations; New Campaign Ads Will Air During Games; Georgia School Shooting Suspect And Father Appear In Court; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Touts Climate Investments And Union Labor; Border Patrol and Law Enforcement Targeting Immigrant Members Of Venezuela Prison Gang Tren De Aragua In U.S.; Republican And Democratic New York City Councilmembers Discuss Issue Of Immigrant Violence; U.S. Economic Report For August Has Jobs Added At 142,000, Unemployment Rate At 4.2 Percent; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Preparing For Upcoming Debate.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, David Spunt, Nate Foy, Aishah Hasnie, Mark Meredith,  Peter Doocy, Steve Harrigan, Bill Melugin, Howard Kurtz \par \uc2 7,895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. That's it for us, everybody. Have a great night and a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hey, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Hi.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think they let me join the gang?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No. You don't want to join this gang.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Because we're going to defeat them. But we're going to be maidens.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Make a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I will see you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Bye.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier, "BREAKING TONIGHT", another judge has agreed to postpone another key legal battle for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  until after the November election. Manhattan Judge Merchan delayed the former president's sentencing in his New York criminal case, citing the unique facts and circumstances.\par \par \uc2 It comes after a different judge officially delayed the president's election interference case in Washington earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking just moments ago, a Pakistani citizen is awaiting extradition to the United States from Canada after authorities allege, he was planning a mass shooting at a Jewish Center in New York City around October 7th, the one-year anniversary of the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack in Israel.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt here with what we know about this breaking story at this hour. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 He is 20 years old. His name is Mohammad Shazeb Khan. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  was tracking his movements before he was arrested two days ago in Canada. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  led the international operation and discovered that Khan planned to travel to New York, specifically Brooklyn, to carry out a mass shooting at an unnamed Jewish Center in Brooklyn around October 7th.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say Khan began posting on social media and communicated with others on an encrypted messaging app in November of last year. That's when authorities say he pledged his support for \uc2 ISIS\uc2  and distributed \uc2 ISIS\uc2  propaganda.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , in a statement, said, late this afternoon, "The \uc2 FBI\uc2  will continue to work closely with our partners to investigate and hold those accountable, who seek to commit violence in the name of \uc2 ISIS\uc2 , or other terrorist organizations. Fighting terrorism remains the \uc2 FBI\uc2  top priority."\par \par \uc2 Once the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Bret, learned he had imminent plans to enter the United States, and he spoke to undercover \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, he was picked up 12 miles approximately from the U.S.-Canada border, after authorities say he used three separate cars to travel across Canada.\par \par \uc2 He is in Canada right now, awaiting extradition to the United States. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we've just had that CPB -- Custom Borders Protection about all the different countries, people from those various companies, coming -- countries, coming over the northern border, and this fits that bill. David, we'll follow this story. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: We will. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, to our other breaking story, we have several new legal developments tonight in cases involving former President Trump. Correspondent Nate Foy is outside the U.S. Court of Appeals in New York. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The sentencing in former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Manhattan criminal trial will now happen on November 26th. That's of course, after the presidential election, and two weeks before the sentencing, Judge Juan Merchan, will decide on whether or not he's going to throw the conviction out on the grounds of presidential immunity after that Supreme Court decision back in July.\par \par \uc2 Trump is reacting to the sentencing delay on Truth Social today, posting, "The Manhattan D.A. witch hunt has been postponed because everyone realizes that there was no case, I did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 Judge Merchan wrote in his decision that he doesn't want the jury's verdict to be "diluted" by the enormity of the upcoming presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Merchan also said he wants to avoid any appearance of election interference. The Manhattan D.A.'s Office didn't oppose the delay, telling Fox News "A jury of 12 New Yorkers swiftly and unanimously convicted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of 34 felony counts. The Manhattan D.A.'s Office stands ready for sentencing on the new date set by the court."\par \par \uc2 The jury convicted Trump of falsifying business records for payments made to his former lawyer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . Cohen facilitated payments to adult film actress Stormy Daniels. Here is Trump talking about the case earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I had nothing to do with it. That was done by an attorney, a sleazebag named \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . And he did the whole thing. And you're allowed to have reliance on attorney. It's called reliance on attorney.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Bret, before that, Trump appeared in court to appeal the verdict from last year that he sexually abused and defamed writer E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 Trump's lawyers argue the court allowed inadmissible evidence, including testimony from other women and the inclusion of the Access Hollywood tape.\par \par \uc2 Now, lawyers for Trump and Carroll made their cases before a three-judge panel. A decision will come at a later date. But today, Trump took issue with the panel itself, noting that all three judges were appointed by Democrats. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy outside the Court of Appeals in New York. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Another legal battle in North Carolina has also fallen in former President Trump's favor. The state was scheduled to be the first state to send out mail ballots today. But a legal effort seeking to remove \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 's name has the ballots now on back order to be reprinted.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, former President Trump wrapped up remarks in North Carolina. Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is there with details on today's legal wins for the Trump campaign. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you and the former president addressed it all tonight from the podium behind me as he picked up the endorsement from the FOP.\par \par \uc2 He got a big standing ovation tonight as he announced that he supports the death penalty for anyone who kills a police officer. He also slammed liberal D.A.'s and his opponent. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: As we gather today, American cities, suburbs, and towns are totally under siege. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the communists left have unleashed a brutal plague of bloodshed, crime, chaos, misery and death upon our land.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The Harris campaign is reacting tonight by releasing a number of statements from law enforcement officers, like former Capitol Police Officer Harry Dunn, saying that Trump is not an ally to police.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Trump also commented on the upcoming debate making fun of Harris' debate prep.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She's locked herself in a room, and they have one problem. You know what the problem is? They have MAGA people outside screaming, we love Trump. We love Trump. We love Trump, and they can't focus.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And finally, Trump gave his first reaction to a North Carolina State appeals court stopping the counties here from sending out mail-in ballots with RFK Jr.'s name on them, at least until his appeal is resolved.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That was a big thing. It means that all of those who love Bobby and there's a lot of them and all that he stands for, especially regarding the health and well-being of us can vote. They vote for me now. So, all of the Bobby people are going to vote for me, and I appreciate the decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, today, RFK Jr. is telling all of his supporters to just vote for Trump no matter what state they live in, red, or blue, just to avoid any confusion as he continues to try to get off the ballots in several battleground states. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie in Charlotte, North Carolina. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Former Republican Vice President \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  says, like his daughter, former Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , he too will vote for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for president.\par \par \uc2 In a statement this afternoon, he said, in part, "In our nation's 248-year history, there has never been an individual who is a greater threat to our republic than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is why I will be casting my vote for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The vice president, meantime, preparing for Tuesday's debate in Pittsburgh, as members of her campaign team make appearances on her behalf. They argue the vice president is the underdog, despite touting major fundraising success.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith reports from Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, Vice President Harris remains hunkered down in Pittsburgh, preparing for her first and potentially only debate with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 In a radio interview, Harris says voters are tired of her opponent.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And we need to turn the page on this Trump era. You know, this person who has talked about how immigrants poison the blood of America. This person who seeks to divide our nation.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): But the Harris campaign claims it's still Trump who has the upper hand after spending the last eight years in the presidential spotlight.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, SPOKESPERSON, SENIOR NATIONAL SPOKESMAN, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN: She is going to explain to the country on Tuesday night what the difference in this election is in the choice between President Trump and her and who has the right vision for the future.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): As the Harris campaign lowers debate expectations, it's bragging about new fundraising data, showing it raised $361 million in August, and now holds a staggering $400 million cash on hand, a $100 million advantage over Trump.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Party chairman, writing today, "One thing has been clear, voters are excited and motivated to send her to the Oval Office."\par \par \uc2 Less than a week until the debate, the Harris web site, still does not list any platform specifics, but her campaign tells Axios, the V.P. has changed her mind on another environmental issue. First, it was fracking, which she once vowed the outlaw. Now, team Harris says she no longer wants to ban plastic straws, despite saying this in 2019.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you ban plastic straws?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think we should. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Some voters think Harris is only pivoting for popularity.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And every Democrat gives back and forth. Once they get back in office, they contemplate --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She is going to go back to decide it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): While the vice president is expected to lay low this weekend between now and the debate, football fans are going to be seeing plenty over. The campaign launching new ads targeting black voters that is set to air on college football and \uc2 NFL\uc2  games, Bret, with millions of people expected to be tuned in. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith, live in Pittsburgh. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This was the obvious answer, but Judge Merchan has repeatedly ruled against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, I think we were all kind of holding our breath.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, here we are within 40 odd days of the election, and sentencing the defendant, where he would get a stay regardless of what the sentence is, doesn't make a whole lot of sense.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And the DOJ is behind everything. Every one of these cases. It's political interference.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's get some analysis now on former President Trump's legal cases from Fox News, chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon. Bream.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, good afternoon. And this is quite something, this cascading of judges that essentially are pushing back these decisions. And this one, for some people, was a surprise.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Yes, because sitting through the weeks, Bret, of that trial there in New York, and Judge Merchan consistently making decisions that were not positive to the Trump legal team or to the former president. We really just didn't know what he would do with the sentencing.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is also the school of thought that had he sentenced the former president to something significant on September 18th, it would have actually been politically beneficial to the Trump campaign, they would have been able to point to that again, as you heard the president saying, that all of this has been a political persecution or prosecution.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is now this sentencing is going to come after November, if at all, Judge Mershon still has to make a decision on whether the Supreme Court's immunity decision will have an impact on that New York case.\par \par \uc2 Listen, if he comes out November 12 and says, I don't think the immunity case from Supreme Court, in any way, you know, takes this off the table for President Trump, you got to know the Trump team is almost going to immediately appeal that, and maybe we don't get to that sentencing on November 26th after all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And that's very possible, right? I mean, that immunity throws in a lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, based on the way, again, the Judge Merchan has handled this case, I don't expect a favorable decision for the Trump team on November 12th when it comes to immunity.\par \par \uc2 They could separate parts of the case, as Jack Smith has done in the federal case here, but they are going to argue that there's still enough of a case there, prosecutors that it can survive that scrutiny of the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, expect the Trump to -- team to immediately appeal that decision, and I would expect that appellate court then to take the jurisdiction away from Judge Merchan, and maybe he doesn't get to make that decision on the week of Thanksgiving about sentencing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. So, for those following at home without a whiteboard of all the legal cases. That essentially clears the deck through the election. Does it not?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It does. Because there's almost nothing that could possibly get to trial before 2025 or to a sentencing decision before the election. You know, we heard Judge Chutkan in the federal January 6th case here that Jack Smith case against President Trump in the courtroom yesterday, saying, hey, listen the election calendar, that's not any of my problem, not my purview, but pushing most things into 2025.\par \par \uc2 What could happen, though, is Jack Smith could be making some arguments, setting up some proof he has, he says, of the President's wrongdoing. So, that could trickle out into the public if she doesn't censor or, you know, give any privilege to those documents, that could come out in October before the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And the interesting part is that the former president is saying that this is all a witch hunt and that it's banana Republican kind of stuff. They are also going to file to remove things from federal court, and that's still pending.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is. And so, there are all kinds of different appeals. Today, the president was in court over one of the E. Jean Carroll cases. And remember, there are two of them that added up to damages of more than $85 million, both of those cases are on appeal.\par \par \uc2 That Mar-a-Lago decision, Judge Cannon down there who threw out the entire documents case, saying there was not a proper appointment of Jack Smith as special counsel. That case is on appeal to the 11th Circuit.\par \par \uc2 Trump team may or may not win there. So, there are all kinds of things pending Georgia and other places as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Shannon, as always, thank you. Who is on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", quickly?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are going to have senators Murphy and Cornyn, both sides of the aisle represented.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, fair and balanced.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you then.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the school shooting in Georgia as we learn more about the teen suspect's background.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX5 in Las Vegas, where a man who went viral for leaping to attack a judge, has pleaded guilty to attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 A judge he attacked testified before the plea was issued, saying her attacker knocked her almost through the wall.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Navajo dam in New Mexico. One of the big stories there from our affiliate, KRQE. The empty Starliner capsule makes its return to Earth. It's expected to land in New Mexico this evening. The two astronauts it carried to the International Space Station will remain in orbit until February, and the return on a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  rocket.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're learning more about the 14-year old Georgia school shooting suspect's troubled home life. Public records reveal warning signs that could have prevented Wednesday's tragedy. Colt Gray and his father, Colin, appeared in court today in back to back hearings. Correspondent Steve Harrigan reports from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're charged with four counts of felony murder.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The suspect in the deadliest school shooting in Georgia's history, making his first court appearance today, 14-year old Colt Gray will be tried as an adult and now faces life in prison after police say he shot and killed two fellow students and two teachers at Apalachee High.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Colin Gray?\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Minutes later, the suspect's father appeared in the same courtroom. Law enforcement arrested Colin Gray, claiming he provided the AR style rifle used in the massacre. Crying and shaking in court, the father is charged with felony murder, involuntary manslaughter, and cruelty to children.\par \par \uc2 BRAD SMITH, PIEDMONT JUDICIAL CIRCUIT D.A.: I would hope that prosecutors would use every arsenal, or every tool in their arsenal to hold people accountable for crimes.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Family members of those killed were also in the courtroom. Not present, the suspect's mother, who lived apart and has a long arrest record for drugs, theft and family violence. She would reportedly lock her children out of the house.\par \par \uc2 More than one year ago, local law enforcement interviewed both father and son after the son was accused of making an online threat after he says he was bullied at school.\par \par \uc2 COLIN GRAY, FATHER OF COLT GRAY: He knows the seriousness of weapons and what they can do and how to use them and not use them. It's kind of a little bit of a shock.\par \par \uc2 So, in whatever you all are telling him, please instill in him that what -- if this is whatever or wherever this is coming from is no joke. Like, it's no joke.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Seven months after that, the father allegedly bought his son a rifle for Christmas. The district attorney acknowledged charging the parent is relatively new territory.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This is an attack on an entire community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The father faces up to 180 years in prison, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was in Michigan today where he signed an executive order promoting high quality American jobs. It comes amid recent layoffs in the state's key industry and a sluggish jobs report.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy reports from the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What a great way to cap off a Labor Day week.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): But in the car capital of the world, the president's policies may be a liability for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When I think climate, I think jobs. Good paying union jobs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the Ram 1500 pickup truck maker that benefited from more than 500 million in Energy Department grants to build E.V.s and is turning a profit, announced last month 2,000 layoffs in Michigan. And the company has long warned some of those jobs could be moved to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT NARDELLI, FORMER CEO, CHRYSLER: E.V.s will happen. But it has to be an evolution, i.e. hybrid versus a revolution pure E.V.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A weaker than expected 142,000 jobs were created in August, according to jobs numbers released today.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They just came out and their basic disaster.\par \par \uc2 And the labor secretary is admitting the 700,000 manufacturing jobs the administration often touts come with a big asterisk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But a lot of those manufacturing jobs were added back. They were lost in the pandemic and then added back and it's been 147,000 jobs that have been created in the manufacturing sectors.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, 147,000 jobs is 147,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And for President Biden, a good economy is critical to his legacy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Kamala are so proud of our record. The greatest job creation record of any single presidential term in American history, because of you.\par \par \uc2 They told us we couldn't do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Nothing has changed with his stump speech since he was giving it and tanking in the polls and then dropped out.\par \par \uc2 But still, the Harris campaign has had him out there as their headliner for the last two days. For the next two, he will be in Delaware, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down today over that lackluster jobs report. The Dow lost 410, the S&P 500 fell 95. The NASDAQ dropped 437.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost almost three percentage points. The S&P 500 dropped 4-1/4. The NASDAQ had its worst week since January 2022, falling more than 5-3/4.\par \par \uc2 Up next, suspected members of a Venezuelan street gang come to America through the southern border. Then, one Republican and one Democrat, both from New York City, come together to try to find common ground on the city's migrant crisis.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel on the latest from the campaign trail, a debate preview, plus winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Law enforcement officials are growing very concerned over the increasing footprint of a Venezuelan gang on U.S. soil. Border officials say targeting members of the Tren de Aragua gang is now a priority. Correspondent Bill Melugin reports on some of the latest incidents involving suspected gang members.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The tentacles of the Venezuelan prison gang Tren de Aragua are reaching further across the United States. In Aurora, Colorado, police announced the arrest of four known or suspected Tren de Aragua gang members in connection with the shooting in July. ICE confirms to FOX News all four of the men are Venezuelan illegal immigrants who were caught and released at the Texas border by the Biden administration around the same time the White House was saying this --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a secure border.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: In a completely separate event in Aurora, suspected Tren de Aragua gang members armed with guns were caught on video trying to break a door down in an apartment complex they had reportedly taken over, an incident former President Trump brought up today.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If you look at Aurora, Colorado, they are taking over the place. They took over buildings. And this is just the beginning. You haven't seen anything yet.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: This gang also now popping up in Dallas where police tell FOX, in part, "We have had gang activity in the north Dallas area linked to the Tren de Aragua gang from Venezuela. Our department is collaborating with other agencies to address possible crimes linked to this and other gangs in our city.\par \par \uc2 Tren de Aragua has already established presences in New York City and Chicago and has been tied to violent crimes in those cities, including the shooting of two \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers in June.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unfortunately, every state is indeed now a border state. And we've got stop it because these gangs are coming to a city near you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, Border Patrol agents have repeatedly told us when they encounter these Venezuelan men at the southern border, they have really no way of vetting them most of the time. Venezuela simply does not cooperate with the United States and share law enforcement records. And as a result of that, a large majority of these Venezuelan illegal immigrants end up just getting released into the country. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Bill, we have been doing this story about California and the possible plan to help illegal immigrants there purchase homes. Some news about that?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, so California Democrats out here had been pushing a bill that would have allowed illegal immigrants to participate in this public lottery loan program that would have given them up to $150,000 in taxpayer funded assistance, down payment assistance, to help them buy a elm who. Well, just a few hours ago, California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he vetoed that bill, not because of the merits of the bill but because of what he describes as funding issues and the impact it would have had on California's state budget. Send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Bill Melugin live in L.A. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO, (D) FORMER NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: I said on this issue of how police work with immigrants, please talk to the police chiefs of this country. God forbid, they have been a victim of crime or they're a witness to a crime. Immigrant New Yorkers documented and undocumented feel safe going to the police and working with them, and we will never let that change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Then New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  defending sanctuary cities. Now there is a problem with that, and both sides of the aisle are coming together.\par \par \uc2 It's time for our "Common Ground" segment. Joining us tonight, New York City Councilman, Republican Joe Borelli, Democrat Kalman Yeger. Councilmen, thank you very much for being here. I want to talk to you about the issue and as you see it. The headlines recently have been all over the place, but "New York Post," "Migrants flooding New York City's justice system, making up 75 percent of arrests in midtown. The elites opened the doors to migrants and chaos." And "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", a different take, "Migrant crime wave not supported by data despite high profile cases."\par \par \uc2 Councilman Yeger, how do you see this? Like, what's the ground truth about New York City and dealing with this on all levels?\par \par \uc2 KALMAN YEGER, (D) NEW \uc2 YORK CITY COUNCIL\uc2 : Well, anybody who says that every single person who comes over the border lawfully or unlawfully is here to commit crimes is simply wrong. But at the same time, it's naive and false to say nobody who comes here is committing crimes. And the reality is when a city like New York, with the numbers at approximately, as of two months ago, over 205,000 migrants have come to New York City in the last two years, that is -- it's simply not believable to say that our crime numbers are not seeing the result of that policy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Council Borelli, you formed this commonsense caucus, and you have a number of proposals here together, allowing the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  to communicate with ICE, enable ICE to collect suspects in custody, ballot measure to repeal restrictions on deportation, detention, and removal. Obviously, that's not in the purview of the New York City police. But what do you see coming together on this issue you'll be able to get done?\par \par \uc2 JOE BORELLI, (R) NEW \uc2 YORK CITY COUNCIL\uc2 : Look, we believe we have public support. We believe that New Yorkers are frustrated. They are seeing rising crime numbers where there are high concentrations of migrants. Whether you agree with the policy or not, if you are a resident of even the most progressive neighborhoods in New York, you are noticing that there is more crime.\par \par \uc2 And not just the crime aspect, but also the money aspect. This year it's going to cost us $5 billion to house and care for this population. That would make our migrant spending the ninth most expensive city budget in the United States. We would be about the same as the city of Dallas. In other words, New York can just pay Dallas' budget for what we are paying for this migrant crisis.\par \par \uc2 And I think New Yorkers have finally said enough is enough. We are not getting any value out of this. It's not playing out as the Democrats have said that this would somehow enhance New York City. And unfortunately, until we see a change at the top, I don't think anything is going to change at the southern border to alleviate those coming in.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting, Councilman, you mentioned Dallas. The Dallas police have really been expressing themselves, how tough it is, and they have seen some violent crime in these migrant communities. Just take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAIME CASTRO, DALLAS POLICE ASSOCIATION: The days of these simple 911 calls, you know, loud music, you know, a family disturbance -- those days are gone. We are responding to acts of war.\par \par \uc2 As a nation, we really need to sit down and think what are we asking of our officers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Councilman Yeger, how much can you get done on a city level? How much do you think Democrats are listening to what your commonsense cause proposals are?\par \par \uc2 YEGER: Well, to put this in perspective, the number of migrants who have come into New York City exceeds the number of residents of a council district that I represent and that Joe represents. Each of our districts have about 170,000 people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 YEGER: So we have already taken in more than the entire New York City council district into our city the last two years. It's just simply not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 And when we talk about this $5 billion number, think about what New York City can do with $5 billion. We just adopted a budget three months ago, and the biggest fight on the table was whether or not we can keep our libraries open. That's the battle. Whether or not we can afford to keep our cops on the street, to hire more cops for retiring cops, because they are leaving the job, to hire firefighters, to hire teachers. The services that are required by the migrants who are coming to New York City are immense. They are costing us enormous amounts of money. We just simply do not have the resources to keep on footing this bill.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Councilman Yeger, do you think this will be a major issue in the national election?\par \par \uc2 YEGER: I don't see how it's not. This is a national problem. And just because a city like New York, a city like Chicago, a city like Dallas are the -- are the poster child and at the forefront of seeing the problem, this is an absolute national problem that is foisted upon cities like ours where people believe that they can come here, they are going to get a room at the Roosevelt hotel. They are going to get a phone, and they all seem to have motorcycles, and they seem to be doing just fine. And this is a policy we don't -- we do not in the city of New York control the borders. All we can control is what's going to happen to people once they show up in our city.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask one more thing, quickly, Councilman Borelli. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says there is a flurry of inquiries about the mayor, "The remarkable avalanche of investigations and raids struck at the heart of the mayor's circle is no ordinary distraction." It comes as all of these things are happening. Can the mayor do his job in this current environment?\par \par \uc2 BORELLI: It's going to be tough. In the past, these folks who have been investigated have been sort of the second tier of individuals. Now with this next round right now, this is the inner sanctum of his world. So it's going to be increasingly difficult for him to do that. But we'll see what he decides to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Councilmen, we really appreciate your time. Thanks for coming on "Common Ground."\par \par \uc2 YEGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Trump's sentencing delay, the new jobs numbers, the campaign trail, and Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, an American woman has been shot and killed in the West Bank. Witnesses in the Palestinian media say Israeli troops shot the 26-year-old protesting against settlement expansion. No word officially from the Israelis. It comes as Israeli forces appear to withdraw from three refugee camps in the West Bank. The more than weeklong military operation left dozens dead there.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is calling for the west to loosen weapons use restrictions. He made the case to U.S. and allied leaders in Germany as they gathered to coordinate weapons aid. Kyiv has called for capabilities that would allow it to target Russian air bases far from the border.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rio de Janeiro, courtesy of Earth Cam. One of the big stories there tonight, the \uc2 NFL\uc2  plays its first game in South America. The \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  will take on the \uc2 Green Bay Packers\uc2  in Sao Paulo, Corinthians Arena. The Eagles are a designated home team there. They wear the same colors as the arena soccer club. The Corinthians official says only visiting teams, in this case the Packers, are allowed to wear green. Good thing. They both have green.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX): One of the people described a seven-year-old boy who got basically beat up and his sister molested because they opened the door and these gangs went in, emptied all of the apartment. These are migrants from Columbia that were being attacked by these gang migrants from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE JURINSKY, AURORA, COLORADO, CITY COUNCIL: I wanted this to come out, how people were living, what they were being exposed to, and the shear fear that they were under. The bottom line is this isn't just an \uc2 Aurora\uc2  issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aurora, Colorado, and that Venezuelan gang story, you heard from Bill Melugin. Obviously, it's going to be a major factor. You heard from the councilman in New York City, the immigration and the border.\par \par \uc2 As you look at the Real Clear Politics average of polls in the swing states, the battleground states, there you see very tight races in a number of different states, and you see where the breakdown is right now. That's the average of recent polls.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," and "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers.\par \par \uc2 Byron, that \uc2 Aurora\uc2  story really boggles my mind, that that can happen, would happen. It adds to a lot of individual stories around the country, and you just heard from the New York City councilmen how the city is being bombarded. That becomes, if it's not, a really hot issue for the election.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": It plays precisely into former President Trump's hands. I mean, for Republicans, immigration, specifically the border, is probably the most important issue in this election, and for the electorate as a whole it's maybe the second most important issue only after inflation.\par \par \uc2 And even though the Biden administration and Vice President Harris has tried to say, oh, the Republicans and Trump stopped this bill that could have solved everything, the fact is they waited for three years to even come up with that. And Trump has a very, very strong case which, if he can do it right, is what he will be doing Tuesday night at the debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Most people say the economy will drive it. We had jobs numbers out, so-so jobs numbers. Jobs added 142,000, unemployment rate now at 4.2 percent. Vice President Harris was pressed on the economy from Univision. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My plans will actually reduce the deficit by $1 trillion, as compared to my opponent who could keep, because he gives tax breaks to the richest people, contributing to the deficit and not helping the middle class.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL BABY, UNIVISION: Still people believe that he can handle the economy better. What would you say to them that are listening to you right now?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, you should look at what I have accomplished thus far.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So Francesca, that's the issue, what she has accomplished thus far as vice president for three-and-a-half years and where the economy is and how people feel about it. That's the uphill battle, really.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": And in terms of her economic plan, a lot of what she has laid out is either a continuation of the plans of what President Biden has put forward, or in some areas, as we saw this past week, she has a difference of opinion from him on them, including on capital gains. But, indeed, she still wants to increase taxes on the wealthy to pay for her plans, and a lot of the credits that she has put forward are things that she has really doubled down on from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 But notably she is not using the word "Bidenomics," and when she was asked about that in the CNN interview sidestepped it because she has tried to at least shift away from that wording, shift away from maybe negative feelings that Americans have about the economy during the current administration as she tries to chart a positive, forward-looking vision.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Big moment Tuesday for that debate, Howie. There is a lot of anticipation for it. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm going into very hostile territory shortly on a debate with ABC. I think they are the worst. They are the nastiest. The reason I'm doing it is because that's the only one that she would do it with.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She knows millions of people are going to be watching. She understands this is a real moment in this debate, in this campaign to really have a discussion with the American people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is a skilled debater. Don't underestimate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "Axios" has this piece, Howie. It says "How Harris dodges scrutiny. With her debate with former President Trump coming up Tuesday, Harris has big questions to answer in two areas that go to the heart of running America. One, why did President Biden's top advisers routinely leak word they found her performance as vice president disappointing or episodically problematic? And, two, how did her views change in five years from liberal to centrist on healthcare, immigration, and energy? Why should voters believe her new views are the ones she would stick with inside the White House?" Those are the key points, and that debate couldn't be bigger.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes, it's absolutely make-or-break. I see the pressure as being on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  because everybody on the planet knows what they think about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Maybe there's 10 people who haven't made up their mind. And if she can just hold her own, I think it's a win for her because she shows -- she closes the stature gap and shows she can go toe to toe with the former president.\par \par \uc2 However, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can rattle her and tie the Biden record around her neck, then I think he might come out ahead. But again, I think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the one who has to show that she can do it, because Trump has debated so many times, and she has only done the CNN interview.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, obviously she debated \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in the vice presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes. I meant recently.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You'll see everything right here on FOX News Channel on Tuesday night. We'll have a full team covering it.\par \par \uc2 Let's go Winners and Losers, winner first, then loser. Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, my winner is U.S. tennis. It has been 18 years -- I didn't know that -- 18 years since an American man played in the U.S. Open final. Now there is going to be one. It's going to be either Taylor Fritz or Frances Tiafoe, they're going to have a shot at the championship.\par \par \uc2 My loser is network late night TV. NBC announced that the once legendary "Tonight Show" is going to be cut to four days a week, matching the other shows. It is a slowly dying form.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Francesca, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: OK, my winner was Beetlejuice the movie. I know, hot take here. I enjoyed it. I thought it was delightful.\par \par \uc2 My loser was autumn because, guys, I just don't like cold weather. I enjoy summer. I want it to stay around forever.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're not a pumpkin spice person? OK.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: I just like the warm weather.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Howie, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: My winner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . He suggests a waste commission under Trump, and guess what? He is going to chair the thing if, in fact, Trump wins the election. My loser are the \uc2 NFL\uc2  kickoff returners. You won't be seeing any more of those 80-yard exciting returns because of new rules that deflates the whole thing because of safety and concussion concerns.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I have a winner. I usually don't, but I have a winner, and that is Tom Brady, because he joins the broadcast booth this weekend with FOX, and after seven Super Bowl rings and all those years in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , we get to hear how he calls the plays. I'm looking forward to seeing that this weekend.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know that sound. That means it's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Barrow County sheriff's office received alerts about reports of an active shooter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My friend, my neighbor, she was going to get shot, but Miss Irimie stood up and took the bullet for her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Finally my sweet boy, finally, finally, finally, finally you are free.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  killed the hostages.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: The pressure internationally must be directed at these killers, at \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What is your reaction to the six hostages being found dead in Gaza?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D) MINNESOTA: Thanks, everybody.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You've got to vote for me. You've got to vote for me.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Even if you don't like me.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let's not pay too much attention to the polls because we are running as the underdog.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As long as adversaries keep trying to influence and interfere in our society, we're going to keep calling it out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Russia today sent this statement to FOX News, "Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, 2016 wants its cliches back."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm sure that was much funnier in the original Russian.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: You better thank a union member for paid leave. You better thank a union member for vacation times.\par \par \uc2 You better thank unions for five-day workweek.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Since when does the vice president have what sounds like a southern accent?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I have no idea what you are talking about.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The 105th \uc2 NFL\uc2  season is underway.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're all looking forward to it. Let's go, let's play.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's go. I hear you.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, former Commerce Secretary \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2  on the state of the economy and his new book. Plus, this weekend, join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests are Texas Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  and Connecticut Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Next week we have a busy week. We'll be up in New York for most of the week with the debate coverage, full coverage on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240907ek9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Israel And Hezbollah Exchange Strikes; Interview With Retired Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg; RFK Jr. Suspends Presidential Campaign, Endorses Trump; Interview With \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 ; Harris Proposes $25k Down Payment Assistance For First-Time Homebuyers; Growing Calls for Harris to Talk to Media; Patriotism A Major Theme At DNC; Harris-Walz Momentum Out Of Convention; Impact Of Abortion Policy Debate On Election; Investigations into Trump Assassination; Israel and Hezbollah Exchange Strikes; U.S. to Bolster Forces in the Middle East; Update on the Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Strikes\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Keith Kellogg, Lucas Tomlinson, Mollie Hemingway,  Richard Fowler, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 10,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Israel launches what it calls a preemptive strike on Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Lebanon, sparking fears of an all-out regional war in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Israel says it eliminated thousands of rockets, but \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  counters it still managed to launch hundreds of warheads and drones to avenge the killing of one of its top commanders last month. We'll take you live to Israel.\par \par \uc2 Then, shake up in the final weeks of the race as \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  suspends his long shot campaign and throws his support behind former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after a long conversation with him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (I), SUSPENDED 2024 PRESIDENTIAL RUN: We talked about -- not about the things that separate us because we don't agree on everything, but on the values and the issues that bind us together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Could RFK Jr.'s supporters make the key difference for Trump in battleground states where the race is neck and neck against Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? We'll talk exclusively with Kennedy in his first interview since his big announcements.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Trump and Harris battle over two of the biggest issues for voters.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Every day brings another story about innocent Americans being tortured, raped, murdered and massacred by illegal aliens that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has set free in our country.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He doesn't actually fight for the middle class. Instead, he fights for himself and his billionaire friends.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we learned this week that the U.S. economy added nearly a million fewer jobs than previously reported. We'll get reaction from Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  of Colorado.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday panel is here to give us their take on the next phase in the race for the White House.\par \par \uc2 All this week on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin in the Middle East where the \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  says 100 of its fighter jets hit more than 40 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  launch areas in a pre-dawn strike today, ahead of what it called an extensive attack planned by Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to FOX News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Israel launched preemptive strikes against the \uc2 Lebanese militant group Hezbollah\uc2 . The Israeli military says \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was prepared to launch hundreds of rockets and missiles toward central and northern Israel when the decision was made to strike first. A senior Israeli defense official described the strikes as precise and meant to remove imminent threats as large explosions rock southern Lebanon, the Iran back group began attacking northern Israel. Sirens sounded in communities as deep as 20 miles from the border as Israel's missile defense system worked to intercept the incoming fire.\par \par \uc2 We are learning more this morning about the preemptive Israeli strikes. The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  say more than 100 fighter jets took part in the air strikes and eliminated thousands of targets.\par \par \uc2 Initially, Israel's international airport was closed but has since reopened. Israel has now entered a special security situation for the next 48 hours. Now, despite the heightened tension along the northern border, today, ceasefire talks are set to resume in Cairo, Egypt. Both parties looking to work out the details of a possible agreement to end the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Just last hour, a senior Israeli defense official telling FOX News they are hopeful still about the possibility of a deal -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Trey Yingst, we are going to check in with you later on in the hour. Thank you very much\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg.\par \par \uc2 General, welcome back. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what we've seen over the last 24 hours? There's some say it's provocative. Others say it's a real push back to shut down what could have happened.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET), FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER TO TRUMP, PENCE: Yeah, Shannon. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 I thought it was a great move. Look, the preemptive strike was very, very important because if they had launched the strike -- they the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  -- into -- in Israel with thousands of missiles and they were talking thousands, what would have happened with the loss of lives of Israelis and also the facilities?\par \par \uc2 It would have pushed the Israelis into a doctrine they call Dahiya, and what Dahiya is called disproportionate response.\par \par \uc2 And you -- I think what they did, they prevented and the next or the third Israeli-Lebanese war, and that is smart. And because of that, I think it's kind of reset the battlefield there.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- it's important they did it. I think they did it quite well. The attacks will continue. They're just diminishing the ability of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to attack.\par \par \uc2 You know I have to go back when you think about what what's happened there is really the United States hasn't played a major role there in kind of de- escalating the situation because President Biden hasn't done a lot in talking to Netanyahu. Vice President Harris hasn't done very much. I mean she actually stiffed Netanyahu when he came here into the joint session of Congress when he spoke. And you've got Blinken going over there on his ninth trip no ceasefire. And you got the National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan headed to China instead of going to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know CQ Brown's going to be there, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, but you kind of got to focus in on what's going to happen there and prevent something happening in into the future. And they haven't done it.\par \par \uc2 But the Israelis have done it. So the Israelis have done actually is reset deterrence in the region and I think it's smart. And I think it's put \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  on their -- on their back foot, and it's clearly put the Iranians on the back foot, that they haven't done anything at all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -- so we do have assets in the region. We beefed up our presence.\par \par \uc2 This administration keeps publicly saying Israel has every right to defend itself and we will back them in that, but where do we go from here? Because as you mentioned, we've still got the threat out there looming from Iran saying we're going to take action when we decide. You've got \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And never mind what's going on in Gaza -- I mean, Israel is stretched in a lot of different positions.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yeah, the problem you get is if something like this extends which it's done, it makes it harder to put back in the box to make it go -- put it in into a situation which is sustainable and peace in the region. And that is because technically (ph) we kind of backed away and we said, well, there's pox on both sides of the house.\par \par \uc2 Wrong. The way you handle that is you tell the entire region, you tell the world, we are behind the Israelis, full stop. There's a good side and a bad side and we need to do it.\par \par \uc2 Because we haven't done it, we've created a seam, a gap, and adversaries look for seams and gaps and they've basically done that.\par \par \uc2 And so, they say, okay, we can attack because the United States is not going to come back hard. And I think how you have to reset the fight, you have to tell everybody in the region who we're for and who we're not for and we really haven't done that.\par \par \uc2 So it's going to take a bit to put it back in the box because \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is going to keep attacking, Iran's going to keep supporting the attackers in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fight is virtually over. I think Israelis has kind of solved that problem. Look, when the leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has said, I want -- if we have peace discussions and we have a ceasefire, I want to make sure that I'm not a target -- Sinwar.\par \par \uc2 Nope, you started that fight. You're -- you know, you're at the risk -- at risk right now, but I think that fight is virtually over now. They -- Israelis can pivot to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we know those ceasefire negotiations will continue in the coming days in Egypt, primarily.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you. Always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Let's get reaction to all of this from the White House where we find FOX News correspondent Lucas Tomlinson.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Officials say national security adviser Jake Sullivan kept President Biden updated throughout the night during the attack and \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , President Biden's defense secretary, spoke to his Israeli counterpart, offering continued support of those two aircraft carrier strike groups now on station in the region.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  says this is not the Democratic Party of his uncle and father.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: It have become the party of war, censorship, corruption, big pharma, big tech, big ag and big money.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): After endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for president, the DNC responded: Like RFK Jr. Donald Trump is at a low point and acting out of desperation, embracing RFK Jr. now when he has nothing to offer but months of disqualifying revelations is not a decision a campaign makes when they're acting from a position of strength.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says the economy is strong.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Record 16 million new jobs.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Turns out that's about 1 million jobs too many, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  in a revised report that says there were $818,000 fewer jobs than previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 One number not in dispute, grocery prices up 20 percent since Biden and Harris came into office.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: Should she accept any blame for that?\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: I -- no -- I think overwhelmingly what people are seeing is that the United States got through the recovery with greater jobs, greater growth.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: With the Democrats gathered in Chicago, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made another visit to the border, in the swing state of Arizona, flanked by members of the border patrol and families who have lost loved ones to illegal aliens, including the mother of a -year-old girl strangled to death in Houston.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We had a border czar who was -- the border czar, she loved the title but she didn't want to do the work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: RFK Jr. also said if the system was honest, he would have won this election -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy\uc2 , Jr.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (I), SUSPENDED 2024 PRESIDENTIAL RUN: Shannon, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So let's get this out of the way up front. You and President Trump not always fans of each other. He posted on social media earlier this year: He's one of the most liberal lunatics ever to run for office. A phony radical left fool whose poll numbers are terrible and getting worse.\par \par \uc2 "The New Yorker" said just a couple of weeks ago this: In a recent text exchange, Kennedy told one person that Trump was a terrible human being, the worst president ever and barely human. He is probably a sociopath.\par \par \uc2 And I haven't read your text so you can speak to that\par \par \uc2 But how did you get from that position to Friday night?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, you know, I -- it became clear to me that I did not have a path to victory. Sixteen months of censorship, of not being able to get on any network really except for FOX, and I had -- in fact, when -- when \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  ran in the 10 months that he ran, he had 34 appearances on the networks. I had two appearances in months.\par \par \uc2 I was blocked out the networks. I was blocked out from the debate. I had no path to victory.\par \par \uc2 President Trump had been reaching out to me and I talked to him a few hours after the assassination attempt and we had a long conversation by phone. I then had two extensive meetings and there were issues -- the broad issues that were most important to me the ones that brought me into the campaign which was ending the Ukraine war, ending the censorship and protecting children's health. We're all into reforming our food supply, all the things that we need to do to make our children healthy again.\par \par \uc2 Those are all things that President Trump also wanted to work on, and he invited me to form a unity government. We agreed that we'd be able to continue to criticize each other on issues on which we don't agree. But these issues are so important and their way of unifying our country.\par \par \uc2 We need in this country to reach a point where we love our children more than we hate each other.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's a good, unifying theme that I think a lot of people could get around. It's how we get there that's always the devil is in the details.\par \par \uc2 Have you all negotiated over or talked about a cabinet position, another position within a Trump government in exchange for your endorsement?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: No, there was -- there's no commitments but I -- you know, I met with President Trump, with his family, with his closest advisors and we just made a general commitment that we were going to work together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What about the states where you're trying to get off of a number of swing state ballots to say, I'm going to stay on a number of state ballots but in these swing states, I'm going to try to withdraw myself and ask my supporters -- or at least you're telling them think about supporting President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Did you negotiate over which states you would try to withdraw from?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yeah, I mean, we all knew which states they were. There basically is 10 swing states where my presence in the race would have helped Vice President Harris and would have harmed President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So I'm going to get off the ballot in those states and then we're going to stay on the ballot in 30 states and, you know, I'm encouraging people to vote for me in those states. Those are states with the votes -- they're either all red or all blue states where their votes are not going to change the outcome of the race.\par \par \uc2 And -- but in the -- in the red -- in the states where I would have been spoiler, I'm going to get out. It's about 10 states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So there were reports a couple weeks ago citing an official from your camp and also a Democratic official saying that there had been outreach to the Harris camp as well to possibly discuss you working within the Harris administration in exchange for an endorsement of her.\par \par \uc2 Is that true? And what happened with that?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: It -- you know, I reached out to them on the same basis that I reached out to President Trump and I -- we actually talked to other presidential candidates, including Chase Oliver in the Libertarian Party about figuring out ways that we could end the polarization and the hatred and the vitriol, start talking about issues.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I -- and I would have -- I was welcome to listen to anything from the Democrats that they were going to do something about the war -- about the Ukraine war, about the censorship, about the child health crisis, this epidemic of chronic disease that is now disabled about 60 percent of our kids.\par \par \uc2 And you know, when my uncle is President Shannon, only 6 percent of Americans had chronic disease. Today, over 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it's hard to find a kid today that's not been damaged by it, and it's coming from our food supply, from contain -- from pollution in our environment, from toxics in our environment, and mainly from corruption in our government that allows that to happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've talked about or there's been discussion that if you were to join the Trump administration in some health related position, that you have real interest in dismantling things like the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , the CDC, \uc2 NIH\uc2 . You would like to see focus away from infectious diseases to what you're talking about more chronic diseases.\par \par \uc2 Is it fair to say that you would try to dismantle some of those organizations?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: No, I wouldn't dismantle them. I would change the focus and I would end the corruption. I would -- right now, 75 percent of \uc2 FDA\uc2 's budget is coming from pharmaceutical companies. That is a perverse incentive.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 NIH\uc2 , the -- if you -- scientists and officials in \uc2 NIH\uc2  who work on drug development, who incubate drugs for the pharmaceutical company, get to collect lifetime royalties from those products. These are regulators. They're supposed to be looking for problems in those products. We have these agencies that have become sock puppets for the industries they're supposed to regulate. They're not really interested in public health.\par \par \uc2 Everybody, the most profitable thing today in America is a sick child. Everybody is making money. The hospitals are making money. The pharmaceutical companies are making money. Even the insurance companies make money.\par \par \uc2 And we need to end those perverse incentives. We need to get the corruption out of \uc2 FDA\uc2 , out of \uc2 NIH\uc2 , out of the CDC and make them function as they're supposed to function, which is to protect public health and particularly children's health.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me go back to your decision from Friday. Both you and Nicole Shanahan, your running mate, have talked about how you feel like the Democrats completely mistreated you, excluded you from the process. How much of your decision Friday is about revenge or motivated by punishing Democrats in some way?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Zero. I really -- I don't act in anything in my life. I don't act out of anger or revenge or resentment. It's a -- it's a bad motivation. It's like swallowing poison and hoping someone else will die. And so I don't do it.\par \par \uc2 I'm -- you know, I am very pragmatic and practical and I am focused on one thing, which is how do we restore health for our children? And that touches many, many other issues. How do we regenerate our soils in this country? How do we protect small family farmers? The incentives are all going to commodity agriculture and that's what is feeding this chronic disease epidemic. The processed foods that are created by commodity agriculture.\par \par \uc2 So we need to do all of these things at once. Protect small farmers, restore our soils, regenerative agriculture and to get the corruption out of the federal government and -- and make these agencies do what they were created to do, which is to protect our health and not serve the mercantile interests of the pharmaceutical companies in the processed food industry.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This decision is not without personal cost for you. Your own family went to the White House on St. Patrick's Day, took a huge picture with President Biden, made clear who they were supporting. Friday night, your siblings issued this statement.\par \par \uc2 "We believe in Harris and Walz. Our brother Bobby's decision to endorse Trump today is a betrayal of the values that our father and our family hold most dear."\par \par \uc2 You posted on "X" Friday night saying, you're grateful to your amazing wife because the decision that you've made is one that she's uncomfortable with. So talk to us about the personal backlash you have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yeah, I mean, you know, my family is at the center of the Democratic Party. I have five members of my family that are working for the Biden administration. President Biden has a -- a -- a bust of my father behind him at the Oval Office. He's been a family friend for many, many years.\par \par \uc2 And my family is -- I understand that they're troubled by my decisions, but you know, I love my family. I feel like we were raised in a milieu where we were encouraged to debate each other and debate ferociously and passionately about things, but just still love each other.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, they can -- they're free to take their positions on these issues. There are many, many members of my family who are working in my campaign, who are supporting me. I have a very big family. There's a few of them that are troubled, but you know, I think we all need to be able to disagree with each other and still love each other.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "Wall Street Journal" has this. They say it's a two -- a double- edged sword, essentially, that it may help President Trump to pick up some of your followers if they're willing to go over there, but they warn about what you would do in his administration. They say this, "RFK Jr., if he's anywhere near the health care or environmental agencies in a Trump administration, look out. Mr. Trump's best response is to thank RFK Jr. for his support, make no promises about the future, and by all means avoid joint campaign appearances."\par \par \uc2 You've already had one. Will you have others? Will you campaign for President Trump?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yeah, I'm going to be campaigning actively. I think President Trump is going to make a series of announcements about other Democrats who are joining his campaign, and you know, I want to make America healthy again, and so does President Trump. So those are objectives.\par \par \uc2 My -- "The Wall Street Journal" may be worried about shareholder value for pharmaceutical companies, and I think, you know, I talked specifically to President Trump about that issue, and he said that he wanted to leave, as his legacy, healthy children, and that, you know, that he was unconcerned about the other implications.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , thank you for your time today. We'll see you on the campaign trail then.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Shannon, thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the economy takes center stage in the race for the White House, as we learn the U.S. created nearly a million fewer jobs than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , the Harris-Walz campaign surrogate, joins us to talk about that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fresh off the convention, we are still awaiting more details on Democratic presidential nominee Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' plans to turn around the economy. If elected, though, one of the things she says is Harris is proposing to give $25,000 to some first-time homebuyers. Not yet clear exactly how that would work and some experts actually feel it could inflate the market for homes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , also a surrogate for the Harris-Walz campaign and somebody who has great concerns about the housing market.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO), HARRIS-WALZ SURROGATE: Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start with that proposal. There was some skepticism across the board about how it would work and get paid for. Here's \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , Shark Tank fame, business investor. Here's his take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , "SHARK TANK" INVESTOR: There are bad ideas, bad, bad ideas, and really bad ideas and that falls into this category. When you give $25,000 to anybody in a constrained market, you cause inflation. So if there's three houses for sale on the street and everybody bidding on it gets another $25,000, all of that attributes to the seller and you cause the price of the house to go up because there's no supply.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, knowing that this is an issue you've worked desperately on to try to get these things under control, what do you make of the criticism of this plan?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, look, if that was her entire housing plan, then some of that would be valid. That's a small piece of it that some of the opponents have glommed on to. The key piece of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's housing plan is removing barriers and red tape and regulations that prevent housing from being built, effectively streamlining the process of building homes, allowing the quantity of homes to increase, especially the kinds of starter homes that people can afford, $200,000, $300,000. That's the bulk of her plan. That $25,000, if it's a way to move renters into owners, that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 We live in an ownership society. That's how people build wealth. If people, instead of having their monthly check go every month to rent, if they can have a good mortgage and build equity, that's good. But of course, not alone. It needs to be coupled with, and it is under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' detailed housing plan, increasing the quantity of housing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so she does want to get to, I think the goal is 300 -- excuse me, 3 million new homes in four years, but you talk about the barriers that exist to getting those homes built.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says this, she's going to have to deal with regulations. The only real way to get there is via the one promise Harris will find hardest to keep, that is to make it much easier for builders to build. They go on to say actually getting local governments to expedite reviews is as much a management problem as a legal one, and her administration cannot micromanage every county seat and town hall. You alluded to that problem.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Yeah, and we have similar issues in Colorado getting rid of, you know, local red tape to get things built. We welcome the help of the federal government. Most Americans looking for housing affordability, they don't care if it's their mayor, their governor, or their president that does it, they just want it done, and President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is really speaking to that.\par \par \uc2 So I think you can look forward to how we can effectively tie in a wide variety of federal incentives to these kinds of land-use zoning reforms that effectively reduce red tape, reduce the size of local government, and make it easier to build homes. That's what's going to lead to the millions of new homes and more affordability for the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I know that Colorado, we've talked with the Denver mayor and with others there, that -- that part of the issue with housing is this influx of a lot of new people showing up.\par \par \uc2 "The Daily Caller" says this, "The U.S. currently has an estimated shortage of 4 million to 7 million housing units. The recent influx of illegal immigrants is putting even more stress on the system by aggravating the current shortage of supply in the market at a rate that developers can't keep up with."\par \par \uc2 Under this administration, we have had millions of new people come to this country, and in a way that it is unregulated, it doesn't follow our laws, and it is impacting housing markets. Is that part of the problem in Colorado and beyond?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Look, there's no doubt we'd be in a better place today if former President Trump hadn't torpedoed the bipartisan border security plan that would have finally locked down the border to provide real security --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but that comes three and a half years into this administration. You're repealing executive orders and doing all kinds of things that led us to these historically high numbers.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, look, I mean, Democrats have supported border security for decades. I supported it in Congress, sponsored bills to do it, you know, and again, we can't let politics stand in the way of solving this, and so while, again, there might be blame on both sides, the most recent attempt to fix it was clearly torpedoed by former President Trump, and that's why we have a chaotic border today. Very clear, that's the matter.\par \par \uc2 Of course, look --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well you know that there are a number of senators we've had on this show and beyond who will say that they weren't going to vote for that bill regardless of whether President Trump liked it or not. They had real concerns with it in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, the key question to ask them then is what amendments would you like to make to improve it? I mean, any idea that improves border security --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, they'll say they were allowed to make amendments.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: -- I'm sure Democrats are for. Well, then that's their problem, but look, that's what they're there for. We want ideas from the left and the right, but again, the key thing is the border would be locked down today if not for President Trump thwarting that effort, preventing that effort to provide border security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wait, wait, wait. OK, but you --\par \par \uc2 POLIS: But again, there's plenty of blame on both sides for over a decade on this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it fair to say that the bill was not going to lock down the whole border? I mean, in all honesty, I mean, that bipartisan plan was not going to completely stem the tide of what's happening at the border.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: It would have cut it by at least two-thirds, and look, if there's a way to do it a hundred percent, I'm sure that President Harris can deliver on that once she has the opportunity to serve. This is somebody who fought crime, she's a prosecutor, she's put criminals behind bars, she knows how to keep people safe, and that's exactly what we need here.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the dimension connecting it to the housing. Of course, that's a small part of it. I mean, housing costs have gone up in Colorado long before the recent influx here. The average home price in the Denver metro area, $600,000. Why? Because it's a great place to live. Why does it cost that much? Because we've artificially constrained the ability to build housing because of government interference. We need to remove that government interference, allow the housing to be built, and that's what Vice President Harris' plan is all about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's talk about that because we would like to see more from her. We're all waiting for a sit-down interview, a press conference. We look forward to the debate on September 10th. We'd love to host additional ones. But in the meantime, we're sort of left to guess where she is on a number of things, where it appears she's changed positions on fracking and the Green New Deal, gun buybacks, mandatory gun buybacks, all kinds of things that we get a trickle from people within her campaign who, you know, off the record are giving us guidance about where she is now. Is it time for her to sit down, give us more information, put a policy primer on her website?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I'm sure that she'll be doing a lot more interviews in the future. Of course, the debate is going to be very exciting. She's already taking questions at a number of different venues as she's moving in and out, but of course, the truth of the matter is, yes, she's come to the middle. She's pragmatic. She's a tough leader. She's the leader we need for the future.\par \par \uc2 So again, while she may have in the past ran on things just as President Trump ran on single-payer, supported single-payer health care years ago and has moved away from that, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did support things that she's now moderated. She's going to be a president for all the American people.\par \par \uc2 Look, whether you're conservative, whether you're moderate, whether you're liberal, I think you're going to see a lot of your priorities reflected in President Harris, who's really just out for the people, as she said, fighting to make housing more affordable, improve our schools, make education more affordable and strengthen the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, speaking of the jobs market, we talked about we got this downgrade in the year ending March 2024, 818,000 jobs that didn't actually happen.\par \par \uc2 There's been a revision there, Alfred Ortiz, the CEO of Job Creators Network says this, the labor market is far weaker than top-line numbers suggest and has been dominated by government and quasi government jobs. This fuller view of the jobs market nullifies a year's worth of exaggerated headlines that have helped the Biden-Harris administration present a false picture of economic strength.\par \par \uc2 It may be why in the polling, while the administration will say things are getting better, they are moving in the right direction, people say it's not their reality. I mean, they're paying 20 to 30 percent more for household items over January 2021.\par \par \uc2 Does president -- Vice President Harris plan to break with the Biden administration policies?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, right now, nationally, we have about 1.2 unemployed people for -- I'm sorry, 1.2 job openings for every unemployed person.\par \par \uc2 So there are more job openings than unemployed people. That doesn't mean that there's not a skills gap, meaning some of the unemployed people while there are jobs might need additional training. Three weeks, six weeks, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 I know that President -- Vice President Harris has been a big fan of making sure the people have access to the training that they need to get the good jobs that are out there today.\par \par \uc2 I'm also excited, of course, by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' middle class tax cut, putting more money back in the hands of middle class families, increasing their buying power. Very strong contrast to former President Trump's tariff tax plan that would increase the cost of household items by 10 to 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll wait to hear from both of them because we need details on both those things. These claims about the tariff situation and also the economic plans of Vice President. We look forward to more details and we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Governor Polis being with us.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Thank you. Take care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OK. The 2024 showdown, Harris and Trump, both back on the campaign trail this week, making stops in crucial battleground states that will likely determine the outcome of the entire election.\par \par \uc2 Our panel is here to break it down as the campaigns gear up for that first Trump-Harris debate showdown, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will be a president who unites us around our highest aspirations and always fights for the American people.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: When we win this November, we will end the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  border nightmare, once and for all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  accepting the democratic presidential nomination this week, as former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took his campaign to the border.\par \par \uc2 It's time now for our Sunday group. Francesca Chambers, "USA Today" White House correspondent. "The Federalist" editor-in-chief and Fox News contributor, Mollie Hemingway. Richard Fowler, Fox News contributor and Forbes contributing writer. And former \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  National Security official, \uc2 Michael Allen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. Welcome, everybody. Everybody who made it back from the DNC. We all still need naps.\par \par \uc2 But let's talk about what happened there. There was a lot of messaging. It was interesting because it very much was the vice president is the challenger and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the incumbent. It was kind of one of the vibes we got.\par \par \uc2 We also have this. From "The Hill." Democrats try to take patriotism back from the GOP. It says USA chants, camo and American flag hats filled the convention in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 And when Vice President Harris wrapped up her speech, a balloon drop of red, white, and blue was met with dozens of giant American flags.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, was there -- in the United Center and they had this very organized group that would take out the signs and the flags, whatever they were going to be, for that particular night. And they had, I would say, thousands of flags in there.\par \par \uc2 Was this a purposeful shift in some way? Because at some points we've been told that the American flag was a symbol of not so positive things.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, I do think they focused on the theme of patriotism to your point. And, of course, people have commented on like the \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  camo hats as well.\par \par \uc2 With regards to her speech itself though, I think there were three main goals for the vice president that night, party, unity, reintroduce herself to voters and do no harm.\par \par \uc2 I say do no harm because if you pay attention to what she actually said in her speech, not a lot in there. That independence, the swing voters could be upset about.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the other things that she was saying in this speech in terms of the progressive policies, most of that was being done by those people who were speaking before her. They focused a lot that night on those anti-Trump Republicans as well.\par \par \uc2 So was her speech profound in some ways? I didn't hear that from Democrats. I'll let Richard speak to that, but she certainly met the bar that they were trying to set that evening.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Richard, not a lot of substance. A lot of substance, we don't get that in convention speeches. We get broad themes. We get things that are hopeful and make people feel good. It's the minutiae that people really need to know about before they vote.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think a couple of things. I think -- I think Francesca is right when it comes to three principles that the Harris campaign wanted to get across.\par \par \uc2 And I think also to this happens in a moment where if this convention were happening in June or July, you probably wouldn't have seen party unity. You probably would have seen more, you know, ceasefire protests.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we were talking about that a couple of weeks ago on this show that you're going to see chaos in Chicago and that chaos did not result in any real form or fashion. I think that has lots to do with the work.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there were dozens of -- over at south side.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No. There were dozens but there were --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But there was no --\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Thousands.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- organized, you know, inside.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I heard some shouts of free Palestine in that case.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But nothing organized.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: And I think it has a lot to do with the work of the mayor of Chicago, Brandon Johnson.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, I think that all sorts to do with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , three weeks ago, really uniting this party in a new and invigorative way.\par \par \uc2 And I think her fundraising numbers today, over half a billion dollars raised, 200,000 volunteer shifts, people are signing up for today speaks to the fact that she has organized the party and united the party.\par \par \uc2 With 77 days to go in this election, you have a united Democratic Party. You pretty much have a united Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And the question now becomes, for both of them, is who can turn out their base and who can talk to independent voters and voters who are likely not to vote in this election in a new way to get them out to vote, with ballots going out in a couple of weeks in some states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, it is soon.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There are a couple reasons why Democrats are really trying to push this message of unity. The first one is pretty obvious. They just ousted the President of the United States from the ticket and replaced him with someone who hadn't won a single electoral vote from any Democrat.\par \par \uc2 So it's really important to sort of pretend that that didn't take place, to pretend that there's unity. The first guest on your program today, RFK Jr., lifelong Democrat from a really important Democrat family shows that that unity is quite overblown.\par \par \uc2 But the bigger problem for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is that she's the incumbent vice president in a failed presidential administration.\par \par \uc2 And so that was the big takeaway I took from this convention, was how much they were trying to make this a referendum on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There are two problems with that. Again, one, she's the incumbent of what's going on right now. If she has these ideas that she claims she has, she's had three and a half years to implement them.\par \par \uc2 But the other problem is that the Trump presidency was very successful. And a lot of Americans view it far more favorably now than they did even when they were -- when they were finishing it and gave Biden a chance.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But the net favorability of Trump himself are actually very negative. And I think that's why you see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  having the bump in the polls that she currently has.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the policies though, when you ask people about how you were doing financially, did the Trump. We have recent polling on this too, where the Trump era policies better for you and your family?\par \par \uc2 It was like two to one over what they felt like. Many of them said was actually negative for them, that the Biden-Harris administration policies that actually hurt their families.\par \par \uc2 So do people, is it really still just about the economy stupid?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: It's not just about the economy. I think it's about all the issues that we know that President Trump has an advantage on.\par \par \uc2 But I think all of you were correct and that this was the second switch. The first switch was, let's put in \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And now let's switch out who the incumbent is.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that President Trump needs to be able to put her on the defensive and very, very soon.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, is that I think she hasn't done anything amazing yet. Bear with me for a second. She's riding a wave of relief that it's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that's at the top of the ticket. I think she brought home soft Democrats. I think she performed with a teleprompter. She did fine. She didn't make any mistakes.\par \par \uc2 But the hard part starts now. She's got to be able to demonstrate that she has political dexterity and agility and a give-and-take campaign environment. And I don't think she's done that yet. And that's what we need to look forward to starting this week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. So the debate prep is underway. We know that the Trump team is using \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , former congresswoman who really sparred with then Senator Harris when they were on the debate stage together. We'll see how that works.\par \par \uc2 But I want to bring this up too this note that the more that President Trump has tried to say, listen, no federal ban on abortion. Also this week talking about the Comstock Act that he will not use any federal action to block abortion polls going through the mail. There are many within the pro- life community who are starting to get a little frustrated with him.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" quotes, \uc2 Tony Perkins\uc2  of the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2  saying this. It's not a pro-life position. It's not an acceptable position and it does not provide the contrast on this issue to the degree that we had in the past between him and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , what President Trump is doing is suppressing his own support.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, it's not necessarily the pro-lifers are going to go vote for President Biden, but -- or excuse, Vice President Harris, but do they stay home?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Right. Pro-lifers are very pleased with President Trump for how he ran his first administration. And they are very grateful for what he did with judges and other issues. And there's no question that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who just, I mean, pretty much the only policy that was talked about on stage at the Democratic Convention, was abortion through all nine months of pregnancy, up until the moment of birth, not a single protection for any child, no matter the circumstances. That is a strong contrast.\par \par \uc2 But President Trump should understand that his base of support includes these people who care about violence in the womb and they want to protect children and their mothers. And he should be mindful of how much -- how much help he gets from those people and not discourage them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Richard, I see you shaking your head. We're almost out of time.\par \par \uc2 But I didn't hear Democrats articulate anything short of what Mollie said.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Well, a couple of points here. I rarely agree with Mr. Perkins, but I do agree with him here.\par \par \uc2 I do think in Trump's pivot to try to get independence, he's losing right- leaning voters. I think beyond that, I think Republicans continue to make these arguments that Democrats believe in this abortion after birth, which is not a rule.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No, no. You didn't -- you said up to.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Up to birth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just to be clear, because, listen, we just had \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  on -- Governor Polis --\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- out of Colorado. He signed a measure, as \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  did in Minnesota that allows for abortion at any time during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure. And I think those are very, very, very, very rare cases. In the case where a mother gets in a car accident, it's a life of the mother - - the life of the baby.\par \par \uc2 And I think doctors make them in very rare cases. I think the idea of these are happening every single day in hospitals across America is a total misnomer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there are -- but no, I will say this. The American Institute, which is not a conservative group, says there are thousands of these every year.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Thousands in a country that has 300 million Americans is a very, very small number.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: But Americans would love some protection of children.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But I think what you see in poll -- in poll after poll after poll and ballot issue after ballot issue after ballot issue is the American people stand on the side of a woman and her doctor making the best decision for them over the government making that decision.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there are a number of those on key critical states.\par \par \uc2 We'll see how that plays out because they're on a lot of ballot initiatives in the fall.\par \par \uc2 OK. Do not go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 We've got this. Multiple Secret Service agents are now on leave following the assassination attempt on former President Trump last month. Our panel discusses new revelations as we preview two events on Monday that are aimed at getting more information about exactly how this happened, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Secret Service are moving at a snail's pace. They're acting like this is a one-time incident, that we can take our time to really unpack. No, these plots are happening right now as we speak. We have a foreign enemy trying to kill our former president. We need to be releasing information as we get it, and they need to move a lot faster in my view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman Michael Waltz on "FOX AND FRIENDS" yesterday, pushing for a swifter, more transparent probe into the attempted assassination of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, five Secret Service members have been put on leave following the incident that left Trump wounded, Corey Comperatore dead and two others seriously injured.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 Molly, you were pretty fired up about this during the commercial break.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, what happened with President Trump nearly being assassinated was traumatic, not just for him and his family, but the entire country. We came within centimeters of a horrific situation. You had, again, RFK Jr. on at the top of the hour. We all saw what happened with his father and uncle. And seeing that on live television is traumatic for people.\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., the permanent D.C. apparatus is not that interested in what happened, even as normal Americans are horrified by it.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Secret Service can't investigate themselves on their own failings in this matter, but Congress also need - you know, Congress needs to do more. The media need to do far more and be far more interested in this situation and the political climate that leads to these types of situations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, a couple of things that have surfaced. Senator Hawley has yet another whistleblower he says that's come forward. He sent a letter to the acting director of the Secret Service saying this, "a whistleblower with knowledge of the planning of the whole thing alleges that officials at Secret Service headquarters encouraged agents in charge of the trip not to request any additional security assets in its formal manpower request, effectively denying these assets through informal means." Hawley goes on to write that Roe (ph) had told him that no security assets had been denied. So, he says there - he needs an explanation. Somethings is in conflict.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, "FORBES" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Listen, I think the American people deserve an explanation for this. If you look at what happened to former President Trump, and I think it's - it's heartbreaking, versus what happened to the last time we seen an attempted presidential assassination attempt which was in the `80s against \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , they're completely - the stark - the contrast can't be starker, right? And I say that in a world in which the heroic picture that many have celebrated is a picture that did not happen during the Reagan assassination because the Secret Service moved so swiftly to get \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  out of it that there wasn't time for a photo.\par \par \uc2 In this scene, what we saw three weeks ago, there were three or four, maybe five or six different photos that were taken that shouldn't have been allowed in a world when you're trying to get a protectee to a safe zone when there's an active shooter.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there's a lot of question that should be asked. I agree with Mollie, this is very disgusting. And I think Congress should move a lot faster and the Secret Service should move a lot faster to get the American people the answers they deserve because this was an utter failure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Congressman \uc2 Clay Higgins\uc2  has already released a little bit of what he found in this. He said, "the Secret Service did not retrieve radios that had been set aside for them by Butler County's tactical command, even though they had been reminded to do so by Butler County tactical command the day before and the morning of the shooting." That's according to Higgins.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have the bipartisan group from the House visit Butler tomorrow, but there's also this other forum that's happening with a couple of other members of Congress who are holding - you can't call it a hearing but something else. They say they're running in parallel. But hard to get anything done here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Well, right. It is an election year and right now Congress is out. They've been out for several weeks now. They'll be back in September. But there's also an appropriations process. And that is just, to your point, like, what is happening in this town heading into a November election.\par \par \uc2 The president of the United States has said he wants to see a thorough and transparent investigation because, to Richard's point, he believes that the American people need to know what happened here. So, certainly we'll - all of our eyes will be on this heading - heading into November as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And this morning we all woke up to - if you weren't up late last night, to this news on the international stage, Michael, that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Israel have been trading fire. Israel had a commanding 100 of their jets apparently flew over \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , they said targeted at what they had been told was a preparation for some 6,000 potential missiles coming their way.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: That's right. So, Israel, of course, making a preemptive strike in part to take out all of this \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  capability. Imagine here in the United States if 10 percent of our territory were inaccessible because one of our neighbors was able to arrange it with rockets or missiles. Israel cannot live forever. They honestly can't live another year with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  able to attack their territory. This is why Netanyahu says what happened last night is just the beginning, not the end. Expect a regional situation to possibly escalates as Israel feels like they need to go more into Lebanon, to push \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  back.\par \par \uc2 By the way, this is the right way to do it. The way we put al Qaeda back on their heels was to keep a boot on their throat. And I think Israel knows that's the best solution for them moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How worried are you about that escalation you talk about, with the fact that Iran is still warning, oh, yes, we're still going to, you know, unleash our retribution on - or on Israel for the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader who was taken out. I mean there's a lot of threat flying back and forth here.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: I'm still concerned that Iran will try to do something beyond what they already do every day, which is act through their proxies. I believe that they feel like they look weak because Israel was able to target one other their top terrorist proxies, in their guest house, at their administration. So, I feel like they need to do that to show their toughness. They want to restore deterrence also. So, I think Iran's just trying to figure out a couple different things.\par \par \uc2 How do we get through and not embarrass ourselves with missiles and drones, but not get through in such a way that Israel wants to come back and attack us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: They don't want a total war because they would lose. And so they have to walk this line and it's very tight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It feels like what they did in - in April I think it was when they did a lot of incoming Iran into Israel. Israel was able to block most of that. But Iran was able to say, we did unleash on them. And there's this weird choreography that seems that happen with these sometimes.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the meantime, I want to put up a look at some assets we have in the region. And, Francesca, this is clearly something that the White House has got to deal with. I mean, what do you make of the criticism - we had General Kelly at the beginning of the show saying essentially that this administration has not handled this well.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, the president continues to get updates on this. He sent CQ Brown to the region. And if we could, just for a second, talk about the ceasefire and hostage negotiation as well that are taking place in the region right now, you had the president, who called \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  on Wednesday to try and press him on some of the finer points.\par \par \uc2 We have not seen inside those negotiations. So, when they keep saying that they're getting closer, we - we don't know that. We haven't seen it. But that is what the White House says. But they have to keep going back and forth between - between \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And that can sometimes take days.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And having these peace talks in the middle of a new incursion involving \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Iran's threats still there certainly complicates things.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 We're going to take you back to Israel up next, an update on those major Israeli strikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the return. All of that with Trey Yingst, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to take you back to Israel. Our top story, the \uc2 IDF\uc2 's preemptive attack on Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Lebanon. Their return fire as well.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst joins us again.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, hey, Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We continue to gather the latest information from the ground. I've talked with members of the Israeli military and top defense officials here in Tel Aviv. They say right now it is the status quo unfolding. Israeli officials do not believe there will be a large attack from \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  following the preemptive strikes that took place overnight, but they do remain on high alert across this country.\par \par \uc2 And it also comes as we continue to look back at this war that's unfolded for the past 326 days. And we have some new reporting for you from my upcoming book "Black Saturday" that is out on October 1st.\par \par \uc2 We learned as part of our reporting for the book that the Israelis actually planned a preemptive strike against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  just four days after the October 7th massacre. Jets were in the sky. And just an hour before that strike was set to take place it was called off following a conversation with President Biden. We'll certainly have more information about that in the book coming out October 1st.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Can't wait. Got mine on order.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And just a quick note, my podcast "Livin' the Bream" drops today. This week I had the privilege of talking with one my heroes, speaker, author and disability advocate Joni Eareckson Tada. We talked what her new book, "The Practice of the Presence of Jesus: Daily Meditations on the Nearness of our Savior." Get it anywhere you like to get your books. And check out the podcast, where you like to get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240825ek8p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Holds First Outdoor Rally Since Shooting; Job Growth Revised Downward; DNC Protests; Will \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  Support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?; Interview With Sen. Michael Bennet (D-CO); Interview With Sen. Steve Daines (R-MT); Interview With Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE)\b0\par\par\uc2 Grady Trimble, Mark Penn, Mike Tobin, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Only last night Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  was touting the Biden/Harris economic boom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We needed a leader with the determination to drive what would become the world's strongest recovery, 15 million jobs, higher wages, lower health care costs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, make that about 14 million jobs. FOX on top of a big jobs announcement announced today that has Republicans saying this recovery is proving a real piece of work and Democrats insisting more Americans than ever are still finding work.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign co-chair Democratic Senator Chris Coons about the boom he says still holds, and Montana Republican Senator Steve Daines on the bust he argues is looking a whole lot closer.\par \par \uc2 All that and \uc2 Obama\uc2  pal Don Peebles on the pressure on Democratic V.P. picked him Walz tonight, and the latest on all of those protesters gathering outside the convention hall as we speak right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin in Chicago on those protesters. How big are the crowds right now, my friend?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's really not that big.\par \par \uc2 This is the latest of the anti-Israel demonstrations forming up at Union Park. And it's not that big at the moment. They do plan to grow a little bit as the hour goes on and they also plan a march. Now, the demonstrators were out last night.\par \par \uc2 A group calling themselves Behind Enemy Lines marched in front of the Israeli Consulate on Madison Street. There -- that was a different demonstration. They burned American flags on the street. They burned the Israel flag on the street. And they deliberately conflicted with police that ultimately resulted in mass arrests.\par \par \uc2 Some 50 to 60 people were arrested. And one of the demonstrators is not looking at a couple of felony counts because the officer that he knocked to the ground ended up with a concussion.\par \par \uc2 Here is Chicago Police Superintendent Larry Snelling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY SNELLING, CHICAGO, ILLINOIS, POLICE CHIEF: We attempted to avoid the clash, to no avail. An officer who cannot protect himself or himself, themselves cannot protect this city. Enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Now, Jewish groups are getting their voices heard today. The Israeli-American Committee has established Hostage Square.\par \par \uc2 They're there featuring artists that are highlighting the plight of the hostages still being held in Gaza. And today is the day that the \uc2 IAC\uc2  got a slot at the speakers platform. For 45 minutes, they can speak, make their voices be heard. And when they're doing, that's happening in another park nearby here. They're calling it Park 587 -- or Park 586. Pardon me.\par \par \uc2 That's about 10 blocks, 10 minutes walking from here. They're going to be joined by the Chicago Jewish Alliance. So they're attempting to turn their 45 minutes at the speaker's platform into their own rally to make the Jewish voices heard -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Mike, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado to get his take on what's going on, former presidential candidate himself.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 How do you think this convention is going so far, these protests notwithstanding?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): I think it's going really well. The unity and the excitement in the room is phenomenal, and it's just a very exciting moment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There's a lot of pressure, obviously, on a lot of folks, but it comes with this backdrop, and I'm sure you have heard it, Senator, where jobs were trimmed a little bit in the latest adjustment out of the Department of Labor statistics, showing that there's still robust, but about 800,000-plus fewer than we thought over roughly the last year.\par \par \uc2 You worried about that?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Well, I'm worried about it for the country in the sense that we have got to make sure that we have got an economy that's delivering for working people.\par \par \uc2 And we have got, in my view, a decades-long challenge in America that's resulted in a world where the bottom 50 percent of Americans today have less wealth than they had when \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 The bottom 50 percent of Americans own 2 percent of the wealth in America. The top 10 percent of Americans have 75 percent of the wealth. I'm not a Bolshevik. I'm not a socialist. Those are the facts, Neil, of our economy, and we have to find a way to address them as Americans.\par \par \uc2 And I think reversing the kind of trickle-down economics that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's tax policy represented and replacing it with something that actually supports working people, acknowledging that the Biden administration is the first administration to bring back an entire industry from overseas, when Reagan was the one that began the outsourcing to Southeast Asia and to China.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, this has been a problem on Democratic watches and Republican watches, but the American people desperately need us to have an economy that produces economic mobility for most Americans when it grows.\par \par \uc2 That is what we have to do. And, if we don't, that is what is going to cause us to lose the democracy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has come up with a plan to try to freeze prices, and that's already gotten some criticism from the likes of \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . And some former \uc2 Obama\uc2  officials themselves who ran the economy for him say it sounds gimmicky. Where are you on this?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I haven't been able to read it, and I don't know what the details of it are.\par \par \uc2 I will say that she was one of the first people to sign on to my child tax credit which the Biden administration implemented and which cut childhood poverty for a moment in America by 50 percent; 90 percent of America's children benefited from that; 90 percent of the children in Colorado benefited from that.\par \par \uc2 That is a huge difference than the Trump tax cuts, 65 percent of which went to the top 5 percent at a moment when we have got the worst income inequality that we have had since the 1920s. So, it's that kind of tax policy, I think, that I am trying to get this party to endorse.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting to me that nobody less than J.D. Vance himself has now said we should have a tax credit of $5,000, which is more than I ever had proposed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, the differences over the party's platforms and views on what will stimulate growth notwithstanding, I told you about this job adjustment. And it still does show a little bit more than two million jobs gained over this roughly one-year period that's in question --\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: -- but not the 2.9 million the administration said.\par \par \uc2 And that's spurred talk that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has to do something, come to the rescue, because things could be fading fast. Are you in that camp that says things are fading fast?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: No, I'm not in the camp that things are fading fast. I'm not surprised that things are moving a little bit less quickly than we thought they were.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: And I do think that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  needs to pay attention to these numbers.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, you know I believe this because we have talked about it before. I think the Fed kept us at zero for far too long. I think that that was lousy monetary policy. And now they kind of jerked us around by adjusting us in the other direction. So I hope, if they move, they move with caution and the American people are able to have an economy in the long run that, when it grows, they get to share in the benefit of that economic growth, that they can see themselves in the unbelievable economic power that is the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 That's what the kids that I used to work for when I was the school superintendent in Denver --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: -- before I was in this job, that is what their families need is broad-based economic growth that lifts and restores the middle class in this country. That's what we have to do.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think that -- again, I don't think that's a Bolshevik idea. I think the greatest American invention is our middle class. And both parties should be competing to how to restore that middle class.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator, very good catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Good seeing you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Great to see you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, RFK possibly supporting DJT? We're on it after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We are getting word that \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  plans to make a major address to the American people come Friday. Now, there is talk out there that he is considering dropping out of the race, throwing his support behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When asked about this, DNC senior adviser Mary Beth Cahill said -- and I quote -- "Desperate men do desperate things. RFK Jr. was recruited by MAGA, funded by MAGA, and parroted MAGA talking points. No one should be shocked if he formalizes his relationship in an attempt to maintain relevance."\par \par \uc2 Well, again, none of this is a sure thing, or even an indication of something that could happen.\par \par \uc2 But let's get the read on all of it with \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , the former Clinton adviser, Democratic pollster.\par \par \uc2 Mark, first off, what do you make of this talk that RFK Jr., first off is going to quit the race and throw his support behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, of course, anything can happen in this year's presidential race. You never know who's running for what.\par \par \uc2 I think his vice presidential candidate, I think Nicole Shanahan, she was really quite clear --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She was.\par \par \uc2 PENN: -- that the Democratic Party had pissed them off, had done everything to inhibit the campaign and having a fair primary, which we know that had there been a fair and open primary, probably there would have been a real primary if the president had to go to debates.\par \par \uc2 But in any event, I think she was clear. I think it's a real possibility. I think ultimately it could make about a point difference. I mean, I don't know that it would make four or five points or anything crazy like that, but it could tip a point in Trump's favor.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I was thinking too, Mark, in battleground states, much like \uc2 Ralph Nader\uc2  and his presence in the Florida ticket back in 2000, you could argue then drew a lot of votes away from \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 . We know how that whole thing turned out. I'm not saying it's the exact type of instance.\par \par \uc2 But in a battleground state where polls are tight and you can have someone like Bobby Kennedy Jr. picking up 3, 4, I believe, in Michigan, 5 percent of the vote, that could be influential, couldn't it?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, yes, but remember, the people who are voting for \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , they probably don't like politics. They really don't like either of the political figures.\par \par \uc2 They're really more likely to kind of drop out of voting if he drops out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 PENN: But I do think still, if they do an explicit endorsement and go into that camp, still could be worth a point in key battleground states. It's not zero. That's for sure. It's just not just -- it's not like an automatic, oh, my God, this race has changed, but it is worth certainly something.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it comes at a time, especially that initial Democratic response from the senior adviser, did remind me of President Kennedy's grandson speaking before the convention yesterday, never uttering his cousin's name.\par \par \uc2 And it got me into the discussion, or one that I recently had with \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , on just the status of that relationship, not only within the party, but within his own family. I want you to respond to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I talked to one who's a big admirer of your family, but not of you, and very bitter and angry at you, saying that you're hurting Democrats and that you wouldn't be welcomed back.\par \par \uc2 Does that bother you when you hear that kind of talk? Because I'm sure you have heard it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yes, it does bother me, and I don't think that that's unique to that individual. I think there's a number of people who feel that way.\par \par \uc2 It puzzles me, Neil. I feel if you went down the list of every issue that my father believed in, every issue that my uncle felt was a priority, that I would check off every single one of those. I don't think -- I think I represent almost perfectly the Democratic Party of Robert Kennedy and John Kennedy, but I think the tribalism is so pronounced in our country and this division that people cannot see beyond the tribal identities.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's really bad for our country that every issue and every person now has to choose its side. And if you're perceived as choosing the wrong side, then you're dead to them. And that is not healthy for our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What did you think of that, Mark?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, it was a pretty rational comment. I mean, I think it was pretty straightforward.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think that, as somebody running as an independent, saying that he's sick and tired of the tribalism, and I think the read on this is that, look, he went into the Democratic primary and they sued him and they threw him out and they blasted him and they didn't just treat him like a candidate who could be defeated easily or not at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's obviously alienated him, and that may turn out to be a political mistake than if they had just run an open primary season and not really gone after Phillips or Kennedy in any meaningful way and let it run out, and it probably would have been better for the country.\par \par \uc2 And maybe people are going to realize that campaigns have to be open, ballot access has to be fair, debates and position papers are not optional. They are more important than getting somebody's tax returns. We need to know where people stand, what they're going to do and what votes they're going to get after they talk to the people.\par \par \uc2 So I think they have managed to take somebody who probably could have been brought into the fold easily and completely alienate them and they just may go over to the other side and one point might just make the whole difference in this race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wouldn't that be ironic if that were the case?\par \par \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Again, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  will spell this out to the nation on Friday. We will get a sense of where that stands. He himself has not directly discussed any of this. His running mate was the one who first offered it in a podcast, a streamed podcast that got all of this response leading to Democrats saying what they did.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, updating you on that big job revision that has a lot of people saying, if we only knew then what we know now, all of that on top of protests that could be a big factor tonight -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're looking at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who was at an outdoor rally today, the first time he has attempted to do one since the assassination attempt. It all went very, very well, but some questions now. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hitting the battleground state of North Carolina today. They all seem like battleground states, right? And you're not imagining it.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people holding their breath. They were worried for the former president. This was his first outdoor rally since the attempt on his life a little more than a month ago. It went down OK.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble there in Asheboro, North Carolina with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And we actually had a chance to speak with the former president after the event here. He seemed in good spirits. Didn't seem phased by the return to outdoor events. My first question to him was if he had heard that there are reports RFK Jr. is going to drop out of the race. He said he hadn't heard that. I was the first person to tell him that.\par \par \uc2 But he also said that he would welcome an endorsement from RFK Jr. Both men are going to be in Arizona on Friday, by the way. So we will see if they appear together in any capacity. We also talked about the possibility that the Fed could raise interest rates. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: I know that you have said that you will let Fed Chair Powell stay as Fed chair until the end of his term. But what if he cuts rates before the election?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, if you take a look at the numbers and this 800-and-something-thousand fake jobs that they said existed, which is I think a terrible thing -- they did that in order to look like their economy is good.\par \par \uc2 That shows the economy is horrible. That's the biggest number to -- I mean, we check back a long ways and there's never been a number like that.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Fifteen years, but it also means they're probably going to cut rates next month before election.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, it means they probably have to because you will end up with a depression, perhaps. Who knows?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: And former President Trump tells me that he's been catching some of the DNC. He said they bring up his name quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 One of the people who spoke this week out in Chicago is \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain, who recently said at a Harris for vice president -- a rally for Vice President Harris that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't know you-know-what about the auto industry. Here's Trump's response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I know a lot, and I know a lot about business, but I know a lot about that industry, and they're going to be going to all-electric cars. They're all going to be made in China. There won't be an auto industry left if she wins this election. There will be no auto industry left.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that most of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  are going to be voting for Trump. I'm going to bring back factories into this country, and they're going to all have great jobs making cars of all types, including electric.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: And, Neil, you might be able to see behind me they're breaking down the stage.\par \par \uc2 But that's the podium up there. And on each side of it, the front and the back, is bulletproof glass. That's one of the new changes to protocol that the Secret Service has put in place to protect and keep President Trump safe as he hits the campaign trail again with these outdoor events -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Grady, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Grady Trimble.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Grady referred to it with the former president, the big auto jobs that are potentially in peril right now as the E.V. push seems to be falling on deaf ears. Ford, for example, has just canceled plans for an electric SUV, also shelving for the time being what would have been a plan to -- factory to make those, a $1.9 billion charge as a result.\par \par \uc2 So, more and more of the automakers, even those that were pushing E.V.s, are not pushing them as much right now.\par \par \uc2 All of this with the backdrop also that something Grady got into with President Trump, this revision, this adjustment. It happens every year thereabouts, where, in this period from April 2023 to March 2024, they revised the numbers with a number of seasonal adjustments, other things.\par \par \uc2 I could go into the weeds here. Suffice it to say, the end result was 818,000 fewer jobs than originally reported. So, in this one-year period, the 2.9 million jobs that the Biden administration claims we had gained, turns out to be about 2.1 million. They also argue that, since they first came into office, it's been, even with these adjustments, about 14 million jobs gained.\par \par \uc2 The Trump people counter and say, you're just talking about a lot of people who are returning to work. So they hadn't lost those jobs after COVID. They were simply returning. Be that as it may, Democrats dismissing it, Republicans having a field day with this.\par \par \uc2 Senator Steve Daines, the Republican of Montana, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, you have heard about this job adjustment news, and it does happen, but this is particularly big, like record big adjustment. So some people are wondering what's next. Are you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): Well, Neil, it's interesting to note this is the largest downward adjustment in the numbers going back to 2009.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: This, I think, is a major hit to the Democrats. They tout these job numbers. And this is not a good report for the Democrats this week, right in the middle of their convention.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Senator, you're in charge of making sure Republicans can retake the Senate. It's very close. It's a Herculean task. News like this might make a big difference in battleground states.\par \par \uc2 The administration comes back and says, gains are gains. And even with the one million adjustment, roughly, the fact of the matter is, you're still up 14 million under the Biden/Harris administration. That's the way they're playing it. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, I think the voters are looking at even more important data, and that's their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 And they realize the most important vehicle most voters in America drive today, the most expensive vehicle is their grocery cart. The affordability issue is the number one issue we're hearing from the American people. It's inflation. Those Americans living paycheck to paycheck are struggling.\par \par \uc2 So these job reports come out. What they see is either they have lost their job, concerned about their wages, concerned about the fact there's not enough months to go around here to -- in terms of paycheck to meet their daily needs.\par \par \uc2 I think the affordability, the inflation issue, the paycheck-to-paycheck battles that so many Americans fight today will be the biggest issue coming into this election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is very convinced that Democrats can hang on to this 50/50 Senate and gain a couple of seats. I'm just wondering what you make of that. I don't know what polls he's looking at. They don't seem to bear out that prediction, but it is tight. What do you see?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, just look at it objectively, Neil.\par \par \uc2 First, he would have to win Texas and Florida. He'd have to beat Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . That's just not going to happen. Both Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  are running great races in their states. Look at just objective RealClearPolitics averages. They're both well up in their races. They're working hard.\par \par \uc2 They're running like they're five points behind, when indeed they are eight to nine points ahead. So I certainly bet strongly that Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  are going to win their reelects.So, Ted -- so, Schumer doesn't have a chance in those two states.\par \par \uc2 But let's look at where we have an opportunity to win additional Republican seats. Let's start with West Virginia. \uc2 Jim Justice\uc2 , Governor Justice, is going to be the next senator from West Virginia. There's one seat that we have already picked up there. That takes us to 50/50. The Democrats have given up on West Virginia. Nobody's spending a lot of money there. They have conceded that race to us.\par \par \uc2 That's 50/50. So we need one more seat out of eight to nine battleground competitive races as we speak, Neil, for the Democrats having to defend either an open seat or an incumbent race in nine other races. Look at Montana. Since President Trump came to Montana for a big rally on August 9, \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2  now is showing with polling data that's public up around six to seven points.\par \par \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2 , he has got a very good chance to beat \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  here in Montana. He likely will. There's one pickup seat. That takes us to 51. Look at Ohio. There's a state that President Trump's going to win by eight to 10 points. Bernie Moreno is an outstanding candidate there. That race is closing.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be tough for \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  to overcome an eight-to-10-point victory by President Trump and hold that seat. You look at three open seats. Democrats have retired in places like Michigan. You just talked about the autoworkers. The E.V. mandates have been a disaster for Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And, consequently, you have got a guy like \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  right now tied in Michigan for that open seat. That's it. We have got a real good chance to win Michigan. Go out to Arizona, another open seat. Kari Lake right now is tied running against her opponent, who is the most liberal Senate candidate on the ballot, \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 . We like our odds in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Look at Maryland. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , outstanding candidate, another open seat in a state that Larry was the elected Republican governor twice in a state that we know that the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will win Maryland. That's nothing new. But \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is one of these maverick, kind of independent guys that could win that seat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, he certainly is. But in the case of his situation in Maryland, Kari Lake, of course, in Arizona, the latest polls seem to show about a four-to-five-point gap favoring the Democrat. Now, that could change, to your point.\par \par \uc2 But one way or the other, it will be kind of a battle right down to November, right?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, it will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: And just remember, Schumer's got to pitch a perfect game here in every one of these races, including Montana. And he's got to beat either Ted Cruz or \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  in those states if he ever wants to hold this majority.\par \par \uc2 So just looking at it objectively --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: -- you would certainly say the Republicans are in a strong position here to win back the majority.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator Daines --\par \par \uc2 DAINES: And, by the way --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: -- we didn't talk about Pennsylvania. We didn't talk about Nevada and Wisconsin, three battleground states right now for the presidency. We have got great candidates in each of those states, very competitive races. And New Mexico with Nella Domenici as well is a keep your eye on kind of state.\par \par \uc2 So we like our odds.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: We're just going to keep our heads down, work hard and we will see what happens in November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have obviously done your homework on this stuff. So you're following it very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you. Very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Fair and balanced, Democratic Senator of Delaware Chris Coons, I think he has a slightly different view of those same races and, of course, the big national race. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the big night tonight, where we're going to hear from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' vice presidential pick, also from \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With us right now is Delaware Democrat Chris Coons, the Biden reelection campaign co-chair. He's been working very closely with the vice president to make sure she is the next president of the United States. He was listening intently, I'm sure, to Steve Daines of Montana and agrees with everything the Republican senator said about taking back the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Always good to have you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 You heard him rattle through the states.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And the numbers look very favorable for him in his eyes. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Senator Daines is the chairman of the National Republican Senatorial Campaign Committee.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 COONS: And he gave a very rosy assessment of all the different campaigns.\par \par \uc2 I will point to one of the last ones he cited. He said that Kari Lake and \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  in Arizona are neck and neck, that it's tied. I can't find any polls that suggest that it's tied. Ruben has consistently been three to five to six points up in that race.\par \par \uc2 Our Democratic incumbents, like Bob Casey in Pennsylvania, have consistently done well over the last six to nine months. So, while I would say that the map does favor Republicans, we are defending a lot of our best incumbents, our incumbents have held up very well --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- and are ahead of their competitors in race after race around the country, from Wisconsin, to Pennsylvania, to Ohio, to other key states, where there are open seats like Michigan and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, yes, and I had raised, even in the case of Maryland, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , as well-regarded a governor he was in that blue state --\par \par \uc2 COONS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: -- I believe he's down about five points. Lake, and your polls are accurate to what I have seen, a similar five-point gap.\par \par \uc2 COONS: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that could change.\par \par \uc2 I did want to get your take on another development concerning the convention and time running out for Sean O'Brien of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  to address the Democratic Convention, as he did the Republican one. He had some doubts when he was with me a couple of days. I want you to react to this, Senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Have you gotten a decision on the part of Democrats to let you speak to them?\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: As of today, not yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, I mean, I could take it personal, but I honestly think maybe my invitation got lost in the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2 , and next time, they should try and ship it UPS. It'll guarantee delivery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, he was being a little funny about that, but Senator, the basic message is, Republicans were open to hear from me, Democrats are not.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that? Should he be able to speak to the floor?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, I heard -- excuse me, I have lost my voice from yelling too much last night. I was very excited.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I hear you.\par \par \uc2 COONS: We heard a great speech from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, and one of the things he was recognizing was that President Biden has been the most pro- union president in modern history, walked a picket line, helped the \uc2 UAW\uc2  get a strong new contract.\par \par \uc2 So we certainly have heard from a labor leader, an important labor leader, as well as from \uc2 Ken Chenault\uc2 , the former CEO of \uc2 American Express\uc2 , who also spoke and said very positive things about the Biden/Harris record on low unemployment, solid job creation, stock market at an all-time high, record production of energy.\par \par \uc2 So I think there's a lot of positive economic news, and I think it was important that we heard from one of America's most respected labor leaders.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the other one is a pretty respected labor leader, too, head of the 1.3 million-member \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , right?\par \par \uc2 COONS: He is. And I --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I noticed -- and you know this schedule better. You're co-chair of the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have got some big things, obviously, to digest, and not just who's who in the speaking lineup.\par \par \uc2 But it just seems, when I look at the schedule of those who are allowed to speak, some for no more than two minutes or three minutes, but they squeeze them in there, this guy can't be squeezed in there?\par \par \uc2 COONS: They squeezed me in for just three minutes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 COONS: And, yes, there have been a number of my colleagues who have been canceled from speaking because we have got so many folks speaking and so many issues we want to make sure we get to in this convention.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. So it's not personal, Senator, for the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president?\par \par \uc2 COONS: No. No, no.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were going to tell them, all right, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tripped over broken glass to get me to speak, and I did, but not the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Yes. He is welcome.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 COONS: And the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are, of course, an important member of the labor family.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, as co-chair of the Harris campaign, would you recommend that Vice President Harris meet with him?\par \par \uc2 COONS: I would.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 COONS: I think it's important that there be active dialogue and engagement with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  326 in Delaware represents folks who are critical. The reference he made to UPS, I have visited with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  who work in delivery and in local law enforcement in Delaware. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are a long respected member of the family of labor.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 I wanted -- you obviously are a very close friend of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And everyone remembers Monday night, with his remarks getting pushed back further and further. You talk about crushing schedules. I get that, moving some people in and out to accommodate.\par \par \uc2 It didn't seem on that opening night that was done for the president. Or am I imagining it?\par \par \uc2 COONS: I'm not sure -- what's your question, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Whether he was deliberately pushed back to speak late, after 11:00 p.m., on the East Coast of the United States.\par \par \uc2 COONS: No.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Then how did it happen?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, I wasn't involved in the scheduling.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 COONS: But I -- as someone who was waiting backstage, noticed that several of the speakers before me went significantly longer than their allotted time. But I'm not the stage manager of the convention.\par \par \uc2 I will say this. There was a lot of excitement in the room. I hope you got to see the chants of "We love, Joe" that went on for minutes and minutes. He got one of the longest, loudest, most supportive and affectionate responses of any audience at any convention I have been to.\par \par \uc2 And I have been going to convention since 1988.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Were you surprised he left so fast?\par \par \uc2 COONS: No, I think it was important that he speak to the convention. And he then went on a family vacation. He's got a lot to do in the five months left that he's going to be president and then after his presidency.\par \par \uc2 He and the first lady have done a lot to honor our military families, to fight for the Cancer Moonshot, to invest in health care, to advance education and training for the work force. And I expect him to be working on those issues here at home, as well as making sure that we're defending Ukraine's democracy and working in close partnership with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies and other allies around the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, take care of that voice. Thank you for taking the time to speak with us.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator Chris Coons, a busy night for him and, of course, all the prominent Democrats on this, the third night of the Democratic Convention.\par \par \uc2 We're also keeping a close eye outside the convention hall, where those protesters are gathering some steam. It doesn't appear to be too divisive just yet, but there are planned protests. We just don't know how they will go and how many will participate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're looking live at another anti-Israeli protest taking shape outside the Democratic National Convention.\par \par \uc2 Mike Tobin is there.\par \par \uc2 Mike, what does it look like right now? Mike, we're having some problems with your audio here. Hopefully, they can adjust that.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I'm looking at crowds that have built up certainly in the last half-hour from when we last spoke to you. Can you hear me now? Let's see how you're sounding.\par \par \uc2 OK. I apologize for that.\par \par \uc2 Again, we're watching this closely. This got a little heated the last night. In fact, a couple of these protesters were able to get into the Convention Center. They're trying to make sure there's an orderly movement to all of this. We will keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 They're not expecting any real trouble, as the choice for running mate for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , of course, gets his big chance to speak tonight, as will \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Again, crowds are building, but so far tensions are not.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The image you get, Jim -- and you're closer to this than I will ever be -- is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't jump. He was pushed. And among those pushing him was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, I think it was clear that the president looked at his numbers, looked at this race, and said: I'm not helping my party. And it's time to let someone else do that.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that means he got pushed.\par \par \uc2 MARIANNE WILLIAMSON\uc2  (D), FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You were right. Messina was wrong, and we all know it. And I think it was unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well it's a done deal. It doesn't matter now at this point does it. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  left the convention after making a speech that was pushed way past prime time on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Don Peebles with us right now, the former \uc2 Obama\uc2  fund-raiser, friend of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Peebles Corporation\uc2  CEO and founder.\par \par \uc2 Don, I know you're very close to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , but a lot of people say, and maybe you have no problem with this yourself, that he was the guy who helped orchestrate, along with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  quietly leaving this race. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 DON PEEBLES, FORMER OBAMA BACKER: Well I think that it was definitely \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . I think at some point Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  told him that he did not have a pathway to victory.\par \par \uc2 And I think others in the party were also encouraging Biden to get out. What they saw is a certain defeat, and wanted to make an adjustment to go to their other option, and that was Harris, who initially was, as you may recall, the least attractive of the two candidates initially between Biden and Harris.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you surprised the party has rallied around and this ticket to the extent it has?\par \par \uc2 There was a great deal of agita back and forth about the way this went down, but it went down and the party had this brief period where it looked like it was going to be friction city. That disappeared. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES: Well, I don't think the party had any alternative. Once Biden left the race and he endorsed Harris as he left the race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : It made it very difficult for the Democrats to go in any other direction, and they needed a candidate that could step in and hit the ground running and basically jump into Biden's infrastructure and take the money, the hundreds of millions of dollars that he raised.\par \par \uc2 And that was only -- the only choice here was Harris.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, there's a lot going on in this next-to-last-night of the convention. We're going to hear from \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , who's not too bad a speaker himself, and obviously the vice presidential pick.\par \par \uc2 But I wanted to get your thoughts on this back and forth about the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president wanting to speak to Democrats, as he had an opportunity to do with Republicans. And so far, Sean O'Brien has said his phone isn't ringing. What do you make of that? Would it be a mistake for Democrats not to let him speak?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : Absolutely, it would be a mistake for Democrats not to let him speak. But the challenge has been with our party is that if you say something contrary or you are receptive to hearing from Republicans, then you're ostracized.\par \par \uc2 And that's what's happening here. They are penalizing him for speaking to Republicans. And I think that the idea here we're going to unify the country, as President \uc2 Obama\uc2  said last night, then we need to unify the country by speaking to people across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  is a very important union. But they're a more moderate union. And I think that the Democrats are making their point here, incorrectly so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I did want to point out that I don't know whether it was our interview or our chat, but Harris campaign officials are saying they do hope to meet with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, Sean O'Brien, or the greater \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  representation, et al.\par \par \uc2 We will see what happens with that. But I did want to step back as well and talk about something that union guys often talk about, and that is jobs. And we learned today in these latest revisions from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , they do this every year or so, that shows we really had 818,000 fewer than we thought we had.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that and what it portends?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : One, I think it's disturbing that they can't get their math right, but also it makes you wonder, were they beefing these numbers up intentionally?\par \par \uc2 The reality is, is that the economy was growing at a very rapid pace from 2017 to 2019, and then COVID hit, and there was massive job loss. The airlines are still recovering from it, as are other industries. And so, as a result, they're picking back up labor to the degree they can find it.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, we have a shortage, because people are working remotely, they're taking offline jobs and the like. So I think that the economy is in much worse condition than some of these numbers portend to, because I think that they are inaccurate, like the job numbers that we have, almost a million less.\par \par \uc2 That's a pretty significant number. And I think inflation, as we have talked about before, is far worse than people think it is because of the reality of the high cost of interest that's tripled during this administration.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, interest rates were tumbling today, as people, as you know this better than anyone, were buying bonds like crazy. That drives the yield down. We're at about a 1.5-year low on interest rates, mortgage rates at close to 1.33-year low.\par \par \uc2 That could be wind at the back of Democrats or if it gets really bad wind hitting them in the face. Where are you on this?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : Oh, I don't think that that's going to have much of a consequence. I think Americans are going to look at the last 3.5 years of their experience with this administration and in the country and some of the policies.\par \par \uc2 And I think that they're going to react. Many are, reacting to the economic conditions they face every day. So I think it's not going to have a consequence here. I will say this. I thought that the Democratic Convention is much more on emotion and much less on policy.\par \par \uc2 And I'm hopeful that they will start talking about some policies that can move the country forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, real quickly, you had urged \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to do the same when last week we chatted. He appears to be talking policies and issues fairly nonstop now, not so much of the personal attacks.\par \par \uc2 They're always in the eye of a holder because the other side does it to him. Is it your sense that that is a winning strategy for him?\par \par \uc2 PEEBLES\uc2 : Yes, well, let me say this. I think that the party that beats -- the person that beats \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , then it's going to be a tough race for Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Don, thank you very much. Good catching up with you, Don Peebles, big \uc2 Obama\uc2  fund-raiser, worried about Democrats, a little less so Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240821ek8l0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. Steve Daines (R-MT)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,383 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All of this with the backdrop also that something Grady got into with President Trump, this revision, this adjustment. It happens every year thereabouts, where, in this period from April 2023 to March 2024, they revised the numbers with a number of seasonal adjustments, other things.\par \par \uc2 I could go into the weeds here. Suffice it to say, the end result was 818,000 fewer jobs than originally reported. So, in this one-year period, the 2.9 million jobs that the Biden administration claims we had gained, turns out to be about 2.1 million. They also argue that, since they first came into office, it`s been, even with these adjustments, about 14 million jobs gained.\par \par \uc2 The Trump people counter and say, you`re just talking about a lot of people who are returning to work. So they hadn`t lost those jobs after COVID. They were simply returning. Be that as it may, Democrats dismissing it, Republicans having a field day with this.\par \par \uc2 Senator Steve Daines, the Republican of Montana, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Senator, you have heard about this job adjustment news, and it does happen, but this is particularly big, like record big adjustment. So some people are wondering what`s next. Are you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): Well, Neil, it`s interesting to note this is the largest downward adjustment in the numbers going back to 2009.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: This, I think, is a major hit to the Democrats. They tout these job numbers. And this is not a good report for the Democrats this week, right in the middle of their convention.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Senator, you`re in charge of making sure Republicans can retake the Senate. It`s very close. It`s a Herculean task. News like this might make a big difference in battleground states.\par \par \uc2 The administration comes back and says, gains are gains. And even with the one million adjustment, roughly, the fact of the matter is, you`re still up 14 million under the Biden/Harris administration. That`s the way they`re playing it. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, I think the voters are looking at even more important data, and that`s their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 And they realize the most important vehicle most voters in America drive today, the most expensive vehicle is their grocery cart. The affordability issue is the number one issue we`re hearing from the American people. It`s inflation. Those Americans living paycheck to paycheck are struggling.\par \par \uc2 So these job reports come out. What they see is either they have lost their job, concerned about their wages, concerned about the fact there`s not enough months to go around here to -- in terms of paycheck to meet their daily needs.\par \par \uc2 I think the affordability, the inflation issue, the paycheck-to-paycheck battles that so many Americans fight today will be the biggest issue coming into this election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is very convinced that Democrats can hang on to this 50/50 Senate and gain a couple of seats. I`m just wondering what you make of that. I don`t know what polls he`s looking at. They don`t seem to bear out that prediction, but it is tight. What do you see?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, just look at it objectively, Neil.\par \par \uc2 First, he would have to win Texas and Florida. He`d have to beat Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . That`s just not going to happen. Both Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  are running great races in their states. Look at just objective RealClearPolitics averages. They`re both well up in their races. They`re working hard.\par \par \uc2 They`re running like they`re five points behind, when indeed they are eight to nine points ahead. So I certainly bet strongly that Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  are going to win their reelects. So, Ted -- so, Schumer doesn`t have a chance in those two states.\par \par \uc2 But let`s look at where we have an opportunity to win additional Republican seats. Let`s start with West Virginia. \uc2 Jim Justice\uc2 , Governor Justice, is going to be the next senator from West Virginia. There`s one seat that we have already picked up there. That takes us to 50/50. The Democrats have given up on West Virginia. Nobody`s spending a lot of money there. They have conceded that race to us.\par \par \uc2 That`s 50/50. So we need one more seat out of eight to nine battleground competitive races as we speak, Neil, for the Democrats having to defend either an open seat or an incumbent race in nine other races. Look at Montana. Since President Trump came to Montana for a big rally on August 9, \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2  now is showing with polling data that`s public up around six to seven points.\par \par \uc2 Tim Sheehy\uc2 , he has got a very good chance to beat \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  here in Montana. He likely will. There`s one pickup seat. That takes us to 51. Look at Ohio. There`s a state that President Trump`s going to win by eight to 10 points. Bernie Moreno is an outstanding candidate there. That race is closing.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be tough for \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  to overcome an eight-to-10-point victory by President Trump and hold that seat. You look at three open seats. Democrats have retired in places like Michigan. You just talked about the autoworkers. The E.V. mandates have been a disaster for Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And, consequently, you have got a guy like Mike Rogers right now tied in Michigan for that open seat. That`s it. We have got a real good chance to win Michigan. Go out to Arizona, another open seat. Kari Lake right now is tied running against her opponent, who is the most liberal Senate candidate on the ballot, \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 . We like our odds in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Look at Maryland. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , outstanding candidate, another open seat in a state that Larry was the elected Republican governor twice in a state that we know that the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will win Maryland. That`s nothing new. But \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is one of these maverick, kind of independent guys that could win that seat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, he certainly is. But in the case of his situation in Maryland, Kari Lake, of course, in Arizona, the latest polls seem to show about a four-to-five-point gap favoring the Democrat. Now, that could change, to your point.\par \par \uc2 But one way or the other, it will be kind of a battle right down to November, right?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, it will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: And just remember, Schumer`s got to pitch a perfect game here in every one of these races, including Montana. And he`s got to beat either Ted Cruz or \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  in those states if he ever wants to hold this majority.\par \par \uc2 So just looking at it objectively --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: -- you would certainly say the Republicans are in a strong position here to win back the majority.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator Daines --\par \par \uc2 DAINES: And, by the way --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: -- we didn`t talk about Pennsylvania. We didn`t talk about Nevada and Wisconsin, three battleground states right now for the presidency. We have got great candidates in each of those states, very competitive races. And New Mexico with Nella Domenici as well is a keep your eye on kind of state.\par \par \uc2 So we like our odds.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: We`re just going to keep our heads down, work hard and we will see what happens in November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have obviously done your homework on this stuff. So you`re following it very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you. Very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240821ek8l0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O`Brien\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 2,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a scab. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is all talk. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  walks the walk.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 SEAN O`BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: President Trump is a candidate who is not afraid of hearing from new, loud and often critical voices.\par \par \uc2 And I think we all can agree, whether people like him or they don`t like him, in light of what happened to him on Saturday, he has proven to be one tough SOB.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, that last gentleman you saw is Sean O`Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president.\par \par \uc2 That was only a couple of days, when he had an opportunity speak to the Republican Convention, after that assassination attempt on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It was very unusual even then for a union leader to be addressing Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Sean O`Brien had been trying to get the same opportunity with Democrats. Have yet to hear whether that`s happening, because Democrats have had no problem hearing from his counterpart at the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , Shawn Fain.\par \par \uc2 So, Sean O`Brien, to you then. Have you gotten a decision on the part of Democrats to let you speak to them?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: As of today, not yet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, I mean, I could take it personal, but I honestly think maybe my invitation got lost in the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2 , and next time, they should try and ship it UPS. It`ll guarantee delivery.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Have you had any overtures with the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign itself?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, the only -- the only commitment we got on Friday of last week, that they finally agreed to come to the roundtable and address our General Executive Board and the roundtable of rank-and-file members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of people are wondering where you`re tipping your hand right now, where you lean\par \par \uc2 And these comments that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had in a streamed interview with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  apparently got your goat. First, I want you to react to what at the time was something that took you by surprise, again, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  discussing strikes. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You, you`re the greatest cutter. I mean, I look at what you do. You walk in and you just say, you want to quit?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, OWNER, X: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They go on strike. They -- I won`t mention the name of the company, but they go on strike and you say, that`s OK, you`re all gone. You`re all gone. So every one of you is gone.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Sean O`Brien, who spoke at the Republican National Convention, saying that firing workers who are wanting to organize or striking is economic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 So, just to be clear, that`s something that you are uncomfortable with?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, that`s not -- yes, they weren`t organizing against Elon. He let them go because he was having a lot of problems in California. He was making -- they were making it impossible for him in California. What they were doing with the taxes and everything were making it impossible for him.\par \par \uc2 And Sean O`Brien`s a great guy from the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , you`re talking? Yes, Sean is great. I think Sean would understand it better than anybody. He`s - - don`t forget, the unions are really threatened by what`s coming in by these millions and millions of people coming in.\par \par \uc2 Sean O`Brien understands that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, it`s interesting, Sean, because the other Shawn, spelled differently, said that really showed Donald Trump`s true colors.\par \par \uc2 I want you to respond to what Shawn Fain had to say about that that.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, Shawn Fain...\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Sean Trump is all talk. And talk is cheap. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t know (EXPLETIVE DELETED) about the auto industry.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 FAIN: And he doesn`t give a damn about the working class in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To be fair, Sean -- and I apologize. That was not in response to Donald Trump`s remarks.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you have an opportunity. He did respond very kind of bluntly, but that was not the response.\par \par \uc2 But how did you feel about that? Because you had a colorful response yourself. What did you think of it?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, look, I think everybody that knows me knows that I call balls and strikes. And if someone`s saying something that we don`t agree with -- and I don`t agree with any employer ever firing workers who exercise their right to form unions.\par \par \uc2 I think I called it economic terrorism. And that`s what it was. And, look, whether I like you or I don`t like you, if you`re going to take on American workers and you`re not going to treat them fairly, then you`re fair game, and you`re going to have to deal with the consequences. And everybody has their issues.\par \par \uc2 I don`t speak for Shawn Fain. Shawn Fain certainly doesn`t speak for the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  or myself. But the reality is, is, look, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  -- it`s no secret. For the last 2.5 years, I have taken on corporate America. I have fought with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . I have fought with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . I have fought with \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . I have fought with all these elitists.\par \par \uc2 And the one thing that`s clear is that record profits equal unpaid wages. And that`s what we have to focus on. That comment by President Trump, just like if anybody else made that, if President Biden made a comment or anybody, I would have responded accordingly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But President Biden hasn`t made a comment like that. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has not made a comment like that. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: My thoughts are, look, he made a comment, and he got called out on it.\par \par \uc2 Look, the DNC failed to invite one of the most powerful unions in the country there. And I`m going to criticize them for that as well. I`m a lifelong Democrat, always will be a lifelong Democrat, never going to change my party affiliation.\par \par \uc2 Look, you heard my speech at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . It was all about the American work, all about labor. Any opportunity that I get as a leader of the greatest union in the world to showcase our value and our worth to the greatest country in the world, I`m going to take that opportunity to do it. And if the far left want to criticize me and attack me, that`s OK. If the far right wants to do the same, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 I represent 1.3 million hardworking men and women.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the Democrats to which you express this affinity, either they`re taking you for granted or they don`t care about inviting you, period. So they`re not all into you. Maybe they`re just not into you, period.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, you know what it is? For so long, I think both parties -- and I talked about corporate elitists. I talked about the Ubers of the world, the Lyfts of the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I talked about \uc2 Amazon\uc2  at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, those are big donor classes for the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , but they`re also big donor classes for the DNC. And there`s relationships there at the DNC that maybe they didn`t want me to speak there, because I would have talked to that same group. I would have exposed those same corporate elitists who attack us every single day.\par \par \uc2 Look, Teamster jobs are attacked by companies like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , the contractor model, the autonomous vehicle proposals out there. And there`s no secret that there`s relationships on both sides with the \uc2 Uber\uc2 , Lyfts, the Amazons of the world. And the reality of it is maybe they don`t want my voice heard there.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you one thing. Our members, 1.3 million, their voices will be heard.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but you`re not invited to the Democratic Convention. You`re not invited to talk with anyone on the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  staff yet. Maybe that could change.\par \par \uc2 You were invited to the Republican Convention. You got high praise from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . As things stand now, given those dynamics, where do you personally lean?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: So the most important thing to me is, I got high praise from my members. We polled our members on my appearance at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , and we polled my speech.\par \par \uc2 And only 11 percent of the members disapproved with me being there. I didn`t get into partisan issues. I got into issues that affect working people in this country. Also -- and I didn`t hear this last night. I also encouraged collaboration both sides, because, right now, there`s such a line drawn on the sand and there`s such venom throughout this country.\par \par \uc2 There`s nothing that can get done. And we need to stop that. We need to focus.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that`s not what I asked, Sean. I mean, given all of that, of what Republicans liked you and liked hearing from you, Democrats don`t give a damn whether they get a chance to hear from you.\par \par \uc2 Just on that level, what do you think? Real quick.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, listen, they have taken their position, and I`m going to take my position moving forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: And we have given the Democrats a lot of money over the last 2.5 years, much more than we have given the Republicans. And, look, that`s what it comes down to at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Will you stop doing that if you`re not invited, if they ignore you?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Listen, I`m not -- we`re going to invest in candidates on both sides that are actually going to represent working people. And that`s what we`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 There`s going to be no more free rides for anybody.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, wait a minute. They`re not inviting you. You would not consider now withholding funds until they do and until something changes, what?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, we`re going to look at situations.\par \par \uc2 Look, this...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: The DNC`s over with. We`re going to look at situations. Like they always do, both sides come to us asking us for support. And if the situation isn`t right for our members, they`re not going to get the free ride that they have gotten under our previous administration or anybody else.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is -- this is -- we`re not upset about it. It`s just, the reality is, they didn`t invite us. And we`re going to -- we have been around 121 years. We`re going to continue to be around for another 121 years. And if people`s noses are out of their joints because we have a voice in America -- everybody talked about how essential \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  were during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 You haven`t heard one, one iota from both sides on how the essential workers are now. You don`t hear anything about essential workers. We are still very relevant and essential to this country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you wouldn`t know it at the Democratic Convention thus far.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I guess that`s how you can look at it, but I`m looking at it as an opportunity, an opportunity to, again, showcase how valuable we are. And we will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Sean O`Brien, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240820ek8k0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Harris Outlines Economic Plan In North Carolina Speech; Harris Pricing Proposals Draw Criticism; Venezuela Suffers Food Shortages From Price Controls; Trump Says Entitled To Attack Democratic Opponents; Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Outlines Inflation And Jobs Agenda; Former Chief of Staff To New Jersey Democratic Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  To Replace \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  In \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ; Class Action Lawsuit Reveals Personal Information Of Nearly Three Billion People Stolen; U.S. Continues Efforts To Return American Journalist Austin Tice Who Was Captured In Syria In 2012; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's Economic Platform Includes Anti Price Gouging Measures And Providing Help With Down Payments For First Time Home Buyers\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Edward Lawrence, Aishah Hasnie, Jeff  Paul, Larry Kudlow, Gillian Turner, Ben Domenech, Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 7,718 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: In addition to Mayo, the fragrance also has notes of lemon, parsley, vanilla, and coffee. He has a lifetime supply now coming to him, a Mayo, and it's already sold out. But it will be restocked, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Oh, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SSGT. JOHNNY JOEY JONES (RET), FOX NEWS MEDIA CONTRIBUTOR: He puts -- he put a mayonnaise in his coffee, that's why he do this.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: This is produced -- you can smell like -- so, what is it? It's cologne.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's a Mayo scented cologne.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. We got to go. That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, Judge, I think you should come to Chicago anyway.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I want to come to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Just come on.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's just that right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Come on.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Come on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I will work to pass the first ever federal ban on price gouging on food. My plan will include new penalties for opportunistic companies that exploit crises and break the rules, and we will support smaller food businesses that are trying to play by the rules and get ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We begin tonight with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  outlining her economic plan, or part of it. The Democratic presidential nominee, speaking this afternoon in Raleigh, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 She says she will build what she calls an opportunity economy the centerpiece, as you just heard there is a federal ban on price gouging for food, but it also includes steps to address the U.S. housing crisis.\par \par \uc2 The proposals have raised eyebrows with some economists, and some of them from previous Democratic administrations.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live in Raleigh. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Biden had Bidenomics, Harris has opportunity economy. Her agenda unveiled today has some proposals that build off of Bidens, like capping prescription drug costs and expanding the child tax credit, but the new ones focus on bringing down the cost of housing and groceries, calling to construct 3 million new starter homes and provide 25 grand in down payment assistance to 4 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 The most controversial though, addressing food prices is expanding the authority of the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to go after corporate price gouging for groceries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Together, we will build what I call an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Vice President Harris's agenda to curb costs for groceries and housing getting mixed reviews and not from the usual critics. Obama's former economic adviser \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  saying of her proposed federal ban on grocery price gouging, "This is not sensible policy. There is no upside here, and there is some downside."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  opinion columnist writes, "At best, this would lead to shortages, black markets and hoarding. At worst, it might accidentally raise prices. If your opponent claims you're a communist, maybe don't start with an economic agenda that can accurately be labeled as federal price controls."\par \par \uc2 Harris says this is about punishing companies that exploit crises.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I know most businesses are creating jobs, contributing to our economy, and playing by the rules, but some are not.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): And her supporters say she's done this before.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR WHITE HOUSE ADVISOR: What she did when she was attorney general of California, she wasn't out there trying to set prices for people. She was going after bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The \uc2 National Association of Home Builders\uc2  praised Harris' plan to fix the supply shortage with builder tax credits for more starter homes. But also warned, down payment assistance and tax credits for first time buyers will further drive demand.\par \par \uc2 Trump has likened Harris' plans to communism, but she took aim at his proposal to hike tariffs on imports.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A Trump tax on gas, a Trump tax on food, a Trump tax on clothing, a Trump tax on over-the-counter medication.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's plan would cost a typical family $3,900 a year.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The nonpartisan Committee for A Responsible Federal Budget says Harris's plans come with a cost too. Increasing deficits by $1.7 trillion over a decade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget notes that the Harris campaign says this would all be paid for through taxes on corporations and high earners. But they have not put forward a specific set of offsets, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And obviously it all has to get through Congress. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris is not the first politician to advocate government pricing controls in an effort to fight inflation. Tonight, Edward Lawrence of Fox Business looks at how the idea has worked out in the past.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Price controls sound good on paper, especially in times of economic hardship.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: As president, I will take on the high costs that matter most to most Americans.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But some research suggests, they should stay in the history books. The St. Louis Federal Reserve researched price controls and found higher prices help regulate demand for more limited items. If you set a price ceiling, like with rent control, more people will be able to buy the product or afford the apartments, causing shortages.\par \par \uc2 If you set a floor like minimum wage, it raises prices, causing what economists call a glut, like when Rubio's coastal grill closed 48 locations in California, when minimum wage went to $20 an hour because the company could not afford to pay all its employees.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON\uc2 , 37th PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am today ordering a freeze on all prices and wages throughout the United States for a period of 90 days.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (voice over): That was August 1971, former President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  froze prices and wages for the first time since World War II. That move a political success, but economic disaster, leading to recession and stagflation, which is marked by simultaneous inflation and high unemployment.\par \par \uc2 PBS\uc2  documented the scene in the late 70s as the Carter administration could not recover from price controls on gasoline.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: By the time the pump is opened, many had been waiting two and three hours for gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I've been here since 4:30 this morning. It's ridiculous waiting in line here. I couldn't get gas Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Republicans say going down the road of price controls leads down the wrong path.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): What you'll see, if she has her way are lower supplies of goods and services to people who need them most.\par \par \uc2 This stuff has never worked in any country and at any time in the history of the world, let alone the history of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): And countries like Venezuela, institute of food price controls in 2014 set by Nicolas Maduro, the country ill prepared to deal with the demand, still seeing food shortages. Other countries like Argentina and Greece, also seeing from the side effects, problems, you know, largely around the world, price controls has not worked. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Edward Lawrence at the White House. Edward, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're getting reaction to all of these plans from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he adds a familiar face to his campaign team. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the story. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. So, tonight, the Trump campaign is calling the Harris economic plan, B.S.\par \par \uc2 The president expanding on that on Truth Social, saying, "If you think things are expensive now, they will get 100 times worse, if Kamala gets four years as president. Under her plan, Kamala will implement Soviet style price controls."\par \par \uc2 It is a battle over who can fix the economy. And last night, the former president posted a TikTok video about the rising price of groceries like eggs, and milk, and butter, saying it's a "disaster".\par \par \uc2 Also, last night, Trump pushed back on allies who say he needs to tone it down on the personal attacks. Trump says that he is "earned the right" to go after Harris and President Biden because they use the justice department to go after him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm very angry at her that she weaponized the justice system against me and other people -- very angry at her. I think I'm entitled to personal attacks.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): So, meanwhile, Trump's newly appointed senior adviser, \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2 , pouring water today, cold water on reports that he's been brought in because Trump may want to push out co-campaign chairs, Chris Lacivita and Susie Wiles.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: There is going to be no change at the top?\par \par \uc2 COREY LEWANDOWSKI\uc2 , TRUMP 2024 CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: I believe that's to be -- I believe that to be true, Martha. I absolutely do.\par \par \uc2 Look, Chris, and Susie are consummate professionals. They have steered a very impressive shift through here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Trump's running mate, Senator J.D. Vance, in the meantime, was in Wisconsin today, speaking to the Milwaukee Police Association. The pair and their surrogates, we are told, will be offering some counter programming next week in an effort to try to steal some of the spotlight from Harris and Walz at the Democratic National Convention.\par \par \uc2 Lewandowski tells Fox News that Trump is going to ramp up his travel in these last 80 days. Gosh, 80 days. That's it. Getting in front of voters he is rallying in Pennsylvania tomorrow. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Countdown is on. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Market comeback rolled on for a seventh consecutive day. Thanks to fading recession fears. The Dow gaining 97. The S&P 500 was up 11. The NASDAQ rose 37. It was the best week for Wall Street since November.\par \par \uc2 The Dow surging almost three percentage points. The S&P 500 gained almost four. The NASDAQ jumped 5-1/3.\par \par \uc2 President, Biden says negotiators are closer than they have ever been to a cease fire and hostage deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Talks are expected to resume next week, as relatives and friends of hostages are making their feelings known in Israel.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent Jeff Paul reports from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): They march in the streets of Tel Aviv, many of them, family of hostages, knowing this latest round of negotiations might be their last chance.\par \par \uc2 GADI AMICHAI, NEPHEW OF \uc2 HAMAS\uc2  HOSTAGE: If it won't happen now, we believe that none of the hostages will be alive.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): As the fighting between Israeli forces and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  continues, we're learning so too will the negotiations to bring the war to an end. After two days of talks in Doha, described by officials as both positive and constructive, another round starts next week in Cairo.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, the U.S. Egypt, and Qatar, said, in part, "This proposal builds on areas of agreement over the past week, and bridges remaining gaps in the manner that allows for a swift implementation of the deal."\par \par \uc2 That deal, the work of President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are closer than we ever been. I don't want to jinx anything. It is much, much closer than it was three days ago. So, keep your fingers crossed.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): Without providing specifics, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has reportedly accused Israel of not sticking to the original deal. But Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , says, "Israel appreciates the efforts of the U.S. and the mediators to dissuade \uc2 Hamas\uc2  from its refusal to a hostage release deal."\par \par \uc2 But adding to the tension, an attack by Israeli settlers on a Palestinian village in the West Bank that killed at least one. In the latest of a series of attacks condemned by both the White House and the Israeli military, witnesses say the settlers burned cars and at least one was hurt by gunfire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is set to travel here to Israel tomorrow to continue ongoing diplomatic efforts. With the ongoing negotiations, a senior White House official also saying they are working on closing the remaining gaps in this deal next week in Cairo to finally solidify a ceasefire once and for all. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul, live in Tel Aviv. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 There is new reporting tonight about an alleged Ukrainian operation to sabotage the Nord Stream pipeline. Wall Street Journal, writing, Ukrainian private businessmen funded a shoestring operation that was initially approved by President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . The CIA reportedly warned him to call it off, and he tried, but the general in charge ignored him -- according to the reporting. In May of 2022, the plan was allegedly carried out by six people on a small rented yacht. The twin natural gas pipelines targeted had carried Russian natural gas to Europe.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  blamed the U.S. Ukrainian officials deny any involvement in the attack. An investigation by German authorities has yielded one arrest. It is now focusing on the general in charge and his aides. That general was replaced by President Zelenskyy. He told The Wall Street Journal he knows nothing of any such operation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we get some analysis of Vice President Harris's economic plan from \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  of Fox Business. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am today ordering a freeze on all prices and wages throughout the United States for a period of 90 days, while the wage price freeze will be backed by government sanctions, if necessary, it will not be accompanied by the establishment of a huge price control bureaucracy. I am relying on the voluntary cooperation of all Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that was President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  with his plan to have the government control wages and prices back in 1971. Now, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to try a version of it.\par \par \uc2 The headlines back then in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , not that complimentary, this 90 day wage price freeze. And there were a lot of articles about it.\par \par \uc2 Now, the articles that we see about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , when your opponent calls you communists, maybe don't propose price controls. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's economic plan, good politics, meh policy, how the Biden-Harris economy left most Americans behind.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's get some analysis from \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  of Fox Business. He joins us from New York. Good evening, Larry, I can imagine your take, but let me have it.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Thanks for having me, Bret. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is price controls by any other name. I mean, it's like a sheep in sheep's clothing. It's price controls because it's price controls.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing, I mean, we know how bad that turned out for Nixon. Price controls never worked in Russia, Soviet Union, Venezuela, Cuba, they don't -- they never work. They cause shortages, they cause black markets, and eventually they cause higher prices, because the controls are lifted, because people are yelling about shortages.\par \par \uc2 But no one really knows what she means by price gouging. There's no definition. There's a lot of blah, blah blah in the rule -- in their announcement about rules in the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  has tried a bunch of cases about price gouging, or so called corporate greed, and they have lost them all over the past 3-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 And one more thing, I just look -- and I mean, she -- Ms. Harris yelling about beef producers, I guess, and poultry producers and grocery stores. So, I looked at their corporate margins. Are they making any money?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if they're gouging somebody, Bret, they must be enormously profitable and they are not, OK? The grocery stores profit margins 1.2 percent. Meat, beef and poultry, 4.7 percent. All of American business, 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Look, you should -- if she wants to go after somebody for making too much money. Here's what? \uc2 Apple\uc2 , 26 percent. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , 36 percent. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , 53 percent. You see where I'm going on this, there's no case to be made. No one knows what it is, but it's bad. If it ever worked, it would be bad.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The grocery stores, if they were price gouging, they're just really, really bad at it, because at 1.43 percent, it's not that good of a margin.\par \par \uc2 Let me play a couple sound bites from Democrats who are defending as we're trying to right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been clear he wants to further cut taxes for the most profitable and biggest corporations and for the richest Americans, there will be a stark contrast in the economic policies of these two candidates.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FORMER BARACK OBAMA ADVISER: This is really going to be a plan about lowering costs and making things more affordable. And you know, no one is saying that it's easy out there, but we're certainly getting an economy that's getting better and better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not a lot of specifics there on defending the specifics of the plan. What she did say was the tariffs that former President Trump wants to install will then essentially be a tax on people. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Well, we don't know that he's going to have a baseline tariff. Actually, in his North Carolina speech, he didn't mention the baseline tariff. He mentioned reciprocity that is unfair trading practices and negotiations with, say, China or other countries that may be hurting us.\par \par \uc2 So, in a sense, that's a straw horse. It's a lot of phony stuff. We don't know that.\par \par \uc2 Trump will cut. Look, the Trump tax cuts and you know, every study, \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2 , different Federal Reserve people, economists from Princeton and \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 , the biggest beneficiaries of the Trump tax cuts were the middle class and the lower middle class, working folks. Because corporations make more money, small businesses make more money. They pay their employees more money. You can't have jobs. You can't have an employment without a good employer.\par \par \uc2 But I just want to say something else in the plan that we heard today. This is the troublesome part. I know the price controls are the headline, and I get that but, there's a massive volume of spending through the tax code. They are tax credits for almost everything under the sun.\par \par \uc2 And I talked to a bunch of people who know about these things, you are going to run at least $2 trillion in additional spending and deficits and therefore borrowing.\par \par \uc2 And I want to make that point, because nobody could be serious about getting inflation down unless we get spending down, and unless we get deficit spending down.\par \par \uc2 Bret, this is so important, and it's overlooked in the criticisms about price controls, but it's part of that plan. It's going to be upwards --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Larry, the Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget put it at 1.7 trillion over a decade, and would go to two trillion if the temporary housing policy is in there.\par \par \uc2 You have to -- you have to also admit and you worked in the Trump administration, that there was not a lot of deficit debt reduction in the Trump administration either in the big picture. So, both Republicans and Democrats have added significantly to the national debt.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes, but can I just tell you, I mean, he had to spend emergency money on COVID. OK, you can't deny that, it was bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Wall Street -- the Wall Street Journal editorial today, which I think was very important, it basically shows that Biden deficits were twice what Trump's deficits were. That's the Wall Street Journal editorial. It's not my view. That's what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, look, there's just -- look, let's face it, if you want a permanent end to the inflation, then we are going to have to have spending. One thing Trump said that I really liked, said it in North Carolina, and he said it at Bedminster yesterday. He wants to rescind unspent -- you know, obligated but unspent funds, and that in the misnamed Inflation Reduction Act and other acts, the Building Acts, there's probably over a trillion dollars, he wants to go after that. It's in his platform, and there will be more discussion about that.\par \par \uc2 So, my sense of it is, right now the Democrats, they never mentioned spending, Bret, come on, they never mentioned deficits. They're going to give out tax credits to everything under the sun, and they want a socialist type price control policy.\par \par \uc2 Look, it won't work economically. Everybody knows that. I'm going to suggest to you politically, it's going to be very unpopular. People do not want to hear that government is running the economy. I don't believe they want that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, yes, and the other question is whether it's realistic at all to get through any place like Congress. Larry, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Thank you. Thank you. Bret, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, could you be one of the nearly three billion people whose personal information has ended up on the dark web? We'll have a live report from Miami on that.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are coming tonight, WJZY in Charlotte, a police capture a convicted murderer who escaped from the hospital during a checkup. Ramone Alston was detained in an overnight raid on a hotel that ended a three day manhunt for that fugitive.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Memphis, with the arrest of a Missouri woman charged in connection to the bogus foreclosure sale of Graceland. The Justice Department says Lisa Janine Findley claimed Lisa Marie Presley owed almost $4 million to her company. Prosecutors say that company does not exist.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Honolulu, one of the big stories there from KHON, a sunny Honolulu today, the city hosts the 2024 Pokemon world championships. Did you know there was that?\par \par \uc2 The competition runs through Sunday at the Hawaii Convention Center. About 14,000 people expected to attend, including around 3,000 competitors. Pokemon. There you go.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has denied the Biden administration's efforts to enforce its new policies protecting LGBTQ+ students from classroom discrimination. Ten Republican led states had challenged the revised rules to cover sexual orientation and gender identity in the Title IX sex discrimination law. The cases continue to be litigated on the merits in the lower courts, but, for now, those new policies cannot go into effect.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey Democratic Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  has picked his former chief of staff to replace \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Menendez resigned after being convicted of corruption. Murphy named fellow Democrat George Helmy to fill Menendez's seat. The move will maintain the party's 51 to 49 majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 It could be the biggest data breach in history. The personal information of almost 3 billion, with a "b", people has been leaked onto the dark web. This could mean identity theft, fraud, other crimes against you and your family. Correspondent Danamarie McNicholl has details tonight from Miami.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Nearly 3 billion personal information records, including names, Social Security numbers, and addresses were reportedly exposed by hackers, according to a class action lawsuit. Court documents say the criminals stole the information from National Public Data, a background check company that sells access to personal information.\par \par \uc2 CLIFF STEINHAUER, NATIONAL CYBERSECURITY ALLIANCE: We didn't give permission for them to collect that data. There's hundreds of data collection companies out there.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: A California resident filed the suit after learning his and other personal information had been found for sale on the dark web. Christopher Hoffman was alerted of the breach only by his identity theft protection subscription rather than by National Public Data. Just yesterday, after the lawsuit made headlines, did the company confirm the sensitive data was leaked. The suit claimed NPD was negligent since the data was not encrypted, which experts say is a basic cybersecurity measure.\par \par \uc2 LOU RABON, CYBER DEFENSE GROUP CEO: Why is this company, you know, why do they have all of that information? And when they had it, who was making sure that they were protecting it?\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: Cyber experts also say it's hard for feel know if they were affected. They recommend preventative measures like freezing your credit, which can stop scammers from taking out loans or opening new credit cards in your name.\par \par \uc2 STEINHAUER: These are highly skilled, highly trained people who are very good at their job, whose job it is every single day is to come to work and try to attack American businesses and people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (on camera): And the lawsuit is one of several. It asks the background check company not only for $5 million in damages, but to start encrypting their data as well as hiring a cybersecurity company to monitor their measures for the next 10 years. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Danamarie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "Whatever Happened To?" segment, the effort to win freedom for journalist Austin Tice who was kidnapped in Syria in August of 2012. President Biden issued a statement this week on the 12 year anniversary of Tice's captivity, saying, quote, "I will continue to do everything possible to advocate for and pursue his release and support his loved ones until he is safely returned home."\par \par \uc2 State Department correspondent Gillian Turner looks at where exactly those efforts stand tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The return of Austin Tice is a top priority for the United States that we continue to work on, really, every day.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The Biden administration says 12 years on from the shock disappearance of American journalist and Marine veteran Austin Tice in Syria, bringing him home still tops their agenda.\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL, STATE DEPARTMENT PRINCIPAL DEPUTY SPOKESMAN: And this is something that is so personal to not just Secretary Blinken but also personal to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Tice's parents have said they lost faith in the administration.\par \par \uc2 DEBRA TICE, MOTHER: We have seen what commitment and dedication and determination means in trying to receive hostages, right? Austin has never experienced that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Tice, a seventh generation Texan covering the civil war in northern Syria was kidnapped three days after his 31st birthday in August 2012 under murky circumstances.\par \par \uc2 TICE: He was taken at a check point by a private person. And it was through a series of unfortunate events that he landed with the federal government of Syria.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The Tices told FOX News they were full of hope in November of 2020 when then President Trump wrote directly to Syrian dictator \uc2 Bashar Al-Assad\uc2  asking to work together.\par \par \uc2 TICE: We have never been closing to having Austin home.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We are working very hard with Syria to get him out. We hope the Syrian government will do that.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Those hopes again renewed just weeks ago when President Biden negotiated a complex prisoner swap involving seven countries and 24 people.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And now their brutal ordeal is over and they're free.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But no tice. The U.S. believes he remains captive inside enemy territory.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every day when the sun rises, it's still a story for me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Syria insists it doesn't have Tice but President Biden said back in 2022 he knew, quote, "with certainty" that Tice had been held by the Syrian governments. Right now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is offering a reward of up to $1 million for information that leads directly to Tice's recovery. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with reaction to Vice President Harris's economic policy reveal, setting up the DNC in Chicago, and the latest from the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I will work to pass the first ever federal ban on price gaging.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): This really is a spot-on agenda when you consider the number one issue on voters' minds, and it's how to cope with the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 JON TAFFER, "BAR RESCUE" HOST: Unless you freeze every single cost of every business in the country, you cannot cap some of them. And, of course, Neil, if you were to do that, then our free market economy is gone. And this is socialism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of reaction to the economic plans as laid out by Vice President Harris today. As mentioned before, the Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget did a list of all the possible things talked about today and what that cost would be over a 10-year deficit impact. And there you see the total at $1.7 trillion.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " had this quote, ""Egg prices went up last year -- it's because there weren't as many eggs, and it caused more egg production," said \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , a Harvard economist formerly in the Obama administration. "This is not sensible policy, and I think the biggest hope is that it ends up being a lot of rhetoric and no reality," he said. "There's no upside here and there is some downside.""\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," and Democratic campaign consultant \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2 . Kevin, is this going to sell?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC CAMPAIGN CONSULTANT: I think it is. Listen, the vice president is actually taking a play out of the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  playbook of 2016, talking like a populist, talking about her middle class roots. I think she gave this effective speech tonight in Raleigh where she talked about the fact that she was working at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's to put herself through college, that her mom saved up over 10 years to afford their first house growing up. These are all things that resonate with the American people, as she is still continuing to introduce herself to the American people. Of course, that will be on display next week in Chicago as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Here's Jonathan Martin of "Politico", a guy I respect, writes a lot about politics. His thought on this rollout.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN, "POLITICO": Price gouging is obviously not ideal from a sort of marketplace standpoint. But look, she is trying to sort of buy enough credibility with the voters.\par \par \uc2 This is all window dressing. This is entirely about how do we find a way to offer just enough to the American voter to say that we're proposing something, when you and I know really the centerpiece of this election for Democrats is stop Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, Kimberley, all window dressing.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": It's pretty radical window dressing. I mean, look, how did we get here? Essentially it was massive amounts of spending and regulation. And look, her problem is she can't just continue to say what Biden has been saying, this is all good and it's getting better. She is also not going to refute that spending. So what are they doing here? They are doubling down on Bidenomics, massive new spending, you just put up that list, for housing, childcare, et cetera. And then not only more regulation, but the worst type, the type that everybody in the whole world has ever tried knows doesn't work, which is price controls.\par \par \uc2 So, look, true, it's got go through Congress. The question can be, does the Trump campaign have enough focus to really make that distinction point out about what this is in reality? Bidenomics, we know that's not popular with Americans. I think if they make that point, then this is a liability for her.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, two years ago this week on the Senate floor, this happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: On this vote, the yeas are 50, the nays are 50. The Senate being equally divided, the vice president votes in the affirmative, and the bill as amended is passed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was $1.9 trillion, and a lot of people point to that and that vote and that bill that was called the Inflation Reduction Act, which didn't, as one of the biggest problems.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: That is the key bit of tape for the next 80 days, Bret, because when she voted for that, she unleashed this massive tidal wave of federal spending which caused, of course, increased demand for everything and skyrocketing prices.\par \par \uc2 I was astonished today. It took her 25 days. She hadn't been seen or heard from on policy for 25 days -- 25 days to come forward with a policy that failed 50 years ago. I ran the Nixon library. I worked for President Nixon out of college. I built the library and ran the foundation twice. Do you know what we do not have an exhibit on? Wage and price controls, because they were an absolute failure. No one liked wage and price controls.\par \par \uc2 And for her to start with that -- one more point. She talked a lot about housing today. The last people you want to consult on housing is anyone from California, and the very last person is anyone from San Francisco. The housing shortage there is so chronic and so longstanding, I can't even believe -- it gives "chutzpah" an entire new meaning for her to talk about housing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's play that. Here's the vice president on credits for home buyers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My administration will provide first-time home buyers with $25,000 --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: -- to help with the down payment on a new home.\par \par \uc2 I will work in partnership with industry to build the housing we need both to rent and to buy. We will take down barriers and cut red tape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Kevin, critics of this point out this could mean that housing prices go up $25,000. And they find it a little tough that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to be the person cutting red tape and regulations when you point to, as Hugh mentioned, California, which is chockfull of regulations and red tape that she was a part of for years.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Bret, listen, I think she is speaking to an issue a lot of Americans care about. I know a lot of people in 20s and 30s that are really struggling to get into their first-time home. We had a pilot program here in D.C. that did something very similar that was very successful in putting middle class young people into their first homes. That's the key to a successful and secure retirement. And that's the key to building equity and wealth. So, I think work it from both sides, making it more affordable by these tax credits that would be helpful. A lot of the biggest hurdles are down payments for a lot of young people and middle class people trying to get into their first home, while at the same time, increasing supply out there, working with industry, cutting red tape, to your point, Bret, and making 3 million more housing units a reality in this country in the next couple years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Kimberley, I guess the skepticism comes from a candidate in 2019 that wanted to ban fracking and get rid of plastic straws. The regulation and cutting red tape is, you know, it's a new thing.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes. Well, I mean, let's ask \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The reality is this is also a bit of an oversold promise because presidents can't really much impact things like local zoning and all the red tape that actually are the problem for a lot of the home builders these days. But let's look, this presidency and administration had plenty of opportunities to work with Republicans on actual permitting reform at the federal level for all kinds of projects. We have got reports right now about President Biden, the number of projects that were part of that infrastructure act that are going nowhere because they are absolutely drowning under federal red tape. So, we don't have much of a track record here to believe any of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, stand by, panel. Up next, the Friday Lightning Round, and then "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY DUCKWORTH (D-IL): We very much are welcoming people to express their right to freedom of speech, and, in fact, there will be an area that protesters can be in to express themselves. We even created a march route, a parade route so that people can actually mount protest marches if that's what they want to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Tammy Duckworth talking about the DNC convention in Chicago and what will happen there. We're back with the panel, Hugh, these cease- fire talks are continuing into next week, don't know if they are going to come to fruition. But it looks like there will be some anti-Israel protests in pretty decent numbers in Chicago. We just don't know what they look like.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: That's a given, Bret. I don't think it's going to be 1968. I have to think the city of Chicago is better prepared than they were in 1968 for violence. Yes, big protests, yes, anti-Israel protests, but the Democrats have been trying to ignore them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's do some Winners and Losers. Winner first, then loser. Kimberley?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: So my winner is \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who realized a little bit more of his commitment to free speech, social media this week with the return of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to X in a well-watched event.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And your loser?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Oh, and my loser, Minouche Shafik, the president of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  whose failure to deal consistently with some moral clarity with the demonstrators on her campus now appears to have lost her her job.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Kevin, winner and loser.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Sure. My winners this week are Martin Sheen, Melissa Fitzgerald, and Mary McCormack, all actors from the West Wing. We celebrate 25 years since that groundbreaking show premiered next month. They were all back at the White House with President Biden celebrating Mary and Melissa's new book, which is called "What's Next" out this week.\par \par \uc2 My loser this week is Mayor Tiffany Henyard from Dolton, Illinois, who took in her small town record surplus, turned it into a record deficit, and is now on the run. She has charged up to $40,000 just in one day on credit cards for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  on her city credit card. So not the best local leader, and certainly a loser in my book.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go. Hugh, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: My winner is the state of Israel. A week ago we were afraid Iran would be attacking the Jewish state. They backed off because it was made clear that Israel would punch back hard. And last night \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at Bedminster held a pro-Israel rally, making Israel a center point of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 The loser, though, is Governor Walz of Minnesota. He is badly dented up after this, what Mary Katharine Ham called on "The Ruthless Podcast", a pattern of purposeful leaving of misperception of his record. I'm not going to call it lying or stolen valor, but he's badly dented after this week, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kevin, as we head to Chicago, what do you think about this week? We'll hear from President Biden, we expect, on Monday. And then the rest of the week, it's definitely a packaged performance, just like the \uc2 RNC\uc2  did. What do you look for for the DNC?\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Well, I'm looking for -- I was out in Milwaukee for the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , so hopefully as well a run convention as Milwaukee was, speaking just as a Democrat on your panel. I think you are going to have a lot of Democratic luminaries in terms of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Monday night, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . So you're going to have a deep bench of Democratic leadership on that stage passing the torch to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And, Kimberley, Steven Spielberg, he can package well?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Look, this is going to be their opportunity to try to roll her out. And if you are looking at her campaign, what she has been trying to do is make a distinction with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And, by the way, that's why I think he is getting hidden on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go. Panel, thanks a lot. We'll see you in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Thanks, bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, it's Friday. It is Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's nice to have a forum like this where I can discuss something at length. You think that Kamala could do this interview?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We've got 84 days to take control of our own destiny, 84 damn days. And I have said this, we'll sleep when we're dead.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2  (R), FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We need him to win. But you've got to go out and do the work. And the one thing Republicans have to stop, quit whining about her.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I fought Nikki very hard. I beat her in her own state by legendary numbers.\par \par \uc2 I would love to have her go around and campaign.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He ought to get a job.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Two people, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , who aren't comfortable in their own skin because they aren't comfortable with their policy positions for the American people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, did you know your seen reached out to a U.S. ambassador about \uc2 Burisma\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president was not in business with his son.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As Matthew Perry's ketamine addiction grew, he wanted more, and he wanted it faster and cheaper.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We were we need to see the hostages released, released for Palestinian civilians in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are closer than we have ever been. I don't want to jinx anything.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: What is that, like mayonnaise and tuna? What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Pretty much ground beef and cheese.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was something. That's one week covering Washington.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests will be Republican vice presidential nominee J.D. Vance and Harris-Walz national co-chair Chris Coons.\par \par \uc2 Sunday, we come to you live from Chicago, site of the Democratic convention, for a special edition of SPECIAL REPORT. Our convention conch continues throughout next week.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. It's been a busy week. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240817ek8g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Trump Campaign Accuses Dem VP Candidate Walz of "Stolen Valor"; Calls for Harris to Define Foreign Policy Agenda; U.S. Job Growth Slows in July Report, Unemployment Unexpectedly Jumps; Harris Shuns Media Interviews And News Conferences; Israel On Alert For Retaliatory Strike; Harris: We Need A Hostage Deal; Majority Leader Schumer Predicts Democrats Will Keep Control of Senate; Virginia Governor Youngkin Issues an Election Executive Order; Trey Yingst is Interviewed about His New Book "Black Saturday"\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Robert Wolf, Gillian Turner, Juan Williams, Trey  Yingst, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Tensions rising in the Middle East -- an Israeli strike aimed at taking out \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders sparks new criticism as dozens of civilians are also dead, and the U.S. warns Iran not to spark a broader regional war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We want to see de- escalation -- tensions deescalated in the region.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): As the war grinds on -- tensions mount over Iran's threats to retaliate after high-profile assassinations of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leaders. We'll get reaction from Republican Tom Cotton who serves on the Senate Intel and Armed Services Committees, and Democrat \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 , a member of the House Intel and Foreign Affairs Committees.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Since becoming a presidential candidate, she has refused to do a single interview. You know why? Because she's dumb.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  slams \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for so far refusing to do sit-down interviews or news conferences since rising to the top of the Democratic ticket and sticking primarily to scripted moments.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATE AND (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Do we believe in the promise of America?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And are we ready to fight for it?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As Harris prepares to make her first public appearance with President Biden since he left the race.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel on whether her past positions still hold.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am prepared to get rid of the filibuster to pass a green new deal.\par \par \uc2 We need to have Medicare for all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 A look at your headlines quickly.\par \par \uc2 International outrage is growing after Israel hit a reportedly being used to shelter Palestinians in Gaza early Saturday, killing at least 90 people. Israel says \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Islamic Jihad militants were operating a command and control center within the school.\par \par \uc2 Israel has ordered new mass evacuations from a humanitarian zone in Khan Younis in southern Gaza where the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been firing rockets.\par \par \uc2 And Iran not backing off threats of revenge against Israel despite international calls not to broaden the Middle East conflict. Elite Revolutionary Guards are holding military drills in Western Iran that will continue until Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get reaction from Senator Tom Cotton and Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, get to our team coverage with Lucas Tomlinson at the White House and Trey Yingst in Haifa Israel, where we begin with you, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning. A dangerous waiting game is unfolding right now across the Middle East. Israel is still bracing for an attack from Iran and its proxies as U.S. officials are reportedly scrambling to put together a ceasefire agreement between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The key date to look at here is August 15th this Thursday, when Egyptian, Qatari, and American negotiators are set to meet. The gathering is largely seen as a last ditch effort to end the war in Gaza and avert the situation unraveling into a wider regional conflict.\par \par \uc2 Reports indicate CIA Director \uc2 William Burns\uc2  will lead the American negotiating team while U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is looking at a trip to the region this week.\par \par \uc2 The X factor in the entire equation is \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , Iran's largest proxy that has launched daily attacks against Israel since October 8th. The group has promised to target major Israeli population centers in response to the killing of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander Fu'ad Shukr in Beirut earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Now, overnight, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  launched rockets and drones into northern Israel, in response to a targeted strike in southern Lebanon the day before. It's unclear if \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  will be held back by Iran, its main funder and supporter in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Now an Israeli defense official told FOX News over the weekend that coordination continues between the Americans and the Israelis to prepare for a worst case scenario of a large scale attack against Israel, and the possibility of a larger war -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, reporting from Israel. We will join you again live in just a little bit later in the show, Trey. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We go now to Lucas Tomlinson, live at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gave unscheduled remarks late yesterday where she addressed the war in Gaza and the importance of ending it soon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yet again, there are far too many civilians who have been killed. I mean, Israel has a right to go after the terrorists that are \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but as I have said many, many times, they also have I believe an important responsibility to avoid civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 The president and I have been working around this around the clock. We need to get the hostages out. We need a hostage deal and we need a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden enjoyed some downtime at his beach house in Delaware where he gave a familiar one-word warning to Iran.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What's your message to Iran?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Don't.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: While the White House awaits Iran's response, it's Ukrainian forces taking the world by surprise, launching an incursion into Russia's Kursk region, an operation that has the support from the Pentagon with one caveat.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, DEPUTY PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It is consistent with our policy and we have supported Ukraine from the very beginning. We don't support long-range attacks into Russia.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Days before Russia launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spoke at the Munich Security Conference.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: If Russia further invades Ukraine, the United States together with our allies and partners will impose significant and unprecedented economic cost.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  insists Russia would not have invaded under his watch. He also claimed he would have made a deal with Iran.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Iran can't have a nuclear weapon, it's very simple. We would have gotten along with Iran. I was looking forward to it -- I was fine with Iran. You know we hit them a couple of times pretty hard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): officials say Iran and its proxies are not likely to launch a major attack on Israel until after the Olympics, the closing ceremony is later today -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson from the White House -- Lucas, thank you\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Thank you, Shannon. Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you've heard what the vice president said. She said she's repeatedly talked about this civilian toll that's going on with Israel and the strikes in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 We got a statement from NSC spokesperson Sean Savett yesterday on this, saying: We know \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been using schools as locations to gather and operate out of, but we've also had to say repeatedly and consistently that Israel must take measures to minimize civilian harm.\par \par \uc2 So you've got this delicate balance, get this thing wrapped up, get it over with, but also act with precision to get to the bad guys.\par \par \uc2 How does this thing end? And what do you make of the continued criticism that Prime Minister Netanyahu is doing this and extending this for political reasons?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Shannon, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's comments yesterday, like her policy towards Israel for the last 10 months, just an example of how she's unprepared to be the commander-in-chief. She immediately took at face value \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's claims about the number of people killed and what they were doing.\par \par \uc2 Israel has to strike on occasion at places like hospitals and schools because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  uses them for command and control or to fire mortars and rockets.\par \par \uc2 There are civilian casualties in Gaza, no doubt, but those are solely the responsibility of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , not Israel. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , though, have put more pressure on Israel than they put on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 Just look back a couple weeks ago, when Prime Minister Netanyahu was in town. She refused to preside over the joint session, her only constitutional duty as president of the Senate, she refused to have a meeting in public with them and she came out and again blamed Israel for the civilian casualties in Gaza, only emboldening Iran and Iranian-backed terrorists.\par \par \uc2 And what did you have two days later, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , another Iranian-backed terrorist, shot in rockets to Israel and blew up children playing ball at a playground.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is naive and she's not prepared to be the commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: To be fair, we did see her publicly with the prime minister. We didn't get the content of their meeting but there are those visuals of them together, but she's trying to balance, it seems like, as many in the Democrat Party are this pull from the progressive side that wants to see this wrapped up and wants to rally on the side of what's happening to the Palestinian people.\par \par \uc2 So how does this end?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: But look at what's happened just in the past few days at her rally. She's been interrupted twice by pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  radicals. The first time she accused him of helping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  get elected, not telling they were demented for supporting terrorists, not telling them that United States stands with Israel, the victim of the worst slaughter of Jews since the Holocaust, but that they might help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  get elected.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, though that was too tough even for her in her campaign because when she was interrupted next, she literally had a script in front of her. She had a piece of paper that she started reading from in which she sympathized with these pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  radicals.\par \par \uc2 She said: I hear you. I hear your voice. We need a ceasefire immediately.\par \par \uc2 Rather than saying that we need a ceasefire immediately, she should have been saying like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should have said from the beginning, we need an Israeli victory immediately.\par \par \uc2 And Israel has done a great job despite the constraints that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have put on them, despite the fact that they're operating under a significant arms embargo which \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  won't even say that she would worsen if she be becomes elected president.\par \par \uc2 Again, we cannot have peaceful stable relations in the Middle East with daylight between the United States and Israel and our Arab partners.\par \par \uc2 That's what President Trump understood.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 COTTON: That's why we had peace under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You heard what you said there though, that Israel has every right to defend itself against terrorist factions. Is that not strong enough for you?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, she says it all the time like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does, and then she immediately says "but". And usually, Shannon, when a politician says "but", what matters is what comes after the "but", not what came before it.\par \par \uc2 And what comes after the "but" with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is always implying that Israel is responsible for all the civilian suffering in Gaza, not \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , that Israel is the one being provocative when it's waging a defensive war after the October 7th atrocity and that Israel is the one that should pull in its horns, as opposed to supporting Israel and standing strong against Iran and Iranian-backed terrorist throughout the region.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. I want to make sure we get to a couple of other things. There is this issue of stolen valor. You know what this is like. You've had people question your record - your portrayal of your record in the past. It's now coming up with Governor Walz.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " has a piece saying this: It took precisely no time to figure out that Walz filed the paperwork to run for Congress and submitted his request to retire months before any official order to deploy to Iraq was received. According to a National Guardsman who served with him, he'd been weighing the decision to run for Congress for quite a while.\par \par \uc2 So there's been a lot made about the timeline the fact that he had announced in February of 2005, I'm thinking about a congressional run. It wasn't until much later that his unit was officially called up. He had put in retirement paperwork.\par \par \uc2 So what do you say to those who push back like this is a non-issue, you're a veteran, this call for respect of other veterans and you know what it's like to be questioned?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yeah. Well, first off, I want to say I respect the service of all veterans, to include \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  who served for more than 20 years. But now even the campaign acknowledged he's made past inconsistent statements about his service. I think you should answer questions about those statements.\par \par \uc2 I think he also needs to answer questions about his 35-year-long financial relationship with the Chinese Communist Party, someone who took 30 trips to China and took students with them so they could be exposed to communist propaganda.\par \par \uc2 But more than anything, Shannon, we need to remember that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is the vice presidential nominee. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is at the top of the ticket. She's the one that needs to take questions.\par \par \uc2 She refuses to take questions about what direction she would take this country in. She hasn't had a single proposal since she became the presidential nominee. Well, I take that back last night she said that she was going to eliminate taxes on tips.\par \par \uc2 So the only proposal she's had since she became a presidential nominee is to steal one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's ideas at a time when her administration said that they would crack down on the taxation of tips. That's why they needed $87 billion for more \uc2 IRS\uc2  enforcement.\par \par \uc2 This is the kind of flip-flopping and deception you get from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who is a dangerous San Francisco liberal, who has taken positions in the past like decriminalizing illegal immigration and giving health care to illegal aliens. She claims -- or her campaign claims that she's changed. She needs to answer questions about why she changed and when she changed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's also important that we hear from her, just so there is clarification on these issues and she's going to have to respond to the attacks from you and other Republicans about that.\par \par \uc2 Real quickly on the China issue, their campaign put out a statement this week saying that throughout his career, Governor Walz stood up to the Communist Chinese Party, fought for human rights and democracy and always put American jobs in manufacturing first, and that his back and forth with the educational groups and that kind of thing was to foster some kind of understanding that would make things better for the Chinese people, open these conversations so that the U.S. would be stronger in its position with regard to China as well.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yeah. It's just not the case. It's spin.\par \par \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has a terrible record on China, just like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does, that he's consistently appeased and conciliated the Chinese Communist Party, and had repeatedly said we don't need to have an adversarial relationship with China, when China is insistent on having an adversary relationship with the United States because they wanted to replace us as the world's dominant superpower.\par \par \uc2 Again, what the Chinese Communist Party did with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  going back years is a classic example of how they cultivate younger, local, state leaders, teachers and others, to try to get mouthpieces in American politics and maybe one day hit it big if that person goes on to be say a congressman or a governor or a vice presidential nominee.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: This is -- this is why he needs to answer questions about his relationship with China and why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  needs to come out and answer questions too about what she would do as president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Door is always open to both of them. We would love to host that conversation with either.\par \par \uc2 But with all of this playing out and all of your critique of her, the new polls out from "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " in these key critical swing states show President Trump down in places like Michigan, Wisconsin and Pennsylvania. Now, we have polling with her officially that ticket firmed up.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board is asking the question now if President Trump is in their words going to blow another election. They say he doesn't seem to realize he's now in a close race that requires discipline and a consistent message. His struggles are hurting GOP candidates for the House and Senate. This is still Mr. Trump's election to lose, but as we learned in 2020, he's more than capable of doing it.\par \par \uc2 Has there been a shaky pivot to a different ticket?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: No, I don't think so, Shannon. We always knew this would be a close election, whoever the Democrats chose to put up against President Trump. But the more the American people learn about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the less they're going to like her.\par \par \uc2 If you think that your family has struggled under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the last four years, the worst is yet to come if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets elected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But the polls seem that people have an open mind about her. They seem buoyed by the idea that she's on the ticket now.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Once she has to face the American people, answer questions through the media, just like any other presidential candidate would. You know if you earn the nomination like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in 2016 or \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  did in 2008, you have to do town halls and debates and interviews. She had it gifted to her, and she thinks she can hide out for a hundred more days before the election, but eventually, she's going to have to come out and she's going to explain what she would do for this country, and once the American people learn that she wants to do things like decriminalize illegal immigration or take away your health insurance on the job. They're not going to like what they hear. And --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Those positions may have changed because it seems like at least from hearing from campaign members and not from her but from staff that some of those positions have changed. But again --\par \par \uc2 COTTON: But all these positions are things she said on tape --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: -- in her own voice, when she ran for president the last time. Everything else is a Friday night news dump from anonymous campaign aid.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, and we get the economic plan we're told this week so we'll have that to break down by next Sunday. Senator, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 OK joining us now, Colorado Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome, and to both of you, thank you for your service because both of you have worn the uniform, and we appreciate that beyond your congressional service as well.\par \par \uc2 So let's start here with the stolen valor conversation that we had with Senator Cotton. We talked about this timeline in February of 2005 that Governor Walz was notified, it's possible that his team was going to be called up.\par \par \uc2 In March of that year, March 20, 2005, he says this, as Command Sergeant Major, I have a responsibility not only to ready my battalion for Iraq but also to serve if called on. I'm dedicated to serving my country to the best of my ability whether that is in Washington D.C. or in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 But Congressman, by the time his team was called up he had retired, and despite that statement about he would serve if called on, he ultimately didn't.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO): Yeah Shannon, thanks for having me. You know, military service is tough, and serving in the military unit is tough, so you have to be able to rely on your fellow veteran, your fellow service member. You know, that is a deep ethos in the military that we have each other's backs.\par \par \uc2 So there's really very few things that sadden me more when you see a veteran maligning and attacking falsely another veteran, and that's why it's so sad to see Senator Vance turning on \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and, you know, perpetuating these false attacks.\par \par \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  served for 24 years in uniform. He started when he was 17, enlisted as a private, just like me. I actually have a special place in my heart for fellow privates. These are the junior enlisted. You start at the bottom and you work your way up, just like I did when I was in the military.\par \par \uc2 And what's really exciting about \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is not only that he served for 24 years, that he held the position of Command Sergeant Major in his unit, which is the highest enlisted position in the unit, but he is now the highest enlisted person to ever be on a presidential ticket. That is exciting for the middle class, that is exciting for working Americans, and I'm excited for this ticket.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you talk about though the reliance that men and women in uniform have to be able to have on their fellow brothers and sisters, but there are National Guardsmen who served with him in that period who felt abandoned by him, the man that was supposed to command and take them forward. They're now speaking out saying they did feel like he left them behind.\par \par \uc2 CROW: No, they are not, Shannon, just to be very clear. Overwhelmingly, the people that served with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  said that he served with distinction.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But there are couple of ways he --\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yeah, and military units are like families, Shannon. They're like families, right? You're brothers and sisters, and they don't always get along. People have personal grievances, people are angry at each other, and it's very sad to see that.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what's motivating these folks that have spoken out. I'm sure there's some kind of personal grievance there, but overwhelmingly, people who served with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  know what type of person he is. Even those who are Republicans, even those who said that they're not going to vote for him, said that he served with distinction.\par \par \uc2 That's the true story here. His record is very clear, and it's very exciting that we're going to have the -- the most senior enlisted person on a presidential ticket in American history. That is a monumental thing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, in the meantime, we've got questions now about what kind of commander-in-chief \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would be. The vice president should she become president, critical to you and to everybody else who wears the uniform or has worn it.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" Editorial Board says it's a mystery because there's so much going on in foreign policy, and we don't know exactly where she would land on these things. They say she's all but telling Americans they'll have to elect her to find out what she really believes. This is bad enough on domestic issues, but on foreign policy, it could be perilous.\par \par \uc2 The world is more dangerous than it's been in decades, and Americans deserve to know how the woman aiming to be commander-in-chief Harris would confront these threats. What obligation does she have now to start taking the questions, to start explaining to the American people in in-depth probing ways about what her positions are?\par \par \uc2 CROW: The world is a very dangerous and volatile place right now. There is no doubt about it. There are more dangers right now than any time certainly in my lifetime, and there's nobody who's more capable of taking on those dangers and leading as commander-in-chief than Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She was a United States senator for years. She's been the vice president for three and a half years. She's met with over 150 foreign leaders. I've been with her when she's led the U.S. delegation to the Munich Security Conference multiple times, which is one of the largest, most consequential, high-profile security conferences, and people know where she stands. In this critique, this hit, this hit that she's not --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But a lot of people will say that they don't know where she stands. Is she in direct alignment with President Biden? She talks about being the last person in the room that led to this conversation and these decisions about Afghanistan, that disastrous withdrawal there. Is she going to separate herself from some of his policies, or is she in lockstep with them? Do we not deserve to know, for somebody who wants to become Commander-in-Chief?\par \par \uc2 CROW: Of course you know, and people do know, A, and this critique that she doesn't take questions is just not true. She's been the Democratic nominee for five days, Shannon, for five days. She's been running in this race as the primary top of the ticket for just a few weeks, and in that time, mind you, she's been building a presidential campaign from the ground up. She's had to select a running mate, and she's been running around in key places of the country, holding rallies with tens of thousands of Americans who are very excited for her candidacy.\par \par \uc2 So she's been pretty busy, but there's no doubt you're going to see the type of leader she is. You're going to see that she's ready from day one to tackle these threats. And, you know, I served three combat tours in Iraq and Afghanistan as a paratrooper, as an army ranger. I know the dangers. I know what it's like to serve, and there's no question in my mind that she is ready to be the commander-in-chief that the United States needs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we're left with a little bit of a muddle when it comes from, you know, things from, you know, taking away privatized health care to, you know, mandatory gun buybacks. A lot of people will call that confiscation to where she is on ICE and the border and all kinds of things because there were positions, as Senator Cotton said, that we heard from her full- throatedly and clearly back in 2019 that it seems like we still are not getting clarification from her. It's, you know, secondhand from a campaign staffer, somebody off the record saying that she's pivoted on many of these things.\par \par \uc2 Tim Carney\uc2  writing over the "Washington Examiner" says, "Out loud, in person, and on the record, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has staked out extreme and destructive policy proposals for the past decade or more. Harris said far left stopped when she needed to win far left votes, and now her priority is winning moderate votes. That raises the question, was she lying back then or is she lying now?"\par \par \uc2 Congressman.\par \par \uc2 CROW: You know, the contrast in this election, Shannon, couldn't be more clear, right? There is great clarity as to what both candidates will actually do, Vice President Harris and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but is it a contrast between Harris 2019 and Harris 2024? We don't know.\par \par \uc2 CROW: No, it's very, very clear. She's been very -- no, we do know. We know that Vice President Harris will protect reproductive rights, will defend working class Americans, will keep America safe, will help reduce costs for regular Americans.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will do the opposite. He's actually proposing to raise costs and raise taxes. And by the way, when he was president, he passed a tax cut for the wealthiest of Americans, for the biggest corporations where 80% of the value of that tax cut went to stock buybacks for the billionaires and for the CEOs of these corporations and the stockholders and shareholders of these corporations, not for regular working Americans. That's why unions -- that's why unions and that's why working-class Americans --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, non-partisan analysis of those.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- to middle class and average Americans as well. But Congress is going to have to have that conversation about whether they get extended.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Shannon, that's why unions and working-class Americans are behind the Harris-Walz ticket. That's why, you know, labor is behind this ticket. That's why Americans know, Americans know that if you're a working class American, if you're middle class, you're trying to build a business, own a home, support a family. That's why you know you can trust Vice President Harris and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a lot of those average Americans are struggling in their pocketbooks, business and personal. So we'll see. But we look forward to the Vice President rolling out her economic plan this week and interviews beyond that to flesh out the rest of her policy as well.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, we thank you for coming on as an advocate and again for your service, sir.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A rollercoaster week on Wall Street, many panicking after two days of massive selloffs as they watch their retirement account balances plunge.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we've got two experts to come and debate the data and talk about the impact on the race for the White House, too.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wall Street saw its worst day since 2022 this past Monday in a global market route. Some of the losses were recouped as the week moved on, but many are wondering what comes next.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now, Robert Wolf, a former economic advisor to President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and a Fox News contributor, and E.J. Antoni, public finance economist with \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . Welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Glad to have you with us. Let's talk about what the data says this week about what we saw. I want to start with this from "USA Today."\par \par \uc2 "U.S. employers added just 114,000 jobs last month, well below expectations for 175,000 gains, unemployment rose 4.1 percent up to 4.3. That's the highest in nearly three years."\par \par \uc2 They go on to say that, "One gauge tracking the rise of unemployment signals the U.S. could already be in a recession. Hiring is dipped below pre-COVID levels. Meanwhile, manufacturing activity shrank in July, falling to an eight-month low. Consumer spending, the credit card debt is at a record high."\par \par \uc2 Robert, that does not sound like a good collection of information.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Shannon, thanks for having me on. And I know we're going to discuss a lot of those points. I would probably flip it completely. We've had 43 consecutive months of jobs growth. And to be clear, over the last two years, the Trump team and even E.J. from \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  was working on Project 2025, and now they're sprinting away from it. The Biden-Harris administration, as you know, they passed the infrastructure policy.\par \par \uc2 And look what we've done. We've had manufacturing gains over almost 800,000, big private sector investment, and factory construction at record highs.\par \par \uc2 And don't take my word. Just look at the projects, the Arizona chips plant, the Georgia battery plant, the North Carolina semiconductor plant, Wisconsin data center. Yes, last month was below expectations, but we've had 14 million job gains since this president has been in office. And we also know that we've had better GDP, we've had better economic policy. And yes, inflation has been a long tail problem for the country, but that's a global phenomenon, both on agriculture and energy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let me bring E.J. in here. So I know that we talk about a lot, 14 million jobs. We know a lot of that was rebound jobs added back from the COVID pandemic. But what do you make of what Robert says about your work and about, you know, a view that is somewhat more pessimistic being inaccurate when it comes to our economy?\par \par \uc2 E.J. ANTONI, THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION PUBLIC FINANCE ECONOMIST: Well, I'm certainly very proud of the work we've done on Project 2025. We've made a lot of really great conservative recommendations for the next administration, whoever it may be, and we hope they take us up on it.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the job market, you know, one of the things we have to remember is although we continue to see payrolls rising, the number of people actually employed has been falling. It's actually 600,000 below where it was back in November of last year. So the labor market is not all -- not all well, certainly not as well as it seems, I would say. Yes, we are seeing unemployment tick up.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing literally all of the net job gains from the last year not go to native-born Americans, but going to foreign-born workers. That's a trend that has actually been going on for the last several years.\par \par \uc2 And in terms of GDP, yes, it's true GDP is growing, but it's essentially growing because of government debt. Under the Biden-Harris administration, we've increased debt by about 7.2 trillion. The Treasury has drawn down its cash balance about another trillion. So that's about 8.2 trillion in the red.\par \par \uc2 And what did we get for it? About 6.6 trillion in GDP growth down to 2.2, if you adjust for inflation.\par \par \uc2 Essentially, it's a debt-fueled spending binge that gave us 40-year high inflation, record-high credit card interest rates, and a tripling of mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How does that end?\par \par \uc2 ANTONI: Not well. You know, we can look back at different points in our history when we did essentially the exact same thing.\par \par \uc2 The 1970s, unfortunately, was a very good example of this. And the result was back-to-back recessions and mortgage rates that eventually hit 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Robert, we're going to get a lot of economic data this week. CPI inflation coming out Wednesday. Retail sales, Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Friday, we'll get that University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment survey as well. What are you going to look to, to tell you where we're moving forward?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I mean, listen, we saw the stock market get hit and the world was concerned.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, in 1987, I was at Salomon Brothers. We saw this -- the Black Monday crash. And you saw what happened the last few days. One, the stock market came back.\par \par \uc2 And when we should be clear, that was a thousand-point swing, and it was a big swing. We had 3,000, 2,000-point downturns under President Trump, the three largest in history.\par \par \uc2 I would also say the idea that \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  is also fiscal conservatives all of a sudden, I mean, come on, that's complete BS. They were touting the Trump tax cuts, and that it was going to pay for itself with five percent GDP. My bet, I see more. Couldn't wait to talk about it paying for himself.\par \par \uc2 They had eight trillion in debt in a four-year period. The most in the history of a president went from 20 to 28 trillion, and the deficit ballooned.\par \par \uc2 So this idea that all of a sudden, Heritgae is worried about fiscal conservatism, we should just reflect on what really happened during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. And very quickly, E.J., I want to give you a response to that, because it came up with our last guest too, that these tax cuts under President Trump benefited the wealthy, and they haven't been good for the country.\par \par \uc2 ANTONI: Well, it's interesting because all of the different news organizations that did an analysis on the Trump tax reform found that middle-class Americans had a lower tax burden after the reform was passed, not more.\par \par \uc2 And in terms of the very wealthy, especially in high-tax states like New York, New Jersey, Illinois, California, those individuals actually paid more, because although their top marginal tax rate was reduced, they lost deductions under that tax reform. In other words, it got rid of tax loopholes.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, the Democrats now want to get rid of that, because doing so would be a hand-out to their donor base, again, in those high-tax blue states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I feel like I could do an hour with you two. And this conversation is just beginning.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So please come back, because I think there'll be a lot more for us to debate in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Robert and E.J., thank you both.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, the mainstream media's love affair with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . Quite the contrast with the reaction we saw to J.D. Vance's selection to joining the Trump ticket.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel weighs in on that.\par \par \uc2 And growing questions about the Harris-Walz ticket's willingness to sit down. We're ready for an in-depth media interview or press conference.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, ABC NEWS HOST: All American definition of a man from Middle America, high school teacher, football coach.\par \par \uc2 SELINA WANG, ABC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: He has this folksy personal informal vibe that has really appealed to a lot of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: They're totally approachable. You get the feeling that he's got plenty of neighbors that wear MAGA hats and he picks up their paper and brings it to their front door and they're his friends.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN HOLMES, CNN CORRESPONDENT: Walz has an ability to be -- I know one person said, cuddly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, much of the media delivering a glowing assessment of Vice President Harris' running mate, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , Harris announcing her VP pick Monday after weeks of speculation.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group to break it down.\par \par \uc2 "Axios" politics reporter, Stef Kight. Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . Fox News senior political analyst, Juan Williams.\par \par \uc2 By the way, also the author of the "New Prize for These Eyes: The Rise of America's Second Civil Rights Movement." It is coming out in months.\par \par \uc2 And Gillian Turner, Fox News State Department and foreign policy correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Great to have all of you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Matthew, cuddly, folksy, joyful. They like them a little bit better than they like J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, FELLOW, \uc2 AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE\uc2 : Yes. With all the talk of vibes in the past few weeks, joy, enthusiasm, weirdness, confidence, I feel like this presidential campaign has become "Inside Out 2," right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Into the exclusion of all the --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's all the emotions and adjectives.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: -- important issues that are at stake here. And some of those clips you showed, Shannon, I felt like the reporters were ready to give \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  a hug.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI But I would say to this that there's a very unusual moment here, which is that you have a new vice presidential selection in \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , but you also have a new vice president now running for president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who needs to be defined.\par \par \uc2 And I think strategically for the Trump campaign, they need to train their focus on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , even if \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  presents a lot of unanswered questions about his record and his past.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, speaking of unanswered questions, again, the door is open for the vice president or for her running mate. We'd love to have you on FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 "The Hill" says this. It's a piece by an opinion piece by a journalism professor. He says, certainly Harris' campaign handlers can cobble together a 15-minute presser in which he calls on only preselected and sympathetic reporters and then gives succinct answers based on stump speeches.\par \par \uc2 It would come off as superficial, of course, but at least it would show the campaign is going through the motions of engaging the press and stuff.\par \par \uc2 We would love for them to engage with us.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICS REPORTER, AXIOS: Yes, we would. And, you know, pressure is mounting for Harris to actually answer for some of her policy decisions. And it's especially important because, you know, we've seen Republicans and others point out just a few years ago when she was running for president.\par \par \uc2 She is on the record making policy stances that were considered pretty progressive. And now I and other reporters have reached out to her campaign and we've received, you know, background comments saying the opposite, whether it's on fracking bans or decriminalizing illegal border crossings or, you know, buyback programs for assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 They're all these huge policy issues that she was on the record saying a few years ago. And now her campaign is on background saying, she no longer believes those things.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's very important for us to hear from her directly about where she stands on these issues.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and that piece in U.S. -- or excuse me, "The Hill, goes on to say this, let's face it, if Harris is unwilling to risk a back-and-forth discussion with CNN's Wolf Blitzer or NBC's Today host Savannah Guthrie, she can hardly be expected as president to handle an extemporaneous give and take with China's leader Xi Jinping or Russian diplomat or even House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Juan, you're chuckling.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I mean, to me governing is different than campaigning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And right now, I don't care if you're a Republican or Democrat, just looking at the numbers of the polls. You'd say, boy, she's doing pretty good right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Look at the rallies. Look at the enthusiasm. And you'd say, well, you know, crowd size has been amazing. It's so important to Trump. Now, she's the one who's bragging about crowd size. She's getting out a good vibe message.\par \par \uc2 And so to me --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But a lot of vibes, not a lot of policy.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, hold on. But I think that what you've got here is, would she be deluding the good vibe, big crowd size, popular VP pick, by suddenly starting to discuss policy?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And so she's going to have to do it at some point. But right now, I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 I think it would be pleasing to me and you, Shannon, and legacy media all around. Let's get her in. We want to ask some questions.\par \par \uc2 But I'm not sure it's a good campaign strategy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm. Which may be the whole point.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says this, though. You mentioned the polls are going really well for the Harris-Walz ticket so far. They've gone through key critical swing states, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 They say that she is up on President Trump, the vice president is, 50 percent to 46 percent among likely voters in each state.\par \par \uc2 The polls, some of the first high-quality surveys in those states since Mr. Biden announced he was not seeking re-election, come after nearly a year of surveys that showed either a tied contest or a slight lead for Mr. Trump over Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, whiplash. But really just in the last three weeks, we've seen major changes in the momentum, the polling, all of that, with a big switch on one of the tickets.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT AND FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT: But, you know, what happens with whiplash. You go one way and then you end up ricocheting back in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 This is a point, interestingly, David Axelrod has been making this week that a lot of the enthusiasm and hype we're seeing surrounding Vice President Harris, and now her selection of \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , is really that, is a ricochet effect. It's kind of a trauma response to the fact that Democrats felt so down in the doldrums about the Biden-Harris ticket for so long that they are now euphoric. He feels it's going to wear off very quickly.\par \par \uc2 Quick note about Harris and her policies. It's very weird that there's this much hype surrounding a candidate before they have laid out their policy platforms.\par \par \uc2 Normally, things go in the other direction. That's what campaigning is supposed to be based around. All the slogans in the world don't really mean anything until you have proposals out there for people to evaluate.\par \par \uc2 So I would caution that some of the crowd turnout, those kinds of things, is all nice, but how substantive or meaningful is it really when people don't have her policy proposals on their website yet?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Juan, fair to say that Democrats are reacting from what Gillian said, is sort of the sense of relief.\par \par \uc2 This -- you know, we've heard about the double haters for the past year who didn't want this rematch, didn't want to want either of these tickets, but Democrats obviously buoyed by the fact that they have a whole new option.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that's driving crowds and everything else.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. I think -- you call them double haters, people who didn't like Trump or Biden.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's also this sense of, you know, we've been through that race. It's like a TV show that's a rerun. We've seen that show. Oh, this is new. This is different. I think people are like, oh, what's this about? Right? So some of that enthusiasm that Gillian's talking about.\par \par \uc2 I would say that if I just look at the polls, what I see is that Harris has, to my mind, consolidated now some of the undecideds in addition to revving up her base, the base that really was drifting away from Biden. And I think that's what's driving her numbers.\par \par \uc2 I would just caution people that Trump's numbers haven't gone down. Trump is still in that high 40s bracket.\par \par \uc2 And remember, Trump won the election in 2016, even though he lost the popular vote. He won the Electoral College. That's still a possibility.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He didn't help himself this week, by the way, going after the governor -- Republican governor of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't help himself saying he's got a bigger crowd than \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. Or saying he's in a helicopter crash with Willie Brown.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. That story continues. He says he's got flight logs that will give us more data on that.\par \par \uc2 But I would note that the Trump team also says on "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," pulling that they point back to 2020 and how far it was off with what the ultimate results in those key states were.\par \par \uc2 So they say there's a lot more to that story.\par \par \uc2 So, panel, stay with us because we've got a lot more.\par \par \uc2 What does the Harris-Walz taken down mean for down ballot races and which party controls the Senate and the House? That debate, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROGERS\uc2  (R), MICHIGAN SENATE NOMINEE: This feels momentum like we haven't seen here in a long time.\par \par \uc2 This is a good sign for the state of Michigan that we're going to be finally actually sending a Republican back.\par \par \uc2 ELISSA SLOTKIN (D), MICHIGAN SENATE NOMINEE: I will work as hard as I can to make sure that we hold the Senate seat. The entire course of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is dependent on what we do in this seat. It's count - well, they're counting on us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, that was former Congressman \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 , Republican, and Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , Tuesday won their primaries for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race in Michigan. They face off in November. It's one of the pivotal races in deciding which party will hold the power in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel. OK, I want to put up this map that shows all the races that are out there. Republicans had felt like this is a good map for them this year. You see, you know, the red states, the blue states, the toss-ups, the leans.\par \par \uc2 And on the heels of the new Harris-Walz ticket, current majority leader in the Senate, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , who would like to stay that way, says this. He's "predicting Democrats will keep control of the Senate in November." "He feels exhilarated and gratified by the surge of enthusiasm with the vice president at the top of the ticket." "What had been a dismal outlook for Senate Democrats," AP says, "has shifted dramatically in the short time since Harris took hold of the party, sending a surge of enthusiasm, donors and volunteers to the Senate race."\par \par \uc2 Stef, we like to play this game where we figure out the balance of power. Republicans had felt very bullish on this year and now some of that waning.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I mean the reality of the Senate math this year is that it looks like it should be favorable for Republicans. There are something like nine seats that are competitive, if you count West Virginia, which is not even really competitive. Most people are expecting that to flip red. Still -\par \par \uc2 TURNER: (INAUDIBLE) says 10. Sorry to interrupt.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes, 10.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: There could be - yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Republicans have felt stronger about other places, including like New Mexico, other races that weren't considered some of the most competitive. But it looks good. But each race is very competitive. Once you actually zoom in on these states, these are battleground states, or potentially red states where the Democratic senator happens to be pretty popular and something like the Harris-Walz switch could boost their chances there.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I've spoken to Republicans recently who kind of acknowledge that a 50/50 Senate is actually more realistic than most people are willing to admit, even in Republican circles, and that there's also a possibility that the House is really up for grabs as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, there's that part of the conversation too.\par \par \uc2 I want to put up a couple of headlines and little results from key states, Arizona, KTAR, they're reporting on results of a new poll that show Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , 2.8 percentage point lead over President Trump and U.S. Congressman \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  up on Kari Lake by a whopping 11 points.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, similar things. They say a Navada poll has Harris up, biggest lead over Trump yet. And also the three Democratic congressional incumbents there are leading their re-election races too.\par \par \uc2 So, Gillian, at this point, House, Senate, either way.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That's what it seems like. I mean I'm not an electoral strategist, but listening to my friends at this network who are, like Karl and so forth, it really does seem to be a tossup right now, meaning things could conceivably go either way because of the dynamics we just talked about in the last bloc, this surge of voter enthusiasm for the Vice President Harris and the Walz ticket and the effect that could have on down ballot races.\par \par \uc2 Also, Republicans are still making the argument, they have not yet had the time to really carve outs sort of their profile on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and when more people in the general electorate are presented with her profile as a long time California lifetime politician who leans very far to the left and people start looking at California as a sort of microcosm for the entire country, that's going to dim some of that enthusiasm is some of the key states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, in some of these key states, including Virginia, they're doing some sort of data cleanup to their voter rolls, that kind of thing. Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  had an executive order out this week. He says he wants dead voters, felons, voters who can't, you know, prove that they're citizens, people have moved all that, they need to be off. And he says this as part of his executive order. "According to data from ELECT," which is the state Democrat - excuse me, Election - Department of Elections there, "between January 2020 and July '24, records indicate we removed 6,303 non- citizens from the voter rolls."\par \par \uc2 And, Matthew, it was something like 11,000 people who had moved?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Right. Well, I think every state confronts these problems.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: And Governor Youngkin is taking the offensive here. And I think he should be applauded for it.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this, with all these races, especially in the Senate, Senate races break late. And who wins the state matters. And so that only reinforces the idea that it is the Trump-Harris fight that is going to be determined. And that means that Trump has to take that Etch-a-Sketch away from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She is having her Etch-a-Sketch moment, redesigning all of her policy platforms. Trump needs to define her, not let her get away with redefining herself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 A quick final word to you, Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I just notice where he - former President Trump was this weekend. He's in Montana involved in a Tester case. That's a bad sign for Republicans. That's a red state. He shouldn't have to be there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, but it's a Democratic senator.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I'm telling you. But all of a sudden Tester's raised three times as much as a challenger. You would think that's a state Republicans would say, oh, that - we're going to win, but there's Trump this weekend.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, that's interesting. I think that Senator Tester is one of the Democratic candidates who's not going to the DNC. And so you have to ask what that's all about. But maybe he just feels like the time is better spent at home campaigning.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Trey Yingst and his team in Israel have been working around the clock since the massacre on October 7th. He's taking you deep inside the realities of war. He's back live, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: On what they didn't know.\par \par \uc2 Get down, everyone. Get to the - lay down flat. Everyone down. Flat. Cover your head.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Right now the Israelis are engaged in a gun battle with the (INAUDIBLE). You can hear that gunfire.\par \par \uc2 One of those rockets just fired from the Gaza Strip, slammed into a house behind me.\par \par \uc2 You can see behind me, Israeli artillery units.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just a sampling of some of the moments that show you the reality of war.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst has encountered all of that while covering the war with his team in Israel. He's documenting his expenses in a new book, "Black Saturday," which tracks the realities of what's happened in the month since the October 7th attacks. He joins us now from Haifa, Israel, to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Trey, we are so in debt, our viewers are, the world is, to you and your team for what you've been covering. You started tracking and documenting, photographing all of these things very early on. What do you want people to get from this book?\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Yes, Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Look, "Black Saturday" is a book about the day this war began. You and I spoke earlier this hour about the rapidly expanding conflict across the Middle East. And we were there when it all started on the morning of October 7th along the Gaza border as the massacre was unfolding. In Black Saturday you will hear stories that are not yet told. You will learn information that's not yet released to the public. There is truly unprecedented access in this book. I speak many times in person with Israel's defense minister, Yoav Gallant. I speak with senior \uc2 Hamas\uc2  officials, with Israeli and Palestinian civilians. Multiple times I go into the Gaza Strip with the Israeli military on embeds, sometimes during the day and sometimes at night to get a firsthand look at the battles that are still unfolding today.\par \par \uc2 If you're someone that's interested in Israel, interested in Gaza, interested in the Middle East, in current events, you'll want to read "Black Saturday" to learn more about what happened on that fateful morning.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Trey, we sometimes are separated from - here, you know, an ocean, continents away from where you are. But for the Israeli people that I talk to, I mean this is still very much a daily, painful thing for them. And, of course, in Gaza, where civilians are being caught up in this, tens of thousands of dead there, how are the people doing in the region as this has drawn on longer than most people would have thought from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: It's a great question and it's really what I want this book to focus on. "Black Saturday" speaks with the people involved, because it's a book about humans. This entire conflict is about people that exist on a spectrum. And so we're talking with everyone involved to get the true story about what happened that morning and in the early weeks of the war.\par \par \uc2 So, I speak with hostages that were released as part of the November ceasefire agreement, about what the experience was like, dragged into the tunnels beneath Gaza. I speak with Palestinian civilians inside the Gaza Strip who have survived the war so far.\par \par \uc2 And again, this is a story about humans. "Black Saturday" takes the reader through the experience of that morning and the early days of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Trey, we look forward to all of your insights and behind-the- scenes. As you said, information we've never heard before.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, in Israel this morning. We will check back with you again soon.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, you can preorder Trey's book "Black Saturday" at foxnewsbooks.com. It is out October 1st.\par \par \uc2 And just a quick note, my podcast "Livin the Bream" drops today. This week I sat down with former Miss Universe, Demi-Leigh Tebow. Also happens to be the wife of Tim Tebow. We talked about her brand-new book, "A Crown that Lasts." It's about finding your purpose and meaning no matter where you are in life. And even Miss Universe has some identity struggles, how she worked through them.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, a head's up, FOX NEWS SUNDAY will be live from Chicago next Sunday ahead of the Democratic National Convention. We'll take you behind the scenes and gets you ready for the big event. We'll have coverage for you all that following week.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a meaningful, wonderful week. We'll see you back here for next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240811ek8b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Taylor Swift Concerts Canceled Over Terror Threat; Tropical Storm Debby Pounds East Coast; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Holds Press Conference; Interview With Fmr. Gov. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  (R-MD); Interview With \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  President Shawn Fain; Interview With \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  President Austan Goolsbee\b0\par\par\uc2 Brandy Campbell, Aishah Hasnie, Grady Trimble, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 And you thought the markets were all over the place. Just try keeping up with these candidates, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  taking questions, a lot of questions, from reporters in Mar-a-Lago, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  talking the economy in Detroit, fired up and, to hear the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  president tell it, fed up.\par \par \uc2 Did you hear what Shawn Fain said last night at that Harris rally? Well, kids and adults, actually, cover your ears, because he's back and he's here, and only here. And so is Maryland Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  trying to turn a blue state red, and Chicago Federal Reserve President \uc2 Austan Goolsbee\uc2  on trying to keep a restless market calm.\par \par \uc2 Tall test, big show, and it all starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. I'm very happy to have you with us on what has turned out to be once again a very busy market and news day.\par \par \uc2 First, let's go to Grady Trimble on that Harris/Walz event at the \uc2 UAW\uc2  -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, President Biden has always said he is the most pro-union president in history. But, today, Vice President Harris and her V.P. pick, Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  of Minnesota, they are here in Michigan trying to win over those same union voters.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they're just up the road from where we are right now. Harris is speaking as we speak, and she's at a local union hall, a \uc2 UAW union\uc2  hall across from a Ford assembly plant. And earlier last -- or later last year, I should say, around September, summertime, those workers at that particular factory, they were on strike.\par \par \uc2 And Governor Walz, when he was speaking to the workers today, remarked about that strike and talked about how he stood with the workers on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for the privilege of walking that picket line with you last year. Thank you for allowing us to lift up our voices. And in Minnesota, the work that you did, it didn't just benefit you \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers. It benefited all workers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: But we have heard from some rank-and-file members who are concerned about some Biden/Harris policies, including that electric vehicle mandate and how it could impact their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, on the other hand, at a press conference this afternoon, he is trying to halt the momentum of Harris and Walz and highlight the fact that Harris hasn't had a press conference of her own since she replaced President Biden at the top of the Democratic ticket.\par \par \uc2 Trump warned of economic doom if she is elected.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You could end up in a depression of the 1929 variety, which would be a devastating thing, took many years, took decades to recover from it.\par \par \uc2 And we're very close to that. And we're very close to a world war, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: And maybe the biggest piece of news out of that press conference this afternoon is the fact that Trump says he's agreed to three debates this September against Vice President Harris, including one on FOX.\par \par \uc2 ABC says that, separately, both candidates have agreed to their debate, so, therefore, they believe it's happening. But, Neil, we reached out to the Harris campaign today to confirm whether any or one of the three are happening. And we haven't heard back -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, my friend, very much, Grady Trimble in Michigan following all of those developments.\par \par \uc2 You might have heard the former president referring to what's going on right now as our hurtle toward a depression.\par \par \uc2 Well, if that's the case, the markets had a funny way of showing it, at least today, with the S&P 500 putting in its best single-day performance we have seen in a couple of years, the Dow tracking its biggest gain of the year.\par \par \uc2 But, again, these are volatile market conditions, something I'm sure our next guest monitors. I don't think he necessarily depends on it one way or the other, but Austan Goolsbee is the Chicago Federal Reserve president, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Austan, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PRESIDENT, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF CHICAGO: Yes, Neil, good to see you. And I appreciate that intro.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, here we go, right? We have got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying we're headed toward a depression. Are we?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, as you know, when you become a sworn member of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , you're out of the elections business. So I don't have any opinion what any candidate has to say.\par \par \uc2 The part that I appreciate about the intro is, the Fed's job is given to it by law, which is maximize employment and stabilize prices. That's the dual mandate for the Fed. That's exactly what we look at. And that is only what we look at.\par \par \uc2 There's nothing in the mandate that says go make sure that the market feels comfortable. And, some days, the market's way down, and, some days, it's way up. And that's -- we watch it, but that's not what should drive the Central Bank behavior, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, so let's take \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out of it for the time being.\par \par \uc2 Do you see a depression yourself?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I mean, we -- I don't see a depression. We got a number, a jobs number, that was disappointing last week. It was below what the market expected. It was below what a lot of people expected.\par \par \uc2 And the question now is, as the job market is cooling, but it's cooling back to levels that in the old days we would have said that's about full employment or that's kind of the steady state, is it going to stop there, or is it going to keep getting worse? And that's a very important question.\par \par \uc2 To me, that's not one you can answer with one month's number. It was definitely a disappointing number in the last jobs -- the jobs read we got. But we got to watch a lot more than just the payroll jobs numbers, and we got to watch it for a lot more than one month.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, the sentiment seems to be building, Austan, that you guys on the Fed are now less worried about inflation, more worried about holding off on interest rate cuts in fear that we're going to slow things down. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: There's a sense in which that's true.\par \par \uc2 You know the rules. I'm not allowed to speak for the committee or for anybody else on the committee.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I understand. I completely understand.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I only speak for myself. When I got into the Fed...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I hoped to get you in an embarrassing moment where you flubbed something, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. No, exactly. They're going to drag me off the screen.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: What -- I started at the Fed a little over a year-and-a-half ago.\par \par \uc2 And, overwhelmingly, what the focus I thought we should have and that you can see in the statements that we had was overwhelmingly about inflation, because, in our dual mandate, we were not succeeding on inflation. It was way too high. And the job market remained very strong. So we were doing fine on that side of the mandate.\par \par \uc2 All of our focus had to be on inflation. Now, 2023, inflation came down almost as much as it has come down in any year in recorded economic history for this country. And that happened without a recession. So, on dual mandate grounds, that was quite a year. And the question now is, can that magic continue to '24?\par \par \uc2 So, for sure, it's putting more focus on the other side of the mandate than it was before, because it was close to 100 percent inflation, zero percent employment. In my view, we're now at kind of a balanced risk kind of posture, because the job market has cooled and inflation has come down.\par \par \uc2 So, now we're getting back to more normal conditions, if you want to think of it that way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were quoted right after we saw some of the report, the employment report, and all that, saying, we can fix this. I don't want to misstate what you said. But that's what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's goal is, after all, to fix things and to make sure you don't go too extreme either way.\par \par \uc2 But are you more worried, you personally and as a voting member of the Fed, more worried now about the economy slipping into something bad, and inflation less so?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I wouldn't say -- I would say more than I was when I got to the Fed, because, like I say, a year, a year-and-a-half ago, we were all about...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How about more than it was last week? How about more than last, Austan? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: More than last week -- when we got the jobs numbers and they were disappointing, definitely more than the day before that.\par \par \uc2 But the through line, the -- my line about fixing it was about the dual mandate, and it was said in the context of...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: ... there were a bunch of market participants saying, the stock market is down so much, the Fed has to take an emergency action.\par \par \uc2 And I said, we're not in the business of responding to the stock market. We are in the business of maximizing employment and stabilizing prices. And if those start to go wrong, then our job is to try to address that on the real side of the economy. And I still think that.\par \par \uc2 And I have concerns.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Look, we set the interest rate high. We're tight, and we set this rate a year ago. And conditions were very different a year ago than they are today. You have seen a lot more cooling of the job market and you have seen a lot more improvement on the inflation side.\par \par \uc2 So we need to be thoughtful, because, if we're too tight for too long, we're going to have to come to grips with what that's going to do to the real side of the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, real quickly, it sounds like what you're saying as well, Austan, is that you're not going to let the markets bully you.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, look, you know my old mentor and friend was Paul Volcker. And Volcker's line to me that I think of all the time is, our job is to act, and the market's job is to react. And let's not get the order mixed up.\par \par \uc2 And I think, at times like this, it's important to remember that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you feel uncomfortable that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been thrust in this position of potentially cutting rates or just moving on rates so close to an election?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: A little. What -- I think the only answer to that are -- elections come on a regular basis. So whatever the Fed does, somebody's going to say they didn't like that.\par \par \uc2 All we can do in the Fed is commit to the thing that will be driving our decisions is the dual mandate of what's happening to employment, what's happening to inflation. And we have been laying out for a year-and-a-half what the criteria would be, what we're looking for, what data are we observing, what has to happen for rates to go down, for rates to stay the same, for rates to go up.\par \par \uc2 So that's -- the Fed's out of the election business. The Fed is in the economic business.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: And we have been pretty clear about what the reaction function would need to be and what conditions, if you saw in the data, would warrant rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what's a bigger headache for you, your job on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  or rooting for the \uc2 Chicago White Sox\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Oh, man, that's a cold -- this is a cold day, this is a cold day for you to pull that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: The -- there's always next year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: We could start rooting for the Cubs, but I understand they're only a half-game out of last place also.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. See, I didn't want to rub that in, but just wanted to get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 Austan Goolsbee, great seeing you again. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Great to see you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2  president, Austan Goolsbee.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did weigh in on all of these developments.\par \par \uc2 And our own Aishah Hasnie had a chance to ask him some very revealing questions -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, as usual, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  making a lot of news during these press conferences here in Mar-a-Lago. Coming up. I'm going to tell you what he thinks about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and why he thinks that she's seeing this small bump in the polls, even though he says he's not changing his strategy at all.\par \par \uc2 It's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you don't see this very often out of presidential contenders, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  taking on any and all questions at Mar-a-Lago today, meanwhile, focus as well on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as she tries to electrify a Detroit audience.\par \par \uc2 But, again, it was that exchange with reporters and a lot of them, and for a long time, in Florida that was pretty riveting stuff.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has more -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, Neil, this presser comes on the heels of some polling that's come out recently that suggests that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is now beating Trump by at least a few points, a small bump, sometimes six to eight points.\par \par \uc2 And so there's been reporting that the former president has been really frustrated with the campaign, the state of the play, and where they go from here. So, today, it was really all about getting the message across to the media, creating this juxtaposition between his campaign and the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign, basically saying that, look, I'm doing this press conference with reporters for more than an hour.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking to the media. My vice presidential pick is talking to the press. But \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who's now been the presidential nominee for a number of days, hasn't even taken a question from the press, even at these rallies. So that's the juxtaposition that they're trying to create.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, he's pushing back and telling reporters today that they are not changing their campaign strategy at all. Even with the slight bump in polling, they are calling that a honeymoon period, saying that the fundamentals of this race, the issues, at the end of the day, the pocketbook issues, the economy, inflation, that has not changed.\par \par \uc2 As to why Harris might be seeing this small bump in polling, this is how the former president responded to that question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: To what do you attribute her rising in the polls? It's becoming a more competitive race since she's taken over for Biden?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, she's a woman. She represents certain groups of people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, I also got to ask him a question about this big headline that broke this week about the current president.\par \par \uc2 President Biden gave an interview to \uc2 CBS\uc2  and basically said that he feels that there would not be a peaceful transfer of power if the former president does not win this election. And the former president got a chance to respond to that, and he said that there would absolutely be a peaceful transfer of power if there is a fair election.\par \par \uc2 He was also again asked repeatedly, and I asked him, I think, a third time, would he change his strategy if the polling didn't change? Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I haven't recalibrated strategy at all. It's the same policies, open borders, weak on crime. She's -- I think she's worse than Biden because he got forced into the position.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, some more news that came out of the campaign today, we had an off-the-record meeting with them, a private meeting with them, and they basically told us that they are targeting two battleground states, Pennsylvania and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 They feel like, if the former president can secure those two battleground states, that he might be able to clinch this in November. So, of course, I have to ask him about his relationship with the very popular governor of Georgia, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I asked him, would there ever be a situation where this relationship could be repaired? Listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't know. I don't know if I can or not. I got him elected. Without me, he wouldn't be governor. I got him elected. He was doing terribly. I got him elected.\par \par \uc2 With that being said, I hope we can repair it. But...\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, he is a relationships guy. And he said that he would try to repair it. At least he could hope to repair it.\par \par \uc2 Neil, last thing, a big one, at least for us here at FOX News, that the former president has agreed to doing three separate debates. The first would be with FOX News on September 4, the second with ABC on the 10th, and then the final one with NBC on the 25th of September.\par \par \uc2 A lot of action to come. Feels like the election is never going to get here. But we're seeing a lot of action between these two campaigns.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: A lot of shots being taken.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, the \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  stuff was amazing, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn't take a guy who was doing terribly and get him elected. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  pretty much did that on his own.\par \par \uc2 But we will see how it all sorts out and whether they can make amends. Thank you very much for that, Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, that's the kind of stuff that really bugs a lot of Republicans, when the president, the former president, gets distracted and takes on intraparty battles, including this back-and-forth over \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the former Maryland governor, is worried about the signal that sends as he's running to flip a red Senate seat his way.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Still no word as to whether the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  is going to support either candidate running for president, but Shawn Fain made it very clear last night exactly where he stands, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the intramural party fighting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has led, attacking governors like Georgia's \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , a very popular Republican, is rattling \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  a bit, the former Maryland governor who's trying to flip a blue seat red to become that state's senator, says this kind of stuff hurts the party and hurts \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2  (R-MD), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I really just think that we have got to focus on the issues and kind of avoid all this kind of gender, race, kind of wild attacks.\par \par \uc2 We -- I think on the issues there's clear differences between the candidates, and that's what they ought to be focused on. And I just don't think it's helpful to be kind of shrinking the tent and alienating swing voters and turning people off. We can't just play to the base and expect to keep winning elections.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's one thing to do to go after \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and whether she's black or any of that, but the bigger concern among some in your party, maybe yourself as well, is when he attacks a very popular Georgia governor who is trying to help get him over the top in the state that he lost four years ago.\par \par \uc2 What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, first real quickly on the attacking of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for whether she's black or Asian or what she is, I was personally insulted by that, because I'm a mixed-race family and I have biracial grandkids. And, I mean, what kind of a signal does that send to them?\par \par \uc2 On the attacking \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , Kemp is a good friend of mine. I served on the \uc2 RGA\uc2  Executive Committee, and I was chairman of the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2 , and I just don't think it's useful at all to have a Republican nominee for president attacking a Republican governor over some perceived slight from years ago.\par \par \uc2 It just -- none of this stuff is helpful if you're trying to win an election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is blowing it if he keeps this up?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I have heard him say he's going to focus on the issues. And that's sort of what I have been suggesting for quite a while. And I think a lot of people in the party, although not everybody's speaking out, I think they would all like to see a campaign based on the issues that are important to Americans.\par \par \uc2 I don't think they really care about squabbles between governors or their wives, the first ladies or questioning people's race or making fun of cat ladies. That's just not how we win elections.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You also had called \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  to congratulate him and some even in your own party had said that that was a mistake and a bad thing to do. What did you think of the reaction you got for congratulating him?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, yes, I didn't really even call him. I just tweeted out a, hey, congratulations to he and his family on being selected. As I said, I chaired the nation's governors. I got along with all 50 governors, Republican and Democrat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I wasn't cheering him on. I didn't endorse him or say I was going to support them. I was just congratulating a fellow governor on being selected.\par \par \uc2 In the old days, that's what would always happen in politics. And...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, you're quite right. That was quite common. That kind of thing was quite common.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess the reason why I'm mentioning it is, is you were and are trying to stick to the issues without sticking to the other team personally.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You had gone into \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' record on dealing with crime, particularly her comments years ago that more police isn't the answer.\par \par \uc2 Could you explain why that is your focus now?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, when this first -- when the defund the police movement first started, I was one of the first statewide elected leaders in America to strongly speak out against it. I thought it was a terrible mistake. And I was -- I pushed the first-in-the-nation refund the police initiative, where I put 50 percent more into funding for police organizations in the state of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 My -- \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and my opponent, the county executive in Prince George's County, both had some very concerning, shocking comments in a story that just appeared and tape that just showed them talking about how bad police were. And they both have a record of trying to take money away from police officers.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have got to back the hardworking, dedicated men and women of law enforcement who put their lives on the line every day. And it's a huge difference in this election. I'm going to be the pro-law and order guy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you were trying to focus on an issue that resonates. And, again, we talk about some of these other distractions that are bedeviling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 And that might correct itself, but not if they keep going the way they're going. So you're saying that this example here that you're using and focusing on crime, that's something you know something about when you were governor, that that's the kind of stuff that they should be focused on?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I think they have got three great issues to focus on. Look, on -- the border is a terrible crisis, and we have got to secure the border. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has direct responsibility for that.\par \par \uc2 We have got the crime is a major issue, the border is an issue, and the economy is a major issue. And so that's what we ought to be having a real debate on between all the candidates at the national level and people running for the Senate and running for Congress, instead of just the name- calling and finger-pointing and talking about race and gender and all this other stuff.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it was a little more than a few weeks ago, Governor, that there were fears with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as the Democratic nominee. And, apparently, this was among the reasons why he was forced to step out of the race, the downballot damage he was doing, the Democratic candidates for governor, Congress, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 In some of the polls I see on you, sir, you have fallen eight points behind your Democratic opponent. Do you assign any of that to the downballot concerns from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Not really, no. That poll was not really a good poll, but it was also done prior to the switch-out.\par \par \uc2 Look, I have said I'm an underdog in this race. Although I was a successful governor, I just left office last year with a 77 percent approval, we have not elected a Republican senator since 1980 in 44 years. So, obviously, it's a tough race I'm in. But I don't know that it makes any difference whether it's \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because I'm going to focus on my race, on my record, and on the issues that I think are important to the people of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, none of them are talking about these kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It sounds like you and President Trump and his daughter-in-law, \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , have buried the hatchet. She raised some hackles when she all but intimated that she would not use party resources to help you in your run.\par \par \uc2 I guess that's not the case now?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, look, that's not -- I didn't even respond to those comments. I think she made a mistake in saying things. She was caught off guard and probably says something she regrets.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: But we're all working together.\par \par \uc2 But, look, my campaign is about my race in my state on the issues that Marylanders care about. And my campaign is financed by 20,000 people, the majority of which are Marylanders, and they're average very small contributions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I am curious, to that extent then, Governor, that has the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  in any way, shape, or form provided monies and support for you? It's tough to turn a blue seat red in your state. I just wonder, any updates on that front?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yes. No, it's -- the \uc2 RNC\uc2  doesn't really put money directly into Senate races, I don't believe. They do some things to help the state party.\par \par \uc2 But the NRSC...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: ... the National Republican Senatorial Committee, has done what they do for other candidates in other states.\par \par \uc2 But the vast majority of all the things we're doing are people that are supporting me in the state. Some are Republicans. Some are independents and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, the NRSC is not supporting you less -- I'm sorry -- sir -- is not supporting you any less or more than those other candidates?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Exactly. That's correct.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I wanted to get your thoughts. We have had shaky markets this week, concerns about a financial meltdown. It probably is a tad premature. But that would not be a good environment for Democrats, not all the time. But I'm wondering how that is working into your own campaign, looking at what's going on and fears that people have about how volatile and scary things are.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: I think it's a very big issue. It's actually -- we talked about the border and crime, which are the number two and number three issues.\par \par \uc2 But the number one issue is the economy. It was already a soft spot. It was -- as I go out there talking to folks, that's the main thing they're concerned about, affordability and inflation, the cost of groceries, the cost of housing. And the market, the dramatic drop in the market certainly isn't going to help.\par \par \uc2 There's people who are very concerned out there at every level, hardworking folks that are concerned about the economy. I think that's going to be a big issue. It's also an area where I'm strongest on, because I took the 49th out of 50 states economy and turned it into number six, the biggest economic turnaround in America.\par \par \uc2 And I got a Democratic legislature to cut taxes eight years in a row by $4.7 billion and took the largest deficit in history and turned it into the largest surplus. So, I think the people in Washington haven't balanced the budget in forever. We had to do it every year.\par \par \uc2 I think people are concerned about the debt. And these are all issues I'm going to focus on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By the way, we did reach out to his Democratic opponent, Prince George's County Executive Angela Alsobrooks. We have yet to hear back, but hope springs eternal. As you know, we don't bite here. We are fair and balanced and we stress and we want to hear from all sides. And we're optimistic we will hear from her.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the latest on Debby. It's on the move. And this tropical storm just doesn't stop with the flooding and with the disaster, and now with tornadoes -- the latest after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Tropical Storm Debby keeps barreling up the Eastern Coast and leaving a lot of disaster and indeed some deaths in its wake.\par \par \uc2 Brandy Campbell is in Lumberton, North Carolina with more -- Brandy.\par \par \uc2 BRANDY CAMPBELL, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, Hur -- or Tropical Storm Debby made landfall 2:00 a.m. overnight, and now we're starting to see some of the impacts of it. And this is miles and miles away from the coast. We're in Lumberton, and what we're really monitoring at this point is the high river levels that this town could be impacted by.\par \par \uc2 The Lumber River could reach or is expected to reach major flood stage at 2:00 a.m. overnight, once again. So, right now, we're at a local park. Just to give you guys perspective, I want to show you what it looked like a couple of hours ago, earlier in the day, before the river levels were what they are right now.\par \par \uc2 So, you can see the street and some water going across it. The parking lot has some water filling into it, but it's nothing compared to what we are seeing right now. At this point, we can't fully enter this park, and we're pretty much stopped at the entrance of it.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're also going to show you guys here live the river that is flowing close to this park, the reason why we are seeing this water fill in here in Lumberton. You can see the water just pretty much rushing through the town where the river continues.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Lumber River, currently at moderate levels measuring at just over 18 feet, it's expected to crest, again, at 2:00 a.m. at 19.10 feet. So, if so, it would be the seventh highest river level for the area. Public works, they did tell me it is predicted to crest and shouldn't threaten the city overall.\par \par \uc2 They did put up a temporary HESCO barrier ahead of the storm to protect areas that could have been impacted, also telling me that staff will be up overnight monitoring this area. But, overall, it's not just river levels impacting some of the cities here, but flash flooding. If you look onto our FOX Weather app, you will see there are several cities in Northwest North Carolina currently under warnings -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Brandy, thank you very much. Be safe.\par \par \uc2 Brandy following all of that.\par \par \uc2 And the storm is expected to dump still more rain as it moves northward, all the way up to Vermont, I believe, before it moves out to sea.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, say this about the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president. He speaks his mind bluntly, clearly, and, sometimes, you have to beep him a little bit. But, again, he is showing his unswerving support for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the Democratic ticket, and never even considered the other side.\par \par \uc2 He will explain why -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN FAIN, PRESIDENT, UNITED AUTO WORKERS: The working class has been left behind, and I don't -- I don't know how you feel, but I'm (EXPLETIVE DELETED) angry.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAIN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is all talk, and talk is cheap!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 FAIN: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  walks the walk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that is Shawn Fain's unequivocal way of saying the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ticket is the one he's going for, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  president joins us right now on the phone, because this, of course, this \uc2 UAW\uc2  event, is occurring concurrently right now.\par \par \uc2 Shawn, very good to have you back with us.\par \par \uc2 You never entertained really supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at all. Why?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Look, it's just -- it's the body of work. I mean, look at him in his own words and his own actions. I mean, I have never seen him stand for working- class people. I mean, in 2008, when it came to the auto crisis that was brought on by the banking failure and the economy was thrown into collapse, I mean, he blamed autoworkers then as the problem with the Big Three.\par \par \uc2 In 2015, he talked about doing a rotation of our good-paying jobs in the Midwest somewhere else where they pay less, and we'd be begging for our jobs back for less money. He never supported us when he was president. Plants were leaving the country. He did nothing, not one thing. He didn't say anything.\par \par \uc2 He told workers at \uc2 Lordstown\uc2 , don't sell your houses. And then he did nothing. And they all -- the plant closed. They all got displaced over the country, and he kept on doing what he does. I mean, workers were on strike in 2019. He didn't do a thing, say anything. I mean -- I mean, he has a long history of not supporting autoworkers.\par \par \uc2 And we know what he stands for. So I don't know why we would entertain an endorsement, when he has never shown us anything. His actions speak a lot more than his words do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That might be the case, but, Shawn, it's not as if the present White House occupant, with his push for electric vehicles and putting the Big Three automakers behind the eight ball -- many of them are delaying plans to bring out more E.V.s because they're just not selling. So that has put them in a crimp and, by extension, your workers, right?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, the interesting part of this is, I mean, there's jobs being created in America in the auto industry in the battery E.V. field. There are multiple plants already up and running. There are over almost -- well, there will be over 30 in this country when they -- when they complete building them all.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and our workers are manning -- we have a battery plant organized in Ohio in \uc2 Lordstown\uc2 , where workers were left behind where their plant was closed under Trump. The Biden administration and Harris administration brought in a new battery plant there. And now \uc2 Lordstown\uc2  workers who were displaced under Trump are going back home.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration went to work for us to secure funding to bring Belvidere, Illinois, plant back, also to put a battery plant there. And so...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you don't find that the push for E.V.s, Shawn, and the aggressive agenda puts your workers potentially in a bind here, that the move to even hybrid vehicles that we see \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and others doing is something that puts you behind the eight ball?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: What puts our workers in a bind is if we don't go after that work, which is what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 And what the Biden administration has done is, Neil, they came up with to help these companies transition with money with the IRA...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: ... and billions -- hundreds of billions of dollars to assist these companies in transitioning and also ensuring these are good-paying union jobs or good-paying jobs where workers can keep the standard of living up.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I find a great irony, though, in the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was all against it and now, all of a sudden, since \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is giving him $45 million a month, now all of a sudden he says that he supports it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, literally, this country is for sale under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And it's a shame. America is not for sale to the highest bidder.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Well, just to be clear, Shawn, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not said that he supports, is a big fan of E.V.s. He -- he still shares the reservation that Americans should be able to decide what kind of cars they want to drive.\par \par \uc2 But rather than go down that alley, I did want to get your take on another Sean. I believe he's your friend, Sean O'Brien of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , who is open \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I don't believe he or the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have committed to a candidate, but he said of President Trump that he's a candidate who is not afraid of hearing from new, loud and often critical voices.\par \par \uc2 Is that his way of saying you're not?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: No, I don't think so at all.\par \par \uc2 I just think -- I think, look, Sean O'Brien has the right to lead his union how he feels he needs to lead it. And, I mean, we have our way of doing things in the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . We listen to our members. We listen to our local leaders. We listen to our political reps all over the country.\par \par \uc2 And we know that a massive majority of our members will be voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in this election, just as they were going to for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you personally happy with a ticket with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on top than you were with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on top?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: Yes, I'm ecstatic about this ticket. I think it's complete.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look at the -- look at the results. I mean, you're seeing rallies with 10,000 people and more. We had almost 15,000 here yesterday in Detroit. There's a lot of excitement and energy. I mean, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  are a great -- are a great couple of people. They come from working-class roots.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  worked at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's when she was younger. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  was born and raised on a farm, worked on a farm. He's been a teacher. I mean, they know -- they know what it's like to struggle. They know what it's like to live paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 And, I mean -- and talking about that whole situation, look, I mean, I think one of the greatest sacrifices someone ever made, I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you talk about a selfless act, that he was willing to step aside and pass the baton and do the right thing, put the country first.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would never do that, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  only puts himself first.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, do you think, though, Shawn, that it wasn't so much that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stepped back, but he was pushed?\par \par \uc2 FAIN: No, I mean, it was his decision, obviously. I mean, he was the candidate. He was already -- he was already the candidate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 FAIN: And he had to make that -- so...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Shawn, thank you very much. I know you have been busy, so we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  president, making it very clear and very blunt, no gray shades, where he feels and how he stands, aggressively supporting this Democratic ticket.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the latest on this threat against Taylor Swift at concerts that are now off in Austria. But this is pretty scary stuff. Among those arrested, two \uc2 ISIS\uc2  suspects who planned to drive a bomb-laden car into a crowd. It gets creepier -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, by now you have heard the Taylor Swift had to cancel all three concerts in Vienna after two men were arrested planning to drive a bomb-laden car into a crowd.\par \par \uc2 But here's the kicker on that story. And one of them is still missing. They're trying to look for a third player. They were hired as stadium security.\par \par \uc2 Chris Swecker, the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  assistant director.\par \par \uc2 Chris, the implications of this are stunning. What did you make of these details?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SWECKER, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR: Yes, I mean, to cancel three days of a concert after you arrested the principals tells me that maybe they're not convinced they rounded everybody up and they haven't rounded out all their facts, so -- and then completed their investigation.\par \par \uc2 So that's what strikes me. That we haven't heard from Al Qaeda or \uc2 ISIS\uc2  recently and we now see that they're very actively recruiting and getting - - sort of inspiring people to conduct attacks, I don't think that's any accident. I think this is all as a result of the tensions in the Middle East and all the escalation back and forth between Middle East and Israel, so -- the other countries in Israel.\par \par \uc2 So, it's going to be -- I think there's an ongoing investigation. We will see if there are others involved.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, there's certainly a pattern to this, Chris, right? I mean, I remember the Ariana Grande concert in 2022 in Manchester, England; 22 were killed there. There were multiple attacks in Moscow by Chechen rebels, over the years, hundreds killed at such venues.\par \par \uc2 But they're usually in theaters or playhouses, very large seating venues. It does make you wonder if there's a pattern to that.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Yes, I mean, I think what's interesting here is that their planned attack was outside the venue. And that's always been my concern.\par \par \uc2 I ran the Salt Lake City Olympics after 9/11, and one of my biggest concerns was the crowd queuing up to go. In and you have so many people gathered in very -- sort of packed in trying to get into the venue. And these three young 19-, 17- and 15-year-old individuals decided that they were going to try to do -- you know, set off explosives and do their own suicide mission outside the venue.\par \par \uc2 And that's a fear here in the United States as well. That is a threat here in the United States. I think that's what keeps a lot of intelligence officials up at night.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and the idea that you get copycats, right, who hear this and, say, hey, we can do this too, right? Scary, yes.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 Yes, these are not card-carrying \uc2 ISIS\uc2  members. These are young kids. And it appears to me they have never been to the Middle East, as far as I can -- as far as the information goes. They have been inspired, probably on the Internet, probably not in person by anyone who's come in personal contact with them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: So I think this is that dynamic that we have been talking about for quite a while now, the inspired attacks from people who are disturbed.\par \par \uc2 This is three individuals. Usually, it's a lone wolf. This is three individuals working together.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing. Amazing. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 Chris, thank you very much, Chris Swecker.\par \par \uc2 This could have been quite tragic. Again, a lot of unanswered questions. We will continue to follow it, as we will the markets and what they do tomorrow as they try to balance out what's been a crazy week, a good day today.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240809ek8800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Campaigns With V.P. Pick \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  In Pennsylvania; Harris: Walz Voted Most Inspiring Faculty Member; Harris: Walz Worked To Raise To Minimum Wage; Trump Will End Affordable Care Act; Harris: Walz Will Be Ready On Day One; Democratic Presidential Nominee \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Chooses Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  As Her Vice Presidential Running Mate; \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  Challenges Republican Vice Presidential Candidate J.D. Vance To Debate During Speech At Rally In Philadelphia; U.S. Stock Market Recovers After Previously Day Losses; Israel Preparing For Retaliatory Attack From Iran After Assassination Of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Leader In Tehran\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Aishah Hasnie, Charles Payne, Trey  Yingst \par \uc2 6,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Good evening. Good evening. Good evening. Good evening. Good evening. Good evening. Oh, it is good to be back in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 So, let me say, on behalf of myself and the first second gentleman of the United States, my husband, Doug Emhoff. Thank you for the warm welcome.\par \par \uc2 And let me just say, it's good to be here with all of the friends, all of the leaders who are here. I want to thank former Governor \uc2 Ed Rendell\uc2 . Senator Bob Casey, who we will reelect this November. Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Mayor Cherelle Parker. And Chairman \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it is so good to be here with your incredible Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I will say, Josh is a dear, dear friend, and an extraordinary leader. He and I have been spending a lot of time together over the years. And I told Josh, look, I am so, so invested in our friendship and doing this together because together with \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , we will win Pennsylvania. We will win Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And I thank you, Josh. I thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, Philadelphia, I launched my campaign for the president of the United States a mere two weeks ago. And it's been a bit of a whirlwind.\par \par \uc2 And just last night the delegates to the Democratic National Convention finished voting and so, I stand -- I stand before you today to proudly announce I am now officially the Democratic nominee for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And so, now, we've got some work to do. We need to move to the general election and win that.\par \par \uc2 And to all the friends, listen, we also need to level set. We are the underdogs in this race. But we have the momentum, and I know exactly what we are up against.\par \par \uc2 Now, many of you know, before I was elected vice president or elected a United States senator, I was an elected attorney general and before that elected district attorney. And before that, I was a courtroom prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 So, in those roles, I took on perpetrators of all kinds. Predators who abused women. Fraudsters who scammed consumers. Cheaters who broke the rules for their own gain.\par \par \uc2 So, hear me when I say. I know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's type.\par \par \uc2 So, but let me just say, let me say, hold on, hold on, hold on. This campaign, our campaign, is not just a fight against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Our campaign, this campaign, is a fight for the future. It's a fight for the future.\par \par \uc2 And, Pennsylvania, we fight for a future with affordable housing, affordable health care, affordable child care, paid leave.\par \par \uc2 We fight for a future where we build a broad-based economy, where every American has the opportunity to own a home, to start a business, and to build wealth.\par \par \uc2 We fight for a future where we bring down prices that are still too high, and lower the cost of living for America's families. So that they have a chance not just to get by, but to get ahead.\par \par \uc2 We fight for a future where we defend our most fundamental freedoms, the freedom to vote. The freedom to be safe from gun violence. The freedom to love who you love openly and with pride.\par \par \uc2 And the freedom of a woman to make decisions about her own body, not having her government tell her what to do.\par \par \uc2 So -- I love you, too -- so, here's the thing, here's the thing, since the day that I announced my candidacy, I set out to find a partner who can help build this brighter future.\par \par \uc2 A leader who will help unite our nation and move us forward, a fighter for the middle-class, a patriot who believes, as I do, in the extraordinary promise of America.\par \par \uc2 A promise -- a promise of freedom, opportunity, and justice not just for some, but for all.\par \par \uc2 So, Pennsylvania, I'm here today because I found such a leader. Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  of the great state of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 So, to those who know him best -- to those who know him best, Tim is more than a governor. To his wife, Gwen, he is a husband. To his kids, Hope and Gus, he is a dad. To his fellow veterans, he is Sergeant Major Walz.\par \par \uc2 To the people -- to the people of Southern Minnesota, for 12 years, he was congressman. To his former high school students, he was Mr. Walz. And to his former high school football players, he was coach. Coach. Coach.\par \par \uc2 And, in 91 days, the nation will know Coach Walz by another name, vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, America, for some folks, they're just getting to know Coach Walz's story.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, he is the proud product of a middle-class family in rural Nebraska. He is a veteran who served our nation in uniform for more than two decades as a member of the Army National Guard. And he went to college on the G.I. Bill. He is someone who long before he entered politics worked as a teacher.\par \par \uc2 When Coach Walz's and his wife, Gwen, moved from his native Nebraska to Minnesota nearly 30 years ago, they both took jobs at the local high school. Coach Walz's taught social studies. Gwen taught English.\par \par \uc2 After school, Tim was the linebackers coach for the football team where I have heard the stories about he had a knack for using the game of football to teach life lessons. He saw the potential in kids who sometimes didn't even see it in themselves.\par \par \uc2 Under those Friday night lights, Coach Walz motivated his players to believe they could achieve anything. And, together, they defied the odds -- hear this now, going from a winless record to the school's first ever state championship. Coach. Coach.\par \par \uc2 And I will say and I will add, Tim wasn't only a role model on the football field. Around that same time, Coach Walz was approached by a student in his social studies class. The young man was one of the first openly gay students at the school and was hoping to start a Gay-Straight Alliance.\par \par \uc2 At a time -- at a time when acceptance was difficult to find for LGBTQ students, Tim knew the signal that it would send to have a football coach get involved.\par \par \uc2 So, he signed up to be the group's faculty adviser. And, as students have said, he made the school a safe place for everybody.\par \par \uc2 In the high school yearbook, the students voted Coach Walz the "most inspiring faculty member."\par \par \uc2 And, as I think everyone here can see, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  was the kind of teacher and mentor that every child in America dreams of having and that every kid deserves. The kind of coach, because he's the kind of person who makes people feel like they belong and then inspires them to dream big, and that's the kind of vice president he will be. And that's the kind of vice president America deserves.\par \par \uc2 So, it was Coach Walz's students who actually helped him decide to run for office, and he served 12 years in Congress, representing a purple district, as he reached across the aisle to get things done.\par \par \uc2 He was the highest-ranking -- he was the highest-ranking enlisted man to ever serve in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the top Democrat on the Veterans Committee, and he was known as one of Capitol Hill's best marksmen, winning a bipartisan sharpshooting contest year after year.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, Tim worked to raise the minimum wage to protect the freedom of workers to join a union.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And he passed one of the critical votes to pass the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Which of course gave health insurance to tens of millions of Americans. I'm going to tell you, when we win, Tim and I will continue to make the Affordable Care Act even stronger.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: We will win! We will win! We will win!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We will win.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Now, let's talk about what we're dealing with on the other side.\par \par \uc2 So, on that last topic, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets a chance, he will end the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And take us back to a time when insurance companies had the power to deny people with pre-existing conditions. Do you remember what that was like? Children with asthma, breast cancer survivors, grandparents with diabetes.\par \par \uc2 Well, Governor Walz and I will not let that happen. Because we believe health care should be a right and not just a privilege for those who can afford it.\par \par \uc2 As Governor, Tim has continued to fight for working families. He secured paid leave for workers in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 And he refused -- he refused, as Governor, to allow any student in their public schools to go hungry. So, he made school breakfast and lunch free for every child.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and I, we agree about many things, including when our middle class is strong, America is strong.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And strengthening the middle class will be my defining goal as I am president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, Pennsylvania, ours is a fight for the future of the middle class, and it is a fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: In this moment, we are witnessing a full-on attack against hard- fought, hard-won freedoms and rights. Take reproductive freedom. Now, think about this. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he wants to punish women.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Boo!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And as a result of his actions today in America, one out of three women live in a state with a Trump abortion ban -- one out of three.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Boo.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Some of these bans go back to the 1800s. Even before women had a right to vote. Think about that. Well, Tim and I have a message for Trump and others who want to turn back the clock on our fundamental freedoms -- we're not going back.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: We're not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We're not going back.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: We're not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And so, let me say about \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , he has shown up to stand against these attacks long before he stood on the stage with me. After Roe was overturned, he was the first governor in the country to sign a new law that enshrined reproductive freedom as a fundamental right.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  by my side, when I am president of the United States and we win majorities in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , we will pass a bill to restore reproductive freedom, and I will proudly sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has also defended the sacred freedom to vote. As governor -- as governor, he signed the most significant expansion of voting rights in Minnesota in over 50 years.\par \par \uc2 And with Governor Walz's help, when I am president, we are going to finally pass the John Lewis Voting Rights Act and the Freedom to Vote Act. We're going to get it done.\par \par \uc2 So, Tim is a hunter and a gun owner who believes, as the majority of gun owners do, that we need reasonable gun safety laws in America.\par \par \uc2 So, as governor, he expanded background checks and increased penalties for illegal firearm sales.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And together, when we win in November, we are finally going to pass universal background checks, red flag laws, and an assault weapons ban.\par \par \uc2 Through his work, Tim, he, you know, the way I think about it, he really does shine a light on a brighter future that we can build together. In his state, he has been a model chief executive. And with his experience, I'm telling you, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will be ready on day one.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: In fact -- in fact, when you compare his resume.\par \par \uc2 Shall we? To Trump's running mate, well, well, some might say it's like -- It's like a matchup between the varsity team and the J.V. squad.\par \par \uc2 So, Pennsylvania, ultimately in this election, we each face a question. What kind of country do we want to live in? A country -- a country of freedom, compassion, and rule of law? Or a country of chaos, fear, and hate?\par \par \uc2 And here is the beauty of our democracy. We each have the power to answer that question. We each have the power to answer that question. The power is with the people!\par \par \uc2 We love our country. And I believe it is the highest form of patriotism to fight for the ideals of our country.\par \par \uc2 That -- that is how we preserve the promise of America. And after all, you know, the promise of America is what makes it possible for two middle-class kids, one a daughter of Oakland, California, who was raised by a working mother. The other, a son of the Nebraska plains who grew up working on a farm.\par \par \uc2 It's the promise of America. Because only in America, only in America, is it possible for them together to make it all the way to the White House.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Only in America. Only in America.\par \par \uc2 Only in America.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: USA! USA! USA! USA! USA! USA!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's right. That's right. And so, Coach Walz and I may hail from different corners of our great country, but our values are the same. And we both believe in lifting people up, not knocking them down.\par \par \uc2 He and I, we both know, the vast majority of people in our country have so much more in common than what separates them.\par \par \uc2 When we look at folks, we see in our fellow Americans, neighbors, not enemies. Not enemies.\par \par \uc2 And so, my promise to you is this. Our campaign will reach out to everyone, from red states to blue states.\par \par \uc2 From the heartland to the coast. In rural, urban, suburban, and tribal communities.\par \par \uc2 We are running a campaign on behalf of all Americans. And when elected, we will govern on behalf of all Americans.\par \par \uc2 And so, with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  by my side, and with all of you at our side, let us fight for the promise of our future. And, with that, I ask Pennsylvania, are you ready to make your voices heard?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Do we believe in freedom? Do we believe in opportunity? Do we believe in the promise of America?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And are we ready to fight for it?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And when we fight, we win!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We win!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And now, welcome the next vice president of the United States, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D) MINNESOTA: Thank you. Wow. Thank you, Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Thank you, Madam Vice President, for the trust you put in me, but maybe more so, thank you for bringing back the joy.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I'm thrilled to be on this journey with you and Doug, this incredible journey. And Pennsylvania, I know you know this, but my God, what a treasure you have in \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Holy hell can this guy bring the fire. He can bring the fire. This is a visionary leader. Also, I have to tell you, everybody in America knows, when you need a bridge fixed, call that guy.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And I think sometimes we forget, and you see people a little one dimensional, but seeing a guy who cares so deeply about his family, a man with compassion, vision. And I have tell you this -- I know this from experience -- there is no one you would rather go a Springsteen concert in Jersey with than him, than that guy.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Bruce!\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Bruce!\par \par \uc2 And I can't wait for all of you and America to get to know my incredible wife Gwen, a 29-year public school educator.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Don't ever underestimate teachers.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And our two beautiful kids, Hope and Gus. I couldn't be prouder to be on this ticket and to help Vice President Harris become what we all know is very, very good for us to think about, next president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: From her first day as a prosecutor, as a district attorney, attorney general of the great state of California, a United States senator, and vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Vice President Harris has fought on the side of the American people.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: She took on the predators. She took on the fraudsters. She took down the transnational gangs. She stood up against powerful corporate interests, and she never hesitated to reach across the aisle if it meant improving people's lives.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And I want all of you to hold this, and don't ever underestimate the power of this. She does it all with a sense of joy.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I know a little something about that commitment to people. I was born in West Point, Nebraska. I lived in Butte, a small town of 400, where community was a way of life. Growing up, I spent the summers working on the family farm. My mom and dad taught us, show generosity towards your neighbors and work toward a common good.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: My dad served in the Army during the Korean War. And with his encouragement, at 17 I joined the Army National Guard.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: For 24 years I proudly wore the uniform of this nation.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: The National Guard gave me purpose. It gave me the strength of a shared commitment to something greater than ourselves. And just as it did for my dad and millions of others, the G.I. Bill gave me a shot at a college education.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: My dad was a teacher. My brothers and sisters and I followed in their footsteps. Three out of four of us married teachers.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: It's what we do.\par \par \uc2 For nearly 20 years, I had the privilege of teaching high school social studies and coaching football.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Including winning that state championship. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Don't ever close the yearbook.\par \par \uc2 But it was my students, they encouraged me to run for office. They saw in me what I was hoping to instill in them, a commitment of common good, a belief that one person can make a difference. So, in 2006 -- 2006 I took a leap and I ran for Congress.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And because high school teachers are super optimistic, I was running in a district that had one Democrat since 1892.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: My neighbors graced me with an opportunity to represent them in the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I'm proud of the work we did there together. I worked across the aisle on veterans' issues, on agriculture, and on ways to grow rural economies. I learned the art of compromise without compromising my values.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And now as governor of the great state of Minnesota, I bring those experiences to bear in tackling the challenges that are facing our great state. Minnesota's strength comes from our values, our commitment to working together, to seeing past our differences, to always being willing to lend a helping hand. Those are the same values I learned on the family farm and tried to still in my students. I took it to Congress and to the state capital, and now Vice President Harris and I are running to take those very values to the White House.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sees the world a little differently than us.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: First of all, he doesn't know the first thing about service.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: He doesn't have time for it because he is too busy serving himself.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Again and again and again Trump weakens our economy to strengthen his own hand.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: He mocks our laws. He sews chaos and division. And that's to say nothing of his record as president.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: He froze in the face of the COVID crisis. He drove our economy into the ground. And make no mistake, violent crime was up under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: That's not even counting the crimes he committed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: You know, some of us, some of are --\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Lock him up! Lock him up! Lock him up!\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Some of us in here are old enough to remember -- I see you down there. I see those old white guys. Some of us are old enough to remember --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- when it was Republicans who were talking about freedom. It turns out now what they meant was the government should be free to invade your doctor's office.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: In Minnesota we respect our neighbors and their choices that they make.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Even if we wouldn't make the same choice for ourselves, there is a golden rule -- mind your own damn business.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: These guys are after my heart chanting "Mind your own damn business." That feels good, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 Look, that includes IVF. And this gets personal for me and my family. When my wife and I decided to have children, we spent years going through infertility treatments. And I remember praying every night for a call for good news. The pit in my stomach when the phone rang and the agony when we heard that the treatments hadn't worked. So it wasn't by chance that when we welcomed our daughter into the world, we named her Hope.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: When the vice president and I talk about freedom, we mean the freedom to make your own healthcare decisions.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And for our children to be free to go to school without worrying they will be shot dead in their classrooms.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: By the way, as you heard, I was one of the best shots in Congress, but in Minnesota, we believe in the Second Amendment, but we also believe in common sense gun violence laws.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Vice President Harris's idea for freedom is a ticket, for education to be that ticket to the middle class, not crippling debt. Air that's clean, water that's pure, communities that are safe.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- place where we settle our political differences not through violence, but with our votes.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And that's what this election is about. What direction will this country go in? We're not going back. Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would damn sure take us backwards. Let's be clear about that.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And don't believe him what he plays dumb. He knows exactly what Project 2025 will do to restrict our freedoms.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: To rig the economy to help the superrich. If Trump gets a chance to return, he's going to pick up exactly where he left off four years ago, only this time it will be much, much worse. Raising costs on middle class family, he will repeal the Affordable Care Act. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: He'll gut Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And when somebody tells you who they are, believe them. He said he would ban abortion across this country, and he'll do it whether or not Congress is there or not.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not fighting for you or your family. He never set that that kitchen table like the one I grew up at wondering how we were going to pay the bills. He sat in his country club in Mar-a-Lago wondering how he could cut taxes for his rich friends.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 And I got to tell you, his running mate shares his dangerous and backward agenda for this country.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: J.D. Vance literally, literally wrote the forward for the architect of the Project 2025 agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Like all regular people I grew up with in the heart of land, J.D. studied at Yale --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- had his career funded by Silicon Valley billionaires and then wrote a bestseller trashing that community. Come on! That's not what middle America is. And I got tell you, I can't wait to debate the guy.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: That is, if he's willing to get off the couch and show up.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: You see what I did there?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I got to tell you, pointing out just an observation of mine that I made. I just have to say it. You know it. You feel it. These guys are creepy, and, yes, just weird as hell. That's what you see. That's what you see.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: So you know what's out there, so say it with me. We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 WALZ: We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 WALZ: We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 WALZ: We are not going back!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We aren't going back! We aren't going back! We aren't going back!\par \par \uc2 WALZ: So we got 91 days. My God, that's easy. We'll sleep when we're dead.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Over those next 91 days and every day in the White House, I'll have Vice President Harris's back, every single day. And we'll have yours.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: You know how this works. We can't do it alone. We need you, each and every one of you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Go over to KamalaHarris.com, get on board, because we need you. Free to make our own choices. This leader, this compassionate, careful, joyous leader believes in each and every one of you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: My God, you came here tonight to sit at the very top because you love this country, and you're not going back.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: She believes in the opportunity for every single person to join the middle class. She believes in the promise of America. We just got to fight. We just got to fight. Because, as soon-to-be President Harris says, when we fight, we win!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We win!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Thank you, Philadelphia, thank you, Vice President, God bless America!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her just- chosen nominee, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , the rollout in Philadelphia, a packed crowd there as Vice President Harris introducing her new running mate, talking about his history as a hunter, as a football coach, as a veteran, and as a congressman and a governor, Packaging him in very moderate sounding tones. and, obviously, Republican critics point to his governing in Minnesota as much more progressive.\par \par \uc2 This is a packed house here in Philadelphia, and an excited democratic base that is ready to move this ticket forward. Obviously, Republicans have a lot to say about the policies that they are putting forward, talked about some of them in these speeches tonight. Senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy is traveling with the vice president, is there at the rally. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. And can you see that the vice president and her new running mate have entered the little buffer area to take some photos. That is something that we have not seen any of the major candidates do since the assassination attempt on President Trump. Security obviously big.\par \par \uc2 And everything that we have seen in this room for the last couple hours proves President Biden right. When he dropped out, he explained that part of the reason is that he thought there was a time for new voices to come forward. And there are 10,000 people here, Democrats, young people from the Philadelphia area who are very excited about this ticket in a way that they were not at Biden events that we were going to before Biden dropped, Harris rose, and announced Walz today.\par \par \uc2 This event did not have much to do with what Republicans are talking about which are some of Walz's very progressive positions. It was very biographical. And then the Democratic platform that they put forward was pretty boilerplate. They will protect abortion access, protect the Affordable Care Act, increase gun control, call J.D. Vance weird, and stop Trump. That's what we are going to hear a lot of in the next couple of weeks as the ticket has now been officially debuted here in Philadelphia. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The big rollout obviously coming up in the Chicago convention for the DNC. Peter Doocy live in Philadelphia. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Republican vice presidential nominee J.D. Vance is on his own battleground state tour. He appeared in Philadelphia a few hours before the Democrats arrived. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is also in Philadelphia tonight with that part of the story. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. The Trump-Vance team is really trying to frame this brand-new Democratic ticket as an appeasement to the far left. In fact, they came out pretty strong and hard today with a brand-new attack ad calling \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  a, quote, "leftwing extremist."\par \par \uc2 And it comes as J.D. Vance was in Philadelphia today rallying here on the ground. He has been following -- he's going to follow the Harris-Walz campaign across seven of the swing states this week.\par \par \uc2 Now, some in the GOP do feel that these states are now still in play, Pennsylvania, Arizona, and that the down ballot races are also in play as well. A source tells FOX News that Senate Republicans were actually relieved today with the pick of Walz because Pennsylvania Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  and Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , they were nervous that those two picks could really cost them those two states.\par \par \uc2 Now, with that, some Republican voters in a state say that Trump and Vance really need to start focusing and sticking to those kitchen table issues, not personal attacks, so we know for those moderates and independents.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, finally, as far as a possibly vice presidential debate, Vance says that he is ready to go toe to toe with Walz. But he doesn't want to get into specifics until the DNC is over and Walz is officially the vice presidential nominee. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live in Philadelphia. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to the markets. Stock markets rallied after Monday's big selloff. The Dow was up 294. The S&P 500 gained 54. The Nasdaq jumped 167.\par \par \uc2 Let's look at Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's economic record and what this ticket may mean for the economy. Charles Payne from FOX Business joins us now. He hosts "Making Money with Charles Payne." Charles, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You sat through this speech. You've kind of looked at how this ticket has formed. What's your thought on how it may play?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's ironic because a lot of the things I heard from both the current vice president and vice president nominee were, it seems like they were being articulated against the current administration. I heard Walz mention debt, and, of course, our debt level is through the roof, and at this very moment we are spending at breakneck speed. In fact, we are spending now the kind of spending a government does, that we have done historically only in war time.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, the notion of the wealthy or the rich doing much better under Republicans, Bret, let me tell you right now, the wealthy have never had it so good as to right now. They have made more money because of President Biden than at any time in history, in part because of all the -- when you put trillions of dollars into the economy -- and you do it for two reasons. A, you know people are going to get these checks and spend it. B, you know they are going to spend more than they get. So you give them $1,400 bucks, they're going to spend $2,400 bucks.\par \par \uc2 And where does that money eventually go? To the top one percent. And that's who has benefited. They have benefited the most from high interest rates. They get dividend checks. They have bonds. Everyone else, the 80 percent of America out there, all they get is higher credit card rates.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it was interesting to hear a couple of things. One was a lot of focus on personality and bio and less about policy. But, also, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  coming out and saying that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  brought the joy back.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Poor \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't have any joy, I guess, in his race.\par \par \uc2 You had somebody on his show that said this is following a 1970s script about inflation, about heavy regulation, and how the impact of those policies effects the market and people's economy at home.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And also, the central issue now, how does it affect the Federal Reserve? I know the federal reserve usually goes over people's heads. But they are in charge of taming inflation. That's their job. And they are having a hard time doing it because their other job is making sure that we have what they call maximum employment. How do you do one? One, you have to -- to quell inflation is like putting out a forest fire. You have to do serious damage. If you ever see firemen arrive to building, they don't care. They are breaking down buildings, they're breaking windows. They have to break things. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has to break things and at the same time hope they don't break them too much. And they've never had this sort of dilemma before because there has never been this amount of money poured into an economy.\par \par \uc2 And again, it's so skewed because economists look at it. Imagine a bucket. Let's say we fill a bucket and put it on a scale. And economists will say, wow, that's a heavy bucket. It's a strong economy. And then imagine the same bucket, all of the water is on 20 percent of the side of the bucket. The economists are looking at the scale and saying it's still a strong bucket. When you look inside, 80 percent is bone dry. Bone dry. That's the American public right now. But the economists don't know how to tackle this. How do you tackle this situation? They have never been faced with it before.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's fascinating. And we appreciate --\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: By the way, that jobs report Friday was the first major red flag, but I think there's going to be a whole lot more. It was a very disappointing number.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Charles, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, presidential candidates will have to answer questions about the Middle East, and its' very tense over there. Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst joins us from Haifa, Israel. He has a book coming out this fall about the October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  massacre. It's called "Black Saturday." And with that comes a new title as well. Trey, it's great to see you. Good evening. I think this is a very tense time. I had the former Israeli prime minister on "THE STORY" this afternoon, saying they expect Iran to attack somewhere, some way in the next 24 to 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Bret, exactly. The attack from Iran could be just hours away. And the region is bracing for the possibility of this unraveling quickly. Local media is reporting tonight the Israeli military is considering a preemptive strike against Iran's largest proxy in the region, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , that sits just about 17 miles from where we are standing right now. Earlier today there was yet another drone attack in the northern part of this country, injuring a number of people.\par \par \uc2 And as we look forward about how all of this is going to end, the Israelis are looking very close at the November election. They understand that Vice President Harris and the Biden administration says the only way out of in rising tension is a ceasefire, but the Israelis say before any sort of ceasefire takes place, there will be offensive action as they brace for this attack from Iran and what the Israeli response will be. In "Black Saturday" we discuss how this war began back on October 7th and the days just after. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We look forward to the book, and obviously we will be tuning in very closely to see what happens over there in Israel. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we took this rollout of the new Democratic ticket, Vice President Harris choosing Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and that rollout in Philadelphia, as we did when former President Trump chose J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, House Republicans introduce a bill to boost taxes on universities with massive endowments.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 We promise we'll go back to normal tomorrow. There won't be a rally during this hour.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. We've got you covered for all things politics and around the world, all the news that's fit to give you in one hour. We'll get it in.\par \par \uc2 THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240807ek8600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Democrats Tries To Spin Away \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's Radical Record; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Blames \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  For Bad Jobs Report; Debating A Debate: Trump, Harris Squabble Over Date And Place For Showdown; Mutiny To Monument: After Leading Coup, Pelosi Wants Biden On Mt. Rushmore; RFK Jr. Admits Dumping Dead Bear In Central Park\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Katie Pavlich, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,359 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: To Art's point, though, people are nervous, and there is talk that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could jump the gun here and start cutting rates aggressively as soon as September, something sooner than that.\par \par \uc2 And forget a quarter-point cut, they're talking half-point cut, maybe three-quarters point cut, scary times, scary reactions. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Hello everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, along with Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Katie Pavlich and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Democrats beware, your Kamala mania party is about to get crashed. Right now, Vice President Harris is riding a wave of fawning media coverage and favorable polls as the press helped sugarcoat her radical and dangerously liberal track record, but \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's former adviser David Axelrod has some bad news for Democrats who think that this Harris honeymoon will last forever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, FORMER BARACK OBAMA'S ADVISER: You know, there's a lot of irrational exuberance on the side of on the Democratic side of the aisle right now, because there was despair for some period of time about what November was going to look like. Now people feel like there's a chance, but it is absolutely Trump's race to lose right now. He is ahead, and he is ahead most of the battleground states. Nobody should be -- everybody should be sober about that on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And you can expect Kamala mania to fade as more Americans realize what a disaster she is on the issues, Harris has been flip-flopping like crazy on her support of far-left policies like abolishing ICE, banning fracking and eliminating private insurance, and it's leading Democrats to come up with some ridiculous excuses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: What do you think that voters should think when she's reversed herself on several issues just in the last four years?\par \par \uc2 SEN. LAPHONZA BUTLER (D-CA): I think that voters should think that she's a human being who learns new things every single day.\par \par \uc2 BASH: So, no concern about flip-flopping?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: No, no concern about flip-flopping. This is a part -- our country changes every single day. The conditions of our country changes every single day. The vice president has been consistent in her values, and there are lots of different ways to solve the challenges that are in front of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Jesse, should we attribute Kamala's flip-flopping to A, political opportunism. B, her effort to become a Republican or C, her just being a human being and learning about life.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: D, all of the above. I had a little irrational exuberance last week when Greg was gone. Anybody but Greg was sitting in that chair, and it was fantastic. Same thing with Biden. It's anybody but Biden and the Dems are excited.\par \par \uc2 This Democrat machine, I am in awe of. They can take a woman who was probably less popular than \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 , and now she's the pantsuit Messiah. This is someone they tried to rip off the ticket a year ago, and now she's a cultural phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 I bet if I switched parties and registered as a Democrat, identified as African-American.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Hear, hear.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And tapped Harold Ford Jr. as vice president, and just said all the things I've been saying on THE FIVE, I'm a human being, and I've listened and I've learned, I might probably do well against Trump in the battlegrounds. That's how powerful this machine is.\par \par \uc2 I know Harold Ford Jr. likes to give people the benefit of the doubt, so let's just give her the benefit of the doubt. Let's just say she did listen and she learned, because she's a human being.\par \par \uc2 Well, she stopped listening and learning after she got into the White House because we've been telling her to close the border. We've been telling her to stop spending money. We've been telling her to stop putting boys in girl's locker rooms. She hasn't listened at all. She doesn't learn from any of that.\par \par \uc2 And not only you have to listen to be a leader, you have to lead. When has \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  led anything? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  led on tariffs, border, China, \uc2 NATO\uc2 . You can look at his website right now, he has a vision for what he wants to do.\par \par \uc2 You go to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's website right now. She doesn't even have a policy proposal if she gets elected.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, Harold, why is Kamala relying on surrogates to get her message out?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Good to be around the table, I had a good weekend. I'd say a couple of things. I like the pantsuit Messiah thing, that was good.\par \par \uc2 The -- I'd say a couple things about what she's doing, and some of it I've said in the past, and I think it applies to both candidates, laying out a vision for the future is how you win a presidential race, how you win big political races, gubernatorial races, if you're in one of the big states, for that matter, any state, Senate races, but certainly when you're running -- when you're running for president.\par \par \uc2 George Wallace was a Democrat, was an Alabama governor who was a segregationist. He campaigned in 1968 on basically codifying that I couldn't marry my wife because she's white. He said he didn't believe that Blacks and whites should be able to get married. He believed that blacks had a certain place in society.\par \par \uc2 Before he died, you know who hugged him? \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  hugged him. Andy Young hugged him. He made a -- he made a change.\par \par \uc2 I think when people make changes, the question you're really getting at Jesse, and I take you seriously when you talk about these things, she's got to prove that she's not just ambitious. She's got to prove that she's authentic. I think people can change. You got to give people space to change, because that's the only way South Africa turned out the way it turned. And Mandela became president because people changed.\par \par \uc2 I hope people in the Middle East can change. The jury is still out on a lot of those issues, but in our political system, people jump from being Democrat to Republican, just as -- just as President Trump did, and I think many people believe he's more Republican than his Democrat, if he's not an altogether a Republican. But I don't dismiss your point lightly. Vice President Harris --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, but George Wallace didn't change his opinion on everything when he was running for president the next time, it was on his deathbed.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Thank God. No, it was before he died.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right, so \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not on her deathbed.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Well, I'm a Christian. I give people an -- I wish she had changed sooner. Maybe he was changing sooner, and when he died, the question is, do you believe in those things and he believed in a greater America?\par \par \uc2 Your bigger point is this, she in a debate is going to have to explain what accounts for these changes. J.D. Vance will have to answer in his debate what accounts for your changes, and if they're authentic, if they're believable, if they're credible, then American people will believe.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But the question is, why is she relying on surrogates? Why isn't she doing it herself?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: But I think she's been out there, and she will continue that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She hasn't talk with the presser in 15 days.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: She should, I agree with you. She should. Forgive me. You're absolutely right, she should.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you. OK, Katie, how is it that Trump's lead has pretty much been diminished. And how do Republicans come back from this mania?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , people do change. They change their positions. But she is using these surrogates and these anonymous sourc