e one of the hottest issues of the 2024 election, Harris and Biden team up to shake up the Supreme Court with a plan that includes presidents being able to pick new justices when they are two years in, thanks to term limits.\par \par \uc2 We'll speak live with \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  member, Dan Goldman. But first, let's get to the breaking news out of Israel as they make clear that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  will pay a heavy price for the bombing that brutally killed 12 children on a soccer field in the Golan Heights. The White House says Israel has every right to respond, but nobody wants an all-out war, they say. This as hostilities have clearly escalated.\par \par \uc2 Former Secretary of State and CIA Director \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  is standing by but first to correspondent Trey Yingst reporting live on this as he has throughout the weekend from Tel Aviv. Hi Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. Overnight, the Security Cabinet of Israel met to discuss operational plans to target \uc2 Hezbollah in Lebanon\uc2  following this deadly rocket attack over the weekend that killed 12 Druze teenagers and children. We were there in the aftermath. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The cousin of 11-year-old, Alma Fakir Eldin, weeps as she clutches her photo. It's all that she has left of the young Druze child, one of 12 people killed in Saturday's \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket attack. At their family home, Alma's father stands in her bright pink room.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This was her schoolwork.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: "Eliminate \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . That's it. That's what I want," Aimin Fakir Eldin says. What do you remember about her most?\par \par \uc2 "When she was alive or when I had to collect the pieces of her," he responds with a population of just 11,000 people. The trauma of this attack affected every resident of Majdal Shams, a Druze community in the Golan Heights that have a large funeral today for the dead.\par \par \uc2 The grief that Majdal Shams is experiencing collectively right now is unimaginable. There are 12 young members of their community being buried in the aftermath of this horrific \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket attack.\par \par \uc2 At the scene of the attack, teams are still going inch by inch, searching for any remains of those killed. Between the shrapnel, bikes and scooters, a crater marks the site of the massacre.\par \par \uc2 "The wounded and the dead were children wearing sports gear," this first responder says it's a disaster.\par \par \uc2 The region is now bracing for the possibility of a much larger war with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . Already multiple international airlines have suspended flights to and from Beirut and many countries are urging civilians to leave Lebanon immediately. Martha.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very moving reporting over the weekend and what an incredibly tragic story that community will never, never forget what happened over the course of this weekend. And Israel -- thank you, Trey, and Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  met with Vice President Harris in Washington, as you remember, last week. Now, after the meeting, Harris did include this in her comments about Israeli actions in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: To everyone who has been calling for a ceasefire and to everyone who yearns for peace, I see you and I hear you. What has happened in Gaza over the past nine months is devastating. We cannot look away in the face of these tragedies, we cannot allow ourselves to become numb to the suffering, and I will not be silent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, let's bring in former Secretary of State and CIA Director \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , a Fox News Contributor. Mike, good to see you. Always good to have you with us, former secretary.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tell me your reaction to this attack, what you think it means about what Iran is trying to potentially provoke here and what you think about Vice President Harris's comments about Gaza?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Well, first of all, condolences to the families who lost loved ones. What happened in the north in Golan, the Israeli territory, not Israeli-controlled territory, that's Israel that they fired into. Second, look, we've, we've been talking about this on your show for months. The real risk is from the north, the far greater capabilities, precision-guided munitions they can fire into cities in Jerusalem, Tel Aviv, out of Lebanon, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  can.\par \par \uc2 And so, Israel's got to get 80,000 people back into their homes in the north and to see this tragedy only reminds us of the risk. This is all, Martha, this is all Iran. This is coordinated by the Iranians. It is approved by the Iranians, it is funded by the Iranians and so no matter how much Israel strikes back at \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , it will matter, but it's not going to determine the outcome. The outcome will be determined by will the world stand up and demand that Iran be sanctioned, that we deny them the wealth that they need to continue their terror campaign.\par \par \uc2 And then, finally, you asked about the vice president's comments. When I hear her say we hear you, we listen, it fundamentally tells me that she doesn't understand the moral distinction between \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the Israeli people. She says, we, you know, I will not be silent. Well, America and Israel would be an awful lot safer if she would be silent. You can't say something that supports our Israel, Israeli ally, and Israeli really going to deliver peace and get the American hostages return home.\par \par \uc2 You should just be quiet because what you're doing there, when you speak that is coded language, and she's speaking to uh... pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  members of her party pro-Palestinian members of her party and she is also implicitly messaging \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leadership saying hang in there stay the course continue to present risk don't release the hostages because America is trying to get a ceasefire which will give you continued victory and allow \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to return to the places from which they've departed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And of course, that means continue to hold civilians, women and children as human shields in this fight on the Gaza side of the border. You know, this is the headline of the New York Post: "The \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Israel bashing has made all-out war versus \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  more likely." And then this from the Wall Street Journal: weakness won't deter \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  after its soccer field attack. "The better way to make war less likely is to announce that American munitions transfer to Israel will be expedited immediately as they were earlier in the war and as Congress has approved, and that oil sanctions on Iran will be enforced again. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and its controllers in Iran will get that message even without a translator," says the editorial board at the Wall Street Journal. \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Yes, those statements from the Post and the Journal are spot on. Frankly, when the Biden administration lost deterrence, it was a direct result, Martha, of violating those central principles, right? They rewarded Iran, they tried to get back in the nuclear deal, they essentially ceased enforcing all the sanctions that had been put in place during the Trump administration. That emboldened the Iranians, and you see the result of that today, right?\par \par \uc2 On October 7th you saw it, and you saw it in the attacks in the Golan. These will only continue, the Houthi missiles in the Red Sea, these will only continue until such time as the United States and Israel work together to punish Iran. That's the nexus, that's the center point, that is the head of the snake, and that's where the costs have to be imposed. Short of that, Martha, we're going to see continued escalation, continued risk, not only for Israel, but for Gulf Arab states and for the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And we've seen in policy in the past, both in the Trump administration and the Bush administration, that isolating Iran is what improves the situation. In building the ally around the United States helps to isolate Iran. But you know, as has been said by Senator Cotton and others, he can't understand why the administration is tougher on Israel than they are on Iran. And does this window of 99 days before the election provide a window that will be taken advantage of by Iran, in your opinion, Secretary Pompeo?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: It does. Undoubtedly. These next 99 days, the Iranians will push they will test, they will find out how far the Biden administration, and, and Vice President Harris's administration are going to allow that. The American people and the Israeli people to be pushed around. Strength will work. We demonstrated that for four years. We were able to successfully deter much of Iranian terrorism when you when you show weakness, Martha. They're going to they're going to drive a truck through that hole and you we've seen it and sadly the Israeli people are experiencing that now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, Secretary Pompeo, thank you very much. So, James Carville's warning to Democrats with the election just 99 days away. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: What happened was that people wanted something different and they got it, and there is some good -- look, they're going to she's going to get slaughtered. They're coming out, they're coming, and this is just part of that, no different than it was any other time.\par \par \uc2 And they have got to get up and get ready, and they've got to be able to defend and attack at the same time. And there's going to be a time of struggle, a definition as to who Vice President Harris is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Brand new polling showing an eight-point jump in favorability for Vice President Harris over the past week since she rose to the top of the Democratic nominee list.\par \par \uc2 She will announce her running mate, we're told, over the course of this next week, six or seven days or so. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reporting that it is down to Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , Pennsylvania Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  and Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  also on that list, rising on that list a bit since it has shaken out over the past few days.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Tomi Lahren, host of "Tomi Lahren is Fearless" on OutKick, and Robert Walsh, former Economic Advisor to President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , both are Fox News Contributors. Great to have both of you with us, so welcome to you.\par \par \uc2 Tomi, let me start with you. Your thoughts on the big moves in favorability and this very dramatic tightening in the polls since \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  rose to the top of the list for the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, OUTKICK HOST: Well, Martha, there is no doubt that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the new shiny thing for the Democrat Party. I think this was by design. I think there's a reason that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took so long to drop out of this race. I think it was very strategic to wait that long because now the Democrats are working with what could be a 90-something-day honeymoon period where they try to reinvent Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for maybe the sixth time now.\par \par \uc2 And this time just might work because the media is in on it. The whole Democrat Party seems to be in and all four \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We haven't seen that really in the last three and a half years the cheerleading for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  but we're seeing it now. So, I do think that there is a real danger for the Trump-Vance Campaign that the honeymoon period could well last until early voting and people will forget her record, they will forget that she's a radical California Liberal.\par \par \uc2 But the best thing that the Trump-Vance Campaign can do is put her on the spot on the policy issues. Enough with the DEI, enough with a childless cat lady talk. Let's talk about policies and make \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  answer for her policies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Robert, I hear that lot coming from the Republican side that they really want this to be all about her policies. And I think you know the best way to do that is to put these two candidates head-to-head in a debate environment. What do you think about how she's doing now? What about Jim Carville's comments about, you know, potentially the honeymoon period?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FORMER OBAMA ECONOMIC ADVISER: Well, certainly -- you know, you and I have talked a lot after President Biden's debate the party was fractured and we were licking our wounds. But in just a week, the -- after Vice President Harris really took the nomination, it's clear that the donors are off the sideline, the base is off the sideline and the excitement is real, the favorability is real which you just talked about. And I think that she's bringing it right to former President Trump. She said let's debate, September 10th. He's the one that's backpedaling, not her.\par \par \uc2 You know I'd like to see it on Fox and not ABC but, you know, she -- he's the one that's backpedaling. And I read Tomi's--\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not backpedaling on debating, just on the structure on where it is. But you know he loves to debate.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: September 10th.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He can't wait to debate.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: You don't have to stick up for him. September 10th, come on. Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm not. I'm just telling you exactly what -- I was telling you exactly what he said himself which is that he can't wait to debate. So, you know, worry about that.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No, I'm sure that will happen.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: And just to repeat Tomi's comments on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I mean, she knows better than anyone. Vance is like the gift that keeps on giving. I mean, he can't get out of his own way. It was an absolutely worst V.P. pick possible. And we know President Trump wishes he had a mulligan so -- and in this idea of debating policy, I'm totally for that. I think it should be about policy. I think it should be about, you know, getting bipartisan immigration, we should debate the economy, we should debate all that. It should be substantive.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, let's take a look at this because it goes to the issue of who \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will pick, the Vice President will pick for her vice- presidential candidate. And these are brand new comments that Aishah Hasnie just got on the Hill from \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): I really like Governor Walz because I like the things that he's been able to do. I like that he's from a rural town. I like that he's got a military background. I like that he --\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: He's got Wisconsin ties I think too.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: He's the governor of Wisconsin --\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I mean, sorry, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: -- Minnesota. He's Governor of Minnesota but he has Wisconsin roots.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: He's Governor of Minnesota but he's got Wisconsin ties, yes.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: He has a house or something --\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Family there, yes.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: -- family there. And so, I think he's -- he would be -- he would be a great choice.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What about Shapiro in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: I'm not really commenting on other -- I'm commenting on who I would be a great choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Does this strong pro-labor record rule out \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: I mean, I think the -- I just don't see how you can put somebody in that does not have a strong pro --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Tomi, your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: I think that the Harris Campaign would be smart to pick somebody who's from a Midwest or a Rust Belt State, somebody that can appeal to the working class, because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  doesn't do that at all. And I think that the Harris Campaign is really going to try to distance itself from California radical Liberal policies, and that's everything that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stands for.\par \par \uc2 But I think that she needs to be put to question. Does she really support fracking now because we've been told that maybe she's changed her policy position? Does she really think that her addressing the root causes of the border crisis invasion has worked? I think she needs to be put on the spot on all of these policy positions. Her running mate is going to help in some ways to soften her radical image but it's not going to do the work for her. For the first time in in three and a half years, she's going to have to do the work.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is the cover of New York Magazine, welcome to Kamalot. And it has cheering former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , Beyonce, the whole crew on the cover of New York Magazine. And she's sitting on top of a coconut, something that she was made fun of for her comments about falling out of a coconut tree, Robert.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. Well, I'm not on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  or TikTok so it's hard for me to know all that stuff.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's New York Magazine, friend.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. I don't -- I don't have that either. But you know, here's what I would say to Tomi's comments. One, when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was an attorney general, she took on the banks. I ran one of the largest banks in the world. The banks came and looked to settle for $4 billion. JP Chase, and Wells, and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  paid $20 billion. When she was a prosecutor, she moved conviction rates up dramatically and changed truancy. When she went after gun reform, she didn't have to go after the Second Amendment, but she went after things like, you know, ERPO and making sure, you know, that there were risk protection. So, I think we should have that debate on crime and where it went with her as a prosecutor and an attorney general.\par \par \uc2 And with respect to all these other memes in TikTok, man, if it can get our base moving and the excitement and get out the vote, I'd say go for it. So, you know, it's not where I spend my time but, you know, why not?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: And on V.P. you know I'm all in from \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  or Shapiro. I'm a -- I'm friends with \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , former Navy Captain, astronaut. Governor Shapiro, Pennsylvania, the swing state you have to win. So, those would be my comments.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it looks like there's a lot of discussion about unions and who is pro-union enough. And you know we saw the \uc2 Teamsters Union\uc2  President on the stage at the GOP, so some interesting times we're living in. Thank you both, Robert and Tomi. Good to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this breaking right now. One of the suspects in this January attack on \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers in Times Square accepted a plea today. 19-year-old Darwin Gomez-Izquiel will serve a 364-day sentence. He is here in the United States illegally. And a month after that attack, he was arrested again for shoplifting and attacking a \uc2 Macy's\uc2  employee. So, latest update on that situation.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden did not approve of the Supreme Court's finding that presidents are immune from prosecution for their official acts even though that was essentially what had been in place all along. So, now he wants to overturn it with a new amendment. House Democrat Dan Goldman weighs in next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and President Biden seek to make major changes to the Supreme Court with a change to the U.S. Constitution. In an op-ed today, President Biden is calling for a "Constitutional Amendment called No One Is Above The Law, stating there is no immunity for crimes a former president committed while in office, and proposes the Supreme Court implement term limits for justices and enforce a binding code of conduct.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in House Oversight Committee Member and Democratic Congressman from New York, Dan Goldman. Congressman Goldman, good to have you with us. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, it's interesting going through the op-ed that was written by President Biden. He says there should be no immunity for crimes a former president committed in office. And essentially, what the Supreme Court found was that you have immunity for official acts but not for unofficial acts and that it would fall to courts on specific instances to determine whether or not those actions that the president committed were official or unofficial.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, that is not different from the basic understanding that we've had about what the Constitution said on this all along. So, why would we need to change it based on the Supreme Court finding?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, first of all, that's not entirely right. They said there was a core constitutional powers over which there is absolute immunity. That would for example include the pardon power. So, if someone were to pay \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  $1 million to get a pardon, he could not be charged for giving that pardon.\par \par \uc2 The other category which is very expansive in the Supreme Court's language does refer to the lower courts what they should find as an official act, but it is very permissive and very expansive. And what I think President Biden is saying is, he's saying hey, I am the President right now. In theory, I could really take advantage of this. I could use it. But I don't want to because I want to make sure that no one is above the law.\par \par \uc2 So, not only and I'm not going to take advantage of this new immunity, but I'm actually going to try to prevent future presidents from doing it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's not new immunity though. I mean, when you go through what they said in this -- in this decision, it is not new immunity. The court decided that the nature of Presidential Power entitles a former president to absolute immunity from criminal prosecution for actions within his conclusive and preclusive constitutional authority. He's also entitled to at least presumptive immunity from prosecution for all his official acts.\par \par \uc2 There is no immunity for unofficial acts. So, if a president goes outside his official acts -- and there's no determination yet on whether or not former President Trump did that, but that would be for the court to decide. So, you know, to bring about a change to the Constitution, an amendment, would be a huge bar given the likely construct of Congress. So, is this -- you know, is this a political statement that he's making given the unlikelihood that it could ever get through the high bar for an amendment to the Constitution?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, I'm going to have to disagree with you that this existed before. It never has. It's never been enumerated. And for example, the Supreme Court did say that any conversations that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or a former president would have with his attorney general could not only not be used as charged criminal conduct but can't even be used as evidence of a larger conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 This is completely new, born out of total whole cloth. And so, the notion that this Supreme Court has now protecting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  having been packed with three \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  judges is allowing -- is trying to undermine an indictment against him for his efforts to overturn the 2020 election. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is saying no, that's not OK. No one should be above the law, the President of the United States or no one else.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So, let me ask this then. Just -- you know, this is an example that obviously is a painful example from the exit from Afghanistan. Could those families whose service members were killed at Abbey Gate, could they sue the president for recklessly carrying out his roles as commander in chief? If that were their opinion, and that were their legal argument, could they do that?\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): Well, no. There's no cause of action because of qualified immunity for those civil decisions. But even if you were to try to charge someone for a criminal action, you'd have to show that there was a conspiracy and an intent to violate the law and commit a crime. And it's the same argument that unfortunately, my colleagues on the Republican side make about Secretary Mayorkas as if the fact that there are migrants coming across the country who may commit crimes, and by the way, they commit far less crimes than Americans, that somehow he is complicit or an accomplice to those crimes. That is bogus.\par \par \uc2 That is not how our criminal law works. There are many protections --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's the whole idea that there are protections --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: -- to make sure that someone is not charged.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. And to decide what is an official and what --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, there are protections by -- beyond a reasonable doubt by evidence --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: -- by trying prove mens rea, a criminal intent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right. All those things are already (inaudible) --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: There are many protections --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- and already understood, exactly. And that's --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Right. Why does anyone need immunity?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm not sure why we need an amendment is what I'm saying, based on the way that the government has functioned around presidential powers up until now. But let me ask you a quick question about term limits. You say that President Trump packed the court with three conservative justices.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's interesting because Gorsuch and Kavanaugh have disagreed on many different cases. They also enacted the ability to protect the right for people to use Mifepristone, which is an abortion drug. So they're not in lockstep at all. And to suggest that he packed the court when he just -- he had three opportunities to nominate someone to the court isn't packing the court. It's just using your presidential ability that when one justice leaves you get to nominate someone.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, let's remember how he was able to nominate his first justice, which was that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  in the Senate refused to give a hearing to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  for over nine months, which was absolutely preposterous.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But that has nothing to do with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: But then at the tail end of -- well, that's how he got his first one.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I mean, he didn't --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Then at the end of his term --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He had nothing to do with that when it happened, so he benefited from it but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, I'm addressing the ultimately addressing the term limits, because at the end of his term, they then ran through \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  in the span of three weeks, which was a completely different standard than what they used for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . The point of this 18-year term limit to be done every two years, is that it would take the timing, the out of this process. And it would be much more regularized, it would allow whoever is president to have exactly the same number of appointments so that this is no longer an effort to squeeze in an appointment or to retire because it's the right president. So I can be renominated someone else to replace me.\par \par \uc2 This would be much more regimented and routinized, so that we can take out a lot of these partisan shenanigans, and just make sure that we have a fully settled, settled process.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's not without support, this idea, in the polls. You know, we've had decades of liberal courts, we've had decades of conservative courts in this system that we have, so I think it's going to be very interesting to see the debate over all of this over the next three months.\par \par \uc2 Representative Goldman, thank you very much. It's good to have you here, sir.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thank you. So Democrats have a new line of attack on the GOP ticket, and it's not very specific. And this one may seem a little bit juvenile but here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to pick anyone is weird and creepy as JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 ANA NAVARRO, "THE VIEW" CO-HOST: JD Vance, just dumb Vance, is pretty weird.\par \par \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D), MICHIGAN GOVERNOR: The agenda the way they talk to people the way they address people. It is bizarre, it's weird. It is weird.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl Rove on the winning message that Republicans can use to counter those attacks. We'll see what Karl has to say about his strategy there coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), US VICE PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And you may have noticed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been resorting to some wild lies about my record, and some of what he and his running mate are saying, well, it's just plain weird.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Harris campaign testing out a new line of attack calling foreign President Trump and running mate JD Vance weird. Vance telling fox that it doesn't bother him, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Does it hurt my feelings? Look, the price of admission, meaning the price of getting to serve the people of this country is the Democrats are going to attack us with everything that they have. I think it's an honor. As \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  once said, if you can't take the heat, stay out of the kitchen. We want to make this country better.\par \par \uc2 I think President Trump has taken a lot more than I have over the past eight years, and I think he's shown a good example for how to persevere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's bring in Karl Rove, former White House Chief Deputy Chief of Staff for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and Fox News contributor. Karl, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 You know, obviously this is the new attack line. They're trying to create this weird narrative around Trump and Vance. What do you make of it? You know, there's a piece in Axios today that's titled "Trump's Insult Trap: Republicans Hunt for Elusive Harris Attack Line," "The MAGA universe that surrounds Trump raging from conservative influencers to his campaign is grasping for new attack lines that won't alienate women or black voters they need to win." Your thoughts on all that, on both sides.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, JD Vance had a, you know, sort of a lengthy, in fact I think too lengthy response to it. The better responses to go on the attack, say, look, calling us names is -- what they're going to say if they can't deal with the fact that the cost of living has exploded on their watch, that the border is a disaster, that the respect for America has deteriorated around the world, and that, you know, Harris has beliefs that are not in the mainstream of America. Go on the attack.\par \par \uc2 Now, don't throw the kitchen sink at them. Pick one thing at a time or two things at a time, and go after those things. But respond by saying, you know what, that's what you say if you don't want to talk about the fact you got a failed record on inflation, which is eating up family's ability to provide for their children and for themselves, and then pivot.\par \par \uc2 You need to get back what would Trump do. People want to know what these people want to do. So it's not just enough to say you have failed on cost of living or you failed on the border, but here's what we're going to do. So dismiss them and minimize them by spending less time not more time. The problem with JD Vance in that response was fine, very artfully, you know, detailed blah-blah-blah-blah-blah, but way too long and missed the opportunity to go on the offense.\par \par \uc2 We have 100 days. We have less than that when it comes to the number of days before people voted. And the side that gets on the offense and stays on the offense is going to have the upper hand in what could be a very, very close race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very close. Pennsylvania, I think starts in I don't know, 40 days, maybe a little bit less than that now, Karl. You know, what about the impact of all of this on some of these Senate races across the country? How are you gaming this? How are you discussing it?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, one of the things that would concern me if I was in the high command of the Trump campaign is, is that they are being outspent at the state level by the state Democratic Party's over the state Republican Party's on the ground game. And unless the Trump campaign is going to be able to even that up, they're going to have a problem with marginally bigger turnout because of two things.\par \par \uc2 One is more excitement about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than you had about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And second of all, a much larger ground game activity undergoing in places like Wisconsin, where I saw a report today that suggested that the Republicans over the course of this entire year, which did include a special election for the Supreme Court earlier in the spring, that the Democrats have outspent the Republicans on ground game activity 10 to 1. That's a problem and you got to get that evened up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl, I mean, this is something that has been talked about since the 2022 midterms. And certainly after the 2020 election, ground game, early voting, getting people out the Democrats are better at it than Republicans. So, what you know, whatever, Republicans, why aren't they better at it by now? You're less than 100 days from this election cycle. So what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, you know, maybe they are, you know, I'm not in a battleground state but I was talking to somebody in a battleground state namely Michigan, who told me that a person who had been involved -- a lawyer who'd been involved in Election Day activity in 2020 and 2022, said, you know, what, nobody's bothered to call me for 2024.\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe that's because the Michigan Republican Party was absolutely a disaster area. They kicked out the old chairman who had bankrupted the party. The new chairman had very able guy named Pete Hoekstra, former congressman, former ambassador, took over and found nothing in the headquarters. So maybe the list of names is gone. But that's a problem if the Republicans don't have their game -- their ground game together to get this election right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they haven't had any warning over the last couple of cycles so it's in the -- you've had a change at the top, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  out of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , and Whatley and \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  in, in order to get the act together on that front. So it means it's going to be, I think, you know, a story if that is -- has not been changed. That that game is just dead in the water.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes. It's not just though at the national level, you got to get this thing done at the state and local level. You have to have strong party organizations at the state and local level, it can all be done out of Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, all right. Amazing. Karl, thank you very much. Karl Rove, always good to see you. All right.\par \par \uc2 So what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  revealed today about the would be assassin of former President Trump as Pennsylvania SWAT team snipers expose a major security misstep by the Secret Service. Wait until you see this new video. It is just unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON WOOD, LEAD SHARPSHOOTER, BEAVER COUNTY SWAT TEAM: We were supposed to get a face to face briefing with the Secret Service snipers whenever they arrived, and that never happened. So I think that that was probably a pivotal point where I started thinking things were wrong because that never happened. And we had no communication with the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking today, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says that they are still searching for the motive in the attempted assassination of former President Trump now more than two weeks after the shooting in Butler, Pennsylvania. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  will also interview President Trump as part of their investigation.\par \par \uc2 And that is text messages from local law enforcement show that they raised suspicions about this person who was lurking around, Thomas Crooks, more than 90 minutes before the shooting, which took the life of a firefighter and injured two other people, and the bullet hit President Trump as well, eight bullets in all they believe.\par \par \uc2 Now, local officers tell ABC News that there were serious communication issues that day. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREGORY NICOL, BEAVER COUNTY SWAT TEAM: He was looking up and down the building just wandering around just seemed out of place\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY, ABC NEWS REPORTER: What do you think is transpiring once you send in what you've sent in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I assume that there would be somebody coming out to, you know, speak with this individual or find out what's going on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we all felt that day.\par \par \uc2 KATERSKY: How so?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Life was lost.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People died.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Amazing. Let's bring in Frank Loveridge, retired Secret Service Special Agent in Charge. He spent 22 years with the agency serving as a supervisor in the Presidential Protective Division during \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's time in office. Frank, good to have you back today.\par \par \uc2 And we've leaned on your expertise throughout the course of the last couple of weeks investigating this. But, you know, I'm struck by the fact that there's remorse on the part of these local officials. They are saddened and they say we failed. We have yet to see that from the head of the Secret Service, or the agents involved. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LOVERIDGE, RETIRED SECRET SERVICE SPECIAL AGENT IN CHARGE: Well, Martha, thank you for having me on this afternoon. And it's been over two weeks now and we still have questions that have not the answer. It's very frustrating.\par \par \uc2 The communication failure, obviously, I'd like to know why we weren't attending that meeting in the morning, we didn't attend that meeting, and was there adequate communication relays in the security room? Because the most important thing, the absolute most important thing, is to communicate a threat to the detail leader, so they can take the appropriate action and protect the president.\par \par \uc2 They weren't able to do that until after the shots were fired. So they realized there was a threat as bullets were coming down range. And that can't happen. We have to do a better job in communications. So that would be one of the pivotal mistakes that was made that day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Um, let's listen to another piece of sound from these agents taking responsibility for what went wrong that day, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WOODS: We were supposed to get a face to face briefing with the Secret Service snipers whenever they arrived, and that never happened. So I think that that was probably a pivotal point where I started thinking things were wrong because that never happened. And we had no communication with the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 KATERSKY: You had no communication with the Secret Service at all on that Saturday,\par \par \uc2 WOODS: No, not until after the shooting, I believe. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KATERSKY: And by then --\par \par \uc2 WOODS: It was too late.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It was too late. It is amazing that -- so if you don't have that morning meeting, you can't say it, look, so if you guys see something over here, or you see something over there, this is the person that you have to let know immediately. This is going to be how we're going to communicate, right?\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: That's correct. And if you look at the timeline, 4:26PM, they identify the shooter, something should have went out immediately, a protective intelligence team should have intercepted that individual, that should have interviewed them. We could have ended this whole thing right then in there. 5:38, they send out the picture, 5:40 someone calls or someone -- and actually one of the snipers who wanted someone to interact and meet this individual.\par \par \uc2 And they obviously, you know, at 6:11, the first shots were fired. So all of this played out, and the lack of communication, getting that to the right people so we could put the right people in place to mitigate this before it ever happened. So communication -- I still want to know what happened at the AGR building. I'm still confused about the over watch, and was there a team that was supposed to be on there, I still haven't gotten the correct answers yet.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I don't understand why that wasn't covered. You had some line of sight issues with the tree coverage, you also had a sniper that was supposed to be taking care of that location. If they had a blind spot, they should overcame that blind spot, taking that out of play. I don't understand what happened that day. I think some answers need to be given to that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A hundred percent. And we need to hear from Mayorkas at Homeland Security and we need to hear from Cheatle again about what did and didn't happen. There's a new person in charge, her deputy, Rowe, at this point. But, you know, to hear these people taking it all on themselves, and we haven't heard a lot from the federal organizations that are supposed to be in charge of this. And it's so frustrating.\par \par \uc2 Frank Loveridge, thank you as always. Good to have you here raising exactly the important questions we need to think about. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' attempt to stand as a law and order candidate is being called "laughable" by one victim of a crime carried out by an illegal migrant while Harris was San Francisco DA next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Vice President Harris campaigning partly on her record as a former prosecutor, but one crime victim says that her attacker, an immigrant in the country illegally, was on the streets because of a program that Harris ran as San Francisco DA. Senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse reporting live from Los Angeles. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, as DA she felt strongly about two things, alternatives to jail or prison and sanctuary laws that protected criminal aliens from being deported. Well, both now the Republicans are trying to exploit in a new ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Harris was liberal on illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So in 2004, she created a program called "Back on Track" where convicts could avoid jail if they have a job and were put into community service.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As a prosecutor in San Francisco, Harris allowed illegal immigrant drug dealers to enter job training and have their criminal records wiped clean.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: That included a 20-year-old Honduran arrested for theft and twice for selling cocaine. Now while in the program, he stole a woman's purse and tried to run her over fracturing her skull. Trump had talked about it about four years ago. But by law, illegal immigrants are not allowed to work. Harris called this case a pimple on the face of a good program and close the loophole, but still she supports sanctuary laws and comprehends of immigration reform that gives amnesty for those who are here illegally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So the victim in that case, Martha, is now talking out saying the government should not be giving jobs to people who legally can't have one. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: William, thank you very much. That's "The Story" for today. We'll see you right back here tomorrow when "The Story" goes on.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240730ek7t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Arrives At The \uc2 RNC\uc2 ; Trump Arrives At The \uc2 RNC\uc2  Ahead Of Appearance; Trump Receives Thunderous Welcome At \uc2 RNC\uc2 ; Trump Greeted With Huge Applause At \uc2 RNC\uc2 ; Trump Arrives At \uc2 RNC\uc2  After Assassination Attempt; Crowd Cheers As Trump, Vance Arrive At \uc2 RNC\uc2 ; Trump Appears Alongside VP Pick Vance At \uc2 RNC\uc2 ; Trump Attends \uc2 RNC\uc2  Just Days After Shooting; President Of \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' Union Sean O'Brien Speaks At Republican National Convention; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Attends First Day Of Republican National Convention Days After Assassination Attempt; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Picks J.D. Vance As His Vice Presidential Running Mate\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Harold Ford Jr., Brit Hume, Dana  Perino, Shannon Bream, Karl Rove, Kellyanne Conway, Bill Hemmer \par \uc2 8,732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be coming out in a second. Stay with Fox News and our continuing coverage right here on the Fox News Channel. Set your DVR, never ever miss an episode. In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Bret and Martha take it from here. Bret, Martha, take it away.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: History in the making here in Milwaukee, with President Trump appearing night one of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . Welcome to Democracy '24 coverage of the Republican National Convention, live from Fiserv Forum. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. The crowd here exploding moments ago when they saw President Trump entering the arena. They could see him on the big screen, he does have a bandage over his ears, the man of the hour, the 45th President of United States now the nominee of his party. We're about to see him on the stage as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you can imagine what this moment means, just two days after the former President survived an assassination attempt, again, the bandage over the right side of his ear as he's going to walk on here, Lee Greenwood with a song beforehand, and this place is going to go through the roof.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. I think you're going to see a lot of emotion in this moment as we see the President step on the stage shortly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's get you in there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES: The God will make some changes in this country, and he's about to make a change in the current administration, (inaudible). Thank you, everybody for being here tonight. Tonight we make decision of what's right for America. More prosperity, less gas prices, less food prices,, health for our veterans, and God bless our military wherever they are in this United States, and abroad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ladies and gentlemen, please welcome the next President of the United States, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LEE GREENWOOD: (SINGING SONG) If tomorrow all the things were gone, I'd worked for all my life And I had to start again with just my children and my wife, I'd thank my lucky stars to be living here today, because the flag still stands for freedom, and they can't take that away.\par \par \uc2 And I'm proud to be an American where at least I know I'm free. And I won't forget the men who died, who gave that right to me. And I'd gladly stand up next to you and defend her still today, because there ain't no doubt, I love this land. Sing it. God bless the USA.\par \par \uc2 We are here tonight at (inaudible) and that is to re-elect \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  as the next President of the United States. He is here tonight to show his courage, his defiance against somebody who tried to kill him. You will not take this man down. He has the courage, the strength, and he will be the next President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING SONG) From the lakes of Minnesota, to the hills of Tennessee, across the plains of Texas, from sea to shining sea.\par \par \uc2 Ladies and gentlemen, the 45th President of the United States and soon to be the 47th President of the United States. Please welcome \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (SINGING SONG) Well, that I'm proud to be an American where at least I know I'm free. And I won't forget the men who died, who gave that right to me. And I'd gladly stand up next to you and defend her still today, because there ain't no doubt, I love this land. God bless the USA.\par \par \uc2 Well, that I'm proud to be an American where at least I know I'm free. And I won't forget the men who died, who gave that right to me. And I'd gladly stand up next to you and defend her still today, because there ain't no doubt. I love this land. God bless the USA. Mr. President. (CROWD CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please welcome Mark Laws from Cedar Springs, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A pretty amazing scene there as the 45th President hoping to be the 47th President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fight, fight, fight, is what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He is walking in, and there you see the first time we see the ticket. Trump Vance, with JD Vance, being announced the Vice Presidential nominee today. We've got an amazing team here alongside as Brit Hume, Dana Perino, and Harold Ford, Jr. Brit, we haven't seen a moment like this.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: No, I --I've covered a lot of these. I've always loved all this hoopla. And ever since Lee Greenwood made that song popular more than 40 years ago, it's been an anthem of the Republican Party, particularly at the Reagan era. And now it is back.\par \par \uc2 And I suppose you couldn't have picked a song that would want this crowd up any better than that one did as he was making this entrance here tonight, that bandage on his left ear being the, or his right ear excuse me, being the result of what could have been an assassin successful bullet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, it's a stunning moment, Dana. Two days ago, this man came within a millimeter of losing his life in a very violent manner. And we just watched him walk in these hallways, and you could feel this crowd just building and building. And he does have that bandage over his ear, the mark of that bullet.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: I haven't felt energy like this in a long time and that we haven't been here, and there's been no one in the crowd. Tonight is the first night and it is electric. I remember the pitch in 2004 at the Madison Square Garden, unbelievable moment for me, I would say, this is just as much for me like that. But even more, the energy in the room is incredible. He's got his VP pick, and he is strong and standing in this crowd is so grateful, they are ready to fight for him, for sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He's appeared to be moved tonight, you know, in a -- in a pensive way that, you know, we're so used to seeing him fired up and pumping.\par \par \uc2 HUME: He looked a little grim.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. He looked --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Teary eyed, almost, teary eyed, almost. I would agree. I would agree. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, 52 hours ago, the President was shot. And I remember where I was with my kids in my kitchen watching. I'm not a supporter of the President's politics. I know him personally, I like him. But, you couldn't help but feel a rush of patriotism and pride, and gratitude watching him walk in that hallway, and welcome to this floor.\par \par \uc2 He's a different guy than he was 52 hours ago. He looked a little different, he didn't look weak, he just looked -- he looked a little different. And I couldn't have been prouder for him. We'll now have to get on to a race where we'll talk about the differences between the parties, talk about the differences between the vice presidential candidates. But this moment here is one that I think all of us want to linger a little bit as we watch the 45th President of United States walk into this Republican Hall tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, each one of the speeches is different. There's a couple of people on here that they're calling EDAs, Everyday Americans, that they want to hear from. Let's listen in to one of them right now speaking in the hall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Prices keep going up, but my income stays the same. I just keep putting things off hoping that I'll be able to afford them again someday. It's gotten me thinking, I've been hoping for better days. But life is happening right now. And we can't waste any more time on the big boy, Joe, terrible economic policies.\par \par \uc2 We need to bring Donald back right now. We need to know and share the 20 promises that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made. We need to get involved with the Trump 47. And you remember, ballots win election, so that's the best way to do it. I know a lot of folks just kind of tune the politics out, but we really can't afford to anymore, because we're not living in the same country that we grew up in.\par \par \uc2 Cutesy time is over. We need -- we need to all of us, put our shoulder to the wheel, and push along and give Mr. Trump -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to be number 47 again. Thank you. And God bless America.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING) (inaudible) just scared to death, Biden's inflation has retirees at risk of running out of money. The cost of groceries is forcing many seniors to choose between food and their prescriptions. Their social security isn't keeping up with inflation. And thanks to Biden and Harris' open border disaster (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to see a number of these everyday Americans take the stage tonight and talk about their experience living in under the Biden Administration. And you're going to hear them making their voices known in terms of what they -- what policy changes they would like to see.\par \par \uc2 And we're just, you know, sort of still taking in the President as he sits here chatting with Byron Donalds now of Florida and many of the people who are in this arena tonight are several of them, I should say, were also on the vice presidential list, but he's making it clear in embracing their presence here tonight that they may play a role as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We should talk about that, JD Vance, that's the big selection today. Senator from Ohio, Marine, father of three, lawyer, somebody who speaks to MAGA. And that crowd, he's a real advocate and Rust Belt states, Brit, and will be an advocate on the road, maybe even a pitbull for this press.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, we saw tonight in his interview he did with Sean -- \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , excuse me, at considerable length. Why was that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would choose him, he's spectacularly articulate. He's well informed. He makes the case for himself and for the ticket, probably as well as anybody can.\par \par \uc2 So, you can see the upside with him, and it was very clear in that interview, and he got the question, right, the question that a lot of people are going to have, how did you go from being an ardent hardcore, never Trump, was saying the most extraordinarily strong things against Trump in 2016 to the Trump running mate and defender you are now.\par \par \uc2 And the answer he gave was, Trump proved me wrong. I'm not sure a lot of people will be satisfied with that answer, but I think that's the best answer he could have given.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, he got the call, Dana, we were told, about 20 minutes before the truth social hit. This was really a pretty good secret that was kept, and almost in an apprentice like rollout for this choice. What do you think of JD Vance?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, here's the thing. I loved his book, Hillbilly Elegy. And I think for a lot of people that read it, and/or watch the movie or both, kind of know who he is, they might have fallen off and not paid too much attention to the politics of the past couple of years. They're about to relearn a little bit more about him.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting, because we do this all the time. So, we have very -- we're very familiar with him. America's not necessarily, when they learn about this story, I think they will be very moved. I do think that President Trump had already won the voters that he could get. So, I believe that the choice is one that Trump said, this is who I want to see every day. This is who I can trust to carry out my vision, and my policies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's listen in to this Everyday American, Benjamin Joseph. He's trying to buy a starter home with his -- with his wife, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN JOSEPH, EVERYDAY AMERICAN: He knew we could create millions of great jobs if given the chance, and we did. The Trump's tax cuts and regulation cuts empowered entrepreneurs like you and me, and enabled us, to expand our businesses. We need him back in the White House.\par \par \uc2 As a representative of Michigan at this convention, I want to give a special Michigander thank you to President Trump for being our champion. He has inspired so many of us even in this room to step up and get involved in the fight to save America. With his leadership, and our help, we will make America affordable again, wealthy again, and great again. May God bless each and every one of you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A young man who is trying to buy a home like so many Americans across the country having a hard time doing it. Bill Hemmer is watching all of this from the floor. And Bill, give us a sense, we could feel it so intensely up here, but I'm sure you've felt out quite shake the -- probably shake the floor down there.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yeah, good evening to you, Martha and Bret. It's been extraordinary to stare across this arena and think that just 52 hours ago, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was screaming, let me get my shoes. Let me get my shoes from underneath that podium in Butler, Pennsylvania. Really incredible. And I think, you know, we count the hours, it's not even days now. It's -- it's hours still since that sniper's bullet just grazed his right ear.\par \par \uc2 Here down on the floor, I will say, from my position here, I can see three large monitors. And the monitors have the teleprompter in them. And I've been covering conventions going back to the year 2000 in the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and George Bush in Philadelphia. And the thinking is that Democrats have a very good message, and they stick to the message, and Republicans don't.\par \par \uc2 And that's the main reason why there was a memo that went out yesterday afternoon from the leadership of Trump's campaign that said, stick to the script. And for the most part tonight, Martha, that has been the case, they have stuck to the script, and the theme is, make America wealthy again. They have hammered that theme relentlessly, talking about gas prices and grocery prices, and that continues now into the 10 o'clock hour, Eastern time. Back up you, upstairs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Bill, we're listening to Amber Rose, American Model, and she's saying why she supports former President Trump. Listen to it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMBER ROSE, AMERICAN MODEL: I know this, because for a long time, I believed those lies. So, I'm here to set the record straight. The first person I knew who supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was my father. I was shocked. My entire family is racially diverse. And I believe the left wing propaganda that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a racist.\par \par \uc2 My father said, no, he's not Amber, what are you talking about? And when I insisted, he said, prove it. So, to prove my father wrong, I did my research and looked into, all things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . People have to do their research. I watched all the rallies. And I started meeting so many of you, his red hat wearing supporters.\par \par \uc2 I realized \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters don't care if you're Black, White, gay or straight, it's all love. And that's when it hit me, these are my people, this is where I belong. Say I let go of my fear of judgment of being misunderstood, of getting attacked by the left, and I put the red hat on too. Thank you. Love you too.\par \par \uc2 I never felt more free and more love for my country than I do now. I want to thank my father who's in the audience tonight for opening my eyes. He served over 20 years in the U.S. Military. Thank you for your service, dad. I love you dad. I love you. I love you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When I met the President and Melania for the first time, he was kind and generous, and funny as Hoh. Very funny. The First Lady was gracious and smart with a smile that will brighten up any room. If you're watching this tonight, you know our country is in trouble.\par \par \uc2 Just like me, when you go to the store and buy food for your family, you're shocked. When you fill up your gas tank, you're pissed. I know I am. And when you turn on the news, you are just exhausted. Inflation is out of control. And you know in your heart it was not like this under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 My message sheet tonight comes from a humble place. The left told me to hate Trump and even worse, to hate the other side, the people who support him. When you cut through the lies, you realize the truth. American families were better when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was President.\par \par \uc2 We were safer, wealthier and stronger. A vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a boat to put money back in our pockets and good food on our kids' plates. Yes. Or as Trump would say, it's a vote to make America great again. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please welcome --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amber Rose. And now we'll hear from another EDA, Everyday American. Listen to it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VANESSA FAURA, PERUVIAN IMMIGRANT, EVERYDAY AMERICAN: 60 years ago, I left Nicaragua for a better life in Fabulous Las Vegas, Nevada. America welcome me and I embrace the values of hard work, learning the language, and respecting the laws, is the American thing to do. In 2022, my three boys and I faced a devastating house fire. We lost everything.\par \par \uc2 And starting over in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's economy was almost impossible. The Biden economy is driving prices through the roof; rent, gas, groceries, everything. As a life insurance worker, I see the struggle of people trying to save for the future, while burly managing their basic needs.\par \par \uc2 I'm living that same struggle every day. Life is too hard right now. One of my son's works three jobs, and the other holds down too. That maze seeps jobs for three people just to survive in Biden's economy. Our open borders are making this problem worse.\par \par \uc2 It's upsetting -- it's upsetting to see millions of dollars being sent to help immigrants who came here illegally while hardworking families who did it the right way are left struggling. The fact is that illegal immigration hurts legal immigrants the most.\par \par \uc2 The Biden system is backwards and broken. In various America opportunities to get ahead, I blame them, and our education system is failing our children. What happened to teaching, reading, writing and math?\par \par \uc2 Now, it's like our kids are getting a degree in pronoun studies, he/she/ they/then, I have made my son to come home with our diploma in (inaudible). Life was completely different under President Trump. We prosper. To my beloved Latino community, it's time to wake up and smell the coffee\par \par \uc2 To my beloved Latina community, it's time to wake up and smell the Cappuccino. In 2020, I made a mistake. A mistake that is hard to admit, especially in this room. In 2020, I voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I'm sorry, I'm sorry. He has made me regretted.\par \par \uc2 This November, my vote won't be taken for granted. And this time, I will regret it. Please join me in voting for \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please welcome the President of the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , Sean O'Brien.\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, PRESIDENT, \uc2 INTERNATIONAL BROTHERHOOD OF TEAMSTERS\uc2 : Greetings, delegates and guests. I'm Sean O'Brien, General President of the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 . I'd like to give my peeps from the greatest state in the nation, Massachusetts, some props what's up? First, I want to thank the hard working teachers and union members here in Milwaukee, who played vital roles in the building and operations of this convention.\par \par \uc2 I also want to thank President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for opening the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's door to the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  and invited me to speak before you tonight. I travel all across this country and meet with my members every week. You know what I see? An American worker being taken for granted.\par \par \uc2 Workers being sold out to big banks, big tech corporates in the elite. And I'm not the only one who sees this, every day family see it. The American people aren't stupid. They know the system is broken. We all know how Washington is run. Working people have no chance of winning this fight. That's why I'm here today, because I refuse to keep doing the same things my predecessors did.\par \par \uc2 Today -- today, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are here to say, we are not beholden to anyone or any party. We will create an agenda and work with a bipartisan coalition ready to accomplish something real for the American worker. And I don't care about getting criticized. It's an honor to be the first Teamster, in our 121-year history, to address the Republican National Convention.\par \par \uc2 Several months ago, I asked the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and the DNC for the opportunity to speak. To be frank, when President Trump invited me to speak at this convention, there was political unrest on the left and on the right. Hard to believe. Anti-union groups demanded the President rescind his invitation. The left called me a trader. And this is precisely why it's so important for me to be here today.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Think about this. Think about this. The teamsters are doing something correct if the extremes in both parties think I shouldn't be on this stage.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: President Trump had the backbone to open the doors to this Republican Convention, and that's unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: No other nominee in the race would have invited the teamsters into this arena. Now, you can have whatever opinion you want, but one thing is clear, President Trump is a candidate who is not afraid of hearing from new, loud, and often critical voices. And I think we all can agree, whether people like him or they don't like him, in light of what happened to him on Saturday, he has proven to be one tough S-O-B.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Now, when I won the presidency of the teamsters in a national election two-and-a-half years ago, we started reaching across the aisle. In the past, the teamsters have endorsed GOP candidates, including Nixon, Reagan, and George H.W. Bush. But over the last 40 years, the Republican Party has really pursued strong relationships with organized labor. There are some in the party who stand in active opposition to labor unions. This, too, must change.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: And I want to be clear. At the end of the day, the teamsters are not interested if you have a "D", "R", or an "I" next to your name. We want to know one thing. What are you doing to help American workers?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: As a negotiator, I know that no window or door should ever be permanently shut. In my administration, the teamsters reached out to eight Republican senators who stood up for railroad teamsters over our fight for paid sick leave. Josh Hawley was one of them.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We started talking. Senator Hawley changed his position on national right to work. Then we started walking. Senator Hawley walked a teamsters' picket line in St. Louis and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  picket line in Wentzville, Missouri. More than that, I want to recognize Senator Hawley for his direct, relentless, and pointed questioning of corporate talking heads, lawyers, CEOs, and apologists.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: He has shown he is not willing to accept their pillaging of working people's pocketbooks. I know from a career in negotiating that you get nowhere by slamming your fist on the table. The first step is to listen. The teamsters and the GOP may not agree on many issues, but a growing group has shown the courage to sit down and consider points of view that aren't funded by big money think tanks.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Senators like J.D. Vance, Roger Marshall and Representatives \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2 , Michael Lawler, and \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  are among elected officials who truly care about working people. And this group is expanding and is putting fear into those who have monopolized our very broken system in America today. There are far too many people on both sides of the aisle still caught up in kneejerk reactions to unions, who subscribe to the same tired claptrap that unions destroy American companies. Take a moment to consider \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , which is the largest private sector logistics company, and it's been unionized for more than 100 years. More than 350,000 teamsters make it run. We work for good middle class wages, quality health care, and secure pensions. There are work rules that ensure fairness and due process for both sides. UPS is the most efficient package delivery company in the world.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: But let's not forget that UPS doesn't provide these great wages and benefits out of the kindness of its heart. UPS does it because the teamsters fight for it, all 350,000 of us.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: You know, corporatists hate when working people join together to form unions. But for a century, major employers have waged a war against labor by forming corporate unions of their own. We need to call the Chamber of Commerce and the business roundtables what they are. They are unions for big business.\par \par \uc2 And here's another fact. Against gigantic multinational corporation, an individual worker has zero power. It's only when Americans band together in democratic unions that we win real improvements on wages, benefits, and working conditions. Companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  are bigger than most national economies. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is valued at over $2 trillion. That makes it the 14th largest economy in the world. What is sickening is that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has abandoned any national allegiance. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's sole focus is on lining its own pockets. Remember, elites have no party. Elites have no nation. Their loyalty is to the balance sheet and the stock price at the expense of the American worker.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: In my office in Washington, D.C., I can see the United States Capitol from my window. I see well-intentioned people arrive in Washington and get eaten up by an unforgiving system. The responsibility to average Americans takes a backseat. The objective now becomes survival -- fundraiser after fundraiser, corporate consultants hedge every initiative. The Hill crawls with lifers bouncing from government jobs to corporate jobs and back again. I think we can all agree, D.C. is a pretty treacherous area. Most legislation is never meant to go anywhere, and it's all talk. And in America, talk isn't cheap. It's very expensive, and it comes at the cost of our own country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Working people know our system is broken. The elites are not laboring on behalf of workers. There is a political caste system that prevents citizens from accessing their representatives to hold them accountable. For a moment in time, working people in America were seen as essential. Sadly, it took a global pandemic for political and corporate elites to notice this fact. But ask yourself this question -- since the end of the pandemic, when was the last time you heard major news outlets regularly refer to workers as essential? You haven't. The men and women who provide goods and services, deliver packages, stock grocery shelves, care for patients, pick up your trash, and keep our community safe are taken for granted. All the while the stock market booms, housing prices hit record highs, and corporate salaries skyrocket. But the income of everyday Americans are shrinking in the face of inflation, at the gas pumps, at the grocery store, with the electrical bill, and with the car insurance. This has got to change.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Never forget American workers own this nation.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We're not renters. We're not tenants. But the corporate elite treat us like squatters, and that is a crime. We've got to fix it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Now, this will shock you. This will shock you. To paraphrase Senator Markwayne Mullin, it's time for both sides of Congress to stand their butts up.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We need trade policies that put American workers first. It needs to be easier for companies to remain in America.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We need legal protections that make it safer for workers to get a contract. We must stop corporations from abandoning local communities to inflate the bottom line. We need meaningful bankruptcy reform. Today, corporate vultures buy up companies like Yellow Freight with the intent of driving them into bankruptcy and feasting on their remains. The courts leave workers begging for crumbs as third tier creditors.\par \par \uc2 Labor law must be reformed. Americans vote for a union but can never get a union contract. Companies fire workers who try to join unions and hide behind toothless laws that are meant to protect working people but are manipulated to benefit corporations. This is economic terrorism at its best. An individual cannot withstand such an assault. A fired worker cannot afford corporate delays, and these greedy employers know it. There are no consequences for the company, only the worker. We need corporate welfare reform. Under our current system, massive companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , and \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  take zero responsibilities for the workers they employ. These companies offer no real health insurance, no retirement benefits, no paid leave, relying on underfunded public assistance.\par \par \uc2 And who foots the bill? The individual taxpayer. The biggest recipients of welfare in this company are corporations, and this is real corruption. We must put workers first!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: What could be more important to the security of our nation than a long-term investment in the American worker?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: In 2021, teamsters nationwide elected me to fight for them, and that's precisely what I'm doing. Something is wrong in this country, and we need to say it out loud. I will always speak for America and the American worker, both union and non-union.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I challenge each and every one of you and especially my friends on the Democratic side to embrace cooperation, to truly collaborate to achieve meaningful and productive change, to ensure we make this great nation and this world the bigger, faster, and strongest nation in the entire world.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I love this country. The teamsters love this country. Our 1.3 million members move America on the roads, in the ports, on the rails, and in the air. And at the end of the day, if the powers to be stop me from raising my voice on behalf of American workers, I will not have one single regret. I still carry my commercial driver's license. I still have my place on the union seniority list. You'll find me back in Boston driving a tractor-trailer, delivering equipment for Shaughnessy because I have the protection of a union contract that gives me the freedom to speak my mind and to fight like hell!\par \par \uc2 God bless the greatest nation. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sean O'Brien, the teamsters' president, the first teamsters' president to ever speak to a Republican National Convention. I don't remember a speaker at a Republican convention railing against the Chamber of Commerce. That is new here. But he was fighting for workers, blue-collar workers all across the country. We heard from everyday Americans, then we heard from the teamsters' president. He credited former President Trump for opening up the doors of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . And he said after what happened Saturday, this is one tough S-O-B.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that got a huge response from the crowd. Clearly this is a group that President Biden would consider right in his wheelhouse, the unions. So this is an obvious direct attempt to pull those union voters away. And huge response from the crowd here. So a lot of people who are very open to it. Also Linda Fornos, the Hispanic woman who spoke, she talked about the fact that she voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last time around, and she said I regret it and I won't make that mistake again as they try to pull in more Hispanic voters to this cause.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She said it's time to wake up and smell the cafecito.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So let's go to Kellyanne Conway and Karl Rove. They join us now. Karl, what about this effort, everyday Americans and the teamsters?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I love these everyday Americans. This has become a standard part of every Republican convention, and sometimes they are the most compelling speakers available because they are ordinary Americans sharing their ordinary concerns, which are so important to the Republican message.\par \par \uc2 I thought that was a very unusual speech by the teamsters' president. Looks, let's be clear, there was something in it for both sides. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets to show that he is open to letting the voices of labor speak, and the teamster president wanted to do this because, if you look at the surveys, the workers in the trade unions like the teamsters and those that are involved in making things have increasingly become Republican while their leadership, like the president of the teamsters, have remained Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I noticed this was a guy who did not support Trump's tax cut to make American companies more competitive and make it easier to remain in America. He didn't support Trump's regulatory relief, which made it easier for companies to operate and stay in America and employ Americans. But he got tonight the opportunity to send a message to Democrats, you're losing my people to people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , so you better open up your doors at the DNC and give me a bigger piece of the leadership opportunities.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Kellyanne, your reaction when you saw President Trump, who you have worked with very closely in your career and at the White House, walk in here tonight?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I saw a man who knows he got a second lease on life. I see gratitude and graciousness. People obviously here are so excited, it's palpable. But I think he got to witness what is now \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Republican Party, Martha. The Hispanic woman who became a U.S. citizen, the African American vote as seen through wonderful South Carolina Tim Scott, the young people through Charlie Kirk, David Sax representing the business community.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the capper of the evening, Amber Rose, I guess a public figure, a popular figure in entertainment. But also, you see the head of the teamsters. Everybody that Trump's bringing together is reflected in these polls. He seems to burrow into the Democratic coalition. He doesn't need to win 50 percent of any of those groups to become the next president.\par \par \uc2 Last thing, make America wealthy. I think you saw tonight not a contest of personalities, not personal insults. This is putting the Biden-Harris record on trial. Bidenomics versus Maga-nomics. The people who are young are saying, when am I going to get out of debt, when can I buy my first house, when can get on with the natural accoutrements of an adult life?\par \par \uc2 So people know gas and groceries are up, but you heard other things tonight. It's housing. It's utilities. It's repairs. It's maintenance. People think everyday life is unaffordable. That's unfair. They want the energy independence. They want to return to the party of the worker. And I feel this is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  showing a year after Bret and you moderated the debate on that stage with eight or nine other qualified men and women in the Republican Party and not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You're seeing that this is the party that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is building, and he's bringing so many of those candidates that ran against him into this house this week to also address us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, that is a good point. That debate was here in the Fiserv Forum. The former president was not there. He is here tonight and is poised to potentially take advantage in a bump in the polls after this and after what happened Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Shannon Bream down on the convention floor. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: OK, guys. I was here, got stuck in a spot we weren't anticipating being in, but we were right in the right places at the right time as President Trump was coming in, and this place went absolutely crazy. I don't think they've calmed down yet. They were very much anticipating and hoping that he would be here. He's here with that bandaged ear, and these folks have not calmed down yet.\par \par \uc2 They were rapt with attention. As you said, these everyday speakers made a real impact. People were clapping and cheering. We heard folks saying I hear you, I feel you. So it very much resonated with the folks here.\par \par \uc2 I've got to tell you, though, of all the colorful delegates we've seen tonight, I want to introduce you to the one who's become the most popular. This is Lambeau (ph) from Wisconsin, an official delegate. And while this place has been off the charts on the decibel levels, Lambeau (ph) has yet to be moved by it. He's a faithful service dog, and as the folks get ready to wrap up tonight, they're already buzzing with excitement about tomorrow. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, they're taking care of some convention business. They're going to do a couple of benedictions.\par \par \uc2 Back to our panel, Brit Hume, Dana Perino, Harold Ford Jr. Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think that the everyday Americans is working. And what you see here is this we're living in the middle of a political realignment on both sides. And you can see that President Trump, one, he is uniting the Republican Party and then growing it, reaching out to different types of people. That is the Latino vote, the black American vote, especially black men, and young people. And I think that the Democrats are looking at this going we can't afford to lose chunks of each of those groups and still win.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I think that the president's path, President Biden's path to 270 keeps getting much more difficult as you have states like Nevada taken off the table. But adding battleground states perhaps in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Virginia.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Maybe even Virginia. And so there's going to be just a huge amount of changes this week as you see that. And I can imagine that the Democrats are watching all this going, I don't know how I feel about our chances in November, let alone the August convention.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let's ask Harold about that. Harold, what do you say? What are you thinking here tonight?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So this has been thus far a great convention for the president and for the Republicans. I agree with everything about Sean O'Brien and how he presented a first. I think the president got what he wanted from that speech, to Karl's point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But not an endorsement.\par \par \uc2 FORD: But what's shaping up -- he didn't get an endorsement. What's shaping up, the A's are the big issues in this campaign. Affordability is being talked here, and abortion will be talked about at the convention that my party holds. The question becomes, which one of those is more powerful? Or which one resonates more with voters? Other issues, \uc2 NATO\uc2  and national security, will certainly play. But what the president and Republicans have done thus far this evening, they have to view this as a huge success, and Democrats ought to be learning how do you counter this over the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Brit, one of the things that really strikes me tonight is this really positive energy. There's a lot of enthusiasm, and that's something that, this is a race that most Americans for a long time said they don't want to see Biden and Trump back in the ring. But this crowd, I haven't felt a crowd like this in quite some time.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Remember, these are Trump delegates. They populate this hall.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 HUME: They're expected to be excited about it, especially, Martha, on top of what happened over the weekend. And the moment in this night, make no mistake about it, was when Trump walked out here, the bandage covering his ear, and how he seemed to them, obviously they were thrilled. He could have been their fallen hero. Now he's their surviving hero. And how he seemed above all. He seemed subdued in a way as he was walking out. He seemed serious. And he was hearing the cheers. That revved him up in the past. He seemed more subdued tonight. He seemed at times nearly emotional, almost as if there may have been tears welling in his eyes. Obviously, you got Lee Greenwood out there. He can make tears come to almost anybody's eyes if you're the kind of person who likes him, and I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  obviously is. But I think that's the moment tonight. It's still going on out there. I think I just saw he's out there shaking hands with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 . So he has his adherents at his side.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I talked a couple days over the past two days, and he's -- he feels blessed to have escaped that assassination attempt. He did sound to me like he's going to change something in this convention. He told me he was going to rip up a 20-page speech and do something different, different than what he was planning. Not the attack on Democrats -- there will be some of that, but he does, at least what he said, he's going to call for a unity in the country. If that message is delivered, Dana, it's one that's probably going to be welcomed in this hall and across the country.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I feel like what this is giving him is even more of the big mo, as George H.W. Bush called it, the big momentum. And so he had that a little bit in the polls even beforehand. The debate gave him more. And now tonight this electric crowd with a band that he loves and, as Melania Trump, even said in her statement, he loves music, and he's throwing a party for his constituents. But he said, I love you all in America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just think about this --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm watching -- you're watching the Secret Service guys around him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Just think about where we were a couple of days ago. For any person who went through what he went through on Saturday to walk into a crowd like this, even though you know it is full of supporters as that crowd was in Butler, Pennsylvania, as well, except for one man. And this is a courageous human being. And I think that that is what is being felt across the country.\par \par \uc2 Think about the things that have been -- the arrows that have been slung at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  since he had the audacity to beat at Hillary Clinton, pretty much, back in 2016. He's been under fire, whether it's been impeachment, whatever you think about those efforts, right, they have been constant. And here he is accepting the nomination of his party once again. It's an extraordinary political story.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do we still have Kellyanne and Karl Rove plugged in? The indelible picture of that surviving the assassination attempt with the blood on his ear and his face, fist in the air. We've seen that picture many, many times over the past couple of days, Kellyanne. What do you think changed in this race since Saturday to today?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, that picture is going to be the picture of the 2024 presidential race, and in the years from now, people will be reading in history books about that moment. And it crystalized in the American people's view that this was a strong man who took adversity and overcame it. And tonight I can just imagine the emotions in that box where he was sitting with his wife, his children, their wives, their husbands, and to know that just a few short hours ago there, that he was in such a moment of danger. And yet tonight you saw the crowd's reaction. And I suspect all across America there are people who say, you know what, I may disagree with him or I may agree with him, but he is one tough customer and worthy of being president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Kellyanne, thoughts on your party as we watch J.D. Vance wave to the crowd here? He's 39 years old. Seven years ago, he was a best-selling author with no particular designs on politics. He's come a long way in a short time.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes. That's the American dream story, and J.D. Vance has lived it all the way from coming from nothing to going to Yale, meeting his wife there. The career he's had. I think he taps into the forgotten man, forgotten woman.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this. President Trump was asked today, how do you plan to use J.D. Vance? And he said, I think I'll leave him in Pennsylvania. That's smart. Because friends, if they get North Carolina, Pennsylvania, and Georgia, that's 270. It's over. You don't even have to go to those other states like Wisconsin, Michigan, Arizona, and the rest. So that's important here, because if you basically run a governor's campaign in Pennsylvania, that's a smart strategy. And he can deploy all these other people. The people he didn't pick for V.P. are going to be out there campaigning for him, like Rubio and Burgum and Scott and the rest.\par \par \uc2 I want to say one last thing. President Trump shows again this country loves a story of survival, resilience. We all face adversity. We all eat the dirt at some point, many points in our lives. What do we do with it? How do we handle it? And I think that he's giving everybody a little lesson that the Democrats have taken a big risk in making their campaign about Trump, Trump, Trump, because this week is all about Trump, Trump, Trump, just not how the Democrats anticipated.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of that, President Biden gave an interview to Lester Holt on NBC. Harold, for people who watched these interviews, there's not a lot of confidence that looking at the interview he's getting his point across a lot of times. He went back to inflation was at nine percent when he took over, which was really at 1.4 percent. He talked about a number of different things that were challenged. But his point about speaking to unity, is he going to keep that up as he goes on the campaign trail tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think he has to be better prepared and better organized for these presentations and interviews. You've got to talk about what you've done and what you're going to do going forward. This convention here and how this president has organized this is something Democrats need to pay very, very close attention to. We're going to be critical of J.D. Vance. He deserves some of it. But he's young. And the contrast between that youth and that energy with our ticket is going to be something on full display.\par \par \uc2 And to your point, Bret, I want to hear the president's voice over the next few days. He was a little subdued tonight. But I've got to tell you, as an American, you were proud of him. And I want to see if he lives up to his changed approach that you and others have suggested and told us that he shared with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It will be fascinating to see. We have a number more nights to go, and each night has a theme. And it's all about making America great again, making America wealthy again, making America safe again. And then --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to hear from \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  actually tomorrow night, which was unexpected. And then Wednesday is the vice president's big night. And Thursday, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the 45th president, will take the stage for his big moment here on this stage in Milwaukee. An extraordinary open, lots of energy here tonight. We'll see how the rest of the week goes, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Everyday Americans are going to factor in big. You've saw some of them talking about affordability. You saw some of them talking about struggles. We'll see more as this convention continues throughout the week.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us on night one of the Republican National Convention.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll be right back here tomorrow night at 10:00 p.m. eastern. But stay right here for a live edition of "GUTFELD!" from Milwaukee. That's pretty fun. And it starts right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let the band play us out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020240716ek7f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O`Brien\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 2,333 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: In selecting J.D. Vance, much has been made of the fact that he`s so young.\par \par \uc2 Well, he`s very young. We haven`t had a vice presidential candidate this young. I guess you would have to go back to almost, what, 1900, when William McKinley chose Teddy Roosevelt. Of course, sadly, William McKinley, as he started out his second term, was assassinated, and the 42-year-old Teddy Roosevelt emerged as President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 You obviously hope that history doesn`t repeat itself here, but young men have been thrust into this position sometimes earlier than is planned. That worked out pretty well, with Teddy Roosevelt arguably considered one of our better presidents.\par \par \uc2 Sean O`Brien joins me right now. In case that doesn`t ring a bell to a lot of you, I should point out he is the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president. And you`re probably thinking, then what the heck is he doing at the Republican National Convention? Well, he`s going to speak to that convention tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, first, he`s speaking to us.\par \par \uc2 Sean, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEAN O`BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: Great to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You could almost look like a fish out of water. How do you feel there?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I feel great. I feel very confident.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m here representing working people in America. I`m here representing the greatest union in the entire country. And my message is clear. We want to make certain that people understand, whether you`re a Democrat, Republican or Independent, and how important it is to respect working-class America and also to encourage bipartisan participation on issues that face working people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Obviously, you raised this issue with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You want the same opportunity to speak to Democratic conventioneers when they gather in August.\par \par \uc2 What has \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  told you about that convention?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I haven`t heard anything, sir. The last time I was on your show, we talked about how we asked both the Republican National Convention and the Democratic National Convention.\par \par \uc2 The Republican National Convention welcomed me. I`m still waiting to hear on the Democratic National Convention. And, look, it`s controversial. We understand that. But the reality is, our system is broken right now. And we`re divided right down the middle.\par \par \uc2 I have got a constituency of 1.3 million members. Half vote Republican, half vote Democrat. So we have got to serve both equally.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you have not heard back from the White House on speaking at the convention, the Democratic Convention, after all this time?\par \par \uc2 We spoke first over a month ago.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: We did, yes. No, we haven`t heard anything. Crickets.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What does that tell you? Are you offended?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, I`m not offended at all.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m a lifelong Democrat, but I have no problem working with people that are going to help support working people. Look, I`m getting criticized from the left. I`m getting criticized from the far right. And at the end of the day, we have got to do what`s best for this country, number one, and working people.\par \par \uc2 And I believe it`s to work bipartisan-wise to find solutions to problems. Often, too, times, we take a position each side, and there`s a line drawn on the sand. It`s not working. So the system`s broken. We need to fix it. And I`m committed to working with anybody that`s going to support working people, but, more importantly, this country.\par \par \uc2 We are -- we live in the greatest country in the world, and we need to make certain that we protect it. We need to make sure we preserve it and, more importantly, promote it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it says a lot that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was very interested in hearing from you and letting conventioneers hear from you. He didn`t wait a month to say yes or no.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No. And I`m sure he got criticized for when the announcement happened. You know, he far right called him to rescind the invitation. And, thankfully, he had the courage and conviction not to do that.\par \par \uc2 I hope the Democrats give us the same opportunity. You know, e have supported the Democrats for the last 20 years unwavering, much to a lot of dissatisfaction within the union. And, again, I will state clearly I`m a lifelong Democrat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: But times have changed. Wants and needs need to be addressed regardless of whether you`re a Democrat, Republican or an Independent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So you`re also an astute reed of the tea leaves here. And you know, in the past, regardless of what leadership that your union or other unions have recommended for president, the sort of rank and file, the core members, of which you started out as one, generally did prefer and like Republicans, certainly by much greater numbers than was reflected in union endorsements\par \par \uc2 Is the same true at \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ? You you have many more members who like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than they do \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, I don`t know if that`s accurate. I mean, we`re doing polling right now. We have done a lot of polling as far as reaching out to a certain demographic.\par \par \uc2 We have also done polling, straw poll meetings within the 331 locals that we represent in the United States. And, currently, we -- by the end of this month, we have a publication that goes out to 1.6 million active and retired \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , which will have a Q.R. code, which will give us a good gauge on where we`re at.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, it is neck and neck right now. And, again, it`s a decision that`s going to be made with a lot of transparency and, most importantly, inclusiveness of the rank-and-file members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you personally leaning toward \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I`m personally going to lean towards the person that`s going to lead this company -- country in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 I haven`t made a decision, but I will tell you what, Neil. As soon as I do, I`m going to give you the exclusive.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It sounds like you`re leaning in that direction, though.\par \par \uc2 I mean, for one thing, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got back to you, at great risk to his own base, that many of whom did not think this was a good idea, but he did it anyway. And I`m just wondering, given the fact you have had no response from the White House -- maybe, eventually, you will, maybe after this interview you will -- it just seems weird, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: It just seems strange that, you know, we wouldn`t be given an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 You know, in this day and age, for people to get upset because you want your voice heard on behalf of a diverse membership, you know, it`s kind of -- it`s kind of discouraging. But I don`t -- I think me by now. I don`t take anything personal. And, look, we will just -- we will deal with the cards we have and we will make the best decision we possibly can make on behalf of our members and on the working-class America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of Donald Trump`s vice president pick? J.D. Vance, a young man, all of 39?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: So, J.D. --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, J.D. Vance, the short time that we have worked together, I mean, he`s been great on Teamster issues. He is supporting -- co-sponsored an airline manufacturer bill that addresses outsourcing of critical in-line maintenance to China. He`s also supported paid sick leave for our railroad workers.\par \par \uc2 You remember that situation a couple of years ago --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: -- when they weren`t getting sick time. He was -- he stepped right up.\par \par \uc2 He`s also been very vocal and supportive of holding employers accountable who try and skirt their obligation under an independent contract, a model known as DSP. So he`s been right there on all our issues. We`ve publicly stated it.\par \par \uc2 And, look, at this day and age, there`s nothing better than having a U.S. Marine represented as a vice president candidate. Youth is great. Oftentimes, you know, people are overlooked because of their age, but they should be looked at because of their ability.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I get that all the time, Sean. They say, you`re so young. You`re so young. What are you doing?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What is your goal --\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Right. You and me both.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What is your goal tonight?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Look, my goal tonight is to let everybody know how important the working-class America is, how important we have been.\par \par \uc2 And I want to encourage people to have an open mind, to work together. It`s unfortunate this country is divided right down the middle. And we have got other world leaders looking at us, laughing at us because they know there`s a fracture in the infrastructure in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And, look, we`re not always going to agree on everything, but at the end of the day, when there are situations where we can come together, work together, and support working-class Americans, but more importantly support the greatest nation in the world, that`s my goal tonight.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so when you look at the -- particularly with the Democrats here, I was making the assumption that, obviously, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 There are still people who doubt that that`s going to be the case. Many of your colleagues in the union movement have been quietly looking for alternatives, convinced that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a losing candidate, and they`re looking at other names even now, even after this weekend`s horrific developments for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the near-assassination.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts on that? Would you be more suited to talk at a Democratic Convention, if they let you, if it were a different nominee?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: No, I`m ready to speak whoever the nominee is, whether it`s President Biden or whoever that may be moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And you are correct. You know, here`s been a lot of talk about other unions looking for alternative candidates. There`s been a lot of legislatures that have been looking for an alternative candidate.\par \par \uc2 You know, the reality of it is, whoever is the nominee, whether it`s President Biden or anybody else, we`re hopeful to get an audience with them and explain what our concerns are, what our wants and needs are, just like we`re doing here this week.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when look at the economy right now, the White House likes to say it`s improving, inflation is off its worst levels, that`s all us doing that, all this job creation, all us doing that.\par \par \uc2 And some of them might look at you right now on the floor of the Republican Convention, saying, Sean, what gives?\par \par \uc2 What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, look, I can only speak for my membership. I mean, look, there`s no disputing the facts. Inflation`s high. We have got to curb that.\par \par \uc2 You know, there is layoffs throughout our system in different industries. So, you know, sometimes, the picture that`s painted isn`t always accurate. But the fact of the matter is, I think we`re all caught up in this political -- political fight right now, which is not helping the economy. It`s not helping inflation.\par \par \uc2 And, again, this goes to my point of, we have got to find solutions to these problems. It can`t be -- we have got to remember who we actually work for. You know, the politicians work for their constituents who live in their communities.\par \par \uc2 You know, our members are part of those communities where they live. So these solutions need to be found. Do I believe we`re in the greatest economy we`re in right now? I don`t believe we are. I think we could be. You know, here`s still a lot of people that aren`t working, a lot of industries that are suffering.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, we are doing pretty good, but we always can do better.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess America just wants to know this, as a final question.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to wear a tie tonight when you address the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I am definitely going to wear a tie tonight, sir.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I wear a tie most of the time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: But I will be honest with you. I didn`t want to look like -- I didn`t want to look like I was wearing the same thing twice.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I understand.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: So, plus, I`m a working guy. We don`t always wear ties.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, nor should you.\par \par \uc2 Sean, thank you for joining us today. And we`re looking forward to your remarks tonight, historically unprecedented. I don`t believe we have seen this, at least in the last century or so, a union chieftain addressing the Republican Convention.\par \par \uc2 So, all eyes on Sean O`Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president, tonight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240715ek7f0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Open Borders Invite Worst Of The World; Chaos And Violence Reign In Dem Sanctuary Cities; Biden Fast Tracks The New Dem Coalition; Explosive New Documents Reveal Children's Hospital Never Stopped So-Called Gender- Affirming Care; Protecting Kids Could Get You Prison Time; DOJ Targets Doctor Who Exposed Trans Surgery On Kids; Media Rekindles Love Affair With Dr. Fauci; Fauci Cashes In On COVID Again; Congresswoman Bush Claims To Be A Miracle Worker; Joe Scarborough Says Trump Will Take Biden's Mic; San Francisco Mayor Challenges Opponent To Name Drag Queens\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Tom Homan, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 7,532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copy