When you need to restore trust, when you need to get people civil to each other, you have to admit error. And his errors were so heinous, so horrendous, it is the most awful legacy in modern public health history.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, Laura, it's your field, really, that supports him. The people in the media have so abandoned their role as ethical journalists for probably, I guess, political gain. It's very frightening to me as an American.\par \par \uc2 I don't want the country to be passed on to my own children, I'm a father, in the state that it's in right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Dr. Atlas, just like on social distancing, that was a total made-up lie. The masks, they had already been proven with cloth masks they don't stop the spread of influenza-like viruses. None of that stuff made any sense and they knew it, and they knew that natural immunity was a thing.\par \par \uc2 Remember he blew off the natural immunity stuff or acquired immunity. He still blows that off. I mean, he says it's a factor but not an overwhelming one. Really quick.\par \par \uc2 ATLAS: Yeah, two points. It was known, I said it from March 2020, April, May, June, July, August, September, all the data was known. And secondly, we have somebody here who's displaying the worst form of lack of integrity. He won't admit he's wrong. His career was based on this. And he would gain a lot of points.\par \par \uc2 But he won't. They'll dig in. And we cannot let history be rewritten or the country's finished as an ethical and objective society. So, we don't give up. We have to say the truth, and hopefully truth will prevail.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, and accountability. Admission of error and accountability. Dr. Atlas, you're a bright shining light and I'm proud to have had you on. I think the first on national television on this. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, who needs doctors? You have \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 . Jimmy Failla on that and a lot more, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now it's time for WTF, What the Failla. Joining me now, Jimmy Failla, host of Fox News Saturday night. All right, Jimmy, an interview with squad member, \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 . She kind of gets short shrift because it's always AOC. But just resurfaced where she claimed that she had miraculous powers. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CONG. CORI BUSH (D-MO): This lady came to us and she had these tumors. I mean, she wanted us to like feel them. And I just remember I put my hand on her and my hand just began to move, and the lumps that were there were no longer there, and she was so happy. And she, like, went on about her day and I never saw her again.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So, you think the tumors disappeared?\par \par \uc2 BUSH: I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy, we got to call the Vatican. That's -- wow. OK.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS SATURDAY NIGHT HOST: You know, the sad part though is after listening to \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  talk, the woman wanted the tumors back. She was like, I can't. I don't want to live anymore.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I hate to call the woman who wanted to defund the police, but had her own police protection of fraud, but she's a fraud. You know. It takes a lot to make AOC look smart, but I believe that's \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 's role in the squad.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, it's kind of sad when you're a squad member but nobody remembers you. I mean, yeah, it's like the odd squad. Like, she doesn't get -- you didn't get any of the attention. But we decided she needed some tonight, Jimmy, that's why we did that, the miraculous healing powers. Yeah, heal the squad. First start with, physician heal thyself.\par \par \uc2 Morning Joe, Jimmy, had an interesting analysis or prediction about next week's debate.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MORNING JOE: He cannot debate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on the merits. So, he's going to be a disruptor. And the Biden team better be prepared for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to walk -- I mean, what would a disruptor do? OK, you're going to cut off my microphone, I'm going to walk over to your microphone and I'm going to get in your face. I'm going to come -- he could do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Joe Scarborough likes to talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , like it's a guy who dumped him in seventh grade. It always like, oh, my god, did you see what Donald did? Like who would do that? Donald's like the worst.\par \par \uc2 Listen, Trump's obviously not going to do that, but I wish he would because at least somebody would use Biden's mic at the debate. I'm not sure Biden's allowed to talk at this debate. After the week he just had, his mic's probably going to be muted the whole time anyway.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. It is very interesting, these never Trumpers, now they're just exposed as total leftists.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, they're never Trump -- they're the real Republican. Now, OK, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's endorsed Trump, so what does that make you, Joe?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: They're coming around.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's embarrassing. All right, Jimmy, naming drag queens now has become a litmus test for Dems in San Fran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You were at the debate last week and couldn't name any drag queens on your own. This is an opportunity to redeem yourself, and if you could name three LGBTQ advisors for your campaign and three drag queens in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: And this is why it costs more money to get a U-Haul out of San Francisco than it does to move in. What does it solve if the candidate -- you know, crime is bad. What does it solve by saying I know Cinnabons? Nothing. OK.\par \par \uc2 Naming drag queens doesn't get crime under control, doesn't lower taxes, doesn't bring back businesses --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: -- singles.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. I was trying to see if Jimmy knew any drag queen names, but we'll end it at that. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240619ek6i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Florida Floods; Federal Debt Woes; Flight Attendants on the Picket Line?; No Supreme Court Decision Yet on Trump Immunity; Princess of Wales Set to Make Public Appearance; Dash For Campaign Cash\b0\par\par\uc2 Abby Hornacek, Rick Reichmuth, Nicole Valdes, Garrett Tenney, Andy  McCarthy, David Spunt, Alex Hogan, Danamarie McNicholl, William La  Jeunesse, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,385 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: It's a good thing I cover money, because it's all about the money, and especially right now.\par \par \uc2 Money, that is what the Biden and Trump campaigns want, and they're hoping to haul in lots of it. President Biden, fresh off his European trip, and planning now to sweep into Los Angeles tomorrow for a star-studded Hollywood fund-raiser, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , Julia Roberts and \uc2 George Clooney\uc2  said to be among the big headliners.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , for his part, is attending a big birthday bash tonight at Club 47 in West Palm Beach with about 5,000 of his closest supporters, possibly more.\par \par \uc2 We have got \uc2 FOX\uc2  team coverage with Danamarie McNicholl on that Trump event expected to kick off in about three hours and William La Jeunesse in Los Angeles on the big money that President Biden is banking on right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. You're watching "Your World" right now. I'm Neil Cavuto. Glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right first to West Palm Beach. That's where you will find Danamarie on this big, well, Trump event tonight -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, and that birthday bash just started about an hour ago, as you mentioned, 5,000 people.\par \par \uc2 They're trickling into the Palm Beach Convention Center. The energy is building. They are excited. Trump is expected to be speaking and arrive for the festivities around 7:00 p.m. tonight. So we have a couple of hours until that starts.\par \par \uc2 Now, the former president is turning 78 years old today. He shares his birthday with Flag Day, the holiday. The event is being hosted by Club 47, a group who calls himself the largest club of Trump supporters in America, this after Trump's Capitol Hill visit yesterday with House and Senate Republicans that brought a rare moment of GOP unity.\par \par \uc2 He stressed Republicans should not attack one another, promising to -- quote -- "work out" any lingering differences within the party. Senators even gave him an American flag cake with 45 candles and then 47 referring to the next presidency.\par \par \uc2 Over on social media, Trump was interviewed by influencer and professional wrestler Logan Paul on his podcast with almost 1.8 million views in less than 24 hours. The former president is reaching a younger audience by covering topics from UFOs to the rap beef between Drake and Kendrick Lamar.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's going to be a couple of busy days for former President Trump, as this weekend he heads to battleground Michigan to court black voters, listening to residents at roundtable discussions. He will also be speaking at a Turning Point USA event in Detroit, a solidly Democratic city where President Biden won by 94 percent in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And back out here live, it's a sold-out crowd. People were instructed to wear red, white and blue to this birthday bash. So most are rocking out to live music on the stage anxiously awaiting for former President Trump to speak and address the crowd for his birthday -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Danamarie McNicholl in West Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to William La Jeunesse right now out in Los Angeles, a big event planned for the president tomorrow with some pretty big A-list names.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us, William?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, a lot of money, maybe 20 million or more, but the president also needs to energize his campaign, and the validation that comes with appearing to be supported by popular celebrities, even though most attending Saturday have virtually nothing in common with the average voter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARAH BRYNER, OPENSECRETS: Not looking at \uc2 George Clooney\uc2  for voting advice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But \uc2 George Clooney\uc2  has something others don't.\par \par \uc2 This Saturday, he and Julia Roberts will activate the Hollywood money machine for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That's character in real life. And that's \uc2 George Clooney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: In 2020, the entertainment industry gave Democrats $104 million, almost 10 times more than Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I know what it's like to struggle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Biden highlights his blue-collar roots while calling out Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looks at the world differently than you and me, how he can help his billionaire friends gain power and control.\par \par \uc2 BRYNER: Biden has his little cadre of billionaires, as does Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Both candidates are raising a lot of money from big tech and Wall Street. Biden's big donors include hedge fund executives and wealthy individuals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAY GOODLIFFE, \uc2 BRIGHAM YOUNG UNIVERSITY\uc2 : What Biden is trying to do is say, look at the policies I'm doing. Don't look where I'm raising money. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to say, I'm the guy that fights for you, even though I am rich.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Big money often buys attack ads. Will celebrity endorsements help move the needle? Biden is banking on them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and I approve this message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Biden hit up California four years ago for $300 million, his number one source of cash.\par \par \uc2 Tickets up to $125,000 on Saturday, but the event also offers him something equally valuable, a beeline into the social media into the social media accounts of celebrities who've offered their name and likeness to the campaign for fund-raising and access to their millions of followers, again, young voters -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, William La Jeunesse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So where are you on this, America? And who do you favor? What are you more impressed by?\par \par \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , GOP pollster with us, Carly Cooperman, Democratic pollster.\par \par \uc2 Carly, obviously, the president is going to say, I have got the big Hollywood heavyweights behind me. But, to be fair, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has very galvanized crowds with him. Many of them are A-listers, but many more are not. And they're the ones who are jazzed.\par \par \uc2 And I keep thinking back to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who could fill a venue as long as she had some of those celebrities, without them, not so much. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CARLY COOPERMAN, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER: I think, for Biden, he can't turn away from these kind of fund-raising events. The money race is so important in this election right now.\par \par \uc2 This was an advantage that Biden had for some period of time. And now it seems like Trump has closed in and might be on par in terms of how much money he has to spend. And Biden needs the money that he can raise in terms of reaching voters, getting his message out there, connecting with groups that are less than enthusiastic right now about voting for him that he needs to turn out in the fall.\par \par \uc2 And if there's celebrities that can help attract young people who historically have voted Democrat, but currently are showing lagging support for Biden, then that's all to the better. And I think both Trump and Biden are going to use whatever celebrities they can to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 And I think the argument that points to the cynicism kind of just falls flat at this point, because that's just what politics does these days.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I think you raise a very good point on that. I mean, you get what you can from whom you can.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , it does come back to who has the most galvanized based. Now, maybe Hollywood and some of these big names can help the president galvanize that base. It doesn't appear to be for the time being all that galvanized. But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: I don't think that celebrities have the same kind of pull as they used to. I think it used to mean a lot to have celebrity endorsements. I think we saw in 2016 Hillary Clinton had the entire Hollywood establishment behind her and it didn't help her in the end.\par \par \uc2 And I think right now we have got this whole counterculture movement where people are trying to think for themselves and express themselves differently. And they're -- in many ways, we're seeing this whole revolt against the elite telling us what we should and should not do.\par \par \uc2 So I'm not sure how much the Hollywood elite pull in the same way that they used to, but I do agree with Carly that the numbers here are really important. The dollar amount Biden needs to raise is significant. He needs his ground game. And because he can't really capture the media's attention the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can, he has to have paid media.\par \par \uc2 He has to be getting his message out there in ways that are going to connect with his audience. And unless he has enough money, he's not going to be able to do that. So the dollars are very, very important to him. I think they're less significant for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who really pulls from a small donor base. And he's got a lot more count, not just in the coffers, but in the number of people that are supporting him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and I think, as you have both reminded me as well in the past, it's how you spend that dough. They're -- both sides are going to get a considerable amount of dough. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's been on a roll. President Biden had an early lead in that.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, how do you spend that money? And I'm just wondering on that, Carly, the issue then becomes where that dough goes. Now, I remember people laughing at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back in 2016 when he was pouring so much energy into these battleground states, where a lot of people in his own party were telling him, you're wasting your time. I mean, you don't have a chance held it to win these.\par \par \uc2 He did. And he's once again targeting these very battleground states, and from a much greater poll position, including Michigan tomorrow, where he's going to continue to woo African-American and other voters. What do you make of that and how Democrats respond to that?\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: Yes, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  absolutely has to win the blue wall of those states of Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania if he's going to be successful this election. These are states that he won in 2020 and were critical to him winning the presidency.\par \par \uc2 And these are states that he cannot afford to lose. I mean, there are a lot of ways to play with the electoral map and the math that comes with it right now. But the general consensus is that these three states are ones that Biden must win.\par \par \uc2 And right now, in Michigan, the polls show that the two are virtually tied. They're in a dead heat right now for this. There is concern that Biden's losing support among black voters and other voter groups that are key to him being successful. And so Trump is being very purposeful by going to Michigan and doing what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 I think that, ultimately, it would be a large realignment for Trump to really pull that level of support from black voters. But this is something that Biden -- an area that Biden is going to have to focus heavily on over the next few months.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you make of that, Lee, and the strategy?\par \par \uc2 Obviously, focus on the battleground states, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has -- he had no choice sometimes when this hush money trial was going on -- to focus on New York, for example, to do a lot of activities in New Jersey, blue, very blue states, but he got big crowds.\par \par \uc2 So what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: So I think what he was doing is capturing media attention. So no matter where you were, you were seeing it, and it was the element of surprise.\par \par \uc2 To see a huge turnout in the Bronx for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or in New Jersey, traditionally blue states -- or not traditionally, but blue states right now -- it was surprising to all. And a lot of people watched that wouldn't have otherwise watched. So I think he made the most of the time that he wasn't able to travel.\par \par \uc2 And now that he is able to travel, he's got to go to those battleground states, where Carly's absolutely right. Those are the make-or-break states. This election is really going to be decided by those three, plus four more.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's making his case.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, Carly, the debate coming up on the 27th, how crucial will that be?\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: I think it's important. I think it's going to be the first time that some people are starting to tune into the election. We know that voters are really unenthusiastic and not excited about these candidates.\par \par \uc2 And the summer is historically when people start to tune in and pay a little bit more attention. And for the Biden administration, and for Biden himself, this is an opportunity to try to reset on his terms and get his legislative accomplishments out there and show that he's energetic and kind of try to counter the narrative, as he's been lagging in the polls and hasn't been able to turn things around.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you both. Have a wonderful weekend on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, good days and bad days, that's how the princess of Wales revealed to the public how she's doing right now and what she's up to right now and how she is battling the cancer she's been dealing with for the better part of a month right now.\par \par \uc2 Alex Hogan has more from London -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, this big news coming out of Kensington Palace today, with the princess of Wales revealing a little bit more about her condition and sharing this photo today. She says, as you mentioned, that she has these good days and bad days and that she's not out of the woods just yet, as she undergoes chemotherapy.\par \par \uc2 And it's not been revealed what kind of cancer the 42-year-old has been diagnosed with. But in this statement, she again reiterated the struggles that she faces certain days versus other days being a little bit more positive, something that anyone who has had cancer or anyone who has a loved one who has had cancer can relate to.\par \par \uc2 Now, Kate is not back in the swing of official duties, but the other part of today's news is that she will be attending the Trooping of the Color tomorrow, which is the monarch's official birthday parade in London, something that no one thought she was going to attend.\par \par \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  himself is also undergoing cancer treatment and it was just last week when he traveled to France for the 80th anniversary of D- Day. Now, as far as tomorrow's ceremony, the Trooping of the Color, it's filled with pomp and pageantry. There's as many as 1,400 parading soldiers, hundreds of horses and musicians.\par \par \uc2 And Kate is expected to be at that ceremony in a carriage along with her children for the parade, and then she will be on the balcony of Buckingham Palace to watch the Royal Air do a fly-pass or a flyover, as we call it back home, so a very public event that we're expecting to see her at tomorrow as she undergoes what is a very private matter, something the royal family has asked repeatedly for some respect and privacy in this time.\par \par \uc2 Neil, that being said, Kate did in her message reiterate these messages that she's received from people and how touched and overwhelmed she has been by the gratitude and the kindness that she's received.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed, and a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 Alex Hogan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The princess herself has said that this is an event that she wants to make, this -- \uc2 King Charles\uc2 ' birthday parade, and that she spoke candidly in this letter to her subjects, as it were: "I know I am not out of the woods yet."\par \par \uc2 All right, we will be following that tomorrow, because a lot of this will be transpiring while my weekend show is on. We will be going back and forth to London, but also all over the world, with some of the other developments happening across the world, particularly in the Middle East. That's another issue we will be getting into.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the Supreme Court making a raft of decisions. That one on immunity that's coming up for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whether he is entitled to that as a former president, that's still undecided -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we did get some Supreme Court decisions today, but not the one that a lot of folks have been looking forward to since back in February, and that is this immunity decision and whether a former president is allowed to grant that to protect he and other presidents to come from the very sort of thing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is facing.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, David Spunt on this and a lot more in Washington -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, ticktock goes the clock. The longer this takes to get a Supreme Court decision, the further this pushes back \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's potential trial back.\par \par \uc2 But as with just about everything, there are, of course, caveats. The first, does this even go to trial? Well, the former president is charged with trying to overturn the 2020 election results. He was supposed to go to trial beginning in early March. That deadline came and went.\par \par \uc2 The case itself has essentially been on ice since late December once an appeal was announced to the local D.C. Circuit. Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2 , who's overseeing the case, has stopped all activity on the docket at least publicly until this issue resolves at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first appeal was to the D.C. Circuit. They ruled that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not have absolute immunity to avoid prosecution, but the Supreme Court decided to get involved a few months ago and weigh in, even though the D.C. Circuit ruled unanimously he does not have full immunity from prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we don't want to speculate what the High Court will do. The case was argued at the end of April. They typically have until the end of June, which means two more weeks to release this opinion. Depending on what they do, it could be good or bad for special counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 , that man right there, who indicted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 If the court holds that Trump enjoys broad immunity, expect this case to never happen. If they rule he has none, expect the case to perhaps happen later this summer. If it's a mixed bag, that could also delay things.\par \par \uc2 Now, switching gears to another big Justice Department story, Neil, in a letter to House Speaker Mike Johnson just a few hours ago, the DOJ made it clear, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  will not be criminally charged after he was held in contempt of Congress just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, House Republicans held Garland in contempt after he declined to turn over audiotapes from an October interview between President Biden and then special counsel Robert Hur at the White House. This was in that classified documents case, where Biden was not charged.\par \par \uc2 Garland turned over the transcripts months ago, which has the same content, just not the audio. Now, the wording in the letter to Speaker Johnson is almost the same as a 2019 letter in which DOJ announced it would not criminally charge then-Attorney General Bill Barr for contempt of Congress, really almost word for word, almost the same letter, and the same with a 2020-2012 letter, I should say, regarding then-Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , who was the first to be held in contempt.\par \par \uc2 He too was not prosecuted -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, the next crack at this immunity thing, if it were to pass or they are to take it up, would be next week, right, next Thursday?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Yes, exactly. Next Thursday, the Supreme Court has opinions listed on the calendar. They could add days. They like to try to wrap these up by June 28, which will be the last Friday in the month, so it's possible they could add maybe next Tuesday, Wednesday, or Friday, but, as of now, just Thursday on the calendar.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 OK, thank you very much, David Spunt, following all of that.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of legal issues here to get into.\par \par \uc2 Let's get into it with Rebecca Rose Woodland, the New York trial attorney. We have got Tom Dupree with us, a former deputy assistant attorney general, Andy McCarthy, also former assistant attorney general, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all.\par \par \uc2 So, Andy, end it with, begin with you. First off on this DOJ saying we will not prosecute \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and that there's a distinction between this branch going after officials within the Department of Justice. Just not going to happen. Move on. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's not surprising.\par \par \uc2 We went 223 years, Neil, with no attorney general of the United States being held in contempt or censured in that way. Now three, I think, of the last five attorneys general have, so it's kind of been depleted as a -- as anything other than a partisan weapon in these very partisan times. It's not surprising that the Biden Justice Department would not prosecute Biden's attorney general.\par \par \uc2 But we should be clear on what's going on here, and that is that Biden and Garland have made a political determination that they would be more harmed by the release of that tape than the political hit they're taking for stonewalling over it.\par \par \uc2 But the position they're taking is a political position. It's not a legal one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what happens now? I'm thinking the position that they're in now, Tom Dupree, and, obviously, Congress is going to say, whoa, whoa, whoa.\par \par \uc2 But then there's the brakes, right? Is this going to change? I mean, where do you go?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Yes. Well, I don't think it's going to change.\par \par \uc2 Look, I mean, they're basically asking \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to prosecute himself, which we pretty much all knew was going to be a nonstarter. And I think the congressional leverage here, Neil, really just comes from the ability to put a spotlight on these issues.\par \par \uc2 As Andy said, the administration clearly made a political judgment that it would rather take the contempt hit, or at least the contempt citation, or the public opprobrium of being an attorney general held in contempt, rather than turn over the material that Congress is demanding.\par \par \uc2 So I think Congress' next move is just to ensure that they can't get away from the consequences of this decision. To keep the spotlight on the issue, they may call hearings. There may be other public statements from commission chairmen, committee chairmen, or other members of the House or the Senate really saying, look, you owe the American people this information.\par \par \uc2 And if you're going to continue stonewalling, fair enough, you're not going to get prosecuted for it, but we're going to ensure that the public holds you to account.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, separately, and, Rebecca, I want to explore this a little more with you, the Supreme Court has not yet ruled on this Trump immunity claim.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering how you think that will go, because it's been -- it's been sort of dragging its feet, to hear a lot of Trump people put it, a lot of prosecutors put it, on this particular issue. How do you see it going?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROSE WOODLAND, ATTORNEY: Well, first of all, the delay certainly doesn't hurt President Trump, right? Former President Trump, to delay is -- it can only help, because the stay remains in effect.\par \par \uc2 What seems to be -- it seems to be a trend, that the Supreme Court may decide this on very narrow factual issues, the absolute immunity. Presidents have enjoyed absolute immunity over the course of the existence of the United States for liability for any sort of official acts.\par \par \uc2 Whether this will be considered official acts, what happened on July -- January 6, that will be the big question. The overall scope of immunity has to remain in place. Presidents cannot be subject to constant lawsuits and being derided and bogged down with that.\par \par \uc2 So I think what we're going to see, I do think the president will be victorious with the Supreme Court, but I think most of the opinion will be specific as to very factually specific regarding what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Andy, if that is the case, and let's say they do rule on this, but quite the opposite of what Rebecca was pointing out, it doesn't go the former president's way, at the very least, things have been delayed just waiting for this, right?\par \par \uc2 So the idea that at least two of the cases involved here could get adjudicated before the election are next to nil, aren't they?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I don't think so, Neil. I think there's still a chance that the January 6 case goes to trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hmm.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Here's why.\par \par \uc2 I think Trump's lawyer John Sauer at the oral argument gave a lot of ground and he admitted that, under questioning by both Justices Barrett and Kagan, that a number of the acts in the indictment were private acts that aren't amenable of an immunity claim.\par \par \uc2 If -- I think if Smith is willing, when the case comes back in whatever form it comes back in, if he's willing to say, look, I'm willing to leave the rest of my case on the cutting room floor, let's just go to trial on what they concede is private, that case could still get to trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow. We will watch very closely. Guys, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I don't know if you are taking off to the airports this weekend or the weeks ahead. It's supposed to be a very busy travel season. So, you will be dealing with long TSA lines. That's probably an understatement, but you're also going to be dealing with some picket lines, some flight attendant picket lines.\par \par \uc2 Get ready -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we wrapped up largely another winning week for the markets, especially the Nasdaq, its fifth record in a row. Technology stocks limped to the finish line.\par \par \uc2 Not so much the Dow -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and you thought those lines of TSA were long. Well, you could be bumping into this, as a flight attendant strike begins to take hold.\par \par \uc2 Garrett Tenney has more from Chicago O'Hare International Airport -- Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, planes can't fly without the flight attendants.\par \par \uc2 So these protests and the potential for a strike could throw a wrench in the travel plans for millions of Americans this summer. These negotiations for a new contract have been going on for more than five years now, going back to before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And this week, the two sides are in Washington meeting with federal mediators to give the talks one last chance before flight attendants walk off the job. The soonest that could happen would be late next month, right in the middle of the summer travel season.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARA NELSON\uc2 , PRESIDENT, ASSOCIATION OF FLIGHT ATTENDANTS: We're fighting hard to get those deals in place, not to disrupt people's travel.\par \par \uc2 But if it comes to that, this could be a major disruption. In the case of American, we're talking about shutting down the entire system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Yesterday, flight attendants at dozens of airports were picketing as part of a worldwide day of action, since both United and \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  also have ongoing negotiations with their flight attendants.\par \par \uc2 The biggest sticking point in the negotiations is pay. Inflation has gone through the roof over the past five years, but flight attendant' salaries haven't really changed. The average starting salary for a flight attendant is now around $27,000, $28,000.\par \par \uc2 It has been 14 years since we had an airline strike here in the U.S. Everyone involved is hoping to keep that streak alive, especially here during the travel summer season -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much for that. Hopefully, they settle that.\par \par \uc2 Garrett Tenney in Chicago on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you have heard a lot about the debt, $34 trillion and counting, closing in on $35 trillion. The Treasury secretary of the United States making some waves when \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said, we're in a reasonable place. And it got us thinking here, on a big money show, if that's reasonable, what exactly would be unreasonable?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: I think that if the debt is stabilized relative to the size of the economy, that we're in a reasonable place.\par \par \uc2 The way I look at it is that we should be looking at the real interest cost of the debt that's real and what the burden is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I know what she was trying to say in that CNBC interview, but the Treasury secretary of the United States, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , alarmed a lot of people when she was clearly referring to our nearly $35 trillion in debt as a reasonable place.\par \par \uc2 It is anything but according to my next guest. \uc2 Maya MacGuineas\uc2  is that guest. She's been bemoaning this, well, when it was a whole lot less, $35 trillion, the Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget president.\par \par \uc2 Maya, what did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MAYA MACGUINEAS, COMMITTEE FOR A RESPONSIBLE FEDERAL BUDGET PRESIDENT, \uc2 NEW AMERICA FOUNDATION\uc2 : Well, I mean, in the big picture, there's absolutely nothing reassuring about our fiscal situation, really, not one good piece of news that you can find in looking at all the numbers.\par \par \uc2 I understand what the secretary was saying, which is, we used to talk about balancing the budget. Unfortunately, our fiscal situation is so bad, that's not going to happen. And finding a plan that would stabilize the debt and then put it on a downward path so it doesn't grow as a share of GDP, that would be a healthy step that a lot of people are talking about as the right metric.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, we're not on track to do anything close to that. We are on track to borrow $20 trillion over the next decade. The debt would not be stabilized. In fact, it would have hit the record of the highest as a share of the economy it's ever been four years