ake changes, because they're headed for insolvency. And yet we have a political environment where nobody's talking about the truth anymore.\par \par \uc2 You just talked about sort of claims that the deficit have come down. That was just unrealistic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACGUINEAS: The president had put more borrowing on the books. The deficit only came down as we recovered from COVID. President Trump also borrowed much, much more before COVID hit than we needed to.\par \par \uc2 They both have made the debt worse. We're soon going to release the numbers on how much they both put into new borrowing, and it's really a ugly, frightening picture.\par \par \uc2 And I think the worst component of all of it is where we are on interest. Our interest payments are the second largest item in the budget.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 MACGUINEAS: The only program that's bigger is Social Security. They are larger than defense spending. That is very dangerous for our country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we have tapped our credit lines here to the max, and only Uncle Sam can try to keep increasing the credit line, but the fact is, we're the ones stuck with the bill.\par \par \uc2 Maya, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACGUINEAS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Maya MacGuineas\uc2  on all of that.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be exploring this a lot more with \uc2 Ken Fisher\uc2 , whether he's worried about this, the celebrated investor on why he thinks the markets are ignoring this sort of thing right now and running at the pace they are. That's tomorrow on my weekend show.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we will also be updating you on these floods going on in Florida. They have been really the case all week, and they're not getting better.\par \par \uc2 Besides the floods there, we're dealing with a heat wave that could grip much of the nation, maybe upwards of half the nation, as well -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have been through it. This is the worst we have seen. I have been here 20 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have never seen this. In this area specifically, I mean, it floods, but not like this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we're not even in the tropical storm phase here, the hurricane phase here. We're just in Florida four days running of incredible flooding there.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes has more right now from Miami -- Nicole.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: South Florida still underwater after record rainfall with, even more expected to fall today. Check out this North Miami neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 You will notice just how far, I mean, maybe a mile's worth of water that's not only covered this main road here, but has flooded several of the homes you see in just this neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 It is the grim reality for hundreds of people across South Florida, as they continue to struggle with massive flood concerns and a renewed flood threat today, with another chance for heavy storms, Miami-Dade County one of five counties that remain under a state of emergency after record rainfall in places like Hallandale Beach clocking more than 20 inches of rain in just a three-day period.\par \par \uc2 That's where officials tell us they have rescued nearly 200 people, including a mother and her 7-month-old child. Those are people who were either trapped in their homes or forced to ditch their cars when they realized they were not going to be able to drive through deep water, some people wading through it, lifting up their pants, taking their shoes off, not advised.\par \par \uc2 But, unfortunately some people realized they didn't have a choice.\par \par \uc2 We met Dionte Robison earlier today at Aventura Mall, where he was trying to figure out whether he could salvage his car. And he's got a message for anyone who's thinking about driving through those floodwaters, just like he did.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DIONTE ROBISON, MIAMI RESIDENT: Unfortunately, I learned the hard way due to a lack of patience. I was safely secure in a parking lot. No rain, no water was touching me, but my ego got in the way. And I wanted to go home. I wanted to be comfortable.\par \par \uc2 And it cost me. I literally saw people submerged in water as I was coming out the parking lot. And then the water filled up the passenger side, the back, my side, and the rear driver's side as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VALDES: Now, it's dry and warm for now. We are expecting those showers to start to move through here in just a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But travel, of course, in and out of the Sunshine State has been a mess, as you can imagine, tons of delays and cancellations still today at both Fort Lauderdale and Miami International airports.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible. Thank you, Nicole.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Valdes in the middle of that.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth said, all right, you might be able to dodge, if you're not in the Florida area, these floods, but, boy, you have other things to worry about because a good chunk of the country could be enduring it.\par \par \uc2 Rick, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Yes, we have severe weather going on this evening across big cities of the Northeast, and behind this, get ready for, I think it's going to be a prolonged heat wave that's going to be building.\par \par \uc2 Let's show you what's going on. This is our severe weather threat, cities from back towards Parkersburg all the way up through Hartford in towards Boston, Portland, New York City. These are the storms already beginning to fire here, eastern parts of Pennsylvania, getting ready to go towards Jersey. Within a couple of hours or so, they will be around the New York City area.\par \par \uc2 And it's going to be kind of a few rounds of these storms throughout the evening hours, clearing out of here by tomorrow morning. By tomorrow, though, we're going to see the temperatures are going to be feeling pretty good. Right now, we have got a heat index in New York City of 75. It's really humid out there.\par \par \uc2 Today's a humid day, but this cold front that's bringing the severe weather comes on through here throughout tonight into tomorrow, and then we have got this weekend. It's going to be really pretty spectacular. You're going to want to be outside as much as you can. Look at this, mid-low-70s in Boston, 80, mid-70s on Sunday in New York, a really nice, not humid weekend.\par \par \uc2 Enjoy it, because it is all about to change. By the time we get to, say, towards Tuesday of this week, we are going to watch this ridge move in across much of the East, and it is beginning to begin to get really warm.\par \par \uc2 And the extended outlook here remains warm. So, Saturday, Sunday, the heat is in the Plains. By the time we get towards Monday, it's the Great Lakes towards the Northeast. Take a look here. This is next Thursday through Monday. We're talking about temps right here very likely above average, well above average along all the big cities.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, if I look a little past this, looks like we're going to remain the same way for maybe the following week. We might be talking about a heat wave that builds in here, last a couple of weeks. That becomes dangerous and taxing for so many people. And a lot of people living in this area are going to be dealing with this heat -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we should be careful.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Rick, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth on that.\par \par \uc2 Well, again, another reminder, moms win again. We're told, again, with the planned spending on Father's Day versus Mother's Day, kids just love their mothers more. It's OK. It's OK. I can deal with that.\par \par \uc2 So I'm going to tell you what dads really want, and it will save you a fortune. And this is why we really get less, because we want less -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Here's how out to lunch I am. I didn't even know it was Father's Day weekend, that it's coming up. Apparently, it's a big deal, and it is a big deal, because I'm a dad.\par \par \uc2 And, anyway, I'm not at all offended by the fact that moms always get more, that, for them, it works out to about $33.5 billion that people spend on gifts for them. When it comes to dads, we're about $11 billion shy of that,$22.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 But I don't take it personally. I think I speak for other dads. We realize, kids, you love your mom more, and that moms actually love themselves more. So I get that.\par \par \uc2 But I want to explore this in more detail with Alli Breen, comedian extraordinaire. Abby Hornacek, you know her, a FOX Nation host.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, I'm not bitter. I understand where you're coming from. You probably agree with that huge disparity in love.\par \par \uc2 But, Alli, what do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 ALLI BREEN, COMEDIAN: I mean, I think dads are lucky to get a phone call for the most. Poor dads.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Whoa. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: A lot of the money that's being spent is their own money too. That's the other part of it.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's fair. It's maybe the opposite of the perceived wage gap. Women, they say, make two-thirds less than men. So now moms get two-thirds more from others. All fair in love and war.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 So, Abby, is that what's going on here? It's just a vendetta?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ABBY HORNACEK, "PARK'D" HOST: I think there are a lot of explanations, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And just so you know, just because they're spending less does not make you any less appreciated. I hope, though, that being said, your kids got you Adele tickets for Father's Day, because you deserve that.\par \par \uc2 But I think another explanation --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they're very pricey. So they usually just download a song and leave it at that.\par \par \uc2 But it is interesting, right?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: That works too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, we see this year in and year out. And I'm just wondering what's behind it.\par \par \uc2 You're both saying, obviously, there's more affection for mom and all that, all she does. I get that. And she brought you into the world and blah, blah, blah.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, though, why this gap widens every year. It actually does. It gets widened. I could dare say, within another few years, it's going to be pennies for dads.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead, Alli, you first.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Go ahead, Alli.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Oh, I was just going to say, I think the part of it is moms want more stuff. And it's easier. You can get them spa treatments. You can get them jewelry.\par \par \uc2 For dads,they were saying golf, fishing equipment. They don't want it because they pick their own. And I guess getting barbecue stuff is the equivalent of getting a washing machine for mom. You're just putting them to work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, I see.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: So, yes, I think he probably wants less, and moms maybe want a little more.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, well that's interesting, Abby, because they have done surveys on this of what fathers really want. And apparently the fishing and the golf gear and the wine shirts, maybe we want to pick that all out ourselves. That, I can understand.\par \par \uc2 Dads, apparently -- and I -- this, I really rallied around. We just want to eat. And anything that goes with eating, like going out for a meal, that's a big deal, a special getaway. I would add in there, that getaway better involve food. Beer is important. Spending time with the kids, which is interesting, falls dead last.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just following between that and the hotels staying, all that is good. But what does that tell you about dads and their priorities? I think they're actually very economical. Abby, to you on that.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Yes, maybe I feel like dads are very simple. They don't want much.\par \par \uc2 I was a little offended that spending time with kids was number four.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. What do you mean by simple? What do you mean by simple?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: They find joy in the little things, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, there you go.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: And so do moms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You covered well.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: But it's funny, because I called my dad in preparation for this, and I just -- I already got him something, but I called him and I said, hey, what do you want for Father's Day? And he goes, nothing, which makes it worse, because then you have to think of something else.\par \par \uc2 And then he goes, I just want to play ping-pong. And so maybe you could just give your dad a win in ping-pong. I had to say that, let dad win on Father's Day, because I think my dad's watching this, just in case I lose.\par \par \uc2 But I think they just want memories and they want the simple things in life, because that's what brings them joy. Maybe we have argued for dad for once.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, that's interesting.\par \par \uc2 Alli, I think moms and dads have a lot in common. And I think that they're just loving to be with their children. That's something.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, sometimes, kids get to a certain age, I'm sure you never did this, where they don't want to be with either mom or dad. I mean, I was a rock star when my kids were little.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that wore off fast.\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I just wonder, can you convey the importance of just kids being with their dads on this special day, Alli?\par \par \uc2 BREEN: Yes, I think it's important, for sure.\par \par \uc2 And I think kids will have fun with their parents. It sounds like, according to the numbers you listed, the dads need some beer before they hang out with the kids.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREEN: So that went right in order.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That was not what was said there.\par \par \uc2 Abby, your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I mean, if you're hanging out with your kids and they're of age, you could drink beer with your kids.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Touche.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: And you could make them dinner. You could make all of those top five happen in one single day, if you really thought about it.\par \par \uc2 So I'm all about it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's brilliant. That's brilliant.\par \par \uc2 By the way, sixth on that list was processed meats and cheeses. I didn't tell you guys. So you're right. You're onto something there.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, ladies, thank you both very much. Good having your insights on this.\par \par \uc2 And, guys and fellow dads out there, we should gather this day and appreciate the day, because few other people do.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240614ek6e0002t\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgap